// SignatureIndex — the WHOLE-PROGRAM DECLARATION index: every function, every ranged typealias and
// every top-level constant DECLARED in any file, read straight off the TOKENS before any body is
// parsed.
//
// WHY IT MUST EXIST, AND WHY IN THE PARSER. Three source-level decisions cannot be postponed past
// the parser, and each needs a declaration that may live in another file:
//
//   • A CALL'S RESULT TYPE. A bool `and`/`or` is CONTROL FLOW (`Parser.emitShortCircuit` — blocks
//     and a merge phi, which no later pass can retrofit); `not` picks a different OPCODE per
//     operand type; and every operand rule in TypeRules reads a value's tag. By the time a later
//     pass could supply a callee's return type, every decision that needed it has already been
//     made.
//
//   • A SHIFT'S FILL. `shr` is ARITHMETIC on a signed left operand and LOGICAL on an unsigned one
//     (TypeRules' ⭐ THE SHIFT RULE), and Maxon's unsigned type is a ranged alias whose low bound
//     is 0. The two are DIFFERENT x64 instructions, and the Std tier they lower into is TYPELESS —
//     an i64 there carries no signedness — so the opcode must be chosen while the declared type is
//     still in view, which is here. `u64.max shr 60` is 15 or -1 depending on it.
//
//   • A TOP-LEVEL CONSTANT'S VALUE. A `let` at module scope is a COMPILE-TIME CONSTANT that every
//     use INLINES as a literal (`Parser.topLevelConstantReference`), so the parser needs the NUMBER,
//     not a promise of one. It cannot fold the initializer itself: a forward reference
//     (`let TOTAL = FIRST + SECOND` above `let FIRST = 30`) and a cross-file `export let` are both
//     ordinary, and neither is in view while one file is parsed. So the fold happens HERE, after
//     every file's declarations are known — see `evaluateInitializers`.
//
// So the RANGES travel beside the return types, and the CONSTANTS beside both. An alias declared in
// `a.maxon` and used in `b.maxon` has to reach `b`'s parse, or the shift in `b` is a silent wrong
// answer — and reading the file-local alias table instead would be the identical mistake this index
// exists to fix, wearing a different hat.
//
// The FILE-LOCAL version of this — one token sweep of the file being parsed — is why the
// single-file case was right. Cross-file it was not, and the failure was a WRONG ANSWER, not a
// rejection:
//
//     a.maxon:  export function isReady() returns bool
//     b.maxon:  let x = isReady()
//               return x + 41        ⇒ shv2 COMPILED it, and returned 42
//                                    ⇒ bootstrap: E2004: Cannot operate on bool and int
//
// The parser reported an out-of-file callee as `MaxonType.unresolved`; TypeRules maps that to
// `TypeClass.unknown`; and `unknown` AGREES WITH EVERYTHING. That deferral is the right move for a
// type you genuinely cannot see, and a catastrophic one for a type you simply did not LOOK for.
// **The deferral was never the defect. Not looking was.** So this index looks — at every file's
// tokens, before any file's parse — and `unresolved` is no longer an answer for a callee some file
// declares. (Its twin was worse: agreement satisfied by deferral let `(boolean, unresolved)` reach
// `wordOpResultTag`'s bool-first arm, so `flag and crossFileInt()` MINTED a merge phi tagged
// `boolean` carrying the int 7.)
//
// THE ID PROBLEM — why a signature travels by NAME. A `MaxonType.named(id)` is meaningless outside
// the `TypeNameInterner` that minted the id, and this index is built BEFORE, and APART FROM, the
// per-file artifact interners the parser fills (ParseStaging's parallel-ready discipline: a file's
// parse touches no shared state). So the index owns its own interner, and a parser ADOPTS a
// callee's return type out of it — the type's NAME is re-interned into the parser's own artifact
// table, so the `MaxonType` handed back is indistinguishable from one that file had parsed itself.
// `returnTypeOf` is the one door, and it is the one place the two id spaces meet.

// The shape discriminators the signature hash mixes — one per arm of the two type unions, so two
// declarations that differ only in shape (`returns bool` vs `returns int`) cannot hash alike.
//
// Small distinct constants, NOT the enums' ordinals: an ordinal is not a value in Maxon, and — the
// real reason — a hash that moved when an enum's arms were REORDERED would invalidate every parse
// memo in the project for a change that means nothing.
let ReturnShapeVoid = 1 as ContentHash
let ReturnShapeValue = 2 as ContentHash
let TypeShapeBoolean = 3 as ContentHash
let TypeShapeInteger = 4 as ContentHash
let TypeShapeFloat = 5 as ContentHash
let TypeShapeNamed = 6 as ContentHash
let TypeShapeExitCode = 7 as ContentHash
let TypeShapeUnresolved = 8 as ContentHash

// A ranged typealias's declaration, mixed into the same hash as a function's. Its own
// discriminator, so a file that replaces a function with an alias of the same name moves the hash
// rather than colliding two differently-shaped declarations on it.
let DeclShapeAlias = 9 as ContentHash

// A declared struct type's shape discriminator — `DeclShapeAlias`'s twin, for its reason: a name
// that stops being a `typealias` and starts being a `type` must move the hash, not collide on it.
let DeclShapeStruct = 14 as ContentHash

// A declared enum/union's shape discriminator — `DeclShapeStruct`'s twin, for its reason: a name
// that stops being a `type` and starts being an `enum` must move the hash rather than collide.
let DeclShapeEnum = 20 as ContentHash

// Whether a declared type is a `union` (vs an `enum`), mixed into the hash beside its name. Two
// distinct constants because the only thing separating a payload-free union from an enum is the
// diagnostic WORDING, and a reader that meets a non-exhaustive match must re-parse — and so print
// "union" rather than "enum" — when `enum` becomes `union`.
let EnumKindEnum = 21 as ContentHash
let EnumKindUnion = 22 as ContentHash

// Whether any case of a declared enum/union WROTE a raw value (W81) — two distinct constants, mixed beside
// the kind above and for exactly its reason: the two shapes are otherwise indistinguishable to this hash, and
// what separates them is how a reading file renders an interpolated value of the type. See
// `EnumLayout.declaresRawValues`.
let EnumAutoTagged = 101 as ContentHash
let EnumDeclaresRawValues = 102 as ContentHash

// ⭐ **HOW A DECLARED ENUM'S TAGS ARE TO BE READ** (`EnumBacking`), mixed beside the case list for
// `EnumKindEnum`'s reason one step sharper: the two backings can produce the SAME tag column
// (`z = 0` and `z = 0.0` both fold to 0), so without a discriminator of its own the edit between them is
// invisible to every reading file's parse memo — while it changes what those files compile `"{z}"` and
// `z * 2.0` to. Three constants because there are three states and `unresolved` (an enum with no cases
// yet) is one of them.
let EnumBackingUnresolved = 67 as ContentHash
let EnumBackingInteger = 68 as ContentHash
let EnumBackingFloat = 69 as ContentHash
let EnumBackingString = 79 as ContentHash
let EnumBackingCharacter = 80 as ContentHash
let EnumBackingFunctionRef = 81 as ContentHash
let EnumBackingStructRecord = 82 as ContentHash

// The discriminator for one backing. A `match` rather than a chained `if`, so a new backing cannot be added
// without being given a hash constant here — which is exactly how the string and character ones arrived.
function enumBackingDiscriminator(backing EnumBacking) returns ContentHash
	return match backing 'b'
		unresolved gives EnumBackingUnresolved
		integer gives EnumBackingInteger
		float gives EnumBackingFloat
		string gives EnumBackingString
		character gives EnumBackingCharacter
		functionRef gives EnumBackingFunctionRef
		structRecord gives EnumBackingStructRecord
	end 'b'
end 'enumBackingDiscriminator'

// ⭐ One FIELD of a constant struct backing, mixed per field so a second field cannot be absorbed by the
// first. Its own discriminator for `PayloadShapeSlot`'s reason: a reading file compiles `op.rawValue.latency`
// down to the CONSTANTS this column holds, so `add = Meta{value: 1}` edited to `Meta{value: 2}` changes what
// every other file emits while that file's own bytes never move — the staleness `mixEnumCase`'s raw-value
// line already exists to catch, for the one backing whose value is not in `caseRawValues` at all.
let EnumBackingFieldSlot = 83 as ContentHash

// The ENUM-CASE form of such a field's value (`Priority.low`), separated from the LITERAL form so
// `Priority.low` and the bare number `0` cannot fold alike — they are resolved by different rules and one
// of them moves when `Priority`'s case list is edited.
let EnumBackingFieldEnumCase = 84 as ContentHash

// The NESTED STRUCT form (`inner: Inner{value: 1}`), separated from the other two for their reason: it holds
// a type NAME where the enum-case form holds two, and an entry that owns a group must not fold like a leaf
// that happens to carry the same bytes.
let EnumBackingFieldNestedStruct = 88 as ContentHash

// ⭐ Which LITERAL a backing field was written as. `1` and `1.0` carry the same `ParsedInt`, and they
// type-check differently against the field's declared type — so without this they fold alike and a file that
// edits one into the other leaves every reader's parse memo standing. `enumBackingDiscriminator`'s reason,
// one column further in, and a `match` for the same reason: a fourth literal kind cannot be added without
// being given a number here.
let EnumBackingLiteralInteger = 85 as ContentHash
let EnumBackingLiteralFloat = 86 as ContentHash
let EnumBackingLiteralBoolean = 87 as ContentHash

function enumBackingLiteralDiscriminator(kind EnumBackingLiteralKind) returns ContentHash
	return match kind 'k'
		integerLiteral gives EnumBackingLiteralInteger
		floatLiteral gives EnumBackingLiteralFloat
		booleanLiteral gives EnumBackingLiteralBoolean
	end 'k'
end 'enumBackingLiteralDiscriminator'

// One `implements <Interface>` name on a declaration (A1s wave 2), mixed per name so a SECOND interface
// moves the hash rather than being absorbed by the first. Its own discriminator for `DeclShapeStruct`'s
// reason: the list is a distinct fact from the case or field list beside it. The KIND it hangs off is
// already in the hash (`DeclShapeEnum` / `DeclShapeStruct`), so one discriminator serves both.
let DeclShapeDeclaredConformance = 55 as ContentHash

// One `with <TypeArg>` an `implements` clause BINDS (conditional extensions). ⚠ It needs a discriminator of
// its OWN, and sharing the one above would be a live collision rather than a tidiness point:
// `implements A, B` and `implements A with B` are two different declarations that both offer the byte
// sequences `A` then `B` to this mixer, and they decide different things (which extensions apply, versus
// what the extended interface's associated type is bound to).
//
// ⚠ **IT HELD 68 — `EnumBackingInteger`'s NUMBER — until BATCH22, which is the very collision the paragraph
// above argues it must not have.** The two are mixed into the ONE rolling `self.hash` from the two sides of
// one declaration space: this one from `recordStruct`'s `conformanceBindings` fold, `EnumBackingInteger` from
// `recordEnum`'s backing fold, and the hash is a single running value across every declaration in the
// program. So the argument for a discriminator of its own was written down and then not applied. Found while
// giving a union PAYLOAD its own pair; every number from 72 up was free.
//
// ⚠ The BATCH22 commit message says `recordEnum` mixes both "four lines apart"; it does not — `recordEnum`
// mixes the backing and `DeclShapeDeclaredConformance`. Corrected here rather than left standing, because a
// WHY that names the wrong site is what the next reader would check against.
let DeclShapeConformanceBinding = 72 as ContentHash

// ⭐ **THE `implements` LIST'S CONTRIBUTION TO A DECLARATION'S CONTENT HASH — ONE STATEMENT, BOTH KINDS AND
// BOTH HALVES.** The reason the list must ride is the same on each and it is `recordStruct`'s rule: a
// conformance changes what ANOTHER file compiles to while that file's own bytes never move. For an enum it
// decides whether a `throws E` conformance is accepted (`ConformanceCheck.requireImplErrorConformsToRequirement`);
// for a `type` it decides which INTERFACE EXTENSION methods that type has at all, which is a whole set of
// functions appearing in or vanishing from the program — and, through the `with` BINDINGS, which of a
// CONDITIONAL extension's methods survive its `where` clause. `shape` names which half is being mixed.
function mixDeclaredConformances(state ContentHash, conformsTo ByteArrayArray, shape ContentHash) returns ContentHash
	var out = state
	for name in conformsTo 'eachConformance'
		out = mixBytes(mix(out, value: shape), bytes: name)
	end 'eachConformance'
	return out
end 'mixDeclaredConformances'

// ⭐ **THE `with` BINDINGS' HALF OF THE STATEMENT ABOVE, NOW THAT A CLAUSE MAY BIND AT EVERY NAME (W95)** —
// one list per interface, so the column is nested and a flat walk of it could not tell
// `implements A with X, B` from `implements A, B with X`.
//
// ⚠ **EACH LIST'S LENGTH IS MIXED AHEAD OF ITS CONTENTS, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES THE NESTING VISIBLE.**
// Without it `[[X], [Y]]` and `[[X, Y], []]` hash identically — two different programs (one binds `Y` at the
// second interface, the other binds it at the first), and a reader served the wrong memo would substitute
// one conformer's associated type for another's.
function mixConformanceBindings(state ContentHash, bindings ConformanceBindingArray) returns ContentHash
	var out = state
	for perInterface in bindings 'eachConformance'
		out = mixDeclaredConformances(mix(out, value: perInterface.count()), conformsTo: perInterface, shape: DeclShapeConformanceBinding)
	end 'eachConformance'
	return out
end 'mixConformanceBindings'

// The `MaxonType.structRef` arm's discriminator. It is DISTINCT from `TypeShapeNamed`, and that is
// load-bearing rather than tidy: `named(Point)` and `structRef(Point)` carry the same NAME and mean
// different things in every reader — one is a ranged int, the other a pointer — so a hash that
// could not tell them apart would hand a reader a memo built under the wrong one.
let TypeShapeStructRef = 15 as ContentHash

// The `MaxonType.string` arm's discriminator (P1.2 wave A). Distinct like every other shape: a
// `String` return type hashes differently from an `int` one, so a memo built under one cannot be
// served for the other.
let TypeShapeString = 23 as ContentHash

// A FUNCTION value's type shape (P1.5-A1) — so a `returns UnaryOp` return type hashes distinctly. Its
// own discriminator for `TypeShapeStructRef`'s reason. (A function type reaches the SCANNED signature
// path only if a future producer stores one; the sweep records a function-alias reference as `named`
// until the alias is known, so today this arm is exhaustiveness insurance, hashed not panicked.)
let TypeShapeFunction = 29 as ContentHash

// A TYPE-PARAMETER reference's shape (P1.6-A) — `get() returns T` hashes distinctly. Its TOKEN is
// mixed after the shape (W14: a digest of `(declaring type, parameter name)`, so mixing it IS mixing
// those two names and NOT an intern-order id — see `mixType`'s arm, which carries the argument), so
// `uses A, B`'s `returns A` and `returns B` hash apart, and so do two DIFFERENT types' first
// parameters, which the position it replaced hashed alike. A distinct prime for `TypeShapeStructRef`'s
// reason.
let TypeShapeTypeParameter = 31 as ContentHash

// A GENERIC-INSTANCE's shape (P1.6-A) — a `returns IntBox` hashes distinctly. The BASE name and each
// ARG's shape are mixed after it (names, never ids), so `Box with Integer` and `Box with Bool` hash
// apart. A distinct prime for `TypeShapeStructRef`'s reason.
let TypeShapeGenericInstance = 37 as ContentHash

// A `Character`'s shape (P1.8 slice B) — a builtin with no name to mix, exactly like `TypeShapeString`.
// A DISTINCT prime from it, and that is the whole point: a Character and a String share a RECORD but not
// a TYPE, so `returns String` and `returns Character` must hash apart or a signature change between them
// would serve a stale parse from every other file's memo.
let TypeShapeCharacter = 41 as ContentHash

// An EXISTENTIAL's shape (P1.7a-existentials slice 1) — the INTERFACE's name is mixed after it, so
// `returns Producer` and `returns Consumer` hash apart. A DISTINCT prime from `TypeShapeStructRef`, and
// for a sharper reason than that one's: an interface name and a struct name live in the same interner, so
// sharing the prime would make `returns Backend` (the interface) and a hypothetical `returns Backend` (a
// struct of that name) hash identically — and a declaration that changed which one the name denotes would
// serve every other file a stale parse whose dispatch is a witness slot rather than a direct call.
let TypeShapeInterfaceRef = 43 as ContentHash

// A declared FIELD's mutability, mixed into the hash beside its name and type (P1.1a wave 2). The
// struct twin of `DeclShapeConstant`/`DeclShapeMutable`, and distinct constants rather than a reuse
// of them: those two discriminate a top-level `let` from a `var`, and colliding a field's `let` with
// a global's would let an edit that swaps one for the other land on the hash the other already
// occupied. What rides is ACCEPTANCE — `var count` → `let count` must turn every other file's
// `c.count = 5` into E2013, and a reader whose memo did not move would go on compiling it.
let FieldShapeMutable = 16 as ContentHash
let FieldShapeImmutable = 17 as ContentHash

// Whether a field declares a DEFAULT, mixed beside the payload when it does. Two constants and not
// one, because "has no default" must hash differently from "defaults to 0" — the payload alone
// cannot say so, and those two declarations accept different programs (`Self{}` is E3086 under the
// first and legal under the second).
let FieldShapeNoDefault = 18 as ContentHash
let FieldShapeDefaulted = 19 as ContentHash

// ⭐ A field whose default is an ARBITRARY EXPRESSION (S2f) — a THIRD constant beside those two, for their
// exact reason one step out: a field that gains an expression default accepts programs the other two do
// not (`Self{}` is legal, and the box holds a freshly-called value rather than a folded constant), so it
// must hash differently from both. It rides ALONE — see `mixFieldDefault` for why the expression must not.
let FieldShapeDefaultHelper = 75 as ContentHash

// The two shapes a top-level constant's ANSWER hashes under: it evaluated to a value, or it did
// not. Their own discriminators for `DeclShapeAlias`'s reason — a name that stops being a function
// and starts being a constant must move the hash rather than collide.
let ConstShapeValue = 10 as ContentHash
let ConstShapeNoValue = 11 as ContentHash

// The THIRD shape a top-level constant's answer hashes under (P1.7a): it folded to a managed LITERAL.
// Its own discriminator for `ConstShapeValue`'s reason — a `let X = 0` that becomes `let X = "0"` must
// move the hash, and the payload alone cannot say so (the bytes `"0"` and the number 0 are different
// facts that a shared shape would let collide).
let ConstShapeManagedValue = 45 as ContentHash

// Which managed literal a constant folded to, mixed beside its bytes (P1.7a). Two constants because a
// `String` and an `Array with Byte` over the SAME bytes are different types with different records, so
// a reader that saw one must re-parse when it becomes the other.
let ManagedShapeString = 46 as ContentHash
let ManagedShapeByteArray = 47 as ContentHash

// The FOURTH shape a top-level binding's answer hashes under (P1.7 slice 2b): it folded to an ARRAY
// LITERAL. Its own discriminator for `ConstShapeManagedValue`'s reason — `let X = "ab"` becoming
// `let X = [1, 2]` turns every reader's materialized literal into a `.data` load, and nothing else on
// the hash says so.
let ConstShapeArrayValue = 48 as ContentHash

// Which ELEMENT type an array literal folded to, mixed beside its label (P1.7 slice 2b). Two constants
// for `ManagedShapeString`/`ManagedShapeByteArray`'s reason: `Array with Integer` and `Array with String`
// are different types, and a reader types its load from the instance this picks.
let ArrayElementShapeInteger = 49 as ContentHash
let ArrayElementShapeString = 50 as ContentHash

// The FIFTH shape a top-level binding's answer hashes under (spec-port `ownership`): it folded to a
// CONTAINER FACTORY CALL. Its own discriminator for `ConstShapeArrayValue`'s reason — `var g = [1, 2]`
// becoming `var g = IntArray.create()` keeps the label and the load and changes the RECORD every reader's
// program is built over, and nothing else on the hash says so.
//
// ⚠ What rides beside it is the instance's MANGLED NAME and not its gid: a gid is interned per compile and
// is a function of what else the program happened to intern, so hashing one would invalidate every reader's
// memo whenever an unrelated instance appeared ahead of it. The mangled name is a function of the type
// alone, which is exactly what a reader depends on.
let ConstShapeContainerValue = 70 as ContentHash

// A top-level binding that folded to a USER STATIC FACTORY CALL (spec-port `map-struct-bytearray`). Its
// own discriminator for `ConstShapeContainerValue`'s reason, one step further: `var db = IntArray.create()`
// becoming `var db = Database.create(…)` keeps the label and the load and changes the slot's TYPE, so
// every reader's field access is built over a different record.
//
// ⚠ What rides beside it is the CALLEE's mangled `Type.method` name — a function of the source alone, for
// the reason stated above — and deliberately not the arguments, which reach only `__module_init`.
let ConstShapeFactoryValue = 71 as ContentHash

// ⛔⛔ **`ConstShapeCharacterSetValue` HELD 103 AND WENT AT W129 WITH THE OUTCOME IT DISCRIMINATED, BUT ITS
// LESSON IS THE NUMBER SPACE AND THAT DOES NOT GO WITH IT.** It held **72** — `DeclShapeConformanceBinding`'s
// number — for eight days, the THIRD time this space has had two claimants for one number (W81 review):
// BATCH22 (`7523c881c1`) took 72 for the express reason that sharing a discriminator "would be a live
// collision rather than a tidiness point", and the `lazy-static` port (`dc395d6958`) then took the same 72
// for a constant whose own header claimed a discriminator "of its own". Both are mixed into the ONE rolling
// `self.hash` running across every declaration in the program, so that claim was false the moment it was
// written — exactly as BATCH22's header says its own predecessor's was.
//
// ⚠ **AND THE NUMBER SPACE IS STILL WRITTEN DOWN RATHER THAN DERIVED**, so nothing in the compiler can
// refuse the fourth occurrence: these are ~60 free-standing `let`s, and a duplicate compiles. The cure is to
// make the space a declaration order rather than a literal — reported for its own rung, not taken here.

// A declaration's MUTABILITY, mixed into the hash beside its value. `let X = 5` and `var X = 5`
// are the same name and the same number and compile to COMPLETELY different code — one inlines
// as a literal, the other loads from `.data` — so a reader's parse memo must move when one
// becomes the other. Without these, changing `let` to `var` would leave every reader's memo
// valid and every reader still inlining a number that now has storage behind it.
let DeclShapeConstant = 12 as ContentHash
let DeclShapeMutable = 13 as ContentHash

// A function's PER-PARAMETER CONSUME BITS (P1.4a wave 2), mixed into the hash beside its name so a
// cross-file caller re-parses when a callee's consume set changes. What rides is ACCEPTANCE and OWNERSHIP:
// a param that becomes CONSUMED turns every caller's `f(ownedLocal)` into a move (the local is poisoned,
// E3102 on reuse) and its `f(borrowedValue)` into E2015, while the caller's own bytes never move — so a
// reader whose memo did not move would keep the old drop behaviour, a double-free or a leak. Its own
// discriminator for `DeclShapeAlias`'s reason. `ConsumeBitSet`/`ConsumeBitClear` distinguish a set bit
// from a clear one so "param 0 consumed, param 1 not" hashes differently from the reverse.
let DeclShapeParamConsume = 24 as ContentHash
let ConsumeBitSet = 25 as ContentHash
let ConsumeBitClear = 26 as ContentHash

// A function's `throws E` clause discriminator (P1.4b). Its own discriminator for `DeclShapeAlias`'s
// reason: a callee that GAINS or CHANGES a throws type changes whether each call site needs a `try`
// (E3055/E3057) and what type the `(e)` binding recovers, so a caller's parse memo must move when the
// callee's throws clause does. Only THROWING functions are recorded (absence = non-throwing default),
// exactly as `recordParamConsume` records only consuming ones — so a non-throwing function costs no
// hash movement.
let DeclShapeThrows = 27 as ContentHash

// ⭐ A declared RETURN TYPE's pre-erasure SPELLING (A2j / A2m) — the `DeclaredSurface` word `mixDeclaration`
// folds in beside the return type. It is the one declaration fact `mixReturnType` structurally cannot carry:
// `__ManagedMemory` resolves to the SAME `genericInstance` a `ByteArray` does, so an edit between the two
// spellings moves the return TYPE not at all while changing which member roster every caller's `let m = f()`
// may then reach. A DISCRIMINATOR plus the word, rather than a tag pair, because A2m made the fact a word
// and not a bit: mixing the word alone would let a plain surface (0) collide with the seed.
let ReturnShapeSurface = 56 as ContentHash

// ⭐ A declared struct FIELD's pre-erasure SPELLING (A2j / A2m) — `ReturnShapeSurface`'s twin, one
// declaration kind over, and load-bearing for the identical reason: `var buf as __ManagedMemory` and
// `var buf as ByteArray` fold identically through `mixField`'s type, while a read of that field reaches a
// different roster. Mixed by `mixField`, so it rides `recordStruct`'s one hash write like every other
// per-field fact.
let FieldShapeSurface = 58 as ContentHash

// ⭐ ONE union case PAYLOAD SLOT (BATCH22) — the discriminator that opens a payload's contribution to
// `mixEnumCase`, so a payload's NAME cannot be absorbed by the case name it follows and a case that GAINED a
// slot cannot collide with the one it grew from. `DeclShapeDeclaredConformance`'s reason, one column over.
let PayloadShapeSlot = 73 as ContentHash

// ⭐ A union PAYLOAD's pre-erasure SPELLING (BATCH22) — `FieldShapeSurface`'s twin at the fifth declared
// position, load-bearing for the identical reason: `mem(m __ManagedMemory)` and `mem(m ByteArray)` fold
// identically through `mixPayloadField`'s type, while the `match` binding out of them reaches a different
// member roster.
let PayloadShapeSurface = 74 as ContentHash

// A FUNCTION typealias's shape discriminator (P1.5-A1) — `DeclShapeAlias`'s twin, for its reason: a name
// that stops being a ranged alias and becomes a function one, or whose function return type changes,
// changes what a caller binding a value of it compiles to, so a caller's parse memo must move with it.
let DeclShapeFunctionAlias = 28 as ContentHash

// A generic-instance TYPEALIAS's shape discriminator (P1.6-A) — `DeclShapeAlias`'s twin. `IntBox =
// Box with Integer` changing base or args changes what `IntBox.create(…)` resolves to and type-checks
// against in ANY file, so a reader's parse memo must move with it.
let DeclShapeGenericAlias = 30 as ContentHash

// A declared generic type's TYPE-PARAMETER shape discriminator (P1.6-A). A change to `type Box uses T`'s
// param list — a rename or, load-bearingly, a change in COUNT — changes which `Box with …` instantiations
// are the right arity in any file, so a reader's parse memo must move with the count.
let DeclShapeTypeParam = 32 as ContentHash

// A declared generic type's `where`-CONSTRAINT shape discriminator (P1.7a slice 2a). A change to `type Box
// uses T where T is Digest`'s constraint list — an interface swapped, added, or dropped — changes which
// `Box with …` instantiations are LEGAL (E3017) in any file and which witness table each dispatch reads, so
// a reader's parse memo must move with it. The `DeclShapeTypeParam` twin for the constraint half.
let DeclShapeWhereConstraint = 44 as ContentHash

// A constructor parameter's TYPE-PARAMETER-FEED shape discriminators (P1.6-B2). Which type-parameter
// position a parameter feeds decides whether a caller consumes a managed argument there, so a change to a
// constructor's feed map must move a caller's parse memo — the consume twin of `ConsumeBitSet`/`Clear`.
let FeedShapeFeeds = 38 as ContentHash
let FeedShapeNone = 39 as ContentHash

// The feed's SINK (W10). It decides whether the CALLEE is enrolled owner of the fed parameter, and whether a
// BORROWED argument at that position is retained or given a scope lifetime — two different emitted programs
// for the same position, so a change to it must move a caller's parse memo exactly as the position does.
let FeedSinkConstructedValueShape = 76 as ContentHash
let FeedSinkCalleeStorageShape = 77 as ContentHash

// A generic type's INNER ranged typealias shape discriminator (P1.6-C) — `DeclShapeAlias`'s twin, for its
// reason: a change to `type Wrapper`'s `typealias Idx` (its range, or whether it exists) changes what
// `Instance.Idx` resolves and type-checks against in ANY file, so a reader's parse memo must move with it.
let DeclShapeInnerAlias = 40 as ContentHash

// An `interface` DECLARATION's shape discriminator (R7 for its `uses` arity, R8 for its requirements) —
// `DeclShapeTypeParam`'s twin, and load-bearing for exactly its reason. The arity decides how many arguments
// an unparenthesized `implements I with A, B` binds (`Parser.interfaceUsesArity`), so adding or removing a
// `uses` name changes what a conformance in ANY file PARSES — the comma after an argument stops being another
// argument and becomes another interface. A reader that did not re-parse would go on binding the old count.
let DeclShapeInterface = 51 as ContentHash

// One interface METHOD's shape discriminator (R8) — `DeclShapeInterface`'s per-requirement twin, and its own
// so that moving a requirement between two interfaces moves the hash. A witness dispatch in any file reads
// this method's slot index, result type, formals and ABI (`Parser.findWitnessDispatchCandidates`) and bakes all of them
// into its own emitted code, so a change here has to re-parse every reader. See `recordInterfaceDeclaration`
// for which fields ride and which deliberately do not.
let DeclShapeInterfaceMethod = 52 as ContentHash

// One `extends` PARENT NAME's shape discriminator (R10c) — `DeclShapeInterfaceMethod`'s twin, and its own
// for that twin's reason: a witness dispatch numbers its slot against the interface's TRANSITIVE requirement
// list (`interfaceWitnessSlots`), so gaining, losing or REORDERING a parent moves a slot index that another
// file has already baked into its emitted code. Only the NAME is mixed — the parent's own requirements reach
// this hash through the parent's own `recordInterfaceDeclaration`, one hash being folded over every
// declaration in the program.
let DeclShapeInterfaceParent = 60 as ContentHash

// Does a declaration carry a hidden `self`? — a PRESENCE PAIR for `mixThrowsClause`'s reason and not a
// marker on the receiverless arm alone: both kinds are possible in every position that mixes it, so a hash
// that mixed nothing for the instance arm would let a declaration GAIN or LOSE `static` without any reader's
// memo moving. ONE pair for both positions that ask it, because it is one distinction:
//
//   • an INTERFACE REQUIREMENT's (A2w review). It rides because a PARSE reads it:
//     `Parser.requireWitnessDispatchCandidates` REFUSES a dispatch whose requirements are all `static`
//     (E3116) and `IrInterface.witnessTargetIsProtocol` refuses a `static` look-alike as
//     `Equatable`/`Comparable` (E3005), so flipping the keyword in file A decides whether file B compiles at
//     all while file B's own bytes stay put — this hash's stated rule, exactly.
//   • a DECLARATION's, through `mixDeclaration` — see there for the bare-sibling-call reader that spends it.
//
// The receiverless arm covers a top-level free function as well as a `static` method, which is why the pair
// is named for the RECEIVER rather than for the keyword.
let ReceiverShapeAbsent = 61 as ContentHash
let ReceiverShapePresent = 62 as ContentHash

// ⭐ A function's PER-PARAMETER DEFAULTS (BATCH2 slice 4), mixed into the hash beside its name for
// `DeclShapeParamConsume`'s reason and with the identical PRESENCE-PAIR shape. What rides is what a CALL
// SITE compiles to: a parameter that GAINS a default turns a caller's `f()` from E3036 into a legal call
// that emits a helper invocation, and one that LOSES it does the reverse — in both directions the caller's
// own bytes never move, so only this hash can carry it. The pair distinguishes "param 0 defaulted, param 1
// not" from the reverse, exactly as `ConsumeBitSet`/`ConsumeBitClear` do.
// ⭐ **AND THE TWO CALLER-LOCATION ARMS ARE THEIR OWN MARKS, NOT A THIRD SPELLING OF `DefaultBitSet`
// (W72)** — because what a caller COPIES differs between them. A `helper` slot compiles to a `call` whose
// bytes never move; a `callerLine` slot compiles to the caller's own line NUMBER and a `callerFile` slot to
// its path, so a declaration that changes `= __line__` to `= __file__`, or either to an ordinary
// expression, changes what every caller emits while the callers' own bytes stay put. That is exactly the
// condition this hash exists for, and a shared "has a default" bit would fail it silently.
let DeclShapeParamDefault = 63 as ContentHash
let DefaultBitSet = 64 as ContentHash
let DefaultBitClear = 65 as ContentHash
let DefaultBitCallerLine = 99 as ContentHash
let DefaultBitCallerFile = 100 as ContentHash

// ⭐ A method's OPAQUE (bare type-parameter) PARAMETER POSITIONS (ARRR), mixed for `DeclShapeParamConsume`'s
// reason and carrying exactly what a CALL SITE compiles to: which `uses` slot a parameter names decides
// which of the receiver instance's type arguments the range guard in front of that call is built from
// (`Parser.recordOpaqueArgRangeChecks`). Retyping `put(value T)` to `put(value Integer)` — or moving `T` to
// another `uses` slot — changes the caller's emitted code while the caller's own bytes never move, so only
// this hash can carry it. The param NAMES ride for `recordParamConsume`'s reason: a rename changes which
// labelled argument slots to the opaque position.
// ⚠ **89, NOT 82 — IT COLLIDED WITH `EnumBackingStructRecord` UNTIL PBR-2 (found while taking the next free
// number).** Two declaration shapes mixing one discriminator is exactly what a discriminator exists to
// prevent: it lets two DIFFERENT declarations fold to one hash, and that hash is the parse-memo key, so the
// consequence is a reader served a memo from a program it no longer describes. Nothing pinned it because a
// collision needs the two shapes to line up positionally as well; the number is simply wrong, so it is
// corrected rather than left for the day it lines up. The renumber invalidates every memo once, harmlessly.
let DeclShapeOpaqueParams = 89 as ContentHash

// ⭐ A function's per-parameter BY-REFERENCE columns (PBR-2), mixed for `DeclShapeParamConsume`'s reason and
// carrying the strongest thing in this table: the callee's ABI. A parameter that GAINS a reassignment turns
// every caller's argument from a value into a cell address — the caller's own bytes never move, so only this
// hash can carry it, and a stale memo here is not a wrong diagnostic but a dereferenced integer. The param
// NAMES ride for `recordParamConsume`'s reason (a rename moves which labelled argument slots to a by-ref
// position), and the two columns ride separately because `isByRef` combines them with a whole-program gate
// that a single merged bit could not express. The PRESENCE PAIR distinguishes "param 0 by-ref, param 1 not"
// from the reverse, exactly as `ConsumeBitSet`/`ConsumeBitClear` do.
let DeclShapeParamByRef = 90 as ContentHash
let ByRefBitSet = 91 as ContentHash
let ByRefBitClear = 92 as ContentHash

// The three `ForwardCall` shapes and the three `DeclCallability` answers, as distinct hash marks — a
// declaration that MOVES into or out of a type body, and a call that gains or loses a receiver, each change
// an ABI without changing a parameter name, so both must move the memo key.
let BareCallShape = 93 as ContentHash
let ReceiverCallShape = 94 as ContentHash
let SelfCallShape = 95 as ContentHash
let RootFreeFunctionDecl = 96 as ContentHash
let RoutableFreeFunctionDecl = 97 as ContentHash
let MethodDecl = 98 as ContentHash

// ⭐ A function's IMMUTABLE-GLOBAL-RETURN raw material (W117), mixed for `DeclShapeParamConsume`'s reason.
// A body that starts or stops returning a `let` global — or starts returning what some OTHER function
// returns — changes what every caller may do with the result while not one byte of any caller moves, so
// only this hash can carry it. The declaring FILE rides because the same spelling resolves to different
// declarations from different files (`declFor` probes file-scoped before bare), so two identical bodies in
// two files genuinely state two different facts.
let DeclShapeGlobalAliasReturn = 104 as ContentHash

// ⭐ A free function's NAMESPACE-QUALIFIED SPELLING (N1b) — `utils.helper` for a `helper` declared in
// `utils/`. Its own discriminator for `DeclShapeAlias`'s reason, and load-bearing for a reason NO other
// entry in this table carries: it is the one fact keyed on a file's DIRECTORY rather than on its bytes.
// MOVING `utils/helper.maxon` to `tools/helper.maxon` changes not one byte of any file, yet it decides
// whether another file's `utils.helper()` resolves at all — so without this the reader's parse memo would
// survive an edit that changes what the reader compiles to. The bare name rides through `record`; only the
// qualified spelling can carry the directory.
let DeclShapeNamespaceQualifiedFunction = 66 as ContentHash

// ⭐ A `<Conformer>.<method>` PUBLISHED BY EXTENSIONS IN MORE THAN ONE FILE (X-contains) — the entry above's
// property one declaration kind over, and load-bearing for the same reason. The contest decides the
// REGISTRATION NAME every one of those declarations takes (`Parser.overloadRegistrationNameFor`), and it is
// a fact about WHICH FILES declare the name rather than about any one file's bytes: moving an `extension`
// from one file to another can start or end a contest without changing what either declaration SAYS. The
// bare name rides through `record`; only this can carry the contest.
let DeclShapeContestedExtensionMethod = 78 as ContentHash

// ⭐ **THE WHOLE-PROGRAM RE-READ OF ONE `implements` CLAUSE (W95)** — its own discriminator, and not
// `DeclShapeDeclaredConformance`, because the two are mixed at DIFFERENT MOMENTS over the same declaration:
// `recordStruct` mixes what the sweep could prove, and `refoldConformanceClause` mixes what the complete
// index re-read. Sharing one shape would let a clause whose extent CHANGED between the two hash as a clause
// that was read once and correctly — which is exactly the memo staleness this re-read exists to close.
let DeclShapeRefoldedConformance = 91 as ContentHash

// A `throws` clause's PRESENCE discriminators (R8) — the two arms of `ThrowsClause`, so `none` and
// `throwing(E)` cannot hash alike in a position where both are possible. `mixThrowsClause` is the one place a
// throws clause enters a content hash; before it existed the only mixer recorded THROWING clauses alone, where
// absence was itself the fact and no `none` marker was needed.
let ThrowsShapeNone = 53 as ContentHash
let ThrowsShapeThrowing = 54 as ContentHash

// The separator between a file's path and a constant's bare name in a FILE-PRIVATE constant's
// storage key. `|` is legal in neither a Maxon identifier nor a Windows path, so the composite is
// unambiguous. Ported verbatim from v1's `fileScopedTopLevelKey` (Project.maxon:189-191), which
// solves the identical problem the identical way.
let FileScopedKeySeparator = "|"

// An index into the constant ARENA (`ProgramSignatures.decls`). Wide range: declaration counts
// are unbounded.
export typealias TopLevelDeclIndex = int(0 to u64.max)
export typealias TopLevelDeclIndexArray = Array with TopLevelDeclIndex
typealias TopLevelDeclIndexMap = Map with (ByteArray, TopLevelDeclIndex)

// How many of those declarations have a `.data` slot — a SUBSET of the arena, so the same wide range.
// See `ProgramSignatures.storedGlobalCount`.
export typealias StoredGlobalCount = int(0 to u64.max)

// WHERE a top-level constant is filed, and the ONE place a constant's visibility becomes a fact
// about its NAME.
//
// A FILE-PRIVATE constant is filed under a key carrying its declaring file's path; an EXPORTED one
// under its bare name. That single distinction is what makes `file-private-same-name-cross-file`
// work — two files may each declare `let SHARED` with a different value, and each file's reads
// resolve to its OWN — and it is why "reject duplicates" is not merely unnecessary here but
// FORBIDDEN by the spec.
//
// Ported from v1's `topLevelConstStorageName` (Parser.maxon:3221-3226) + `fileScopedTopLevelKey`
// (Project.maxon:189-191). v1's rule has three conditions (a bare name, file-private, immutable);
// shv2's has ONE, and P1.0d.5b is why the third is gone.
//
// ⭐ **THE KEY EXTENDS TO A `var`. THE LABEL IS WHAT DOES NOT.** This comment used to say the
// opposite — "when the `var` rung lands it MUST NOT extend this scoping" — and it was half right
// in a way worth preserving, because the half it saw is real:
//
//   • IDENTITY must be scoped. Two files may each declare a file-private `counter`
//     (`global-file-private-same-name.md`, and `top-level-let.md` states the same rule for a
//     `let`), and each file's reads must resolve to ITS OWN. That is a property of the KEY, it is
//     the same property for both kinds of declaration, and rejecting the second declaration is not
//     an available reading of the spec.
//   • What the old comment correctly saw is that a `var` needs something a `let` never did: a
//     **LABEL**. A constant inlines at every read, so there is no slot and no name in the IR. A
//     var's slot is real, and a label carrying a FILE PATH would put a temp directory into every
//     golden fragment and into the emitted binary.
//
// It concluded that the key therefore could not be scoped. The actual conclusion is that these are
// TWO facts and want two fields: **keep ONE key, and DERIVE the label from it** (`dataLabelFor`) —
// path-free, bare-name-based, disambiguated only where two keys collide on one label.
//
// ⚠ **v1 CONCEDES THE BUG THIS AVOIDS.** Its `not mutable` condition leaves file-private vars
// sharing one bare key, and its own comment admits the reason is empirical, not structural: "In
// practice no two file-private vars in the self-hosted/stdlib tree share a bare name"
// (Parser.maxon:3206-3214). The C# bootstrap has the same defect and it is MEASURED: its resolver
// is file-scoped while its `.data` label is global-by-name, so `global-file-private-same-name.md`
// returns 212 where 118 is correct — `bumpA()` reads featB's slot. Correctness resting on a
// property of the corpus is not correctness. NONE of v1's four bare-key sites is ported.
function topLevelStorageKey(name ByteArray, visibility Visibility, declFilePath FilePath) returns ByteArray
	if visibility == Visibility.file 'filePrivate'
		return fileScopedDeclKey(name, declFilePath: declFilePath)
	end 'filePrivate'
	return name
end 'topLevelStorageKey'

// The key a FILE-PRIVATE declaration is filed under. Also the first probe `declFor` makes, which
// is why it is a function and not an inlined interpolation at the two sites: a reader and a writer
// that spelled this key differently would file a declaration nothing could ever find.
//
// ⚠ **IT COSTS ~4 ALLOCATIONS PER CALL** — `String.from`, the interpolation, `toByteArray` — and
// `declFor` builds one on EVERY top-level-name probe, before it knows whether the name is even
// file-private. Ranged typealiases resolve by the same scoped-first rule and deliberately do NOT
// come through here: they are asked once per named TYPE reference and would have paid this per use
// of any name two files declare (measured at 24 allocations per use — see `RangedAliasRegistry`).
// They use a two-level map instead, which needs no composite key. This site keeps the string key
// because `declIndex` is one flat map shared with `topLevelStorageKey`'s writes; giving it the same
// treatment is a change to that map's shape and to its writer, not to this function.
// EXPORTED because the P1.9 collision-suppression set is keyed the same way and for the same reason —
// "which FILE's declaration is this?" — and a second composite-key builder would be free to spell the
// separator differently from this one.
export function fileScopedDeclKey(name ByteArray, declFilePath FilePath) returns ByteArray
	return "{declFilePath.toString()}{FileScopedKeySeparator}{String.from(name)}".toByteArray()
end 'fileScopedDeclKey'

// The prefix every top-level `var`'s `.data` label carries — a NAMESPACE, and today nothing more.
//
// ⚠ **IT DOES NOT ROUTE THE SECTION, and an earlier draft of this comment claimed it did.** What
// puts a global in writable `.data` is the RELOCATION KIND: `encodeLeaRegGlobal` (X64Backend) files
// every global's `lea` as `RdataRelocKind.dataSectionRipRelDisp32` unconditionally, and
// `patchRdataRelocationsX86` measures that kind — and only that kind — from `.data`'s RVA. The
// label's TEXT is never consulted on the way. MEASURED: with this constant set to `"zzz_"` the
// suite's globals still land in `.data`, still take their stores, and the program still returns the
// right answer. The claim it "would be classified `rdata` and fault on its first store" was false.
//
// So what it buys is READABILITY — `__data_counter` in a golden fragment says what it is — plus
// v1's convention, which the day a global is registered BY NAME will matter for real.
//
// ⭐ **THIS IS NOW THE ONLY COPY OF THIS STRING.** There was a second — a literal `"__data_"` inside
// `classifyGlobalLabel` (CodeResult.maxon) — and the two could not disagree only because that whole
// family was DEAD: zero callers, ported ahead of a use that never came. It was left in place on the
// prediction that it "plausibly becomes live when the `__slab_`/`__gt_` runtime globals arrive"; P1.0r
// then shipped `__slab_cursor` and `__slab_end` and wired nothing to it, which falsified the
// prediction, so the family was deleted rather than kept as a copy waiting to go stale. A future
// name-based classifier single-sources its prefix HERE.
let DataLabelPrefix = "__data_"

// ⭐ What separates a `.data` label from its collision-breaking ordinal — and the character is
// load-bearing, not cosmetic. **`$` is legal in no Maxon identifier**, so `__data_counter$1` is a
// label only this disambiguator can ever mint: no user-written `var` can collide with one. That is
// exactly what lets `uniqueDataLabel` COUNT instead of SEARCH (below).
let DataLabelDisambiguator = "$"

// How many `.data` labels `assignDataLabels` has minted for each BARE label so far.
//
// A COUNTER, not a set of taken labels — and the difference is a quadratic. The obvious shape is
// "probe `__data_counter`, then `$1`, then `$2`, … until one is free", which is O(k) probes for the
// k-th global of a name and therefore **Θ(n²) for n same-named file-private globals** — precisely
// the shape `global-file-private-same-name.md` is about, so precisely the shape that must not be
// the slow path. The counter answers in O(1) because it does not have to ask whether a candidate is
// free: `$` is unwritable in source (see `DataLabelDisambiguator`), so this map is the ONLY minter
// of suffixed labels and its count IS the next free ordinal. Nothing else can have taken it.
typealias DataLabelCountMap = Map with (ByteArray, MachineWord)

// `name`'s `.data` label, and the bump of `minted` that reserves it. The FIRST global of a bare name
// gets the bare label; the k-th gets `__data_<name>$<k-1>`.
//
// O(1), with no search — see `DataLabelCountMap`. Deterministic because `assignDataLabels` walks the
// arena in declaration order (source-path, then in-file), so WHICH declaration gets the bare name is
// a function of the program and not of a hash iteration order.
function uniqueDataLabel(name ByteArray, minted DataLabelCountMap) returns ByteArray
	let bare = "{DataLabelPrefix}{String.from(name)}".toByteArray()
	let alreadyMinted = try minted.get(bare) otherwise 0
	minted.upsert(bare, value: alreadyMinted + 1)

	if alreadyMinted == 0 'firstOfThisName'
		return bare
	end 'firstOfThisName'

	return "{String.from(bare)}{DataLabelDisambiguator}{alreadyMinted}".toByteArray()
end 'uniqueDataLabel'

// A top-level constant's state, from `pending` through to its answer. ONE union rather than a value
// table plus a side set of in-progress names, because the two questions the evaluator asks — "what
// is this worth?" and "am I already inside it?" — are the same question asked at different times,
// and a name that could be in one structure but not the other is exactly how a cycle goes undetected.
export union TopLevelConstant
	// Read by the sweep, not yet evaluated. Every decl starts here.
	pending
	// On the current evaluation's DFS stack: a reference that reaches this decl again is a CYCLE.
	evaluating
	// It evaluated. `payload` is the number (a bool's is its 0/1 — TypeRules' `BoolTrueValue`), and
	// `tag` says which of the two it is. This is what a use INLINES.
	value(payload ParsedInt, tag ValueTypeTag)
	// THIS declaration is defective, and `error` is the positioned diagnostic for it. Held rather
	// than reported, because the sweep that found it is deliberately TOLERANT and has no business
	// vetoing a program (see `foldDeclaredSignaturesInto`). The REAL parse raises it, once, when it
	// reaches this line — which is also the only place that knows the diagnostic is about source the
	// user actually wrote rather than a name some other file mentioned.
	failed(error ParseError)
	// It has no value only because a constant it READS has none. Silent by design: a second
	// diagnostic here would name a line whose only fault is mentioning a broken name. v1 does the
	// same, dropping cycle victims so their readers see a plain "Undefined name" rather than a
	// cascade (TypeResolution.maxon:8332-8338).
	poisoned
	// ⭐ **IT EVALUATED TO A MANAGED LITERAL** (P1.7a top-level managed `let`) — `bytes` is the decoded
	// payload and `kind` says which literal it came from. The `value` arm's twin, and a SEPARATE arm
	// rather than a widened one because the two payloads are different things: a number a reader copies
	// into a `literal` op, versus bytes a reader re-materializes as a record.
	//
	// ⚠ **APPENDED AT THE END, on purpose.** Three range arms read this union (`value to poisoned`,
	// `failed to poisoned`, `pending to evaluating`); appending leaves every one of them EXCLUDING this
	// case, so each site fails to compile until it has been visited and decided — where inserting it
	// beside `value` would have let `value to poisoned` swallow it silently. Same rule, same reason, as
	// `StdOp`'s bands.
	managedValue(bytes ByteArray, kind ManagedConstKind)
	// ⭐ **IT EVALUATED TO AN ARRAY LITERAL** (P1.7 slice 2b) — its already-folded `elements` and the one
	// `element` kind they all share. Its own arm rather than a widened `managedValue` because an array's
	// value is neither a number nor bytes: it is a LIST, and the record built from it is a HEAP record
	// with a `.data` slot — so a binding that folds to one is STORED whichever keyword declared it (see
	// `TopLevelDecl.hasStorage`), where a `managedValue` `let` is inlined at every read.
	//
	// ⚠ APPENDED AT THE END, for `managedValue`'s reason and now with one more range arm reading this
	// union. Appending leaves `value to poisoned`, `failed to poisoned`, `pending to evaluating` and
	// `value to managedValue` all EXCLUDING this case, so each site fails to compile until it has been
	// visited and decided.
	arrayValue(elements ConstArrayElementArray, element ConstArrayElementKind)
	// ⭐ **IT EVALUATED TO A CONTAINER FACTORY — `var g = ItemArray.create()`** (spec-port `ownership`). The
	// instance is the whole value: an EMPTY container has no elements to carry, and its record is fixed by
	// the `Array with T` the alias names. Its own arm rather than an `arrayValue` with no elements because
	// the two are typed from different facts — an array literal's instance is INFERRED from its first
	// element's kind (`constArrayValueType`), and there is no element here to infer from, so the giid the
	// alias resolved to is the only thing that can say what the slot holds.
	//
	// ⚠ APPENDED AT THE END, for `managedValue`'s reason: every range arm over this union then EXCLUDES it
	// and fails to compile until it has been visited and decided.
	containerValue(giid GenericInstanceId, elements ConstArrayElementArray)
	// ⭐ **IT EVALUATED TO A USER STATIC FACTORY CALL — `var db = Database.create(…)`** (spec-port
	// `map-struct-bytearray`). Its own arm because it is the one outcome whose value the compiler does not
	// KNOW: the other stored arms carry a record's contents, this one carries the CALL TREE that produces
	// them — flat, with `root` naming the outermost call, exactly as `ConstInitializerValue.factoryCall`
	// carries it and for the reason stated there. `returnType` is the ROOT callee's declared return type,
	// resolved in this index's own interner when the outcome is settled — it is what types the `.data` slot
	// and what routes the cleanup's drop.
	//
	// ⚠ APPENDED AT THE END, for `managedValue`'s reason: every range arm over this union then EXCLUDES it
	// and fails to compile until it has been visited and decided.
	factoryValue(calls ConstFactoryCallArray, root InitializerCallId, returnType MaxonType)
	// ⚠ **A `characterSetValue` ARM WAS APPENDED PAST THIS ONE AND WENT AT W129**, with the compiler-owned
	// `CharacterSet` record a global could hold. What it demonstrated is this union's own rule and is why the
	// rule is written here: appended at the END, it fell OUTSIDE six existing range arms — `managedValue to
	// containerValue`, `managedValue to factoryValue` (×3), `arrayValue to factoryValue` and `value to
	// poisoned` — so every one of those sites failed to compile until it had been visited and decided. Placed
	// beside `containerValue`, where it semantically belonged, four of them would have swallowed it silently.
end 'TopLevelConstant'

// ⭐⭐ **DOES A TOP-LEVEL DECLARATION'S INITIALIZER RUN THE PROGRAM'S OWN CODE, AND WHOSE?** — ONE
// projection of the union above, because two readers need different halves of the same fact and a
// disagreement between them is not a missed optimization but a compiler panic:
//
//   * `ProgramSignatures.contributesStorage` reads the BOOLEAN — a global whose initializer calls keeps
//     its slot however dead it looks, because the call may print, mutate or throw.
//   * `StdlibSource.deriveStdlibFacts` reads the CALLEES — it must ROOT them, since the body that will make
//     the calls (`__module_init`) does not exist yet when the reachability walk runs, and is called by the
//     entry stub rather than from `main` even once it does.
//
// A boolean beside a separate callee scan would let an outcome appended past `factoryValue` be KEPT by the
// first and left UNROOTED by the second — which is `lowerMaxonToStd` skipping a body that
// `DeadFunctionElimination` then reaches, i.e. `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead`.
//
// ⚠ **A LIST, NOT ONE NAME, BECAUSE ONE INITIALIZER MAKES SEVERAL CALLS (W41).** It carried a single callee
// while a factory call was the only calling form and every ARGUMENT was a constant or a runtime-built
// container. A DECLARED generic's empty record is neither: `var db = Database.create(EntryMap.create(), …)`
// calls `Map.create` as well as `Database.create`, and `__module_init` emits both. MEASURED with the single
// name: the argument's `create` was left out of the root set and the compile died in
// `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead` — the exact failure this union's header already described, arriving
// through the ARGUMENT rather than through a new outcome arm.
export union TopLevelInitializerCall
	none
	calls(callees ByteArrayArray)
end 'TopLevelInitializerCall'

// ONE folded ELEMENT of a top-level CONTAINER literal (P1.7 slice 2b) — what `__module_init` materializes
// and adds. Deliberately NOT `ConstInitializerValue` (which would make it recursive): a NESTED container
// literal is not an element any of them accepts, in a function body or here, so the type says so rather than
// leaving a case for a walk to reject.
export union ConstArrayElement
	// An integer element: the folded number, materialized as an ordinary `literal` op. The whole scalar
	// evaluator stands behind it, so `[BASE, BASE + 2]` is as much an element as `[1, 2]` is.
	integer(payload ParsedInt)
	// A String element: the literal's decoded bytes, materialized as the immortal `.rdata` record and then
	// CLONED — the container's `element_destroy@40` walk drops every live slot, so each element must be a
	// record it may own (exactly what `Parser.moveElementIntoContainer` promotes a borrowed String literal to).
	string(bytes ByteArray)
	// ⭐ **A Character element** (spec-port `lazy-static`) — the same `.rdata`-record-then-CLONE the String
	// arm takes, differing only in the TAG the value is minted at, which is what makes it a `Character` and
	// not a one-byte String. It carries the same decoded bytes because a `Character` record IS a String
	// record in shv2 (`emitCharacterLiteral` emits a `stringLiteral` op at the `character` tag).
	//
	// ⚠ **ONLY THE SET-LITERAL WALK PRODUCES ONE, AND `constArrayElementKindOf` SAYS SO.** A character
	// LITERAL is not a constant at module scope (`Parser.evalConstCharLiteral` refuses it as E2004 — the
	// value it denotes is a record), so `evalConstArrayElement` has no character arm and a top-level
	// `['a', 'b']` is refused exactly as it was. What `CharSet from ['a', 'e']` supplies that a bare bracket
	// list cannot is the INSTANCE, which is why the element is admitted there and nowhere else.
	character(bytes ByteArray)
end 'ConstArrayElement'

export typealias ConstArrayElementArray = Array with ConstArrayElement

// The element list every EMPTY container global carries — one shared array, never mutated, for the reason
// every other `sharedEmpty*` in this compiler exists: a `var g = IntArray.create()` allocates nothing to say
// it has no elements, and `ItemArray.create()` is the commonest managed global in the corpus.
export let sharedEmptyConstArrayElements = ConstArrayElementArray.create()

// ⭐ **DOES A FOLDED MEMBER FIT A SET'S DECLARED KEY TYPE?** The folding twin of the member argument
// `Parser.parseSetMemberList` parses,
// which holds a body's `insert` argument to the same `setKeyType`. Without it, `StrSet from [1, 2]` would
// build a set whose slots hold integers and whose teardown walk `__str_decref`s them — a wrong answer at run
// time on a program the compiler accepted.
//
// It is one MAP and one comparison rather than a per-key-type ladder, so the element a global is built from
// and the type its container was declared at cannot be judged by two readings.
export function constSetMemberMatchesKeyType(element ConstArrayElement, keyType MaxonType) returns bool
	return maxonTypeTag(keyType) == constSetMemberKeyTag(element)
end 'constSetMemberMatchesKeyType'

// The key tag one folded member IS — `ConstArrayElement`'s classification for a SET, where
// `constArrayElementKindOf` is its classification for an ARRAY. Two classifications because the two
// containers ask different questions: an array INFERS its instance from this, a set CHECKS its stated one.
function constSetMemberKeyTag(element ConstArrayElement) returns ValueTypeTag
	return match element 'e'
		integer gives ValueTypeTag.integer
		string gives ValueTypeTag.string
		character gives ValueTypeTag.character
	end 'e'
end 'constSetMemberKeyTag'

// WHICH element type a top-level array literal settled on — the ONE fact its `Array with T` instance, its
// element size and its element destructor all follow from, decided from the FIRST element and enforced on
// every later one (`ProgramSignatures` reads it and never re-inspects the elements).
//
// It mirrors `ConstArrayElement`'s arms because it IS their classification, and `constArrayElementKindOf`
// is the one function that connects the two — so the kind a global is typed by and the elements the init
// emits cannot come to disagree.
export enum ConstArrayElementKind
	integer
	string
end 'ConstArrayElementKind'

// The classification of one folded element — the single bridge between `ConstArrayElement` (what an element
// IS) and `ConstArrayElementKind` (what the ARRAY is). Both the first element's settling and every later
// element's homogeneity check read it, so a literal cannot be typed by one reading and checked by another.
export function constArrayElementKindOf(element ConstArrayElement) returns ConstArrayElementKind
	return match element 'e'
		integer gives ConstArrayElementKind.integer
		string gives ConstArrayElementKind.string
		// ⚠ **A CHARACTER ELEMENT HAS NO ARRAY KIND, AND THAT IS THE INVARIANT RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.**
		// This function's whole job is what an ARRAY LITERAL infers its instance from, and no array literal
		// can hold one: `evalConstArrayElement` has no character arm, because a character literal is not a
		// constant at module scope. A character element reaches the arena only through a SET literal, which
		// STATES its instance (`ConstFoldedValue.containerCreate`'s giid) and never asks this.
		character panic("constArrayElementKindOf: a Character element has no array-literal kind — it can only come from a set literal, whose instance is stated by its alias rather than inferred from its elements")
	end 'e'
end 'constArrayElementKindOf'

// The NUMBER one folded element lays down in a `.rdata` blob. The `string` arm is unreachable and says so
// rather than answering: a String element makes the array's instance MANAGED, and
// `arrayElementMayBeConstantBlob` — which every caller of this asks first — refuses a managed element
// because its slot holds a loader-chosen address rather than a value an image can contain.
export function constArrayElementScalar(element ConstArrayElement) returns ParsedInt
	return match element 'e'
		integer(payload) gives payload
		// A String and a Character element alike hold a heap address, which is why `arrayElementMayBeConstantBlob`
		// refuses the instance before this is asked — and a Character element cannot reach a blob at all, its
		// container being a `Set` rather than an `Array`.
		string to character panic("constArrayElementScalar: a managed element has no constant image — its slot holds a heap address, which is why `arrayElementMayBeConstantBlob` refuses the instance before this is asked")
	end 'e'
end 'constArrayElementScalar'

// WHICH managed literal a top-level `let` folded to — the ONE fact a use site needs beyond the bytes,
// because the two kinds materialize through different record shapes: a String literal is a wholly
// immortal `.rdata` record, while a byte string is an owned `Array with Byte` record over an immortal
// `.rdata` blob. Both are `LowerMaxonToStd`'s existing literal lowerings, reached through the ops the
// use site emits — this enum picks WHICH op, and nothing else.
export enum ManagedConstKind
	string
	byteArray
end 'ManagedConstKind'

// ⭐ **ONE MANAGED TOP-LEVEL BINDING — its `.data` label, the bytes its initializer folded to, and the
// type it denotes** (P1.7 slice 2a). What `managedGlobals` hands the module-init/cleanup synthesis, so the
// slot the layout reserves, the record the init builds and the drop the cleanup emits all read ONE
// description of the same global rather than three re-derivations off the arena.
//
// `range` is the initializer's first token, so every op the synthesis emits carries a span pointing at the
// declaration the user wrote — the ops exist because of that line and nothing else.
export type ManagedGlobal
	export let label as ByteArray
	// ⭐ **THE RECORD `__module_init` BUILDS, AS A DESCRIPTION** — a union rather than a `kind` beside a
	// `bytes` that only two of the three kinds have anything to put in (P1.7 slice 2b). Each arm carries
	// exactly what its own construction needs and nothing else, so an array's elements cannot be read off a
	// String global and a String's bytes cannot be read off an array's.
	export let initializer as ManagedGlobalInitializer
	export let valueType as MaxonType
	// ⭐ THE RUNTIME ENTRY `__maxon_global_cleanup` DROPS THIS SLOT'S OCCUPANT THROUGH — decided HERE, in
	// the arena walk, because the routing (`managedFieldDropCallee`) has to resolve the type's ids against
	// the SIGNATURES interner and only this side holds it. Carrying the answer is what lets the synthesis
	// take no `ProgramSignatures` at all, so there is no second place a drop callee could be derived from.
	export let dropCallee as ByteArray
	export let range as SourceRange
	// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS SLOT FILLED ON FIRST ACCESS?** — the DECIDED BIT, `ProgramSignatures.declSlotIsLazy`'s
	// answer, carried rather than re-derived. It routes three things in `ModuleInit` — which globals get a
	// `__lazy_init#…` routine, which ones `__module_init` skips, and which ones `__maxon_global_cleanup`
	// guards — and one more at every access site (`Parser.emitGlobalAddr`, through the label set the same
	// predicate fills).
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT IS THE VERDICT, NOT THE PROVENANCE, AND THAT DISTINCTION IS THE §6 REVIEW'S** (finding 3). It
	// carried `TopLevelDecl.scope` — WHERE the binding was declared — and `ModuleInit` routed on that alone
	// while `declSlotIsLazy` decided the same question from THREE conditions (the scope, having storage, and
	// the outcome being a stored record). The two agreed only because `managedGlobals` happens to admit
	// exactly the record-outcome arms that predicate's range arm names, and nothing forced it: the day a
	// file-scope binding becomes lazy, or a `static` member acquires a slot that is not a stored record,
	// `declSlotIsLazy` fails to compile (its range arm covers the union) while three `match global.scope`
	// answer silently — a slot nobody initializes, or a `call __lazy_init#X` with no routine behind it.
	// One predicate, four readers.
	export let filledOnFirstAccess as bool

	export static function create(label ByteArray, initializer ManagedGlobalInitializer, valueType MaxonType, dropCallee ByteArray, range SourceRange, filledOnFirstAccess bool) returns ManagedGlobal
		return Self{label: label, initializer: initializer, valueType: valueType, dropCallee: dropCallee, range: range, filledOnFirstAccess: filledOnFirstAccess}
	end 'create'

	// ⭐ **THE SYNTHESIZED ROUTINE THAT FILLS THIS SLOT ON FIRST ACCESS** — `__lazy_init$<label>`, derived
	// from the label so the routine and the slot cannot come to name different things. `__`-prefixed, so
	// `isCompilerInternalCallee` treats it as the compiler's own and `TargetPrinter.isRuntimeFunction` keeps
	// its body out of every golden fragment; what DOES reach a golden is the one `call` each access emits.
	//
	// The bootstrap names its equivalent `<Type>.<field>.__lazy_init` (MEASURED, off a cycle's stack trace).
	// shv2 keys on the LABEL instead of the source name because the label is already the slot's IR identity
	// and is already collision-resolved (`assignDataLabels`), so two file-private statics of the same name
	// get two routines without a second disambiguator.
	export function lazyInitCallee() returns ByteArray
		return lazyStaticInitCallee(self.label)
	end 'lazyInitCallee'
end 'ManagedGlobal'

// The `__lazy_init$<label>` spelling, written ONCE and read from both sides — `ManagedGlobal.lazyInitCallee`
// (which builds the routine) and `ProgramSignatures.lazyStaticInitCalleeFor` (which makes an access call it).
// ⛔ **THE JOIN IS `#`, AND IT IS NOT `$` — the §6 review's finding 7.** `Project.OverloadNameSeparator`
// states the rule ("a symbol built by joining two names joins them with a character no name can hold") and
// states that `$` is ALREADY SPENT on joining a lifted closure to its enclosing function. A third meaning
// for it made `FnRefThunk.isLiftedClosureName` — a bare search for `$` — answer TRUE for this routine, which
// is the right answer (it needs no env thunk) for entirely the wrong reason. `#` is the character that
// already means "the compiler joined two names here".
export function lazyStaticInitCallee(label ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	var callee = b"__lazy_init#".clone()
	callee.append(label)
	return callee
end 'lazyStaticInitCallee'

export typealias ManagedGlobalArray = Array with ManagedGlobal

// ⭐⭐ **HOW ONE MANAGED GLOBAL'S SLOT COMES TO HOLD A RECORD — TWO ARMS, AND THE SPLIT IS BETWEEN THE TWO
// WAYS A RECORD CAN COME TO EXIST**: the compiler MATERIALIZES it from what the initializer folded to, or
// the program's own code RETURNS it.
export union ManagedGlobalInitializer
	// A record `__module_init` builds itself, out of folded constants and nothing else.
	record(build ManagedGlobalRecord)
	// ⭐ **A USER STATIC FACTORY — `var db = Database.create(EntryMap.create(), sourcePaths: …)`** (spec-port
	// `map-struct-bytearray`). The slot's occupant is whatever the ROOT call RETURNS, so `__module_init`
	// materializes each argument and then CALLS it: this is the one initializer form that runs user code
	// before `main`.
	//
	// ⭐ **AND AN ARGUMENT MAY ITSELF BE A CALL** (spec-port `module-level-struct-var`), which is why this
	// carries a flat ARENA and a root id rather than one callee and one argument list. It is the fold tier's
	// `ConstInitializerValue.factoryCall` resolved entry-for-entry, so an id names the same call on both
	// sides and the flattening is stated once — see there for why the tree cannot be held as a tree.
	factoryCall(calls GlobalInitCallArray, root InitializerCallId)
end 'ManagedGlobalInitializer'

// ⭐ **A RECORD `__module_init` BUILDS WITHOUT RUNNING USER CODE** — exactly the MATERIALIZED constructions a
// global's own slot may start life holding, and exactly the materialized ones a factory ARGUMENT may be. Each
// arm differs in whether the literal's own materialization is ALREADY the owned record the slot must hold,
// which is a fact about the record formats rather than a choice `ModuleInit` makes.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS ITS OWN UNION BECAUSE MAXON FORBIDS A RECURSIVE TYPE (E4014).** A factory call carries arguments
// and an argument may be any of these, so one union holding both arms would name itself. The split therefore
// STATES what this union is: everything `__module_init` can build for itself. A CALL is the other way a slot
// or an argument comes to hold a record, and it is not an arm here — it is an entry in
// `ManagedGlobalInitializer.factoryCall`'s flat arena, reached by an INDEX, which is the same E4014 breaking
// the same cycle one tier up (spec-port `module-level-struct-var`).
export union ManagedGlobalRecord
	// A `String` literal is a WHOLLY immortal `.rdata` record shared by every read of those bytes, so the
	// slot's occupant is a CLONE of it — the `(box) -> newBox` dual of the `__str_decref` the cleanup drops
	// it with, so the pair balances by construction.
	stringRecord(bytes ByteArray)
	// ⭐ A CONSTANT ARRAY literal ALREADY materializes as an owned heap 48-byte `Array` record over an
	// immortal `.rdata` blob, which is precisely what the slot must hold — so there is nothing to clone, and
	// nothing to build element by element either. `bytes` is the blob at `elementSize`'s stride.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT WAS `byteStringRecord(bytes)` UNTIL `codegen-internals`, WHICH IS THE SAME ARM AT STRIDE 1.** A
	// `b"…"` literal is a constant array of bytes; `[10, 20, 30]` at file scope is a constant array of
	// machine words. Keeping them two arms would have meant two builders emitting one op, and the second one
	// arriving is what made the duplication visible.
	constArrayRecord(elementSize ElementStride, elementCount ParsedInt, bytes ByteArray)
	// ⭐ A CONTAINER (P1.7 slice 2b, widened by spec-port `map-struct-bytearray`, and to DECLARED generics at
	// W41) has no literal record at all: it is BUILT, `create` then one `__managed_push` per element. `create`
	// carries which callee and which stamps this instance fixes, resolved HERE (where the instance registry is)
	// rather than re-derived by the synthesis — the same discipline `ManagedGlobal.dropCallee` follows. An
	// array LITERAL has elements and an empty container factory has none, which is the same fact the
	// container-factory arm has always stated: a container factory IS an array literal with no elements. A
	// `CharSet from ['a', 'e']` is the third point on that line — an instance STATED by its alias, with
	// elements — and it is what made the ADD callee ride `create` rather than being `__managed_push` written
	// into the emitter (a `Set` inserts).
	containerRecord(create ContainerCreateCall, elements ConstArrayElementArray)
	// ⚠ **A `characterSetRecord` ARM STOOD HERE AND WENT AT W129.** It was the ONE compiler-owned record a
	// global could hold — a member set plus the mask `__cs_make` sealed it into a box with — and it was here
	// rather than in the factory arena because it was not a CALL the author wrote: shv2 had no
	// `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon` to declare `whitespacesAndNewlines` as a callee. `W115` listed that module,
	// so a preset IS a call now and rides `ManagedGlobalInitializer.factoryCall` like any other.
end 'ManagedGlobalRecord'

// ONE argument a top-level factory call passes: its source-level `name:` LABEL (empty for a positional
// argument, exactly the "no label" spelling `MaxonOp.call`'s `argLabels` uses), the value, the TYPE that
// value is minted at — which the synthesis needs and cannot re-derive, an argument's type being a fact about
// the initializer that produced it rather than about the global's own slot — and WHO FREES IT.
export type GlobalInitArgument
	export let label as ByteArray
	export let value as GlobalInitArgumentValue
	export let valueType as MaxonType
	export let cleanup as GlobalInitArgumentCleanup

	export static function create(label ByteArray, value GlobalInitArgumentValue, valueType MaxonType, cleanup GlobalInitArgumentCleanup) returns GlobalInitArgument
		return Self{label: label, value: value, valueType: valueType, cleanup: cleanup}
	end 'create'
end 'GlobalInitArgument'

export typealias GlobalInitArgumentArray = Array with GlobalInitArgument

// ⭐ **ONE NODE OF A FACTORY-CALL GLOBAL'S FLATTENED TREE, RESOLVED FOR THE SYNTHESIS** — the fold tier's
// `ConstFactoryCall` with every argument turned into a materialization instruction, arm for arm.
//
// It carries no return type for `ConstFactoryCall`'s reason: the receiver of the result already holds it.
export union GlobalInitCall
	// `callee` is the mangled `Type.method` name (`qualifiedMethodName`), which is the same key a body's call
	// site emits and the signature registry files the declaration under — so the argument reordering
	// `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerCall` does for a labelled call happens here for free.
	call(callee ByteArray, args GlobalInitArgumentArray)
	// ⭐ **A STRUCT LITERAL'S BOX (W116)** — the payload size `__mm_alloc` is asked for, and one instruction
	// per declared field.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY LAYOUT FACT IS RESOLVED HERE AND TRAVELS WITH THE NODE**, which is the discipline
	// `ManagedGlobal.dropCallee` and `ContainerCreateCall` already set: `SynthesizedFunction` holds neither
	// the interner nor the struct registry, so a size, a byte offset or a slot width re-derived over there
	// would be a second reading of a layout — and a field stored at one width and loaded at another corrupts
	// the box silently (`Parser.emitIndirectStore`'s header states the same hazard for the body's two doors).
	construct(sizeBytes ByteOffset, fields GlobalStructFieldArray)
end 'GlobalInitCall'

export typealias GlobalInitCallArray = Array with GlobalInitCall

// ⭐ **ONE SLOT OF A STRUCT-LITERAL BOX, RESOLVED (W116)** — where it goes, how wide the store is, and what
// produces the value.
//
// ⚠ **IT CARRIES NO `GlobalInitArgumentCleanup`, AND THE ABSENCE IS THE OWNERSHIP RULE RATHER THAN AN
// OMISSION.** A factory ARGUMENT asks who frees it because the callee may only have borrowed it; a FIELD is
// consumed by construction — the box becomes its sole owner and `__destruct_<Struct>` releases every managed
// slot — so there is no second answer for a per-field bit to carry. That is the same handoff
// `Parser.moveInPayload` makes at a body's literal, expressed here by there being no drop rather than by a
// de-listing, because `__module_init` has no statement whose pending temporaries a value could be on.
export type GlobalStructField
	// The slot's byte offset — `StructLayout.offsetOfField`.
	export let offset as ByteOffset
	// The width the store moves — `Parser.fieldStorageType`'s answer for this slot, so a field written by
	// `__module_init` and the same field written by an assignment land at one width.
	export let storageType as MaxonType
	// What produces the value, and the type it is minted at — the same pair a `GlobalInitArgument` carries,
	// materialized through the very same producer.
	export let value as GlobalInitArgumentValue
	export let valueType as MaxonType

	export static function create(offset ByteOffset, storageType MaxonType, value GlobalInitArgumentValue, valueType MaxonType) returns GlobalStructField
		return Self{offset: offset, storageType: storageType, value: value, valueType: valueType}
	end 'create'
end 'GlobalStructField'

export typealias GlobalStructFieldArray = Array with GlobalStructField

// `factoryCallAt`'s twin over the resolved arena, and the reason the two arenas are built entry-for-entry:
// one id, one miss message, whichever tier is holding the array.
export function globalInitCallAt(calls GlobalInitCallArray, id InitializerCallId) returns GlobalInitCall
	return try calls.get(id) otherwise panic("globalInitCallAt: no call {id} in a resolved arena of {calls.count()} — it is built entry-for-entry from the fold arena the ids were minted in")
end 'globalInitCallAt'

// ⭐⭐ **WHO FREES A FACTORY ARGUMENT'S RECORD — the ownership fact `__module_init` has no statement to end
// in.** A body's call site materializes an argument as an owned TEMP (`Parser.trackOwnedTemp`) and drops it
// at the end of the statement unless the callee consumed it; the synthesis has no statement and no temp
// list, so the same decision is made HERE, off the callee's recorded consume bits, and travels with the
// argument it is about.
//
// ⛔ It was missing outright, and the leak was on an ERROR-FREE path: `var c = Counter.create(Bytes.create())`
// against a `create` that only READS its parameter allocated the array and freed nothing — exit 101. The
// spec that landed this door consumes both of its parameters, which is exactly why the corpus could not see
// it.
export union GlobalInitArgumentCleanup
	// Nothing to free: a scalar owns no heap, and a record the callee CONSUMES is the callee's to release.
	none
	// The callee only BORROWED this record, so `__module_init` is still its sole owner and drops it through
	// `callee` once the call has returned — the same runtime entry `__maxon_global_cleanup` would drop a
	// global of that type through, resolved off the same `managedFieldDropCallee` router.
	dropAfterCall(callee ByteArray)
end 'GlobalInitArgumentCleanup'

// What ONE factory argument evaluates to — a folded number, one of the records above, or another CALL.
// The three are different instructions to the emitter (a `literal` op, a record construction, a `call`),
// which is the whole of the distinction.
export union GlobalInitArgumentValue
	// A folded number: an integer, a bool's 0/1 (TypeRules' `BoolTrueValue`) or a float's IEEE bit pattern.
	scalar(payload ParsedInt)
	record(build ManagedGlobalRecord)
	// ⭐ **A NESTED FACTORY CALL** (spec-port `module-level-struct-var`), by its position in the same arena
	// this argument's own call lives in. `__module_init` emits it before the call that takes it; the record
	// it returns is owned by that frame exactly as a materialized one is, so `cleanup` above answers for it
	// through the identical consume-bit rule.
	call(id InitializerCallId)
end 'GlobalInitArgumentValue'

// A record construction and the TYPE its value is minted at — the two facts `ProgramSignatures.globalRecordOf`
// settles together off ONE folded value. They travel as a pair because re-deriving the type from the
// construction would mean asking a literal's element kind a second time, and the two answers must not be able
// to disagree about what the slot holds.
export type GlobalRecordAndType
	export let build as ManagedGlobalRecord
	export let valueType as MaxonType

	export static function create(build ManagedGlobalRecord, valueType MaxonType) returns GlobalRecordAndType
		return Self{build: build, valueType: valueType}
	end 'create'
end 'GlobalRecordAndType'

// ⭐ **ONE CONSTANT, RESOLVED INTO A MATERIALIZATION INSTRUCTION AND THE TYPE IT IS MINTED AT** — the pair
// `ProgramSignatures.globalInitValue` hands back, and the reason it is a pair: the two are read together at
// every site and must not be able to disagree about what the value IS. `GlobalRecordAndType` above makes the
// same argument one arm down; this is that arm plus the scalar and the nested-node ones.
export type GlobalInitValueAndType
	export let value as GlobalInitArgumentValue
	export let valueType as MaxonType

	export static function create(value GlobalInitArgumentValue, valueType MaxonType) returns GlobalInitValueAndType
		return Self{value: value, valueType: valueType}
	end 'create'
end 'GlobalInitValueAndType'

// ⭐ **THE `create` ONE CONTAINER INSTANCE IS BORN THROUGH** — which callee, which stamps it is born holding,
// and whether the record is then SIZED. ONE description, so a global `Array`, a global `List` and a global
// instance of a DECLARED generic reach `__module_init` through one emitter rather than one arm apiece.
//
// ⚠ **THE CALLEE IS A RUNTIME ENTRY *OR* A DECLARED STATIC, AND THE DESCRIPTION DOES NOT DISTINGUISH THEM**
// (W41). It does not need to: `ModuleInit` emits `callee(stamps…)` either way, and the two differ only in
// how many stamps there are — a runtime `create` cannot look up a stride or a column destructor and must be
// handed both, while a declared `create()` builds its own fields from its own declaration and is handed none.
// Who owns a given instance's `create` is `ProgramSignatures.instanceCreateIsDeclared`'s single answer, read
// by the producer here and by the two liveness decisions, never re-derived from this callee's spelling.
//
// ⚠ **AND WHICH CALLEE ADDS AN ELEMENT, for the same reason the `create` callee rides here** (spec-port
// `lazy-static`). `ModuleInit` used to spell `__managed_push` itself, which was true while an `Array` was
// the only container that could arrive holding elements; a `CharSet from ['a', 'e']` INSERTS. Routing it
// through the same per-container answer `containerCreateCall` already gives keeps one router deciding what
// a container is, rather than a second container test inside the synthesis.
export type ContainerCreateCall
	export let callee as ByteArray
	export let stamps as ContainerCreateStampArray
	export let sizing as ContainerSizing
	export let elementAdd as ContainerElementAdd

	export static function create(callee ByteArray, stamps ContainerCreateStampArray, sizing ContainerSizing, elementAdd ContainerElementAdd) returns ContainerCreateCall
		return Self{callee: callee, stamps: stamps, sizing: sizing, elementAdd: elementAdd}
	end 'create'
end 'ContainerCreateCall'

// ⭐ **HOW ONE ELEMENT JOINS A CONTAINER `__module_init` BUILT** — a runtime callee taking `(record, value)`,
// or NOTHING for a container no top-level form can hand elements to.
//
// ⚠ **`unsupported` IS NOT "we have not got round to it": it is the honest answer for a container the
// initializer grammar cannot give elements.** A `Map` global is only ever `MapAlias.create()` (there is no
// top-level map literal) and a DECLARED generic's `create()` takes no arguments at all
// (`Parser.requireDeclaredGenericGlobalCreate`), so both arrive with an EMPTY element list and never ask.
// `ModuleInit` panics rather than defaulting, so the day a form does hand one elements, the missing callee is
// a compile-time-loud gap and not a silently dropped element.
export union ContainerElementAdd
	push(callee ByteArray)
	unsupported
end 'ContainerElementAdd'

// ONE argument a container's `create` is stamped with — an `element_size@24` stride, or the
// `element_destroy@40` / `elemDrop@40` / `element_drop@24` destructor a container's teardown walk calls
// through a `callIndirect` on each live slot.
//
// A union rather than a callee name whose emptiness means "trivial": the two are different instructions to
// the emitter (a `functionRef` op versus a `literal 0`), and a sentinel would let a genuinely missing name
// read as a deliberate absence.
export union ContainerCreateStamp
	// A number the record is born holding — a stride, or the 0 that says a column owns nothing and its
	// teardown walk is skipped.
	inline(value ParsedInt)
	// The RAW `.text` ADDRESS of a managed column's drop callee.
	dropCallee(callee ByteArray)
end 'ContainerCreateStamp'

export typealias ContainerCreateStampArray = Array with ContainerCreateStamp

// ⭐ **MAY A `.data` SLOT HOLD WHAT THIS CALLEE RETURNS?** — the whole of what a top-level factory's
// signature has to answer, and each arm carries exactly what its refusal needs to say. A union rather than a
// bool because three of the four answers are REASONS, and a bool would leave each caller to re-derive which.
export union GlobalFactoryReturn
	// A heap record — a struct, a String, a Character, a boxed union or a container. Its slot holds a
	// pointer `__module_init` fills before `main`.
	record
	// A number. Its slot holds the VALUE, written into the image, which happens before any code can run.
	// `typeName` is the type as a diagnostic prints it, rendered on the index's side because only that side
	// holds the interner the id lives in.
	scalar(typeName String)
	// ⛔ **A RECORD STILL CARRYING THE BARE `named` TAG (S2j)** — a boxed union, a generic alias, a
	// rendered generic instance. It IS a record and its slot IS one pointer; what it has not got is a
	// CONCRETE type for the two synthesized functions to access that slot through.
	// `__module_init`'s store and `__maxon_global_cleanup`'s load carry the declared type straight onto a
	// `storeIndirect`/`loadIndirect` (`ModuleInit`), where `maxonTypeToStdType` panics on a `named`; user
	// code has `Parser.fieldStorageType` to bridge that and `SynthesizedFunction` holds neither the
	// interner nor the registry it asks. ⇒ REFUSED here, at the declaration, rather than panicking three
	// passes later.
	//
	// ⚠ **A DECLARED `type` AND A TUPLE ALIAS ARE NOT IN THIS ARM**, and the tuple was listed here until
	// the BATCH28 review measured it: `RecordedReturnTypeRule.declaredStruct` re-tags both to `structRef`
	// before this is asked, so a `returns Pair` reaches the `record` arm. The refusal's own sentence said
	// otherwise, which is this batch's defect committed by the batch — a second reader stating a fact it
	// re-derived rather than asking.
	unbridgedRecord(typeName String)
	void
	// No file declares this name. Unreachable from the initializer door, which admits the form only for a
	// declared callee — kept as an arm so the answer is total rather than an assumption.
	undeclared
end 'GlobalFactoryReturn'

// Is the record `create` hands back already the size it will always be? A `Vector with N T` is — its
// elements are addressable from birth, so its create is followed by one `__managed_resize` — and every other
// builtin container GROWS. Read off `ProgramSignatures.genericInstanceFixedSize`, so the count a global
// vector is born with and the count `Vector.create()` gives one in a body are the same fact.
export union ContainerSizing
	grown
	fixed(count FixedElementCount)
end 'ContainerSizing'

// WHICH record a managed LITERAL global's bytes build — the `ManagedConstKind` → `ManagedGlobalRecord`
// map, in one place so the kind a constant folded to and the construction its slot gets cannot drift apart.
function managedLiteralRecord(bytes ByteArray, kind ManagedConstKind) returns ManagedGlobalRecord
	return match kind 'k'
		string gives ManagedGlobalRecord.stringRecord(bytes)
		byteArray gives ManagedGlobalRecord.constArrayRecord(ByteStringElementSize as ElementStride, elementCount: bytes.count() as ParsedInt, bytes: bytes)
	end 'k'
end 'managedLiteralRecord'

// The value a managed global's `.data` slot holds BEFORE `__module_init` runs: a null pointer. Named
// rather than a bare 0 because it is a CLAIM — "no record exists yet" — and it is the one state the
// cleanup's unconditional decref relies on never being observed (see `ModuleInit`).
let ManagedGlobalInitialSlot = 0 as ParsedInt

// The span every op a declaration's module-init/cleanup synthesis emits carries: the declaration's
// INITIALIZER, which is the source the record is built from. `TopLevelDecl` keeps its declaring file's
// token array precisely so a reference from anywhere can be resolved without the query layer, and this
// is the same read `evalConstInitializer` seats its cursor on.
function declInitializerRange(decl TopLevelDecl) returns SourceRange
	return SourceRange.ofToken(try decl.tokens.get(decl.initStart) otherwise panic("declInitializerRange: '{String.from(decl.name)}' has no token at its recorded initializer start {decl.initStart} — the sweep records a range inside the array it carries"))
end 'declInitializerRange'

// A top-level `let` OR `var`, as the declaration sweep found it: WHAT it is called, WHETHER it is
// mutable, WHERE it was written, and the TOKEN RANGE of its initializer — read, never evaluated on
// the spot.
//
// ⭐ **ONE ARENA FOR BOTH KINDS, AND THAT IS THE RUNG'S CENTRAL CLAIM.** A top-level `var` is not a
// new declaration form; it is this one with `mutable: true` and a home. Its initializer obeys the
// IDENTICAL rule (`static-variables.md`: "Top-level `var` initializers must be constant expressions
// (same rules as `let`)"), so it wants the identical evaluator, the identical memoized DFS, the
// identical cycle detection and the identical forward-reference-by-deferred-evaluation trick. A
// second arena would be a second reading of the same grammar — the duplication this file's header
// says the compiler pays for hardest — and the two would be free to disagree about what `BASE * 2`
// is worth. What a `var` adds is settled in exactly two fields: `mutable` and `dataLabel`.
//
// **THE RANGE, AND NOT A TREE.** v1 lowers each initializer into a flat arena of structural nodes
// (`Project.unresolvedConstExprs`, Project.maxon:3361-3432) because its parse and its resolution are
// separated by whole passes: by the time TypeResolution evaluates a constant, the Parser that read it
// is long gone. shv2 has no such gap — the sweep and the evaluation are two halves of ONE query
// (`Queries.queryProgramSignatures`), and the token memo is live throughout — so the range is
// enough, and a node union would be a THIRD representation of an expression this compiler already
// has two of. The C# bootstrap makes the same call for the same reason: its `ConstantDecl` is a
// `(TokenStart, TokenEnd)` pair (2-Parser.cs:1678) and `EvaluateConstant` rewinds a cursor onto it
// (2-Parser.cs:4960-4964).
//
// `tokens` is the DECLARING file's token array, carried so that a reference FROM ANOTHER FILE can be
// resolved without reaching back into the query layer for it. It is a reference to the very array
// `queryTokens` memoized, never a copy — the token cache retains it for the whole build regardless,
// so carrying it here costs one pointer.
export type TopLevelDecl
	// The key this declaration is filed under — `topLevelStorageKey`'s answer, computed once at
	// construction so that the sweep's write and every later read cannot spell it differently.
	export var storageKey as ByteArray
	// The BARE name, kept for the diagnostics that must print what the user wrote rather than the
	// path-qualified key the compiler files it under. (v1 needs a `displayTopLevelKey` to strip that
	// prefix back off at E2012 time; carrying the bare name costs one pointer and needs no stripping.)
	export var name as ByteArray
	// `var` or `let` — WHETHER THIS NAME MAY BE WRITTEN, and nothing else. It used to double as "does it
	// have a slot?", and P1.7 slice 2b separated the two: an array `let` is immutable AND stored, so which
	// door a USE goes through is `hasStorage` below and this bit is only what the two WRITE sites ask.
	export var mutable as bool
	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS DECLARATION HAVE A `.data` SLOT?** — the ONE criterion deciding which of the two doors
	// a USE goes through (`constantValueOf` materializes a value, `globalDeclFor` loads a slot), which decls
	// the layout reserves storage for, and which ones `assignDataLabels` labels. Read from all four, so it is
	// ONE fact rather than four re-derivations that could give a binding a slot the layout never laid down.
	//
	// ⚠ **A `var` IS NOT THE WHOLE ANSWER** (P1.7 slice 2b). Every `var` is stored — that is what a `var` IS,
	// so this starts life equal to `mutable`. But so is an immutable ARRAY: its value is a heap record built
	// by `__module_init`, and no READ can re-materialize one (a `let` String's read borrows the ONE immortal
	// `.rdata` record; an array's record is mutable by nature and per-program, so a per-read copy would be a
	// different array every time). `settleStoredRecordValue` is the ONLY writer that flips it, which is the same
	// place `outcome` becomes `arrayValue`/`containerValue` — so the two cannot disagree, and an array binding gets a slot
	// while `let` keeps refusing the write (E2013 at an assignment, E3019 at a receiver-writing method).
	// A container factory (`var g = ItemArray.create()`) settles through the same one writer for the same
	// reason — its value is a heap record too.
	//
	// ⚠ **A FIELD RATHER THAN A FUNCTION THAT RE-READS `outcome`.** The obvious spelling is
	// `mutable or outcome is arrayValue or outcome is containerValue`, asked afresh at each of the four readers. A bool written where the
	// outcome is written says the same thing once, is O(1) at every ask, and — the part that matters — makes
	// the answer available in the window where the outcome is not yet settled, which the four readers
	// straddle (see below). MEASURED: it costs no allocations and about 24 bytes per top-level declaration.
	//
	// ⚠ It is FALSE for an unevaluated decl, and that is the right answer rather than a placeholder: the
	// only reader in that window is the evaluator's own name resolution (`evalConstNamed` → `constantDeclFor`),
	// mid-DFS, and answering "inlined" merely lets the reference through to `evaluateDecl` — which settles the
	// decl and then answers `notFound` for an array exactly as it does for a managed literal. That is what
	// makes `let A = [1, 2]` / `let B = A` E2004 in BOTH declaration orders (measured against the oracle),
	// and it is what keeps the forward reference `let TOTAL = FIRST + SECOND` above `let FIRST = 30` working.
	export var hasStorage as bool
	// ⭐ **THE TIER THE DECLARATION WROTE (N1a)** — kept ALONGSIDE `storageKey`, which is derived from it, and
	// not merely folded into it: the key answers "which declaration is this?" and separates the file-private
	// ones, while a `module` declaration shares the bare key with an exported one and is told apart only by
	// asking the READER's file. `declFor` needs both.
	export var visibility as Visibility
	export var declFilePath as FilePath
	export var tokens as TokenArray
	export var initStart as TokenIndex
	export var initEnd as TokenIndex
	// The NAME's position — where a diagnostic about this declaration points.
	export var nameLine as LineNumber
	export var nameColumn as ColumnNumber
	// The evaluator's answer, mutated in place as the DFS reaches and leaves this decl.
	export var outcome as TopLevelConstant
	// ⭐ THE `.data` LABEL — this var's IR IDENTITY, and EMPTY for a `let`, which has no storage.
	//
	// DERIVED FROM THE KEY, not a second spelling of it: `assignDataLabels` walks the arena in
	// declaration order and hands each mutable decl its bare name, disambiguated ONLY where an
	// earlier decl already took that label. So the common program gets `__data_counter` and the
	// pathological one gets `__data_counter` + `__data_counter$1` — and NEITHER carries a file path,
	// which is what keeps a golden fragment free of the temp directory the spec runner compiled in.
	//
	// Empty is not a sentinel here: it is the literal truth that an immutable declaration has no
	// slot, and every reader gates on `mutable` before looking (`dataLabelOf` panics rather than
	// handing back an empty label).
	export var dataLabel as ByteArray
	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH BODY DECLARED THIS BINDING — file scope, or a `type` body's `static let`/`static var`.**
	// shv2 reads the two through ONE reader (`Parser.readTopLevelBinding`, W65) because their STORAGE is one
	// thing; this is the one place where WHEN they are initialized differs, so the fact is recorded where it
	// is known rather than re-derived downstream from whether the name carries a `Type.` qualifier — which
	// would be a lossy key used as an identity, and a file-scope `let` named with a dot is not a thing the
	// grammar can produce but is exactly the kind of assumption this repo has been bitten by.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT IS NOT A STYLE DISTINCTION — IT IS MEASURED, AND CANONICAL STATES BOTH HALVES.**
	// `specs/lazy-static.md`: a static field's initializer is *"evaluated the first time the static field is
	// accessed"*. `specs/dead-top-level-var-elim.md:31`: a module-level var's initializer *"still runs even
	// when the slot is dead"*. MEASURED on the runnable oracle with ONE program moved between the two
	// positions (a never-read binding whose initializer bumps a counter): **`static var` prints 0, module-level
	// `var` prints 1**. See `ModuleInit` for the two mechanisms that answer.
	export var scope as TopLevelBindingScope

	export static function create(name ByteArray, mutable bool, visibility Visibility, declFilePath FilePath, tokens TokenArray, initStart TokenIndex, initEnd TokenIndex, nameLine LineNumber, nameColumn ColumnNumber, scope TopLevelBindingScope) returns TopLevelDecl
		return Self{
			storageKey: topLevelStorageKey(name, visibility: visibility, declFilePath: declFilePath),
			name: name,
			visibility: visibility,
			mutable: mutable,
			hasStorage: mutable,
			declFilePath: declFilePath,
			tokens: tokens,
			initStart: initStart,
			initEnd: initEnd,
			nameLine: nameLine,
			nameColumn: nameColumn,
			outcome: TopLevelConstant.pending,
			dataLabel: b"",
			scope: scope
		}
	end 'create'
end 'TopLevelDecl'

// ⭐ **THE BODY A TOP-LEVEL BINDING WAS DECLARED IN** — see `TopLevelDecl.scope` for the measurement that
// makes this a semantic fact rather than a provenance note.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A UNION RATHER THAN A TWO-CASE ENUM, BECAUSE *WHICH* TYPE IS PART OF THE SAME FACT (W116).** The
// enclosing type name is what `readTopLevelBinding` is already handed (`qualifier`) and was throwing away
// after reducing it to a bit; a `static var origin = Pair{…}` needs it back, because E3076's rule is about
// WHERE the literal is written and the const evaluator is a throwaway `Parser` seated on a token range with
// no enclosing declaration of its own. Recovering it by splitting `Type.member` back out of `decl.name` would
// be exactly the "lossy key used as an identity" `TopLevelDecl.scope`'s header refuses; carrying it is the
// same fact stated once, by the one party that is TOLD it.
export union TopLevelBindingScope
	// `var x = …` / `let X = …` at file scope. Its initializer runs in `__module_init`, before `main`,
	// unconditionally.
	fileScope
	// `static var x = …` / `static let X = …` inside `type <typeName>`'s body. Its initializer runs on FIRST
	// ACCESS, and it is written INSIDE that type — which is what makes `Self`-shaped construction legal there
	// and nowhere else (`Parser.seatInDeclaringTypeBody`).
	typeMember(typeName ByteArray)
end 'TopLevelBindingScope'

// Was this binding declared inside a `type` body? The one place the union above is reduced to the BIT
// `declSlotIsLazy` reads, so the payload the other reader needs cannot make the lazy rule read two ways.
function declaredInATypeBody(decl TopLevelDecl) returns bool
	return match decl.scope 'declaredIn'
		fileScope gives false
		typeMember gives true
	end 'declaredIn'
end 'declaredInATypeBody'

export typealias TopLevelDeclArray = Array with TopLevelDecl

// What the arena knows about a bare name — `CalleeReturn`'s twin, and a union for its reason: "no
// constant of this name is visible here" is an ANSWER (the name is probably a local), not a failure.
union TopLevelDeclLookup
	found(index TopLevelDeclIndex)
	notFound
end 'TopLevelDeclLookup'

// The result of the managed-opaque-`T`-`Array`-field walk (P1.7 slice 3b-ii): `present(element)` carries the
// field's SUBSTITUTED element type (the destructor the descriptor `destroyFunc@40` needs), `absent` when the
// instance holds no such field. A union for its reason — "this instance owns no managed opaque array" is an
// ANSWER (most instances do not), not a failure — and so both the reloc-need predicate and the reloc's
// destructor resolve from ONE walk. See `managedOpaqueArrayElementOf`.
union ManagedOpaqueArrayElement
	present(element MaxonType)
	absent
end 'ManagedOpaqueArrayElement'

// What a constant expression evaluates to: the PAYLOAD, and the TAG that says what the payload
// means. A bool's payload is its 0/1 (TypeRules' `BoolTrueValue`), which is why one integer field
// serves both — and why the tag is not optional: a `boolean` 1 and an `integer` 1 are the same
// number and different values.
//
// A `type` (so, one heap object per operand folded), which the per-VALUE rules in TypeRules go to
// real lengths to avoid — and the trade is the other way here. Those run on every operator of every
// expression of every function; this runs once per top-level constant DECLARATION, of which a
// program has a handful. A tagged pair is what the code wants to say, and there is no hot path to
// pay for it.
export type ConstValue
	export var payload as ParsedInt
	export var tag as ValueTypeTag

	export static function create(payload ParsedInt, tag ValueTypeTag) returns ConstValue
		return Self{payload: payload, tag: tag}
	end 'create'
end 'ConstValue'

// ⭐ **WHAT ONE TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER FOLDED TO** — the evaluator's ENTRY-POINT answer, and the one
// place the scalar/managed split is made.
//
// A constant's value is either a NUMBER or a managed literal's BYTES, and those cannot share
// `ConstValue`'s scalar payload. Answering with this union at the entry point keeps every fold BELOW
// it (`evalConstBinary`, `foldConstBinOp`, `applyConstCast`, …) scalar and untouched — so a managed
// literal is never an operand of an operator that would have to reject it, and the arithmetic rules
// stay one reading of the grammar rather than two.
export union ConstInitializerValue
	// Everything that folds WITHOUT running user code — and therefore, exactly, everything a factory
	// ARGUMENT may be.
	direct(value ConstFoldedValue)
	// ⭐ **A USER STATIC FACTORY CALL — `Database.create(EntryMap.create(), sourcePaths: …)`** (spec-port
	// `map-struct-bytearray`). Nothing is folded: what this answer carries is WHICH functions to call and
	// WHAT to pass them, and the record itself is built before `main` by `__module_init`. The return TYPE is
	// resolved on the index's side, where the interner the global's drop callee is routed against lives.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE CALL TREE IS FLAT, AND THAT IS WHAT LETS A CALL NEST INSIDE A CALL** (spec-port
	// `module-level-struct-var`). `calls` is the initializer's whole tree — `Outer.create(Inner.create(0))`
	// is two entries — and `root` names the OUTERMOST one, the call whose result the slot holds. Every
	// other entry is reached from an argument that names it by INDEX, which is an integer and therefore
	// not a type naming itself (see `ConstFactoryArgumentValue`).
	//
	// ⚠ **`root` IS STATED, NOT DERIVED.** The parser happens to append each call once its arguments are
	// walked, which puts the outermost one last — but that is a property of a recursive descent, not a
	// contract, and a reader computing `count() - 1` would be depending on it. One integer, written down by
	// the only walk that knows.
	factoryCall(calls ConstFactoryCallArray, root InitializerCallId)
end 'ConstInitializerValue'

// ⭐ **ONE NODE OF A TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER'S FLATTENED CONSTRUCTION TREE.**
//
// ⭐⭐ **A STRUCT CONSTRUCTION IS A CALL WHOSE CALLEE IS A TYPE RATHER THAN A FUNCTION, AND WHOSE LABELLED
// ARGUMENTS ARE ITS FIELDS (W116).** That one sentence is why `Pair{a: 1, b: 2}` lives in THIS arena and not
// in a structure of its own: a field's value is measurably anything a factory argument is — `Outer{inner:
// Inner.create(7)}` makes it a CALL and `Holder{xs: [1, 2, 3]}` makes it an ARRAY LITERAL — so a private
// element union could hold neither, and widening one until it could would rebuild `ConstFactoryArgumentValue`
// over a second flat arena. Both already exist here, for the E4014 reason stated below.
//
// A UNION rather than a `kind` beside a `callee` only one arm can fill — `ManagedGlobalInitializer`'s own
// argument, one tier down: each arm carries exactly what its construction needs and nothing else, so a
// callee cannot be read off a construction and a field roster cannot be read off a call.
//
// Neither arm carries a return TYPE, and that absence is deliberate: a node's result type is a fact its
// RECEIVER already holds — `ManagedGlobal.valueType` for the root, `GlobalInitArgument.valueType` for a
// nested one — so recording it here too would be the same type written down twice, on two paths that could
// drift. `ProgramSignatures.constFactoryCallResultType` is the one place either is derived.
export union ConstFactoryCall
	// A user static factory: the mangled `Type.method` name (`qualifiedMethodName`, which is the same key a
	// body's call site emits and the signature registry files the declaration under) and the arguments the
	// author wrote, in SOURCE order.
	call(callee ByteArray, args ConstFactoryArgumentArray)
	// ⭐ **A STRUCT LITERAL — `Pair{a: 1, b: 2}` inside `type Pair`'s own body** (W116). `typeName` is the
	// declared struct's name, and `fields` is LAYOUT-PARALLEL rather than source-ordered: entry `i` is the
	// value for the field the type declares at `i`, whichever position the literal wrote it in and whether it
	// was written at all (a slot the literal omitted carries its declared DEFAULT, resolved by the same walk).
	// That is the ordering `Parser.emitStructLiteral` already stores in, made explicit here because this arena
	// has no layout to walk at emit time.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE VALUES ALONE, WITHOUT THE `name:` LABELS AN ARGUMENT CARRIES — because a field's DESTINATION IS
	// ITS INDEX.** A call's labels exist so `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerCall` can slot them against the callee's
	// declared parameter names; a field is already in its slot, so a label here would be a second spelling of
	// the position, read by nothing.
	construct(typeName ByteArray, fields ConstFactoryArgumentValueArray)
end 'ConstFactoryCall'

export typealias ConstFactoryCallArray = Array with ConstFactoryCall

// ⭐ **WHICH CALL OF A TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER'S FLATTENED TREE** — the purpose, which is neither tier's, and
// that is deliberately what the name says. It indexes `ConstFactoryCallArray` on the fold side AND the
// `GlobalInitCallArray` the index tier resolves from it entry-for-entry, so ONE id names the same call on
// both sides. A per-tier `…FactoryCallId` / `GlobalInitCallId` pair would be one fact written twice, and
// nothing would then catch the two drifting apart — the ids the emitter reads are the ids the evaluator
// minted, unchanged.
export typealias InitializerCallId = int(0 to u64.max)

// The one get-or-panic over a fold-tier call arena. An id is minted by the walk that pushed the call it
// names (`Parser.evalConstFactoryCallInto`) and travels only inside the arena it was minted from, so a miss
// is an arena the walk and its readers disagree about rather than anything a program can write.
export function factoryCallAt(calls ConstFactoryCallArray, id InitializerCallId) returns ConstFactoryCall
	return try calls.get(id) otherwise panic("factoryCallAt: no call {id} in an initializer arena of {calls.count()} — every id is the index the evaluator pushed that call at")
end 'factoryCallAt'

// ⭐ **THE NAME A ROOT NODE CONTRIBUTES TO A DECLARING FILE'S CONTENT HASH** — the callee for a call, the
// STRUCT for a construction (W116). One derivation, so the two spellings of "what fixes this slot's type"
// cannot be mixed two ways; see `ProgramSignatures.mixConstant`'s `factoryValue` arm for why the arguments
// and the fields are deliberately left out of the hash.
export function constFactoryCallHashName(call ConstFactoryCall) returns ByteArray
	return match call 'node'
		call(callee, _) gives callee
		construct(typeName, _) gives typeName
	end 'node'
end 'constFactoryCallHashName'

// What a top-level initializer folds to when it does NOT call. Its own union for `ManagedGlobalRecord`'s
// reason — Maxon forbids a recursive type (E4014) — and the two say the same thing at the two tiers: this
// is everything an initializer can be WITHOUT running user code, stated once on each side of the fold.
// A call is not an arm here; it is an arena entry an argument names by index.
export union ConstFoldedValue
	scalar(value ConstValue)
	managed(bytes ByteArray, kind ManagedConstKind)
	// An ARRAY LITERAL (P1.7 slice 2b) — its folded elements and the kind they share. It is its own answer
	// rather than a `managed` with a different payload for `TopLevelConstant.arrayValue`'s reason: the
	// binding it settles has STORAGE, where a `managed` `let` has none.
	arrayLiteral(elements ConstArrayElementArray, element ConstArrayElementKind)
	// A CONTAINER whose INSTANCE IS STATED rather than inferred — `ItemArray.create()` and `EntryMap.create()`
	// (empty), and `CharSet from ['a', 'e']` (with members). Its own answer for `arrayLiteral`'s reason: the
	// binding it settles has STORAGE, and what it stores is a record no read can re-materialize — and it is
	// separate from `arrayLiteral` because the two are TYPED from different facts: a literal's instance is
	// INFERRED from its first element's kind (`constArrayValueType`), where this one is the giid the alias
	// resolved to. That difference is exactly why the `from` form exists at all: a bare `['a', 'e']` is an
	// array of characters, and only the alias can say the container is a `Set` over them.
	containerCreate(giid GenericInstanceId, elements ConstArrayElementArray)
	// ⚠ **A `characterSet` ARM STOOD HERE AND WENT AT W129** — `containerCreate` plus the mask `__cs_make`
	// sealed the member set into a box with. It is `ManagedGlobalRecord`'s deleted `characterSetRecord` at
	// this tier, and the two went together because the pair is the same fact stated on each side of the fold.
end 'ConstFoldedValue'

// ONE argument of a top-level factory call, as the evaluator walked it: the source-level `name:` LABEL
// (empty for a positional argument) and the value. The `GlobalInitArgument` this becomes carries a TYPE as
// well, resolved on the index's side against its own interner.
export type ConstFactoryArgument
	export let label as ByteArray
	export let value as ConstFactoryArgumentValue

	export static function create(label ByteArray, value ConstFactoryArgumentValue) returns ConstFactoryArgument
		return Self{label: label, value: value}
	end 'create'
end 'ConstFactoryArgument'

export typealias ConstFactoryArgumentArray = Array with ConstFactoryArgument

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT ONE ARGUMENT OF A TOP-LEVEL FACTORY CALL IS — a value the compiler FOLDED, or another CALL.**
// The second arm is what `module-level-struct-var`'s `Outer.create(Inner.create(0))` needs, and it carries
// an INDEX rather than the call itself for the reason the whole arena exists: a `ConstFactoryCall` holds
// these arguments, so an arm holding a `ConstFactoryCall` would close a reference cycle and Maxon forbids
// one outright (E4014, `type 'X' contains a reference cycle`). An integer is not a cycle, so the tree is
// held flat in `ConstInitializerValue.factoryCall`'s arena and every edge of it is one of these ids.
export union ConstFactoryArgumentValue
	// A constant, a literal or an empty container — anything `evalConstFoldedValue` produces, which is
	// exactly everything an initializer can be without running user code.
	folded(value ConstFoldedValue)
	// A NESTED factory call, by its position in the enclosing initializer's arena. `__module_init` emits it
	// BEFORE the call that takes it, and the result is an owned record this argument then answers for
	// (`GlobalInitArgumentCleanup`) exactly as a materialized one does.
	call(id InitializerCallId)
end 'ConstFactoryArgumentValue'

// The layout-parallel field column a `ConstFactoryCall.construct` carries — see there for why a field is an
// argument VALUE with no label.
export typealias ConstFactoryArgumentValueArray = Array with ConstFactoryArgumentValue

// Is this value a folded NUMBER, and which? A union rather than a bool beside an accessor, because "it is a
// record" is an ANSWER here and not a failure: the two askers are the range check (which has nothing to
// compare a record against) and the float widening (for which anything else is an invariant break).
export union ConstScalarLookup
	scalar(value ConstValue)
	notAScalar
end 'ConstScalarLookup'

export function constScalarOf(value ConstFactoryArgumentValue) returns ConstScalarLookup
	match value 'value'
		call then return ConstScalarLookup.notAScalar
		folded(folded) then return foldedConstScalarOf(folded)
	end 'value'
end 'constScalarOf'

// The tail of the question above, split out because a `match` arm may not open one.
function foldedConstScalarOf(folded ConstFoldedValue) returns ConstScalarLookup
	match folded 'folded'
		scalar(constant) then return ConstScalarLookup.scalar(constant)
		managed to containerCreate then return ConstScalarLookup.notAScalar
	end 'folded'
end 'foldedConstScalarOf'

// ⭐ **WHETHER A CONSTANT MAY FILL A STRUCT-LITERAL SLOT (W116)** — `ProgramSignatures.constStructFieldCheck`'s
// answer. The two halves of `mismatch` arrive ALREADY RENDERED because only the index side holds the interner
// that can name them; the fold tier's job is to raise the diagnostic, at the position only it can see.
export union ConstStructFieldCheck
	agrees
	// The slot is an f64 and the value an integer constant. The fold tier converts through the very primitive
	// `Parser.fillFieldDefault` uses (`constFloatBits`), so a written value and a declared default reach an
	// f64 slot as the same bits.
	widensToFloat
	mismatch(got String, expected String)
end 'ConstStructFieldCheck'

// ⭐ **A SLOT A TOP-LEVEL STRUCT CONSTRUCTION CANNOT FILL, OR THE ABSENCE OF ONE (W116 review)** — a union
// rather than a nullable pair, for `ConstScalarLookup`'s reason one door over: "there is none" is the answer
// for every program in the corpus and not a lookup that failed.
// ⭐ **WHAT A STRUCT-LITERAL SLOT HOLDS, OR THE ADMISSION THAT THIS TIER CANNOT SAY (W116 review)** — see
// `ProgramSignatures.constFieldSlotType`, and `ConstStructFieldCheck` for what the second arm buys: a door
// that cannot resolve a name must DECLINE rather than name a type nobody wrote.
export union ConstFieldSlotLookup
	holds(slot MaxonType)
	unresolvableHere
end 'ConstFieldSlotLookup'

// The slot a lookup resolved to, or `MapError.keyNotFound` for the tier that could not say — so the one
// caller reads it with the `try … otherwise` its "then do not judge" answer already wants, rather than a
// `match` whose second arm would have to invent a type to return.
export function slotTypeHeld(lookup ConstFieldSlotLookup) returns MaxonType throws MapError
	match lookup 'lookup'
		holds(slot) then return slot
		unresolvableHere then throw MapError.keyNotFound
	end 'lookup'
end 'slotTypeHeld'

export union ConstConstructionFieldLookup
	none
	existentialField(layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex)
end 'ConstConstructionFieldLookup'

// The result of asking the evaluator for a constant's value. `failed` is not an error to report — the
// diagnostic is already recorded on the decl that owns it — it is the instruction to STOP.
export union ConstEvalOutcome
	value(payload ParsedInt, tag ValueTypeTag)
	failed
	notFound
end 'ConstEvalOutcome'

// Why a constant's initializer produced no value. Distinct from `ParseError` because the evaluator
// has one outcome `ParseError` cannot express: "the defect is somewhere else, and is already
// reported".
export union ConstEvalError implements Error
	// THIS initializer is defective; `error` is the positioned diagnostic for it.
	defective(error ParseError)
	// A constant this one READS has already been diagnosed. Carries no diagnostic of its own — see
	// `TopLevelConstant.poisoned`.
	dependencyFailed
end 'ConstEvalError'

// The outcome of a USE-SITE constant lookup, after evaluation has finished. Deliberately NOT
// `ConstEvalOutcome`: a use site cannot trigger an evaluation (they are all done by then) and must
// not be able to, so it is given a type with no `failed` arm to mishandle.
export union TopLevelConstantLookup
	found(payload ParsedInt, tag ValueTypeTag)
	// The MANAGED twin of `found` (P1.7a top-level managed `let`): a use re-materializes the literal
	// from these bytes rather than inlining a number. It sits BESIDE `found` — unlike
	// `TopLevelConstant.managedValue`, which is appended — because the two "a constant of this name is
	// visible here" cases want to be one range arm at the caller that only asks whether it EXISTS
	// (`Parser.rejectNonGlobalAssignment`: assigning to either is E2013).
	foundManaged(bytes ByteArray, kind ManagedConstKind)
	notFound
end 'TopLevelConstantLookup'

// The outcome of a USE-SITE lookup for a top-level `var` — `TopLevelConstantLookup`'s twin, and
// deliberately a DIFFERENT type carrying DIFFERENT payload. A constant's use needs its VALUE (it
// inlines a number); a var's use needs its LABEL and TYPE (it emits a load against a slot). One
// union carrying both would have an arm every caller had to prove it was not holding.
//
// ⚠ **THE WHOLE `MaxonType`, NOT A BARE TAG** (P1.7 slice 2a). A `var Buffer = b"…"` loads a value of the
// CONCRETE `Array with Byte` instance, and an instance is its gid — a tag alone cannot say which one, and
// a use site that re-derived it would be a second answer to "what type is this global?" free to name a
// different instance than the slot was laid out for.
//
// ⚠ **AND THE MUTABILITY, because a STORED binding is no longer necessarily a `var`** (P1.7 slice 2b). An
// array `let` has a slot — its value is a heap record no read can re-materialize — so "which slot does this
// name denote?" and "may it be written?" came apart, and this door answers the first. The second is asked by
// the two sites that WRITE (`Parser.parseTopLevelAssignment`, and the synthetic receiver binding a method
// call is dispatched on), off this bit; without it an array `let` would silently accept both.
export union TopLevelGlobalLookup
	found(label ByteArray, valueType MaxonType, mutable bool)
	notFound
end 'TopLevelGlobalLookup'

// Which kind of top-level declaration a lookup is asking for. The two are resolved by ONE probe
// (`declFor`) and are never interchangeable at a use site: an inlined binding materializes its value, a
// stored one loads from a slot.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS `inlined` vs `stored`, NOT `let` vs `var`** (P1.7 slice 2b). Those two questions were the same
// question until an array literal reached the top level: an array binding's value is a HEAP RECORD, which
// no read can re-materialize, so it gets a `.data` slot whichever keyword declared it — and a `let` differs
// from a `var` only in refusing the WRITE. `TopLevelDecl.hasStorage` is the one criterion, and naming this enum
// after the storage rather than the keyword is what stops a reader assuming they still coincide.
//
// An enum, not the bare `mutable bool` the decl carries: at a CALL SITE `wanted: DeclKind.inlined` says
// what is being asked for, where `wanted: false` would be a magic value the reader has to decode against
// the field's polarity.
enum DeclKind
	inlined
	stored
end 'DeclKind'

// The per-argument classification the three "does any interned instance of `baseName` classify its type
// parameter at `position` as …?" instance-scan gates in `ProgramSignatures` differ on — the ONE thing that
// varies between them, named here so the registry SCAN (match base name, bounds-guard the arg) lives in one
// place (`instanceWithTypeArgKind`) and a fix to the loop or the bound cannot reach one gate and silently
// miss the other two.
enum TypeArgInstantiationKind
	// The box MOVES the argument in and the caller may not reuse it (`typeArgIsOwned`): a String, an owning
	// struct, a boxed union, a managed generic instance — the managed-field DROP and the reassign's
	// descriptor-gated old-value drop.
	consumeMoved
	// The box CO-OWNS a BORROWED trivial struct/instance (`typeIsManaged and not typeArgIsOwned`): the
	// retain-escaping rung's `__mm_incref`-at-feed + destructor-decref pair, which a shared-body reassign
	// cannot re-balance and therefore rejects.
	coOwnedTrivial
	// Not deep-cloneable through the single-`copyFunc` element mechanism (`not typeSupportsDeepClone(as
	// element)`): a managed-element array or a non-Array generic instance — the opaque-copy front-end reject.
	uncopyableElement
	// A HEAP AGGREGATE (`typeIsManaged`): a String, a struct, a boxed union, a nested container — anything
	// whose slot is a POINTER rather than the value. The front-end reject for the one container that
	// PUBLISHES its slots at birth (`Vector with N <type parameter>`, S2u): `create()` zeroes every slot, and
	// a zeroed pointer is an ABSENCE, not a value.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT IS NOT `consumeMoved`, AND THE TWO ARE ONE WORD APART IN ENGLISH AND A WHOLE CLASS APART IN
	// FACT.** That one is the CONSUME boundary (`typeArgIsOwned` — does the box take the caller's value
	// away), and it answers FALSE for an all-scalar struct, which is BORROWED at the call site. A `Vector`
	// slot standing for one is still a null pointer with a live `destroyFunc@40` behind it, so the boundary
	// this kind needs is the one `containerElementIsManaged` — the CONCRETE vector's own element gate —
	// already reads. The two gates then agree on what "managed" means, which is what a shared body's refusal
	// and a concrete instance's refusal must do.
	managedElement
end 'TypeArgInstantiationKind'

// Which instance of the declaring base classified its argument as the asked-for kind, or that none did.
// A UNION rather than a bool because the two questions have different answers to give: an EMISSION gate
// wants membership, and a REFUSAL wants the instance itself — it is what the user WROTE, and so the only
// route from the refusal back to a source anchor the author can read (`LibraryBlame`).
export union TypeArgKindMatch
	noInstance
	instance(id GenericInstanceId)
end 'TypeArgKindMatch'

// ⭐ **WHICH OF THE FOUR KINDS REFUSE A PROGRAM RATHER THAN SHAPE AN EMISSION** — the direction test
// `instanceWithTypeArgKind` applies, stated here beside the kinds it splits so the reason travels with them.
//
// The two emission kinds answer *"must this shared body carry a drop?"*, where a row describing a shape that
// never occurs buys a descriptor-gated drop nothing executes — a no-op at run time — and ignoring one would
// omit a drop that is owed. They therefore keep reading every row. The two REFUSING kinds answer *"must this
// program be REFUSED?"*, where such a row is not a conservative margin but a wrong answer about a program
// that does not contain the instantiation being refused for.
//
// ⛔ **TWO CLASSES OF ROW ARE NOT FACTS ABOUT THE PROGRAM, AND ONLY A REFUSAL MAY NOT READ THEM** — a
// SPECULATIVELY minted one (A4q) and one minted by substituting into an instantiation E3017 already owns
// (W90). They are unrelated in origin and identical in consequence, which is why one predicate answers for
// both; `instanceWithTypeArgKind` applies it twice and carries both measurements.
function kindIsARefusal(kind TypeArgInstantiationKind) returns bool
	match kind 'kind'
		consumeMoved then return false
		coOwnedTrivial then return false
		uncopyableElement then return true
		managedElement then return true
	end 'kind'
end 'kindIsARefusal'

// The `StdType` a global whose `.data` bytes ARE ITS VALUE occupies its slot as — which is to say WHICH
// TAGS MAY BE A FOLDED GLOBAL AT ALL, because the width itself is not this function's to know.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT ANSWERS FOR THE INLINE-VALUE PATH ONLY, AND SERVING BOTH PATHS IS WHAT WENT WRONG (S2j).**
// A RECORD-valued global reserves a POINTER instead, and its width is a fact about the SLOT rather than
// about the tag; it goes through `recordPointerSlotType`, which carries its own reachability guard. While
// this door served both, its guard — written for a folded scalar and perfectly right about one — was
// applied to a factory's declared RETURN type, where the `named` tag means a boxed union or a tuple alias
// and not a ranged alias at all. A legal program PANICKED, told that its `var` was *"tagged 'int'"* in a
// program with no int in it. So the arms below now say what is true of THIS caller, and the three record
// tags that used to break through here name the door that took them.
//
// ⚠ **IT NO LONGER CARRIES ITS OWN TABLE, AND THAT IS THE POINT.** It used to answer `integer`→i64
// and `boolean`→i1 in its own two arms, which made it a SECOND copy of the tag→`StdType` collapse
// that `LowerMaxonToStd.valueTagToStdType` already is — one fact written down twice, in the
// project's signature failure mode. The two copies had already begun to drift in the only way they
// could: this one had no `float` arm, so `var pi = 3.14` panicked here while the lowering was
// perfectly willing to give it an f64. A global's width and a value's width are THE SAME QUESTION,
// and `data-section-f64-8byte` pins the 8 bytes that answer proves.
//
// ⭐ **THE REACHABILITY GUARD IS WHAT IS LOCAL** — which folded constants may be a global's inline value
// — and that is a different question from how wide the type is. Every tag but the three the evaluator
// folds panics by name; a bare `default` here would silently size a tag nothing in this door understands.
// `named` needs an `as` cast (P1.9), so it panics rather than defaulting to a width.
//
// `boolean` is ONE byte (`i1`, whose `storageBytes` is 1), which is what
// `static-variables.md`'s `data-section-bool-1byte` pins; `integer` is eight (`i64`); `float` is
// eight (`f64`). All of them come from the one table.
function globalStdType(tag ValueTypeTag, name ByteArray) returns StdType
	match tag 'reachable'
		boolean to float then break 'reachable'
		named then panic("globalStdType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a folded global's type is inferred from a bare constant initializer, so it is never a ranged alias (that needs an `as` cast, P1.9). A factory's declared `named` RETURN type — a boxed union, a tuple alias — is a RECORD and takes `recordPointerSlotType` instead")
		exitCode to void then panic("globalStdType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — the constant evaluator folds integers, bools and floats only")
		// ⭐ THE THREE RECORD TAGS. A struct (`var db = Database.create(…)`), a `String` or `b"…"` literal,
		// and an array/container instance are all globals whose `.data` bytes are a POINTER rather than a
		// value, so their width is `recordPointerSlotType`'s to answer and not this door's. They used to
		// break through here, which is how this guard's `named` arm came to be applied to a factory return
		// type it was never written about — see this function's header.
		//
		// ⚠ ONE SENTENCE, not one per tag: the three differ by nothing the message does not already carry,
		// because `typeTagName(tag)` names which of them arrived. Three spellings of it were three chances
		// for one to drift from the door it points at.
		structRef to string then panic("{recordSlotGlobalPanic(name, tag: tag)}")
		genericInstance then panic("{recordSlotGlobalPanic(name, tag: tag)}")
		// A FUNCTION global is unreachable (P1.5-A1): a global takes no type annotation and a function
		// reference is not a constant initializer, so a global's inferred type is never `function` (a bare
		// function name at top level is rejected before this — `var g = double` is E2045, no call in a
		// constant initializer). Were it to reach here, the honest answer is still not a width.
		function then panic("globalStdType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a function value is not a constant initializer, so a global's inferred type is never a function")
		// A TYPE-PARAMETER global is unreachable (P1.6-A): a type parameter names no value outside a
		// generic body, and a global's inline value is a folded scalar constant.
		typeParameter then panic("globalStdType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a type parameter names no value at module scope")
		// A CHARACTER global is unreachable: a top-level binding's initializer is a folded constant, and a
		// multi-byte character literal folds to a managed `TopLevelConstant.managedValue` only for the two
		// TEXT kinds the evaluator knows (a String literal and a `b"…"` byte string). Were a Character
		// global ever admitted, its slot would be a record POINTER — the other door's answer, not a width —
		// so this panics rather than silently sizing one.
		character then panic("globalStdType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a character literal is not a foldable constant initializer, so a global's inferred type is never a Character")
		// An EXISTENTIAL global is unreachable for `structRef`'s reason and one more of its own: a global
		// takes no type annotation, so the only way to reach an interface type here is a folded initializer,
		// and there is none. Were one ever admitted, the honest answer is still not a width — a `.data` slot
		// for a fat pointer is TWO words, which neither single-width door can express.
		interfaceRef then panic("globalStdType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — an interface-typed value is a two-word fat pointer, and a folded global's inferred type comes from a scalar constant")
	end 'reachable'

	return valueTagToStdType(tag)
end 'globalStdType'

// The one sentence the three RECORD tags share when they reach the INLINE-VALUE door by mistake. Written
// once because the only thing that distinguishes them — which tag arrived — is already interpolated into
// it, and because the door it redirects to is a name that can be renamed.
function recordSlotGlobalPanic(name ByteArray, tag ValueTypeTag) returns String
	return "globalStdType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a record-valued global's slot holds a POINTER `__module_init` fills, so it is typed by `recordPointerSlotType`; this door answers only for a global whose `.data` bytes ARE its value"
end 'recordSlotGlobalPanic'

// What the index knows about a callee — the TWO outcomes the old `MaxonType.unresolved` sentinel
// collapsed into one, plus the third (`void`) that it dropped on the floor.
export union CalleeReturn
	// Some file declares this function, and `returnType` is its declaration UNDISTORTED: `void`
	// means it returns nothing at all (using the call as a value is an error — `Parser.parseCall`),
	// `value(t)` means it returns a `t`. The old code mapped BOTH of these and the case below onto
	// one `unresolved`, which is how `let x = noop()` then `x + 4` compiled.
	declared(returnType MaxonReturnType)
	// NO file declares it. The parser DEFERS — it types the call `unresolved` — rather than
	// reporting, and that is deliberate: the authoritative answer belongs to
	// `SemanticCheck.validateCall`, which reads the registry the REAL parse built (E3004, "call to
	// undefined function"). This token sweep is the TOLERANT half of the pair (it records only the
	// exact `function NAME ( … ) returns TYPE` shape and skips anything else without complaint), so
	// it must never gain a veto: a sweep that missed a declaration the parser accepts would reject
	// a correct program. Two whole-program tables that could each veto a call would also be one
	// fact written down twice — and this one is not the authority.
	undeclared
end 'CalleeReturn'

// ⭐ **THE INSTANCE A BARE GENERIC TYPE NAME DENOTES IN A TYPE-PARAMETER SCOPE, or `notInScope`** —
// see `ProgramSignatures.scopedGenericInstanceOf`. A union rather than a sentinel for `DeclaredAlias`'s
// reason: "this scope binds nothing for that name" is an ANSWER — the name then means exactly what it always
// meant — and `GenericInstanceId` has no value free to say it with.
export union ScopedGenericInstance
	instance(id GenericInstanceId)
	notInScope
end 'ScopedGenericInstance'

// ⭐ **DOES `scopeParamNames` BIND EVERY ONE OF `paramNames`? — the ALL-OR-NOTHING half of
// `ProgramSignatures.scopedGenericInstanceOf`, and v1's own rule**
// (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/TypeResolution.maxon:4366-4374`). A PARTIAL overlap binds
// nothing rather than binding what it can: `type Pair uses A, B` named inside `type Outer uses A` has no `B`
// for the scope to stand for, and inventing one would give the instance an argument the author never wrote.
// The name then stays what it was, and the ordinary refusals speak for it — which for the reaching program
// is a positioned `E3005` where the C# bootstrap accepts it silently and dies in the assembler
// (`E9001: Unresolved label`).
//
// A linear scan over both, for `Parser.typeParamPositionOf`'s reason: a type declares a handful of
// parameters, so a name→index map would be a heap object bought to serve one or two lookups.
function scopeBindsEveryTypeParam(paramNames ByteArrayArray, scopeParamNames ByteArrayArray) returns bool
	for paramName in paramNames 'eachParam'
		var bound = false
		for scopeName in scopeParamNames 'eachScopeName'
			if scopeName.equals(paramName) 'hit'
				bound = true
				break
			end 'hit'
		end 'eachScopeName'

		if not bound 'notInScope'
			return false
		end 'notInScope'
	end 'eachParam'

	return true
end 'scopeBindsEveryTypeParam'

// What the index knows about a NAMED type — `CalleeReturn`'s twin, and a union for the same
// reason: "no file declares this" is an ANSWER, not a failure, and spelling it as a sentinel range
// (`low: 0`) would make an undeclared name read as UNSIGNED, which is the one thing it must never
// silently do.
export union DeclaredAlias
	declared(alias RangedTypeAlias)
	undeclared
end 'DeclaredAlias'

// What the index knows about a FUNCTION typealias (P1.5-A1) — `DeclaredAlias`'s twin, and a union for
// the identical reason: "no file declares a function alias of this name" is an ANSWER, not a failure.
// THE door `Parser.parseTypeReference` asks "is this name a function type?" through — a hit re-tags the
// reference `MaxonType.function`; a miss leaves it a `named` for the ranged/struct/enum registries.
export union DeclaredFunctionAlias
	declared(alias FunctionTypeAlias)
	undeclared
end 'DeclaredFunctionAlias'

// What the index knows about a declared STRUCT TYPE — `DeclaredAlias`'s twin, and a union for the
// identical reason. "No file declares a `type` of this name" is an ANSWER: the name is probably a
// ranged alias, or `ExitCode`, or a typo — and each of those is somebody else's question. Handing
// back an empty layout instead would make an undeclared name read as a struct with NO FIELDS, so
// `p.x` would report "no field 'x'" for a type that is not a struct at all.
export union DeclaredStruct
	declared(layout StructLayout)
	undeclared
end 'DeclaredStruct'

// What the index knows about a declared ENUM/UNION type — `DeclaredStruct`'s twin, and a union for
// the identical reason. "No file declares an `enum`/`union` of this name" is an ANSWER: the name is
// probably a struct, a ranged alias, or a typo — each somebody else's question. THE door the parser
// asks every enum question through: is this scrutinee an enum? what are its cases and tags?
export union DeclaredEnum
	declared(layout EnumLayout)
	undeclared
end 'DeclaredEnum'

// ⭐ **HOW A UNION PAYLOAD FIELD IS STORED AND DROPPED — P1.3 slice 2.** The ONE classifier the
// construct (move-in), the match extract (move-out), the drop routing, and the synthesized
// `__destruct_<U>` cascade all read, so "is field F managed, and if so String or struct?" is decided in
// exactly ONE place. That is the whole defence against this rung's signature bug: a field moved IN but
// not dropped leaks; dropped but not moved in double-frees — and neither can happen when one function
// decides both. It is v1's `unionHasManagedPayload` predicate generalised to also name the KIND.
export union PayloadClass
	// int / bool / exitCode / a ranged-int alias / a payload-free enum (whose value IS its i64 tag).
	// Stored inline; owns nothing; the cascade skips it.
	scalar(storage MaxonType)
	// A String (`isString`) or a struct box — an owned heap pointer. Construct MOVES it in (no incref);
	// a match binding MOVES it out; the cascade drops it (String via `__str_decref`, struct via
	// `__mm_decref`). `storage` is the field's own type, which `maxonTypeToStdType` gives a machine word.
	managed(storage MaxonType, isString bool)
	// A `float` payload — a scalar-only union is int-tagged, so a float payload must go in as bits and come
	// back out as a float. BOTH bitcast directions now exist (`bitcastF64ToI64` shipped with `float.hash`,
	// `bitcastI64ToF64` with the decimal-reader convergence); what no site does yet is APPLY them at the
	// construct and the extract, which is why this case is still a rejection and not a lowering.
	floatPayload
	// A boxed-union field/payload (a nested payload-bearing union) — a MANAGED owned heap pointer, exactly
	// like a `managed` struct box: construct MOVES it in (no incref), and the cascade drops it through its
	// OWN `__destruct_<U>` (or the trivial `__mm_decref` for a scalar-only union box). `storage` is the
	// field's `named(<union>)` type — the value tag a slot type-check compares against the value and the tag
	// a moved-out load carries, which is what makes a nested `match` on the extracted payload work.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS A CASE DISTINCT FROM `managed` BECAUSE ITS SLOT WIDTH AND ITS VALUE TYPE ARE DIFFERENT
	// ANSWERS, AND FOR NO OTHER REASON (W55)** — stated in full, arm by arm, at `payloadStorageOf`, which is
	// the one home of that split. What is local to this case: nesting costs NO codegen at any depth, because a
	// nested box's drop closes at RUN TIME through the destructor pointer in its own allocation header. That is
	// why the UNION-payload path's old refusal (`requirePayloadLowerable`) was a missing width derivation and
	// never the missing cascade its message claimed.
	nestedUnion(storage MaxonType)
	// A name no file declares — `TypeResolution` reports it as E3011; the caller keeps the op
	// well-formed for the instant before the pipeline's error gate throws.
	undeclaredName
	// ⭐ **A STRUCT FIELD DECLARED AT AN INTERFACE TYPE (P1.7a-existentials slice 2)** — the two-slot fat
	// pointer. MANAGED (`payloadClassIsManaged`), so the construct MOVES it in and the cascade drops it — but
	// kept a case DISTINCT from `managed` by ITS OWN arithmetic: a conformer takes TWO slots (a value half and
	// a witness half), and a union payload has exactly ONE, because every case of a union shares one slot
	// region and there is nowhere for a second word to widen into. So a union PAYLOAD at an interface type is
	// refused — at its DECLARATION (`Parser.requireStorableDeclaredType`), with `requirePayloadLowerable`'s arm
	// as the backstop — while the STRUCT-field path carves out both slots and lowers it. That refusal is
	// PERMANENT and arithmetic, which is what distinguishes it from `nestedUnion`'s, now retired: that one was a
	// missing width derivation for a payload that always fitted in the one slot it had. Its storage is the VALUE
	// half's machine word; the witness half is stored beside it by `Parser.emitFieldStore`, which is the only
	// site that knows the field's base offset.
	//
	// ⚠ **APPENDED AT THE END, per the band-append rule** — `payloadClassIsManaged`/`payloadClassIsString` are
	// exhaustive `match`es over this union, so a case inserted mid-list would be silently absorbed by nothing
	// while a case appended is a compile error at every one of them until it is answered.
	existential
	// ⭐⭐ **A SLOT DECLARED AT THE ENCLOSING GENERIC'S OWN TYPE PARAMETER (W41-opaque)** — one opaque machine
	// word in the shared body, whose ownership is a fact only the INSTANTIATION has. It is NOT
	// `payloadClassIsManaged`: nothing in the declaration view drops it (the base struct's own
	// `__destruct_<Base>` skips it — a per-INSTANCE cascade drops the substituted field, which is a different
	// symbol), so calling it managed here would drop it twice in the one view that can see neither.
	//
	// ⚠ **BUT IT IS NOT A PLAIN SCALAR EITHER, AND THAT WAS THE DEFECT.** It classified as `scalar` and so was
	// stored RAW — while the record it was stored into is CONCRETE at the caller (a `(String, Integer)` tuple,
	// a `Box with String`) and its destructor drops that slot. Two owners, one reference. The store now takes
	// its own reference through the descriptor's `retainFunc@64` (`Parser.moveInPayload`), which is inert on a
	// trivial instantiation and so leaves those instances storing exactly the word they always stored.
	//
	// `storage` is the `typeParameter` type itself — the same value the `scalar` arm carried, so every reader
	// asking about the SLOT (its width, a loaded value's type) is unchanged.
	typeParam(storage MaxonType)
end 'PayloadClass'

// ⭐⭐ **HOW A CONFORMER'S VALUE HALF IS OWNED AND RELEASED — ONE DECISION, TWO WITNESS WORDS
// (P1.7a-existentials slice 2b).** The witness table carries a `destroyFunc@8` and a `retainFunc@16`, and
// both are derived from THIS answer (`ProgramSignatures.existentialOwnershipProtocol`) rather than each
// re-deciding "is this conformer managed, and how". A second independent derivation is exactly how the two
// words would come to disagree about a conformer — a table whose drop frees a record its retain never took.
//
// ⚠ **THE THREE CASES ARE THREE DIFFERENT PROTOCOLS, NOT THREE DEGREES OF ONE.** A byte record COPIES on
// co-ownership and an aggregate CO-OWNS, and the split is not a preference: a `String` LITERAL is a wholly
// immortal `.rdata` record, so an incref of one is a write to read-only memory (see
// `IrInterface.WitnessRetainFuncOffset` for the measured access violation). It is the same split
// `Parser.promoteBorrowedToOwned` makes for a CONCRETE String, reached here through a witness because the
// conformer is a run-time fact.
export union ExistentialOwnershipProtocol
	// The value half is not a pointer at all — an `int`/`bool` conforming intrinsically
	// (`isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance`). BOTH words stay 0, and both runtime entries are inert on it.
	none
	// A `String`/`Character` fused byte record: released by `__str_decref`, co-owned by COPYING
	// (`__str_clone`), which is what an immortal literal source requires.
	byteRecord
	// A struct/union/generic-instance box: released by its own `__destruct_<T>`/`__mm_decref`, co-owned by
	// `__mm_retain` — an aggregate has no cheap owning copy and its alias is observable, so it must stay the
	// same box.
	aggregate
end 'ExistentialOwnershipProtocol'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE SPELLING OF "HOW DO I TAKE A REFERENCE UNDER THIS PROTOCOL" (W41-opaque).** Two runtime words
// are filled from it — a witness table's `retainFunc@16` (`existentialRetainCallee`) and a layout descriptor's
// `retainFunc@64` (`typeParamRetainCallee`) — and they are the same question asked of a conformer and of a
// type argument. Written twice, the pair's one genuinely surprising arm is what would drift: a `String`'s
// answer is a COPY and not an incref, because a literal's record is immortal `.rdata` and increfing one writes
// read-only memory (MEASURED as 0xC0000005 — see `IrInterface.WitnessRetainFuncOffset`).
//
// EMPTY is the complete answer for a protocol that owns nothing, never a sentinel: both readers stamp nothing
// for it, and the zero word left behind is the encoding both runtime entries treat as inert.
export function retainCalleeForProtocol(protocol ExistentialOwnershipProtocol) returns ByteArray
	match protocol 'protocol'
		byteRecord then return StrCloneName
		aggregate then return MmRetainName
		none then return EmptySourceText
	end 'protocol'
end 'retainCalleeForProtocol'

// ⭐⭐ **THE CONCRETE `MaxonType` A SLOT HOLDING A `declared`-TYPED VALUE IS ACCESSED AS** — what a
// `loadIndirect`/`storeIndirect` carries, and the only type in the compiler that says how wide the access is.
// `Parser.fieldStorageType` is the parser-side door onto it and carries the rest of the argument; it is
// homed HERE, in `classifyUnionPayload`'s shape and for its stated reason, because the askers hold their
// layout ids in DIFFERENT interners and a name is the one currency all of them have.
//
// THREE kinds of slot ask it, and they are one question: a struct/union FIELD, a capturing closure's ENV
// SLOT, and — since W116 — a struct-literal BOX built by `__module_init`, whose asker has no `Parser` at all.
//
// It resolves a `named` alias through `signatures.aliasOf`, the SAME whole-program door THE SHIFT RULE asks
// (`valueIsUnsigned`), for the same reason: an alias declared in another file is still an alias here, and a
// single file's own table would answer for neither it nor a forward reference.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS THE SECOND STATEMENT OF "WHAT A `named` TYPE DENOTES", AND `TypeResolution.resolveNamedType` IS
// THE FIRST.** That is the standing shape of the two registries (see `ProgramSignatures.structTypes`): the
// sweep answers the PARSER, the project registry answers the AUTHORITY, and the alias half has been written
// in both places since ranged typealiases landed. What forces a slot's type to be resolved this early is the
// op: `resolveTypes` never rewrites an op's type, so whoever EMITS is the last tier that can make it concrete.
export function slotStorageType(signatures ProgramSignatures, declared MaxonType, typeNames TypeNameInterner, readerFilePath FilePath) returns MaxonType
	match declared 'kind'
		// Already concrete. `structRef` included — a struct FIELD is a pointer, and
		// `maxonTypeToStdType` gives it the machine word without help.
		boolean to float then return declared
		exitCode then return declared
		structRef then return declared
		unresolved then panic("slotStorageType: a slot is declared `unresolved` — `parseTypeReference` produces only `named`, `structRef` and the three primitive keywords, and the one other producer of the sentinel is a VOID callee's result type, which `voidCallResultUsed` refuses to bind; so neither a field declaration nor a captured binding can carry one")
		// A String field stores an i64 POINTER, exactly like a struct-ref field — `maxonTypeToStdType`
		// gives it the machine word. A managed field IS now constructed (`moveInPayload`), and its
		// self-field READ loads through this same width.
		string then return declared
		// A FUNCTION-typed field (P1.5-A1) stores an i64 code POINTER, exactly like a struct/String
		// field — `maxonTypeToStdType`/`valueTagToStdType` give it the machine word. `emitFieldLoad`
		// functionizes a `named` function alias BEFORE this, so a function field arrives here concrete.
		function then return declared
		// A TYPE-PARAMETER field stores an opaque i64 and a GENERIC-INSTANCE field a struct POINTER
		// (P1.6-A) — both already concrete, so `maxonTypeToStdType` gives the machine word directly.
		// This is what a `b.value` read on `type Box uses T` loads through.
		typeParameter then return declared
		genericInstance then return declared
		// A CHARACTER field stores a POINTER to the fused byte record, exactly as a String field
		// does — already concrete, so `maxonTypeToStdType` gives the machine word directly.
		character then return declared
		// ⭐ **AN INTERFACE-TYPED FIELD ACCESSES ITS VALUE HALF AS ONE MACHINE WORD**
		// (P1.7a-existentials slice 2). The field occupies TWO slots — `(value@+0, witness@+8)` — but a
		// `loadIndirect`/`storeIndirect` moves ONE of them, and `emitFieldLoad`/`emitFieldStore` emit two
		// ops at the two offsets rather than one op of a doubled width. So the width this answers is the
		// word, exactly as a declared enum/union field's is: `integer`, because `maxonTypeToStdType` panics
		// on the declared `interfaceRef` the way it panics on a `named`.
		//
		// ⚠ A CAPTURED interface binding — this function's other caller, a closure env slot — is still
		// refused by the capture path's own E2015, so no env slot reaches this arm.
		interfaceRef then return MaxonType.integer
		named then break 'kind'
	end 'kind'

	let name = try typeNames.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(declared)) otherwise panic("slotStorageType: a slot's named type has an id that is not in the interner it was asked against — a field's type is adopted through `fieldTypeOf`, which re-interns into exactly that table, and a captured binding's was interned by its own file's `parseTypeReference`")
	match signatures.aliasOf(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'alias'
		declared(alias) then return alias.underlying
		undeclared then break 'alias'
	end 'alias'

	// A declared enum/union field's width, through the accessor the UNION-PAYLOAD slot reads too
	// (`declaredAggregateSlotWidth`) — so the two slot kinds cannot come to store the SAME union at two
	// different widths, a divergence no diagnostic and no exit code can report. `TypeResolution.
	// resolveNamedType` is the third deriver; that accessor's header names it.
	if signatures.containsEnum(name) 'enumOrUnion'
		return declaredAggregateSlotWidth()
	end 'enumOrUnion'

	// A name NO file declares, or a `type` the sweep recorded but whose reference the parser typed
	// `named` rather than `structRef` (a field declared with its own struct's name is `structRef` and
	// never reaches here). The first is a user error — E3011, which `TypeResolution` reports against
	// the merged registry, the authority — and it is not this routine's to report: the sweep is the
	// tolerant half. Returning `integer` keeps the op well-formed for the instant before the
	// pipeline's error gate throws, exactly as `resolveNamedType`'s own recovery does.
	return MaxonType.integer
end 'slotStorageType'

// Classify a union payload field's storage and ownership. `typeNames` resolves a `named` field's id to
// a NAME; pass the interner the LAYOUT's ids live in — the file-local artifact interner at the
// construct/extract sites, `project.typeNames` at the destructor-synthesis site. Every lookup
// below is keyed by that name, so they are interner-independent once the name is in hand.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A PAYLOAD IS A SLOT, AND WHAT ITS BARE `named` DENOTES IS ASKED THROUGH THE SLOT DOOR (A4k).**
// This used to answer that with a cascade of its own, and the two disagreed exactly where the copy was
// thinner: `declaredSlotType` re-tags a function alias and a generic alias, this did not — so a
// `boxed(b PB)` payload fell through to `undeclaredName`, the construct stored the pointer without
// consuming the temporary (the box was freed at the end of the construct statement while the union kept
// pointing at it), the cascade dropped nothing, and reading the binding was `E3011 Unknown type 'PB'` on
// a program the oracle compiles and runs. See `denotedSlotType`, which is now the only home of that
// resolution.
//
// `slotScopeFilePath` is the UNION's `declFilePath` — the file whose reading of a contested generic
// alias fixes this payload's identity, exactly as a struct field's is its own declaring file's. It is
// the same file `resolveRecordedGenericAliasTypes` settled the recorded payload column against (N3), so
// the column and this classifier cannot come to mean two different declarations of one name.
export function classifyUnionPayload(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, fieldType MaxonType, slotScopeFilePath FilePath) returns PayloadClass
	match fieldType 'kind'
		boolean then return PayloadClass.scalar(fieldType)
		integer then return PayloadClass.scalar(fieldType)
		exitCode then return PayloadClass.scalar(fieldType)
		float then return PayloadClass.floatPayload
		// A String / struct reference is a managed heap pointer whose move-in and drop are this rung's
		// work. A payload field of a struct's own name resolves to `structRef` (`Self` and the enclosing
		// type); a reference to ANOTHER struct arrives as `named` and is resolved below.
		string then return PayloadClass.managed(fieldType, isString: true)
		structRef then return PayloadClass.managed(fieldType, isString: false)
		unresolved then panic("classifyUnionPayload: a payload field is declared `unresolved` — `parseTypeReference` produces only `named`, `structRef` and the three primitive keywords, so no payload declaration can carry one")
		// A FUNCTION-typed payload (P1.5-A1) is a SCALAR: a code pointer is an i64 stored inline, owns no
		// heap, and the cascade skips it (nothing to drop). It stores through `maxonTypeToStdType(function)`
		// → i64, exactly as a scalar does.
		function then return PayloadClass.scalar(fieldType)
		// ⭐ A TYPE-PARAMETER slot is its own class (`PayloadClass.typeParam`) — one opaque machine word in the
		// DECLARATION view, dropped by nothing there (the per-INSTANCE cascade drops the substituted field
		// instead), which is what keeps `type Box uses T`'s own destructor the trivial `__mm_decref`. What it is
		// NOT is a scalar: a MANAGED instantiation makes the concrete record the slot's owner, so a store into
		// it takes a reference through the descriptor at run time. See `PayloadClass.typeParam`, whose header
		// carries the double free the `scalar` classification was.
		typeParameter then return PayloadClass.typeParam(fieldType)
		// A generic-INSTANCE payload is an OWNED base-struct box (P1.6-B2), exactly like a `structRef`
		// payload: construct MOVES it in, a match binds it out, the cascade drops it through its own
		// `__destruct_<instance>` / `__mm_decref` (`managedFieldDropCallee` routes the `genericInstance`
		// tag). Its storage is the `genericInstance` type itself, which `maxonTypeToStdType` gives an i64
		// (a pointer), so `payloadStorageOf`/`payloadValueTypeOf` lower it like any managed pointer.
		//
		// ⚠ **EXCEPT A `Promise` INSTANCE, WHICH IS A SCALAR** (P1.5-B2c) — a green-thread handle owning no
		// heap record. It MUST agree with `typeIsManaged`'s instance arm: these are the two SEPARATE
		// implementations of "does this own heap?" that `Parser.requireContainerElementType`'s header warns
		// about, and a slot they classify differently is moved in by one protocol and dropped by the other.
		genericInstance then return promiseOrManagedInstanceClass(signatures, typeNames: typeNames, fieldType: fieldType)
		// ⭐ A CHARACTER payload is an OWNED byte record — the same record a `string` payload is, so it is
		// managed and `isString: true`: `isString` selects the fused-record COPY/DROP protocol
		// (`promoteToOwnedString` in, `__str_decref` out), which is a question about the REPRESENTATION and
		// not about the type. A Character answering `false` here would take the aggregate path and try to
		// co-own a record that has no `__mm_retain` owner.
		character then return PayloadClass.managed(fieldType, isString: true)
		// ⭐ A STRUCT FIELD at an interface type is the two-slot fat pointer (P1.7a-existentials slice 2) — see
		// `PayloadClass.existential`. A UNION PAYLOAD at one is still refused at the declaration
		// (`Parser.requireStorableDeclaredType`), because a payload slot IS one machine word — the case list
		// shares one slot region across every case, so a two-slot payload has nowhere to widen into — and this
		// classifier serves both, so the refusal lives at the payload's own lowering gate
		// (`requirePayloadLowerable`) rather than here.
		interfaceRef then return PayloadClass.existential
		named then break 'kind'
	end 'kind'

	let name = try typeNames.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(fieldType)) otherwise panic("classifyUnionPayload: a payload field's named type id is not in the interner it was resolved against — pass the interner the layout's ids live in")

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SHARED CASCADE, and it answers every payload whose name denotes a DIFFERENT type** — a
	// STRUCT (a managed heap pointer, re-tagged `structRef` because `maxonTypeToStdType` panics on a `named`
	// and a struct binding's field access requires the tag), a TUPLE alias (the tuple's own `structRef` —
	// `Pair` is a registered nothing, and unresolved it was `E3011 Unknown type 'Pair'` on a program the
	// other walk order ran), a GENERIC alias (the instance), a FUNCTION alias (a code pointer), and a ranged
	// FLOAT alias (a genuine f64). Re-entered rather than re-classified by hand: `denotedSlotType` never
	// answers with a `named`, so the recursion is exactly one deep and the tag→class mapping above stays the
	// only one there is.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND THAT TERMINATION FACT IS ASSERTED, NOT MERELY WRITTEN DOWN (A4k review).** It holds today by
	// construction — every arm of that door answers `function`, `genericInstance`, `structRef` or `float`,
	// and each is a RETURNING arm of the `match` above — but it is a property of the OTHER function's arms,
	// and that door serves `declaredSlotType` too. An arm added for that caller which answered with the bare
	// `named` it was handed (the int-alias shape its own header describes) would make this an UNBOUNDED
	// recursion: a compiler that hangs, with nothing naming the edit that did it. A `named` here is loud
	// instead — the same rule `resolveRecordedGenericAliasTypes` states for a spelling its pass cannot reach.
	if let denoted = try signatures.denotedSlotType(name, into: typeNames, slotScopeFilePath: slotScopeFilePath) 'theNameDenotesAnotherType'
		if maxonTypeTag(denoted) == ValueTypeTag.named 'wouldNotTerminate'
			panic("classifyUnionPayload: `denotedSlotType('{String.from(name)}')` answered with a `named`, which this re-entry cannot make progress on — every arm of that door must answer with a type the tag match above RETURNS on")
		end 'wouldNotTerminate'

		return classifyUnionPayload(signatures, typeNames: typeNames, fieldType: denoted, slotScopeFilePath: slotScopeFilePath)
	end 'theNameDenotesAnotherType'

	// A ranged INT alias is a scalar of its underlying width. It is asked HERE and not in the shared door
	// because it is a different question: that door answers "what type does this name denote", and an int
	// alias's answer is the bare `named` it already is (its NAME carries the range) — what a PAYLOAD needs
	// on top is the STORAGE primitive the slot occupies, which only this side wants. A float-underlying
	// alias cannot reach this line; the door re-tagged it `float` and the `floatPayload` arm above took it.
	if let underlying = try signatures.aliasUnderlyingInAnyFile(name) 'rangedIntAlias'
		return PayloadClass.scalar(underlying)
	end 'rangedIntAlias'

	// A payload-FREE enum's value is an i64 tag — a scalar; a BOXED union is a managed heap pointer
	// (its box carries the field's `named(<union>)` id, which `enumPayloadClass` reads off `fieldType`).
	// Also asked here rather than in the shared door, and for the same reason: an enum name denotes no
	// other type — a `named` IS what both forms are stored as — so there is nothing for that door to
	// re-tag, and only the CLASS differs between them. An undeclared name is a user error TypeResolution
	// reports as E3011; the caller keeps the op well-formed until the pipeline's error gate throws.
	match signatures.enumOf(name) 'asEnum'
		declared(enumLayout) then return enumPayloadClass(enumLayout, fieldType: fieldType)
		undeclared then return PayloadClass.undeclaredName
	end 'asEnum'
end 'classifyUnionPayload'

// The class of a generic-INSTANCE slot: an owned base-struct box, or the one instance that is a bare
// green-thread handle (P1.5-B2c). Split out because the arm above sits in a `match` and the question needs
// a labelled `if`; it reads `typeIsManaged` rather than re-asking `instanceIsPromiseHandle`, so the two
// classifiers this file exports cannot come to disagree about which instances own heap.
function promiseOrManagedInstanceClass(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, fieldType MaxonType) returns PayloadClass
	if signatures.typeIsManaged(fieldType, from: typeNames) 'aBox'
		return PayloadClass.managed(fieldType, isString: false)
	end 'aBox'

	return PayloadClass.scalar(fieldType)
end 'promiseOrManagedInstanceClass'

// A payload whose type is another enum/union: a payload-FREE enum is a scalar (its value is its i64
// tag, stored inline); a BOXED union is a MANAGED heap pointer carrying the field's own `named(<union>)`
// type (`fieldType`) — the value tag its move-in/move-out/drop all read.
function enumPayloadClass(enumLayout EnumLayout, fieldType MaxonType) returns PayloadClass
	if enumLayout.isBoxed() 'boxed'
		return PayloadClass.nestedUnion(fieldType)
	end 'boxed'
	return PayloadClass.scalar(MaxonType.integer)
end 'enumPayloadClass'

// ⭐ **THE ONE LOOP THREE QUESTIONS SHARE.** Does any case carry a payload that needs a DROP? Keeping it ONE
// function, not one copy per site, is what makes the destructor's dropped set, the construct's moved set and
// the union destructor's declared runtime needs agree on what "managed" means by construction.
//
// ⚠ **IT USED TO TAKE A `wantStringOnly` FLAG, AND THAT FLAG WAS A CASCADE-NEEDS DEFECT WEARING A
// PARAMETER'S CLOTHES.** Its one `true` caller asked "does this union's destructor call `__str_decref`?" —
// a question about `String` and about nothing else, so a payload of any OTHER managed type with its own
// install bit declared no bit at all and linked against a runtime nothing installed (measured on the
// then-compiler-owned `CharacterSet`: `resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function '__cs_decref'`, W129
// having since deleted that runtime — the DEFECT class is the point, not the type). That caller now derives its
// bits from the drop CALLEE instead (`MmRuntime.declareUnionPayloadDropNeeds`), which cannot have a
// favourite type, and the flag went with it.
export function unionHasManagedField(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, layout EnumLayout) returns bool
	for ci in 0 upto layout.caseCount() 'eachCase'
		if caseHasManagedField(signatures, typeNames: typeNames, layout: layout, ci: ci) 'found'
			return true
		end 'found'
	end 'eachCase'
	return false
end 'unionHasManagedField'

// The per-CASE half — the destructor's tag switch emits a drop block only for a case this answers true
// for, so the switch and the union-wide gate above read the exact same classification.
export function caseHasManagedField(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, layout EnumLayout, ci CaseIndex) returns bool
	let payloads = layout.payloadsAt(ci)
	for fi in 0 upto payloads.count() 'eachField'
		let field = try payloads.get(fi) otherwise panic("caseHasManagedField: payloadsAt count disagrees with get at {fi}")
		let class = classifyUnionPayload(signatures, typeNames: typeNames, fieldType: field.declaredType, slotScopeFilePath: layout.declFilePath)
		if payloadClassIsManaged(class) 'found'
			return true
		end 'found'
	end 'eachField'
	return false
end 'caseHasManagedField'

// Is a classified payload a managed (String/struct) one — the single reader of "does this class own a
// heap drop?", so every site testing it (the predicate above, the destructor synthesis, the usage scan)
// agrees.
export function payloadClassIsManaged(class PayloadClass) returns bool
	return match class 'c'
		managed gives true
		nestedUnion gives true
		// An existential owns its conformer's record — see `PayloadClass.existential` and `typeIsManaged`.
		existential gives true
		scalar gives false
		floatPayload gives false
		undeclaredName gives false
		// ⭐ **FALSE, AND IT IS THE DECLARATION VIEW'S ANSWER RATHER THAN THE SLOT'S (W41-opaque).** Every
		// reader here is a DECLARATION-view drop question — the base struct's own cascade, the usage gates,
		// `caseHasManagedField` — and a `T` slot is dropped by none of them: `genericInstanceFieldIsManaged`
		// answers the substituted question for the per-instance cascade that does drop it. Answering `true`
		// would give `type Box uses T` a `__destruct_Box` that decrefs an opaque word, which for a trivial
		// instantiation is a raw scalar. The STORE-side reference the slot does owe is taken by
		// `Parser.moveInPayload`, which routes on this case by NAME and not through this predicate.
		typeParam gives false
	end 'c'
end 'payloadClassIsManaged'

// ⭐ **MAY A MATCH-ARM BINDING ON THIS SLOT BE WRITTEN BACK INTO THE BOX (`specs/mutable-enums.md`)?**
// The write-back (`Parser.writeBackPayload`) must state, statically, who owns the value the slot held
// BEFORE it and who owns the one going in — and only these three classes state it:
//
//   • `scalar` — the slot owns nothing, so a store is the whole of the write.
//   • `managed` — writable only where the payload was MOVED OUT, which nulls the slot: there is then no
//     previous owner to release, and the box simply takes its own reference to the new value.
//   • `nestedUnion` — a boxed union is an owned heap pointer with a statically named release
//     (`unionBoxDropCallee`), so the two sentences above are true of it WORD FOR WORD: the move-out nulls
//     the slot, and the `__mm_incref` the write-back emits is the box's own reference. It answered `false`
//     only while a nested boxed-union payload could not be constructed at all (W55).
//
// ⚠ **THIS PREDICATE IS NOT THE WHOLE GATE, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT OF THIS PARAGRAPH.** It answers
// about the CLASS. Whether a managed payload was actually moved out is a fact about the ACQUISITION, and
// `Parser.declarePayloadBindings` decides it separately (`scrutOwned`) — from PROVENANCE, where this
// door's caller decides writability from MEMBERSHIP, and the two disagree on a loop-carried phi. An
// earlier version of this comment claimed they could not ("a BORROWED scrutinee has no mutable binding to
// write through"), which conflated "borrowed" with "the provenance bit says false"; the phi is the second
// thing, and it MEASURED as an exit-101 leak. The retain arm there demotes such a binding to read-only.
//
// Every other class keeps today's read-only binding rather than a guessed protocol. `typeParam` is the
// one that could be wrongly waved through: its slot's reference protocol is a RUN-TIME fact reached
// through the instance descriptor (`Parser.moveOpaqueValueInto`), so a plain store would drop the
// reference the box still holds — and it is unreachable today besides, a generic union having no
// construct syntax. `floatPayload` never reaches a binding at all
// (`Parser.requirePayloadLowerable` refuses it), and `undeclaredName` is an error path.
//
// A `match` like its siblings, so a case appended to `PayloadClass` fails to compile here rather than
// being silently absorbed into either answer.
export function payloadBindingAcceptsWrites(class PayloadClass) returns bool
	return match class 'c'
		scalar gives true
		managed gives true
		nestedUnion gives true
		typeParam gives false
		existential gives false
		floatPayload gives false
		undeclaredName gives false
	end 'c'
end 'payloadBindingAcceptsWrites'

// Is a classified slot an EXISTENTIAL (P1.7a-existentials slice 2) — the two-slot fat pointer, whose
// admitted values and whose DROP both differ from every other managed kind? Its readers are the field-type
// door (`Parser.requireFieldType`) and nothing else today; it is a `match` like its two siblings rather than
// an `is` test so that a case appended to `PayloadClass` fails to compile here.
export function payloadClassIsExistential(class PayloadClass) returns bool
	return match class 'c'
		existential gives true
		managed gives false
		nestedUnion gives false
		scalar gives false
		floatPayload gives false
		undeclaredName gives false
		typeParam gives false
	end 'c'
end 'payloadClassIsExistential'

// Is a classified slot the enclosing generic's own TYPE PARAMETER (W41-opaque) — one opaque machine word whose
// reference protocol is a run-time fact? Its reader is the store door (`Parser.moveInPayload`) and nothing
// else: every OTHER question about the slot (its width, a loaded value's type, whether the DECLARATION view
// drops it) is answered identically to a scalar's, which is why those readers stay unaware of the case. A
// `match` like its siblings so a case appended to `PayloadClass` fails to compile here.
export function payloadClassIsTypeParam(class PayloadClass) returns bool
	return match class 'c'
		typeParam gives true
		existential gives false
		managed gives false
		nestedUnion gives false
		scalar gives false
		floatPayload gives false
		undeclaredName gives false
	end 'c'
end 'payloadClassIsTypeParam'

// Is a classified payload a STRING (drops through `__str_decref`) rather than a struct (`__mm_decref`)?
// The single reader of "which managed drop?", so the destructor synthesis and the usesStrDecref gate route
// the same way.
export function payloadClassIsString(class PayloadClass) returns bool
	return match class 'c'
		managed(_, isString) gives isString
		scalar gives false
		floatPayload gives false
		nestedUnion gives false
		// An existential's value half is a conformer's record, which may or may not BE a String — that is
		// exactly the fact only its witness knows, and the reason its drop is `__drop_existential` and its
		// co-ownership `__retain_existential`. `false` here does NOT mean "the aggregate protocol applies":
		// for a byte-record conformer the protocol is COPY, never co-own (`ExistentialOwnershipProtocol`), and
		// this reader's earlier claim that co-owning "is right whatever the conformer is" was measurably false
		// — it is an incref of read-only `.rdata` for a literal. `false` means only "do not take the STATIC
		// String route", which is right because the choice belongs to the witness at run time.
		existential gives false
		undeclaredName gives false
		// An opaque `T` may BE a String at some instantiation — which is exactly the fact the shared body
		// cannot know, and the reason its store dispatches through the descriptor's `retainFunc@64`. `false`
		// means "do not take the STATIC String route" here, precisely as it does for an existential.
		typeParam gives false
	end 'c'
end 'payloadClassIsString'

// ⭐⭐ **THE SLOT'S OWN WIDTH — the type a `loadIndirect`/`storeIndirect` through this slot CARRIES**, and
// nothing else. **THE ONE HOME of the width-vs-identity split** (W55): `payloadValueTypeOf` answers the other
// half and points here rather than restating it, and so do `PayloadClass.nestedUnion` and
// `Parser.requirePayloadLowerable`. It was written out in full at three sites and had already drifted on which
// tags a `managed` payload's storage can be.
//
// ⛔ **THE WIDTH IS NOT THE IDENTITY, AND FOR MOST CLASSES IT IS NOT EVEN THE SAME TYPE.** Only `managed`
// (whose `storage` is already a concrete machine-word tag — `structRef`/`string`/`genericInstance`/
// `character`) and `typeParam` (one opaque word, whose declared type IS that word) answer identically at both
// doors. Every OTHER class answers a width here that is not what a value filling the slot must BE:
//
//   • `scalar` — a ranged alias classifies as `scalar(<underlying primitive>)`, so the width is the bare
//     `integer` while the IDENTITY keeps the alias NAME, which is what lets the shift rule read its low bound.
//     Ask this door the identity question and a `Byte` silently becomes an `int`.
//   • `existential` — the slot is a TWO-word fat pointer and a `storeIndirect` moves ONE word, so the width is
//     that word; the identity is the declared `interfaceRef` every witness-dispatching reader needs. The
//     witness half rides at `+InterfaceWitnessHalfOffset`, stored beside it by `Parser.emitFieldStore`, the
//     only site holding the field's base offset.
//   • `nestedUnion` — a boxed union IS a pointer, so the width is the word; the identity is the union's own
//     `named(<union>)`. ⚠ **The one class where handing the identity to an op does not merely lose information
//     but PANICS** (`maxonTypeToStdType` has no `named` arm) — and deriving the width from the union's name is
//     exactly what made a nested boxed-union payload unlowerable until W55.
//   • `undeclaredName` — an error path: the width keeps the op well-formed for the instant before the
//     pipeline's error gate throws, while the identity stays the name the user wrote, for the diagnostic.
//
// ⇒ the three `MaxonType.integer` arms below are deliberately NOT collapsed under the redundant-arm rule:
// they are three DIFFERENT reasons that happen to share an answer, and a range arm over them would be the
// band-append trap one union down besides. A `float` payload is rejected before any store reaches here.
//
// ⚠ **"A DECLARED ENUM/UNION OCCUPIES ONE MACHINE WORD" HAS TWO DERIVERS LEFT, AND THEY NOW SHARE A HOME** —
// `declaredAggregateSlotWidth` below. It had THREE, and the note here declined to unify them on the grounds
// that *"a single home would have to sit where all three can reach it"* — which was a false dichotomy the
// review caught: **two of the three are in these two files** (`Parser.fieldStorageType`'s `containsEnum` arm,
// the STRUCT-FIELD slot, and the `nestedUnion` arm below, the UNION-PAYLOAD slot), so unifying two of three is
// a real reduction and the third being out of reach never argued against it.
//
// The remaining party is `TypeResolution.resolveNamedType`, which erases a declared enum/union to `integer` in
// a file outside this rung. **What one accessor buys is that the divergence mode this note originally named —
// a struct field and a payload slot storing the SAME union at two different widths, reportable by no
// diagnostic and no exit code — is now unrepresentable**, because there is one expression to change. Only the
// remaining two-party agreement rests on prose.
// ⭐⭐ **THE WIDTH A SLOT HOLDING A DECLARED ENUM OR UNION IS ACCESSED AT — one machine word, stated ONCE.**
// A payload-FREE enum's value IS its i64 tag; a payload-bearing union's value is an i64 POINTER to its box
// (P1.4b wave 2c). Two different representations, one access width, and that coincidence is exactly why the
// two slot kinds could drift apart while each looked right on its own.
//
// Its two callers are the two slot kinds: `Parser.fieldStorageType`'s `containsEnum` arm (a struct FIELD) and
// `payloadStorageOf`'s `nestedUnion` arm (a union PAYLOAD). See the ⚠ above for the third deriver that is out
// of reach and for what having one home for these two buys.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A WIDTH AND NEVER AN IDENTITY.** `payloadValueTypeOf` answers the other half; handing this answer
// to a reader that needed the identity loses the union's NAME, and handing the identity to an op PANICS
// (`maxonTypeToStdType` has no `named` arm).
export function declaredAggregateSlotWidth() returns MaxonType
	return MaxonType.integer
end 'declaredAggregateSlotWidth'

export function payloadStorageOf(class PayloadClass) returns MaxonType
	match class 'c'
		scalar(storage) then return storage
		managed(storage, _) then return storage
		// One machine word — the box POINTER. Not the `named(<union>)` the case carries: that is the slot's
		// IDENTITY, and it panics the lowering. See the ⛔ above. Through the shared accessor, so this width and
		// the STRUCT-FIELD one cannot come apart.
		nestedUnion then return declaredAggregateSlotWidth()
		// The VALUE half's word, one of the fat pointer's two. See the ⛔ above.
		existential then return MaxonType.integer
		// An error path's placeholder width. See the ⛔ above.
		undeclaredName then return MaxonType.integer
		// The opaque word itself — the same value the `scalar` arm carried before this case split off, so the
		// store's width is unchanged for every instantiation.
		typeParam(storage) then return storage
		floatPayload then panic("payloadStorageOf: a float payload has no lowerable storage — requirePayloadLowerable rejects it before any store")
	end 'c'
end 'payloadStorageOf'

// ⭐ **THE MaxonType THE SLOT'S VALUE IS TAGGED WITH — read in BOTH directions**: what a LOADED payload
// reads back as, and what a value STORED into the slot must already be (`Parser.requireFieldType`,
// `requireContainerElementType`; `requirePayloadArgType` asks the same question but is itself a `match` over
// this union, so it reads the case's own field rather than calling in). It is one question — the slot's
// value IDENTITY — and asking it once is what keeps a slot from admitting a type it cannot hand back.
//
// A managed payload's concrete storage (so a struct binding reads as `structRef` — required by
// `requireStructBase` — and a String as `string`); a boxed-union payload's `named(<union>)` (so a moved-out
// load is recognized as a boxed union by `enumMatchFor`/`decrefCalleeFor`, and so a store into one is
// refused a DIFFERENT union — `requireSlotAggregateIdentity` compares that name); a scalar's DECLARED type
// (so a ranged alias's low bound stays readable by the shift rule, exactly as `emitFieldLoad` keeps a
// scalar field's alias).
//
// ⛔ **THIS IS NOT THE SLOT'S WIDTH, AND FOR EVERY CLASS BUT `managed`/`typeParam` IT IS NOT EVEN THE SAME
// TYPE** — `payloadStorageOf` is the one home of that split and states it arm by arm. Handing a `scalar`'s
// answer to an op loses a ranged alias's bound; handing a `nestedUnion`'s PANICS the lowering.
export function payloadValueTypeOf(class PayloadClass, declared MaxonType) returns MaxonType
	match class 'c'
		scalar then return declared
		managed(storage, _) then return storage
		nestedUnion(storage) then return storage
		// The DECLARED `interfaceRef`, so a loaded existential is recognised as one by every reader that
		// dispatches on the tag (`witnessOfValue`'s pairing, the argument widening, the drop router).
		existential then return declared
		undeclaredName then return declared
		// The DECLARED type, exactly as the `scalar` arm answers: a loaded `T` must read back as the opaque
		// `typeParameter` its slot is declared at, which is what the caller's `retypeOpaqueMethodResult`
		// substitutes and what `decrefCalleeFor` routes to `__drop_type_param` on.
		typeParam then return declared
		floatPayload then panic("payloadValueTypeOf: a float payload has no lowerable value type — requirePayloadLowerable rejects it before any load")
	end 'c'
end 'payloadValueTypeOf'

// ⭐ **A SOURCE-LEVEL TYPE'S NAME for a function-type display (P1.5 #81).** A declared alias/struct/union by
// its interned NAME (so a `Integer` parameter prints `Integer`, matching the reference oracle — NOT `int`,
// which `typeTagName(named)` would give), and a bare primitive by its keyword. Reads the type's id out of
// `typeNames` — pass the interner the type's ids live in (`project.typeNames` at the whole-program merge
// site). Fed PRE-RESOLUTION types (a ranged alias is still `named(Integer)`), which is exactly why the check
// that uses it drains before `resolveTypes` erases those names to `integer`.
// ⚠ A `function`-tagged type is rendered by its ALIAS NAME for the same reason a `named` one is, and it is
// not cosmetic: `typeTagName(function)` is the bare word `function`, so a function type nested inside another
// printed as `fn(function) returns int` on BOTH sides of a mismatch — a diagnostic saying a type does not
// match itself. Its id names the alias exactly as a `named` type's does.
export function maxonTypeName(t MaxonType, typeNames TypeNameInterner) returns String
	let tag = maxonTypeTag(t)
	// ⭐ **AN EXISTENTIAL RENDERS AS ITS INTERFACE'S NAME, AND THAT MAKES THE RENDERING ORDER-INDEPENDENT.**
	// `parseTypeReference` mints `interfaceRef` only once the interface registry is whole (`allFilesFolded`),
	// so the SAME annotation is a `named` during the declaration sweep and an `interfaceRef` in the real
	// parse. Both spell the same interned name here, which is what keeps a rendered interface REQUIREMENT
	// (`Parser.renderInterfaceTypeName` → `renderDeclaredTypeName` → here) identical whichever pass produced
	// it — and conformance is decided by comparing two of those strings.
	if tag == ValueTypeTag.named or tag == ValueTypeTag.structRef or tag == ValueTypeTag.function or tag == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'namedType'
		return String.from(try typeNames.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(t)) otherwise panic("maxonTypeName: type-name id {typeNameIdOf(t)} is not in the interner — pass the interner the type's ids live in"))
	end 'namedType'
	return typeTagName(tag)
end 'maxonTypeName'

// Render a function type as `fn(P1, P2, …) returns R` from its parameter and return types (P1.5 #81). A void
// return carries no `returns` clause. The canonical signature STRING the merge check compares by name — the
// reference oracle's `MergeTernaryArmFnTypes` model, where two functions agree iff this string is equal.
export function formatFnTypeDisplay(paramTypes MaxonTypeArray, returnType MaxonReturnType, typeNames TypeNameInterner) returns String
	var rendered = "fn("
	for i in 0 upto paramTypes.count() 'eachParam'
		if i > 0 'separator'
			rendered = "{rendered}, "
		end 'separator'
		rendered = "{rendered}{maxonTypeName(try paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("formatFnTypeDisplay: paramTypes.get OOB at {i}"), typeNames: typeNames)}"
	end 'eachParam'
	rendered = "{rendered})"

	match returnType 'return'
		value(returnValue) then rendered = "{rendered} returns {maxonTypeName(returnValue, typeNames: typeNames)}"
		void then break 'return'
	end 'return'
	return rendered
end 'formatFnTypeDisplay'

export typealias PayloadClassArray = Array with PayloadClass

export typealias TypeParamPositionArray = Array with TypeParamPosition

// ⭐⭐ **THE TOKEN A TYPE PARAMETER IS IDENTIFIED BY — a digest of `(declaring type name, parameter name)`
// (W14). THE ONE SPELLER; nothing else may compute one.**
//
// ⛔ **IT REPLACED A BARE POSITION, AND A POSITION IS NOT AN IDENTITY.** `typeParameter(0)` named EVERY
// generic type's first parameter, so `GenericInstanceRegistry.instanceKey` — which mixes each argument's
// `(tag, id)` pair — gave `Array with U` inside `type HoldA uses U` and `Array with V` inside
// `type HoldB uses V` ONE key. Two different types, one interned instance, and therefore one entry in the
// display index that `deriveInstanceDisplayNames` settles by FIRST DECLARATION. MEASURED on a program and
// its declaration-order twin: a `HoldB` field was cited as `HoldA.Items` in one order — a build-order
// artifact in a user-visible diagnostic, which the 2026-07-24 user ruling recorded at
// `StdlibLoader.maxon:39-63` bars. `specs-shv2/generic-types.md`'s
// `error.a-type-parameter-belongs-to-the-type-THAT-DECLARES-IT{,-swapped}` pair pins it: the same program,
// the declarations exchanged, ONE byte-identical sentence.
//
// ⭐ **WHY A DIGEST AND NOT v1's REGISTRY.** v1 interns the genuine name pair
// (`TypeParamRegistry.intern`, `maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Project.maxon:1876-1889`) and compares the
// resulting integer, which is a sound identity — but shv2 cannot copy it, for two reasons the code states
// elsewhere and this comment collects:
//
//   • **THE PAYLOAD MUST STAY ONE `int`** (`MaxonDialect.TypeParamId`): it rides the dense `valueNameIds`
//     column every SSA value carries, and widening that column measured **+4,165 allocations on the
//     smallest scale rung**. A digest is one int; a `(name, name)` pair is not.
//   • **NO REGISTRY EXISTS AT MINT TIME** (`Parser.parseTypeReference`): a bare `T` is recognized by NAME
//     against the enclosing type's `uses` list precisely so the tolerant declaration sweep and the real
//     parse answer it identically WITHOUT one, exactly as `Self` is. An intern-ORDER id would differ
//     between the two passes.
//
// A digest of source text has neither problem: it is one int, and it is the same int in both passes and in
// every build. It is `tupleElementTokenFor`'s mechanism on the sibling problem, one rung later — read that
// function for the collision argument, which is this one's too.
//
// ⭐ **THE JOIN IS `mixBytes`' TRAILING LENGTH TAG, NOT A SEPARATOR CHARACTER**, and that is stronger than
// a `#`: it is what keeps `("ab","c")` apart from `("a","bc")` (`ContentHash.mixBytes`, and
// `mixInterfaceFormals` already spends it on exactly this shape). It also allocates NOTHING — no joined
// String is built — which matters because this runs at every bare `T` in every generic body, in both the
// sweep and the real parse.
//
// ⚠ **64 BITS IS LOAD-BEARING AND IS WRITTEN DOWN HERE.** At N = 10^4 distinct type parameters,
// P(collision) ≈ N²/2^65 ≈ 5×10⁻¹²; a 40-bit digest would be ≈ 5×10⁻⁵ — a number a real program can hit.
// Do not narrow it, and note that a collision is DETECTED rather than assumed:
// `ProgramSignatures.recordTypeParamToken` stops the compile on one.
export function typeParamTokenFor(declaringTypeName ByteArray, paramName ByteArray) returns TypeParamId
	return mixBytes(mixBytes(fnvOffsetBasis(), bytes: declaringTypeName), bytes: paramName)
end 'typeParamTokenFor'

// ⭐ **WHICH POSITION IN `declarationName`'s OWN `uses` LIST THE TOKEN `token` NAMES — the door for a caller
// that HOLDS THE DECLARATION (W14).**
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A PURE FUNCTION AND NEEDS NO REGISTRY, WHICH IS NOT A CONVENIENCE — IT IS THE ONLY WAY TWO OF
// ITS CALLERS CAN ASK AT ALL.** `Parser.renderInterfaceTypeName` runs INSIDE `readInterfaceDeclaration`,
// before that interface is recorded, and the parser's own scope questions run mid-body in a per-file
// fan-out that may not write a shared index. Both hold exactly `(declarationName, paramNames)`, which is
// precisely what `typeParamTokenFor` takes — so the question is answered by re-spelling the token, never by
// a lookup. `ProgramSignatures.typeParamOwnerOf` is the OTHER direction (a caller holding only a token) and
// they are not two spellings of one rule: they differ in what is available, not in what is true.
//
// A linear scan, for `Parser.typeParamPositionOf`'s reason: a declaration names a handful of parameters.
// Absence is a THROW rather than a `count()` sentinel — `IrInterface.namePositionIn`'s rule, and
// its reason: a sentinel index is exactly the shape that let a POSITION stand in for an IDENTITY here.
export function typeParamPositionIn(token TypeParamId, declarationName ByteArray, paramNames ByteArrayArray) returns TypeParamPosition throws MapError
	for i in 0 upto paramNames.count() 'eachParam'
		let paramName = try paramNames.get(i) otherwise panic("typeParamPositionIn: paramNames.get OOB at {i} below count {paramNames.count()}")
		if typeParamTokenFor(declarationName, paramName: paramName) == token 'thisOne'
			return i
		end 'thisOne'
	end 'eachParam'

	throw MapError.keyNotFound
end 'typeParamPositionIn'

// ⭐ **THE EXISTENCE HALF OF THE DOOR ABOVE — "does `token` name ANY parameter of `declarationName`?"**
//
// ⚠ **IT IS ITS OWN DOOR AND NOT A DISCARDED `typeParamPositionIn`, AND THAT IS A LANGUAGE RULE, NOT A
// PREFERENCE.** `typeParamPositionIn` is PURE, and a pure function's result may not be dropped in ANY
// spelling: `_ = f()` and `if let _ = try f()` are both E3064, and the `if try f()` this caller used to be
// written as is E3124. The rule's whole point is that a pure call whose answer nobody wants is the WRONG
// call — so the caller that wants only WHETHER gets a function that answers only that.
//
// The two do not duplicate the RULE: what makes a token name a parameter is `typeParamTokenFor`, which both
// ask and neither states. What differs is only the answer's shape, which is the same reason both exist.
export function namesTypeParamOf(token TypeParamId, declarationName ByteArray, paramNames ByteArrayArray) returns bool
	for i in 0 upto paramNames.count() 'eachParam'
		let paramName = try paramNames.get(i) otherwise panic("namesTypeParamOf: paramNames.get OOB at {i} below count {paramNames.count()}")
		if typeParamTokenFor(declarationName, paramName: paramName) == token 'thisOne'
			return true
		end 'thisOne'
	end 'eachParam'

	return false
end 'namesTypeParamOf'

// ⭐ **WHAT MINTED A TYPE-PARAMETER TOKEN — the reverse of `typeParamTokenFor`, and the record that makes a
// digest collision a DETECTED event rather than a silent shared identity (W14).** `position` rides with the
// two names because it is recorded in the same act (the declaration's own `uses` order), so a reader that
// holds only a token gets all three from one lookup instead of re-deriving the third.
export type TypeParamOwner
	export let declaringTypeName as ByteArray
	export let paramName as ByteArray
	export let position as TypeParamPosition

	export static function create(declaringTypeName ByteArray, paramName ByteArray, position TypeParamPosition) returns TypeParamOwner
		return Self{declaringTypeName: declaringTypeName, paramName: paramName, position: position}
	end 'create'

	// Do two records name the SAME parameter of the SAME declaration? The test `recordTypeParamToken` tells a
	// genuine 64-bit collision (two DIFFERENT parameters, one token) from a re-record of one parameter — which
	// a redeclared type name produces and which is E3006's business, not a reason to stop the compile.
	export function namesTheSameParameterAs(other TypeParamOwner) returns bool
		return self.declaringTypeName.equals(other.declaringTypeName) and self.paramName.equals(other.paramName)
	end 'namesTheSameParameterAs'
end 'TypeParamOwner'

// Token ⇒ the `(declaring type, parameter, position)` that minted it. See `TypeParamOwnerMap`.
export typealias TypeParamOwnerMap = Map with (TypeParamId, TypeParamOwner)

// ⭐⭐ **WHERE A FED TYPE-PARAMETER ARGUMENT ENDS UP — AND IT IS A PER-PARAMETER FACT, NOT A PER-FUNCTION ONE
// (W10).** The two sinks want OPPOSITE things of the callee, so which one a parameter feeds decides whether the
// shared body enrols it owned, whether the method reserves a layout descriptor to drop it through, and what a
// BORROWED argument at that position costs the caller.
//
// ⚠ **IT USED TO BE READ OFF `ParamConsumeInfo.isInstanceMethod()`, AND THAT WAS A LOSSY KEY.** It happened to
// be exact while the recognizer saw only `self`-headed stores: `Self{f: p}` feeds were marked for receiverless
// functions only (`scanBodyFacts`'s `detectFieldStores`) and array/field-reassign feeds only under a literal
// `self` token, so "is there a receiver" and "which sink" agreed by accident. A STATIC that pushes its
// type-parameter argument into a LOCAL `Array with T` and hands that array to the `Self` it returns feeds
// callee storage with no receiver at all, and there the accident stops holding — so the fact is recorded
// rather than inferred.
export enum FeedSink
	// `Self{item: p}` in a receiverless constructor: the parameter is moved into a field of the value the call
	// RETURNS, so the CALLER's value co-owns it and this frame drops nothing. Enrolling it owned in the callee
	// would drop it a second time.
	constructedValue
	// An opaque `Array` element (`items.push(p)`, wherever the array lives) or a reassigned opaque `T` field
	// (`self.saved = p`): the callee's own storage takes ownership and releases the value exactly once, so the
	// shared body must enrol the parameter owned — move-tracked into the sink, and dropped once through the
	// descriptor-gated `__drop_type_param` on any path that does not move it.
	calleeStorage
end 'FeedSink'

// The content-hash discriminator of one feed sink — the enum's own spelling for `mixTypeParamFeed`, kept beside
// the enum so a third sink cannot be added without a discriminator of its own.
function feedSinkShape(sink FeedSink) returns ContentHash
	return match sink 'sink'
		constructedValue gives FeedSinkConstructedValueShape
		calleeStorage gives FeedSinkCalleeStorageShape
	end 'sink'
end 'feedSinkShape'

// ⭐ **A CONSTRUCTOR PARAMETER'S TYPE-PARAMETER FEED (P1.6-B2)** — for a parameter MOVED into a
// type-PARAMETER field (`Box.create(v T)` → `Self{value: v}`, `v` feeds `T` at position 0), the type
// parameter POSITION it fills; `none` for every other parameter (a scalar, a direct-field store, an
// unstored one). The shared body cannot decide whether `T` is managed (it is opaque there), so this
// carries the mapping OUT to the CONCRETE call site, which knows the instance's argument at that position
// and consumes the argument iff it OWNS managed heap (`genericInstanceArgIsOwned`). Trivial arguments
// (`PointBox`/`IntBox`) feed too, but the call site borrows them — the feed is inert unless the argument
// owns heap, so `generic-trivial-struct-arg` is byte-identical.
//
// ⚠ **A POSITION AND NOT A `TypeParamId` (W14).** It is spent as an INDEX into the concrete instance's
// argument list, and since W14 those are two different numbers — see `MaxonDialect.TypeParamPosition`.
export union TypeParamFeed
	feeds(position TypeParamPosition, sink FeedSink)
	none

	// Does this carry a feed position — the `feeds(…)` case rather than `none`? The boolean the transitive
	// array-feed fixpoint (P1.7 slice 3b-iv) seeds its reverse-reachability BFS from, without re-spelling the
	// `match` at every node.
	export function isFeed() returns bool
		return match self 'f'
			feeds gives true
			none gives false
		end 'f'
	end 'isFeed'

	// Does the fed value land in storage the CALLEE owns and releases (W10)? The one question the callee-side
	// enrolment (`bindParameters`), the descriptor-need seed (`Queries.foldDescriptorNeeds`) and the
	// borrowed-argument promotion (`handleEscapingBorrowFeed`) all ask, so the sink is read through ONE door
	// and the three cannot come to disagree about who frees the value.
	export function sinkIsCalleeStorage() returns bool
		return match self 'f'
			feeds(_, sink) gives sink == FeedSink.calleeStorage
			none gives false
		end 'f'
	end 'sinkIsCalleeStorage'
end 'TypeParamFeed'

export typealias TypeParamFeedArray = Array with TypeParamFeed

// ⭐ **A FUNCTION'S PER-PARAMETER CONSUME FACTS (P1.4a wave 2, P1.6-B2)** — the ordered `paramNames` (so a
// call site can slot a LABELLED argument `inner: i` to its position without the `FuncSignature`, which is
// not yet built at parse time), the `bits` (`bits[i]` true iff parameter `i` is CONSUMED — moved into a
// NON-type-parameter struct field, so the callee owns it whatever the concrete type), and the `feeds`
// (`feeds[i]` names the type-parameter position parameter `i` fills, for the concrete-site managed consume).
// The sweep computes them from tokens (the only whole-program input a pure per-file parse may read), one
// entry per function that consumes OR feeds, and travels by NAME with no interner (names, bools, positions —
// no ids), exactly like `enumTypes`. `feeds` is EMPTY for a non-generic function (the common case).
export type ParamConsumeInfo
	export let paramNames as ByteArrayArray
	export let bits as BoolArray
	export let feeds as TypeParamFeedArray

	export static function create(paramNames ByteArrayArray, bits BoolArray, feeds TypeParamFeedArray) returns ParamConsumeInfo
		return Self{paramNames: paramNames, bits: bits, feeds: feeds}
	end 'create'

	export function isConsumed(index ParamCount) returns bool
		if index >= self.bits.count() 'past'
			return false
		end 'past'
		return try self.bits.get(index) otherwise panic("ParamConsumeInfo.isConsumed: bits.get OOB at {index} despite index < count")
	end 'isConsumed'

	// Does ANY parameter feed CALLEE-OWNED storage (P1.7 slice 3b-v, W10)? The whole-`feeds`-column twin of
	// `typeParamFeedOf`'s single-index read — the descriptor-need seed asks it per method (via
	// `ProgramSignatures.functionFeedsCalleeStorage`). Empty for a non-generic function, so it answers false.
	//
	// ⚠ It asks the SINK, not merely `isFeed()`: only a callee-storage feed is enrolled owned, so only it can be
	// left live at an exit and dropped through the descriptor. A `Self{item: p}` CONSTRUCTOR feed is settled
	// entirely at the concrete call site, and seeding the descriptor off it would reserve a slot a generic
	// constructor has no receiver to source (the refusal at `parseFunction`'s `staticLayoutNeedsSelfReturn`).
	export function feedsCalleeStorage() returns bool
		for i in 0 upto self.feeds.count() 'each'
			if (try self.feeds.get(i) otherwise panic("feedsCalleeStorage: feeds.get OOB at {i} despite i < count")).sinkIsCalleeStorage() 'feed'
				return true
			end 'feed'
		end 'each'
		return false
	end 'feedsCalleeStorage'

	// The type-parameter position parameter `index` feeds, or `none` — the concrete-site managed-consume
	// map. Out of range (a non-generic function's empty `feeds`, an unknown label) answers `none`, exactly
	// as `isConsumed` answers `false` for its short `bits`.
	export function typeParamFeedOf(index ParamCount) returns TypeParamFeed
		if index >= self.feeds.count() 'past'
			return TypeParamFeed.none
		end 'past'
		return try self.feeds.get(index) otherwise panic("ParamConsumeInfo.typeParamFeedOf: feeds.get OOB at {index} despite index < count")
	end 'typeParamFeedOf'
end 'ParamConsumeInfo'

// ⭐⭐ **`takesReceiver` RIDES ON EVERY BY-NAME SWEEP ENTRY, AND IT IS THE ONLY THING THAT TELLS THE TWO
// MEMBERS OF A `static`/INSTANCE PAIR APART (W75).** The scan pushes one entry per DECLARATION keyed by the
// name the source wrote, and a pair's two members wear one source name — so without this bit the fold sees
// two entries it cannot attribute, keeps the last, and files one member's answer under the other's key.
// It is the same bit `FuncReturnTypeEntry` carries for the same reason, and `sweepRegistrationKey` is the
// one reader of both.
export type ParamConsumeEntry
	export let name as ByteArray
	export let info as ParamConsumeInfo
	export let takesReceiver as bool

	export static function create(name ByteArray, info ParamConsumeInfo, takesReceiver bool) returns ParamConsumeEntry
		return Self{name: name, info: info, takesReceiver: takesReceiver}
	end 'create'
end 'ParamConsumeEntry'

export typealias ParamConsumeEntryArray = Array with ParamConsumeEntry
export typealias ParamConsumeMap = Map with (ByteArray, ParamConsumeInfo)

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH OF A FUNCTION'S PARAMETERS ARE PASSED BY REFERENCE (PBR-2) — the ABI fact, and the ONE
// whole-program input the cell decision is made from.**
//
// A parameter a callee REASSIGNS (`x = 99`) must be observable from the caller's frame, so it is passed as
// the address of an 8-byte heap CELL rather than as a value: the callee's reads become `loadIndirect` and
// its rebind a `storeIndirect`, and the caller's own binding lives in that same cell for its whole life.
// ⇒ **caller and callee must agree, exactly, before either is parsed** — a caller that passed a value where
// the callee expects an address would dereference an integer.
//
// ⭐ **THAT IS WHY IT LIVES HERE AND NOT IN A LATER PASS, AND THE REASON IS shv2's OWN THESIS.** A binding
// does not survive parsing: `VarInfo.boundValue` says a reference *ALIASES* its initializer's ValueId, so
// `let m = n` and `n` are ONE value and no post-parse pass can tell a use of one from a use of the other.
// A pass that turned "every use of `n`" into a load would rewrite `m`'s reads too — a wrong answer with no
// diagnostic. So the emission is the PARSER's, and the parser needs this before it binds a single name.
// `queryProgramSignatures` is the one whole-program query that completes before any body is parsed, and
// `ParamConsumeInfo` above is the precedent down to the shape: a per-parameter fact derived from BODIES by
// the token sweep, travelling by NAME, slotted at a call site through `argSlotPosition`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A PARAMETER IS BY-REFERENCE FOR ONE OF TWO REASONS, AND THE SECOND IS WHY THIS IS NOT JUST A MASK.**
//
//   • it is REASSIGNED here — `p =` at statement position in this body (`direct`, seeded through
//     `Parser.assignsAt`, the same predicate the loop-phi scan is built on). This is the real fact.
//   • it is FORWARDED to a parameter that is by-reference — `outer(x)` calling an `inner` that reassigns.
//     A forwarded parameter must be by-reference too, and that is TRANSITIVE: `forward1` → `forward2` →
//     `reassign`.
//
// The sweep cannot settle the second at fold time (the callee may sit in a file that has not folded), so it
// records the raw material — `forwarded`, a per-parameter bit, and `forwardCalls`, the calls this body hands
// a bare parameter to — and `ProgramSignatures.closeByRefForwarding` settles it once every file is in.
//
// ⚠⚠ **`forwardCalls` IS WHAT KEEPS THE OVER-APPROXIMATION FROM SWALLOWING THE STDLIB, AND WITHOUT IT THIS
// DESIGN IS UNSHIPPABLE.** The obvious rule — *"a parameter handed to any call might be forwarded"* — is
// sound and catastrophic: the moment ONE program reassigns ONE parameter, every `stdlib` parameter that
// appears as a bare argument in its own body (`arr.get(index)`, `push(item)`) becomes an address and every
// call site in the library allocates a cell. Recording WHICH names a body forwards to costs one array per
// stating function and confines the widening to bodies that hand a parameter to something that reassigns.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A BARE-NAME MATCH, NOT A RESOLUTION, AND THAT IS THE CONSTRAINT THE DESIGN WAS BUILT UNDER.**
// The sweep does not resolve overloads, receivers or labels — a second call-resolver would be the
// one-fact-twice bug with an ABI as the fact. So `Forwarder.reassign` is matched by its bare `reassign`, and
// a body forwarding to ANY `reassign` in the program is widened by ALL of them. The precision that matters
// is per-BODY rather than per-parameter: a body that forwards nothing to a reassigning name is untouched,
// which is every function in `stdlib/` on every program written so far.
//
// ⛔⛔ **BUT THE BARE NAME ALONE WAS NOT A SOUND *WIDENING*, IT WAS A FALSE REJECT, AND THE STDLIB PAID IT.**
// The header below used to justify the whole over-approximation as *"a cell is always a legal way to hold a
// value ... wasteful, never wrong"*. That is a claim about CODEGEN and it is true; it is NOT a claim about
// DIAGNOSTICS, and `Parser.requireOverloadableName` turns "this name takes a parameter by reference" into a
// hard E2015 the moment the name is overloaded. MEASURED: a user program declaring a free `swap` that
// reassigns a parameter failed to compile with *"overloading 'Array.sort'"* — because `stdlib`'s
// `Array.swap` wears the bare name `swap`, inherited the user's by-reference-ness, and carried it up
// `compareAndSwap` → `smallSortRange` → the overloaded `Array.sort`. Renaming the user's function to `zzz`
// compiled the identical program. ⇒ **the widening must not cross a boundary the SOURCE SYNTAX already
// draws**, which is what `ForwardCall` below is.
//
// ⛔ **AND IT IS NOT WHOLLY CLOSED — `Parser.requireOverloadableName` carries the exact residual and the
// measured program.** Two declarations a SPELLING cannot tell apart still share a node, so a user METHOD
// whose name reaches a `stdlib` method through a non-`self` dotted call is still refused. Do not read the
// paragraph above as "fixed"; read it as "fixed for the shapes named at that refusal".
//
// ⚠ **THE WIDENING IS PER-BODY, SO A BODY THAT FORWARDS ANYTHING TO A REASSIGNING NAME HAS *ALL* ITS
// FORWARDED PARAMETERS WIDENED.** `helper(cur, f, replacement)` forwarding `cur` and `replacement` into a
// reassigning `reassign` marks both, though only `cur` lands at the by-reference slot. Sound — a cell is
// always a legal way to hold a value, and only the CALLEE's own ABI decides whether it is read as one —
// cheap, and never paid by a program that does not use the feature.
//
// ⇒ **`isByRef` is the ONE reader that combines the two columns**, so the caller side and the callee side
// cannot come to disagree about an ABI. Never test the columns directly.

export type ParamReassignInfo
	// Call-aligned, exactly as `ParamConsumeInfo.paramNames` is: `self` is prepended for an instance method,
	// so a call site can slot a LABELLED argument to its position before any `FuncSignature` exists.
	export let paramNames as ByteArrayArray
	export let direct as BoolArray
	export let forwarded as BoolArray
	// The calls this body hands a bare parameter to — the forwarding edges, un-resolved, each carrying the
	// SHAPE the source wrote it in (see `ForwardCall`). Empty for a body that forwards no parameter, which is
	// exactly when `forwarded` is all-clear.
	export let forwardCalls as ForwardCallArray
	// This function's OWN bare name — `reassign` for a `Forwarder.reassign`, which is the spelling an edge from
	// another body carries. It rides on the record rather than being split back out of the registration key at
	// closure time: the SWEEP holds both spellings already (`nameTok.value` and `declaredMethodName`), and
	// re-deriving one from the other here would be a fourth speller of the `.` join — which this file argues
	// against by name at `TupleElementSeparator`.
	export let bareName as ByteArray
	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH CALL SHAPES CAN NAME THIS DECLARATION** — the declaration half of `ForwardCall`'s partition.
	// See `DeclCallability` for the three answers and where each comes from.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE BARE DIRECTION IS *NOT* NARROWED, AND MEASURING IT IS WHAT KEPT THIS SOUND.** A BARE call
	// reaches a method too — `helper(1)` inside a sibling method resolves through implicit `self` (MEASURED:
	// it returned 2, i.e. `Holder.helper(1)`) — so `bare` widens over EVERY declaration wearing the name,
	// exactly as this whole mechanism did before. Only the DOTTED directions narrow.
	export let callability as DeclCallability

	export static function create(paramNames ByteArrayArray, direct BoolArray, forwarded BoolArray, forwardCalls ForwardCallArray, bareName ByteArray, callability DeclCallability) returns ParamReassignInfo
		return Self{paramNames: paramNames, direct: direct, forwarded: forwarded, forwardCalls: forwardCalls, bareName: bareName, callability: callability}
	end 'create'

	// ⭐ **THE ABI ANSWER FOR ONE POSITION.** `forwardingIsLive` is this function's settled answer to "does any
	// name I forward to take something by reference?" (`ProgramSignatures.closeByRefForwarding`), which is the
	// half no single file could decide. A position past either column is a parameter the sweep recorded
	// nothing for, which is "by value" — the same "absence is the answer" default `isConsumed` reads a short
	// `bits` under.
	export function isByRef(index ParamCount, forwardingIsLive bool) returns bool
		if bitAt(self.direct, index: index) 'reassignsItself'
			return true
		end 'reassignsItself'

		return forwardingIsLive and bitAt(self.forwarded, index: index)
	end 'isByRef'

	// Does this function reassign ANY parameter of its own? The forwarding closure's SEED — read over the
	// `direct` column alone, because a forward is only ever live by reaching one of these.
	export function reassignsAnyParam() returns bool
		return anyBitSet(self.direct)
	end 'reassignsAnyParam'

	// Does this function forward ANY parameter onward? The closure's cheap skip for a body carrying edges with
	// nothing to send along them.
	export function forwardsAnyParam() returns bool
		return anyBitSet(self.forwarded)
	end 'forwardsAnyParam'

	// Is there anything to record at all? A body that neither reassigns nor forwards states nothing and is not
	// recorded — the "absence is the answer" default the whole registry rests on, which here is also what makes
	// the map EMPTY for every program written before this rung.
	export function statesAnything() returns bool
		return self.reassignsAnyParam() or self.forwardsAnyParam()
	end 'statesAnything'
end 'ParamReassignInfo'

// ⭐⭐ **ONE FORWARDING EDGE, CARRYING THE ONE THING ABOUT A CALL THE SWEEP CAN READ WITHOUT RESOLVING IT:
// WHETHER THE SOURCE WROTE A RECEIVER IN FRONT OF THE NAME.**
//
// That single token — the `.` before the callee — is a hard partition of what a call can name, and it needs
// no type, no scope and no overload set to read:
//
//   • **`f(…)`** may name a free `f` OR, through implicit `self`, a sibling METHOD `f`. It widens over both,
//     which is what this mechanism always did.
//   • **`x.f(…)`** can name only a METHOD `f`, or a free `f` reached by the directory ROUTE `utils.f(…)`.
//     It can NEVER name a plain root-level free function — that name has no qualified spelling to be
//     written after a dot. This is the arm that stops `stdlib`'s `Array.swap` inheriting a user program's
//     free `swap` (see `ParamReassignInfo`'s header for the measurement).
//
//   • **`self.f(…)` / `Self.f(…)`** narrows that once more: the receiver's type is the ENCLOSING TYPE,
//     statically and syntactically, so the call can name only THAT type's `f`. It is the same fact
//     `DeclCallability.methodOf` reads off a declaration, asked at the call.
//
// ⚠ **THE `self.` ARM IS SOUND BECAUSE THE SWEEP ENTERS EACH CONFORMER'S SCOPE, NOT THE INTERFACE'S**, and
// that was CHECKED rather than assumed: an interface extension's default body is swept once per conformer
// under `enterTypeScope(conformer)` (`Parser.foldExtensionDeclaration`, the same loop the real parse runs),
// so a `self.f()` inside one is recorded against the CONFORMER — which is also the type its own declaration
// is filed under. An `interface` declaration itself carries signatures and no bodies, so it records no edge
// at all. There is no route by which `self.f()` names a method of a type other than the one enclosing it.
//
// ⚠ **IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CARRY THE ASSEMBLED CHAIN.** `resolvesAsNamespaceQualifiedFunction` could
// answer "is `utils.helper` a route to a free `helper`?" exactly, but asking it would mean assembling the
// written chain at every recorded edge and holding the bytes until the closure runs. The declaration-side
// answer (`DeclCallability`) buys the same soundness for one union case: a free function that HAS a
// namespace is admitted to the receiver-reachable side whether or not this particular chain names it. Wider
// than the exact question, and wider only across `stdlib`'s own free functions, which are few.
export union ForwardCall
	bare(name ByteArray)
	throughAReceiver(name ByteArray)
	throughSelf(typeName ByteArray, name ByteArray)
end 'ForwardCall'

export typealias ForwardCallArray = Array with ForwardCall

// The callee name an edge carries, whichever shape it was written in — the one place the arms' shared
// payload is read, so a fourth reader cannot come to match one arm and forget the others.
export function forwardCallName(call ForwardCall) returns ByteArray
	return match call 'c'
		bare(name) gives name
		throughAReceiver(name) gives name
		throughSelf(_, name) gives name
	end 'c'
end 'forwardCallName'

// The shape as a value that can be COMPARED and HASHED — what the memo fold and the per-body edge
// de-duplication both need, neither of which dispatches on the arm. A `throughSelf` folds its type name in
// too, because `self.f()` in two different types states two different edges.
function forwardCallShapeMark(call ForwardCall) returns ContentHash
	return match call 'c'
		bare gives BareCallShape
		throughAReceiver gives ReceiverCallShape
		throughSelf gives SelfCallShape
	end 'c'
end 'forwardCallShapeMark'

// Do two edges state the SAME thing? Name, shape and — for a `self.` call — the type it is `self` of. Used
// only to de-duplicate a body's own handful of edges (`Parser.noteForwardCall`).
export function forwardCallsAreEqual(a ForwardCall, b ForwardCall) returns bool
	if forwardCallShapeMark(a) != forwardCallShapeMark(b) 'differentShapes'
		return false
	end 'differentShapes'

	return forwardCallName(a).equals(forwardCallName(b)) and forwardCallSelfType(a).equals(forwardCallSelfType(b))
end 'forwardCallsAreEqual'

// One declaration's callability, folded into the memo hash — the twin of `forwardCallShapeMark` on the
// declaration side, and a `match` so a fourth answer fails to compile here rather than hashing as a third.
function foldCallability(state ContentHash, callability DeclCallability) returns ContentHash
	return match callability 'c'
		bareCallOnly gives mix(state, value: RootFreeFunctionDecl)
		bareCallOrDirectoryRoute gives mix(state, value: RoutableFreeFunctionDecl)
		methodOf(typeName) gives mixBytes(mix(state, value: MethodDecl), bytes: typeName)
	end 'c'
end 'foldCallability'

// The type a `self.`-qualified edge is `self` of, or EMPTY for the two shapes that name no type. Empty is
// not a sentinel here: it is compared only against another edge's answer, and the shape mark has already
// established that both are `throughSelf` or neither is.
function forwardCallSelfType(call ForwardCall) returns ByteArray
	return match call 'c'
		bare gives EmptySourceText
		throughAReceiver gives EmptySourceText
		throughSelf(typeName, _) gives typeName
	end 'c'
end 'forwardCallSelfType'

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH CALLS CAN NAME THIS DECLARATION?** — the declaration-side half of `ForwardCall`'s partition,
// and the three answers are decided from two facts the sweep holds outright, with nothing resolved:
// whether the declaration sits inside a `type`/`extension` body, and whether its file has a directory
// namespace.
export union DeclCallability
	// A free function at the compile ANCHOR. Only a BARE call can name it: it has no qualified spelling
	// (`namespaceQualifiedName` panics if asked for one), so no dotted call anywhere can reach it. This is
	// the arm that stops a user program's free `swap` from making `stdlib`'s `Array.swap` by-reference.
	bareCallOnly
	// A free function in a NON-ROOT directory: a bare call, or the directory route `utils.f(…)` (N1b).
	bareCallOrDirectoryRoute
	// A method of `typeName`: a bare call (implicit `self` reaches a sibling — MEASURED), any
	// receiver-qualified call, and a `self.`/`Self.`-qualified call inside `typeName`'s own body.
	methodOf(typeName ByteArray)
end 'DeclCallability'

// One bool column read at a position that may be past its end — the shared short-column read the two
// `ParamReassignInfo` columns are asked through, so "recorded nothing here" has one spelling rather than two.
function bitAt(column BoolArray, index ParamCount) returns bool
	if index >= column.count() 'past'
		return false
	end 'past'
	return try column.get(index) otherwise panic("bitAt: column.get OOB at {index} despite index < count")
end 'bitAt'

// Is any bit of `column` set? The shared body of the whole-column questions above, so "this function states
// something" and "this function seeds the closure" cannot come to read the columns differently.
function anyBitSet(column BoolArray) returns bool
	for bit in column 'each'
		if bit 'set'
			return true
		end 'set'
	end 'each'
	return false
end 'anyBitSet'

// ⭐⭐ **MAY A CALL TO THIS FUNCTION HAND BACK A `let`-DECLARED TOP-LEVEL GLOBAL'S OWN RECORD? (W117)** —
// the whole-program fact that lets the immutability rule be enforced AT THE WRITE (⚖ user, 2026-08-14)
// rather than at the return. Reading a `let` global and returning it is legal; MUTATING through what came
// back is the error, and the caller can only know to refuse that if it knows what the callee's return may
// alias. The callee's body is routinely in another file, so no per-file parse can answer it.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS `ParamReassignInfo`'s SHAPE BECAUSE IT IS `ParamReassignInfo`'s PROBLEM** — a per-declaration
// fact derived from BODIES by the token sweep, whose transitive half no single file can settle, recorded
// raw here and closed by `ProgramSignatures.closeGlobalAliasReturns` once every file is in. The two even
// share the node space and the edge shape, which is the point: a second spelling of "close a per-declaration
// fact under the call graph" would be one concept with two answers.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE SWEEP IS DELIBERATELY NARROW, AND WHAT MAKES THAT SOUND IS A FALLBACK RATHER THAN COVERAGE.**
// A `return` this scan does not recognise records nothing, so the function's bit stays clear — and a bit
// that is clear is exactly the condition under which `Parser.ownedFormOfBorrowedValue` keeps the ORIGINAL
// E2015 refusal at the return. So:
//
//   • **UNDER-approximating (missing a seed or an edge) costs a REFUSAL, never a silent acceptance.** The
//     program is rejected with the message it was rejected with before this rung existed.
//   • **OVER-approximating (a spurious seed or edge) costs a FALSE REJECT at some later write**, which is
//     the only real hazard here.
//
// ⇒ **EVERY AMBIGUITY RESOLVES TOWARD "NOT A SEED".** That is why the returned name must be a bare
// statement (`return A`, `return T.member`) and why a name the body BINDS anywhere is dropped outright
// (`Parser.scanBodyFacts`). A later reader must not "simplify" the narrowness away by widening the scan
// and deleting the fallback: the narrowness is only safe while the fallback stands.
export type GlobalAliasReturnInfo
	// The names this body returns as a whole statement — `A`, or the joined `Box.shared` a static member is
	// read under (`Parser.parseStaticMemberAccess` looks a static member up under exactly that spelling).
	// Whether one of them DENOTES an immutable stored global is decided at closure time, because a file that
	// has not folded yet may be the one that declares it.
	export let returnedNames as ByteArrayArray
	// The calls whose result this body returns — `return f(…)` — as the un-resolved shapes the source wrote
	// (see `ForwardCall`). A function that returns what a marked function returns is marked itself.
	export let returnCalls as ForwardCallArray
	// This declaration's own bare name and callability, for `ParamReassignInfo`'s reason and read through the
	// same `DeclNodeSpace`.
	export let bareName as ByteArray
	export let callability as DeclCallability
	// The file the declaration was swept from — the READER whose scope `returnedNames` must be resolved in.
	// A name is looked up file-scoped-then-bare (`declFor`), so two files' `A` are two different globals and
	// the answer is meaningless without the asking file.
	export let declFilePath as FilePath

	export static function create(returnedNames ByteArrayArray, returnCalls ForwardCallArray, bareName ByteArray, callability DeclCallability, declFilePath FilePath) returns GlobalAliasReturnInfo
		return Self{returnedNames: returnedNames, returnCalls: returnCalls, bareName: bareName, callability: callability, declFilePath: declFilePath}
	end 'create'

	// Is there anything to record at all? A body that returns neither a bare name nor a call states nothing
	// and is not recorded — the "absence is the answer" default the whole registry rests on, and what keeps
	// the map empty for a program with no top-level aggregate at all.
	//
	// ⚡ **OVER THE TWO COLUMNS RATHER THAN OVER A BUILT `Self`, SO THE ANSWER IS AVAILABLE *BEFORE* THE
	// RECORD EXISTS.** The sweep asks it of the raw columns and builds nothing for a body that states nothing,
	// which is most of them; asked of a `Self` it would have cost a record and a `DeclCallability` per swept
	// declaration to discover that neither was wanted.
	export static function statesAnything(returnedNames ByteArrayArray, returnCalls ForwardCallArray) returns bool
		return returnedNames.count() > 0 or returnCalls.count() > 0
	end 'statesAnything'
end 'GlobalAliasReturnInfo'

// `takesReceiver` rides for `ParamConsumeEntry`'s reason (W75) — see there.
export type GlobalAliasReturnEntry
	export let name as ByteArray
	export let info as GlobalAliasReturnInfo
	export let takesReceiver as bool

	export static function create(name ByteArray, info GlobalAliasReturnInfo, takesReceiver bool) returns GlobalAliasReturnEntry
		return Self{name: name, info: info, takesReceiver: takesReceiver}
	end 'create'
end 'GlobalAliasReturnEntry'

export typealias GlobalAliasReturnEntryArray = Array with GlobalAliasReturnEntry
export typealias GlobalAliasReturnMap = Map with (ByteArray, GlobalAliasReturnInfo)

// The registration keys whose function may hand back an immutable global's record — the settled output of
// `ProgramSignatures.closeGlobalAliasReturns`. A key with no entry answers false.
export typealias GlobalAliasReturnKeySet = Set with ByteArray

// `takesReceiver` rides for `ParamConsumeEntry`'s reason (W75) — see there.
export type ParamReassignEntry
	export let name as ByteArray
	export let info as ParamReassignInfo
	export let takesReceiver as bool

	export static function create(name ByteArray, info ParamReassignInfo, takesReceiver bool) returns ParamReassignEntry
		return Self{name: name, info: info, takesReceiver: takesReceiver}
	end 'create'
end 'ParamReassignEntry'

export typealias ParamReassignEntryArray = Array with ParamReassignEntry
export typealias ParamReassignMap = Map with (ByteArray, ParamReassignInfo)

// Function name -> "does any name this body forwards a parameter to take something by reference?" — the
// settled output of `ProgramSignatures.closeByRefForwarding`. A name with no entry answers false.
export typealias ByRefForwardingMap = Map with (ByteArray, bool)

// The BARE names (`reassign`, never `Forwarder.reassign`) that take at least one parameter by reference —
// the closure's answer, and what a forwarding edge is matched against.
export typealias ByRefBareNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// A bare name -> its dense node in the forwarding graph `closeByRefForwarding` closes. The CSR key space must
// be dense and 0-based (see `CsrGraph`), and a bare name is neither, so it is interned into one.
typealias DeclNodeIndex = Map with (ByteArray, CsrIndex)

// A bare name -> every METHOD node wearing it. A method's node is keyed by its QUALIFIED spelling, so a
// call that knows only the final segment (`x.swap(…)`) needs this to reach them; a `self.swap(…)` does not,
// because it knows the type. Built by `DeclNodeSpace.internMethod` as it mints each method node.
typealias MethodNodesByName = Map with (ByteArray, CsrColumn)

// ⭐⭐ **THE NODE SPACE `closeByRefForwarding` CLOSES OVER — ONE NODE PER DECLARATION, THREE WAYS TO FIND
// IT.** A declaration gets exactly one node (that is what makes a mark on it mean one thing); which INDEX
// it is filed in is `ParamReassignInfo.callability`, and which indexes an EDGE consults is
// `ForwardCall`'s shape. The two meet in `forwardCallTargets`, which is the ONLY place the shape→node rule
// is written.
//
// ⚠ **ONE NODE PER DECLARATION IS A CORRECTNESS PROPERTY, NOT A TIDINESS ONE.** Filing a method under both
// its bare name and its qualified one would give it two nodes, and `reverseReachabilityMark` marks NODES —
// so a mark arriving through the bare node would be invisible to a `self.`-qualified edge asking the
// qualified one, which is a MISSED by-reference parameter and therefore a wrong ABI. The bare-name reach is
// served by `methodNodesByName` (an index onto the same node) rather than by a second node.
type DeclNodeSpace
	// Per node, in mint order: the declaration's BARE name (what the published set is keyed by) and whether
	// any declaration on that node reassigns a parameter of its own (the closure's seed).
	export let bareNames as ByteArrayArray
	export let seeds as BoolArray
	// A free function at the compile ANCHOR, keyed by its bare name. Only a bare call reaches these.
	export let rootFreeFunctions as DeclNodeIndex
	// A free function with a directory route, keyed by its bare name. A bare call or any dotted call.
	export let routableFreeFunctions as DeclNodeIndex
	// A method, keyed by its QUALIFIED `Type.name` spelling — which is what makes `Array.swap` and
	// `Holder.swap` two nodes rather than one.
	export let methods as DeclNodeIndex
	export let methodNodesByName as MethodNodesByName

	static function create() returns DeclNodeSpace
		return Self{bareNames: ByteArrayArray.create(), seeds: BoolArray.create(), rootFreeFunctions: DeclNodeIndex.create(), routableFreeFunctions: DeclNodeIndex.create(), methods: DeclNodeIndex.create(), methodNodesByName: MethodNodesByName.create()}
	end 'create'

	// ⭐ **THE NODE A DECLARATION OWNS** — minted on first sight, and the one place a `callability` is turned
	// into an index. The METHOD arm also files the node under its bare name, because that is how a call that
	// wrote only the final segment finds it.
	export function nodeFor(bareName ByteArray, callability DeclCallability) returns CsrIndex
		return match callability 'c'
			bareCallOnly gives self.intern(self.rootFreeFunctions, key: bareName, bareName: bareName)
			bareCallOrDirectoryRoute gives self.intern(self.routableFreeFunctions, key: bareName, bareName: bareName)
			methodOf(typeName) gives self.internMethod(typeName, bareName: bareName)
		end 'c'
	end 'nodeFor'

	function internMethod(typeName ByteArray, bareName ByteArray) returns CsrIndex
		let node = self.intern(self.methods, key: qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName: bareName), bareName: bareName)
		var wearers = try self.methodNodesByName.get(bareName) otherwise CsrColumn.create()
		for existing in wearers 'alreadyListed'
			if existing == node 'sameNode'
				return node
			end 'sameNode'
		end 'alreadyListed'

		wearers.push(node)
		self.methodNodesByName.upsert(bareName, value: wearers)
		return node
	end 'internMethod'

	// `key`'s dense node in `index`, minting one on first sight. The three columns move together — the name,
	// its node and its seed bit — because a node with no seed slot would read a bit belonging to a different
	// declaration, which is the one way a counting-sorted key space can be corrupted silently.
	function intern(index DeclNodeIndex, key ByteArray, bareName ByteArray) returns CsrIndex
		if let existing = try index.get(key) 'alreadyANode'
			return existing
		end 'alreadyANode'

		let node = self.bareNames.count()
		self.bareNames.push(bareName)
		self.seeds.push(false)
		index.upsert(key, value: node)
		return node
	end 'intern'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE NODES A CALL CAN NAME — THE ONE HOME OF THE SHAPE→FAMILY RULE.** Both sides of the closure
	// spend it: `closeByRefForwarding` builds an edge per target, and then asks the SAME function which
	// targets to consult when publishing each key's answer.
	//
	// ⛔ **WRITING IT TWICE IS A WRONG ABI AND NOT A COMPILE ERROR.** Admit some new spelling to a family on
	// the build side while the query side still consults the old set, and `calleeTakesAnyParamByRef` answers
	// FALSE for a callee the graph marked by-reference — so the caller passes a value where the callee
	// dereferences an address. Nothing in the type system catches that; only having one function does.
	//
	// A name no recorded declaration wears yields NO targets, and that is the answer: nothing can ever make
	// it by-reference.
	export function targetsOf(call ForwardCall) returns CsrColumn
		var targets = CsrColumn.create()
		let name = forwardCallName(call)
		match call 'c'
			// `self.f(…)` / `Self.f(…)`: the receiver's type IS the enclosing type, so exactly one node.
			throughSelf(typeName, _) then self.pushIfPresent(targets, index: self.methods, key: qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName: name))
			// `x.f(…)`: a method wearing the name, or a free function with a directory route.
			throughAReceiver then self.pushDottedTargets(targets, name: name)
			// `f(…)`: everything a dotted call reaches, plus a root-level free function.
			bare then self.pushBareTargets(targets, name: name)
		end 'c'
		return targets
	end 'targetsOf'

	// The targets EVERY dotted shape shares: the methods wearing the name, and the free function a
	// directory route could reach. `throughSelf` deliberately does not come through here — it names one
	// method and no free function at all.
	function pushDottedTargets(targets CsrColumn, name ByteArray)
		for node in try self.methodNodesByName.get(name) otherwise sharedEmptyMethodNodes 'eachMethodWearingIt'
			targets.push(node)
		end 'eachMethodWearingIt'
		self.pushIfPresent(targets, index: self.routableFreeFunctions, key: name)
	end 'pushDottedTargets'

	// A bare call reaches everything a dotted one does — implicit `self` makes it name a sibling METHOD, and
	// a free function is nameable bare whether or not it also has a directory route — PLUS the root-level
	// free function no dotted spelling can reach. Stated as "the dotted set plus one" rather than as its own
	// list, because that containment IS the rule and a second list could stop expressing it.
	function pushBareTargets(targets CsrColumn, name ByteArray)
		self.pushDottedTargets(targets, name: name)
		self.pushIfPresent(targets, index: self.rootFreeFunctions, key: name)
	end 'pushBareTargets'

	function pushIfPresent(targets CsrColumn, index DeclNodeIndex, key ByteArray)
		if let node = try index.get(key) 'namesARecordedDeclaration'
			targets.push(node)
		end 'namesARecordedDeclaration'
	end 'pushIfPresent'
end 'DeclNodeSpace'

// The shared EMPTY method-node list `targetsOf` walks when no method wears the name — read-only, so one
// instance serves every miss rather than allocating a column per lookup.
let sharedEmptyMethodNodes = CsrColumn.create()

// ⭐⭐ **A DEFAULT VALUE IS A SYNTHESIZED NULLARY FUNCTION, AND THIS IS ITS NAME (BATCH2 slice 4).**
// `function greet(name String = "World")` declares a second function, `__paramDefault#greet#0`, whose whole
// body is `return "World"`; a call site that omits the argument emits an ordinary call to it and passes the
// result. That is the ENTIRE mechanism, and everything it buys follows from "it is an ordinary call":
//
//   • **Ownership needs no new rule.** shv2 decides drops in the PARSER — a value materialized in a later
//     tier has no scope to be dropped from — so the default's value has to be born at the call site, and a
//     call RESULT is a shape the parser already owns and drops (`enrolOwnedCallTemp`). v1 materializes at
//     LOWERING instead, and pays for it with an explicit `classifyValue(callReturnRc1)` that only works
//     because v1 has a late refcount inserter; shv2 has none, and a lowering-minted `String` would leak.
//   • **The default's EXPRESSION is parsed exactly once, in the file that declares it**, by the ordinary
//     expression parser, against the ordinary scope rules. A call site in ANOTHER FILE could not re-parse
//     it in any case: a shv2 parse is a pure function of one file's `(tokens, filePath, namespace)`, which
//     is precisely what makes the per-file fan-out possible — so the bootstrap's trick of replaying the
//     default's TOKENS at each call site (`CaptureDefaultValueTokens`/`EmitDefaultFromTokens`) is not
//     available here, and would be an architectural regression if it were.
//   • **Dead-function elimination, the linker and the type checker all see it for free**, because they see
//     a `MaxonOp.call`. v1 has to pin its helper in `project.livenessRoots` by hand precisely because its
//     synthesized call is minted at Std level, below the tier DFE walks.
//
// ⚠ **A HELPER IS COMPILED ONCE, AT THE DECLARATION, SO IT CANNOT SEE THE CALL SITE — WHICH IS WHY
// CALLER-LOCATION DEFAULTS ARE A SECOND MECHANISM BESIDE IT AND NOT AN ARM OF IT (W72).** `__line__` /
// `__file__` (`specs/source-location-defaults.md`) need the default materialized AT THE CALLER from
// caller-side facts; this paragraph used to say they were therefore "not expressible", and what was true
// was the narrower sentence above it. They are expressible, by NOT minting a helper: the sweep publishes
// `ParamDefaultProducer.callerLine` / `.callerFile` at that slot and the fill emits a `literal` /
// `stringLiteral` op at the call — the very ops the caller would have emitted had it written the value
// out. So the two doors meet at `ParamDefaultProducer` and share everything downstream of it (the
// call-aligned slotting, the label, `argDefaultSlots`, the arity gate), and differ only in what produces
// the value. v1 has neither door.
//
// ⚠ There is no constant fast path (v1 splits `intLiteral`/`boolLiteral`/`floatLiteralBits`/`byteLiteral`/
// `enumCase` out of `helper`). One mechanism means one place a default's value is produced: a constant arm
// would have to re-derive literal emission — String interning, array construction, enum tagging, ranged and
// float alias widening — at the CALL SITE, in a second writer, for a value the declaring file's own parser
// already types correctly against the parameter's declared type. The cost is one `call`/`ret` per OMITTED
// argument, which no hot path in a real program pays.
//
// The name is minted by `Project.paramDefaultHelperName` (there, so the `#` join keeps its one speller).
//
// ⭐⭐ **WHAT PRODUCES ONE PARAMETER'S DEFAULT VALUE AT A CALL SITE — the per-slot fact the whole-program
// index publishes, and the meeting point of the language's two default doors (W72).**
//
//   • `required` — the parameter declares no default; a call that omits it is E3036.
//   • `helper(name)` — the synthesized nullary function above. The call emits a `call` to it.
//   • `callerLine` / `callerFile` — a CALLER-LOCATION default. Nothing is synthesized and nothing is
//     called: the fill emits the constant the caller's own position names.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A UNION AND NOT A NAME-OR-EMPTY, WHICH IT WAS UNTIL W72.** A `ByteArray` column can say
// "helper" and "no default" but has no third thing to say, and the two candidate encodings for a third are
// both the shape this project keeps paying for: a sentinel name (a tag hidden inside a value) or a second
// parallel column (one fact written twice, free to disagree with the first about whether a slot defaults at
// all). The union makes the three answers exhaustive, so every reader must `match` and none can fall
// through — `recordParamDefaults`' hash bit and `renameParamDefaultHelpers`' skip both got this wrong for
// free under the old shape, because a caller-location slot READ as "no default".
// ⚠ **THE THREE ARMS THAT SYNTHESIZE NOTHING ARE DECLARED FIRST AND CONTIGUOUSLY**, so the readers that
// care only whether a slot OWNS A FUNCTION — `renameParamDefaultHelpers`, and any future walk of the
// synthesized set — say `required to callerFile` in one arm rather than listing the three and being free to
// forget the one added next.
export union ParamDefaultProducer
	required
	callerLine
	callerFile
	helper(name ByteArray)
end 'ParamDefaultProducer'

export typealias ParamDefaultProducerArray = Array with ParamDefaultProducer

// The content-hash mark one slot's producer folds in — see `DeclShapeParamDefault` for why the four answers
// are four marks. A free function because a `match` is the whole body and `recordParamDefaults` is inside a
// loop that has no room for one.
function defaultProducerMark(producer ParamDefaultProducer) returns ContentHash
	return match producer 'produces'
		required gives DefaultBitClear
		callerLine gives DefaultBitCallerLine
		callerFile gives DefaultBitCallerFile
		helper gives DefaultBitSet
	end 'produces'
end 'defaultProducerMark'

// ⭐⭐ **DO TWO DECLARATIONS OF ONE NAME SLOT A CALL'S ARGUMENTS THE SAME WAY? — the half of "may the sweep
// answer for this name from its ONE entry?" that BOTH sweep records ask, spelled once.**
//
// `OpaqueParamInfo.matches` and `ParamDefaultInfo.matches` decide that question over two different facts, and
// each of them has to compare the SAME list first: the CALL-ALIGNED parameter names (receiver prepended),
// which are what a labelled argument is slotted by and therefore what makes "the same call" mean the same
// positions in both declarations. It is one rule, and it decided the admission of one overload set from two
// loops — so a future edit to either (treating a repeated `_` as unnameable, say, or folding a rename that
// no call can observe) would have moved one verdict and not the other, and the set would have been admitted
// by the defaults gate while the opaque gate declined it. Neither copy would fail to compile.
function callAlignedParamNamesAgree(mine ByteArrayArray, other ByteArrayArray) returns bool
	if mine.count() != other.count() 'differentArity'
		return false
	end 'differentArity'

	for i in 0 upto mine.count() 'eachName'
		let name = try mine.get(i) otherwise panic("callAlignedParamNamesAgree: paramNames.get OOB at {i} despite i < count")
		if not name.equals(try other.get(i) otherwise panic("callAlignedParamNamesAgree: the other declaration's paramNames.get OOB at {i} despite the counts being equal")) 'renamedSlot'
			return false
		end 'renamedSlot'
	end 'eachName'

	return true
end 'callAlignedParamNamesAgree'

// ⭐ **A FUNCTION'S PER-PARAMETER DEFAULTS** — the call-aligned `paramNames` (so a call site can slot a
// LABELLED argument to its position before any `FuncSignature` exists, exactly as `ParamConsumeInfo` does)
// and, per position, what PRODUCES that parameter's default (`ParamDefaultProducer`). The sweep computes it
// from tokens — the only whole-program input a pure per-file parse may read — and records one entry per
// function that declares AT LEAST ONE default, so a program with none costs a single empty map. It travels
// by NAME with no interner ids, exactly like `ParamConsumeEntry`.
export type ParamDefaultInfo
	export let paramNames as ByteArrayArray
	export let producers as ParamDefaultProducerArray

	export static function create(paramNames ByteArrayArray, producers ParamDefaultProducerArray) returns ParamDefaultInfo
		return Self{paramNames: paramNames, producers: producers}
	end 'create'

	// What produces parameter `index`'s default, or `required` when it has none — the door the call-site
	// fill asks per unfilled slot. An index past the recorded list answers `required`, the same
	// "absence is the answer" default `ParamConsumeInfo.isConsumed` rests on. Minted rather than served
	// from one shared binding because the branch is reached only when the two walks of a parameter list
	// have come apart — `sweptParamDefaultHelperName`'s panic is the next thing that happens — so there is
	// no allocation here worth sharing, and a shared managed value on a path nothing exercises is a
	// refcount shape nothing would test.
	export function producerFor(index ParamCount) returns ParamDefaultProducer
		if index >= self.producers.count() 'past'
			return ParamDefaultProducer.required
		end 'past'
		return try self.producers.get(index) otherwise panic("ParamDefaultInfo.producerFor: producers.get OOB at {index} despite index < count")
	end 'producerFor'

	// ⭐ **RE-POINT ONE SLOT AT A DIFFERENTLY-NAMED HELPER** — the one mutation this record admits, and it
	// exists for exactly one caller: `ProgramSignatures.renameParamDefaultHelpers`, which follows a
	// contested free function's declaration onto its directory-qualified registration name (N1c review).
	// Written here rather than as a field poke so the slot index is bounds-checked by the record that owns
	// the list, exactly as `producerFor` reads it.
	export function renameHelper(index ParamCount, renamedTo ByteArray)
		try self.producers.set(index, value: ParamDefaultProducer.helper(renamedTo)) otherwise panic("ParamDefaultInfo.renameHelper: producers.set OOB at {index} — the caller walks this list's own count()")
	end 'renameHelper'

	// ⭐⭐ **DO TWO DECLARATIONS OF ONE NAME DESCRIBE THE SAME DEFAULT SHAPE? — the test that decides whether
	// an OVERLOAD SET may carry defaults at all (W74).** `OpaqueParamInfo.matches`' twin, one declaration fact
	// over, and the same reason it exists: the sweep keys by bare name and an overload set shares one key.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT COMPARES THE SHAPE, WHICH IS EVERYTHING EXCEPT THE DEFAULT'S EXPRESSION** — and that is the whole
	// of what makes per-overload defaults expressible. A call's argument list is BUILT while the call is parsed
	// and the overload is picked a whole pass later, so every fact the fill commits to must be one every member
	// agrees on: the call-aligned parameter NAMES (which decide what a labelled argument occupies), and per
	// position what PRODUCES the default (which decides how many arguments the fill appends, and what ops it
	// emits for each). Two members that agree on all of that are filled identically whichever one wins, so
	// nothing the parse decided can be wrong. What is left over — the expression — is a function BODY, and
	// `SemanticCheck.repointDefaultedArgHelpers` re-points the emitted call at the resolved member's own once
	// the member is known.
	//
	// The HELPER NAME rides this comparison and costs nothing: the sweep mints it from the BARE name
	// (`recordScannedParamDefaults`), so two agreeing members publish the identical string. The name each
	// member's helper is EMITTED under is a different question, answered at the declaration from the
	// registration name (`Parser.parseDefaultHelper`).
	export function matches(other ParamDefaultInfo) returns bool
		if self.producers.count() != other.producers.count() or not callAlignedParamNamesAgree(self.paramNames, other: other.paramNames) 'differentShape'
			return false
		end 'differentShape'

		for i in 0 upto self.producers.count() 'eachSlot'
			if not paramDefaultProducersAgree(self.producerFor(i), other: other.producerFor(i)) 'differentProducer'
				return false
			end 'differentProducer'
		end 'eachSlot'

		return true
	end 'matches'
end 'ParamDefaultInfo'

// Do two slots produce their default the same way? A free function because a `match` pair is the whole body,
// and it is spelled over `defaultProducerMark` rather than over the arms so that a producer added to the union
// cannot be compared as equal to a different one by an arm nobody remembered to write: the mark is the one
// place a producer's identity is enumerated, and it already refuses to compile with a case missing.
// A `helper` compares by NAME, which for two declarations of one bare name the sweep mints identically.
function paramDefaultProducersAgree(producer ParamDefaultProducer, other ParamDefaultProducer) returns bool
	if defaultProducerMark(producer) != defaultProducerMark(other) 'differentKind'
		return false
	end 'differentKind'

	return match producer 'produces'
		required to callerFile gives true
		helper(name) gives name.equals(paramDefaultHelperNameOf(other))
	end 'produces'
end 'paramDefaultProducersAgree'

// The helper name a producer the caller has ALREADY established to be a `helper` carries — the extraction the
// comparison above cannot spell inline, because a `match` binding does not outlive the arm that binds it.
function paramDefaultHelperNameOf(producer ParamDefaultProducer) returns ByteArray
	return match producer 'produces'
		required to callerFile panic("paramDefaultHelperNameOf: asked of a producer that synthesizes no function — the caller compares `defaultProducerMark` first, which is what establishes that both sides are helpers")
		helper(name) gives name
	end 'produces'
end 'paramDefaultHelperNameOf'

// `takesReceiver` rides for `ParamConsumeEntry`'s reason (W75) — see there.
export type ParamDefaultEntry
	export let name as ByteArray
	export let info as ParamDefaultInfo
	export let takesReceiver as bool

	export static function create(name ByteArray, info ParamDefaultInfo, takesReceiver bool) returns ParamDefaultEntry
		return Self{name: name, info: info, takesReceiver: takesReceiver}
	end 'create'
end 'ParamDefaultEntry'

export typealias ParamDefaultEntryArray = Array with ParamDefaultEntry
export typealias ParamDefaultMap = Map with (ByteArray, ParamDefaultInfo)

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH OF A METHOD'S PARAMETERS ARE DECLARED AS A BARE TYPE PARAMETER, AND WHICH `uses` SLOT EACH
// NAMES (ARRR)** — the call-aligned `paramNames` (so a call site can slot a LABELLED argument to its
// position before any `FuncSignature` exists, exactly as `ParamConsumeInfo` and `ParamDefaultInfo` do) and,
// per slot, that parameter's position in the declaring type's `uses` list.
//
// ⭐ **IT EXISTS BECAUSE A SHARED GENERIC BODY CANNOT HOLD ITS OWN RANGE GUARD.** A concrete ranged
// parameter gets one at the callee's ENTRY (`RangeCheckParamSite`); a `value T` has no range there to guard,
// and the only frame that knows what `T` was bound to is the CALLER. So the call site needs to know which of
// its arguments land on an opaque formal, and that is a whole-program fact about a callee that may be
// declared in another file — which is the same reason `ParamConsumeInfo` and `ParamDefaultInfo` are
// whole-program. Like both of them it travels by NAME with no interner ids (names, numbers), so it folds
// with no remap.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS NOT A COLUMN ON `ParamConsumeInfo`, AND THE REASON IS MEASURED RATHER THAN AESTHETIC.** That
// record's presence is itself read as a fact: `Parser.requireOverloadableName` refuses an overload set the
// moment `consumeOf` answers `consumes(…)` at all, whatever the bits say. Recording an entry for every
// generic method that merely HAS an opaque parameter would therefore start refusing overload sets that
// transfer nothing — a legal program rejected over a record that decides nothing. Widening it would also put
// this rung inside `publishTransitiveFeeds`' rebuild, the one place a previous rung's review found a SIGSEGV
// caused by reconstructing that record from partial state (W10).
//
// ⚠ **ONE ENTRY PER BARE NAME, LAST DECLARATION WINS** — `ProgramSignatures.record`'s discipline for every
// fact it applies. An OVERLOAD SET therefore publishes one answer for the whole set, which is the boundary
// `requireOverloadableName`'s header already states in full (*"THE FIX IS NOT A BETTER TEST HERE, IT IS
// PER-MEMBER FACTS IN THE SWEEP"*). The consequence here is a MISSED check, never a wrong one: an overloaded
// method whose last declaration takes no opaque parameter records nothing and its call sites are guarded
// exactly as they were before this rung.
export type OpaqueParamInfo
	export let paramNames as ByteArrayArray
	// One entry per CALL-ALIGNED slot: the parameter's position in the declaring type's `uses` list, or
	// `typeParamCount` for a parameter declared with a concrete type. The sentinel is the count for
	// `Parser.paramTypeParamPositionAt`'s reason — it is the value that walk already writes for a plain
	// parameter, so this record stores what the sweep computed rather than a re-encoding of it.
	export let positions as TypeParamPositionArray
	// The declaring type's `uses` ARITY — the boundary above, and the presence bit. Only
	// `sharedNoOpaqueParams` has ZERO, because an info is built only when some position is BELOW the count,
	// which requires the count to be positive.
	export let typeParamCount as TypeParamPosition

	export static function create(paramNames ByteArrayArray, positions TypeParamPositionArray, typeParamCount TypeParamPosition) returns OpaqueParamInfo
		return Self{paramNames: paramNames, positions: positions, typeParamCount: typeParamCount}
	end 'create'

	// Does this declaration take ANY bare type-parameter argument? False for the shared "none", which is
	// what a callee with no recorded entry reads back — the "absence is the answer" default
	// `ParamConsumeInfo.isConsumed` and `ParamDefaultInfo.producerFor` both rest on.
	export function declaresATypeParameterParam() returns bool
		return self.typeParamCount > 0
	end 'declaresATypeParameterParam'

	// Is the parameter at call-slot `index` a bare type parameter? A slot past the recorded list reads as a
	// plain one, exactly as `Parser.paramIsTypeParamTyped` reads a short positions array.
	export function namesATypeParameter(index ParamCount) returns bool
		return self.positionAt(index) < self.typeParamCount
	end 'namesATypeParameter'

	// The `uses` position that slot names — asked only past `namesATypeParameter`, which is the gate that
	// makes the answer meaningful.
	export function positionAt(index ParamCount) returns TypeParamPosition
		return try self.positions.get(index) otherwise self.typeParamCount
	end 'positionAt'

	// ⭐⭐ **DO TWO DECLARATIONS OF ONE NAME DESCRIBE THE SAME OPAQUE PARAMETERS?** — the test that keeps an
	// OVERLOAD SET from being checked against a member the call did not resolve to. The sweep keys by bare
	// name (`requireOverloadableName`'s stated boundary), so `Array.contains(element Element)` and
	// `Array.contains(sequence ElementArray)` arrive under one key; answering for the set from whichever
	// member wrote last would build a range guard for the OTHER member's argument.
	//
	// ⛔ **MEASURED, AND IT WAS A LIVE WRONG ANSWER IN THIS RUNG'S OWN FIRST BUILD**: `a.contains(needle)` on
	// an `Array with Byte` — a legal program that compiled and ran — was handed the `Element` member's record,
	// range-checked its ARRAY POINTER against `0 to 255` and died `panic … value outside typealias 'Byte'`.
	// A disagreeing pair is therefore CONTESTED and declined (`ProgramSignatures.opaqueParamsOf`), which costs
	// a MISSED check on an overloaded generic method and can never cost a wrong one.
	//
	// Equality is by VALUE and not by arrival, deliberately: a declaration re-folded from the same source
	// compares equal and stays answerable, so this cannot be defeated by how many times a file is swept.
	export function matches(other OpaqueParamInfo) returns bool
		if self.typeParamCount != other.typeParamCount or self.positions.count() != other.positions.count() or not callAlignedParamNamesAgree(self.paramNames, other: other.paramNames) 'differentShape'
			return false
		end 'differentShape'

		for i in 0 upto self.positions.count() 'eachPosition'
			if self.positionAt(i) != other.positionAt(i) 'movedSlot'
				return false
			end 'movedSlot'
		end 'eachPosition'

		return true
	end 'matches'
end 'OpaqueParamInfo'

export typealias OpaqueParamMap = Map with (ByteArray, OpaqueParamInfo)

// The bare names two declarations disagreed about — see `OpaqueParamInfo.matches`. `opaqueParamsOf` declines
// for every one of them. Empty for every program that does not overload a generic type's method, which is
// every program in the corpus but one.
export typealias ContestedOpaqueParamNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// The answer for every declaration that takes no bare type-parameter argument — which is every function in a
// program that declares no generic type, and most methods of one. Shared for `sharedEmptyTypeParamFeeds`'
// reason: an instance per declaration would be one allocation per function in the program, on the sweep's
// hottest path, for a record that says "nothing to do".
export let sharedNoOpaqueParams = OpaqueParamInfo.create(ByteArrayArray.create(), positions: TypeParamPositionArray.create(), typeParamCount: 0)

// ⭐ **THE NAMES WHOSE DECLARATIONS DISAGREE ABOUT THEIR DEFAULT SHAPE (W74).** The defaults registry is
// keyed by the name the SOURCE wrote and is last-wins, exactly as `funcParamConsumes` is — so a name declared
// twice leaves ONE entry, and the loser's own parse would read the WINNER's shape out of it and find no helper
// at the position its `=` sits at. `Parser.captureParamDefault` asks this set so that earns a positioned
// refusal instead of the drain's internal-consistency panic, which is about a different defect entirely (the
// sweep and the real parse disagreeing about ONE declaration) and must stay loud.
//
// ⚠ **IT USED TO HOLD EVERY MULTIPLY-DECLARED NAME AND NOW HOLDS ONLY THE DISAGREEING ONES.** An OVERLOAD SET
// whose members publish the IDENTICAL shape has nothing for a call to be told apart by and needs telling apart
// by nothing: the fill is the same whichever member wins (`ParamDefaultInfo.matches` states the property in
// full). A name whose declarations do NOT all publish that one shape is still unanswerable and is still
// refused here — including the case that is not an overload set at all, one `function f` written in two files,
// which is E3006 and is reported only once every file has parsed.
typealias DisagreeingParamDefaultNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// How many declarations of one name the sweep has folded — kept ONLY for a name that has more than one, so a
// program in which every function is declared once carries an empty map and pays a single `contains` probe
// that `record` was already making. `1` is therefore the answer for a name with no entry, and it is a real
// answer rather than a fallback: a name the sweep recorded at all was declared at least once.
//
// ⭐ **TWO COUNTS ARE KEPT, AND THE COMPARISON BETWEEN THEM IS THE POINT.** `paramDefaultsCollide` needs to
// know that EVERY declaration of a name published defaults, and a declaration that publishes none reaches
// `recordParamDefaults` not at all — so its absence is invisible to any test written over the defaults
// registry alone. `f(n Integer = 1)` beside `f(s String)` is exactly that program: one entry, two
// declarations, and a short `f()` filled from the entry would append an argument the OTHER member does not
// take, which excludes it on arity. MEASURED against the oracle on the same shape (`g(a Num)` beside
// `g(a Num, b Num = 5)`, called `g(1)`): the bootstrap selects the ONE-parameter member and answers 2, so a
// fill-then-resolve that silently selected the defaulted member would be a wrong answer rather than a
// missing refusal.
typealias FunctionDeclarationCount = int(1 to u32.max)
typealias FunctionDeclarationCountMap = Map with (ByteArray, FunctionDeclarationCount)

// The shape EVERY "which names publish two different values of one swept fact?" set wears —
// `DisagreeingParamDefaultNameSet` above and `DisagreeingThrowsNameSet` below are both this, and it is the
// parameter type of the ONE routine that reads either (`ProgramSignatures.declarationsDisagreeAbout`). Named
// for the question rather than for a registry, because the routine is asked it about more than one.
typealias DivergentDeclarationNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// The answer to "does this callee declare any parameter default?" — a union so "no recorded entry" is an
// ANSWER (every parameter is mandatory), never confused with an empty-but-present one. `CalleeConsume`'s
// twin, one declaration fact over.
export union CalleeParamDefaults
	defaults(info ParamDefaultInfo)
	allRequired
end 'CalleeParamDefaults'

// One function's `throws E` clause, captured by the declaration sweep (P1.4b) — the argument- and
// call-site dual of its return type, from the SAME token walk. `recordScannedSignature` fills one per
// THROWING function so a call site can decide whether it needs `try` (E3055/E3057) and what type an
// `(e)` binding recovers. Travels by NAME and carries no ids, exactly like `ParamConsumeEntry`.
// `takesReceiver` rides for `ParamConsumeEntry`'s reason (W75) — see there.
export type ThrowsClauseEntry
	export let name as ByteArray
	export let clause as ThrowsClause
	export let takesReceiver as bool

	export static function create(name ByteArray, clause ThrowsClause, takesReceiver bool) returns ThrowsClauseEntry
		return Self{name: name, clause: clause, takesReceiver: takesReceiver}
	end 'create'
end 'ThrowsClauseEntry'

export typealias ThrowsClauseEntryArray = Array with ThrowsClauseEntry
export typealias FuncThrowsMap = Map with (ByteArray, ThrowsClause)

// ⭐ **THE NAMES WHOSE THROWING DECLARATIONS NAME TWO DIFFERENT ERROR TYPES (W77).** `funcThrows` is keyed by
// the name the SOURCE wrote and is last-wins, so a name declared twice leaves ONE clause and every call to it
// — including the one that resolves to the OTHER member — would decode its error as that clause's type.
// `ProgramSignatures.throwsClauseCollide` reads this set as one half of its comparison, exactly as
// `paramDefaultsCollide` reads `DisagreeingParamDefaultNameSet` one registry over.
//
// ⚠ **THE COMPARISON IS BETWEEN TWO SPELLED NAMES, AND THAT IS EXACT HERE RATHER THAN AN APPROXIMATION.** The
// sweep is a TOKEN scan that runs before any file is parsed, so no type exists yet to compare — but a type
// NAME denotes at most one declaration whole-program (two `enum Boom`s in two directories are `E3006:
// duplicate definition of 'Boom'`, MEASURED), and the whole throws mechanism downstream is name-keyed on the
// same terms: `Parser.tryErrorEnumType` interns the clause's name and `ProgramSignatures.enumOf` looks the
// layout up by it. A finer comparison here would be finer than what it is compared FOR.
typealias DisagreeingThrowsNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// The answer to "does this callee consume any parameter?" — a union so "no recorded entry" is an ANSWER
// (a borrow-only callee), never confused with an empty-but-present one.
export union CalleeConsume
	consumes(info ParamConsumeInfo)
	borrowsAll
end 'CalleeConsume'

// ⭐ **DOES THE CALLEE TAKE OWNERSHIP OF THE ARGUMENT AT `argIndex`?** The whole of the call site's consume
// question in one place: the recorded bits, read at the position the label→position mapping puts this
// argument at (`argSlotPosition` — the ONE home of that mapping). `borrowsAll` is a callee with no recorded
// entry, which is most of them, and it answers false without touching a position at all.
//
// The two askers are `Parser.transferConsumedArgs`, which needs the position anyway to report a
// double-consume against it, and `ProgramSignatures.recordArgumentCleanup`, which needs only the answer.
export function calleeConsumesArgument(consume CalleeConsume, argIndex ParamCount, label ByteArray) returns bool
	return match consume 'c'
		borrowsAll gives false
		consumes(info) gives info.isConsumed(argSlotPosition(info.paramNames, argIndex: argIndex, label: label))
	end 'c'
end 'calleeConsumesArgument'

// The answer to "does this callee take any parameter by reference?" — a union so "no recorded entry" is an
// ANSWER (every parameter is passed by value), never confused with an empty-but-present one. The consume
// union's shape one fact over, for the same reason it has that shape.
export union CalleeByRefParams
	takesByRef(info ParamReassignInfo)
	allByValue
end 'CalleeByRefParams'

// ⭐ **DOES ANY CALL THIS BODY MAKES NAME SOMETHING THAT TAKES A PARAMETER BY REFERENCE?** Asked ONCE per
// recorded function, after the mark has settled, to publish the per-registration-key answer. It asks
// `DeclNodeSpace.targetsOf` — the very function that built the edges — so the query and the graph cannot
// come to disagree about which declarations a spelling can name. (It used to be a round's edge test, asked
// once per function PER ROUND; see `closeByRefForwarding` for what that cost.)
function forwardsIntoAMarkedNode(forwardCalls ForwardCallArray, nodes DeclNodeSpace, takesByRef BoolArray) returns bool
	for call in forwardCalls 'eachEdge'
		for node in nodes.targetsOf(call) 'eachTargetItCanName'
			let marked = try takesByRef.get(node) otherwise panic("forwardsIntoAMarkedNode: takesByRef.get OOB at {node}, which `targetsOf` minted below the node count the graph was built over")
			if marked 'reachesOne'
				return true
			end 'reachesOne'
		end 'eachTargetItCanName'
	end 'eachEdge'
	return false
end 'forwardsIntoAMarkedNode'

// ⭐ **IS THE ARGUMENT AT `argIndex` PASSED BY REFERENCE?** — the call site's whole by-reference question in
// one place, the exact twin of `calleeConsumesArgument`: the recorded columns, read at the position the
// label→position mapping puts this argument at (`argSlotPosition`, the ONE home of that mapping), combined
// with the program-wide gate by `isByRef` (the ONE combiner). `allByValue` is a callee with no recorded
// entry — every runtime callee and, on a program that reassigns no parameter, every user function — and it
// answers false without slotting anything.
export function calleeTakesArgumentByRef(byRef CalleeByRefParams, argIndex ParamCount, label ByteArray, forwardingIsLive bool) returns bool
	return match byRef 'b'
		allByValue gives false
		takesByRef(info) gives info.isByRef(argSlotPosition(info.paramNames, argIndex: argIndex, label: label), forwardingIsLive: forwardingIsLive)
	end 'b'
end 'calleeTakesArgumentByRef'

// ⭐ **WHICH RUNTIME ENTRY DROPS A MANAGED FIELD OF DECLARED TYPE `fieldType`** — the ONE router shared by
// the parser's value-keyed `decrefCalleeFor` (a top-level owned value's drop) and the struct/union
// destructor cascade (each managed field's drop), so a field moved IN through one classifier is dropped
// through the matching callee and cannot leak (missed drop) or double-free (wrong drop). It generalises the
// union cascade's `StrDecrefName if isString else MmDecrefName` line, which could not reach a nested managed
// struct/union: a `String` drops via `__str_decref`; a struct or boxed union that itself owns managed heap
// drops via ITS `__destruct_<T>` cascade; a scalar-only struct or scalar-only union box takes the trivial
// `__mm_decref` (its box is all it owns).
//
// `typeNames` resolves a `named`/`structRef` field id to a NAME; pass the interner the field type's ids live
// in (the file artifact interner at a construct/extract site, `project.typeNames` at the synthesis site).
// The field is a precondition MANAGED one — every caller asks `structFieldIsManaged`/`payloadClassIsManaged`
// first, so a scalar reaching here is a classification drift and panics rather than mis-routing.
export function managedFieldDropCallee(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, fieldType MaxonType) returns ByteArray
	let tag = maxonTypeTag(fieldType)
	// ⚠ `tagIsByteRecord`: WHICH DROP a field takes is a question about its record, and a Character's record
	// is a String's. Spelled `tagIsText` a Character field falls through to the named/struct arm and drops
	// through the wrong callee.
	if tagIsByteRecord(tag) 'byteRecord'
		return StrDecrefName
	end 'byteRecord'
	// A nested generic-INSTANCE field (`Box with (Box with String)`, P1.6-B2) is an owned base-struct box:
	// it drops through ITS OWN `__destruct_<instance>` cascade when the instance owns managed heap, else the
	// trivial `__mm_decref` — resolved inside `signatures` over its own registry/interner (a gid is not an
	// interner id), exactly as a struct/union field name is resolved through `managedNameDropCallee`.
	if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'instance'
		return signatures.genericInstanceBoxDropCallee(typeNameIdOf(fieldType))
	end 'instance'
	// ⛔ **AN EXISTENTIAL FIELD HAS NO STATIC DROP CALLEE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF IT**
	// (P1.7a-existentials slice 2). Its conformer — and so its destructor — is a run-time fact read out of the
	// witness table, so it is released by the 2-argument `__drop_existential(witness, value)` and never by a
	// name this 1-argument router could hand back. The cascades ask `structFieldIsExistential` /
	// `genericInstanceFieldIsExistential` BEFORE this (`FieldDropKind`), so reaching here means a drop site was
	// added that classifies the field managed and does not ask which KIND of managed it is — which would emit
	// `<callee>(valueHalf)` and dispatch the wrong conformer's destructor.
	if tag == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existential'
		panic("managedFieldDropCallee: a field declared at an interface type has no statically named drop callee — an existential is released through `__drop_existential(witness, value)` off the witness table's `destroyFunc@8`, so its drop site must route on `FieldDropKind` rather than ask this")
	end 'existential'
	if not (tagIsStructRef(tag) or tag == ValueTypeTag.named) 'notManagedRef'
		panic("managedFieldDropCallee: a field of tag '{typeTagName(tag)}' is not a managed heap reference — every caller classifies the field managed first")
	end 'notManagedRef'
	// Resolved through `denotedAggregateName` HERE — the same tier its structural dual
	// `managedFieldCloneStrategy` resolves at, and for the reason that door states. ⚠ It used to be resolved
	// one frame DOWN, at `managedNameDropCallee`'s head, which made the two duals structurally different
	// about one fact: the name-keyed clone helper panicked on an alias spelling its drop twin accepted, and
	// the `isGenericAlias` test below read the RAW name on this side and the RESOLVED one on that. They agree
	// today only because a tuple alias is never also a generic alias — an agreement nothing enforces. A value
	// cloned by one strategy is dropped by the callee the other returns, so the two must resolve at ONE tier.
	let name = signatures.denotedAggregateName(try typeNames.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(fieldType)) otherwise panic("managedFieldDropCallee: a managed field's type id is not in the interner it was resolved against — pass the interner the field type's ids live in"))
	return managedNameDropCallee(signatures, declaredName: name)
end 'managedFieldDropCallee'

// The drop callee for a managed field NAMED `declaredName` — a declared struct or boxed union. Split from
// `managedFieldDropCallee` so a `structRef` (which is always a struct) and a `named` (struct or union) reach
// the same struct/union lookup once the name is in hand.
//
// ⚠ **IT TAKES NO INTERNER, AND MUST NOT.** Every question below is keyed by the NAME, and the two that need
// a layout (`structHasManagedField`, `unionHasManagedPayload`) fetch the index's OWN and read it over the
// index's OWN interner. It USED to thread the caller's `typeNames` through to the union arm, which is how a
// `signatures` layout came to be resolved against `project.typeNames` — see `unionHasManagedPayload`.
//
// ⚠ **THE NAME IS ALREADY DENOTED** — `managedFieldDropCallee` put it through `denotedAggregateName`, exactly
// as `managedFieldCloneStrategy` does for `managedNameCloneStrategy`, so a tuple typealias (which names the
// tuple's own struct and nothing else) is a canonical tuple name by the time it arrives. The two duals
// resolve at ONE tier so a name cannot be a struct on the drop side and an undeclared panic on the clone
// side. **MEASURED, A3e**: `typeIsManaged` must call a tuple-alias field MANAGED (a tuple is a struct box,
// and without that the field is dropped by nobody and the program exits 101 on the leak gate) — widening it
// and leaving the drop router unresolved turned that leak into the `undeclared` PANIC at the bottom of this
// function, on a program the other walk order compiled and ran.
function managedNameDropCallee(signatures ProgramSignatures, declaredName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	// ⭐⭐ **A COMPILER-OWNED AGGREGATE TAKES A COMPILER-OWNED DROP, AND IS ASKED BEFORE THE DECLARED-STRUCT
	// LOOKUP.** Each has a REGISTERED layout — they need a nominal identity — so the generic route below
	// would answer `__destruct_<T>`, a symbol `installStructDestructors` never builds, because it walks
	// `project.structTypes` and a synthesized type is in no file's declarations. A link failure rather than a
	// wrong answer, but the honest fix is to say which drop each type takes:
	//
	//   ⚠ A third, `CharacterSet` ⇒ `__cs_decref`, went at W129 with that runtime. The corpus declares the type
	//   now (W115), so its box is an ordinary declared struct and takes the synthesized cascade below.
	//   • **`__ManagedFile` OWNS AN OS HANDLE, WHICH IS NOT MEMORY — SO ITS DROP MUST CLOSE, NOT JUST FREE**
	//     (R4.2, and this arm IS the RAII destructor). It could not be the cascade, and that was measured
	//     rather than assumed: `_handle` is a scalar `int` field, so `structFieldIsManaged` is false and
	//     `installStructDestructors` skips the type outright. Widening "managed field" to cover it would be a
	//     lie every other reader of that predicate would act on, and `emitNullGuardedDrop`'s shape is wrong for
	//     it anyway — it loads the FIELD and calls `callee(field)`, while a close needs the BOX (it zeroes
	//     `_handle` to stay idempotent). `__mf_destruct(box)` is the refcount-checked teardown that does both.
	//     ⇒ Answering `MmDecrefName` — which is what R4.1 did — frees the eight bytes and leaks the OS handle
	//     for the process lifetime, invisibly to the exit-101 gate, because a handle is not memory. ONE arm
	//     covers both doors: a `var f as __ManagedFile` FIELD through the struct cascade, and a top-level
	//     `let f = __ManagedFile.openRead(…)` through `Parser.decrefCalleeFor`'s `structRef` arm.
	//   • **`__ManagedDirectory` OWNS A SEARCH HANDLE *AND* A SECOND ALLOCATION** (R4.3), so its drop must
	//     close the search and free the find block. Every argument the file bullet makes applies unchanged, and
	//     `MmDecrefName` here would free eight bytes and leak BOTH the 336-byte block and the OS search handle
	//     — the second of which the exit-101 gate cannot see at all.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE NAMES ARE `compilerOwnedAggregateOf`'s, NOT THIS FUNCTION'S** (A4n). They used to be
	// spelled here and nowhere else, and the CLONE twin — which owes the identical question — did not have
	// them: it answered `__clone___ManagedFile`, a symbol nothing builds, and the backend panicked on a
	// compilable program. One list, three doors.
	match compilerOwnedAggregateOf(declaredName) 'compilerOwned'
		managedFile then return ManagedFileDestructName
		managedDirectory then return ManagedDirectoryDestructName
		userDeclared then break 'compilerOwned'
	end 'compilerOwned'

	// ⭐⭐ **AN INSTANCE ALSO ARRIVES AS ITS OWN CANONICAL RENDERING, AND `declaredNameIsManaged` ALREADY SAID
	// SO — THIS IS THE ROUTER THAT HAD TO AGREE (W58).** That door answers `true` for `UpCursor_Integer` by
	// asking `isCanonicalInstanceName` FIRST, precisely because no registry is keyed by a canonical mint; the
	// drop it thereby promises was never routed, so the walk below fell through to the `otherwise panic` on a
	// program the classification had already accepted. MEASURED: a conformer widened into a held existential
	// whose binding is a generic instance took the compile down at
	// `existentialDestroyCallee` → here, on `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s own conformance shape. The answer is the
	// `genericAlias` arm's, on the same gid — an instance box drops the same way whichever spelling named it.
	//
	// ⚠ It reads AHEAD of `declaredFormOf` and still cannot outrank a declaration, because the precedence is
	// in the DOOR: `renderedInstanceOfUnclaimedName` refuses a spelling any declaration claims. Asked the
	// other way round it routed `type Box_String`'s cascade through `Box with String`'s — see there.
	if let rendered = try signatures.renderedInstanceOfUnclaimedName(declaredName) 'aRenderedInstance'
		return signatures.genericInstanceBoxDropCallee(rendered)
	end 'aRenderedInstance'

	let form = try signatures.declaredFormOf(declaredName) otherwise panic("managedNameDropCallee: managed field '{String.from(declaredName)}' is declared by no registry — the caller classified it managed, and every managed non-String form is a declared struct, a boxed union or a generic-alias instance")
	match form 'form'
		structType then return structBoxDropCallee(signatures, structName: declaredName)
		// ⭐ **BY NAME, exactly as the struct arm is** — and through the SAME `unionBoxDropCallee` a boxed
		// union's scope-exit drop takes, so a union FIELD and a union BINDING cannot be dropped through
		// different callees. See `unionHasManagedPayload` for the reachable compiler panic the
		// layout-and-foreign-interner spelling this replaces produced.
		enumType then return signatures.unionBoxDropCallee(declaredName)
		unionType then return signatures.unionBoxDropCallee(declaredName)
		// An INTERFACE NAME reaching a 1-argument drop router is the `managedFieldDropCallee` guard's case one
		// resolution further down: an existential is released through `__drop_existential(witness, value)`,
		// which this cannot express. Both type registries tag an interface annotation `interfaceRef` (the real
		// parse mints it; `normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes` re-tags the sweep's), so a bare `named` interface here
		// means a THIRD producer of slot types exists that neither does.
		interfaceType then panic("managedNameDropCallee: '{String.from(declaredName)}' is an interface, and an existential is released through `__drop_existential(witness, value)` rather than by any name this router can return — a slot reaching here carries a bare `named` that neither type registry re-tagged `interfaceRef`")
		// A ranged alias IS its primitive and a function value is a bare code pointer; neither owns a record,
		// so neither can have been classified managed. `declaredNameIsManaged` answers `false` for both.
		rangedAlias then panic("managedNameDropCallee: '{String.from(declaredName)}' is a ranged typealias — a scalar, which `typeIsManaged` never classifies managed")
		functionAlias then panic("managedNameDropCallee: '{String.from(declaredName)}' is a function typealias — a bare code pointer, which `typeIsManaged` never classifies managed")
		// ⭐ A GENERIC-INSTANCE-alias field (an `Array`, a `Box`) arrives as a `named` — the sweep recorded
		// the layout before the alias interned — and takes the instance box drop callee (`__managed_decref` for
		// an `Array`, `__mm_decref`/`__destruct_<T>` for a struct box), exactly as a re-tagged
		// `genericInstance` field does. The gid is `signatures`' own, needing no interner.
		genericAlias then return signatures.genericInstanceBoxDropCallee(signatures.genericAliasInstance(declaredName))
		// A tuple alias names the tuple's own struct and nothing else, and `denotedAggregateName` has already
		// resolved it to that name upstream — so this arm is reached only if that resolution is narrowed, and
		// a tuple box drops exactly as any other struct box does.
		tupleAlias then return structBoxDropCallee(signatures, structName: signatures.tupleAliasTarget(declaredName))
	end 'form'
end 'managedNameDropCallee'

// Which entry drops an owned box of the declared STRUCT `structName`: its `__destruct_<T>` cascade when it
// owns managed heap, the trivial `__mm_decref` when its box is all it owns. Split out so the struct arm and
// the tuple arm of the cascade above cannot come to answer it differently — a tuple IS a struct box.
function structBoxDropCallee(signatures ProgramSignatures, structName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	if signatures.structHasManagedField(structName) 'managedStruct'
		return destructorSymbol(structName)
	end 'managedStruct'

	return MmDecrefName
end 'structBoxDropCallee'

// ⭐ **HOW A MANAGED FIELD OF DECLARED TYPE `fieldType` IS DEEP-CLONED (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b).** The exact
// STRUCTURAL DUAL of `managedFieldDropCallee`: a value cloned through the strategy this returns is dropped
// through the callee `managedFieldDropCallee` returns for the SAME type, so the cloned set and the dropped set
// agree by construction (a clone leaks if it is not dropped, double-frees if it is dropped twice — one
// classifier decides both). shv2 is move-only, so an independent copy is a per-element DEEP CLONE, never an
// incref.
export union ManagedCloneStrategy
	// A `(box) -> newBox` clone callee invocable by a plain 1-arg call OR through `callIndirect(fn, box)` (a
	// `funcAddr`): a String → `__str_clone`; a struct / boxed union → its `__clone_<T>` (managed OR scalar-only
	// — a fresh box is needed either way, where the drop side takes `__mm_decref` for a scalar-only box); a
	// TRIVIAL-element Array → `__managed_clone` (its COW clone is an independently-droppable copy). This is the ONLY
	// strategy an ARRAY ELEMENT may carry, because an element cloner is invoked through `callIndirect(fn, elem)`.
	direct(callee ByteArray)
	// A MANAGED-element Array FIELD: `__managed_clone_managed(field, funcAddr(elementClone))`, where `elementClone`
	// is the element's own `direct` clone callee. A 2-arg call, so it is reachable only as a struct/union FIELD
	// (the synthesizer emits the funcAddr), never as a top-level array element (which must be `direct`).
	managedArray(elementClone ByteArray)
	// Not deep-cloneable by this rung's mechanism — a non-Array generic instance (`Box with String`, whose
	// per-instance cloner is a later slice) or a managed-element Array whose element is not itself `direct`
	// (an array-of-managed-arrays). The front end rejects such an element with a positioned E2015.
	unsupported
end 'ManagedCloneStrategy'

// The clone strategy for a managed field of declared type `fieldType`. Mirrors `managedFieldDropCallee`'s
// dispatch exactly: `typeNames` resolves a `named`/`structRef` id to a NAME; pass the interner the field
// type's ids live in (the file artifact interner at a call site, `project.typeNames` at the synthesis site).
// The field is a precondition MANAGED one, so a scalar reaching here is a classification drift and panics.
export function managedFieldCloneStrategy(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, fieldType MaxonType) returns ManagedCloneStrategy
	let tag = maxonTypeTag(fieldType)
	// `tagIsByteRecord`, mirroring `managedFieldDropCallee`: a Character clones by the same fused-record
	// deep copy a String does, because it IS one.
	if tagIsByteRecord(tag) 'byteRecord'
		return ManagedCloneStrategy.direct(StrCloneName)
	end 'byteRecord'
	if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'instance'
		return instanceCloneStrategy(signatures, id: typeNameIdOf(fieldType))
	end 'instance'
	// ⛔ **AN EXISTENTIAL IS NOT DEEP-CLONEABLE, AND THE GATE AND THE STRATEGY HAVE TO SAY SO TOGETHER**
	// (P1.7a-existentials slice 2) — this file's standing rule (see `managedNameCloneStrategy`'s
	// compiler-owned arm): a name the gate and the strategy disagreed about would admit a clone nobody can
	// emit. A witness table names a DESTRUCTOR and no cloner, so there is no per-conformer `(box) -> newBox`
	// to `callIndirect`; `typeSupportsDeepClone` refuses an existential field at the same tier (its
	// `tagMayNameAggregate` guard), so the front end reports a positioned E2015 rather than reaching a
	// backend panic.
	if tag == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existential'
		return ManagedCloneStrategy.unsupported
	end 'existential'
	if not (tagIsStructRef(tag) or tag == ValueTypeTag.named) 'notManagedRef'
		panic("managedFieldCloneStrategy: a field of tag '{typeTagName(tag)}' is not a managed heap reference — every caller classifies the field managed first")
	end 'notManagedRef'
	// Resolved through `denotedAggregateName` for `managedFieldDropCallee`'s reason, and it must be the SAME
	// resolution: a value cloned by this strategy is dropped by the callee that one returns, so a name the two
	// resolved differently would clone one type and drop another.
	let name = signatures.denotedAggregateName(try typeNames.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(fieldType)) otherwise panic("managedFieldCloneStrategy: a managed field's type id is not in the interner it was resolved against — pass the interner the field type's ids live in"))
	return managedNameCloneStrategy(signatures, declaredName: name)
end 'managedFieldCloneStrategy'

// The clone strategy for a managed field NAMED `declaredName` — a declared struct or boxed union clones
// through its per-type `__clone_<T>`. A scalar-only box still takes `__clone_<T>` (not the drop side's
// `__mm_decref`): the clone MUST allocate a fresh independent box, which only a synthesized cloner does.
//
// ⚠ **THE NAME IS ALREADY DENOTED**, exactly as `managedNameDropCallee`'s is — the two duals resolve a tuple
// typealias at ONE tier, in the `-Field` function above each of them.
// ⛔⛔ **AND IT MUST ASK THE COMPILER-OWNED QUESTION ITS DROP TWIN ASKS — IT DID NOT, AND THAT WAS A COMPILER
// PANIC ON A COMPILABLE PROGRAM (A4n).** `managedNameDropCallee` opens with the same name tests; this function
// had none, so a `__ManagedFile` / `__ManagedDirectory` field fell to the struct arm — their
// layouts ARE registered, they need a nominal identity — and it handed back `__clone___ManagedFile`, a symbol
// `installStructCloners` never builds, because that pass walks the PROJECT's declarations and a synthesized
// type is in no file's. MEASURED on this branch, before the fix: `type Holder { export var f as
// __ManagedFile }` + `Array with Holder` + `a.clone()` died with
// `panic at X64Backend.maxon:1905: resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function '__clone___ManagedFile'`.
//
// ⇒ Both answer `unsupported`, which is the honest verdict and not a placeholder: an OS HANDLE cannot be
// deep-copied at all — duplicating one would hand two owners a descriptor whose `__mf_destruct` closes it
// once. `typeSupportsDeepClone` refuses the same names at the same tier, so the GATE and the STRATEGY
// agree by construction and the front end reports a positioned E2015 where the backend used to panic — which
// is what that gate's own header says it exists for: *"a name the gate and the strategy disagreed about would
// admit a clone nobody can emit."*
function managedNameCloneStrategy(signatures ProgramSignatures, declaredName ByteArray) returns ManagedCloneStrategy
	match compilerOwnedAggregateOf(declaredName) 'compilerOwned'
		managedFile to managedDirectory then return ManagedCloneStrategy.unsupported
		userDeclared then break 'compilerOwned'
	end 'compilerOwned'

	// The drop twin's canonical-rendering arm, at the same tier and on the same gid — see it for the measured
	// panic. It is spelled here even though no corpus program is known to reach the CLONE side with a rendered
	// instance name, because these two duals resolve at one tier by contract (`managedFieldDropCallee`'s
	// header states the rule and A3e measured what breaking it costs): a value the drop side routes must be a
	// value the clone side can route, and a divergence between them is a panic on one walk order and a
	// compiling program on the other.
	if let rendered = try signatures.renderedInstanceOfUnclaimedName(declaredName) 'aRenderedInstance'
		return instanceCloneStrategy(signatures, id: rendered)
	end 'aRenderedInstance'

	let form = try signatures.declaredFormOf(declaredName) otherwise panic("managedNameCloneStrategy: managed field '{String.from(declaredName)}' is declared by no registry — the caller classified it managed, and every managed non-String form is a declared struct, a boxed union or a generic-alias instance")
	match form 'form'
		// A scalar-only box still takes `__clone_<T>` (not the drop side's `__mm_decref`): the clone MUST
		// allocate a fresh independent box, which only a synthesized cloner does.
		structType then return ManagedCloneStrategy.direct(clonerSymbol(declaredName))
		enumType then return ManagedCloneStrategy.direct(clonerSymbol(declaredName))
		unionType then return ManagedCloneStrategy.direct(clonerSymbol(declaredName))
		// The three arms below mirror `managedNameDropCallee`'s exactly — an interface slot is E3011 today,
		// and a ranged alias and a function value are scalars `typeIsManaged` never calls managed.
		interfaceType then panic("managedNameCloneStrategy: '{String.from(declaredName)}' is an interface, which no slot can be declared with today (E3011) — the rung that lands existential slots owes this arm a clone")
		rangedAlias then panic("managedNameCloneStrategy: '{String.from(declaredName)}' is a ranged typealias — a scalar, which `typeIsManaged` never classifies managed")
		functionAlias then panic("managedNameCloneStrategy: '{String.from(declaredName)}' is a function typealias — a bare code pointer, which `typeIsManaged` never classifies managed")
		genericAlias then return instanceCloneStrategy(signatures, id: signatures.genericAliasInstance(declaredName))
		// The drop twin's tuple arm, one register over: `denotedAggregateName` has already resolved a tuple
		// alias to the tuple's own struct name upstream, and a tuple box clones exactly as any struct box does.
		tupleAlias then return ManagedCloneStrategy.direct(clonerSymbol(signatures.tupleAliasTarget(declaredName)))
	end 'form'
end 'managedNameCloneStrategy'

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH COMPILER-OWNED AGGREGATE `name` IS — the ONE home of a fact three doors need and two of them
// used to spell for themselves.** These types have a REGISTERED layout (they need a nominal identity, so
// `containsStruct` says yes and `declaredFormOf` answers `structType`) but they are declared in no FILE, so
// `installStructDestructors` / `installStructCloners` — which walk the project's own declarations —
// synthesize neither a `__destruct_<T>` nor a `__clone_<T>` for them. Every question of the shape *"what does
// the generic per-type machinery do with this?"* therefore has to answer for them FIRST, before the form.
//
// It is asked by `managedNameDropCallee` (each takes its own compiler-owned entry), by
// `managedNameCloneStrategy` (none is deep-cloneable) and by `ProgramSignatures.typeSupportsDeepClone` (the
// gate in front of that strategy). The drop door had the list; the other two did not, and the missing copy
// was a backend panic.
export enum CompilerOwnedAggregate
	// ⚠ A `characterSet` case stood first and went at W129 with the compiler-owned `__CharacterSet` layout —
	// once `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon` declares the type, its box is an ordinary declared struct.
	managedFile
	managedDirectory
	// An ordinary declaration, whose drop and clone are the synthesized per-type entries.
	userDeclared
end 'CompilerOwnedAggregate'

export function compilerOwnedAggregateOf(name ByteArray) returns CompilerOwnedAggregate
	if name.equals(ManagedFileTypeName) 'managedFile'
		return CompilerOwnedAggregate.managedFile
	end 'managedFile'
	if name.equals(ManagedDirectoryTypeName) 'managedDirectory'
		return CompilerOwnedAggregate.managedDirectory
	end 'managedDirectory'

	return CompilerOwnedAggregate.userDeclared
end 'compilerOwnedAggregateOf'

// The clone strategy for a generic-INSTANCE field — an `Array` (`__managed_clone` for a trivial element,
// `__managed_clone_managed` for a managed one) or, for any other instance (`Box with String`), `unsupported` (its
// per-instance cloner is a later slice). The managed element's own strategy comes back through the METHOD
// `arrayElementCloneStrategy` (which resolves the element in the index's own interner), so this free function
// never has to reach the index's private `typeNames`.
function instanceCloneStrategy(signatures ProgramSignatures, id GenericInstanceId) returns ManagedCloneStrategy
	if not signatures.isArrayInstance(id) 'notArray'
		return ManagedCloneStrategy.unsupported
	end 'notArray'
	if not signatures.containerElementIsManaged(id) 'trivialElement'
		return ManagedCloneStrategy.direct(ManagedCloneName)
	end 'trivialElement'
	// A managed-element array field clones each element through the element's OWN `(box) -> newBox` callee; if
	// that element is not itself `direct` (an array-of-managed-arrays), the whole field is unsupported here.
	match signatures.arrayElementCloneStrategy(id) 'element'
		direct(elementClone) then return ManagedCloneStrategy.managedArray(elementClone)
		managedArray then return ManagedCloneStrategy.unsupported
		unsupported then return ManagedCloneStrategy.unsupported
	end 'element'
end 'instanceCloneStrategy'

// The single `(box) -> newBox` clone callee of a `direct` strategy. Precondition: the caller has classified the
// element deep-cloneable through the FULL-GRAPH `typeSupportsDeepClone(asElement: true)` (via
// `genericInstanceOpaqueArrayElementIsCopyable` or the concrete copy gate `arrayElementSupportsDeepClone`), which
// is `direct` at the TOP LEVEL for every deep-cloneable element — so a `managedArray`/`unsupported` strategy
// reaching here is an invariant break and panics. (The full-graph gate is the RIGHT precondition, not a mere
// top-level `direct` check: a struct/union that is `direct` at the top but owns a non-clonable field must NOT
// reach a stamp — see `genericInstanceOpaqueArrayElementIsCopyable`.)
export function directCloneCalleeOf(strategy ManagedCloneStrategy) returns ByteArray
	match strategy 'strategy'
		direct(callee) then return callee
		managedArray then panic("directCloneCalleeOf: a `managedArray` strategy has no single-function clone callee — the caller must gate on typeSupportsDeepClone(asElement: true)")
		unsupported then panic("directCloneCalleeOf: an `unsupported` strategy has no clone callee — the caller must gate on typeSupportsDeepClone(asElement: true)")
	end 'strategy'
end 'directCloneCalleeOf'

// --- Generic instances (P1.6-A) ---

// The prefix a compiled instance name is minted behind when a declaration already claims the bare one
// (`reservedIfDeclared`). It is the SAME `__` the language reserves — `E2051` refuses to declare a name
// starting with it.
//
// ⚠ **BE PRECISE ABOUT WHAT THAT RESERVATION BUYS, BECAUSE IT IS NOT THE OBVIOUS HALF.** Disjointness
// from the DECLARED names is bought by `reservedIfDeclared`'s loop, which re-probes until the candidate
// is unclaimed and would hold for any prefix at all. What only `__` buys is disjointness from every
// OTHER INSTANCE's compiled name — the half nothing probes — and it buys it because a compiled name
// always BEGINS with its base name and E2051 bars a `__` base: no bare compiled name can start with the
// prefix, so a minted one is never some other instantiation's bare one. Pick a prefix the language does
// NOT reserve and that fails silently: `XBox_String` is a name `XBox with String` compiles to on its
// own, and the pair check would report E3006 against a legal program. (E2051 also bounds the loop at one
// re-mint on anything that compiles, which is a cost property, not a safety one.)
//
// It is a third module-local copy of that one language rule, beside `Parser.ReservedNamePrefix` (which
// FORBIDS a user declaration from taking it) and `MmRuntime.CompilerInternalPrefix` (which RECOGNIZES an
// emitted callee by it), and for the reason Parser's own comment states: the three answer different
// questions at different tiers — forbidding, recognizing, and here MINTING — and a signature index owning
// the parser's reservation, or the reverse, would be the worse coupling. The language rule binds them,
// not a shared symbol. A drift between the three is LOUD rather than silent: this one's value reaches
// every emitted per-type symbol of a contested instance, so changing it alone moves goldens, and
// emptying it hangs the loop.
let ReservedTypeNamePrefix = b"__"

// ⭐⭐ **THE NAME shv2 SYNTHESIZES THE ARRAY RECORD UNDER WHEN NO LISTED MODULE DECLARES ONE — AND SINCE
// ARR5b THAT IS ALL IT IS.** It was the answer to *"which base is an `Array`?"*, read as a fact by 47 sites;
// the answer is now DISCOVERED from the `BuiltinArrayLiteral` conformance
// (`Queries.discoverArrayLiteralConformers` ⇒ `ProgramSignatures.arrayRecordDeclaredName`), which is the
// user's own stated exception to *"nothing array specific in the shv2 compiler"*.
//
// ⚠ **IT WAS CALLED `ArrayBuiltinBaseName` UNTIL ARR6, AND THE NAME IS WHAT KEPT IT BEING READ AS A FACT.**
// A constant spelled like its four genuine siblings (`SetBuiltinBaseName`, `MapBuiltinBaseName`,
// `ListBuiltinBaseName`, `VectorBuiltinBaseName`, each of which really IS the base of a container the
// compiler owns end to end) invites the next reader to compare against it, which is exactly what the last
// three readers did. Its role is a SEED and its name now says so, so a forty-eighth reader has to notice.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A SEED, NOT A FALLBACK CONSTANT, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THAT IT DESCRIBES A REAL COMPILE.**
// shv2 minted this record with no declaration behind it for the whole of P1.7, before `stdlib/Array.maxon`
// was listed; a corpus that declares no conformer is that compiler, and it still has to file the values it
// mints under some base. `noteArrayLiteralConformer` REPLACES the seed the moment a listed module declares
// one, which every real compile does.
//
// ⚠⚠ **SINCE ARR6 THE SEED BELOW IS ITS ONLY READER, ANYWHERE.** The two refusal sentences that quoted it
// were retired the way every other read was — by ASKING. `twoArrayDeclarationsRefusal` takes the contested
// bare name from `arrayRecordDeclaredName`; the array roster refusal takes the RECEIVER's own declaration
// from its instance, which is strictly better than either constant, because that surface serves every
// conformer the program declares and a `Bag` receiver was being told about an `Array`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND THE LAST THREE — THE CONFORMANCE READERS — WERE A LIVE WRONG ANSWER, MEASURED (ARR6).** They
// compared against this constant while their callers all arrived carrying `conformerNameOfType`'s DISCOVERED
// answer, so they agreed with the program only because `stdlib/Array.maxon` declares the record as `Array`.
// The previous reader called that *"unreachable today and untestable without editing `stdlib/Array.maxon`"*;
// it is testable by respelling THIS CONSTANT, which is the same divergence from the other side and needs no
// corpus edit at all. **MEASURED with it respelled: 5453/4 against a 5457/0 base** — three
// `array-conditional-conformance-withheld` cases and `array-contains/bytearray-array-contains`, and one of
// the three is a conformance GRANTED where the corpus pins a refusal (`intrinsicConformanceProtocolNames`
// answering the empty list makes the `where` clause vacuous, so `hash()` is served for every element). The
// cure is the one ARRO used everywhere else — the discovered fact threaded in as a parameter:
// `IrInterface.isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance` now takes a `DiscoveredConformer`, derived once by
// `discoveredConformerOf`, and `installBuiltinConformanceRuntime` takes the record's name outright,
// because it JOINS a symbol rather than comparing one.
//
// ⛔ **THE SEED ITSELF IS NOT RETIRABLE, AND THAT IS A CONCLUSION RATHER THAN A GAP (ARRO, RE-MEASURED AT
// ARR6).** With no listed module declaring the record, `Array` is a SYNTHESIZED builtin container in exactly
// the sense `SetBuiltinBaseName`, `ListBuiltinBaseName`, `MapBuiltinBaseName` and `VectorBuiltinBaseName`
// are — the compiler owns the record, so the compiler owns the name, and there is nothing left to discover
// it FROM. The user's directive puts `Array` outside the compiler *because it is a stdlib component*; in the
// compile this line describes it is not one. Substituting a reserved spelling (`__ManagedMemory`) was
// considered and rejected: it renames every emitted per-type symbol of a compile no corpus program performs,
// so it would ship an untested behaviour change to buy the appearance of zero.
//
// ⚠⚠ **AND ITS VALUE IS UNOBSERVABLE IN EVERY COMPILE THE CORPUS PERFORMS — MEASURED, NOT ARGUED (ARR6).**
// With the three readers retired, the suite was run with this line respelled `b"ArrayProbe"`: **5457 passed,
// 0 failed**, byte-identical verdicts to the tree with `b"Array"`. That is the seed being REPLACED before
// anything reads it, on every one of 5,457 compiles, and it follows structurally:
// `StdlibLoader.whitelistedStdlibModules` lists `Array.maxon` unconditionally and `listWhitelistedModule`
// hard-errors if the file is absent, so the stdlib half of `discoverArrayLiteralConformers` always finds a
// declaration. ⇒ **The remaining read is dead in every compile that has a corpus, and it may not therefore be
// replaced by a discovered-or-panic**: the compile it describes is the one with no corpus, which is the only
// compile in which it is ever read at all. It is an INITIALIZER for a state that exists, not a fallback for a
// state that does not.
export let SynthesizedArrayRecordSeed = b"Array"

// The name `stdlib/Array.maxon` gives the `Array`'s sole type parameter — `export type Array uses Element`.
// shv2 declares no such type (the container is compiler-owned and synthesized, which is why
// `SynthesizedArrayRecordSeed` exists at all), so a diagnostic that has to SPELL the parameter — the
// `where Element is Equatable` clause `contains` is withheld under — has nowhere to read it from and writes
// it here, once.
export let ArrayElementTypeParamName = b"Element"

// The position of that parameter in the (one-entry) `uses` list — what a `WhereConstraint` about the element
// indexes, and the same 0 `containerElementType` reads. Named where the parameter it points at is named,
// rather than as a bare literal at the one refusal that builds a constraint.
export let ArrayElementTypeParamIndex = 0 as FieldIndex

// The element type name of a `b"…"` byte-string literal's `Array with Byte` instance — the same `Byte`
// spelling a user's `typealias Byte = int(0 to u8.max)` uses, so a byte-string's gid matches a
// `ByteArray`-typed value's gid (type identity). A bare `named` reference: the element's own resolution
// is never needed (a byte-string stamps `element_size = 1` directly and never reads `arrayElementSize`).
let ByteElementTypeName = b"Byte"

// The single type parameter `Array` declares (`Array with Element`) — the arity `checkGenericInstance`
// validates a `Array with …` against.
export let ArrayTypeParamCount = 1 as FieldIndex

// The name of the builtin `Set` generic type (`Set with Element`, P1.7b slice A) — the hash-table twin of
// `SynthesizedArrayRecordSeed`. `Set` has no `type Set uses Element` declaration (shv2 reads no stdlib; its
// runtime is synthesized like `Array`'s), so every `genericInstance*` query that would read a base
// `StructLayout` (`baseLayoutOf`, box size, per-instance drop) special-cases a `Set` instance exactly as it
// does an `Array` one — its box was a fixed fused hash-table record and it dropped through `__set_decref`.
// ⚠ **THAT REGIME IS DELETED (W105).** `stdlib/Set.maxon` declares the type, so a `Set with T` is an
// ordinary declared generic instance and this constant is now only a SPELLING: the base name a desugared
// `Set.create` / `Set.insert` / `Set.contains` call is composed from, and the one a `CharSet from […]`
// member set is interned at (`setInstanceForCharacter`).
export let SetBuiltinBaseName = b"Set"

// The name of the builtin `Map` generic type (`Map with (Key, Value)`) — the two-parameter twin of
// `SetBuiltinBaseName`. Like `Set`, no file declares `type Map uses Key, Value`: shv2 reads no stdlib and
// synthesizes the whole surface, so every `genericInstance*` query that would read a base `StructLayout`
// special-cases a `Map` instance exactly as it does an `Array` or a `Set` one — its box is the fixed
// fused record with a value column and it dropped through `__map_decref`. ⚠ **THAT REGIME IS DELETED
// (W105)** for the reason `SetBuiltinBaseName`'s note gives: `stdlib/Map.maxon` declares the type, so this is
// now only the SPELLING a desugared `Map.create` / `Map.upsert` call and a `[k: v]` literal's instance are
// composed from.
// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A GENERIC INSTANCE REDUCES TO FOR A CONFORMANCE QUESTION (W41)** — the answer
// `ProgramSignatures.instanceConformerReduction` gives its two doors, as a CASE rather than a name.
//
// `unreduced` is a real state of the world and not a sentinel: it means "no conformer name claims this
// instance", which is precisely how a non-conforming `Array with <element>` goes on being refused by E3017
// with no second predicate to keep in step. What each door then ANSWERS with differs — the mangled instance
// name where there is no source spelling, the typealias's own name where there is — and folding those two
// into one `String` return is what put a mangled `Array_Opaque` into a diagnostic that pins `OpaqueArr`.
export union GenericInstanceConformer
	// A conforming `Array with E`: every one of them reduces to the ONE `Array` conformer, whose impls walk
	// raw bytes and are element-independent.
	arrayBuiltin
	// A DECLARED generic (`type Wrapper uses T implements Named`): its conformance is a property of the
	// declaration and its impls are one shared body, so the base name is the conformer for every instance.
	declaredBase(name ByteArray)
	// Neither — a non-conforming array, or an instance of a BUILTIN base that declares nothing.
	unreduced
end 'GenericInstanceConformer'

export let MapBuiltinBaseName = b"Map"

// ⭐ **WHICH TYPE ARGUMENT IS THE KEY — A FACT ABOUT EVERY HASH TABLE, NOT ABOUT `Map`.** A `Set with Key`
// and a `Map with (Key, Value)` both put the key first, and three doors depend on it being the same 0:
// `Parser.gateAndEmitContainerCreate`'s two column gates (the `Set` arm's and, through
// `Parser.parseMapColumnValue`'s key half, `ProgramSignatures.setKeyType` and `mapKeyType`. So it is ONE
// exported constant those read — it was briefly two, a `Map`-named one here and a file-private `Set`-named
// copy in `LowerMaxonToStd`, which made the shared invariant something this header ASSERTED while the code
// that depended on it read a different name.
//
// The VALUE position stays `Map`-only, because only a `Map` has a second column.
export let HashTableKeyArgPosition = 0 as TypeParamPosition
export let MapValueArgPosition = 1 as TypeParamPosition

// The name of the builtin `List` generic type (`List with Element`) — the linked-list twin of
// `SynthesizedArrayRecordSeed`. Like `Array`, `Set` and `Map`, no file declares `type List uses Element` for
// shv2 to read: `stdlib/List.maxon` writes one, and `ListRuntime.maxon`'s header carries the MEASUREMENT that
// makes it uncompilable here (a generic type holding a compiler-owned container over its own type parameter
// erases the element's managed-ness and access-violates at teardown). So every `genericInstance*` query that
// would read a base `StructLayout` special-cases a `List` instance exactly as it does an `Array` one — its
// box is the fixed `ListRecordBytes` chain header and it drops through `__list_decref`.
export let ListBuiltinBaseName = b"List"

// The single type parameter `List` declares (`List with Element`) — the arity `checkGenericInstance`
// validates a `List with …` against, the `Array`/`Set` twin.
export let ListTypeParamCount = 1 as FieldIndex

// ⭐⭐ **`__ManagedList with Element` — THE `List`'s OWN CHAIN, UNDER THE NAME THAT EXPOSES ITS NODES**
// (spec-port `ownership`; `/specs/managed-list.md`, `/specs/internal-types.md`). `/specs/list.md` writes
// `List` as a USER type whose sole field is `var chain as __ManagedList with Element`, so the two are one
// structure in the canonical language too — and in shv2 they are literally one: the same
// `ListRecordBytes` header, the same 24-byte nodes, the same `__list_*` entries, the same `__list_decref`.
// This is `Vector`'s relationship to `Array` (see `VectorBuiltinBaseName`) at a second pair: two TYPES whose
// layouts coincide, kept as separate base names because what may be DONE to them differs — a `List` is
// indexed and iterated, a `__ManagedList` hands out NODE HANDLES.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS `__`-PREFIXED IN THE CANONICAL LANGUAGE, WHICH IS WHY THE NAME IS SPELLED WITH THE PREFIX
// HERE AND NOT RESERVED-RENAMED.** `/specs/internal-types.md` lists it among the types a program may NAME
// but whose representation is the compiler's; `stdlib/List.maxon` names it in its own field declaration.
export let ManagedListBuiltinBaseName = b"__ManagedList"

// The single type parameter `__ManagedList` declares — the `List` twin, and the same one-word element slot.
export let ManagedListTypeParamCount = 1 as FieldIndex

// ⭐⭐ **`__ManagedListNode with Element` — A HANDLE ON ONE NODE, AND THE ONE TYPE IN shv2 NO SOURCE CAN
// CONSTRUCT.** Only an insertion mints a value of it (`__ManagedList.insertFirst`/`insertLast`).
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS AN OWNED HANDLE ON A REFCOUNTED NODE — A SECOND OWNER OF THE NODE ITSELF.** ⚖ USER RULING
// 2026-08-17: refcount the nodes (v1's model, and `/specs/managed-list.md`'s own words — *"__ManagedList
// owns its nodes via reference counting … handles with refcount-based lifetime"*). A handle IS the node
// pointer; it is enrolled as a sole-owned temp and released through `__list_node_decref`, which frees the
// node only when the LAST owner goes — which may be a handle long after the chain is gone.
//
// ⛔⛔ **TWO EARLIER RULINGS ARE RECORDED HERE BECAUSE BOTH WERE MEASURED WRONG AND THE SECOND FAILURE IS
// THE INSTRUCTIVE ONE.** This first said a handle was *"emitted as a BORROW, never as an owned temp, and no
// binding of it is ever dropped"*, with `genericInstanceBoxDropCallee` panicking rather than answering —
// which `stdlib/List.maxon` cannot live with, since it needs a handle in a RETURN (`walkTo`, `:60-70`), a
// FIELD (`ListIterator.node`, `:161`, reassigned at `:173`) and a PARAMETER (`ListIterator.create`,
// `:163`). It then said a handle was an owned box RETAINING ITS LIST — and that was worse than incomplete,
// it was a WRONG ANSWER: `clear` frees nodes, so retaining the chain kept nothing alive, and a handle in a
// struct field read `__mm_free`'s `0x3F` poison at exit 0 with no diagnostic anywhere.
//
// ⭐ **THE LESSON THE SECOND FAILURE CARRIES: A PRECEDENT IS A WHOLE MECHANISM, NOT ITS MOST QUOTABLE
// HALF.** That box was argued from `__ManagedMemoryCursor`, which survives the same escapes — but a cursor
// pays with the retain AND a LIVE RE-READ of its source on every use. Only the retain was ported, and a
// node has nothing live to re-read.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS PARAMETERIZED BY THE ELEMENT, and that is what makes `node.value()` typed at all.** A node's
// value slot is one machine word whatever the element is (see the node-format block below), so the WIDTH
// needs no parameter — but the TYPE a `value()` reads back does, and there is nowhere else to carry it: a
// value's type in shv2 is a `(tag, nameId)` pair, so an un-parameterized node would have to look its
// element up in a side table keyed by `ValueId`, which no phi or merge would survive.
export let ManagedListNodeBuiltinBaseName = b"__ManagedListNode"

// The single type parameter `__ManagedListNode` carries. Same arity as every one-element container, so
// every one-argument door (`containerElementType`, `containerElementIsManaged`, `elementAccessorResultType`)
// serves a node handle unchanged — which is exactly how `value()` is emitted through the SAME element
// accessor a `get` is.
export let ManagedListNodeTypeParamCount = 1 as FieldIndex

// ⭐⭐ **`__ManagedMemoryCursor with Element` — A NAVIGABLE POSITION INSIDE A BUFFER, AND THE SECOND TYPE NO
// SOURCE CAN CONSTRUCT.** Only `__ManagedMemory.createCursor()` mints one. `stdlib/Array.maxon:490-531`'s
// `ArrayIterator` is its whole consumer: it stores one in a field and forwards six members, which is why the
// type must be NAMEABLE in an annotation (`typealias RawCursor = __ManagedMemoryCursor with Element`) where a
// node handle need not be.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS `__`-PREFIXED IN THE CANONICAL LANGUAGE, so the name is spelled with the prefix here and not
// reserved-renamed** — the `__ManagedList` rule, for that entry's reason: `/specs/internal-types.md` lists it
// among the types a program may NAME while their representation stays the compiler's.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS PARAMETERIZED BY THE ELEMENT FOR `ManagedListNodeBuiltinBaseName`'s REASON, VERBATIM.** A
// cursor's `current()`/`peek()` read one machine word whatever the element is, so the WIDTH needs no
// parameter — but the TYPE they hand back does, and a value's type in shv2 is a `(tag, nameId)` pair with
// nowhere else to carry it.
//
// ⭐ **UNLIKE A NODE HANDLE, IT IS OWNED AND IT IS DROPPED.** A node is a borrow into a chain the list frees;
// a cursor is its own 16-byte `__mm_alloc` box holding a RETAINED source, so it has a real box size and a
// real drop callee — see `ManagedMemoryRuntime.ManagedCursorCreateName` for why the retain exists and why the record
// holds the source rather than a snapshot of its fields.
export let ManagedMemoryCursorBuiltinBaseName = b"__ManagedMemoryCursor"

// The single type parameter a cursor carries — the same one-element arity every other one-argument door
// (`containerElementType`, `containerElementIsManaged`, `elementAccessorResultType`) already serves, which is
// how `current()` is emitted through the SAME element accessor an `Array`'s `get` is.
export let ManagedMemoryCursorTypeParamCount = 1 as FieldIndex

// ⭐⭐ **THE FIFTH COMPILER-OWNED CONTAINER: `Vector with <N> <Element>`, A FIXED-SIZE ARRAY.**
// `stdlib/Vector.maxon` declares one, and shv2 synthesizes its own instead.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE THREE-CLAUSE ARGUMENT THAT USED TO STAND HERE IS RETIRED — ALL THREE CLAUSES WERE RE-MEASURED
// BY `W86` (2026-08-13) AND NOT ONE OF THEM IS THE REASON.** It said the file could not be READ, because
// (a) `var managed as __ManagedMemory with Element` is `E2055` against shv2's arity-ZERO
// `__ManagedMemory`; (b) `create()`'s `return Self{}` is `E3086`; and (c) a generic type holding a
// compiler-owned container over its own type parameter erases the element's managed-ness and
// access-violates at teardown. MEASURED on this tree:
//   • **(a) and (b) DO NOT FIRE.** `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/Vector.maxon` exits 1 with
//     **`E3001: No 'main' function found` and nothing else**, and the injection control FIRES
//     (`let bogus = NoSuchType.definitelyUndefined(1)` in `count()`'s body, on a copy outside `stdlib/`,
//     answers `E3001` **+ `E3004`**), so its bodies really are analyzed. (a) died when `Array.maxon` was
//     listed and `__ManagedMemory` became a one-parameter fold (ARR5b); (b) died with the `S2f`/`S2i`
//     empty-container family. **The file the note said shv2 CANNOT READ type-checks clean.**
//   • **(c) IS UNREACHABLE, so it is a prediction and not a measurement.** Two refusals stand in front of
//     it — `Parser.requireVectorElementType`'s opaque arm (board row `S2u`) and its managed arm — and with
//     the module listed neither is what the compile dies of. Its REASON is sound and its refusal is kept:
//     an inline buffer that publishes every slot at `create()` while `element_destroy@40` stays 0 really
//     would leak a `String` element. It is simply not why `Vector` is synthesized.
//
// ⛔⛔⛔ **THE REAL REASON IS ONE FACT, AND IT IS ABOUT THE SIZE RATHER THAN ABOUT THE ELEMENT: A CORPUS
// `Vector` METHOD'S `self` CAN NEVER MATCH ITS RECEIVER.** `Self` inside `type Vector uses Element` is the
// declaration's own instance over its own parameters (`ownRecordSelfInstance`) — a vector with **NO
// count** — while every value a program holds states one, because the count is part of the intern KEY
// (`fixedSizes`, below). MEASURED with `stdlib/Vector.maxon` listed and a member struck from
// `Parser.vectorSurfaceMemberNames`, on `--filter=vector` (47 cases, base 47/0):
// **8 passed, 39 failed, every one of them `E3005 argument type mismatch for 'self': expected 'Vector_Int',
// got 'Vec3'`.** The same wall stands in front of the traversal: letting `for … in` take the corpus
// `createIterator()` — which needs no roster strike at all, only the declaration being visible — is
// `expected 'Vector_Int', got 'Vec4'`.
//
// ⇒ **SO THE RESIDUE IS ALL SIX MEMBERS, FOR ONE CAUSE AND NOT SIX** (`ARR4`'s framing: a roster is the
// residue of what could not retire). `count` `get` `set` `createIterator` are blocked by the `self` wall
// above. The two STATICS take `Self` in RETURN position instead, so the wall reaches them from the other
// side, and they are not blocked alike — a distinction worth keeping, because it is what a later rung
// would size the work from (corrected in review, W86; this paragraph had charged both with `create`'s):
//   • **`create()` carries a SECOND, independent fact.** `return Self{}` means *"N zero-initialized
//     elements"* and the body has no way to name N — there is no size type parameter to name it with —
//     so even a matching `Self` would give a `Vec3.create()` a 0-slot vector.
//   • **`init(managed)` carries no such fact.** It ADOPTS a buffer that already states N in its own
//     `length@8`, so its body never needs to name the count; what it needs is for the fused-wrapper
//     identity arm to RETYPE its countless `Self{managed: managed}` to the receiver's sized instance,
//     which is the `firstFieldIsItsOwnBuffer` mechanism listed below and not this bullet's fact.
//
// ⭐ **TWO CANDIDATE CURES, NAMED SO THE NEXT RUNG DOES NOT RE-DERIVE THEM, NEITHER BUILT HERE.**
//   1. **Size-polymorphic `Self`** — let a sized instance satisfy the declaration's countless `Self` at a
//      parameter position. One body per element serves every size; `count()` would stop folding to a
//      constant and become a `length@8` load; `error.wrong-size-vector-argument` must go on failing, so the
//      rule has to be confined to a declaration's own `Self` and not become size erasure at large.
//   2. **Monomorphize the corpus body over (element, SIZE)**, which is v1's answer: `/specs/vector.md`'s
//      `RequiredIR` shows `x64.call Vector.create` taking a `__layout_Vector_N3_Int` descriptor, i.e. the
//      count travels in the LAYOUT DESCRIPTOR. shv2's descriptors carry a stride and no count, which is the
//      same gap board row `S2u` names, and it costs one copy of every member per size.
//
// ⚠ **AND LISTING THE MODULE COSTS THREE MECHANISMS BEFORE ANY OF THAT.** `W86` named all three and
// withdrew all three; **`W112` LANDED THE FIRST AND WALKED THE OTHER TWO TO A FIXED POINT**, so what
// follows is a MEASURED ORDER rather than a list.
//
//   1. **`ProgramSignatures.noteArrayLiteralConformer` — BUILT AND LANDED (`W112`), and not on the stdlib
//      route's account.** A second STDLIB conformer steals `corpusArrayRecordName` and every program in the
//      tree fails to compile; `W86` judged the fix untestable while the module stays unlisted and was right
//      about that route and wrong about the other. **The USER route needs no whitelist line at all** — a
//      program declaring `type Vector uses Element implements BuiltinArrayLiteral` grew a fixed-size vector
//      through the array surface on `main`, silently. See that function's header for both measurements.
//   2. **`firstFieldIsItsOwnBuffer` + `Parser.emitFusedWrapperLiteral` — the identity arm must admit a
//      `Vector` base and RETYPE its result.** MEASURED past (1) with the module listed:
//      **`E3005 stdlib/Vector.maxon:14:3: Cannot return 'Vector.ElementMemory' from function declared to
//      return 'Vector_T…'`** — `init`'s `Self{managed: managed}`, exactly as `W86` predicted. ⚠ **And the
//      skip in (1) is WHY the buffer's type reads as the raw inner alias**: the in-body `__ManagedMemory`
//      fold learns its target from the conformer set (`array-declared-record`'s
//      `a-generic-conformer-of-another-name-is-the-record-too`), and a `Vector` is no longer in it. So (2)
//      owes the fold target too, not only the retype — a fact (1) created and nobody would predict.
//   3. **`requireVectorElementType`** — an instance stating NO count publishes no slots, so the element rule
//      is vacuous and `create()`'s `Self{}` is `E2015` without it. NOT reached by `W112`'s walk: the wall
//      below stops the compile first.
//
// ⛔⛔ **AND PAST ALL THREE THE `self` WALL IS EXACTLY WHERE `W86` LEFT IT — RE-MEASURED AT THE DOOR BY
// `W112`, WITH NO ROSTER STRIKE AT ALL.** With (1) landed and (2) probed in, `--filter=vector` is
// **51 passed, 4 failed** against a base of 55/0, and all four are this wall:
// `argument type mismatch for 'self': expected 'Vector_Int', got 'Vec3'` /`'Vec4'`, and
// `expected 'Vector_int', got 'Vector_N4_int'` / `expected 'Vector_bool', got 'Vector_N9_bool'` — the
// mangled receiver spelling the size the declaration's own `Self` does not. ⚠ **A ROSTER STRIKE IS NOT THE
// TRIGGER, ONLY AN AMPLIFIER**: `for … in` (through the corpus `createIterator`), `Vector from […]` (through
// the corpus `init`) and a VARIABLE-index `set` (through the throwing accessor) all reach the declaration on
// VISIBILITY alone. The two candidate cures above are unchanged and neither is built.
//
// ⚠⚠ **AND A FOURTH THING IT OWES IS NOT A MECHANISM AT ALL — IT IS A DECISION ABOUT THE `for … in` FORM,
// WHICH FLIPS ON VISIBILITY ALONE (added in review, W86).** `Parser.parseForIn`'s own header states, as a
// present fact, that a counter is *"every source that supplies no cursor of its own: a `Vector`, whose
// record IS an array's and which is compiler-served with no `createIterator`"*, and that `stdlib/Array.maxon`
// being LISTED is exactly why an array is no longer one. So listing this module needs no roster strike and
// no `self` match to change what a loop emits: `materializeIterableIterationSource` would rewrite a vector
// source to the `ArrayIterator` the corpus `createIterator()` returns, trading a counted read against a
// size that is a CONSTANT for a per-loop iterator struct and a call per step.
//
// ⇒ **Decide it deliberately, in both directions, and fix that header in the same edit** — it is a third
// copy of this one fact, and the day the module is listed it goes from true to false with nothing to catch
// it. The trade may well be worth taking for uniformity with `Array`; what must not happen is its arriving
// as a side effect of a whitelist line.
//
// ⚠ **THE `listWhitelistedModule` LINE IS NOT THE DELIVERABLE AND WAS WITHDRAWN TWICE, `W63`'s `Set` RULE
// APPLIED BOTH TIMES.** With the entry live and nothing retired the differing-declarations control comes
// back INERT: `stdlib/Vector.maxon`'s `count()` rewritten to `return managed.length() + 100` leaves a
// `Vec3.create()`+`set`+`get`+`count()` program answering **8**, byte-for-byte identical at **11,795
// bytes**; an UNDEFINED CALL in that same body compiles clean and still answers 8. The entry is byte-neutral
// (`return 7` identical at 5,567 bytes) and buys nothing.
//
// ⭐ **`W112` RE-RAN THAT CONTROL WITH MECHANISMS (1) AND (2) ABOVE IN PLACE — i.e. with the corpus `init`
// GENUINELY REACHED — and it is still INERT for the members that matter.** Same sabotage, same program
// answering **8** at an identical **5,650 code bytes**, and the undefined-call injection still compiles
// clean. So the entry does not become worth taking one door earlier: everything it makes REACHABLE is red on
// the `self` wall, and everything that still PASSES is passing from the synthesized surface.
//
// ⭐ **ITS RUNTIME REPRESENTATION IS AN `Array`'s, EXACTLY — the 48-byte `ManagedRecordBytes` fused record, the
// `__managed_*` helpers, `__managed_decref`.** A fixed-size buffer and a growable one differ in what may be DONE to
// them, not in how the bytes sit, so a second record would have been the same six slots under a second
// name: `genericInstanceBoxSize` and `genericInstanceBoxDropCallee` answer a Vector with the `Array` row.
//
// ⛔⛔ **HOW `Vector.create()` IS EMITTED IS `Parser.emitVectorCreateOp`'s TO STATE, AND THIS HEADER STATED
// IT TOO — WRONGLY (found in review, W86).** It said *"one `__managed_mem_create(N, elementSize)` — the
// entry that PUBLISHES `N` zero-initialized elements"*, which is the shape that emitter's own ⚠ paragraph
// records TRYING and MEASURING as **E3057 throwing function requires try: 'create'** on every case in
// `/specs/vector.md`, because that entry THROWS. What is emitted is `__managed_create` then
// `__managed_resize(N)`, which throws nothing. Two headers, one fact, opposite answers — and the copy a
// reader lands on first was the one naming the route already known not to work. The emitter is the one
// place that says it now.
//
// ⭐⭐ **WHAT IS `Vector`'s ALONE IS THE SIZE, AND IT LIVES IN THE INSTANCE'S IDENTITY** —
// `GenericInstanceRegistry.fixedSizes`, part of the intern KEY, so `Vector with 3 Int` and
// `Vector with 4 Int` are different gids by construction (`/specs/vector.md`: *"The size is part of the
// type"*). ⚠ **AND IT IS A COLUMN RATHER THAN A `MaxonType` ARM, WHICH IS THE DEPARTURE FROM v1.** v1
// prepends a `MaxonType.constInt(size)` to the argument list (`TypeResolution.buildSizedGenericInstanceType`)
// — a VALUE wearing a type's clothes. shv2's `MaxonType` is read through a DENSE PER-VALUE COLUMN
// (`ValueTypeTag` + `TypeNameId`, see MaxonDialect), so a `constInt` arm would owe an answer to every rule
// that reads a tag — `typeClassOf`, `tagIsIntegral`, `valueTagToStdType`, `notOperatorFor` — about "the type
// that is the number 3", and there is no honest one. A number is not a type; it is a second coordinate of
// the instance, and it is stored as one.
export let VectorBuiltinBaseName = b"Vector"

// The single TYPE parameter `Vector` declares. Its SIZE is not a type argument (see above), so a
// `Vector with 3 Int` has arity ONE here exactly as `Array with Int` does, and every one-argument container
// door (`containerElementType`, `arrayElementSize`, `containerElementIsManaged`) serves it unchanged.
export let VectorTypeParamCount = 1 as FieldIndex

// ⭐⭐ **`MapError` — WHAT A MISSING-KEY `Map.get` THROWS, AND IT WEARS THE BARE NAME FOR `ArrayError`'S
// EXACT REASON.** `stdlib/Map.maxon:6-9` declares it and that module is listed, so the DECLARATION is what a
// program resolves — what the name is doing here is being RESERVED program-wide
// (`TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName`), so no other file may take it.
//
// ⚠ It is NOT `__MapError`, because a user function that PROPAGATES a `map.get` failure writes
// `throws MapError` in its own source; under a reserved spelling every one of those would meet
// `E3059: try propagates '__MapError' but enclosing function throws 'MapError'` — one error type under two
// spellings, the very confusion the prefix exists to prevent. ⇒ It is RESERVED PROGRAM-WIDE instead
// (`TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName`), exactly as `ArrayError` is.
export let MapErrorTypeName = b"MapError"

// ⚠ **`MapErrorKeyNotFoundCaseName` / `MapErrorKeyAlreadyExistsCaseName` STOOD HERE AND WENT WITH THE
// ORDINAL CLAIM THAT READ THEM (W105).** `MapRuntime.verifyMapErrorOrdinals` held `stdlib/Map.maxon:7-8`'s
// declaration ORDER against the literal error flags `__map_get` and `__map_insert` wrote into the
// error-flag register. Both graphs are deleted, so nothing transcribes an ordinal and the claim would be a
// dead one — the state `StringError`'s reached at W49 wave 3. A corpus `throw MapError.keyNotFound` resolves
// its ordinal from the declaration itself; there is no second half left to drift from.

// ⭐⭐ **`IterationError` — WHAT A CURSOR'S `advance()` THROWS (conditional extensions).** Unlike the four
// error enums above it, the COMPILER never throws this one: it is thrown and caught entirely by USER code
// (`throw IterationError.exhausted` in an `advance()` body, absorbed by `for … in`'s step block). What the
// compiler owes is that the NAME resolves.
//
// ⭐ **AND IT NOW RESOLVES TO THE CORPUS'S OWN DECLARATION (W3).** `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:28` declares it
// and the stdlib loader LISTS that module, so — unlike `ArrayError`/`StringError`/`MapError`, whose modules
// are unlistable because `Array`/`String`/`Map` are synthesized — this one has no seed in
// `Project.createEnumRegistry` and no transcription anywhere in the compiler. The paragraph that stood here
// said the module could not be admitted because its `extension Iterable` would "land a second definition of
// `map` on every conformer beside the compiler-owned `Array.map`". MEASURED at W3: it does not. An
// interface extension is expanded onto the types whose `implements` clause NAMES that interface
// (`Parser.parseExtensionDeclaration` over `conformersOf`), and `Array`/`Set`/`Map` are synthesized with no
// such clause — so `Iterable.map` reaches exactly the types that declare `implements Iterable`, of which
// the whole corpus and suite have none that also declare their own `map`.
//
// It is BARE rather than `__`-prefixed for `MapError`'s stated reason: a user `advance()` writes
// `throws IterationError` in its own source, so a reserved spelling would make one error type meet itself
// under two names. It stays on `TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName` for a reason a listed module does
// not change: a USER declaration of the name would give one program two numberings of one enum.
export let IterationErrorTypeName = b"IterationError"

// The cases of `IterationError`, in `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:29-30`'s DECLARATION ORDER — positional
// ordinals, so the order is the contract a user's `match` arms are resolved against. `exhausted` is what an
// `advance()` past the last element throws; `atStart` belongs to `BidirectionalIterator.retreat()`.
//
// ⚠ **THESE ARE NAMES, NOT ORDINALS, AND SINCE W3 NOTHING TRANSCRIBES THE ORDER.** The two constants stayed
// when the layout left, exactly as the `__Managed*Error` case names stayed at A1s: the compiler still has to
// NAME the case a cursor protocol declaration must throw (`declaresCursorProtocol`), and it reads the
// numbering off the declaration.
export let IterationErrorExhaustedCaseName = b"exhausted"
export let IterationErrorAtStartCaseName = b"atStart"

// ⭐⭐ **DOES A DECLARED `throws` CLAUSE NAME `IterationError` — the ONE error type the traversal protocol's
// two fallible declarations may carry?** The loop absorbs what `advance()` and `createIterator()` throw
// WITHOUT binding it, which is sound for a compiler-owned payload-free enum and would leak for one carrying
// managed heap, so pinning the clause is what makes the absorption sound by construction.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS ASKED OF A CLAUSE, NOT OF A CALLEE NAME, BECAUSE THE TWO STORES THAT HOLD ONE ARE DIFFERENT
// STORES.** A struct source's protocol method is a DECLARED CALLEE (`ProgramSignatures.throwsOf`); an
// existential's is an interface REQUIREMENT, which is in no signature registry at all — its clause travels
// on the resolved `WitnessDispatchTarget`. One rule over the clause is what stops the two receiver kinds
// coming to accept different error types.
export function throwsClauseIsIterationError(clause ThrowsClause) returns bool
	let thrown = try throwsClauseName(clause) otherwise return false
	return thrown.toByteArray().equals(IterationErrorTypeName)
end 'throwsClauseIsIterationError'

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH PREHEADER A `createIterator()` DECLARATION BUYS, read off its `throws` clause alone.** The
// three answers are separate because the loop emits three different preheaders: an INFALLIBLE factory seeds
// the header's error flag with a literal 0; a THROWING one seeds it with the call's own flag, so an empty
// collection — which `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:132-134` says the constructor reports by throwing `exhausted`
// — runs zero trips through the very test that ends the walk; and a factory throwing anything ELSE is a real
// declaration the loop cannot use, so it is its own answer rather than being folded into `notDeclared`.
//
// Split from `ProgramSignatures.iterableFactory` at the same door `throwsClauseIsIterationError` was, and for
// the same reason: the EXISTENCE half differs per store (a declared callee against an interface requirement)
// while the classification is one rule.
export function iterableFactoryFromClause(clause ThrowsClause) returns IterableFactory
	if not throwsClauseIsThrowing(clause) 'infallible'
		return IterableFactory.infallible
	end 'infallible'

	if not throwsClauseIsIterationError(clause) 'someOtherError'
		return IterableFactory.throwsSomethingElse(throwingClauseTypeName(clause))
	end 'someOtherError'

	return IterableFactory.throwsIterationError
end 'iterableFactoryFromClause'

// ⭐ **THE ERROR TYPE A CLAUSE ALREADY ESTABLISHED AS THROWING NAMES — the ONE spelling of that assertion in
// this file.** `none` here is never a program's property: every caller has already separated the two arms,
// either through `throwsClauseIsThrowing` (`iterableFactoryFromClause` above) or by reading the clause back
// out of a registry only a throwing declaration reaches (`ProgramSignatures.recordThrows` files nothing else
// and returns before writing). So the failure it names is two readings of ONE union disagreeing, and there is
// exactly one thing to say about that — which is why the panic is written here rather than at each door.
function throwingClauseTypeName(clause ThrowsClause) returns String
	return try throwsClauseName(clause) otherwise panic("throwingClauseTypeName: a clause answered `throwing` to `throwsClauseIsThrowing` and `none` to `throwsClauseName` — one union, two readings")
end 'throwingClauseTypeName'

// The `element_size@24` of a MANAGED-element array (P1.7 slice 3) — a managed element (String / struct /
// union / instance / nested Array) is a HEAP BOX addressed by a POINTER, so a slot holds the 8-byte pointer,
// NOT the element's full logical size. A trivial element is stored inline and strides by its own size (an
// `int` 8, a `Byte = int(0 to u8.max)` 1). This is why `arrayElementSize` cannot simply be the source
// `sizeof`: the STORAGE width of a managed element is the pointer — a `String` element's slot is 8, where
// its `sizeof` is the whole record (`StringRuntime.StringRecordBytes`, 56 since W157) — and the drop walk /
// accessors move exactly that pointer.
let ManagedArrayElementBytes = 8 as ByteOffset

// ⭐ **A `bool` ELEMENT IS ONE BIT** — the width `Array with bool` packs at, and the only element whose
// packed width is not read off a declared range (`bool` is a TAG, not a ranged alias, so it has no `low`/
// `high` for `rangedAliasPackedBits` to read). It is a named constant rather than a `1` inside
// `arrayElementPackedBits` because a bare 1 there sits beside the `2` and `4` that ladder hands back and
// reads as one of that ladder's rungs, which it is not: those are derived from bounds, this is definitional.
let BooleanPackedBits = 1 as PackedBitWidth

// ⭐⭐ **THE SLOT A PAYLOAD-FREE ENUM'S VALUE OCCUPIES — the narrowest width holding every declared TAG
// (`specs/enum-narrow-storage.md`).** A payload-free enum's value IS its tag (`Parser.emitEnumTagOf`), so
// the set the slot must hold is exactly `caseTags`, and the min/max of that set is what the ladder is asked.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE TAG SET IS THE RIGHT QUESTION AT EVERY BACKING, AND THAT IS WHY THERE IS NO `EnumBacking`
// DISPATCH HERE — the general sentence, not a case list.** The ladder is asked about what is STORED, and
// what is stored is the tag (`Parser.emitEnumTagOf`: an unboxed enum's value IS its tag). So the answer is
// right whatever the tag MEANS, at every backing there is and at every backing there will be — which is a
// property this function has, where an enumeration of backings would be a claim it maintains by hand.
//
// The spec's ladder is written over "raw values"; for the INTEGER backing the tag IS the raw value, so the
// two readings coincide on every case the spec pins. Where they part it is still the tag that occupies the
// slot: a `character` tag is a codepoint, a `string`/`functionRef` tag is a declaration index (the value
// lives in `.rdata`, reached by a select over the tag), a `structRecord` tag likewise, and a `float` tag is
// an IEEE-754 bit pattern.
//
// ⛔ **DO NOT REPLACE THIS WITH A PER-BACKING ARGUMENT — THE ONE THAT STOOD HERE WAS FALSE.** It claimed a
// float backing lands on the machine word "because a double's pattern does not fit four bytes", and
// `enum Zero: float { z = 0.0 }` has `caseTags = [0]` ⇒ a **ONE-byte** slot. The answer was still correct —
// one byte holds that tag and the tag is what is stored — but for a reason the comment did not give, which
// is how a per-case argument here rots while the code stays right. (A narrow SIGNED float slot needs a tag
// in `[-2³¹, -1]`, i.e. a NEGATIVE NaN or a large-magnitude negative double's pattern; `-1.0` is
// `0xBFF0000000000000`, far outside it, so it is reachable in principle and by nothing anyone writes.)
//
// A declaration with NO cases has an empty value set, so every width holds it; the machine word is the
// answer because there is nothing to gain from narrowing a type no value inhabits, and it is what the
// walk below would otherwise have to invent a first bound out of.
function payloadFreeEnumTagSlot(layout EnumLayout) returns StorageSlot
	// ONE walk for both bounds — `Project.enumTagSpan`'s, shared with the synthesized error union that
	// measures a member the same way. `valueRangeStorageSlot`'s precondition (`low <= high`) is structural
	// because the two ends are derived together there.
	match enumTagSpan(layout) 'span'
		noCases then return StorageSlot.machineWord()
		spanning(low, high) then return valueRangeStorageSlot(low, high: high)
	end 'span'
end 'payloadFreeEnumTagSlot'

// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO NAMES shv2 GIVES A BYTE — the ROSTER, in one place (R4.7 review).** `Byte` is the
// user-visible element a `b"…"` literal, a `ByteArray` and `__ManagedMemory` all share; `__ManagedByte` is
// the compiler-owned element EVERY COMPILER-SYNTHESIZED byte buffer wears (`SynthesizedByteElementName` —
// widened from the String-view-only `__ManagedByte` by the command-line rung; see its header below for the
// two sightings that forced the class name). Everything else a program can name is some OTHER type that
// happens to be narrow.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A ROSTER AND NOT A WIDTH, AND CONFLATING THE TWO WAS A MEASURED WRONG ANSWER.**
// `rangedAliasStorageBytes` hands EVERY non-negative range that fits `u8.max` a one-byte slot, so "strides one
// byte" is a far larger set than "is a byte": `typealias Small = int(0 to 200)` is in the first and not the
// second. Both readers need the roster and neither may substitute the width for it — the width
// FALLBACK above, because these are the two names no file declares and whose stride is therefore the
// compiler's to state, and `byteBufferBoundaryAdmits`, because a door that read the width instead admitted a
// String's byte 223 into an element declared `int(0 to 200)` (MEASURED, R4.7 review, exit 223 with no
// diagnostic).
//
// ⛔⛔ **AND THE ROSTER IS NOT A RANGE EITHER — THIS HEADER ONCE CLAIMED IT CURED THAT READING, AND IT DID
// NOT.** The sentence above was written as if the roster had closed the 223, and for `Small` it had: `Small`
// is not a byte NAME, so the door refuses. Rename the very same declaration `Byte` and the roster answers
// YES — a roster is a question about the name, which is the whole point of the base-name strip below — the
// stride still answers YES, and **the identical 223 came straight back** (MEASURED on `origin/main`, N2:
// `takes(<a cwd buffer>)` against `typealias Byte = int(0 to 200)`, exit 223, no diagnostic). The user's
// most natural spelling was the one nothing checked. What closes it is a third question that is neither a
// name nor a width — the element's declared BOUNDS
// (`RangedAliasRegistry.holdsEveryByteInEveryFile`, asked from `byteBufferBoundaryAdmits`). A cure claimed
// in a header is exactly how the next reader inherits the bug, so: this function is a NAME test, it is
// only ever one third of that door, and it must not be read as more.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT ASKS ABOUT THE *BASE* NAME, AND WITHOUT THAT THE ROSTER SHUTS THE STDLIB'S OWN DOOR (N2).** Once
// a contested `Byte` is qualified per claimant, the stdlib's element is spelled `Byte$0_255` — literally
// equal to neither roster entry — so `stdlib/File.maxon:140`'s `FileWriter.open(path.path.addressableBytes())`
// would stop compiling in exactly the program this scoping exists to fix. `Byte$0_255` IS `Byte`; the suffix
// says WHICH declaration of it, and a roster is a question about the name, never about the range. The two
// sides are asked the same way so a mint can never be on the roster from one side and off it from the other
// — the asymmetry R4.7 already paid for once, in the other direction.
function isByteElementName(name ByteArray) returns bool
	let base = sourceSpelledAliasName(name)
	return base.equals(ByteElementTypeName) or base.equals(SynthesizedByteElementName)
end 'isByteElementName'

// ⭐ **THE INCLUSIVE INTEGER VALUE SET A TRIVIAL ARRAY ELEMENT ADMITS, WHEN IT HAS ONE** — the answer
// `ProgramSignatures.elementIntegerValueRange` gives, and a UNION because *"this element has no integer
// value set"* is an ANSWER and not a failure: a `bool`, an enum and an `ExitCode` are perfectly good
// elements, they simply cannot be compared to another element by RANGE. `DeclaredAlias`'s shape, for
// `DeclaredAlias`'s reason.
union ElementIntegerRange
	notIntegerValued
	inclusive(low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt)
end 'ElementIntegerRange'

// The value set a BARE `int` element admits — the machine word's whole signed range, which is EXACTLY what
// `typealias Integer = int(i64.min to i64.max)` spells. Named because that identity is load-bearing rather
// than incidental: `[1, 2, 3]` mints `Array with int` and `IntArray.create()` mints `Array with Integer`,
// two instances over one value set, and `arrayAppendArgAdmits` joins them on these two numbers alone.
let BareIntegerElementLow = i64.min as ParsedInt
let BareIntegerElementHigh = i64.max as ParsedInt

// A ranged alias's declared bounds as a value set — `notIntegerValued` for a FLOAT alias, whose `low`/`high`
// are IEEE-754 bit patterns and order nothing like integers (`floatBoundOrderKey` is where that is spelled
// out), so the honest answer is that there is no comparison to make rather than an invented one.
//
// ⚠ The float arm is LIVE as of A4d — `typealias Reals = Array with Real` is a spellable element now — and
// `error.a-raw-byte-write-is-refused-on-a-declared-float-element` is the program that reaches it. It was
// written when nothing could, which is exactly when a conservative arm is cheapest to get wrong.
function integerAliasValueRange(alias RangedTypeAlias) returns ElementIntegerRange
	if aliasIsFloat(alias) 'aFloatAliasHasNoIntegerValueSet'
		return ElementIntegerRange.notIntegerValued
	end 'aFloatAliasHasNoIntegerValueSet'

	return ElementIntegerRange.inclusive(alias.low, high: alias.high)
end 'integerAliasValueRange'

// ⭐ **DOES `[destLow, destHigh]` CONTAIN EVERY VALUE OF `[sourceLow, sourceHigh]`?**
//
// ⚠⚠ **A RAW-NEGATIVE BOUND IS AMBIGUOUS AND IS ANSWERED NO — the same conservative direction
// `rangeHoldsEveryByte` argues for at length, for the same reason.** Bounds ride as the RAW 64-bit pattern
// `ParsedInt` holds, so `-1` and `u64.max` are the SAME BITS: `int(0 to u64.max)`'s `high` reads as `-1`,
// and a signed `>=` would cheerfully declare `int(0 to 100)` wide enough to hold it. This door ADMITS a run
// of values into a declared element, so the ambiguous answer has to be refusal.
//
// ⭐ **THE BIT-EQUALITY ARM RUNS FIRST, AND IT IS WHAT MAKES THE FULL-WIDTH CASE WORK AT ALL.** `int` and
// `int(i64.min to i64.max)` are one value set whose `low` IS raw-negative, so the ordered comparison could
// never reach it. Two identical bound pairs are one value set whatever the bits mean, which needs no
// interpretation of them.
function inclusiveRangeContains(destLow ParsedInt, destHigh ParsedInt, sourceLow ParsedInt, sourceHigh ParsedInt) returns bool
	if destLow == sourceLow and destHigh == sourceHigh 'oneValueSetSpelledTwice'
		return true
	end 'oneValueSetSpelledTwice'

	if destLow < 0 or destHigh < 0 or sourceLow < 0 or sourceHigh < 0 'aRawNegativeBoundIsAmbiguous'
		return false
	end 'aRawNegativeBoundIsAmbiguous'

	return destLow <= sourceLow and destHigh >= sourceHigh
end 'inclusiveRangeContains'

// ⭐⭐ **DOES `low to high` CONTAIN EVERY UNSIGNED VALUE OF A `slotBytes`-WIDE SLOT? (A4c)** The range half of
// `ProgramSignatures.elementAdmitsEverySlotValue`, spelled apart from it because it is a statement about a
// RANGE and not about an instance — `Project.rangeHoldsEveryByte`'s own arrangement, which this is the
// general form of, and whose ONE-BYTE rung it therefore asks rather than re-spelling. The slot's value set
// is UNSIGNED because the shared element load zero-extends (`ManagedMemoryRuntime.emitLoadElem`).
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE WHOLE MACHINE WORD IS THE ONE SLOT TWO DIFFERENT DECLARATIONS FILL, AND NEITHER CAN BE ORDERED
// AGAINST IT.** `int(i64.min to i64.max)` fills it from the signed side and `int(0 to u64.max)` from the
// unsigned side; both carry a RAW-NEGATIVE bound (`RangedTypeAlias`'s header), so an ordered `>=` would read
// `int(0 to 100)` as wide enough for either. It is a bit EQUALITY against each spelling — the reason
// `inclusiveRangeContains` above opens with `oneValueSetSpelledTwice` — and every OTHER range at this width
// is refused, which is correct rather than conservative: `rangedAliasStorageBytes` is the UNSIGNED ladder, so
// `int(-5 to 5)` keeps the word without filling it, and MEASURED before this rule one raw byte read back
// **223** out of an element declared `int(-5 to 5)`.
//
// ⚠ Below the word both bounds are honest small non-negative numbers, exactly BECAUSE the ladder gave the
// range a narrow slot at all — a raw-negative bound would have taken the word — so ordering is safe there,
// and the `>=` is the same shape (and, at one byte, literally the same call) `rangeHoldsEveryByte` uses.
//
// ⚠ The LOW end is `SlotUnsignedMin` and NOT `Project.ByteValueSetLow`, which this first read (A4c review):
// the two are the same zero for different reasons, and the reason a SLOT's is zero is the zero-extending
// loader's, not a byte's declared domain. `rawByteWriteSlotDomainMessage` already renders the slot's value
// set from `SlotUnsignedMin`, so with the byte's constant here the PREDICATE and the SENTENCE describing it
// were two facts that happened to agree — the exact shape this rung exists to remove.
//
// ⚠⚠ **A SUB-BYTE-PACKED SLOT ANSWERS NO, AND THAT IS THE ONLY HONEST ANSWER RATHER THAN A CONSERVATIVE
// ONE.** This predicate's caller is admitting RAW BYTES into a declared element (`setByte`, and the byte
// boundary), and a packed element has no byte of its own to admit them into — 8, 4 or 2 of its fields SHARE
// one byte, so a byte write lands across several elements at once and the question "does this element's
// range hold every value of its slot" has no true answer, not merely an unknown one. It is tested first
// because every arm below reads `slotBytes` as a WIDTH, and a negative stride is a bit count.
function rangeHoldsEverySlotValue(low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt, slotBytes ElementStride) returns bool
	if slotBytes < 0 'aSubByteFieldSharesItsByteWithItsNeighbours'
		return false
	end 'aSubByteFieldSharesItsByteWithItsNeighbours'

	// Past the guard the stride is a genuine byte width (1/2/4/8), which is what the ladder below reads.
	let bytes = slotBytes as ByteOffset
	if bytes == (ByteStringElementSize as ByteOffset) 'aByteSlot'
		return rangeHoldsEveryByte(low, high: high)
	end 'aByteSlot'

	if bytes == MachineWordBytes 'theWholeWord'
		return (low == BareIntegerElementLow and high == BareIntegerElementHigh) or (low == SlotUnsignedMin and high == slotUnsignedMax(bytes))
	end 'theWholeWord'

	return low == SlotUnsignedMin and high >= slotUnsignedMax(bytes)
end 'rangeHoldsEverySlotValue'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ELEMENT OF EVERY COMPILER-SYNTHESIZED BYTE BUFFER — AND WHY IT IS NOT `Byte`.**
// A buffer the COMPILER mints is byte-packed by construction: `__str_bytes_view` publishes a record over a
// String's own buffer, whose `element_size@24` is 1 always, and the `__ManagedMemory` an OS runtime entry
// hands back (`emitOwnedManagedMemoryResult`) is a `mm_alloc`'d run of bytes. `Array with Byte` cannot carry
// either, because `Byte` is a name a USER PROGRAM may declare — `typealias Byte = int(0 to 1000)` makes
// `internArrayByteInstance()` stride TWO — and the buffer would then be a static type and a runtime record
// disagreeing about one instance's stride, which is the two-producer shape `arrayElementSize` exists to end.
//
// ⚠⚠ **MEASURED TWICE, THREE RUNGS APART, WITH THE SAME TWO CASES AND THE SAME SENTENCE.** Both times a
// WHITELISTED STDLIB MODULE — which every program parses — stopped compiling because of a `Byte` alias in a
// program that never called it:
//   • BATCH2 slice 1, with the string view typed `Array with Byte`: `E3005 argument type mismatch for
//     'managed': expected '__ManagedMemory', got 'Array_Byte'` raised inside `stdlib/URL.maxon`.
//   • The command-line rung, with `emitOwnedManagedMemoryResult` still minting `internArrayByteInstance()`:
//     the IDENTICAL sentence, raised inside `stdlib/CommandLine.maxon:13` at `String.init(managed)`.
// `specs-shv2/bytearray-element-size.md`'s two wide-`Byte` cases are what caught it on both occasions.
// ⇒ The first fix cured the ONE producer that had bitten, and the rule it wrote down was general while the
// name it wrote it under (`__ManagedByte`) was not — so the next synthesized byte buffer walked into the same
// wall. The element is now named for the CLASS, and every compiler-minted byte buffer wears it.
//
// ⚠ A `b"…"` literal meets the identical wall and is REFUSED for it (E2015, same spec), and that is not an
// inconsistency: a literal's element is the `Byte` the PROGRAM declares and the program can see the refusal.
// So is `__ManagedMemory.create(n, elementSize: 1)`, which is user-written and keeps `Byte`
// (`managedMemoryInstanceForElementSize`, pinned by `array-init.md`). The split is SYNTHESIZED vs WRITTEN,
// not string vs buffer: code the user did not write cannot be refused, so it takes an element the user
// cannot reach instead.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THAT SECOND SENTENCE WAS FALSE WHEN IT WAS WRITTEN AND IS TRUE AS OF A4c.** `create(n, 1)` kept
// `Byte` and was NOT refused for the wall, so a wide-`Byte` file got a record stamped stride 1 under a type
// striding 2 and `set(0, value: 300)` read back **44** — the literal's own incoherence through the twin
// producer, with the comment that would have caught it already on the page. Both producers now ask
// `Parser.requireBytePackedRecordStridesLikeThisFilesByte`, so the split above is what the code does rather
// than what it intended.
//
// It is `__`-RESERVED, so no program can declare it and `undeclaredNamedElementSlot` is the only
// answer for its width — the same reason `StringIndexTypeName` wears one. The rule is that a
// compiler-synthesized buffer's stride is the COMPILER's to state, never a function of which aliases the
// program happens to declare.
//
// ⚠ **THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO CITE `parseDecodedView` AS THE RULE'S OTHER APPLICATION, AND THAT FUNCTION IS
// GONE (W49 wave 6).** It kept `.codepoints()`/`.utf16()` on the plain `integer` instance rather than on
// `Codepoint`/`CodeUnit16`, because those two element names are a compiler-synthesized alias and a RANGED
// typealias in a WHITELISTED module — so the emitted element size of a synthesized view would have been a
// function of which stdlib modules the whitelist listed that day. Both members retired onto the corpus's
// lazy `CodepointView`/`UTF16View`, which mint no array at all. The rule survives its second application
// because it was never about those two members; it is stated here now rather than by reference.
let SynthesizedByteElementName = b"__ManagedByte"

// ⭐⭐ **THE `StringIndex` TYPE — THE CORPUS'S DECLARATION, AND SINCE W49 WAVE 3 THE ONLY ONE.**
//
// `stdlib/String.maxon:917-946` declares it as an ordinary two-field `type` (`var charIdx as
// GraphemeIndex`, `var bytePosition as BytePos`), so the ordinary declaration sweep registers its
// `StructLayout` and the whole struct-value machinery follows — a binding's type, aggregate identity on a
// reassign, and a scope-exit drop through the trivial-struct `__mm_decref` — with no new `ValueTypeTag`
// and no new ownership rule.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT WAS SPELLED `__StringIndex` AND THAT WAS A SECOND DECLARATION OF ONE TYPE, WHICH IS THIS
// PROJECT'S SIGNATURE BUG WEARING A SAFETY PROPERTY'S CLOTHES.** shv2 registered its own two-field layout
// under the RESERVED name because *"what it does not have is a `type` declaration for this one"* — true
// when it was written, and false since W17 listed `stdlib/String.maxon`. What the second declaration then
// bought was not safety but an UNSATISFIABLE argument type: the corpus's own `charAt(idx StringIndex)` and
// `indexBefore(idx StringIndex)` were reachable through `memberBelongsToTheCorpus`, and every
// call to one was **`E3005 argument type mismatch for 'idx': expected 'StringIndex', got '__StringIndex'`**
// — a program could hold an index of one declaration or the other and never one that satisfied both. Wave
// 3 retires the five producers (`startIndex`, `endIndex`, `findFirst`, `findLast`, `indexAfter`) onto the
// corpus, which leaves the corpus's declaration alone, and both members resolve with nothing added.
//
// ⭐ **THE SAFETY PROPERTY THE RESERVED SPELLING BOUGHT IS KEPT, BY THE ROUTE `String` AND `Character`
// ALREADY USE.** The registry is a MAP keyed by name, so a user's `type StringIndex` would land in the
// corpus's bucket and `slice` would then read a box through the USER's field table — silent memory
// corruption decided by file order, and MEASURED to compile on the tree before this rung. `StringIndex` is
// therefore on `TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName` now: `declarationContestsACompilerOwnedName`
// admits the corpus's declaration and drops a user's, and `requireTypeNameNotCompilerOwned` reports E2015
// at it. One declaration, one bucket, and the contest is unrepresentable rather than merely diagnosed —
// which is what the `__` prefix was doing, minus the second layout.
//
// ⇒ And the consequence the reserved name carried is GONE with it: a `StringIndex` can now cross a
// function boundary, because the name it needs in a parameter or return type is the one the program has.
export let StringIndexTypeName = b"StringIndex"

// The two fields' declaration indices — what `Parser.dispatchStringIndexMethod` hands `emitFieldLoad`, against the layout the corpus's declaration registered. Named because an index IS
// the identity here (see `StructLayout.offsetOfField`), so a bare `0`/`1` at a read site is exactly the
// magic value the rule against them is for; and they are the CORPUS's declaration order
// (`stdlib/String.maxon:918-919`), which is the only thing that decides an offset.
export let StringIndexCharIdxFieldIndex = 0 as FieldIndex
export let StringIndexBytePosFieldIndex = 1 as FieldIndex

// ⭐⭐ **`CharacterSet`, AND THE SECOND TIME THE RESERVED-SECOND-LAYOUT ARRANGEMENT WAS RETIRED.** A
// compiler-owned layout used to be registered under the RESERVED name `__CharacterSet` beside this bare
// source spelling, so a user `type CharacterSet` and the synthesized layout could not contest one bucket.
// Its consequence was the cost: such a type CANNOT CROSS A FUNCTION BOUNDARY, because a parameter or return
// would have to NAME the reserved spelling — every use had to flow from an accessor straight into a call or
// a local `let`.
//
// ⚠ `StringIndex` carried the identical arrangement until W49 wave 3, when the corpus's own
// `type StringIndex` made it a SECOND DECLARATION of one type and the reserved name stopped buying safety:
// it made the corpus's `charAt(idx StringIndex)` unsatisfiable. The cure BOTH took is the same — **keep the
// RESERVATION, drop the second LAYOUT** — and `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon` being listed (W115) is what let
// this one follow. The layout and its registration went at W129; the reservation below stays, and it is what
// `TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName` reads.
//
// ⇒ What remains is the user-visible spelling: what a source `CharacterSet.letters()` writes, resolved
// through the CORPUS declaration like any other listed type's name.
export let CharacterSetBuiltinName = b"CharacterSet"

// ⭐ **THE COMPILER-OWNED `__ManagedFile` TYPE (R4.1).** An 8-byte box holding one raw OS file HANDLE,
// with a runtime-owned destructor that closes it on drop — `specs/managed-file.md`'s "Type Structure"
// states the single `_handle` field and the RAII contract.
//
// ⚠ **THE REGISTRY NAME AND THE USER-FACING NAME ARE THE SAME BYTES HERE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON
// THIS TYPE CAN DO WHAT `CharacterSet` COULD NOT.** That one was registered under a reserved
// `__`-prefixed name while the source spelled a bare one, and `CharacterSetBuiltinName`'s comment draws the
// consequence: such a type "cannot cross a function boundary, because a parameter or return type would
// have to NAME it". The corpus REQUIRES this one to cross — `specs/managed-file.md` writes
// `path __ManagedMemory` as a declared parameter and `export var file as __ManagedFile` as a field — and
// it can, because the name a user writes IS the name the layout is registered under, so there is no
// second spelling to fail to resolve.
// ⇒ **The `__` prefix is therefore NOT a barrier here, and that was MEASURED rather than assumed:**
// `requireUnreservedName`'s E2051 refuses a `__`-prefixed DECLARATION (`type __Foo`), not a `__`-prefixed
// name in a TYPE POSITION. A field declared at a reserved spelling nothing registers reports
// `E3011 Unknown type`, not E2051 — it fails to RESOLVE, and resolving is what registering the layout fixes.
// ⇒ And `StringIndex` is the third answer to the same question: it took the bare name outright at W49
// wave 3 and crosses a boundary now, because the CORPUS declares it and a reservation over *whose*
// declaration wins needs no second spelling at all (`StringIndexTypeName`).
export let ManagedFileTypeName = b"__ManagedFile"

// ⭐ **`__ManagedMemory` IS AN `Array`, NOT A TYPE OF ITS OWN (R4.1, generalized by R4.4).** Registered as
// a builtin GENERIC ALIAS — the same door `CharSet` went through until W129 — bound to
// the exact instance `internArrayByteInstance()` mints, which is the instance `"…".toByteArray()` already
// yields and the one a `b"…"` literal already has. So a `path __ManagedMemory` parameter and the argument
// handed to it are ONE type by construction, and `.managed` is an identity rather than a conversion.
//
// ⭐⭐ **R4.4 KEPT THE BINDING AND MADE IT ELEMENT-AGNOSTIC, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF THAT RUNG.** The
// reference declares `__ManagedMemory` generic over ONE parameter named `Element` (v1
// `LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1608-1621`; bootstrap `2-Parser.cs:1254` + `:1391`) and implements `Array` ON it
// (`stdlib/Array.maxon:104-106`: `typealias ElementMemory = __ManagedMemory with Element`, `export var
// managed as ElementMemory`), so `__ManagedMemory with T` and `Array with T` describe ONE record. shv2
// SYNTHESIZES that record instead of compiling `stdlib/Array.maxon`, so for shv2 they ARE one
// `GenericInstanceId` — which is what makes `.managed` an identity read at EVERY element type and leaves
// nothing to reconcile at `String.init`, `.toByteArray()`, `valueIsByteArray` or a `__ManagedFile`
// parameter. A distinct generic base would mint a second id at each of those.
// ⇒ The bare spelling (no `with`) keeps meaning the BYTE instance, because that is what every
// `__ManagedFile` signature declares and what a path buffer is.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO SURFACES OF ONE RECORD ARE THEREFORE TOLD APART BY PROVENANCE, NOT BY TYPE** — see
// `Parser.valueIsBufferSurface`. The reference separates `Array.count()` from `ByteMemory.length()` with
// two TYPES; shv2 has one, so it carries "this value denotes the BUFFER" on the value instead. That is the
// price of the binding, and it is the smaller one: the alternative is two ids for one record.
export let ManagedMemoryTypeName = b"__ManagedMemory"

// ⭐⭐ **`__ManagedMemory with T` IS `Array with T`, WRITTEN OUT (W43)** — the generic-BASE spelling of the
// binding the bare name already has. R4.4 settled that the two "describe ONE record" and that for shv2 they
// ARE one `GenericInstanceId`; what it registered was the ARITY-ZERO alias alone, so the base spelling —
// which `stdlib/Map.maxon`, `Array.maxon`, `Vector.maxon`, `Character.maxon` and `String.maxon` all write —
// was `E2055 "Type '__ManagedMemory' has no associated types"`, the refusal `VectorBuiltinBaseName`'s header
// used to record as reason (a) for synthesizing `Vector` rather than reading its declaration. **W43 IS WHAT
// KILLED THAT REASON, and the note it justified outlived it by seven weeks** — re-measured 2026-08-13, the
// annotation compiles and `stdlib/Vector.maxon` probes `E3001` and nothing else; the surviving reason is a
// different fact entirely and is stated at that header.
//
// ⭐ **CANONICALIZING AT THE READ IS WHAT KEEPS IT ONE INSTANCE.** Every downstream door — the arity check,
// the intern key, `genericInstanceBoxSize`, the drop callee, the element accessors — asks about a base NAME,
// so mapping the spelling once here means none of them learns a second name for one record. The alternative,
// teaching `isBuiltinGenericBaseName` a seventh base, would give `__ManagedMemory with Byte` and
// `Array with Byte` two ids and undo the binding at every one of those doors.
//
// ⚠ **THE SPELLING IS NOT MERELY DISCARDED — IT DECIDES THE MEMBER SURFACE**, which is the whole of A2j/A2m.
// It is carried to the declaration sites separately (`GenericAliasEntry.baseIsBuffer` ⇒
// `ProgramSignatures.aliasDenotesBufferSurface`), because a value's surface is a fact about the SPELLING and
// the type is deliberately the same either way.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **AND SINCE ARR5b THE TARGET IS DISCOVERED, NOT WRITTEN DOWN — SEE
// `Parser.canonicalGenericBaseNameInThisFile`, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF THE FOLD.** The rule that stood here
// was `SynthesizedArrayRecordSeed if baseName.equals(ManagedMemoryTypeName) else baseName`, a constant, and
// it made the compiler know the name `Array`. It now reads the target off the `BuiltinArrayLiteral`
// conformance — the user's own stated exception to *"nothing array-specific in the compiler"* — which is
// why the rule moved to the parser: the answer depends on the READER (a stdlib file spells the corpus's
// declaration under the contest's mint) and on the ENCLOSING DECLARATION (inside a conformer's own body a
// written `__ManagedMemory` is that conformer), and neither is a fact a free function over one name can
// see.

// ⭐⭐ **ONE DECLARED TYPE POSITION'S PRE-ERASURE `__ManagedMemory` SPELLING (A2j / A2m).** A parameter's
// annotation, a struct field's, a return clause's: each is a place where the SOURCE said something no
// `MaxonType` can still say, because `parseTypeReference` folds `__ManagedMemory` into the very
// `genericInstance(Array with Byte)` a `ByteArray` resolves to (`registerManagedFileType`).
//
// TWO facts per position, because a declaration can name the buffer as the position itself or one container
// in, and each decides a different value's member roster:
//   • the position IS the buffer                  — `m __ManagedMemory`                            (A2j)
//   • the position's ARRAY ELEMENT is the buffer  — `a BufArray`, `BufArray = Array with __ManagedMemory`
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND A TUPLE POSITION CARRIES ONE WHOLE SURFACE PER SLOT, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS A TREE AND NOT A
// MASK.** A2m first shipped it as a flat 62-bit mask, one BIT per slot — which meant a slot could say "I am a
// buffer" and nothing else, so `(__ManagedMemory, Int)` worked while `(BufArray, Int)` and
// `((__ManagedMemory, Int), Int)` did not, for no reason a reader of the language could state. A slot is a
// declared position like any other; it gets the same payload as any other, recursively.
//
// ⚠ **AND THAT REMOVES THE WIDTH QUESTION RATHER THAN ANSWERING IT AGAIN.** The mask had an arity ceiling
// (62) and needed a REFUSAL to keep a 63rd slot from silently losing its bit — a lost bit being the wrong
// member roster with no diagnostic either way. A tree has one node per named position and no ceiling in
// either direction: no arity limit, no depth limit, nothing to truncate, and so nothing to refuse. The
// ceiling and its refusal are gone.
//
// ⚠ **THE TREE MAY NOT RIDE THE TUPLE'S `StructLayout` INSTEAD.** `(__ManagedMemory, Int)` and
// `(ByteArray, Int)` canonicalize to ONE interned tuple sharing ONE layout, so a surface written onto that
// layout would hand the buffer's roster to BOTH spellings — a wrong ACCEPTANCE, which no diagnostic reports
// and which `error.a-tuple-of-byte-arrays-keeps-the-array-surface` exists to hold shut. The declaration
// carries the fact and the VALUE carries it onward (`Parser.bufferSurfaceSlots`).

// ⭐ **ONE NODE OF THAT TREE, PACKED INTO A WORD.** Bit 0 is the position itself, bit 1 its array element,
// and everything above is the number of TUPLE SLOTS the node has — zero for a position that is not a tuple.
export typealias DeclaredSurfaceNode = int(0 to u64.max)

// ⭐⭐ **THE TREE, FLATTENED PRE-ORDER: node 0 is the position, and each node's slots follow it in order,
// each with its own subtree.** `(A, (B, C))` is `[tuple(2), A, tuple(2), B, C]`.
//
// ⚠ **FLAT AND PRE-ORDER RATHER THAN A RECORD THAT POINTS AT ITS CHILDREN, WHICH IS THIS COMPILER'S OWN
// ESTABLISHED SHAPE FOR EXACTLY THIS** — see `GenericArgNodeArray`, which flattens a nested `Base with (…)`
// the same way. It buys two things a record cannot: the element is an inline WORD, so a tree costs one
// allocation however deep it is; and a PLAIN position is the shared EMPTY tree, so it costs none at all.
// That matters because every door carries this fact as a column — one per parameter of every function in
// the program, one per field of every type — and all but a handful of them are plain.
export typealias DeclaredSurface = Array with DeclaredSurfaceNode

// One declared surface per declared POSITION, in declaration order — a struct's fields
// (`StructLayout.fieldDeclaredSurfaces`) and a function's parameters (`Parser.parseFunctionParameters`).
export typealias DeclaredSurfaceArray = Array with DeclaredSurface

// ⭐ **A POSITION THAT NAMED THE BUFFER NOWHERE — AN EMPTY TREE, AND THE ONE SHARED INSTANCE OF IT.** It is
// the answer for every type in a program that never writes the alias, so it is handed out rather than built:
// `sharedEmptyWhereConstraints`' pattern, stored in record fields and pushed into columns exactly as that one
// is. Nothing ever writes it — every builder that finds something allocates its own tree first.
export let sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface = DeclaredSurface.create()

// Bit 0: the position itself was spelled `__ManagedMemory`.
export let SurfaceBitIsBuffer = 1 as DeclaredSurfaceNode

// Bit 1: the position named a generic alias whose ELEMENT was spelled `__ManagedMemory`.
export let SurfaceBitElementIsBuffer = 2 as DeclaredSurfaceNode

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE GENERIC-ARGUMENT POSITION EITHER BIT EVER SPEAKS FOR, AND IT IS THE WHOLE REACH OF THE FACT
// RATHER THAN A FIRST CASE.** `SurfaceBitElementIsBuffer` says *argument 0 was spelled the buffer*, and
// argument 0 is the only argument any declaration RECORDS one for (`GenericAliasEntry.elementIsBuffer`, read
// off the first argument's token in `Parser.readGenericInstanceAlias`). So a reader that walked to argument 1
// would be asking about a fact nothing writes. Named here, once, so the recorder and the two readers below
// cannot come to hold different opinions about which argument the bit is about.
//
// ⚠ **THE BIT IS ABOUT AN ARGUMENT POSITION AND ITS NAME IS ABOUT AN ELEMENT, AND THE TWO COINCIDE ONLY
// BECAUSE OF WHO READS IT.** `Map with (__ManagedMemory, Int)` sets it for a KEY, since the recorder reads
// argument 0 whatever the base is. That is harmless and must stay so: every reader of the mark today is
// `Parser.emitContainerElementAccessor`, whose callers are `Array`, `List`, `Vector` and the array cursor —
// containers whose argument 0 IS their element. A derivation that narrowed itself to `Array` would make the
// derived answer disagree with the RECORDED one for the same declaration, which is worse than the overreach.
export let BufferSurfaceArgPosition = 0 as TypeParamPosition

// Everything above the two flag bits is the node's SLOT COUNT. THE one speller of the shift, shared by the
// builder (`Parser.declaredSurfaceAt`) and the readers below, so the two cannot come to disagree about where
// a count ends and a flag begins.
let SurfaceSlotCountShift = 2 as DeclaredSurfaceNode

// The node a TUPLE position contributes: no flags of its own, and `slotCount` children behind it. A tuple
// value is an ordinary record whatever its slots denote, which is why its own two flag bits stay clear.
export function tupleSurfaceNode(slotCount FieldIndex) returns DeclaredSurfaceNode
	// The count is bound rather than cast inline: the bootstrap reads a cast nested inside a shift as already
	// hashed and rejects it as unneeded (E3010), the same false positive `mixDefaultLiteral` binds a local for.
	let widened = slotCount as DeclaredSurfaceNode
	return widened shl SurfaceSlotCountShift
end 'tupleSurfaceNode'

// Does this position name the buffer nowhere at all? The empty tree, which is what every plain declaration
// gets — asked wherever a caller wants to skip the whole mechanism for the overwhelmingly common case.
export function surfaceIsPlain(surface DeclaredSurface) returns bool
	return surface.count() == 0
end 'surfaceIsPlain'

// Was the position ITSELF spelled `__ManagedMemory`?
export function surfaceIsBuffer(surface DeclaredSurface) returns bool
	return (surfaceRootNode(surface) and SurfaceBitIsBuffer) != 0
end 'surfaceIsBuffer'

// Did the position name a generic alias whose ELEMENT was spelled `__ManagedMemory`?
export function surfaceElementIsBuffer(surface DeclaredSurface) returns bool
	return (surfaceRootNode(surface) and SurfaceBitElementIsBuffer) != 0
end 'surfaceElementIsBuffer'

// How many TUPLE SLOTS this position has — zero for a position that is not a tuple, and zero for a plain
// one. It is what a reader asks before `surfaceSlot`, so a non-tuple never reaches the walk.
export function surfaceSlotCount(surface DeclaredSurface) returns FieldIndex
	return surfaceNodeSlotCount(surfaceRootNode(surface))
end 'surfaceSlotCount'

// How many slots ONE node has. THE one decoder of the count field, so the root reader above and the
// pre-order subtree walk below cannot come to disagree about where a count ends and a flag begins.
function surfaceNodeSlotCount(node DeclaredSurfaceNode) returns FieldIndex
	return node shr SurfaceSlotCountShift
end 'surfaceNodeSlotCount'

// ⭐ **THE SURFACE OF TUPLE SLOT `slot` — A WHOLE SURFACE, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THE TREE.** The slot's own
// subtree, copied out so the result is rooted at node 0 and every reader can be handed a `DeclaredSurface`
// with no index alongside it. It allocates, and only here: a caller reaches this line only for a slot of a
// tuple that actually named the buffer somewhere.
//
// `slot` is a precondition, not a case — every caller asked `surfaceSlotCount` first.
export function surfaceSlot(surface DeclaredSurface, slot FieldIndex) returns DeclaredSurface
	let start = surfaceSlotStart(surface, slot: slot)
	let stop = start + surfaceSubtreeSize(surface, at: start)
	var out = DeclaredSurface.create()
	for i in start upto stop 'eachNode'
		out.push(surfaceNodeAt(surface, at: i))
	end 'eachNode'
	return out
end 'surfaceSlot'

// ⭐ **THE SAME SURFACE WITH SPELLING BITS ADDED AT ITS ROOT.** The one WRITER outside the parser's builder,
// and it COPIES rather than mutating: the tree it is handed is the one stored on a `StructLayout` or in the
// whole-program return table, shared by every reader of that declaration, and setting a bit in place would
// teach one file's read to every other file's. Its caller is the door that must DERIVE the root's spelling
// bits rather than store them — see `ProgramSignatures.derivedAliasSurfaceBits`.
//
// ⚠ **IT TAKES THE BITS RATHER THAN NAMING ONE (W49).** It set `SurfaceBitElementIsBuffer` outright while
// the element bit was the only underivable one; W49 found its TWIN underivable at the same door, for the
// same reason, off the same recorded name. A second function beside this one would have been a second copy
// of the copy-the-tree-and-OR-the-root rule — free to disagree the day the node grows a third bit, which is
// this project's own signature bug.
export function surfaceWithRootBits(surface DeclaredSurface, bits DeclaredSurfaceNode) returns DeclaredSurface
	var out = DeclaredSurface.create()
	out.push(surfaceRootNode(surface) or bits)
	for i in 1 upto surface.count() 'eachRemainingNode'
		out.push(surfaceNodeAt(surface, at: i))
	end 'eachRemainingNode'
	return out
end 'surfaceWithRootBits'

// ⭐ **A WHOLE SURFACE FROM ROOT BITS ALONE — and the shared EMPTY tree when there are none, so a position
// that names the buffer nowhere allocates nothing.** THE one home for "bits ⇒ surface", shared by the token
// builder (`Parser.rootLeafSurfaceAt`) and by the two doors that DERIVE a root's bits rather than reading a
// stored tree: the instance-argument crossing (`Parser.slotSurfaceThroughReceiver`) and the alias-spelling
// stamp on a factory result. Written out, the emptiness test is the half a fourth site would forget — and it
// is not cosmetic, since it is what keeps every generic constructor call in the program allocation-free.
export function surfaceOfRootBits(bits DeclaredSurfaceNode) returns DeclaredSurface
	if bits == 0 'namesTheBufferNowhere'
		return sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface
	end 'namesTheBufferNowhere'

	return surfaceWithRootBits(sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface, bits: bits)
end 'surfaceOfRootBits'

// Node `at` of the tree. A precondition, not a case: every index handed here came from a walk that started
// at node 0 and advanced by whole subtrees.
function surfaceNodeAt(surface DeclaredSurface, at FieldIndex) returns DeclaredSurfaceNode
	return try surface.get(at) otherwise panic("surfaceNodeAt: node {at} is out of range for a {surface.count()}-node declared surface — every index came from a pre-order walk of this same tree")
end 'surfaceNodeAt'

// Node 0, or an all-clear node for the shared EMPTY tree. The empty tree is the plain answer, so reading it
// as a node with no flags and no slots is what makes every accessor above answer plainly without its own
// emptiness test.
function surfaceRootNode(surface DeclaredSurface) returns DeclaredSurfaceNode
	if surfaceIsPlain(surface) 'noNodes'
		return 0 as DeclaredSurfaceNode
	end 'noNodes'

	return surfaceNodeAt(surface, at: 0)
end 'surfaceRootNode'

// How many nodes the subtree rooted at `at` occupies — itself plus every descendant. THE one walk that knows
// pre-order layout, so `surfaceSlotStart` and `surfaceSlot` cannot come to disagree about where a subtree
// ends.
function surfaceSubtreeSize(surface DeclaredSurface, at FieldIndex) returns FieldIndex
	var size = 1 as FieldIndex
	let slots = surfaceNodeSlotCount(surfaceNodeAt(surface, at: at))
	for _ in 0 upto slots 'eachSlot'
		size = size + surfaceSubtreeSize(surface, at: at + size)
	end 'eachSlot'
	return size
end 'surfaceSubtreeSize'

// Fold a declared surface into a running hash — every node plus the node COUNT, so a tree that gained a
// slot cannot collide with the one it grew from (`mixBytes`' trailing length tag, for its reason). The
// surface is the one declaration fact `mixType` structurally cannot carry: `__ManagedMemory` and `ByteArray`
// fold to the identical `genericInstance`, so an edit between the two spellings would move no memo key while
// changing which member roster every caller's calls reach.
export function mixSurface(state ContentHash, surface DeclaredSurface) returns ContentHash
	var h = state
	for node in surface 'eachNode'
		h = mix(h, value: node)
	end 'eachNode'
	return mix(h, value: surface.count())
end 'mixSurface'

// The node index tuple slot `slot` starts at — node 0's first child, advanced past each earlier sibling's
// whole subtree.
function surfaceSlotStart(surface DeclaredSurface, slot FieldIndex) returns FieldIndex
	var at = 1 as FieldIndex
	for _ in 0 upto slot 'skipEarlierSlots'
		at = at + surfaceSubtreeSize(surface, at: at)
	end 'skipEarlierSlots'
	return at
end 'surfaceSlotStart'

// ⭐ **THE THIRTEEN `__ManagedFile` METHODS — the surface BOTH references agree on line-for-line**
// (`maxon-sharp/Compiler/2-Parser.cs:1414-1440`, `maxon-selfhosted/.../LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1801-1842`),
// complete as of R4.2. Nine statics and four instance methods; a name outside this set is refused by name
// at its own span rather than mangled into a callee no file declares
// (`Parser.parseManagedFileStaticCall` / `Parser.dispatchManagedFileMethod`).
//
// ⚠ **THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `seek`.** v1 has an `lseek` primitive, but it belongs to a different v1
// abstraction and neither reference's `__ManagedFile` method list carries one — a fourteenth method here
// would be shv2 inventing surface the language does not have.
export let ManagedFileOpenReadMethod = b"openRead"
export let ManagedFileOpenWriteMethod = b"openWrite"

// ⭐ It is a distinct METHOD and NOT a distinct op: its only difference in either reference is a Unix 0755
// permission bit, and shv2's file IO is host-only Windows by the R4.1 ruling — so it lowers to exactly
// `openWrite`'s `osFileOpenWrite`. A POSIX lane splits them on the day it exists.
export let ManagedFileOpenWriteExecutableMethod = b"openWriteExecutable"
export let ManagedFileExistsMethod = b"exists"
export let ManagedFileDeleteMethod = b"delete"
export let ManagedFileRenameMethod = b"rename"
export let ManagedFileStatMethod = b"stat"
export let ManagedFileStatFieldMethod = b"statField"
export let ManagedFileStatFreeMethod = b"statFree"
export let ManagedFileCloseMethod = b"close"
export let ManagedFileSizeMethod = b"size"
export let ManagedFileReadMethod = b"read"
export let ManagedFileWriteMethod = b"write"

// ⭐ **THE PARAMETER NAMES A `__ManagedFile` ARGUMENT MISMATCH QUOTES BACK.** They are the reference's own
// (`2-Parser.cs:1414-1440`), and they are what `builtin-type-checking.error-managed-file-*` pins: the
// oracle reports `argument type mismatch for 'path'`, so the name is a fact about the SIGNATURE, not a
// word this compiler picks.
// ⚠ Shared with `__ManagedDirectory` (R4.3): both references name the path parameter of `openSearch`,
// `exists` and `create` `path` too, so this is one word for one fact rather than a per-family copy.
export let ManagedFilePathParamName = b"path"
export let ManagedFileOldPathParamName = b"oldPath"
export let ManagedFileNewPathParamName = b"newPath"
export let ManagedFileManagedParamName = b"managed"
export let ManagedFileSizeParamName = b"size"
export let ManagedFileBufferParamName = b"buffer"
export let ManagedFileIndexParamName = b"index"

// ⭐ **THE `__ManagedMemory` OBJECT SURFACE, COMPLETE AS OF R4.4** — the twelve members the corpus reaches,
// each of them the reference's own name (v1 `LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1618-1660`, bootstrap
// `2-Parser.cs:1470-1512`). SIX of them are spelled IDENTICALLY to an `Array` method and dispatch to the
// identical runtime entry (`capacity`, `get`, `set`, `append`, `slice`, `clear`); `length` shares the ENTRY
// with `Array.count` under a DIFFERENT spelling, which is why `mm.count()` is refused (D11b's
// `error.buffer-has-no-count`) — a shared emission is not a shared name; four are the buffer's own
// (`setLength`, `setByte`, `grow`, `byteAt`); the twelfth is the `create` static.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE SET IS NOT STATED HERE — IT IS `Parser.bufferSurfaceMemberNames`, WHICH IS ALSO WHAT THE REFUSAL
// PRINTS (D11b).** This block declares the SPELLINGS; the roster declares MEMBERSHIP, and the paragraph
// above is a reader's summary of it rather than a second authority. It had been exactly such a second
// authority, and it was wrong TWICE — it said SIX and listed seven, then D11b said SEVEN and kept the same
// seven, which reads `length` as an `Array` spelling and so contradicts the case above — while the dispatch
// it described accepted twelve `Array` names the message denied.
//
// ⚠ **`elementSize`, `remove`, `swap`, `shiftLeft`/`shiftRight`, `toCString`, `makeCharFromBytes`,
// `fromCString` and `createCursor` are DELIBERATELY ABSENT** — the reference's member table is 19 deep, this
// is the other 8 of it plus the `fromCString` static. None has a caller shv2 can reach: `elementSize` has
// twelve (`specs/ranged-int-bit-packing.md`, whose subject is ranged-int bit packing shv2 does not have) and
// `remove`/`swap`/`shiftRight`/`elementSize`/`toCString`/`makeCharFromBytes` have `stdlib/Array.maxon`, which
// no whitelisted module imports and which shv2 cannot compile at all; the four cstring/cursor members need a
// `cstring` type shv2 has no producer for. A member with no reachable caller is a mechanism handed a consumer
// that cannot exercise it. `Parser.parseManagedMemoryStaticCall` (the statics) and `Parser.dispatchArrayMethod`
// (the members) name them in their refusals so the gap is a diagnostic rather than a silence.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO REFUSALS EACH MISFILE ONE NAME, and the reference's own comment is where it came from:**
// `createCursor` is registered `["self"], [mm]` — an INSTANCE method — under a `// static methods` heading
// (`2-Parser.cs:1517-1523`), so the static refusal calls it a static and the member refusal calls
// `fromCString` (genuinely `isStatic: true`) a member. Neither is a false statement about BUILDABILITY, which
// is what the sentences are for; correcting the category costs 34 committed goldens, since the `are not built`
// clause is a literal in the message rather than rendered from a list the way the roster now is.
export let ManagedMemoryCreateMethod = b"create"
export let ManagedMemorySetLengthMethod = b"setLength"
export let ManagedMemorySetByteMethod = b"setByte"
export let ManagedMemoryLengthMethod = b"length"
export let ManagedMemoryGrowMethod = b"grow"

// ⭐ **`toCString()` — THE BUFFER'S NUL-TERMINATED VIEW**, and the corpus's only producer of a `cstring`
// (`stdlib/String.maxon:195`, whose `cstr()` is its one caller). Named here beside its siblings rather than
// at the arm, so the roster, the dispatch and the refusal all read one spelling.
export let ManagedMemoryToCStringMethod = b"toCString"

// ⭐ **`makeCharFromBytes(pos, len)` — MINT ONE `Character` FROM A BYTE WINDOW** (`stdlib/String.maxon:301`,
// a `module function` whose whole body forwards to the buffer). Its two parameter names are the corpus's,
// read back rather than invented, because the arm accepts them as argument LABELS.
export let ManagedMemoryMakeCharFromBytesMethod = b"makeCharFromBytes"

export let ManagedMemoryCharPosParamName = b"pos"
export let ManagedMemoryCharLenParamName = b"len"
export let ManagedMemoryElementSizeMethod = b"elementSize"

// ⭐⭐ **THE THREE RELOCATORS' SPELLINGS.** `swap` is what every sort moves elements with
// (`stdlib/helpers/sort/smallSort.maxon:31`) and the two shifts are what `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s
// `insert`/`remove` open and close a hole with (`:312`) — so all three are corpus spellings read back, never
// invented, and the roster, the dispatch, the refusal and `ManagedMemoryRuntime.managedMemorySourceMethodName` all
// read these.
export let ManagedMemorySwapMethod = b"swap"
export let ManagedMemoryShiftRightMethod = b"shiftRight"
export let ManagedMemoryShiftLeftMethod = b"shiftLeft"

// ⚠ **`swap`'s SECOND PARAMETER IS LABELLED AND ITS FIRST IS NOT, WHICH IS THE CORPUS'S OWN CALL AND NOT A
// CHOICE.** `smallSort.maxon:31` writes `managed.swap(i, j: j)`, and v1 registers exactly those two names
// (`LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1656`). Both shifts take `(index, count)` and the corpus labels neither
// (`stdlib/Array.maxon:312` writes `managed.shiftRight(index, len - index)`), which the shared
// `parseTwoBuiltinIntegerArgList` already permits — it consumes a matching label when one is written and
// requires none. Their two labels are `ManagedMemoryIndexParamName`/`ManagedMemoryCountParamName`, already
// declared below for `get`/`create` — a parameter-name constant is about a SPELLING, and a second `b"count"`
// beside the first is the drift these blocks exist to prevent.
export let ManagedMemorySwapFirstParamName = b"i"
export let ManagedMemorySwapSecondParamName = b"j"

// ⭐⭐ **`createCursor()` AND THE SIX MEMBERS OF WHAT IT HANDS BACK.** Every spelling is
// `stdlib/Array.maxon:490-531`'s, read back rather than invented: `ArrayIterator` is the cursor's only
// consumer and forwards each member one-for-one, so its declarations ARE the surface. v1 registers the same
// six (`LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1488-1498`).
//
// ⚠ `peek`'s parameter name is the corpus's too, and the arm accepts it as an argument LABEL, which is why
// it is named here beside the methods rather than at the arm. `seek`'s is `index`, which is
// `ManagedMemoryIndexParamName` already — a parameter-name constant is about a SPELLING, and a second
// `b"index"` beside the first is the drift these blocks exist to prevent.
export let ManagedMemoryCreateCursorMethod = b"createCursor"
export let CursorCurrentMethod = b"current"
export let CursorIndexMethod = b"index"
export let CursorAdvanceMethod = b"advance"
export let CursorRetreatMethod = b"retreat"
export let CursorSeekMethod = b"seek"
export let CursorPeekMethod = b"peek"
export let CursorPeekParamName = b"ahead"

// ⭐ **THE `Array` SPELLINGS A SOURCE-METHOD MAP HAS TO NAME (D12, completed by D11c).** Every one of these
// was a PARSER constant, file-private in `Parser.maxon`, and each moved here the moment a runtime family's
// source-method map had to answer with it: a map cannot name a constant its file cannot see, and the
// alternative — a second `b"set"` beside the first — is exactly the drift these blocks exist to prevent.
//
// `set` and `append` came over at D12 for `ManagedMemoryRuntime.managedMemorySourceMethodName` (the buffer's two
// shared spellings). **D11c brought the other six**, because `ManagedMemoryRuntime.arrayAccessorSourceMethodName`
// now answers for all nine throwing `Array` accessors rather than for the buffer subset alone — see that
// map for why the array family stopped being the one family without a spelling.
//
// ⚠ **`append` NO LONGER HAS A SOURCE-METHOD ARM — the 2026-08-07 ruling made the buffer's `append`
// NON-THROWING and folded `__managed_mem_append` away (`ManagedMemoryRuntime.ManagedAppendName`) — AND IT STAYS HERE ON THE
// BLOCK'S OWN RULE RATHER THAN ON THE ONE THAT BROUGHT IT.** A source-method map is only ever the FIRST
// reason a spelling has to be visible outside `Parser.maxon`; the standing one is below, and it is why
// `AppendMemoryMethod`, `ArrayPopMethod` and `ListPrependMethod` — none of which any map names — sit here too.
//
// ⚠ None carries a family prefix, and that is the fact rather than an omission: ONE spelling serves
// `String.append`, `List.append` and `Array.append`/`Array.set` — which since that ruling is the buffer's
// `append` as well — and the parser dispatches all of them on this one byte string. `RemoveMethod` and
// `SliceMethod` are unprefixed for the same reason: the buffer and the `Array` both spell `slice`.
export let ArraySetMethod = b"set"
export let AppendMethod = b"append"

// ⭐ **`appendMemory(source)` — APPEND ENTERED THROUGH THE MEMORY RATHER THAN THE ARRAY.** It is
// `stdlib/Array.maxon:272`'s member and `append`'s whole body (`:262` is `appendMemory(other.managed)`), so
// the two are ONE emission under two names — `length`/`count`'s arrangement, one member over. Since the
// envelope collapse an `Array` IS its `__ManagedMemory`, which is why the corpus keeps both: a caller already
// holding the memory would otherwise allocate an `Array` record per call to hand it over, and
// `StringBuilder.appendBytes` paid exactly that (`stdlib/String.maxon:830`, this member's one corpus caller).
//
// ⚠ **IT IS AN `Array` MEMBER AND ONLY AN `Array` MEMBER, WHICH IS THE OTHER HALF OF `length`/`count`'s
// ARRANGEMENT.** The buffer shares the EMISSION, never the name: `appendMemory` appears in neither reference's
// `__ManagedMemory` member table (v1 `LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1618-1660`, bootstrap `2-Parser.cs:1470-1512`) and
// no corpus caller writes `mm.appendMemory(…)`, so `Parser.bufferSurfaceMemberNames` deliberately omits it and
// `managed-memory-methods.error.buffer-has-no-appendMemory` is the program that says so. It is nonetheless
// unprefixed for this block's rule — one spelling, whichever surfaces come to serve it — as `set`, `append`,
// `slice` and `byteAt` above it each record.
export let AppendMemoryMethod = b"appendMemory"
export let GetMethod = b"get"
export let ArrayFirstMethod = b"first"
export let ArrayPopMethod = b"pop"
export let RemoveMethod = b"remove"
export let SliceMethod = b"slice"

// ⚠ **`insert` JOINED THIS BLOCK WHEN `Map.insert` WAS BUILT**, for the block's own reason: it is the second
// throwing entry point a `Map` had while its runtime existed, so the deleted `mapSourceMethodName` had to name the very byte
// string the parser's dispatch matched. `Array`, `Set` and `Map` all spell it, which is why it carries no
// family prefix — the rule `set`/`append`/`slice`/`remove` above it already state.
export let InsertMethod = b"insert"

// ⚠ **`byteAt` JOINED THIS BLOCK WHEN `String.byteAt` WAS BUILT (BATCH2 slice 6), AND IT WAS SPELLED
// `ManagedMemoryByteAtMethod` ONE SURFACE ABOVE UNTIL THEN.** It belongs here for the same reason `set`,
// `append` and `slice` do — the buffer and the `String` spell it identically and the parser dispatches both
// on this one byte string — and a family-prefixed constant serving two families is the drift these blocks
// exist to prevent, one rename away from a second `b"byteAt"` beside the first.
export let ByteAtMethod = b"byteAt"

// The four spellings only a `List` has. They live in THIS block rather than in `Parser.maxon` for the reason
// the eight above them do: `ListRuntime.listAccessorSourceMethodName` has to name `removeFirst`, `removeLast`
// and `last`, and a map cannot name a constant its file cannot see. `prepend` sits beside them so the
// vocabulary a list adds to the language is read in one place; `append`/`get`/`first`/`count`/`create` are
// already shared above, and so are `insert`/`remove` — a `List` dispatches on the very same byte strings an
// `Array` and a `Map` do, which is this block's standing rule.
//
// ⚠ **`last` IS `List`-PREFIXED THOUGH `first` ABOVE IS `Array`-PREFIXED, and the asymmetry is real rather
// than an oversight**: `Array.first` is still a compiler-dispatched member and `Array.last` is a CORPUS one
// (`stdlib/Array.maxon`), so no `Array` dispatch matches `b"last"` and there is no second surface for an
// unprefixed constant to serve yet. A second one that spelled it would take this constant rather than mint a
// fifth, exactly as `ClearMethod` was un-prefixed the day the chain spelled it.
export let ListPrependMethod = b"prepend"
export let ListLastMethod = b"last"
export let ListRemoveFirstMethod = b"removeFirst"
export let ListRemoveLastMethod = b"removeLast"

// The spellings only the `__ManagedList` surface has (spec-port `ownership`; `/specs/managed-list.md`).
// They live here beside `List`'s pair so the chain's whole vocabulary is read in one place. `create`,
// `count`, `remove`, `clear` and `isEmpty` are NOT among them: those are already spelled once above (or in
// `Parser`'s shared block) and the managed list dispatches on the very same byte strings, which is this
// block's rule.
//
// ⚠ `value` is a METHOD name, and the three other `b"value"` constants in this compiler are a print
// PARAMETER name, a `Map.upsert` argument LABEL and a `__ManagedMemory` parameter name — different name
// spaces, already kept apart here and in `Parser`. This is the first method of that spelling; a second
// container spelling it would share this constant rather than mint a fifth.
//
// ⚠ **`head`/`tail`/`next`/`prev` ARE HERE AND NOT IN `Parser.maxon` FOR `removeFirst`'s REASON, VERBATIM**:
// `ListRuntime.listAccessorSourceMethodName` has to name all four — they are the surface's four THROWING
// navigators — and a map cannot name a constant its file cannot see.
//
// ⚠ **`next` IS ALSO A `__ManagedDirectory` MEMBER (`ManagedDirectoryNextMethod`), AND THE TWO ARE
// DELIBERATELY NOT SHARED.** A directory's `next` yields the next ENTRY and throws when the walk is done;
// a node's yields a node HANDLE. One byte string, two surfaces, two dispatchers — and the shared-constant
// rule this block states is about a member two surfaces spell to mean THE SAME THING (`get`, `remove`,
// `insert`), which these do not.
export let ManagedListInsertFirstMethod = b"insertFirst"
export let ManagedListInsertLastMethod = b"insertLast"
export let ManagedListInsertAfterMethod = b"insertAfter"
export let ManagedListInsertBeforeMethod = b"insertBefore"
export let ManagedListReinsertFirstMethod = b"reinsertFirst"
export let ManagedListReinsertLastMethod = b"reinsertLast"
export let ManagedListHeadMethod = b"head"
export let ManagedListTailMethod = b"tail"
export let ManagedListNodeValueMethod = b"value"
export let ManagedListNodeSetValueMethod = b"setValue"
export let ManagedListNodeNextMethod = b"next"
export let ManagedListNodePrevMethod = b"prev"

// The one member a per-node refcount buys and an unrefcounted node could not have: unlink WITHOUT freeing,
// so the caller's handle keeps the node and its element alive (`/specs/managed-list.md:core.detach`).
export let ManagedListDetachMethod = b"detach"

// Its parameter names, the reference's own (`2-Parser.cs:1470-1511`) for the same reason the file's are.
// ⚠ **`endIndex` IS v1's SPELLING AND THE BOOTSTRAP'S IS `end`** (`LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1657` vs
// `2-Parser.cs:1509`), and `specs/managed-memory-builtin.md:slice` writes `endIndex:` — that spec is
// `status: selfhosted`, so v1's is the one the corpus pins, and it is also `Array.slice`'s own label
// (`EndIndexArgLabel`), which the SAME dispatch arm serves. Measured: the bootstrap answers
// `E3003 unknown parameter name: 'endIndex'` for it.
export let ManagedMemoryCountParamName = b"count"
export let ManagedMemoryElementSizeParamName = b"elementSize"
export let ManagedMemoryNewLengthParamName = b"newLength"
export let ManagedMemoryNewCapacityParamName = b"newCapacity"
export let ManagedMemoryOffsetParamName = b"offset"
export let ManagedMemoryIndexParamName = b"index"

// ⚠ `Array.append`'s parameter carries this SAME label, so both append doors reach this one constant (through
// `Parser.appendArgParamName`) rather than minting a second `b"other"` — one label, one spelling. It is in
// this block because the buffer surface is where the label was first written down, not because the `Array`
// surface has another.
export let ManagedMemoryOtherParamName = b"other"

// ⭐ **`appendMemory`'s PARAMETER, AND THE ONE THING THE `append` FOLD MUST NOT LOSE.** The two spellings
// share an emission and an argument RULE, but the corpus declares two names for the one operation —
// `append(other Self)` at `stdlib/Array.maxon:261`, `appendMemory(source ElementMemory)` at `:272` — and the
// refusal's whole job is to name the parameter the author can act on. `Parser.appendArgParamName` reads the
// spelling off the method token so neither door can answer for the other.
export let ArrayAppendMemorySourceParamName = b"source"
export let ManagedMemoryValueParamName = b"value"

// ⭐ **THE MACHINE-WORD ELEMENT WIDTH A `__ManagedMemory.create(count, 8)` NAMES (R4.4 review)** — the
// second and last of the two widths shv2 can give a buffer an element TYPE for, `ByteStringElementSize`
// being the first. It is `LayoutDescriptor.MachineWordBytes` in the type a PARSED source constant arrives
// as, spelled here for the reason `ManagedArrayElementBytes` is spelled in this file: a `ByteOffset` and a
// `ParsedInt` are distinct types, and the comparison happens on the parsed side.
// ⇒ Read by `managedMemoryInstanceForElementSize` (which width maps to which instance) and by
// `managedMemoryElementSizeIsTypeable` (which widths are admitted at all), so those two answer about ONE
// set of widths rather than two lists that can drift.
let ManagedMemoryWordElementSize = 8 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **THE ERROR A `__ManagedMemory` MEMBER THROWS (R4.2)** — `stdlib/Builtins.maxon:151-160`, and NOT
// `ArrayError`: `invalidLength` and `invalidByteRange` name conditions `ArrayError` has no case for, so a
// handler that binds the error would be shown cases the operation cannot report. The eight cases are the
// reference's, in the reference's ORDER, because the ordinal IS the error flag the runtime returns — the same
// wire format `__ManagedFileError`'s order is.
//
// ⚠ R4.2 delivers three of the eight-case surface's members (`create`/`setLength`/`setByte`), so five of
// these cases are unreachable TODAY. They are declared anyway rather than added one per rung: the ordinals
// are positional, so a later rung that appends `sliceOutOfBounds` between two of them would silently reroute
// every handler already written against the others.
export let ManagedMemoryErrorTypeName = b"__ManagedMemoryError"

// ⭐⭐ **`ArrayError` — WHAT EVERY THROWING `Array` ACCESSOR THROWS, AND WHY IT WEARS A RESERVED NAME (R4.4).**
// `stdlib/Array.maxon:6-9` declares it with exactly two cases, and `specs-shv2/array-slots.md` discriminates
// both (`emptySlot` for an index INSIDE `[0, count())` whose slot was never filled, `indexOutOfBounds` for one
// that is not) — so the enum has to be REGISTERED, or `match e` over an `arr.get` failure reports a missing
// FEATURE for a program whose feature is present. That is the same gap R4.1 closed for `__ManagedFileError` and
// R4.2's review closed for `__ManagedMemoryError`; this is the third and last family.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT IS THE BARE NAME, NOT A RESERVED `__ArrayError`, AND THAT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN CHOSEN.** The
// two sibling builtin error enums are `__`-prefixed (`StringIndexTypeName` gives the argument: a user
// declaration of the same name would land in the same map bucket and the later write would win). This one
// cannot be, because USER SOURCE ALREADY NAMES IT: four committed cases declare `function … throws ArrayError`
// and PROPAGATE an array accessor's failure through it (`array-slice-managed-elements.md` ×3,
// `borrow-liveness.md`). Under `__ArrayError` every one of them became
// `E3059: try propagates '__ArrayError' but enclosing function throws 'ArrayError'` — one error type under two
// spellings, which is the very confusion the prefix exists to prevent.
// ⇒ So `ArrayError` is RESERVED PROGRAM-WIDE here, exactly as whitelisting a stdlib module reserves its type
// names (`StdlibLoader`'s collision rule) and as `CharSet` already is — and for the same reason: shv2
// synthesizes what `stdlib/Array.maxon` declares, so it owes that file's exported names.
export let ArrayErrorTypeName = b"ArrayError"

// ⭐⭐ **`StringError` — WHAT EVERY THROWING `String` OPERATION THROWS, AND IT WEARS THE BARE NAME FOR
// `ArrayError`'S EXACT REASON.** `stdlib/String.maxon:6-9` declares it with exactly two cases, and its
// provenance is `ArrayError`'s to the letter: a module the stdlib loader does not list, so there is no
// declaration to be read and the compiler synthesizes what that file declares
// (`Project.builtinStringErrorEnum`).
//
// ⚠ **"UNLISTABLE BY CONSTRUCTION AND NOT MERELY UNLISTED" is what this paragraph used to claim, on the
// ground that `String` is compiler-owned — and W16 measured that false.** Compiler-ownership reserves a name
// against a USER declaration and has never barred the corpus. What is UNLISTED and what is UNLISTABLE were
// one word apart here, and the difference decides whether this synthesis is permanent or scaffolding: it is
// scaffolding, and it retires when String's representation converges with the corpus declaration's.
//
// ⚠ It cannot be `__StringError` for the reason `ArrayError` cannot be `__ArrayError`: SOURCE ALREADY SPELLS
// THE BARE NAME in a `throws` clause — `stdlib/CommandLine.maxon:29,39` write `throws StringError` and
// `throw StringError.notFound` — and a reserved spelling would meet every one of them as
// `E3059: try propagates '__StringError' but enclosing function throws 'StringError'`.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE SPELLING WAS ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN HERE-BUT-ELSEWHERE, AS A `String`.** `StringIndexRuntime` held
// `let StringErrorTypeName = "StringError"` for the `throws` clause it stamps on the two searches; that was
// the same fact in a second home and a second TYPE, so it now reads this one.
export let StringErrorTypeName = b"StringError"

// ⚠ **`StringErrorNotFoundCaseName` / `StringErrorInvalidIndexCaseName` STOOD HERE AND ARE DELETED — THEY
// HAD NO READER LEFT.** They named `stdlib/String.maxon:6-9`'s two cases for two consumers, and both are
// gone: the ordinal drift GUARD was retired when nothing was found to transcribe those ordinals
// (`Compiler.maxon`'s `StringError WAS THE SIXTH` note — the corpus's own `throw StringError.notFound`
// resolves its tag from the declaration), and `land-the-listing` retired the last one, the
// `builtinStringErrorEnum` seed that restated the case list a listed module already declares. A constant
// whose only remaining mention is its own declaration is a third copy of the case order waiting to
// disagree with the other two, which is the defect this whole neighbourhood exists to prevent — so it is
// removed rather than kept warm for a guard that has no ordinal to check.

// ⭐ **`ArrayError`'s TWO CASES ARE `__ManagedMemoryError`'s FIRST TWO, IN THE SAME ORDER, AND THAT IS THE
// REFERENCE'S OWN ARRANGEMENT** (`stdlib/Array.maxon:6-9` against `stdlib/Builtins.maxon:151-153`). So ONE pair
// of name constants serves both enums: writing them twice would let the two orders drift apart while each file
// still read "correct", and the ordinal IS the error flag a throwing runtime returns in both families.
export let ManagedMemoryIndexOutOfBoundsCaseName = b"indexOutOfBounds"
export let ManagedMemoryEmptySlotCaseName = b"emptySlot"
export let ManagedMemorySliceOutOfBoundsCaseName = b"sliceOutOfBounds"
export let ManagedMemoryInvalidLengthCaseName = b"invalidLength"
export let ManagedMemoryInvalidCapacityCaseName = b"invalidCapacity"
export let ManagedMemoryShiftOutOfBoundsCaseName = b"shiftOutOfBounds"
export let ManagedMemoryInvalidByteRangeCaseName = b"invalidByteRange"
export let ManagedMemoryInvalidAllocationCaseName = b"invalidAllocation"

// `String.init(managed ByteMemory)` — `stdlib/String.maxon:114`, the corpus's way back from a
// `__ManagedMemory` to a `String`. Its parameter name is the reference's.
export let StringInitMethod = b"init"
export let StringInitManagedParamName = b"managed"

// ⭐ **`c.asciiValue()` — `stdlib/Character.maxon:154-166`, the one THROWING member left with a SYNTHESIZED
// arm.** It lives here rather than as a file-private `let` in the parser because
// `GtRuntime.runtimeCalleeSourceMethod` has to answer E3057 with the very constant
// `dispatchCharacterMethod` matches the source token against, and a parser-private spelling would force a
// second `b"asciiValue"` in the runtime layer — one fact in two places.
//
// ⚠ **IT USED TO HAVE THREE NEIGHBOURS, AND THEY LEFT WITH THEIR ARMS (W49 wave 3).**
// `StringFindFirstMethod`/`StringFindLastMethod` (D12) and `StringIndexAfterMethod` were here for exactly
// this reason and no other: `__strix_first`/`__strix_last`/`__strix_after` were the throwing runtime
// callees behind `findFirst`/`findLast`/`indexAfter`, and `stringIndexSourceMethodName` mapped each back
// to the source spelling so the E3057 named the author's own construct. All three members are the corpus's
// now, so their E3057 comes from the ORDINARY declared-function path, which reads the callee's real name
// and needs no map — the three constants, the map and the predicate over it are gone together.
export let CharacterAsciiValueMethod = b"asciiValue"

// ⭐ **THE ERROR EVERY `__ManagedFile` OPERATION THROWS (R4.1)**, and the type a handler's `match e`
// discriminates. It is DECLARED at `stdlib/Builtins.maxon:175` and read from there (A1s); the reason its
// case ORDER is load-bearing is `Project.BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck`'s, which holds this file's ordinals to it.
export let ManagedFileErrorTypeName = b"__ManagedFileError"

// The three case names, in the order `specs/managed-file.md` states them — which IS their ordinal order,
// and therefore the error flag the runtime returns. Not reorderable; see the layout's comment.
export let ManagedFileNotFoundCaseName = b"notFound"
export let ManagedFileAccessDeniedCaseName = b"accessDenied"
export let ManagedFileOpenFailedCaseName = b"openFailed"

// The eight R4.2 cases, APPENDED in v1's exact order (`LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1781-1795`). Appending is not
// a style choice: the ordinal IS the wire format the runtime returns as an error flag, so inserting or
// reordering one silently reroutes every handler in the corpus.
export let ManagedFileReadFailedCaseName = b"readFailed"
export let ManagedFileWriteFailedCaseName = b"writeFailed"
export let ManagedFileSizeFailedCaseName = b"sizeFailed"
export let ManagedFileDeleteFailedCaseName = b"deleteFailed"
export let ManagedFileStatFailedCaseName = b"statFailed"
export let ManagedFileInvalidStatBufferCaseName = b"invalidStatBuffer"
export let ManagedFileInvalidStatIndexCaseName = b"invalidStatIndex"
export let ManagedFileClosedCaseName = b"closed"

// The FIELD that yields it from a byte array — `stdlib/String.maxon:68`'s `var managed as ByteMemory`,
// which the corpus reaches as `"…".toByteArray().managed`. An identity read; see the arm in
// `Parser.dispatchArrayMethod`.
export let ManagedMemoryFieldName = b"managed"

// ⭐ **THE ONE PLACE THAT ANSWERS "is this a compiler builtin MANAGED type?"** — read by
// `Parser.requireConstructible` to raise E3072, and the list a later slice extends when
// `__ManagedMemory` and `__ManagedDirectory` arrive (E3072's own registry doc already names all three).
//
// ⚠ **DELIBERATELY NOT FOLDED INTO `isCompilerOwnedTypeName`, and the distinction is load-bearing rather
// than tidy.** That predicate answers "does the compiler RESERVE this name?" — it gates D4's
// undeclared-name verdict and `requireTypeNameNotCompilerOwned`'s refusal of a user declaration binding
// one of its six. This one answers "is this an opaque OS-handle type a struct literal may never build?".
// The two sets are disjoint today (none of those six is managed, and this type is not reserved under a
// bare alias) and they are asked at different doors for different reasons; merging them would give one
// predicate two meanings and make the next `CharacterSet`-shaped misreport inevitable.
export function isBuiltinManagedTypeName(name ByteArray) returns bool
	return name.equals(ManagedFileTypeName) or name.equals(ManagedDirectoryTypeName)
end 'isBuiltinManagedTypeName'

// Its one field, per the spec's "Type Structure". Unexported and immutable: the corpus never reads or
// writes it — `__ManagedFile{_handle: 0}` is E3072 (`managed-file.error-direct-construction`) precisely
// so the handle cannot be forged — and the runtime reaches it through `ManagedFileHandleOffset`.
export let ManagedFileHandleFieldName = b"_handle"
export let ManagedFileHandleFieldIndex = 0 as FieldIndex

// ⭐ **THE COMPILER-OWNED `__ManagedDirectory` TYPE (R4.3)** — an 8-byte box holding one pointer to a
// separately allocated FIND BLOCK (the search handle, the `WIN32_FIND_DATAA` the OS refills, and the
// advance-first pending flag), with a runtime-owned destructor that closes the search on drop.
// `specs/managed-directory.md`'s "Type Structure" states the single `_block` field and the RAII contract;
// `ManagedDirectoryRuntime`'s header states why the record is two-level and why the two references disagree
// about it.
//
// ⚠ Like `__ManagedFile` and unlike the retired `__StringIndex`/`__CharacterSet`, the registry name and the user-facing
// name are the SAME bytes — which is what lets the type cross a function boundary, and the corpus requires
// exactly that: `specs/managed-directory.md`'s `search-and-list` declares `export var dir as
// __ManagedDirectory` and returns a `TestDir` built from one.
export let ManagedDirectoryTypeName = b"__ManagedDirectory"

// ⭐ **THE SEVEN `__ManagedDirectory` METHODS — the surface BOTH references declare, line for line**
// (`maxon-sharp/Compiler/2-Parser.cs:1449-1463`, `maxon-selfhosted/.../LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1903-1919`).
// Four statics and three instance methods; a name outside this set is refused by name at its own span rather
// than mangled into a callee no file declares (`Parser.parseManagedDirectoryStaticCall` /
// `Parser.dispatchManagedDirectoryMethod`).
export let ManagedDirectoryOpenSearchMethod = b"openSearch"
export let ManagedDirectoryExistsMethod = b"exists"
export let ManagedDirectoryCreateMethod = b"create"
export let ManagedDirectoryCurrentPathMethod = b"currentPath"
export let ManagedDirectoryNextMethod = b"next"
export let ManagedDirectoryFilenameMethod = b"filename"
export let ManagedDirectoryCloseMethod = b"close"

// ⭐ **THE ERROR EVERY THROWING `__ManagedDirectory` OPERATION THROWS (R4.3)**, and the type a handler's
// `match e` discriminates. It is DECLARED at `stdlib/Builtins.maxon:190` and read from there (A1s); the
// reason its case ORDER is load-bearing is `Project.BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck`'s, which holds this file's
// ordinals to it.
export let ManagedDirectoryErrorTypeName = b"__ManagedDirectoryError"

// The eight case names in `stdlib/Builtins.maxon:189-199`'s order — which IS their ordinal order, and
// therefore the error flag the runtime returns. Not reorderable; see the layout's comment.
//
// ⚠ **THE ORDER IS THE ENUM'S, NOT THE BOOTSTRAP COMMENT'S.** `2-Parser.cs:1442-1444` lists SEVEN and omits
// `iteratorInvalid`, which would shift every later ordinal by one. It is the third reference comment found
// stale about its own enum.
export let ManagedDirectoryNotFoundCaseName = b"notFound"
export let ManagedDirectoryAccessDeniedCaseName = b"accessDenied"
export let ManagedDirectoryOpenSearchFailedCaseName = b"openSearchFailed"
export let ManagedDirectoryNextFailedCaseName = b"nextFailed"
export let ManagedDirectoryIteratorInvalidCaseName = b"iteratorInvalid"
export let ManagedDirectoryCreateFailedCaseName = b"createFailed"
export let ManagedDirectoryCurrentPathFailedCaseName = b"currentPathFailed"
export let ManagedDirectoryClosedCaseName = b"closed"

// ⭐ **THE ERROR A POSSIBLY-ZERO `/` OR `mod` THROWS (A1)**, and the type an `otherwise (e)` handler's
// `match e` discriminates. Its ONE case lives with the layout, in `Project.builtinDivisionByZeroErrorEnum`.
//
// ⚠ The `__` prefix is what keeps this name out of user source (`requireUnreservedName`, E2051) — the same
// reason the file/directory/memory families wear it, and the reason `ArrayError` (which cannot, because the
// corpus spells it) needed a place in `isCompilerOwnedTypeName` instead.
export let DivisionByZeroErrorTypeName = b"__DivisionByZeroError"

// The single case name. Its ordinal is 0 because it is the enum's first (and only) case, which the layout
// DERIVES from declaration position (`addBuiltinEnumCase`) and `verifyDivisionByZeroOrdinals` holds the
// lowering's transcription to.
export let DivisionByZeroCaseName = b"divisionByZero"

// ⭐ **THE ERROR `<Enum>.fromRawValue(raw)` THROWS WHEN NO CASE CARRIES THAT RAW VALUE.** Declared in no
// corpus file, so it is SEEDED (`Project.builtinEnumLookupErrorEnum`) exactly as the division one above is,
// and its ordinal is transcribed in exactly one place (`Runtime/EnumLookupRuntime.maxon`).
//
// ⚠ **IT WEARS THE `__` PREFIX, WHERE `ArrayError`/`MapError` COULD NOT, AND THE TEST IS WHO WRITES THE NAME.**
// Those two are spelled by CORPUS source (`stdlib/Array.maxon`, `stdlib/Map.maxon`) and by any user function
// that propagates one, so a reserved spelling would make one error type meet itself under two names. Every
// use of `fromRawValue` in the reference corpus CATCHES rather than propagates — v1's own decoders are
// `try <E>.unionCases.fromRawValue(raw) otherwise throw CacheCorruption.invalidEncoding`, eight of them —
// and `/specs` names no type for it at all, so nothing outside this compiler spells it. The prefix therefore
// keeps it out of user source (`requireUnreservedName`, E2051) at no cost, which is the division family's
// position exactly.
export let EnumLookupErrorTypeName = b"__EnumLookupError"

// The single case name. Its ordinal is 0 because it is the enum's first and only case, DERIVED from
// declaration position by `addBuiltinEnumCase`.
export let EnumLookupNoSuchRawValueCaseName = b"noSuchRawValue"

// ⭐ **THE ERROR `Character.asciiValue()` THROWS ON A CLUSTER THAT IS NOT ONE ASCII BYTE.** The CORPUS
// declares it (`stdlib/Character.maxon:15-17`), so it cannot wear the `__` prefix — `ArrayError`'s position
// exactly — and `Runtime/GraphemeRuntime.buildCharAscii` transcribes its ordinal as the literal error flag
// `__char_ascii` returns on the failure edge. That is the two-halves shape `BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck` exists
// for, and it is the criterion `Compiler.compileToCodeResult`'s roster states for itself when it explains why
// `StringError` and `MapError` LEFT: a family is on the roster exactly while a throwing runtime graph
// transcribes its ordinals.
//
// ⛔ **IT WAS THE ONE FAMILY MEETING THAT CRITERION AND MISSING FROM THAT ROSTER — MEASURED (W135 review).**
// Inserting a case ahead of `notAscii` in `stdlib/Character.maxon` moved the declared ordinal to 1 while
// `buildCharAscii` went on emitting 0, and the compile exited **0 with no diagnostic**. No wrong answer was
// reachable on that tree only because the decode half is unbuilt — `match e` over this enum is E2015, since
// `runtimeThrowsClause` does not seed it — so the drift was a wire format waiting for a reader, which is
// precisely what a guard is for rather than an argument that one is unnecessary.
export let CharacterErrorTypeName = b"CharacterError"

// The single case name. Its ordinal is 0 because it is the enum's first and only case — a fact about the
// DECLARATION, which is the direction `verifyCharacterErrorOrdinals` reads it in: the corpus states the
// order and the runtime's literal is held to it, never the reverse.
export let CharacterNotAsciiCaseName = b"notAscii"

// Its one field, per the spec's "Type Structure". Unexported and immutable: the corpus never reads or writes
// it — `__ManagedDirectory{_block: 0}` is E3072 (`managed-directory.error-direct-construction`) precisely so
// the block pointer cannot be forged — and the runtime reaches it through `ManagedDirectoryBlockOffset`.
export let ManagedDirectoryBlockFieldName = b"_block"
export let ManagedDirectoryBlockFieldIndex = 0 as FieldIndex

// The alias for a `CharacterSet`'s member set — `typealias CharSet = Set with Character`, which
// `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon:19` declares and the corpus spells (`specs/unicode-category.md:121`). The
// compiler upserted its own copy into `genericAliases` until W129; the corpus declaration is the authority
// now, and this constant survives as the RESERVATION's key: `TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName` names
// it, and `compilerOwnedTypeMeaning` gives it the one non-`nominalIdentity` meaning in that whole set — so a
// USER file writing the SAME alias is admitted and one writing `Set with String` is refused.
export let CharacterMemberSetAliasName = b"CharSet"

// The one-element `[integer]` argument list of the `Array with integer` literal instance — built fresh
// per call (a `MaxonTypeArray` is owned by the instance it is interned into).
function singletonInteger() returns MaxonTypeArray
	var args = MaxonTypeArray.create()
	args.push(MaxonType.integer)
	return args
end 'singletonInteger'

// ⭐⭐ **A TUPLE IS A SYNTHESIZED STRUCT, NOT A STRUCTURAL TYPE VARIANT (P1.10).** `(10, 32)` mints a
// `StructLayout` under a reserved, MANGLED name and the value is typed with the existing
// `MaxonType.structRef(TypeNameId)` — so a tuple adds no `MaxonType.tuple` / `ValueTypeTag.tuple` arm, and
// none of the ~25 exhaustive `match` sites over those two unions grows a case. Every tuple mechanism then
// falls out of machinery that already exists and is already debugged: the box is `emitStructLiteral`'s
// `__mm_alloc` plus one `storeIndirect` per element, `t.0` is `emitFieldLoad` at `offsetOfField(0)`, and a
// tuple holding a `String` gets its whole drop cascade from `installStructDestructors`, which synthesizes one
// for any layout in `project.structTypes` that owns a managed field.
//
// The bootstrap does exactly this (`IrStructType … isTuple: true`, positional fields `_0`/`_1`, mangled
// `__Tuple_<elem>_<elem>` — `maxon-sharp/Compiler/MLIR/Core/MlirType.cs:282-295`). v1 instead mints a
// `__TupleN` BASE struct plus a `genericInstance` over the element types (`Parser.maxon:17127`), and shv2
// takes the bootstrap's MONOMORPHIC form for two reasons: `StructLayoutMap` is name-keyed and `structRef`
// already resolves id → name → layout, so a mangled name drops straight in; and a generic instance's RANGED
// argument is part of its identity (e4146cf8e), which would make `(Integer, Integer)` and `(int, int)` two
// different tuple types — the very thing `specs-shv2/tuples.md`'s `tuple-as-param` requires them not to be.
//
// ⭐⭐ **BUT A NESTED ELEMENT IS INTERNED, NOT INLINED (W9) — v1's structural gid, spent inside the
// monomorphic form rather than instead of it.** The bootstrap's mangling spells a nested tuple by its whole
// name, which flattens a DAG of tuple types into a TREE: see `tupleElementTokenFor` for the measurement
// that closed `A3j`. v1 never had that cost because its element is a `GenericInstanceId` — an integer that
// names one interned entry however deep it is — and that is exactly what the token below restores. The two
// halves are independent: the LAYOUT stays monomorphic and name-keyed (both reasons above still hold), and
// only the way one tuple's identity CITES another's changes.
export let TupleTypeNamePrefix = b"__Tuple"

// ⚠ **THE ARITY RIDES IN FRONT AND THE ELEMENTS JOIN WITH A CHARACTER NO ELEMENT NAME CAN HOLD** — the
// injectivity rule the Architecture states for `witnessTableLabel`, spent here. An `_` join is NOT injective
// (`_` is inside the identifier alphabet), so `(A_B, C)` and `(A, B_C)` would both spell `__Tuple_A_B_C` and
// the second would silently BECOME the first: one layout, two element lists, and a drop cascade taken from
// whichever registered last. `.` is outside the alphabet the lexer admits inside an identifier.
//
// ⭐ **AND SINCE W9 THE RULE IS TOTAL RATHER THAN NEARLY-TOTAL: NO element token may hold the separator.**
// A nested tuple used to be the one exemption — its own name holds separators, and it was admitted on the
// argument that the leading arity made the flattened list self-delimiting (`((i,i),i,i)` against
// `((i,i,i),i)`). That argument is gone with the thing it excused: a nested element is now a single
// `.`-free token (`tupleElementTokenFor`), so splitting the join on `.` recovers the element list outright
// and `requireSeparatorFreeElementName` has no case to exempt.
//
// ⚠ Deliberately its own constant rather than `IrInterface`'s `WitnessLabelSeparator` or the parser's
// `MethodNameSeparator`, for the reason the first of those states at length: three joins whose admissible
// sets differ are three facts, and one constant would couple them so a change to any moved the others.
export let TupleElementSeparator = "."

// The positional field name of tuple element `indexDigits` — `_0`, `_1`, … THE one speller, shared by the
// layout builder here and the parser's `.N` → field-name rewrite (`Parser.consumeMemberNameToken`), so the
// slot a literal fills and the slot `t.0` reads cannot come to disagree. It is also the name the SOURCE may
// spell directly (`p._0`, which `specs-shv2/tuples.md` writes in five cases): a tuple's fields are ordinary
// struct fields, so the positional sugar and the bare name resolve through one `indexOfField`.
//
// ⚠ **IT TAKES THE INDEX AS ITS DIGITS, and that is what makes it genuinely the ONE speller.** The parser
// holds exactly that — the `intLiteral` token's own text — so a `FieldIndex` parameter would leave it
// re-rendering `_`+digits for itself (the shape this comment used to CLAIM was shared while the parser
// spelled its own), and decoding the text to a number first would turn `t.<huge>` from the "no field named
// '_<huge>'" it reports today into a ranged-cast panic.
export function tupleFieldName(indexDigits ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	return "_{String.from(indexDigits)}".toByteArray()
end 'tupleFieldName'

// `__Tuple<N>` — the head BOTH tuple-name builders start from: the mangled NAME (which appends its
// elements, `ProgramSignatures.tupleTypeNameOf`) and the ELEMENT TOKEN (read back off a built name by
// `tupleTypeNameHeadOf`, so that only ONE of the two ever spells it). One speller, so the two cannot come
// to disagree about how a tuple's arity is written — `instanceNameStem`'s arrangement, for its reason.
function tupleTypeNameHead(arity FieldIndex) returns String
	return "{String.from(TupleTypeNamePrefix)}{arity}"
end 'tupleTypeNameHead'

// The head READ BACK OFF an already-built tuple NAME — the very `tupleTypeNameHead` that name starts with.
// `tupleElementTokenFor` needs it and holds only the name.
//
// ⚠ **THE REASON IS NOT "ONE SPELLER" — `tupleTypeNameHead` IS ALREADY THAT, AND AN ARITY PARAMETER WOULD
// CALL IT TOO (W14b review).** The reason is that an arity parameter is an input NOTHING TIES TO `name`: a
// caller that passed an arity the name was not built from would mint a token whose head contradicts the
// very string it digests, and no compile error anywhere would say so — the lossy-key shape this file's join
// rules exist to make unstateable. Read back, the head and the name's head are the same bytes by
// construction, and the question cannot be asked wrong.
//
// The first separator always exists in a registered tuple name: a tuple has at least two elements (`()` and
// `(x)` are both refused), so every name holds at least two of them — and a sweep key's `#<ordinal>` rides
// at the END, behind every element, so it can never arrive in front of the head's terminator.
function tupleTypeNameHeadOf(name ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	let separator = try TupleElementSeparator.toByteArray().get(0) otherwise panic("tupleTypeNameHeadOf: `TupleElementSeparator` renders no byte, so nothing ends a tuple name's head")
	var head = ByteArray.create()
	for i in 0 upto name.count() 'eachByte'
		let b = try name.get(i) otherwise panic("tupleTypeNameHeadOf: i < name.count() by the loop bound, but get({i}) failed")
		if b == separator 'headEnds'
			return head
		end 'headEnds'
		head.push(b)
	end 'eachByte'

	panic("tupleTypeNameHeadOf: `{String.from(name)}` holds no `{TupleElementSeparator}`, so it is not a tuple name `tupleTypeNameOf` built — every one of those is a head followed by at least two joined elements")
end 'tupleTypeNameHeadOf'

// ⭐⭐ **THE BOUNDED TOKEN ONE TUPLE'S NAME CITES ANOTHER BY — v1's structural gid, in shv2's name-keyed
// world (W9). THE cure for `A3j`, and since W14b a PURE FUNCTION OF THE TUPLE'S OWN NAME.**
//
// A tuple's identity is its element list, and an element that is ITSELF a tuple used to contribute its
// whole name. That flattens a DAG of tuple types into a TREE: `typealias A_k = (A_{k-1}, int)` gives d
// names of Θ(d) bytes, and `typealias A_k = (A_{k-1}, A_{k-1})` — a program whose SOURCE is linear —
// doubles the name at every level.
//
// ⛔ MEASURED both ways with `--metrics`, total allocated BYTES (exact and reproducible, unlike the CPU
// column). The ladder DOUBLES, so a linear cost shows ×2 in the successive deltas:
//
//   chain d = 50/100/200/400   before  23.10 / 24.51 / 29.05 / 45.23 MB   deltas ×3.2 then ×3.6
//                              after   22.86 / 23.45 / 24.60 / 26.95 MB   deltas ×1.97 then ×2.03
//   branch d = 8/12/16         before  22.48 / 24.02 / 48.02 MB           ×2 per LEVEL, not per doubling
//                              after   22.39 / 22.44 / 22.48 MB           flat
//
// The branching form is the one that says it plainly: after the change d = 24 costs **22.58 MB and
// 0.14 s**, on a 27-line program the old spelling put at roughly **11 GB** (`A3j` extrapolated it; d = 18
// already cost 2.47 s and 184 MB and was as far as it could be run).
//
// ⚠ **RE-MEASURED AT W14b ON THE SAME LADDER, because a digest is 15 bytes longer than the ordinal it
// replaced and a longer citation is a real cost:**
//
//   chain d = 50/100/200/400   W9   22.29 / 22.86 / 23.95 / 26.18 MB   deltas ×1.95 then ×2.02
//                              W14b 22.41 / 23.09 / 24.40 / 27.09 MB   deltas ×1.95 then ×2.05
//   branch d = 8/12/16/24      W9   21.86 / 21.91 / 21.96 / 22.06 MB   flat
//                              W14b 21.88 / 21.94 / 22.00 / 22.12 MB   flat
//
// **The EXPONENT is what `A3j` bought and it is unmoved**; what W14b spends is a CONSTANT — 13.4 against
// 11.1 KB per nesting level, all of it the longer name — and it buys a citation that is a statement about
// the program. (An earlier arrangement recomputed the digest at every citation instead of memoizing it and
// cost 23.3 KB/level; see `TupleElementTokenMap` for why that map exists.)
//
// ⭐ **WHY A DIGEST OF THE NAME IS BOTH BOUNDED AND STRUCTURAL, AND WHY THE RECURSION CLOSES.** `name` is
// what `tupleTypeNameOf` built, and it renders a NESTED element as this very token — so a depth-0 tuple's
// name is fully structural, its digest therefore is, so a depth-1 tuple's name is, and so on by induction.
// The digest is a fixed 64 bits however deep the nesting goes, which is the whole asymptote above; and it
// names nothing but the program, which is what W14b bought.
//
// ⚠ **THE INDUCTION'S BASE CASE IS THE NAME `tupleTypeNameOf` BUILT, AND A SWEEP-PHASE NAME IS NOT YET
// RESOLVED — SO ONE CITATION IN THE TREE STILL CARRIES A SOURCE SPELLING (W14b review).** Before
// `allFilesFolded` an element renders as the bare name the source wrote, and `sweepScopedTupleName` may add
// its first-free `#<ordinal>`; such a name reaches a citation through exactly one door —
// `canonicalTupleName`'s A3e re-entry, which resolves a SELF-REFERENTIAL tuple alias's element back to the
// sweep spelling it already carries. ⛔ MEASURED on `tuples.md`'s `error.self-referential-tuple-alias`:
// renaming `typealias Int` to `Signed` and `P` to `Q` — which changes no type's SHAPE — moves the declared
// type's citation from `#41e3ea25fc813804` to `#303df4d4860b223c`, because the chain bottoms out at the
// sweep name `__Tuple2.P.Int`. A self-referential tuple alias is an infinite type, so nothing constructible
// reaches that door; the residue is therefore confined to spellings the program itself wrote, and the
// remaining FILESYSTEM half of it (a CONTESTED sweep key taking `#<ordinal>` by walk order) is
// `sweepScopedTupleName`'s, filed as its own row rather than fixed here.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT REPLACED A MINT-ORDERED ORDINAL, AND THAT ORDINAL WAS A WALK-ORDER ARTIFACT (W14b).** The token
// used to be `tupleElementTokens.count()` — the map's own size, i.e. the order `Directory.list` handed the
// files over. MEASURED, with a pre-W9 control: two projects differing only in WHICH sibling file declares
// which tuple alias gave ONE type two names, `__Tuple2.__Tuple2#0.int` in one walk order and
// `__Tuple2.__Tuple2#2.int` in the other, reaching an `E3005` sentence, an emitted `__destruct_` symbol and
// (through `mangleTypeArg`) a descriptor label. It sat against the standing 2026-07-24 user ruling recorded
// at `StdlibLoader.maxon:39-63` — *"the compiler must not depend on file order to work correctly ... the
// repair belongs at the point of emission, deriving the order from something stable"*. A digest of the name
// IS that stable thing, and `specs-shv2/tuples.md`'s
// `error.nested-tuple-name-does-not-depend-on-which-file-declares-it{,-swapped}` pair pins it: the same
// program, the same file names, the contents swapped, ONE byte-identical sentence.
//
// ⭐ **AND THAT INVERTS WHAT v1 SAYS ABOUT THIS DOOR.** `maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Passes/BuildLayoutDescriptors.maxon:62-75`
// records that v1's gid numbering "is NOT stable across build scenarios" (`Array<String>` is gid 6 cold and
// gid 8 warm) and therefore emits descriptors sorted **by the mangled label, "a pure function of the
// instance's structural shape"**, precisely so interning order cannot reach `.rdata`. v1 gets away with a
// dense id because that id is an INTERNAL identity that never enters a NAME; this token does enter one, so
// shv2 cannot copy v1's registry — but it now spends v1's actual PRINCIPLE, at the only place it can: the
// citation itself is the pure function of the structural shape.
//
// ⚠ **THE ARITY RIDES IN FRONT SO THE TOKEN STILL SAYS SOMETHING TO A READER.** A tuple's name reaches
// diagnostics and emitted symbols (`__destruct_<name>`), and `__Tuple2.__Tuple2#<digest>.int` at least
// tells its reader that element 0 is a 2-tuple. It is not free of cost: the inner element TYPES no longer
// appear, which is a real loss of detail at nesting depth ≥ 2 — bought with the asymptote above, and paid
// only by nested tuples, since a depth-1 tuple's name is unchanged to the byte.
//
// ⭐ **THE DIGEST IS `ContentHash`'s FNV-1a, NOT A SECOND HAND-ROLLED HASH.** `mixBytes` takes the
// `ByteArray` a name already is (no `String` round-trip), folds a trailing LENGTH TAG so a prefix cannot
// alias its extension, and runs in `int(0 to u64.max)` where the multiply WRAPS rather than traps — the one
// arithmetic property a cross-target-identical digest needs, already shipping on every target because the
// query spine keys on it. 64 bits is the width the collision argument rests on: at N = 10^4 distinct tuple
// types P(collision) ≈ N²/2^65 ≈ 5×10⁻¹², where a 40-bit digest would be ≈ 5×10⁻⁵ — a number a real program
// can hit. Do not shorten it to make a golden prettier; `recordTupleElementToken` panics on a collision, and
// that panic is a compile that STOPS.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THIS RUNG MADE THE COMPILER A SECOND PARTY TO `fnvOffsetBasis`, AND THAT CHANGES WHAT EDITING IT
// COSTS.** `ContentHash.maxon`'s own header records that its offset basis was WRONG for months
// (`0xCBF86344…` for `0xCBF29CE4…`) and nothing noticed, because *"a content-addressed cache key only has to
// be stable, and a wrong basis is perfectly stable"* — it was caught only when a second implementation had
// to produce the same number. That is no longer the situation: this digest is baked into committed goldens
// and into emitted symbols on every target, so **changing the basis or the prime now renames every nested
// tuple in the tree**. It is a strengthening (the constant finally has a second party checking it), but it
// is a constraint that did not exist before W14b and is recorded here because nothing else records it.
//
// The rendering is HEX, the same `0-9a-f` the compiler already spells a machine value in. The alphabet is
// what matters and hex satisfies it exactly: no `.`, so a token cannot hold the element separator, and no
// `#`, so `overloadRegistrationName`'s join stays the only one in the string. It is `"{n:x}"` — the
// stdlib's own unsigned rendering — rather than a nibble loop here, because a third speller of "a value in
// hex" is a third copy of a fact this tree already writes twice (`TargetPrinter.hexDigit` for a chunk dump,
// the format specifier for everything else), and a frontend door reaching into the TARGET tier for it would
// be a worse coupling than reusing the language's own.
//
// The join is `overloadRegistrationName`'s for `sweepScopedTupleName`'s reason: `#` appears in no source
// identifier and — critically — in no `TupleElementSeparator`, so the token cannot collide with a spelled
// element and cannot be mistaken for two. It cannot collide with a registered tuple NAME either, by
// character count rather than by convention: a tuple has at least two elements, so every registered name
// holds at least two separators and a token holds none.
//
// It is derived here and NOT in `mangleTypeArg` for the reason that function's own header gives: a
// descriptor label must never come "from a gid number ... a build-order artifact". Since W14b this token is
// not one, which is why that header's tuple exception is gone rather than merely narrowed.
function tupleElementTokenFor(name ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	let digest = mixBytes(fnvOffsetBasis(), bytes: name)
	return overloadRegistrationName(tupleTypeNameHeadOf(name), suffix: "{digest:x}".toByteArray())
end 'tupleElementTokenFor'

// Is `name` a synthesized tuple type's name? The prefix is inside the `__` reserved space `E2051` bars every
// declaration from (`Parser.requireUnreservedName`), so a user `type` can never contest one and this test can
// never answer yes for a declared name.
export function isTupleTypeName(name ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(name, prefix: TupleTypeNamePrefix)
end 'isTupleTypeName'

// The layout of a tuple whose elements are `elements`, ALREADY canonical and already interned against the
// table the caller wants the layout to live in. Fields are positional (`_0`, `_1`, …) in element order — which
// IS the layout, since `StructLayout.offsetOfField` derives an offset from the index — and every one is
// `export var`: a tuple has no encapsulation to enforce (`requireFieldAccessible` must let `t.0` through from
// anywhere) and `t.0 = 20` is legal on a `var` binding.
//
// The ONE builder, called once per interner: `ProgramSignatures` builds its own copy against its own table and
// the parser builds the artifact's against the file's, because `remapArtifact` REWRITES an artifact layout's
// field types in place and a shared object would corrupt the index's copy.
//
// ⚠⚠ **EVERY ELEMENT'S DECLARED SURFACE IS PLAIN HERE, AND IT MUST STAY THAT WAY (A2m).**
// `(__ManagedMemory, Int)` and `(ByteArray, Int)` CANONICALIZE TO ONE NAME and therefore to THIS ONE
// LAYOUT — the spelling is already gone from `elements`, which holds the resolved types. Writing a buffer
// surface into this column would hand the buffer's roster to both spellings, decided by whichever interned
// first: a wrong ACCEPTANCE, which no diagnostic reports. A tuple slot's surface rides the VALUE instead
// (`Parser.bufferSurfaceSlots`), and `error.a-tuple-of-byte-arrays-keeps-the-array-surface` is what holds
// this shut.
// ⚠⚠ **`declFilePath` IS THE FILE WHOSE SPELLING MINTED THIS TUPLE, AND IT IS NOT `CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath`
// (A3v).** A tuple TYPE is synthesized and declared in no file, so the compiler-owned path reads like the
// honest answer — and it was, until a tuple ELEMENT could name a per-file thing. `(Bag, Num)` written in
// `adef.maxon` means adef's `Bag`; resolving it as a file declaring no `Bag` at all made the DECLARING file
// unable to construct its own field. What a tuple has is not a declaration site but a SPELLING site, and
// every spelling has one — so the path a tuple layout carries is the file that wrote the element list.
//
// It is the same column a declared `type` uses because it answers the same question at the same doors:
// `resolveRecordedGenericAliasTypes` re-scopes this layout's recorded `named` elements against it (which is
// what settles a SWEEP-minted spelling, whose elements are still bare names), and `fieldTypeOf` scopes an
// element read through it. Visibility is `global` exactly as before — a tuple is nameable wherever its
// elements are, and the path is read for SCOPING, never for reach.
export function tupleStructLayout(name ByteArray, elements MaxonTypeArray, declFilePath FilePath) returns StructLayout
	var layout = StructLayout.create(name, visibility: Visibility.global, declFilePath: declFilePath)
	for i in 0 upto elements.count() 'eachElement'
		layout.addField(tupleFieldName("{i}".toByteArray()), declaredType: try elements.get(i) otherwise panic("tupleStructLayout: elements.get OOB at {i} below count {elements.count()}"), mutable: true, tier: Visibility.global, defaultValue: FieldDefault.none, declaredSurface: sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface)
	end 'eachElement'
	return layout
end 'tupleStructLayout'

// ⚠ **A TUPLE HAS AT LEAST TWO ELEMENTS, AND `(a)` STAYS GROUPING FOREVER.** `(2 + 3) * 4` means 20 because
// the parentheses group; a one-element tuple would make that spelling ambiguous with nothing to disambiguate
// it, so v1 rejects a 1-tuple at four separate sites and shv2 rejects it at the one place a tuple is minted.
export let MinTupleArity = 2 as FieldIndex

// Why a type cannot be a tuple ELEMENT — raised by `ProgramSignatures.internTupleType` and turned into a
// positioned `E2015` by the parser, which is the only party holding a token to anchor it on.
//
// It is a THROW rather than a `panic` because every case is reachable from source: a `(T, int)` inside a
// generic body, and a tuple built out of a value whose type the front end never resolved.
export union TupleElementRejection
	// The element's type tag has no place in a tuple's identity — a `typeParameter` (the shared body has no
	// concrete type to key the layout on, and the drop cascade would be built against a borrow), an
	// `unresolved` (nothing to mangle), or a `void` (no value at all). See `requireTupleElementType`.
	unsupportedTag(position FieldIndex, tagName String)
	// The element's canonical name holds the element SEPARATOR, so the join that spells the tuple's name
	// would stop being injective (see `TupleElementSeparator`). Exactly one name form in the language can do
	// this — a PER-INSTANCE qualified alias, `StrWrapper.Idx` — since the BASE-TEMPLATE form collapses to its
	// underlying primitive in `canonicalTupleElement`, a NESTED TUPLE is cited by a separator-free token
	// (`tupleElementTokenFor`) rather than by its own name, and every other spelling is a bare identifier.
	separatorInElementName(position FieldIndex, elementName String)
end 'TupleElementRejection'

// The name id `id` denotes in the interner it was MINTED against — `ProgramSignatures.nameOfId`'s twin for the
// direction that reads a FOREIGN table (`adoptTypeFrom`). A miss is an invariant break, not a case: every id
// handed to that door was minted by the caller's own `parseTypeReference` against exactly this table.
function foreignNameOf(typeNames TypeNameInterner, id TypeNameId) returns ByteArray
	return try typeNames.nameOf(id) otherwise panic("foreignNameOf: type-name id {id} is not in the interner it was passed with — a type crossing INTO the signature index must carry ids minted against the interner named alongside it")
end 'foreignNameOf'

// Is `element` a type a tuple can be keyed on? An `unresolved` is not: there is nothing to mangle. A `void`
// is not: it is no value at all. Both are reachable from source, so each is a positioned rejection rather
// than a panic.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A `typeParameter` IS ONE NOW (W43), AND ALL THREE OF THE REASONS IT WAS NOT HAVE BEEN ANSWERED —
// EACH BY A DIFFERENT RUNG, WHICH IS WHY THIS TOOK SIX ATTRIBUTION ROUNDS TO SEE.**
//
//   • **IDENTITY — answered by `W14`.** The refusal used to argue that `mangleTypeArg` "would spell it by
//     POSITION, so two instantiations would silently share one layout". True of a position, false of the
//     digest token that replaced it: two distinct parameters mangle and intern apart. `W14`'s own note
//     retired this half and left the rest standing.
//   • **LAYOUT — answered by `W6`'s premise 1, measured not argued.** `LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:146` lowers a
//     type parameter to `StdType.i64`, "AN OPAQUE MACHINE WORD (P1.6-A)", and `Project.fieldSlotCount` gives
//     every non-existential field exactly one 8-byte slot. So `(Key, Value)` has a statically known 16-byte
//     layout. There was never a layout to be missing.
//   • **DROP CASCADE — the one that actually survived, and it is answered by the LAYOUT DESCRIPTOR the
//     instance already carries**, not by knowing the concrete type here. A tuple over parameters is dropped
//     the way every other opaque aggregate is: through the per-instance descriptor. Refusing the TYPE to
//     avoid a question about a VALUE was the category error — the two are separable, and the value-side
//     hazard keeps its own refusal at the construction site (see the borrowed-opaque rule), which is where
//     ownership is actually decided.
//
// ⚠ **The value-side refusal is NOT lifted here and must not be**: aliasing a BORROWED opaque element into a
// tuple is still refused where it is built, because the tuple would store the borrow without a reference and
// its caller's concrete drop would release it twice. That is a claim about a construction, and this function
// is asked about a TYPE.
function requireTupleElementType(element MaxonType, position FieldIndex) throws TupleElementRejection
	let tag = maxonTypeTag(element)
	if tag == ValueTypeTag.unresolved or tag == ValueTypeTag.void 'unsupported'
		throw TupleElementRejection.unsupportedTag(position, tagName: typeTagName(tag))
	end 'unsupported'
end 'requireTupleElementType'

// The composite-key index a `GenericInstanceRegistry` interns through, and the alias-name map that
// resolves `IntBox` to its interned instance. Keyed on `ByteArray` — a cheap string key built from
// the base id and the arg (tag, id) pairs — so interning is O(1) amortized (NOT v1's linear
// structural scan over every prior entry, `Project.maxon:2002-2014`).
export typealias GenericInstanceIndexMap = Map with (ByteArray, GenericInstanceId)
export typealias GenericAliasMap = Map with (ByteArray, GenericInstanceId)

// ONE contested generic-alias name's declarations: the DECLARING FILE's path ⇒ the instance that file's
// declaration names. Probed with the READER's path, which is what makes a file's own `typealias Slots =
// Array with Big` authoritative in that file (N3).
//
// ⚠ Keyed by the path STRING and deliberately NOT by `FilePath`, for `RangedAliasByDeclaringFile`'s
// measured reason: `FilePath.hash` case-folds on Windows and so ALLOCATES A STRING PER PROBE. The two
// structures also keep ONE notion of "the same file" between them by using one key type.
typealias GenericAliasByDeclaringFile = Map with (String, GenericInstanceId)

// The instance a contested alias name denotes ⇒ the buffer-spelling bits the declarations naming it wrote
// (W49). See `GenericAliasDeclarationGroup.bitsByInstance` for why the key is the instance.
typealias GenericAliasSpellingBitsMap = Map with (GenericInstanceId, DeclaredSurfaceNode)

// The generic-alias names whose declarations name DIFFERENT instances (N3) ⇒ every declaration of each, by
// declaring file. EMPTY for every program in the corpus — see
// `ProgramSignatures.settleGenericAliasContest` for why that emptiness is what keeps every interned
// instance, every mangled symbol and every committed golden where it was.
typealias ContestedGenericAliasMap = Map with (ByteArray, GenericAliasByDeclaringFile)

// ⭐ THE ONE EMPTY contest map every index is born holding — so a program with no contested generic alias,
// which is every program in the corpus, allocates NO index for a question whose answer is "none". The
// discipline `sharedEmptyRangeContests` established, for its reason: copy-on-write off this handle at the
// single writer (`settleGenericAliasContest`); NEVER mutated.
let sharedEmptyGenericAliasContests = ContestedGenericAliasMap.create()

// A contested generic-alias name ⇒ the instance a declaration NAMEABLE FROM ANYWHERE gives it, last-wins
// (N3). Tier 2 of `ProgramSignatures.scopedGenericAliasInstance`, and the exact twin of
// `RangedAliasRegistry.nameable`: a file that declares none of a contested name must resolve to a
// declaration it may actually WRITE DOWN, never to whichever file-private one folded last.
typealias NameableGenericAliasMap = Map with (ByteArray, GenericInstanceId)

// The shared empty twin of the above, for the same reason and written by the same single writer.
let sharedEmptyNameableGenericAliases = NameableGenericAliasMap.create()

// The generic-alias names ONE ROUND of the contest found declarations disagreeing about — the first pass's
// whole output, and the filter its second pass builds groups through. It is a name SET and not a group map
// because that is the entire difference between the two passes: the verdict needs one instance per name,
// the index needs every declaration of the few names that failed it.
//
// ⭐ **THE SAME TYPE ALSO CARRIES THE PRIOR QUESTION** — `ProgramSignatures.multiplyDeclaredGenericAliases`,
// the names MORE THAN ONE declaration mentions. The two are the same shape because they are the same kind of
// answer narrowing the same walk, and the outer one is what makes the whole contest free: a name only ONE
// declaration mentions cannot disagree with anything, whatever any scoping does to it.
typealias DisagreeingAliasNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// ⭐ THE ONE EMPTY set both of those start from — copy-on-write at their single `insert` sites, following
// `sharedEmptyRangeContests` and its family. NEVER mutated.
let sharedEmptyDisagreeingAliasNames = DisagreeingAliasNameSet.create()

// ⭐ **EVERY DECLARATION OF ONE DISAGREEING GENERIC-ALIAS NAME, ACCUMULATED** — the working record the
// contest's SECOND pass builds, and exactly what `ContestedGenericAliasMap` and `NameableGenericAliasMap`
// want per name. It is built only for a name the first pass has already condemned, which is why it may
// afford a map and a `FilePath.toString()` per declaration where that pass may not.
export type GenericAliasDeclarationGroup
	// Declaring file path ⇒ the instance that file's declaration names — published verbatim as this name's
	// entry in `ContestedGenericAliasMap`.
	export var byDeclaringFile as GenericAliasByDeclaringFile
	// ⭐ **THE BUFFER-SPELLING BITS OF EACH DECLARATION, KEYED BY THE INSTANCE (W49).** Keyed by instance and
	// not by file because the MINT this feeds is derived from the instance
	// (`recordGenericAliasContest`), so this is the exact granularity that pass can act at. Two declarations
	// naming one instance therefore share one entry and their bits are OR-ed — which is not a loss, because
	// they would reach the identical mint name and no per-declaration answer could be filed against it.
	export var bitsByInstance as GenericAliasSpellingBitsMap
	// Tier 2 of `ProgramSignatures.scopedGenericAliasInstance`: did any declaration make the name writable
	// from ANY file, and which instance did the last such declaration name? Last-wins among the nameable
	// ones, exactly as `RangedAliasRegistry.record` files `nameable`.
	export var anyNameable as bool
	export var nameableInstance as GenericInstanceId

	export static function create() returns GenericAliasDeclarationGroup
		return Self{byDeclaringFile: GenericAliasByDeclaringFile.create(), bitsByInstance: GenericAliasSpellingBitsMap.create(), anyNameable: false, nameableInstance: 0}
	end 'create'

	// Fold one declaration in.
	//
	// ⚠ **A FILE THAT DECLARES THE NAME TWICE *DOES* REACH HERE, AND THE KEYED WRITE KEEPS THE LAST (N3
	// review).** E3061 is reported after `queryProgramSignatures`, so both declarations are in
	// `genericAliasDecls` while this runs. That program is refused either way; what the write must not do is
	// pretend otherwise, so it is stated rather than assumed away.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SPELLING BITS ACCUMULATE WHERE `byDeclaringFile` OVERWRITES**, deliberately: a bit says "some
	// declaration naming this instance wrote the buffer", and dropping an earlier one would silently narrow
	// a member surface the source did ask for.
	export function record(instance GenericInstanceId, declFilePath FilePath, nameable bool, spellingBits DeclaredSurfaceNode)
		self.byDeclaringFile.upsert(declFilePath.toString(), value: instance)
		self.bitsByInstance.upsert(instance, value: self.spellingBitsOf(instance) or spellingBits)
		if nameable 'writableFromAnyFile'
			self.anyNameable = true
			self.nameableInstance = instance
		end 'writableFromAnyFile'
	end 'record'

	// The accumulated spelling bits of every declaration naming `instance` — plain for an instance no
	// declaration in this group names, which is what makes it safe to ask before the fold as well as after.
	export function spellingBitsOf(instance GenericInstanceId) returns DeclaredSurfaceNode
		return try self.bitsByInstance.get(instance) otherwise 0 as DeclaredSurfaceNode
	end 'spellingBitsOf'
end 'GenericAliasDeclarationGroup'

// Generic-alias name ⇒ its declarations, for the contest's second pass.
typealias GenericAliasDeclarationGroupMap = Map with (ByteArray, GenericAliasDeclarationGroup)

// Base type-name id ⇒ the instances interned for THAT base — the second index on the instance registry,
// beside the payload⇒id one above. It exists for the per-instantiation GATES
// (`genericTypeFieldHas{Managed,CoOwnedTrivial,Uncopyable}Instantiation`), which ask "does any instance OF
// THIS BASE classify its argument at `position` as …?" and used to answer it by walking the whole registry
// and `.equals`-ing every base name — O(call sites × TOTAL instances), a cost that allocates nothing and so
// is invisible to `scale-test`'s memory columns. Keyed by base ID rather than base name because the
// registry interns by id and holds no interner of its own: the one writer is `intern`, so the buckets
// cannot drift from the columns they index.
export typealias GenericInstanceIdArray = Array with GenericInstanceId
export typealias GenericInstancesByBaseMap = Map with (TypeNameId, GenericInstanceIdArray)

// ⭐⭐ **gid ⇒ ITS COMPILED NAME, DERIVED ONCE.** A generic instance's mangling is a pure function of its
// base name and its args, both FIXED at intern time and neither ever revised — so it is derivable once
// and asked many times. It was derived many times: EIGHT call sites ask — `mangleTypeArg`'s own nested
// arm, the drop-callee router, the descriptor label, four `MmRuntime` cascade passes and the E3006
// compiled-name check — and each ask REBUILT the whole string.
//
// ⚠ **THAT REBUILD IS RECURSIVE, WHICH MADE IT QUADRATIC.** `mangleTypeArg`'s nested arm re-enters
// `mangleGenericInstance`, so deriving `Wrap with (Wrap with (… Small))` at depth d re-derived all d
// enclosing names — Θ(d) per ask, Θ(Σd) = Θ(N²) over the program, at every one of those sites.
// MEASURED on a hand-written chain of N inline-nested instantiations (a ~5 KB source file): allocations
// inside `mangleGenericInstance` were 9,897 / 39,797 / 159,597 at N = 50 / 100 / 200 — **x4.02 and x4.01
// per doubling, a textbook quadratic** — and 87% of every allocation the compile made. Compile time was
// 51 / 109 / 344 / 1,360 / 5,922 ms at N = 100…1,600 (x4.0-4.4 per doubling), and 97% of it was
// UNATTRIBUTED in the per-phase table, because the passes that ask sit between phase brackets.
//
// With the memo each name is built ONCE, from its argument's ALREADY-BUILT name: O(1) map probe per ask,
// O(arity) appends per distinct instance. Total allocations become O(instances); total BYTES stay Θ(N²)
// for a nested chain and that half is irreducible — name `i` genuinely IS `i` levels long.
//
// Keyed by gid rather than positional, so it does not care that instances are interned in two waves
// (the signatures sweep, then `Array`/`Set` literals during parse) — a gid is append-only and its
// mangling is settled the moment it exists.
export typealias CompiledInstanceNameMap = Map with (GenericInstanceId, ByteArray)

// The CANONICAL (comparison) name of each instance — see `canonicalInstanceName`. A SECOND map, deliberately
// separate from `CompiledInstanceNameMap`: that one names EMITTED symbols (`__destruct_…`, `__layout_…`) and
// must not move, while this one exists only to answer "are these two values the same type?".
export typealias CanonicalInstanceNameMap = Map with (GenericInstanceId, ByteArray)

// ⭐⭐ **THE NAME THE *AUTHOR* WROTE FOR AN INSTANCE — a THIRD map, and the third question about one type.**
// `CompiledInstanceNameMap` names EMITTED SYMBOLS, `CanonicalInstanceNameMap` answers *"are these two values
// the same type?"*, and this answers *"what does a DIAGNOSTIC call it?"* — `StringArray`, not `Array_String`.
// The three are separate because the first two may never move (a symbol name is an ABI fact; an identity is a
// comparison key) while this one exists only to be read back to a person, and a person did not write
// `Array_String`.
//
// ⚠ **A MISS IS AN ANSWER, NOT AN ABSENCE** (`instanceDisplayName` falls back to the canonical name): an
// instance the COMPILER minted — `Array with int` behind `[1, 2, 3]`, `Array with __ManagedByte` behind a
// synthesized buffer, the `Array with Byte` `__ManagedMemory` is bound to — has no source line naming it, so
// there is no author's spelling to quote and the mint is the only thing that can distinguish it.
export typealias InstanceDisplayNameMap = Map with (GenericInstanceId, ByteArray)

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH INSTANTIATION AN UNWRITTEN INSTANCE WAS SUBSTITUTED OUT OF (BLAME).** A generic body's inner
// `typealias ElementArray = Array with Element` is re-interned per enclosing instance
// (`substituteInstanceArgsThrough`), so `Container with Bag` mints `Array with Bag` — an instance the PROGRAM
// NEVER WROTE and which therefore has no `GenericInstantiationSite`. That is exactly the instance a
// library-body refusal may have to be blamed on, and the only construct an author can be shown for it is the
// enclosing instantiation they DID write. One edge per mint records that.
//
// ⚠ **FIRST WRITE WINS.** One instance can be the substitution of several enclosing instantiations
// (`Container with Bag` and `Holder with Bag` both mint `Array with Bag`); the blame must not depend on which
// call happened to run last, so the edge is written once and never overwritten. Which one that is remains a
// property of declaration order, the same order every other last-wins/first-wins rule in this index reads.
//
// ⚠ It is a DIAGNOSTIC edge and nothing else — no emission, no check and no hash reads it, so a missing edge
// costs precision in one message and never an answer. It is deliberately NOT filed into `genericAliasSites`,
// which the E3017 where-constraint check walks: a synthesized instantiation is not one the author wrote, and
// reporting constraints against it would be a real behaviour change wearing a diagnostic's clothes.
typealias SubstitutedInstanceOriginMap = Map with (GenericInstanceId, GenericInstanceId)

// ⭐ **THE REVERSE OF `CanonicalInstanceNameMap` — every interned instance's canonical name back TO ITS ID,
// so "is this NAME an instance?" is a probe rather than a walk.** `isCanonicalInstanceName` used to answer it
// by scanning `0 upto genericInstances.count()`, which its own header justified with *"a program interns a
// handful of instances"* and *"the two askers are whole-program CHECKS that run once per declared
// associated type"*. Both premises were true when written and BOTH went stale: `declaredNameIsManaged`
// then began asking it FIRST, on every name, and that is `typeIsManaged`'s per-FIELD door — so the walk ran
// once per (field × instance) and minted a fresh `GenericInstanceIdArray` inside `canonicalInstanceName` on
// every step of it, memo hit or not. **MEASURED on a ladder of N generic instances each holding a managed
// struct: `phase:deriveRuntimeNeeds` allocated 5,403 / 11,504 / 31,186 / 100,518 at N = 25/50/100/200 —
// x2.13, x2.71, x3.22 across a DOUBLING knob, fitting `1,814 + 94N + 2.0N²` to the digit, the 2.0 being the
// array record plus its buffer.** It also broke `declaredNameIsManaged`'s own stated obligation two lines
// above it — *"Allocation-free throughout, which is `typeIsManaged`'s standing obligation"*.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT CARRIES THE ID AND NOT MERELY MEMBERSHIP (W58), BECAUSE A CANONICAL NAME IS AN ARRIVAL AND NOT
// ONLY A CLASSIFICATION.** A conformance's `with` argument is a RENDERED name, so a binding to a generic
// instance reaches `ConformanceCheck` as `UpCursor_Integer` and nothing downstream can say what it is a
// rendering OF — which base declares it, and which `typealias` the author wrote — without the id. Both
// answers already exist keyed by id (`genericInstanceBaseName`, `instanceDisplayName`); this is the one
// edge that was missing, and holding a `GenericInstanceId` beside a name the walk already inserts costs one
// word per interned instance. The mapping is injective by construction: the registry interns by structural
// key and `canonicalInstanceName` is a pure function of that key, so no two live ids share a name.
typealias CanonicalInstanceNameIndex = Map with (ByteArray, GenericInstanceId)

// ⭐ A tuple name ⇒ its CANONICAL tuple name (P1.10). Keyed by name rather than by an id because a tuple has
// no id: it IS its name (`ProgramSignatures.tupleCanonicalNames`).
export typealias TupleCanonicalNameMap = Map with (ByteArray, ByteArray)

// ⭐ A tuple name ⇒ the BOUNDED TOKEN that cites it as an ELEMENT of another tuple (W9) —
// `__Tuple2#90fac2b26f6571d1`. Since W14b the token is a pure FUNCTION of the name
// (`tupleElementTokenFor`), so this map no longer decides anything: it is a MEMO, and its key space is the
// set of distinct tuple types, so a tuple's citation is one entry however deeply it nests.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A MEMO AND NOT AN ORNAMENT — MEASURED (W14b).** Recomputing the digest at every citation
// instead of holding it costs **+7.4 KB per nesting level** on the `A3j` chain ladder (marginal cost 23.3
// against 15.9 KB/level at d = 50→400) and **+1.9% of a whole compile's allocations** on the 24-deep
// branching form, because a nested element's name is rebuilt at every canonicalization re-entry and each
// rebuild would re-render the head, the hex and the join. The digest is what makes the token an IDENTITY;
// this map is what keeps reading it cheap.
//
// See `ProgramSignatures.recordTupleElementToken` — the sole writer, which fills this and
// `TupleElementTokenOwnerMap` in ONE act — and `tupleElementTokenOf`, the sole reader.
typealias TupleElementTokenMap = Map with (ByteArray, ByteArray)

// ⭐ **THE REVERSE OF `TupleElementTokenMap`, and the only part of the pair that DECIDES anything (W14b):**
// every minted element token ⇒ the ONE tuple name it belongs to. The forward map is derivable and this one
// is not — it holds the fact computation cannot supply, WHICH name a token was first minted for — and that
// is what turns the digest's injectivity from an assumption into a DETECTED property. A second distinct
// name digesting to a live token stops the compile at the WRITE; a citation whose token does not lead back
// to the citing name stops it at the READ.
//
// The same index + reverse-index arrangement `CanonicalInstanceNameSet` carries for generic instances, and
// for a related reason: a question you cannot ask of a map in the direction it is keyed is not a question
// you may answer by walking it.
typealias TupleElementTokenOwnerMap = Map with (ByteArray, ByteArray)

// The NAMES every top-level MANAGED binding in the program is declared under — `let` and `var` alike
// (P1.7a / P1.7 slice 2a) — the filter `Parser.managedTopLevelReceiverAt` asks before it will build a
// file-scoped key. See `ProgramSignatures.managedTopLevelNames` for why the answer has to be a name and
// not a bool.
typealias ManagedTopLevelNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// The `.data` labels whose slot is filled ON FIRST ACCESS (spec-port `lazy-static`) — every `static`
// member with a record slot. Read by `Parser.emitGlobalAddr` at every global access, which is why it is a
// SET keyed by the label rather than a walk of the arena: the access door holds a label and nothing else.
typealias LazyStaticSlotSet = Set with ByteArray

// The functions each lazy slot's initializer calls, keyed by that same label. Keyed by LABEL rather than by
// arena index for `LazyStaticSlotSet`'s reason, and read by the ONE site that holds a label and a
// reachability worklist at once (`StdlibSource.markLazyStaticInitCallees`).
typealias LazyStaticInitCalleeMap = Map with (ByteArray, ByteArrayArray)

// The answer for a label with no lazy initializer call — which is every label in almost every program, and
// the answer given once per `globalAddr` op of every reached function. Shared for `sharedEmptyTypeParamNames`'
// reason: a fresh empty array per miss would be an allocation per OP.
let sharedEmptyLazyStaticInitCallees = ByteArrayArray.create()

// ⭐ **DOES AN ACCESS TO A GLOBAL SLOT HAVE TO RUN AN INITIALIZER FIRST?** A union rather than an
// empty-bytes sentinel, because "this global is eager" is an ANSWER — it is what nearly every global is —
// and not a missing callee.
export union LazyStaticInit
	none
	callee(name ByteArray)
end 'LazyStaticInit'

// Function name ⇒ the pre-erasure `__ManagedMemory` SPELLING of its declared return type (A2j / A2m) — the
// door `Parser.emitCall` asks before it marks a call's result. See `ProgramSignatures.declaredReturnSurfaces`
// for why only a NON-PLAIN surface gets an entry.
typealias DeclaredReturnSurfaceMap = Map with (ByteArray, DeclaredSurface)

// The NAMES of every generic-instance typealias whose FIRST type argument was SPELLED `__ManagedMemory`
// (A2m) — `typealias BufArray = Array with __ManagedMemory`. For an `Array`/`Set` instance argument 0 IS the
// element, so this is the door "does a value of this alias hold BUFFERS?" is answered through. It has to be
// keyed on the ALIAS NAME and can never be keyed on the `GenericInstanceId`: `Array with __ManagedMemory` and
// `Array with ByteArray` intern to the SAME instance, which is the whole reason the question survives.
typealias BufferElementAliasNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// The `<Conformer>.<method>` keys of the extension-publication columns (W43, X-contains) — see
// `ProgramSignatures.extensionPublishedMethods` for what each set means and why one cannot serve for both.
typealias ExtensionMethodNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// The `<Conformer>.<member>` keys of every PER-INSTANCE nested `typealias` an `extension` body declares —
// see `ProgramSignatures.extensionInnerAliasMembers`.
typealias ExtensionInnerAliasNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// The `<Type>.<method>` SOURCE names a `static` member and an INSTANCE member both wear — see
// `ProgramSignatures.staticInstanceContests`.
typealias StaticInstanceContestNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// `<Conformer>.<method>` ⇒ the file whose `extension` published it FIRST (X-contains). It exists to answer
// exactly one question — *"has a SECOND FILE published this name?"* — which is why it holds a path and not a
// count: two `extension` declarations in ONE file are the per-file overload registry's business
// (`Parser.overloadRegistrationNameFor` sees both), and counting would read that legal, already-working case
// as a contest. See `ProgramSignatures.contestedExtensionMethods`.
typealias ExtensionMethodDeclFileMap = Map with (ByteArray, FilePath)

// The `<declaring file>|<type name>` keys of every type name a top-level initializer NAMES — a cast's
// target (P1.9) or a container factory's alias (spec-port `ownership`). Scoped rather than bare because
// the question it answers — "was this alias used in its OWN file?" — is a file's. See
// `ProgramSignatures.constInitializerTypeNames`.
typealias ConstInitializerTypeNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// The SIMPLE (unqualified) names of methods, keyed by nothing else — see
// `ProgramSignatures.bareTypeParamReturnerNames`, whose whole point is that the qualifying type is exactly
// what its reader cannot supply.
typealias MethodNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// The type names one PROVENANCE declares (A1s wave 2) — ONE alias for both halves of the shadow contest,
// `ProgramSignatures.userDeclaredTypeNames` and `stdlibDeclaredTypeNames`. They hold the same KIND of fact
// about two different sets of files, and a second alias over `Set with ByteArray` would only invite the two
// to be described as different things.
typealias DeclaredTypeNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// ⭐⭐ **THE SHADOW RENAME TABLE — bare name ⇒ the reserved name a LISTED STDLIB module's declaration of
// that name is moved to (A1s wave 2).**
//
// ⚠ **THE RULE IT IMPLEMENTS IS THE ORACLE'S, MEASURED, AND IT IS A SCOPING RULE RATHER THAN A TIE-BREAK.**
// Against `maxon-sharp`, a user `enum ParseError` and a call to `int.fromString("41")` COEXIST in one
// program (exit 42) — the user's declaration answers user code while `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s own body
// goes on throwing its own `ParseError.invalidFormat`. The bootstrap gets that from parse ORDER: it binds
// every reference inside `stdlib/` before the user file overwrites the registry entry. shv2 cannot, and
// that is its thesis working as designed — `queryProgramSignatures` folds EVERY file's declarations before
// any file is parsed, so there is no "already bound" state and no file order produces one.
//
// ⚠ **AND A PLAIN "the user's declaration displaces the stdlib one" RULE IS A WRONG ANSWER, MEASURED.**
// Built that way, `type`/`union`/`interface`/ranged-`typealias` shadowing all worked, and a user
// `enum ParseError { Invalid = 1 }` produced **`E3034: stdlib/Builtins.maxon:222:11: unknown enum case:
// 'invalidFormat'`** — a diagnostic inside a file the author never opened, because that module REFERENCES
// the name it declares. Two declarations of one name have to coexist, and shv2 identifies aggregates by
// NAME all the way down (`structRef(name)`, `aggregateNameFor`, `__destruct_<name>`, `__layout_<name>`), so
// coexisting means one of them is RENAMED.
//
// ⇒ The stdlib one moves, into the space `E2051` already reserves — the identical trade
// `ProgramSignatures.reservedIfDeclared` makes for a contested COMPILED name, one namespace over, and for
// the identical reason: `__` is a prefix no declaration may take, so the moved name cannot contest anything
// and no user program can reach it. Re-probed rather than prefixed once, and ON CONTEST ONLY — a program
// that shadows nothing renames nothing and compiles to the bytes it did before this existed.
typealias StdlibShadowRenameMap = Map with (ByteArray, ByteArray)

// ⭐ **HOW MANY ELEMENTS A SIZED CONTAINER INSTANCE HOLDS** — `Vector with 3 Int`'s 3. It is a COUNT of
// elements the compiler must be able to fold into a `__managed_mem_create` argument and into a `count()`
// constant, so it is bounded by what a buffer of machine words can address rather than by `u64.max`.
export typealias FixedElementCount = int(0 to u32.max)
export typealias FixedSizeArray = Array with FixedElementCount

// The size an UNSIZED instance carries. It is not a sentinel standing in for a real answer: `Vector with 0`
// is REFUSED at its own `with` (`Parser.readSizedGenericArgs`), exactly as v1 refuses a non-positive sized
// typealias, so 0 is a size no instance can ever have and "unsized" is the only thing it can mean.
export let NoFixedSize = 0 as FixedElementCount

// The counts a sized spelling may state — the ends of `FixedElementCount`'s own domain, minus the 0 the
// constant above reserves. They are the bounds E2069 reports, read from here so the message and the test
// cannot come to disagree about what is accepted.
export let MinFixedElementCount = 1 as FixedElementCount
export let MaxFixedElementCount = u32.max as FixedElementCount

// ⭐ **HOW A SIZE APPEARS IN AN INSTANCE'S NAME** — `Vector with 3 Int` is `Vector_N3_Int`. The letter keeps
// the count from reading as a type argument's mangling, and it is v1's own spelling for the same symbol
// (`__layout_Vector_N3_Int`, pinned in `/specs/vector.md`'s RequiredIR blocks), so the two compilers name one
// type one way.
export let FixedSizeNameTag = "N"

// ⭐ **THE GENERIC-INSTANCE INTERNER (P1.6-A)** — `Box with Integer` ⇒ a `GenericInstanceId`, with the
// `(baseId, argTypes)` payload held OUT OF LINE (inlining args into `MaxonType` would make the union
// recursively aggregate — E4014). Two instances with the same base and structurally equal args share
// an id, so a downstream identity check is an integer compare.
//
// ⚠ **INTERNED THROUGH A MAP INDEX, NOT A LINEAR SCAN.** v1's `GenericInstanceRegistry.intern`
// (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Project.maxon:2002-2014`) walks every prior entry doing a full
// structural arg-array compare — O(n²) over a self-compile. Here a cheap composite key (`instanceKey`)
// keys `index`, so interning is O(1) amortized. The parallel `baseIds`/`argLists` columns are the id
// ⇒ payload direction; `index` is the payload ⇒ id direction.
export type GenericInstanceRegistry
	var baseIds as TypeNameIdArray
	var argLists as MaxonTypeArrayArray
	// ⭐ **THE SIZED CONTAINER'S SECOND IDENTITY COORDINATE** — `Vector with 3 Int`'s 3, and
	// `NoFixedSize` for every instance whose base is not sized (see `VectorBuiltinBaseName` for why a
	// size is a column here and not a `MaxonType` arm). It rides `instanceKey`, so two vectors of
	// different length can never share a gid.
	var fixedSizes as FixedSizeArray
	var index as GenericInstanceIndexMap
	// Base id ⇒ its instances, in intern order. The SECOND payload⇒id index (see
	// `GenericInstancesByBaseMap`), written only by `intern` alongside the columns it indexes.
	var byBase as GenericInstancesByBaseMap
	// ⭐⭐ **WAS THIS ROW MINTED BY A GUESS RATHER THAN BY THE SOURCE (A4q)?** True only of a row
	// `ProgramSignatures.internAggregateElementInstances` materialized for a `[<identifier>…]` literal whose
	// element type the token sweep could not name — a candidate, not a fact. Read by
	// `instanceWithTypeArgKind`, whose copy gate refuses on what it finds and so may not count a guess; see
	// `kindIsARefusal` for why the other two gates still do.
	var speculative as BoolArray

	export static function create() returns GenericInstanceRegistry
		return Self{baseIds: TypeNameIdArray.create(), argLists: MaxonTypeArrayArray.create(), fixedSizes: FixedSizeArray.create(), index: GenericInstanceIndexMap.create(), byBase: GenericInstancesByBaseMap.create(), speculative: BoolArray.create()}
	end 'create'

	// The cheap composite key that makes interning O(1): the base id, the fixed size, and each arg's
	// (tag, id) pair, joined. Two structurally equal instances build the SAME key — the arg TAG plus its
	// name/registry id is the whole of a slice-A type's identity (a trivial arg is a primitive, a struct
	// name id, or a nested-instance giid), so equal keys mean equal instances.
	function instanceKey(baseId TypeNameId, args MaxonTypeArray, fixedSize FixedElementCount) returns ByteArray
		var key = "{baseId}#{fixedSize}"
		for arg in args 'eachArg'
			key.append("|{maxonTypeTag(arg).rawValue}:{typeNameIdOf(arg)}")
		end 'eachArg'
		return key.toByteArray()
	end 'instanceKey'

	// Intern `(baseId, args, fixedSize)`, returning an existing id for a structural repeat. O(1)
	// amortized via `index`. `fixedSize` defaults to `NoFixedSize` — every base but `Vector` is unsized, so
	// the default is what the call sites that MINT an instance of some other base mean, and none of them
	// spells it.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE DEFAULT IS A TRAP FOR A CALLER THAT IS RE-INTERNING AN INSTANCE IT ALREADY HOLDS, and that
	// caller exists: `fileScopedInstance`.** Rebuilding a key from an existing instance means carrying every
	// component of that instance, and the one that can be lost by silence is this one. See that function's
	// ⛔ paragraph for the wrong answer it shipped.
	// ⭐ **`asSpeculative` MARKS A ROW NOTHING IN THE SOURCE NAMED** — see the `speculative` column. It defaults
	// to false because that is what every ordinary caller means: an instance interned because a declaration,
	// a type reference or a resolved literal element ASKED for it. Only the `[<identifier>…]` candidate walk
	// passes true, through `ProgramSignatures.internAggregateElementInstances`.
	//
	// A repeat NAMED intern of a row that was minted speculatively CLEARS the mark: the guess has since been
	// confirmed by the source, and from that moment it is evidence like any other. Marking is therefore
	// mint-only in the other direction — a speculative intern that HITS an existing row leaves it named,
	// which is what keeps a spelling the sweep already folded (`typealias Bag = Array with (Array with
	// String)`, interned before this walk runs) from being demoted to a guess by it.
	export function intern(baseId TypeNameId, args MaxonTypeArray, fixedSize FixedElementCount = NoFixedSize, asSpeculative bool = false) returns GenericInstanceId
		let key = self.instanceKey(baseId, args: args, fixedSize: fixedSize)
		if let existing = try self.index.get(key) 'hit'
			if not asSpeculative 'theSourceNamesIt'
				try self.speculative.set(existing, value: false) otherwise panic("GenericInstanceRegistry.intern: speculative desync at GenericInstanceId {existing} — the column is pushed by this function beside `baseIds`, so every interned id indexes it")
			end 'theSourceNamesIt'
			return existing
		end 'hit'
		let id = self.baseIds.count()
		self.baseIds.push(baseId)
		self.argLists.push(args)
		self.fixedSizes.push(fixedSize)
		self.speculative.push(asSpeculative)
		self.index.upsert(key, value: id)
		self.recordBase(baseId, id: id)
		return id
	end 'intern'

	// Was `id` minted by the `[<identifier>…]` candidate walk and never named by the source since? See the
	// `speculative` column, and `kindIsARefusal` for which kinds ask.
	export function instanceIsSpeculative(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return try self.speculative.get(id) otherwise panic("GenericInstanceRegistry.instanceIsSpeculative: speculative desync at GenericInstanceId {id}")
	end 'instanceIsSpeculative'

	// The fixed element COUNT of a sized instance — `NoFixedSize` for every unsized one.
	export function instanceFixedSize(id GenericInstanceId) returns FixedElementCount
		return try self.fixedSizes.get(id) otherwise panic("GenericInstanceRegistry.instanceFixedSize: fixedSizes desync at GenericInstanceId {id}")
	end 'instanceFixedSize'

	// File the new instance under its base. Appended, never re-sorted, so a base's bucket is in intern
	// order — the same order the gates' old whole-registry walk visited them in.
	function recordBase(baseId TypeNameId, id GenericInstanceId)
		if var siblings = try self.byBase.get(baseId) 'seenBase'
			siblings.push(id)
			self.byBase.upsert(baseId, value: siblings)
			return
		end 'seenBase'

		var first = GenericInstanceIdArray.create()
		first.push(id)
		self.byBase.upsert(baseId, value: first)
	end 'recordBase'

	// The instances interned for base `baseId`. Throws for a base with none — which is an ANSWER (no
	// instantiation of that type exists), not a failure, and is why the gates ask through `if let`.
	export function instancesOfBase(baseId TypeNameId) returns GenericInstanceIdArray throws MapError
		return try self.byBase.get(baseId)
	end 'instancesOfBase'

	export function instanceBaseId(id GenericInstanceId) returns TypeNameId
		return try self.baseIds.get(id) otherwise panic("GenericInstanceRegistry.instanceBaseId: invalid GenericInstanceId {id}")
	end 'instanceBaseId'

	export function instanceArgs(id GenericInstanceId) returns MaxonTypeArray
		return try self.argLists.get(id) otherwise panic("GenericInstanceRegistry.instanceArgs: argLists desync at GenericInstanceId {id}")
	end 'instanceArgs'

	// The number of interned instances — the iteration bound the B2 destructor-synthesis and needs-closure
	// passes walk (`0 upto count()` enumerates every gid).
	export function count() returns GenericInstanceId
		return self.baseIds.count()
	end 'count'
end 'GenericInstanceRegistry'

// ⭐ **ONE NODE OF A `Base with Args` ARGUMENT TREE, PRE-ORDER FLATTENED (P1.6-A/B2)** — a LEAF is an
// ordinary type reference (a primitive, a struct name, a ranged alias) whose `MaxonType` the parser already
// produced; a NESTED instance (`Box with (Box with Integer)`) is an `instanceHeader(baseName, argCount, fixedSize)`
// followed IN THE LIST by its `argCount` args' nodes (recursively). The tree is FLATTENED rather than
// self-referential because a directly recursive union is rejected (E4014); the gid is not known at parse
// time anyway — interning lives in `recordGenericAlias`, which walks the flat list DEPTH-FIRST (the inner
// instance interned before the outer, so the outer's arg is a real `genericInstance(innerGid)`), exactly
// what the composite key's "nested-instance giid" and `mangleTypeArg`'s nested arm already anticipated.
export union GenericArgNode
	leaf(argType MaxonType)
	// ⭐ `fixedSize` is the nested instance's own element COUNT — `NoFixedSize` for every base but `Vector`.
	// It rides the NODE because the node is all `resolveArgNode` has when it interns the instance, and the
	// size is half of that instance's identity (`GenericInstanceRegistry.instanceKey`).
	instanceHeader(baseName ByteArray, argCount FieldIndex, fixedSize FixedElementCount)
end 'GenericArgNode'

export typealias GenericArgNodeArray = Array with GenericArgNode

// A mutable read cursor over a flat `GenericArgNodeArray` — `resolveGenericArgs` walks the pre-order tree by
// advancing it (Maxon lacks tuples, so the recursion threads position through this shared reference).
export type ArgCursor
	export var pos as FieldIndex

	export static function create() returns ArgCursor
		return Self{pos: 0}
	end 'create'
end 'ArgCursor'

// One generic-instance TYPEALIAS the sweep found (P1.6-A): `typealias IntBox = Box with Integer` ⇒
// `(IntBox, Box, argCount 1, [leaf(Integer)])`. `argCount` is the number of TOP-LEVEL arguments (the flat
// `args` list also holds nested instances' nodes); a leaf's `MaxonType` is minted against the SCANNING
// file's interner, which `foldFile` remaps into the index's (and interns nested instances) before interning
// the instance. It travels by NAME (alias name + base name), the way every whole-program registry entry does.
export type GenericAliasEntry
	export var aliasName as ByteArray
	export var baseName as ByteArray
	export var argCount as FieldIndex
	export var args as GenericArgNodeArray
	// ⭐ **WAS ARGUMENT 0 SPELLED `__ManagedMemory` (A2m)?** Read off the TOKEN in `Parser.parseGenericArgNode`,
	// because the very next line destroys it: `parseTypeReference`'s generic-alias arm folds the spelling into
	// `genericInstance(Array with Byte)`, which is bit-for-bit what a `ByteArray` argument resolves to. For an
	// `Array` instance argument 0 is the ELEMENT, so this is what makes `Array with __ManagedMemory` an array of
	// BUFFERS while `Array with ByteArray` — the same `GenericInstanceId` — stays an array of arrays.
	export var elementIsBuffer as bool
	// ⭐ **WAS THE *BASE* SPELLED `__ManagedMemory` (W43)?** The `elementIsBuffer` twin one position up: that
	// one records how ARGUMENT 0 was written, this one how the BASE was, and both exist for the identical
	// reason — `canonicalGenericBaseName` folds the spelling into `Array` the moment it is read, and the
	// spelling is what decides the MEMBER SURFACE a value of this alias wears (`initKeys.length()` is a buffer
	// method and `Array` serves no `length`). It cannot be recovered from the `GenericInstanceId`, because the
	// whole point of the binding is that `__ManagedMemory with T` and `Array with T` are one instance.
	export var baseIsBuffer as bool
	// The instantiation-site anchor (P1.7a slice 2a) — where `typealias X = Base with Args` is declared, so the
	// where-constraint check (E3017) can land its diagnostic ON the offending instantiation. Zero for a
	// synthesized alias (e.g. an inner generic alias); the check skips a zero anchor.
	export var nameLine as LineNumber
	export var nameColumn as ColumnNumber
	export var filePath as FilePath
	// ⭐ **WHERE THIS DECLARATION NAMES ITSELF** — the second `A` of `typealias A = Array with A`, which is
	// the word the author has to change and the anchor a SELF-cycle is blamed at (`specs/type-checking.md`
	// pins column 26 of that line, the argument, against column 11 for a cycle that runs through other
	// aliases). Read by `Parser.selfNamingArgToken` because that is the only place the RHS's TOKENS still
	// exist: `GenericArgNode` carries resolved `MaxonType`s and no positions, and the whole-program cycle walk
	// runs long after the parser is gone.
	//
	// It is an ANCHOR, never an absence: a declaration whose RHS does not spell its own name records its
	// alias-name token here, so no reader has to decode "did it?" — only `checkGenericAliasCycles` asks, and
	// only for a cycle it has already proven is the alias alone. Zero for a SYNTHESIZED alias, which is the
	// same "no source anchor at all" `nameLine` records and is decided in the same place.
	export var selfRefLine as LineNumber
	export var selfRefColumn as ColumnNumber
	// ⭐ **THE DECLARATION'S OWN VISIBILITY (N3)** — the `RangedTypeAlias.visibility` twin, and read for the
	// same one question: when a file that declares NONE of a contested name reads it, which declaration may
	// it actually mean (`ProgramSignatures.scopedGenericAliasInstance`, tier 2)? Already put through
	// `Parser.typeAliasVisibility`, so a `stdlib/` declaration is `global` whatever it wrote — one door
	// decides that for both alias forms.
	//
	// An alias with no declaring FILE (the base-qualified inner form, `Container.ElementArray`) takes the
	// default `file` tier and never reaches the read: its name is qualified by the enclosing type, which no
	// two files may declare, so it cannot be contested.
	export var visibility as Visibility
	// ⭐ **THE INSTANCE THIS DECLARATION INTERNED TO (N3)** — stamped by `recordGenericAlias`, which is the
	// only place the args have been remapped into the index's own interner and the base interned, so it is
	// the only place the answer exists. Retained rather than re-derived, because the two readers below both
	// need THIS declaration's instance and the only other route to one is `genericAliases`, which is keyed
	// by the bare NAME and therefore answers last-wins for exactly the names that need distinguishing:
	//
	//   • the CONTEST (`settleGenericAliasContest`) compares one name's declarations against each other,
	//     and the `TypeNameRemap` that produced them is long gone by then;
	//   • the E3091 cycle report (`firstCyclicAliasDecl`) asks "which instance does THIS declaration name?"
	//     and used to re-probe the bare map for it.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE INSTANCE THE SWEEP INTERNED, NOT A RESOLVED ONE**, and the distinction is the whole rung:
	// its arguments are the bare spellings the sweep left, and `fileScopedInstance` is what turns it into the
	// instance a particular file means. Every reader below composes the two rather than trusting this alone.
	//
	// ⚠ **`sweptInstanceRecorded` IS A PARALLEL BOOL AND NOT A RESERVED ID**, following `arrayByteInstance` /
	// `arrayByteInstanceComputed`: every value of `GenericInstanceId` is a real instance (0 is the first one
	// interned), so no id can mean "not stamped yet". Both fields are written by the single `recorded` copy
	// below, so they cannot drift.
	export var sweptInstance as GenericInstanceId
	export var sweptInstanceRecorded as bool
	// ⭐ **THE SIZE A SIZED DECLARATION WROTE** — `typealias Vec3 = Vector with 3 Int`'s 3, read off the
	// literal by `Parser.readSizedGenericArgs` and carried here because `recordGenericAlias` is where the
	// instance is interned and the size is half of that instance's identity. `NoFixedSize` for every other
	// declaration, which is every declaration whose base is not `Vector`.
	export var fixedSize as FixedElementCount
	// ⭐⭐ **WAS THIS ALIAS DECLARED INSIDE A `type` (W17)** — `type Character`'s `typealias ByteMemory =
	// __ManagedMemory with Byte`, as against a FILE-scope `typealias ByteArray = Array with Byte`. Written by
	// the one re-key that scopes such a declaration, `scopedToEnclosingType` below.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS READ BY THE DISPLAY INDEX AND BY NOTHING ELSE**, and the narrowness is the rule rather than
	// today's caller list: an inner alias RESOLVES everywhere it always did (`parseTypeReference`'s inner-alias
	// arm, `scopedGenericAliasInstance`, the buffer-surface derivation), and it is only QUOTING one back at an
	// author that is wrong. `deriveInstanceDisplayNames` carries the argument in full.
	//
	// ⚠ **A BOOL AND NOT A TEST FOR A `.` IN `aliasName`.** The qualified spelling is what the re-key
	// PRODUCES, so reading the fact back out of it would be a second, lossy statement of it — the lexer admits
	// only `[A-Za-z0-9_]` inside an identifier today, which is the only reason such a test would work at all,
	// and a name form that ever carried a `.` for another reason would silently join this class. The producer
	// states the fact; nothing decodes it.
	export var typeScoped as bool

	export static function create(aliasName ByteArray, baseName ByteArray, argCount FieldIndex, args GenericArgNodeArray, elementIsBuffer bool = false, baseIsBuffer bool = false, nameLine LineNumber = 0, nameColumn ColumnNumber = 0, filePath FilePath = FilePath from "", visibility Visibility = Visibility.file, fixedSize FixedElementCount = NoFixedSize, selfRefLine LineNumber = 0, selfRefColumn ColumnNumber = 0) returns GenericAliasEntry
		return Self{aliasName: aliasName, baseName: baseName, argCount: argCount, args: args, elementIsBuffer: elementIsBuffer, baseIsBuffer: baseIsBuffer, nameLine: nameLine, nameColumn: nameColumn, filePath: filePath, visibility: visibility, sweptInstance: 0, sweptInstanceRecorded: false, fixedSize: fixedSize, selfRefLine: selfRefLine, selfRefColumn: selfRefColumn, typeScoped: false}
	end 'create'

	// This declaration, carrying the instance the index interned it to — the form `genericAliasDecls`
	// retains. A COPY rather than a mutation so the parser's own entry, whose instance is not yet knowable,
	// can never be mistaken for a recorded one.
	export function recorded(id GenericInstanceId) returns GenericAliasEntry
		return Self{aliasName: self.aliasName, baseName: self.baseName, argCount: self.argCount, args: self.args, elementIsBuffer: self.elementIsBuffer, baseIsBuffer: self.baseIsBuffer, nameLine: self.nameLine, nameColumn: self.nameColumn, filePath: self.filePath, visibility: self.visibility, sweptInstance: id, sweptInstanceRecorded: true, fixedSize: self.fixedSize, selfRefLine: self.selfRefLine, selfRefColumn: self.selfRefColumn, typeScoped: self.typeScoped}
	end 'recorded'

	// ⭐⭐ **THIS DECLARATION SCOPED TO THE `type` THAT HOLDS IT — the NAME becomes base-qualified and the one
	// other thing that changes is the RECORD OF THAT.** Its one caller is `Parser.qualifiedInnerGenericAlias`,
	// which re-keys a nested alias under its enclosing type's spelling (`Container.ElementArray`); a re-key is
	// not a new declaration and must not lose the declaration's facts.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT USED TO BE `renamedTo` AND TO CHANGE THE NAME ALONE, AND THAT LEFT THE ONE FACT THE NAME IS THE
	// EVIDENCE FOR UNRECORDED** (W17). Being inside a `type` is not a property of the spelling — it is what
	// PRODUCED the spelling — and the display index needs it as a fact. See `typeScoped` above.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT EXISTS BECAUSE THE HAND-WRITTEN RE-KEY DROPPED SEVEN OF THEM, AND ONE OF THOSE PANICKED THE
	// COMPILER (review, 2026-08-04).** Rebuilding through `create` took the defaults for `filePath`,
	// `visibility`, `fixedSize`, both anchors and both self-reference anchors, so a nested alias arrived in
	// `genericAliasDecls` claiming to have been declared by the file `""` — and `deriveInstanceDisplayNames`
	// asks every entry's declaring file for its module directory. **MEASURED: two files each declaring a
	// `type Container` whose nested `ElementArray` denotes a different element contested that base-qualified
	// name, and `moduleDirectoryOfPathText('')` panicked** — on a program whose real defect is the duplicate
	// `type` E3006 reports one pass later. A copy that names every field is the only shape that cannot
	// silently omit one.
	export function scopedToEnclosingType(qualifiedName ByteArray) returns GenericAliasEntry
		return Self{aliasName: qualifiedName, baseName: self.baseName, argCount: self.argCount, args: self.args, elementIsBuffer: self.elementIsBuffer, baseIsBuffer: self.baseIsBuffer, nameLine: self.nameLine, nameColumn: self.nameColumn, filePath: self.filePath, visibility: self.visibility, sweptInstance: self.sweptInstance, sweptInstanceRecorded: self.sweptInstanceRecorded, fixedSize: self.fixedSize, selfRefLine: self.selfRefLine, selfRefColumn: self.selfRefColumn, typeScoped: true}
	end 'scopedToEnclosingType'

	// The instance this declaration names. Precondition: the entry came from `genericAliasDecls`, which
	// `recordGenericAlias` fills with `recorded` copies exclusively.
	export function internedInstance() returns GenericInstanceId
		if not self.sweptInstanceRecorded 'neverRecorded'
			panic("GenericAliasEntry.internedInstance: '{String.from(self.aliasName)}' carries no interned instance — only the copies `recordGenericAlias` retains do, and every reader of this comes from `genericAliasDecls`")
		end 'neverRecorded'

		return self.sweptInstance
	end 'internedInstance'

	// ⭐ **HOW THIS DECLARATION WAS WRITTEN, AS ONE `DeclaredSurfaceNode`** — the shape both consumers of the
	// two `bool`s above actually want. The content hash mixes it, and the contest's per-instance mint files
	// it (`recordGenericAliasContest`), and each had otherwise spelled the same two-`if` mapping from a pair
	// of flags to a pair of bits. One speller, so a third bit cannot reach one of them and not the other.
	export function spellingBits() returns DeclaredSurfaceNode
		var bits = 0 as DeclaredSurfaceNode
		if self.baseIsBuffer 'baseNamesTheBuffer'
			bits = bits or SurfaceBitIsBuffer
		end 'baseNamesTheBuffer'

		if self.elementIsBuffer 'elementNamesTheBuffer'
			bits = bits or SurfaceBitElementIsBuffer
		end 'elementNamesTheBuffer'

		return bits
	end 'spellingBits'
end 'GenericAliasEntry'

export typealias GenericAliasEntryArray = Array with GenericAliasEntry

// Which generic-alias DECLARATION closes a type cycle (P1.7, E3091), or that none does. A union rather
// than an index-plus-sentinel because declaration 0 is a real alias. Asked once per compile, so the one
// heap box it costs is not on any hot path.
// How many rounds `ProgramSignatures.settleGenericAliasContest`'s fixpoint has taken. Bounded by the
// declaration count, because a round can only deepen an argument that was still bare and an acyclic
// declaration graph cannot nest deeper than it has declarations. Its own type rather than `ElementCount`,
// which counts ELEMENTS of something: a round is not one.
typealias GenericAliasRoundCount = int(0 to u32.max)

export union AliasCycleResult
	at(index FieldIndex)
	none
end 'AliasCycleResult'

// ⭐ **ONE `Base with Args` INSTANTIATION SITE (P1.7a slice 2a)** — an interned instance paired with the
// source anchor of the `typealias X = Base with Args` that names it, for the where-constraint check (E3017)
// to land its diagnostic on. Collected by `recordGenericAlias` for every alias with a real (non-zero) anchor.
export type GenericInstantiationSite
	export let gid as GenericInstanceId
	export let nameLine as LineNumber
	export let nameColumn as ColumnNumber
	export let filePath as FilePath

	export static function create(gid GenericInstanceId, nameLine LineNumber, nameColumn ColumnNumber, filePath FilePath) returns GenericInstantiationSite
		return Self{gid: gid, nameLine: nameLine, nameColumn: nameColumn, filePath: filePath}
	end 'create'
end 'GenericInstantiationSite'

export typealias GenericInstantiationSiteArray = Array with GenericInstantiationSite

// Whether the program WROTE the instantiation being asked about, and where. See
// `ProgramSignatures.instantiationSiteOf`, which owns the argument for why `unwritten` is a real answer
// and not a lookup failure.
export union InstantiationSiteLookup
	unwritten
	written(site GenericInstantiationSite)
end 'InstantiationSiteLookup'

// One RANGED typealias declared INSIDE a generic type (P1.6-C): `type Wrapper uses T` with
// `export typealias Idx = int(0 to u64.max)` ⇒ `(Wrapper, Idx=int(0..u64.max))`. The sweep
// (`recordScannedType`) collects one per nested ranged alias; `foldFile` keys it by `base.member`
// ("Wrapper.Idx") into `innerAliases`. It carries NO type-name ids — a ranged alias's underlying is
// the `int`/`float` KEYWORD — so it travels by name like `RangedTypeAlias` and needs no remap.
export type InnerAliasEntry
	export var baseName as ByteArray
	export var alias as RangedTypeAlias

	export static function create(baseName ByteArray, alias RangedTypeAlias) returns InnerAliasEntry
		return Self{baseName: baseName, alias: alias}
	end 'create'
end 'InnerAliasEntry'

export typealias InnerAliasEntryArray = Array with InnerAliasEntry

// The inner-alias PARAMETER shape of one method/factory (P1.6-C) — its SOURCE parameter names, and per
// source position the bare inner-alias MEMBER (`Idx`) or `EmptySourceText` for a non-inner-alias
// parameter. The two arrays move in lockstep (one entry per source parameter). Recorded per method that
// has at least one inner-alias parameter, so the parser's per-instance argument check can re-qualify the
// parameter with the concrete receiver's instance alias at the call site.
export type InnerAliasParamInfo
	export var paramNames as ByteArrayArray
	export var members as ByteArrayArray

	export static function create(paramNames ByteArrayArray, members ByteArrayArray) returns InnerAliasParamInfo
		return Self{paramNames: paramNames, members: members}
	end 'create'

	// The number of source parameters this info describes — the lockstep length of both columns.
	export function paramCount() returns ParamCount
		return self.paramNames.count()
	end 'paramCount'

	// Does any source parameter carry a bare inner-alias member? The gate that decides whether a method earns
	// an index entry (a method with none is the common case and costs nothing).
	export function hasInnerAliasMember() returns bool
		for member in self.members 'eachMember'
			if not member.isEmpty() 'found'
				return true
			end 'found'
		end 'eachMember'
		return false
	end 'hasInnerAliasMember'

	// The bare inner-alias member at source position `index` (`Idx`), or `EmptySourceText` for a
	// non-inner-alias parameter. Out-of-range reads empty (a slotted argument past the recorded columns is
	// not an inner-alias parameter — the arity error is SemanticCheck's to report).
	export function memberAt(index ParamCount) returns ByteArray
		return try self.members.get(index) otherwise EmptySourceText
	end 'memberAt'
end 'InnerAliasParamInfo'

export typealias InnerAliasParamInfoMap = Map with (ByteArray, InnerAliasParamInfo)

// What the index knows about a method's inner-alias parameters (P1.6-C) — `DeclaredAlias`'s twin, a union
// for the same reason: "this method has no inner-alias parameter" is an ANSWER (the common case), not a
// failure, and the parser's per-instance argument check simply does nothing for it.
export union InnerAliasParamLookup
	found(info InnerAliasParamInfo)
	noInnerAliasParams
end 'InnerAliasParamLookup'

// One method's inner-alias parameter info, as the sweep found it (P1.6-C) — the qualified method name
// (`Wrapper.setTag`) and its `InnerAliasParamInfo`. `foldFile` keys it into `methodInnerAliasParams`.
// It travels by name (no ids), the way every whole-program registry entry does.
export type MethodInnerAliasParamEntry
	export var methodName as ByteArray
	export var info as InnerAliasParamInfo

	export static function create(methodName ByteArray, info InnerAliasParamInfo) returns MethodInnerAliasParamEntry
		return Self{methodName: methodName, info: info}
	end 'create'
end 'MethodInnerAliasParamEntry'

export typealias MethodInnerAliasParamEntryArray = Array with MethodInnerAliasParamEntry

// ⭐ **WHERE ONE `interface` DECLARATION SITS IN ITS FILE'S TOKEN STREAM (R8)** — the file, and the index of
// its `interface` keyword. Recorded by the tolerant declaration sweep, which is the one phase that walks every
// file's tokens and already decides what a top-level declaration IS; read by `foldInterfaceDeclarations`,
// which jumps straight to each site instead of walking the program a second time. So "is this token an
// interface declaration?" stays ONE decision made in one place, and the second pass costs O(interfaces)
// rather than O(tokens).
//
// ⚠ **A SITE AND NOT THE DECLARATION ITSELF, AND THE REASON IS THE ONE HAZARD OF HAVING A PRE-SCAN AT ALL.**
// An interface method's parameter types are stored as RENDERED SOURCE STRINGS, and
// `IrInterface.renderDeclaredTypeName` spells a `genericInstance` as `ProgramSignatures.canonicalInstanceName`
// — an answer only the COMPLETE index can give. Read during the per-file sweep, `IntArray`'s alias is not yet
// in `genericAliases`, so the same type would render as the raw alias name from the sweep and as the canonical
// instance name from the real parse: two spellings of one type, which is a false ACCEPT on the conformance
// (the R5 review's finding) and a wrong `paramTypeNames` at every witness dispatch. Recording a POSITION here
// and reading the declaration only once `allFilesFolded` is true makes that divergence unrepresentable rather
// than merely unlikely — the renderer sees exactly the index the real parse will.
export type InterfaceDeclSite
	export var path as FilePath
	export var tokenPos as TokenIndex
	// The DECLARED name, read by the sweep off the token after the keyword (A1s wave 2). The declaration
	// itself is not read until `foldInterfaceDeclarations`, which runs after the whole sweep — and the
	// stdlib/user shadow contest is settled BEFORE that, so the name has to travel with the site or an
	// `interface` would be the one declaration kind the contest could not see.
	export var name as ByteArray

	export static function create(path FilePath, tokenPos TokenIndex, name ByteArray) returns InterfaceDeclSite
		return Self{path: path, tokenPos: tokenPos, name: name}
	end 'create'
end 'InterfaceDeclSite'

export typealias InterfaceDeclSiteArray = Array with InterfaceDeclSite

// ⭐ **WHERE ONE `extension <Interface>` DECLARATION SITS IN ITS FILE'S TOKEN STREAM.** The same
// "note the position, read the declaration once the program is whole" split `InterfaceDeclSite` makes, and
// here the second half of it is not an optimization at all — it is the only order that can work. An
// extension declares its methods on EVERY type whose `implements` clause names the interface, and those
// types may be declared in files this sweep has not reached; read per-file, an extension would publish
// methods for the conformers declared ABOVE it and none for the ones below.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS ITS OWN TYPE RATHER THAN AN `InterfaceDeclSite` WITH A DIFFERENT MEANING, BECAUSE OF WHAT IT
// DOES *NOT* CARRY.** An interface site holds a `name` the file DECLARES, and that name travels because the
// stdlib/user shadow contest is settled before the declaration is read and has to see it. An extension
// declares no name at all, so there is nothing for a contest to see and this site is a POSITION and nothing
// else — which is exactly what `Parser.recordScannedExtension`'s header already says it is.
//
// ⛔ **IT CARRIED A `targetName` UNTIL W133, WRITTEN BY THE SWEEP AND READ BY NOTHING IN THE COMPILER.** The
// sweep filled it with `identifierNameAt`, which answers the EMPTY name for a keyword — so an
// `extension int` would have travelled here under no name at all, and the field would have gone from dead to
// dead-and-wrong. The target is read from the TOKENS by `readExtensionHeader`, once, at each of the three
// passes that need it (that function's header is why there is one reader); a copy on the site would be that
// answer derived twice, and the copy is the one that cannot see a keyword.
export type ExtensionDeclSite
	export var path as FilePath
	export var tokenPos as TokenIndex

	export static function create(path FilePath, tokenPos TokenIndex) returns ExtensionDeclSite
		return Self{path: path, tokenPos: tokenPos}
	end 'create'
end 'ExtensionDeclSite'

export typealias ExtensionDeclSiteArray = Array with ExtensionDeclSite

// ⭐⭐ **WHERE ONE `type`/`enum`/`union` DECLARATION THAT WRITES AN `implements` CLAUSE SITS IN ITS FILE'S
// TOKEN STREAM (W95)** — the THIRD "note the position, read the declaration once the program is whole" site,
// after `InterfaceDeclSite` and `ExtensionDeclSite`, and the one whose deferral is forced by the FIRST of
// them.
//
// A conformance clause's own EXTENT depends on the whole program: whether the comma in
// `implements Marker with T, Holder with T` opens a second argument or a second interface is `Marker`'s
// `uses` arity, and that arity is `foldInterfaceDeclarations`' answer — a pass that cannot run until every
// file has folded. So the sweep reads what it can prove (`Parser.readOptionalImplementsClause` stops at the
// first unparenthesized `with`) and `Queries.foldConformanceClauses` re-reads the SAME tokens with the same
// reader once the arity is known.
//
// ⛔ **MEASURED before it existed: `implements Marker with T, Holder with T` gave `extension Holder`'s
// methods to nobody, while `implements Holder with T, Marker with T` — the identical clause, reordered —
// compiled and ran.** Clause ORDER decided semantics. The corpus's own `stdlib/Set.maxon` carried it:
// `Set.map()` returned `unknown` because `Iterable` sat after a `with`.
//
// ⚠ **IT CARRIES THE DECLARED NAME AND THE DECLARATION KIND, AND BOTH ARE LOAD-BEARING.** The name is how
// the re-read finds the layout to record onto — the registry's, not a fresh one — and the KIND chooses which
// registry, exactly as `Parser.enterTypeScope` takes the two together for the reason its header states.
export type ConformanceClauseDeclSite
	export var path as FilePath
	// The DECLARATION keyword (`type`/`enum`/`union`), not the `implements` one. The re-read has to re-enter
	// the declaration's own scope before it can render a `with` argument — `Self` and a `uses T` parameter
	// both resolve against it — and re-running `readTypeHeader` + `openTypeParamScope` from here is that
	// scope, taken through the doors that already own it rather than reconstructed beside them.
	export var tokenPos as TokenIndex
	export var declName as ByteArray
	export var declIsEnum as bool

	export static function create(path FilePath, tokenPos TokenIndex, declName ByteArray, declIsEnum bool) returns ConformanceClauseDeclSite
		return Self{path: path, tokenPos: tokenPos, declName: declName, declIsEnum: declIsEnum}
	end 'create'
end 'ConformanceClauseDeclSite'

export typealias ConformanceClauseDeclSiteArray = Array with ConformanceClauseDeclSite

// ⭐⭐ **WHERE ONE SET OF A GENERIC TYPE'S METHODS BEGINS — the site list the whole-program
// LAYOUT-DESCRIPTOR-NEED fixpoint walks (`Queries.foldDescriptorNeeds`).** A generic `type`'s own body is
// one site; every `extension` body over a generic target is another, one per conformer it publishes onto.
// `typeName` is the type those methods belong to — the CONFORMER for an extension — so every method the
// fixpoint collects is keyed by the same `Type.method` name a call resolves to.
//
// ⭐ **ONLY A GENERIC TYPE IS RECORDED, AND THAT IS WHAT KEEPS THE COST OFF EVERY OTHER PROGRAM.** A
// descriptor exists to carry a TYPE PARAMETER's layout at run time, so a type with no `uses` clause can
// never need one — and a program that declares no generic type records no site, which makes
// `foldDescriptorNeeds` an empty-array test that allocates nothing. That is `typeDescriptorNeeds`' old
// "a non-generic type allocates NOTHING here" property, moved up a level and made whole-program.
export type DescriptorScanSite
	export var path as FilePath
	// The first token of the member list — a `type` body's first token, or an extension body's. Recorded by
	// the walk that already stands there (`recordScannedType`, `foldExtensionDeclarationInto`), so the
	// fixpoint's later walk starts exactly where a member walk starts and needs no header re-read.
	export var bodyStart as TokenIndex
	export var typeName as ByteArray

	export static function create(path FilePath, bodyStart TokenIndex, typeName ByteArray) returns DescriptorScanSite
		return Self{path: path, bodyStart: bodyStart, typeName: typeName}
	end 'create'
end 'DescriptorScanSite'

export typealias DescriptorScanSiteArray = Array with DescriptorScanSite

// The qualified names of the methods that carry the hidden layout-descriptor parameter (P1.6-B1), solved
// whole-program. A Set rather than a name->bool map for the reason the answer is one bit: membership IS the
// need, so there is no `false` entry to disagree with an absence.
export typealias DescriptorNeedNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// The descriptor-need set a program with no generic type carries — none, shared, for
// `sharedEmptyWhereConstraints`' reason: the whole scale corpus declares no generic type, and one allocation
// per compile for an answer that is always empty is exactly the linear regression `typeDescriptorNeeds`'
// own header refused.
let sharedEmptyDescriptorNeeds = DescriptorNeedNameSet.create()

// The `uses` list an UNDECLARED name answers with — none, shared, for `sharedEmptyDescriptorNeeds`' reason.
let sharedEmptyTypeParamNames = ByteArrayArray.create()

// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO DOORS INTO THE EXTENSION MECHANISM, told apart once** (conditional extensions). `extension`
// takes an INTERFACE name or a declared TYPE name, and the difference is not cosmetic — it decides what the
// conformer set is, what a `where` clause's parameter names mean, and WHEN the constraint can be checked:
//
//   • **An INTERFACE extension expands per CONFORMER, and its `where` clause is a COMPILE-TIME FILTER.** The
//     parameters are the interface's associated types, and each conformer binds them CONCRETELY in its own
//     `implements … with …` clause, so "does this conformer satisfy the constraint?" has an answer the fold
//     can take. A conformer that fails is silently skipped, which is what the spec's `constraint-not-met`
//     case rests on: the method simply does not exist on that type.
//   • **A TYPE extension expands ONCE, onto the type itself, and its `where` clause is a WITNESS CONSTRAINT.**
//     The parameters are the type's own `uses` list, which is bound per INSTANTIATION rather than per
//     conformer — and shv2 shares one compiled body across every instance of a generic type (P1.6-A), so
//     there is nothing to filter and nothing to specialize. The constraint becomes exactly what a type-level
//     `where` already is: hidden witness parameters, threaded by the dictionary-passing ABI.
// ⚠ **THE TWO ARMS CARRY THE SAME PAYLOAD SHAPE AND ARE NOT ONE ARM.** What separates them is not their
// contents but what a `where` clause MEANS under each — a filter the fold can evaluate, or hidden parameters
// the ABI must thread — and that is a fact about the arm, not about a field it could carry as a flag.
export union ExtensionTarget
	// `paramNames` is the interface's `uses` list — the positions a `WhereConstraint.typeParamIndex` and a
	// conformer's `StructLayout.conformanceBindings` are both indexed by.
	interfaceExtension(paramNames ByteArrayArray, conformers ByteArrayArray)
	// `paramNames` is the type's own `uses` list, and `conformers` is the extended type alone.
	typeExtension(paramNames ByteArrayArray, conformers ByteArrayArray)
	// A name that declares neither an interface nor a type. It expands nothing; the real parse reports it with
	// a position.
	undeclaredTarget
end 'ExtensionTarget'

// The declaration whose parameter positions an `extension`'s `where` clause names — an interface's associated
// types, or a type's `uses` list. Empty for an undeclared target, which makes every `where` clause on one a
// refusal at `readWhereClause` rather than a silent acceptance.
export function extensionTargetParamNames(target ExtensionTarget) returns ByteArrayArray
	match target 'target'
		interfaceExtension(paramNames, _) then return paramNames
		typeExtension(paramNames, _) then return paramNames
		undeclaredTarget then return sharedEmptyDeclaredConformances
	end 'target'
end 'extensionTargetParamNames'

// The types an `extension`'s methods are declared ON, in the order BOTH passes must walk them in — see
// `conformersOf` for why one answer rather than two derivations.
export function extensionTargetConformers(target ExtensionTarget) returns ByteArrayArray
	match target 'target'
		interfaceExtension(_, conformers) then return conformers
		typeExtension(_, conformers) then return conformers
		undeclaredTarget then return sharedEmptyDeclaredConformances
	end 'target'
end 'extensionTargetConformers'

// ⭐ **AN `extension <Name> [implements …] [where …]` HEADER, AS BOTH PASSES READ IT** —
// `Parser.readExtensionHeader` returns it with the cursor left on the body's first token. The two passes
// must agree about all four of these (which types get methods, what conformance the extension ADDS, under
// what condition, and where to blame a malformed clause), so they read one header through one reader rather
// than each taking the parts it happens to need.
export type ExtensionHeader
	export let nameTok as Token
	export let target as ExtensionTarget
	// ⭐ **THE `implements I1, I2` AN EXTENSION DECLARES ON BEHALF OF THE EXTENDED TYPE — a CONDITIONAL
	// conformance when the header also carries a `where`.** Empty for the plain `extension X` that only adds
	// methods, which is the whole corpus bar `stdlib/Array.maxon:637` and `stdlib/PrimitiveExtensions.maxon`.
	//
	// It is read HERE rather than by the real parse alone because the cursor must land on the body's first
	// token identically in both passes: the fold reads the same header and would otherwise stop at
	// `implements` and publish no signature for a single member of the body.
	export let interfaces as InterfaceConformanceArray
	// Empty for an unconditional `extension` — the whole existing corpus.
	export let constraints as WhereConstraintArray

	export static function create(nameTok Token, target ExtensionTarget, interfaces InterfaceConformanceArray, constraints WhereConstraintArray) returns ExtensionHeader
		return Self{nameTok: nameTok, target: target, interfaces: interfaces, constraints: constraints}
	end 'create'
end 'ExtensionHeader'

// ⭐ **ONE CONDITIONAL EXTENSION METHOD WITHHELD FROM ONE CONFORMER, kept so the CALL SITE can say why.**
// Without it the author of `h.isGreater(…)` is told only that no such method exists, which is true and
// useless: the method is written, in a file they can see, and what they need to know is the condition it was
// written under and which of their bindings fails it. Filed by the fold under the qualified method name the
// call resolves to (`MyHolder.isGreater`), because that is the key the refusal has in hand.
export type ConditionalExtensionSkip
	export let typeName as ByteArray
	export let methodName as ByteArray
	// The extension's WHOLE `where` clause, rendered as written (`where Item is Comparable`) — the condition,
	// not merely the part of it that failed. A reader deciding whether to satisfy the constraint or to stop
	// using the method needs all of it.
	export let clause as String
	// The FIRST constraint the conformer fails, and what it binds the constrained parameter to. First rather
	// than all: one unmet requirement is one edit, and listing the rest would name consequences of the same
	// gap.
	export let unmetInterfaceName as ByteArray
	export let bindingName as ByteArray

	export static function create(typeName ByteArray, methodName ByteArray, clause String, unmetInterfaceName ByteArray, bindingName ByteArray) returns ConditionalExtensionSkip
		return Self{typeName: typeName, methodName: methodName, clause: clause, unmetInterfaceName: unmetInterfaceName, bindingName: bindingName}
	end 'create'
end 'ConditionalExtensionSkip'

export typealias ConditionalExtensionSkipMap = Map with (ByteArray, ConditionalExtensionSkip)

// ⭐ **INTERFACE NAME -> ITS ASSOCIATED POSITIONS' HOLDINGS, one row per interface that holds at least one**
// (see `ProgramSignatures.settleAssociatedTypeHoldings`). The row is indexed by the interface's OWN
// associated-type position and holds the interface that position is held at, or the empty name where the
// program holds it at nothing.
//
// ⚠ **A ROW RATHER THAN A JOINED `<interface>.<position>` KEY, because the hot ask is not the one this
// feature is for.** Every witness table minted in the program asks "does this interface hold anything at
// all" (`interfaceHoldsAnAssociatedType`), which a per-interface key answers with one probe and no join; a
// joined key would allocate a String per table per position to ask a question whose answer is almost always
// no.
export typealias AssociatedTypeHoldingMap = Map with (ByteArray, ByteArrayArray)

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A `with` ARGUMENT THAT DENOTES AN INTERFACE ACTUALLY IS — an INSTANTIATION of one, or the bare
// name of one (W57).** `ProgramSignatures.heldArgOf` is the single reading, and the split exists because its
// two askers want two different halves: the holding record and E3125 want the interface NAME, which both
// arms have; a DISPATCH through the held value wants that interface's own `with` arguments, which only the
// instantiated arm carries. An argument denoting no interface at all is neither — it is `MapError.keyNotFound`
// at that door, exactly as it was before the split.
export union HeldAssociatedArg
	instantiated(gid GenericInstanceId)
	bare(interfaceName ByteArray)
end 'HeldAssociatedArg'

// The witness constraints of an extension method that are NOT its enclosing type's own — see
// `ExtensionTarget`'s type arm. Keyed by the qualified method name, which is the key both the reservation
// side (`Parser.witnessParamCountFor`, through the constraints the extension body is parsed under) and the
// supply side (`LowerMaxonToStd.appendWitnessArgs`) resolve a callee by.
//
// ⚠ **IT HOLDS THE EXTRAS, NOT THE WHOLE SLOT LIST.** A method's witness slots are the type's own constraints
// FOLLOWED by these (`ProgramSignatures.typeExtensionWitnessConstraints`), so an entry here is one INPUT to
// that answer and never the answer itself. The distinction is what lets the same entry serve the other
// question this map exists for — which constraints are validated NOWHERE ELSE, and so must be checked at the
// call (`Parser.requireReceiverSatisfiesExtensionConstraints`) — since a type's own constraints are already
// settled at the instantiation site by E3017.
export typealias ExtensionMethodConstraintMap = Map with (ByteArray, WhereConstraintArray)

// The answer to "was this missing method WITHHELD, or is it simply absent?" — a case rather than an empty
// record, because the two readings lead to two different diagnostics and a caller must not be able to take
// the wrong one by forgetting to test a field.
export union ConditionalExtensionSkipLookup
	withheld(skip ConditionalExtensionSkip)
	notWithheld
end 'ConditionalExtensionSkipLookup'

// The constraint list a callee with no enclosing type carries — none, shared, for
// `sharedEmptyWhereConstraints`' reason: every free function in the corpus asks and none has one.
let sharedEmptyMethodWitnessConstraints = WhereConstraintArray.create()

// ⭐ **DO TWO `MaxonType`s DENOTE THE SAME TYPE? — the (tag, id) pair, which is the identity
// `GenericInstanceRegistry.instanceKey` already builds an instance's key out of.** A tag with no payload
// answers `typeNameIdOf` uniformly, so the one comparison serves every arm.
//
// It exists because a SUBSTITUTION has to be able to say it moved NOTHING: `substituteThroughInstance` returns
// the argument unchanged for a type with no declaration view, and its callers — the recursion itself, and the
// parser's field read, which must not re-resolve a slot the instance did not touch — decide on exactly that.
// ⚠ It is NOT a general structural equality: two `named` ids from DIFFERENT interners are incomparable, which
// is why every caller compares a substitution's output against its own input, both from one table.
export function maxonTypesDenoteSame(a MaxonType, b MaxonType) returns bool
	return maxonTypeTag(a) == maxonTypeTag(b) and typeNameIdOf(a) == typeNameIdOf(b)
end 'maxonTypesDenoteSame'

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A DECLARATION MAKES A TYPE NAME *MEAN*** — the thing a redeclaration of a COMPILER-OWNED name
// has to be compared against, because W3's rule is that a name has ONE meaning and not that the compiler
// must be the one to write it down (`Project.declarationContestsACompilerOwnedName`).
//
// ⚠ **THE DEFECT THE RESERVATION EXISTS TO PREVENT IS A *DISAGREEING* DECLARATION, AND ONLY THAT.**
// `specs-shv2/character-set.md`'s `charset-alias-is-compiler-owned` records it: before the name was
// reserved, `typealias CharSet = Set with String` silently OVERWROTE the compiler's meaning and the author
// was then blamed at the first USE four lines down (*"requires a Character key — got a 'String' value"*).
// A declaration that says exactly what the compiler already says overwrites nothing and can produce no such
// report, so refusing it is a false reject — and it refused `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon:19`'s own line,
// which `specs/lazy-static.md` writes verbatim.
export union DeclaredTypeMeaning
	// A `type`, an `enum`/`union` or a FUNCTION typealias — each MINTS a nominal identity of its own, which
	// by construction is not any other declaration's and not the compiler's. So every such declaration of an
	// owned name contests it, and that is said HERE, once, by the comparison declining this arm — never by a
	// per-name list that could go stale as names join `isCompilerOwnedTypeName`.
	nominalIdentity
	// A GENERIC-INSTANCE typealias — `Base with Args`, which mints no identity at all: it NAMES the instance
	// its base and arguments intern to, so two of them agree exactly when those agree. The flat `args` array
	// is the pre-order argument tree `GenericAliasEntry` carries, and base + args + shape is the same content
	// `GenericInstanceRegistry.instanceKey` builds an instance's identity out of — compared here BEFORE that
	// interner exists, because the declaration door runs in the sweep that builds it.
	genericInstance(baseName ByteArray, args GenericArgNodeArray, argCount FieldIndex)
end 'DeclaredTypeMeaning'

// ⭐⭐ **THE MEANING THE COMPILER ALREADY HOLDS FOR AN OWNED NAME — ONE TABLE, FOR THE WHOLE SET.**
//
// `CharSet` is the only entry, and that is a consequence rather than a shortlist: it is the only owned name
// that DENOTES a generic instance rather than minting an identity — `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon:19` declares
// it as `typealias CharSet = Set with Character`, and a USER file writing that exact line therefore says what
// the compiler already says. (The compiler upserted its own copy of the alias until W129; the meaning stated
// here is what survived, because it is the RESERVATION's rule and not the registration's.) Every other owned
// name denotes a builtin TAG (`String`, `Character`, `ExitCode`, `Codepoint`), a corpus TYPE
// (`CharacterSet`, `StringIndex`) or a corpus enum/alias (`Ordering`, `HashValue`, the `__Managed*Error`s),
// and none of those is a thing a declaration in a user file can BE. They therefore fall to
// `nominalIdentity`, which the comparison below declines for every input — so the set is closed by the
// comparison and not by this function having to enumerate it.
export function compilerOwnedTypeMeaning(name ByteArray) returns DeclaredTypeMeaning
	if name.equals(CharacterMemberSetAliasName) 'theCharacterMemberSet'
		var args = GenericArgNodeArray.create()
		args.push(GenericArgNode.leaf(MaxonType.character))
		return DeclaredTypeMeaning.genericInstance(SetBuiltinBaseName, args: args, argCount: 1)
	end 'theCharacterMemberSet'

	return DeclaredTypeMeaning.nominalIdentity
end 'compilerOwnedTypeMeaning'

// Do two declarations make a name denote the SAME type?
export function typeMeaningsAgree(owned DeclaredTypeMeaning, declared DeclaredTypeMeaning) returns bool
	match owned 'owned'
		// ⚠ **A MINTED IDENTITY AGREES WITH NOTHING, NOT EVEN A STRUCTURALLY IDENTICAL TWIN.** Two `type
		// Point` declarations are two types — which is why a redeclaration is E3006 and not a no-op — so a
		// nominal meaning on EITHER side is a disagreement, and this arm is what closes the owned-name set.
		nominalIdentity then return false
		genericInstance(baseName, args, argCount) then return declaredInstanceMeaningAgrees(declared, baseName: baseName, args: args, argCount: argCount)
	end 'owned'
end 'typeMeaningsAgree'

// The second half of the pair, written out because Maxon matches ONE scrutinee at a time.
function declaredInstanceMeaningAgrees(declared DeclaredTypeMeaning, baseName ByteArray, args GenericArgNodeArray, argCount FieldIndex) returns bool
	match declared 'declared'
		nominalIdentity then return false
		genericInstance(otherBase, otherArgs, otherCount) then return baseName.equals(otherBase) and argCount == otherCount and genericArgNodesDenoteSame(args, other: otherArgs)
	end 'declared'
end 'declaredInstanceMeaningAgrees'

// Node for node over the two pre-order argument trees. The COUNT is compared first because a nested instance
// contributes nodes past the top-level arity, so equal `argCount`s do not imply equal trees.
function genericArgNodesDenoteSame(args GenericArgNodeArray, other GenericArgNodeArray) returns bool
	if args.count() != other.count() 'differentTrees'
		return false
	end 'differentTrees'

	for i in 0 upto args.count() 'eachNode'
		let node = try args.get(i) otherwise panic("genericArgNodesDenoteSame: args.get OOB at {i} below count {args.count()}")
		let peer = try other.get(i) otherwise panic("genericArgNodesDenoteSame: other.get OOB at {i} below the count this loop already matched")
		if not genericArgNodeDenotesSame(node, other: peer) 'differs'
			return false
		end 'differs'
	end 'eachNode'

	return true
end 'genericArgNodesDenoteSame'

function genericArgNodeDenotesSame(node GenericArgNode, other GenericArgNode) returns bool
	match node 'node'
		leaf(argType) then return declaredLeafDenotesSame(other, argType: argType)
		instanceHeader(baseName, argCount, fixedSize) then return declaredHeaderDenotesSame(other, baseName: baseName, argCount: argCount, fixedSize: fixedSize)
	end 'node'
end 'genericArgNodeDenotesSame'

function declaredLeafDenotesSame(other GenericArgNode, argType MaxonType) returns bool
	match other 'other'
		leaf(otherType) then return maxonTypesDenoteSame(argType, b: otherType)
		instanceHeader then return false
	end 'other'
end 'declaredLeafDenotesSame'

function declaredHeaderDenotesSame(other GenericArgNode, baseName ByteArray, argCount FieldIndex, fixedSize FixedElementCount) returns bool
	match other 'other'
		leaf then return false
		instanceHeader(otherBase, otherCount, otherSize) then return baseName.equals(otherBase) and argCount == otherCount and fixedSize == otherSize
	end 'other'
end 'declaredHeaderDenotesSame'

// ⭐ **AN INTERFACE'S POSITION IN DECLARATION ORDER — the whole-program, interner-FREE handle a
// `witnessDispatch` names its requirement's interface by.** See `ProgramSignatures.interfaceNamesInDeclOrder`
// for why an index and not a name or an interner id. The range is the machine word rather than a tight
// bound because nothing caps how many interfaces a program declares.
export typealias InterfaceDeclIndex = int(0 to u64.max)

export typealias InterfaceDeclIndexMap = Map with (ByteArray, InterfaceDeclIndex)

// The name → position map for the SYNTHESIZED protocols, built from the very roster that seeds the ordered
// list so the two halves of the relation are one act here exactly as they are in
// `recordInterfaceDeclaration`. `ProgramSignatures.create` is the only caller.
function builtinInterfaceDeclIndices() returns InterfaceDeclIndexMap
	var indices = InterfaceDeclIndexMap.create()
	let names = builtinInterfaceNames()
	for i in 0 upto names.count() 'eachProtocol'
		indices.upsert(try names.get(i) otherwise panic("builtinInterfaceDeclIndices: builtinInterfaceNames().get OOB at {i} below count {names.count()}"), value: i)
	end 'eachProtocol'
	return indices
end 'builtinInterfaceDeclIndices'

// ⭐ **THE ONE PLACE A `throws` CLAUSE ENTERS A CONTENT HASH.** Both arms are marked, because both are
// possible in the positions this is asked from and a hash that mixed nothing for `none` would let a
// requirement LOSE its `throws` without any reader's memo moving — and the ABI a witness dispatch picks is
// exactly that fact (`WitnessDispatchTarget.throwsClause`).
function mixThrowsClause(state ContentHash, clause ThrowsClause) returns ContentHash
	return match clause 'k'
		none gives mix(state, value: ThrowsShapeNone)
		throwing(throwsName) gives mixBytes(mix(state, value: ThrowsShapeThrowing), bytes: throwsName.toByteArray())
	end 'k'
end 'mixThrowsClause'

// The rendered FORMALS of one interface requirement, in declaration order (R8). BOTH columns ride and they
// are parallel: a witness dispatch slots its call's arguments against the NAMES (`WitnessDispatchTarget`'s
// authoritative label vocabulary — the interface's names, never a conformer's) and rejects a concrete value
// against the TYPES, so renaming a formal in file A changes what `f(x, other: y)` means in file B. The COUNT
// leads because arity alone decides E3036 at every dispatch; per-slot markers are unnecessary because
// `mixBytes` already carries a length tag, which is what keeps `("ab","c")` apart from `("a","bc")`.
function mixInterfaceFormals(state ContentHash, names ByteArrayArray, types ByteArrayArray) returns ContentHash
	var out = mix(state, value: names.count())
	for i in 0 upto names.count() 'eachFormal'
		let name = try names.get(i) otherwise panic("mixInterfaceFormals: names.get OOB at {i} below count {names.count()}")
		let typeName = try types.get(i) otherwise panic("mixInterfaceFormals: types.get OOB at {i} — an InterfaceMethodSig's paramNames and paramTypeNames are parallel by construction ({names.count()} names, {types.count()} types)")
		out = mixBytes(mixBytes(out, bytes: name), bytes: typeName)
	end 'eachFormal'
	return out
end 'mixInterfaceFormals'

// The "." that joins a per-instance / inner alias's parts (P1.6-C). Its own constant so the JOIN and the
// SPLIT below cannot spell the qualifier two ways; it is also what `Parser.parseTypeReference` reads an
// `Instance.Member` type reference off, so all three agree by construction.
let QualifiedAliasSeparator = "."

// The BYTE value of `QualifiedAliasSeparator` (ASCII '.'), for the allocation-free pre-check
// `containsQualifier`. Named so the byte and the string form of the separator cannot drift.
let QualifiedAliasSeparatorByte = 46

// The byte a compound NAME is divided at — the argument `lastSeparatorIndex` takes so the two divided
// namespaces this file mints ("." for a namespace chain, "$" for a range-qualified element) share one scan.
// ASCII by construction: both separators are punctuation the lexer can spell, and a name is UTF-8, so a
// separator is never a continuation byte.
typealias NameSeparatorByte = int(0 to 127)

// ⭐ **THE INDEX OF THE LAST `separator` BYTE IN `name`, or `name.count()` when it holds none** — the ONE
// scan EVERY separator-divided name in this file is answered from, so "does it divide?", "where?" and
// "what is the part before it?" cannot come to disagree about which byte the separator is.
//
// ⚠ **IT TAKES THE SEPARATOR RATHER THAN NAMING ONE, BECAUSE THIS FILE MINTS TWO DIVIDED NAMESPACES AND
// THE SECOND ARRIVED AS A COPY OF THIS SCAN (N2 review).** The "." divides a namespace-qualified name
// (`lib.fmt.Score`) and the "$" divides a range-qualified element (`Byte$0_255`); they are two separators,
// not two algorithms, and a second hand-rolled "find the byte, slice before it" is exactly the shape whose
// copies drift into a WRONG ANSWER rather than a compile error — the split silently lands one byte off and
// a name roster quietly stops matching.
//
// ⚠ **THE *LAST*, NOT THE FIRST, AND THAT IS WHAT LIFTED THE TWO-SEGMENT CAP (N1b).** A namespace is a
// DIRECTORY CHAIN — `lib.fmt.Score`, `lib.inner.deepHelper` — so the qualifier is everything before the
// final separator and the member is the final segment. For the two-segment names that were the only ones
// before that rung (`Wrapper.Idx`, `Point.create`) first and last are the same byte, so no existing answer
// moves; for a three-segment one the last is the only split that names the member the author wrote.
// For the "$" reader the two coincide unconditionally: a mint's base is a name `multiplyDeclared` holds,
// and a mint itself is never contested (it has exactly one declaration), so no name carries two of them.
//
// An INDEX loop over a byte range, not `for b in name`: this is a per-named-value-coercion hot path, and an
// index range allocates no iterator object where the collection form might.
function lastSeparatorIndex(name ByteArray, separator NameSeparatorByte) returns ElementCount
	var found = name.count()
	for i in 0 upto name.count() 'scan'
		if (try name.get(i) otherwise panic("lastSeparatorIndex: get OOB despite i < count()")) == separator 'here'
			found = i
		end 'here'
	end 'scan'
	return found
end 'lastSeparatorIndex'

// The index of the FIRST `separator` in `name`, or `name.count()` when there is none — `lastSeparatorIndex`'s
// twin, and the one `sourceSpelledAliasName` divides at (see there for why the two ends differ). Written as
// its own scan rather than derived from the other: an early return is what makes it stop at the first hit,
// which is the entire difference between them.
function firstSeparatorIndex(name ByteArray, separator NameSeparatorByte) returns ElementCount
	for i in 0 upto name.count() 'scan'
		if (try name.get(i) otherwise panic("firstSeparatorIndex: get OOB despite i < count()")) == separator 'here'
			return i
		end 'here'
	end 'scan'
	return name.count()
end 'firstSeparatorIndex'

// ⭐ Does `name` contain the qualifier byte at all? The cheap, ALLOCATION-FREE guard that keeps the
// per-instance machinery off the plain-alias hot path (P1.6-C): a plain name (`Score`) has no ".", so this
// returns false WITHOUT the two slices `splitQualifiedName` would otherwise allocate on every named-type
// resolution and every aggregate coercion. Derived from `lastSeparatorIndex` rather than scanning for
// itself, so the byte this asks about and the byte the split divides on are one line of code.
export function containsQualifier(name ByteArray) returns bool
	return lastSeparatorIndex(name, separator: QualifiedAliasSeparatorByte) < name.count()
end 'containsQualifier'

// `prefix.member` — the base-qualified name of an inner alias ("Wrapper.Idx") and the qualified name of a
// per-instance alias ("IntWrapper.Idx"), built the ONE way so the value column, the registries and the
// checks cannot disagree. `prefix`/`member` are identifiers and carry no ".", so `splitQualifiedName`
// inverts it exactly.
export function joinQualifiedName(prefix ByteArray, member ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	return "{String.from(prefix)}{QualifiedAliasSeparator}{String.from(member)}".toByteArray()
end 'joinQualifiedName'

// ⭐ **`{namespace}.{name}` — THE DIRECTORY-QUALIFIED SPELLING OF A DECLARATION (N1b).** `utils` + `helper`
// ⇒ `utils.helper`; `lib.fmt` + `Score` ⇒ `lib.fmt.Score`.
//
// It routes through `joinQualifiedName` rather than spelling a separator of its own, so the JOIN and the
// SPLIT stay one fact however many segments the namespace brought with it: a namespace's own internal
// separators come from `namespaceForModuleDirectory` (which renders the `/`-joined module directory), and
// this adds the LAST one — which is exactly the one `splitQualifiedName` divides at.
//
// ⚠ **THE NON-EMPTY PRECONDITION IS CHECKED HERE AND NOT MERELY WRITTEN DOWN**, because it is a rule with
// TWO INDEPENDENT GUARDS in front of it — `Parser.recordScannedSignature`'s `not self.namespace.isEmpty()`
// and `Parser.pushNamespaceQualifiedAlias`'s `self.namespace.isEmpty()` — and a third caller that forgot
// would file a declaration under `.helper`, a key no call site can ever spell, with nothing red anywhere.
// A root-level file's namespace is `""`; there is no qualified spelling of a root declaration, because the
// root IS the unqualified namespace. Once per DECLARATION, so the test is free.
export function namespaceQualifiedName(namespace String, name ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	if namespace.isEmpty() 'noNamespaceToQualifyWith'
		panic("namespaceQualifiedName: '{String.from(name)}' was asked for its directory-qualified spelling with an EMPTY namespace — a root-level declaration has none, and every caller must have gated on that before reaching here")
	end 'noNamespaceToQualifyWith'

	return joinQualifiedName(namespace.toByteArray(), member: name)
end 'namespaceQualifiedName'

// The TYPE half of a `Type.method` callee, or the callee itself when it names a free function. Written
// through `splitQualifiedName` rather than as a second scan for the separator, so the one inverse of
// `joinQualifiedName` stays the only place the split is spelled.
export function qualifiedCalleeHead(callee ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	return match splitQualifiedName(callee) 'shape'
		parts(prefix, _) gives prefix
		notQualified gives callee
	end 'shape'
end 'qualifiedCalleeHead'

// The (prefix, member) of a `prefix.member` name, or `notQualified`. An identifier carries no ".", so the
// split at the LAST separator is EXACT: a plain alias (`Score`) yields `notQualified`, a per-instance/inner
// name (`Wrapper.Idx`) yields its two parts, and a namespace-qualified one (`lib.fmt.Score`) yields the
// whole directory chain and the member — which is what every reader of this union wants (the unqualified
// name for a diagnostic, the receiver half for a per-instance probe).
export union QualifiedNameParts
	parts(prefix ByteArray, member ByteArray)
	notQualified
end 'QualifiedNameParts'

// ⚠ **IT USED TO REFUSE ANYTHING BUT EXACTLY TWO SEGMENTS, AND THAT WAS A SILENT WRONG ANSWER ONCE N1b
// MADE THREE REACHABLE.** A three-segment name answered `notQualified` — so `BorrowCheck.calleeDisplayName`
// would have quoted `lib.inner.deepHelper` where the user reads `deepHelper`, and every "is this qualified?"
// probe would have said no about a name that plainly is. The cap was correct only while the sole producers
// joined two identifiers; the split is now the inverse of the join for any number of them.
export function splitQualifiedName(name ByteArray) returns QualifiedNameParts
	// The allocation-free short-circuit: a name with no "." is not qualified, so it never reaches the two
	// slices below — the plain-alias hot path pays only the byte scan.
	let split = lastSeparatorIndex(name, separator: QualifiedAliasSeparatorByte)
	if split == name.count() 'notQualified'
		return QualifiedNameParts.notQualified
	end 'notQualified'

	let prefix = try name.slice(0, endIndex: split) otherwise panic("splitQualifiedName: slice [0, {split}) of a {name.count()}-byte name is in range by the index `lastSeparatorIndex` just found")
	let member = try name.slice(split + 1, endIndex: name.count()) otherwise panic("splitQualifiedName: slice [{split + 1}, {name.count()}) is in range by the index `lastSeparatorIndex` just found")
	return QualifiedNameParts.parts(prefix, member: member)
end 'splitQualifiedName'

// ⭐ **IS THIS RANGED-ALIAS ENTRY THE NAMESPACE-QUALIFIED MIRROR RATHER THAN A DECLARATION THE USER WROTE
// (N1b)?** — the ONE test, so the two readers that must tell them apart (`commitTypeAliases`, which owes
// E3105 exactly once per real pair, and `ProgramSignatures.noteUserDeclaredTypeNames`, whose set is defined
// as the names a user file DECLARES) decide it the same way.
//
// ⚠ **THE DOT IS EXACT HERE, NOT A HEURISTIC**, and the argument is the same one `joinQualifiedName`'s header
// makes: a source-written typealias name is an IDENTIFIER, which cannot hold a "." — the lexer never puts one
// in an identifier token — and the OTHER dotted ranged aliases in the tree (a generic type's inner alias,
// `Wrapper.Idx`) travel in `innerAliasEntries`, a different array with a different fold. So a dotted name in a
// `RangedTypeAliasArray` is exactly and only the mirror `Parser.pushNamespaceQualifiedAlias` minted.
export function mirrorsANamespaceQualifiedAlias(alias RangedTypeAlias) returns bool
	return containsQualifier(alias.name)
end 'mirrorsANamespaceQualifiedAlias'

// ⭐⭐ **THE "$" THAT DIVIDES A RANGE-QUALIFIED ELEMENT NAME FROM ITS BASE (N2)** — `Byte$0_255`.
//
// ⚠ **IT MAY NOT BE THE "." ABOVE, AND THAT IS NOT A PREFERENCE.** `splitQualifiedName` reads a dotted name
// as `<directory chain>.<member>`, so a mint spelled `Byte.0_255` would present itself to every
// qualified-name reader in the compiler as the member `0_255` of a namespace `Byte` — including
// `mirrorsANamespaceQualifiedAlias` directly above, whose whole claim is that a dotted alias name is
// EXACTLY the namespace mirror.
//
// ⭐ **AND NO SOURCE CAN COLLIDE WITH A MINT.** The lexer's identifier run admits letters, digits, `_` and
// `.` and nothing else, so a `$` cannot appear in a declared name at all: the minted half of the element
// namespace and the declarable half are disjoint BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by a check, which is the
// property `ReservedTypeNamePrefix` buys one namespace over.
let CompilerQualifiedAliasSeparator = "$"

// The BYTE value of `CompilerQualifiedAliasSeparator` (ASCII '$'), for the allocation-free scan below. Named
// so the byte and the string form cannot drift — `QualifiedAliasSeparatorByte`'s discipline.
let CompilerQualifiedAliasSeparatorByte = 36

// ⭐⭐ **THE RANGE-QUALIFIED SPELLING OF ONE DECLARATION OF A CONTESTED ALIAS (N2)** — `Byte` +
// `int(0 to 255)` ⇒ `Byte$0_255`. The ONE speller, so the MINT that registers the declaration
// (`rangeQualifiedMint`) and the READ that resolves an element to it (`rangeScopedName`) build the same
// bytes; a second spelling would be a name the registry holds and no instance ever asks for.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS DERIVED FROM THE RANGE AND NOT FROM AN ORDINAL, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES IT ORDER-FREE.** An
// ordinal would depend on which claimant the sweep reached first — filesystem enumeration order, which
// `StdlibLoader`'s header forbids relying on. Two files declaring the same range mint the same name, which
// is exactly right: they are the same element type, and the corpus is entirely of that shape.
export function rangeQualifiedAliasName(name ByteArray, low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt) returns ByteArray
	return "{String.from(name)}{CompilerQualifiedAliasSeparator}{low}_{high}".toByteArray()
end 'rangeQualifiedAliasName'

// ⭐⭐ **THE INSTANCE-QUALIFIED SPELLING OF ONE DECLARATION OF A CONTESTED GENERIC ALIAS (N3)** — `Bag` +
// `Array with String` ⇒ `Bag$Array_String`. `rangeQualifiedAliasName`'s sibling one layer up, sharing its
// separator and therefore its strip, and derived from the same principle: the qualifier is the very thing
// the declarations DISAGREE about, so two declarations that name one instance mint one name and only a real
// disagreement splits the namespace.
//
// ⭐ **THE QUALIFIER IS THE INSTANCE'S CANONICAL NAME AND NOT THE DECLARING FILE'S PATH.** A path would be
// unique per declaration, which is more than the question needs — and it would put a machine-specific
// absolute path into a type-name key, where a spec fragment staged in a temp directory would spell one thing
// here and another on the next host. `canonicalInstanceName` is structural over the base and the args, so it
// is a fact about the program and about nothing else.
export function instanceQualifiedAliasName(name ByteArray, canonicalInstance ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	return "{String.from(name)}{CompilerQualifiedAliasSeparator}{String.from(canonicalInstance)}".toByteArray()
end 'instanceQualifiedAliasName'

// ⭐⭐ **THE NAME AS *SOURCE* SPELLS IT** — `Byte$0_255` ⇒ `Byte`, and any name the compiler never
// qualified handed back UNCHANGED, by reference, so an unqualified name costs one byte scan and no
// allocation. The exact inverse of BOTH mints (`rangeQualifiedAliasName`, `instanceQualifiedAliasName`), so
// a mint and the strip cannot come to disagree about where a name divides.
//
// ⚠ **IT DIVIDES AT THE FIRST `$`, NOT THE LAST (N3), AND THE TWO STOPPED AGREEING WHEN THE MINTS NESTED.**
// A generic alias's qualifier is an instance's canonical name, which CONTAINS a range qualifier whenever its
// element is a contested one — `Bytes$Array_Byte$0_255`. Splitting at the last `$` hands that back as
// `Bytes$Array_Byte`, a name no source line holds and no registry answers for. `$` is unspellable in an
// identifier, so every `$` in a name begins the compiler's own bookkeeping and the SOURCE spelling is
// everything before the first one — one rule, whatever depth the mints reach.
//
// **ITS READERS WANT THE SAME THING FOR TWO REASONS, AND THAT IS WHY THERE IS ONE FUNCTION** (no count is
// written down: a count is a copy of a list that lives elsewhere, and this one had already gone stale once,
// at the rung that gave the diagnostics a shared speller):
//
//   • A name ROSTER (`isByteElementName`) asks about `Byte`, and must go on getting a yes after the
//     element it names has been qualified — without the roster learning anything about ranges.
//   • A DIAGNOSTIC quotes a typealias back at the author, and a mint is a name NO SOURCE LINE HOLDS.
//     ⛔ MEASURED (N2 review) before this door existed, on a program declaring `typealias Byte =
//     int(0 to 1000)`: the compile-time narrowing said `Value 2000 is outside the range of
//     'Byte$0_1000' (int(0 to 1000))` and the runtime guard panicked `value outside typealias
//     'Byte$0_1000'` — quoting a declaration the program does not contain, with the real bounds printed
//     in the very same sentence, so the suffix carried nothing but the compiler's bookkeeping. Worse, it
//     was INCONSISTENT with itself: the identical alias guarding a plain PARAMETER printed the bare
//     `Byte`, because only a generic instance's element is ever qualified. One alias, two spellings,
//     decided by which slot the value happened to flow into.
export function sourceSpelledAliasName(name ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	let split = firstSeparatorIndex(name, separator: CompilerQualifiedAliasSeparatorByte)
	if split == name.count() 'notCompilerQualified'
		return name
	end 'notCompilerQualified'

	return try name.slice(0, endIndex: split) otherwise panic("sourceSpelledAliasName: slice [0, {split}) of a {name.count()}-byte name is in range by the index `firstSeparatorIndex` just found")
end 'sourceSpelledAliasName'

// A set of declared type NAMES, for a walk that has to recognize a name it has already reached
// (`ProgramSignatures.structIsConstructibleWithin`). The `Set with ByteArray` the compiler's other
// name-set walks are spelled with — `ClonerSet` (the deep-clone walk over this very field graph),
// `LiveGlobalLabelSet`, `InterfaceNameSet` — rather than a list plus a linear scan, which is what makes
// "linear in the graph" a fact about this walk rather than a hope.
typealias StructNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// Source-file path (stringified) ⇒ that file's MODULE DIRECTORY. See
// `ProgramSignatures.fileModuleDirectories`.
typealias FileModuleDirectoryMap = Map with (String, String)

// The module directories a CONTESTED alias name's claimants sit in, for the one question
// `ProgramSignatures.contestedAliasNamespacesAreDistinct` asks of them: does any two of them coincide?
typealias ModuleDirectorySet = Set with String

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH FILE DECLARED A FUNCTION WHOSE RETURN TYPE IS STILL A BARE `named`** — see
// `ProgramSignatures.funcReturnDeclFiles`. Only those: every other recorded return type is already
// concrete and there is no name left in it for a later reader to resolve as ITS file means.
typealias FuncReturnDeclFileMap = Map with (ByteArray, FilePath)

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT THE WHOLE PROGRAM KNOWS ABOUT ONE DECLARATION, BEYOND ITS TYPES.** Filed for EVERY
// declaration, so a name with no entry means no file declares it rather than "declared, details unknown" —
// which is the property every reader here rests on.
//
// `visibility` and `declFilePath` are one question's inputs and travel together for that reason: a tier
// alone cannot say who may name the declaration (`isVisibleFrom`), and a file alone cannot say whether
// anyone but itself may.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`takesReceiver` IS A THIRD FACT AND NOT A REFINEMENT OF THOSE TWO** — it joins them because the
// alternative is a second table keyed identically to this one, which two writers would then have to keep in
// lockstep. It answers the half of "may this bare call resolve to `<Type>.<name>`?" that existence cannot:
// a `static` handed the enclosing `self` fails an arity check on a parameter its author never wrote, and an
// instance method denied one is called on nothing. `Parser.ensureSiblingReceivers` reads the same fact off
// a TOKEN walk for a declaration whose body it can see; this is the answer for one it cannot.
export type FunctionDeclSite
	export let visibility as Visibility
	export let declFilePath as FilePath
	export let takesReceiver as bool

	export static function create(visibility Visibility, declFilePath FilePath, takesReceiver bool) returns FunctionDeclSite
		return Self{visibility: visibility, declFilePath: declFilePath, takesReceiver: takesReceiver}
	end 'create'
end 'FunctionDeclSite'

typealias FunctionDeclSiteMap = Map with (ByteArray, FunctionDeclSite)

// The verdict `ProgramSignatures.calleeVisibilityFrom` hands back. A union rather than a bool because the
// refusing arm owes the caller the TIER — it is what picks the diagnostic's code and sentence — and a bool
// would send every caller back for a second lookup to find out.
export union CalleeVisibility
	visible
	hidden(tier Visibility)
end 'CalleeVisibility'

// Does this recorded return type still carry a NAME a later reader will have to resolve? Exactly the bare
// `named` arm: `void` and every concrete tag are already decided, and `Parser.resolveNamedAlias` — the one
// reader that turns a swept name into a type — takes the same guard, so the column and its spender agree on
// which declarations matter by construction rather than by two lists staying in step.
function returnTypeNamesAType(rt MaxonReturnType) returns bool
	return match rt 'r'
		void gives false
		value(inner) gives maxonTypeTag(inner) == ValueTypeTag.named
	end 'r'
end 'returnTypeNamesAType'

// Every FREE function's namespace-qualified spelling (N1b). See
// `ProgramSignatures.namespaceQualifiedFunctions`.
typealias NamespaceQualifiedFunctionSet = Set with ByteArray

// Bare free-function name ⇒ the module directory of the file that FIRST declared it (N1c). See
// `ProgramSignatures.freeFunctionDirectories`.
typealias FreeFunctionDirectoryMap = Map with (ByteArray, String)

// Bare free-function name ⇒ every namespace-qualified spelling of it, for the names more than one
// DIRECTORY declares (N1c). See `ProgramSignatures.contestedFreeFunctions`.
typealias ContestedFreeFunctionMap = Map with (ByteArray, ByteArrayArray)

// Every REGISTRATION name a contested free function was filed under (N1c) — the same strings
// `ContestedFreeFunctionMap`'s values hold, indexed the way the CALL DOOR asks for them. See
// `ProgramSignatures.contestedRegistrationNames`.
typealias ContestedRegistrationNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// ⭐ **WHAT ONE FREE-FUNCTION DECLARATION DID TO ITS BARE NAME (N1c)** — the answer
// `ProgramSignatures.noteFreeFunctionDeclaration` gives the fold, and the reason the fold has anything to do
// at all. `newlyContested` carries the INCUMBENT's registration name because that declaration's sweep
// entries were written under the bare key back when it was the only one, and only this moment knows where
// they now belong.
union FreeFunctionContest
	uncontested
	newlyContested(incumbentRegistrationName ByteArray)
	alreadyContested
end 'FreeFunctionContest'

// ⭐ THE ONE EMPTY spelling list every UNCONTESTED free-function name answers with — so the E3095 probe
// costs one map miss and no allocation on every call the compiler validates. NEVER mutated.
let sharedEmptyContestedSpellings = ByteArrayArray.create()

// ⭐⭐ **THE EIGHT SWEEP FACTS TRAVEL TOGETHER ON A RE-KEY, AND THEY DID NOT UNTIL W75.** The two contests
// that move a declaration — a free function declared in more than one DIRECTORY, and a `static` member whose
// name an instance member of its type also wears — used to disagree about which facts a move may carry: the
// pair's copy took only the four `record` writes, because the four BY-NAME scans folded AHEAD of the
// declaration loop and both members' entries were already piled onto the one source name, UNATTRIBUTABLE.
//
// The by-name folds now run after that loop and key by the member each entry belongs to
// (`ParamConsumeEntry.takesReceiver`), so the source key holds one declaration's facts at every moment a
// re-key reads it — which is what retired the selector, the six booleans behind it, and the per-file walk of
// the scan arrays that derived them.

// The ordinal the SECOND file to spell one sweep tuple key takes — see
// `ProgramSignatures.sweepScopedTupleName`, where the FIRST minter deliberately keeps the UNSUFFIXED key.
// It starts at 1 rather than at `Parser.FirstOverloadNameOrdinal`'s 2 for exactly that reason: an overload
// name's own first candidate is already suffixed, so its counter starts one past the name it would repeat.
let FirstLaterFileTupleOrdinal = 1

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH RULE `ProgramSignatures.rewriteRecordedReturnTypes` IS WALKING `returnTypes` UNDER.** Three
// whole-program passes re-decide a recorded return type, and until BATCH28's review each carried its own
// copy of the collect-then-apply walk. The walk is one fact; these three are what actually differ.
//
// An `enum` and not three functions passed as values: it is `match`ed in one place
// (`rewrittenReturnTypeUnder`), so a fourth rule fails to compile there rather than silently taking a
// neighbour's line.
// ⭐ **WHICH POPULATION `ProgramSignatures.nameInstancesFromDeclarations` IS CLAIMING INSTANCES FOR (W49b)** —
// the two rounds of `deriveInstanceDisplayNames`, run in this declaration order because an unscoped spelling
// outranks a type-scoped one. See that walk for the ruling.
//
// An `enum` and not the `bool` it replaced, for a reason the LANGUAGE settles rather than taste: a lone
// leading argument cannot be labelled (E2052), so a `bool` here reads `nameInstancesFromDeclarations(false)`
// at the call site — a magic value whose meaning lives only in the callee's parameter list. It is the same
// shape `RecordedReturnTypeRule` below takes for the same walk-with-two-rules problem.
export enum DisplayNameRound
	// A file-scope `typealias ByteArray = Array with Byte`. Claimed FIRST, so it wins every contest with a
	// type-scoped spelling of the same instance whatever order the loader reached the files in.
	unscoped
	// A nested `typealias UArr = Array with U`, keyed `Inner.UArr` by `Parser.qualifiedInnerGenericAlias`.
	// Claimed SECOND, so it names an instance only when nothing unscoped does — "the only name".
	typeScoped
end 'DisplayNameRound'

export enum RecordedReturnTypeRule
	// ⭐ **N3 — the contested generic ALIAS a `returns` clause names, rescoped against the file that
	// DECLARED the function.** See `contestScopedReturnTypeOf`.
	contestScopedAlias
	// ⭐⭐ **S2j — SETTLE WHAT A RECORDED RETURN TYPE NAMING A STRUCT BOX IS SPELLED AS: the ONE repair
	// that makes a `returns Inner` and a `returns Self` be the same recorded declaration.**
	//
	// It claims TWO shapes, and the second was found by BATCH28's review: a bare `named` that denotes a
	// declared `type` OR a tuple typealias (re-tagged to the box's `structRef`), and an already-`structRef`
	// TUPLE whose name is not yet the canonical spelling. Both are the same defect — the sweep recorded a
	// type before it could say what everything downstream would call it — and neither is visible from the
	// tag alone, which is why `sweptStructTypeOf` asks "what should this name be?" rather than "is this tag
	// still bare?". See there for the two measurements.
	//
	// `Parser.parseTypeReference` resolves the enclosing type's own name — `Self`, and the type's own
	// spelling — to a `structRef` SYNTACTICALLY, because that is a fact about the tokens and needs no
	// registry, and the declaration sweep therefore answers it identically to the real parse. A FOREIGN
	// name it cannot: the sweep reads a `returns` clause while `structTypes` is still being filled, so the
	// answer would depend on the filesystem's walk order, and the arm deliberately records a bare `named`
	// instead. ⇒ **one struct, two recorded spellings**, and this rule is where the second becomes the
	// first.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE READ DOORS ALREADY REPAIR IT AND THAT IS NOT THE SAME THING.** `Parser.resolveNamedStruct`
	// re-tags a call RESULT, which is why `let p = Outer.build()` inside a function body has always worked.
	// A TOP-LEVEL initializer has no such door: `evaluateInitializers` copies the recorded return type
	// straight into the declaration's outcome, and the two readers of that copy then disagreed —
	// `Parser.requireFactoryReturnsARecord` asked `slotTypeIsManaged`, which is TRUE for a
	// struct-denoting `named` and ADMITTED the program, and `globalStdType` then met a tag its width table
	// has no case for and PANICKED, with a sentence written for its other caller (a scalar global's ranged
	// alias) claiming a `var` was *"tagged 'int'"* in a program containing no int.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS A NORMALIZATION, NOT A SECOND RESOLUTION RULE** — `declaredInterface`'s words below, and
	// the same discipline. The rule is the READ DOOR's, statement for statement: a `named` naming a
	// declared `type` becomes a `structRef`; a `named` naming a UNION or a ranged int ALIAS stays a
	// `named`, because that IS the tag those carry (`enumMatchFor` reads a union's, and a ranged alias is
	// the integer). It asks `containsStruct` — the same door that read door asks — so the two cannot come
	// to disagree about which names move.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT RUNS BEFORE `evaluateInitializers` AND `declaredInterface` RUNS AFTER, AND NEITHER POSITION
	// IS FREE.** Each normalization can only run once the registry it consults is whole: `structTypes` is
	// filled by the sweep itself, so this may run the moment every file is folded, while `interfaces` is
	// filled by `foldInterfaceDeclarations`, which runs later. The constant evaluator sits between them and
	// COPIES what it reads, so a re-tag after it cannot reach the copy — which is exactly why this one is
	// early. They do not race: a name is a struct or an interface and never both (`declaredFormOf` answers
	// one form, and E3006/E3061 refuse a name two registries hold), so this walk leaves every interface
	// name a `named` for the rule below to claim.
	declaredStruct
	// ⭐⭐ **P1.7a-existentials slice 1 — RE-TAG EVERY SWEPT `named` THAT DENOTES AN `interface` AS AN
	// `interfaceRef`.** The return-type half of `normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes`, whose header carries the
	// wrong SIZE this closes and why the field half cannot ride this walk (it writes through a layout, not
	// through this map).
	declaredInterface
end 'RecordedReturnTypeRule'

export type ProgramSignatures
	// Function name -> its declared return type. Last-wins on a duplicate name, exactly as the
	// project's own `funcReturnTypes` registry is; the duplicate itself is reported by
	// `commitFuncSignatures` (E3006), which fails the build, so the overwrite is never observed.
	var returnTypes as FuncReturnTypeMap
	// Typealias name -> its declaration, with the RANGE the shift rule needs. It carries no
	// type-name IDS — a ranged alias's underlying type is always the `int` KEYWORD, never a name —
	// so unlike `returnTypes` it needs no interner and no remap: it travels by name and arrives by
	// name.
	var typeAliases as RangedAliasRegistry
	// FUNCTION typealias name -> its declaration (P1.5-A1). Like `typeAliases` it carries NO type-name
	// ids — a function alias's return type is stored interner-free as a (tag, name-ByteArray) pair (see
	// FunctionTypeAlias) — so it travels by name and arrives by name, needing no interner and no remap.
	// THE door `Parser.parseTypeReference` asks "is this name a function alias?" through, and the one an
	// indirect call recovers the callee's return type from.
	var functionTypeAliases as FunctionTypeAliasMap
	// ⭐ Struct-type name -> its declaration, with the FIELD ORDER the parser needs. THE door `p.x`
	// asks through, and the reason it must be here rather than in `Project`: a field's offset is
	// needed at PARSE time, and `type Point` may be declared BELOW the function that reads a
	// `Point` or in another file entirely. The same argument the header makes for a callee's return
	// type, about a different declaration.
	//
	// ⚠ **UNLIKE `typeAliases`, IT CARRIES IDS** — a field's declared type is a `MaxonType`, and
	// `named(Integer)` is meaningless outside the interner that minted it. So it obeys the header's
	// ID PROBLEM: `foldFile` remaps each file's ids into `typeNames` on the way in, and
	// `fieldTypeOf` re-interns on the way out. It is the second table to need that treatment, and
	// it needs it for exactly `returnTypes`' reason.
	var structTypes as StructLayoutMap
	// ⭐ **EVERY DECLARED `type`'s NAME, IN SWEEP ORDER — the ORDERED key list `structTypes` cannot be.** One
	// reader: an INTERFACE EXTENSION expands onto every conformer of its interface, and the ORDER it finds
	// them in is the order the monomorphized methods are emitted in — a fact that reaches the object file.
	// Walking the map instead would make the emitted function order a property of a hash table's bucket
	// layout rather than of the program, which is exactly the shape `interfaceNamesInDeclOrder` exists to
	// avoid one registry over. Written only by `recordStruct`, in the same act as the map entry.
	var structNamesInDeclOrder as ByteArrayArray
	// ⭐ Enum/union name -> its declaration, with the CASE ORDER and TAGS the parser needs. THE door
	// an enum `match` asks through — is `c` an enum? what tag is `red`? which cases does `low to
	// high` cover? — and here for `structTypes`' reason: the answer is needed at PARSE time and the
	// `enum` may be declared below its use or in another file.
	//
	// ⚠ **LIKE `structTypes`, IT CARRIES IDS** — a case NAME and a tag INT carry none, but a
	// PAYLOAD field's declared type is a `MaxonType`, and `holds(inner BoxA)`'s `named(BoxA)` is
	// meaningless outside the interner that minted it. So a payload-bearing union obeys the header's
	// ID PROBLEM exactly as `structTypes` does: `foldFile` remaps each file's payload ids into
	// `typeNames` on the way in (`remapEnumLayoutPayloadTypes`, the enum twin of
	// `remapStructLayoutFieldTypes`), and the parser re-interns on the way out (`adoptType`, the
	// same door `fieldTypeOf` uses). A payload-FREE enum carries no ids and travels by name alone.
	//
	// ⭐ **SEEDED, NOT EMPTY** (P1.7a slice 2b-ii): `createEnumRegistry` builds it holding the
	// compiler-known `Ordering`, so the parser resolves `Ordering.lessThan`, `match <compare result>` and a
	// `o Ordering` parameter through the ordinary enum doors. The seed deliberately does NOT ride
	// `self.hash` (which `recordEnum` folds a DECLARED enum into): the hash exists to invalidate a parse
	// memo when a declaration a reader COPIED FROM changes, and this declaration is byte-identical in every
	// program — mixing a constant would move every program's hash and invalidate nothing.
	// ⚠ **EXPORTED FOR ONE READER (W3)**: `requireCorpusDeclaredEnum`, the single door to a compiler-owned
	// enum's corpus declaration, which serves the PARSER off this index and the Std tier off
	// `Project.enumTypes` — one function over one type, rather than a message written twice. Everything else
	// still goes through `enumOf`/`containsEnum` below.
	export var enumTypes as EnumLayoutMap
	// ⭐ The generic-alias DECLARATIONS, in record order (P1.7) — the deterministic list `firstCyclicAliasDecl`
	// walks. `genericAliases` is keyed by NAME and is a Map, so it can answer "which instance?" but never
	// "which alias was declared first?"; the E3091 blame must be a property of the program, not of a map's
	// iteration, so the order is kept explicitly here.
	var genericAliasDecls as GenericAliasEntryArray
	// ⭐ Generic-instance interner (P1.6-A) — `Box with Integer` ⇒ a `GenericInstanceId`, id ⇒
	// `(baseId, argTypes)`. LIKE `structTypes` IT CARRIES IDS (an arg is a `MaxonType`), so `foldFile`
	// remaps each entry's args into `typeNames` before interning. Read by the parser to resolve a
	// generic-alias call base and to check a generic instance's arity / managed args.
	var genericInstances as GenericInstanceRegistry
	// Each interned instance's COMPILED name, derived on first ask and kept — see `CompiledInstanceNameMap`
	// for the quadratic this removes. Empty for a program that instantiates no generic.
	var compiledInstanceNames as CompiledInstanceNameMap
	var canonicalInstanceNames as CanonicalInstanceNameMap
	// ⭐ Each interned instance's DISPLAY name — the `typealias` an author declared for it. See
	// `InstanceDisplayNameMap`. Filled once by `deriveInstanceDisplayNames`, which is the only writer, so the
	// parse (which fans out per file) only ever reads it.
	var instanceDisplayNames as InstanceDisplayNameMap
	// Which written instantiation each SUBSTITUTED instance came out of — see `SubstitutedInstanceOriginMap`.
	var substitutedInstanceOrigins as SubstitutedInstanceOriginMap
	// The reverse of `canonicalInstanceNames` — see `CanonicalInstanceNameIndex` for the quadratic it removes.
	// Filled by `indexCanonicalInstanceNames`, which is the ONLY writer, so the index and the watermark below
	// cannot disagree about which ids are in it.
	var canonicalInstanceNameIndex as CanonicalInstanceNameIndex
	// How far `canonicalInstanceNameIndex` has been filled: every id BELOW this is in the set. Instances are
	// append-only, so advancing it is monotone and the total work of keeping the set complete is O(instances)
	// across the whole compile however often it is asked.
	var canonicalNamesIndexedThrough as GenericInstanceId
	// ⭐ Every tuple name this index has seen ⇒ its CANONICAL one (P1.10). A name the DECLARATION SWEEP minted
	// spells its elements the way the source wrote them (`__Tuple2.Integer.Integer`), because the alias
	// registry that says what `Integer` means is still being built when the sweep runs; `adoptType` rewrites
	// it to the canonical form (`__Tuple2.int.int`) at every read, and this memo is what makes that rewrite a
	// map hit rather than a re-derivation. A canonical name maps to itself, so one probe answers both
	// directions. Empty for a program with no tuples.
	var tupleCanonicalNames as TupleCanonicalNameMap
	// ⭐ Every registered tuple layout ⇒ the bounded token another tuple's name cites it by (W9). A MEMO of
	// `tupleElementTokenFor` since W14b — see `TupleElementTokenMap` for the cost that buys. Empty for a
	// program with no tuples.
	var tupleElementTokens as TupleElementTokenMap
	// ⭐ The reverse of the above: every minted token ⇒ its one owning tuple name (W14b), the half that makes
	// a digest collision a DETECTED event. `recordTupleElementToken` writes both in one act, so a divergence
	// between them stops the compile rather than handing two layouts one citation.
	var tupleElementTokenOwners as TupleElementTokenOwnerMap
	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY TYPE-PARAMETER TOKEN ⇒ THE `(declaring type, parameter, position)` THAT MINTED IT (W14)** —
	// `tupleElementTokenOwners`' twin one digest over, and it earns its keep twice. It makes a 64-bit digest
	// collision a DETECTED event (`recordTypeParamToken` STOPS the compile on one rather than handing two
	// parameters one identity), and it is the ONLY route from a token back to its declaration for a reader
	// holding nothing else — which is what let the three `genericTypeFieldHas…Instantiation` gates stop
	// taking a `(baseName, position)` PAIR their callers had to pair up by hand.
	//
	// ⚠ **WRITTEN AT THE FOLD, READ DURING THE PARSE — never the other way round.** `recordStruct` and
	// `recordInterfaceDeclaration` are its only writers, and both run while the declaration sweep is folding,
	// single-threaded. That is the same discipline `deriveInstanceNames` states for the name memos: the
	// per-file parse fan-out may READ this index and may not write it. Empty for a program with no generics.
	var typeParamOwners as TypeParamOwnerMap
	// ⭐ A TUPLE typealias name ⇒ the tuple type it names (P1.10) — `Pair` ⇒ `__Tuple2.int.int`. The tuple
	// twin of `genericAliases`, and it exists for the same reason: a tuple type has no source-spellable name,
	// so `typealias Pair = (Num, Num)` is the only way to write one in a place that takes a bare identifier
	// (`Array with Pair`). Like a RANGED alias and unlike a function/generic one it mints NO nominal identity
	// — a `Pair` reference resolves straight through to the tuple's own `structRef` — so nothing downstream
	// ever sees the alias name. Read through `tupleAliasTarget`, which canonicalizes: the sweep writes the
	// spelling it saw and the real parse the canonical one, exactly as every other tuple name does.
	var tupleAliases as TupleCanonicalNameMap
	// ⭐ **DOES ANY FILE IN THE PROGRAM MATERIALIZE A `[<identifier>…]` ARRAY LITERAL?** (P1.7 slice 3.) Set
	// by the sweep, read ONCE by `queryProgramSignatures` after the last file folds, which is what makes
	// `internLiteralContainerInstances` a single whole-program walk instead of a per-file one. A bool
	// and not a count: the over-interning is over every declared type, so one such file asks for exactly
	// what a thousand do.
	var sawAggregateArrayLiteral as bool
	// ⭐ **DOES ANY FILE IN THE PROGRAM MATERIALIZE A `List from […]` CONSTRUCTION?** The `List` half of the
	// bit above, read at the same moment and for the same reason: the construction's element type is the
	// source array literal's, so the `List with <T>` instances it may need are the same whole-program set of
	// declared types — one walk after the last file folds, not one per file.
	var sawListFromConstruction as bool
	// ⭐ **DOES ANY FILE IN THE PROGRAM NAME `<Enum>.allCases`?** The third bit of the same kind, read at the
	// same moment: the member's `Array with <Enum>` instance is one per declared enum and the sweep cannot
	// narrow which — its base may be a dotted `<Union>.unionCases` companion, and the enum registry is still
	// growing while the sweep runs — so it is the whole-program set, walked once.
	var sawEnumAllCasesAccess as bool
	// ⭐⭐ **THE OTHER EIGHT BITS OF THE SAME KIND — AND THEY BECAME WHOLE-PROGRAM FOR A CORRECTNESS REASON,
	// NOT THE THREE ABOVE'S MEASURED ONE (ARRO).** Each names a construct whose instance the compiler MINTS,
	// because the source writes no type for it to be read from: a `[…]`, a `b"…"`, a `.bytes()`, a
	// `Set`/`List`/`Vector from […]`, or a producer of one of the three compiler-owned types. The mint's base
	// is the corpus's array record (`arrayRecordBaseName`), whose spelling the STDLIB/USER CONTEST may move —
	// and the contest is decided BETWEEN the two halves of the sweep, so a per-file mint in a USER file asked
	// before the answer existed and filed the program-wide memo under the pre-contest bare name. In a program
	// that declares its own `type Array` that name is the PROGRAM's container, so `b"Hi!".isEmpty()` was
	// refused as a member the program's own array does not have — a value whose type the reader never wrote.
	// ⇒ The file NOTES; `internWellKnownContainerInstances` interns, once, after the contest.
	var sawIntArrayLiteral as bool
	var sawByteStringLiteral as bool
	var sawStringArrayLiteral as bool
	var sawSetFromConstruction as bool
	var sawManagedFileProducer as bool
	var sawManagedDirectoryProducer as bool
	// The one need with a PAYLOAD rather than a bit: a vector's instance is keyed by its SIZE as well as its
	// element, so the set of sizes the program constructs is what has to be carried (see
	// `Parser.foldDeclaredSignaturesInto`'s `vectorFromSizes`). Accumulated across files with duplicates left
	// in — interning is idempotent, and a `Set` here would trade a handful of repeated interns for a hash of
	// every size in the program.
	var vectorFromSizes as FixedSizeArray
	// ⭐ Generic-instance TYPEALIAS name ⇒ its interned instance (P1.6-A) — `IntBox` ⇒ the giid for
	// `Box with Integer`. THE door `Parser.parseQualifiedCall` resolves `IntBox.create` to `Box.create`
	// through, and the one the instantiation checks read the base + args from. It carries no ids of its
	// own (the giid indexes `genericInstances`), so it travels by name.
	var genericAliases as GenericAliasMap
	// ⭐⭐ **`<Interface>.<member>` ⇒ THE INSTANCE ITS RIGHT-HAND SIDE NAMES (W61)** — `Holder.ElementArray`
	// ⇒ the giid for `Array with <Holder's Element>`, from `typealias ElementArray = Array with Element`
	// written inside `interface Holder uses Element`.
	//
	// ⚖ **IT IS A SEPARATE MAP FROM `genericAliases` ABOVE, AND THE SPLIT IS THE USER RULING.** That map is
	// the set of names a READER may write, and putting an interface's member in it would make
	// `parseTypeReference` resolve the requirement's `ElementArray` to a `genericInstance` — which would
	// render the requirement as a mangled `Array_T…` and leave a diagnostic quoting a digest at an author who
	// wrote a name. An interface's `typealias` is LOCAL SHORTHAND: it names no type outside its own body, and
	// its one job downstream is to say what a requirement that spells it actually requires. So the requirement
	// keeps rendering `Holder.ElementArray`, and exactly one consumer — `ConformanceCheck` — expands it.
	//
	// ⛔ **BOTH REFERENCE COMPILERS MATCH THE ALIAS'S SPELLING INSTEAD, AND shv2 DELIBERATELY DOES NOT.**
	// The bootstrap compares the two bodies' member NAMES (measured: a conformer spelling its own alias
	// `NumStore` over the identical right-hand side is refused, `expected absorb(value ElementArray)`); v1
	// discards the line outright (`Parser.maxon:6578-6585`). So `stdlib/Set.maxon` and `stdlib/List.maxon`
	// conform through `InitableFromArrayLiteral` only because two files independently wrote `ElementArray`.
	// A conformer CANNOT avoid inventing a name — `value Array with Element` is E2010 in a parameter list and
	// E3005 in a field — so under the references the language forces every conformer to guess the interface's
	// private spelling. ⚖ **USER RULING: conformance is decided on the UNDERLYING TYPE**, so shv2 accepts
	// programs both references refuse. `specs-shv2/associated-types.md`'s `w61.*` cases carry the measurement.
	var interfaceAliases as GenericAliasMap
	// Which interfaces declared one of the above — the per-interface half of the same fact, and the only
	// form of it the conformance check's fast path can use (see `interfaceDeclaresAlias`). Derived in the
	// same act as the map, so the two cannot disagree about whether an interface has an alias.
	var interfaceAliasOwners as InterfaceNameSet
	// ⭐⭐ **THE GENERIC-ALIAS NAMES TWO FILES DISAGREE ABOUT (N3)** — the map above is keyed by the bare NAME
	// alone, which is exactly what `RangedAliasRegistry` was before file scoping and had exactly its defect:
	// the last file folded won and every other file silently got its instance. A non-exported `typealias` is
	// FILE-LOCAL whatever it aliases (`specs/duplicate-typealias.md`), so a generic one is no more
	// whole-program than a ranged one is.
	//
	// A derived index over `genericAliasDecls`, settled in ONE pass once every file has folded
	// (`settleGenericAliasContest`) rather than maintained per declaration: whether a name is contested is a
	// fact about ALL of its declarations at once, exactly as `RangedAliasRegistry.rangeContested` is.
	//
	// ⭐ **A NAME WHOSE DECLARATIONS NAME ONE INSTANCE IS NOT IN HERE**, and that is the load-bearing half.
	// Two files declaring `typealias Counts = Array with Count` over one `Count` intern the SAME instance, so
	// there is nothing to choose between and nothing moves — which is the entire existing corpus, and why not
	// one golden changes.
	var contestedGenericAliases as ContestedGenericAliasMap
	// ⭐⭐ **THE GENERIC-ALIAS NAMES MORE THAN ONE DECLARATION MENTIONS (N3)** — the gate that makes the whole
	// contest cost NOTHING for a program that has none, which is every program in the corpus. A name only one
	// declaration mentions cannot disagree with anything, so an EMPTY set here is a complete answer and
	// `settleGenericAliasContest` returns on its first line.
	//
	// ⭐ **MAINTAINED BY `recordGenericAlias` AND DERIVED FROM A PROBE IT ALREADY MAKES** — the `upsert` into
	// `genericAliases` needs the name hashed either way, so "have I seen this name before?" is one extra probe
	// of a map already in hand rather than a second index of its own. It is `RangedAliasRegistry`'s
	// `multiplyDeclared` invariant exactly, narrowed to the half this side needs: WHICH names, not which
	// declarations, because the declarations are retained in `genericAliasDecls` already.
	var multiplyDeclaredGenericAliases as DisagreeingAliasNameSet
	// ⛔⛔ **THE DECLARATION THE CONTEST FIXPOINT GAVE UP ON, OR `none` (N3 review).** A round mints a contested
	// argument a spelling derived from that argument's instance, so each round adds one nesting level — which
	// converges on an acyclic declaration graph, whose nesting is bounded by its depth, and never converges on
	// a CYCLIC one. Recorded here rather than panicked, and answered by `firstCyclicAliasDecl`, because the
	// program that produces it is a program with a type cycle and **E3091 is the answer it is owed**.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT REDUNDANT WITH THAT WALK, AND THE CASE THAT PROVES IT WAS MEASURED.** `buildInstanceArgGraph`
	// resolves a `named` argument through `denotedInstance`, which reads the bare LAST-WINS map — so two files
	// declaring `P = Array with Q` / `Q = Array with P` between them, where one file's `P` is `Array with High`,
	// present an ACYCLIC last-wins view while the per-file view cycles. The graph walk answers `none` there and
	// the fixpoint is the only thing that can see it.
	var divergedGenericAliasDecl as AliasCycleResult
	// Tier 2 of the same lookup — see `NameableGenericAliasMap`. Written by the same single writer, over the
	// same declarations, so the two indexes cannot come to disagree about which declarations exist.
	var nameableGenericAliases as NameableGenericAliasMap
	// ⭐ Every `typealias X = Base with Args` INSTANTIATION SITE (P1.7a slice 2a), with its source anchor — the
	// list the where-constraint check (E3017) walks to validate each concrete argument conforms to the
	// interface its type parameter is constrained to. A LIST, not a map: an instance may be named by several
	// aliases, each a distinct site to check + anchor; a `Box with Foo` with no `where` constraint contributes
	// entries the check skips in O(1) (empty constraint list). Empty for a program with no generic aliases.
	var genericAliasSites as GenericInstantiationSiteArray
	// ⭐ INNER ranged typealias registry (P1.6-C) — a RANGED `typealias Idx` declared INSIDE a generic
	// type, keyed by its BASE-QUALIFIED name ("Wrapper.Idx"). It is what makes a per-instance alias
	// (`IntWrapper.Idx`) nominal: `isPerInstanceAlias` composes an instance alias's base with this
	// registry to decide whether `Instance.Member` names one. Like `typeAliases` it carries no ids and
	// travels by name. The stored `RangedTypeAlias.name` is the BARE member ("Idx") — the piece the
	// method-return retype re-qualifies with a concrete receiver's alias.
	var innerAliases as RangedTypeAliasMap
	// ⭐⭐ **THE `<Conformer>.<member>` NAME OF EVERY PER-INSTANCE NESTED `typealias` AN `extension` BODY
	// DECLARES — THE SCOPE, RECORDED AHEAD OF THE DECLARATIONS (F10).** A nested `typealias` is a MEMBER of
	// the type it is written in, and `stdlib/helpers/sort/` is where that stops being a same-body question:
	// six files each write `export extension Array`, `insertionSort.maxon:14` declares `SortIndex` and the
	// other five spell it BARE from their own bodies. The three registries above answer *what* `Array.SortIndex`
	// resolves to; this set answers the question that comes first — *is `SortIndex` a member of `Array` here?* —
	// and it has to be answerable before any of them is filled.
	//
	// ⚠ **A SEPARATE SET BECAUSE OF WHEN IT IS WRITTEN, NOT BECAUSE OF WHAT IT HOLDS.** A `type` body's nested
	// aliases are folded with their file (`recordScannedType` ⇒ `foldFile`), so `declaresInnerAlias` already
	// answers for them whole-program. An `extension` body's are folded one site at a time by
	// `Queries.foldExtensionDeclarations`, which READS each body's signatures as it goes — so a body folded
	// before `insertionSort.maxon`'s would resolve `SortIndex` differently from one folded after it, and the
	// answer would be the directory walk's order (`StdlibLoader`'s header rules that out). This set is filled by
	// a NAME-ONLY pre-pass over every extension site before the first body is read, which is what makes the
	// answer a property of the program.
	//
	// ⚠ **PER-INSTANCE FORMS ONLY — a nested FUNCTION alias is deliberately absent**, for the reason
	// `readNestedTypeAlias` keeps one out of `Parser.enclosingInnerAliases`: it is a file-scope name written
	// inside a body (`publishNestedFunctionAlias` files it BARE as well as qualified, which is already why a
	// cross-file `SortComparator` resolves today), and `parseTypeReference`'s inner-alias arm would stamp it
	// `named(...)` where it must reach the `function` arm and carry a `function` tag.
	var extensionInnerAliasMembers as ExtensionInnerAliasNameSet
	// ⭐ Per-method INNER-ALIAS PARAMETER info (P1.6-C) — for a method/factory that declares a parameter
	// whose type is a bare inner alias of the enclosing generic type (`setTag(t Idx)`), the source
	// parameter NAMES and, per source position, the inner-alias MEMBER (`Idx`) or empty. Keyed by the
	// method's qualified name (`Wrapper.setTag`). THE door the parser's per-instance argument check asks
	// through at a concrete call site: the shared method signature is opaque to the receiver's instance,
	// so the check needs the parameter's inner-alias member here (params are SKIPPED by the sweep's
	// return-type read, so nothing else records them at this phase). Only methods that HAVE such a
	// parameter get an entry — absence is "no inner-alias parameter", the common case.
	var methodInnerAliasParams as InnerAliasParamInfoMap
	// ⭐⭐ **INTERFACE NAME -> ITS WHOLE DECLARATION (R8)** — the one whole-program answer to *"which interface
	// does this name denote?"* at PARSE time, and here for `structTypes`' reason exactly: the answer is needed
	// while a file is being parsed and the `interface` may be declared BELOW its use or in another file.
	// Two parse-time doors read it, and both used to answer at their own scope:
	//   • `Parser.interfaceUsesArity` — `associatedTypeNames.count()`, how many arguments an unparenthesized
	//     `implements Duo with A, B` binds (R7 moved this one and stopped there);
	//   • `Parser.requireInterfaceForParse` — the METHOD SIGNATURES a witness dispatch resolves against
	//     (`findWitnessDispatchCandidates` reads a method's name, return type, formal types, formal names and `throws`).
	//     It scanned the PARSING FILE's `artifact.interfaces`, which fills as the linear parse reaches each
	//     declaration — so `type Box uses T where T is Digest` with `interface Digest` written below it was
	//     `E2015 … not declared before its constrained use` on a program that compiles with the two lines
	//     swapped, and every cross-file spelling was the same reject.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE WHOLE `IrInterface`, NOT THE FIVE FIELDS THE DISPATCH READS, AND THAT IS THE LESS DUPLICATIVE
	// CHOICE.** A bespoke record holding exactly what `findWitnessDispatchCandidates` consumes would need its own
	// builder, and a SECOND builder of an interface signature is precisely what drifts from the real parse's —
	// the standing hazard of having a pre-scan at all. `foldInterfaceDeclarations` runs the parser's OWN
	// `readInterfaceDeclaration`, the same function `parseInterfaceDeclaration` calls, so what lands here is
	// the declaration the real parse would build, field for field. What is deliberately narrow is the HASH:
	// only the fields a PARSE copies ride it (see `recordInterfaceDeclaration`).
	var interfaces as IrInterfaceMap
	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY INTERFACE NAME THE PROGRAM CAN DISPATCH THROUGH, IN ONE FIXED ORDER — the interner-FREE
	// scalar handle a witness dispatch names its requirement's interface by.** A `witnessDispatch` op has to
	// say WHICH interface's slot list its `methodIndex` indexes, so that `LowerMaxonToStd` can recover the
	// requirement's declared FORMALS and collapse them to ABI widths where that collapse belongs. It may not
	// say so with a NAME (a `ByteArray` would be a second MANAGED field on the variant, which the bootstrap's
	// synthesized destructor mishandles) and it may not say so with a `TypeNameId`: no `MaxonOp` carries an
	// interner id today, because `ParseStaging.remapArtifact` does not walk the op stream — every id in the
	// module rides the per-value NAME COLUMN precisely so the merge can move it. An INDEX into this list needs
	// no remap: it is whole-program by construction, fixed before the first body is parsed, and identical for
	// every file.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT STORES NAMES, NOT DECLARATIONS, SO THAT AN INDEX RESOLVES BY THE ONE RULE.** `lookupInterface`
	// answers a SYNTHESIZED protocol before it consults any registry, and a user may declare a type of the same
	// name; storing the declaration here would let an index resolve to the user's while every other reader saw
	// the builtin's. A name goes through `lookupInterface` at the asking end instead — with whichever map that
	// caller's PHASE holds, which is exactly the split `IrInterface.lookupInterface`'s header defends.
	//
	// The SYNTHESIZED protocols occupy the fixed prefix (`builtinInterfaceNames`) because they are in no
	// registry and would otherwise have no position; `recordInterfaceDeclaration` — the SINGLE writer of
	// `interfaces` above — appends every declared name after them, in the same act, so the two cannot come to
	// hold different sets. A name already present keeps its index, so an index always names one interface.
	//
	// ⚠ **A PARSE MEMO CANNOT SERVE A STALE INDEX.** `ParseMemo.keyHash` mixes `ProgramSignatures.hash`, and
	// `recordInterfaceDeclaration` folds every interface into that hash — so an added, removed or reordered
	// interface re-parses every file, and no cached op can survive holding an index into a list it no longer
	// describes.
	var interfaceNamesInDeclOrder as ByteArrayArray
	// Name → its position in `interfaceNamesInDeclOrder`. The reverse direction of the one relation above,
	// filled in the same single act; the parser asks it once per resolved dispatch target, so the answer is
	// O(1) rather than a scan of every declared interface per dispatch.
	var interfaceDeclIndexByName as InterfaceDeclIndexMap
	// ⭐ Where each `interface` declaration sits, as the tolerant sweep passed it (R8) — see
	// `InterfaceDeclSite` for why a POSITION and not the declaration. Read once, by
	// `foldInterfaceDeclarations`, after the last file folds.
	export var interfaceDeclSites as InterfaceDeclSiteArray
	// ⭐ Where each `extension <Interface>` declaration sits, as the tolerant sweep passed it — see
	// `ExtensionDeclSite`. Read once, by `foldExtensionDeclarations`, after the last file folds, because an
	// extension's methods belong to conformers the per-file sweep has not necessarily met yet.
	export var extensionDeclSites as ExtensionDeclSiteArray
	// ⭐ Where each `type`/`enum`/`union` that writes an `implements` clause sits (W95) — see
	// `ConformanceClauseDeclSite`. Read once, by `Queries.foldConformanceClauses`, after the INTERFACE fold,
	// because the clause's own extent depends on an interface arity only that pass knows.
	export var conformanceClauseSites as ConformanceClauseDeclSiteArray
	// ⭐ Where each GENERIC type's method list begins — its own body, and every extension body over it. See
	// `DescriptorScanSite`. Read once, by `Queries.foldDescriptorNeeds`, after the extension fold has put
	// every extension method into the program.
	export var descriptorScanSites as DescriptorScanSiteArray
	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH METHODS CARRY THE HIDDEN LAYOUT-DESCRIPTOR PARAMETER (P1.6-B1) — the ONE answer, keyed by
	// the qualified `Type.method` name a call resolves to.** Solved whole-program by
	// `Queries.foldDescriptorNeeds` after `allFilesFolded`; read by `Parser.parseFunction` to reserve the slot
	// before it numbers a method's body values, and by `Parser.requireSelfForDescriptorForwarding` to refuse a
	// forward from a same-type value that is not `self`. (This said "and by nothing else"; the same commit that
	// wrote it added the second reader — corrected at review.) TWO READERS ARE NOT TWO ANSWERS: both ask this
	// one set through `functionNeedsLayoutDescriptor`, under the same qualified name, so the reservation and
	// the refusal cannot disagree about which methods carry the slot.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT WAS A PER-TYPE-BODY MAP ON THE PARSER, AND THAT COST A COMPILER ABORT ON A LEGAL PROGRAM
	// (W23c).** The fixpoint ran from `parseTypeDeclaration` over the tokens of the one body it had just
	// opened, so a method an `extension` contributed was in no fixpoint at all and could never reserve the
	// slot — while `LowerMaxonToStd` still asked it to FORWARD one to a callee that had. **MEASURED on the
	// merge base: `panic at LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1587: forwardCallerLayout: caller 'Bag.absorbTwice' has no
	// layout descriptor to forward to 'Bag.absorb'`**, no position and no `error E….`, on a program the oracle
	// runs. The self-call graph is NOT local to a type declaration — an extension body is outside it and may
	// be in another file — so the fixpoint cannot be either.
	//
	// It points at the SHARED EMPTY until a program actually declares a generic type, so a program with none
	// allocates nothing for it; `recordDescriptorNeeds` replaces it wholesale, copy-on-write, exactly as
	// `whereConstraints` does on a `StructLayout`.
	var descriptorNeeds as DescriptorNeedNameSet
	// ⭐ Every (conformer, method) a CONDITIONAL extension was withheld from, under the qualified name a call
	// to it resolves to — see `ConditionalExtensionSkip`. Written by `foldExtensionDeclarations`, read at the
	// one door a call to a missing method goes through.
	var conditionalExtensionSkips as ConditionalExtensionSkipMap
	// ⭐ Which interface each of an interface's associated positions is HELD AT, keyed by the interface NAME
	// — see `settleAssociatedTypeHoldings`. An interface holding NOTHING has no entry at all, so the whole
	// corpus but the held-associated-type specs leaves this map empty and every probe is one miss.
	var associatedTypeHoldings as AssociatedTypeHoldingMap
	// ⭐ The type -> declared-interface index as THIS phase can see it, memoized — see `sweptConformanceIndex`.
	var sweptConformances as DeclaredConformanceMap
	// Whether the field above has been built. A FLAG rather than an emptiness test, because a program in which
	// no type declares a conformance builds a genuinely empty index and must not rebuild it per question.
	var sweptConformancesBuilt as bool
	// ⭐⭐ **THE CONFORMANCES AN `extension <T> implements <I>` DECLARES — the half `sweptConformances` above
	// STRUCTURALLY CANNOT HOLD, and without which a SUPPRESSION reads a weaker index than the report it defers
	// to (review of W90).** `StructLayout.conformsTo` is the `type`/`enum` declaration's own clause; an
	// extension's conformance is recorded per CONFORMER by the REAL PARSE (`Parser.recordExtensionConformance`
	// into `project.conformances`), so the swept index misses every one of them —
	// `extension Array implements Hashable, Equatable where Element is Hashable and Equatable` most of all.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS DELIBERATELY UNCONDITIONAL — the `where` clause is NOT applied.** The one reader
	// (`instantiationViolatesItsOwnWhereClause`) needs a SUPERSET of what E3017's merged index holds, because
	// its suppression is only sound when `unmet here` implies `unmet there`. Granting a conditional
	// conformance the conformer may not have earned can therefore only WITHHOLD a suppression, which restores
	// the pre-W90 over-refusal — loud, and never a silent accept. See `instantiationMeetsConstraint`.
	var extensionDeclaredConformances as DeclaredConformanceMap
	// ⭐ The witness constraints of each TYPE-extension method — see `ExtensionMethodConstraintMap`. Written by
	// the same fold, in the same act that publishes the method's signature, so a method cannot be published
	// with one witness count and called with another.
	var extensionMethodConstraints as ExtensionMethodConstraintMap
	// ⭐ **THE `<Conformer>.<method>` NAMES AN UNCONDITIONAL TYPE EXTENSION ALSO DECLARES** — the fact the
	// call-site constraint gate needs to know it is NOT the only decider. See
	// `hasUnconstrainedExtensionDeclaration`.
	var unconstrainedExtensionMethods as ExtensionMethodNameSet
	// The interner every `named` id in `returnTypes` belongs to. PRIVATE, and it has to be: an id
	// may not leave this type. `returnTypeOf` re-interns it into the caller's own table.
	var typeNames as TypeNameInterner
	// THE TOP-LEVEL CONSTANT ARENA — every `let` declared at module scope in any file, in
	// DECLARATION order (source-path order, then in-file order).
	//
	// An ARRAY is the driver and a map is only the index into it, which is the opposite of the two
	// tables above and is deliberate: the E2012 cycle message names its members in the order the DFS
	// met them, so the evaluation order is part of what the compiler PRINTS. Driving from a map would
	// make it depend on hash order — v1 drives from a map and has to sort the names back into
	// declaration order to undo exactly that (`sortTopLevelNamesByPosition`,
	// TypeResolution.maxon:8268-8304). Here there is nothing to sort: the sweep builds the array in
	// the order the message needs.
	var decls as TopLevelDeclArray
	// Storage key -> its index in `decls`. FIRST-WINS on a duplicate key (see `recordDecl`).
	var declIndex as TopLevelDeclIndexMap
	// ⭐ Function name -> its per-parameter CONSUME bits (P1.4a wave 2). `paramIsConsumed(callee, i)` is
	// the door a call site asks "does the callee take ownership of my argument at position i?". Filled by
	// `recordParamConsume` from each file's `scanParamConsumes`; last-wins on a duplicate name (a duplicate
	// function is E3006, reported elsewhere, so the overwrite is never observed), exactly as `returnTypes` is.
	var funcParamConsumes as ParamConsumeMap
	// ⭐⭐ Function name -> its per-parameter BY-REFERENCE columns (PBR-2). `paramIsByRef(callee, i)` is the
	// door BOTH ends of the ABI ask — the callee's own `bindParameters`, deciding whether a parameter arrives
	// as a cell address, and the call site, deciding whether to hand one over. Filled by `recordParamReassign`
	// from each file's `scanReassignedParams`; last-wins on a duplicate name exactly as `funcParamConsumes` is.
	// Only a function with something to state is recorded — the "absence is the answer" default
	// `funcParamConsumes` rests on.
	//
	// ⚠ **"SOMETHING TO STATE" INCLUDES MERELY FORWARDING, SO THIS MAP IS NOT EMPTY ON A PROGRAM THAT
	// REASSIGNS NOTHING** — and this comment said it was. `Array.sort` handing `cmp` to `driftsortRange`
	// earns an entry, so every program that links the stdlib carries a few hundred of them (219 at the
	// scale-test ladder's rung 0). Its emptiness is therefore not a usable test for "does this program use
	// pass-by-reference at all"; that is `sawParamReassignment` below, and `programTakesAParamByRef`'s own
	// header records the red suite that reading this map's count instead produced.
	var funcParamReassigns as ParamReassignMap
	// ⚡ **HAS ANY RECORDED BODY REASSIGNED A PARAMETER OF ITS OWN?** — the forwarding closure's precondition,
	// raised by `recordParamReassign` at the instant the fact arrives rather than re-derived by a pass over the
	// map. Reachability from an empty seed set marks nothing, so a false here means `closeByRefForwarding` has
	// nothing to close and returns without building its graph — which is every program written before PBR-2.
	//
	// ⚠ A stale TRUE would be harmless (the graph would be built and mark nothing) and a stale FALSE would be a
	// wrong ABI, so note which way the one non-`record` writer runs: `copyDeclarationSweepEntries` only ever
	// copies an info that `recordParamReassign` already saw, so it cannot introduce a reassignment this has not
	// witnessed.
	var sawParamReassignment as bool
	// ⭐⭐ **PER FUNCTION: DOES ANY NAME IT FORWARDS A PARAMETER TO TAKE SOMETHING BY REFERENCE?** — the
	// settled transitive half of the by-reference question, filled ONCE by `closeByRefForwarding` and empty
	// until then. See `ParamReassignInfo` for why the sweep cannot decide this at fold time.
	var byRefForwardingLive as ByRefForwardingMap
	// Has `closeByRefForwarding` run? The closure needs every file's edges, so it is deferred to the first
	// READ rather than done per fold — and it must run exactly once, because it is a fixpoint over the whole
	// recorded set rather than an accumulation.
	var byRefForwardingClosed as bool

	// ⭐⭐ **W117's THREE FIELDS, THE EXACT TRIPLE PBR-2 KEEPS ONE BLOCK UP AND FOR THE SAME REASONS** — see
	// `GlobalAliasReturnInfo` for what is recorded and `closeGlobalAliasReturns` for what closes it.
	var funcGlobalAliasReturns as GlobalAliasReturnMap
	// ⚡ **HAS ANY RECORDED BODY RETURNED A BARE NAME AT ALL?** — the closure's precondition, raised at the
	// instant the fact arrives rather than re-derived by a pass over the map. Only a returned NAME can be a
	// seed (a `return f(…)` merely forwards), and reachability from an empty seed set marks nothing — so a
	// false here means the graph, its edge columns and the BFS are all skipped, which is every program whose
	// functions all return computed values. It is `sawParamReassignment`'s twin down to the direction a stale
	// answer errs in: a stale TRUE builds a graph that marks nothing, a stale FALSE would lose a refusal.
	var sawGlobalNameReturn as bool
	// The settled answer, by registration key. Empty until the closure runs.
	var globalAliasReturnKeys as GlobalAliasReturnKeySet
	// Has `closeGlobalAliasReturns` run? `byRefForwardingClosed`'s twin, for its reason.
	var globalAliasReturnsClosed as bool
	// ⭐ **THE BARE NAMES A *BARE* CALL CAN REACH SOMETHING BY-REFERENCE THROUGH** — the closure's working set,
	// kept because the PARSER's local-cell prescan needs exactly it. That prescan reads a callee out of TOKENS,
	// so all it has is what the source wrote before the `(` (`reassign`), never the registration key a method is
	// filed under (`Forwarder.reassign`); a lookup by the qualified key would answer false for every method call
	// and the caller's `var` would never get a cell.
	//
	// It is the WIDER of the two published sets (a bare call reaches both node families — see `ForwardCall`),
	// which is what makes it the right one for the prescan and for `programTakesAParamByRef`: over-approximating
	// a LOCAL's cell costs an allocation and can produce no wrong answer, so the prescan takes the widest
	// honest answer rather than a shape-sensitive one.
	var byRefBareNames as ByRefBareNameSet
	// ⭐ Function name -> its `throws E` clause (P1.4b). `throwsOf(callee)` is the door a call site asks
	// "does this callee throw, and what error type?". Filled by `recordThrows` from each file's scanned
	// signatures; last-wins on a duplicate name (E3006 reported elsewhere), exactly as `returnTypes` is.
	// Only THROWING functions are recorded — a callee with no entry is non-throwing, the safe default that
	// makes recording only the throwing ones correct (the same "absence is the answer" `funcParamConsumes`
	// rests on).
	var funcThrows as FuncThrowsMap
	// The names whose throwing declarations do not all name ONE error type — see `DisagreeingThrowsNameSet`.
	// Empty for every program that declares each function once, and for every overload set whose members agree.
	var disagreeingThrows as DisagreeingThrowsNameSet
	// How many declarations of a name THREW, against `funcDeclarationCounts`' how many there were — the pair
	// `throwsClauseCollide` compares, and the only way the sweep can see a member that publishes no clause
	// at all. `funcDefaultingDeclarationCounts`' twin, one registry over and for exactly its reason.
	var funcThrowingDeclarationCounts as FunctionDeclarationCountMap
	// ⭐ Function name -> its per-parameter DEFAULTS (BATCH2 slice 4). `defaultsOf(callee)` is the door
	// `Parser.emitCall` asks before deciding an under-supplied call is an arity error. Filled by
	// `recordParamDefaults` from each file's scanned signatures; last-wins on a duplicate name (E3006
	// reported elsewhere), exactly as `funcParamConsumes` is. Only functions that declare AT LEAST ONE
	// default are recorded — a callee with no entry has no defaults, the "absence is the answer" default
	// `funcParamConsumes` rests on — so the overwhelming majority of programs carry an empty map.
	var funcParamDefaults as ParamDefaultMap
	// ⭐ Method name -> which of its parameters are declared as a bare TYPE PARAMETER (ARRR). See
	// `OpaqueParamInfo`. `opaqueParamsOf(callee)` is the door `Parser.recordOpaqueArgRangeChecks` asks before
	// building a range guard out of the receiver instance's own type argument. Filled by `record` from each
	// file's scanned signatures, beside the return type it accompanies and with the identical last-wins
	// semantics. Only a declaration that HAS such a parameter is recorded — the "absence is the answer"
	// default `funcParamConsumes` rests on — so every program that declares no generic type carries an
	// empty map.
	var funcOpaqueParams as OpaqueParamMap
	// The names two declarations described DIFFERENTLY — an overload set, whose members the sweep cannot tell
	// apart. See `OpaqueParamInfo.matches` for the wrong answer this exists to refuse.
	var contestedOpaqueParams as ContestedOpaqueParamNameSet
	// The names whose declarations do not all describe ONE default shape — see
	// `DisagreeingParamDefaultNameSet` and `ParamDefaultInfo.matches`. Empty for every program that declares
	// each function once, and for every overload set whose members agree.
	var disagreeingParamDefaults as DisagreeingParamDefaultNameSet
	// How many declarations of a name the sweep folded, and how many of them published parameter defaults —
	// two counts, compared by `paramDefaultsCollide`. See `FunctionDeclarationCountMap` for why the comparison
	// is the point and why both maps are empty for a program that declares each function once.
	var funcDeclarationCounts as FunctionDeclarationCountMap
	var funcDefaultingDeclarationCounts as FunctionDeclarationCountMap
	// ⭐ **EVERY FUNCTION WHOSE DECLARED RETURN TYPE NAMED `__ManagedMemory` ANYWHERE, AND WHERE (A2j / A2m).**
	// `declaredReturnSurface(callee)` is the door `Parser.emitCall` asks "does this call's result denote the
	// BUFFER, or a tuple with a buffer in it, or the `Array` around it?" — a question no `MaxonReturnType` can
	// answer, because the alias resolves to `Array with Byte` and the spelling is gone (see
	// `registerManagedFileType`).
	//
	// ONLY A NON-PLAIN SURFACE GETS AN ENTRY, for `managedTopLevelNames`'s measured reason: the reader is on the
	// hot path (every direct, method and static call in the program), and a program that never writes the alias
	// must not pay per call for an answer that is always no. Written by `record`, in BOTH directions, so it can
	// never outlive the return type it describes.
	var declaredReturnSurfaces as DeclaredReturnSurfaceMap
	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH FILE DECLARED EACH FUNCTION WHOSE RECORDED RETURN TYPE IS STILL A BARE `named` — the
	// column that makes a return type answer to ITS OWN file rather than to its READER'S.**
	//
	// ⛔ **WITHOUT IT A CROSS-FILE RETURN OF A CONTESTED GENERIC ALIAS WAS A SILENT WRONG ANSWER, MEASURED.**
	// Two files each declaring `typealias Bytes = Array with Byte` over a DIFFERENT `Byte` range: one file's
	// `returns Bytes` function feeding the other's `Bytes` parameter COMPILED, and the callee read a
	// two-byte-stride record at one-byte stride — `300` came back as **44** (exit 44 on a program the type
	// system exists to refuse). The sweep records `returns Bytes` before any generic alias is interned, so it
	// stores a bare `named("Bytes")`; `Parser.resolveNamedAlias` then resolves that name in the CALLING file
	// (`genericAliasInstanceFrom(name, readerFilePath: self.filePath)`), so the callee's array silently
	// became the caller's homonym and the two instances compared equal.
	//
	// `resolveRecordedGenericAliasTypes` is what spends it, rewriting such a return type to the minted
	// spelling its declaring file means — the same repair that pass already makes for a struct FIELD and a
	// union PAYLOAD, whose header states the rule this column supplies the missing input for: **the scope
	// file is the SLOT's declaring file and never a reader's.** A return type is a slot of the declaring
	// FUNCTION, and it was the one recorded declared type that pass could not reach.
	//
	// ⚠ **ONLY BARE-`named` RETURNS GET AN ENTRY**, `declaredReturnSurfaces`' discipline and its reason: a
	// `returns int` / `returns bool` / already-concrete return carries no name for a reader to re-resolve, so
	// there is nothing a rescope could do with its file. Written and MOVED by `record` and by the three sites
	// that refile a `returnTypes` entry under a new key, so it can never outlive or lag the return type it
	// describes.
	var funcReturnDeclFiles as FuncReturnDeclFileMap
	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY DECLARED FUNCTION'S TIER AND DECLARING FILE (the top-level factory's visibility half).**
	// `SemanticCheck.calleeVisibleFrom` answers the same question for a call in a BODY, off `FunctionNameIndex`
	// — but that index is built long after the constant evaluator has run, and a top-level initializer's call
	// is decided during `queryProgramSignatures`, before any file is parsed. So the sweep publishes the two
	// facts here and `Parser.evalConstFactoryCallInto` asks them through the very same `isVisibleFrom`: two
	// SOURCES for one declaration, never two RULES.
	//
	// ⚠ Written UNCONDITIONALLY by `record` — `returnTypes`' discipline rather than
	// `funcReturnDeclFiles`', because "no entry" has to mean "no file declares this name" for the answer to
	// be exact. It is moved by the same three refiles that move a `returnTypes` entry, so it can never lag
	// the declaration it describes.
	var funcDeclSites as FunctionDeclSiteMap
	// ⭐ **EVERY GENERIC-INSTANCE ALIAS WHOSE ELEMENT WAS SPELLED `__ManagedMemory` (A2m)** — written by
	// `recordGenericAlias`, read by `aliasElementIsBufferSurface`. See `BufferElementAliasNameSet` for why the
	// key is the alias NAME and can never be the instance.
	var bufferElementAliases as BufferElementAliasNameSet
	// ⭐ The NAMES of every generic-instance typealias whose BASE was SPELLED `__ManagedMemory` (W43) —
	// `typealias KeyMemory = __ManagedMemory with Key`. Written by `recordGenericAlias`, read by
	// `aliasDenotesBufferSurface`. The twin of the set above and keyed the same way and for the same reason:
	// `__ManagedMemory with T` and `Array with T` intern to ONE instance, so the id cannot carry the spelling.
	var bufferSurfaceAliases as BufferElementAliasNameSet
	// ⭐⭐ **THE `<Conformer>.<method>` NAMES AN `extension` PUBLISHED, AND THE ONES IT WITHHELD BECAUSE THE
	// CONFORMER DECLARES ITS OWN (W43).** Two sets rather than one because they answer two different halves of
	// one question, and both halves are needed:
	//
	//   • `extensionPublishedMethods` is what tells the CONFORMER'S OWN declaration from an extension's. The
	//     extension fold runs strictly after every type is swept, so "a signature already exists under this
	//     name" is ambiguous without it — it could be the type's, or an earlier extension's. Two extensions
	//     publishing one name stay a genuine E3006, in EITHER fold order, because neither is shadowed.
	//   • `extensionShadowedMethods` is the DECISION, recorded rather than re-derived. `parseExtensionDeclaration`
	//     must emit exactly the bodies `foldExtensionDeclarationInto` published signatures for, and by the time
	//     it runs the first set can no longer answer the question (the fold's own entries are in the index).
	//     One recorded fact, read by both, is the only shape in which they cannot come apart.
	var extensionPublishedMethods as ExtensionMethodNameSet
	var extensionShadowedMethods as ExtensionMethodNameSet
	// ⭐⭐ **AND THE `<Conformer>.<method>` NAMES PUBLISHED BY EXTENSIONS IN TWO DIFFERENT FILES (X-contains)**
	// — the third column, and the one that makes those declarations an OVERLOAD SET instead of a duplicate.
	//
	// A registration name is minted where a declaration is PARSED, and a parser is a pure function of its own
	// file: a later overload of a name the same file already claimed becomes `pick#bool`, while a later
	// overload of a name ANOTHER file claimed had no way to know it was later at all. Both took the bare name
	// and collided at the merge. This fold is the only walk that sees every `extension` in the program, so it
	// is the only place that can say so — the W43 arrangement one fact over: the DECISION is taken here and
	// READ BACK by the parse, never re-derived.
	//
	// ⚠ **A CONTEST NEEDS TWO FILES, NOT TWO DECLARATIONS.** `stdlib/Array.maxon` declares `sort()` and
	// `sort(cmp)` in two extensions of its own, and that pair already works — the file's own registry sees
	// both. Marking it contested would re-mint two symbols to fix nothing.
	var extensionMethodDeclFiles as ExtensionMethodDeclFileMap
	var contestedExtensionMethods as ExtensionMethodNameSet
	// ⭐⭐ **THE `<Type>.<method>` NAMES A `static` MEMBER AND AN INSTANCE MEMBER BOTH WEAR** — the fact that
	// makes `Type.m()` and `receiver.m()` two functions instead of a duplicate definition. `record` fills it
	// the instant the second of the two folds, whichever order they arrive in, and moves the STATIC's own
	// declaration facts onto `memberRegistrationKey`'s answer at the same moment: the sweep files a
	// declaration's return type by NAME, and a pair sharing one key answers the instance's question with the
	// static's return type or the reverse (`same-name-methods.returns-self` — `Counter.reset()` returns
	// `Counter` while `c.reset()` returns nothing, and one key can hold one of those).
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE INSTANCE THAT KEEPS THE BARE KEY**, so every instance call door is untouched and only the
	// one static door has to ask (`Parser.parseQualifiedCall`). Both reference compilers land on the same
	// split for the same reason and neither puts the receiver in its overload key — v1's
	// `tryResolveStaticInstanceCollision` re-keys the static to `~static`, the bootstrap's
	// `ResolveStaticInstanceCollision` to the same spelling.
	//
	// ✅ **THREE SWEEP FACTS USED TO BE UNSPLITTABLE AND WERE REFUSED INSTEAD (W75 CLOSED THAT).** A `throws`
	// clause, a consume set over a MANAGED parameter and a parameter default are published by the BY-NAME scans,
	// which folded AHEAD of the declaration loop — before this contest could exist — and so had no second key
	// to offer. They fold after it now and carry the receiver-presence of the declaration that stated them
	// (`ParamConsumeEntry.takesReceiver`), so each member of a pair states its own and none of the three earns a
	// refusal any more. (A `__ManagedMemory` RETURN SURFACE was never one of them: `record` writes it per
	// declaration, so it always split with the rest.)
	//
	// It is EMPTY for every program in the tree — the shape did not compile before this rung — so
	// `Set.contains` returns at its `capacity == 0` test for every declaration and every static call.
	var staticInstanceContests as StaticInstanceContestNameSet
	// The content hash OF THE DECLARATIONS — not of the source they were read from. This is the
	// second half of a parse memo's key (`QueryEngine.parseMemoKey`), and hashing the folded
	// ENTRIES rather than the FILES is the whole point of it:
	//   • edit a function's BODY  ⇒ that file's content hash moves (it re-parses); this hash does
	//     NOT (no other file re-parses). Incrementality is preserved.
	//   • edit a RETURN TYPE      ⇒ this hash moves, and every file re-parses. Which is exactly
	//     right: every call to that function, in any file, may have changed meaning.
	// Keying the parse memo on the composite SOURCE hash instead would be sound and would throw
	// away the first property — one file's edit would re-parse the whole program.
	//
	// ⚠ A TOP-LEVEL CONSTANT PUTS ITS **VALUE** IN HERE, NOT JUST ITS NAME, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING
	// (`mixConstant`). A constant is the one declaration whose value is COPIED INTO its readers — a
	// use inlines it as a literal — so editing `export let MAGIC = 42` to `43` changes what every
	// other file COMPILES TO while leaving those files' own bytes, and so their own content hashes,
	// untouched. Only this hash can carry that. Measured, not assumed: with the warm-rebuild gate's
	// declaration probe pointed at a constant, a 7 → 8 edit re-parses every file in the project; the
	// gate's `invalidation` property is what keeps it that way.
	export var hash as ContentHash
	// ⭐ **THE NAMES OF EVERY MANAGED TOP-LEVEL BINDING IN THE PROGRAM** — `let` AND `var` (P1.7a / P1.7
	// slice 2a) — written once per managed binding by `settleManagedValue`, read once per probe by
	// `Parser.managedTopLevelReceiverAt`. It covers both kinds because the evaluator settles both the same
	// way and the probe behind it has to consider both: `msg.append("!")` on a managed `var` receiver has
	// the identical token shape `Live.count()` on a managed `let` does.
	//
	// It exists because the reader is on a HOT path: `methodCallsAt` runs at every dotted-call shape in
	// the program, and the probe behind it builds a file-scoped STRING key (~4 allocations —
	// `fileScopedDeclKey`) before it knows whether the name is a constant at all.
	//
	// ⚠ **A BOOL IS NOT ENOUGH, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A WHOLE-PROGRAM CLIFF.** This was first written as
	// `anyManagedConstant as bool`, which spares a program that declares NO managed constant — but the
	// moment ONE exists anywhere, a bool is permanently true and every *static* dotted call in the whole
	// program (`Widget.create(…)`, `String.from(…)` — a base that is neither a local nor a capture) pays
	// the full scoped probe to be told "not a constant". **MEASURED at ~5 allocations per static
	// dotted-call site, linear in call sites** (+390/+710/+1,350/+2,630/+5,190 over a 64→1,024 ladder).
	// A NAME set answers the same question for the same one bool's worth of work in the empty case, and
	// keeps answering it cheaply once the set is non-empty: a base whose spelling matches no managed
	// constant costs ONE hash of bytes it already holds and allocates nothing.
	//
	// It is a FILTER, never the answer: a hit still goes through `constantValueOf`, which is what applies
	// file-private scoping and `let`-vs-`var`. So a false positive (this file cannot see that other
	// file's constant) costs one wasted probe and resolves correctly, and there is no second copy of the
	// resolution rule to drift.
	var managedTopLevelNames as ManagedTopLevelNameSet
	// ⭐ **HOW MANY DECLARATIONS HAVE A `.data` SLOT** (P1.7 slice 3) — written by `assignDataLabels`,
	// which is already the one walk that visits exactly them, and read once per compile by
	// `DeadGlobalElimination.liveGlobalLabels` to decide whether its module walk has anything to look
	// for. Zero for the whole existing corpus and for every rung of the scale ladder, which is what
	// keeps a dead-global pass free for a program that has no global.
	//
	// A COUNT rather than a bool because it is what the walk that produces it naturally has, and
	// because "how many slots does this program declare?" is the honest fact; the caller's question
	// ("any at all?") is one comparison away and does not need its own field.
	export var storedGlobalCount as StoredGlobalCount
	// ⭐ **THE LAZY SLOTS, BY LABEL** — filled by `assignDataLabels` (which is the walk that MINTS the labels,
	// so the key and the slot are created in one act) and read by the parser's access door. See
	// `lazyStaticInitCalleeFor`.
	export var lazyStaticSlots as LazyStaticSlotSet
	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT EACH LAZY SLOT'S INITIALIZER CALLS, BY LABEL** — filled by the SAME walk, for the same
	// reason: the label is minted there and the outcome is settled by then, so the key and its callees are
	// recorded in one act. It exists because those callees are NOT program roots (`globalInitCallees`) and
	// have to be credited at the ACCESS instead — see `lazyStaticInitCalleesOf`.
	export var lazyStaticInitCallees as LazyStaticInitCalleeMap
	// ⭐ **HAS EVERY FILE BEEN FOLDED IN YET?** False while `queryProgramSignatures` is still sweeping
	// files into this index, true from the moment the last one lands. It is the index's own answer to
	// "can an absence be trusted?" — during the sweep a name that is genuinely declared reads as
	// undeclared simply because its file has not been folded yet (and a file's OWN declarations are
	// folded only after its whole token walk), so a `not declared` verdict there is a fact about the
	// sweep's progress, not about the program.
	//
	// It lives HERE and not on the `Parser` because it is a property of the INDEX, not of the reader:
	// three parsers read this index (the sweeper, the real parse, the constant evaluator) and only the
	// index knows which side of the fold each of them is on. A flag threaded through `Parser.create`
	// would be the same fact written down at three call sites, free to be passed wrongly at any of them.
	export var allFilesFolded as bool
	// ⭐ **IS THE PROJECT ROOT ITSELF UNDER `stdlib/` (BATCH2 slice 1)?** A per-COMPILE fact, probed once by
	// `Queries.queryProgramSignatures` and read at every `Parser.create` to derive that parser's
	// `livesUnderStdlibDirectory` — see `Queries.livesUnderStdlibDirectory` for the derivation, its proof and
	// the ladder measurement that rejected asking it per FILE instead.
	//
	// ⚠ It lives HERE rather than on `Project` because this index is the one thing already built once per
	// compile AND threaded to every `Parser.create` site, which is exactly the shape the fact has. It is set
	// before any file is swept, so no reader can see the seeded `false`.
	export var rootIsUnderStdlibDirectory as bool
	// ⭐ **THE TYPE NAMES TOP-LEVEL CONSTANT INITIALIZERS CAST TO, scoped by declaring file** (P1.9) —
	// the one `use` the E3062 check cannot get from the parser. Written by `recordConstInitializerTypeName` from
	// inside the constant evaluator, read by `Parser.reportUnusedTypeAliases`; both doors carry the
	// argument. Empty for every program with no top-level `let X = … as T`, which is nearly all of them.
	var constInitializerTypeNames as ConstInitializerTypeNameSet
	// ⭐ **THE `Array with Byte` INSTANCE, DERIVED ONCE PER PROGRAM.** `internArrayByteInstance` is asked on
	// HOT parser paths — every byte-string literal, every `.toByteArray()`, and since R4.2 every array
	// METHOD dispatch (`dispatchArrayMethod`'s `isManagedMemory`) — and each ask built a one-element
	// `MaxonTypeArray` to key the registry with, which is a heap ALLOCATION per array method call. The
	// answer cannot change: `GenericInstanceRegistry` only ever grows and interning is idempotent, so the
	// id the first ask mints is the id for the life of this index (which is one compile —
	// `queryProgramSignatures` builds a fresh one per signatures miss).
	//
	// A `computed` flag beside the value rather than a sentinel id, following `TokenMemo`/`ParseMemo`:
	// **0 IS A REAL `GenericInstanceId`**, so there is no spare value to mean "not yet".
	var arrayByteInstance as GenericInstanceId
	var arrayByteInstanceComputed as bool
	// ⭐⭐ **THE SIMPLE METHOD NAMES SOME DECLARATION IN THE PROGRAM RETURNS A BARE TYPE PARAMETER FROM
	// (W58)** — `calleeReturnsBareTypeParameter` asked of a name rather than of a qualified callee, because
	// the descriptor-need pre-scan sees a `.get(` and cannot resolve its receiver. See
	// `methodNameEverReturnsBareTypeParameter` for what the answer is used for and why the weaker,
	// name-keyed question is the right one to ask there.
	//
	// A `computed` flag beside the set rather than an empty-set sentinel, following `arrayByteInstance`: a
	// program in which NO method returns a bare type parameter has a legitimately empty answer, and rebuilding
	// it on every ask would be O(functions) per member-call token — the quadratic this index's headers keep
	// naming. Built at most once per compile, and only for a program that has a generic type at all
	// (`Queries.foldDescriptorNeeds` returns before the first ask otherwise).
	//
	// ⚠ **LAZY IS SAFE HERE ONLY BECAUSE THE ONE ASKER IS PINNED IN THE PASS ORDER.** `returnTypes` is
	// REWRITTEN after the sweep — three times, by `rewriteRecordedReturnTypes`' contest / struct / interface
	// rules — so a memo folded before those would answer about a column that no longer exists. Its sole
	// reader is `Parser.scanDescriptorSignalsIn`, reached only from `foldDescriptorNeeds`, which
	// `Queries.queryProgramSignatures` runs strictly AFTER all three. A second reader elsewhere in the
	// pipeline would have to re-establish that, which is why there is not one.
	var bareTypeParamReturnerNames as MethodNameSet
	var bareTypeParamReturnerNamesComputed as bool
	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH TYPE NAMES A *USER* FILE DECLARES (A1s wave 2)** — one half of the shadow contest. Filled
	// by `foldFile` for every non-stdlib file, and read only while deciding which of a LISTED STDLIB
	// module's declarations a user program has taken the name of. See `StdlibShadowRenameMap`.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT HOLDS EVERY NAME A USER DECLARATION WRITES, INCLUDING THE ILLEGAL ONES, AND THE LEGALITY
	// FILTER LIVES AT THE DECISION INSTEAD (A1s wave 2 review).** The set answers two questions and they want
	// opposite treatments of a `__` name: "may a user declaration TAKE this name from the stdlib?" (no — the
	// declaration is E2051) and "is this candidate reserved name already SPOKEN FOR?" (yes — the user wrote
	// it, legally or not). Filtering on the way IN answered the first and silently got the second wrong; the
	// filter is now `contestStdlibTypeName`'s, which is the only reader that wants it.
	var userDeclaredTypeNames as DeclaredTypeNameSet
	// The other half: every type name a LISTED STDLIB module declares, collected by the contest's own token
	// walk (`Queries.contestStdlibDeclarationsAgainstUserNames`) before any name is contested. Read only by
	// `shadowRenameIsTaken` — a mint must not land on a name the stdlib ALREADY declares, which is the third
	// claimant on the reserved space and the one no re-probe of the other two would ever notice.
	var stdlibDeclaredTypeNames as DeclaredTypeNameSet
	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY DECLARATION IN THIS PROGRAM THAT CARRIES THE ARRAY-LITERAL MARKER (ARR5b)** — filled from
	// the TOKENS by `Queries.discoverArrayLiteralConformers` before the first file is folded, because the
	// fold itself has to know the answer (`Parser.canonicalGenericBaseNameInThisFile`, `isArrayBaseName`).
	// The declared spelling, not the post-contest one: the contest is decided between the two halves of the
	// sweep and this runs ahead of both, so the rename is applied at the READ exactly as it is for the
	// corpus name below.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN ARRAY AND NOT A SET, BECAUSE ONE READER NEEDS THE ORDER AND ALL OF THEM NEED IT STABLE.**
	// `arrayDeclarationNames` feeds the descriptor pre-scan, which records one edge per serving declaration;
	// a hash set's iteration order would make that edge order a function of the hash rather than of the
	// program. A whole program declares one such type, or two under a contest, so membership is a scan of
	// one or two names — cheaper than the hash it replaces and one representation instead of two.
	var arrayLiteralConformers as ByteArrayArray
	// Does any declaration OTHER than the corpus's carry the marker? The gate `isArrayBaseName` puts in front
	// of the scan — false for every program that declares no container of its own, which is the whole
	// existing corpus and every one of its goldens.
	var hasNonCorpusArrayConformer as bool
	// ⭐⭐ **THE NAME A LISTED STDLIB MODULE DECLARES THE RECORD UNDER — the fold target for every
	// `__ManagedMemory` written OUTSIDE a conformer's own body.** `stdlib/Array.maxon:104` gives it today, so
	// this reads `Array` and every emitted per-type symbol keeps the spelling it had.
	//
	// ⚠ **SEEDED WITH `SynthesizedArrayRecordSeed`, AND THAT SEED IS THE COMPILER'S SYNTHESIZED RECORD AND NOT
	// A FALLBACK TO A CONSTANT.** shv2 minted the record under that name for the whole of P1.7, before
	// `stdlib/Array.maxon` was listed; a program compiled against a corpus that declares no
	// `BuiltinArrayLiteral` conformer at all is that program, and it still needs a name for the record it
	// mints. Discovery REPLACES the seed the moment a listed module declares one.
	var corpusArrayRecordName as ByteArray
	// The contest's ANSWER: bare name ⇒ the reserved name the stdlib declaration is moved to. EMPTY for
	// every program that shadows nothing, which is the whole existing corpus — and that emptiness is what
	// makes this mechanism byte-neutral rather than merely cheap (see `Parser.shadowRenamedTokens`).
	var stdlibShadowRenames as StdlibShadowRenameMap
	// ⭐ **EVERY SOURCE FILE'S MODULE DIRECTORY (N1a)** — path ⇒ its directory relative to the compile
	// anchor, `/`-joined (see `deriveModuleDirectory`). Written once per file by `Queries.foldOneFileInto`,
	// which is the one place that holds both the file and the anchor it is measured from; read by
	// `isVisibleFrom`'s `module` arm, and by every `Parser.create` through `namespaceOf`.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE DIRECTORY IS STORED AND THE NAMESPACE IS DERIVED, NOT THE OTHER WAY ROUND**, because only
	// one of the two is invertible — see `ModuleDirectorySeparator`, which records the two wrong answers
	// a `.`-joined store produced.
	//
	// Keyed by the path's STRING for `RangedAliasRegistry.multiplyDeclared`'s measured reason: `FilePath.hash`
	// case-folds and allocates on Windows, and the whole value of an index is that a probe costs one hash of a
	// string that already exists.
	var fileModuleDirectories as FileModuleDirectoryMap
	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY FREE FUNCTION'S NAMESPACE-QUALIFIED SPELLING (N1b)** — `utils.helper` for a `helper`
	// declared in `utils/`, `lib.inner.deepHelper` for one in `lib/inner/`. THE door a dotted CALLEE asks
	// through to be read as a directory-qualified free call rather than as `Type.method`.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS A SET AND NOT A MAP, BECAUSE THE ANSWER IS ALREADY IN THE NAME.** shv2's function registry is
	// bare-name and whole-program (`commitFuncSignatures` makes a second declaration of one name E3006), so
	// `utils.helper` and bare `helper` are necessarily the SAME function — the qualified spelling ROUTES a
	// call, it never names a different callee. The rewrite target is therefore the last segment, which
	// `splitQualifiedName` already hands back, and the emitted symbol is byte-identical to the one a bare
	// call emits.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT CARRIES NO VISIBILITY, ON PURPOSE.** A qualified call is resolved to the bare declared name and
	// then meets `SemanticCheck.calleeVisibleFrom` exactly as a bare call does — so `utils.hidden()` is
	// refused by the tier the declaration wrote, in that check's own words (E3008 / E3088), rather than
	// silently becoming "no such function" here. Gating this set on the tier would move ONE decision into TWO
	// places and make the blunter message win. (v1 gates its qualified typealias key on `visibility != file`
	// for a registry that has no second check behind it; shv2's does.)
	//
	// ⚠ **METHODS ARE ABSENT AND MUST STAY ABSENT.** A method is already keyed `Type.method`, whole-program
	// and namespace-free, in both references (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.maxon:2062`) — so a
	// `utils.Point.create` entry would be a second spelling of a name that already has one, competing with
	// the type reading of the same tokens for nothing.
	var namespaceQualifiedFunctions as NamespaceQualifiedFunctionSet
	// ⭐⭐ **THE MODULE DIRECTORY OF THE FILE THAT FIRST DECLARED EACH BARE FREE-FUNCTION NAME (N1c)** — the
	// whole input to the cross-namespace contest, and one entry per free function name in the program.
	//
	// It stores only the FIRST directory because that is all the contest needs: a second declaration either
	// matches it (same namespace ⇒ an ordinary duplicate, which `commitFuncSignatures` still refuses) or does
	// not (⇒ contested, and from then on the answer lives in `contestedFreeFunctions`). A per-name array of
	// every directory would allocate for every free function in the program to record a fact almost no name
	// has.
	var freeFunctionDirectories as FreeFunctionDirectoryMap
	// ⭐⭐ **THE BARE NAMES DECLARED AS A FREE FUNCTION IN MORE THAN ONE DIRECTORY ⇒ EVERY QUALIFIED SPELLING
	// OF THEM (N1c).** EMPTY for every program in the corpus and for the compiler's own source, which is what
	// makes this mechanism byte-neutral: a name nobody contests is registered under exactly the bytes it was
	// before, so no committed fragment moves.
	//
	// ⭐ **MEMBERSHIP IS THE FLAG AND THE VALUE IS THE CANDIDATE LIST — one structure, not two.** A separate
	// "is contested" set beside a "spellings" map would be the same fact filed twice, which is the shape every
	// cross-file defect in this compiler has had.
	//
	// ⚠ **A ROOT-LEVEL DECLARATION CONTRIBUTES NO SPELLING**, because it has none: the root IS the
	// unqualified namespace. Its entries keep the BARE key, so a bare call still resolves to it — which is the
	// nearest-enclosing rule (the root encloses every reader) reached by construction rather than by a test.
	var contestedFreeFunctions as ContestedFreeFunctionMap
	// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME CONTEST, INDEXED THE WAY THE CALL DOOR ASKS (N1c review).**
	// `resolvesAsNamespaceQualifiedFunction` is handed an ASSEMBLED chain (`alpha.pick`) and has no bare
	// name to split out of it — `joinQualifiedName` is the only place that split may be spelled — so the
	// question "is this chain a contested free function's registration name?" needs the answer keyed by the
	// chain.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT A SECOND FACT: `claimContestedRegistrationName` is the ONE writer**, and it writes here
	// with the very `registrationName` the declaration's entries are filed under. A name reaches this set
	// exactly when a contested free function CLAIMS it — which is not quite the same as being filed under it,
	// because a spelling another declaration already holds is claimed and then declined (see the set below).
	var contestedRegistrationNames as ContestedRegistrationNameSet

	// ⚠ **THE CLAIMED SPELLINGS A CONTESTED FREE FUNCTION MAY NOT ACTUALLY TAKE** — a `<directory>.<name>`
	// that is already some METHOD's mangled key. Its declarations keep the bare name and
	// `commitFuncSignatures` reports the duplicate; see `claimContestedRegistrationName` for the measured
	// symptom the overwrite produced, and for why the answer has to be remembered rather than re-derived.
	// EMPTY for every program that does not name a directory after a type that declares the same member.
	var contestedKeysAnotherDeclarationHolds as ContestedRegistrationNameSet

	// ⚠ **THE SEEDED ALIASES' DECLARING FILES ARE STAMPED HERE, AND NOTHING ELSE CAN DO IT (N1c).**
	// `RangedAliasRegistry.create` supplies two declarations of files the sweep never visits, so
	// `moduleDirectoryOf` — whose miss is deliberately a panic — has a hole exactly where a seed is.
	// Stamping in the one constructor that builds both indexes closes it for every reader, present and
	// future; `seededStdlibAliasDeclFiles` records what measured the hole.
	//
	// ⚠ **`os` IS THE COMPILE TARGET'S, AND IT IS HERE FOR EXACTLY ONE INDEX (BATCH27)** — the
	// `RangedAliasRegistry` below, whose builtin `ExitCode` bound is platform-shaped and must be resolved
	// where a target is known (see `RangedAliasRegistry.exitCodeHigh`). This index is built once per
	// compile, so it costs one enum argument on a cold path.
	export static function create(os Os) returns ProgramSignatures
		var signatures = ProgramSignatures.freshIndexes(os)
		for declFile in seededStdlibAliasDeclFiles() 'eachSeededAliasDeclaringFile'
			signatures.recordFileModuleDirectory(declFile, moduleDirectory: seededStdlibAliasModuleDirectory(declFile))
		end 'eachSeededAliasDeclaringFile'
		return signatures
	end 'create'

	// Every index, empty. Split out of `create` only so the seed stamping above reads as the two steps it
	// is; this is its one caller.
	static function freshIndexes(os Os) returns ProgramSignatures
		return Self{returnTypes: FuncReturnTypeMap.create(), typeAliases: RangedAliasRegistry.create(os), functionTypeAliases: FunctionTypeAliasMap.create(), structTypes: StructLayoutMap.create(), structNamesInDeclOrder: ByteArrayArray.create(), enumTypes: createEnumRegistry(), genericAliasDecls: GenericAliasEntryArray.create(), genericInstances: GenericInstanceRegistry.create(), compiledInstanceNames: CompiledInstanceNameMap.create(), canonicalInstanceNames: CanonicalInstanceNameMap.create(), instanceDisplayNames: InstanceDisplayNameMap.create(), substitutedInstanceOrigins: SubstitutedInstanceOriginMap.create(), canonicalInstanceNameIndex: CanonicalInstanceNameIndex.create(), canonicalNamesIndexedThrough: 0, tupleCanonicalNames: TupleCanonicalNameMap.create(), tupleElementTokens: TupleElementTokenMap.create(), tupleElementTokenOwners: TupleElementTokenOwnerMap.create(), typeParamOwners: TypeParamOwnerMap.create(), tupleAliases: TupleCanonicalNameMap.create(), sawAggregateArrayLiteral: false, sawListFromConstruction: false, sawEnumAllCasesAccess: false, sawIntArrayLiteral: false, sawByteStringLiteral: false, sawStringArrayLiteral: false, sawSetFromConstruction: false, sawManagedFileProducer: false, sawManagedDirectoryProducer: false, vectorFromSizes: FixedSizeArray.create(), genericAliases: GenericAliasMap.create(), interfaceAliases: GenericAliasMap.create(), interfaceAliasOwners: InterfaceNameSet.create(), contestedGenericAliases: sharedEmptyGenericAliasContests, multiplyDeclaredGenericAliases: sharedEmptyDisagreeingAliasNames, divergedGenericAliasDecl: AliasCycleResult.none, nameableGenericAliases: sharedEmptyNameableGenericAliases, genericAliasSites: GenericInstantiationSiteArray.create(), innerAliases: RangedTypeAliasMap.create(), extensionInnerAliasMembers: ExtensionInnerAliasNameSet.create(), methodInnerAliasParams: InnerAliasParamInfoMap.create(), interfaces: IrInterfaceMap.create(), interfaceNamesInDeclOrder: builtinInterfaceNames(), interfaceDeclIndexByName: builtinInterfaceDeclIndices(), interfaceDeclSites: InterfaceDeclSiteArray.create(), extensionDeclSites: ExtensionDeclSiteArray.create(), conformanceClauseSites: ConformanceClauseDeclSiteArray.create(), descriptorScanSites: DescriptorScanSiteArray.create(), descriptorNeeds: sharedEmptyDescriptorNeeds, conditionalExtensionSkips: ConditionalExtensionSkipMap.create(), associatedTypeHoldings: AssociatedTypeHoldingMap.create(), sweptConformances: DeclaredConformanceMap.create(), sweptConformancesBuilt: false, extensionDeclaredConformances: DeclaredConformanceMap.create(), extensionMethodConstraints: ExtensionMethodConstraintMap.create(), unconstrainedExtensionMethods: ExtensionMethodNameSet.create(), typeNames: TypeNameInterner.create(), decls: TopLevelDeclArray.create(), declIndex: TopLevelDeclIndexMap.create(), funcParamConsumes: ParamConsumeMap.create(), funcParamReassigns: ParamReassignMap.create(), sawParamReassignment: false, byRefForwardingLive: ByRefForwardingMap.create(), byRefForwardingClosed: false, byRefBareNames: ByRefBareNameSet.create(), funcGlobalAliasReturns: GlobalAliasReturnMap.create(), sawGlobalNameReturn: false, globalAliasReturnKeys: GlobalAliasReturnKeySet.create(), globalAliasReturnsClosed: false, funcThrows: FuncThrowsMap.create(), disagreeingThrows: DisagreeingThrowsNameSet.create(), funcThrowingDeclarationCounts: FunctionDeclarationCountMap.create(), funcParamDefaults: ParamDefaultMap.create(), funcOpaqueParams: OpaqueParamMap.create(), contestedOpaqueParams: ContestedOpaqueParamNameSet.create(), disagreeingParamDefaults: DisagreeingParamDefaultNameSet.create(), funcDeclarationCounts: FunctionDeclarationCountMap.create(), funcDefaultingDeclarationCounts: FunctionDeclarationCountMap.create(), declaredReturnSurfaces: DeclaredReturnSurfaceMap.create(), funcReturnDeclFiles: FuncReturnDeclFileMap.create(), funcDeclSites: FunctionDeclSiteMap.create(), bufferElementAliases: BufferElementAliasNameSet.create(), bufferSurfaceAliases: BufferElementAliasNameSet.create(), extensionPublishedMethods: ExtensionMethodNameSet.create(), extensionShadowedMethods: ExtensionMethodNameSet.create(), extensionMethodDeclFiles: ExtensionMethodDeclFileMap.create(), contestedExtensionMethods: ExtensionMethodNameSet.create(), staticInstanceContests: StaticInstanceContestNameSet.create(), hash: fnvOffsetBasis(), managedTopLevelNames: ManagedTopLevelNameSet.create(), storedGlobalCount: 0, lazyStaticSlots: LazyStaticSlotSet.create(), lazyStaticInitCallees: LazyStaticInitCalleeMap.create(), allFilesFolded: false, rootIsUnderStdlibDirectory: false, constInitializerTypeNames: ConstInitializerTypeNameSet.create(), arrayByteInstance: 0, arrayByteInstanceComputed: false, bareTypeParamReturnerNames: MethodNameSet.create(), bareTypeParamReturnerNamesComputed: false, userDeclaredTypeNames: DeclaredTypeNameSet.create(), stdlibDeclaredTypeNames: DeclaredTypeNameSet.create(), arrayLiteralConformers: ByteArrayArray.create(), hasNonCorpusArrayConformer: false, corpusArrayRecordName: SynthesizedArrayRecordSeed, stdlibShadowRenames: StdlibShadowRenameMap.create(), fileModuleDirectories: FileModuleDirectoryMap.create(), namespaceQualifiedFunctions: NamespaceQualifiedFunctionSet.create(), freeFunctionDirectories: FreeFunctionDirectoryMap.create(), contestedFreeFunctions: ContestedFreeFunctionMap.create(), contestedRegistrationNames: ContestedRegistrationNameSet.create(), contestedKeysAnotherDeclarationHolds: ContestedRegistrationNameSet.create()}
	end 'freshIndexes'

	// --- MODULE DIRECTORIES and the ONE visibility decision (N1a) ---

	// File this file's module directory. Called once per file by `Queries.foldOneFileInto`, before the file's
	// declarations are folded, so `isVisibleFrom` can answer about any declaration the moment the sweep ends.
	// LAST-WINS is unreachable rather than tolerated: the sweep visits each path exactly once.
	export function recordFileModuleDirectory(path FilePath, moduleDirectory String)
		self.fileModuleDirectories.upsert(path.toString(), value: moduleDirectory)
	end 'recordFileModuleDirectory'

	// The module directory of a file the sweep folded. A MISS IS A COMPILER BUG, not a file at the root: the
	// root's directory is the empty string and is recorded like any other, so an absent entry means a path
	// reached a visibility question without ever having been swept — and answering `""` would silently place
	// that file in the root module, where every `module` declaration in the program is visible to it.
	export function moduleDirectoryOf(path FilePath) returns String
		return self.moduleDirectoryOfPathText(path.toString())
	end 'moduleDirectoryOf'

	// The same answer for a caller already holding the STRINGIFIED path — which is the map's key, so a
	// `contestedGenericAliases` group (keyed by declaring-file text, `GenericAliasByDeclaringFile`) reads it
	// without a `FilePath` round trip. Split out rather than probed a second time so the miss, and the
	// paragraph above explaining why a miss is a compiler bug, exist once.
	function moduleDirectoryOfPathText(pathText String) returns String
		return try self.fileModuleDirectories.get(pathText) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.moduleDirectoryOf: '{pathText}' has no module directory — every source path is stamped by `Queries.foldOneFileInto` before any declaration of it is folded")
	end 'moduleDirectoryOfPathText'

	// The `.`-joined NAMESPACE a file is shown as — what `Parser.create` stamps onto `Parser.namespace`, from
	// which a declaration's qualified spelling is minted (`namespaceQualifiedName`). ⚠ It used to say
	// `IrFunction.namespace`, and that FIELD IS GONE (N1b deleted it as write-only — see its tombstone);
	// nothing downstream of the parser holds a namespace, because a `dir.fn()` reference resolves to the bare
	// declared name at parse time.
	// Rendered from the stored directory rather than stored beside it: two fields would be two chances to
	// disagree, and only the directory can be inverted back into segments.
	export function namespaceOf(path FilePath) returns String
		return namespaceForModuleDirectory(self.moduleDirectoryOf(path))
	end 'namespaceOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **MAY `readerFilePath` NAME A DECLARATION MADE IN `declFilePath` AT TIER `visibility`?** — the ONE
	// place a `Visibility` becomes an answer, for every kind of declaration there is: a function, a nominal
	// type, a ranged typealias, a top-level binding, a field.
	//
	// Ported from v1's `isVisibleFrom` (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Project.maxon:161-167`), tier for tier. The
	// `module` arm is the one that diverges, and only in WHERE the string comes from: v1 stringifies both
	// `FilePath.parent()`s at every ask, shv2 reads a per-file INDEX. Both then run the same
	// separator-guarded prefix test — see `moduleDirectoryContains`, and `ModuleDirectorySeparator` for what
	// the guard costs when the joiner is a character a directory name may contain.
	//
	// ⚠ **A DECLARATION'S OWN FILE ALWAYS MAY**, in every tier — visibility governs who ELSE may see a
	// declaration, never whether it is in scope where it was written. That is not a fourth arm: `file` says it
	// directly, and both wider tiers contain their own file by construction.
	export function isVisibleFrom(visibility Visibility, declFilePath FilePath, readerFilePath FilePath) returns bool
		match visibility 'tier'
			global then return true
			file then return declFilePath.toString().equals(readerFilePath.toString())
			module then return moduleDirectoryContains(self.moduleDirectoryOf(declFilePath), inner: self.moduleDirectoryOf(readerFilePath))
		end 'tier'
	end 'isVisibleFrom'

	// ⭐ **MAY `readerFilePath` NAME THIS RANGED ALIAS (P1.9)?** The alias-shaped face of `isVisibleFrom`, kept
	// as its own name because the two answers it separates are the point: a hidden alias is still FOUND, which
	// is what tells "no declaration binds this name anywhere" (E3011, at the `as`) from "one does, elsewhere,
	// and did not export it" (E2003, at the target name). Folding the test into `lookup` would collapse the two
	// into the sharper one's wording and report the wrong mistake.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND IT IS ASKED ONLY AT AN `as` CAST TARGET TODAY** (`Parser.hiddenAliasCastTarget`), which is the
	// position the corpus pins and the position v1 checks (`validateNamedTypeVisibility` walks `convert` ops and
	// nothing else). A hidden alias written as a PARAMETER or FIELD type still resolves; widening that is a
	// cross-file name-resolution rung, not a line here, because the message this reports names the `as` and
	// there is no ported wording for the other positions.
	export function aliasVisibleFrom(alias RangedTypeAlias, readerFilePath FilePath) returns bool
		return self.isVisibleFrom(alias.visibility, declFilePath: alias.declFilePath, readerFilePath: readerFilePath)
	end 'aliasVisibleFrom'

	// --- The stdlib/user SHADOW CONTEST (A1s wave 2). See `StdlibShadowRenameMap`. ---

	// Every type name ONE user file declares, over the arrays `foldFile` was handed — the SAME declaration
	// arrays the registries are built from, so "what the user declared" and "what the index holds" are one
	// walk and cannot come to disagree. An `interface` comes through its SITE rather than through an
	// `IrInterface`, because the declaration itself is not read until `foldInterfaceDeclarations` — which
	// runs AFTER the contest is settled. See `InterfaceDeclSite.name`.
	//
	// Unfiltered on purpose; the field's own header says which reader owns the legality question and why.
	function noteUserDeclaredTypeNames(aliases RangedTypeAliasArray, functionAliases FunctionTypeAliasArray, structs StructLayoutArray, enums EnumLayoutArray, genericAliasEntries GenericAliasEntryArray, interfaceSites InterfaceDeclSiteArray)
		// The namespace-qualified MIRROR is skipped: this set's contract is "every type name ONE user file
		// DECLARES", and no file declares `api.Score` — the compiler minted it. A dotted name could never
		// contest a stdlib one anyway (no stdlib type name holds a "."), so the filter buys correctness of the
		// set's stated meaning, not a different answer.
		for alias in aliases 'eachAlias'
			if not mirrorsANamespaceQualifiedAlias(alias) 'aDeclarationTheUserWrote'
				self.userDeclaredTypeNames.insert(alias.name)
			end 'aDeclarationTheUserWrote'
		end 'eachAlias'
		// The BASE-QUALIFIED copy of a nested function alias is skipped for the mirror's reason, and the dot is
		// exact here for `mirrorsANamespaceQualifiedAlias`'s: a source-written alias name is an identifier and
		// cannot hold a ".", and the function form takes no NAMESPACE mirror (`pushNamespaceQualifiedAlias`
		// files into the RANGED array alone) — so a dotted name in this array is exactly and only the
		// `<Type>.<member>` copy `Parser.publishNestedFunctionAlias` minted.
		for functionAlias in functionAliases 'eachFunctionAlias'
			if not containsQualifier(functionAlias.name) 'aDeclarationTheUserWrote'
				self.userDeclaredTypeNames.insert(functionAlias.name)
			end 'aDeclarationTheUserWrote'
		end 'eachFunctionAlias'
		for layout in structs 'eachStruct'
			self.userDeclaredTypeNames.insert(layout.name)
		end 'eachStruct'
		for enumLayout in enums 'eachEnum'
			self.userDeclaredTypeNames.insert(enumLayout.name)
		end 'eachEnum'
		for genericAlias in genericAliasEntries 'eachGenericAlias'
			self.userDeclaredTypeNames.insert(genericAlias.aliasName)
		end 'eachGenericAlias'
		for site in interfaceSites 'eachInterface'
			self.userDeclaredTypeNames.insert(site.name)
		end 'eachInterface'
	end 'noteUserDeclaredTypeNames'

	// Every type name a LISTED STDLIB module declares, in the order the contest's token walk found them.
	// Recorded BEFORE any of them is contested, because `shadowRenameIsTaken` has to be able to ask about a
	// module the walk has not reached yet — see `stdlibDeclaredTypeNames`.
	export function noteStdlibDeclaredTypeNames(names ByteArrayArray)
		for name in names 'each'
			self.stdlibDeclaredTypeNames.insert(name)
		end 'each'
	end 'noteStdlibDeclaredTypeNames'

	// ⭐⭐ **RECORD ONE `type <Name> … implements … BuiltinArrayLiteral` DECLARATION (ARR5b).** Called by
	// `Queries.discoverArrayLiteralConformers`, once per such declaration, before the first file is folded.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE STDLIB'S IS SINGLED OUT BECAUSE IT ANSWERS A DIFFERENT QUESTION FROM THE OTHERS.** Every
	// conformer is a name whose instances ARE the managed record (`isArrayBaseName`); exactly one of them is
	// the record every stdlib SIGNATURE is written against, and that one is the fold target a
	// `__ManagedMemory` written outside any conformer's body must reach. A user's own conformer cannot serve
	// that role — a `ByteArray` handed back by `String.toByteArray()` is the corpus's record, and folding
	// `__ManagedMemory` in a user file onto `Bag` would give one record two ids at every crossing.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **"LAST STDLIB DECLARATION WINS, AND THE CORPUS HAS EXACTLY ONE" WAS A DATED MEASUREMENT, AND `W86`
	// IS THE DATE IT EXPIRED.** `stdlib/Vector.maxon` carries the marker too, and the note that stood here
	// said it was "deliberately NOT listed" — an argument that evaporates the moment a rung lists it.
	// MEASURED, on the first build of that entry: `Vector` is walked after `Array`, so it took
	// `corpusArrayRecordName`, `isArrayBaseName(b"Array")` went FALSE, and `stdlib/Array.maxon:114`'s own
	// `Self{managed: managed}` — the record's identity — was refused
	// **`E2015 … shv2 mints that record only for the two names it owns the record FOR`**. Every program in
	// the tree, including `function main() returns ExitCode / return 7`, failed to compile. A single-valued
	// fact derived from a marker two declarations carry is decided by walk order, and the note admitted as
	// much while resting on the count staying at one.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE TIEBREAK IS THE SKIP BELOW, AND `W112` BUILT IT BECAUSE THE TREE COULD RUN IT AFTER ALL.**
	// `W86` named it, judged it unreachable while `Vector.maxon` stays unlisted, and withdrew it as a fix no
	// case could turn red. ⛔ **That judgement was scoped to the STDLIB route and the USER route was open the
	// whole time**: `noteArrayLiteralConformer` is called for a USER file too, and a user program declaring
	// `type Vector uses Element implements BuiltinArrayLiteral` needs no whitelist line to reach it. **MEASURED
	// on `main`, no compiler edit**: `Vec3.create()` answered count **0**, four `push`es **4**, `resize(9)`
	// **9** — the size that is part of the type, gone, with no diagnostic. See
	// `isBuiltinGenericBaseNameBesidesTheRecord` for the full measurement and for the `List` twin; the two
	// `array-declared-record.error.a-conformer-named-for-…` cases pin both.
	//
	// The rule the skip states: a name shv2 already serves a container under is not made an ARRAY declaration
	// by the marker. A `Vector`'s size is the second coordinate of its instance identity
	// (`GenericInstanceRegistry.fixedSizes`, see `VectorBuiltinBaseName`), so `Vector with 3 Int` is not the
	// record a `__ManagedMemory with Int` folds onto and never can be — one instance would have to be two —
	// and its literal already has its own door (`Parser.parseVectorFromConstruction`, a RETYPE of the array
	// literal rather than an identity). A `List`'s record is a chain of nodes and shares nothing but the word.
	//
	// ⇒ **ONE SKIP CLOSES BOTH HALVES, WHICH IS WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT AT EITHER SYMPTOM.** The loud half is
	// the stdlib one: a second STDLIB conformer takes `corpusArrayRecordName` outright, so listing
	// `stdlib/Vector.maxon` made `isArrayBaseName(b"Array")` FALSE and refused `stdlib/Array.maxon:114`'s own
	// identity — every program in the tree, `return 7` included. The quiet half is the user one above, where
	// `isArrayBaseName` goes TRUE through the `hasNonCorpusArrayConformer` widening and
	// `Parser.dispatchMethodOnReceiver` — which asks the array arm FIRST — serves `push`/`resize` on a
	// fixed-size receiver. Same cause, opposite volume.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SKIP IS SILENT HERE AND THE REFUSAL IS AT THE CONSTRUCTION**, because that is where the noun is
	// right: `Parser.requireNameIsNotABuiltinContainerBase`. The marker's own conformance requires a
	// `static init(value __ManagedMemory) returns Self` (E3016 without one), so every legal conformer builds
	// the identity this compiler cannot honour under such a name, and the declaration is refused there rather
	// than being quietly ignored.
	//
	// ⚠ v1 breaks the same tie by PROBING THE NAME `"Array"` first
	// (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.maxon:16034-16049`) under a comment claiming the compiler knows
	// nothing about `Array` — the probe is the knowledge. Not to be ported: the fact shv2 reads instead is
	// one it genuinely holds, and it names the declaration that is DISQUALIFIED rather than the one that wins.
	export function noteArrayLiteralConformer(name ByteArray, isStdlibSource bool)
		if self.isBuiltinGenericBaseNameBesidesTheRecord(name) 'shv2AlreadyServesAContainerUnderThisName'
			return
		end 'shv2AlreadyServesAContainerUnderThisName'

		if not self.declaresTheArrayLiteralMarker(name) 'firstDeclarationOfThisName'
			self.arrayLiteralConformers.push(name)
		end 'firstDeclarationOfThisName'

		if isStdlibSource 'theCorpusDeclaresTheRecord'
			self.corpusArrayRecordName = name
			return
		end 'theCorpusDeclaresTheRecord'

		self.hasNonCorpusArrayConformer = true
	end 'noteArrayLiteralConformer'

	// ⭐ **DOES A DECLARATION OF THIS NAME CARRY THE ARRAY-LITERAL MARKER?** THE one reader of the discovered
	// list, so no caller can half-apply the "one or two names" rule the list's own header states.
	export function declaresTheArrayLiteralMarker(name ByteArray) returns bool
		for conformer in self.arrayLiteralConformers 'eachDeclaredConformer'
			if conformer.equals(name) 'thisOne'
				return true
			end 'thisOne'
		end 'eachDeclaredConformer'

		return false
	end 'declaresTheArrayLiteralMarker'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE NAME THE CORPUS DECLARES THE MANAGED RECORD UNDER, AS `readerIsStdlibSource` SPELLS IT** —
	// the fold target for every `__ManagedMemory` written outside a conformer's own body (F31), in ONE place
	// so a caller cannot half-apply it. A stdlib file spells a contested corpus declaration under the
	// contest's mint; a user file spells it bare, because there the bare name is what the user's own tokens
	// already mean.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE RENAME IS APPLIED AT THE READ AND NOT AT DISCOVERY**, for `stdlibShadowRenameOf`'s own reason:
	// the contest is decided between the two halves of the sweep and this is asked on both sides of it.
	export function arrayRecordBaseNameIn(readerIsStdlibSource bool) returns ByteArray
		return self.stdlibShadowRenameOf(self.corpusArrayRecordName) if readerIsStdlibSource else self.arrayRecordDeclaredName()
	end 'arrayRecordBaseNameIn'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE NAME THE DECLARATION ITSELF WRITES, BEFORE ANY CONTEST — the record's spelling as a READER
	// knows it.** It answers the questions whose subject is a NAME on the page: the CONFORMER an array
	// instance reduces to (`conformerNameOfInstanceSpelling`, and through it every witness lookup and the
	// hash-table key roster), and every diagnostic that has to quote the type. It is deliberately the
	// pre-contest spelling for both: a refusal quoting this compile's mint would send a reader to a name no
	// author wrote and `Parser.requireUnreservedName` forbids them from writing.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT IS NOT THE BASE A COMPILER-MINTED INSTANCE IS INTERNED UNDER — that is `arrayRecordBaseName`,
	// and the two were ONE until ARRO.** A `b"…"`, an `[1, 2, 3]`, `<Enum>.allCaseNames` and `String.split`'s
	// result are all the CORPUS's record, so they must carry the base the corpus's own declaration is
	// registered under; under a contest that declaration has MOVED and this name is the PROGRAM's container
	// instead. See `internWellKnownContainerInstances` for the wrong answer that cost and for why interning
	// after the contest — rather than deciding the contest earlier — is the fix.
	export function arrayRecordDeclaredName() returns ByteArray
		return self.corpusArrayRecordName
	end 'arrayRecordDeclaredName'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS `name` THE CONFORMER EVERY CONFORMING ARRAY REDUCES TO? — the ONE comparison behind the
	// intrinsic-conformance table's array row (ARR6).** Every door that asks *"does this conformer name the
	// array record?"* asks it here: the E3017 where-constraint check, the witness-slot impl join, and the
	// element clause. They were three comparisons against the constant `SynthesizedArrayRecordSeed` while their
	// callers all arrived carrying `conformerNameOfType`'s DISCOVERED answer, so the three agreed with the
	// program only because `stdlib/Array.maxon` happens to declare the record as `Array`.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT `isArrayBaseName`, AND THE TWO MUST NOT BE COLLAPSED.** That predicate answers *"is this
	// name an array DECLARATION in this compile?"* and is deliberately wide: a user's own
	// `BuiltinArrayLiteral` conformer and the corpus declaration MOVED by the shadow contest both answer true
	// there. A CONFORMER is narrower — `instanceConformerReduction` collapses every conforming instance,
	// whatever declaration it came from, onto this single name — so admitting the wide set here would file a
	// `Bag with Integer` under a conformer whose `Bag.hash` nothing installs.
	export function namesTheArrayRecordConformer(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return name.equals(self.arrayRecordDeclaredName())
	end 'namesTheArrayRecordConformer'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ARRAY ROW OF THE INTRINSIC-CONFORMANCE TABLE, AS A LIST — `Hashable, Equatable`, fetched and
	// never transcribed (ARR6).** It is BOTH halves of `extension Array implements Hashable, Equatable where
	// Element is Hashable and Equatable`: the protocols the record supplies AND the clause its element must
	// satisfy, which is not a coincidence but the shape of a container promising exactly what it demands of
	// its element (see `IrInterface.isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance`'s array arm, which owns the row).
	//
	// ⚠ **IT EXISTS BECAUSE THE TWO ARGUMENTS ARE ONE FACT AND THREE DOORS NEEDED THEM PAIRED** — the
	// withheld-method refusal, the hash-table key refusal (`Parser`) and the element clause
	// (`arrayElementCarriesConformance`). Each wrote `intrinsicConformanceProtocolNames` out with the record's
	// DISCOVERED name, and the row's key is now a question rather than a name, so the pairing of "the record's
	// own spelling" with "this IS the record" is stated once here instead of agreed three times.
	export function arrayConformanceProtocolNames() returns ByteArrayArray
		return intrinsicConformanceProtocolNames(self.arrayRecordDeclaredName(), discovered: DiscoveredConformer.arrayRecord)
	end 'arrayConformanceProtocolNames'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH COMPILER-GRANTED CONFORMER A NAME IS — the ONE discovery every door that consults the
	// intrinsic-conformance table routes through (enum-hashable).** Two of that table's rows are keyed by a
	// declaration rather than by a compiler-owned spelling, so neither can be recognized without the program:
	// the ARRAY RECORD, whose name the corpus chooses (ARR6), and a payload-free `enum`, whose name the author
	// chooses. They are answered together because they are the same question — *"is this name one the compiler
	// grants a conformance to without an `implements`?"* — and a caller that asked only one of them would
	// silently withhold the other's row.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ENUM ROW IS WITHHELD FROM A `union`, WHICH IS THE LANGUAGE'S RULE AND NOT A REPRESENTATION
	// ONE.** `/specs/enum-hashable.md` grants the pair to enums *"without associated values"* and pins the
	// refusal of a union that has them; the bootstrap withholds it from EVERY union (`2-Parser.cs:4530`,
	// `if (!isUnion)`), including a payload-free one, and this follows that reading — a union is a nominal
	// choice the author made and the conformance is the enum keyword's.
	//
	// ⚠ **`isBoxed()` IS A SECOND CONDITION AND NOT A RESTATEMENT OF THE FIRST**: it is the REPRESENTATION
	// premise the granted impls rest on (`enumConformanceImplName` — the value must BE its i64 tag), and the
	// keyword alone does not establish it. An `enum` whose case carries a payload is heap-boxed exactly as a
	// union is, so hashing it as an i64 would hash a POINTER; whether such a declaration is refused earlier is
	// a question about a diagnostic, and this predicate must not depend on the answer.
	export function namesAPayloadFreeEnum(name ByteArray) returns bool
		if let layout = try self.enumTypes.get(name) 'declared'
			return not layout.isUnion and not layout.isBoxed()
		end 'declared'
		return false
	end 'namesAPayloadFreeEnum'

	export function discoveredConformerOf(name ByteArray) returns DiscoveredConformer
		if self.namesTheArrayRecordConformer(name) 'theArrayRecord'
			return DiscoveredConformer.arrayRecord
		end 'theArrayRecord'
		if self.namesAPayloadFreeEnum(name) 'anEnum'
			return DiscoveredConformer.payloadFreeEnum
		end 'anEnum'

		return DiscoveredConformer.none
	end 'discoveredConformerOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE IMPL A PAYLOAD-FREE ENUM'S GRANTED `Hashable`/`Equatable` SLOT NAMES — the ONE answer, read by
	// the WITNESS SLOT (`LowerMaxonToStd.witnessSlotImpl`) and by the DIRECT dispatch
	// (`Parser.dispatchEnumConformanceMethod`), because a map that hashes a key one way and an `e.hash()` that
	// answers another is a wrong answer no diagnostic can reach.**
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS `int`'s IMPL, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF WHY THE GRANT SYNTHESIZES NO BODY.** An enum value IS
	// its i64 tag — one `MaxonOp.literal` at every `Color.red` (`Parser.emitEnumTagLiteral`), resolving away to
	// `MaxonType.integer` below the parser (`TypeResolution.denotedNamedType`) — so hashing an enum IS hashing
	// that i64 and comparing two enums IS comparing two i64s. `int.hash`/`int.equals` are already synthesized,
	// already witness-reachable and already the symbols `__witness_int.*` names, so the enum row reaches the
	// same two bodies rather than minting a pair per enum in the program.
	//
	// ⭐ **AND THE TAG IS THE RIGHT THING TO HASH FOR EVERY BACKING, INCLUDING THE THREE WHERE IT IS NOT THE
	// RAW VALUE.** A string-backed enum's tag is its declaration index and a function-backed one's likewise;
	// a character-backed enum's is the codepoint; a float-backed one's is the f64's BIT PATTERN. In all four
	// the tag is UNIQUE per case (E3031 refuses a duplicate raw value), so two cases are equal exactly when
	// their tags are — which is the property a hash table needs, and it costs no String allocation per probe.
	// The bootstrap reaches the same arrangement from the other side, hashing the ORDINAL rather than
	// materializing a `String` rawValue (`2-Parser.cs`'s `SynthesizeEnumHashAndEquals`).
	//
	// ⚠ **A `float` BACKING IS WHERE THIS DEPARTS FROM v1, DELIBERATELY.** v1 dispatches a float-backed enum's
	// hash to `float.hash` on the reinterpreted value (`Parser.maxon:19355`); shv2 hashes the BITS. The
	// difference is visible on exactly one input — a NaN-backed case, which `float.equals` reports as unequal
	// to ITSELF, so a map could never find the key it just inserted. Case identity is what an enum's
	// `Equatable` means, and the bit compare is that; numeric equality is not.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN ENUM THAT DECLARES ITS OWN `hash`/`equals` KEEPS IT.** Its method is registered under this very
	// key (`Parser.declaredMethodName`), it is what `d.hash()` already resolves to (D1), and v1 states the
	// same precedence — *"a user method wins, matching 'user override beats synthesis'"* (`Parser.maxon:19342`).
	// Answering `int.hash` regardless would leave the two doors disagreeing about one enum.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND THAT BRANCH IS SOUND ONLY BECAUSE E3130 REFUSES A MEMBER THAT DOES NOT IMPLEMENT THE
	// REQUIREMENT — it was a WRONG ANSWER AND A LEAK before that check existed.** Nothing validates an enum's
	// members against `Hashable`/`Equatable` the way `checkConformance` validates a declared `implements`:
	// the grant is the compiler's, so there is no `ConformanceDecl` to walk and `witnessSlotImpls` never
	// holds a selection for one. MEASURED with the branch unguarded: an enum declaring
	// `hash() returns String` was stamped into a slot the probe calls as `returns` i64, so `map.get`
	// answered **0 against the oracle's 20 and the process exited 101** — a String pointer used as a hash
	// and then leaked, compiling clean with no diagnostic anywhere.
	// ⇒ `ConformanceCheck.checkGrantedEnumConformance` is the other half of THIS function and may not be
	// removed while this branch stands.
	export function enumConformanceImplName(enumName ByteArray, methodName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		if self.enumDeclaresOwnConformanceMethod(enumName, methodName: methodName) 'theEnumWritesItsOwn'
			return qualifiedMethodName(enumName, methodName: methodName)
		end 'theEnumWritesItsOwn'

		return qualifiedMethodName(IntConformerName, methodName: methodName)
	end 'enumConformanceImplName'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS ENUM DECLARE A MEMBER OF ITS OWN UNDER A GRANTED REQUIREMENT'S NAME? — the ONE home of
	// the override probe, read by all THREE doors that turn on it.** `Parser.enumGrantedConformanceMethod`
	// decides whether `e.hash()` is a method call or the granted conformance; `enumConformanceImplName`
	// decides which symbol the witness slot is stamped with; `ConformanceCheck.checkGrantedEnumConformance`
	// decides which members it must hold to the requirement.
	//
	// ⚠ **THEY WERE TWO COPIES OF ONE JOIN AND THAT IS EXACTLY WHY THIS EXISTS**: refine the rule at one and
	// the other keeps the old one, so `e.hash()` would call one body while the map stamped another — the
	// single-home invariant this whole grant rests on, broken by the edit that was meant to tighten it.
	export function enumDeclaresOwnConformanceMethod(enumName ByteArray, methodName ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.declaresCallee(qualifiedMethodName(enumName, methodName: methodName))
	end 'enumDeclaresOwnConformanceMethod'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME NAME AS THE *STDLIB* SPELLS IT — and THE base every compiler-MINTED array instance is
	// interned under.** What `isArrayBaseName` recognizes as the corpus declaration MOVED; what a reader with
	// no file of its own (the conformance runtime, the reachability walk) has to use, because a corpus body is
	// compiled under the stdlib's spelling; and what the mint has to use, because a minted array IS the
	// corpus's record and must share the gid of the corpus's own `ByteArray`.
	//
	// ⚠ **EVERY CALLER THAT INTERNS OR LOOKS UP AN INSTANCE READS THIS ONE**, on both sides of the sweep and
	// in the real parse, so the sweep's write and the parser's read cannot land on two gids. The single thing
	// that makes that possible is that no mint runs before the contest — see
	// `internWellKnownContainerInstances`.
	export function arrayRecordBaseName() returns ByteArray
		return self.arrayRecordBaseNameIn(true)
	end 'arrayRecordBaseName'

	// ⭐ **DECIDE THE CONTEST FOR ONE NAME A LISTED STDLIB MODULE DECLARES.** A no-op unless a USER file
	// declares it too; otherwise it mints the reserved name the stdlib declaration will be keyed under and
	// files it. Called once per stdlib-declared name, between the two sweep phases — see
	// `Queries.contestStdlibDeclarationsAgainstUserNames`.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **A NAME NO USER DECLARATION MAY LEGALLY HOLD IS NOT A CONTEST, AND THE SWEEP CANNOT TELL —
	// SO THIS DOOR DOES.** The declaration sweep is deliberately TOLERANT (`Parser.recordScannedType`): it
	// folds a declaration in before the real parse gets to refuse it, so `userDeclaredTypeNames` holds names
	// the program is about to be rejected for writing. MEASURED: a user `enum __ManagedDirectoryError` —
	// which `requireUnreservedName` refuses with E2051 — contested `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s own declaration
	// of that name, renamed it out from under the runtime, and added a second, bogus **E4016** beside the
	// E2051 that `managed-directory.error-enum-name-is-reserved` pins. Both refusals are re-stated here
	// rather than deferred to the parse, because the contest is decided BEFORE any file is parsed.
	//
	// ⚠ **RE-PROBED, NOT PREFIXED ONCE, and against ALL THREE CLAIMANTS on the reserved space** — a user
	// declaration (E2051 forbids it, so only ever in a program already being refused), an earlier contest's
	// mint, and a stdlib declaration that spells the candidate itself. The loop terminates because each pass
	// makes the candidate strictly longer while all three sets are finite and already complete. It is
	// `reservedIfDeclared`'s loop, one namespace over.
	export function contestStdlibTypeName(name ByteArray)
		if not self.userDeclaredTypeNames.contains(name) 'uncontested'
			return
		end 'uncontested'

		if bytesStartWith(name, prefix: ReservedTypeNamePrefix) 'reservedSpace'
			return
		end 'reservedSpace'

		if isCompilerOwnedTypeName(name) 'compilerOwned'
			return
		end 'compilerOwned'

		if self.stdlibShadowRenames.contains(name) 'alreadyContested'
			return
		end 'alreadyContested'

		var renamed = "{String.from(ReservedTypeNamePrefix)}{String.from(name)}".toByteArray()
		while self.shadowRenameIsTaken(renamed) 'stillTaken'
			renamed = "{String.from(ReservedTypeNamePrefix)}{String.from(renamed)}".toByteArray()
		end 'stillTaken'
		self.stdlibShadowRenames.upsert(name, value: renamed)
	end 'contestStdlibTypeName'

	// Is this candidate reserved name already spoken for? THREE claimants, and all three matter — two names
	// must not land on ONE reserved name, which is the collision `reservedIfDeclared`'s own header warns a
	// non-reserved prefix would make silent.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE STDLIB HALF IS THE ONE NOTHING ELSE WOULD CATCH (A1s wave 2 review).** `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`
	// already declares `__`-prefixed types of its own, so a listed module declaring `X` while any listed
	// module declares `__X` would have had the mint for a shadowed `X` land ON that declaration — an E3006
	// raised inside `stdlib/`, or worse a last-wins upsert of two layouts under one key. It is unreachable
	// with today's four listed modules and is checked rather than argued, because the thing that would make
	// it reachable is ADDING A WHITELIST ENTRY, which is a one-line change made by someone reading
	// `StdlibLoader`'s collision rule and not this function.
	function shadowRenameIsTaken(candidate ByteArray) returns bool
		if self.userDeclaredTypeNames.contains(candidate) 'aUserDeclarationHoldsIt'
			return true
		end 'aUserDeclarationHoldsIt'

		if self.stdlibDeclaredTypeNames.contains(candidate) 'aStdlibDeclarationHoldsIt'
			return true
		end 'aStdlibDeclarationHoldsIt'

		return self.isMintedStdlibShadowName(candidate)
	end 'shadowRenameIsTaken'

	// Does this compile have any contest at all? The ONE gate every cost of this mechanism sits behind: for
	// the whole existing corpus it answers false, the token rewrite never runs, and the emitted bytes are
	// what they were before the mechanism existed.
	export function hasStdlibShadowRenames() returns bool
		return self.stdlibShadowRenames.count() > 0
	end 'hasStdlibShadowRenames'

	// ⭐ **IS THIS A NAME *THIS COMPILE* MINTED FOR A CONTESTED STDLIB DECLARATION?** Asked by
	// `Parser.requireUnreservedName`, which would otherwise refuse the compiler's own mint as a user's
	// reserved-prefix declaration. A linear scan of the table, which is the right shape: it is asked only
	// while a contest exists (never, for the whole existing corpus), the table holds one entry per contested
	// name, and a second index keyed the other way would be the same fact stored twice.
	export function isMintedStdlibShadowName(name ByteArray) returns bool
		for (_, minted) in self.stdlibShadowRenames 'eachMinted'
			if minted.equals(name) 'thisMint'
				return true
			end 'thisMint'
		end 'eachMinted'
		return false
	end 'isMintedStdlibShadowName'

	// ⭐ **IS THIS CALLEE ONE OF THIS COMPILE'S OWN MINTS, or a METHOD OF ONE?** `__Clock.nowMs` is what
	// `stdlib/Clock.maxon`'s own `Clock.nowMs` becomes when a user program declares `type Clock`, and the
	// reserved-CALL door (A1r) would otherwise refuse it as a user reaching for a compiler intrinsic. The
	// HEAD is what carries the mint, so the test splits the callee rather than matching a prefix — a prefix
	// test would admit `__Clockwork.tick`.
	export function isMintedStdlibShadowCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.isMintedStdlibShadowName(qualifiedCalleeHead(callee))
	end 'isMintedStdlibShadowCallee'

	// The name a STDLIB file must spell this identifier under — the renamed one if the name is contested,
	// and the name itself otherwise. THE one reader of the table, so a caller cannot half-apply the rename.
	export function stdlibShadowRenameOf(name ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		return try self.stdlibShadowRenames.get(name) otherwise name
	end 'stdlibShadowRenameOf'

	// Could `name` denote a top-level MANAGED binding — `let` or `var` — anywhere in this program? A
	// `false` is final; a `true` still has to be resolved by `constantValueOf`/`globalDeclFor`, which is
	// what applies file-private scoping and tells the two kinds apart. See `managedTopLevelNames`.
	export function namesManagedTopLevel(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.managedTopLevelNames.contains(name)
	end 'namesManagedTopLevel'

	// Fold ONE file's scanned declarations into the index. Called in SOURCE-PATH order, so the
	// index — and its hash — are a deterministic function of the program, not of the order the
	// files happened to be visited in.
	//
	// `fileTypeNames` is the interner the scan interned into (a `Parser`'s own artifact-local
	// table), so its ids are file-local. They are folded in and remapped through exactly the
	// machinery `mergeArtifact` uses for the identical job — `TypeNameInterner.foldInto` +
	// `remapMaxonReturnType`. One mechanism, two callers: a second copy of "reconcile a file's ids
	// into a bigger table" would be free to disagree with this one about what an id means.
	export function foldFile(scanned FuncReturnTypeEntryArray, aliases RangedTypeAliasArray, functionAliases FunctionTypeAliasArray, structs StructLayoutArray, enums EnumLayoutArray, genericAliasEntries GenericAliasEntryArray, innerAliasEntries InnerAliasEntryArray, methodInnerAliasParamEntries MethodInnerAliasParamEntryArray, interfaceSites InterfaceDeclSiteArray, extensionSites ExtensionDeclSiteArray, conformanceSites ConformanceClauseDeclSiteArray, scanSites DescriptorScanSiteArray, bindings TopLevelDeclArray, paramConsumes ParamConsumeEntryArray, paramReassigns ParamReassignEntryArray, globalAliasReturns GlobalAliasReturnEntryArray, paramDefaults ParamDefaultEntryArray, throwsEntries ThrowsClauseEntryArray, freeFunctionNames ByteArrayArray, moduleDirectory String, filePath FilePath, fileTypeNames TypeNameInterner, isStdlibSource bool)
		// ⭐ **THE USER HALF OF THE SHADOW CONTEST (A1s wave 2)** — recorded from the SAME declaration arrays
		// the registries below are built from, so "what the user declared" and "what the index holds" are one
		// walk. Every KIND is noted, not just the four nominal ones: the contest is asymmetric on purpose (see
		// `Queries.contestStdlibDeclarationsAgainstUserNames`), because a user's ranged `typealias ParseError`
		// takes the name from a stdlib `enum ParseError` exactly as a user `type` would.
		if not isStdlibSource 'theUsersOwnFile'
			self.noteUserDeclaredTypeNames(aliases, functionAliases: functionAliases, structs: structs, enums: enums, genericAliasEntries: genericAliasEntries, interfaceSites: interfaceSites)
		end 'theUsersOwnFile'

		for alias in aliases 'eachAlias'
			self.recordAlias(alias)
		end 'eachAlias'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE FREE-FUNCTION NAMESPACE PASS, AND IT RUNS BEFORE ANY PER-FUNCTION TABLE IS FOLDED (N1c).**
		// It does three jobs off one walk: it files each free function's directory-qualified spelling (N1b's
		// set, which a dotted callee asks through); it decides whether the bare name is now declared in more
		// than one DIRECTORY; and it CLAIMS the key this file's own declarations of a contested name will be
		// filed under.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE POSITION IS LOAD-BEARING, not tidiness**, and W78 gave it a second reason. When this file
		// is the one that makes a name contested, the INCUMBENT's sweep entries have to be moved off the bare
		// key — and they are readable there only until this file's own fold overwrites them, which is the very
		// next thing that happens. One line later and the incumbent's return type, throws clause and defaults
		// would be this file's. And every recorder below now keys through `sweepRegistrationKey`, which reads
		// exactly what this pass decides, so the contest has to be settled for this file before the first of
		// them runs.
		// Rendered ONCE per file, not once per declaration: it is a property of the file, and building it in
		// the loop below would allocate a namespace string for every free function in the program.
		let namespace = namespaceForModuleDirectory(moduleDirectory)
		for bareName in freeFunctionNames 'eachFreeFunction'
			if not namespace.isEmpty() 'namespacedFile'
				self.recordNamespaceQualifiedFunction(namespaceQualifiedName(namespace, name: bareName))
			end 'namespacedFile'

			match self.noteFreeFunctionDeclaration(bareName, moduleDirectory: moduleDirectory) 'contest'
				// Nothing to claim and nothing to move: one directory declares this name, so the bare key is
				// its own and every recorder below files there without asking.
				uncontested then continue
				// Already contested ⇒ the incumbent was moved when the SECOND directory appeared, and a third
				// has nothing left to move. This file still claims its OWN key below.
				alreadyContested then break 'contest'
				// Every entry at the bare key was written by the INCUMBENT directory's own fold, which is
				// premise `rekeyDeclarationSweepEntries` rests on — nothing else has declared the name yet.
				newlyContested(incumbentRegistrationName) then self.refileContestedFreeFunction(bareName, registrationName: incumbentRegistrationName)
			end 'contest'

			// ⭐⭐ **THIS FILE'S OWN KEY IS CLAIMED HERE, BEFORE ANY RECORDER RUNS, AND THAT ORDER IS THE POINT
			// (W78).** Every fold below asks `sweepRegistrationKey` which key a declaration of this name goes
			// under, and the answer must be the same for the file's first declaration and its fifth — so the
			// one question that can change under this file's own writes ("does another declaration already
			// hold the qualified spelling?") is asked once, here, while the answer is still about somebody
			// else. See `claimContestedRegistrationName`.
			self.claimContestedRegistrationName(bareName, registrationName: freeFunctionRegistrationName(bareName, moduleDirectory: moduleDirectory))
		end 'eachFreeFunction'

		// Inner ranged typealiases (P1.6-C) travel by NAME (a ranged alias's underlying is the `int`/
		// `float` keyword — no ids), so they fold alongside the ranged aliases, before the interner
		// reconciliation. Keyed by base-qualified name so `Wrapper.Idx` and a same-named inner alias of
		// another generic type stay distinct.
		for innerAlias in innerAliasEntries 'eachInnerAlias'
			self.recordInnerAlias(innerAlias.baseName, alias: innerAlias.alias)
		end 'eachInnerAlias'

		// Per-method inner-alias parameter info (P1.6-C) travels by NAME (member strings, no ids), so it
		// folds here too. Last-wins on a duplicate method name (a duplicate function is E3006, reported
		// elsewhere), exactly as `returnTypes` is.
		for methodEntry in methodInnerAliasParamEntries 'eachMethodInnerAlias'
			self.methodInnerAliasParams.upsert(methodEntry.methodName, value: methodEntry.info)
		end 'eachMethodInnerAlias'

		// Function aliases travel by NAME and carry no interner ids (their return type is stored as a
		// tag + a name ByteArray), so they fold here alongside the ranged aliases, before the interner
		// reconciliation below — nothing about them needs remapping (like `paramConsumes`/`throwsEntries`).
		for functionAlias in functionAliases 'eachFunctionAlias'
			self.recordFunctionTypeAlias(functionAlias)
		end 'eachFunctionAlias'

		// Interface declaration SITES (R8) carry no ids at all — a file path and a token index — so they fold
		// here with the consume facts and the throws clauses, ahead of the interner reconciliation and ahead of
		// the identity-remap early return below, which no by-name fold may sit after. They are only
		// ACCUMULATED: the declarations behind them are read once the last file has folded, because a
		// requirement's rendered type depends on the whole program (see `InterfaceDeclSite`) — the same
		// "noted here, performed later" split `noteWellKnownInstanceNeeds` uses.
		for site in interfaceSites 'eachInterfaceSite'
			self.interfaceDeclSites.push(site)
		end 'eachInterfaceSite'

		// EXTENSION declaration sites ride beside the interface ones, carry no ids for the same reason, and
		// are ACCUMULATED for a sharper version of the same one: an extension's methods belong to conformers
		// that may be declared in a file this sweep has not reached. See `ExtensionDeclSite`.
		for site in extensionSites 'eachExtensionSite'
			self.extensionDeclSites.push(site)
		end 'eachExtensionSite'

		// CONFORMANCE CLAUSE sites ride beside both, carry no ids for the same reason, and are ACCUMULATED
		// for a reason that is the INTERFACE fold's own output: a clause's extent depends on an interface
		// arity that pass computes. See `ConformanceClauseDeclSite`.
		for site in conformanceSites 'eachConformanceClauseSite'
			self.conformanceClauseSites.push(site)
		end 'eachConformanceClauseSite'

		// The generic-type method-list sites the descriptor-need fixpoint walks — see `DescriptorScanSite`.
		// A program that declares no generic type contributes none, which is what leaves
		// `Queries.foldDescriptorNeeds` an empty-array test.
		for site in scanSites 'eachDescriptorScanSite'
			self.descriptorScanSites.push(site)
		end 'eachDescriptorScanSite'

		// Bindings — `let` and `var` alike — are RECORDED here and EVALUATED later
		// (`evaluateInitializers`), and the gap between the two is the whole reason a forward
		// reference works — see that function.
		for binding in bindings 'eachBinding'
			self.recordDecl(binding)
		end 'eachBinding'

		let remap = self.typeNames.foldInto(fileTypeNames)

		// A declared struct's field types are reconciled IN PLACE, before the record loop below, so
		// there is ONE recording path rather than a mapped and an unmapped one. That is the opposite
		// shape from the signatures below, and the difference is what each fold owns: a
		// `MaxonReturnType` is a union VALUE, so remapping it can only mean constructing a new one
		// (hence the two loops, and the identity fast path that skips an allocation per declared
		// function); a `StructLayout` is a heap `type` this sweep just built and still owns, so its
		// field list is simply rewritten. `remapStructLayoutFieldTypes` is the single mechanism
		// `mergeArtifact` folds the same records with.
		// A declared type's id-bearing member types — a struct's field types and a union/enum's
		// PAYLOAD field types (`walk(dir Direction)`) — are reconciled IN PLACE before the record
		// loops below, so there is ONE recording path rather than a mapped and an unmapped one. A
		// payload-free enum carries no ids, so its loop body rewrites nothing; the record loop still
		// runs unconditionally (the ids are irrelevant to WHETHER a type is declared, only to what its
		// members mean).
		if not remap.isIdentity 'typeMemberIdsMoved'
			for layout in structs 'eachMovedStruct'
				remapStructLayoutFieldTypes(layout, remap: remap)
			end 'eachMovedStruct'
			for enumLayout in enums 'eachMovedEnum'
				remapEnumLayoutPayloadTypes(enumLayout, remap: remap)
			end 'eachMovedEnum'
		end 'typeMemberIdsMoved'

		// The fold already knows the file's provenance, so the corpus/user split a compiler-owned type name
		// turns on (`Project.declarationContestsACompilerOwnedName`) is DERIVED here once per file rather than
		// per declaration — and derived through the one predicate that owns it, never re-spelled
		// (`livesUnderStdlibDirectory`). Both declaration loops below read it, so it precedes both.
		let declaredByTheCorpus = livesUnderStdlibDirectory(self, isStdlibSource: isStdlibSource)
		for layout in structs 'eachStruct'
			self.recordStruct(layout, declaredByTheCorpus: declaredByTheCorpus)
		end 'eachStruct'

		for enumLayout in enums 'eachEnum'
			self.recordEnum(enumLayout, declaredByTheCorpus: declaredByTheCorpus)
		end 'eachEnum'

		// Generic-instance typealiases (P1.6-A). Folded BEFORE the return-type split below (which returns
		// early on the identity fast path), so they are recorded whether or not ids moved. Each entry's
		// args are minted against the file's interner, so `recordGenericAlias` remaps them into this
		// index's interner (`remap`) before interning the instance — exactly as struct field types above.
		for genericAlias in genericAliasEntries 'eachGenericAlias'
			self.recordGenericAlias(genericAlias, remap: remap)
		end 'eachGenericAlias'

		// The remap is loop-INVARIANT, so the identity test is hoisted OUT of the per-declaration
		// loop rather than asked once per function — the same shape `mergeArtifact` uses for the same
		// fold. And the identity case is not merely a shortcut, it avoids an ALLOCATION per declared
		// function in the program: `remapMaxonReturnType` CONSTRUCTS a `MaxonReturnType`, and a Maxon
		// union value is a heap object (every case), so remapping ids that did not move would allocate
		// a fresh copy of the object it was handed, for nothing.
		//
		// ⚠ Written as two loops over one `record`, NOT as `x if remap.isIdentity else remap(x)`. The
		// ternary form LEAKS under the C# bootstrap — its arms mix a BORROWED field read with a
		// freshly-OWNED value, and the conditional does not reconcile the two ownerships (measured:
		// `MM leak: 7 allocation(s) remain` on every build, exit 101). Recorded in OPEN.md.
		if remap.isIdentity 'idsUnchanged'
			for entry in scanned 'eachUnmapped'
				self.record(entry.name, moduleDirectory: moduleDirectory, returnType: entry.returnType, declaredSurface: entry.declaredSurface, visibility: entry.visibility, declFilePath: filePath, takesReceiver: entry.takesReceiver, opaqueParams: entry.opaqueParams)
			end 'eachUnmapped'
		end 'idsUnchanged' else 'idsMoved'
			for entry in scanned 'eachRemapped'
				self.record(entry.name, moduleDirectory: moduleDirectory, returnType: remapMaxonReturnType(entry.returnType, remap: remap), declaredSurface: entry.declaredSurface, visibility: entry.visibility, declFilePath: filePath, takesReceiver: entry.takesReceiver, opaqueParams: entry.opaqueParams)
			end 'eachRemapped'
		end 'idsMoved'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE FOUR BY-NAME FOLDS, AND THEY RUN *AFTER* THE DECLARATION LOOP ABOVE (W75).** Each keys
		// through `sweepRegistrationKey`, so a declaration's consume facts, by-reference columns, parameter
		// defaults and `throws` clause land on the same key its return type and declaration site did — which
		// is the key the parser asks with, and the key its tallies are counted under.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE POSITION IS THE `static`/INSTANCE PAIR'S, and it is why these four moved.** A pair is
		// DISCOVERED by `noteStaticInstanceDeclaration`, inside `record`: the second member to fold is the
		// first moment anything in the program can see both. Folded ahead of that loop, as they were, these
		// four arrived before the contest existed and had no key to be told apart by — both members' facts
		// piled onto the one source name and the map kept whichever folded last. MEASURED before W75:
		// `T.m()` against a `static m(a Num = 7)` beside an instance `m(b Num)` asked the defaults registry
		// for `T.m#__static`, found nothing to fill, and was refused
		// `E3036: 'T.m#__static' expects 1 argument(s) but 0 were provided` — a symbol no source wrote,
		// blamed on a call the author got right.
		//
		// They carry no interner ids (names, numbers, a bare enum name), so nothing about them needs
		// remapping and the position costs them nothing else.
		for entry in paramConsumes 'eachConsume'
			self.recordParamConsume(self.sweepRegistrationKey(entry.name, moduleDirectory: moduleDirectory, takesReceiver: entry.takesReceiver), info: entry.info)
		end 'eachConsume'

		// This loop is also where the program-wide by-reference gate is raised (`recordParamReassign`), and
		// every file passes through it before any body is parsed — which is what makes the gate complete at
		// the first read.
		for entry in paramReassigns 'eachByRef'
			self.recordParamReassign(self.sweepRegistrationKey(entry.name, moduleDirectory: moduleDirectory, takesReceiver: entry.takesReceiver), info: entry.info)
		end 'eachByRef'

		// W117's raw material rides the same loop and the same key for the by-reference columns' reason: it is
		// another per-declaration fact a CALL SITE reads about a callee it cannot see.
		//
		// ⚡ **THE DECLARING FILE IS FOLDED HERE, ONCE, RATHER THAN INSIDE THE LOOP** — see
		// `GlobalAliasReturnInfo.declFilePath` for why the file has to ride the hash at all. Every entry in this
		// array was swept by ONE `Parser` off ONE file, so `entry.info.declFilePath` is `filePath` for all of
		// them; folding it per entry meant a `String.toByteArray()` — a `managed.slice` COPY plus a
		// `ByteArray.init` — per DECLARATION, for a fact that changes per FILE. The hash still distinguishes two
		// files stating identical bodies, which is the whole reason the file rides.
		if globalAliasReturns.count() > 0 'thisFileStatesOne'
			self.hash = mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeGlobalAliasReturn), bytes: filePath.toString().toByteArray())
		end 'thisFileStatesOne'

		for entry in globalAliasReturns 'eachGlobalAliasReturn'
			self.recordGlobalAliasReturn(self.sweepRegistrationKey(entry.name, moduleDirectory: moduleDirectory, takesReceiver: entry.takesReceiver), info: entry.info)
		end 'eachGlobalAliasReturn'

		for entry in paramDefaults 'eachParamDefault'
			self.recordParamDefaults(self.sweepRegistrationKey(entry.name, moduleDirectory: moduleDirectory, takesReceiver: entry.takesReceiver), info: entry.info)
		end 'eachParamDefault'

		for entry in throwsEntries 'eachThrows'
			self.recordThrows(self.sweepRegistrationKey(entry.name, moduleDirectory: moduleDirectory, takesReceiver: entry.takesReceiver), clause: entry.clause)
		end 'eachThrows'

		// ⭐⭐ **THIS FILE'S OWN RE-KEYED DECLARATIONS: THE ONE THING THE LOOPS ABOVE COULD NOT KEY FOR
		// THEMSELVES — THE NAME OF A SYNTHESIZED DEFAULT HELPER.**
		//
		// Everything else is already keyed: every recorder above files under `sweepRegistrationKey`'s answer.
		// A helper is different because its NAME was minted by the token SCAN, from the name the source wrote,
		// a pass before any contest was known — two directories each declaring a defaulting `pick` both minted
		// `__paramDefault#pick#0`, MEASURED as
		// `E3006: duplicate definition of function '__paramDefault#pick#0'` against a program the rule accepts,
		// and a `static`/instance pair's two members mint one `__paramDefault#T.m#0` between them.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT HAS TO BE HERE, AFTER THE `record` LOOP**, because the rename moves the helper's OWN swept
		// signature with it and that signature is written by that loop.
		//
		// It walks `paramDefaults` rather than the file's free-function names because BOTH contests re-key a
		// helper and only this array knows which declarations have one; a program that declares no default
		// does not enter the loop at all.
		for entry in paramDefaults 'eachDeclarationThatDefaultsSomething'
			let key = self.sweepRegistrationKey(entry.name, moduleDirectory: moduleDirectory, takesReceiver: entry.takesReceiver)
			if key.equals(entry.name) 'theHelperKeepsTheNameTheScanMintedForIt'
				continue
			end 'theHelperKeepsTheNameTheScanMintedForIt'

			self.renameParamDefaultHelpers(entry.info, bareName: entry.name, registrationName: key)
		end 'eachDeclarationThatDefaultsSomething'
	end 'foldFile'

	// Add one declaration to the index AND fold it into the hash. The one write, so the table and the
	// hash that keys every parse memo on it can never disagree about what the index contains.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE BUFFER-SPELLING WORD IS WRITTEN HERE, WITH THE RETURN TYPE IT DESCRIBES (A2j / A2m)** — one
	// line, so the table and the claim beside it are written from one place about one declaration.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **A DECLARATION'S SURFACE IS OR-ED IN AND NEVER OVERWRITTEN, AND THAT ASYMMETRY IS LOAD-BEARING
	// RATHER THAN A SAVING.** It makes the table answer "does ANY declaration of this name name the buffer,
	// and where?", which is what `Parser.requireOverloadableName` needs to refuse an overload set the sweep
	// cannot answer for — the same "absence is the answer" shape `recordThrows` and `recordParamConsume` rest
	// on, and for their reason. MEASURED before the refusal existed, with the mark written in both directions
	// instead: an overload set `make() returns __ManagedMemory` beside `make(n Int) returns ByteArray` took the
	// LAST declaration's answer for BOTH members, so one order refused `make().length()` (the member that does
	// return the buffer) and the other refused `make(1).count()` (the member that does not). A wrong SURFACE
	// either way, and one no later pass can repair: `resolveOverloadedCalls` rebinds the callee long after the
	// parser has decided which roster the result carries. ⇒ record the positive, and refuse the contested name.
	//
	// So `declaredReturnSurface` is exact for every name that reaches a call: a name with no entry names the
	// buffer in no declaration anywhere, and a name with one is either unique or already refused.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE KEY IS NOT ALWAYS `name`: A `static` MEMBER WHOSE NAME AN INSTANCE MEMBER ALSO WEARS IS FILED
	// UNDER ITS OWN** — see `noteStaticInstanceDeclaration`, which is asked first because every write below has
	// to land on one key.
	function record(name ByteArray, moduleDirectory String, returnType MaxonReturnType, declaredSurface DeclaredSurface, visibility Visibility, declFilePath FilePath, takesReceiver bool, opaqueParams OpaqueParamInfo)
		let contested = self.isContestedFreeFunction(name)
		self.noteStaticInstanceDeclaration(name, takesReceiver: takesReceiver)
		let key = self.sweepRegistrationKey(name, moduleDirectory: moduleDirectory, takesReceiver: takesReceiver)
		// ⭐⭐ **THE ARRIVAL TEST IS ASKED OF TWO DIFFERENT MAPS, AND THE SPLIT IS THE RESIDUE'S (W78).** For
		// every ordinary key `returnTypes` IS the arrival counter: each declaration reaches here exactly once
		// and writes it, so an entry already present is another declaration's. A CONTESTED free function's
		// BARE key is the one place that is false — `refileContestedFreeFunction` leaves the incumbent's return
		// type and declaration site behind there so a bare call to the name reaches E3095 rather than a panic,
		// and that residue is indistinguishable from a declaration of the key a ROOT contestant files under.
		// The tally is therefore its own arrival marker for a contested name, materialized at the contest
		// (`materializeDeclarationCount`) so both conventions describe the same number.
		let secondDeclaration = self.keyAlreadyHoldsADeclaration(key, contested: contested)
		// ⭐ **THE ARRIVAL COUNT, KEPT ONLY ONCE A NAME HAS MORE THAN ONE DECLARATION (W74)** — the half of
		// `paramDefaultsCollide`'s comparison that a declaration publishing NO defaults contributes, and the
		// only place in the sweep that sees one. See `FunctionDeclarationCountMap`.
		if secondDeclaration 'anotherDeclarationOfThisName'
			self.noteRepeatedDeclaration(self.funcDeclarationCounts, name: key)
		end 'anotherDeclarationOfThisName' else 'theFirstDeclarationUnderThisKey'
			// A contested key's tally is the only record that it has been reached at all, so the FIRST
			// declaration under it must write the `1` the sparse convention would otherwise leave implicit.
			self.materializeDeclarationCount(key, contested: contested)
		end 'theFirstDeclarationUnderThisKey'
		self.returnTypes.upsert(key, value: returnType)
		if not surfaceIsPlain(declaredSurface) 'namesTheBufferAlias'
			self.declaredReturnSurfaces.upsert(key, value: declaredSurface)
		end 'namesTheBufferAlias'
		if returnTypeNamesAType(returnType) 'aNameThisFileMustBeAbleToResolveLaterOn'
			self.funcReturnDeclFiles.upsert(key, value: declFilePath)
		end 'aNameThisFileMustBeAbleToResolveLaterOn'
		self.funcDeclSites.upsert(key, value: FunctionDeclSite.create(visibility, declFilePath: declFilePath, takesReceiver: takesReceiver))
		// ⭐ **THE OPAQUE-PARAMETER POSITIONS ARE WRITTEN HERE, WITH THE DECLARATION THEY DESCRIBE (ARRR)** —
		// the same one-act discipline `declaredSurface`, `visibility` and `takesReceiver` ride on, and for
		// their reason: they are facts about ONE declaration, and a second writer is a second chance for them
		// to come to describe two.
		//
		// ⚠ IT IS KEYED BY `key` LIKE EVERY OTHER WRITE HERE, so a re-key carries it by construction and there is
		// nothing for the contest registries to strand — which was already true of the free-function contest for
		// a reason of its own: a bare type-parameter parameter can only be declared inside a generic type's
		// body, and a free function is by definition outside every one, so this map never holds an entry a
		// NAMESPACE contest could be about.
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **W78 CLAIMED THIS SET'S READER COULD NOT BE REACHED BY ANY PROGRAM THAT COMPILES. IT IS
		// REACHED BY `stdlib/Array.maxon` ON EVERY COMPILE (found at review).** The argument was that the
		// three ways an opaque parameter can be USED each close the door first — STORING one makes the set a
		// consuming overload (`Parser.requireOverloadableName`'s first refusal), FORWARDING one to a storing
		// sibling reaches the same refusal, RETURNING one is refused for want of a layout descriptor — and
		// that leaving it unused is E3012. Two things are wrong with it. A fourth use was missed: `_ = item`
		// DISCARDS the parameter, silencing E3012 while storing, forwarding and returning nothing. And the
		// premise was never tested against the stdlib, where `Array`'s conditional extension declares
		// `contains(element Element)` beside `contains(sequence ElementArray)` and puts `Array.contains` in
		// this set for every program in the tree.
		//
		// ⇒ **WHAT IT COSTS IS A MISSING RANGE CHECK, MEASURED — see `opaqueParamsOf`**, which is where the
		// decline is taken and where the cure is stated. It is a standing gap, not this rung's.
		if secondDeclaration and not self.storedOpaqueParams(key).matches(opaqueParams) 'twoDeclarationsDisagree'
			self.contestedOpaqueParams.insert(key)
		end 'twoDeclarationsDisagree'
		if opaqueParams.declaresATypeParameterParam() 'takesABareTypeParameter'
			self.funcOpaqueParams.upsert(key, value: opaqueParams)
		end 'takesABareTypeParameter'

		// The KEY is mixed and not the source name, which is what makes a static/instance pair move the hash
		// without a discriminator of its own: the static's declaration is folded under a different key the
		// moment the pair exists, so a program that starts or stops being one cannot leave a reader's parse
		// memo valid. (`DeclShapeContestedExtensionMethod` needs one precisely because THAT contest is minted by
		// the parser, leaving nothing here to move.)
		self.hash = self.mixDeclaration(self.hash, name: key, returnType: returnType, declaredSurface: declaredSurface, visibility: visibility, takesReceiver: takesReceiver, opaqueParams: opaqueParams)
	end 'record'

	// ⭐⭐ **NOTE ONE DECLARATION AGAINST THE `static`/INSTANCE CONTEST, AND ANSWER THE KEY IT BELONGS UNDER** —
	// the bare source name for an instance member and for every uncontested declaration in the program, the
	// STATIC twin for a `static` member an instance member of the same type shares a name with. See
	// `staticInstanceContests` for what the contest means; the KEY itself is `memberRegistrationKey`'s, asked
	// here rather than composed, so this routine and the parser's two doors cannot spell one symbol three ways.
	//
	// **THE CONTEST IS DETECTED AT THE SECOND OF THE TWO DECLARATIONS TO FOLD**, because that is the first
	// moment anything in the program can see both — so the two ORDERS need two different things done, and
	// exactly one of them is a refile:
	//
	//   • **The STATIC is the NEWCOMER** ⇒ nothing to move. It is handed its own key and `record` writes all
	//     four of the facts it owns under that key on the next line, and its BY-NAME facts fold afterwards
	//     under the key this contest has just given them.
	//   • **The STATIC is the INCUMBENT** ⇒ its facts are sitting on the bare key, written by a fold that could
	//     not yet know, and this instance's own writes are about to overwrite them. They move first. What may
	//     travels is `rekeyDeclarationSweepEntries`' subject — everything, because the by-name folds have not
	//     run for this file yet and every entry at the source name was written by the incumbent.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ASYMMETRY IS ALSO WHAT KEEPS `record`'s `secondDeclaration` HONEST.** That flag reads "does this
	// key already hold a declaration?" off `returnTypes`, and a copy into the static key would have made it
	// answer yes about a key no declaration had reached — mis-marking the static as an opaque-parameter contest
	// with itself.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE INCUMBENT IS READ OFF `funcDeclSites`, WHICH IS THE ONE HOME OF "does this declaration take a
	// receiver?"** (`FuncSignature.takesReceiver` states the same fact for a PARSED signature). A name with no
	// site is the first declaration of it and has nothing to contest.
	//
	// ⚠ **A THIRD DECLARATION OF THE NAME NEEDS NO SPECIAL CASE**: two members of the SAME kind are a duplicate
	// definition, which `commitFuncSignatures` reports with both positions — the pair's own two keys are already
	// distinct, so the third simply collides with whichever of them it belongs to.
	//
	// ⚠ **NO HASH MIXING HERE**, for `refileContestedFreeFunction`'s reason: every fact the copy moves was
	// already mixed under the bare name by the write that made it, and the key change itself rides `record`'s
	// own `mixDeclaration` (see there).
	function noteStaticInstanceDeclaration(name ByteArray, takesReceiver bool)
		let incumbent = try self.funcDeclSites.get(name) otherwise return
		if incumbent.takesReceiver == takesReceiver 'oneKindOfMemberOnly'
			return
		end 'oneKindOfMemberOnly'

		// Inserted before the key is derived, because `memberRegistrationKey` reads exactly this set — so this
		// routine composes the contest and the mint through the ONE reader that does, rather than spelling the
		// composition a second time here. `record` re-asks through `sweepRegistrationKey` on the next line and
		// gets the newcomer's own key from it, which is why nothing is returned.
		self.staticInstanceContests.insert(name)
		if not takesReceiver 'theStaticIsTheNewcomerAndOwnsItsOwnKeyOutright'
			return
		end 'theStaticIsTheNewcomerAndOwnsItsOwnKeyOutright'

		self.rekeyDeclarationSweepEntries(name, to: self.memberRegistrationKey(name, takesReceiver: false))
		// ⚠ **AND THE SOURCE KEY IS EMPTIED OUTRIGHT, WHICH THE FREE-FUNCTION CALLER DELIBERATELY DOES NOT
		// DO.** There the bare name keeps a residue so a bare call to a contested name reaches E3095; here the
		// source name is not a leftover but the INSTANCE member's own key, and `record` is about to write every
		// one of these four under it on the next line. Left in place, the static's return type would still be
		// standing there for the instant between — which `record`'s own arrival test reads as "another
		// declaration of this key", counting the instance as the second `T.m` and refusing its default.
		self.clearRecordOwnedSweepEntries(name)
	end 'noteStaticInstanceDeclaration'

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE KEY A MEMBER OF `sourceName` WITH THIS RECEIVER-PRESENCE IS FILED UNDER — AND THEREFORE THE
	// NAME A CALL TO IT MUST ASK FOR. THE ONE COMPOSITION OF THE CONTEST AND THE MINT.**
	//
	// Three sites need it and every one of them must produce the SAME BYTES: this index's own `record` (the
	// sweep key), `Parser.overloadRegistrationNameFor` (the base a declaration claims, and so the emitted
	// symbol), and `Parser.parseQualifiedCall` (the callee a static call carries). Nothing would make three
	// spellings of `contest? ⇒ static key : source key` agree, and divergence is not a compile error — it is a
	// callee whose sweep facts read back from a key nothing wrote: a wrong return type at a parsed call, or a
	// backend `call to unknown function` with no position. That is the failure
	// `Project.paramDefaultHelperName`'s header records from history ("A name derived in one place cannot
	// disagree with itself"), and it is why the composition lives here rather than at each of the three.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN INSTANCE MEMBER ALWAYS KEEPS THE SOURCE NAME**, contest or not — which is what leaves every
	// instance call door in the parser untouched, and what makes the static side the only one that has to ask.
	// A `Type.m(…)` call therefore asks with `takesReceiver: false`, and that argument is the reason rather
	// than a convention: there is no receiver at a type-qualified call.
	//
	// It costs one `Set.contains` on a set that is EMPTY for every program in this tree (the shape did not
	// compile before this rung), and that returns at its `capacity == 0` test before hashing — so the two asks
	// asks a declaration makes are free rather than merely cheap.
	export function memberRegistrationKey(sourceName ByteArray, takesReceiver bool) returns ByteArray
		if takesReceiver or not self.isStaticInstanceContest(sourceName) 'theSourceNamesOwnKey'
			return sourceName
		end 'theSourceNamesOwnKey'

		return staticMemberRegistrationName(sourceName)
	end 'memberRegistrationKey'

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE KEY THE SWEEP FILES A DECLARATION UNDER — AND THEREFORE THE KEY ITS TALLIES ARE KEPT UNDER
	// (W78). THE SECOND HALF OF `memberRegistrationKey`'s COMPOSITION, AND THE LAST ONE.**
	//
	// A declaration is re-keyed by exactly two contests, and this routine is where they meet: a `static`
	// member whose name an instance member of its type also wears (`memberRegistrationKey`, above) and a free
	// function whose bare name a second DIRECTORY declares (`freeFunctionRegistrationName`, N1c). Every
	// recorder in the fold routes through here, so a declaration's return type, its `throws` clause, its
	// parameter defaults and the tally that says how many declarations wear the key all land on ONE key —
	// the same key the parser asks with (`Parser.declaredMethodName` mints it for the free-function contest,
	// `overloadRegistrationNameFor` for the pair).
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THEY WERE FILED UNDER TWO KEYS UNTIL W78, AND THE COST WAS A SILENT WRONG ANSWER.** The entries
	// moved onto `alpha.pick` while the disagreement verdict and both counts stayed on the bare `pick`, so
	// every gate keyed on the registration name read maps nothing had written for it and answered "these
	// declarations agree" about a name whose declarations do not. MEASURED: `pick(a Num)` beside
	// `pick(a Num, b Num = 5)` in `alpha/` with a `beta/pick` contesting the bare name compiled and answered
	// **210** — the DEFAULTED member — where the oracle answers 2 and where the identical program without
	// `beta/` is refused by W74's own gate.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TWO CONTESTS CANNOT BOTH APPLY, WHICH IS WHY THE COMPOSITION IS A SEQUENCE AND NOT A CHOICE.**
	// A pair's name is a member spelling (`T.m`, always dotted) and a free function's is a bare identifier,
	// so `isContestedFreeFunction` is false for every name `memberRegistrationKey` moves and vice versa.
	//
	// It costs one `Map.contains` on a map that is EMPTY for every program that declares no name in two
	// directories, which returns at its `capacity == 0` test before hashing.
	function sweepRegistrationKey(sourceName ByteArray, moduleDirectory String, takesReceiver bool) returns ByteArray
		if self.isContestedFreeFunction(sourceName) 'moreThanOneDirectoryDeclaresThisName'
			// Minted ONCE: the join allocates, and this is asked per declaration and per by-name entry.
			let qualified = freeFunctionRegistrationName(sourceName, moduleDirectory: moduleDirectory)
			return qualified if self.contestedKeyIsTakeable(qualified) else sourceName
		end 'moreThanOneDirectoryDeclaresThisName'

		return self.memberRegistrationKey(sourceName, takesReceiver: takesReceiver)
	end 'sweepRegistrationKey'

	// ⭐ **DO A `static` MEMBER AND AN INSTANCE MEMBER OF ONE TYPE SHARE THIS SOURCE NAME?** The bare flag, for
	// the readers that need the CONTEST itself rather than the key it implies: `Parser.overloadRegistrationNameFor`
	// asks it to put a pair member through the overload refusals, and `ParseStaging.duplicateFunctionMessage` to
	// explain a collision on the minted spelling. A reader that wants a NAME asks `memberRegistrationKey`.
	export function isStaticInstanceContest(sourceName ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.staticInstanceContests.contains(sourceName)
	end 'isStaticInstanceContest'

	// The opaque record already stored for `name`, or the shared "declares none" — split out for
	// `storedReturnSurface`'s reason: `Map.get` is a `try` and a name with no entry is the common case
	// rather than an error.
	function storedOpaqueParams(name ByteArray) returns OpaqueParamInfo
		return try self.funcOpaqueParams.get(name) otherwise sharedNoOpaqueParams
	end 'storedOpaqueParams'

	// ⭐ **WHICH OF `callee`'s PARAMETERS TAKE A BARE TYPE-PARAMETER ARGUMENT (ARRR)** — the door
	// `Parser.recordOpaqueArgRangeChecks` asks once it knows the call has a concrete generic-instance
	// receiver. A callee with no entry reads back the shared "declares none", which every predicate on the
	// record answers negatively for, so the caller needs no second presence test.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **A CONTESTED NAME ANSWERS "NONE", AND THAT IS A MEASURED HOLE, NOT A REFUSAL (found at W78's
	// review).** The sweep keys by bare name, so an overload set whose members disagree about their opaque
	// parameters has no answer this index can give — and giving one anyway builds a range guard for the member
	// the call did not resolve to (`OpaqueParamInfo.matches` carries that measured wrong answer). But the
	// caller reads the decline as *"this callee guards nothing"* and emits NO guard, which is this family's
	// one failure mode that is silent rather than loud.
	//
	// ⛔ **MEASURED (W78 review): a second declaration of an unrelated overload DELETES a range check on the
	// first.** A `Holder uses Element` declaring `add(item Element)` — whose body pushes `item` into an
	// `Array with Element` field — instantiated `Holder with Small` where `Small = int(0 to 10)` and handed a
	// runtime 100: with `add` declared once the program panics
	// `Range check failed: value outside typealias 'Small'`; add a `add(flag bool)` beside it and the same
	// program RUNS, storing 100 in a slot whose declared range is 0 to 10. Removing this arm alone does not
	// cure it — the guard then comes from whichever declaration the sweep recorded last, which is the wrong
	// guard rather than a missing one.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS LIVE IN THIS TREE, on `Array.contains`** (`stdlib/Array.maxon`), whose conditional extension
	// declares `contains(element Element)` beside `contains(sequence ElementArray)` — slot 0 opaque in one and
	// concrete in the other. So this arm is taken on every compile, and it is NOT the unreachable check W78's
	// `record` header first claimed it was.
	//
	// ⇒ **THE CURE IS PER-MEMBER OPAQUE COLUMNS, OR RECORDING THE GUARD AFTER OVERLOAD RESOLUTION — a rung,
	// and not a refusal.** A blanket refusal at `Parser.requireOverloadableName`, on the terms the `throws`
	// and consume refusals use, was written and MEASURED to reject `stdlib/Array.maxon:568` — the stdlib
	// itself relies on the set this index cannot answer for.
	export function opaqueParamsOf(callee ByteArray) returns OpaqueParamInfo
		if self.contestedOpaqueParams.contains(callee) 'twoDeclarationsDisagree'
			return sharedNoOpaqueParams
		end 'twoDeclarationsDisagree'

		return self.storedOpaqueParams(callee)
	end 'opaqueParamsOf'

	// The surface already recorded for `name`, or the plain one. Split out because `Map.get` is a `try` and a
	// name with no entry is the common case rather than an error.
	function storedReturnSurface(name ByteArray) returns DeclaredSurface
		return try self.declaredReturnSurfaces.get(name) otherwise sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface
	end 'storedReturnSurface'

	// ⭐ **WHERE DOES A CALL TO `callee` NAME THE `__ManagedMemory` BUFFER (A2j / A2m)?** The door
	// `Parser.emitCall` asks of every direct, method and static call, so a result whose callee DECLARED the
	// alias — outright, or at a tuple slot, or as an array element — is marked at the one site that mints it.
	// It must be whole-program: the callee may be declared in another file, and the SPELLING is gone from
	// every type by the time the call is parsed.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN ALIAS SPELLING'S BITS ARE DERIVED HERE RATHER THAN TAKEN FROM THE STORE**, because the SWEEP that
	// captured this declaration ran while the alias registries were still being built — `returns BufArray`
	// cannot be asked "is `BufArray` an array of buffers?", nor `returns ByteBuffer` "is `ByteBuffer` the
	// buffer itself?" (W49), at the moment it is read. What the sweep DOES preserve is the alias NAME, inside
	// the recorded return type, and by the time anything asks this question every file is folded. The stored
	// bits are OR-ed in rather than replaced, which is what covers the other file order. See
	// `derivedAliasSurfaceBits`.
	//
	// A callee with no entry and a non-alias return type costs one `Map.contains` on an empty map plus a tag
	// test per call — the safe default `funcThrows`/`funcParamConsumes` rest on. It is also the question
	// `Parser.requireOverloadableName` asks to refuse an overload set this index cannot answer for.
	export function declaredReturnSurface(callee ByteArray) returns DeclaredSurface
		let stored = self.storedReturnSurface(callee)
		if let declared = try self.returnTypes.get(callee) 'declaresAReturnType'
			return match declared 'r'
				void gives stored
				value(inner) gives self.surfaceWithDerivedAliasBits(stored, declared: inner)
			end 'r'
		end 'declaresAReturnType'

		return stored
	end 'declaredReturnSurface'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE FOLD OF A STORED SURFACE WITH THE SPELLING BITS THAT MUST BE DERIVED.** Every declared
	// position whose surface the SWEEP wrote asks it — a struct FIELD (`fieldSurfaceOf`), a declared RETURN
	// (above), and a union PAYLOAD (`payloadSurfaceOf`) — because NEITHER spelling bit is a fact that sweep
	// could answer; see `derivedAliasSurfaceBits` for why. It was written out at the first two doors, and
	// BATCH22's third would have been a third copy of one rule.
	function surfaceWithDerivedAliasBits(stored DeclaredSurface, declared MaxonType) returns DeclaredSurface
		let derived = self.derivedAliasSurfaceBits(declared)
		if derived == 0 'namesNoBufferAlias'
			return stored
		end 'namesNoBufferAlias'

		return surfaceWithRootBits(stored, bits: derived)
	end 'surfaceWithDerivedAliasBits'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH SPELLING BITS DOES THIS DECLARED POSITION'S ALIAS NAME CARRY (A2m + W49)?** THE one
	// derivation, reached by every SWEPT position through `surfaceWithDerivedAliasBits` — the struct FIELD,
	// the RETURN clause and (BATCH22) the union PAYLOAD — so no two of them can come to disagree about what
	// `var bufs as BufArray` and `var buf as ByteBuffer` mean.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **NEITHER BIT IS A FACT THE SWEEP THAT RECORDED THE POSITION COULD ANSWER, AND THAT IS *ONE* CAUSE
	// RATHER THAN TWO (W49).** Both registries are written by `recordGenericAlias` on ADJACENT LINES, and
	// `recordGenericAlias` is called from `foldFile` — i.e. after the whole file has been swept. So a position
	// spelled with an alias its OWN file declares is read while both sets are still empty, whichever bit it
	// wanted. A2m saw this for the element bit and derived it here; W49 MEASURED the identical inversion for
	// the outright one — `var buf as ByteBuffer` refused `length`, the buffer roster's FIRST member, seven
	// lines below `typealias ByteBuffer = __ManagedMemory with Byte` — and it joins this derivation rather
	// than acquiring a second mechanism free to disagree with its twin.
	//
	// It reads the `named` the SWEEP recorded and not a resolved type, and that is the whole mechanism: an
	// alias-typed position arrives as a bare `named` carrying the name (the very property `declaredSlotType`'s
	// re-tags exist to undo). A position that is ALREADY a `genericInstance` was written where the alias was
	// registered, so `parseTypeReference` folded the spelling away and there is nothing left in it to ask —
	// which is correct, because `Array with __ManagedMemory` and `Array with ByteArray` are one instance,
	// exactly as `__ManagedMemory with Byte` and `ByteArray` are.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AND SINCE W49 IT IS THE SOLE CARRIER FOR AN ALIAS SPELLING, NOT A COMPLEMENTARY HALF.** It was
	// written as one half of a pair with the sweep's token test, on the premise that a swept alias-typed
	// position is sometimes already a `genericInstance` — which A3e made false when it gated
	// `parseTypeReference`'s alias arms on `allFilesFolded`. `Queries.foldOneFileInto` folds EVERY file before
	// that flag is set, so a swept alias-typed position keeps its bare `named` in every file order, and this
	// answers for it in every file order. The sweep's own registry read was therefore redundant AND wrong —
	// wrong because it consulted a whole-program set for a FILE-SCOPED name — and it is gone
	// (`Parser.spellingDenotesBufferSurface`). What the sweep still stores is the LITERAL `__ManagedMemory`
	// spelling, which is a token fact needing no registry, and the tuple-slot mask.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT NEEDS NO READER FILE, AND THAT IS A PROPERTY OF ITS INPUT RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.** A name
	// reaching here is either uncontested — one declaration, so every file means the same one — or has already
	// been rewritten to its per-instance MINT by `resolveRecordedGenericAliasTypes`, and a mint is never itself
	// contested. The file-scoping this cannot do is therefore never asked of it; the door that IS asked is
	// `readerScopedAliasName`.
	//
	// ⚠ The name is asked exactly as RECORDED, which is BASE-QUALIFIED for an inner alias
	// (`Character.ByteMemory`): `parseTypeReference`'s inner-alias arm and `qualifiedInnerGenericAlias` build
	// that one string between them, so this needs no second key and no knowledge that inner aliases exist.
	function derivedAliasSurfaceBits(declared MaxonType) returns DeclaredSurfaceNode
		if maxonTypeTag(declared) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notANamedAlias'
			return 0 as DeclaredSurfaceNode
		end 'notANamedAlias'

		return self.bufferAliasSurfaceBits(self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(declared)))
	end 'derivedAliasSurfaceBits'

	// ⭐ **WHICH SPELLING BITS AN ALIAS NAME THE SOURCE WROTE CARRIES — `derivedAliasSurfaceBits`' twin for a
	// spelling that is still a TOKEN rather than a recorded type.** A factory called through the alias
	// (`BufCell.make(…)`) is the position that needs it: the value it mints has no declared type to read, only
	// the name the call site wrote. It is READER-SCOPED where the twin is not, and that difference is its
	// input's: the twin reads a name the sweep already rewrote to a per-instance mint, this one reads what a
	// particular file's tokens say, and two files may declare one alias name (`readerScopedAliasName`).
	export function aliasSpellingSurfaceBits(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns DeclaredSurfaceNode
		return self.bufferAliasSurfaceBits(self.readerScopedAliasName(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath))
	end 'aliasSpellingSurfaceBits'

	// THE one decoder of an already-scoped alias name's two spelling bits, so the two doors above cannot come
	// to disagree about which set carries which bit — the pairing W49 states once at `derivedAliasSurfaceBits`.
	// It takes the name SCOPED, because scoping is exactly what the two callers differ on.
	function bufferAliasSurfaceBits(aliasName ByteArray) returns DeclaredSurfaceNode
		var bits = 0 as DeclaredSurfaceNode
		if self.bufferSurfaceAliases.contains(aliasName) 'aliasIsItselfTheBuffer'
			bits = bits or SurfaceBitIsBuffer
		end 'aliasIsItselfTheBuffer'

		if self.bufferElementAliases.contains(aliasName) 'aliasHoldsBuffers'
			bits = bits or SurfaceBitElementIsBuffer
		end 'aliasHoldsBuffers'

		return bits
	end 'bufferAliasSurfaceBits'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SPELLING BITS A DECLARATION-VIEW POSITION INHERITS FROM THE INSTANCE ARGUMENT IT IS WRITTEN IN
	// TERMS OF — THE FOURTH DECLARED POSITION, and the only one that never had a word.** `ReturnShapeSurface`,
	// `FieldShapeSurface` and `PayloadShapeSurface` each carry a pre-erasure spelling because
	// `__ManagedMemory` and `ByteArray` resolve to the SAME `genericInstance`; a generic type ARGUMENT is the
	// fourth position where that is true, and `substitutedInstanceArg` — the reader every substituted slot goes
	// through — hands back the resolved argument with the spelling already gone.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SPELLING CANNOT LIVE ON THE INSTANCE, WHICH IS WHY THIS TAKES `id` AND ANSWERS ONLY BITS.**
	// `Array with __ManagedMemory` and `Array with ByteArray` intern to ONE `GenericInstanceId` deliberately —
	// that identity is what lets one `Array` body serve both, and A2j's whole design rests on the two being one
	// TYPE with two SURFACES. So the argument's spelling is carried by the VALUE (`Parser.bufferSurfaceElements`,
	// which says *argument `BufferSurfaceArgPosition` of this value's instance was spelled the buffer*), and
	// what this door answers is the other half: WHERE in a declaration-view type that argument lands.
	//
	// Two shapes, which are exactly the two bits a `DeclaredSurfaceNode` has:
	//   • `returns Element` — the position IS the argument ⇒ the value is a buffer;
	//   • `returns ArrayIterator with Element` — the position HOLDS it at its own argument 0 ⇒ the value's
	//     elements are buffers, and a `current()` on THAT value reaches the first arm one hop later.
	// The two compose, which is what carries the surface across `for m in bufs` under a corpus `Array`:
	// `createIterator()` takes the second, `current()` the first.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE OWNER IS CHECKED, NOT ONLY THE POSITION** — `substituteThroughInstance`'s guard, for its reason:
	// since W14 a parameter token identifies `(declaring type, parameter name)`, so another declaration's
	// parameter that happens to sit at position 0 must not be read as this instance's argument.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT TAKES AN INTERNER, AND THAT IS `substituteThroughInstanceInterned`'S REASON EXACTLY.** The two
	// arms above key on whole-program payloads — a `typeParameter` carries W14's digest, a `genericInstance` a
	// registry id — but the SECOND arm's input may arrive as a bare `named`, whose payload is an id in the
	// caller's own table. A caller therefore hands the table its type was minted from; reading such an id here
	// against `self.typeNames` is OPEN #56 verbatim, right exactly while two insertion orders coincide.
	export function surfaceBitsThroughInstanceArg(declared MaxonType, id GenericInstanceId, interner TypeNameInterner, readerFilePath FilePath) returns DeclaredSurfaceNode
		if self.namesBufferArgOf(declared, id: id) 'thePositionISTheArgument'
			return SurfaceBitIsBuffer
		end 'thePositionISTheArgument'

		match self.declarationViewInstanceOf(declared, scopeOwner: self.genericInstanceBaseName(id), interner: interner, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'holder'
			notInScope then return 0 as DeclaredSurfaceNode
			instance(nested) then return self.elementBitOfInstanceArg(nested, id: id)
		end 'holder'
	end 'surfaceBitsThroughInstanceArg'

	// ⭐ **THE INSTANCE A DECLARATION-VIEW POSITION DENOTES — three spellings of one thing.** A position
	// written OUT (`Cell with T` as a generic argument) already carries the instance; the other two arrive as
	// a bare `named`, and each has its own resolver, which is why this composes rather than picking:
	//   • the body's own inner `typealias` (`returns TCell`) — `parseTypeReference` records it BASE-QUALIFIED
	//     (`Holder.TCell`), which is the generic-alias registry's key for it;
	//   • a bare generic BASE in a type-parameter scope (`returns Inner` inside `type Outer uses T`) — the
	//     shape `Parser.promoteBareGenericResultToCalleeScope` exists for, minted by `scopedGenericInstanceOf`.
	//
	// The scope that binds the second is the CALLEE'S DECLARING TYPE, which here is the receiver instance's
	// base: `Parser.retypeOpaqueMethodResult` has already refused any callee not qualified under it, so the
	// two cannot disagree.
	//
	// Both resolvers are the ONE door for their spelling — `genericAliasInstanceFrom` is what the parser
	// itself resolves an alias through, reader-scoped so two files' same-named alias stay two, and
	// `scopedGenericInstanceOf` is the single mint v1 has three copies of.
	function declarationViewInstanceOf(declared MaxonType, scopeOwner ByteArray, interner TypeNameInterner, readerFilePath FilePath) returns ScopedGenericInstance
		if maxonTypeTag(declared) == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'writtenOut'
			return ScopedGenericInstance.instance(typeNameIdOf(declared))
		end 'writtenOut'

		if maxonTypeTag(declared) != ValueTypeTag.named 'spellsNoInstance'
			return ScopedGenericInstance.notInScope
		end 'spellsNoInstance'

		let name = try interner.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(declared)) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.declarationViewInstanceOf: `named` id {typeNameIdOf(declared)} is not in the interner it was read with — a caller must hand the table its own type was minted from")
		if self.isGenericAlias(name) 'spellsAnAlias'
			return ScopedGenericInstance.instance(self.genericAliasInstanceFrom(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath))
		end 'spellsAnAlias'

		return self.scopedGenericInstanceOf(name, declaringTypeName: scopeOwner, scopeParamNames: self.structTypeParamsOf(scopeOwner))
	end 'declarationViewInstanceOf'

	// Does instance `nested` hold instance `id`'s buffer-spelled argument at its OWN
	// `BufferSurfaceArgPosition`? The second arm of the door above, split out because a `match` arm may not
	// open one.
	function elementBitOfInstanceArg(nested GenericInstanceId, id GenericInstanceId) returns DeclaredSurfaceNode
		if self.genericInstanceArgCount(nested) <= BufferSurfaceArgPosition 'noArgumentToHoldIt'
			return 0 as DeclaredSurfaceNode
		end 'noArgumentToHoldIt'

		if not self.namesBufferArgOf(self.substitutedInstanceArg(nested, position: BufferSurfaceArgPosition), id: id) 'itHoldsSomethingElse'
			return 0 as DeclaredSurfaceNode
		end 'itHoldsSomethingElse'

		return SurfaceBitElementIsBuffer
	end 'elementBitOfInstanceArg'

	// Is `t` the type parameter instance `id` binds at `BufferSurfaceArgPosition`? The predicate both arms
	// above ask, of the two positions a declaration view can put that parameter in.
	//
	// A token with no owner is the same invariant break `substituteThroughInstance` panics on, and it is the
	// same population of tokens: every one reaching here was minted inside a declaration the sweep folded.
	function namesBufferArgOf(t MaxonType, id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		if maxonTypeTag(t) != ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'notAFormalParameter'
			return false
		end 'notAFormalParameter'

		let owner = try self.typeParamOwnerOf(typeNameIdOf(t)) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.namesBufferArgOf: type-parameter token {typeNameIdOf(t)} has no owner — `recordStruct` and `recordInterfaceDeclaration` file every declared parameter as the sweep folds, so a token with none was minted inside a declaration neither fold saw")
		return owner.position == BufferSurfaceArgPosition and owner.declaringTypeName.equals(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id))
	end 'namesBufferArgOf'

	// Does the generic-instance alias `name` hold BUFFERS — was it declared `Array with __ManagedMemory`
	// (A2m)? The read side of `bufferElementAliases`; `Set.contains` on the shared empty set returns before it
	// hashes, so a program that never writes the alias does not pay to ask.
	export function aliasElementIsBufferSurface(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns bool
		return self.bufferElementAliases.contains(self.readerScopedAliasName(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath))
	end 'aliasElementIsBufferSurface'

	// Does the generic-instance alias `name` DENOTE a buffer — was it declared `__ManagedMemory with T`
	// (W43)? The read side of `bufferSurfaceAliases`, and the door that makes `initKeys.length()` legal where
	// the parameter is declared `KeyMemory` rather than `__ManagedMemory`. `Set.contains` on the shared empty
	// set returns before it hashes, so a program with no such alias does not pay to ask.
	export function aliasDenotesBufferSurface(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns bool
		return self.bufferSurfaceAliases.contains(self.readerScopedAliasName(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath))
	end 'aliasDenotesBufferSurface'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE KEY THESE TWO REGISTRIES HOLD *THIS READER'S* DECLARATION UNDER (W49).** Both are keyed by the
	// bare alias name with no file in the key, but a plain `typealias` is FILE-LOCAL — so on a contested name
	// the bare key answers "did ANY file's declaration spell the buffer?", which is exactly the question a
	// contest makes meaningless. `recordGenericAliasContest` files each declaration's own spelling under the
	// per-instance MINT, and this is the one place that spelling is looked up again.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SCOPING RULE IS NOT RESTATED HERE — it is `scopedGenericAliasInstance`'s**, the same three tiers
	// N3 resolves the INSTANCE by (the reader's own file, then a declaration the reader may name, then bare
	// last-wins). A second rule for the SURFACE would be free to disagree with the one that picks the TYPE,
	// which is how one written word comes to denote two things.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN UNCONTESTED NAME COSTS ONE MISS ON AN EMPTY MAP AND ALLOCATES NOTHING** — no `toString`, no mint
	// spelling — which is `scopedGenericAliasInstance`'s own discipline and matters for the same reason: this
	// is asked of every identifier written in a type position.
	function readerScopedAliasName(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns ByteArray
		if not self.contestedGenericAliases.contains(name) 'oneDeclarationOfThisNameOrNone'
			return name
		end 'oneDeclarationOfThisNameOrNone'

		return instanceQualifiedAliasName(name, canonicalInstance: self.canonicalInstanceName(self.scopedGenericAliasInstance(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath)))
	end 'readerScopedAliasName'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THE CONFORMER DECLARE `qualified` IN ITS OWN BODY, so an `extension` must NOT publish its
	// same-named method onto it (W43)?** An interface extension supplies DEFAULT implementations; a conformer
	// that declares the name has already said what it means there. Asked once per (extension, conformer,
	// member) by `Parser.foldExtensionDeclarationInto`, whose answer it then records for the real parse.
	//
	// ⚠ **A SIGNATURE AN EXTENSION PUBLISHED DOES NOT COUNT**, which is what keeps the verdict independent of
	// FOLD ORDER: two extensions publishing one `<Conformer>.<method>` are a genuine duplicate definition and
	// stay E3006 whichever folds first, because neither of them is the conformer's own declaration.
	// `specs-shv2/extension-overload-set.md` pins that in BOTH orders, because the failure the exclusion
	// prevents is a SILENT acceptance of whichever declaration folded second, not a different diagnostic.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THIS IS NOT THE PREDICATE FOR "DOES `<Type>.<method>` EXIST?", AND IT WAS USED AS ONE.** The
	// exclusion above is what makes the two questions different, and every method an `extension` publishes is
	// where they part: it IS a method of the conformer, at every call site, and this answers `false` for it.
	// Asked as an existence test by the corpus fall-through (`Parser.corpusServesMemberOffRoster`), it refused
	// `"…".filter(…)` — published onto `String` by `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon`'s `extension Iterable` — with the
	// roster sentence that door exists to retire, and it would have refused `Array.sort` for the identical
	// reason once `stdlib/Array.maxon` is listed. **Existence is `declaresCallee`**; this has exactly one
	// caller and it is the fold.
	export function conformerDeclaresOwnMethod(qualified ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.funcDeclSites.contains(qualified) and not self.extensionPublishedMethods.contains(qualified)
	end 'conformerDeclaresOwnMethod'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES `qualified` CARRY A HIDDEN `self`?** — the receiver half of resolving a bare sibling call to a
	// declaration whose body the parser cannot see (`Parser.isImplicitSelfMethod`). Wherever the parser CAN see
	// the body it reads the same fact off the tokens (`functionDeclIsStatic`), and both answers come from that
	// one test: the sweep captures it at the declaration and files it here.
	//
	// ⚠ **A NAME NOTHING DECLARES PANICS RATHER THAN ANSWERING "receiverless"** — the caller has already
	// established that the declaration exists (`declaresCallee`), because a name with no declaration is not a
	// sibling at all and takes a different door entirely. Answering `false` for it would be a sentinel: a typo
	// would silently become a receiver-less call and be reported against its ARGUMENTS.
	export function declaredMethodTakesReceiver(qualified ByteArray) returns bool
		let site = try self.funcDeclSites.get(qualified) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.declaredMethodTakesReceiver: nothing declares '{String.from(qualified)}' — the caller establishes that through `declaresCallee` first, and the two registries are written by one act (`record`)")
		return site.takesReceiver
	end 'declaredMethodTakesReceiver'

	// Note that the `extension` declared in `declFilePath` published `qualified` onto a conformer — the write
	// side of the test above, AND the write that discovers a cross-file contest (X-contains).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CONTEST RIDES THE HASH, unlike the two publication sets beside it, and the difference is real
	// rather than an inconsistency.** Those two are DERIVED from declarations the hash already carries, so a
	// source edit that changes them moves it anyway. This one is not: it turns on WHICH FILE each declaration
	// sits in, and moving an `extension` between two files can start or end a contest while every `record`
	// call this index makes stays byte-identical. It decides a SYMBOL NAME, so a reader's parse memo has to
	// move with it — `recordNamespaceQualifiedFunction`'s argument, one declaration kind over.
	export function noteExtensionPublishedMethod(qualified ByteArray, declFilePath FilePath)
		self.extensionPublishedMethods.insert(qualified)

		if let firstDeclFile = try self.extensionMethodDeclFiles.get(qualified) 'anExtensionAlreadyPublishedIt'
			if not firstDeclFile.equals(declFilePath) 'andItWasAnotherFile'
				self.contestedExtensionMethods.insert(qualified)
				self.hash = mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeContestedExtensionMethod), bytes: qualified)
			end 'andItWasAnotherFile'
			return
		end 'anExtensionAlreadyPublishedIt'

		self.extensionMethodDeclFiles.upsert(qualified, value: declFilePath)
	end 'noteExtensionPublishedMethod'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS `qualified` DECLARED BY AN `extension` IN MORE THAN ONE FILE (X-contains)?** The one flag
	// `Parser.overloadRegistrationNameFor` asks before it hands a declaration the bare name: a contested name
	// is registered under its parameter-type suffix by EVERY claimant, so two whose parameters differ are two
	// live overloads and two whose parameters agree claim one name and collide (E3006). See that function's
	// escalation ladder for why "nobody keeps the bare spelling" is what makes the refusal fall out of the
	// mint instead of out of a second check.
	//
	// ⚠ Asked once per FUNCTION DECLARATION in the program, so it is on a hot path — and it costs nothing on
	// it: `Set.contains` returns at its `capacity == 0` test before it hashes, and the set stays empty for
	// every program that declares no such contest, which today is every program the corpus compiles.
	export function isContestedExtensionMethod(qualified ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.contestedExtensionMethods.contains(qualified)
	end 'isContestedExtensionMethod'

	// Note that an `extension`'s method was WITHHELD from a conformer because the conformer declares its own
	// (W43). The recorded DECISION, read back by the real parse so the body it emits and the signature the
	// fold published are one answer rather than two derivations of it.
	export function noteExtensionShadowedMethod(qualified ByteArray)
		self.extensionShadowedMethods.insert(qualified)
	end 'noteExtensionShadowedMethod'

	// Was `qualified` withheld from its conformer by the rule above? `Set.contains` on the shared empty set
	// returns before it hashes, so a program with no such shadow does not pay to ask.
	export function extensionMethodIsShadowed(qualified ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.extensionShadowedMethods.contains(qualified)
	end 'extensionMethodIsShadowed'

	// ⭐ **DID AN `extension` PUBLISH `qualified`, rather than the conformer declaring it?** — the DIRECT read
	// of the set above, and a genuinely different question from `conformerDeclaresOwnMethod`'s. That one is a
	// CONJUNCTION asked while the fold is still running, and it answers `false` both for an extension's method
	// and for a name nothing declares at all; a caller that needs to know the method's signature is the
	// INTERFACE's — because only then does it know what that signature says without reading it — must ask
	// about publication itself. `Parser.extensionElementTransformHint` is that caller: it offers a closure
	// parameter type only for a `map` whose declaration it can name, and a conformer's own `map` is free to
	// declare anything.
	export function methodPublishedByExtension(qualified ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.extensionPublishedMethods.contains(qualified)
	end 'methodPublishedByExtension'

	// Add one function's consume bits to the index AND fold them into the hash. The one write, for
	// `record`'s reason: the table and the hash that keys every parse memo on it cannot disagree about what
	// the index contains. A function with NO consumed parameter is not recorded (its default answer is "not
	// consumed"), so the common function costs no map entry and no hash movement — the same "absence is the
	// answer" the sweep's over-approximation rests on.
	function recordParamConsume(name ByteArray, info ParamConsumeInfo)
		self.funcParamConsumes.upsert(name, value: info)
		var state = mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeParamConsume), bytes: name)
		// The param NAMES ride too: a rename changes which labelled argument a caller slots to a consumed
		// position, so a caller's memo must move when they do (`transferConsumedArgs` slots against them
		// through `argSlotPosition`).
		for i in 0 upto info.paramNames.count() 'eachName'
			let paramName = try info.paramNames.get(i) otherwise panic("recordParamConsume: paramNames.get OOB at {i} despite i < count")
			let bit = info.isConsumed(i)
			state = mix(mixBytes(state, bytes: paramName), value: ConsumeBitSet if bit else ConsumeBitClear)
		end 'eachName'
		// The TYPE-PARAMETER FEEDS ride too (P1.6-B2): which position a parameter feeds decides whether a
		// caller consumes a managed argument there, so a change to it must move the caller's parse memo. Only a
		// generic constructor HAS a feed list (a non-generic function's `feeds` is EMPTY, so this loop does not
		// run and adds nothing — matching that such a function can never feed a type parameter). The STORED
		// feeds are read directly, never through `typeParamFeedOf`, whose out-of-range `none` would box a union
		// value per parameter on every consuming function in the program.
		for i in 0 upto info.feeds.count() 'eachFeed'
			state = self.mixTypeParamFeed(state, feed: try info.feeds.get(i) otherwise panic("recordParamConsume: feeds.get OOB at {i} despite i < count"))
		end 'eachFeed'
		self.hash = state
	end 'recordParamConsume'

	// Fold one parameter's type-parameter feed into the hash — `feeds(position, sink)` mixes both, `none` its own
	// discriminator (so a parameter that GAINED a feed hashes distinctly from one that never had one). The SINK
	// rides because the two sinks emit different programs for the same position (W10): one enrols the callee
	// owner, the other leaves ownership with the constructed value.
	function mixTypeParamFeed(state ContentHash, feed TypeParamFeed) returns ContentHash
		return match feed 'f'
			feeds(position, sink) gives mix(mix(mix(state, value: FeedShapeFeeds), value: position), value: feedSinkShape(sink))
			none gives mix(state, value: FeedShapeNone)
		end 'f'
	end 'mixTypeParamFeed'

	// ⭐ **DOES `callee` CONSUME (take ownership of) ITS ARGUMENT AT POSITION `index`?** THE door the
	// callee-side enrollment asks per parameter. A callee with no recorded entry, or an index past its
	// recorded bits, is a BORROW (false) — the safe default, and what makes recording only the consuming
	// functions correct.
	export function paramIsConsumed(callee ByteArray, index ParamCount) returns bool
		return match self.consumeOf(callee) 'c'
			consumes(info) gives info.isConsumed(index)
			borrowsAll gives false
		end 'c'
	end 'paramIsConsumed'

	// ⭐ **THE TYPE-PARAMETER FEED of `callee`'s parameter at position `index`, or `none`** (P1.7 slice 3b-v).
	// The callee-entry twin of `paramIsConsumed`: an opaque-`T` FEED parameter (`push(item Element)` whose
	// `item` moves into `self.items`, or a sibling forwarder the transitive fixpoint marked) is enrolled OWNED
	// in the shared body so a double / conditional move is move-tracked and dropped once. A callee with no
	// recorded entry, or an index past its recorded feeds, is `none` — the same borrow-default `paramIsConsumed`
	// answers false for.
	export function paramFeedOf(callee ByteArray, index ParamCount) returns TypeParamFeed
		return match self.consumeOf(callee) 'c'
			consumes(info) gives info.typeParamFeedOf(index)
			borrowsAll gives TypeParamFeed.none
		end 'c'
	end 'paramFeedOf'

	// ⭐ **DOES `callee` FEED ANY parameter into storage IT owns?** (P1.7 slice 3b-v, W10) — the descriptor-need
	// seed reads this: a method that enrols an opaque feed parameter owned may DROP it on a path that does not
	// move it (a conditional `push`/forward), and that drop reads the enclosing instance's layout descriptor
	// `destroyFunc@40` at run time, so the method must carry the descriptor parameter. A borrow-only callee (no
	// entry) feeds nothing, and a CONSTRUCTOR feed enrols nothing, so neither can need a descriptor.
	export function functionFeedsCalleeStorage(callee ByteArray) returns bool
		return match self.consumeOf(callee) 'c'
			consumes(info) gives info.feedsCalleeStorage()
			borrowsAll gives false
		end 'c'
	end 'functionFeedsCalleeStorage'

	// ⭐ **THE CALLEE'S CONSUME FACTS, or `borrowsAll`** — the door a CALL SITE asks once, then slots each
	// argument against `info.paramNames` to decide which ones are moved. `borrowsAll` (no recorded entry) is
	// every runtime callee, every borrow-only user function, and the overwhelming common case — so the call
	// site's fast path is a single `match` with nothing to slot.
	export function consumeOf(callee ByteArray) returns CalleeConsume
		let info = try self.funcParamConsumes.get(callee) otherwise return CalleeConsume.borrowsAll
		return CalleeConsume.consumes(info)
	end 'consumeOf'

	// Add one function's by-reference columns to the index AND fold them into the hash — the one write, for
	// `recordParamConsume`'s reason, and here that reason is at its strongest: this table decides an ABI, so a
	// table and a memo key that disagreed would compile a caller against a signature the callee no longer has.
	//
	// A function that states NOTHING (no reassignment, no forward) is not recorded, so the common function
	// costs no map entry and no hash movement. The GATE is raised here rather than at a second walk, off the
	// `direct` column alone — one pass over the fact, at the moment it arrives.
	function recordParamReassign(name ByteArray, info ParamReassignInfo)
		self.funcParamReassigns.upsert(name, value: info)
		self.sawParamReassignment = self.sawParamReassignment or info.reassignsAnyParam()

		var state = mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeParamByRef), bytes: name)
		for i in 0 upto info.paramNames.count() 'eachName'
			let paramName = try info.paramNames.get(i) otherwise panic("recordParamReassign: paramNames.get OOB at {i} despite i < count")
			// BOTH columns ride, UN-COMBINED: whether a forward is live is a whole-program fact that is not this
			// entry's to state, and folding the combined answer would hash two different declarations alike
			// whenever the forwarding happened to be dead.
			state = mixBytes(state, bytes: paramName)
			state = mix(state, value: ByRefBitSet if bitAt(info.direct, index: i) else ByRefBitClear)
			state = mix(state, value: ByRefBitSet if bitAt(info.forwarded, index: i) else ByRefBitClear)
		end 'eachName'
		// The forwarding EDGES ride too, and they must: a body that starts or stops handing a parameter to a
		// reassigning name changes its OWN ABI without changing one byte of any caller. The BARE name rides for
		// the mirror-image reason — it is what OTHER bodies' edges match against, so renaming a method changes
		// which of them are widened, and so does MOVING it into or out of a type body (which is what
		// `callability` says, and why it rides beside the name rather than being re-derived).
		state = mixBytes(state, bytes: info.bareName)
		state = foldCallability(state, callability: info.callability)
		for call in info.forwardCalls 'eachEdge'
			// The SHAPE rides with the name: `swap(x)`, `x.swap(x)` and `self.swap(x)` reach different nodes, so
			// two bodies differing only in a receiver state different things and must not hash alike. The `self.`
			// arm's TYPE rides inside the name it is folded through (`targetsOf` joins the same two halves).
			state = mixBytes(state, bytes: forwardCallName(call))
			state = mixBytes(mix(state, value: forwardCallShapeMark(call)), bytes: forwardCallSelfType(call))
		end 'eachEdge'
		self.hash = state
	end 'recordParamReassign'

	// Add one function's immutable-global-return raw material to the index AND fold it into the hash — the one
	// write, for `recordParamReassign`'s reason. A function that states nothing is not recorded, so the common
	// function costs no map entry and no hash movement. The GATE is raised here, off the returned-NAME column
	// alone, because only a name can seed the closure.
	function recordGlobalAliasReturn(name ByteArray, info GlobalAliasReturnInfo)
		self.funcGlobalAliasReturns.upsert(name, value: info)
		self.sawGlobalNameReturn = self.sawGlobalNameReturn or info.returnedNames.count() > 0

		// The declaring FILE is NOT folded here — `foldFile` folds it once for the whole file, because that is
		// the granularity the fact has. See there.
		var state = mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeGlobalAliasReturn), bytes: name)
		for returned in info.returnedNames 'eachName'
			state = mixBytes(state, bytes: returned)
		end 'eachName'
		state = mixBytes(state, bytes: info.bareName)
		state = foldCallability(state, callability: info.callability)
		for call in info.returnCalls 'eachEdge'
			// The SHAPE rides with the name for `recordParamReassign`'s reason, through the same two folds: the
			// three call shapes reach different nodes, so two bodies differing only in a receiver state
			// different things and must not hash alike.
			state = mixBytes(state, bytes: forwardCallName(call))
			state = mixBytes(mix(state, value: forwardCallShapeMark(call)), bytes: forwardCallSelfType(call))
		end 'eachEdge'
		self.hash = state
	end 'recordGlobalAliasReturn'

	// ⭐⭐ **MAY A CALL TO `callee` HAND BACK A `let`-DECLARED GLOBAL'S OWN RECORD? (W117)** — the ONE door the
	// parser asks, at a call site, to decide whether the result must carry the immutable-global mark. A key
	// with no entry answers false, which is every function in every program that returns no global.
	export function returnsImmutableGlobalAlias(callee ByteArray) returns bool
		self.closeGlobalAliasReturns()
		return self.globalAliasReturnKeys.contains(callee)
	end 'returnsImmutableGlobalAlias'

	// ⭐ **DOES ANY FUNCTION IN THE PROGRAM RETURN ONE?** The whole-program skip, asked before a call site
	// spends a hash on the question above — `programTakesAParamByRef`'s twin, and true of very few programs.
	export function programReturnsAnImmutableGlobalAlias() returns bool
		self.closeGlobalAliasReturns()
		return self.globalAliasReturnKeys.count() != 0
	end 'programReturnsAnImmutableGlobalAlias'

	// ⭐⭐ **CLOSE THE IMMUTABLE-GLOBAL-RETURN RELATION — the fixpoint that turns `get()` returning what
	// `inner()` returns into "`get()` may hand back a `let` global too", transitively (W117).**
	//
	// It is `closeByRefForwarding` in every structural respect and deliberately so — same node space, same
	// edge shapes, same reachability engine, same run-once-at-first-read discipline, and the same measured
	// reason for being a single reverse-BFS rather than round-based rescanning (see that function's header for
	// the 199M-vs-452M numbers). What differs is only the SEED and what the mark is published as.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE SEED CANNOT BE DECIDED BY THE SWEEP, AND THAT IS WHY THE RAW NAMES ARE CARRIED.** "Is `A` an
	// immutable stored global?" needs every file's declarations folded and every initializer evaluated, and
	// the sweep runs while files are still arriving. Here, both are done.
	//
	// ⚠ **A SCALAR `let` IS NOT A SEED, AND THAT FALLS OUT RATHER THAN BEING EXCLUDED**: `DeclKind.stored`
	// admits only a binding with a `.data` slot, and an inlined scalar constant has no record to alias in the
	// first place — the whole rule is about a record two names can reach.
	function closeGlobalAliasReturns()
		if self.globalAliasReturnsClosed 'alreadySettled'
			return
		end 'alreadySettled'
		self.globalAliasReturnsClosed = true

		// ⚡ **NO SEED, NO GRAPH** — `closeByRefForwarding`'s gate, for its reason and with its direction of
		// error. A body is recorded merely for returning a CALL, so the map is non-empty on most programs;
		// what decides whether anything can be marked is whether some body returns a NAME at all.
		if not self.sawGlobalNameReturn 'noReturnedNameCanSeedIt'
			return
		end 'noReturnedNameCanSeedIt'

		// ⚡⚡ **AND THE SAME GATE ASKED EXACTLY, WHICH THE ONE ABOVE CANNOT BE.** `sawGlobalNameReturn` is
		// raised at the SWEEP, where no name can be resolved, so it says only *"some body returned a bare
		// name"* — true of nearly every program, because `return EmptySourceText` and `return SomeConstant`
		// are ordinary. Here every declaration is folded and every initializer evaluated, so the real
		// question — does any of those names DENOTE a `let`-declared stored global? — has an answer, and a
		// `false` means reachability would run from an empty seed set and mark nothing. Skipping it skips
		// the node space, both edge columns, the CSR build and the BFS.
		//
		// ⚠ **MEASURED, and it is why this test exists rather than the sweep gate alone**: this closure is
		// asked lazily, and its FIRST asker is `Parser.beginFunction` — so its whole cost was billed to
		// `phase:parse`, which is where the ladder read it (+5,496 allocations at rung 0, +11,278 at rung 5,
		// the entire `phase:parse` movement of `W117`). Attributed by a probe that made nothing ask it:
		// `phase:parse` fell back to the merge base's count to the allocation.
		if not self.anyRecordedReturnNamesAnImmutableGlobal() 'noSeedResolvesToOne'
			return
		end 'noSeedResolvesToOne'

		var nodes = DeclNodeSpace.create()
		for (_, info) in self.funcGlobalAliasReturns 'eachRecorded'
			let node = nodes.nodeFor(info.bareName, callability: info.callability)
			if self.returnsAnImmutableGlobalDirectly(info) 'namesOneItself'
				try nodes.seeds.set(node, value: true) otherwise panic("closeGlobalAliasReturns: seeds.set OOB at {node} despite the node having just been interned")
			end 'namesOneItself'
		end 'eachRecorded'

		// The return-forwarding edges, REVERSED — a forward `caller -> callee` is recorded as `callee -> caller`,
		// so a seed's row lists the declarations that return INTO it and the mark propagates back up the chain.
		var edgeCallee = CsrColumn.create()
		var edgeForwarder = CsrColumn.create()
		for (_, info) in self.funcGlobalAliasReturns 'eachForwarder'
			let forwarder = nodes.nodeFor(info.bareName, callability: info.callability)
			for call in info.returnCalls 'eachEdge'
				for callee in nodes.targetsOf(call) 'eachTargetItCanName'
					edgeCallee.push(callee)
					edgeForwarder.push(forwarder)
				end 'eachTargetItCanName'
			end 'eachEdge'
		end 'eachForwarder'

		let mayAlias = reverseReachabilityMark(buildCsr(edgeCallee, to: edgeForwarder, nodeCount: nodes.bareNames.count()), seeds: nodes.seeds)

		// ⭐ Published per REGISTRATION KEY and not per node, for `closeByRefForwarding`'s reason: the parser
		// asks under the key it resolved a call to, and two keys may share a node.
		for (name, info) in self.funcGlobalAliasReturns 'publishKeys'
			let marked = try mayAlias.get(nodes.nodeFor(info.bareName, callability: info.callability)) otherwise panic("closeGlobalAliasReturns: mayAlias.get OOB for a node interned before the graph was built")
			if marked 'marked'
				self.globalAliasReturnKeys.insert(name)
			end 'marked'
		end 'publishKeys'
	end 'closeGlobalAliasReturns'

	// ⚡ **IS THERE A SEED AT ALL?** — the closure's exact precondition, over the SAME per-declaration test the
	// seed loop uses, so the two cannot come to disagree about what a seed is. It walks and resolves nothing
	// the seed loop would not have resolved anyway; what it buys is the right to stop before the graph exists.
	function anyRecordedReturnNamesAnImmutableGlobal() returns bool
		for (_, info) in self.funcGlobalAliasReturns 'eachRecorded'
			if self.returnsAnImmutableGlobalDirectly(info) 'namesOneItself'
				return true
			end 'namesOneItself'
		end 'eachRecorded'

		return false
	end 'anyRecordedReturnNamesAnImmutableGlobal'

	// Does this body return a name that DENOTES an immutable stored global, read from its own file? The
	// closure's seed test, asked once per recorded declaration.
	function returnsAnImmutableGlobalDirectly(info GlobalAliasReturnInfo) returns bool
		for returned in info.returnedNames 'eachName'
			if self.namesImmutableStoredGlobal(returned, readerFilePath: info.declFilePath) 'namesAnImmutableGlobal'
				return true
			end 'namesAnImmutableGlobal'
		end 'eachName'

		return false
	end 'returnsAnImmutableGlobalDirectly'

	// ⭐ **DOES `name`, READ FROM `readerFilePath`, DENOTE A `let`-DECLARED TOP-LEVEL BINDING WITH A RECORD?**
	// The one place the two halves of that question are joined — the SAME scoped-then-bare probe every other
	// reader of a top-level name goes through (`declFor`), and the declaration's own `mutable` bit. It answers
	// about STORAGE rather than about constants, because an inlined `let` materializes a fresh value at each
	// use and has no shared record for a second name to write through.
	export function namesImmutableStoredGlobal(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns bool
		return match self.declFor(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath, wanted: DeclKind.stored) 'lookup'
			found(index) gives not self.declAt(index).mutable
			notFound gives false
		end 'lookup'
	end 'namesImmutableStoredGlobal'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS `callee`'s PARAMETER AT `index` PASSED BY REFERENCE?** THE door both ends of the ABI ask, and
	// the only one — `Parser.bindParameters` for the callee's own parameters and `parseCallArgs` for an
	// argument. A callee with no recorded entry, or an index past its recorded columns, is BY VALUE: the safe
	// default, and what makes recording only the stating functions correct.
	export function paramIsByRef(callee ByteArray, index ParamCount) returns bool
		return match self.byRefOf(callee) 'b'
			takesByRef(info) gives info.isByRef(index, forwardingIsLive: self.forwardingIsLiveFor(callee))
			allByValue gives false
		end 'b'
	end 'paramIsByRef'

	// ⭐ **DOES `callee` FORWARD A PARAMETER INTO SOMETHING THAT TAKES IT BY REFERENCE?** — the settled
	// transitive half, and the ONE door that reads the closure's output. A name with no entry answers false,
	// which is every function on a program that reassigns nothing.
	export function forwardingIsLiveFor(callee ByteArray) returns bool
		self.closeByRefForwarding()
		return try self.byRefForwardingLive.get(callee) otherwise false
	end 'forwardingIsLiveFor'

	// ⭐⭐ **CLOSE THE FORWARDING RELATION — the fixpoint that turns `outer(x)` calling a reassigning `inner`
	// into "`outer`'s `x` is by reference too", transitively.**
	//
	// It runs ONCE, at the first read, because it needs every file's edges and the sweep folds files one at a
	// time. A program that reassigns no parameter answers on one bool (`sawParamReassignment`), which is what
	// makes the whole feature free for every program written before this rung.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT IS A REACHABILITY QUESTION, SO IT IS ASKED OF THE REACHABILITY ENGINE** — `buildCsr` +
	// `reverseReachabilityMark`, the shared O(nodes + edges) worklist the descriptor-need and array-feed
	// fixpoints already close through. A bare name takes something by reference iff it REASSIGNS (the seed) or
	// forwards into a name that does, which is exactly "reaches a seed over the forwarding edges".
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT WAS A ROUND-BASED FIXPOINT — RE-SCAN EVERY RECORDED FUNCTION UNTIL NOTHING CHANGES — AND THAT IS
	// O(depth x recorded), WITH THE SOURCE PROGRAM'S OWN CALL DEPTH AS THE MULTIPLIER.** The recorded set is not
	// small and is not the user's program: a function is recorded merely for HANDING A PARAMETER ON, so all of
	// `stdlib` that forwards an argument is in it (`Array.sort` handing `cmp` to `driftsortRange` is the case
	// `programTakesAParamByRef` already records), and every round re-walked all of it plus a
	// `byRefForwardingLive` lookup per entry.
	//
	// MEASURED on the round-based version, at a CONSTANT 512-function program where only WHICH function each
	// one forwards to changed — same tokens, same declarations, same emitted code:
	//
	//   forwarding depth    2      16      64     128     256     512
	//   phase:parse CPU   199M    207M    229M    262M    322M    452M
	//
	// The cost doubled with the DEPTH at a fixed program size, which is the multiplier stated as a measurement;
	// grown together (depth proportional to program) it read x2.9 per doubling against a flat control's x1.2.
	// The scale-test ladder cannot see any of it, and that is the CORPUS's silence rather than the code's:
	// COUNTED, not assumed — 191 function declarations across the 49 generated files, and not one reassigns a
	// parameter, so `sawParamReassignment` is false at every rung and this returns before the graph exists.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE MATCH IS ON WHAT THE SOURCE WROTE, NEVER ON A RESOLUTION** — see `ForwardCall`. An edge carries
	// only the spelling before the `(`, so widening is what lets this stay a closure over recorded edges
	// instead of a second overload resolver, and it is why the graph's NODE is a spelling while the published
	// answer is per registration KEY.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **BUT A SPELLING IS NOT THE WHOLE NODE, AND THE REST OF IT IS THE RECEIVER.** Nodes and the rule
	// that maps a call onto them live in `DeclNodeSpace`; `targetsOf` is the ONE place the mapping is
	// written, and both the edge build and the per-key publish below spend it. Without that split, `stdlib`'s
	// `Array.swap` shared a node with a user program's free `swap`, inherited its by-reference-ness and
	// carried it up to the overloaded `Array.sort`, which E2015 then refused.
	function closeByRefForwarding()
		if self.byRefForwardingClosed 'alreadySettled'
			return
		end 'alreadySettled'
		self.byRefForwardingClosed = true
		// ⚡ **NO SEED, NO GRAPH — and the test is NEITHER `funcParamReassigns.count() == 0` NOR A SCAN.** A
		// function is recorded merely for FORWARDING a parameter, so the map is non-empty on every program that
		// links the stdlib (`Array.sort` hands `cmp` to `driftsortRange`); it is empty only of programs that call
		// nothing with a bare parameter. What decides whether anything can become by-reference is whether some
		// recorded body actually REASSIGNS one, and reachability from an empty seed set marks nothing — so the
		// graph, its two edge columns and the BFS are all skipped for every program written before this rung.
		//
		// ⚡ **AND THE ANSWER IS A FLAG RAISED AT RECORD TIME, NOT A PASS OVER THE MAP** — see
		// `sawParamReassignment`. MEASURED on the scale-test ladder, both steps: building the graph
		// unconditionally cost +320 allocations at rung 0 rising to +865 at rung 5; asking the question with a
		// scan instead of the flag cost one boxed map-iteration step per recorded function, which is 219 of them
		// at rung 0 and 746 at rung 5, at a flat 16 bytes each.
		if not self.sawParamReassignment 'everyForwardIsDead'
			return
		end 'everyForwardIsDead'

		// One node per recorded declaration, plus its SEED bit: does it reassign a parameter of its own? Only a
		// reassignment can seed — a body that merely forwards cannot make itself live.
		var nodes = DeclNodeSpace.create()
		for (_, info) in self.funcParamReassigns 'eachRecorded'
			let node = nodes.nodeFor(info.bareName, callability: info.callability)
			if info.reassignsAnyParam() 'reassignsItsOwn'
				try nodes.seeds.set(node, value: true) otherwise panic("closeByRefForwarding: seeds.set OOB at {node} despite the node having just been interned")
			end 'reassignsItsOwn'
		end 'eachRecorded'

		// The forwarding edges, REVERSED — a forward `caller -> callee` is recorded as `callee -> caller`, so a
		// seed's row lists the declarations that forward INTO it and the mark propagates back up the chain.
		var edgeCallee = CsrColumn.create()
		var edgeForwarder = CsrColumn.create()
		for (_, info) in self.funcParamReassigns 'eachForwarder'
			if not info.forwardsAnyParam() 'carriesNothingAlong'
				continue
			end 'carriesNothingAlong'

			let forwarder = nodes.nodeFor(info.bareName, callability: info.callability)
			for call in info.forwardCalls 'eachEdge'
				for callee in nodes.targetsOf(call) 'eachTargetItCanName'
					edgeCallee.push(callee)
					edgeForwarder.push(forwarder)
				end 'eachTargetItCanName'
			end 'eachEdge'
		end 'eachForwarder'

		let takesByRef = reverseReachabilityMark(buildCsr(edgeCallee, to: edgeForwarder, nodeCount: nodes.bareNames.count()), seeds: nodes.seeds)
		// The BARE names of every marked node — the widest answer, and the only one published as a name set.
		// Its two readers (`bareNameTakesAParamByRef`, `programTakesAParamByRef`) both want the widest.
		for node in 0 upto nodes.bareNames.count() 'publishNames'
			let marked = try takesByRef.get(node) otherwise panic("closeByRefForwarding: takesByRef.get OOB at {node} below the node count it was built over")
			if marked 'takesSomethingByRef'
				self.byRefBareNames.insert(try nodes.bareNames.get(node) otherwise panic("closeByRefForwarding: bareNames.get OOB at {node} below its own count"))
			end 'takesSomethingByRef'
		end 'publishNames'

		// ⭐ The per-KEY answer, asked of the NODES rather than of a name set — the same `targetsOf` that built
		// the edges, so the question "did any callee this call can name end up marked?" cannot come to disagree
		// with the graph that answered it. `forwardingIsLiveFor` is asked under the REGISTRATION name, and two
		// keys wearing one bare name may carry different edges, so it is per key and not per node.
		for (name, info) in self.funcParamReassigns 'publishKeys'
			if info.forwardsAnyParam() and forwardsIntoAMarkedNode(info.forwardCalls, nodes: nodes, takesByRef: takesByRef) 'forwardsIntoOne'
				self.byRefForwardingLive.upsert(name, value: true)
			end 'forwardsIntoOne'
		end 'publishKeys'
	end 'closeByRefForwarding'

	// ⭐ **THE CALLEE'S BY-REFERENCE FACTS, or `allByValue`** — the door a CALL SITE asks once, then slots each
	// argument against `info.paramNames`. `allByValue` (no recorded entry) is the overwhelming common case, so
	// the call site's fast path is a single `match` with nothing to slot. `consumeOf`'s twin.
	export function byRefOf(callee ByteArray) returns CalleeByRefParams
		let info = try self.funcParamReassigns.get(callee) otherwise return CalleeByRefParams.allByValue
		return CalleeByRefParams.takesByRef(info)
	end 'byRefOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES ANY FUNCTION SPELLED `bareName` TAKE A PARAMETER BY REFERENCE?** — the door the parser's
	// LOCAL-CELL prescan asks, and the reason it is a BARE-name question is that the prescan reads its callee
	// out of tokens (see `byRefBareNames`). Over-approximate by construction: every `reassign` in the program
	// answers for every other, so a `var` handed to an unrelated same-named callee gets a cell it does not
	// need. Sound — a cell is a legal home for any value — and confined to programs that reassign a parameter.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT ASKS THE WIDER SET AND DOES NOT SPLIT ON THE CALL'S SHAPE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE RATHER THAN AN
	// OVERSIGHT.** The shape split exists because an over-approximation there becomes a hard E2015 through the
	// overload refusal; here the only consequence is a heap cell a local did not need, which is the case the
	// paragraph above already argues is free. Narrowing it would buy an allocation and risk a MISSING cell,
	// which is a wrong answer.
	export function bareNameTakesAParamByRef(bareName ByteArray) returns bool
		self.closeByRefForwarding()
		return self.byRefBareNames.contains(bareName)
	end 'bareNameTakesAParamByRef'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES ANY FUNCTION IN THE PROGRAM TAKE A PARAMETER BY REFERENCE?** The whole-program skip, read by
	// the parser before it prescans a body for cell-resident locals: with nothing by-reference anywhere, no
	// local can ever need a cell for a call, and the prescan is not worth walking.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT IS THE CLOSURE'S SEED SET, NOT "THE MAP IS NON-EMPTY", AND THE DIFFERENCE WAS A RED SUITE.**
	// A function that merely FORWARDS a parameter is recorded too — `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s `sort(cmp)` hands
	// `cmp` to `driftsortRange` and so carries an entry — so a map-emptiness test answers TRUE for every
	// program that links the stdlib, which is all of them. MEASURED: it opened the gate globally and made the
	// by-reference overload refusal fire on `Array.sort` itself. `byRefBareNames` is seeded ONLY by a real
	// reassignment, which is the fact this question is actually asking about.
	export function programTakesAParamByRef() returns bool
		self.closeByRefForwarding()
		return self.byRefBareNames.count() != 0
	end 'programTakesAParamByRef'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES `callee`'s OWN BODY REASSIGN ITS PARAMETER AT `index`?** — the DIRECT column alone, and the
	// seed E3019 needs.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT `paramIsByRef`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE OF WHY THE TWO MASKS STAY APART.**
	// By-reference-ness is TRANSITIVE (a forwarder takes its parameter by reference without writing a thing),
	// while "does passing an immutable binding here make it an error?" is closed over the call graph by
	// `buildParamMutationSummary`'s own fixpoint from bodies' OWN writes. Seeding E3019 from the wider column
	// would refuse `let` arguments at every forwarding position in the program.
	export function paramIsDirectlyReassigned(callee ByteArray, index ParamCount) returns bool
		return match self.byRefOf(callee) 'b'
			takesByRef(info) gives bitAt(info.direct, index: index)
			allByValue gives false
		end 'b'
	end 'paramIsDirectlyReassigned'

	// ⭐ **DOES `callee` TAKE ANY PARAMETER BY REFERENCE?** — the per-callee twin, and what the overload
	// refusal must ask: a name that merely forwards into something harmless states no ABI to disagree about.
	export function calleeTakesAnyParamByRef(callee ByteArray) returns bool
		return match self.byRefOf(callee) 'b'
			takesByRef(info) gives info.reassignsAnyParam() or (self.forwardingIsLiveFor(callee) and info.forwardsAnyParam())
			allByValue gives false
		end 'b'
	end 'calleeTakesAnyParamByRef'

	// Record one function's per-parameter DEFAULTS AND fold them into the hash — the one write, for
	// `recordParamConsume`'s reason. A function with NO default is not recorded (its default answer is
	// "every parameter is required"), so the common function costs no map entry and no hash movement.
	function recordParamDefaults(name ByteArray, info ParamDefaultInfo)
		// ⚠ **THE OVERWRITE IS COMPARED, BECAUSE THE KEY IS NOT UNIQUE.** Two declarations may wear one name —
		// legitimately, as an OVERLOAD SET, or by accident, as the same `function f` written in two files —
		// and the map keeps one of them. Whether that matters is exactly whether the two describe the same
		// SHAPE: agreeing members are filled identically whichever one a call resolves to, and a disagreement
		// leaves the loser's own parse looking up a position the winner holds no helper at.
		// `ParamDefaultInfo.matches` carries the property in full; this records the verdict, and the count
		// beside it is what catches the third shape, a sibling that publishes nothing at all
		// (`FunctionDeclarationCountMap`).
		//
		// ⚠ **THE VERDICT IS NOT MIXED INTO THE HASH, and it does not need to be.** It is DERIVED from the
		// infos this loop mixes one per declaration, and from `record`'s per-declaration mix — so a program
		// that gains or loses a sibling declaration moves the hash through the write that made it, which is
		// `refileContestedFreeFunction`'s "no hash mixing" argument at a second door.
		if let incumbent = try self.funcParamDefaults.get(name) 'anotherDeclarationOfThisName'
			self.noteRepeatedDeclaration(self.funcDefaultingDeclarationCounts, name: name)
			if not incumbent.matches(info) 'twoDeclarationsDisagree'
				self.disagreeingParamDefaults.insert(name)
			end 'twoDeclarationsDisagree'
		end 'anotherDeclarationOfThisName'

		self.funcParamDefaults.upsert(name, value: info)
		var state = mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeParamDefault), bytes: name)
		// The param NAMES ride for `recordParamConsume`'s reason — a rename changes which labelled argument
		// slots to a defaulted position — and the per-position presence bit rides because it is what decides
		// whether a caller's short call is legal at all.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE DEFAULT'S EXPRESSION DELIBERATELY DOES NOT RIDE**, and that is ARCHITECTURE.md's "mix what
		// a reader COPIES" rule, not an omission. A reader copies NOTHING out of the expression: it emits
		// `call __paramDefault#f#0`, byte-identical whether the default is `"World"` or `"Maxon"`, and the
		// expression reaches only the DECLARING file's own helper body — which re-parses on that file's own
		// content hash. What a reader does depend on is the helper's RETURN TYPE, and that rides already,
		// through the ordinary `FuncReturnTypeEntry` the same sweep publishes for it. Mixing the expression
		// would be sound and WRONG: every keystroke inside any default would re-parse the whole program,
		// which is the `var`-payload mistake one registry over.
		for i in 0 upto info.paramNames.count() 'eachName'
			let paramName = try info.paramNames.get(i) otherwise panic("recordParamDefaults: paramNames.get OOB at {i} despite i < count")
			state = mix(mixBytes(state, bytes: paramName), value: defaultProducerMark(info.producerFor(i)))
		end 'eachName'
		self.hash = state
	end 'recordParamDefaults'

	// ⭐ **THE CALLEE'S DEFAULT FACTS, or `allRequired`** — the door a CALL SITE asks once, and only when it
	// supplied fewer arguments than it might have. `allRequired` (no recorded entry) is every runtime callee
	// and every function that declares no default, which is the overwhelming common case — so the call
	// site's fast path is a single map miss and nothing to slot.
	export function defaultsOf(callee ByteArray) returns CalleeParamDefaults
		let info = try self.funcParamDefaults.get(callee) otherwise return CalleeParamDefaults.allRequired
		return CalleeParamDefaults.defaults(info)
	end 'defaultsOf'

	// ⚡ **DOES `callee` DECLARE A DEFAULT ON ANY PARAMETER? — the presence-only half of `defaultsOf`, and
	// the one a HOT PATH may ask.** Membership IS the answer, because only a function that declares a default
	// is recorded, so this is a `contains` and not a `get otherwise` — whose fallback would mint a
	// payload-free `allRequired` union case (still a heap object) at every one of the program's calls that
	// is not to a defaulting callee, i.e. almost all of them. The same accounting `throwsMap.contains` is
	// written for one registry over.
	//
	// Two askers: `Parser.fillOmittedArgDefaults`' gate, and `Parser.overloadRegistrationNameFor`'s refusal
	// to overload a name whose defaults the by-name sweep could not tell apart.
	export function declaresParamDefaults(callee ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.funcParamDefaults.contains(callee)
	end 'declaresParamDefaults'

	// ⭐ **DO THIS NAME'S DECLARATIONS DISAGREE ABOUT THEIR DEFAULT SHAPE? — the door
	// `Parser.captureParamDefault` refuses at (W74).** The whole question is
	// `declarationsDisagreeAbout`'s, asked of the defaults registry; `throwsClauseCollide` asks the identical
	// question of the throws registry one door over, and the reason it is not written twice is stated there.
	//
	// A name with no defaults entry has nothing to disagree about. That is the answer for a declaration whose
	// defaults the tolerant sweep ABANDONED (a parameter type it could not read), and it is the right one: the
	// real parse is about to report that declaration with a position of its own.
	export function paramDefaultsCollide(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.declarationsDisagreeAbout(self.funcParamDefaults.contains(name), disagreeing: self.disagreeingParamDefaults, publishedBy: self.funcDefaultingDeclarationCounts, name: name)
	end 'paramDefaultsCollide'

	// ⭐⭐ **DO THIS NAME'S DECLARATIONS DISAGREE ABOUT A FACT ONLY SOME OF THEM PUBLISH? — the ONE comparison
	// `paramDefaultsCollide` and `throwsClauseCollide` both rest on, written once (W77 review).** The two
	// registries are different (a default shape, a `throws` clause) but the TEST is the same test, because both
	// are absence-is-the-answer: only a declaration that HAS the fact ever reaches the recorder, which is also
	// why the presence probe is the caller's to pass in — the two maps hold different value types and there is
	// no one `contains` that could ask both.
	//
	// ⚠ **THAT IS WHY IT IS TWO HALVES AND NOT ONE, and why they must stay together.** `disagreeing` catches two
	// PUBLISHED values that differ — the recorder compares its own overwrite and files the name. The counts catch
	// the half the recorder structurally cannot see: a sibling that published NOTHING never reached it, so every
	// test written over the registry alone reads that program as unanimous. `record` counts EVERY declaration and
	// each recorder counts its own, so a difference IS the silent sibling.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT IS WRITTEN ONCE BECAUSE A THIRD WAY TO DISAGREE WOULD OTHERWISE HAVE TO BE ADDED TWICE**, and the
	// copy that missed it would not fail to compile — it would answer "these declarations agree" about a name
	// whose declarations do not, which at both doors is a silently mis-compiled call rather than a diagnostic.
	function declarationsDisagreeAbout(somebodyPublishesIt bool, disagreeing DivergentDeclarationNameSet, publishedBy FunctionDeclarationCountMap, name ByteArray) returns bool
		if not somebodyPublishesIt 'noDeclarationOfThisNameStatesTheFactAtAll'
			return false
		end 'noDeclarationOfThisNameStatesTheFactAtAll'

		if disagreeing.contains(name) 'twoPublishedValuesDiffer'
			return true
		end 'twoPublishedValuesDiffer'

		return self.declarationCountIn(self.funcDeclarationCounts, name: name) != self.declarationCountIn(publishedBy, name: name)
	end 'declarationsDisagreeAbout'

	// Count one more declaration of `name` in `counts`, seeding at the one already folded — see
	// `FunctionDeclarationCountMap` for why the map holds only names with more than one, and why `1` is the
	// answer a missing entry gives rather than a fallback. ONE routine for both counts: they are the same
	// tally taken over two populations, and two spellings of it would be two chances to seed differently.
	function noteRepeatedDeclaration(counts FunctionDeclarationCountMap, name ByteArray)
		counts.upsert(name, value: self.declarationCountIn(counts, name: name) + 1)
	end 'noteRepeatedDeclaration'

	function declarationCountIn(counts FunctionDeclarationCountMap, name ByteArray) returns FunctionDeclarationCount
		return try counts.get(name) otherwise 1
	end 'declarationCountIn'

	// ⭐⭐ **HAS A DECLARATION ALREADY BEEN FILED UNDER THIS KEY? — the seed for `record`'s tally, and it has
	// to ask two different maps because ONE key in the program is not its own declaration's (W78).**
	//
	// For every key but one, `returnTypes` answers exactly: `record` writes it for every declaration and
	// nothing else does, so an entry means another declaration reached this key. The exception is the BARE
	// name of a CONTESTED free function, which `refileContestedFreeFunction` deliberately leaves the
	// incumbent's return type on so a bare call to the name reaches E3095 instead of a panic — a residue no
	// membership test can tell from the declaration of a ROOT contestant, whose registration key IS that bare
	// name. For a contested name the TALLY is therefore its own marker: it is materialized at the contest and
	// by the first declaration under each side's key, so "absent" there means zero rather than one.
	function keyAlreadyHoldsADeclaration(key ByteArray, contested bool) returns bool
		if contested 'theBareKeyMayBeAnotherDeclarationsResidue'
			return self.funcDeclarationCounts.contains(key)
		end 'theBareKeyMayBeAnotherDeclarationsResidue'

		return self.returnTypes.contains(key)
	end 'keyAlreadyHoldsADeclaration'

	// Write the `1` a contested key's tally cannot leave implicit — see `keyAlreadyHoldsADeclaration` for why
	// only a contested key needs it, and `FunctionDeclarationCountMap` for why every other name pays no entry.
	function materializeDeclarationCount(key ByteArray, contested bool)
		if not contested 'theSparseConventionAnswersForThisKey'
			return
		end 'theSparseConventionAnswersForThisKey'

		self.funcDeclarationCounts.upsert(key, value: self.declarationCountIn(self.funcDeclarationCounts, name: key))
	end 'materializeDeclarationCount'

	// Record one function's `throws E` clause AND fold it into the hash — the one write, for `record`'s
	// reason. A NON-throwing function is not recorded (its default answer is "does not throw"), so the
	// common function costs no map entry and no hash movement, exactly as `recordParamConsume` treats a
	// borrow-only one.
	function recordThrows(name ByteArray, clause ThrowsClause)
		if not throwsClauseIsThrowing(clause) 'nonThrowing'
			return
		end 'nonThrowing'

		// ⚠ **THE OVERWRITE IS COMPARED, BECAUSE THE KEY IS NOT UNIQUE** — `recordParamDefaults`' rule at the
		// registry next door, and it is the same rule for the same reason. Two declarations may wear one name
		// legitimately, as an OVERLOAD SET, and the map keeps one of them; whether that matters is exactly
		// whether the two name the same error type, because that type is what every `try` at the shared name
		// decodes its flag as. The count beside it catches the third shape, a sibling that throws NOTHING and
		// so never reaches this recorder at all (`FunctionDeclarationCountMap`).
		//
		// ⚠ **THE VERDICT IS NOT MIXED INTO THE HASH**, for `recordParamDefaults`' reason: it is DERIVED from
		// the clauses this routine mixes one per declaration, so a program that gains or loses a throwing
		// sibling moves the hash through the write that made it.
		if let incumbent = try self.funcThrows.get(name) 'anotherThrowingDeclarationOfThisName'
			self.noteRepeatedDeclaration(self.funcThrowingDeclarationCounts, name: name)
			if not throwingClauseTypeName(incumbent).equals(throwingClauseTypeName(clause)) 'twoErrorTypes'
				self.disagreeingThrows.insert(name)
			end 'twoErrorTypes'
		end 'anotherThrowingDeclarationOfThisName'

		self.funcThrows.upsert(name, value: clause)
		// The THROWS TYPE NAME rides too — a callee that changes which error it throws changes what type a
		// caller's `(e)` binding recovers — and it rides through the SHARED `mixThrowsClause`, so a function's
		// clause and an interface requirement's clause enter the hash the one way.
		self.hash = mixThrowsClause(mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeThrows), bytes: name), clause: clause)
	end 'recordThrows'

	// ⭐⭐ **DO THIS NAME'S DECLARATIONS DISAGREE ABOUT WHAT THEY THROW? — the question
	// `Parser.requireOverloadableName` refuses on (W77).** `paramDefaultsCollide`'s twin one registry over,
	// and the same two lines, because it is the same question over a different published fact: they name two
	// error types, or a sibling throws nothing at all.
	//
	// ✅ **IT ANSWERED *WHY* AND NOT MERELY *WHETHER* UNTIL W78, AND IT NO LONGER HAS TO.** Two of the three
	// answers it distinguished were not about the declarations at all but about the KEY they were filed
	// under — a `static`/instance pair's clause filed under the one name the source wrote, and a
	// directory-contested free function's tallies left on the bare name — and each earned its own sentence at
	// the refusal because there was genuinely nothing to compare. The sweep now files a declaration's clause
	// and its tallies under the key the parser asks with, so both keys have a verdict of their own and the
	// union collapses to the bool it always was underneath.
	export function throwsClauseCollide(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.declarationsDisagreeAbout(self.funcThrows.contains(name), disagreeing: self.disagreeingThrows, publishedBy: self.funcThrowingDeclarationCounts, name: name)
	end 'throwsClauseCollide'

	// ⭐ **THE CALLEE'S `throws` CLAUSE, or `none`** — the door a call site asks: does this callee throw,
	// and what error type does it throw? `none` (no recorded entry) is every non-throwing function and the
	// common case. Read by the parser (to type an `(e)` binding) and by SemanticCheck (E3055/E3057).
	export function throwsOf(callee ByteArray) returns ThrowsClause
		// ⭐ **A COMPILER-INTERNAL RUNTIME CALLEE IS NOT IN `funcThrows`, AND CANNOT BE (R4.1).** That map is
		// filled by the declaration sweep from DECLARED functions; `__mf_open_read` is minted at lowering and
		// no file declares it, so the map will never hold it however the sweep is extended.
		//
		// ⚠ **Until this arm existed the consequence was invisible and specific:** `try … otherwise (e)` bound
		// `e` with NO type, so `match e` fell to the scalar path and reported "enum-case patterns arrive in a
		// later wave" — a message about a missing FEATURE, for a program whose feature is present. MEASURED
		// against the control: the identical `match` over a USER enum's error compiles and runs, so the gap
		// was the binding's type, not the match.
		//
		// ⚠ WHICH families answer is `runtimeThrowsClause`'s to say, not this function's — see it for why a
		// family answering `none` is a scoped statement rather than an oversight. It is asked ONCE here so a
		// family added there is typed at every `(e)` binding without this door growing a second `match`.
		// (R4.2's `__ManagedMemory` members were the second family, and they were added in review: the rung
		// declared an eight-case error enum that nothing could discriminate.)
		let runtimeClause = runtimeThrowsClause(callee)
		if throwsClauseIsThrowing(runtimeClause) 'runtimeCallee'
			return runtimeClause
		end 'runtimeCallee'

		return try self.funcThrows.get(callee) otherwise ThrowsClause.none
	end 'throwsOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **ADD ONE DECLARED INTERFACE TO THE INDEX AND FOLD IT INTO THE HASH (R8)** — the one write, for
	// `record`'s reason. Last-wins on a duplicate interface name (a duplicate declaration is the real parse's
	// to report, with a position), exactly as `returnTypes` is.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHAT RIDES THE HASH IS WHAT A *PARSE* COPIES OUT OF THIS DECLARATION, AND THAT IS LESS THAN THE
	// RECORD HOLDS.** This hash keys every parse memo, so the rule is the one stated at `ProgramSignatures.hash`
	// and sharpened at "MIX WHAT A READER COPIES": a fact rides iff changing it changes what ANOTHER file
	// compiles to while that file's own bytes stay put.
	//
	//   • The `uses` ARITY rides (R7's fact, unchanged): it decides whether the comma in `implements Duo with
	//     A, B` opens a second ARGUMENT or a second INTERFACE, so it changes what a conformance in any file
	//     PARSES — a reader that did not re-parse would go on binding the old count.
	//   • Each method's NAME, RETURN TYPE, FORMAL TYPES, FORMAL NAMES and `throws` ride: a witness dispatch in
	//     any file picks its table SLOT, its result type, its `Self`-formal rejection, its `name:` label
	//     vocabulary and its calling ABI off exactly those five (`Parser.findWitnessDispatchCandidates`), and every one
	//     of them is baked into that file's emitted code. A dispatch compiled against the previous `throws`
	//     reads a flag register the callee never writes.
	//   • **`extendsInterfaces` RIDES, and it did NOT until R10c — which is a fact about the PARSE changing, not
	//     a correction.** The claim it used to carry ("nothing in a PARSE reads it") was true while a witness
	//     dispatch resolved against `iface.methods` alone. It stopped being true the moment the dispatch began
	//     numbering its slot against the interface's TRANSITIVE requirement list: adding `extends Base` to an
	//     interface now moves every slot index a `where T is Derived` dispatch compiles into ANOTHER file,
	//     while that file's own bytes stay put — the exact test this rule states. Only the parent NAMES ride;
	//     the parents' own methods reach the hash through their own entries, since one hash is folded over
	//     every declaration in the program.
	//   • **`isStatic` RIDES, and it did NOT until the A2w review — `extendsInterfaces`' story exactly, one
	//     rung later.** The claim it used to carry ("nothing in a PARSE reads it; `ConformanceCheck` does, but
	//     off `project.interfaces`") was true while a witness dispatch bound whatever requirement the name
	//     search reached. A2w made a PARSE read it: `Parser.requireWitnessDispatchCandidates` REFUSES a
	//     dispatch whose requirements are all `static` (E3116), and `IrInterface.witnessTargetIsProtocol`
	//     refuses a `static` look-alike as `Equatable`/`Comparable` (E3005). So `function origin()` becoming
	//     `static function origin()` here decides whether a `where T is Origin` body in ANOTHER file compiles
	//     at all, while that file's own bytes stay put — the exact test this rule states. It is NOT the
	//     "sound, and useless" key: a receiver kind is not a payload nobody outside the file can observe, and
	//     the edit that moves it is rare.
	//     MEASURED before the fix (`verify-warm-rebuild`, 2-file project + stdlib): a `static`-flag edit
	//     re-parsed **1** file where the property demands **8**. `VerifyWarmRebuild.ReceiverKindEditBefore`
	//     is the probe, and it is the only one here that can see this — the other four move a NAME or a
	//     VALUE, and a key mixing those moves on all of them.
	export function recordInterfaceDeclaration(iface IrInterface)
		self.interfaces.upsert(iface.name, value: iface)

		// ⭐ **AN INTERFACE'S ASSOCIATED TYPES ARE TYPE PARAMETERS AND THEY MINT TOKENS (W14)** — inside
		// `interface Seq uses Element`, a written `Element` resolves to `typeParameter` exactly as a `type`'s
		// `uses` name does (`Parser.readInterfaceDeclaration` installs them in the same field), so the token
		// ledger has to know them or a `Seq`'s `Element` and a conformer's would be a token with no owner.
		// The one other declaration form that installs a parameter scope — a TYPE extension's body — borrows
		// the extended type's own list, which `recordStruct` has already filed.
		for i in 0 upto iface.associatedTypeNames.count() 'eachAssociatedType'
			self.recordTypeParamToken(iface.name, paramName: try iface.associatedTypeNames.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.recordInterfaceDeclaration: associatedTypeNames.get OOB at {i} below count {iface.associatedTypeNames.count()}"), position: i)
		end 'eachAssociatedType'

		// The declaration-order handle, minted in the SAME act as the map entry (see
		// `interfaceNamesInDeclOrder`). A name already present keeps its index — a redeclaration, and a user
		// type sharing a synthesized protocol's name, both resolve through `lookupInterface` at the asking
		// end, so one position per NAME is exactly right and nothing here has to decide which wins.
		if not self.interfaceDeclIndexByName.contains(iface.name) 'firstDeclarationOfThisName'
			self.interfaceDeclIndexByName.upsert(iface.name, value: self.interfaceNamesInDeclOrder.count())
			self.interfaceNamesInDeclOrder.push(iface.name)
		end 'firstDeclarationOfThisName'

		self.hash = mix(mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeInterface), bytes: iface.name), value: iface.associatedTypeNames.count())
		for parentName in iface.extendsInterfaces 'eachParent'
			self.hash = mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeInterfaceParent), bytes: parentName)
		end 'eachParent'
		for method in iface.methods 'eachMethod'
			self.hash = mixBytes(mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeInterfaceMethod), bytes: method.name), bytes: method.returnTypeName)
			self.hash = mixInterfaceFormals(self.hash, names: method.paramNames, types: method.paramTypeNames)
			self.hash = mixThrowsClause(self.hash, clause: method.throwsClause)
			self.hash = mix(self.hash, value: ReceiverShapeAbsent if method.isStatic else ReceiverShapePresent)
		end 'eachMethod'
	end 'recordInterfaceDeclaration'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE DECLARED-INTERFACE STORE A *PARSE* MAY ASK, HANDED OUT WHOLE (R8; widened to the map by
	// R10c)** — the whole-program half of the parse-time answer, `structOf`'s twin. `project.interfaces` is not
	// merged until every file has parsed, so this map is the only interface store that exists mid-parse.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT REPLACED AN `interfaceOf(name)` LOOKUP, AND THE REASON IS THIS RUNG'S OWN THESIS.** That
	// accessor answered for ONE name, so its caller (`Parser.lookupInterfaceForParse`) had to spell the
	// "synthesized protocol FIRST, then the store" rule for itself — a second spelling of
	// `IrInterface.lookupInterface`, which the header of the one it collapsed forbids in so many words:
	// *"what may NOT stand is two readers in the SAME phase"*. R10c made that live rather than latent: a
	// witness dispatch resolves its CONSTRAINT's interface through the parser's spelling and every `extends`
	// PARENT through `lookupInterface`, one call apart. Two rules that agree today and would diverge silently
	// — a parent that resolves for the slot walk but not for the constraint is a wrong slot COUNT, with no
	// diagnostic. Handing out the store instead of an answer leaves exactly one statement of the rule.
	export function interfaceMap() returns IrInterfaceMap
		return self.interfaces
	end 'interfaceMap'

	// The declaration-order handle for an interface a dispatch resolved against — see `interfaceNamesInDeclOrder`
	// for what a `witnessDispatch` needs it for. It throws rather than returning a sentinel: every caller
	// holds an `IrInterface` that came OUT of `interfaceMap()`, so a miss is a compiler defect and the caller
	// says so.
	export function interfaceDeclIndexOf(name ByteArray) returns InterfaceDeclIndex throws MapError
		return try self.interfaceDeclIndexByName.get(name)
	end 'interfaceDeclIndexOf'

	// The interface NAME an `interfaceDeclIndexOf` handle names — the read half, asked by `LowerMaxonToStd`
	// once per witness call. It answers a NAME and not a declaration for the reason
	// `interfaceNamesInDeclOrder` gives: the caller resolves it through `lookupInterface`, with the map its
	// own phase holds, so there is one rule for what a name denotes and this is not a second one.
	export function interfaceNameAtDeclIndex(index InterfaceDeclIndex) returns ByteArray
		return try self.interfaceNamesInDeclOrder.get(index) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.interfaceNameAtDeclIndex: index {index} is outside the {self.interfaceNamesInDeclOrder.count()} interface positions — a witness dispatch is naming an interface this program does not have, which means the op outlived the signature index it was numbered against")
	end 'interfaceNameAtDeclIndex'

	// Add one declared struct type to the index AND fold it into the hash. The one write, for
	// `record`'s reason: the table and the hash that keys every parse memo on it cannot disagree
	// about what the index contains.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE HASH CARRIES THE FIELD LIST, NOT MERELY THE NAME**, and that is `recordAlias`'s
	// argument about ranges applied to a layout. Every reader of `type Point` COPIES its layout into
	// its own code — `p.y` becomes `loadIndirect … offset 8`, and `Point.create` becomes an
	// `__mm_alloc` of 16 — while the reading file's own bytes never move. So reordering Point's
	// fields, renaming one, or adding a third changes what every other file COMPILES TO, and only
	// this hash can carry it. Without it, adding a field would leave every reader inlining the old
	// offsets against a box that is now a different shape: a silent out-of-bounds read.
	//
	// ⚠ **EVERY FACT A FIELD CARRIES RIDES THIS HASH, AND `mixField` IS THE ONE PLACE THEY DO.** The
	// two P1.1a-wave-2 facts are here for the reason the field list is, one each way:
	//   • a DEFAULT changes CODEGEN — `export var value = 0` → `= 1` moves the `literal` op every
	//     other file's `Self{}` emits, while that file's own bytes never move;
	//   • MUTABILITY changes ACCEPTANCE — `var count` → `let count` must turn every other file's
	//     `c.count = 5` into E2013, and a reader that did not re-parse would go on compiling it.
	//
	// v1 is the cautionary tale, and its bug is live: `StdlibCache.maxon:1494-1506` serializes 6 of
	// `StructField`'s 7 payload fields — `isMutable` survives, `hasDefault` was never written — so
	// every cached stdlib field arrives claiming it has no default. A hand-listed copy of a record's
	// fields is a copy that can be short by one, silently. Keep the list in ONE function.
	function recordStruct(layout StructLayout, declaredByTheCorpus bool)
		// ⭐ **THE PARAMETER TOKENS ARE FILED FIRST, AHEAD OF EVERY REASON THIS FOLD MIGHT DROP THE LAYOUT
		// (W14).** A token is a fact about the DECLARATION'S TEXT, not about whether the registry keeps its
		// layout — and the real parse reads the body of a contested declaration (`type Array uses Element`
		// under a compiler-owned name) even while its E2015 is being reported, minting `typeParameter` values
		// whose owner a later instance query must still be able to name. Filing them here costs one map entry
		// per declared parameter and removes a whole class of "this token has no owner" panic.
		for i in 0 upto layout.typeParamCount() 'eachDeclaredTypeParam'
			self.recordTypeParamToken(layout.name, paramName: try layout.typeParamNames.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.recordStruct: typeParamNames.get OOB at {i} below count {layout.typeParamCount()}"), position: i)
		end 'eachDeclaredTypeParam'

		// ⭐⭐ **A DECLARED `type` UNDER A COMPILER-OWNED NAME MAY COME FROM THE CORPUS AND FROM NOTHING ELSE
		// — `upsertDeclaredEnum`'s rule, one registry over, now spelled the SAME WAY (W16).**
		//
		// The parse-time reject
		// (`Parser.requireTypeNameNotCompilerOwned`, E2015) is what the AUTHOR sees, but it cannot be what
		// keeps the registry honest: the declaration SWEEP is deliberately not a validator, so it folds the
		// declaration in before the real parse gets to refuse it, and every reader that resolves the name
		// through this registry then follows the user's declaration while `denotedNamedType` erases the name
		// to the builtin's own type.
		//
		// ⚠ MEASURED, on `type Codepoint … end` with `stdlib/helpers/string/utf16.maxon` whitelisted: the
		// E2015 was reported correctly AND `utf16Width`'s `codepoint < 65536` became
		// **`E3005 cannot compare struct with int`, TWICE, positioned inside a stdlib file the author never
		// opened** — the user's struct having captured the parameter type of a module they did not write.
		// Dropping the declaration at the single write makes the collision unrepresentable rather than merely
		// diagnosed, which is the property the enum registry already has and for the same stated reason.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **THE PROVENANCE BIT IS W16's, AND IT CURES TWO REACHABLE COMPILER PANICS.** This arm used to
		// drop EVERY declaration of such a name, corpus included, on the stated premise that no listed module
		// could ever declare one. That premise described the loader's whitelist, not the language — and the
		// two doors over this one rule had parted: `requireTypeNameNotCompilerOwned` ADMITS a stdlib file
		// (`livesUnderStdlibDirectory`), so the real parse walked into `type String`'s body while this write
		// had just thrown its layout away. The parser then asked for the layout of the declaration it was
		// standing inside and found none.
		// ⚠ MEASURED on the unchanged tree, and they are PANICS rather than diagnostics:
		//   `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/String.maxon`     ⇒ panic, `Parser.enclosingLayout`
		//   `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/Character.maxon`  ⇒ panic, `Parser.requireConstructible`
		// Both blamed the declaration sweep for disagreeing with `parseTypeDeclaration` about what opens a
		// type declaration. They agreed perfectly; it was this REGISTRY that dropped what both had read.
		// ⇒ The rule is now the one sentence `upsertDeclaredEnum` already states — **a compiler-owned type
		// name may be declared by the CORPUS and by nothing else** — and a USER declaration is dropped here
		// exactly as before, which is what keeps `isCompilerOwnedTypeName`'s two measured silent wrong answers
		// (a user `hash()` answering 7 where djb2 answers 5381; `Set with String` counting 3 where the control
		// says 2) closed. `specs-shv2/string-conformance.md`'s two `may-not-bind-the-name` cases pin it.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **THE PREDICTION THIS ARM CARRIED CAME TRUE, AND THE ANSWER IS RECORDED RATHER THAN THE
		// WARNING (W16 review, settled by W115/W129).** The old header said *"the day a listed module declares
		// one of these names, this arm gains the same bit"*, and named `CharacterSet` as the sharp case: the
		// compiler registered its own layout under the RESERVED spelling `__CharacterSet` while the user-facing
		// name was the bare one, so `containsStruct` answered FALSE for `CharacterSet` — a fact
		// `Parser.requireDeclaredBaseTypeName` was written against — and listing the module would land a corpus
		// layout here under the BARE name, giving one concept two layouts under two keys.
		// ⇒ **`W115` listed it and `W129` deleted the second layout, which is the cure the whole class takes:
		// KEEP THE RESERVATION, DROP THE SECOND LAYOUT.** `String`, `Character` and `StringIndex` each went the
		// same way before it. The bit below is what makes the corpus declaration WIN rather than be dropped, and
		// it is the only thing standing between two numberings of one type.
		// A struct layout MINTS an identity of its own, so it can agree with no compiler-owned meaning — see
		// `DeclaredTypeMeaning.nominalIdentity`.
		if declarationContestsACompilerOwnedName(layout.name, declaredByTheCorpus: declaredByTheCorpus, declares: DeclaredTypeMeaning.nominalIdentity) 'aUserContest'
			return
		end 'aUserContest'

		// The declaration-order handle, minted in the SAME act as the map entry — `recordInterfaceDeclaration`'s
		// discipline, for the reason stated at `structNamesInDeclOrder`. Asked BEFORE the upsert, so a
		// redeclaration of a name (E3006's business, reported elsewhere) keeps its one position.
		if not self.structTypes.contains(layout.name) 'firstDeclarationOfThisName'
			self.structNamesInDeclOrder.push(layout.name)
		end 'firstDeclarationOfThisName'

		self.structTypes.upsert(layout.name, value: layout)

		var state = mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeStruct), bytes: layout.name)
		// The type PARAMETERS ride the hash (P1.6-A): a change to `uses T` — a rename, or a change in
		// COUNT — changes which `Box with …` instantiations are the right arity in any file, so a reader's
		// parse memo must move with it. Mixed BEFORE the fields (a field's `typeParameter(token)` is only
		// meaningful against this list).
		for i in 0 upto layout.typeParamCount() 'eachTypeParam'
			let paramName = try layout.typeParamNames.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.recordStruct: typeParamNames.get OOB at {i}")
			state = mixBytes(mix(state, value: DeclShapeTypeParam), bytes: paramName)
		end 'eachTypeParam'
		// The `where` constraints ride the hash (P1.7a slice 2a): a change to `where T is Digest` changes
		// which `Box with …` are legal (E3017) and which witness table a dispatch reads in any file, so a
		// reader's parse memo must move with it. Each entry's constrained-param INDEX and interface NAME are
		// mixed, in list order — the witness-slot order depends on that order too.
		for i in 0 upto layout.whereConstraintCount() 'eachConstraint'
			let constraint = try layout.whereConstraints.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.recordStruct: whereConstraints.get OOB at {i}")
			state = mixBytes(mix(mix(state, value: DeclShapeWhereConstraint), value: constraint.typeParamIndex), bytes: constraint.interfaceName)
		end 'eachConstraint'
		// The `implements` list rides too — see `mixDeclaredConformances`. For a `type` it decides which
		// INTERFACE EXTENSION methods the type has, so adding or removing a name here adds or removes whole
		// functions from the program while the extension's own file never moves.
		state = mixDeclaredConformances(state, conformsTo: layout.conformsTo, shape: DeclShapeDeclaredConformance)
		// And its `with` BINDINGS, for the sharper version of that same reason: they decide whether a
		// CONDITIONAL extension's methods exist on this type at all (`ConformanceCheck.checkExtensionConstraints`), so
		// editing `implements Holder with Score` to `with Tally` adds or removes functions from the program
		// with neither the extension's file nor this type's field list moving.
		state = mixConformanceBindings(state, bindings: layout.conformanceBindings)
		for i in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachField'
			state = self.mixField(state, layout: layout, index: i)
		end 'eachField'
		self.hash = state
	end 'recordStruct'

	// Add one declared enum/union to the index AND fold it into the hash — `recordStruct`'s twin.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE HASH CARRIES THE CASE LIST AND THE TAGS, NOT MERELY THE NAME**, for `recordStruct`'s
	// reason applied to an enum. Every reader of `enum Color` COPIES its facts into its own code:
	// `Color.red` becomes an i64 `literal` of the tag, and a `match` over it becomes a chain of
	// compares against the tags plus an exhaustiveness check over the case NAMES. So changing a tag
	// (`red = 5`), reordering the cases (which moves a range arm's coverage), renaming one, or adding
	// a case changes what every other file COMPILES TO — while the reading file's own bytes never
	// move. Only this hash can carry it. The `isUnion` bit rides too: `enum` → `union` changes the
	// word a non-exhaustive-match diagnostic prints, and a reader that met that diagnostic must
	// re-parse to reprint it.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AND EACH CASE'S PAYLOAD SLOTS RIDE (BATCH22)**, for that same rule one column further in — the case
	// name and tag alone said nothing about what a `mem(x)` binding LOADS. See `mixPayloadField`.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AND THE BACKING RIDES, BECAUSE THE TAG COLUMN CANNOT CARRY IT (A4o).** `enum Z  z = 0` and
	// `enum Z  z = 0.0` fold to the IDENTICAL name + tag (`floatLiteralBits(0.0)` is 0), and a reading file
	// compiles `"{z}"` to `__int_to_string` for one and `__float_toString` for the other. Editing the `0`
	// to `0.0` would otherwise leave every other file's parse memo valid and serve a stale answer, which is
	// exactly the class of staleness this hash exists to prevent — see the rule at `recordStruct`.
	function recordEnum(layout EnumLayout, declaredByTheCorpus bool)
		upsertDeclaredEnum(self.enumTypes, layout: layout, declaredByTheCorpus: declaredByTheCorpus)

		var state = mix(mix(mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeEnum), bytes: layout.name), value: EnumKindUnion if layout.isUnion else EnumKindEnum), value: enumBackingDiscriminator(layout.backing))
		// ⭐⭐ **THE BACKING STRUCT'S NAME, WHICH THE DISCRIMINATOR ABOVE CANNOT CARRY.** `Meta` and `Other`
		// are both `structRecord`, so the backing bit says only THAT a struct backs the cases, never WHICH —
		// and which one it is decides what every reading file compiles a `.rawValue.<field>` to, because the
		// field POSITIONS come from that struct. Concretely: two structs with different field orders, and
		// `add = Meta.create(1)` edited to `Other.create(1)`. The argument is positional so no field name
		// moves, the constant is unchanged and the discriminator is unchanged — without this line the decl
		// hash does not move while a reader compiles a different constant. That is exactly the staleness
		// class `mixEnumCase`'s raw-value line exists for, and it is a per-DECLARATION fact rather than a
		// per-case one, so it rides here and not there. `EmptySourceText` for every other backing.
		state = mixBytes(state, bytes: layout.backingStructName)
		// ⭐⭐ **WHETHER ANY CASE *WROTE* A RAW VALUE, WHICH NOTHING ELSE FOLDED HERE CAN CARRY (W81).**
		// `enum C red green` and `enum C red = 0 green = 1` agree on the name, the kind, the backing
		// discriminator, the backing struct name, the conformances and — case by case — on the name, the tag
		// and (the identity being `theTag`) nothing further. So the two declarations hashed IDENTICALLY, while
		// a file interpolating one of them compiles `green` for the first and `1` for the second: a warm
		// rebuild across that edit would have served a parse of the reading file that renders the other one.
		// A per-DECLARATION fact, so it rides here beside the struct name and not in `mixEnumCase`.
		state = mix(state, value: EnumDeclaresRawValues if layout.declaresRawValues else EnumAutoTagged)
		// The `implements` list rides for the field list's reason (A1s wave 2) — see `mixDeclaredConformances`,
		// which states it once for both declaration kinds.
		state = mixDeclaredConformances(state, conformsTo: layout.conformsTo, shape: DeclShapeDeclaredConformance)
		for i in 0 upto layout.caseCount() 'eachCase'
			state = self.mixEnumCase(state, layout: layout, index: i)
		end 'eachCase'
		self.hash = state
	end 'recordEnum'

	// Add one generic-instance typealias to the index AND fold it into the hash (P1.6-A) —
	// `recordStruct`'s twin. The base NAME is interned into this index's own interner, the args are
	// REMAPPED into it (they were minted against the scanning file's interner), the instance is
	// interned to a `GenericInstanceId`, and the alias name is mapped to it. Last-wins on a duplicate
	// alias (a duplicate typealias is reported elsewhere), exactly as `recordAlias` is.
	function recordGenericAlias(entry GenericAliasEntry, remap TypeNameRemap)
		let baseId = self.typeNames.intern(entry.baseName)
		let args = self.resolveGenericArgs(entry, remap: remap)
		let giid = self.genericInstances.intern(baseId, args: args, fixedSize: entry.fixedSize)
		// ⭐ **THE PRIOR PROBE (N3)** — asked BEFORE the upsert, because after it every name is present. A name
		// two declarations mention is the only kind that can ever be contested, so this is what lets
		// `settleGenericAliasContest` cost nothing at all for a program with none.
		if self.genericAliases.contains(entry.aliasName) 'anotherDeclarationAlreadyMentionsThisName'
			if self.multiplyDeclaredGenericAliases is sharedEmptyDisagreeingAliasNames 'stillShared'
				self.multiplyDeclaredGenericAliases = DisagreeingAliasNameSet.create()
			end 'stillShared'
			self.multiplyDeclaredGenericAliases.insert(entry.aliasName)
		end 'anotherDeclarationAlreadyMentionsThisName'

		self.genericAliases.upsert(entry.aliasName, value: giid)
		// ⭐ The retained copy carries the instance THIS declaration interned to (N3) — see
		// `GenericAliasEntry.sweptInstance` for why the bare map above cannot answer that question for the
		// names that need it answered.
		self.genericAliasDecls.push(entry.recorded(giid))

		// ⭐ The ELEMENT SPELLING (A2m) — the one fact `giid` structurally cannot carry, since
		// `Array with __ManagedMemory` and `Array with ByteArray` intern to the identical instance. Recorded
		// beside the instance it belongs to, so an alias cannot end up in one table and not the other.
		if entry.elementIsBuffer 'elementNamesTheBuffer'
			self.bufferElementAliases.insert(entry.aliasName)
		end 'elementNamesTheBuffer'

		// ⭐ The BASE SPELLING (W43) — the same fact one position up, filed beside the instance for the same
		// reason: `__ManagedMemory with T` and `Array with T` intern identically, so only this can say which
		// surface a value declared with this alias wears.
		if entry.baseIsBuffer 'baseNamesTheBuffer'
			self.bufferSurfaceAliases.insert(entry.aliasName)
		end 'baseNamesTheBuffer'

		// Record the instantiation SITE (P1.7a slice 2a) for the E3017 where-constraint check — only when the
		// alias carries a real source anchor (a top-level `typealias X = Base with Args`; a synthesized alias
		// has none). The `giid` + anchor is all the check needs: the base's constraints and the substituted args
		// are read off `giid`.
		if entry.nameLine != 0 'hasAnchor'
			self.genericAliasSites.push(GenericInstantiationSite.create(giid, nameLine: entry.nameLine, nameColumn: entry.nameColumn, filePath: entry.filePath))
		end 'hasAnchor'

		// The alias name, its base name, its ELEMENT SPELLING and each arg's SHAPE ride the hash — a change to
		// any of them changes what a `IntBox.create(…)` in another file resolves and type-checks against, and
		// the spelling is the one `mixType` cannot carry (both arguments fold to one `genericInstance`).
		// ⭐ The SIZE rides for the element spelling's exact reason: it is half of the instance's identity and
		// `mixType` cannot carry it (`Vector with 3 Int` and `Vector with 4 Int` fold to the same base name and
		// the same one argument), so an edit from 3 to 4 would change what every `Vec3.create()` in another
		// file compiles to while this declaration's hashed bytes never moved.
		// ⭐ The BASE SPELLING rides in the SAME word (W43), for the element spelling's exact reason: it decides
		// the member SURFACE every declared position naming this alias wears, and `mixType` cannot carry it —
		// `__ManagedMemory with T` and `Array with T` fold to one `genericInstance`. A separate bit rather than
		// a second `mix`, so a declaration that changes only its base's SPELLING still moves the hash.
		let sizeBits = entry.fixedSize as ContentHash
		var state = mix(mix(mixBytes(mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeGenericAlias), bytes: entry.aliasName), bytes: entry.baseName), value: entry.spellingBits()), value: sizeBits)
		for arg in args 'eachHash'
			state = self.mixType(state, t: arg)
		end 'eachHash'
		self.hash = state
	end 'recordGenericAlias'

	// ⭐⭐ **RECORD ONE `interface` BODY'S GENERIC-INSTANCE TYPEALIASES (W61)** — `recordGenericAlias`'s twin,
	// differing in exactly two things and in nothing else: the entries land in `interfaceAliases` rather than
	// `genericAliases` (see that field for the ruling), and they do NOT ride the content hash.
	//
	// ⚠ **THEY DO NOT RIDE THE HASH BECAUSE NO PARSE COPIES THEM** — ARCHITECTURE's "MIX WHAT A READER
	// COPIES" rule. The index's hash keys every parse memo, and a requirement's rendered parameter type is
	// `Holder.ElementArray` whether this map holds the alias or not; the ONE consumer is `checkConformance`,
	// which runs on the merged project on every compile and memoizes nothing. Mixing it would re-parse the
	// whole program whenever an interface edited a private shorthand, which is the "sound, and useless"
	// pessimal key the query spine's header names.
	//
	// It takes the reader's own interner rather than a `TypeNameRemap` because the FOLD, unlike `foldFile`,
	// has no remap in hand: `foldInterfaceDeclarations` builds one reader per FILE and seats it at each site,
	// so the fold is per DECLARATION and the interner is the file's. The fold is skipped entirely for an
	// interface that declares no such alias — every interface in the corpus but a handful — so the walk of
	// the reader's interner is paid only where there is something to remap.
	export function recordInterfaceAliases(entries GenericAliasEntryArray, interfaceName ByteArray, fileTypeNames TypeNameInterner)
		if entries.count() == 0 'declaresNone'
			return
		end 'declaresNone'

		self.interfaceAliasOwners.insert(interfaceName)
		let remap = self.typeNames.foldInto(fileTypeNames)
		for entry in entries 'eachAlias'
			let baseId = self.typeNames.intern(entry.baseName)
			let args = self.resolveGenericArgs(entry, remap: remap)
			self.interfaceAliases.upsert(entry.aliasName, value: self.genericInstances.intern(baseId, args: args, fixedSize: entry.fixedSize))
		end 'eachAlias'
	end 'recordInterfaceAliases'

	// Does the program declare ANY interface-scoped generic-instance typealias? The whole-program gate the
	// conformance check gets its fast path from: a program with none — every program in the corpus outside
	// `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` and this rung's cases — can have no requirement to expand, so the check never
	// asks a second question.
	export function declaresInterfaceAliases() returns bool
		return self.interfaceAliases.count() != 0
	end 'declaresInterfaceAliases'

	// ⭐ **AND THE SAME QUESTION OF ONE INTERFACE — the gate that keeps the whole-program bool above from
	// being the whole answer.** `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` declares `InitableFromArrayLiteral`'s
	// `typealias ElementArray`, so the program-wide bool is TRUE for every program that loads the stdlib,
	// which is every program: used alone it would put every conformance in the corpus back on the
	// substituting path. Asked per DECLARING interface it is exact — a requirement can only spell an alias
	// its own interface declared — and it is a set membership rather than an allocation.
	export function interfaceDeclaresAlias(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.interfaceAliasOwners.contains(name)
	end 'interfaceDeclaresAlias'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A REQUIREMENT SPELLING `<Interface>.<member>` ACTUALLY REQUIRES, under this conformance's
	// bindings (W61)** — the alias's RIGHT-HAND SIDE with each of the declaring interface's associated types
	// replaced by what the conformance bound it to, rendered to the SAME canonical instance name the
	// conformer's own declared parameter renders to (`renderDeclaredTypeName` → `canonicalInstanceName`).
	// That is what makes the expansion arrive at `ConformanceCheck`'s ONE substitution site as an ordinary
	// substituted name, so the verdict and the diagnostic still read one string (R5).
	//
	// `boundSpellings` is parallel to `associatedTypeNames` — the caller resolves each name through the same
	// two-pair lookup every other position uses, so ORDER is: EXPAND first (the alias is what says an
	// associated type is used at all), then SUBSTITUTE into what the expansion exposed. The reverse order is
	// not merely slower, it is undefined: before expansion the requirement mentions no associated type.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT BUILDS THE NAME AND MUST NOT INTERN ONE.** Interning the substituted instance would MINT a
	// fresh `GenericInstanceId` on the FAILING path — the path where no conformer declares that type — long
	// after `deriveInstanceNames` warmed the set and while `checkTypeSymbolNamespace`'s compiled-name walk
	// still has the whole instance space to enumerate. A name is all any caller wants; the memoized namer is
	// still what names every part of it that did not move.
	export function expandInterfaceAlias(qualifiedName ByteArray, associatedTypeNames ByteArrayArray, boundSpellings ByteArrayArray, conformerName ByteArray, conformerTypeParams ByteArrayArray, declaringInterfaceName ByteArray) returns ByteArray throws MapError
		let id = try self.interfaceAliases.get(qualifiedName)
		return self.substitutedInstanceName(id, associatedTypeNames: associatedTypeNames, boundSpellings: boundSpellings, conformerName: conformerName, conformerTypeParams: conformerTypeParams, declaringInterfaceName: declaringInterfaceName)
	end 'expandInterfaceAlias'

	// The canonical name of `id` with every associated-type argument substituted — the recursion above seeds.
	// It mirrors `canonicalNameWithin`'s join (stem, then `_` and each argument's canonical form) and cannot
	// simply CALL it, because that one names the instance as interned and this one names the instance the
	// conformance means. A NESTED instance argument recurses, which is what makes
	// `typealias Nested = Array with (Array with Element)` expand as far as it is written.
	//
	// No cycle guard, and none is reachable: an interface alias's arguments are the ones `readGenericInstanceAlias`
	// parsed, and an argument naming another alias of the same interface is refused at that parse
	// (`E3011 Unknown type 'Holder.ElementArray'` — measured), so this walk descends only into instances
	// spelled inline and therefore strictly smaller than their parent.
	function substitutedInstanceName(id GenericInstanceId, associatedTypeNames ByteArrayArray, boundSpellings ByteArrayArray, conformerName ByteArray, conformerTypeParams ByteArrayArray, declaringInterfaceName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		var out = self.instanceNameStem(id)
		for arg in self.genericInstances.instanceArgs(id) 'eachArg'
			out.append("_")
			out.append(self.substitutedArgName(arg, associatedTypeNames: associatedTypeNames, boundSpellings: boundSpellings, conformerName: conformerName, conformerTypeParams: conformerTypeParams, declaringInterfaceName: declaringInterfaceName))
		end 'eachArg'
		return out.toByteArray()
	end 'substitutedInstanceName'

	// ONE argument of the walk above. Three answers, and only the first is this rung's: an argument that IS
	// one of the declaring interface's associated types becomes whatever the conformance bound that name to;
	// a nested instance recurses; anything else is named exactly as `canonicalTypeArgName` names it, which is
	// what keeps a concrete argument (`typealias Bytes = Array with Byte`, an interface declaring no `uses`
	// at all) reaching the same string the conformer's own declaration renders.
	function substitutedArgName(arg MaxonType, associatedTypeNames ByteArrayArray, boundSpellings ByteArrayArray, conformerName ByteArray, conformerTypeParams ByteArrayArray, declaringInterfaceName ByteArray) returns String
		// `typeParamPositionIn` is the ONE reader of a type-parameter token, and it is the stronger test than a
		// bare tag check: a token minted by some OTHER declaration fails it rather than subscripting this
		// interface's list at a position that happens to be in range.
		if maxonTypeTag(arg) == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'maybeAnAssociatedType'
			if let position = try typeParamPositionIn(typeNameIdOf(arg), declarationName: declaringInterfaceName, paramNames: associatedTypeNames) 'oneOfTheDeclarersOwn'
				return self.canonicalArgNameOfSpelling(try boundSpellings.get(position) otherwise panic("substitutedArgName: boundSpellings.get OOB at {position} — it is built parallel to associatedTypeNames, which is the list that position was just read out of"), conformerName: conformerName, conformerTypeParams: conformerTypeParams)
			end 'oneOfTheDeclarersOwn'
		end 'maybeAnAssociatedType'

		if maxonTypeTag(arg) == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'aNestedInstance'
			return String.from(self.substitutedInstanceName(typeNameIdOf(arg), associatedTypeNames: associatedTypeNames, boundSpellings: boundSpellings, conformerName: conformerName, conformerTypeParams: conformerTypeParams, declaringInterfaceName: declaringInterfaceName))
		end 'aNestedInstance'

		var path = GenericInstanceIdArray.create()
		return self.canonicalTypeArgName(arg, path: path)
	end 'substitutedArgName'

	// The canonical argument name of a SPELLING — a conformance's `with` argument is a source name, and this
	// is the one place one is turned into the string an interned argument of the same type would carry.
	// It routes BOTH answers through `canonicalTypeArgName`, the one speller, rather than restating either:
	// a name the conformer declares as its own type PARAMETER is that parameter's token (which is how the
	// conformer's own `Array with Element` was interned), and every other name denotes whatever it denotes —
	// a generic alias resolves through the alias table, a struct or ranged alias is its own name — which is
	// exactly the `named` leaf that speller already decides.
	//
	// ⚠ It INTERNS the spelling, and that is safe where interning an INSTANCE would not be: `intern` is
	// append-only and idempotent, nothing reads this interner by index after the fold, and a name carries no
	// place in the instance space `checkTypeSymbolNamespace` enumerates. The read-only half (`idOf`) cannot
	// serve here — a name it misses has no id to build a `MaxonType` from, and the miss is exactly the case
	// that still has to be named.
	function canonicalArgNameOfSpelling(spelling ByteArray, conformerName ByteArray, conformerTypeParams ByteArrayArray) returns String
		var path = GenericInstanceIdArray.create()
		if namesInclude(conformerTypeParams, name: spelling) 'theConformersOwnParameter'
			return self.canonicalTypeArgName(MaxonType.typeParameter(typeParamTokenFor(conformerName, paramName: spelling)), path: path)
		end 'theConformersOwnParameter'

		return self.canonicalTypeArgName(MaxonType.named(self.typeNames.intern(spelling)), path: path)
	end 'canonicalArgNameOfSpelling'

	// Resolve an alias entry's flat pre-order arg tree to concrete `MaxonType`s in THIS index's interner
	// (P1.6-B2) — interning nested instances DEPTH-FIRST. A leaf remaps its file-interner type through
	// `remap`; a nested instance header resolves its own args (advancing the shared cursor), interns the
	// instance, and yields `genericInstance(gid)`. The top-level list starts a FRESH cursor; a nested
	// instance shares its parent's, so both walk the flat tree through the one `resolveArgList` loop.
	function resolveGenericArgs(entry GenericAliasEntry, remap TypeNameRemap) returns MaxonTypeArray
		var cursor = ArgCursor.create()
		return self.resolveArgList(entry.args, count: entry.argCount, cursor: cursor, remap: remap)
	end 'resolveGenericArgs'

	// Resolve `count` consecutive pre-order arg nodes from `nodes` at `cursor`, advancing it past each — the
	// ONE loop the top-level alias args and a nested instance's args share (they differ only in whether the
	// cursor is fresh or their parent's). A nested instance is interned before the outer arg that holds it.
	function resolveArgList(nodes GenericArgNodeArray, count FieldIndex, cursor ArgCursor, remap TypeNameRemap) returns MaxonTypeArray
		var out = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		for _ in 0 upto count 'each'
			out.push(self.resolveArgNode(nodes, cursor: cursor, remap: remap))
		end 'each'
		return out
	end 'resolveArgList'

	function resolveArgNode(nodes GenericArgNodeArray, cursor ArgCursor, remap TypeNameRemap) returns MaxonType
		let node = try nodes.get(cursor.pos) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.resolveArgNode: cursor {cursor.pos} out of range for a {nodes.count()}-node arg tree — the pre-order flattening and its counts disagree")
		cursor.pos = cursor.pos + 1
		return match node 'k'
			leaf(argType) gives remapMaxonType(argType, remap: remap)
			instanceHeader(baseName, argCount, fixedSize) gives self.internNestedNode(baseName, argCount: argCount, fixedSize: fixedSize, nodes: nodes, cursor: cursor, remap: remap)
		end 'k'
	end 'resolveArgNode'

	function internNestedNode(baseName ByteArray, argCount FieldIndex, fixedSize FixedElementCount, nodes GenericArgNodeArray, cursor ArgCursor, remap TypeNameRemap) returns MaxonType
		let baseId = self.typeNames.intern(baseName)
		let args = self.resolveArgList(nodes, count: argCount, cursor: cursor, remap: remap)
		return MaxonType.genericInstance(self.genericInstances.intern(baseId, args: args, fixedSize: fixedSize))
	end 'internNestedNode'

	// Fold one case's NAME, TAG and PAYLOAD SLOTS into the enum's hash. Split out so the SIGNED tag
	// (`ParsedInt`) crosses to the UNSIGNED hash domain through a local — `recordAlias`/`mixDefaultPayload`
	// have the same two lines for the same reason (the bootstrap rejects the cast as unneeded, E3010, when
	// it is written inline as a nested `mix` argument).
	function mixEnumCase(state ContentHash, layout EnumLayout, index CaseIndex) returns ContentHash
		let named = mixBytes(state, bytes: layout.caseNameAt(index))
		let tagBits = layout.tagOfIndex(index) as ContentHash
		var folded = mix(named, value: tagBits)

		// ⚠ **A RAW VALUE THE TAG DOES NOT CARRY MUST RIDE SEPARATELY** — `a = "x"` and `a = "y"` fold to the
		// identical name + tag + backing, which is the same blind spot the backing discriminator above exists
		// for, one column further in. Where the tag IS the raw value it is already mixed on the line above, so
		// nothing more is owed. WHICH column holds it is `EnumLayout.rawValueIdentity`'s single answer, not a
		// second `== EnumBacking.string` test that a later backing could silently fall past — and the third
		// answer is the reason that classifier is not a bool: a struct backing's per-case value lives in
		// `caseBackingFields`, so `rawValueAt` cannot carry it.
		//
		// ⚠ **`rawValueAt` FOR A STRUCT-BACKED CASE IS NOT EMPTY — IT IS THE STRUCT'S NAME**
		// (`Parser.appendStructBackedEnumCase` writes `String.from(typeTok.value)`), and an earlier draft of
		// this comment said it was empty and used that as the reason for skipping it. The reason is the one
		// above; and because the struct's name is a per-DECLARATION fact rather than a per-case one, it is
		// mixed once by `recordEnum` instead of once per case here.
		match layout.rawValueIdentity() 'identity'
			theTag then break 'identity'
			recordedText then folded = mixBytes(folded, bytes: layout.rawValueAt(index).toByteArray())
			incomparable then folded = self.mixEnumBackingFields(folded, layout: layout, index: index)
		end 'identity'

		for field in layout.payloadsAt(index) 'eachSlot'
			folded = self.mixPayloadField(folded, field: field)
		end 'eachSlot'

		return folded
	end 'mixEnumCase'

	// ⭐ **FOLD A CASE'S CONSTANT STRUCT BACKING IN — `mixPayloadField`'s twin for the one backing whose value
	// lives in a column of its own.** The struct's NAME rides once per DECLARATION, mixed by `recordEnum`
	// beside the backing discriminator (see there), so what is owed here is per case: each entry's written
	// label, its POSITION, its GROUP and its value.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE POSITION AND THE GROUP RIDE EXPLICITLY, AND FOLDING ONLY THE ORDER WOULD NOW BE A COLLISION.**
	// The position used to BE the entry's place in this array, so walking in order carried it — that stopped
	// being true when a case's entries became a TREE held flat (`EnumBackingField`): `Outer.create(Inner.
	// create(1), flag: true)` and a shape whose two entries sit in the other group write the same labels and
	// the same numbers in the same order and mean different things.
	function mixEnumBackingFields(state ContentHash, layout EnumLayout, index CaseIndex) returns ContentHash
		var folded = state
		for field in layout.backingFieldsAt(index) 'eachField'
			let slotted = mix(folded, value: EnumBackingFieldSlot)
			let labelled = mixBytes(slotted, bytes: field.name)
			let placed = mix(mix(labelled, value: field.position), value: field.group)
			match field.value 'value'
				// The literal's KIND rides beside its bits: `1` and `1.0` are different declarations that fold
				// to the same `ParsedInt` (`floatLiteralBits(0.0)` is 0), and they type-check differently.
				literal(bits, kind) then folded = mix(mix(placed, value: bits), value: enumBackingLiteralDiscriminator(kind))
				enumCase(enumName, caseName) then folded = mixBytes(mixBytes(mix(placed, value: EnumBackingFieldEnumCase), bytes: enumName), bytes: caseName)
				// The nested struct's own NAME, because it is checked against the field's declared type and a
				// case naming a different one is a different declaration (`enumBackingNamedValueSuitsDomain`).
				// Its constants ride as the entries of the group it owns, folded by this same loop.
				nestedStruct(structName) then folded = mixBytes(mix(placed, value: EnumBackingFieldNestedStruct), bytes: structName)
			end 'value'
		end 'eachField'

		return folded
	end 'mixEnumBackingFields'

	// ⭐ **FOLD ONE PAYLOAD SLOT IN — `mixField`'s twin, and it rides for `recordStruct`'s rule (BATCH22).**
	// A reading file COPIES a payload's declared type into its own code: the `match` binding's load width, its
	// ownership class (moved out, retained, or a scalar copy), and whether the slot is a nested union at all
	// are all read off it (`classifyUnionPayload`). So editing `mem(m Bytes)` to `mem(m String)` changes what
	// every other file COMPILES TO while that file's own bytes never move — the exact staleness the case list
	// already rides for, one column further in, and the case NAME and TAG alone could not carry it.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND THE SPELLING RIDES BESIDE THE TYPE, for `FieldShapeSurface`'s reason** (this rung's own fact):
	// `__ManagedMemory` and `ByteArray` fold to the identical `genericInstance` through `mixType`, while a
	// binding destructured out of the two reaches a different member roster.
	function mixPayloadField(state ContentHash, field PayloadField) returns ContentHash
		let named = self.mixType(mixBytes(mix(state, value: PayloadShapeSlot), bytes: field.name), t: field.declaredType)
		return mixSurface(mix(named, value: PayloadShapeSurface), surface: field.declaredSurface)
	end 'mixPayloadField'

	// Fold EVERYTHING the compiler reads off field `index` into the index's hash. See `recordStruct`
	// for why each fact must ride, and why they must all ride from one place.
	function mixField(state ContentHash, layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex) returns ContentHash
		let fieldName = try layout.fieldNames.get(index) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.mixField: fieldNames.get OOB at {index}")
		let fieldType = try layout.fieldTypes.get(index) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.mixField: fieldTypes.get OOB at {index} — the field columns fell out of lockstep")

		let named = self.mixType(mixBytes(state, bytes: fieldName), t: fieldType)
		let withMutability = mix(named, value: FieldShapeMutable if layout.fieldIsMutable(index) else FieldShapeImmutable)
		// The declared SPELLING (A2j / A2m) — the one fact here `mixType` structurally cannot carry, since
		// `__ManagedMemory` and `ByteArray` fold to the identical `genericInstance`. See `FieldShapeSurface`.
		let withSpelling = mixSurface(mix(withMutability, value: FieldShapeSurface), surface: layout.fieldDeclaredSurface(index))
		return self.mixFieldDefault(withSpelling, defaultValue: layout.fieldDefault(index))
	end 'mixField'

	// The DEFAULT half of `mixField`. `none` mixes its own shape rather than nothing: a field that
	// gained a default must hash differently from one that never had it, and `= 0` on a field whose
	// payload would otherwise fold to the same 0 is exactly the collision that would hide it.
	// ⭐⭐ **THE HELPER ARM MIXES ITS PRESENCE AND NOTHING ELSE, AND THAT IS THE `recordParamDefaults`
	// RULING APPLIED VERBATIM (S2f).** What a READER of `type Bag` copies out of an expression default is
	// exactly one thing — `call __fieldDefault#Bag#items` — and those bytes are byte-identical whether the
	// default is `IntArray.create()` or `IntArray.withCapacity(4)`. The expression itself reaches only the
	// DECLARING file's own helper body, which re-parses on that file's own content hash. So mixing it would
	// be sound and WRONG: every keystroke inside any default would re-parse the whole program.
	//
	// ⚠ The two facts a reader DOES depend on both ride already, and neither needs an arm here: the helper's
	// NAME is `(type name, field name)`, both mixed by `mixField`/`recordStruct` above; and its RETURN TYPE
	// is the field's declared type, mixed by `mixField`'s own `mixType`. What is left is the presence BIT —
	// a field that gained an expression default must hash differently from one with none and from one with
	// a literal — which is this constant.
	function mixFieldDefault(state ContentHash, defaultValue FieldDefault) returns ContentHash
		return match defaultValue 'shape'
			none gives mix(state, value: FieldShapeNoDefault)
			literal(payload, tag) gives self.mixDefaultLiteral(state, payload: payload, tag: tag)
			helper gives mix(state, value: FieldShapeDefaultHelper)
		end 'shape'
	end 'mixFieldDefault'

	// The literal half of a defaulted field's hash. Split from `mixFieldDefault` so the payload
	// arrives as a bound PARAMETER: it is SIGNED (`ParsedInt` — and a float default's is a bit
	// pattern that routinely has its top bit set) while the hash domain is unsigned, so it crosses
	// by an explicit cast, and that cast needs a local. `recordAlias` has the same two lines for the
	// same reason: written inline as an argument to a nested `mix`, the bootstrap misreads it as
	// already-hashed and rejects the cast as unneeded (E3010) — a false positive that costs nothing
	// to route around.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TAG RIDES, AND THE PAYLOAD ALONE WOULD NOT CARRY IT.** A default is stored as the source
	// wrote it and widened only when it fills a slot (`Parser.fillFieldDefault`), so the tag is a real
	// input to what a reader compiles to — and it is the one input the payload cannot stand in for:
	// `= 0` and `= 0.0` have the SAME `ParsedInt` (zero is the one fixed point of int→f64 bits) and
	// differ only here. Hashing the pair keeps a memo from surviving an edit between them.
	//
	// It rides through `mixValueTag`, the door a top-level constant's tag already uses, and NOT through
	// the enum's ordinal — see the shape constants' own header: an ordinal is not a value in Maxon, and a
	// hash that moved when `ValueTypeTag`'s arms were REORDERED would invalidate every parse memo in the
	// project for a change that means nothing.
	function mixDefaultLiteral(state ContentHash, payload ParsedInt, tag ValueTypeTag) returns ContentHash
		let payloadBits = payload as ContentHash
		return mix(self.mixValueTag(mix(state, value: FieldShapeDefaulted), tag: tag), value: payloadBits)
	end 'mixDefaultLiteral'

	// Add one ranged typealias to the index AND fold it into the hash. The hash carries the RANGE
	// and not merely the name, and that is load-bearing: widening `int(0 to 100)` to
	// `int(0 to u64.max)` makes the type UNSIGNED, which changes what every `shr` on it MEANS. Every
	// file must re-parse on that edit, and this is what makes it happen.
	function recordAlias(alias RangedTypeAlias)
		// The collision outcome is DISCARDED here on purpose: E3061 and E3105 are each raised once, at
		// the merge (`commitTypeAliases`), which is the whole-program drain that owns
		// `project.diagnostics`. Reporting from the pre-parse sweep as well would double every
		// diagnostic — the sweep and the merge both see every declaration. The sweep still calls
		// `record` rather than a plain upsert so that THIS registry and the project's are built by one
		// function: a sweep that filed declarations differently would resolve a contested name
		// differently from the merge, which is the divergence file scoping exists to remove.
		_ = self.typeAliases.record(alias)

		// The bounds are SIGNED (`ParsedInt`) and the hash domain is UNSIGNED, so each crosses by an
		// explicit cast. Bound to locals rather than written inline: as arguments to a nested `mix`
		// the bootstrap misreads them as already-hashed and rejects the casts as unneeded (E3010) —
		// a false positive, and one that costs nothing to route around.
		let low = alias.low as ContentHash
		let high = alias.high as ContentHash

		// ⭐ **VISIBILITY RIDES FOR THE RANGE'S OWN REASON, now that P1.9 made it load-bearing** (review):
		// widening a range changes what a `shr` on a value of the alias means, and adding or removing
		// `export` changes what ANOTHER file's `as` cast to the name means (E2003, `visibleFrom`) and
		// whether this file's own declaration can be reported unused (E3062, `recordTypeName`). Both are
		// decisions a memoized parse of a DIFFERENT file bakes in, so both must move its memo key.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE WHOLE TIER, NOT ONE BIT (N1a).** It was one bit while every reader asked `!= Visibility.file`
		// and the only way to get `global` was to be a stdlib file, which no edit can change. Now `module` is a
		// modifier a user writes, and `module typealias Score` vs `export typealias Score` is a different answer
		// to another file's `as Score` — so the two tiers must key different parses. The ordinal is used rather
		// than a second bit because it is what a tier IS; a fourth case rides automatically.
		let exported = tierOrdinal(alias.visibility) as ContentHash

		let named = mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeAlias), bytes: alias.name)
		self.hash = mix(mix(mix(named, value: low), value: high), value: exported)
	end 'recordAlias'

	// Add one INNER ranged typealias (P1.6-C) under its BASE-QUALIFIED name AND fold it into the hash —
	// `recordAlias`'s twin. The RANGE rides for `recordAlias`'s reason (widening changes what a `shr` on a
	// value of the alias means), and the qualified name rides so that a rename or a new inner alias moves
	// every reader's parse memo — `Instance.Idx` resolves and type-checks against this in any file. The
	// stored `RangedTypeAlias.name` stays the BARE member (`Idx`); the key is `base.member`.
	function recordInnerAlias(baseName ByteArray, alias RangedTypeAlias)
		let qualified = joinQualifiedName(baseName, member: alias.name)
		self.innerAliases.upsert(qualified, value: alias)

		// Bound to locals for `recordAlias`'s reason (the bootstrap's E3010 false positive on an inline cast).
		let low = alias.low as ContentHash
		let high = alias.high as ContentHash

		let named = mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeInnerAlias), bytes: qualified)
		self.hash = mix(mix(named, value: low), value: high)
	end 'recordInnerAlias'

	// ⭐ **NOTE THAT AN `extension` BODY DECLARES A PER-INSTANCE NESTED `typealias` CALLED `qualified`** — see
	// `extensionInnerAliasMembers`. NAMES ONLY: the declaration itself is recorded a moment later by
	// `Parser.foldExtensionDeclarationInto`, through `recordInnerAlias` / `recordGenericAlias` /
	// `recordTupleAlias` like every other nested alias, so nothing about what the name MEANS is decided here.
	//
	// ⚠ It folds NOTHING into `self.hash`, and that is not an omission. The hash keys the parse memo on the
	// program's declarations, and this set is a pure function of tokens the three recorders above already mix
	// in under the same qualified name — the same declaration, seen one pass earlier. A second mix would key
	// the memo twice on one fact.
	export function recordExtensionInnerAliasMember(qualified ByteArray)
		self.extensionInnerAliasMembers.insert(qualified)
	end 'recordExtensionInnerAliasMember'

	// ⭐⭐ **NOTE ONE FREE-FUNCTION DECLARATION, AND ANSWER WHAT THE FOLD OWES IT (N1c).**
	//
	// **THE RULE THIS IMPLEMENTS:** two declarations of one bare name in DIFFERENT directories are both
	// accepted, and each is registered under its own namespace-qualified spelling — exactly as a method is
	// registered `Type.method`. Two in the SAME directory keep the bare name and collide as they always did
	// (`commitFuncSignatures`, E3006). ⇒ **a flat root-level pair still collides**, because root is one
	// directory; the relaxation reaches only pairs a directory tells apart.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS DECIDED ON MODULE DIRECTORIES AND NEVER ON RENDERED NAMESPACES**, for N1a's measured reason:
	// `namespaceForModuleDirectory` is not injective (`my/dir` and `my.dir` both render `my.dir`), so a
	// contest decided on the rendered form would merge two structurally different directories into one
	// namespace — the same defect the `.`-joined store produced for `Visibility.module`.
	//
	// It is v1's rule reached by a different route. v1 qualifies EVERY free function's key with its namespace
	// (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.maxon:2062-2074`), so cross-namespace pairs land in different slots
	// and never meet. shv2 qualifies ONLY a contested name, which gives the identical acceptance and identical
	// refusals while leaving every uncontested function — that is, every function in the corpus, in `stdlib/`
	// and in this compiler — registered under exactly the bytes it had before. Adopting v1's blanket
	// qualification would rename every symbol in every namespaced file to buy nothing this does not.
	export function noteFreeFunctionDeclaration(bareName ByteArray, moduleDirectory String) returns FreeFunctionContest
		if self.contestedFreeFunctions.contains(bareName) 'alreadyContested'
			self.addContestedSpelling(bareName, moduleDirectory: moduleDirectory)
			return FreeFunctionContest.alreadyContested
		end 'alreadyContested'

		let incumbentDirectory = try self.freeFunctionDirectories.get(bareName) otherwise 'firstDeclarationOfThisName'
			self.freeFunctionDirectories.upsert(bareName, value: moduleDirectory)
			return FreeFunctionContest.uncontested
		end 'firstDeclarationOfThisName'

		if incumbentDirectory.equals(moduleDirectory) 'sameDirectoryIsAnOrdinaryDuplicate'
			return FreeFunctionContest.uncontested
		end 'sameDirectoryIsAnOrdinaryDuplicate'

		// The INCUMBENT is filed alongside the newcomer, and its registration name is handed back so the fold
		// can move the entries it already wrote — the same "every declaration or none" invariant
		// `RangedAliasRegistry.record` keeps for a contested alias, and for the same reason: an index holding
		// only the newcomer's would answer with the WRONG declaration for the incumbent's own callers.
		// The INCUMBENT is filed BEFORE the newcomer so the candidate list reads in declaration order, and its
		// registration name is handed back so the fold can move the entries it already wrote.
		self.addContestedSpelling(bareName, moduleDirectory: incumbentDirectory)
		self.addContestedSpelling(bareName, moduleDirectory: moduleDirectory)

		return FreeFunctionContest.newlyContested(freeFunctionRegistrationName(bareName, moduleDirectory: incumbentDirectory))
	end 'noteFreeFunctionDeclaration'

	// File one contested declaration's qualified spelling.
	//
	// ⚠ **A ROOT DECLARATION CONTRIBUTES NONE, and the entry may therefore be created by the SECOND call** —
	// which is why the flag is the entry's existence and the two calls above are unconditional. A root
	// declaration has no qualified spelling to offer a user and no qualified key for a call to route
	// through; its entries keep the bare key, and a bare call finds it.
	function addContestedSpelling(bareName ByteArray, moduleDirectory String)
		if moduleDirectory.isEmpty() 'theRootHasNoQualifiedSpelling'
			return
		end 'theRootHasNoQualifiedSpelling'

		var spellings = try self.contestedFreeFunctions.get(bareName) otherwise ByteArrayArray.create()
		spellings.push(namespaceQualifiedName(namespaceForModuleDirectory(moduleDirectory), name: bareName))
		self.contestedFreeFunctions.upsert(bareName, value: spellings)
	end 'addContestedSpelling'

	// Is this bare name declared as a free function in more than one directory? The one flag every reader
	// asks — the parser's declaration mint, its call doors, and `SemanticCheck`'s E3095.
	export function isContestedFreeFunction(bareName ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.contestedFreeFunctions.contains(bareName)
	end 'isContestedFreeFunction'

	// Is this MANGLED name one a contested free function was filed under? Two readers, and they want it for
	// opposite reasons: `resolvesAsNamespaceQualifiedFunction`, so the type-reading veto does not decline the
	// free function's own registration name; and `commitFuncSignatures`, so a duplicate reported against such
	// a name can say WHERE the name came from — the user wrote neither `Point` nor `.` next to `create`, and
	// a bare "Duplicate function 'Point.create'" names a symbol that appears nowhere in the source.
	export function isContestedRegistrationName(mangled ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.contestedRegistrationNames.contains(mangled)
	end 'isContestedRegistrationName'

	// Every qualified spelling of a contested bare name, in DECLARATION order. `SemanticCheck` filters it by
	// what the reader may actually see before rendering E3095's candidate list.
	export function contestedFreeFunctionSpellings(bareName ByteArray) returns ByteArrayArray
		return try self.contestedFreeFunctions.get(bareName) otherwise sharedEmptyContestedSpellings
	end 'contestedFreeFunctionSpellings'

	// ⭐ **THE NAME A FREE FUNCTION DECLARED IN `moduleDirectory` IS REGISTERED UNDER once its bare name is
	// contested** — and the BARE name when that directory is the root, which is not a fallback but the rule:
	// the root is the unqualified namespace, so a root declaration has no qualifier to be named by.
	export function freeFunctionRegistrationName(bareName ByteArray, moduleDirectory String) returns ByteArray
		if moduleDirectory.isEmpty() 'theRootIsTheUnqualifiedNamespace'
			return bareName
		end 'theRootIsTheUnqualifiedNamespace'

		return namespaceQualifiedName(namespaceForModuleDirectory(moduleDirectory), name: bareName)
	end 'freeFunctionRegistrationName'

	// ⭐⭐ **MOVE THE *INCUMBENT'S* ALREADY-FOLDED SWEEP ENTRIES FROM THE BARE KEY TO ITS QUALIFIED ONE
	// (N1c).** ONE caller now: `foldFile`'s contest pass, at the moment a SECOND directory declares the name
	// and the first directory's entries — written when it was the only declaration — stop belonging on the
	// bare key. Every LATER declaration of a contested name files under its own key to begin with
	// (`sweepRegistrationKey`), so there is no second, end-of-fold refile and no A5l question about whose
	// entries the bare key is holding: the incumbent's are the only ones that were ever written there
	// unknowingly.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FOUR *RECORD-OWNED* FACTS ARE LEFT BEHIND AS A RESIDUE; THE FOUR BY-NAME ONES ARE NOT (W78).**
	// A bare name whose declarations all moved must still ANSWER "some declaration binds this name" — that is
	// what lets `SemanticCheck` report E3095 ("declared, twice, qualify it") where a removal would leave the
	// bare call reaching an undeclared callee. But a CONDITIONAL fact left behind is answered for a
	// declaration that never stated it (A5l): MEASURED, `beta.pick()` compiled by borrowing `alpha.pick`'s
	// parameter default, and `try beta.pick()` was accepted by borrowing its `throws` clause. So the two
	// unconditional facts and the two the return type implies stay as the residue, and the four the by-name
	// scans publish are struck — `clearByNameSweepEntries`' split, read in the other direction.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A ROOT DECLARATION NEEDS NO REFILE AT ALL, AND THAT IS W78's DELETION OF THE SHADOW.** The root is
	// the unqualified namespace, so `freeFunctionRegistrationName` hands it back the BARE name — which is now
	// the key nothing else writes, because every subdirectory contestant records under its own. Before the
	// re-key each of them overwrote the bare key on its way past and the root's entries had to be mirrored to
	// a reserved shadow and restored afterwards; there is nothing left to restore them from because there is
	// nothing left to overwrite them.
	//
	// ⚠ **NO HASH MIXING.** Every fact moved here was already mixed into the parse-memo hash under its bare
	// name by the record function that wrote it, and the qualified key is DERIVED from declarations that hash
	// already covers — mixing again would make the memo key depend on the move rather than on the program.
	// The same holds of the DEFAULT-HELPER rename: its inputs are this declaration's bare name and its module
	// directory, both of which already move the hash.
	export function refileContestedFreeFunction(bareName ByteArray, registrationName ByteArray)
		// The root incumbent keeps the key it already has. Its TALLY is materialized, because from this moment
		// on every declaration of the name is counted from the tally map rather than from `returnTypes` — see
		// `keyAlreadyHoldsADeclaration` for the residue that forces the two conventions apart.
		if registrationName.equals(bareName) 'aRootDeclarationAlreadyOwnsTheBareKey'
			self.materializeDeclarationCount(bareName, contested: true)
			return
		end 'aRootDeclarationAlreadyOwnsTheBareKey'

		self.claimContestedRegistrationName(bareName, registrationName: registrationName)
		if not self.contestedKeyIsTakeable(registrationName) 'anotherDeclarationHoldsThisKey'
			// ⚠ **THE INCUMBENT IS STRANDED ON THE BARE KEY, SO ITS TALLY IS MATERIALIZED HERE TOO (found at
			// review).** Declining the qualified key leaves these declarations counted under the bare name —
			// and from this line on the name is contested, which is to say counted from the tally map rather
			// than from `returnTypes` (`keyAlreadyHoldsADeclaration`). Without this write the map says nothing
			// about a key that already holds declarations, and a LATER contestant filing under the same bare
			// key — a ROOT one, whose registration name IS that key — would read "absent" as zero and count
			// itself the first. Every program that reaches this arm is refused by `commitFuncSignatures` for
			// the collision that sent it here, so nothing observes the difference today; the invariant is
			// stated in code rather than rested on that argument.
			self.materializeDeclarationCount(bareName, contested: true)
			return
		end 'anotherDeclarationHoldsThisKey'

		self.rekeyDeclarationSweepEntries(bareName, to: registrationName)
	end 'refileContestedFreeFunction'

	// ⭐⭐ **MOVE ONE ALREADY-FOLDED DECLARATION FROM THE KEY IT WAS FILED UNDER TO THE KEY A CONTEST HAS
	// JUST GIVEN IT — THE ONE RE-KEY, ASKED BY BOTH CONTESTS.** A declaration folded before its contest was
	// visible is filed under the name the source wrote, and everything about it has to travel together: the
	// eight facts a call site reads, the three tallies and two verdicts that judge them, and the names of the
	// helper functions its parameter defaults compile to. Both callers reach it at an instant when the source
	// key holds this declaration's facts and nothing else's — the free-function contest fires at the SECOND
	// directory, and the pair contest at the second member.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FOUR BY-NAME FACTS ARE STRUCK FROM THE SOURCE KEY, and that is not tidiness.** A conditional
	// fact left behind is answered for a declaration that never stated it (A5l): MEASURED, `beta.pick()`
	// compiled by borrowing `alpha.pick`'s parameter default, and `try beta.pick()` was accepted by borrowing
	// its `throws` clause. What the two callers do with the four `record` writes differs, and each says why.
	function rekeyDeclarationSweepEntries(from ByteArray, to ByteArray)
		self.copyDeclarationSweepEntries(from, to: to)
		self.moveDeclarationTallies(from, to: to)
		self.clearByNameSweepEntries(from)
		self.renameContestedParamDefaultHelpers(from, registrationName: to)
	end 'rekeyDeclarationSweepEntries'

	// ⭐⭐ **CLAIM THE DIRECTORY-QUALIFIED KEY THIS FILE'S DECLARATIONS OF A CONTESTED NAME WILL BE FILED
	// UNDER — AND RECORD WHETHER IT WAS ALREADY SOMEBODY ELSE'S (N1c review).**
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **A REGISTRATION NAME ANOTHER DECLARATION ALREADY OWNS IS NOT TAKEN — IT IS LEFT TO BE REPORTED.**
	// A contested free function is filed as `<directory>.<name>`, which is the SAME construction — and the
	// same flat key space — a method is filed under. So a `create` declared in a directory called `Point/`
	// claims the exact key `type Point`'s static factory holds, and until this guard the sweep OVERWROTE it.
	//
	// ⛔ **THAT OVERWRITE IS WHAT MADE THE COLLISION LOOK LIKE SOMETHING ELSE.** `commitFuncSignatures`
	// catches the duplicate at merge either way (MEASURED: E3006 for both the field-less program and the one
	// that never calls the name) — but the clobbered RETURN TYPE is read during the earlier PARSE, so
	// `let p = Point.create()` typed `p` as the free function's `int` and `p.x` died first, as
	// `E2015: a field access on 'p', which is declared 'int' and not a struct type`. A true refusal,
	// pre-empted by a false symptom of its own cause, and the position it named was the innocent line.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE ANSWER IS REMEMBERED BECAUSE THE TEST STOPS BEING TRUE UNDER THIS CONTEST'S OWN WRITES.** The
	// probe is "does some declaration already hold this key?", and once `alpha/`'s first `pick` has been
	// recorded under `alpha.pick` a SECOND `pick` in `alpha/` — an overload beside it, or a later file of the
	// same directory — would read its own sibling as the foreign owner and be stranded on the bare key alone.
	// So the decision is taken ONCE per (contest, directory), at the first moment this file touches the name,
	// and `sweepRegistrationKey` reads it back rather than re-deriving it.
	//
	// A root declaration has no qualified spelling to claim (`addContestedSpelling`'s rule, one registry
	// over), and the bare key is its own by construction, so it claims nothing.
	function claimContestedRegistrationName(bareName ByteArray, registrationName ByteArray)
		if registrationName.equals(bareName) 'aRootDeclarationHasNoQualifiedKeyToClaim'
			return
		end 'aRootDeclarationHasNoQualifiedKeyToClaim'

		if self.contestedRegistrationNames.contains(registrationName) 'thisContestHasAlreadyDecided'
			return
		end 'thisContestHasAlreadyDecided'

		// The spelling is filed whether or not the key turns out to be takeable: the contest HAPPENED, and both
		// the call door's type-reading veto and `duplicateFunctionMessage`'s sentence have to know it.
		self.contestedRegistrationNames.insert(registrationName)
		if self.returnTypes.contains(registrationName) 'aMethodKeyOfTheSameSpelling'
			self.contestedKeysAnotherDeclarationHolds.insert(registrationName)
		end 'aMethodKeyOfTheSameSpelling'
	end 'claimContestedRegistrationName'

	// May a contested free function's declarations be filed under their directory-qualified key? — the
	// remembered half of `claimContestedRegistrationName`. A key nothing else holds is takeable, which is
	// every ordinary contest: `alpha.pick` is a name only `alpha/`'s declaration mints.
	function contestedKeyIsTakeable(registrationName ByteArray) returns bool
		return not self.contestedKeysAnotherDeclarationHolds.contains(registrationName)
	end 'contestedKeyIsTakeable'

	// ⭐ **THE THREE TALLIES AND THE TWO DISAGREEMENT VERDICTS MOVE WITH THE ENTRIES THEY DESCRIBE (W78) —
	// and that they did NOT is the whole of the defect this rung closes.** The incumbent's declarations are
	// the only ones counted under the bare key at this instant, so the counts are exactly theirs; leaving
	// them behind made `paramDefaultsCollide` and `throwsClauseCollide` read, at the registration name,
	// maps nothing had ever written for it — and an absent tally reads as "one declaration, agreeing".
	//
	// The DECLARATION count is materialized rather than moved-if-present, because from here on this key is
	// counted from the tally map alone (`keyAlreadyHoldsADeclaration`). The other two keep the sparse
	// convention: their recorders test their own registry, which carries no residue.
	function moveDeclarationTallies(from ByteArray, to ByteArray)
		self.funcDeclarationCounts.upsert(to, value: self.declarationCountIn(self.funcDeclarationCounts, name: from))
		_ = self.funcDeclarationCounts.remove(from)
		self.moveRepeatedDeclarationCount(self.funcDefaultingDeclarationCounts, from: from, to: to)
		self.moveRepeatedDeclarationCount(self.funcThrowingDeclarationCounts, from: from, to: to)
		self.moveDivergentDeclarationName(self.disagreeingParamDefaults, from: from, to: to)
		self.moveDivergentDeclarationName(self.disagreeingThrows, from: from, to: to)
	end 'moveDeclarationTallies'

	// One tally, moved. Absent is not a failure — it is the sparse convention's "exactly one declaration"
	// (see `FunctionDeclarationCountMap`), and the destination inherits it by having no entry either.
	function moveRepeatedDeclarationCount(counts FunctionDeclarationCountMap, from ByteArray, to ByteArray)
		if let counted = try counts.get(from) 'moreThanOneDeclarationWasCounted'
			counts.upsert(to, value: counted)
			_ = counts.remove(from)
		end 'moreThanOneDeclarationWasCounted'
	end 'moveRepeatedDeclarationCount'

	// One disagreement verdict, moved. Written once for both sets for `noteRepeatedDeclaration`'s reason:
	// they are the same move over two populations, and two spellings would be two chances to forget the
	// removal — which is what would leave a LATER directory's declarations inheriting the incumbent's verdict.
	function moveDivergentDeclarationName(names DivergentDeclarationNameSet, from ByteArray, to ByteArray)
		if not names.contains(from) 'theseDeclarationsAgreed'
			return
		end 'theseDeclarationsAgreed'

		names.insert(to)
		_ = names.remove(from)
	end 'moveDivergentDeclarationName'

	// ⭐ **RE-POINT A CONTESTED DECLARATION'S SYNTHESIZED DEFAULT HELPERS AT THE KEY ITS OWN FACTS NOW SIT
	// UNDER.** The defaults entry is read back from the REGISTRATION key rather than from the bare name,
	// which is what makes it this declaration's by construction: nothing else files there. A name whose
	// declaration states no default has no entry and no helper, so the common contested name costs one map
	// miss.
	function renameContestedParamDefaultHelpers(bareName ByteArray, registrationName ByteArray)
		if let defaults = try self.funcParamDefaults.get(registrationName) 'hasParameterDefaults'
			self.renameParamDefaultHelpers(defaults, bareName: bareName, registrationName: registrationName)
		end 'hasParameterDefaults'
	end 'renameContestedParamDefaultHelpers'

	// ⭐ **THE EIGHT PER-DECLARATION FACTS THE SWEEP FILES UNDER A FUNCTION NAME, COPIED FROM ONE KEY TO
	// ANOTHER.** They travel together because they are exactly what a CALL SITE reads about a callee whose
	// declaration it cannot see — `returnTypeOf`, the DECLARATION SITE (visibility, declaring file and
	// receiver-presence), `declaredReturnSurface`, the recorded return type's declaring file, `consumeOf`,
	// `byRefOf`, `defaultsOf` and `throwsOf` — so a key holding seven of them answers seven questions about one
	// declaration and the eighth about another. ⚠ **THE COUNT WAS SEVEN AND THIS HEADER SAID SIX**, having left
	// out the declaration site while the sibling clear counted it; PBR-2's by-reference columns make it eight —
	// see `clearByNameSweepEntries` for why the two counts are load-bearing rather than decorative.
	//
	// ⚠ **TWO CONTESTS RE-KEY A DECLARATION AND BOTH COME THROUGH HERE**: a free function declared in more
	// than one DIRECTORY (N1c, `refileContestedFreeFunction`), and a `static` member whose name an INSTANCE
	// member of its type also wears (`noteStaticInstanceDeclaration`). They differ only in which key a
	// declaration moves to and in what its caller can establish about the source key (`moving`); the facts
	// that must travel with a declaration are the same eight, and a second copy of that list is a second answer
	// to "what does a function name carry".
	//
	// ⚠ **AN ABSENT FACT IS NOT COPIED, AND ABSENCE IS AN ANSWER IN SIX OF THE EIGHT** ("the plain surface",
	// "no name left to resolve", "borrows every parameter", "passes every parameter by value", "every parameter
	// required", "does not throw"). The destination is a key nothing has written, in both callers, so there is
	// nothing there for an absence to have to displace.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **A PRESENT FACT IS NOT NECESSARILY *THIS* DECLARATION'S, AND THAT IS WHAT `moving` DECIDES
	// (A5l).** This routine's own header used to rest on "an absent fact is not copied" alone — true only
	// while the source key holds ONE declaration's facts, which is exactly what the `static`/instance caller
	// cannot establish for the four BY-NAME facts — which is why they now fold AFTER the declaration loop, under
	// the key each belongs to, leaving this copy with one declaration's facts at both callers.
	//
	// The DEFAULT-HELPER rename is NOT part of this: it renames the helper a declaration's own real parse
	// synthesizes, which is a property of the destination NAME rather than of the facts.
	function copyDeclarationSweepEntries(from ByteArray, to ByteArray)
		// The return type is unconditional at every recorder (`record` writes one per declaration), so the
		// source key holds this declaration's own by construction and `moving` has nothing to say about it.
		if let returnType = try self.returnTypes.get(from) 'hasReturnType'
			self.returnTypes.upsert(to, value: returnType)
		end 'hasReturnType'
		// The declaration SITE is unconditional at `record` for the same reason the return type is, so it
		// moves on the same terms and `moving` has nothing to say about it either.
		if let site = try self.funcDeclSites.get(from) 'hasDeclarationSite'
			self.funcDeclSites.upsert(to, value: site)
		end 'hasDeclarationSite'
		if let surface = try self.declaredReturnSurfaces.get(from) 'namesTheBufferAlias'
			self.declaredReturnSurfaces.upsert(to, value: surface)
		end 'namesTheBufferAlias'
		if let declFilePath = try self.funcReturnDeclFiles.get(from) 'returnsANameStillToBeResolved'
			self.funcReturnDeclFiles.upsert(to, value: declFilePath)
		end 'returnsANameStillToBeResolved'
		if let consumes = try self.funcParamConsumes.get(from) 'consumesAParameter'
			self.funcParamConsumes.upsert(to, value: consumes)
		end 'consumesAParameter'
		if let byRef = try self.funcParamReassigns.get(from) 'takesAParameterByReference'
			self.funcParamReassigns.upsert(to, value: byRef)
		end 'takesAParameterByReference'
		if let globalAliasReturn = try self.funcGlobalAliasReturns.get(from) 'mayReturnAGlobalsRecord'
			self.funcGlobalAliasReturns.upsert(to, value: globalAliasReturn)
		end 'mayReturnAGlobalsRecord'
		if let defaults = try self.funcParamDefaults.get(from) 'hasParameterDefaults'
			self.funcParamDefaults.upsert(to, value: defaults)
		end 'hasParameterDefaults'
		if let clause = try self.funcThrows.get(from) 'throws'
			self.funcThrows.upsert(to, value: clause)
		end 'throws'
	end 'copyDeclarationSweepEntries'

	// Drop the FOUR facts the by-name scans publish, leaving the four `record` writes behind — the residue a
	// contested bare name keeps so a bare call to it reaches E3095 rather than an undeclared callee. The
	// booleans are discarded because a key with no entry is the ordinary case here, not an error: all four
	// are written only by a declaration that has the fact to state. It is the exact complement of the split
	// `copyDeclarationSweepEntries` copies on, and the two must move together or one of them is lying about
	// which facts a re-keyed declaration takes with it.
	function clearByNameSweepEntries(name ByteArray)
		_ = self.funcParamConsumes.remove(name)
		_ = self.funcParamReassigns.remove(name)
		_ = self.funcGlobalAliasReturns.remove(name)
		_ = self.funcParamDefaults.remove(name)
		_ = self.funcThrows.remove(name)
	end 'clearByNameSweepEntries'

	// The other four — the ones `record` writes for every declaration. Split from the by-name four rather
	// than folded into one eight-way clear because exactly one caller wants both, and the split is the same
	// one `copyDeclarationSweepEntries`' header draws: which PASS wrote the fact.
	function clearRecordOwnedSweepEntries(name ByteArray)
		_ = self.returnTypes.remove(name)
		_ = self.funcDeclSites.remove(name)
		_ = self.declaredReturnSurfaces.remove(name)
		_ = self.funcReturnDeclFiles.remove(name)
	end 'clearRecordOwnedSweepEntries'

	// ⭐⭐ **A CONTESTED FUNCTION'S SYNTHESIZED DEFAULT HELPERS ARE RENAMED WITH IT (N1c review), AND THAT IS
	// CORRECTNESS RATHER THAN TIDINESS.** `Project.paramDefaultHelperName`'s own header rests the mechanism's
	// uniqueness on a premise THIS RUNG REMOVED — *"`funcName` is unique whole-program (a second declaration
	// of one name is E3006)"*. It no longer is: two directories may each declare `pick`, and the sweep,
	// which runs before any contest is known, minted `__paramDefault#pick#0` for BOTH.
	//
	// ⛔ **MEASURED as `E3006: duplicate definition of function '__paramDefault#pick#0'`** — a refusal naming
	// a symbol absent from the source, against a program this rung's own rule accepts. The name has to follow
	// the DECLARATION's identity, which is exactly what this fold has just decided.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE HELPER'S OWN SWEPT SIGNATURE MOVES WITH ITS NAME, and both halves are one move.** The real
	// parse synthesizes the helper's body under the name it READS BACK from here
	// (`Parser.requireSweptHelperName`), and refuses a name the sweep never published — so a rename that
	// left the signature behind would trade one panic for another.
	//
	// The OLD name is RE-MINTED from the bare name rather than read out of the info, so the two spellings
	// come from one join and cannot disagree about what the sweep published.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE FOUR COLUMN MOVES BELOW READ `__paramDefault#<BARE>#<i>`, WHICH ACCUMULATES ACROSS FILES —
	// AND THEY ARE UNGATED ON PURPOSE, because the FOLD ORDER decides the question a filter would have to
	// ask.** A helper's swept name is minted from the BARE function name by the token scan, so two
	// directories each declaring a defaulting `pick` mint the same `__paramDefault#pick#0`; what keeps the
	// key unambiguous HERE is that both callers reach it at a moment when it holds this declaration's facts:
	//   • the end-of-fold rename (`foldFile`), where this file's own `record` for the helper ran EARLIER IN
	//     THE SAME FOLD, after every predecessor's and before any successor's;
	//   • the `newlyContested` INCUMBENT refile, where only the incumbent directory has folded at all.
	// The `funcParamDefaults` entry each of them reads is looked up under the REGISTRATION key, which no
	// other declaration writes, so "is this default this declaration's?" is answered by the key rather than
	// by a filter over the file's scan arrays — which is what it took while every declaration was filed
	// under the bare name (A5l).
	function renameParamDefaultHelpers(info ParamDefaultInfo, bareName ByteArray, registrationName ByteArray)
		for i in 0 upto info.producers.count() 'eachCallAlignedSlot'
			let slot = i as ParamCount
			// ⚠ A CALLER-LOCATION slot is skipped with the undefaulted ones, and for a stronger reason than
			// theirs: it has no synthesized function anywhere to rename, so the four column moves below would
			// each find nothing and `renameHelper` would invent a helper the drain never declared (W72).
			match info.producerFor(slot) 'produces'
				required to callerFile then continue
				helper then break 'produces'
			end 'produces'

			let swept = paramDefaultHelperName(bareName, paramIndex: slot)
			let renamed = paramDefaultHelperName(registrationName, paramIndex: slot)

			if let returnType = try self.returnTypes.get(swept) 'theSweepDeclaredIt'
				self.returnTypes.upsert(renamed, value: returnType)
			end 'theSweepDeclaredIt'
			if let surface = try self.declaredReturnSurfaces.get(swept) 'namesTheBufferAlias'
				self.declaredReturnSurfaces.upsert(renamed, value: surface)
			end 'namesTheBufferAlias'
			if let declFilePath = try self.funcReturnDeclFiles.get(swept) 'returnsANameStillToBeResolved'
				self.funcReturnDeclFiles.upsert(renamed, value: declFilePath)
			end 'returnsANameStillToBeResolved'
			if let site = try self.funcDeclSites.get(swept) 'theSweepFiledItsSite'
				self.funcDeclSites.upsert(renamed, value: site)
			end 'theSweepFiledItsSite'

			info.renameHelper(slot, renamedTo: renamed)
		end 'eachCallAlignedSlot'
	end 'renameParamDefaultHelpers'

	// Add one FREE function's namespace-qualified spelling to the index AND fold it into the hash (N1b) —
	// `recordAlias`'s discipline, one write, so the set and the hash that keys every parse memo on it cannot
	// disagree about what the index contains. See `namespaceQualifiedFunctions` for why the set carries the
	// spelling alone and `DeclShapeNamespaceQualifiedFunction` for why it must ride the hash at all.
	function recordNamespaceQualifiedFunction(qualifiedName ByteArray)
		self.namespaceQualifiedFunctions.insert(qualifiedName)
		self.hash = mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeNamespaceQualifiedFunction), bytes: qualifiedName)
	end 'recordNamespaceQualifiedFunction'

	// Add one FUNCTION typealias to the index AND fold it into the hash — the one write, for
	// `recordAlias`'s reason. The RETURN type rides the hash (its tag, then its name): a caller that
	// binds a function value of this alias and calls it recovers the result type FROM this return type,
	// so a change to it changes what that caller compiles to and its parse memo must move with it.
	function recordFunctionTypeAlias(alias FunctionTypeAlias)
		self.functionTypeAliases.upsert(alias.name, value: alias)
		let named = mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeFunctionAlias), bytes: alias.name)
		self.hash = mixBytes(mix(named, value: alias.returnTag.rawValue as ContentHash), bytes: alias.returnTypeName)
	end 'recordFunctionTypeAlias'

	// Add one top-level DECLARATION (a `let` or a `var`) to the arena. No value yet:
	// `evaluateInitializers` supplies that once every file has been folded in.
	//
	// FIRST-WINS on a duplicate storage key, matching the bootstrap's `decls.FirstOrDefault(d =>
	// d.Name == name)` (2-Parser.cs:4950). A file that declares the same name twice — `let A = 1` then
	// `let A = 2`, or a `let`/`var` collision — keeps the FIRST here and is diagnosed E3006 by the real
	// parse (`isRedeclaredBinding` → `Parser.raiseBindingFailure`), the top-level twin of the positioned
	// duplicate-FUNCTION check `commitFuncSignatures` makes. It is NOT the cross-file same-name case,
	// which is legal and which `topLevelStorageKey` handles by giving the two decls different keys.
	function recordDecl(decl TopLevelDecl)
		if self.declIndex.contains(decl.storageKey) 'duplicate'
			return
		end 'duplicate'

		self.declIndex.upsert(decl.storageKey, value: self.decls.count())
		self.decls.push(decl)
	end 'recordDecl'

	// ⭐ EVALUATE EVERY TOP-LEVEL CONSTANT IN THE PROGRAM, and fold each answer into the index's hash.
	// Called once, by `queryProgramSignatures`, AFTER every file's declarations have been folded in.
	//
	// **THAT ORDERING IS THE FEATURE.** A forward reference — `let TOTAL = FIRST + SECOND` written
	// above `let FIRST = 30` — resolves precisely because nothing is evaluated until every declaration
	// is KNOWN. A single forward pass cannot do it, and neither reference compiler tries: v1 defers
	// the whole job to TypeResolution, and the bootstrap pre-scans the entire token stream before it
	// parses a line (`CollectAndEvaluateTopLevelDecls`, 2-Parser.cs:1680-1784).
	//
	// Driving over the ARENA in declaration order is what makes the cycle message deterministic — see
	// the `decls` field.
	export function evaluateInitializers()
		for i in 0 upto self.decls.count() 'eachDecl'
			let decl = self.declAt(i)
			match decl.outcome 'state'
				// Already settled by an earlier decl's DFS — it referenced this one. A managed constant is
				// settled too; it is simply not reachable BY a reference (see `evaluateDecl`).
				value to poisoned then break 'state'
				managedValue to factoryValue then break 'state'
				evaluating then panic("ProgramSignatures.evaluateInitializers: '{String.from(decl.name)}' is still marked `evaluating` between two top-level walks — every DFS pops what it pushes")
				pending then self.startDecl(i)
			end 'state'
		end 'eachDecl'

		self.assignDataLabels()

		// The hash covers the ANSWERS, folded in arena order once they all exist. It cannot be done
		// inside the loop above: a decl's outcome is not final until every DFS that could reach it has
		// run (a cycle rewrites its members' outcomes from the inside).
		for j in 0 upto self.decls.count() 'eachOutcome'
			let decl = self.declAt(j)
			self.hash = self.mixConstant(self.hash, decl: decl)
		end 'eachOutcome'
	end 'evaluateInitializers'

	// ⭐ **GIVE EVERY TOP-LEVEL `var` ITS `.data` LABEL — the second half of the key/label split**
	// (see `topLevelStorageKey`). The KEY answers "which declaration is this?" and is scoped by file;
	// the LABEL answers "which slot does the IR name?" and must not be, because a label carrying a
	// path would land in every golden fragment and in the emitted binary.
	//
	// **BARE NAME, SUFFIXED ONLY ON COLLISION.** Two file-private `counter`s have DIFFERENT keys and
	// would want the SAME label; the second one to be reached takes `__data_counter$1`. So:
	//   • the ordinary program — every global uniquely named — gets stable, readable, path-free
	//     labels, and its goldens never mention a temp directory;
	//   • the pathological one is CORRECT rather than aliased, which is exactly what the bootstrap
	//     is not (`global-file-private-same-name.md`: it returns 212, aliasing `bumpA` onto featB's
	//     slot, where 118 is correct).
	//
	// Walked in ARENA ORDER, which is source-path order then in-file order — so which of two colliding
	// decls gets the bare name is a deterministic function of the program, not of a hash iteration.
	// That is the same property `decls` is an array for.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT RUNS AFTER THE EVALUATION LOOP, AND THAT ORDER IS NOW LOAD-BEARING** (P1.7 slice 2b): which
	// decls have a slot is `TopLevelDecl.hasStorage`, and `settleStoredRecordValue` is what sets it for an immutable
	// ARRAY binding. Hoisted above the loop it would label the `var`s and silently skip every array `let`.
	//
	// ⚠ **A LABEL IS MINTED FOR EVERY STORED DECL, LIVE OR NOT, AND THAT ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING TOO**
	// (P1.7 slice 3). Dead-global elimination reads liveness off the `globalAddr` ops the parser emitted,
	// which CARRY these labels — so labelling has to precede it, and a dead global keeps its claim on the
	// bare name it took here. That is the correct outcome rather than a leftover: renumbering the `$1`
	// disambiguator once a neighbour is dropped would rename a SURVIVING global whose ops already spell
	// the old label.
	//
	// It also counts them, into `storedGlobalCount` — this walk visits exactly the decls that have a slot,
	// so the count is a by-product of the labelling rather than a second arena pass to keep in step.
	//
	// ⭐ **AND IT RECORDS WHICH OF THOSE SLOTS ARE LAZY** (spec-port `lazy-static`), for the same reason and
	// in the same act: the key of that set IS the label being minted on this line, so there is no window in
	// which a slot exists under a label the lazy set was keyed by differently. It runs inside
	// `evaluateInitializers`, which completes before any file is parsed — which is what makes the answer
	// available to the parser's access door at the moment it needs it.
	function assignDataLabels()
		var minted = DataLabelCountMap.create()
		var stored = 0 as StoredGlobalCount
		for i in 0 upto self.decls.count() 'each'
			var decl = self.declAt(i)
			if not decl.hasStorage 'inlinedHasNoSlot'
				continue
			end 'inlinedHasNoSlot'
			decl.dataLabel = uniqueDataLabel(decl.name, minted: minted)
			stored = stored + 1
			if self.declSlotIsLazy(decl) 'filledOnFirstAccess'
				self.lazyStaticSlots.insert(decl.dataLabel)
				// ⭐ **THE CALLEES ARE RECORDED HERE BECAUSE THIS IS THE ONE WALK THAT HOLDS BOTH HALVES.** The
				// label has just been minted and the outcome is settled (`evaluateInitializers` runs its whole
				// DFS before calling this), so asking anywhere else would mean either a second arena walk or a
				// memo that can be asked too early.
				match self.declInitializerCall(decl) 'initializer'
					none then break 'initializer'
					calls(names) then self.lazyStaticInitCallees.upsert(decl.dataLabel, value: names)
				end 'initializer'
			end 'filledOnFirstAccess'
		end 'each'
		self.storedGlobalCount = stored
	end 'assignDataLabels'

	// Open a fresh DFS at arena index `index`. The stack is per-ROOT rather than shared across the
	// whole loop, which costs one empty array per unevaluated root and buys the invariant the panic
	// above checks: a walk that ends leaves nothing marked `evaluating`.
	function startDecl(index TopLevelDeclIndex)
		var stack = TopLevelDeclIndexArray.create()
		_ = self.evaluateDecl(index, stack: stack)
	end 'startDecl'

	// Evaluate the constant at arena index `index` — MEMOIZED, and with CYCLE DETECTION.
	//
	// The answer is recorded ON THE DECL and only summarized in the return: a failure's diagnostic
	// belongs to the declaration that caused it, and is raised (once) by the real parse when it
	// reaches that line. Callers get `failed` — an instruction to stop, not an error to report.
	//
	// ⭐ **A MANAGED CONSTANT ANSWERS `notFound` HERE, AND THAT IS THE SLICE'S HARD BOUNDARY** (P1.7a).
	// The only caller that reads this answer for a NAME is `evaluateNamed`, i.e. a reference from inside
	// another initializer — and a managed constant is not a number, so there is nothing an arithmetic
	// fold could do with it. `notFound` makes `let A = "x"` / `let B = A` report **E2004 `Undefined
	// constant 'A'`**, which is MEASURED to be exactly what the runnable oracle does, in BOTH declaration
	// orders (its bare literal goes to the managed bucket while its bare identifier goes to the scalar
	// folder, and the scalar folder never sees managed declarations). So there is no managed-to-managed
	// dependency to order and no managed cycle to detect: the arena's DFS never reaches one.
	function evaluateDecl(index TopLevelDeclIndex, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstEvalOutcome
		var decl = self.declAt(index)

		match decl.outcome 'state'
			value(payload, tag) then return ConstEvalOutcome.value(payload, tag: tag)
			failed to poisoned then return ConstEvalOutcome.failed
			// An ARRAY binding answers `notFound` for a managed literal's reason (P1.7 slice 2b): the only
			// caller reading this for a NAME is a reference from inside another initializer, and there is
			// nothing an arithmetic fold could do with a heap record. MEASURED against the runnable oracle —
			// `let A = [1, 2]` / `let B = A` is E2004 `Undefined constant 'A'` in BOTH declaration orders.
			// ⭐ A FACTORY-CALL binding joins them (spec-port `map-struct-bytearray`) and WIDENS the boundary
			// by nothing: its value is a record built by running code, so there is even less for an
			// arithmetic fold to do with it than with a literal's bytes. `var db = Database.create(…)` /
			// `let N = db` is `Undefined constant 'db'`, in both declaration orders, exactly as the other
			// record kinds already are — which is what keeps the arena free of any initialization ORDER to
			// get wrong: no admitted initializer can name another global.
			managedValue to factoryValue then return ConstEvalOutcome.notFound
			// Reached a decl already on this walk's stack: the reference chain has closed on itself.
			evaluating then return self.recordCycle(index, stack: stack)
			pending then break 'state'
		end 'state'

		decl.outcome = TopLevelConstant.evaluating
		stack.push(index)
		let outcome = self.walkConstInitializer(decl, stack: stack)
		_ = try stack.pop() otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.evaluateDecl: stack.pop failed for '{String.from(decl.name)}' — every push is matched here")

		return outcome
	end 'evaluateDecl'

	// Walk one initializer's tokens and settle its decl's outcome.
	//
	// A transient `Parser` is what walks them, and that is the point: the evaluator needs a token
	// cursor, integer-literal decoding, `u16.max` bound resolution, character-literal decoding, and
	// the operator precedence table — every one of which a `Parser` already has and single-sources.
	// Re-spelling any of them here would be a second reading of the grammar, which is the duplication
	// this compiler pays for hardest (see TypeRules' header). The parser it builds emits NO ops: a
	// constant's initializer produces a NUMBER, not code.
	//
	// It is created per DECL, not per file, and per decl is per-constant-in-the-program — this runs
	// once per declaration, never per reference (the memo above sees to that). Programs with no
	// top-level constant build no parser here at all.
	function walkConstInitializer(decl TopLevelDecl, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstEvalOutcome
		// `isStdlibSource: false` is exact rather than a default: this walk reads an INITIALIZER, and the
		// only thing the flag governs is a `typealias` DECLARATION, which an initializer cannot contain.
		// `declaresCompilerInternalNames: false` is exact for the same reason and its own: an initializer
		// declares no NAME at all, so the reserved-prefix rule this walk would exempt has no site to fire
		// at — and the decl whose initializer this is has already been through the real parse's check.
		// `livesUnderStdlibDirectory: false` is exact for the third variant of that reason: it gates the
		// two `module`-visible `String` METHODS, and a constant initializer is const-EVALUATED — it reaches
		// no method dispatch at all, so the gate has no site to fire at either. This walk never asks the
		// project for a path, and giving it a reason to would put a `Directory.currentPath()` probe on the
		// per-declaration path to answer a question nothing here can pose.
		var parser = Parser.create(decl.tokens, filePath: decl.declFilePath, namespace: self.namespaceOf(decl.declFilePath), signatures: self, isStdlibSource: false, declaresCompilerInternalNames: false, livesUnderStdlibDirectory: false, buildsBodies: true)
		let folded = try parser.evalConstInitializer(decl, stack: stack) otherwise (err) 'noValue'
			return self.recordFailure(decl, error: err)
		end 'noValue'

		match folded 'kind'
			direct(value) then return self.settleFoldedValue(decl, value: value)
			// ⭐ A FACTORY CALL settles like any other stored record — the slot, the storage bit and the
			// receiver-name set are the same three facts — and additionally resolves the callee's declared
			// RETURN TYPE, which is what types the slot. It is resolved here rather than in the evaluator
			// because it must live in THIS index's interner: the drop callee the cleanup emits is routed
			// against `self.typeNames`, and a type adopted into a file's artifact interner would name an id
			// this side cannot resolve.
			factoryCall(calls, root) then return self.settleStoredRecordValue(decl, outcome: TopLevelConstant.factoryValue(calls, root: root, returnType: self.constFactoryCallResultType(factoryCallAt(calls, id: root), name: decl.name)))
		end 'kind'
	end 'walkConstInitializer'

	// ⭐ **THE TYPE ONE ARENA NODE PRODUCES, IN THIS INDEX'S OWN INTERNER (W116)** — the ONE derivation, read
	// by the ROOT (which types the global's `.data` slot and routes its drop) and by a NESTED node (which
	// types the argument or field receiving it). A call's is its callee's declared return; a construction's is
	// the struct it names, which is the whole of what makes `Outer{inner: Inner.create(7)}` and
	// `Pair.create(Pair{…})` type each other's results without either side asking how the other was written.
	function constFactoryCallResultType(call ConstFactoryCall, name ByteArray) returns MaxonType
		return match call 'node'
			call(callee, _) gives self.resolvedReturnValueType(callee, name: name)
			construct(typeName, _) gives MaxonType.structRef(self.typeNames.intern(typeName))
		end 'node'
	end 'constFactoryCallResultType'

	// ⭐ **THE DECLARED RETURN, WITH A STILL-BARE `named` SETTLED AS ITS OWN DECLARING FILE MEANS IT (W116).**
	//
	// ⚠ It is the SECOND reader of `funcReturnDeclFiles` and not the first: N3's `contestScopedReturnTypeOf`
	// spends the same column, through `resolveRecordedGenericAliasTypes`, BEFORE this evaluator runs. The two
	// are not one rule written twice — that one rescopes a CONTESTED generic alias and declines every other
	// name, this one asks the whole slot cascade — but they read one column, so a name that pass has already
	// settled arrives here concrete and the tag guard inside `declaredSlotType` leaves it alone.
	//
	// It is a no-op for every callee that was here before: a `returns Self` / `returns Database` factory is
	// recorded already concrete, which is what lets `recordPointerSlotType` panic on a bare `named` rather than
	// handle one. What arrives unresolved is the FIELD-DEFAULT HELPER — `recordScannedFieldDefaultHelper`
	// records the field's own SWEPT type, before any alias is known — so `var items as IntArray =
	// IntArray.create()` would otherwise type its value `named` (which reads as an INT: see `denotedSlotType`)
	// and mint the call's result at a tag the slot cannot hold.
	function resolvedReturnValueType(callee ByteArray, name ByteArray) returns MaxonType
		let declared = self.declaredReturnValueType(callee, name: name)
		if let scope = try self.funcReturnDeclFiles.get(callee) 'namesATypeStillToResolve'
			return self.declaredSlotType(declared, into: self.typeNames, slotScopeFilePath: scope)
		end 'namesATypeStillToResolve'

		return declared
	end 'resolvedReturnValueType'

	// The VALUE type `callee` returns, in this index's own interner. The evaluator has already established
	// that the callee is declared and that its return type is a managed value (`Parser.requireGlobalFactory`),
	// so both refusals here are invariant breaks rather than cases a program can reach.
	function declaredReturnValueType(callee ByteArray, name ByteArray) returns MaxonType
		let declared = try self.returnTypes.get(callee) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.declaredReturnValueType: '{String.from(name)}' names the factory '{String.from(callee)}', which no file declares — the evaluator admits the form only after `declaresCallee` says otherwise")
		match declared 'returns'
			void then panic("ProgramSignatures.declaredReturnValueType: '{String.from(name)}' is initialized by '{String.from(callee)}', which returns nothing — the evaluator refuses a void factory before it folds one")
			value(valueType) then return valueType
		end 'returns'
	end 'declaredReturnValueType'

	// Settle a decl from the value its initializer FOLDED to — the four non-call answers, each keeping the
	// arm it has always had. One door rather than five call sites, so the evaluator's union and the arena's
	// outcome union are mapped in exactly one place.
	function settleFoldedValue(decl TopLevelDecl, value ConstFoldedValue) returns ConstEvalOutcome
		match value 'kind'
			scalar(folded) then return self.settleScalarValue(decl, value: folded)
			managed(bytes, managedKind) then return self.settleManagedValue(decl, bytes: bytes, kind: managedKind)
			arrayLiteral(elements, element) then return self.settleStoredRecordValue(decl, outcome: TopLevelConstant.arrayValue(elements, element: element))
			containerCreate(giid, elements) then return self.settleStoredRecordValue(decl, outcome: TopLevelConstant.containerValue(giid, elements: elements))
		end 'kind'
	end 'settleFoldedValue'

	// Settle a decl whose initializer folded to a NUMBER — the arm this arena has always had.
	function settleScalarValue(decl TopLevelDecl, value ConstValue) returns ConstEvalOutcome
		decl.outcome = TopLevelConstant.value(value.payload, tag: value.tag)
		return ConstEvalOutcome.value(value.payload, tag: value.tag)
	end 'settleScalarValue'

	// Settle a decl whose initializer folded to a MANAGED literal (P1.7a). It answers `notFound` for
	// `evaluateDecl`'s reason — a managed constant is invisible to the scalar folder — while the decl
	// itself carries the bytes every USE site re-materializes (`resolvedValueOf`).
	function settleManagedValue(decl TopLevelDecl, bytes ByteArray, kind ManagedConstKind) returns ConstEvalOutcome
		decl.outcome = TopLevelConstant.managedValue(bytes, kind: kind)
		self.managedTopLevelNames.insert(decl.name)
		return ConstEvalOutcome.notFound
	end 'settleManagedValue'

	// Settle a decl whose initializer folded to a HEAP RECORD — an array literal (P1.7 slice 2b) or a
	// container factory call (spec-port `ownership`). It joins `managedTopLevelNames` for
	// `settleManagedValue`'s reason — the name is a METHOD RECEIVER (`counters.get(0)`, `globalArr.push(x)`),
	// and that set is the allocation-free filter in front of every receiver probe — and answers `notFound`
	// for the same reason a managed literal does.
	//
	// ⭐ **ONE FUNCTION FOR BOTH RECORD KINDS, taking the outcome its caller already built.** What differs
	// between them is only WHICH outcome; the three facts settled here — the outcome, the storage bit and
	// the receiver-name set — are the same three, and a per-kind copy is how a third record kind would come
	// to get a slot the layout never laid down (the storage bit) or a receiver probe that skips it.
	function settleStoredRecordValue(decl TopLevelDecl, outcome TopLevelConstant) returns ConstEvalOutcome
		decl.outcome = outcome
		// A record binding is STORED whichever keyword declared it — written HERE, beside the outcome it is
		// derived from, so the two are one decision (see `TopLevelDecl.hasStorage`).
		decl.hasStorage = true
		self.managedTopLevelNames.insert(decl.name)
		return ConstEvalOutcome.notFound
	end 'settleStoredRecordValue'

	// Settle a decl that produced no value: it either OWNS the defect or merely inherited one.
	function recordFailure(decl TopLevelDecl, error ConstEvalError) returns ConstEvalOutcome
		match error 'kind'
			defective(parseError) then decl.outcome = TopLevelConstant.failed(parseError)
			dependencyFailed then self.poisonIfUndecided(decl)
		end 'kind'

		return ConstEvalOutcome.failed
	end 'recordFailure'

	// Mark a decl as a casualty of someone else's defect — UNLESS a verdict was already handed to it.
	// `recordCycle` settles every member of a cycle from the inside, and those decls then unwind
	// through here carrying `dependencyFailed`; overwriting would blank the one diagnostic the cycle
	// produced.
	function poisonIfUndecided(decl TopLevelDecl)
		match decl.outcome 'state'
			// `recordCycle` already ruled on this decl.
			failed to poisoned then break 'state'
			evaluating then decl.outcome = TopLevelConstant.poisoned
			value then panic("ProgramSignatures.poisonIfUndecided: '{String.from(decl.name)}' holds a scalar value AND failed to evaluate — an initializer produces one or the other")
			managedValue to factoryValue then panic("ProgramSignatures.poisonIfUndecided: '{String.from(decl.name)}' holds a managed value AND failed to evaluate — an initializer produces one or the other")
			pending then panic("ProgramSignatures.poisonIfUndecided: '{String.from(decl.name)}' is `pending` while unwinding its own evaluation — `evaluateDecl` marks it `evaluating` before the walk")
		end 'state'
	end 'poisonIfUndecided'

	// **THE CYCLE.** `index` is already on `stack`, so the arc from its first appearance to the top is
	// a closed reference chain in which no member can ever be evaluated. Settle all of them here,
	// from the inside, and give exactly ONE the diagnostic.
	//
	// WHICH ONE: the LAST-DECLARED member. `stack` holds arena indices and the arena is in declaration
	// order, so that is simply the largest index in the arc — no source positions to compare. The
	// bootstrap picks the same declaration (`decls.Last(d => evaluating.Contains(d.Name))`,
	// 2-Parser.cs:4954) and anchors on its NAME token, which is what `specs/top-level-let.md`'s
	// `circular-dependency-error` gates byte-for-byte (`3:5` — the `B` of `let B = A + 1`). Every
	// other member is POISONED, so the program reports one E2012 and not one per member.
	//
	// ⚠ DIVERGENCE FROM THE BOOTSTRAP, and it is deliberate. It names the whole in-progress set
	// (`string.Join(", ", evaluating)`), which for `X -> A -> B -> A` prints "X, A, B" — but `X` is
	// not circular, it merely LEADS to a cycle, and a message that says it is is simply wrong. shv2
	// names the ARC (`A, B`). The two agree on every cycle whose root is a member — which is every
	// cycle the corpus has, including the spec's — and disagree only where the bootstrap is
	// misreporting.
	function recordCycle(index TopLevelDeclIndex, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstEvalOutcome
		let arcStart = self.stackPositionOf(index, stack: stack)

		var names = ""
		var owner = index
		for i in arcStart upto stack.count() 'eachMember'
			let member = try stack.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.recordCycle: stack.get OOB at {i}")
			if i > arcStart 'separator'
				names = "{names}, "
			end 'separator'
			let memberDecl = self.declAt(member)
			names = "{names}{String.from(memberDecl.name)}"
			if member > owner 'laterDeclaration'
				owner = member
			end 'laterDeclaration'
		end 'eachMember'

		var ownerDecl = self.declAt(owner)
		ownerDecl.outcome = TopLevelConstant.failed(ParseError.circularConstants(names, line: ownerDecl.nameLine, column: ownerDecl.nameColumn))

		for j in arcStart upto stack.count() 'eachVictim'
			let member = try stack.get(j) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.recordCycle: stack.get OOB at {j}")
			if member == owner 'ownsTheDiagnostic'
				continue
			end 'ownsTheDiagnostic'
			var victim = self.declAt(member)
			victim.outcome = TopLevelConstant.poisoned
		end 'eachVictim'

		return ConstEvalOutcome.failed
	end 'recordCycle'

	// Where `index` sits on the DFS stack. Its presence is a precondition — `recordCycle` is reached
	// only from the `evaluating` arm, which means this decl is on the current walk — so a miss is an
	// invariant break and not a case to handle.
	function stackPositionOf(index TopLevelDeclIndex, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns TopLevelDeclIndex
		for i in 0 upto stack.count() 'each'
			let member = try stack.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.stackPositionOf: stack.get OOB at {i}")
			if member == index 'found'
				return i
			end 'found'
		end 'each'

		let target = self.declAt(index)
		panic("ProgramSignatures.stackPositionOf: '{String.from(target.name)}' is marked `evaluating` but is not on the DFS stack — the mark and the stack fell out of lockstep")
	end 'stackPositionOf'

	// Resolve `name` from `readerFilePath` and evaluate what it denotes — the door `Parser`'s
	// constant evaluator asks a NAME REFERENCE through, and the recursion that makes a forward
	// reference (and a cross-file one) work.
	//
	// ⚠ **A `var` IS INVISIBLE HERE, AND THE SPEC PINS IT.** `var b = a + 1` where `a` is itself a
	// top-level `var` is `E2004: Undefined constant 'a'` in the reference compiler — MEASURED, not
	// inferred. And it must be: a constant initializer is folded at COMPILE TIME into bytes in the
	// `.data` image, while a var's value is whatever the program last STORED there. Reading one from
	// the other would bake a snapshot of a mutable location into the image and call it a constant.
	// So `mutableIsInvisible` filters the lookup, and the reader gets the same `notFound` it would
	// get for a name nobody declared — which is exactly the diagnostic the reference produces.
	export function evaluateNamed(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstEvalOutcome
		match self.constantDeclFor(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'lookup'
			found(index) then return self.evaluateDecl(index, stack: stack)
			notFound then return ConstEvalOutcome.notFound
		end 'lookup'
	end 'evaluateNamed'

	// The value `name` denotes when read from `readerFilePath` — the door a USE SITE asks through
	// (`Parser.topLevelConstantReference`), after every constant has been evaluated.
	//
	// A constant that FAILED reads as `notFound`, so its reader reports a plain undefined-name error
	// rather than inlining a value that does not exist. v1 does exactly this, and says why: it drops
	// the cycle's victims from its registry "so readers get a plain 'Undefined name'"
	// (TypeResolution.maxon:8332-8338). The declaration's own diagnostic has already failed the build,
	// so nothing is lost by the reader being vague about it.
	//
	// A `var` reads as `notFound` here too — not because it is undefined, but because it is not a
	// CONSTANT and this door only answers for constants. Its reader (`Parser.parseVariableReference`)
	// asks `globalDeclFor` next, and emits a LOAD.
	export function constantValueOf(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns TopLevelConstantLookup
		match self.constantDeclFor(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'lookup'
			found(index) then return self.resolvedValueOf(index)
			notFound then return TopLevelConstantLookup.notFound
		end 'lookup'
	end 'constantValueOf'

	// ⭐ **THE STORED-BINDING DOOR** — what `name` denotes as a top-level global with a `.data` slot, read
	// from `readerFilePath`. The twin of `constantValueOf`, and the reason the two are separate: an inlined
	// binding's use MATERIALIZES its value and a stored one's LOADS from a slot, so a caller that could not
	// tell them apart would have to guess which code to emit. It is every `var` plus an array `let`
	// (P1.7 slice 2b) — see `TopLevelDecl.hasStorage` — and the answer carries which of those it found.
	//
	// It resolves through the IDENTICAL scoped-then-bare probe (`declFor`), which is the whole point
	// of extending the storage key to vars: this is what makes featA's `counter` and featB's
	// `counter` two slots instead of one.
	// ⚠ **`into` IS THE READER'S OWN INTERNER, and it is `returnTypeOf`'s discipline applied to a SLOT.** A
	// factory-built global's type is the callee's declared return type, which lives in THIS index's interner
	// — and a `MaxonType.structRef(id)` is meaningless outside the table that minted the id. So the type is
	// ADOPTED on the way out, exactly as a call's return type is. Every other arm's type carries no
	// interner id (a gid, a builtin, a tag) and crosses unchanged, which is why the parameter arrived only
	// when the struct arm did.
	export function globalDeclFor(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath, into TypeNameInterner) returns TopLevelGlobalLookup
		match self.declFor(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath, wanted: DeclKind.stored) 'lookup'
			found(index) then return self.resolvedGlobalOf(index, into: into)
			notFound then return TopLevelGlobalLookup.notFound
		end 'lookup'
	end 'globalDeclFor'

	// The `.data` label and TYPE of the global at `index` — everything a load or a store needs to
	// name it. A var whose initializer failed reads as `notFound` for `resolvedValueOf`'s reason: the
	// declaration's own diagnostic has already failed the build, and a slot with no value is a slot
	// with no bytes to emit.
	function resolvedGlobalOf(index TopLevelDeclIndex, into TypeNameInterner) returns TopLevelGlobalLookup
		let decl = self.declAt(index)
		match decl.outcome 'state'
			value(_, tag) then return TopLevelGlobalLookup.found(self.dataLabelOf(decl), valueType: constantValueType(tag), mutable: decl.mutable)
			failed to poisoned then return TopLevelGlobalLookup.notFound
			// ⭐ A MANAGED `var` (P1.7 slice 2a) reads exactly as a scalar one does — the slot is real, it
			// is just a POINTER rather than a value. Its type is the literal's own, so a read of the name
			// and a use of the literal it was initialized from are the same type by construction.
			managedValue(_, kind) then return TopLevelGlobalLookup.found(self.dataLabelOf(decl), valueType: self.managedConstValueType(kind, elementFilePath: decl.declFilePath), mutable: decl.mutable)
			// ⭐ AN ARRAY BINDING (P1.7 slice 2b) reads as a managed `var` does — a POINTER slot holding the
			// heap record `__module_init` built. It is the one arm `mutable` can arrive `false` from, and that
			// bit is the whole of what makes `Live.push(1)` an E3019 and `Live = […]` an E2013.
			arrayValue(_, element) then return TopLevelGlobalLookup.found(self.dataLabelOf(decl), valueType: self.constArrayValueType(element), mutable: decl.mutable)
			// ⭐ A CONTAINER FACTORY reads as an array literal does — a POINTER slot holding the record
			// `__module_init` built — and its type needs no inference at all: the alias named the instance.
			containerValue(giid, _) then return TopLevelGlobalLookup.found(self.dataLabelOf(decl), valueType: MaxonType.genericInstance(giid), mutable: decl.mutable)
			// ⚠ A `CharacterSet` PRESET was a sixth arm until W129. Its ONE surviving lesson is the ADOPTION the
			// factory arm below still performs: a `structRef` id is meaningless outside the table that minted it,
			// and that one was minted in the INDEX's — so handing it to a file's parse unadopted panicked in
			// `Parser.internedName` the first time a body read the global. The container arm above needs no
			// adoption because a `GenericInstanceId` is whole-program by construction, which is why they differ.
			// ⭐ A FACTORY-CALL GLOBAL reads the same way, at the callee's declared RETURN type — a struct
			// reference for `Database.create(…)`, which is what makes `db.sourceFiles` an ordinary field
			// load off an ordinary struct pointer.
			factoryValue(_, _, returnType) then return TopLevelGlobalLookup.found(self.dataLabelOf(decl), valueType: self.adoptType(returnType, into: into), mutable: decl.mutable)
			pending to evaluating then panic("ProgramSignatures.resolvedGlobalOf: '{String.from(decl.name)}' is unevaluated at a use site — `evaluateInitializers` runs to completion before any file is parsed")
		end 'state'
	end 'resolvedGlobalOf'

	// ⭐ **THE `MaxonType` A MANAGED TOP-LEVEL BINDING OF `kind` DENOTES** — the SAME type its literal gives
	// a value in expression position (`Parser.emitStringLiteral` / `emitByteStringLiteralValue`), stated once
	// so a `let`'s inlined literal, a `var`'s slot, the module-init store and the cleanup's drop cannot
	// disagree about what the binding IS.
	//
	// A METHOD rather than a free function because a byte string's type is the CONCRETE `Array with Byte`
	// instance, and an instance is a gid only the interner can mint — the very `internArrayByteInstance` the
	// literal path uses, so `b"…"` has ONE type however it is reached.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHOSE `Byte` THE ELEMENT IS DEPENDS ON WHICH OF THE TWO STORAGE MODELS THE CALLER IS IN (N2), so
	// the file is the caller's to state.** A STORED binding is one record, built once by `__module_init`
	// from its declaring file's literal, so its `.data` slot, its initializer and every load of it pass
	// `TopLevelDecl.declFilePath`. An INLINED `let` re-materializes the literal AT THE USE
	// (`Parser.emitManagedConstant`), under the reading file's own byte-string gate, so it passes the
	// READER's path — which is what makes the synthetic receiver's `varType` and the value the same emit
	// site produced one type rather than two.
	export function managedConstValueType(kind ManagedConstKind, elementFilePath FilePath) returns MaxonType
		return match kind 'k'
			string gives MaxonType.string
			byteArray gives MaxonType.genericInstance(self.internArrayByteInstance(elementFilePath))
		end 'k'
	end 'managedConstValueType'

	// ⭐ **THE `Array with T` INSTANCE A TOP-LEVEL ARRAY LITERAL DENOTES** (P1.7 slice 2b) — the
	// `managedConstValueType` twin, and a METHOD for its reason: an instance is a gid only the interner can
	// mint, and these are the VERY instances a `[…]` literal in a function body resolves to
	// (`Parser.arrayLiteralInstanceFor`), so a global's slot and a body's literal of the same elements are
	// the same type by construction.
	//
	// Both are a pure LOOKUP here: the declaration sweep interns them whenever a file's tokens contain a `[`
	// (plus a following string literal for the String one), which every array initializer does by definition
	// — the same sweep-writes/parser-reads discipline `internBuiltinIntegerInstance` states.
	export function constArrayValueType(element ConstArrayElementKind) returns MaxonType
		return match element 'e'
			integer gives MaxonType.genericInstance(self.internBuiltinIntegerInstance(self.arrayRecordBaseName()))
			string gives MaxonType.genericInstance(self.builtinInstanceForElement(self.arrayRecordBaseName(), element: MaxonType.string))
		end 'e'
	end 'constArrayValueType'

	// The `.data` label of a STORED decl. Having storage is a precondition, not a case: an inlined binding
	// has no slot, and every caller reached this through a `stored`-filtered lookup or the same
	// `hasStorage` filter the arena walks use.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT TAKES THE DECL, NOT ITS ARENA INDEX** (P1.7 slice 3 review). Every caller already holds the
	// decl — each one fetched it to read `outcome` or `name` — so an index parameter would be a SECOND way
	// to say which declaration is meant, with nothing forcing the two to agree: a caller that paired
	// `declAt(j)` with index `i` would be answered about `i`'s label under `j`'s storage bit, which is a
	// wrong answer rather than a compile error. One parameter cannot desynchronize from itself, and it
	// drops the re-fetch this made every caller pay.
	function dataLabelOf(decl TopLevelDecl) returns ByteArray
		if not decl.hasStorage 'inlined'
			panic("ProgramSignatures.dataLabelOf: '{String.from(decl.name)}' is an inlined top-level constant and has no .data slot — a constant materializes its value at every read")
		end 'inlined'
		if decl.dataLabel.count() == 0 'unlabelled'
			panic("ProgramSignatures.dataLabelOf: top-level '{String.from(decl.name)}' has no .data label — `assignDataLabels` labels every STORED decl in the arena, and it runs inside `evaluateInitializers`")
		end 'unlabelled'
		return decl.dataLabel
	end 'dataLabelOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS DECLARATION STILL CONTRIBUTE STORAGE?** — the ONE filter both arena walks below
	// apply, and the whole of what makes a dead global's THREE PIECES drop TOGETHER (P1.7 slice 3).
	// The `.data` slot `declaredGlobals` reserves, the `__module_init` store `managedGlobals` causes and
	// the `__maxon_global_cleanup` drop it causes are all downstream of this single answer, so they cannot
	// disagree — and every way they COULD disagree is broken: a slot with no initializer holds null where
	// a record must be, an initializer with no slot stores into an address the image never laid down, and
	// a cleanup drop with no slot decrefs one.
	//
	// TWO independent reasons a declaration contributes nothing, in the order that makes the second one
	// askable: an INLINED binding has no slot at all (a `let` scalar materializes its value at every read,
	// and `dataLabelOf` panics rather than inventing a label for one), and a STORED binding whose label no
	// `globalAddr` in the program names is dead.
	function contributesStorage(decl TopLevelDecl, live LiveGlobalLabelSet) returns bool
		if not decl.hasStorage 'inlinedHasNoSlot'
			return false
		end 'inlinedHasNoSlot'

		// ⛔ **A FILE-SCOPE GLOBAL WHOSE INITIALIZER CALLS IS NEVER DROPPED, EVEN WHEN NOTHING READS IT** — the
		// obligation `DeadGlobalElimination`'s header filed against the day an initializer may call, landing
		// here (spec-port `map-struct-bytearray`). Dropping the arena entry drops the `__module_init` ops
		// TOO, and for every other kind that is exactly right: they are compiler-synthesized allocation with
		// no user-visible effect. A user factory can `print`, mutate another global or throw, and
		// `specs/dead-top-level-var-elim.md:31` is explicit that "the call still runs even when the slot is
		// dead". Keeping the slot is the conservative half of that — a dead 8-byte word, never a lost effect
		// — and it is deliberately NOT the oracle's `callee.EndsWith(".create")` naming heuristic, which
		// makes renaming a method change whether its side effects run (see that header).
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **AND IT IS FILE-SCOPE ONLY, BECAUSE FOR A `static` MEMBER THE OPPOSITE IS CANONICAL**
		// (spec-port `lazy-static`). `specs/lazy-static.md` says a static field's initializer runs *"the first
		// time the static field is accessed"* — so a static nothing accesses must run NOTHING, and keeping it
		// alive to preserve a side effect would preserve the very side effect the language says must not
		// happen. MEASURED on the oracle: the same never-read binding prints `1` at file scope and `0` as a
		// static member. A lazy static's storage is therefore decided by liveness alone, which is exactly
		// right — its initializer runs only from an access, so no access means no run to preserve.
		match self.declScopeInitializerCall(decl) 'initializer'
			none then break 'initializer'
			calls then return true
		end 'initializer'

		return live.contains(self.dataLabelOf(decl))
	end 'contributesStorage'

	// `declInitializerCall`, silenced for a LAZY slot — the two places the eager "keep it for its side
	// effects" rule is asked (`contributesStorage` above, `globalInitCallees` below). It is a separate
	// function rather than a condition inside `declInitializerCall` because that answer has a SECOND reader
	// that must still see a lazy slot's callees: they are reached from `__lazy_init$…`, which is real code
	// that has to be lowered.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHICH READER THAT IS MOVED AT W115, AND THIS HEADER NAMED THE OLD ONE.** It used to be
	// `StdlibFacts.deriveStdlibFacts`, reached through `globalInitCallees` — rooting a lazy static's callees
	// beside `main`, which is the very claim W115 measured as false (see `globalInitCallees`). The reader now
	// is `assignDataLabels`' lazy branch, which files them under the slot's label for
	// `lazyStaticInitCalleesOf` to answer AT THE ACCESS. The split still earns its keep for the same reason,
	// against a different second reader.
	function declScopeInitializerCall(decl TopLevelDecl) returns TopLevelInitializerCall
		if self.declSlotIsLazy(decl) 'runsOnFirstAccessOrNotAtAll'
			return TopLevelInitializerCall.none
		end 'runsOnFirstAccessOrNotAtAll'

		return self.declInitializerCall(decl)
	end 'declScopeInitializerCall'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS DECLARATION'S SLOT FILLED ON FIRST ACCESS?** — the ONE predicate behind the lazy-init
	// map, the cleanup's null guard and the storage rule above, so the three cannot disagree about which
	// slots can legally be null at run time.
	//
	// TWO conditions, and both are necessary. **The SCOPE**, which is canonical (see `TopLevelDecl.scope`).
	// And **having a RECORD slot at all**: a scalar static's value is written into the `.data` image before
	// anything runs — `specs/lazy-static.md` says constant initializers "continue to be evaluated at compile
	// time" — so there is no initializer to defer and, decisively, no null a guard could test. An inlined
	// managed `let` has no slot either. The record arms are named as the same band `managedGlobals` walks.
	export function declSlotIsLazy(decl TopLevelDecl) returns bool
		if not declaredInATypeBody(decl) or not decl.hasStorage 'eagerOrSlotless'
			return false
		end 'eagerOrSlotless'

		match decl.outcome 'state'
			// Everything that is not a stored RECORD: unevaluated, a folded scalar, or a failure. None has a
			// slot that can be null, so none can be lazy.
			pending to poisoned then return false
			managedValue to factoryValue then return true
		end 'state'
	end 'declSlotIsLazy'

	// Does this declaration's initializer RUN USER CODE, and whose? Asked of the OUTCOME rather than of a
	// name so it cannot be answered by a spelling, and answered ONCE for both readers — see
	// `TopLevelInitializerCall`, whose header carries why the two halves may not be derived separately.
	//
	// A range arm over the whole union, ending at the current last variant, so an outcome appended past
	// `factoryValue` FAILS TO COMPILE here rather than being silently filed as "does not call".
	function declInitializerCall(decl TopLevelDecl) returns TopLevelInitializerCall
		match decl.outcome 'state'
			pending to arrayValue then return TopLevelInitializerCall.none
			containerValue(giid, _) then return self.containerInitializerCall(giid)
			factoryValue(calls, _, _) then return TopLevelInitializerCall.calls(self.factoryInitializerCallees(calls))
		end 'state'
	end 'declInitializerCall'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS DECLARATION CONSTRUCT A STRUCT WITH AN INTERFACE-TYPED FIELD? (W116 review)** — asked by
	// the REAL PARSE, which is the FIRST door that can answer it and the first that can report it.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE CONSTANT EVALUATOR STRUCTURALLY CANNOT SEE ONE, WHICH IS WHY THE REFUSAL IS NOT THERE.** A
	// swept field annotated with an interface is a bare `named` until `normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes` re-tags
	// the COLUMN, and `Queries` runs that AFTER `evaluateInitializers` — `RecordedReturnTypeRule`'s header
	// states that ordering and why neither half of it is free. So the evaluator's field check compared a
	// SCALAR against a slot nothing had yet called an interface, `Holder{s: 5}` "agreed", and the box got ONE
	// store into a TWO-slot field: the witness half stayed whatever `__mm_alloc` left there and the first
	// dispatch through it was an ACCESS VIOLATION (MEASURED, exit 0xC0000005). By the real parse the column is
	// re-tagged and `raiseBindingFailure` already has the positioned channel, so nothing new is invented —
	// the refusal is the BODY path's own sentence, through the one producer both throw it from.
	//
	// It answers for the WHOLE arena rather than for the root, because a nested construction fills a box the
	// same way (`Outer{inner: Inner{…}}` is refused earlier by E3076 today, but nothing about THIS rule may
	// depend on that staying true).
	//
	// ⚠ **IT TAKES THE SETTLED OUTCOME AND NOT THE DECL, WHICH IS NOT AN ECONOMY.** The real parse hands its
	// binding door a decl it has just READ from the tokens, and that one's own `outcome` field is the
	// `pending` every freshly-read binding carries — the settled one lives on the SWEEP's record. A parameter
	// that cannot be given the wrong one is worth more here than one that reads convenient.
	export function existentialFieldOfConstConstruction(outcome TopLevelConstant) returns ConstConstructionFieldLookup
		match outcome 'state'
			// Every outcome that builds no box from a layout: unevaluated, failed, a folded scalar, a
			// materialized record, a container. ONE range, ending where the arena-carrying arm begins — it was
			// three arms while `characterSetValue` sat past `factoryValue` and could not join a range (W129
			// deleted it). The arena arm is the only thing this question parts on.
			pending to containerValue then return ConstConstructionFieldLookup.none
			factoryValue(calls, _, _) then return self.existentialFieldOfArena(calls)
		end 'state'
	end 'existentialFieldOfConstConstruction'

	// The walk of the question above, split out because a `match` arm may not open one.
	function existentialFieldOfArena(calls ConstFactoryCallArray) returns ConstConstructionFieldLookup
		for call in calls 'eachNode'
			match call 'node'
				call then continue
				construct(typeName, _) then return self.existentialFieldOfStruct(typeName)
			end 'node'
		end 'eachNode'

		return ConstConstructionFieldLookup.none
	end 'existentialFieldOfArena'

	// The first interface-typed slot of one constructed layout. `structOf` is asked rather than
	// `constStructLayoutOf` because a MISS here is not this door's to panic on: the same name is resolved
	// again, with the panic, where the box is actually built (`globalStructConstruct`).
	function existentialFieldOfStruct(typeName ByteArray) returns ConstConstructionFieldLookup
		match self.structOf(typeName) 'declared'
			undeclared then return ConstConstructionFieldLookup.none
			declared(layout) then return self.existentialFieldOfLayout(layout)
		end 'declared'
	end 'existentialFieldOfStruct'

	// The tail of the question above, split out for its sibling's reason.
	function existentialFieldOfLayout(layout StructLayout) returns ConstConstructionFieldLookup
		for i in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachField'
			if maxonTypeTag(self.fieldTypeOf(layout, index: i, into: self.typeNames)) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'aFatPointerSlot'
				return ConstConstructionFieldLookup.existentialField(layout, index: i)
			end 'aFatPointerSlot'
		end 'eachField'

		return ConstConstructionFieldLookup.none
	end 'existentialFieldOfLayout'

	// ⭐ **EVERY FACTORY IN THE TREE AND EVERY DECLARED GENERIC AMONG THEIR ARGUMENTS (W41)** — the calls
	// `ModuleInit.calledFactoryRecord` emits. The list is built from the SAME arena `globalInitCalls` resolves
	// into materialization instructions, so what is rooted and what is emitted are two readings of one outcome
	// rather than two walks that could disagree.
	//
	// ⭐ **A FLAT LOOP, AND THAT IS THE ARENA EARNING ITS KEEP** (spec-port `module-level-struct-var`). A
	// NESTED factory is a call like any other and must be rooted like any other — its body is reached only
	// from `__module_init`, which does not exist when the reachability walk runs — and because the tree is
	// held flat, "every callee in this initializer" is one pass over `calls` rather than a recursive descent
	// that a later arm could forget to make. The `call` argument arm therefore contributes NOTHING here: the
	// call it names is its own entry, and pushing it again would root one name twice.
	// ⭐ **A `construct` NODE NAMES NO CALLEE OF ITS OWN, AND ITS FIELDS STILL CONTRIBUTE (W116).** Its record
	// is an `__mm_alloc` plus one store per slot — compiler-synthesized allocation with no source body to
	// root, exactly as a builtin container's `create` is — but a FIELD is admitted from the same set an
	// argument is, so a `Holder{xs: StrMap.create()}` reaches a declared generic's static and a defaulted
	// field reaches its synthesized `__fieldDefault#…` helper. Both are rooted here or they are lowered by
	// nothing and `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead` fires.
	function factoryInitializerCallees(calls ConstFactoryCallArray) returns ByteArrayArray
		var callees = ByteArrayArray.create()
		for call in calls 'eachCall'
			match call 'node'
				call(callee, args) then self.appendCallNodeCallees(callees, callee: callee, args: args)
				construct(_, fields) then self.appendConstructNodeCallees(callees, fields: fields)
			end 'node'
		end 'eachCall'
		return callees
	end 'factoryInitializerCallees'

	// A CALL node: the function it names, then whatever its arguments name.
	function appendCallNodeCallees(callees ByteArrayArray, callee ByteArray, args ConstFactoryArgumentArray)
		callees.push(callee)
		for arg in args 'eachArgument'
			self.appendArgumentValueCallee(callees, value: arg.value)
		end 'eachArgument'
	end 'appendCallNodeCallees'

	// A CONSTRUCT node: its FIELDS only. The box is an `__mm_alloc` plus one store per slot, so the node names
	// no function of its own — compiler-synthesized allocation with no source body to reach, exactly as a
	// builtin container's `create` is.
	function appendConstructNodeCallees(callees ByteArrayArray, fields ConstFactoryArgumentValueArray)
		for value in fields 'eachField'
			self.appendArgumentValueCallee(callees, value: value)
		end 'eachField'
	end 'appendConstructNodeCallees'

	// What ONE argument or field value contributes. The `call` arm contributes NOTHING: the call it names is
	// its own arena entry, which this walk reaches on its own pass, and pushing it again would root one name
	// twice.
	function appendArgumentValueCallee(callees ByteArrayArray, value ConstFactoryArgumentValue)
		match value 'value'
			call then break 'value'
			folded(folded) then self.appendFoldedArgumentCallee(callees, folded: folded)
		end 'value'
	end 'appendArgumentValueCallee'

	// The callee a FOLDED argument contributes, which is one only for a declared generic's empty container.
	//
	// A range arm over every non-container folded kind, ending at the current last variant, so an arm
	// appended past `containerCreate` FAILS TO COMPILE here rather than being silently filed as "calls nothing".
	function appendFoldedArgumentCallee(callees ByteArrayArray, folded ConstFoldedValue)
		match folded 'value'
			scalar to arrayLiteral then return
			containerCreate(giid, _) then self.appendDeclaredInstanceCreateCallee(callees, giid: giid)
		end 'value'
	end 'appendFoldedArgumentCallee'

	// ⭐ **A CONTAINER GLOBAL CALLS IFF ITS `create` IS DECLARED CODE (W41)** — a BUILTIN container's record is
	// compiler-synthesized allocation with no source body to reach and no user-visible effect, which is why
	// this whole arm used to be `none`. Both halves of what `calls` then means are the union header's.
	//
	// It contributes through the same `appendDeclaredInstanceCreateCallee` the factory-ARGUMENT loop uses, and
	// an EMPTY list is what "the runtime builds this one" reads as — so the decision has one home and a
	// container rooted in one position cannot be missed in the other.
	function containerInitializerCall(giid GenericInstanceId) returns TopLevelInitializerCall
		var callees = ByteArrayArray.create()
		self.appendDeclaredInstanceCreateCallee(callees, giid: giid)
		if callees.count() == 0 'runtimeCreate'
			return TopLevelInitializerCall.none
		end 'runtimeCreate'

		return TopLevelInitializerCall.calls(callees)
	end 'containerInitializerCall'

	// The DECLARED code a container value contributes to the root set, or nothing when the runtime builds it.
	// It asks `instanceCreateIsDeclared` rather than re-reading the base roster, so which callee this reports
	// and which callee `containerCreateCall` emits are one derivation.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT IS THE `create` AND THE ELEMENT-ADD, BECAUSE `ModuleInit.builtContainerRecord` EMITS BOTH
	// (W90).** Rooting only the create is what a `Set from […]` global measured as
	// `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead: 'Set.insert' is in StdlibFacts.unreachable` — the walk never saw
	// the edge, no body was lowered, and dead-function elimination reached the empty function `__module_init`
	// calls. Both come off `containerCreateCall`'s own routers, so what is rooted here and what is emitted
	// there stay two readings of one answer.
	//
	// ⚠ The add is rooted whether or not this global HAS elements — an over-rooted leaf is lowered and then
	// dropped by dead-function elimination, while a missing root is the panic above.
	function appendDeclaredInstanceCreateCallee(callees ByteArrayArray, giid GenericInstanceId)
		if not self.instanceCreateIsDeclared(giid) 'runtimeCreate'
			return
		end 'runtimeCreate'

		callees.push(self.declaredInstanceCreateCallee(giid))
		match self.declaredContainerElementAdd(giid) 'add'
			push(callee) then callees.push(callee)
			unsupported then break 'add'
		end 'add'
	end 'appendDeclaredInstanceCreateCallee'

	// ⭐ **EVERY FUNCTION A TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER CALLS**, in ARENA order — the roots
	// `StdlibSource.deriveStdlibFacts` walks from BESIDE `main`, because `__module_init` is synthesized
	// after that walk and is called by the entry stub rather than from `main`. Without them a stdlib
	// factory named only by an initializer is filed unreachable, its body is never lowered, and
	// dead-function elimination — which DOES root `__module_init` — reaches the empty function it left.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT TAKES NO `LiveGlobalLabelSet`, AND NEEDING NONE IS WHAT BREAKS THE ORDERING CYCLE.** The walk
	// this feeds runs BEFORE `liveGlobalLabels`, which runs before `installManagedGlobalInit`; a query here
	// that depended on liveness would close the loop. It does not have to: an EAGER declaration whose
	// initializer calls contributes storage whatever names it (`contributesStorage`, one arm above), so the
	// filter that walk is computing cannot change this answer.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND THAT IS WHY IT ASKS `declScopeInitializerCall` RATHER THAN `declInitializerCall` (W115).**
	// The sentence above is true of a file-scope global and FALSE of a lazy static, whose storage
	// `contributesStorage` decides by liveness alone — *"its initializer runs only from an access, so no
	// access means no run to preserve"*. Rooting one here says the opposite: it runs unconditionally, beside
	// `main`. That went unmeasured while no LISTED module declared a lazy static, and `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon`
	// declares ELEVEN, each building a `Set with Character`. MEASURED with them rooted, on a program whose
	// whole text is `var flag = true`: `Set.create`/`Set.insert` and the `Array` cone behind them reached in
	// EVERY compile, `scanRuntimeUsage` turned the heap on, and the image came out with
	// `data { __slab_cursor@0  __slab_end@8  __mm_alloc_count@16 }` and `panic at Array.maxon:146…` at
	// `.rdata` byte 0 — the identical pair of symptoms W17 repaired for the witness seed
	// (`StdlibSource.conformersMintedWithoutTheirOwnMethods`), reached by a second road, and five
	// `static-variables/data-section-*` pins plus
	// `stdlib-whitelist.a-listed-modules-literals-cannot-reach-the-rdata-image` read it red.
	//
	// ⇒ **A LAZY STATIC'S CALLEES ARE CREDITED AT THE ACCESS INSTEAD** — `lazyStaticInitCalleesOf`, read by
	// `StdlibSource.markLazyStaticInitCallees` off the `globalAddr` that IS the call to its `__lazy_init$`.
	// That is the true call graph rather than an approximation of it, and it needs no liveness: the walk
	// asking it is the walk liveness would have been derived from.
	export function globalInitCallees() returns ByteArrayArray
		var callees = ByteArrayArray.create()
		for i in 0 upto self.decls.count() 'each'
			match self.declScopeInitializerCall(self.declAt(i)) 'initializer'
				none then break 'initializer'
				calls(names) then callees.append(names)
			end 'initializer'
		end 'each'
		return callees
	end 'globalInitCallees'

	// ⭐ **WHAT THE LAZY SLOT `label` NAMES CALLS, or nothing when `label` is eager, unknown, or initialized
	// by no call.** The half of the root set `globalInitCallees` deliberately does not carry; see there.
	// Answering with the SHARED EMPTY for the overwhelmingly common miss keeps this allocation-free at the
	// one call site that asks it — once per `globalAddr` op of every reached function.
	export function lazyStaticInitCalleesOf(label ByteArray) returns ByteArrayArray
		return try self.lazyStaticInitCallees.get(label) otherwise sharedEmptyLazyStaticInitCallees
	end 'lazyStaticInitCalleesOf'

	// EVERY LIVE top-level global in the program, in ARENA (declaration) order, with the label and value
	// its `.data` slot carries — the door the backend's data-section layout asks through.
	//
	// It is asked ONCE per compile (`Compiler.declaredGlobals`), never per file: it is O(globals),
	// and a whole-program query under a per-file one is asked once per FILE, which is how a linear
	// walk becomes quadratic (see the ARCHITECTURE's warning on this exact query).
	//
	// A var whose initializer FAILED contributes no slot: the build has already failed on its
	// diagnostic, and there is no value to lay down.
	export function declaredGlobals(live LiveGlobalLabelSet) returns DataSectionEntryArray
		var entries = DataSectionEntryArray.create()
		for i in 0 upto self.decls.count() 'each'
			let decl = self.declAt(i)
			if not self.contributesStorage(decl, live: live) 'noReachableSlot'
				continue
			end 'noReachableSlot'
			match decl.outcome 'state'
				value(payload, tag) then entries.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(self.dataLabelOf(decl)), valueType: globalStdType(tag, name: decl.name), value: payload))
				failed to poisoned then continue
				// ⭐ EVERY RECORD BINDING reserves a POINTER slot holding NULL, and a LAZY one keeps that null until
				// its first access rather than only until `__module_init` — a managed literal (P1.7 slice
				// 2a), an array literal (slice 2b) and a container factory alike, and now for a `let` too. The
				// record it will point at cannot be baked into a constant image — a data→data pointer has no
				// compile-time VA under ASLR (`specs/static-variables.md:729-730`) — so the bytes are zero
				// here and `__module_init` writes the address before `main` runs.
				managedValue to containerValue then entries.push(self.nullPointerSlot(decl, valueType: self.foldedValueType(self.foldedValueOf(decl.outcome, name: decl.name), elementFilePath: decl.declFilePath)))
				// ⭐ A FACTORY-CALL GLOBAL reserves the SAME null POINTER slot, for the same reason and one
				// more: the record does not exist until the callee has RUN, which is strictly later than the
				// image is written.
				factoryValue(_, _, returnType) then entries.push(self.nullPointerSlot(decl, valueType: returnType))
				pending to evaluating then panic("ProgramSignatures.declaredGlobals: '{String.from(decl.name)}' is unevaluated — `evaluateInitializers` runs to completion inside `queryProgramSignatures`")
			end 'state'
		end 'each'
		return entries
	end 'declaredGlobals'

	// The `.data` entry every RECORD-valued global reserves: one machine word of zero, holding the POINTER
	// `__module_init` writes before `main`. One function rather than one spelling per outcome arm, because
	// the bytes are the same zero in every case.
	function nullPointerSlot(decl TopLevelDecl, valueType MaxonType) returns DataSectionEntry
		return DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(self.dataLabelOf(decl)), valueType: self.recordPointerSlotType(valueType, name: decl.name), value: ManagedGlobalInitialSlot)
	end 'nullPointerSlot'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE `StdType` A RECORD-VALUED GLOBAL'S SLOT OCCUPIES (S2j)** — the RECORD path's own door, and
	// a door of its own because it and `globalStdType` ask DIFFERENT questions of the same tag. That one
	// asks *"may a global's INLINE VALUE be of this tag?"*, where the value IS the `.data` bytes and the
	// tag is the whole of what they hold. This one asks *"is this record reached through a single
	// pointer?"*, where the bytes are that pointer and the tag names only what it points AT.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE RECORD PATH USED TO ASK THE OTHER DOOR, AND THAT DOOR'S GUARD IS FALSE HERE.**
	// `globalStdType`'s `named` arm panics saying a global's type *"is inferred from a bare constant
	// initializer, so it is never a ranged alias"* — exactly right about a FOLDED SCALAR, and simply not
	// about a factory's declared RETURN type, which may be a BOXED UNION or a TUPLE ALIAS. Both of those
	// carry the bare `named` tag by design (`enumMatchFor` reads a union's; a tuple alias names the
	// tuple's own struct), both are records held by one pointer, and both are already dropped by a router
	// that accepts the tag — `managedFieldDropCallee` takes `structRef` OR `named`. Only the width was
	// refusing them, and the sentence it refused with described an `int` such a program does not contain.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT A SECOND WIDTH TABLE.** The machine word is still named exactly once, in
	// `valueTagToStdType`, which this returns through; what is local here is the REACHABILITY guard, and
	// that is the half that differs between the two callers.
	//
	// ⛔ **AN EXISTENTIAL IS THE ONE RECORD A SINGLE-POINTER SLOT CANNOT HOLD, and that refusal really is
	// the SLOT's** rather than inherited: an interface-typed value is the 16-byte fat pointer
	// `(value, witness)`, so its `.data` slot is TWO words. It is unreachable today for a reason that is
	// an ORDERING accident and not a rule — a swept interface annotation is still a bare `named` when
	// `evaluateInitializers` runs, so `slotTypeIsManaged` reads it as a scalar and
	// `requireFactoryReturnsARecord` refuses it with an E2015 first — which is precisely why the
	// case is stated here rather than assumed away.
	function recordPointerSlotType(valueType MaxonType, name ByteArray) returns StdType
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(valueType)
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'fatPointer'
			panic("recordPointerSlotType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is built by a factory returning an interface-typed value, which is the two-word fat pointer (value, witness) — a `.data` slot for one is TWO words, which this single-pointer door cannot express")
		end 'fatPointer'
		// A record still spelling a bare `named` — a boxed union, a tuple alias, a generic alias — is
		// REFUSED at the declaration (`GlobalFactoryReturn.unbridgedRecord`), because the synthesized init
		// and cleanup have no width to access its slot through. Typing the slot anyway would be right about
		// the eight bytes and would leave that refusal to a panic three passes later, which is the shape
		// this whole door exists to end.
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.named 'noConcreteSlotAccess'
			panic("recordPointerSlotType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is built by a factory whose return type is still the bare name '{maxonTypeName(valueType, typeNames: self.typeNames)}' — `Parser.requireFactoryReturnsARecord` refuses that at the declaration, so reaching here means a record was admitted without a concrete slot type")
		end 'noConcreteSlotAccess'
		// Asked through the very predicate that ADMITTED the declaration, so the slot cannot be typed for a
		// shape the admission did not pass. A scalar reaching here is a classification drift, not a program.
		if not self.slotTypeIsManaged(valueType) 'ownsNoHeap'
			panic("recordPointerSlotType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is typed '{maxonTypeName(valueType, typeNames: self.typeNames)}', which owns no heap — a record global's slot holds a POINTER, and every caller classifies the value a record first (`Parser.requireFactoryReturnsARecord` for a factory, `foldedValueType` for a literal)")
		end 'ownsNoHeap'

		return valueTagToStdType(tag)
	end 'recordPointerSlotType'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES A READ OR WRITE OF `label` HAVE TO RUN AN INITIALIZER FIRST, AND WHICH?** — the query
	// `Parser.emitGlobalAddr` asks at EVERY access to a global slot (spec-port `lazy-static`).
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS ASKED OF THE ARENA, NOT OF THE LABEL'S SPELLING.** A `static` member's label is a bare name
	// exactly like a file-scope var's (`assignDataLabels` strips the qualifier so goldens carry no type
	// prefix), so nothing about the text can answer this — only the declaration can, through the
	// `TopLevelDecl.scope` its reader recorded.
	//
	// ⚠ **A SCALAR STATIC IS NOT LAZY, AND THAT IS CANONICAL RATHER THAN AN OPTIMIZATION.**
	// `specs/lazy-static.md` ends its Semantics list with *"Constant initializers (integer, float, bool
	// literals) continue to be evaluated at compile time"* — their value IS the `.data` image, written before
	// anything runs, so there is nothing to defer and no null a guard could test. The filter is therefore the
	// same `managedTopLevelNames`/record split every other door here makes, expressed as: only a decl whose
	// outcome is a stored RECORD gets a routine.
	export function lazyStaticInitCalleeFor(label ByteArray) returns LazyStaticInit
		if not self.lazyStaticSlots.contains(label) 'eagerOrNotAGlobal'
			return LazyStaticInit.none
		end 'eagerOrNotAGlobal'

		return LazyStaticInit.callee(lazyStaticInitCallee(label))
	end 'lazyStaticInitCalleeFor'

	// ⭐ **EVERY LIVE MANAGED TOP-LEVEL GLOBAL IN THE PROGRAM, in ARENA (declaration) order** — the door
	// `installManagedGlobalInit` asks through, and the ONLY enumeration of them.
	//
	// It is the `declaredGlobals` twin and deliberately a SECOND walk rather than a widened first: that one
	// answers "what does the `.data` image hold?" (a label, a width, some bytes) and this one answers "what
	// must run before and after `main`?" (a label, the literal's bytes, its type). Fusing them would hand
	// the layout a payload it has no use for and the synthesis a `StdType` it cannot build a record from.
	//
	// Asked ONCE per compile (`Compiler.compileToCodeResult`), never per file — it is O(declarations), and a
	// whole-program query under a per-file one is asked once per FILE, which is how a linear walk becomes
	// quadratic (see the ARCHITECTURE's warning on this query).
	//
	// A var whose initializer FAILED contributes nothing: the build has already failed on its diagnostic.
	export function managedGlobals(live LiveGlobalLabelSet) returns ManagedGlobalArray
		var entries = ManagedGlobalArray.create()
		for i in 0 upto self.decls.count() 'each'
			let decl = self.declAt(i)
			if not self.contributesStorage(decl, live: live) 'noReachableSlot'
				continue
			end 'noReachableSlot'
			match decl.outcome 'state'
				value to poisoned then continue
				// ⭐ A MANAGED LITERAL, AN ARRAY LITERAL (P1.7 slice 2b) AND A CONTAINER FACTORY are all managed
				// globals whichever keyword declared them — the record is built before `main` and released
				// after, and `let` refuses only the WRITE. All three route through `globalRecordOf`, which is
				// also what a factory ARGUMENT of the same shape routes through: a container factory IS an
				// array literal with no elements, and that is a fact about the record rather than a shortcut.
				managedValue to containerValue then entries.push(self.recordGlobalOf(decl))
				// ⭐ A FACTORY-CALL GLOBAL's slot holds whatever the callee returns, so its construction is the
				// CALL and its type is the callee's declared return type — settled when the outcome was.
				factoryValue(calls, root, returnType) then entries.push(self.managedGlobalEntry(decl, initializer: ManagedGlobalInitializer.factoryCall(self.globalInitCalls(calls, declName: decl.name, elementFilePath: decl.declFilePath), root: root), valueType: returnType))
				pending to evaluating then panic("ProgramSignatures.managedGlobals: '{String.from(decl.name)}' is unevaluated — `evaluateInitializers` runs to completion inside `queryProgramSignatures`")
			end 'state'
		end 'each'
		return entries
	end 'managedGlobals'

	// One managed global's full description. Split out so each `match` arm above stays one line, and it takes
	// the TYPE its arm already built rather than re-deriving one: the drop callee is routed off that same
	// type, so the record the init builds and the drop the cleanup emits cannot name two different types.
	function managedGlobalEntry(decl TopLevelDecl, initializer ManagedGlobalInitializer, valueType MaxonType) returns ManagedGlobal
		return ManagedGlobal.create(self.dataLabelOf(decl), initializer: initializer, valueType: valueType, dropCallee: managedFieldDropCallee(self, typeNames: self.typeNames, fieldType: valueType), range: declInitializerRange(decl), filledOnFirstAccess: self.declSlotIsLazy(decl))
	end 'managedGlobalEntry'

	// The entry for a global whose slot the compiler MATERIALIZES: resolve the folded value to its record and
	// its type together, then file both. It resolves the outcome itself rather than taking one, so the record
	// arm cannot pass a type derived from anything but its own build.
	function recordGlobalOf(decl TopLevelDecl) returns ManagedGlobal
		let record = self.globalRecordOf(self.foldedValueOf(decl.outcome, name: decl.name), elementFilePath: decl.declFilePath)
		return self.managedGlobalEntry(decl, initializer: ManagedGlobalInitializer.record(record.build), valueType: record.valueType)
	end 'recordGlobalOf'

	// ⭐ **A FACTORY-CALL GLOBAL'S WHOLE CALL TREE, RESOLVED FOR THE SYNTHESIS** — the fold arena mapped
	// ENTRY-FOR-ENTRY, which is what lets the ids the evaluator minted travel into the emitter unchanged.
	// Re-numbering here (say, by walking from the root) would be a second identity for the same call, and the
	// `root` the outcome carries would then name a different entry on each side.
	function globalInitCalls(calls ConstFactoryCallArray, declName ByteArray, elementFilePath FilePath) returns GlobalInitCallArray
		var resolved = GlobalInitCallArray.create()
		for call in calls 'eachCall'
			match call 'node'
				call(callee, args) then resolved.push(GlobalInitCall.call(callee, args: self.globalInitArguments(args, callee: callee, calls: calls, declName: declName, elementFilePath: elementFilePath)))
				construct(typeName, fields) then resolved.push(self.globalStructConstruct(typeName, fields: fields, calls: calls, declName: declName, elementFilePath: elementFilePath))
			end 'node'
		end 'eachCall'

		// ⚠ **THE MAPPING IS ONE-TO-ONE, AND A FILTER HERE WOULD BE A WRONG ANSWER RATHER THAN A CRASH.** Every
		// id already minted — `root`, and every `call(id)` argument — indexes BOTH arenas, so dropping or
		// merging an entry would silently make each one name a different call and `__module_init` would build
		// the wrong tree. This cannot see a REORDER, which nothing here does; it pins the half a later filter
		// would break, at the one point both counts are in hand.
		if resolved.count() != calls.count() 'notEntryForEntry'
			panic("ProgramSignatures.globalInitCalls: resolved {resolved.count()} of {calls.count()} calls for '{String.from(declName)}' — the two arenas are indexed by ONE set of ids, so this loop must map entry-for-entry and may not filter")
		end 'notEntryForEntry'

		return resolved
	end 'globalInitCalls'

	// ⭐ **EVERY ARGUMENT OF ONE CALL, RESOLVED FOR THE SYNTHESIS.** Each folded argument becomes either a
	// number to materialize as a `literal` or one of the record constructions `globalRecordOf` resolves — the
	// same door the global's own slot goes through — and a NESTED call rides through as the id it already is.
	// The LABEL rides through untouched: `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerCall` reorders a labelled call against the
	// callee's declared parameter names, so the synthesis emits the arguments in SOURCE order exactly as a
	// body's call site does.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE CALLEE'S CONSUME FACTS ARE ASKED ONCE, HERE, AND SLOTTED PER ARGUMENT** — the shape
	// `Parser.applyCallerConsume` uses at a body's call site, over the same `consumeOf` entry and the same
	// `argSlotPosition` label→position mapping, so "did the callee take this one?" has one answer whichever
	// position the call was written in.
	function globalInitArguments(args ConstFactoryArgumentArray, callee ByteArray, calls ConstFactoryCallArray, declName ByteArray, elementFilePath FilePath) returns GlobalInitArgumentArray
		let consume = self.consumeOf(callee)
		var resolved = GlobalInitArgumentArray.create()
		for i in 0 upto args.count() 'eachArgument'
			let arg = try args.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.globalInitArguments: args.get OOB at {i} below count {args.count()}")
			resolved.push(self.globalInitArgument(arg, consume: consume, argIndex: i, calls: calls, declName: declName, elementFilePath: elementFilePath))
		end 'eachArgument'
		return resolved
	end 'globalInitArguments'

	// ONE argument, resolved. A SCALAR carries its number and the type it is minted at — rebuilt from the
	// folded tag alone (`UnnamedTypeId`, because the evaluator folds an integer, a bool or a float and none
	// of the three names a type). The call site's DECLARED parameter type is deliberately not consulted:
	// `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerCall` slots the argument against the signature exactly as it does for a body's
	// call, so a second reading of the declaration here could only ever disagree with the one that matters.
	// A scalar owns nothing, so it needs no cleanup; a RECORD's and a nested CALL's is the question below.
	//
	// ⭐ **A NESTED CALL'S TYPE IS ITS CALLEE'S DECLARED RETURN, resolved through the very door the ROOT
	// call's is** (`declaredReturnValueType`) — so the type `__module_init` mints the inner result at and the
	// type a global of the same factory would be slotted as are one derivation. The evaluator has already
	// established it is a record (`Parser.requireFactoryReturnsARecord`, at the nested position), which is what
	// makes the cleanup router below answerable for it.
	function globalInitArgument(arg ConstFactoryArgument, consume CalleeConsume, argIndex ParamCount, calls ConstFactoryCallArray, declName ByteArray, elementFilePath FilePath) returns GlobalInitArgument
		let produced = self.globalInitValue(arg.value, calls: calls, declName: declName, elementFilePath: elementFilePath)
		return match produced.value 'produced'
			// A scalar owns nothing, so it needs no cleanup.
			scalar gives GlobalInitArgument.create(arg.label, value: produced.value, valueType: produced.valueType, cleanup: GlobalInitArgumentCleanup.none)
			record to call gives self.ownedRecordArgument(arg, consume: consume, argIndex: argIndex, valueType: produced.valueType, value: produced.value)
		end 'produced'
	end 'globalInitArgument'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT ONE CONSTANT BECOMES FOR THE SYNTHESIS, AND THE TYPE IT IS MINTED AT — the ONE producer, read
	// by a factory ARGUMENT and by a struct-literal FIELD (W116).** The two differ only in what they do with the
	// answer: an argument asks who frees it (`recordArgumentCleanup`), a field is consumed by the box and asks
	// nothing. Splitting the production too would let a `String` argument and a `String` field come to be
	// materialized two ways — which is precisely the divergence `globalRecordOf`'s header exists to prevent one
	// tier down.
	function globalInitValue(value ConstFactoryArgumentValue, calls ConstFactoryCallArray, declName ByteArray, elementFilePath FilePath) returns GlobalInitValueAndType
		return match value 'value'
			call(id) gives GlobalInitValueAndType.create(GlobalInitArgumentValue.call(id), valueType: self.constFactoryCallResultType(factoryCallAt(calls, id: id), name: declName))
			folded(folded) gives self.foldedInitValue(folded, elementFilePath: elementFilePath)
		end 'value'
	end 'globalInitValue'

	// The scalar/record fork over a value the compiler MATERIALIZES — the walk this side has always had,
	// reached through one arm of the union above so each union is matched exactly once. A SCALAR's type is
	// rebuilt from the folded tag alone (`UnnamedTypeId`, because the evaluator folds an integer, a bool or a
	// float and none of the three names a type).
	function foldedInitValue(folded ConstFoldedValue, elementFilePath FilePath) returns GlobalInitValueAndType
		match folded 'kind'
			scalar(value) then return GlobalInitValueAndType.create(GlobalInitArgumentValue.scalar(value.payload), valueType: self.foldedMintType(folded, elementFilePath: elementFilePath))
			managed to containerCreate then break 'kind'
		end 'kind'

		let record = self.globalRecordOf(folded, elementFilePath: elementFilePath)
		return GlobalInitValueAndType.create(GlobalInitArgumentValue.record(record.build), valueType: record.valueType)
	end 'foldedInitValue'

	// ⭐ **A STRUCT LITERAL'S BOX, RESOLVED (W116)** — the payload size and one instruction per DECLARED field,
	// in layout order, off the very layout the fold tier held the fields to. The columns are walked here rather
	// than carried from the parser because each of them — the offset, the slot width, the value's own type —
	// resolves against THIS index's interner and registries, which is the discipline
	// `GlobalInitCall.construct` states.
	function globalStructConstruct(typeName ByteArray, fields ConstFactoryArgumentValueArray, calls ConstFactoryCallArray, declName ByteArray, elementFilePath FilePath) returns GlobalInitCall
		let layout = self.constStructLayoutOf(typeName, declName: declName)
		if fields.count() != layout.fieldCount() 'notLayoutParallel'
			panic("ProgramSignatures.globalStructConstruct: '{String.from(declName)}' constructs `{String.from(typeName)}` with {fields.count()} field value(s) against a layout of {layout.fieldCount()} — the fold tier fills one entry per DECLARED field (`Parser.evalConstStructConstructionInto`), from the literal or from the field's default, and refuses the declaration otherwise")
		end 'notLayoutParallel'

		var resolved = GlobalStructFieldArray.create()
		for i in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachField'
			let fieldType = self.fieldTypeOf(layout, index: i, into: self.typeNames)
			let produced = self.globalInitValue(try fields.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.globalStructConstruct: fields.get OOB at {i} below the count this loop was just held to"), calls: calls, declName: declName, elementFilePath: elementFilePath)
			resolved.push(GlobalStructField.create(layout.offsetOfField(i), storageType: slotStorageType(self, declared: fieldType, typeNames: self.typeNames, readerFilePath: layout.declFilePath), value: produced.value, valueType: produced.valueType))
		end 'eachField'

		return GlobalInitCall.construct(layout.sizeBytes(), fields: resolved)
	end 'globalStructConstruct'

	// ⭐⭐ **MAY THIS CONSTANT FILL THIS SLOT? (W116)** — asked by the FOLD tier, answered HERE, because both
	// halves of the comparison have to be resolved against the interner and the registries this index owns.
	// It is the const twin of `Parser.requireFieldType`, which cannot serve: that one reads a `ValueId` out of
	// the parser's value columns, and a top-level initializer mints no values at all.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT COMPARES THE DENOTED DECLARED TAG, PLUS THE STRUCT'S NAME WHERE THE TAG IS `structRef` — AND
	// THAT LIMIT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN CHOSEN.** The tag is what makes the STORE itself right or wrong: a
	// pointer written into a scalar slot reads back as a number and a number written into a managed slot is a
	// wild pointer, which is the class `Pair{a: "hi"}` falls in (E3005 on the oracle, MEASURED). `structRef`
	// earns its second half because a nested node returning the WRONG struct is the same hazard wearing a
	// matching tag, and both sides' ids are minted in `self.typeNames`.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT COMPARED THE *STORAGE WIDTH* AND THAT ADMITTED A SLOT KIND NOTHING MAY FILL** (W116 review).
	// `slotStorageType` is the width a `storeIndirect` MOVES, and a width is a LOSSY PROJECTION of a type: it
	// collapses a declared `enum`/`union` to `declaredAggregateSlotWidth()`, so `Paint{c: 7}` against
	// `let c as Color` "agreed", compiled, and stored a 7 no `Color` names — MEASURED on this tree, and the
	// runnable oracle refuses the same program (E3005). `constFieldSlotType` below answers with what the
	// field DECLARES instead, which refuses it, and leaves every width-equal case it already admitted
	// exactly where it was.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND WHERE IT CANNOT RESOLVE THE SLOT, IT DECLINES RATHER THAN GUESSING.** The width's recovery for
	// an unresolvable name was `integer`, and a rendered mismatch then said the slot held `'int'` for a field
	// declared `Sized` — a diagnostic naming a type the program does not contain. `unresolvableHere` says so
	// instead, and the two things that name IS reach it with the positions and the registries this door
	// lacks: `TypeResolution`'s E3011 for a name no file declares, and
	// `existentialFieldOfConstConstruction`'s E2015 for the interface case, at the real parse.
	//
	// ⛔ **A GENERIC INSTANCE IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* COMPARED BY IDENTITY, and comparing it would REFUSE A
	// PROGRAM THE ORACLE RUNS.** A top-level array literal is typed by the canonical instance its ELEMENT KIND
	// fixes (`constArrayValueType` — `Array with Integer`), never by the alias the slot was declared with, so
	// `Holder{xs: [1, 2, 3]}` against `var xs as CountArray` is two instance ids for one array. That is the
	// fold tier's own typing rule and not something this door may overrule; the argument position has never
	// compared them either.
	export function constStructFieldCheck(layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex, value ConstFactoryArgumentValue, calls ConstFactoryCallArray, declName ByteArray, elementFilePath FilePath) returns ConstStructFieldCheck
		let lookup = self.constFieldSlotType(layout, index: index)
		let slot = try slotTypeHeld(lookup) otherwise 'thisTierCannotSay'
			return ConstStructFieldCheck.agrees
		end 'thisTierCannotSay'

		let held = self.constFieldValueType(value, calls: calls, declName: declName, elementFilePath: elementFilePath)
		if maxonTypeTag(slot) == maxonTypeTag(held) and (maxonTypeTag(slot) != ValueTypeTag.structRef or typeNameIdOf(slot) == typeNameIdOf(held)) 'fits'
			return ConstStructFieldCheck.agrees
		end 'fits'

		// The ONE conversion a slot performs on the way in — the same one `Parser.fillFieldDefault` applies to a
		// declared `= 3` meeting a `float(lo to hi)` field, so a written value and a defaulted one reach an f64
		// slot through one rule rather than two.
		if tagIsFloat(maxonTypeTag(slot)) and maxonTypeTag(held) == ValueTypeTag.integer 'widens'
			return ConstStructFieldCheck.widensToFloat
		end 'widens'

		return ConstStructFieldCheck.mismatch(maxonTypeName(held, typeNames: self.typeNames), expected: maxonTypeName(slot, typeNames: self.typeNames))
	end 'constStructFieldCheck'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE TYPE A FIELD'S CONSTANT IS CHECKED AGAINST — WHAT THE FIELD *DECLARES*, resolved through the
	// ONE cascade (`declaredSlotType`), and deliberately NOT `slotStorageType`'s width (W116 review).** The
	// two answer different questions and the box needs both: the width is what the `storeIndirect` MOVES and
	// stays `globalStructConstruct`'s, while what may legally stand in the slot is an IDENTITY question the
	// width cannot carry — see `constStructFieldCheck`'s header for the two wrong answers that proved it.
	//
	// ⚠ **A STILL-BARE `named` COMING BACK OUT OF THE CASCADE IS AN ANSWER, AND WHICH ANSWER IS
	// `declaredFormOf`'s TO SAY** — the very registry `denotedSlotType` consulted to decide to leave it
	// alone, asked again rather than guessed at, so this door cannot come to disagree with that one about
	// what a name is.
	function constFieldSlotType(layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex) returns ConstFieldSlotLookup
		let denoted = self.declaredSlotType(self.fieldTypeOf(layout, index: index, into: self.typeNames), into: self.typeNames, slotScopeFilePath: layout.declFilePath)
		if maxonTypeTag(denoted) != ValueTypeTag.named 'denotesAConcreteType'
			return ConstFieldSlotLookup.holds(denoted)
		end 'denotesAConcreteType'

		let name = self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(denoted))
		let form = try self.declaredFormOf(name) otherwise 'noFileDeclaresIt'
			// A name no registry this tier can see holds. TWO programs land here and neither is this door's
			// to judge: a field type NO file declares (E3011's subject, reported by `TypeResolution` against
			// the merged registry — the authority), and an INTERFACE-typed field, whose registry is filled
			// after the constant evaluator has run. The sweep is the tolerant half, and a verdict invented
			// here would MASK whichever diagnostic actually names the mistake.
			return ConstFieldSlotLookup.unresolvableHere
		end 'noFileDeclaresIt'

		return self.constFieldSlotTypeOfForm(form, name: name, denoted: denoted)
	end 'constFieldSlotType'

	// The tail of the question above, split out because a `match` arm may not open one.
	function constFieldSlotTypeOfForm(form TypeDeclKind, name ByteArray, denoted MaxonType) returns ConstFieldSlotLookup
		match form 'form'
			// An enum/union name IS its type (`denotedSlotType`'s own words — a payload-free enum's value is
			// its i64 tag), so it is compared AS the `named` it is and every constant mismatches it by tag.
			// That is the verdict the body path reaches through `requireFieldType`, in the same E3005.
			enumType to unionType then return ConstFieldSlotLookup.holds(denoted)
			// ⛔ **UNREACHABLE WHILE THE CONSTANT EVALUATOR IS THIS DOOR'S ONLY ASKER, AND SAYING SO IS THE
			// POINT.** `self.interfaces` is filled by `Queries.foldInterfaceDeclarations`, which runs AFTER
			// `evaluateInitializers` — `RecordedReturnTypeRule.declaredStruct`'s header states that ordering
			// and why neither position is free — so an interface name is answered by NO form at all here and
			// takes the `unresolvableHere` arm above. That is exactly why an interface-typed field is refused
			// at the REAL PARSE instead (`existentialFieldOfConstConstruction`). Should this door ever gain an
			// asker on the far side of that fold, DECLINING is still the right answer and not a second-best
			// one: the refusal that names a fat pointer belongs to the door that can see one.
			interfaceType then return ConstFieldSlotLookup.unresolvableHere
			// An INTEGER ranged alias, the one alias form the cascade deliberately leaves bare: its NAME
			// carries the range and the signedness, and the RANGE is checked on its own
			// (`Parser.requireConstScalarSuitsFieldRange`). A FLOAT one never arrives — `rangedAliasSlotType`
			// re-tags it — so what this admits is exactly an integer.
			rangedAlias then return ConstFieldSlotLookup.holds(MaxonType.integer)
			// A GENERIC alias reaches here only when the cascade DECLINED to re-tag it, which
			// `slotTypeOfGenericAlias` does for exactly one shape — an instance over an `interface` base, i.e.
			// an existential wearing an alias's spelling. So it declines for the arm above's reason, and by
			// the same argument: a slot this door could not resolve is not a slot it may judge.
			genericAlias then return ConstFieldSlotLookup.unresolvableHere
			structType then panic("ProgramSignatures.constFieldSlotTypeOfForm: field type '{String.from(name)}' is a declared `type` and still reads `named` — `denotedSlotType`'s `structType` arm re-tags every one of them to `structRef`, so arriving here means that arm did not run for a name the same registry answers for")
			functionAlias then panic("ProgramSignatures.constFieldSlotTypeOfForm: field type '{String.from(name)}' is a function alias and still reads `named` — `denotedSlotType`'s `functionAlias` arm re-tags every one of them to `function`, unconditionally")
			tupleAlias then panic("ProgramSignatures.constFieldSlotTypeOfForm: field type '{String.from(name)}' is a tuple alias and still reads `named` — `denotedSlotType`'s `tupleAlias` arm re-tags every one of them to the canonical tuple's `structRef`, unconditionally")
		end 'form'
	end 'constFieldSlotTypeOfForm'

	// The type one field VALUE is minted at — a nested node's result, or what the constant folded to. It is
	// `globalInitValue`'s type half asked on its own, because the check runs at PARSE time where no record may
	// be built yet: `globalRecordOf` interns stamps and resolves drop callees, work that belongs to the
	// synthesis and would be thrown away here.
	function constFieldValueType(value ConstFactoryArgumentValue, calls ConstFactoryCallArray, declName ByteArray, elementFilePath FilePath) returns MaxonType
		return match value 'value'
			call(id) gives self.constFactoryCallResultType(factoryCallAt(calls, id: id), name: declName)
			folded(folded) gives self.foldedMintType(folded, elementFilePath: elementFilePath)
		end 'value'
	end 'constFieldValueType'

	// `foldedValueType` plus the arm it deliberately refuses. That one is a RECORD builder's door and panics
	// on a scalar for a good reason — a number occupies its slot as a value and has no record to describe —
	// while a TYPE CHECK must be able to ask about a number, so the scalar arm lives here and the other five
	// route through the one home they already had.
	function foldedMintType(folded ConstFoldedValue, elementFilePath FilePath) returns MaxonType
		match folded 'kind'
			scalar(value) then return maxonTypeOfTag(value.tag, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
			managed to containerCreate then break 'kind'
		end 'kind'

		return self.foldedValueType(folded, elementFilePath: elementFilePath)
	end 'foldedMintType'

	// The layout a `construct` node names. The fold tier admitted the node only after `Parser.requireConstructible`
	// answered `declared` for this very name, so a miss here is the declaration sweep and the arena disagreeing
	// about what a `type` is — `recordedDeclFor`'s drift guard, stated for a struct.
	function constStructLayoutOf(typeName ByteArray, declName ByteArray) returns StructLayout
		match self.structOf(typeName) 'declared'
			declared(layout) then return layout
			undeclared then panic("ProgramSignatures.constStructLayoutOf: '{String.from(declName)}' is initialized by a `{String.from(typeName)}\{…\}` literal, and no file declares `type {String.from(typeName)}` — the evaluator admits the form only after `Parser.requireConstructible` has resolved that very layout")
		end 'declared'
	end 'constStructLayoutOf'

	// An argument whose value is an owned RECORD — materialized here or returned by a nested call — paired
	// with the answer to who frees it. ONE assembly point, so the two producers cannot come to ask the
	// ownership question differently: a record is a record however it came to exist.
	function ownedRecordArgument(arg ConstFactoryArgument, consume CalleeConsume, argIndex ParamCount, valueType MaxonType, value GlobalInitArgumentValue) returns GlobalInitArgument
		return GlobalInitArgument.create(arg.label, value: value, valueType: valueType, cleanup: self.recordArgumentCleanup(consume, argIndex: argIndex, label: arg.label, valueType: valueType))
	end 'ownedRecordArgument'

	// ⭐ **WHO FREES THIS RECORD ARGUMENT?** A CONSUMED parameter is the callee's — it stored the record, and
	// dropping it here would be a double-free — so nothing is emitted. Everything else is still
	// `__module_init`'s, and its drop is the very callee `__maxon_global_cleanup` would use for a global of
	// that type, off the one router (`managedFieldDropCallee`) rather than a second reading of the type.
	//
	// ⭐ **A NESTED CALL'S RESULT IS ASKED THE SAME QUESTION** (spec-port `module-level-struct-var`). Where
	// the record came from is not the question — `Outer.create(Inner.create(0))` leaves `__module_init` sole
	// owner of the `Inner` the inner call returned, exactly as `Counter.create(Bytes.create())` leaves it
	// sole owner of a materialized one — so the only thing that decides is whether the enclosing factory took
	// it. That is why this takes a TYPE and a consume answer and never asks how the value was produced.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE DEFAULT IS TO DROP, and that direction is the load-bearing one.** `consumeOf` answers
	// `borrowsAll` for a callee with no recorded entry — the overwhelming majority — so a callee whose
	// consume the sweep cannot see leaks nothing; the failure mode of a wrong answer here is a double-free
	// only in the direction the sweep is POSITIVE about, which is the direction it is exact in.
	function recordArgumentCleanup(consume CalleeConsume, argIndex ParamCount, label ByteArray, valueType MaxonType) returns GlobalInitArgumentCleanup
		if calleeConsumesArgument(consume, argIndex: argIndex, label: label) 'movedIntoTheCallee'
			return GlobalInitArgumentCleanup.none
		end 'movedIntoTheCallee'

		return GlobalInitArgumentCleanup.dropAfterCall(managedFieldDropCallee(self, typeNames: self.typeNames, fieldType: valueType))
	end 'recordArgumentCleanup'

	// The record construction a CONTAINER-RECORD global asks for: this instance's `create` with the stamps it
	// fixes, then one push per element. The stamps come from the instance the global is TYPED by, so the
	// strides the record is born with and the type every use loads through are one answer rather than two.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE INSTANCE IS THE CALLER'S, because the two kinds of container global know it differently.**
	// A literal INFERS it from its element kind (`constArrayValueType`); a container factory READ it off the
	// alias the author wrote. Deriving it here would mean re-deriving one of those two from the other, and
	// an empty container has no element kind to derive from at all.
	// ⭐⭐ **AN ALL-CONSTANT ARRAY LITERAL IS ONE `.rdata` BLOB HERE TOO, AND IT IS THE SAME DECISION THE
	// PARSER MAKES INSIDE A FUNCTION** (`Parser.parseCollectedArrayLiteralBody`) — one rule about what a
	// constant array literal IS, asked at each of the two places a literal can be written rather than made
	// true in a body and false at file scope. `specs/codegen-internals.md` pins both spellings of it: three
	// of its cases write the literal in `main` and three write it at module scope, all six against the same
	// `RequiredRdata` bytes, and the runnable oracle lays down the identical 24 bytes for each.
	//
	// ⚠ **A `var` GLOBAL TAKES THIS ARM TOO, AND THE FILE HEADER'S "NOT AN IMMORTAL RECORD" RULE IS
	// UNBROKEN.** That rule is about SHARING one record: a `constArrayLiteral` mints a FRESH owned heap
	// 48-byte record per materialization over a blob that is merely read-only, stamped
	// `capacity@16 = RdataBufferCapacity` so the first receiver-writing method detaches onto a private heap
	// buffer. It is exactly what already makes a mutable `b"…"` global work, and
	// `rdata-global-var-array-cow-preserves-original` is the case that pins the whole of it.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ELEMENT COUNT IS WHAT SEPARATES A LITERAL FROM A FACTORY**, which is this union's own stated
	// rule ("a container factory IS an array literal with no elements"). An empty `IntArray.create()` global
	// must stay a growable heap record, so it falls through — an immortal empty blob would be a record that
	// cannot receive its first `push` without detaching from nothing.
	function containerRecordOf(elements ConstArrayElementArray, giid GenericInstanceId) returns ManagedGlobalRecord
		if elements.count() == 0 or not self.arrayElementMayBeConstantBlob(giid) 'notAConstantBlob'
			return ManagedGlobalRecord.containerRecord(self.containerCreateCall(giid), elements: elements)
		end 'notAConstantBlob'

		var constants = ParsedIntArray.create()
		for element in elements 'eachElement'
			constants.push(constArrayElementScalar(element))
		end 'eachElement'

		let blob = constArrayBlobOf(constants, stride: self.arrayElementSize(giid))
		return ManagedGlobalRecord.constArrayRecord(blob.stride, elementCount: blob.elementCount, bytes: blob.bytes)
	end 'containerRecordOf'

	// ⭐ **THE TYPE ONE FOLDED RECORD VALUE DENOTES** — what its `.data` slot is sized as, what its value is
	// minted at and what routes its drop. ONE derivation, read by the `.data` walk, the init walk and the
	// factory-argument builder alike; it used to be spelled once per arm in each of the first two, which is
	// three copies of a three-line map.
	function foldedValueType(value ConstFoldedValue, elementFilePath FilePath) returns MaxonType
		match value 'folded'
			scalar(folded) then panic("ProgramSignatures.foldedValueType: a folded scalar of tag '{typeTagName(folded.tag)}' names no record type — a scalar occupies its slot as a VALUE, and every caller splits the two before asking")
			managed(_, kind) then return self.managedConstValueType(kind, elementFilePath: elementFilePath)
			arrayLiteral(_, element) then return self.constArrayValueType(element)
			containerCreate(giid, _) then return MaxonType.genericInstance(giid)
		end 'folded'
	end 'foldedValueType'

	// ⭐ **RESOLVE ONE FOLDED VALUE INTO WHAT `__module_init` DOES WITH IT** — the bridge between the
	// evaluator's answer and the synthesis's instruction, and the ONE place the mapping is written. Both a
	// global's own slot and a factory ARGUMENT come through it, so a `String` argument is cloned exactly as a
	// `String` global is and a `Map` argument is stamped exactly as a `Map` global is.
	//
	// It hands back the TYPE beside the construction because the two are read together and must not be able
	// to disagree about what the record is.
	function globalRecordOf(value ConstFoldedValue, elementFilePath FilePath) returns GlobalRecordAndType
		let valueType = self.foldedValueType(value, elementFilePath: elementFilePath)
		match value 'folded'
			scalar(folded) then panic("ProgramSignatures.globalRecordOf: a folded scalar of tag '{typeTagName(folded.tag)}' has no record construction — `foldedValueType` above refuses the same value for the same reason")
			managed(bytes, kind) then return GlobalRecordAndType.create(managedLiteralRecord(bytes, kind: kind), valueType: valueType)
			// The instance is read off `valueType` — which `foldedValueType` above resolved from this very
			// element kind — rather than resolved a second time from the kind. One derivation, so the record's
			// stamps and the slot's type cannot come from two answers to one question.
			arrayLiteral(elements, _) then return GlobalRecordAndType.create(self.containerRecordOf(elements, giid: typeNameIdOf(valueType)), valueType: valueType)
			containerCreate(giid, elements) then return GlobalRecordAndType.create(self.containerRecordOf(elements, giid: giid), valueType: valueType)
		end 'folded'
	end 'globalRecordOf'

	// The folded value the three STORED-RECORD outcomes came from. It exists so ONE resolver serves a global's
	// own slot and a factory ARGUMENT: the outcome union records what a declaration SETTLED to (and carries
	// two arms an argument can never be), while `ConstFoldedValue` records what an initializer FOLDED to, and
	// these three arms are the same three facts wearing the first union's name.
	function foldedValueOf(outcome TopLevelConstant, name ByteArray) returns ConstFoldedValue
		match outcome 'o'
			pending to poisoned then panic("ProgramSignatures.foldedValueOf: '{String.from(name)}' settled to an outcome that stores no record — every caller filters on the stored-record arms first")
			managedValue(bytes, kind) then return ConstFoldedValue.managed(bytes, kind: kind)
			arrayValue(elements, element) then return ConstFoldedValue.arrayLiteral(elements, element: element)
			containerValue(giid, elements) then return ConstFoldedValue.containerCreate(giid, elements: elements)
			factoryValue then panic("ProgramSignatures.foldedValueOf: '{String.from(name)}' settled to a factory CALL, which is not a folded value — its record is built by calling user code, not by materializing a constant")
		end 'o'
	end 'foldedValueOf'

	// The recorded arena entry a top-level `decl` resolves to, keyed on the decl's OWN storage key
	// rather than on a bare name (a declaration resolves to ITSELF, never through the scoped-then-bare
	// probe a USE goes through). The single get-or-panic that `declaredOutcome` and `isRedeclaredBinding`
	// share, so the drift guard below reads exactly one way at both.
	//
	// **THE DRIFT GUARD LIVES HERE.** The sweep decides what is top level by counting block depth over
	// tokens (`Parser.foldDeclaredSignaturesInto`); the real parse decides it by actually parsing.
	// Those are two readings of one question, and a miss means they disagreed — after which the
	// declaration is absent from the index, every use of it reports "undefined variable", and a correct
	// program is rejected for a reason that names nothing the user wrote. It panics instead, naming
	// the drift, in the discipline `mergeArtifact`'s span-lockstep assert follows.
	function recordedDeclFor(decl TopLevelDecl) returns TopLevelDecl
		let index = try self.declIndex.get(decl.storageKey) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.recordedDeclFor: the declaration sweep never recorded top-level '{String.from(decl.name)}' of {decl.declFilePath.toString()}, which the parser is dispatching at top level — the sweep's block-depth gate and the parser's structure disagree about what is top level")
		return self.declAt(index)
	end 'recordedDeclFor'

	// What the DECLARATION the real parse just read came to — the door `Parser.raiseBindingFailure`
	// asks through.
	export function declaredOutcome(decl TopLevelDecl) returns TopLevelConstant
		return self.recordedDeclFor(decl).outcome
	end 'declaredOutcome'

	// ⭐ **IS `decl` A REDECLARATION — a top-level name the sweep already recorded under the same storage
	// key from a DIFFERENT source position?** `recordDecl` is first-wins, so the winner is the earliest
	// declaration and every later `let`/`var` of that name is a duplicate. The real parse asks this for
	// each top-level binding statement (`raiseBindingFailure`) and reports E3006 at the later one — the
	// top-level twin of the duplicate-FUNCTION check `commitFuncSignatures` makes, positioned the same
	// way (at the offending later declaration).
	//
	// ⚠ SCOPED TO ONE FILE, and — for an EXPORTED name — BOUNDED BY THE GLOBAL WINNER. Same storage key
	// means same name; for a FILE-PRIVATE name the key includes the path, so the winner is THIS file's
	// first declaration and same-file detection is complete. For an EXPORTED name the key is the bare
	// name (`topLevelStorageKey`), so the winner is the globally-first `export let A` across ALL files:
	// when it lives in another file the `crossFileExported` guard returns false — correctly for the pure
	// cross-file collision (which belongs to cross-file name resolution, see `commitStructTypes`), but it
	// ALSO misses a genuine SAME-FILE exported duplicate whenever another file's export wins the key
	// (order-dependent on file sort). That completeness rides with the same deferred cross-file rung; it
	// never falsely rejects a legal program and adds no wrong runtime answer. Re-deriving each side's
	// file-qualified key makes the guard a pure FILE comparison (equal names). Two distinct declarations
	// in one file cannot share a source position, so a differing `(line, column)` is exactly a
	// redeclaration.
	// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS THE SECOND DECLARATION OF A TOP-LEVEL NAME? — asked of the WHOLE PROGRAM, not of one
	// file (N1c).**
	//
	// **A GLOBAL'S NAME IS FLAT AND PROJECT-WIDE, and that is the ruling rather than an implementation
	// detail.** Namespacing scopes typealiases (E3063) and free functions (E3095); it deliberately does NOT
	// scope top-level bindings, because neither reference gives a binding a directory-qualified spelling at
	// all — v1 keys a module-scope `let` under its ENCLOSING TYPE (empty at file scope) and never under its
	// namespace, and `dir.CONSTANT` is not a form the language has. ⇒ two `export let LIMIT` are one name
	// declared twice, wherever the two files sit.
	//
	// ⛔ **THIS USED TO EXEMPT EXACTLY THAT CASE, AND THE EXEMPTION WAS A SILENT WRONG ANSWER.** An arm
	// labelled `crossFileExported` returned `false` whenever the winner's file differed from this
	// declaration's — so `alpha/` and `beta/` each exporting `LIMIT` compiled clean and every reader in the
	// program got ALPHA's value, `recordDecl` being first-wins. MEASURED at 42 where `beta`'s 34 was
	// unreachable; v1 refuses the same program outright (`reportDuplicateConstant`).
	//
	// ⚠ **AND DELETING IT COSTS THE FILE-PRIVATE RULE NOTHING, which is why the exemption was never needed.**
	// A file-private binding is filed under a FILE-SCOPED storage key (`topLevelStorageKey`), so two files'
	// private `counter`s never share a winner and never reach this comparison — `recordedDeclFor` hands each
	// file its own. The exemption only ever fired for declarations that DID share a key, which is precisely
	// the collision it was letting through. `file-private-same-name-cross-file-var` is the control, and it
	// stays green.
	//
	// The position comparison is what tells a SECOND declaration from the winner itself: `recordedDeclFor`
	// answers with the first declaration of the key, so a decl that is not at the winner's position is a
	// later one.
	export function isRedeclaredBinding(decl TopLevelDecl) returns bool
		let winner = self.recordedDeclFor(decl)
		//
		// ⚠ **THE FILE TEST COMPARES THE PATH STRINGS AND DELIBERATELY NOT `FilePath.equals` (N1c review).**
		// The stdlib's `equals` is the HOST FILESYSTEM's identity — case-INSENSITIVE on Windows — while every
		// "same file" question this index already answers is keyed on the raw string: `fileScopedDeclKey`
		// interpolates `declFilePath.toString()`, and `fileModuleDirectories` is keyed by it. Using `equals`
		// here would put a THIRD semantics beside those two rather than removing one, and it would pay two
		// `clone().toLower()` allocations at every top-level binding in the program. The two can only diverge
		// for paths differing in case alone, which one directory walk does not produce.
		return winner.nameLine != decl.nameLine or winner.nameColumn != decl.nameColumn or not winner.declFilePath.toString().equals(decl.declFilePath.toString())
	end 'isRedeclaredBinding'

	function resolvedValueOf(index TopLevelDeclIndex) returns TopLevelConstantLookup
		let decl = self.declAt(index)
		match decl.outcome 'state'
			value(payload, tag) then return TopLevelConstantLookup.found(payload, tag: tag)
			managedValue(bytes, kind) then return TopLevelConstantLookup.foundManaged(bytes, kind: kind)
			failed to poisoned then return TopLevelConstantLookup.notFound
			// ⭐ **AN ARRAY BINDING IS NOT A CONSTANT A USE CAN INLINE** (P1.7 slice 2b): its value is a heap
			// record with a `.data` slot, so a read is a LOAD and the caller's NEXT probe (`globalDeclFor`)
			// is the one that answers. That makes `notFound` the right answer here rather than merely a
			// defensive one — it is the same "no CONSTANT of that name is visible" a `var` has always given.
			arrayValue to factoryValue then return TopLevelConstantLookup.notFound
			pending to evaluating then panic("ProgramSignatures.resolvedValueOf: '{String.from(decl.name)}' is unevaluated at a use site — `evaluateInitializers` runs to completion before any file is parsed")
		end 'state'
	end 'resolvedValueOf'

	// **THE RESOLUTION RULE for a bare name that might be a top-level declaration** — and the reason
	// two files may each declare `let SHARED`, or `var counter`, with a different value.
	//
	// SCOPED FIRST, BARE SECOND. A file-private declaration is filed under a key carrying its declaring
	// file's path, so this file's own `SHARED` is found before anyone's exported one of that name.
	// Ported from v1's `lookupTopLevelConst` (TypeResolution.maxon:2857-2868), which orders the two
	// probes identically and for the identical reason.
	//
	// ⭐ **ONE PROBE FOR BOTH KINDS, FILTERED BY `wanted`.** The scoped-then-bare order is the
	// resolution RULE, and it is the same rule for a `let` and a `var` — so it is written once. What
	// differs is only which kind the caller can use, and a caller asking for the wrong kind gets
	// `notFound`, which is the truth: no CONSTANT of that name is visible here, even though a var is.
	// A second copy of this walk for vars would be free to drift on the probe order, and the failure
	// mode of that drift is silent — featA's `counter` resolving to featB's slot.
	function declFor(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath, wanted DeclKind) returns TopLevelDeclLookup
		if let scoped = try self.declIndex.get(fileScopedDeclKey(name, declFilePath: readerFilePath)) 'filePrivate'
			return self.lookupIfKind(scoped, wanted: wanted)
		end 'filePrivate'

		// ⭐ **THE BARE KEY HOLDS EVERY NON-FILE-PRIVATE DECLARATION, WHICH IS NO LONGER ONE TIER (N1a)** —
		// `module let LIMIT` and `export let LIMIT` both file here, and only the second is visible to every
		// reader. So the second test this comment used to say was unnecessary is now exactly what v1 needs
		// `isVisibleFrom` for, and it arrives the day the distinction did.
		//
		// A hidden declaration answers `notFound` rather than a refusal of its own: the reader gets
		// `Undefined constant 'LIMIT'`, which is the reference's answer for it
		// (`module-keyword/error.module-let-different-directory`).
		//
		// ⚠ **THAT ANSWER IS ONLY TRUE WHILE THE NAME HAS ONE NON-FILE-PRIVATE DECLARATION, AND THE BARE KEY
		// CANNOT HOLD TWO.** `recordDecl` is first-wins, so two files declaring `LIMIT` at any tier wider than
		// `file` put ONE of them in this index and drop the other — MEASURED, and wrong in two ways: two
		// `export let LIMIT` in different directories silently resolve BOTH readers to the first (a wrong
		// VALUE, and unchanged from before N1a), and two `module let LIMIT` make the loser's OWN FILE fail to
		// see its own declaration. The cure is a namespace-scoped key plus a precedence rule, which is
		// exactly N1b/N1c's content — the same collision refuses a program outright for FUNCTIONS and TYPES
		// today (E3006), so top-level bindings are the one kind where it is silent.
		if let exported = try self.declIndex.get(name) 'exported'
			let decl = self.declAt(exported)
			if not self.isVisibleFrom(decl.visibility, declFilePath: decl.declFilePath, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'outOfScope'
				return TopLevelDeclLookup.notFound
			end 'outOfScope'

			return self.lookupIfKind(exported, wanted: wanted)
		end 'exported'

		return TopLevelDeclLookup.notFound
	end 'declFor'

	// `index`, but only if the decl there is the KIND the caller can use.
	//
	// ⚠ IT DOES NOT FALL THROUGH TO THE NEXT PROBE. A file-private `var counter` SHADOWS an exported
	// `let counter` for a constant reader too — the reader gets `notFound`, not the exported constant.
	// That is the same shadowing every scope in the language has (a local `let` shadows a top-level
	// one — see `Parser.parseVariableReference`), and the alternative would be worse than wrong: a
	// constant initializer would silently reach PAST this file's own declaration of the name to
	// somebody else's, and inline a number the author never wrote.
	function lookupIfKind(index TopLevelDeclIndex, wanted DeclKind) returns TopLevelDeclLookup
		let hasStorage = self.declAt(index).hasStorage
		let isWanted = match wanted 'k'
			inlined gives not hasStorage
			stored gives hasStorage
		end 'k'
		return TopLevelDeclLookup.found(index) if isWanted else TopLevelDeclLookup.notFound
	end 'lookupIfKind'

	// `declFor` restricted to the INLINED kind — the probe both constant doors share, named once so the two
	// cannot drift on which kind they meant.
	function constantDeclFor(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns TopLevelDeclLookup
		return self.declFor(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath, wanted: DeclKind.inlined)
	end 'constantDeclFor'

	function declAt(index TopLevelDeclIndex) returns TopLevelDecl
		return try self.decls.get(index) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.declAt: decls.get OOB at {index} — every index handed out is one this arena minted")
	end 'declAt'

	// Mix one top-level constant's ANSWER into the running hash: the key it is filed under, then its
	// VALUE.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE VALUE, AND NOT MERELY THE NAME.** This is the half a READER's parse memo depends on, and
	// the easy half to leave out. A use INLINES the constant — it becomes a `literal` op carrying the
	// number — so a file that merely reads `MAGIC` compiles differently when `MAGIC` changes, while
	// its OWN bytes, and therefore its own content hash, do not move at all. Without this, editing
	// `export let MAGIC = 42` to `43` would re-parse the declaring file and hand every reader a memo
	// with 42 still baked into it. (`QueryEngine.parseMemoKey` is the other half — see the `hash`
	// field.)
	function mixConstant(state ContentHash, decl TopLevelDecl) returns ContentHash
		let keyed = mixBytes(self.mixMutability(state, mutable: decl.mutable), bytes: decl.storageKey)
		match decl.outcome 'o'
			value(payload, tag) then return self.mixDeclAnswer(keyed, decl: decl, payload: payload, tag: tag)
			// ⭐ **THE BYTES RIDE, for `mixDeclAnswer`'s reason applied to a managed constant.** A use
			// re-materializes the literal from exactly these bytes, so `export let G = "a"` → `"b"` changes
			// what every OTHER file compiles to while their own bytes never move. The KIND rides too: the
			// same bytes as a `String` and as a `b"…"` produce different records and different types.
			managedValue(bytes, kind) then return mixBytes(self.mixManagedKind(mix(keyed, value: ConstShapeManagedValue), kind: kind), bytes: bytes)
			// ⭐ **AN ARRAY BINDING MIXES ITS ELEMENT KIND AND ITS LABEL, AND NOT ITS ELEMENTS** (P1.7 slice
			// 2b) — `mixDeclAnswer`'s `var` branch, for the identical reason and now for a `let` too. An array
			// binding is STORED whichever keyword declared it, so every reader emits `globalAddr <label>` +
			// `loadIndirect` and that is byte-identical whether the literal said `[1, 2]` or `[9, 9, 9]`: the
			// elements reach only `__module_init`, which is rebuilt from this arena whenever this query
			// misses. What a reader DOES depend on is the element KIND — it fixes the `Array with T` instance
			// the load is typed with, and `[1]` becoming `["a"]` retypes every use — and the LABEL, which is
			// the slot the emitted `globalAddr` names. Mixing the elements too would be SOUND and WRONG: the
			// "sound, and useless" key that re-parses the program on every keystroke in any initializer.
			arrayValue(_, element) then return mixBytes(self.mixArrayElementKind(mix(keyed, value: ConstShapeArrayValue), element: element), bytes: decl.dataLabel)
			// ⭐ **A CONTAINER FACTORY MIXES ITS INSTANCE AND ITS LABEL** — the array-literal arm's two facts,
			// with the instance stated outright instead of inferred from an element kind. There are no
			// elements to leave out.
			containerValue(giid, _) then return mixBytes(mixBytes(mix(keyed, value: ConstShapeContainerValue), bytes: self.mangleGenericInstance(giid)), bytes: decl.dataLabel)
			// ⭐ **A FACTORY-CALL GLOBAL MIXES ITS ROOT CALLEE AND ITS LABEL, AND NOT ITS ARGUMENTS** — the
			// array-literal arm's rule, applied to the one initializer that calls. Every reader emits
			// `globalAddr <label>` + `loadIndirect`, byte-identical whichever arguments the call passes, so
			// the arguments reach only `__module_init`, which is rebuilt from this arena whenever this query
			// misses. That covers a NESTED call too: it is an argument, and no reader's bytes move when
			// `Outer.create(Inner.create(0))` becomes `Outer.create(Inner.create(1))`. What a reader DOES
			// depend on is the ROOT CALLEE — it fixes the slot's type, and swapping `Database.create` for
			// `Database.empty` can retype every use — and the LABEL, which is the slot the emitted
			// `globalAddr` names.
			// ⭐ A STRUCT-LITERAL global (W116) mixes the STRUCT NAME in the callee's place, and its fields in
			// the arguments' — i.e. not at all — because the two facts a reader depends on are the same two:
			// the name fixes the slot's type, and `Pair{a: 1}` becoming `Pair{a: 2}` moves no reader's bytes.
			factoryValue(calls, root, _) then return mixBytes(mixBytes(mix(keyed, value: ConstShapeFactoryValue), bytes: constFactoryCallHashName(factoryCallAt(calls, id: root))), bytes: decl.dataLabel)
			// A constant with no value bakes no number into a reader, so every failure hashes alike —
			// but it must still hash differently from every VALUE, or `let X = compute()` becoming
			// `let X = 0` would leave a reader's memo valid. The failure's DETAILS are deliberately not
			// mixed: the build fails either way, and no reader's code depends on which diagnostic it
			// failed with.
			failed to poisoned then return mix(keyed, value: ConstShapeNoValue)
			pending to evaluating then panic("ProgramSignatures.mixConstant: '{String.from(decl.name)}' is unevaluated — `evaluateInitializers` folds the hash only after every walk has finished")
		end 'o'
	end 'mixConstant'

	// ⚠ **MUTABILITY RIDES THE HASH, and it is the half a `var` adds.** `let X = 5` and `var X = 5`
	// carry the same key and the same value and compile to COMPLETELY different code in every reader:
	// a constant becomes a `literal` op, a var becomes `globalAddr` + `loadIndirect`. A reader's own
	// bytes do not move when `let` becomes `var`, so — exactly as for the constant's VALUE — only this
	// hash can carry it, and without it every reader would keep a memo that inlines a number the
	// declaration no longer promises. (`ProgramSignatures.hash`'s field comment is the whole argument;
	// this is the second fact it applies to.)
	function mixMutability(state ContentHash, mutable bool) returns ContentHash
		return mix(state, value: DeclShapeMutable if mutable else DeclShapeConstant)
	end 'mixMutability'

	// Mix a managed constant's KIND (P1.7a). Its own discriminator per kind, so `let G = "ab"` and a
	// hypothetical byte-string of the same bytes hash apart — they materialize different records and
	// give the reader a different TYPE, which is exactly the acceptance a stale memo would keep.
	function mixManagedKind(state ContentHash, kind ManagedConstKind) returns ContentHash
		return match kind 'k'
			string gives mix(state, value: ManagedShapeString)
			byteArray gives mix(state, value: ManagedShapeByteArray)
		end 'k'
	end 'mixManagedKind'

	// Mix an array binding's ELEMENT kind (P1.7 slice 2b) — `mixManagedKind`'s twin, and its own
	// discriminator per kind for the same reason: `Array with Integer` and `Array with String` are different
	// types, so every reader's load is typed differently and a memo that did not move would keep the old one.
	function mixArrayElementKind(state ContentHash, element ConstArrayElementKind) returns ContentHash
		return match element 'e'
			integer gives mix(state, value: ArrayElementShapeInteger)
			string gives mix(state, value: ArrayElementShapeString)
		end 'e'
	end 'mixArrayElementKind'

	// ⭐⭐ **MIX WHAT A READER COPIES — AND A `var` DOES NOT COPY ITS VALUE.**
	//
	// This is the one place a `let` and a `var` genuinely diverge, and getting it backwards costs
	// incrementality rather than correctness, which is exactly why it is worth being exact about.
	//
	//   • A `let`'s VALUE is COPIED INTO every reader — a use becomes a `literal` op carrying the
	//     number — so `let X = 7` → `8` changes what other files COMPILE TO while their own bytes
	//     never move. Only this hash can carry that. (`verify-warm-rebuild` property 3(c).)
	//
	//   • A `var`'s value is copied into NOTHING. A reader emits `globalAddr __data_X` +
	//     `loadIndirect` and that is byte-identical whether the initializer said 7 or 8 — the number
	//     reaches only the `.data` IMAGE, which is rebuilt from the arena whenever this query misses.
	//     Verified, not assumed: `globalDeclFor` hands a use site `(label, tag)` and no payload
	//     (`TopLevelGlobalLookup`), and `emitGlobalLoad` deliberately does NOT `recordConst` — a
	//     mutable location's value is not a constant the folder may use.
	//
	// So a var mixes its TAG and its LABEL and stops there:
	//   - the TAG because it decides the load's WIDTH (`i1` moves one byte, `i64` eight), so
	//     `var flag = false` → `var flag = 0` really does change every reader's code;
	//   - the LABEL because it IS the reader's IR identity — the slot the emitted `globalAddr` names.
	//
	// ⚠ Mixing the PAYLOAD too would be SOUND and WRONG: it would re-parse every file in the program
	// whenever any global's initial value moved, which is precisely the "sound, and useless"
	// failure `verify-warm-rebuild`'s property (b) exists to reject — the composite-source-hash key
	// wearing a different hat. `globalValueEditIsLocal` is the probe that keeps this honest.
	function mixDeclAnswer(state ContentHash, decl TopLevelDecl, payload ParsedInt, tag ValueTypeTag) returns ContentHash
		if not decl.mutable 'constantInlinesItsValue'
			return self.mixConstantValue(state, payload: payload, tag: tag)
		end 'constantInlinesItsValue'

		return mixBytes(self.mixValueTag(mix(state, value: ConstShapeValue), tag: tag), bytes: decl.dataLabel)
	end 'mixDeclAnswer'

	// The TAG rides the hash beside the payload: a `boolean` 1 and an `integer` 1 are the same number
	// and different values, and they compile to different code (an i1 versus an i64).
	//
	// Exhaustive over `ValueTypeTag`, and the four unreachable arms panic rather than hashing: the
	// evaluator produces `integer` and `boolean` and nothing else (its literals are ints, chars and
	// bools; `foldIntCompare` yields a bool; `binOpResultTag` yields one of the two given two of the
	// two). Hashing them "just in case" would be inventing a key for a value that cannot exist, and
	// the day one can, this is where it must be decided rather than defaulted.
	function mixConstantValue(state ContentHash, payload ParsedInt, tag ValueTypeTag) returns ContentHash
		return mix(self.mixValueTag(mix(state, value: ConstShapeValue), tag: tag), value: payload as ContentHash)
	end 'mixConstantValue'

	// Mix a FOLDED LITERAL's TAG. Shared by both arms of `mixDeclAnswer`, so a `let` and a `var`
	// cannot come to disagree about what distinguishes a bool from an int — the difference between
	// them is which EXTRA facts ride along (a constant's payload; a var's label), never this one.
	//
	// ⭐ **AND SHARED WITH THE STRUCT FIELD DEFAULT** (`mixDefaultLiteral`), which is the same question
	// about the same three tags: a `boolean` 1 and an `integer` 1 are the same number and different
	// values. A second tag→shape mapping would be one lattice hashed from three directions instead of
	// two, and a tag that hashed differently depending on which door it came through is a cache that
	// answers a stale parse for a changed file — the very failure the `TypeShapeFloat` reuse below was
	// introduced to prevent.
	//
	// Exhaustive over `ValueTypeTag`, and the unreachable arms panic rather than hashing. Both producers
	// bottom out in the same three tags, by different routes: the constant EVALUATOR produces `integer`,
	// `boolean` and `float` and nothing else (its literals are ints, chars, bools and floats;
	// `foldIntCompare` yields a bool; `binOpResultTag` yields one of them given two of them), and a
	// LITERAL field DEFAULT is a TOKEN-KIND lookup over exactly three literal kinds
	// (`Parser.readFieldDefaultLiteral`, entered only after `Parser.defaultRegionIsLiteral` has said the
	// whole default IS one of those three). So each arm's argument below is stated for the evaluator —
	// the producer that can reach furthest — and holds a fortiori for the field default. Hashing the
	// rest "just in case" would be inventing a key for a value that cannot exist, and the day one can,
	// this is where it must be decided rather than defaulted.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN EXPRESSION FIELD DEFAULT NEVER REACHES HERE AT ALL (S2f)** — it is a `FieldDefault.helper`,
	// which `mixFieldDefault` hashes as a presence bit and never as a value. This door stays the
	// three-literal question it always was.
	//
	// P1.0d.4 takes the invitation the old `float to named` arm left ("a float needs P1.0d.4"):
	// `let PI = 3.14` and `var ratio = 2.5` are constants now, so a float tag is REACHABLE and gets
	// the one thing a content hash owes it — a distinct key. It reuses `TypeShapeFloat`, the shape
	// `mixType` already gives a float from the `MaxonType` side: the two functions hash one lattice
	// from two directions, and a float that hashed differently depending on which door it came
	// through is a cache that answers a stale parse for a changed file.
	function mixValueTag(state ContentHash, tag ValueTypeTag) returns ContentHash
		match tag 'k'
			integer then return mix(state, value: TypeShapeInteger)
			boolean then return mix(state, value: TypeShapeBoolean)
			float then return mix(state, value: TypeShapeFloat)
			// `named` stays out: a constant's initializer is a bare expression and shv2 infers
			// `int`/`bool`/`float` from it, never an alias — that needs an `as` cast (P1.9).
			named then panic("ProgramSignatures.mixValueTag: a folded literal is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a constant's type is inferred from a bare initializer, so it is never a ranged alias (that needs an `as` cast, P1.9)")
			exitCode to void then panic("ProgramSignatures.mixValueTag: a folded literal is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — the evaluator folds integers, bools and floats only")
			// A struct is unreachable here for `globalStdType`'s reason and one more: the only way to
			// build one is `T.create(…)`, and a CALL in a top-level initializer is E2045 in every
			// compiler in the tree. It stays out of the hash rather than being given a key "just in
			// case" — inventing one for a value that cannot exist is what `mixValueTag` has refused to
			// do since it was written, and the day a struct constant is legal the question is which of
			// its FIELDS a reader copies, which is a decision, not a default.
			structRef then panic("ProgramSignatures.mixValueTag: a folded literal is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a struct constant would have to be folded from a `T.create(…)` call, and a call in a constant initializer is E2045")
			// A String top-level binding never reaches here, and P1.7's two managed slices did not change
			// that: a managed literal folds to `TopLevelConstant.managedValue`, whose hash is
			// `mixConstant`'s own arm (the BYTES and the KIND ride there), so it never comes through
			// `mixDeclAnswer` to this tag-only door at all.
			string then panic("ProgramSignatures.mixValueTag: a folded literal is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a managed binding hashes through `mixConstant`'s `managedValue` arm, never through this one")
			// A FUNCTION top-level binding never reaches here (P1.5-A1): a function reference is not a
			// constant initializer, so the const evaluator never folds one to a `function`-tagged value.
			function then panic("ProgramSignatures.mixValueTag: a folded literal is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a function value is not a constant initializer, so it is never folded to a top-level constant")
			// A TYPE-PARAMETER / GENERIC-INSTANCE top-level binding is unreachable (P1.6-A) for
			// `structRef`'s reason: neither is a folded scalar constant, so the const evaluator never
			// produces one at module scope. A `b"…"` global IS a generic instance, but it hashes through
			// `mixConstant`'s `managedValue` arm and never reaches this tag-only door.
			typeParameter then panic("ProgramSignatures.mixValueTag: a folded literal is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a type parameter names no value at module scope")
			genericInstance then panic("ProgramSignatures.mixValueTag: a folded literal is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a `b\"…\"` global hashes through `mixConstant`'s `managedValue` arm, never through this one")
			// A CHARACTER top-level binding is unreachable for `string`'s reason: the const evaluator folds
			// no character literal, so none is ever a top-level constant to hash.
			character then panic("ProgramSignatures.mixValueTag: a folded literal is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a character literal is not folded to a top-level constant")
			// Unreachable for `structRef`'s reason: an existential is only ever built by widening a value the
			// const evaluator cannot fold, so none is ever a top-level constant to hash.
			interfaceRef then panic("ProgramSignatures.mixValueTag: a folded literal is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — an interface-typed value is built by widening a conformer, which is not a constant initializer")
		end 'k'
	end 'mixValueTag'

	// The ranged typealias `name` denotes when read FROM `readerFilePath` — the reader's OWN file's
	// declaration first, then one the reader may actually NAME, and only then anyone else's
	// (`RangedAliasRegistry.lookup`, which carries the whole rule and its E3063 boundary). THE door the
	// shift rule asks through — see `Parser.valueIsUnsigned` — and the reason a file's `int(0 to 500)`
	// governs the casts written in it even where another file declares that name more widely.
	export function aliasOf(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns DeclaredAlias
		return self.typeAliases.lookup(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath)
	end 'aliasOf'

	// Is SOME declaration of `name` nameable from any file? The all-declarations question the door
	// above cannot answer, asked only where `aliasOf`'s single answer is hidden — see
	// `RangedAliasRegistry.nameableFromAnyFile`, which carries the whole argument.
	export function aliasNameableFromAnyFile(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.typeAliases.nameableFromAnyFile(name)
	end 'aliasNameableFromAnyFile'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE DECLARATIONS A BARE `name` COULD MEAN FROM `readerFilePath` — E3063's candidate set, and
	// NON-EMPTY ONLY WHEN IT IS AMBIGUOUS (N1c).** Two files in different directories may both `export
	// typealias Score`; that is legal at declaration time and becomes an error only where a reference could
	// mean either. This is the "could mean either" question, and the answer is the list the user is told to
	// qualify with (`api.Score`, `legacy.Score`).
	//
	// ⭐ **THE FOUR NARROWING RULES, IN v1's ORDER** (`reachableTypealiasDefinitions`,
	// `maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Project.maxon:2558-2706`), each of which is a case the corpus pins:
	//
	//   1. **THE READER'S OWN FILE WINS OUTRIGHT.** A file that declares the name means its own
	//      declaration, whatever anyone else exported — the same first probe `RangedAliasRegistry.lookup`
	//      makes, asked here so the verdict and the resolution cannot part company. (v1 spells this as a
	//      separate `hasLocalShadow` scan because its candidates are a flat array; shv2's are already
	//      keyed by declaring file, so it is one probe.)
	//   2. **VISIBILITY.** A declaration the reader may not name was never a candidate —
	//      `exported-typealias-file-private-doesnt-collide`.
	//   3. **PROVENANCE.** A stdlib declaration is dropped before the count, so a project export shadows
	//      it — `project-export-shadows-stdlib-export`. See `isStdlibModuleDirectory`.
	//   4. **NEAREST ENCLOSING SCOPE.** If any surviving candidate's directory CONTAINS the reader's, only
	//      the deepest such candidates remain: a `Compiler/Coverage/` export is not a competitor from
	//      `Compiler/`'s point of view — `nested-export-shadowed-by-enclosing-dir`. If none encloses the
	//      reader (the `api/` vs `legacy/` read from `app/` shape) every candidate survives, which is what
	//      makes that case ambiguous at all.
	//
	// ⭐ **DEDUPLICATION BY DECLARING FILE IS STRUCTURAL HERE AND IS A LOOP IN v1** — `contestedDeclarations`
	// is keyed by path, so one file cannot contribute two candidates. (A same-file redeclaration is E3061
	// and never reaches this map; `RangedAliasRegistry.record` says why.)
	//
	// ⚠ **TWO PASSES, AND THE FIRST ALLOCATES NOTHING** — which is what keeps this affordable at every named
	// type reference in the program. A name only one file declares misses the map; a contested name whose
	// reader declares it, or whose competitors are all hidden, all stdlib, or fewer than two, returns the
	// shared empty before a single candidate string is built. `Byte` and `Codepoint` are contested inside
	// `stdlib/` in every compile there is, so that is the path that must stay free.
	export function ambiguousAliasCandidates(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns StringArray
		let contested = self.typeAliases.contestedDeclarations(name)
		if contested.contains(readerFilePath.toString()) 'theReadersOwnDeclarationIsWhatItMeans'
			return sharedEmptyCandidateNames
		end 'theReadersOwnDeclarationIsWhatItMeans'

		let readerDirectory = self.moduleDirectoryOf(readerFilePath)
		var reachable = 0 as ElementCount
		var scope = EnclosingScope.none
		for (_, alias) in contested 'countReachable'
			if not self.candidateIsReachable(alias, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'hiddenOrLibrary'
				continue
			end 'hiddenOrLibrary'

			reachable = reachable + 1
			scope = deeperEnclosingScope(scope, declDirectory: self.moduleDirectoryOf(alias.declFilePath), readerDirectory: readerDirectory)
		end 'countReachable'

		if reachable < 2 'atMostOneMeaning'
			return sharedEmptyCandidateNames
		end 'atMostOneMeaning'

		var candidates = StringArray.create()
		for (_, alias) in contested 'renderSurvivors'
			if not self.candidateIsReachable(alias, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'hiddenOrLibrary'
				continue
			end 'hiddenOrLibrary'

			let declDirectory = self.moduleDirectoryOf(alias.declFilePath)
			if not scopeAdmits(scope, declDirectory: declDirectory) 'aFartherScopeLosesToTheNearestOne'
				continue
			end 'aFartherScopeLosesToTheNearestOne'

			insertCandidateName(candidates, candidate: qualifiedCandidateName(declDirectory, bare: name))
		end 'renderSurvivors'

		if candidates.count() < 2 'oneScopeWon'
			return sharedEmptyCandidateNames
		end 'oneScopeWon'

		return candidates
	end 'ambiguousAliasCandidates'

	// May `readerFilePath` mean this declaration at all — rules 2 and 3 of `ambiguousAliasCandidates`, in
	// ONE predicate because both passes must apply exactly the same ones. A pass that filtered differently
	// from the pass that COUNTED would render a list whose length disagrees with its own verdict.
	function candidateIsReachable(alias RangedTypeAlias, readerFilePath FilePath) returns bool
		if not self.isVisibleFrom(alias.visibility, declFilePath: alias.declFilePath, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'hidden'
			return false
		end 'hidden'

		return not isStdlibModuleDirectory(self.moduleDirectoryOf(alias.declFilePath))
	end 'candidateIsReachable'

	// ⭐ **THE TYPE NAMES A FILE'S TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZERS NAME (P1.9) — a `use` the real parse
	// structurally cannot see.**
	//
	// `let SENTINEL = 5 as Slot` is the only mention of `Slot` in many real files, and `var g =
	// ItemArray.create()` is very often the only mention of `ItemArray` — and the E3062 check would call
	// both aliases unused. It cannot see either because the real parse SKIPS a top-level
	// initializer's tokens wholesale (`readTopLevelBinding`) — the initializer is folded once, before
	// any file is parsed, by the const evaluator (`evaluateInitializers`), whose `Parser` is transient
	// and whose artifact is discarded.
	//
	// So the use is recorded HERE, on the index that evaluator is building and that every parse then
	// reads. That is the same "record a use where the name is RESOLVED" rule the two parser hooks
	// follow (`Parser.recordTypeNameUse`); this is simply the door for the one reader that is not the
	// parser. It is keyed per DECLARING FILE, because "used in its OWN file" is the whole question.
	//
	// ⚠ It records EVERY such name, including `as int` and `as ExitCode`. Filtering
	// to alias names would mean asking the alias registry mid-sweep, when it is still being built and
	// an absence means only "not swept yet"; and a name that is not a declared alias matches no
	// candidate, so the extra entries answer nothing and cost one set insert each.
	export function recordConstInitializerTypeName(name ByteArray, declFilePath FilePath)
		self.constInitializerTypeNames.insert(fileScopedDeclKey(name, declFilePath: declFilePath))
	end 'recordConstInitializerTypeName'

	// Did a top-level binding declared in `declFilePath` name `name` in its initializer? The read half of
	// the above.
	// It pays `fileScopedDeclKey`'s ~4 allocations, and pays them once per FILE-PRIVATE TYPEALIAS
	// DECLARED — not per use and not per name reference — which is why the composite key is affordable
	// here where `RangedAliasRegistry` refuses it.
	export function namesTypeInConstInitializer(name ByteArray, declFilePath FilePath) returns bool
		return self.constInitializerTypeNames.contains(fileScopedDeclKey(name, declFilePath: declFilePath))
	end 'namesTypeInConstInitializer'

	// The bare-name door, for the readers that have no file to ask from — see
	// `RangedAliasRegistry.underlyingInAnyFile`, which is where the (narrow) residual is written down
	// and why it hands back the primitive rather than the alias.
	export function aliasUnderlyingInAnyFile(name ByteArray) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		return try self.typeAliases.underlyingInAnyFile(name)
	end 'aliasUnderlyingInAnyFile'

	// Is `name` a ranged FLOAT typealias (`typealias Weight = float(f64.min to f64.max)`)? The `bool` half
	// of `aliasUnderlyingInAnyFile`, and the sibling of `isFunctionAlias`/`isGenericAlias` — the three
	// questions `fieldTypeOf` asks of a field declared with a bare NAME, whose meaning the declaration
	// SWEEP that recorded the layout could not yet know.
	//
	// ⚠ An INT alias is deliberately NOT re-tagged by any of them: `named` already reads as an integer
	// everywhere (`tagIsIntegral`), and its NAME is what carries the range and the signedness that
	// `valueIsUnsigned` and the range checks read off it. A FLOAT alias is the one `named` type whose
	// VALUE lives in a different register file, so it is the one that cannot be left overloaded.
	//
	// Bare-name, exactly as `isFunctionAlias`/`isGenericAlias` are and for `classifyUnionPayload`'s
	// reason: a `StructLayout` does not record the file its fields were declared in, so there is no
	// reader file to resolve from.
	export function isFloatAlias(name ByteArray) returns bool
		if let underlying = try self.aliasUnderlyingInAnyFile(name) 'rangedAlias'
			return tagIsFloat(maxonTypeTag(underlying))
		end 'rangedAlias'

		return false
	end 'isFloatAlias'

	// --- Inner + per-instance ranged aliases (P1.6-C) ---

	// Is `name` a BASE-TEMPLATE inner alias ("Wrapper.Idx") — the shared-body form a generic method's bare
	// `Idx` return/parameter resolves to? Membership in `innerAliases`.
	export function isInnerAlias(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.innerAliases.contains(name)
	end 'isInnerAlias'

	// ⭐ **DOES THE BASE-QUALIFIED NAME `qualified` NAME A NESTED TYPEALIAS OF *ANY* PER-INSTANCE FORM
	// (W43)?** Three registries carry those — a RANGED one lands in `innerAliases`, a GENERIC-INSTANCE one in
	// `genericAliases` and a TUPLE one in `tupleAliases` — and every one of them is keyed `<Type>.<member>`,
	// so "did this type declare a nested alias called X?" is one question with three places to look. Spelled
	// once here rather than at the asker, because the day a fifth alias form arrives the asker would be the
	// copy that did not learn about it.
	//
	// ⚠ **A FUNCTION alias is deliberately absent even though it IS qualified by its enclosing type since
	// W49b** (`Parser.publishNestedFunctionAlias` files `<Type>.<member>` beside the bare name). The question
	// this asks is not "is the name keyed?" but "is it a MEMBER of the type?" — every caller uses the answer
	// to reach the per-instance machinery, and a function alias is not per-instance: it declares a file-scope
	// name too, and its qualified copy carries the identical signature. `isFunctionAlias` is the door for it.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FOURTH SOURCE IS A NAME SET RATHER THAN A REGISTRY, AND IT IS WHAT MAKES THE ANSWER WHOLE-PROGRAM
	// FOR AN `extension` BODY (F10).** `foldExtensionDeclarations` fills the three registries above one site at
	// a time WHILE reading each body's signatures, so during that pass they can only answer for the sites
	// already visited — the directory walk's order deciding what a written name means, which is exactly what
	// `StdlibLoader`'s header forbids. `extensionInnerAliasMembers` is filled by a name-only pre-pass ahead of
	// the whole fold, so it answers the same for every site. It is a SUPERSET during the fold and identical
	// afterwards, and the excess is bounded by the same tolerance every sweep entry point has: a member the
	// fold cannot read publishes no declaration, and the program is being refused for that a moment later.
	export function declaresInnerAlias(qualified ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.innerAliases.contains(qualified) or self.genericAliases.contains(qualified) or self.tupleAliases.contains(qualified) or self.extensionInnerAliasMembers.contains(qualified)
	end 'declaresInnerAlias'

	// The inner alias `name` names ("Wrapper.Idx"), or `undeclared`. Its stored `.name` is the BARE member
	// (`Idx`) the method-return retype re-qualifies with a concrete receiver's instance alias.
	export function innerAliasOf(name ByteArray) returns DeclaredAlias
		let alias = try self.innerAliases.get(name) otherwise return DeclaredAlias.undeclared
		return DeclaredAlias.declared(alias)
	end 'innerAliasOf'

	// The BARE member of the base-qualified inner alias `name` (`Wrapper.Idx` ⇒ `Idx`), or `EmptySourceText`
	// when `name` is not an inner alias. What the method-return retype re-qualifies (`{receiverAlias}.{member}`).
	export function innerAliasMember(name ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		return match self.innerAliasOf(name) 'm'
			declared(alias) gives alias.name
			undeclared gives EmptySourceText
		end 'm'
	end 'innerAliasMember'

	// ⭐ **IS `name` A REGISTERED PER-INSTANCE ALIAS ("IntWrapper.Idx")?** — the nominal-identity gate the
	// aggregate check reads. Its prefix must be a generic-instance ALIAS whose base declares the inner alias
	// named by its member. Keyed on the INSTANCE-ALIAS NAME, not the gid: `WrapperA` and `WrapperB` share one
	// gid yet are distinct aliases, so `WrapperA.Idx ≠ WrapperB.Idx` — the whole point of the rung.
	export function isPerInstanceAlias(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns bool
		return match self.perInstanceAliasOf(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'p'
			declared gives true
			undeclared gives false
		end 'p'
	end 'isPerInstanceAlias'

	// The inner alias a per-instance `name` resolves to (its RANGE + bare member), or `undeclared`. The one
	// door `isPerInstanceAlias` and the erasure both read, so "is it per-instance?" and "what does it erase
	// to?" answer from one place.
	export function perInstanceAliasOf(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns DeclaredAlias
		return match splitQualifiedName(name) 'split'
			notQualified gives DeclaredAlias.undeclared
			parts(prefix, member) gives self.perInstanceAliasFromParts(prefix, member: member, readerFilePath: readerFilePath)
		end 'split'
	end 'perInstanceAliasOf'

	// The inner alias a per-instance name's (prefix, member) resolves to — `undeclared` unless `prefix` is a
	// generic-instance alias whose base declares an inner alias `member`.
	// ⚠ **THE PREFIX IS RESOLVED AS `readerFilePath` MEANS IT (N3), AND THE FILE-LESS FORM WAS A COMPILER
	// PANIC.** The prefix is a generic-alias NAME, so for a name two files declare over different bases the
	// bare door refuses outright — MEASURED, from `Parser.aggregateNameOf` on a program writing `W.Idx` where
	// two files declare `W`: the compiler aborted where it had previously answered last-wins. Every caller
	// that resolves a SOURCE spelling has a file; the two that structurally cannot pass
	// `CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath`, which resolves like a stranger — the same convention
	// `contestScopedDeclaredType` states for a layout no file declares.
	function perInstanceAliasFromParts(prefix ByteArray, member ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns DeclaredAlias
		if not self.isGenericAlias(prefix) 'notInstanceAlias'
			return DeclaredAlias.undeclared
		end 'notInstanceAlias'
		// ⭐ **`scopedGenericAliasInstance` AND NOT `genericAliasInstanceFrom`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS BOTH HALVES
		// OF WHAT THIS SITE NEEDS.** The first picks WHICH DECLARATION the reader means — a pure map probe. The
		// second then re-scopes that instance's ARGUMENTS, which interns, and interning MUTATES: this door is
		// asked from `SemanticCheck.aggregateNameFor`, which holds the memoized index immutably and would be
		// refused (E3019). It is also work this site cannot use — an instance's BASE is invariant under
		// argument scoping, and the base is the whole of what a per-instance alias reads off the prefix.
		let base = self.genericInstanceBaseName(self.scopedGenericAliasInstance(prefix, readerFilePath: readerFilePath))
		return self.innerAliasOf(joinQualifiedName(base, member: member))
	end 'perInstanceAliasFromParts'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE AGGREGATE CONFLICT RULE — the ONE authority every coercion site shares**: the parser's five
	// symmetric ones (return, reassignment, `otherwise`, match arm, union-payload / struct-literal store) and
	// SemanticCheck's three (a call argument, an overload candidate's score, a generic call's type-parameter
	// argument), so they cannot come to disagree about when a value may cross.
	//
	// It answers TWO questions in the order a coercion asks them, and each has its own measured wrong answer
	// below: does a value with no nominal identity DECAY into the place (P1.6-C), and — when the two
	// identities genuinely differ — is this the one pair of DIFFERENT names that denotes ONE record
	// (`byteBufferBoundaryAdmits`, R4.7)?
	//
	// ⭐ **`expected` IS THE SIDE WHOSE DECLARATION DECIDES**, which is not always a `declare`d type: the
	// binding's declared type, the function's declared return, the parameter, the instance's type argument —
	// and, at the two symmetric merges, the value the phi ADOPTS (the try's result, the first arm). The byte
	// boundary is one-way for that reason and no other; see `byteBufferBoundaryAdmits`.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE (tag, nameId) PAIRS RIDE BESIDE THE NAMES RATHER THAN REPLACING THEM.** Three producers spell
	// the name (`Parser.aggregateNameOf`, `SemanticCheck.aggregateNameFor`, `typeArgAggregateName`), one
	// per interner, so this index cannot re-derive it — but the pair it needs is interner-independent, since
	// a `genericInstance` carries a whole-program `GenericInstanceId` in all three.
	//
	// A per-instance `Idx` is a NOMINAL wrapper over a SCALAR int, so it DECAYS to plain int wherever a
	// value with NO nominal identity of its own is expected — exactly as a per-instance argument into a
	// plain-numeric parameter decays. `expected.isEmpty()` IS that condition: an empty aggregate name is this
	// file's one spelling of "carries no nominal identity" (`aggregateNameOf`, `aggregateNameFor` and
	// `typeArgAggregateName` all answer it for a scalar, a `String` and a ranged alias). There is nothing
	// for an `Idx` to decay INTO when the target is a struct, a boxed union/enum or a generic instance, so
	// against a NAMED target the conflict stands and `namedAggregatesConflict` reports it.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE DECAY USED TO BE SPELLED "the target is not ITSELF a per-instance alias", AND THAT WAS A
	// REACHABLE WILD FREE.** It let an `Idx` — an int — decay into every named aggregate as well as into a
	// scalar. The safety argument written here was that "a struct value into a plain-numeric target is caught
	// by the TAG check that runs BEFORE every one of these sites", and that premise is a SECOND fact, held at
	// six call sites, that nothing made agree with this one. It is FALSE at the sixth:
	// `SemanticCheck.checkTypeParameterArgsAt` compares against `substitutedInstanceArg`, which returns the
	// type argument AS INTERNED — a struct spelled by name is a `named` LEAF, never `structRef` — so its tag
	// check is `checkDeclaredType(named, named)`, which AGREES. `Wrapper with Integer`'s `Idx` therefore
	// passed into a `Box with Leaf`'s `T` formal, was stored in the box's `T` field, and was freed by
	// `__destruct_Box_Leaf` as a `Leaf`: a wild free on the integer's value (MEASURED: exit 0xC0000005).
	// Deciding it from `expected` alone needs no premise held anywhere else.
	export function aggregatesConflict(expected ByteArray, expectedTag ValueTypeTag, expectedNameId TypeNameId, got ByteArray, gotTag ValueTypeTag, gotNameId TypeNameId) returns bool
		// `not self.isPerInstanceAlias(expected)` is not spelled: an EMPTY name can never be one
		// (`splitQualifiedName` answers `notQualified`), and a redundant clause is a fact stated twice.
		// ⚠ **THE STRANGER'S FILE (N3)** — this authority is asked from eight coercion sites, five of them in the
		// parser and three in SemanticCheck, and it is handed two NAMES and their tags rather than a position.
		// A per-instance prefix two files declare over different BASES therefore has no reader here to choose
		// by, and `CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath` is this file's one spelling of that: resolve as a file declaring
		// none of it would. It decides a BOOL — whether `got` carries a nominal identity that decays — and both
		// declarations answer it alike unless their bases differ AND only one declares the member.
		if expected.isEmpty() and not got.isEmpty() and self.isPerInstanceAlias(got, readerFilePath: CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath) 'perInstanceDecays'
			return false
		end 'perInstanceDecays'

		if not namedAggregatesConflict(expected, got: got) 'theTwoIdentitiesAgree'
			return false
		end 'theTwoIdentitiesAgree'

		// Asked only once the names have already disagreed, so every value that crosses every coercion site
		// in a compiling program pays nothing for it. TWO boundaries answer here, and they are disjoint by
		// construction — one is about a 1-byte record and the other about a machine-word one — so the order
		// between them decides nothing; they are two arms of the single question *"do these two differently
		// named instances denote one record?"*, asked where all eight coercion sites already ask it.
		if self.byteBufferBoundaryAdmits(expectedTag, declaredNameId: expectedNameId, gotTag: gotTag, gotNameId: gotNameId) 'oneByteRecord'
			return false
		end 'oneByteRecord'

		return not self.inferredIntColumnBoundaryAdmits(expectedTag, declaredNameId: expectedNameId, gotTag: gotTag, gotNameId: gotNameId)
	end 'aggregatesConflict'

	// The underlying primitive of a qualified alias (base-template OR per-instance) — TypeResolution's
	// erasure door: both collapse to their `int`/`float` underlying before lowering, exactly as a plain
	// ranged alias does; only the per-instance one ALSO carries nominal identity (kept in the value column).
	export function qualifiedAliasOf(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns DeclaredAlias
		match self.innerAliasOf(name) 'inner'
			declared(alias) then return DeclaredAlias.declared(alias)
			undeclared then break 'inner'
		end 'inner'
		return self.perInstanceAliasOf(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath)
	end 'qualifiedAliasOf'

	// Does ANY method in the whole program declare a bare inner-alias parameter (P1.6-C)? The
	// allocation-free bool gate the per-instance argument check reads BEFORE `methodInnerAliasParamsOf` —
	// which mints an `InnerAliasParamLookup` union (a heap box in the bootstrap) on every call. When this
	// answers false — every generics-free program, and any program with no per-instance method parameter —
	// no callee could ever be found, so the check returns without the per-call box. Mirrors the parser's
	// own `enclosingInnerAliases.count() != 0` gate and `aggregateNameFor`'s `containsQualifier` gate.
	export function hasInnerAliasParams() returns bool
		return self.methodInnerAliasParams.count() != 0
	end 'hasInnerAliasParams'

	// The inner-alias parameter info of `methodName` (`Wrapper.setTag`), or `noInnerAliasParams` when the
	// method declares no bare-inner-alias parameter — the common case, which costs no map entry. THE door the
	// parser's per-instance argument check asks through at a concrete call site.
	export function methodInnerAliasParamsOf(methodName ByteArray) returns InnerAliasParamLookup
		let info = try self.methodInnerAliasParams.get(methodName) otherwise return InnerAliasParamLookup.noInnerAliasParams
		return InnerAliasParamLookup.found(info)
	end 'methodInnerAliasParamsOf'

	// ⭐ **IS `name` A FUNCTION TYPEALIAS, AND WHAT DOES IT RETURN?** (P1.5-A1). THE door
	// `Parser.parseTypeReference` re-tags a `named` reference `MaxonType.function` through, and the one an
	// indirect call recovers the callee's return type from. `undeclared` is the common answer (most names
	// are not function aliases), spelled as a union arm rather than a sentinel for `DeclaredAlias`'s reason.
	// It carries no interner ids (the return type is a tag + a name ByteArray), so it needs no `into`.
	export function functionAliasOf(name ByteArray) returns DeclaredFunctionAlias
		let alias = try self.functionTypeAliases.get(name) otherwise return DeclaredFunctionAlias.undeclared
		return DeclaredFunctionAlias.declared(alias)
	end 'functionAliasOf'

	// Is `name` a declared FUNCTION typealias — the `bool` half of `functionAliasOf`, and a HOT-PATH
	// allocation guard, exactly like `containsStruct`/`containsEnum` are for their `*Of` twins. Asked of
	// EVERY `named` type REFERENCE (`parseTypeReference`), field READ (`fieldTypeOf`) and call RESULT
	// (`resolveNamedAlias`) — most of them ranged int aliases, none of them function aliases — so it
	// must not pay `functionAliasOf`'s `DeclaredFunctionAlias` box (a Maxon union value is a heap object
	// EVERY case, `undeclared` included) just to throw it away. A `Map.contains` allocates nothing. Only
	// the cold indirect-call return-type recovery, which needs the alias itself, calls `functionAliasOf`.
	export function isFunctionAlias(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.functionTypeAliases.contains(name)
	end 'isFunctionAlias'

	// ⭐ **A FUNCTION TYPEALIAS SUBSTITUTED IN AS A TYPE ARGUMENT IS A `function` PLACE, AND IT ARRIVES AS A
	// `named` LEAF (W43b).** `substitutedInstanceArg` answers the argument AS INTERNED, and a function alias
	// is not in the type registries `denotedNamedType` walks — it lives in `functionTypeAliases` — so
	// `Map with (String, UnaryOp)`'s value argument reads `named`, whose tag prints "int". Handed to the
	// scalar coercion rule unresolved, `m.upsert("d", value: double)` was refused *"cannot pass a value of
	// type 'function' … which holds 'int'"* on a program the oracle runs
	// (`specs/map.md:function-valued.dispatch`).
	//
	// It is the same resolution `SemanticCheck.witnessFormalDeclaredType` makes for an interface
	// requirement's formal, for the same reason and with the same answer — `MaxonType.function(id)`, the
	// type a declared parameter of that alias carries — so a function alias means one thing at both doors.
	export function functionAliasResolved(t MaxonType) returns MaxonType
		if maxonTypeTag(t) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notANamedLeaf'
			return t
		end 'notANamedLeaf'
		if not self.isFunctionAlias(self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(t))) 'notAFunctionAlias'
			return t
		end 'notAFunctionAlias'
		return MaxonType.function(typeNameIdOf(t))
	end 'functionAliasResolved'

	// ⭐ **A RANGED FLOAT ALIAS SUBSTITUTED IN AS A TYPE ARGUMENT IS A `float` PLACE, AND IT ARRIVES AS A
	// `named` LEAF** — `functionAliasResolved`'s twin one line up, for the identical reason and reached
	// through the identical chain. `substitutedInstanceArg` answers the argument AS INTERNED, and `named`
	// reads as an INTEGER everywhere (`tagIsIntegral`, whose own header says why it cannot do otherwise), so
	// `Array with Real`'s element read `int`. Handed to the scalar coercion rule unresolved,
	// `a.growFilled(4, value: 2.5)` was refused **E3009 *"cannot implicitly convert 'float' to 'int'"*** —
	// a lossy-narrowing diagnostic about a program in which nothing narrows — while the BARE spelling of the
	// same element (`[1.5, 2.5]`, whose instance argument IS `float`) passed the check.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN INT ALIAS IS DELIBERATELY LEFT ALONE, which is why this is not `canonicalizeRangedAlias`.** That
	// door erases BOTH domains to their underlying primitive, and an int alias's NAME is what carries the
	// range the argument check reads (`checkArgAgainstDeclaredTag` is handed the substituted type for exactly
	// that). A FLOAT alias is the one `named` type whose VALUE lives in a different register file — see
	// `isFloatAlias` — so it is the one that may not stay overloaded.
	export function floatAliasResolved(t MaxonType) returns MaxonType
		if maxonTypeTag(t) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notANamedLeaf'
			return t
		end 'notANamedLeaf'
		return MaxonType.float if self.isFloatAlias(self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(t))) else t
	end 'floatAliasResolved'

	// The struct type `name` denotes, declared in ANY file — `aliasOf`'s twin, and the door the
	// PARSER asks every struct question through: is this name a `type`? how big is its box? which
	// slot is `x`?
	//
	// ⚠ **THE LAYOUT IT HANDS BACK CARRIES THIS INDEX'S IDS, so a caller may read its NAMES, its
	// COUNT and its OFFSETS but never its field TYPES.** Those are `MaxonType`s minted against
	// `self.typeNames`, which is private and stays private (the header's ID PROBLEM). `fieldTypeOf`
	// is the one door a field's type comes out of, and it re-interns on the way. The split is not
	// a nicety: `indexOfField`/`offsetOfField`/`sizeBytes` are pure arithmetic over the name column
	// and need no interner at all, so making every caller adopt a whole layout to ask "which slot?"
	// would allocate a copy per field access to answer a question about integers.
	export function structOf(name ByteArray) returns DeclaredStruct
		let layout = try self.structTypes.get(name) otherwise return DeclaredStruct.undeclared
		return DeclaredStruct.declared(layout)
	end 'structOf'

	// ⭐ **EVERY DECLARED `type` WHOSE `implements` CLAUSE REACHES `interfaceName`, IN DECLARATION ORDER** —
	// the set an `extension <Interface>` declares its methods on. Asked by BOTH halves of the extension
	// mechanism, which is why it is here and not at either: `foldExtensionDeclarations` publishes one
	// SIGNATURE per (conformer, method) and `Parser.parseExtensionDeclaration` emits one BODY per pair, and
	// the two must walk the identical list in the identical order or a call would type against a signature no
	// function was emitted for.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **"REACHES", NOT "NAMES" — CONFORMANCE IS TRANSITIVE THROUGH `extends` HERE TOO.** A clause naming a
	// SUB-interface is a conformance to its parents, so `extension Iterator`'s methods land on
	// `type ArrayIterator implements BidirectionalIterator` as well as on the types that name `Iterator`
	// directly. This asked a DIRECT name test, and the corpus paid for it in a shape no refusal could have
	// caught: `ArrayIterator` had `retreatBy` (declared on the interface its clause names) and not `advanceBy`
	// (declared on that interface's parent), from one file, ten lines apart.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND IT ASKS `ConformanceCheck.typeDeclaresInterface` — THE SAME DOOR E3016/E3017 ASKS — RATHER
	// THAN A SECOND WALK OF ITS OWN.** "Does this type's `implements` clause reach that interface?" is one
	// question; it was answered here and there in two places, which had already produced two different costs
	// for one rule before it produced two different ANSWERS. The index handed over is
	// `sweptConformanceIndex()`, which is `StructLayout.conformsTo` in set form — the exact column this used
	// to read off each layout, so nothing about WHICH relation is consulted has changed, only how many
	// spellings of the lookup exist.
	//
	// ⚠ **THAT MEMO'S BUILD POINT IS ALREADY A PINNED INVARIANT AND THIS DOES NOT ADD A PREMISE.**
	// `Queries.foldConformanceClauses`' own ⚠ names these two together — the W95 re-read must land *"before
	// the second, because `StructLayout.conformsTo` is the relation the extension fold expands over
	// (`ProgramSignatures.conformersOf`) and `sweptConformanceIndex` MEMOIZES it on first ask"*. Both readers
	// were already required to sit after that pass; they are now literally one read of one snapshot, which is
	// strictly harder to violate than two.
	//
	// ⚖ **v1 SEEDS THE RELATION INSTEAD, AND shv2 DELIBERATELY DOES NOT.** There, `validateConformances`
	// records `X implements <parent>` alongside the declared row so the later lookup is a direct hit
	// (`TypeResolution.findInterfaceExtensionCallee`: *"transitive entries seeded by `validateConformances` …
	// are visible without re-walking the interface-extends-interface chain"*). That shape cannot be taken
	// here: shv2's `conformsTo` is PAIRED BY INDEX with `StructLayout.conformanceBindings`, so a seeded row
	// would have to invent a binding list for an interface the author wrote no `with` for — and the same
	// column is read by the E3016 conformance check, by `sweptConformanceIndex` and by the declaration hash.
	// Widening a stored column to answer a query is exactly the over-grant those readers would each have to be
	// taught to undo; shv2 owns the relation as a WALK instead, so the query asks it.
	//
	// ⚠ **A TYPE IS PUSHED ONCE HOWEVER MANY ROUTES REACH THE INTERFACE**, because the walk is per DECLARED
	// TYPE and not per clause entry — `type RangeIterator implements Iterator with RangeBound,
	// BidirectionalIterator` reaches `Iterator` twice and conforms once, which is what keeps the two halves
	// above from publishing a signature twice and emitting one body.
	//
	// ⚠ **A `type` ONLY.** An `enum`/`union` carries the same `conformsTo` column and is deliberately not
	// walked here: shv2 has no case where an extension method on an enum is reached, and the omission is a
	// missing method (E3004 at the call) rather than a wrong one. `sweptConformanceIndex` is keyed off
	// `structNamesInDeclOrder` too, so the index and this walk agree about that domain by construction.
	//
	// A full walk per extension, not an interface⇒conformers index: a program declares a handful of
	// extensions, this is asked twice per extension per compile, and the index would be built by every
	// program that declares none.
	export function conformersOf(interfaceName ByteArray) returns ByteArrayArray
		var conformers = ByteArrayArray.create()
		for name in self.structNamesInDeclOrder 'eachDeclaredType'
			if typeDeclaresInterface(self.sweptConformanceIndex(), interfaces: self.interfaces, typeName: name, interfaceName: interfaceName) 'conforms'
				conformers.push(name)
			end 'conforms'
		end 'eachDeclaredType'
		return conformers
	end 'conformersOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **REPLACE WHAT THE SWEEP RECORDED FOR ONE `implements` CLAUSE WITH THE WHOLE-PROGRAM RE-READ'S
	// ANSWER (W95)** — the write half of `Queries.foldConformanceClauses`. It writes THROUGH the registry's
	// own layout (`normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes`' shape one column over), so every reader that already holds
	// one sees the corrected list; nothing is re-keyed and no map is rewritten.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE DECLARING FILE IS CHECKED, AND IT IS THE ONLY THING STANDING BETWEEN THIS AND A DROPPED
	// DECLARATION.** `recordStruct` DROPS a user declaration that contests a compiler-owned name, leaving the
	// compiler's own layout under that key — and this re-read is driven by a SITE, which knows only that some
	// file declared the name there. Writing without the check would give the builtin's layout the user
	// declaration's conformances, which is `recordStruct`'s measured "user's struct captured the parameter
	// type of a module they did not write" with the clause instead of the fields. The same test settles a
	// name two files declare (E3006's business): the registry keeps one, and only that one's site writes.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT MIXES THE RE-READ INTO `self.hash`, AND THAT IS NOT BOOKKEEPING.** Before this pass the truncated
	// list was BOTH what the compiler used and what it hashed, so the two agreed by accident; now the used
	// list is this one. Editing `implements A with X, B` to `implements A with X, C` moves no byte
	// `recordStruct` can see — it hashes the truncated `[A]` either way — while it adds and removes whole
	// extension methods from the program. Only this mix carries that to the reader's parse memo.
	export function refoldConformanceClause(site ConformanceClauseDeclSite, clause ScannedConformanceClause)
		if site.declIsEnum 'anEnum'
			let enumLayout = try self.enumTypes.get(site.declName) otherwise return
			if not enumLayout.declFilePath.equals(site.path) 'theRegistryHoldsAnotherDeclaration'
				return
			end 'theRegistryHoldsAnotherDeclaration'

			enumLayout.recordConformances(clause.names)
		end 'anEnum' else 'aType'
			let layout = try self.structTypes.get(site.declName) otherwise return
			if not layout.declFilePath.equals(site.path) 'theRegistryHoldsAnotherDeclaration'
				return
			end 'theRegistryHoldsAnotherDeclaration'

			layout.recordConformances(clause.names, bindings: clause.bindings)
		end 'aType'

		let named = mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeRefoldedConformance), bytes: site.declName)
		self.hash = mixConformanceBindings(mixDeclaredConformances(named, conformsTo: clause.names, shape: DeclShapeDeclaredConformance), bindings: clause.bindings)
	end 'refoldConformanceClause'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE DECLARED-CONFORMANCE INDEX AS THIS PHASE CAN SEE IT — the same map `ConformanceCheck` builds,
	// from the only store that exists this early.** `declaredConformanceIndex(project)` reads
	// `project.conformances`, which the REAL PARSE fills; a CONDITIONAL extension's `where` clause has to be
	// decided inside `queryProgramSignatures`, before any file is parsed, so this reads the sweep's own column
	// (`StructLayout.conformsTo`) instead. Two STORES of the declarations, at two moments — but one map type
	// and one predicate (`typeConformsTo`), so what "conforms" MEANS is stated once.
	//
	// Memoized for `declaredConformanceIndex`'s reason: a conditional extension asks it once per conformer per
	// constraint, and rebuilding per question would be O(questions x declarations).
	export function sweptConformanceIndex() returns DeclaredConformanceMap
		if self.sweptConformancesBuilt 'alreadyBuilt'
			return self.sweptConformances
		end 'alreadyBuilt'

		var declared = DeclaredConformanceMap.create()
		for name in self.structNamesInDeclOrder 'eachDeclaredType'
			let layout = try self.structTypes.get(name) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.sweptConformanceIndex: `structNamesInDeclOrder` holds '{String.from(name)}' with no entry in `structTypes` — `recordStruct` writes both in one act")
			if layout.conformsTo.count() == 0 'declaresNone'
				continue
			end 'declaresNone'

			var declaredInterfaces = InterfaceNameSet.create()
			for interfaceName in layout.conformsTo 'eachInterface'
				declaredInterfaces.insert(interfaceName)
			end 'eachInterface'
			declared.upsert(name, value: declaredInterfaces)
		end 'eachDeclaredType'

		self.sweptConformances = declared
		self.sweptConformancesBuilt = true
		return declared
	end 'sweptConformanceIndex'

	// ⭐⭐ **FILE ONE `extension <T> implements <I…>` AGAINST ONE CONFORMER (review of W90).** Written by
	// `Parser.foldExtensionDeclarationInto` for every conformer of every extension that declares a
	// conformance — the same (conformer, interfaces) pairs `Parser.recordExtensionConformance` will later put
	// into `project.conformances`, minus the per-conformer `where` verdict, which is what makes this a
	// SUPERSET rather than a copy. See `extensionDeclaredConformances` for why the superset is the direction
	// its one reader needs.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SET IS COPIED RATHER THAN ADDED TO, for `ConformanceCheck.copyDeclaredInterfaces`' reason** — a
	// value fetched out of a `Map` BORROWS the map, so an `insert` on it is E3019 and an `upsert` while that
	// borrow is live is E3070. Paid once per (extension, conformer) pair, which is three declarations in the
	// whole corpus.
	export function recordExtensionDeclaredConformances(conformer ByteArray, declares InterfaceConformanceArray)
		if declares.isEmpty() 'declaresNoConformance'
			return
		end 'declaresNoConformance'

		var declaredInterfaces = InterfaceNameSet.create()
		if let already = try self.extensionDeclaredConformances.get(conformer) 'anotherExtensionReachedItToo'
			for interfaceName in already 'eachEarlier'
				declaredInterfaces.insert(interfaceName)
			end 'eachEarlier'
		end 'anotherExtensionReachedItToo'

		for i in 0 upto declares.count() 'eachInterface'
			declaredInterfaces.insert((try declares.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.recordExtensionDeclaredConformances: declares.get OOB at {i} below count {declares.count()}")).interfaceName)
		end 'eachInterface'

		self.extensionDeclaredConformances.upsert(conformer, value: declaredInterfaces)
	end 'recordExtensionDeclaredConformances'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT AN `extension <Name>` IS AN EXTENSION *OF* — the one place the two doors part company**
	// (conditional extensions). Both halves of the mechanism ask it and neither may decide for itself: the
	// fold publishes signatures against this answer and the real parse emits bodies against it.
	//
	// An interface and a type may not share a name (`checkTypeSymbolNamespace`, E3006), so the two arms are
	// mutually exclusive and the order they are asked in cannot matter. The type arm is asked first only
	// because `structTypes` is the cheaper lookup.
	export function extensionTargetOf(targetName ByteArray) returns ExtensionTarget
		// ⭐⭐ **A PRIMITIVE IS ITS OWN SOLE CONFORMER — the same answer a non-generic declared `type` gets one
		// arm down, and deliberately the SAME union case rather than a fourth one (W133).** Everything that
		// reads an extension target asks it for two things and only two: `extensionTargetConformers` (which
		// types get the methods) and `extensionTargetParamNames` (what vocabulary the `where` clause may name).
		// A primitive answers both exactly as a non-generic type does — itself, and nothing — so a case of its
		// own would be a fourth arm at every one of those readers whose body is `typeExtension`'s, plus a third
		// arm at `extensionConformerVerdict`, `noteTypeExtensionWitnessConstraints` and
		// `openExtensionBodyScope`. The departure a new case would BUY is nowhere.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE `typeExtension` PATH IS THE ONE THAT REACHES NO `StructLayout` LOOKUP, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS
		// SOUND AND THE INTERFACE PATH WOULD NOT BE.** `Parser.conformerAssociatedBindings` PANICS on a
		// conformer `structOf` misses, and it is reached only from the `interfaceExtension` arms of
		// `openExtensionBodyScope` and `extensionConformerVerdict`; both return before it for a
		// `typeExtension`. The two lookups this arm's conformer DOES reach —
		// `typeExtensionWitnessConstraints` and `structDeclaresTypeParams` — both answer an undeclared name
		// with the shared empty / `false` by construction.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **AND ARM ORDERING IS THE WEAKER OF THE TWO GUARANTEES — THE STRONGER ONE IS THAT A PRIMITIVE
		// CANNOT BE AN INTERFACE CONFORMER AT ALL (W133 review), which is worth stating because the
		// paragraph above would go stale the day someone reorders those arms.** `conformersOf`
		// builds its list by walking `structNamesInDeclOrder`, so `int` is not in any interface's conformer
		// set and `conformerAssociatedBindings` is never handed one however the arms are asked. ⇒ the
		// consequence a reader should expect, MEASURED: an `extension <SomeInterface>` publishes NOTHING onto
		// a primitive, even one whose `extension int implements <SomeInterface>` was accepted here — the call
		// is a clean `E2015 'int' has no method named …`, never a wrong answer. That is the same documented
		// gap this function's own ⚠ already grants `enum` and `union`, and it is a REFUSAL, so it is a
		// capability this rung did not build rather than one it half-built.
		// ⚠ **ASKED FIRST, AND NOTHING CAN SHADOW IT, BECAUSE THE THREE NAMES ARE KEYWORDS** (`Lexer.keywordTable`):
		// no declaration can bind `int`, `float` or `bool`, so there is no struct and no interface for this to
		// pre-empt. `byte` is an ordinary identifier and is NOT in this table (`primitiveTagOfTypeName` states
		// why), so a user's `type byte` still resolves through the struct arm below.
		if primitiveTagOfTypeName(targetName) != ValueTypeTag.unresolved 'aPrimitive'
			var itsOwnConformer = ByteArrayArray.create()
			itsOwnConformer.push(targetName)
			return ExtensionTarget.typeExtension(sharedEmptyTypeParamNames, conformers: itsOwnConformer)
		end 'aPrimitive'

		if let layout = try self.structTypes.get(targetName) 'aDeclaredType'
			var itself = ByteArrayArray.create()
			itself.push(targetName)
			return ExtensionTarget.typeExtension(layout.typeParamNames, conformers: itself)
		end 'aDeclaredType'

		match lookupInterface(self.interfaces, name: targetName) 'iface'
			found(iface) then return ExtensionTarget.interfaceExtension(iface.associatedTypeNames, conformers: self.conformersOf(targetName))
			notFound then return ExtensionTarget.undeclaredTarget
		end 'iface'
	end 'extensionTargetOf'

	// The `where` constraints declared on `typeName` (P1.7a slice 2a), in source order — the witness-slot
	// order. Empty for a non-generic, unconstrained, or undeclared type. Read by the E3017 instantiation
	// check (does each concrete argument conform?) and the witness lowering (which interface / concrete type
	// each hidden witness parameter carries).
	//
	// An undeclared type answers the SHARED empty rather than a fresh one: this sits on the per-call-site path
	// that decides whether to append witness arguments (`witnessConstraintsOfMethod`), where the answer for
	// almost every call in a program is "none".
	export function structWhereConstraints(typeName ByteArray) returns WhereConstraintArray
		let layout = try self.structTypes.get(typeName) otherwise return sharedEmptyMethodWitnessConstraints
		return layout.whereConstraints
	end 'structWhereConstraints'

	// The `uses` parameter names of a declared type, in declaration order — the list a `typeParameter` token
	// is resolved against. Read by `Parser.scanDescriptorNeedsInto`, which scans an EXTENSION body in its
	// TARGET's scope and so has no `uses` clause of its own to parse. An undeclared name answers the SHARED
	// empty, which is also what a non-generic type's layout already holds.
	export function structTypeParamsOf(typeName ByteArray) returns ByteArrayArray
		let layout = try self.structTypes.get(typeName) otherwise return sharedEmptyTypeParamNames
		return layout.typeParamNames
	end 'structTypeParamsOf'

	// ⭐ **DOES `typeName` DECLARE TYPE PARAMETERS — i.e. is it GENERIC?** The whole-program form of the
	// question `Parser.openTypeParamScope` answers locally, asked by the two recorders of a
	// `DescriptorScanSite`: an extension's conformer is a name, not a layout the recorder holds, and a
	// descriptor carries a TYPE PARAMETER's layout, so a non-generic target can never need one. An UNDECLARED
	// name answers `false` — an extension over a name no file declares publishes onto no conformer at all.
	export function structDeclaresTypeParams(typeName ByteArray) returns bool
		let layout = try self.structTypes.get(typeName) otherwise return false
		return not layout.typeParamNames.isEmpty()
	end 'structDeclaresTypeParams'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES `callee` CARRY THE HIDDEN LAYOUT-DESCRIPTOR PARAMETER (P1.6-B1)? — the ONE answer, read by
	// the RESERVATION side (`Parser.parseFunction`) and derived once by `Queries.foldDescriptorNeeds`.** The
	// SUPPLY side does not ask it by name at all: `LowerMaxonToStd.buildLayoutNeedingFuncs` recovers the same
	// fact from the emitted parameter COUNT, which is the strongest form of one-fact — the reservation is not
	// merely agreed with, it is the only thing the supply side reads.
	//
	// Asked with the qualified `Type.method` name, so a method contributed by an `extension` is the same key
	// as one declared in the type's own body. See `descriptorNeeds` for the compiler abort the per-type-body
	// version cost.
	export function functionNeedsLayoutDescriptor(callee ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.descriptorNeeds.contains(callee)
	end 'functionNeedsLayoutDescriptor'

	// Does ANY method in the whole program carry a descriptor — the allocation-free gate `parseFunction` opens
	// with, and it is not an optimization. Asking the question above needs the qualified name BUILT, and a
	// method's qualified name is a fresh `ByteArray`: spent per method declaration in a program that declares
	// no generic type — the entire scale corpus — that is a per-declaration allocation bought to learn `false`.
	// `SemanticCheck.hasInnerAliasParams` is the same guard over the same shape.
	export function anyFunctionNeedsLayoutDescriptor() returns bool
		return self.descriptorNeeds.count() != 0
	end 'anyFunctionNeedsLayoutDescriptor'

	// Publish the solved fixpoint. Wholesale, copy-on-write over the shared empty, and called at most once per
	// compile — `foldDescriptorNeeds` solves over every site in the program at one moment, so there is no
	// partial answer any reader could observe.
	export function recordDescriptorNeeds(needs DescriptorNeedNameSet)
		self.descriptorNeeds = needs
	end 'recordDescriptorNeeds'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE WITNESS CONSTRAINTS THE INSTANCE METHOD `callee` CARRIES — the ONE answer both the reservation
	// side and the supply side of the dictionary-passing ABI resolve a callee by.** Its enclosing type's own
	// `where` clause, UNIONED with the clause a TYPE EXTENSION added, in that order. Asked with the qualified
	// `Type.method` name.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THE EXTENSION'S CLAUSE USED TO *REPLACE* THE TYPE'S, AND THE COMMENT HERE ASSERTED THAT THE
	// REPLACEMENT WAS SAFE "BY CONSTRUCTION". IT WAS SAFE ONLY WHERE THE EXTENSION'S CLAUSE HAPPENED TO CONTAIN
	// THE TYPE'S** — which is why the two shapes that break it are the two the corpus had never written:
	//   • an UNCONDITIONAL `extension Pair` on a `where`-constrained type chose the EMPTY clause, so the body
	//     reserved 0 witness slots while every caller passed 2 — **MEASURED: compiled clean, 2,646 bytes, and
	//     the binary access-violated after its first line of output**;
	//   • a clause DISJOINT from the type's (`extension Pair where A is Comparable`) reserved 1 where the body's
	//     own callee wanted 2 — **MEASURED: `panic at RegisterAllocator.maxon:1913, colorOpForward: use of
	//     value 3 before it was colored`**, a compiler crash whose cause is nowhere near the allocator.
	// A witness-slot count is ABI, so a reservation and a supply that disagree is an argument-register
	// mismatch and not a type error: nothing downstream can notice.
	//
	// ⭐ **BOTH REFERENCES UNION, AND NEITHER HAS A REPLACE ANYWHERE.** v1 collects the enclosing TYPE's
	// constraints unconditionally and then APPENDS the extension's, de-duplicated
	// (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/IR/Maxon/LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:14706-14765`); the bootstrap merges the
	// extension's clause INTO the target struct's with the type's own entry winning
	// (`maxon-sharp/Compiler/2-Parser.cs:4899-4914`).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND THE UNION IS TAKEN IN ONE PLACE THAT BOTH SIDES CALL** (`typeExtensionWitnessConstraints`),
	// never computed a second time at the reservation door — the same fact derived twice is the defect this
	// replaced, not the cure for it. See that function for the order argument and for why it is
	// declaration-order independent.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THE EXTENSION CLAUSE IS A PARAMETER BECAUSE ITS SOURCE DEPENDS ON WHICH NAME THE ASKER HOLDS,
	// AND READING IT HERE OFF THE CALLEE WAS A SEGFAULT (ARR3b).** `extensionMethodConstraints` is keyed by
	// the DECLARED `<Conformer>.<method>`, and a D7 overload member does not wear that name: a `<Conformer>.
	// <method>` declared by extensions in more than one FILE registers every member under a parameter-type
	// suffix and NOBODY keeps the bare spelling. So the supply side, which holds the RESOLVED member name,
	// read an empty clause while the body had reserved a slot from its lexical one — see
	// `FuncSignature.extensionConstraints` for the measured 0xC0000005 and for its mirror.
	//
	// ⇒ each asker states where its clause came from, and there are exactly two honest sources:
	//   • **the PARSE-TIME gates**, which hold the BARE name deliberately (a call is dispatched before its
	//     arguments are read) ⇒ `extensionMethodConstraintsOf(callee)`, the declaration sweep's answer;
	//   • **everything after the merge**, which holds a REGISTRATION name ⇒ that name's own
	//     `FuncSignature.extensionConstraints`, recorded by the parse that minted the name.
	export function witnessConstraintsOfMethod(callee ByteArray, extensionConstraints WhereConstraintArray) returns WhereConstraintArray
		match splitQualifiedName(callee) 'q'
			notQualified then return sharedEmptyMethodWitnessConstraints
			parts(typeName, _) then return self.typeExtensionWitnessConstraints(typeName, extensionConstraints: extensionConstraints)
		end 'q'
	end 'witnessConstraintsOfMethod'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE WITNESS-SLOT LIST OF A TYPE `typeName` WHOSE METHOD ALSO CARRIES THE EXTENSION CLAUSE
	// `extensionConstraints` — the ONE derived list the RESERVATION side and the SUPPLY side of the
	// dictionary-passing ABI both read.** `Parser.openTypeExtensionBodyScope` opens the body with it (so
	// `witnessParamCountFor` reserves exactly these slots) and `witnessConstraintsOfMethod` answers with it (so
	// `LowerMaxonToStd.appendWitnessArgs` fills exactly these slots). Both hand it the SAME two inputs — the
	// extension's target type, and the extension's own clause as `noteTypeExtensionWitnessConstraints` filed it
	// — so the two lists are not merely equal, they are one call.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE TYPE'S OWN CONSTRAINTS COME FIRST, IN DECLARATION ORDER, AND THAT ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING.** A
	// PLAIN method of the type carries exactly the type's list, so the type's list must be a PREFIX of an
	// extension method's — that is precisely the premise `LowerMaxonToStd.forwardCallerWitness` rests on when
	// an extension body drives another method on `self` and forwards slot `i` to slot `i`. Appending the extras
	// after is the only order that makes every such prefix align by construction rather than by luck. (It was
	// luck: with the extension's own source order chosen, `extension Pair where A is Equatable and Comparable,
	// B is Equatable` handed its callee the `A is Comparable` table where `B is Equatable` belonged, and the
	// program was green only because it never followed slot 1.)
	//
	// ⭐ **DE-DUPLICATED AGAINST WHAT IS ALREADY RESERVED**, so an extension that restates one of the type's own
	// constraints — the shape the replace-rule was accidentally right for — reserves one slot for it and not
	// two. The scan is against the list being BUILT rather than against the type's alone, so a clause that
	// repeats itself cannot reserve a slot twice either.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS ORDER-INDEPENDENT BECAUSE OF WHERE IT IS ASKED FROM, NOT BECAUSE OF A FALLBACK.** Both callers
	// run after `ProgramSignatures.allFilesFolded`: an extension's header is read by
	// `Queries.foldExtensionDeclarations`, which waits for every file's declarations for its own older reason
	// (`extensionTargetOf` cannot say what `extension Pair` even extends until `Pair` is folded), and the real
	// parse runs later still. So `structWhereConstraints` is complete at both doors and an `extension` written
	// ABOVE the `type` it extends reserves the same slots as one written below. There is deliberately no
	// "type's clause not available yet" arm: it would be the second source of truth this function exists to
	// remove.
	//
	// The unconditional arm returns the type's own array rather than a copy of it — the answer for every method
	// in a program that declares no conditional extension, which is the whole corpus but one spec.
	export function typeExtensionWitnessConstraints(typeName ByteArray, extensionConstraints WhereConstraintArray) returns WhereConstraintArray
		let declared = self.structWhereConstraints(typeName)
		if extensionConstraints.isEmpty() 'unconditional'
			return declared
		end 'unconditional'

		var reserved = WhereConstraintArray.create()
		reserved.append(declared)
		for constraint in extensionConstraints 'eachExtra'
			if whereConstraintListReserves(reserved, constraint: constraint) 'alreadyReserved'
				continue
			end 'alreadyReserved'
			reserved.push(constraint)
		end 'eachExtra'

		return reserved
	end 'typeExtensionWitnessConstraints'

	// ⭐ The constraints `callee` carries that came from a conditional TYPE EXTENSION rather than from its
	// type's own declaration — empty for every other method. The same map read as above, asked a DIFFERENT
	// question and separated for a reason: a type's own `where` constraints are validated at the
	// INSTANTIATION site (E3017, `checkWhereConstraints`), so by the time a call is typed there is nothing
	// left to check about them. An extension's are validated nowhere — the extension is CONDITIONAL, so an
	// instance that fails them is perfectly legal and merely does not have the method — which makes the CALL
	// the only place the question can be asked. See `Parser.requireReceiverSatisfiesExtensionConstraints`.
	export function extensionMethodConstraintsOf(callee ByteArray) returns WhereConstraintArray
		let constraints = try self.extensionMethodConstraints.get(callee) otherwise return sharedEmptyMethodWitnessConstraints
		return constraints
	end 'extensionMethodConstraintsOf'

	// File the witness constraints of one TYPE-extension method, and the SIGNATURE it was published with, in
	// one act — see `ExtensionMethodConstraintMap`.
	export function recordExtensionMethodConstraints(callee ByteArray, constraints WhereConstraintArray)
		self.extensionMethodConstraints.upsert(callee, value: constraints)
	end 'recordExtensionMethodConstraints'

	// ⭐ **THE `where` CLAUSE A CONDITIONAL TYPE EXTENSION'S REFUSAL QUOTES**, rendered against the OWNER
	// type's own `uses` list — which is the list a `WhereConstraint.typeParamIndex` is a position in. Written
	// here rather than at either refusal site because both of them (`Parser.requireReceiverSatisfiesExtensionConstraints`
	// and `SemanticCheck.checkResolvedExtensionConstraints`) quote the same sentence about the same clause,
	// and a second spelling of the lookup is a second chance to render a different one.
	//
	// A type carrying constraints but absent from the struct registry is unreachable: the constraints were
	// filed under a method OF that type (`Parser.noteTypeExtensionWitnessConstraints`).
	export function renderExtensionClause(typeName ByteArray, constraints WhereConstraintArray) returns String
		match self.structOf(typeName) 'layout'
			declared(layout) then return renderWhereConstraints(constraints, paramNames: layout.typeParamNames)
			undeclared then panic("ProgramSignatures.renderExtensionClause: '{String.from(typeName)}' carries conditional-extension constraints but is in no struct registry — the constraints were filed under a method of that type")
		end 'layout'
	end 'renderExtensionClause'

	// Note that an UNCONDITIONAL type extension declares `callee` — the write side of the test below.
	export function noteUnconstrainedExtensionMethod(callee ByteArray)
		self.unconstrainedExtensionMethods.insert(callee)
	end 'noteUnconstrainedExtensionMethod'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS `<Conformer>.<method>` ALSO DECLARED BY AN EXTENSION WITH NO `where` CLAUSE?** One name,
	// two declarations, only one of them conditional — which `stdlib/Array.maxon` is built out of:
	// `extension Array where Element is Comparable` declares `sort()` and a second, UNCONSTRAINED
	// `extension Array` declares `sort(cmp)`.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT MATTERS BECAUSE THE PARSE-TIME GATE IS KEYED ON THE NAME AND THE CONSTRAINTS ARE TOO.** A call
	// is dispatched before its arguments are read, so the parser holds the BARE name and cannot know which
	// member it means; `extensionMethodConstraintsOf` answers with the CONDITIONAL declaration's clause,
	// because an unconditional one files none. MEASURED with `stdlib/Array.maxon` listed:
	// **`E4006: Type 'Array' has no field named 'sort' ('sort' is available as a conditional extension where
	// Element is Comparable, but 'String' does not implement 'Comparable')`** — refusing
	// `a.sort(byLength)`, a call to the overload that has no clause at all.
	//
	// So when this answers TRUE the parse-time gate DEFERS and `SemanticCheck.checkResolvedExtensionConstraints`
	// decides instead, on the member overload resolution actually picked. It is a set membership costing
	// nothing on a program with no conditional extension: `Set.contains` returns at its `capacity == 0` test.
	export function hasUnconstrainedExtensionDeclaration(callee ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.unconstrainedExtensionMethods.contains(callee)
	end 'hasUnconstrainedExtensionDeclaration'

	// File one conditional extension method withheld from one conformer — see `ConditionalExtensionSkip`.
	// FIRST-WINS: two extensions withholding a same-named method from one type leave the first reason, which
	// is the one whose `where` clause the author reads first going down the file.
	export function recordConditionalExtensionSkip(callee ByteArray, skip ConditionalExtensionSkip)
		if self.conditionalExtensionSkips.contains(callee) 'alreadyExplained'
			return
		end 'alreadyExplained'
		self.conditionalExtensionSkips.upsert(callee, value: skip)
	end 'recordConditionalExtensionSkip'

	// Why `callee` does not exist, when the reason is that a conditional extension withheld it. `none` for
	// every other missing method, which is the overwhelming majority — an ordinary typo.
	export function conditionalExtensionSkipOf(callee ByteArray) returns ConditionalExtensionSkipLookup
		if let skip = try self.conditionalExtensionSkips.get(callee) 'withheld'
			return ConditionalExtensionSkipLookup.withheld(skip)
		end 'withheld'
		return ConditionalExtensionSkipLookup.notWithheld
	end 'conditionalExtensionSkipOf'

	// Every `Base with Args` instantiation site with its source anchor (P1.7a slice 2a) — the list the E3017
	// where-constraint check walks. A constraint-free base's sites are cheaply skipped (its `whereConstraints`
	// is empty), so this is the whole instantiation set, not a pre-filtered one.
	export function instantiationSites() returns GenericInstantiationSiteArray
		return self.genericAliasSites
	end 'instantiationSites'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH ASSOCIATED POSITIONS THIS PROGRAM HOLDS AT AN INTERFACE — one walk of the instantiation
	// sites, with every declaration in and no file yet parsed.**
	//
	// A use site writing `Bag with (Integer, IntegerCursor)`, where `IntegerCursor` denotes an INTERFACE, is
	// not restating a fact the conformances already carry: the conformances say `UpBag` binds `UpCursor` and
	// `DownBag` binds `DownCursor`, and NOTHING in them says the program means to hold those values
	// existentially rather than at one concrete type. That is the one thing a `with` argument genuinely adds,
	// and it is why this is a record where `instanceDenotedType`'s header argues a concrete argument must not
	// be one — a concrete argument is checked against the conformances (E3125), an interface argument
	// DECLARES the position's ABI binding, and the conformers' own bindings are the types widened into it.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT MUST RUN BEFORE THE PARSE, because the parse is what TYPES a dispatch** — a requirement whose
	// result is a held associated type mints an `interfaceRef` result and pairs it with a witness loaded from
	// the receiver's table, and nothing downstream of the parser can retype that. The instantiation sites are
	// complete at the end of the fold (`readGenericInstanceAlias` is the ONE reader of a written
	// `Base with Args`, and it records a site for every one carrying a source anchor), so the answer is
	// whole-program at exactly the moment it is first needed.
	//
	// ⚠ **AFTER `foldInterfaceDeclarations` AND `normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes`**: the first is what fills the
	// interface registry `heldInterfaceNameOfArg` asks, and the second settles the one spelling every other
	// reader sees. A FIRST-WINS record — two sites naming different interfaces for one position are E3125's
	// to report, which needs the settled answer to compare against, so the settle may not refuse.
	export function settleAssociatedTypeHoldings()
		if self.interfaces.count() == 0 'noInterfaceIsDeclared'
			return
		end 'noInterfaceIsDeclared'

		for site in self.genericAliasSites 'eachSite'
			self.noteAssociatedTypeHoldings(site.gid)
		end 'eachSite'
	end 'settleAssociatedTypeHoldings'

	// The holdings ONE instantiation site declares. A site over a `type` base declares none, and neither does
	// an argument naming anything but an interface — the overwhelming majority, so both are one test.
	function noteAssociatedTypeHoldings(gid GenericInstanceId)
		let base = self.genericInstanceBaseName(gid)
		match lookupInterface(self.interfaces, name: base) 'base'
			notFound then return
			found(iface) then self.noteAssociatedTypeHoldingsOf(gid, base: base, iface: iface)
		end 'base'
	end 'noteAssociatedTypeHoldings'

	// The per-position walk of ONE interface-based site, split from the base test above so the `found` arm is
	// a call rather than a body — the shape every `lookupInterface` reader in this file uses.
	function noteAssociatedTypeHoldingsOf(gid GenericInstanceId, base ByteArray, iface IrInterface)
		// A site may bind FEWER arguments than the interface declares (E3016's to report), so the walk is
		// bounded by both — reading past either would be a slot with no position or a position with no name.
		let bound = self.genericInstanceArgCount(gid)
		for position in 0 upto iface.associatedTypeNames.count() 'eachAssociatedType'
			if position >= bound 'boundNothingHere'
				break
			end 'boundNothingHere'

			if let held = try self.instanceArgHeldInterfaceName(gid, position: position) 'namesAnInterface'
				self.recordAssociatedTypeHolding(base, associatedCount: iface.associatedTypeNames.count(), position: position, heldAt: held)
			end 'namesAnInterface'
		end 'eachAssociatedType'
	end 'noteAssociatedTypeHoldingsOf'

	// File one holding, FIRST-WINS (see `settleAssociatedTypeHoldings` for why this may not refuse). The
	// per-interface row is allocated at its full associated-type WIDTH on first write, so a position that no
	// site holds reads as the empty name at its own index rather than needing a second, dense numbering — the
	// same argument `witnessAssociatedSlotOffset` makes about the witness table's own slot block.
	// ⚠ **THE ROW IS MUTATED AND THEN STORED, NEVER STORED AND THEN MUTATED.** An `Array` is COPY-ON-WRITE, so
	// a row handed to `upsert` and kept as a local is two owners of one buffer: the very next `set` forks it,
	// and the map keeps the row as it was. MEASURED — the holding was recorded, `associatedTypeHeldAt` found
	// the row, and every position in it was still empty.
	function recordAssociatedTypeHolding(interfaceName ByteArray, associatedCount ElementCount, position ElementCount, heldAt ByteArray)
		var row = self.associatedTypeHoldingRow(interfaceName, associatedCount: associatedCount)
		if not (try row.get(position) otherwise panic("recordAssociatedTypeHolding: row.get OOB at {position} below width {row.count()} — the row is allocated at the interface's associated-type count and the caller bounds `position` by it")).isEmpty() 'alreadySettled'
			return
		end 'alreadySettled'

		try row.set(position, value: heldAt) otherwise panic("recordAssociatedTypeHolding: row.set OOB at {position} below width {row.count()} — the read one line above succeeded at the same index")
		self.associatedTypeHoldings.upsert(interfaceName, value: row)
	end 'recordAssociatedTypeHolding'

	// This interface's row, or a fresh one at its full associated-type WIDTH — see
	// `AssociatedTypeHoldingMap` for why the row is wide rather than dense, and `recordAssociatedTypeHolding`
	// for why a fresh row is NOT filed here.
	function associatedTypeHoldingRow(interfaceName ByteArray, associatedCount ElementCount) returns ByteArrayArray
		if let existing = try self.associatedTypeHoldings.get(interfaceName) 'alreadyHoldsSomething'
			return existing
		end 'alreadyHoldsSomething'

		var fresh = ByteArrayArray.create()
		fresh.growFilled(associatedCount, value: EmptySourceText)
		return fresh
	end 'associatedTypeHoldingRow'

	// ⭐ **THE INTERFACE A RECORDED TYPE ARGUMENT DENOTES, IN EVERY SPELLING THE SWEEP CAN LEAVE IT IN** —
	// `MapError.keyNotFound` when it denotes none, which is every ordinary type argument.
	//
	// ⚠ **THREE SPELLINGS, AND WHICH ONE ARRIVES IS A FACT ABOUT DECLARATION ORDER RATHER THAN ABOUT THE
	// PROGRAM.** `parseTypeReference` runs during the SWEEP, where the interface registry is still empty, so a
	// bare `Cursor` is left a `named`; an alias `IntegerCursor` whose own declaration was already swept
	// resolves through `instanceDenotedType` and — the registry being empty — arrives as the
	// `genericInstance` over `Cursor` rather than as the existential; and one swept later arrives as a
	// `named` too, to be resolved through the alias registry. Handling only the spelling a test happened to
	// produce would make the feature depend on the order two `typealias` lines were written in.
	// The interface instance `gid`'s argument at `position` denotes; `MapError.keyNotFound` when it denotes
	// none. TWO readers, and one derivation between them: the SETTLE, which records the holding this declares,
	// and E3125, which refuses a second site claiming a different one. Asking two different ways would let a
	// site declare a holding under one reading and be checked against another.
	export function instanceArgHeldInterfaceName(gid GenericInstanceId, position TypeParamPosition) returns ByteArray throws MapError
		return match try self.heldArgOf(self.substitutedInstanceArg(gid, position: position)) 'denoted'
			instantiated(held) gives self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(self.instanceDenotedType(held, into: self.typeNames)))
			bare(name) gives name
		end 'denoted'
	end 'instanceArgHeldInterfaceName'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME ARGUMENT, READ FOR ITS `with` ARGUMENTS RATHER THAN FOR ITS NAME (W57).** A holding is a
	// fact about the interface NAME and that is all E3125 and the settle need; a DISPATCH through the held
	// value needs more, because a requirement returning the held interface's own associated type has to be
	// typed against whatever THIS site bound that position to. `Cursor` alone cannot say what `Element` is;
	// `Cursor with Element` can. `MapError.keyNotFound` for a bare interface name, which genuinely binds
	// nothing. TWO readers: `Parser.associatedReturnThroughReceiverInstance`, which carries the nested
	// arguments onto the held value so a dispatch on it can be typed, and
	// `SemanticCheck.heldPositionNestedClaim`, which is what makes the conformer keep them (W59).
	export function instanceArgHeldInstance(gid GenericInstanceId, position TypeParamPosition) returns GenericInstanceId throws MapError
		match try self.heldArgOf(self.substitutedInstanceArg(gid, position: position)) 'denoted'
			instantiated(held) then return held
			bare then throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'denoted'
	end 'instanceArgHeldInstance'

	// The ONE reading of a recorded type argument that denotes an interface, in every spelling the sweep can
	// leave it in (see the header above) — split from its two askers because they want two different halves of
	// it and a second walk of those three spellings is exactly the declaration-order dependence that header
	// warns about.
	function heldArgOf(arg MaxonType) returns HeldAssociatedArg throws MapError
		if maxonTypeTag(arg) == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'anInstanceOverSomeBase'
			let held = typeNameIdOf(arg)
			if maxonTypeTag(self.instanceDenotedType(held, into: self.typeNames)) != ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'aStructInstance'
				throw MapError.keyNotFound
			end 'aStructInstance'

			return HeldAssociatedArg.instantiated(held)
		end 'anInstanceOverSomeBase'

		if maxonTypeTag(arg) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'alreadyAnExistential'
			return HeldAssociatedArg.bare(self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(arg)))
		end 'alreadyAnExistential'

		// The `named` half is `sweptInterfaceTypeOf`'s exactly — a bare interface name, or an alias whose base
		// is one — and is asked rather than re-written, so a spelling admitted for a struct FIELD and a
		// spelling admitted here cannot come to differ. It runs FIRST so a `named` that denotes no interface at
		// all still leaves by that door's throw; only past it is the written name asked whether it was an
		// ALIAS, which is the half that carries arguments where a bare interface name carries none.
		let denotedInterface = try self.sweptInterfaceTypeOf(arg)
		let written = self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(arg))
		if self.isGenericAlias(written) 'anAliasCarriesArguments'
			return HeldAssociatedArg.instantiated(self.bareGenericAliasInstance(written))
		end 'anAliasCarriesArguments'

		return HeldAssociatedArg.bare(self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(denotedInterface)))
	end 'heldArgOf'

	// Does this program hold ANY of `interfaceName`'s associated positions at an interface? The gate on the
	// witness table's associated slot BLOCK, so a program that does not use the feature mints exactly the
	// bytes it always did — and one map probe on a name, with no key to join.
	export function interfaceHoldsAnAssociatedType(interfaceName ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.associatedTypeHoldings.contains(interfaceName)
	end 'interfaceHoldsAnAssociatedType'

	// The interface `interfaceName`'s associated type at `position` is held at; `MapError.keyNotFound` when
	// that position is held at nothing, which is every position of every interface in a program that does not
	// write an interface as a `with` argument.
	export function associatedTypeHeldAt(interfaceName ByteArray, position ElementCount) returns ByteArray throws MapError
		let row = try self.associatedTypeHoldings.get(interfaceName)
		if position >= row.count() 'pastThisInterfacesPositions'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'pastThisInterfacesPositions'

		let held = try row.get(position) otherwise panic("associatedTypeHeldAt: row.get OOB at {position} below width {row.count()} — the guard above excludes it")
		if held.isEmpty() 'thisPositionIsNotHeld'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'thisPositionIsNotHeld'

		return held
	end 'associatedTypeHeldAt'

	// ⭐ **THE EXISTENCE HALF OF THE READ ABOVE — "is THIS position held at all?"** — for a caller that must only
	// SKIP a held position and has no use for which interface holds it
	// (`SemanticCheck.existentialSiteClaimAgainst`).
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS A SEPARATE DOOR BECAUSE THE LANGUAGE MAKES IT ONE**, which is `IrInterface.namesInclude`'s
	// reason one registry over: `associatedTypeHeldAt` is PURE, so its result may not be dropped in any spelling
	// — `if let _ = try f()` is E3064 and a bare `if try f()` is E3124 — and binding the name only to leave it
	// unread is E3012. A caller that wants only WHETHER is given the function that answers only that.
	//
	// It is NOT `interfaceHoldsAnAssociatedType` with a position bolted on: that one asks whether the interface
	// holds ANY position (the gate on the witness table's whole associated-slot block), and answering it for one
	// position would skip every position of an interface that holds a DIFFERENT one.
	export function associatedTypeIsHeldAt(interfaceName ByteArray, position ElementCount) returns bool
		let row = try self.associatedTypeHoldings.get(interfaceName) otherwise return false
		if position >= row.count() 'pastThisInterfacesPositions'
			return false
		end 'pastThisInterfacesPositions'

		return not (try row.get(position) otherwise panic("associatedTypeIsHeldAt: row.get OOB at {position} below width {row.count()} — the guard above excludes it")).isEmpty()
	end 'associatedTypeIsHeldAt'

	// The COMPILED name of instance `gid`'s type argument at `position` (P1.7a slice 2a) — `mangleTypeArg`'s
	// answer, so a struct/named arg reads as its bare name and a NESTED instance as its mangled form.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE `.rdata`-LABEL SPELLING AND ITS ONE CALLER WANTS EXACTLY THAT**: `ParseStaging`'s E3006
	// pair-collision message, where the compiled join is the SUBJECT of the sentence (see
	// `renderInstantiation`). It was named `instanceArgTypeName` and read by three DIAGNOSTICS as well, which
	// is how `Box_Leaf` — a string no source line holds — reached `expected 'Box_Leaf'`. Those three now ask
	// `instanceArgDisplayName`; this one keeps the mint because the mint is what collided.
	export function instanceArgCompiledName(gid GenericInstanceId, position TypeParamPosition) returns ByteArray
		return self.mangleTypeArg(self.substitutedInstanceArg(gid, position: position)).toByteArray()
	end 'instanceArgCompiledName'

	// ⭐⭐ **HOW A DIAGNOSTIC SPELLS ONE OF AN INSTANCE'S TYPE ARGUMENTS — the display twin of the function
	// above, under the 2026-08-04 ruling that a diagnostic names a type by the `typealias` the author wrote.**
	// Read by every message that quotes a type argument: the generic call's `expected` side
	// (`SemanticCheck.checkTypeParameterArgsAt`), the E3017 `where`-constraint violation, and the withheld
	// conditional-extension refusal.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE NESTED-INSTANCE ARM IS THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE**, and the rest is not written a second time. Every
	// other arm of the compiled renderer already answers with something an author wrote — a struct or a ranged
	// alias by its own name, a primitive by its keyword — so there is one rule there and it stays in one place.
	// MEASURED before this existed, on a program declaring `typealias LeafBox = Box with Leaf`:
	// `argument type mismatch for 'v': expected 'Box_Leaf', got 'OtherBox'` — one sentence spelling one of its
	// two types the compiler's way and the other the author's.
	//
	// It DELEGATES rather than re-spelling `mangleTypeArg(arg)`, which costs a second `substitutedInstanceArg`
	// — a bounds-checked array read of an already-built type, on a path that only a REJECTED program reaches.
	// A second copy of the compiled rule for that is the trade this codebase does not make.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THE OPAQUE ARM IS THE SECOND HALF OF THAT SAME BUG, AND IT WAS STILL OPEN (W57).** `mangleTypeArg`
	// spells a type-parameter argument as its W14 DIGEST — `T5a05ccf53759d5f6` — and its own header says why
	// that is right THERE: it is a lookup key, injective and build-order stable. It is not a type name, and no
	// source line holds it. MEASURED, on a generic body writing `Cursor with Element`: *"this use of 'Cursor'
	// binds its associated type 'Element' to 'T5a05ccf53759d5f6', but 'Walk' binds it to 'Element'"* — one
	// sentence naming ONE parameter twice, once as the author wrote it and once as a hash, and reporting them
	// as a disagreement.
	//
	// ⚠ **SO IT IS NOT ONLY THE PRINTING: E3125 *COMPARES* THIS ANSWER**, against a conformance's `withArgs`,
	// which is the source spelling. `requireWrittenBindingMatchesConformers` says in as many words that both
	// sides are the source spelling and that comparing a compiled name against one *"would report a difference
	// in RENDERING as a difference in type"* — which is exactly what a digest did, refusing every shared
	// generic body that instantiates an interface over its own parameter. The parameter's own NAME is what
	// both sides then hold, and it is the same currency `associatedAbiBindingOf` already compares two
	// CONFORMERS in.
	export function instanceArgDisplayName(gid GenericInstanceId, position TypeParamPosition) returns ByteArray
		let arg = self.substitutedInstanceArg(gid, position: position)
		if maxonTypeTag(arg) == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'nestedInstance'
			return self.instanceDisplayName(typeNameIdOf(arg))
		end 'nestedInstance'

		if maxonTypeTag(arg) == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'opaqueParam'
			let owner = try self.typeParamOwnerOf(typeNameIdOf(arg)) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.instanceArgDisplayName: type-parameter token {typeNameIdOf(arg)} has no owner — `recordStruct` and `recordInterfaceDeclaration` file every declared parameter as the sweep folds, so a token with none was minted inside a declaration neither fold saw")
			return owner.paramName
		end 'opaqueParam'

		return self.instanceArgCompiledName(gid, position: position)
	end 'instanceArgDisplayName'

	// How many type arguments instance `gid` carries — `Box with String` ⇒ 1, `Pair with (A, B)` ⇒ 2.
	// The BOUND on `instanceArgCompiledName`/`instanceArgDisplayName`/`substitutedInstanceArg`'s `position`,
	// for a reader that walks
	// an instance's whole argument list rather than one constrained parameter of it
	// (`renderInstantiation`, which spells a colliding instantiation back out in source form).
	//
	// ⚠ It is NOT `genericInstanceFieldCount`, which is the BASE STRUCT's field count: the two agree for
	// a plain generic struct and disagree for the builtin bases, where `Array with Integer` has one type
	// argument and no base struct at all (`genericInstanceFieldCount` panics on it).
	export function genericInstanceArgCount(gid GenericInstanceId) returns TypeParamPosition
		return self.genericInstances.instanceArgs(gid).count()
	end 'genericInstanceArgCount'

	// Is `name` a declared `type` — the `bool` half of `structOf`, and a HOT-PATH allocation guard, the
	// twin of `containsEnum`. `structOf` returns a `DeclaredStruct`, a Maxon union VALUE that boxes on
	// its `undeclared` case as much as any other, so a caller that only needs "is it a struct?" for the
	// COMMON no answer (`Parser.resolveNamedStruct` asks it of every `named` parameter/return type,
	// most of them ranged int aliases) must not pay that box just to throw it away. A `Map.contains`
	// allocates nothing.
	export function containsStruct(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.structTypes.contains(name)
	end 'containsStruct'

	// ⭐⭐ **CAN A VALUE OF `type name` EXIST AT ALL?** — the question a base that is a STRUCT-TYPED SELF-FIELD
	// ALIAS is admitted or refused on (`Parser.structBaseOfReceiver`).
	//
	// Maxon has no `null`, no optional and no partial initialization, so building a struct means supplying a
	// value for EVERY field, and a field of struct type means first building THAT struct. A CYCLE in the
	// struct-typed-field graph is therefore an infinite regress, and nothing on one — or reaching one — can
	// ever exist. `type Node` with `var next as Node` is the one-node case; `A.b as B` with `B.a as A` is the
	// same fact spread over two declarations, and it is not "self-referential" in any way a name comparison
	// could tell apart. That is why this walks the GRAPH rather than asking whether a field's type equals its
	// enclosing type: the two questions agree on the one-node case and part company on every longer one.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN UNDECLARED NAME ANSWERS `false`, AND THE TEST IS WRITTEN HERE, AT THE ENTRY.** It cannot be
	// proven constructible, and this is a gate whose safe direction is refusal. It is the ENTRY's business
	// because it is the only place a name can arrive unvetted: the walk itself only ever recurses into a name
	// it has just proved declared, so the same test inside the recursion would be dead code that reads as a
	// live policy — and deleting the walk's own `structTypes.contains` as "redundant with this one" would
	// silently make every scalar, String and enum field answer `false`.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CHASE: a type ARGUMENT that closes the cycle** — `var b as Box with
	// Outer` inside `type Outer`, in EITHER of the two spellings such a field arrives in. A directly-tagged
	// `genericInstance` is not an aggregate NAME at all, so the walk's tag guard skips it; a generic ALIAS
	// (`typealias OuterBox = Box with Outer`, `var b as OuterBox`) arrives as a bare `named` — the sweep
	// recorded the field before the alias was interned (`denotesArrayInstance` and `declaredSlotType` both
	// state it) — so it passes the tag guard and is skipped one line later by `structTypes.contains`, which a
	// generic alias's name is not in. Same verdict, different door.
	//
	// The direction is CHOSEN, not overlooked: the other arm of that trade treats every generic-instance
	// field as undecided, which false-REFUSES a base inside any type that merely holds an `IntArray` beside a
	// struct field — a program that runs. Over-admitting here only lets a base compile inside a type nothing
	// can construct, so the code lowered for it is correct and unreachable. It costs a false accept of dead
	// code, never a wrong answer.
	export function structTypeIsConstructible(name ByteArray) returns bool
		if not self.structTypes.contains(name) 'notDeclared'
			return false
		end 'notDeclared'

		return self.structIsConstructibleWithin(name, visiting: StructNameSet.create(), settled: StructNameSet.create())
	end 'structTypeIsConstructible'

	// The walk `structTypeIsConstructible` is. `visiting` holds the types whose own proof is still OPEN, so a
	// name reached while it is in there IS the cycle; `settled` holds the types already proven constructible.
	// Precondition: `name` is a declared struct — every entry into this walk is gated on `structTypes`, at the
	// exported door above and at the field recursion below.
	//
	// ⚠ **`visiting` IS NEVER UNMARKED, AND `settled` IS WHAT MAKES THAT CORRECT rather than merely cheap.** A
	// name is never removed, because the only way a type's proof ends without settling it is the `false` that
	// abandons the whole query — so an unsettled name in `visiting` is always an open chain. `settled` is
	// therefore consulted FIRST: a diamond in the field graph (two fields of one type) re-enters a name that is
	// still in `visiting` from the branch that finished it, and reading that as a cycle would refuse a struct
	// that builds fine. Together they also make the walk linear in the graph rather than once per path into
	// each node.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TWO SETS ARE A REAL DIFFERENCE FROM `typeSupportsDeepClone`'s ONE**, which walks this same field
	// graph with a single `ClonerSet` and answers `true` on a re-entry: a recursive type IS clonable, because
	// the synthesized cloner recurses at RUN time through the same field. Here a re-entry is the infinite
	// regress itself and must answer `false`, so "in flight" and "proven" cannot share one set.
	//
	// A field's type is classified over `self.typeNames` — the interner a layout's field types were minted
	// against (`structFieldIsManaged`'s discipline) — and through `denotedSlotAggregateName`, so a TUPLE-alias
	// field answers under the tuple's own name exactly as it does for every other classifier that reads a raw
	// layout field type. A slot naming no declared struct forces no construction and is skipped: a scalar, a
	// String, a function, an opaque type parameter and a generic instance all have values that need no further
	// struct to exist.
	function structIsConstructibleWithin(name ByteArray, visiting StructNameSet, settled StructNameSet) returns bool
		if settled.contains(name) 'alreadyProven'
			return true
		end 'alreadyProven'

		if visiting.contains(name) 'closesTheCycle'
			return false
		end 'closesTheCycle'

		let layout = try self.structTypes.get(name) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.structIsConstructibleWithin: no `type {String.from(name)}` is declared — `structTypeIsConstructible` gates the entry and the field recursion gates itself on `structTypes.contains`")

		visiting.insert(name)

		for i in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachField'
			let declared = try layout.fieldTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.structIsConstructibleWithin: fieldTypes.get OOB at {i} for `type {String.from(name)}`, which declares {layout.fieldCount()}")

			// A field declared with the ENCLOSING type's own name arrives as a `structRef`
			// (`Parser.enclosingSelfType`); every other struct-typed field arrives as a bare `named`, because
			// the declaration SWEEP that recorded it ran before anything could say what the name denoted.
			// Both spellings are one question, so both are asked here.
			if not tagMayNameAggregate(maxonTypeTag(declared)) 'namesNoAggregate'
				continue
			end 'namesNoAggregate'

			// LOAD-BEARING TWICE: it skips the slots that name no struct at all (a ranged alias, an enum, a
			// generic alias), and it is what proves the recursion's precondition.
			let fieldStruct = self.denotedSlotAggregateName(declared)
			if self.structTypes.contains(fieldStruct) and not self.structIsConstructibleWithin(fieldStruct, visiting: visiting, settled: settled) 'fieldCannotBeBuilt'
				return false
			end 'fieldCannotBeBuilt'
		end 'eachField'

		settled.insert(name)
		return true
	end 'structIsConstructibleWithin'

	// The `StringIndex` layout, for the parser's field reads (`slice`'s two byte positions, and the two
	// accessor arms). It PANICS rather than answering when the type is absent, because the declaration it
	// comes from is `stdlib/String.maxon`'s and that module is LISTED unconditionally — so a miss is not a
	// program that happens not to use indices, it is a corpus the loader failed to fold.
	//
	// ⚠ **THERE IS NO `registerStringIndexType` ANY MORE, AND ITS ABSENCE IS THE POINT (W49 wave 3).** The
	// sweep used to walk every token of every file looking for one of the four producer method names, purely
	// to decide whether to upsert a compiler-owned layout the compiler itself had written
	// (`Parser.atStringIndexProducer`, `StringIndexProducerBit`). The corpus declares the type, so the
	// ordinary declaration fold registers it in every program and there is nothing to decide — the whole
	// probe, its bit, and the second layout it wrote are gone.
	export function stringIndexLayoutOrPanic() returns StructLayout
		return try self.structTypes.get(StringIndexTypeName) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.stringIndexLayoutOrPanic: `{String.from(StringIndexTypeName)}` is not registered — it is declared by `stdlib/String.maxon`, which `StdlibLoader` lists in every program, so the declaration fold has not run or has dropped it")
	end 'stringIndexLayoutOrPanic'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ASSERTION `Project.ManagedMemorySlotCount` HAS OWED SINCE THE ENVELOPE COLLAPSE, PAID NOW
	// THAT BOTH DESCRIPTIONS EXIST IN EVERY COMPILE (W49 wave 9).** That constant's header recorded the debt
	// in so many words: *"the product is not yet checked against the record the runtime reads, because there
	// is nothing to check it against until `stdlib/String.maxon` is listed … the moment a corpus layout is
	// registered under that name, it and `StringRuntime`'s six offsets become two descriptions of one record
	// — and that assertion belongs with the listing"*. Both declaring modules are listed unconditionally now
	// (`StdlibLoader.whitelistedStdlibModules`), so the two descriptions are always both present, and this is
	// what holds them together. It is `managedFileLayout`'s check one type over, with the halves swapped:
	// there the compiler STATES the shape and the runtime writes it, here the CORPUS states it.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT CHECKS THE FIELD *COUNT*, AND THAT IS THE CLAUSE THAT EARNS THE FUNCTION.** The offsets are the
	// obvious half and the easy half; the dangerous half is a field the fused record has NO SLOT FOR.
	// `Parser.emitFusedWrapperLiteral` takes `managed` as its source and `singleByteGraphemesFlag` as its
	// flag and **passes on nothing else** — so a third field added to `type String`, or a second to
	// `type Character`, would be accepted by the parser, discarded at construction, and read back as whatever
	// the record's producer happened to write at its offset. `Parser.requireFusedWrapperTag`'s header carries
	// that defect MEASURED, for conformers of other NAMES: *"`Self{flag: false}` read back TRUE — exit 7 on a
	// program whose only literal wrote `false`, no fault, no leak, no diagnostic"*, with the drop and the
	// clone — both built from the DECLARED field list — then reading past the fused record's end
	// (0xC0000005). That refusal structurally cannot cover `String` and `Character`, because those are
	// exactly the two names it must ADMIT. This is their half of it.
	//
	// ⚠ **A PANIC, NOT A DIAGNOSTIC, AND `Project.requireCorpusDeclaredEnum` IS THE PRECEDENT.** What could
	// make it fire is an edit to a LISTED corpus module — never anything a user program can express — and
	// there is no degraded answer available: the compiler would go on minting a record shape the corpus no
	// longer declares.
	//
	// ⚠ **`Character`'s DECLARED SIZE IS 48 AND THE RECORD IT MINTS IS 56, AND THAT IS EXPLAINED RATHER THAN
	// CHECKED.** `type Character` declares the inline `managed` alone, so the layout's own `sizeBytes()` stops
	// at the whole buffer record, while the record `StringRuntime.emitAllocOwnedInlineString` builds for it is
	// `StringRecordBytes` wide — ONE encoder serves both wrappers and it always writes the flag at `@48`.
	// Nothing reads that slot for a Character, and nothing allocates one from `sizeBytes()`: a fused literal
	// routes to `__str_from_bytes`, never to `emitOwnedStructBox` (MEASURED — `sizeof(Character)` is 8, the
	// pointer a Character value IS, and `sizeof(String)` is `StringRecordBytes` off the byte-record path, so
	// neither reads this size). ⚠ W157 widened BOTH by one slot, so the eight bytes of slack are unchanged;
	// what must hold is the property that MAKES it inert: the field count.
	export function checkFusedWrapperRecordLayouts()
		// `String` — two fields: the inline `managed` occupying the whole buffer record, then the flag at `@48`.
		let string = self.corpusDeclaredFusedWrapper(StringConformerName)
		requireInlineManagedFieldFirst(string)
		if string.fieldCount() != StringDeclaredFieldCount 'stringFieldCount'
			panic("ProgramSignatures.checkFusedWrapperRecordLayouts: `type {String.from(StringConformerName)}` declares {string.fieldCount()} fields and the fused record has slots for {StringDeclaredFieldCount} — `emitFusedWrapperLiteral` passes on `managed` and `{String.from(SingleByteGraphemesFieldName)}` and NOTHING ELSE, so any further field is silently discarded at construction and read back as whatever the record's producer wrote at its offset")
		end 'stringFieldCount'

		let flagIndex = string.indexOfField(SingleByteGraphemesFieldName)
		if flagIndex >= string.fieldCount() 'noFlagField'
			panic("ProgramSignatures.checkFusedWrapperRecordLayouts: `type {String.from(StringConformerName)}` declares no `{String.from(SingleByteGraphemesFieldName)}` field, so `emitFusedWrapperLiteral` would classify every literal's bytes instead of using the flag the corpus states — the record's slot behind its embedded buffer record IS that field, and the compiler reads the corpus's spelling rather than inventing one")
		end 'noFlagField'
		if string.offsetOfField(flagIndex) != StringSingleByteGraphemesOffset 'flagOffset'
			panic("ProgramSignatures.checkFusedWrapperRecordLayouts: `type {String.from(StringConformerName)}` puts `{String.from(SingleByteGraphemesFieldName)}` at {string.offsetOfField(flagIndex)} but every producer and reader of the record uses {StringSingleByteGraphemesOffset} — the corpus's declared shape and the record shv2 mints have drifted")
		end 'flagOffset'
		if string.sizeBytes() != StringRecordBytes 'recordSize'
			panic("ProgramSignatures.checkFusedWrapperRecordLayouts: `type {String.from(StringConformerName)}` lays out {string.sizeBytes()} bytes but the record shv2 mints for it is {StringRecordBytes} — the corpus's declared shape and the record shv2 mints have drifted")
		end 'recordSize'

		// `Character` — the inline `managed` alone. See this function's header for why its declared size stops
		// short of the record it mints, and why the FIELD COUNT is what has to hold instead.
		let character = self.corpusDeclaredFusedWrapper(CharacterConformerName)
		requireInlineManagedFieldFirst(character)
		if character.fieldCount() != CharacterDeclaredFieldCount 'characterFieldCount'
			panic("ProgramSignatures.checkFusedWrapperRecordLayouts: `type {String.from(CharacterConformerName)}` declares {character.fieldCount()} fields and the fused record has a slot for {CharacterDeclaredFieldCount} — `emitFusedWrapperLiteral` takes `managed` and passes on nothing else, so any further field is silently discarded at construction")
		end 'characterFieldCount'
	end 'checkFusedWrapperRecordLayouts'

	// The corpus declaration behind one of the two byte records. Absent, this is not a program that happens
	// not to use strings — `StdlibLoader` lists both declaring modules unconditionally and
	// `listWhitelistedModule` throws `fileNotFound` if either is gone — so what reaches here is a declaration
	// deleted from a listed module. `stringIndexLayoutOrPanic`'s argument, two type names over.
	function corpusDeclaredFusedWrapper(typeName ByteArray) returns StructLayout
		return try self.structTypes.get(typeName) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.checkFusedWrapperRecordLayouts: this compile declares no `type {String.from(typeName)}`, and the compiler mints that type's record FROM the corpus's declaration rather than transcribing it — the module declaring it is listed by `StdlibLoader.whitelistedStdlibModules` and must still declare it")
	end 'corpusDeclaredFusedWrapper'

	// ⚠⚠ **`registerCharacterSetType` AND `characterSetLayout` STOOD HERE AND WENT AT W129, AND THE ONE
	// THING WORTH KEEPING IS WHY THE LAYOUT EXISTED AT ALL.** It was one half of a PAIR: the front end stated
	// the `CharacterSet` box's field offsets and size here, the `__cs_*` runtime wrote them through its own
	// constants, and the registration's whole value was the DRIFT CHECK holding the two descriptions together
	// (`managedFileLayout`/`managedDirectoryLayout` below are the same pair, still live, and their headers carry
	// the same argument). Nothing read the layout otherwise — a `CharacterSet` had no field access at all — so
	// once `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon` declared the type (W115) and the runtime went, both halves went together.
	//
	// ⚠ It also upserted `CharSet` into `genericAliases`. That copy was a FALLBACK rather than an authority, and
	// the measurement is `W115`'s review: `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon:19` declares the same alias and the CORPUS
	// declaration wins — rename every `CharSet` out of that file and a user's `CharSet from ['x','y']` still
	// compiled through the upsert, but leave the file declaring `typealias CharSet = Set with String` and the
	// same user program is REFUSED. ⇒ the two copies could not silently disagree; a disagreement is a
	// diagnostic. The NAME is still reserved — see `CharacterMemberSetAliasName`.

	// ⭐ **REGISTER THE COMPILER-OWNED `__ManagedFile` TYPE (R4.1)** — the last of these, called by the
	// declaration SWEEP when a file's tokens name a producer (`Parser.fileNamesManagedFileProducer`).
	// Idempotent, so the sweep is the WRITE and every parser read is a pure read.
	//
	// ⚠ It registers under the name the SOURCE SPELLS (`__ManagedFile`), where `CharacterSet` and
	// `StringIndex` registered under a reserved name distinct from their user-facing one — and both of those
	// are retired now. See `ManagedFileTypeName` for why the difference is the point rather than an
	// inconsistency: this type MUST be namable in a parameter and a field, and the corpus requires it.
	export function registerManagedFileType()
		// `__ManagedMemory` is an ALIAS, not a layout — see `ManagedMemoryTypeName`. Registered through the
		// SAME `genericAliases` map a user `typealias` folds into, exactly as `CharSet` was until W129, so a
		// `__ManagedMemory` annotation reaches the ordinary `Array` paths with nothing special-cased.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **"NOTHING SPECIAL-CASED" STOPS SHORT OF THE BUFFER SURFACE, AND A2j IS WHY.** The surface is
		// decided by PROVENANCE on a `ValueId` (`Parser.valueIsBufferSurface`), and this alias folds the
		// SPELLING away — so a value bound by a parameter, a struct field or a return type written
		// `__ManagedMemory` was indistinguishable from a `ByteArray` and took the `Array` surface. Measured
		// 2026-07-31: `function f(m __ManagedMemory)` with `m.push(7)`/`m.count()` compiled, linked and RAN,
		// while `m.length()` was refused as an unknown `Array` method — the roster exactly inverted.
		// ⇒ Each of those three declaration sites now carries the pre-erasure spelling to the site that binds
		// the value (`Parser.declaredSurfaceAt` / `markDeclaredSurface`); the return half's
		// column is `declaredReturnSurfaces` in THIS index, since a callee may be in another file. Type
		// resolution is untouched: `__ManagedMemory` still resolves to `Array with Byte`, which is what keeps
		// a `ByteArray` argument assignable to a `__ManagedMemory` parameter.
		// Upserted before the early return below because the two registrations are independent: the alias
		// must exist even on a second call that finds the layout already there.
		// ⚠ **THE BARE INSTANCE, DELIBERATELY (N2).** This entry has no declaring file — the compiler minted
		// it, not a source line — so it is the one generic alias whose element must stay UNSCOPED in the
		// table: `__ManagedMemory` means "a raw byte buffer as the READER's `Byte` is declared", and the
		// reader's spelling is applied at the read (`genericAliasInstanceFrom`). Scoping it here would pick
		// one file's `Byte` for every file in the program, which is the whole defect being repaired.
		self.genericAliases.upsert(ManagedMemoryTypeName, value: self.bareArrayByteInstance())

		// ⭐ **BOTH `__ManagedMemory` ELEMENT INSTANCES ARE INTERNED HERE, BY THE SWEEP (R4.4)** — the
		// machine-word one alongside the byte one, because `__ManagedMemory.create(n, elementSize: 8)` picks
		// it (`managedMemoryInstanceForElementSize`) and the parser may only READ the registry, never be the
		// first to write it. It is OVER-interned exactly as this whole probe is (a file merely NAMING
		// `__ManagedMemory` registers the layout), which is the sweep's standing discipline: over-register
		// rather than miss.
		_ = self.internBuiltinIntegerInstance(self.arrayRecordBaseName())

		if self.structTypes.contains(ManagedFileTypeName) 'alreadyRegistered'
			return
		end 'alreadyRegistered'
		self.structTypes.upsert(ManagedFileTypeName, value: managedFileLayout())
	end 'registerManagedFileType'

	// The `__ManagedFile` layout, held to the runtime's own constants by the same drift check
	// `managedDirectoryLayout` uses and for the same reason — the front end states the shape here, the runtime
	// writes the handle through `ManagedFileHandleOffset`, and two readers of one shape is the pair that
	// drifts. ⚠ Both references register an 8-byte handle struct whose runtime block is a DIFFERENT size,
	// with nothing tying the two; this check is what keeps that from being repeated here.
	function managedFileLayout() returns StructLayout
		var layout = StructLayout.createCompilerOwned(ManagedFileTypeName)
		layout.addField(ManagedFileHandleFieldName, declaredType: MaxonType.integer, mutable: true, tier: Visibility.file, defaultValue: FieldDefault.none, declaredSurface: sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface)

		if layout.offsetOfField(ManagedFileHandleFieldIndex) != ManagedFileHandleOffset 'offsetDrift'
			panic("managedFileLayout: the struct layout puts `_handle` at {layout.offsetOfField(ManagedFileHandleFieldIndex)} but the runtime reads it at {ManagedFileHandleOffset} — the front end's shape and the runtime's field access have drifted")
		end 'offsetDrift'
		if layout.sizeBytes() as ParsedInt != ManagedFileBoxBytes 'sizeDrift'
			panic("managedFileLayout: the struct layout is {layout.sizeBytes()} bytes but the runtime allocates {ManagedFileBoxBytes} — the box the runtime builds and the shape the front end states have drifted")
		end 'sizeDrift'
		return layout
	end 'managedFileLayout'

	// ⭐ **REGISTER THE COMPILER-OWNED `__ManagedDirectory` TYPE (R4.3)** — `registerManagedFileType`'s twin,
	// called by the declaration SWEEP when a file's tokens name it (`Parser.atManagedDirectoryProducer`).
	// Idempotent, so the sweep is the WRITE and every parser read is a pure read.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT INTERNS `Array with Byte`, AND THAT IS NOT REDUNDANT WITH THE FILE FAMILY'S UPSERT.**
	// `filename()` and `currentPath()` PRODUCE a `__ManagedMemory` without the program ever naming one:
	// `let cwd = try __ManagedDirectory.currentPath()` writes no `__ManagedMemory` token, no `b"…"` literal
	// and (until the next line) no `.toByteArray()`, so none of the sweep's existing byte-instance triggers
	// fires and the parser — which may only READ the registry — would have no instance to tag the result
	// with. Over-interned rather than missed, which is this sweep's standing discipline.
	// ⚠ The `__ManagedMemory` ALIAS itself is `registerManagedFileType`'s to upsert and is not repeated here:
	// a file that annotates one has written the name, which is what that probe matches on.
	export function registerManagedDirectoryType()
		_ = self.bareArrayByteInstance()

		if self.structTypes.contains(ManagedDirectoryTypeName) 'alreadyRegistered'
			return
		end 'alreadyRegistered'
		self.structTypes.upsert(ManagedDirectoryTypeName, value: managedDirectoryLayout())
	end 'registerManagedDirectoryType'

	// The `__ManagedDirectory` layout, held to the runtime's own constants by the same drift check
	// `managedFileLayout` uses and for the same reason. ⚠ The check earns more here than there: BOTH
	// references declare an 8-byte one-field struct while v1's runtime block is 40 bytes and the bootstrap's
	// is 336, with nothing tying any of the three together. This is the pair that keeps shv2's front-end
	// shape and its `__mm_alloc` from becoming a fourth combination.
	function managedDirectoryLayout() returns StructLayout
		var layout = StructLayout.createCompilerOwned(ManagedDirectoryTypeName)
		layout.addField(ManagedDirectoryBlockFieldName, declaredType: MaxonType.integer, mutable: true, tier: Visibility.file, defaultValue: FieldDefault.none, declaredSurface: sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface)

		if layout.offsetOfField(ManagedDirectoryBlockFieldIndex) != ManagedDirectoryBlockOffset 'offsetDrift'
			panic("managedDirectoryLayout: the struct layout puts `_block` at {layout.offsetOfField(ManagedDirectoryBlockFieldIndex)} but the runtime reads it at {ManagedDirectoryBlockOffset} — the front end's shape and the runtime's field access have drifted")
		end 'offsetDrift'
		if layout.sizeBytes() as ParsedInt != ManagedDirectoryBoxBytes 'sizeDrift'
			panic("managedDirectoryLayout: the struct layout is {layout.sizeBytes()} bytes but the runtime allocates {ManagedDirectoryBoxBytes} — the box the runtime builds and the shape the front end states have drifted")
		end 'sizeDrift'
		return layout
	end 'managedDirectoryLayout'

	// The `Set with Character` instance a `CharSet from [...]` constructs (P1.8 Slice D) — the
	// `builtinInstanceForElement` twin. Keyed as `MaxonType.character`, the SAME arg a
	// `typealias CharSet = Set with Character` interns, so the instance a `from` literal builds and the one the
	// corpus alias names share ONE gid and are type-identical. Idempotent.
	// ⚠ A `CharacterSet`'s member-set FIELD was interned through this too, while the compiler owned that layout
	// (retired W129); the corpus declaration interns it now, through the ordinary alias path.
	export function setInstanceForCharacter() returns GenericInstanceId
		var args = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		args.push(MaxonType.character)
		return self.genericInstances.intern(self.typeNames.intern(SetBuiltinBaseName), args: args)
	end 'setInstanceForCharacter'

	// --- Synthesized TUPLE types (P1.10) ---
	//
	// ⭐ **THE PARSER IS THE ONLY PRODUCER, AND IT REGISTERS INTO THIS INDEX DIRECTLY.** The other
	// compiler-owned layouts (`__ManagedFile`, `__ManagedDirectory`) are registered by the declaration SWEEP,
	// because a TOKEN scan can see the name that produces one. A tuple's identity is its ELEMENT TYPES, and
	// those are known only where the expression is parsed — `let t = (10, 32)` is two `integer`s and a token
	// scan cannot say so — so the sweep cannot pre-register the set. The registration is nevertheless safe on
	// exactly the terms that make the sweep-then-read discipline work elsewhere: the layout is a PURE FUNCTION
	// of the name (which is a pure function of the element types), and the write is idempotent, so no ordering
	// between files, and no interleaving of a parallel fan-out, can produce two different answers.
	//
	// The parser records the SAME layout into its `FileParseArtifact` as well, so `mergeArtifact` commits it
	// to `project.structTypes` — the registry `installStructDestructors` walks. Two registries, exactly as a
	// DECLARED `type` lives in both and for the reason `StructLayout`'s header states.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE STANDING OBLIGATION THAT COMES WITH THAT, FOR WHOEVER BUILDS THE INCREMENTAL DRIVER.** The two
	// registries are rebuilt on DIFFERENT triggers — `project.structTypes` at every merge (from artifacts
	// that may be cache HITS), this index whenever any file's bytes change — and a tuple minted only inside a
	// function BODY is registered here by the PARSE. So a rebuild in which a body-only edit to one file
	// leaves another file's parse memoized would hand `installStructDestructors` a layout this index has
	// never seen, and its `structHasManagedField(name)` would panic. A DECLARED tuple is never missing: the
	// sweep re-runs `parseTypeReference` over every declaration on every rebuild.
	//
	// It is UNREACHABLE today and was measured so, not assumed: nothing runs the full pipeline twice on one
	// `Project` — `build` and each spec-test worker construct a fresh one, and `verify-warm-rebuild` drives
	// `queryAllModule` alone for its cache and invalidation probes (`compileColdOnce` takes its own
	// `Project`). The fix when that changes is to derive the destructor installer's per-field questions from
	// the layout it ALREADY HOLDS instead of re-asking this index by name; a restoration pass that no code
	// path can exercise would be the unread-index shape the query spine's header warns about.

	// ⭐ **INTERN THE TUPLE TYPE OF `elements`, and answer with its MANGLED NAME.** The one door a tuple type
	// is minted through — a `(a, b)` literal, a `(T, U)` type annotation, a `typealias Pair = (…)` — so every
	// spelling of one tuple type lands on ONE layout.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT CANONICALIZES, AND ONLY ONCE THE WHOLE PROGRAM IS FOLDED.** A ranged alias collapses to its
	// underlying primitive, which is what makes `sum(t (Integer, Integer))` accept the `(10, 32)` a caller
	// built — the identity rule the bootstrap gets from `IrType.Resolve` (`MlirType.cs:292`). It cannot be
	// done during the SWEEP: `Integer`'s own declaration may be below the function, or in a file not yet
	// folded, so the alias registry has no answer yet. A sweep-minted name is therefore the SOURCE spelling
	// (`__Tuple2.Integer.Integer`), and `adoptType` — the ONE door this index's types cross out of — rewrites
	// it to the canonical one at every read. That is the identical arrangement `declaredSlotType`'s three
	// re-tags already live under, and for the identical reason.
	// ⚠ **THE ELEMENT REJECTIONS FIRE ONLY IN THE REAL PARSE, and that is the standing sweep discipline
	// rather than a hole.** During the sweep a generic-instance / function / inner alias is still a bare
	// `named` (its registry is being built), so the canonical form the rejections are asked about does not
	// exist yet — a sweep-time verdict would be decided by file order. The sweep therefore records whatever
	// spelling it sees, the real parse asks the question with the whole program in hand, and a program that
	// fails it is rejected before anything downstream reads the tuple. The sweep's spelling is registered but
	// never referenced, exactly like the instances `internLiteralContainerInstances` over-interns.
	//
	// ⚠ **`slotScopeFilePath` IS THE FILE THAT WROTE THE ELEMENT LIST, and it is what makes a tuple's identity
	// a statement about the program rather than about the filesystem (A3v).** Every spelling reaching here —
	// a `(a, b)` literal, a `(T, U)` annotation, a `typealias Pair = (…)` — was written in exactly one file,
	// and an element naming a CONTESTED generic alias means what that file means by it. See
	// `tupleStructLayout` for why the minted layout carries it too.
	export function internTupleType(elements MaxonTypeArray, from TypeNameInterner, slotScopeFilePath FilePath) returns ByteArray throws TupleElementRejection
		var interned = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		for i in 0 upto elements.count() 'eachElement'
			let adopted = self.adoptTypeFrom(try elements.get(i) otherwise panic("internTupleType: elements.get OOB at {i} below count {elements.count()}"), from: from)
			if not self.allFilesFolded 'tolerantSweep'
				interned.push(adopted)
				continue
			end 'tolerantSweep'

			try requireTupleElementType(adopted, position: i)
			let canonical = self.canonicalTupleElement(adopted, slotScopeFilePath: slotScopeFilePath)
			try self.requireSeparatorFreeElementName(canonical, position: i)
			interned.push(canonical)
		end 'eachElement'

		return self.registerTupleLayout(interned, declFilePath: slotScopeFilePath)
	end 'internTupleType'

	// ⭐ **THE INJECTIVITY ARGUMENT, ENFORCED AT THE JOIN THAT RELIES ON IT** — `witnessTableLabel`'s
	// discipline, asked where it is spent rather than argued above it. EVERY canonical element renders to a
	// token holding no separator, so splitting the join on `.` recovers the element list and two distinct
	// element lists cannot spell one name.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE NESTED-TUPLE EXEMPTION IS GONE, AND ITS REMOVAL IS THE POINT (W9).** A nested tuple used to be
	// admitted with its whole separator-bearing name, self-delimiting by the arity in front of it — the same
	// inlining that made a DAG of tuple types cost like a tree. It now renders as a `.`-free
	// `tupleElementTokenFor`, so the rule that was "every element but one" is simply every element.
	function requireSeparatorFreeElementName(element MaxonType, position FieldIndex) throws TupleElementRejection
		let rendered = self.tupleElementName(element)
		if rendered.contains(TupleElementSeparator) 'separatorInName'
			throw TupleElementRejection.separatorInElementName(position, elementName: rendered)
		end 'separatorInName'
	end 'requireSeparatorFreeElementName'

	// Register the layout for an already-interned, already-canonical element list and answer its name.
	// Idempotent — a second mint of the same tuple type is a `contains` hit that builds nothing.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FIRST MINTER'S FILE IS THE ONE THE LAYOUT KEEPS, and `sweepScopedTupleName` is what makes that
	// exact rather than a race.** A CANONICAL element list holds resolved types, so every file that reaches
	// that name means the same thing by it and the path is never consulted to say otherwise — one layout,
	// one file, no ambiguity. A SWEEP spelling still holds bare `named`s, so it is keyed per file and there
	// is again exactly one minter.
	function registerTupleLayout(elements MaxonTypeArray, declFilePath FilePath) returns ByteArray
		let name = self.sweepScopedTupleName(self.tupleTypeNameOf(elements), declFilePath: declFilePath)
		if self.structTypes.contains(name) 'alreadyRegistered'
			return name
		end 'alreadyRegistered'

		self.structTypes.upsert(name, value: tupleStructLayout(name, elements: elements, declFilePath: declFilePath))
		self.recordTupleElementToken(name)
		return name
	end 'registerTupleLayout'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE INJECTIVITY LEDGER — where the digest's one assumption stops being assumed (W14b).** The token
	// itself is computed, not allocated: see `tupleElementTokenFor` for what it is, why it is bounded, why it
	// is a pure function of the program and why it is 64 bits wide. What this door adds is the record of WHICH
	// name each live token belongs to, the refusal to hand one token to two names, and the memo that keeps
	// every later reader from re-deriving what was just derived here.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE SAME GUARD THE MINT-ORDERED ORDINAL CARRIED, RE-DERIVED FOR THE NEW KEY, not a new one.**
	// That version tested `tupleElementTokens.contains(name)` and rested on the FORWARD map alone: with the
	// map's own size as the ordinal, a second mint under one name would have handed TWO layouts ONE token. The
	// invariant it protected is unchanged — two registered layouts must never share one citation, because two
	// element lists spelling one enclosing name is precisely the non-injective join `TupleElementSeparator`
	// exists to bar, with the drop cascade taken from whichever registered last. What changed is how it can be
	// VIOLATED: a computed token repeats not by counting twice but by two distinct names digesting alike, and
	// that question can only be asked of the REVERSE map, which is why one exists.
	//
	// **The two maps are written in ONE act**, which is the property both readers rest on: a name in the
	// forward map has had its token checked, and a token in the reverse map has an owner. A divergence stops
	// the compile at the next read rather than silently repeating a citation.
	//
	// The collision arm cannot fire in practice (`tupleElementTokenFor` carries the probability), and it is
	// written anyway because the alternative is a silent wrong answer of exactly the kind this file's join
	// rules exist to make impossible.
	function recordTupleElementToken(name ByteArray)
		let token = tupleElementTokenFor(name)
		if let owner = try self.tupleElementTokenOwners.get(token) 'tokenAlreadyOwned'
			panic("ProgramSignatures.recordTupleElementToken: the element token `{String.from(token)}` already cites `{String.from(owner)}` and registering `{String.from(name)}` would give the same token a second owner. If the two names DIFFER this is a genuine 64-bit digest collision between two distinct tuple types, and one of them would be cited by a name the other owns. If they are the SAME name the maps have diverged, because `registerTupleLayout` returns early for a name `structTypes` already holds. Either way two layouts would share one citation, which is the non-injective join `TupleElementSeparator` exists to bar")
		end 'tokenAlreadyOwned'

		self.tupleElementTokens.upsert(name, value: token)
		self.tupleElementTokenOwners.upsert(token, value: name)
	end 'recordTupleElementToken'

	// The element token `name` is cited by. Two questions, two probes, and NEITHER allocates: that the layout
	// is registered at all (the panic W9 wrote, kept in substance), and that the token leads back to the very
	// name it is filed under.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SECOND PROBE IS AN ASSERTION, NOT A SECOND COLLISION DETECTOR — THIS HEADER CLAIMED OTHERWISE
	// AND IT WAS FALSE (W14b review).** It read that the probe "catches a collision at the read too, which is
	// what a memo alone could not give". There is nothing left to catch: `recordTupleElementToken` panics
	// BEFORE it writes either map when a token is already owned, and those two `upsert`s are the ONLY writers
	// of either map in the compiler, so `tupleElementTokens[n] = t` implies `tupleElementTokenOwners[t] = n`
	// by construction and both arms below are unreachable on every program, legal or not. They are kept for
	// what an assertion is for: the day a SECOND writer of either map appears, this fails loudly at the next
	// citation instead of handing one type a name another type owns. Do not cite them as the reason the
	// digest is injective — `recordTupleElementToken` is that reason, and it is one function away.
	function tupleElementTokenOf(name ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		let token = try self.tupleElementTokens.get(name) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.tupleElementTokenOf: the nested tuple `{String.from(name)}` has no element token — every tuple `structRef` in the compiler names a layout `registerTupleLayout` minted, and that is where its token is recorded with it")
		let owner = try self.tupleElementTokenOwners.get(token) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.tupleElementTokenOf: the element token `{String.from(token)}` filed under `{String.from(name)}` has no owner — `recordTupleElementToken` writes both maps in one act, so this is a divergence between them")
		if not owner.equals(name) 'aDigestCollision'
			panic("ProgramSignatures.tupleElementTokenOf: the element token `{String.from(token)}` filed under `{String.from(name)}` is owned by `{String.from(owner)}` — two distinct tuple types share one token, so citing this one would name the other. See `tupleElementTokenFor` for the width that argument rests on")
		end 'aDigestCollision'

		return token
	end 'tupleElementTokenOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE TYPE-PARAMETER INJECTIVITY LEDGER (W14) — `recordTupleElementToken`'s twin, one digest over.**
	// The token itself is computed rather than allocated (`typeParamTokenFor` carries what it is and why it is
	// 64 bits); what this adds is the record of WHICH declaration each live token belongs to, and the refusal
	// to let two distinct parameters share one identity.
	//
	// ⭐ **CALLED ONCE PER DECLARED PARAMETER, AT THE FOLD, BY EXACTLY TWO DOORS** — `recordStruct` for a
	// `type`'s `uses` list and `recordInterfaceDeclaration` for an `interface`'s associated types. Those are
	// the two declarations `Parser.parseTypeReference` can mint a `typeParameter` inside (an extension body
	// borrows its TARGET's list, which is one of them), and both fold before any file is parsed — so the map
	// is complete and READ-ONLY by the time the parse fan-out asks it.
	//
	// ⚠ **A RE-RECORD OF THE SAME PARAMETER IS NOT A COLLISION, AND TELLING THE TWO APART IS THE WHOLE JOB.**
	// Two files may declare `type Box uses T` — that is E3006's business, reported with a position, and it
	// must not become a compiler panic here. So a repeat that names the same `(declaring type, parameter)` is
	// kept as it stands (the FIRST record wins, and the two agree on everything a reader asks except possibly
	// a position the duplicate declaration itself disagrees about). A repeat naming a DIFFERENT parameter is a
	// genuine 64-bit collision: two type parameters would share one identity, one instance key and one
	// diagnostic name — precisely the defect W14 exists to remove — so it STOPS the compile.
	function recordTypeParamToken(declaringTypeName ByteArray, paramName ByteArray, position TypeParamPosition)
		let token = typeParamTokenFor(declaringTypeName, paramName: paramName)
		let owner = TypeParamOwner.create(declaringTypeName, paramName: paramName, position: position)
		if let existing = try self.typeParamOwners.get(token) 'tokenAlreadyOwned'
			if not existing.namesTheSameParameterAs(owner) 'aDigestCollision'
				panic("ProgramSignatures.recordTypeParamToken: type parameter `{String.from(paramName)}` of `{String.from(declaringTypeName)}` digests to the same token as `{String.from(existing.paramName)}` of `{String.from(existing.declaringTypeName)}`. This is a genuine 64-bit digest collision between two DISTINCT type parameters, and admitting it would give them one identity — one `GenericInstanceRegistry` key for `Array with` each of them, and one entry in the display index, which is exactly the defect W14 removed. See `typeParamTokenFor` for the width this rests on")
			end 'aDigestCollision'

			return
		end 'tokenAlreadyOwned'

		self.typeParamOwners.upsert(token, value: owner)
	end 'recordTypeParamToken'

	// ⭐ **WHAT MINTED `token` — the door for a caller holding NOTHING BUT THE TOKEN (W14).** An instance query
	// reads a `typeParameter` off a base struct's field column or a shared method signature and has no
	// declaration in hand; this is where it learns whose parameter that is and at which position, in one
	// lookup. `typeParamPositionIn` is the OTHER direction, for a caller that DOES hold the declaration and
	// therefore needs no registry at all (see there for why both must exist).
	//
	// Absence is a THROW, not a panic, because one of its two callers is allowed to ask about a token it may
	// not own: `anyInstanceTypeArgHasKind` answers `false` for a token no declaration filed, for the reason a
	// base with no instance bucket answers `false` — nothing instantiates it, so no instantiation classifies
	// anything. The other caller, `opaqueTypeParamPosition`, treats absence as an invariant break and says so
	// with its own `otherwise panic`.
	// ⚠ This header used to justify the throw by naming `Parser.isEnclosingTypeParam` as the tolerant caller.
	// **That function does not call this one** — it re-spells the token against the enclosing declaration's
	// own names through `typeParamPositionIn`, which is the whole reason both doors exist — so the reason was
	// true of the design and false of the code beside it (W14 review).
	export function typeParamOwnerOf(token TypeParamId) returns TypeParamOwner throws MapError
		return try self.typeParamOwners.get(token)
	end 'typeParamOwnerOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **ONE SWEEP KEY PER (SPELLING, FILE) — because a sweep spelling is NOT a whole-program identity and
	// a canonical name is (A3v).** A canonical name is derived from RESOLVED elements, so every file that
	// reaches it means the same thing by it; that property is what every reader of a tuple type rests on and
	// nothing here touches it. A sweep spelling is the opposite: its elements are still the bare names the
	// source wrote, because the registries that say what those names MEAN are being built while the sweep
	// runs. So `__Tuple2.Bag.int` written in two files whose `Bag` declarations disagree is two types wearing
	// one key — and the layout that key holds carries ONE declaring file, which is what
	// `resolveRecordedGenericAliasTypes` rescopes its elements against and what `canonicalTupleName` walks
	// them with.
	//
	// ⛔ MEASURED with the key shared: the file that folded SECOND could not construct its own field —
	// `E3005: cannot assign '__Tuple2.Array_String.int' to variable 'Holder.q' of type
	// '__Tuple2.Array_Num.int'`. A wrong answer decided by directory order.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE FIRST MINTER KEEPS THE PLAIN KEY, AND THE SUFFIX IS AN ORDINAL RATHER THAN THE PATH.** Both
	// halves are corrections of a version that qualified every sweep key with `declFilePath`, and each has
	// its own reason:
	//   • **A PATH IS NOT A LEGAL ELEMENT-NAME FRAGMENT.** A sweep key can be embedded as an ELEMENT of
	//     another tuple's name (`canonicalTupleName`'s A3e re-entry does exactly that), and the element join
	//     is `.` — which every path contains. It is also host-dependent, so it would print differently on
	//     every machine.
	//   • **THE FIRST MINTER MUST BE UNSUFFIXED**, because that same re-entry is the one place a sweep key
	//     reaches a user-visible type name. MEASURED: with every key suffixed,
	//     `tuples/error.self-referential-tuple-alias` rendered `…P.Int#<fragment>…` in its E3005. A single
	//     file spelling a tuple — which is that case, and the overwhelming common one — now takes the plain
	//     key and no message moves.
	//
	// The join is `overloadRegistrationName`'s for `paramDefaultHelperName`'s reason: `#` appears in no
	// source identifier and in no canonical element name, so the key is unspellable, `isTupleTypeName` still
	// recognizes it by its intact prefix, and the digits cannot hold the element separator.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ORDINAL PROBE IS THE SECOND ONE IN THE COMPILER, and the other is `Parser`'s
	// `unclaimedRegistrationName`** — same join (`overloadRegistrationName`), different registry, different
	// "taken" test, and a deliberately different FIRST candidate: an overload name is always suffixed, a
	// tuple key is not (see the ⚠⚠ block above). The one fact the two share — how an ordinal is joined onto a
	// name — is already spelled once, in `overloadRegistrationName`, which both call; what is left is four
	// lines of loop around two different questions, so they are not merged. A change to the JOIN belongs
	// there and reaches both.
	function sweepScopedTupleName(name ByteArray, declFilePath FilePath) returns ByteArray
		if self.allFilesFolded 'aCanonicalIdentity'
			return name
		end 'aCanonicalIdentity'

		if self.tupleKeyIsAvailableTo(name, declFilePath: declFilePath) 'theFirstMintOfThisSpelling'
			return name
		end 'theFirstMintOfThisSpelling'

		// Every candidate this walk passes over is held by a layout of ANOTHER file, so the registry itself
		// bounds the search: there cannot be more taken candidates than there are registered layouts.
		for claimed in FirstLaterFileTupleOrdinal upto self.structTypes.count() + 1 'eachLaterFilesMint'
			let candidate = overloadRegistrationName(name, suffix: "{claimed}".toByteArray())
			if self.tupleKeyIsAvailableTo(candidate, declFilePath: declFilePath) 'free'
				return candidate
			end 'free'
		end 'eachLaterFilesMint'

		panic("ProgramSignatures.sweepScopedTupleName: every candidate key for the sweep spelling `{String.from(name)}` through #{self.structTypes.count()} is held by another file's layout, which cannot happen — each taken candidate occupies a registered layout of its own, so the walk is bounded by the registry's own size")
	end 'sweepScopedTupleName'

	// Is `candidate` a key this file may register its tuple layout under — free, or already this file's own
	// mint? A second mint of one spelling by one file is the ordinary case (a `typealias` and a field
	// annotation spelling the same element list), and it must land on that file's existing layout rather
	// than on a fresh suffix.
	function tupleKeyIsAvailableTo(candidate ByteArray, declFilePath FilePath) returns bool
		let existing = try self.structTypes.get(candidate) otherwise return true
		return existing.declFilePath.equals(declFilePath)
	end 'tupleKeyIsAvailableTo'

	// `__Tuple<N>.<e0>.<e1>…` — the mangled name of a tuple over `elements`. See `TupleElementSeparator` for
	// why the arity leads and why the join is `.`.
	function tupleTypeNameOf(elements MaxonTypeArray) returns ByteArray
		var out = tupleTypeNameHead(elements.count())
		for element in elements 'eachElement'
			out.append(TupleElementSeparator)
			out.append(self.tupleElementName(element))
		end 'eachElement'
		return out.toByteArray()
	end 'tupleTypeNameOf'

	// The mangled spelling of ONE canonical element. `mangleTypeArg` — the index's structural, build-stable
	// renderer — answers every tag but two.
	//
	// ⭐ **A NESTED TUPLE IS CITED BY ITS TOKEN, NEVER BY ITS NAME (W9)** — see `tupleElementTokenFor` for the
	// asymptote that buys and for why a digest of the nested name is a complete identity here. The token's
	// OWNER is on record by the time it is asked for: a nested element is a REGISTERED layout, and the only
	// two producers of one — a `(…)` written inside a `(…)`, which the parser interns innermost-first, and
	// `canonicalTupleStructRef`, which registers the canonical form before handing back its `structRef` —
	// both register before the enclosing name is built.
	//
	// ⚠ **A `function`-TYPED ELEMENT IS THE OTHER EXCEPTION, because `mangleTypeArg` renders every function
	// type as the bare tag name `function`.** That is correct for a descriptor label (a code pointer is a code
	// pointer) and WRONG for an identity: `(UnaryOp, int)` and `(BinaryOp, int)` would spell one name, and a
	// value of one would then be accepted where the other is declared. A function type carries its governing
	// alias NAME, so that is what rides here.
	function tupleElementName(element MaxonType) returns String
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(element)
		if tagIsStructRef(tag) 'aStructRef'
			let name = self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(element))
			if isTupleTypeName(name) 'nestedTuple'
				return String.from(self.tupleElementTokenOf(name))
			end 'nestedTuple'
		end 'aStructRef'

		if tag == ValueTypeTag.function 'functionAlias'
			return String.from(self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(element)))
		end 'functionAlias'
		return self.mangleTypeArg(element)
	end 'tupleElementName'

	// ⭐ **THE CANONICAL FORM OF ONE TUPLE ELEMENT — what a tuple's IDENTITY is keyed on.**
	//
	// It routes through `declaredSlotType`, the SAME door a struct FIELD's declared type comes out of, so a
	// tuple element and a field of the same declared type can never be classified differently — a function
	// alias becomes `function`, a generic-instance alias becomes `genericInstance`, a ranged float alias
	// becomes `float`. Then a ranged INT alias collapses through `canonicalizeRangedAlias` (which
	// `declaredSlotType` deliberately leaves alone, because a `named` int alias carries its range and
	// signedness for the shift rule), and an INNER ranged alias (`Wrapper.Idx`) collapses the same way — the
	// only name form in the language that can hold the element separator, so resolving it is what keeps the
	// join injective rather than merely usually-injective.
	//
	// A `named` denoting a declared `type` is re-tagged `structRef` by `declaredSlotType` itself, so the
	// tuple's field type routes to the struct's own drop — and, unlike the copy of that rule that used to
	// stand at the bottom of this function, it then reaches `canonicalTupleStructRef` and a NESTED-tuple
	// element spelled by name canonicalizes like every other `structRef`. A `named` denoting an enum/union
	// stays `named`, which is the tag a union value carries.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AN ELEMENT IS RESOLVED IN THE FILE THAT SPELLED IT, AND THIS DOOR USED TO RESOLVE IT AS A STRANGER
	// (A3v).** The old convention read: a tuple is compiler-synthesized, so it has no honest file, so every
	// element resolves as a file declaring none of the name would. That is true of the tuple's IDENTITY and
	// false of its element SPELLINGS — `(Bag, Num)` is written in one file, and where `Bag` is a contested
	// generic alias the stranger's reading is a THIRD answer, agreeing with neither declaration. ⛔ MEASURED:
	// a file declaring `Bag = Array with Num` and `Pair = (Bag, Num)` could not construct its own field —
	// `E3005: cannot assign '__Tuple2.Array_Num.int' to variable 'Keeper.p' of type
	// '__Tuple2.Array_String.int'`, at the declaring file's own line, naming another file's `Bag`.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE CANONICAL NAME STAYS A WHOLE-PROGRAM KEY, and per-file resolution is what makes it one.** The
	// key is derived from the RESOLVED elements, so two files that mean the same thing by `(Bag, Num)` walk to
	// identical element types and therefore to one name — a tuple stays STRUCTURAL wherever the language says
	// the spellings agree. Two files that mean different things by it get two types, which is not a split of
	// one type but the two types the program always had. `__ManagedMemory` inside a tuple therefore means what
	// the SPELLING'S OWN FILE means by `Byte`, exactly as the same spelling in a struct field does.
	function canonicalTupleElement(element MaxonType, slotScopeFilePath FilePath) returns MaxonType
		let resolved = self.canonicalizeRangedAlias(self.declaredSlotType(element, into: self.typeNames, slotScopeFilePath: slotScopeFilePath))
		if maxonTypeTag(resolved) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return self.canonicalTupleStructRef(resolved)
		end 'notNamed'

		let name = self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(resolved))
		if self.isInnerAlias(name) 'innerRangedAlias'
			match self.innerAliasOf(name) 'inner'
				declared(alias) then return alias.underlying
				undeclared then panic("canonicalTupleElement: `isInnerAlias('{String.from(name)}')` is true but `innerAliasOf` answered undeclared — the two read one registry")
			end 'inner'
		end 'innerRangedAlias'
		return resolved
	end 'canonicalTupleElement'

	// ⭐ **THE `MaxonType`-LEVEL TWIN OF `canonicalNameOfStruct`: a tuple `structRef` under a SWEEP spelling
	// answered as the canonical one, everything else itself.** One derivation, two readers — a NESTED tuple
	// ELEMENT (`canonicalTupleElement`, whose recursion this terminates: each step strips one nesting level
	// off a type written in finite source) and a generic instance's stored ARGUMENT (`containerElementType`).
	// Both ask the same question about the same tag, and the `allFilesFolded` gate that says whether a
	// canonical form exists yet lives once, in `canonicalNameOfStruct`, rather than at each asker.
	function canonicalTupleStructRef(element MaxonType) returns MaxonType
		if not tagIsStructRef(maxonTypeTag(element)) 'notStruct'
			return element
		end 'notStruct'

		let name = self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(element))
		let canonical = self.canonicalNameOfStruct(name)
		if canonical.equals(name) 'alreadyCanonical'
			return element
		end 'alreadyCanonical'
		return MaxonType.structRef(self.typeNames.intern(canonical))
	end 'canonicalTupleStructRef'

	// ⭐ **THE CANONICAL TUPLE NAME FOR A NAME THE SWEEP MINTED.** Re-canonicalizes the registered layout's
	// element list now that every declaration is folded, registers the canonical layout if it is new, and
	// MEMOIZES — so the rewrite `adoptType` performs at every read costs one map hit after the first.
	// A name that is already canonical maps to itself, which is what makes the memo cover both directions.
	export function canonicalTupleName(name ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		if let memoized = try self.tupleCanonicalNames.get(name) 'alreadyDerived'
			return memoized
		end 'alreadyDerived'

		// ⚠⚠ **SEEDED WITH ITSELF BEFORE THE WALK — THE MEMO IS ALSO THIS WALK'S RE-ENTRY MARKER (A3e).**
		// Every other element this walk descends into strips one nesting level off a type written in finite
		// source, which is the argument `canonicalTupleStructRef` terminates on. A tuple-ALIAS element does
		// not: `declaredSlotType` resolves it to its target, and an alias NAME can name the very tuple being
		// canonicalized — `typealias P = (P, Int)` registers `__Tuple2.P.int`, whose element 0 is `named("P")`,
		// whose target is `__Tuple2.P.int`. Re-entry therefore resolves that element to the SWEEP spelling it
		// already carries, which is the answer it had before the element door could follow an alias at all, and
		// the infinite type is refused downstream by the return check instead of by the stack running out. The
		// real answer replaces the seed below, and a walk that never re-enters never reads it.
		self.tupleCanonicalNames.upsert(name, value: name)

		let layout = try self.structTypes.get(name) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.canonicalTupleName: `{String.from(name)}` is not registered — every tuple `structRef` in the compiler was minted by `internTupleType`, which registers the layout before it hands the name back")
		// ⭐ **THE SCOPE FILE COMES OFF THE LAYOUT, because a SWEEP-minted spelling still holds bare `named`
		// elements and this is the walk that resolves them (A3v).** It is the file that wrote the element list
		// — see `tupleStructLayout` — and it rides on to the canonical layout so a re-entrant walk scopes the
		// same way the first one did.
		var canonical = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		for element in layout.fieldTypes 'eachElement'
			canonical.push(self.canonicalTupleElement(element, slotScopeFilePath: layout.declFilePath))
		end 'eachElement'

		let canonicalName = self.registerTupleLayout(canonical, declFilePath: layout.declFilePath)
		self.tupleCanonicalNames.upsert(name, value: canonicalName)
		return canonicalName
	end 'canonicalTupleName'

	// Record `typealias <name> = (<elements>)` (P1.10). Written by BOTH the declaration sweep and the real
	// parse from the same reader, exactly as every other alias registry is; the value is whichever spelling
	// the writing pass could produce, and `tupleAliasTarget` canonicalizes on the way out.
	export function recordTupleAlias(name ByteArray, tupleName ByteArray)
		self.tupleAliases.upsert(name, value: tupleName)
	end 'recordTupleAlias'

	// Is `name` a TUPLE typealias? THE hot-path guard `parseTypeReference` asks of every bare type name, so
	// it must allocate nothing (`Map.contains`) — the `isGenericAlias` twin.
	export function isTupleAlias(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.tupleAliases.contains(name)
	end 'isTupleAlias'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE NAME A DECLARED POSITION'S `named` TYPE ACTUALLY DENOTES — a tuple typealias answered as the
	// tuple's own canonical name, every other name as itself (A3e). THE one home of that resolution.**
	//
	// A tuple alias mints no identity, so `Pair` is not a registered anything: `containsStruct` says no,
	// `enumOf` says no, and every classifier that reads a RAW layout field type (`typeIsManaged`, the drop
	// callee, the clone strategy, the deep-clone gate) reached its `undeclared` arm on it. It arrives under
	// the alias spelling exactly when the declaration SWEEP recorded the position before the alias's own file
	// was walked — which is a fact about the filesystem's walk order and not about the program.
	//
	// ⚠ It is written ONCE and asked at each classifier rather than resolved into the layout, because those
	// classifiers are deliberately allocation-free over the index's own interner (see `structFieldIsManaged`)
	// and a rewritten layout would be a second copy of the declaration that could drift from the first.
	export function denotedAggregateName(name ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		return self.tupleAliasTarget(name) if self.isTupleAlias(name) else name
	end 'denotedAggregateName'

	// **CAN A RAW SLOT TYPE'S TAG NAME A DECLARED AGGREGATE?** — a `structRef` (what a resolved reference
	// carries, and what `Parser.enclosingSelfType` mints for a type's own name) or a bare `named` (what the
	// declaration SWEEP records, having run before anything could say what the name denoted). Every other tag
	// names no struct, union or tuple, so the resolution below has nothing to look up.
	//
	// ⭐ **WRITTEN ONCE BECAUSE FOUR DOORS ASK IT AND HAD COME TO SPELL IT FOUR WAYS** — `tagIsStructRef(tag)
	// or tag == ValueTypeTag.named`, each of its two De Morgan negations, and `tag == ValueTypeTag.named or
	// tag == ValueTypeTag.structRef`. `Parser.tagCarriesStructLayout` is the same shape for the same reason and
	// its header states the cost of the alternative: a third tag taught to one spelling and not the others is
	// a wrong answer at whichever door was not updated, with no compile error at either.
	//
	// ⚠ `typeIsManaged` deliberately does NOT ask it. Its `structRef` arm has already returned `true` (fused
	// with `tagIsByteRecord`/`genericInstance`) by the time it tests `named`, so its remaining test is
	// "is this the ONE tag still undecided?" — a different question that happens to mention the same tags.
	export function tagMayNameAggregate(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
		return tagIsStructRef(tag) or tag == ValueTypeTag.named
	end 'tagMayNameAggregate'

	// **THE DECLARED NAME A RAW SLOT TYPE DENOTES, over THIS index's own interner** — a tuple typealias
	// answered as the tuple's own canonical name, every other name as itself. Precondition:
	// `tagMayNameAggregate(maxonTypeTag(t))`, which is what makes the id a name's.
	//
	// ⭐ The `denotedAggregateName(nameOfId(…))` pair was open-coded at four doors — the type-argument size,
	// the type-argument ownership classifier, the deep-clone gate and the constructibility walk — one of them
	// spelling `nameOfId`'s body out again with a second panic string for the identical invariant. The comment
	// at `typeArgIsOwned` says these must not diverge and records what it cost when they did (a silent wrong
	// `sizeof`, then a false E2015 on a program the other walk order ran); a shared call is that rule enforced
	// rather than restated.
	//
	// ⚠ **`typeIsManaged` IS NOT ONE OF THEM, AND MUST NOT BECOME ONE.** It takes the interner as a PARAMETER
	// (`from`) because a type that crossed into a file-local artifact table has to be read against THAT table;
	// its header records both directions in which resolving it against `self.typeNames` instead was measured
	// to give a silent wrong answer.
	function denotedSlotAggregateName(t MaxonType) returns ByteArray
		return self.denotedAggregateName(self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(t)))
	end 'denotedSlotAggregateName'

	// The tuple type `name` aliases, CANONICAL. Precondition: `isTupleAlias(name)`.
	export function tupleAliasTarget(name ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		return self.canonicalNameOfStruct(try self.tupleAliases.get(name) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.tupleAliasTarget: '{String.from(name)}' is not a tuple typealias — every caller asks `isTupleAlias` first"))
	end 'tupleAliasTarget'

	// A tuple's field type ADOPTED into `into` — `fieldTypeOf`'s answer for every element at once, which is
	// what the parser needs to build the artifact's own copy of the layout (`remapArtifact` rewrites an
	// artifact layout's field types in place, so the index's copy may never be shared with it).
	export function adoptTupleLayout(name ByteArray, into TypeNameInterner) returns StructLayout
		let layout = try self.structTypes.get(name) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.adoptTupleLayout: `{String.from(name)}` is not registered — every caller reached here with a name `internTupleType` handed back")
		var adopted = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		for element in layout.fieldTypes 'eachElement'
			adopted.push(self.adoptType(element, into: into))
		end 'eachElement'
		return tupleStructLayout(name, elements: adopted, declFilePath: layout.declFilePath)
	end 'adoptTupleLayout'

	// ⭐ **THE MIRROR OF `adoptType`: re-intern an id-bearing type INTO this index's own table.** `adoptType`
	// is the door this index's ids leave by; this is the one they arrive by, and the parser is its only caller
	// (a tuple's element types are minted against the FILE's interner and the layout registered here must not
	// carry a foreign id — the ID PROBLEM in this file's header). By NAME, never by raw id, exactly as its
	// mirror is.
	function adoptTypeFrom(t MaxonType, from TypeNameInterner) returns MaxonType
		return match t 'r'
			named(id) gives MaxonType.named(self.typeNames.intern(foreignNameOf(from, id: id)))
			structRef(id) gives MaxonType.structRef(self.typeNames.intern(foreignNameOf(from, id: id)))
			function(id) gives MaxonType.function(self.typeNames.intern(foreignNameOf(from, id: id)))
			// Everything else carries no per-file interner id — a primitive, a `String`/`Character` (no name
			// at all), a type-parameter DIGEST (a pure function of source text since W14, global for that
			// reason rather than for the numbering reason a position was) and a whole-program
			// `GenericInstanceId` — so it crosses unchanged, exactly as `adoptType`'s own arms state.
			boolean to float gives t
			exitCode to unresolved gives t
			string gives t
			typeParameter gives t
			genericInstance gives t
			character gives t
			// An existential carries the INTERFACE's per-file interner id, so it arrives by NAME exactly as
			// `named`/`structRef`/`function` do.
			interfaceRef(id) gives MaxonType.interfaceRef(self.typeNames.intern(foreignNameOf(from, id: id)))
		end 'r'
	end 'adoptTypeFrom'

	// --- Generic-instance queries (P1.6-A) ---

	// Is `name` a declared generic-instance TYPEALIAS (`IntBox`)? THE hot-path guard the parser asks of
	// every type reference and every qualified-call base, so it must allocate nothing (`Map.contains`).
	export function isGenericAlias(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.genericAliases.contains(name)
	end 'isGenericAlias'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE FILE-LESS DOOR, AND IT REFUSES A CONTESTED NAME RATHER THAN GUESSING (N3).** Precondition:
	// `isGenericAlias(name)`.
	//
	// Its callers are the ones that structurally cannot name a reader: the drop/clone cascades and the
	// layout-field probes, which read an instance's SHAPE (is it an `Array`? is its element managed? what is
	// its base?) off a struct FIELD's declared type. A field's type is recorded by the declaration SWEEP, as a
	// bare `named("Slots")` — the alias is not interned yet — and a `StructLayout` reaching those free
	// functions has no path travelling with it.
	//
	// ⚠ **N2's ANSWER TO "WHY IS A BARE ANSWER SAFE HERE?" DOES NOT SURVIVE N3, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A WILD
	// FREE.** N2 could say a range qualifier changes only WHICH DECLARATION of one element name is meant —
	// never the tag, the base or the managed-ness — so the bare answer and the scoped one were the same
	// answer. Two files' `typealias Bag` share nothing of the kind: one may be an `Array with String` and the
	// other an `Array with Num`, so the bare last-wins answer would route a buffer of integers to
	// `__destruct_Array_String` and free each element as a pointer. That is why `settleGenericAliasContest`
	// resolves every RECORDED field type against its own declaring file — and why a spelling it missed must
	// arrive here as a loud panic rather than as a plausible id.
	//
	// ⚠ **A CALLER THAT CANNOT NAME A READER *AND* CANNOT BE HANDED A LIVE SPELLING ASKS
	// `genericAliasSpellingIsSuperseded` FIRST** — the reader-free CLASSIFIERS, which whole-program walks drive
	// over an append-only registry that still holds pre-settle instances. That is not a bypass of this refusal;
	// it is the one case where the refusal is right and the caller must nevertheless not stop. See there.
	export function genericAliasInstance(name ByteArray) returns GenericInstanceId
		if self.contestedGenericAliases.contains(name) 'contestedAndThisDoorNamesNoReader'
			panic("ProgramSignatures.genericAliasInstance: '{String.from(name)}' is declared by two files over different instances, and this door names no reader to choose between them — `settleGenericAliasContest` resolves every recorded declared type against its declaring file, so a spelling reaching here is one that pass does not cover")
		end 'contestedAndThisDoorNamesNoReader'

		return self.bareGenericAliasInstance(name)
	end 'genericAliasInstance'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS A GENERIC-ALIAS SPELLING ONE THE CONTEST HAS ALREADY REPLACED — i.e. is the instance carrying it
	// SUPERSEDED? (A3k)** Precondition: `isGenericAlias(name)`.
	//
	// **THIS IS THE OTHER HALF OF `genericAliasInstance`'s REFUSAL, and it exists because a WHOLE-PROGRAM WALK
	// legitimately meets what that door legitimately refuses.** An instance's TYPE ARGUMENT is stored once, by
	// whichever pass first met `Box with Bag`, and the declaration SWEEP meets it before any contest can be
	// known — so `args[0]` is a bare `named("Bag")`. `settleGenericAliasContest` then re-interns every
	// declaration and every instantiation site SCOPED (`fileScopedArg` rewrites exactly such a `named` to that
	// file's mint), and the real parse resolves every written `Bag` through `genericAliasInstanceFrom`; the
	// registry is APPEND-ONLY, so the pre-settle instance stays in the by-base bucket beside its scoped
	// replacements. It is the registry's MEMORY of a spelling, not an instantiation the program has —
	// `IrModule.ops`' orphans under `liveOpIndices` are the same shape and the same rule.
	//
	// ⚠ **A CLASSIFIER THAT MEETS ONE MUST CONTRIBUTE NOTHING, NOT GUESS.** The two declarations of `Bag`
	// genuinely differ, and the reader-free classifiers (`typeArgIsOwned`, `typeSupportsDeepClone`,
	// `typeArgIsCoOwnedTrivial`) are read by disjunctions over instantiations, where the scoped siblings in the
	// same bucket already carry the real answers. Each therefore returns ITS OWN identity for "classifies
	// nothing" — `false` for "nothing is consumed through a spelling no call site can name", `true` for
	// "nothing is refused for a spelling no copy site can name" — which is the answer the same program written
	// in ONE file gives, verified both ways.
	//
	// ⛔ **THREE DOORS, AND THE THIRD WAS MISSED BECAUSE ITS IDENTITY IS NOT ITS OWN (A3k review).**
	// `typeArgIsCoOwnedTrivial` is `typeIsManaged and not typeArgIsOwned`, so guarding only the OWNED half left
	// it voting `true` — the manufactured CO-OWNED TRIVIAL kind, off a spelling no call site can name. The
	// identity a caller needs is a property of the FOLD (`anyInstanceTypeArgHasKind` is an OR, so it is
	// `false`), never of the door's own polarity; see there for the program it wrongly refused.
	//
	// ⚠ **MEASURED (A3k), and it is not hypothetical**: two files declaring `typealias Bag = Array with
	// <different element>` plus a `typealias BB = Box with Bag` reached `genericAliasInstance`'s refusal from
	// `isManagedOpaqueTypeParamField` AND from `noteDestructorUsage`'s opaque-element drop rooting — two
	// unrelated whole-program walks — while the single-file program compiles. The `Container with Bag`
	// deep-clone twin reaches it from `requireOpaqueArrayCopyable`.
	function genericAliasSpellingIsSuperseded(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.contestedGenericAliases.contains(name)
	end 'genericAliasSpellingIsSuperseded'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME QUESTION ASKED OF A TYPE RATHER THAN A NAME (A3k review)** — for the one classifier door
	// that has no `name` in hand because it is a COMPOSITION of two others, `typeArgIsCoOwnedTrivial`. It is
	// the SAME fact and not a second one: it bottoms out in `genericAliasSpellingIsSuperseded` above, which is
	// the only reader of `contestedGenericAliases` for this purpose, so a change to what "superseded" means
	// cannot reach one door and miss the other. A tag that cannot name an alias at all answers `false` without
	// touching the interner, exactly as `typeArgIsOwned`'s own `tagMayNameAggregate` early-out does.
	function typeArgSpellingIsSuperseded(t MaxonType) returns bool
		if not tagMayNameAggregate(maxonTypeTag(t)) 'cannotNameAGenericAlias'
			return false
		end 'cannotNameAGenericAlias'

		let name = self.denotedSlotAggregateName(t)
		return self.isGenericAlias(name) and self.genericAliasSpellingIsSuperseded(name)
	end 'typeArgSpellingIsSuperseded'

	// The raw map read, with no contest test — the tier `scopedGenericAliasInstance` falls through to, and
	// the one `genericAliasInstance` guards. Split so the guard has exactly one bypass and it is named.
	function bareGenericAliasInstance(name ByteArray) returns GenericInstanceId
		return try self.genericAliases.get(name) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.bareGenericAliasInstance: '{String.from(name)}' is not a generic-instance alias — every caller asks isGenericAlias first")
	end 'bareGenericAliasInstance'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE INSTANCE A GENERIC-INSTANCE ALIAS NAMES, AS `readerFilePath` MEANS IT (N2 + N3)** — the door
	// the PARSER asks through, at every one of its alias resolutions, so a file's `__ManagedMemory`, its
	// `ByteArray.create()` and its `b"…"` literal cannot come to mean three different strides, and so its own
	// `typealias Slots = Array with Big` is what `Slots` means in it.
	//
	// It is a composition and not a third rule: WHICH DECLARATION is `scopedGenericAliasInstance`'s answer,
	// and WHOSE contested names are spelled `fileScopedInstance`'s way. Both are identities in a program with no
	// contest, which is every program in the corpus.
	export function genericAliasInstanceFrom(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns GenericInstanceId
		return self.fileScopedInstance(self.scopedGenericAliasInstance(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath), filePath: readerFilePath)
	end 'genericAliasInstanceFrom'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH DECLARATION OF `name` DOES `readerFilePath` MEAN? (N3)** — the reader's OWN file's
	// declaration first, then one the reader may actually NAME, and only then anyone else's. The identical
	// three tiers `RangedAliasRegistry.lookup` resolves a ranged alias by, applied to the other alias registry
	// a cross-file reference is resolved through, and each tier is there for the reason stated at that door:
	//
	//   1. a file that declares the name means its OWN declaration, whatever anyone else exported;
	//   2. a file that declares none of it resolves to a declaration it may WRITE DOWN — a plain `typealias`
	//      is file-local, so one the reader is forbidden to name cannot be the one it meant;
	//   3. and only when NO declaration of the name is nameable from anywhere does it fall back to the bare
	//      last-wins entry, which is the state the "declared, but hidden from you" diagnostics rest on.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT NARROWS THE LAST-WINS SET; IT DOES NOT ABOLISH IT**, exactly as `lookup` does not: two files that
	// both `export` one generic alias still answer last-wins, on the strictly smaller set the reader may name.
	// That is the E3063 ambiguity case, and its ranged twin was a rung of its own
	// (`ProgramSignatures.ambiguousAliasCandidates`) because choosing between two LEGAL candidates needs the
	// module-directory precedence rules and a diagnostic. What is fixed here is only the case where a
	// candidate was never a candidate.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN UNCONTESTED NAME COSTS ONE MISS ON AN EMPTY MAP AND ALLOCATES NOTHING** — in particular
	// `readerFilePath.toString()` is built only after the contest probe HITS, which is never in the corpus.
	// That is `RangedAliasRegistry.lookup`'s discipline and it matters for the same reason: this is asked at
	// every generic-alias type reference and every `IntArray.create()` base.
	function scopedGenericAliasInstance(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns GenericInstanceId
		if let byFile = try self.contestedGenericAliases.get(name) 'contested'
			if let own = try byFile.get(readerFilePath.toString()) 'readersOwnFile'
				return own
			end 'readersOwnFile'

			if let nameableHere = try self.nameableGenericAliases.get(name) 'someDeclarationIsNameable'
				return nameableHere
			end 'someDeclarationIsNameable'
		end 'contested'

		return self.bareGenericAliasInstance(name)
	end 'scopedGenericAliasInstance'

	// The base STRUCT name a generic-instance id refers to (`Box` for `Box with Integer`) — what
	// `Parser.parseQualifiedCall` rewrites `IntBox.create` to `Box.create` through. The instance's
	// ARGUMENT types are not exposed: at this trivial slice a member type is never SUBSTITUTED (a `T`
	// stays opaque), so nothing reads them out — the checks that need them (arity, managed) read them
	// straight off the parser-local `MaxonType` list at the instantiation site.
	export function genericInstanceBaseName(id GenericInstanceId) returns ByteArray
		return self.nameOfId(self.genericInstances.instanceBaseId(id))
	end 'genericInstanceBaseName'

	// ⭐ **IS INSTANCE `id` A GREEN-THREAD HANDLE RATHER THAN A BOX (P1.5-B2c)?** The one base whose values
	// own no `__mm_alloc` record — see `PromiseType.maxon`, which carries the whole argument. It lives HERE,
	// beside the base-name read, because `typeIsManaged` and `classifyUnionPayload` are both in this file and
	// both must answer it identically; the free function `typeIsPromiseStorage` is the same question asked of
	// a `MaxonType` rather than of an id, and it routes through this.
	export function instanceIsPromiseHandle(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return self.genericInstanceBaseName(id).equals(PromiseTypeName)
	end 'instanceIsPromiseHandle'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT AN INSTANCE DENOTES IN A TYPE POSITION — AND OVER AN `interface` BASE THAT IS AN
	// EXISTENTIAL, NOT A BOX.** `Seq with Integer` names the fat pointer `Seq`, held with `Element` bound;
	// `Box with Integer` names `Box`'s box. Both are written `Base with Args`, and only the base's FORM tells
	// them apart, so the split lives here rather than at each of the three type-resolution doors that mint an
	// instance (`Parser.parseTypeReference`'s alias arm and its inner-alias arm, `Parser.resolveNamedAlias`).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE `with` ARGUMENTS ARE NOT CARRIED ON THE EXISTENTIAL, AND THAT IS THE MECHANISM RATHER THAN A
	// GAP.** An interface this program DISPATCHES through has exactly ONE binding per associated type across
	// the whole program — that is E3119, and it is forced by dictionary-passing: the shared body is compiled
	// once, against one type, and every other conformer reinterprets those bits. Producing an existential IS
	// such a dispatch. So the binding is a function of the interface NAME, the conformances are where the
	// program states it (`StructLayout.conformanceBindings`, folded by `associatedAbiBindingOf`), and a copy
	// riding on the type would be one fact written twice with nothing keeping the two honest. What a use site
	// writes is therefore a CLAIM, verified whole-program by
	// `ConformanceCheck.checkInterfaceInstantiationBindings` (E3125) — never a second source of truth. The one
	// argument shape that is REFUSED outright rather than checked is one naming another interface: see
	// `Parser.DeclaredStoragePosition.associatedTypeBinding`, which is a second witness table and not a claim.
	//
	// ⚠ **`into` IS THE READER'S OWN INTERNER**, exactly as `interfaceNameOfType`'s is: the parser holds its
	// file's, this index holds its own, and an id minted in one is meaningless in the other.
	export function instanceDenotedType(gid GenericInstanceId, into TypeNameInterner) returns MaxonType
		let base = self.genericInstanceBaseName(gid)
		// `isInterfaceName`, NOT a bare `interfaces.contains`: it is the ONE door, and it answers for the
		// SYNTHESIZED protocols (`Stringable`, `Hashable`, …) as well as for the user registry.
		if isInterfaceName(self.interfaces, name: base) 'anInterfaceInstance'
			return MaxonType.interfaceRef(into.intern(base))
		end 'anInterfaceInstance'

		return MaxonType.genericInstance(gid)
	end 'instanceDenotedType'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE INSTANCE A BARE GENERIC TYPE NAME DENOTES INSIDE A TYPE-PARAMETER SCOPE** — `Inner`
	// written in `type Outer uses T`'s body IS `Inner with Outer.T`. shv2 compiles ONE body per generic type,
	// so a member of `Outer` that names another generic type without arguments names it at the parameters the
	// body itself is written in: the arguments are not ABSENT, they are the enclosing scope's. It is the
	// declaration-view sibling of `genericAliasInstanceFrom` — that door answers for a name a `typealias`
	// bound to CONCRETE arguments, this one for a name the SCOPE binds.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE BINDING IS BY PARAMETER NAME, ALL-OR-NOTHING, AND THAT IS v1's RULE VERBATIM**
	// (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/TypeResolution.maxon:4358-4385`, `promoteBareGenericReturnToScope`): each of
	// the named type's own `uses` names must be one the scope declares, or nothing is minted at all. The
	// MEMBERSHIP test is the caller's (`Parser.scopeBindsEveryTypeParam`) because only the caller holds the
	// scope; this door mints, and mints only. v1 writes that algorithm out THREE times
	// (`TypeResolution.maxon:4358`, `LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:16198` and `:7065`, the last two its own comments
	// call mirrors); one copy is the whole reason it lives in the index rather than at a use site.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ARGUMENT AT POSITION `i` CITES THE SCOPE PARAMETER OF THE SAME SPELLING, NEVER POSITION `i` OF
	// THE SCOPE.** Since W14 a parameter's identity is a digest of `(declaring type, parameter name)`, so the
	// argument list is built by looking each of the BASE's names up in the scope — a positional pairing would
	// bind `Inner`'s sole `T` to whatever the enclosing type happens to declare first, which for
	// `type MyMap uses Key, Value` naming a one-parameter helper is a silently wrong type.
	//
	// ⚠ No `fixedSize`: a sized base (`Vector`) is a BUILTIN with no `StructLayout`, so it never reaches the
	// mint — the gate is `structTypeParamsOf`, which answers the shared empty for every name no `type`
	// declares.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THREE SCOPES ASK IT AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME SCOPE**, which is why the scope arrives as a pair of
	// parameters rather than being read off any one of them:
	//   • the DECLARATION the cursor is inside — `Parser.bareGenericInstanceInScope`, for a written type
	//     reference (`returns Inner`) and a static-call base (`Inner.make(…)`);
	//   • the CALLEE's declaring type — `Parser.promoteBareGenericResultToCalleeScope`, because a call is typed
	//     from the token SWEEP's `returnTypeOf`, which records every bare generic name as a plain `named`;
	//   • the FIELD's declaring struct — `fieldTypeOf`, for the same reason one layer down: the layout this
	//     index holds is the SWEPT one;
	//   • the callee's declaring type AGAIN, from the SURFACE side — `declarationViewInstanceOf`, which needs
	//     the same instance the second asker mints and reads it a pass earlier, off the receiver's own base.
	// v1 writes that algorithm out three times over (`TypeResolution.maxon:4358`, `LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:16198`
	// and `:7065`, the last two its own comments call mirrors); one door with the scope as an argument is that
	// trio with the mirrors removed.
	export function scopedGenericInstanceOf(name ByteArray, declaringTypeName ByteArray, scopeParamNames ByteArrayArray) returns ScopedGenericInstance
		// ⛔ **THE SCOPE'S OWN TYPE MEANS `Self`, NOT AN INSTANCE OVER ITS OWN PARAMETERS**, and that holds for
		// all three askers at once: the type's own name in its own body, a `Self`-returning method reached
		// through a receiver of that type, and a field of the very type declaring it. It is
		// `Parser.parseTypeReference`'s ARM ORDER — `namesEnclosingType` answers well above the scoped arm —
		// stated once for the doors that have no arms to order. ⚠ MEASURED without it
		// (`self-type-member-expression/static-call-through-Self-in-a-generic-type`): `Self.make(x)` inside
		// `type Box uses T` minted `Box with Box.T` and its result stopped satisfying `twice() returns Self` —
		// **`E3005: Cannot return 'Box_Tae9ed0372d6f16dc' from function declared to return 'Box'`**.
		if name.equals(declaringTypeName) 'ownNameMeansSelf'
			return ScopedGenericInstance.notInScope
		end 'ownNameMeansSelf'

		// ⚠ **GATED ON `allFilesFolded` for `Parser.parseTypeReference`'s stated reason**: the struct registry
		// is one the declaration SWEEP is still filling, so an ungated answer would depend on the order the
		// loader walked the disk. During the sweep every one of these names stays the bare `named` it always
		// was — which is exactly the answer the READ doors above exist to repair.
		if not self.allFilesFolded 'registryStillFilling'
			return ScopedGenericInstance.notInScope
		end 'registryStillFilling'

		// A NON-GENERIC declared type, and every name no `type` declares at all, answer the shared empty here —
		// so the whole generics-free corpus pays one map probe and allocates nothing.
		let baseParams = self.structTypeParamsOf(name)
		if baseParams.isEmpty() or not scopeBindsEveryTypeParam(baseParams, scopeParamNames: scopeParamNames) 'notBoundByThisScope'
			return ScopedGenericInstance.notInScope
		end 'notBoundByThisScope'

		return ScopedGenericInstance.instance(self.instanceOverScopeParams(name, scopeOwner: declaringTypeName, params: baseParams))
	end 'scopedGenericInstanceOf'

	// The mint the door above ends in, and the one below IS. `params` are the BASE's own `uses` names and
	// `scopeOwner` is whose parameters they cite — see the door's header for why those are two arguments and
	// not one, and why a positional pairing would be a silently wrong type.
	function instanceOverScopeParams(name ByteArray, scopeOwner ByteArray, params ByteArrayArray) returns GenericInstanceId
		var args = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		for paramName in params 'eachParam'
			args.push(MaxonType.typeParameter(typeParamTokenFor(scopeOwner, paramName: paramName)))
		end 'eachParam'

		return self.genericInstances.intern(self.typeNames.intern(name), args: args)
	end 'instanceOverScopeParams'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT `Self` DENOTES INSIDE A GENERIC CONTAINER WHOSE RECORD THE COMPILER ALREADY OWNS (W57)** —
	// the instance over the declaration's OWN parameters, which is exactly what the door above refuses to
	// build at its `ownNameMeansSelf` guard.
	//
	// ⚠ **THAT GUARD IS NOT BEING WEAKENED; ITS PREMISE SIMPLY DOES NOT HOLD FOR THIS ONE SHAPE.** The guard
	// says the type's own name in its own body means `Self`, and `Parser.enclosingSelfType` answers `Self`
	// with the base `structRef` — correct for an ordinary generic, whose body is compiled ONCE over the base
	// and whose results are retyped at the call site (`retypeGenericAliasConstructorResult`). A container the
	// compiler owns the RECORD of has no base box to be a `structRef` to: `Array with T` and
	// `__ManagedMemory with T` are one `GenericInstanceId` (`canonicalGenericBaseName`), so a value of it
	// inside the body is the buffer record, dropped by `__managed_decref` and not by any struct cascade. Answering
	// `structRef` there would be the byte-record defect `Parser.requireFusedWrapperTag` documents, one
	// genericity up: bytes of one record, identity of another.
	//
	// Precondition: `declarationIsTheManagedRecord` below, which is what establishes that the name is declared and
	// generic — so `structTypeParamsOf` is non-empty here and the instance has the arity its base was checked at.
	export function ownRecordSelfInstance(name ByteArray) returns GenericInstanceId
		return self.instanceOverScopeParams(name, scopeOwner: name, params: self.structTypeParamsOf(name))
	end 'ownRecordSelfInstance'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS `name`'s DECLARATION THE `__ManagedMemory` RECORD THIS COMPILER ALREADY OWNS (W57, its SHAPE
	// half made real by ARR5)?** — still TWO facts, but the second one is now a question about the FIELD
	// rather than about the field's name.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SHAPE HALF USED TO BE A QUESTION ABOUT A NAME, AND THAT IS WHAT COULD BE WRONG.** It asked
	// `isArrayBaseName(name)` and then only that the declaration had a SOLE field which was
	// `inlineManagedFieldIndex() == 0` — and that index is a question about the field's NAME (`managed`) and
	// the declaration's MARKER, never about the field's TYPE (`StructLayout.isInlineManagedField`). So a
	// `type Array uses Element implements BuiltinArrayLiteral` whose sole field is `var managed as Num`
	// answered TRUE, and `Parser.emitFusedWrapperLiteral`'s identity arm handed the raw `Num` back as the
	// array — bytes of one record, identity of another, which is the very hazard
	// `Parser.requireFusedWrapperTag` documents. MEASURED on this tree before the change: that program was
	// refused only by the accident of a later type compare, as
	// **`E3005 … Cannot return 'int' from function declared to return 'struct'`**, a sentence about a
	// RETURN in a program whose fault is a field.
	//
	// ⇒ **THE SHAPE IS NOW: a SOLE field, and that field a `__ManagedMemory` under a declaration generic
	// enough to be one.** `firstFieldIsItsOwnBuffer` below is everything except the SOLE, split out because
	// `Parser.emitFusedWrapperLiteral` needs it that way — a declaration that has that field AND a second one
	// is the record with something extra bolted on, which earns its own positioned refusal rather than the
	// "the compiler owns no record for this name" sentence a stranger meets.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE NAME HALF STAYS, AND ARR5 MEASURED WHY IT CANNOT LEAVE YET.** It is not "is this declaration
	// called `Array`" — it is "is this declaration the one `canonicalGenericBaseName` FOLDS `__ManagedMemory`
	// ONTO", and today that fold's target is a constant, so the two coincide. Drop it and a stranger's
	// `type Bag uses E implements BuiltinArrayLiteral` whose sole field is `__ManagedMemory with E` passes
	// the shape — but its field's instance is `Array with E`, the RECORD declaration's, not `Bag with E`, so
	// the identity arm hands back a value of a type `Self` is not and the clean positioned refusal
	// `Parser.requireFusedWrapperTag` gives it today degrades to an `E3005` about a return. The half that
	// CAN be structural has been made structural; the other half becomes so on the day the fold learns its
	// target from the conformance rather than from a constant, and then this line is the one that goes.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT LIVES HERE, NOT IN THE PARSER, BECAUSE THE INDEX ITSELF HAS TO ASK IT (W59).** It began as
	// `Parser.arrayNameIsItsOwnRecord`, serving the construction doors; `typeParamOwnershipProtocol` then needed
	// the same fact to decide whether a DESCRIPTOR carries ownership words, and a second spelling of "is this
	// declaration the record?" is exactly how the construction door and the descriptor stamp would come to
	// disagree about one type — with the disagreement spending itself as a double free rather than a diagnostic.
	export function declarationIsTheManagedRecord(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return match self.structOf(name) 'declaration'
			declared(layout) gives layout.fieldCount() == 1 and self.firstFieldIsItsOwnBuffer(layout)
			undeclared gives false
		end 'declaration'
	end 'declarationIsTheManagedRecord'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS DECLARATION'S FIRST FIELD A `__ManagedMemory`, UNDER A DECLARATION GENERIC ENOUGH TO BE
	// ONE?** — the shape half of the predicate above, and the ADMISSION gate of
	// `Parser.emitFusedWrapperLiteral`'s identity arm.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT ASKS THE DECLARED SURFACE AND NOT THE FIELD'S `MaxonType`, AND THAT IS FORCED BY *WHEN* IT IS
	// ASKED.** The same question arrives at three different resolution stages — the descriptor pre-scan
	// (`Parser.scanDescriptorNeedsInto`), the declaration sweep (through `Parser.enclosingSelfType`) and the
	// real parse — and the column holds a different thing at each. MEASURED on `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s own
	// shape: at the pre-scan `fieldTypes[0]` is still `named('Array.ElementMemory')`, the INNER alias, because
	// only a TOP-LEVEL generic alias is retyped by `resolveRecordedGenericAliasTypes`. A test over the
	// resolved instance therefore answers "how far the index had got", which is the class of bug
	// `Parser.declaredSurfaceAt` records for the tuple mask. The SURFACE is recorded from the SOURCE at
	// declaration time and reads the same at all three (`SurfaceBitIsBuffer`, A2j/W43).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TYPE-PARAMETER CLAUSE IS WHAT KEEPS A NON-GENERIC CONFORMER OUT.** The record is generic over
	// its element by construction, so a `type Holder implements BuiltinArrayLiteral` whose field is the BARE
	// `__ManagedMemory` — a genuine buffer surface — is still not it, and must go on meeting
	// `Parser.requireFusedWrapperTag`'s sentence
	// (`array-declared-record.error.a-conformer-of-another-name-is-still-refused` pins that boundary).
	//
	// ⚠ **WHAT IT DOES NOT SEPARATE, NAMED RATHER THAN LEFT UNSAID**: a generic declaration whose field is
	// the bare `__ManagedMemory` (i.e. `Array with Byte`) also has the buffer surface, so it is admitted
	// here and refused one step later by the type compare against `Self`
	// (`Cannot return 'Array_Byte' from function declared to return 'Array_T…'`, MEASURED). That is a
	// REFUSAL with a poor noun, not a miscompile: the value's bytes and its type agree, they simply are not
	// the element the declaration promised. Separating it needs the alias's ARGUMENT, which is recorded on
	// `GenericAliasEntry` and has no by-name reader today.
	export function firstFieldIsItsOwnBuffer(layout StructLayout) returns bool
		if not self.isArrayBaseName(layout.name) or self.structTypeParamsOf(layout.name).count() != ArrayTypeParamCount 'notTheRecordDeclaration'
			return false
		end 'notTheRecordDeclaration'

		return self.declaredFieldsBeginWithABuffer(layout)
	end 'firstFieldIsItsOwnBuffer'

	// ⭐ **DOES THIS DECLARATION'S FIRST FIELD NAME A `__ManagedMemory` IN THE SOURCE?** — the surface half
	// above, on its own, because `Parser.requireArrayMarkerDeclaresTheBuffer` asks exactly the NEGATION of it
	// and must not re-spell it: that refusal's whole sentence is "your first field is not a buffer", and a
	// second reading of "is it?" is how a refusal comes to be raised about a declaration that satisfies the
	// door it was refused by.
	export function declaredFieldsBeginWithABuffer(layout StructLayout) returns bool
		// ⚠ **TWO HALVES, AND DROPPING EITHER IS A WRONG ANSWER.** `inlineManagedFieldIndex` is the LAYOUT
		// half — is field 0 the one the envelope collapse embeds WHOLE, which it decides from the field's
		// name and the declaration's marker (`StructLayout.isInlineManagedField`). The surface is the TYPE
		// half — did the source name a `__ManagedMemory` there. A field named anything else has an 8-byte
		// POINTER slot however it is typed, so an identity on it would hand back a buffer where the record
		// holds a pointer to one; and a `managed` of any other type is the defect this predicate exists to
		// close.
		// ⚠ A FIELDLESS declaration answers 0 from `inlineManagedFieldIndex` too (it returns the count), and
		// `fieldSurfaceOf` panics on an index its layout does not have — so the count is tested first.
		if layout.fieldCount() == 0 or layout.inlineManagedFieldIndex() != 0 'field0IsNotEmbeddedWhole'
			return false
		end 'field0IsNotEmbeddedWhole'

		return surfaceIsBuffer(self.fieldSurfaceOf(layout, index: 0))
	end 'declaredFieldsBeginWithABuffer'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS `id` THE INSTANCE `declarationName` MAKES OVER ITS OWN PARAMETERS — i.e. what `Self` denotes
	// inside it? (W57)** The RECOGNIZER for what `ownRecordSelfInstance` MINTS, and it is a separate function
	// because its caller may not intern: `IrInterface.renderDeclaredTypeName` runs inside the conformance
	// check and re-spells a type against a declaration's own names rather than looking one up, exactly as its
	// `typeParameter` arm already does. Minting there to compare would put a write on a read path.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ARGUMENTS ARE CHECKED AND NOT ONLY THE BASE NAME.** `type Array uses Element` may perfectly
	// well mention an `Array with Byte`, which is NOT `Self` — rendering that as the bare declaration name
	// would make a genuinely different signature compare equal, a wrong ACCEPTANCE with nothing to report it.
	export function instanceIsDeclarationSelf(id GenericInstanceId, declarationName ByteArray, paramNames ByteArrayArray) returns bool
		if not self.genericInstanceBaseName(id).equals(declarationName) 'anotherBase'
			return false
		end 'anotherBase'

		let args = self.genericInstances.instanceArgs(id)
		if args.count() != paramNames.count() 'anotherArity'
			return false
		end 'anotherArity'

		for i in 0 upto paramNames.count() 'eachArg'
			let arg = try args.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.instanceIsDeclarationSelf: instanceArgs.get OOB at {i} — the count was just compared")
			let paramName = try paramNames.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.instanceIsDeclarationSelf: paramNames.get OOB at {i} — the count was just compared")
			if maxonTypeTag(arg) != ValueTypeTag.typeParameter or typeNameIdOf(arg) != typeParamTokenFor(declarationName, paramName: paramName) 'notItsOwnParameter'
				return false
			end 'notItsOwnParameter'
		end 'eachArg'

		return true
	end 'instanceIsDeclarationSelf'

	// --- The `Array` builtin generic (P1.7 slice 1) ---
	//
	// `Array` is a BUILTIN generic type with one type parameter `Element` — there is no `type Array uses
	// Element` declaration (shv2 does not read stdlib; the managed-memory runtime is synthesized like String's). So
	// its instances (`Array with Integer`) intern like any other, but they have no base `StructLayout`, and
	// every `genericInstance*` method that would read one (`baseLayoutOf`, box size, per-instance drop) must
	// special-case them. The box is a FIXED 48-byte record (`ManagedRecordBytes`, see ManagedMemoryRuntime), not
	// `fieldCount·8`. The one home of "is this instance an `Array`?" so no site respells the base name.

	// Is `name` the builtin `Array` type — the base of every array instance.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A CONTESTED `Array` MOVED INTO THE RESERVED SPACE IS STILL THAT BASE NAME (F31).** When a user
	// program declares its own `type Array`, `contestStdlibTypeName` renames the CORPUS declaration to
	// `__Array` so the user's spelling wins the bare name — and this predicate, keyed on the spelling alone,
	// then answered FALSE about `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s own declaration. MEASURED: **`E2015 … '__Array'
	// implements 'BuiltinArrayLiteral' … shv2 mints that record only for the two names it owns the record FOR`**
	// at `stdlib/Array.maxon:114`, i.e. the compiler refusing the very file whose record it owns, in 21 cases.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SPELLING MOVED; THE IDENTITY DID NOT, AND THE IDENTITY IS WHAT THIS ANSWERS.** It rests on
	// `Array with T` and `__ManagedMemory with T` being one `GenericInstanceId`, a fact about the DECLARATION
	// rather than about the six bytes it currently goes by. Both names answer true at once and must: the
	// user's `Array` is a container it declared, and `declarationIsTheManagedRecord` tells the two apart by SHAPE,
	// exactly as it already had to for a user `Array` with no contest at all.
	//
	// ⚠ **`Array` IS THE ONE CONTESTED NAME THIS BITES, WHICH IS WHY ITS SIBLINGS TAKE NO SUCH ARM.**
	// `Map` and `Set` left the builtin roster entirely at W105, because a corpus `Map` is an
	// ordinary declared generic and nothing about it is served by name. An `Array` never stops being
	// compiler-owned — its record IS its buffer — so its surface is served by name for as long as it exists.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND SINCE ARR5b THE NAMES ARE DISCOVERED FROM THE MARKER, NOT SPELLED HERE.** The first arm is
	// every `type <Name> … implements BuiltinArrayLiteral` the program declares — the corpus's, a user's own
	// `Array` under a contest, and a conformer of any other name. The second is that corpus declaration
	// MOVED, which is a name no declaration spells in its own source.
	export function isArrayBaseName(name ByteArray) returns bool
		if name.equals(self.corpusArrayRecordName) 'theRecordsOwnName'
			return true
		end 'theRecordsOwnName'

		// ⚠ **THE SET IS BEHIND A BOOL, AND THAT BOOL IS FALSE FOR THE WHOLE EXISTING CORPUS.** This predicate
		// sits on every array question in the program and the set lookup is not free, so a program that
		// declares no conformer of its own pays one bool test for the widening — the same gate
		// `hasStdlibShadowRenames` puts in front of the contest's own costs.
		if self.hasNonCorpusArrayConformer and self.declaresTheArrayLiteralMarker(name) 'anotherDeclaredConformer'
			return true
		end 'anotherDeclaredConformer'

		// The shape test, for `Parser.requireUnreservedName`'s measured reason: no ordinary name can reach the
		// contest's mint, so the prefix rules the remaining question out before the map is consulted.
		if not bytesStartWith(name, prefix: ReservedTypeNamePrefix) 'notAMintedName'
			return false
		end 'notAMintedName'

		return name.equals(self.arrayRecordBaseName())
	end 'isArrayBaseName'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE NAME(S) AN `Array` DECLARATION GOES BY IN THIS COMPILE — the ENUMERATION behind the predicate
	// above (ARR2).** One normally. TWO when a user program declares its own container: the user's under the
	// bare `Array` and `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s under this compile's mint.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS FOR THE ASKER THAT HAS NO RECEIVER TO ASK.** A door holding a resolved receiver reads the
	// declaration off the instance (`genericInstanceBaseName`, which is exact); this serves the descriptor
	// pre-scan, which runs before any body is parsed and resolves nothing, so it takes the union and lets the
	// fixpoint's own name→index filter drop the name no function bears. That is the direction
	// `Parser.corpusServesArrayMember`'s header requires it to err in: an edge too many reserves a parameter
	// nothing reads, a missing one aborts the compiler.
	export function arrayDeclarationNames() returns ByteArrayArray
		var names = ByteArrayArray.create()
		for name in self.arrayLiteralConformers 'eachDeclaredConformer'
			names.push(name)
		end 'eachDeclaredConformer'

		if not self.declaresTheArrayLiteralMarker(self.corpusArrayRecordName) 'theCompilerMintsTheRecordItself'
			names.push(self.corpusArrayRecordName)
		end 'theCompilerMintsTheRecordItself'

		let corpus = self.arrayRecordBaseName()
		if not corpus.equals(self.corpusArrayRecordName) 'theCorpusDeclarationMoved'
			names.push(corpus)
		end 'theCorpusDeclarationMoved'

		return names
	end 'arrayDeclarationNames'

	// Is generic instance `id` an `Array with T`? (Reads the base NAME, which needs no base struct.)
	export function isArrayInstance(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return self.isArrayBaseName(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id))
	end 'isArrayInstance'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS (tag, id) PAIR DENOTE AN `Array with T`? — the ONE home of the two-part test, and the
	// TAG HALF IS THE LOAD-BEARING ONE (R4.7 review).** `isArrayInstance` above takes a `GenericInstanceId`,
	// and a `TypeNameId` is the SAME numeric space: handed a plain `named` id it walks the instance registry
	// at that number and can answer TRUE about an instance the caller never had — a wrong answer, never a
	// compile error, because both spellings type-check (`Parser.typeIsArrayInstance` carries the measurement).
	//
	// ⚠ **SO THE TAG TEST MAY NOT BE A CONVENTION THAT EVERY CALLER REMEMBERS.** It was spelled out at SEVEN
	// sites across `Parser` and this file — five value/receiver doors, a declared-TYPE door and two byte
	// doors — each free to drift from the others, and a site that dropped the tag half would look exactly
	// like the six that kept it. One home, so "remembering" is not a thing a caller can fail to do.
	export function isArrayInstanceAt(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns bool
		return tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance and self.isArrayInstance(nameId)
	end 'isArrayInstanceAt'

	// The element TYPE of a ONE-ARGUMENT container instance — `Array with T` or `List with T` — its sole type
	// argument, in this index's own interner.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT SERVES BOTH CONTAINERS AND IS NOT `Array`-SPECIFIC, WHICH IS WHY IT NO LONGER WEARS THAT NAME.**
	// The canonicalization below is a fact about how an INSTANCE ARGUMENT is stored, not about what holds it,
	// and a `List with Tagged` would have hit the identical unresolved-symbol panic through its own
	// `element_drop@24` stamp. A second `listElementType` would have been this whole header copied — and the
	// copy that mattered is the one line of canonicalization, which is exactly the line a copy drops.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A TUPLE ARGUMENT IS CANONICALIZED ON THE WAY OUT (P1.10), and a generic instance's stored ARG is
	// the SECOND door this index's types leave by.** `adoptType` performs the same rewrite for every type
	// that crosses into a FILE's interner; an instance argument crosses no such door — it is read in place —
	// so without the rewrite here the two doors answer differently about one type. MEASURED, not argued:
	// `typealias Tagged = (Num, String)` is folded by the SWEEP, which spells its elements the way the source
	// did, so `typealias TaggedArray = Array with Tagged` stored `structRef(__Tuple2.Num.String)` — while
	// every VALUE the array holds carries the canonical `__Tuple2.int.String`, and only THAT layout reaches
	// `installStructDestructors` (an artifact records only what the real parse minted). The `element_destroy@40`
	// stamp therefore named `__destruct___Tuple2.Num.String` and the x64 backend died on the unresolved
	// symbol — `panic … resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function` (P1.10 review). Every semantic fact the
	// array derives from its element — managed-ness, the drop callee, the clone strategy, the element size —
	// now reads the layout the elements ACTUALLY carry.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE INSTANCE'S OWN COMPILED NAME IS DELIBERATELY NOT CANONICALIZED**: `mangleGenericInstance` reads
	// `instanceArgs` directly. Rewriting it there would give a sweep-spelled instance and a canonically-spelled
	// one the SAME symbol while they remain two gids — the `E3006 duplicate definition` shape
	// `builtinInstanceForNamedElement` documents. That the two stay two TYPES is this rung's named residual:
	// `Array with Tagged` and an `Array` of the same tuple minted from a literal do not unify, reported as an
	// honest argument-type mismatch naming both spellings. Unifying them means MERGING two gids in the
	// registry, which is a change to instance identity itself and not something a read door may make.
	export function containerElementType(id GenericInstanceId) returns MaxonType
		let args = self.genericInstances.instanceArgs(id)
		return self.canonicalTupleStructRef(try args.get(0) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.containerElementType: `{String.from(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id))}` instance {id} has no type argument — arity (1) was checked at instantiation"))
	end 'containerElementType'

	// ⭐ **THE CANONICAL CONFORMER NAME OF A CONTAINER'S ELEMENT** — the key its conformance answer
	// (`ConformanceCheck.typeConformsTo`), its `__witness_<conformer>.<Iface>` table and its
	// `<conformer>.<method>` impl symbol are all filed under. `Integer` reduces to `int`, a struct keeps its
	// own name; the reduction is `conformerNameOfType`'s, shared with the hash-table key path, so an
	// `Array with Integer` and a `Set with Integer` cannot name two different `int` tables.
	//
	// ⚠ It goes through `containerElementType` rather than through `conformerTypeArgName(id, position: 0)`,
	// and that is not a spelling preference: the element accessor CANONICALIZES a tuple `structRef` (see its
	// header for the unresolved-symbol crash that fix is), and a witness label minted off the un-canonical
	// spelling would name a table nothing registers.
	//
	// One question, whatever the caller does with it: `arrayElementConstraintCheck` asks it to decide whether
	// `Array`'s conditional conformance holds on this receiver at all — and through it the front end's
	// withholding and the conformer-name reduction. `LowerMaxonToStd.appendArrayElementWitnessArgs` asked it
	// to name the table a compiler-owned `__managed_contains` dispatched through, and retired with that family at
	// ARR3b. The readers are deliberately NOT counted here: a number beside the thing it counts is one edit
	// away from being wrong, and this one already was.
	export function containerElementConformerName(id GenericInstanceId) returns String
		return self.conformerNameOfType(self.containerElementType(id), idsFrom: self.typeNames)
	end 'containerElementConformerName'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES A CONTAINER INSTANCE'S ELEMENT SATISFY A CONDITIONAL EXTENSION'S `where` CLAUSE? — THE ONE
	// WALK, FOR EVERY DOOR THAT ASKS (array-hashable; unified at the tick's review).** `Array`'s conditional
	// conformance is asked at doors whose CONSEQUENCES differ — the front end WITHHOLDS a method
	// (`Parser.requireArrayConformanceAvailable`), the reduction collapses the instance's conformer name to
	// `Array` (`arrayElementCarriesConformance`, and through it every `__witness_Array.*` stamp, every
	// admitted hash-table key and every `where T is Hashable` slot) — and a second copy of the walk lets those
	// two answer differently with nothing failing to compile: `a == b` refused while the same array is still
	// stamped and keyed, or the reverse. One walk, and each caller keeps only its own consequence.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS THE `where` HALF OF THE CONFORMANCE, ASKED AT THE ONE PLACE THAT STILL HAS AN INSTANCE.**
	// Everything downstream of `conformerNameOfType` works in NAMES — a name cannot carry an element — so the
	// conditional half has to be discharged by the reduction itself; see that function's `Array` arm.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE CLAUSE IS A PARAMETER SO THAT THIS WALK NEVER STATES ONE.** `Array`'s compiler-owned clause is
	// down to the conformance's `where Element is Hashable and Equatable`; `contains`'s `where Element is
	// Equatable` retired to the corpus extension that declares it (ARR3b). See `Parser.arrayWhereClause`.
	//
	// It answers in `ExtensionConstraintCheck`, the verdict a USER's conditional extension is filtered by
	// (`ConformanceCheck.checkExtensionConstraints`), so the compiler-owned clause and the parsed one are
	// reported through one shape: the failing arm carries the constraint and the binding every diagnostic
	// here names, and a caller cannot reach for either after a `satisfied`.
	//
	// ⚠ The OPAQUE element is the CALLER's to handle and is deliberately not an arm: `Array with T` inside a
	// generic body is not an element that fails the clause, it is an element nothing here knows — which the
	// reduction answers with "does not conform" and the parser answers with a WITNESS-FORWARDING refusal
	// naming the type parameter. Two different answers to one question, so the question is asked twice.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS MUTUALLY RECURSIVE and terminates on ELEMENT-NESTING DEPTH**: `containerElementConformerName`
	// -> `conformerNameOfType` -> `instanceConformerReduction` -> `arrayElementCarriesConformance` ->
	// here, which is how an `Array with (Array with E)` gets the inner array's own conditional conformance
	// decided before the outer's. Each hop strictly descends into an ELEMENT, and a type cannot contain
	// itself (a self-naming typealias is E2012 before any of this runs — MEASURED), so there is no cycle to
	// guard against.
	export function arrayElementConstraintCheck(id GenericInstanceId, clauseInterfaces ByteArrayArray) returns ExtensionConstraintCheck
		let element = self.containerElementConformerName(id).toByteArray()
		for interfaceName in clauseInterfaces 'eachConstraint'
			if not typeConformsTo(self.sweptConformanceIndex(), interfaces: self.interfaces, typeName: element, interfaceName: interfaceName, discovered: self.discoveredConformerOf(element)) 'violates'
				return ExtensionConstraintCheck.unmet(interfaceName, bindingName: element)
			end 'violates'
		end 'eachConstraint'

		return ExtensionConstraintCheck.satisfied
	end 'arrayElementConstraintCheck'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS ARRAY INSTANCE'S ELEMENT SATISFY `Array`'s CONDITIONAL CONFORMANCE (array-hashable)?**
	// — `where Element is Hashable and Equatable`, read off `arrayConformanceProtocolNames` so the clause
	// enforced here, the clause a withheld method is refused with, and the row
	// `IrInterface.isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance` states for the array record are one list.
	//
	// ⚠ An OPAQUE element (`Array with T` inside a generic body) is refused rather than admitted: nothing
	// here knows what `T` binds to, and answering `true` would file the instance under a conformer whose
	// element may not conform at the call site that instantiates it.
	export function arrayElementCarriesConformance(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		if self.containerElementIsOpaque(id) 'opaqueElement'
			return false
		end 'opaqueElement'

		return match self.arrayElementConstraintCheck(id, clauseInterfaces: self.arrayConformanceProtocolNames()) 'clause'
			satisfied gives true
			unmet gives false
		end 'clause'
	end 'arrayElementCarriesConformance'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE DECLARED RANGE OF AN `Array` INSTANCE'S ELEMENT, AS `readerFilePath` RESOLVES IT** — the
	// BOUNDS twin of `arrayElementSize`, and the question a value being stored INTO the instance has to be
	// asked (`Parser.requireByteStringBlobFitsItsElement`).
	//
	// ⚠ **IT READS THE ELEMENT OFF THE INSTANCE RATHER THAN RE-DERIVING THE NAME, AND THAT IS THE POINT.**
	// `Byte` is a name a program may declare, so a range-contested one wears the reader's own mint
	// (`RangedAliasRegistry.rangeScopedName`) by the time it is an instance's type argument. Spelling the
	// element a second time here — `aliasOf(ByteElementTypeName, …)` at the call site — would be a second
	// derivation of a fact the instance already carries, free to disagree with the very `arrayElementSize`
	// its answer is paired with. One instance, one element, one range.
	//
	// A non-`named` element (an `int`, a `String`, a nested instance) is `undeclared`: there is no ranged
	// alias to have bounds, which is the same answer a `named` element no file declares gets, and both mean
	// "no declared range constrains this element" to every caller.
	export function arrayElementAliasOf(id GenericInstanceId, readerFilePath FilePath) returns DeclaredAlias
		let element = self.containerElementType(id)
		if maxonTypeTag(element) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notANamedElement'
			return DeclaredAlias.undeclared
		end 'notANamedElement'

		return self.typeAliases.lookup(self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(element)), readerFilePath: readerFilePath)
	end 'arrayElementAliasOf'

	// --- The `Set` builtin generic (P1.7b slice A) ---
	//
	// The hash-table twin of the `Array` section above: `Set` WAS a BUILTIN generic with one type parameter
	// `Element`, synthesized rather than declared, so its instances (`Set with Int`) intern like any other but
	// have no base `StructLayout`. The same three query doors an `Array` instance special-cases — box size,
	// per-instance drop, the managed-field gate — special-case such a `Set` instance here (its box is the
	// fixed fused hash-table record, and it dropped through `__set_decref`).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND `stdlib/Set.maxon` IS LISTED, SO IN THIS CHECKOUT NONE OF THAT IS REACHED (W90).** Every one
	// of those doors was gated on `isSetBaseName`, which answered FALSE the moment a file declared `type Set` —
	// so a `Set with T` is an ordinary declared generic instance with a real `StructLayout`, a
	// `__destruct_Set_<T>` cascade and corpus `create`/`insert`/`contains`/`remove`/`count` bodies. The arms
	// below are what a program declaring no `Set` would get, and they are kept for exactly the reason the
	// `Map` section's are: the switch is a property of the PROGRAM, not of this checkout.

	// ⚠⚠ **THE RETIREMENT SWITCH IS GONE, AND WITH IT `isSetBaseName` / `isMapBaseName` / `isSetInstance` /
	// `isMapInstance` / `isMapInstanceAt` (W105).** `isUnretiredBuiltinBaseName` asked *"does no file declare
	// a type of this name?"*, and a TRUE answer selected the SYNTHESIZED record — the `__set_*` / `__map_*`
	// runtime, its fixed box size and its per-container drop callee. That runtime is deleted, so there is no
	// second regime for a switch to choose between, and a predicate whose true arm selects something that no
	// longer exists is a trap rather than a spare.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT WAS MEASURED DEAD BEFORE IT WAS REMOVED, WITH BOTH HALVES `W52`'s INSTRUMENT ASKS FOR.** A
	// `panic` wired into the switch's TRUE arm read **0 hits across the whole 5,890-case suite**; the same
	// probe with `stdlib/Set.maxon` (resp. `Map.maxon`) struck from `StdlibLoader`'s whitelist fired on the
	// first `typealias` naming the base, inside `Parser.checkGenericInstance`. The listing is unconditional
	// and both modules load on every compile, so the arm was unreachable by construction and the probe says
	// so rather than the reading.
	//
	// ⇒ **THE SHAPE QUESTIONS STAY AND ARE NOW THE ONLY ONES**: `isSetShapedInstance` and
	// `isMapShapedInstanceAt` ask what the container IS, off the base NAME, which is the question every
	// surviving caller had.

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES INSTANCE `id` DENOTE THE `Set` CONTAINER — WHOEVER SERVES IT (W90)?** The SHAPE question,
	// `isMapShapedInstanceAt`'s twin, and genuinely different from the deleted `isSetInstance`, which since the
	// retirement asks *"is this set served by the SYNTHESIZED record"*. See that function's header for the
	// measured programs the split cost the `Map` family; the `Set` half was measured too and is the reason
	// this exists: read through the RUNTIME question, `Parser.namesSetInstanceAlias` answered false for every
	// alias head the moment `stdlib/Set.maxon` was listed, and `CharSet from ['x']` became
	// **`E2004: Undefined variable 'CharSet'`** on four `character-set` cases plus `unicode-category` and
	// `lazy-static`.
	//
	// ⚠ It takes a bare `GenericInstanceId` where the `Map` twin takes a (tag, id) pair, because its caller
	// holds an instance the ALIAS REGISTRY handed back (`genericAliasInstanceFrom`) rather than a value's
	// (tag, nameId) columns — so the numeric-space hazard `isArrayInstanceAt` exists for cannot arise here.
	export function isSetShapedInstance(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return self.genericInstanceBaseName(id).equals(SetBuiltinBaseName)
	end 'isSetShapedInstance'

	// The KEY type of `Set with T` — its sole type argument, in this index's own interner. The `Set` twin of
	// `containerElementType`.
	export function setKeyType(id GenericInstanceId) returns MaxonType
		let args = self.genericInstances.instanceArgs(id)
		return try args.get(HashTableKeyArgPosition) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.setKeyType: `Set` instance {id} has no type argument — arity (1) was checked at instantiation")
	end 'setKeyType'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO QUESTIONS A CONTAINER COLUMN ASKS OF ITS SLOT TYPE, keyed on the TYPE and not on the
	// container.** An `Array`'s element, a `Set`'s key, a `Map`'s key and a `Map`'s VALUE are four columns
	// asking one pair of questions — *does this slot own heap?* and *what drops it?* — and the answer must be
	// the same for all four or a value moved in through one door and dropped through another leaks or
	// double-frees. `Map` is what forced the split: it is the first container with TWO independently managed
	// columns, so a per-container `…KeyIsManaged` / `…KeyDropCallee` pair would have had to be written twice
	// in one type.
	//
	// The drop callee is the SHARED `managedFieldDropCallee`, so a column's stamped destructor equals the drop
	// a managed FIELD of the same type gets — the moved-in value and the dropped value agree by construction.
	// Precondition of the second: `slotTypeIsManaged`.
	export function slotTypeIsManaged(slotType MaxonType) returns bool
		return self.typeIsManaged(slotType, from: self.typeNames)
	end 'slotTypeIsManaged'

	export function slotTypeDropCallee(slotType MaxonType) returns ByteArray
		return managedFieldDropCallee(self, typeNames: self.typeNames, fieldType: slotType)
	end 'slotTypeDropCallee'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES A SLOT THAT IS EXACTLY ONE MACHINE WORD WIDE HOLD THIS TYPE? — the admission rule a
	// `List`/`__ManagedList` ELEMENT and a `Map`'s VALUE column both ask, stated once.** Both slots are one
	// word (`ListRuntime`'s `value@16`, the map's value buffer stride) and both are torn down by the SAME
	// stamped destructor (`Parser.columnDestroyValue` → `slotTypeDropCallee` above), so the set of types they
	// admit is one set: a MANAGED type (String / Character / struct / boxed union / instance / nested
	// container, each held as its 8-byte heap pointer) or a WORD-SHAPED one (`int`, a ranged-int alias, a
	// declared enum, a function alias, `bool`, `Character`… — all stored inline).
	//
	// ⚠ **A `Map`'s VALUE USED TO ASK THE KEY'S QUESTION INSTEAD, AND THAT REFUSED PROGRAMS THE SAME
	// COMPILER RAN ONE CONTAINER OVER.** The key gate (`Parser.requireHashTableKeyType`, deleted at W105) was a
	// three-name whitelist because the probe HASHES and COMPARES the key through its `Hashable`/`Equatable`
	// witnesses, and those are the types a witness can carry. A value is never hashed and never compared: it
	// is stored and dropped, which is the whole of what this asks. Held to the key's rule, an enum value
	// (`/specs/map.md:map-literal-with-enum-values`) and a function value
	// (`:function-valued.dispatch`) were refused *"a later slice"* while `List with TokenKind` and
	// `Array with UnaryOp` — the same word, the same stamp — compiled and ran.
	//
	// ⚠ It is NOT the opaque question: a `typeParameter` slot answers false here only incidentally, and its
	// refusal says something different (there is no destructor to stamp at all), so its askers test
	// `slotTypeIsOpaque` FIRST and say so in their own words.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THIS SAYS WHAT MAY *BE* A COLUMN; `TypeRules.columnValueTagMatches` SAYS WHAT MAY BE *WRITTEN* TO
	// ONE. WIDEN THIS AND GO THERE.** They are two facts in two files with nothing but each other's headers
	// holding them together, and a tag admitted here that no arm answers there is admitted at the `typealias`
	// and refused at every write, with a sentence that argues against itself. MEASURED on `boolean`, which
	// this line admitted while that predicate's integral fall-through refused `m.upsert("a", value: true)` as
	// *"this `Map`'s value is a bool — got a 'bool' value"*.
	export function slotTypeFitsOneWord(slotType MaxonType) returns bool
		if self.slotTypeIsManaged(slotType) 'managedSlot'
			return true
		end 'managedSlot'

		let tag = maxonTypeTag(slotType)
		return tagIsIntegral(tag) or tag == ValueTypeTag.boolean or tag == ValueTypeTag.character
	end 'slotTypeFitsOneWord'

	// Is a container slot's declared type an OPAQUE TYPE PARAMETER — a `T` from an enclosing generic type's
	// shared body (`type W uses T { var xs as List with T }`), rather than a concrete type? A concrete slot
	// knows its destructor at the construction site (the `funcAddr` stamp); an opaque `T` does not — its
	// destructor lives in the enclosing instance's LAYOUT DESCRIPTOR and is read from `destroyFunc@40` at run
	// time, which is why every asker BRANCHES on this rather than composing a static stamp
	// (`requireStaticColumnStamp` is what makes forgetting to branch loud).
	export function slotTypeIsOpaque(slotType MaxonType) returns bool
		return maxonTypeTag(slotType) == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter
	end 'slotTypeIsOpaque'

	// --- The `Map` builtin generic ---
	//
	// ⭐ **A `Map with (Key, Value)` IS THE `Set` ABOVE WITH A PARALLEL VALUE COLUMN**, and that is a
	// REPRESENTATION claim about the DELETED synthesized records, not a metaphor: a map record's first 48 bytes
	// were byte-identical to a set record's, so both tables shared one probe, one grow and one teardown walk
	// (`HashTableRuntime`, deleted at W105). What is different here is exactly the ARITY — two type arguments instead of one — so every `Set` door above
	// that reads "the sole argument" splits in two: a KEY half (which must be `Hashable`/`Equatable`, since
	// the probe hashes and compares it) and a VALUE half (which needs only a drop callee).
	//
	// ⚠ **`Map` IS SYNTHESIZED HERE, NOT READ FROM `stdlib/Map.maxon`, AND THE REASON IS MEASURED.** That
	// file's `Map` is written against dictionary-passing generics — four `Array with Key` fields, a
	// `typealias Entry = (Key, Value)` and an `Iterable` conformance — and shv2 cannot compile it: five
	// separate mechanisms are missing (a tuple over type parameters, expression field defaults, `Array{}`
	// construction of a builtin, `__ManagedMemory with Key` fields, and the transitive-consume fixpoint), and
	// the one shape that DOES compile — a generic type holding an `Array with T` over a managed `T` — was
	// measured to ACCESS-VIOLATE at teardown where the bootstrap exits 0. Synthesizing is what `Array`,
	// `String`, `Character` and `Set` already do, and it is the only route with no wrong answer in it.

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES INSTANCE `id`'s ARGUMENT TREE STILL MENTION AN OPAQUE TYPE PARAMETER — i.e. is this the
	// DECLARATION VIEW of a generic rather than an instance a program actually named (W41)?**
	//
	// Parsing `type Container uses Element` with a `var items as Array with Element` field interns
	// `Container` AT ITS OWN PARAMETERS, and interns everything its body's types mention, before any program
	// has said `Container with Pair`. Those are real registry entries that no program constructs: there is no
	// box of that shape at run time, and a shared generic body reads its layout from the descriptor PASSED IN
	// rather than from one of its own. A whole-registry walk that means *"every instance this program builds"*
	// has to exclude them — see `MmRuntime.rootManagedOpaqueArrayElementDrops`, the walk that measured what
	// including them costs.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT IS A TREE WALK AND NOT A TEST OF THE TOP-LEVEL ARGUMENTS, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF WHY THE FIRST
	// CUT DID NOTHING.** The instance that actually rooted on a listed `stdlib/Map.maxon` is
	// `WithIterIterator with (MapIterator with (Key, Value), (Key, Value))` — minted by expanding `Iterable`'s
	// own `typealias WithIterSelf = WithIterIterator with Iter, Element` onto `Map`'s conformance at Map's
	// opaque parameters. Its top-level arguments are a `genericInstance` and a TUPLE, neither of them a bare
	// `typeParameter`, so an "are all the arguments opaque" test answered FALSE and the byte count did not
	// move. The opacity is one level in.
	//
	// ⭐ **ANY mention, not EVERY argument.** A partly-substituted instance is just as unconstructible as a
	// wholly opaque one: nothing can build a `Map with (String, Value)` while `Value` is still a parameter. A
	// program that writes one writes it FULLY concrete, so "mentions no parameter anywhere" is exactly the set
	// of instances a program can hold a box of.
	//
	// ⚠ It walks the TAGS and does not resolve a `named` argument that might alias an instance mentioning a
	// parameter, so it can answer FALSE where a deeper resolution would answer TRUE. That is the SAFE
	// direction and is deliberate: a false answer merely keeps the old rooting, and over-rooting a leaf
	// destructor is what this walk's own header already licenses ("an over-rooted declared-but-unused instance
	// merely installs a leaf runtime function it never calls"). A false TRUE would leave a baked reloc naming
	// a `.text` VA that was never installed — the `resolveCallFixups` panic — which is why the walk may only
	// ever be narrowed by something structural.
	export function instanceMentionsTypeParameter(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		for position in 0 upto self.genericInstanceArgCount(id) 'eachArg'
			if self.typeMentionsTypeParameter(self.substitutedInstanceArg(id, position: position)) 'opaqueSomewhere'
				return true
			end 'opaqueSomewhere'
		end 'eachArg'

		return false
	end 'instanceMentionsTypeParameter'

	// One node of `instanceMentionsTypeParameter`'s walk: the parameter itself is the answer, a nested
	// instance recurses, and every other tag is a concrete leaf. The two are mutually recursive over the
	// argument TREE, which is finite because `TypeCycleCheck` has already refused a type that owns itself
	// (E4014).
	function typeMentionsTypeParameter(t MaxonType) returns bool
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(t)
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'theParameterItself'
			return true
		end 'theParameterItself'

		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'nestedInstance'
			return self.instanceMentionsTypeParameter(typeNameIdOf(t))
		end 'nestedInstance'

		return false
	end 'typeMentionsTypeParameter'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS (tag, id) DENOTE THE `Map` CONTAINER — WHOEVER SERVES IT (W41)?** The SHAPE question,
	// and since W105 the ONLY one: its counterpart `isMapInstanceAt` asked *"is this map served by the
	// SYNTHESIZED record"*, and that record no longer exists, so the predicate went with it.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TWO CAME APART THE MOMENT `stdlib/Map.maxon` WAS LISTED, AND A DOOR THAT WANTED THE SHAPE GOT
	// THE RUNTIME.** `inferredIntColumnBoundaryAdmits` asks how many COLUMNS a container has, so that a
	// `[1: 1]` literal's inferred `Map with (int, int)` reaches a parameter declared through a
	// `typealias IntMap = Map with (Integer, Integer)`. That has nothing to do with which runtime serves the
	// record — and reading the retirement gate there re-broke the very program its own header records having
	// been built for: **MEASURED, `map.md`'s `for-in.nested` and `tuples`' `for-in-over-map-allocates-no-tuple`
	// both answered `E3005: argument type mismatch for 'm': expected 'IntMap', got 'Map_int_int'`.**
	//
	// ⇒ **THE LESSON OUTLIVED THE SPLIT AND IS WHY THIS HEADER IS KEPT.** A door must ask what the container
	// IS, never what will EXECUTE it, and W41 had to say so with two predicates because both regimes were
	// live. W105 deleted the runtime half, so there is one question and this is it — but a door that starts
	// asking a RUNTIME question of a container is the shape that re-broke those two programs, whatever the
	// predicate is called at the time.
	//
	// ⛔ **DO NOT READ THE DELETION AS PERMISSION TO NAME `isMapInstanceAt`** — this header cited it as a live
	// callee until the W105 review struck the reference. It does not exist.
	export function isMapShapedInstanceAt(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns bool
		return tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance and self.genericInstanceBaseName(nameId).equals(MapBuiltinBaseName)
	end 'isMapShapedInstanceAt'

	// The KEY type of `Map with (K, V)` — type argument 0, the one the probe hashes and compares.
	export function mapKeyType(id GenericInstanceId) returns MaxonType
		let args = self.genericInstances.instanceArgs(id)
		return try args.get(HashTableKeyArgPosition) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.mapKeyType: `Map` instance {id} has no key type argument — arity (2) was checked at instantiation")
	end 'mapKeyType'

	// The VALUE type of `Map with (K, V)` — type argument 1, which the table only stores and drops.
	export function mapValueType(id GenericInstanceId) returns MaxonType
		let args = self.genericInstances.instanceArgs(id)
		return try args.get(MapValueArgPosition) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.mapValueType: `Map` instance {id} has no value type argument — arity (2) was checked at instantiation")
	end 'mapValueType'

	// --- The `List` builtin generic ---
	//
	// The linked-list twin of the `Set` section above, and the SIMPLEST of the four containers: a `List with T`
	// has one column, no hashing and no comparison, so it needs neither a key-type gate nor witness threading.
	// Its element TYPE is read through the shared `containerElementType` (the `Array` door — one argument, one
	// canonicalization), so the only `List`-specific type-level facts are these two predicates, its box size
	// and its drop callee. See `ListRuntime.maxon`'s header for why it is synthesized rather than read from
	// `stdlib/List.maxon`.

	// Is `name` the builtin `List` type — the base of every list instance.
	export function isListBaseName(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return name.equals(ListBuiltinBaseName)
	end 'isListBaseName'

	// Is generic instance `id` a `List with T`? (Reads the base NAME, which needs no base struct.)
	export function isListInstance(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return self.isListBaseName(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id))
	end 'isListInstance'

	// ⭐ **DOES THIS (tag, id) PAIR DENOTE A `List with T`?** — `isArrayInstanceAt`'s twin, and it exists for
	// that function's measured reason rather than for symmetry: a `TypeNameId` and a `GenericInstanceId` share
	// one numeric space, so `isListInstance` handed a plain `named` id can answer TRUE about an instance the
	// caller never had. Both of this predicate's callers (the receiver dispatch and the `for … in` source
	// test) hold a value's (tag, nameId) pair, which is exactly the shape that hazard bites.
	export function isListInstanceAt(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns bool
		return tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance and self.isListInstance(nameId)
	end 'isListInstanceAt'

	// --- The `__ManagedList` builtin generic and its node handle ---
	//
	// The `List`'s own chain under the surface that exposes nodes (see `ManagedListBuiltinBaseName`). Every
	// type-level fact it has is a `List`'s — the element through `containerElementType`, the box through
	// `ListRecordBytes`, the drop through `__list_decref` — so what lives here is only the identity: which
	// base name it is, and the same for the NODE handle whose element it shares.

	// Is `name` the builtin `__ManagedList` type — the base of every managed-list instance.
	export function isManagedListBaseName(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return name.equals(ManagedListBuiltinBaseName)
	end 'isManagedListBaseName'

	// Is generic instance `id` a `__ManagedList with T`? (Reads the base NAME, which needs no base struct.)
	export function isManagedListInstance(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return self.isManagedListBaseName(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id))
	end 'isManagedListInstance'

	// ⭐ **DOES THIS (tag, id) PAIR DENOTE A `__ManagedList with T`?** — `isListInstanceAt`'s twin, needed for
	// its measured reason: a `TypeNameId` and a `GenericInstanceId` share one numeric space, so the id alone
	// can answer TRUE about an instance the caller never had. Its caller is the receiver dispatch, which
	// holds a value's (tag, nameId) pair.
	export function isManagedListInstanceAt(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns bool
		return tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance and self.isManagedListInstance(nameId)
	end 'isManagedListInstanceAt'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS INSTANCE `id` THE CHAIN — under EITHER of its two surfaces?** `ListRuntime.maxon`'s header states
	// the fact this predicate is: *"ONE structure, TWO surfaces … every `__list_*` entry serves both; the split
	// is entirely in the parser's two dispatchers"*. So every door that is about the STRUCTURE — its `create`,
	// its element gate, its top-level record — asks this, and only a door about a SURFACE (which methods exist,
	// which sentence a missing one gets) asks the two identities apart.
	//
	// ⚠ It was spelled `isListInstance(id) or isManagedListInstance(id)` at three call sites in two files, which
	// is the shape a fourth door joins by writing a fourth copy — and the copy that gets forgotten admits one
	// surface and refuses the other for a structure that cannot tell them apart.
	export function isChainInstance(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return self.isListInstance(id) or self.isManagedListInstance(id)
	end 'isChainInstance'

	// Is `name` the builtin `__ManagedListNode` type — the base of every node handle.
	export function isManagedListNodeBaseName(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return name.equals(ManagedListNodeBuiltinBaseName)
	end 'isManagedListNodeBaseName'

	// Is generic instance `id` a `__ManagedListNode with T`?
	export function isManagedListNodeInstance(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return self.isManagedListNodeBaseName(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id))
	end 'isManagedListNodeInstance'

	// ⭐ **DOES THIS (tag, id) PAIR DENOTE A NODE HANDLE?** — the receiver dispatch's door, and the (tag, id)
	// form for `isListInstanceAt`'s reason.
	export function isManagedListNodeInstanceAt(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns bool
		return tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance and self.isManagedListNodeInstance(nameId)
	end 'isManagedListNodeInstanceAt'

	// ⭐ **THE NODE-HANDLE INSTANCE FOR A MANAGED LIST'S ELEMENT** — what an insertion's result is typed
	// with. It is derived from the LIST's instance rather than taken from the caller, so the element a node
	// reads back through `value()` is by construction the element the list holds.
	export function managedListNodeInstanceFor(listGiid GenericInstanceId) returns GenericInstanceId
		return self.builtinInstanceForElement(ManagedListNodeBuiltinBaseName, element: self.containerElementType(listGiid))
	end 'managedListNodeInstanceFor'

	// --- The `__ManagedMemoryCursor` builtin generic ---

	// Is `name` the builtin `__ManagedMemoryCursor` type — the base of every cursor.
	export function isManagedMemoryCursorBaseName(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return name.equals(ManagedMemoryCursorBuiltinBaseName)
	end 'isManagedMemoryCursorBaseName'

	// Is generic instance `id` a `__ManagedMemoryCursor with T`?
	export function isManagedMemoryCursorInstance(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return self.isManagedMemoryCursorBaseName(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id))
	end 'isManagedMemoryCursorInstance'

	// ⭐ **DOES THIS (tag, id) PAIR DENOTE A CURSOR?** — the receiver dispatch's door, and the (tag, id) form
	// for `isListInstanceAt`'s measured reason: a `TypeNameId` and a `GenericInstanceId` share one numeric
	// space, so the id alone can answer TRUE about an instance the caller never had.
	export function isManagedMemoryCursorInstanceAt(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns bool
		return tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance and self.isManagedMemoryCursorInstance(nameId)
	end 'isManagedMemoryCursorInstanceAt'

	// ⭐ **THE CURSOR INSTANCE FOR A BUFFER'S ELEMENT** — what `createCursor()`'s result is typed with.
	// Derived from the SOURCE's instance rather than taken from the caller, so the element a `current()` reads
	// back is by construction the element the buffer holds — `managedListNodeInstanceFor`'s rule, and for the
	// same reason.
	export function managedMemoryCursorInstanceFor(sourceGiid GenericInstanceId) returns GenericInstanceId
		return self.builtinInstanceForElement(ManagedMemoryCursorBuiltinBaseName, element: self.containerElementType(sourceGiid))
	end 'managedMemoryCursorInstanceFor'

	// --- The `Vector` builtin generic ---
	//
	// The FIXED-SIZE array (see `VectorBuiltinBaseName`). Its element is read through the shared
	// `containerElementType`, its stride through the shared `arrayElementSize`, its box and its drop through
	// the `Array` rows of the two routers — so the only `Vector`-specific type-level facts are these three
	// predicates and the SIZE below.

	// Is `name` the builtin `Vector` type — the base of every vector instance.
	export function isVectorBaseName(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return name.equals(VectorBuiltinBaseName)
	end 'isVectorBaseName'

	// Is generic instance `id` a `Vector with N T`? (Reads the base NAME, which needs no base struct.)
	export function isVectorInstance(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return self.isVectorBaseName(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id))
	end 'isVectorInstance'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH CONTAINERS' `create` BUILDS THE FUSED MANAGED RECORD — i.e. is born through
	// `__managed_create` (S2u).** `Parser.emitVectorCreateOp` IS `emitArrayCreateOp` plus the `__managed_resize`
	// that publishes the N slots, so a `Vector` reaches the opaque, descriptor-reading create by exactly the
	// route an `Array` does. Its readers are `containerCreateCall`, which goes on to EMIT that very callee, and
	// the descriptor family below, which is defined FROM it.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT WAS ALSO "the ONE family test the descriptor-need SEED and the descriptor-reading EMIT both
	// read", AND SINCE W134 IT IS NOT — that is `opaqueInstanceCreateReadsTheDescriptor` below**, because a
	// chain is born through `__list_create` and still reads the same descriptor word. The DISCIPLINE the two
	// halves of that pairing owe each other is unchanged, and it is a compiler PANIC rather than a wrong
	// answer when they part: **MEASURED**, the opaque refusal relaxed with the family still spelled
	// `isArrayInstance`, `Holder.create()` writing `Slot.create()` reached *"appendOpaqueArrayCreate: opaque
	// `Array.create()` in 'Holder.create' but the function carries no layout descriptor parameter"*. It is
	// `opaqueContainerConstructionAt`'s own stated discipline, one axis over: that door already reads the SAME
	// `containerElementIsOpaque` the emit branches on, and this is the other half of the same pairing.
	export function instanceCreateBuildsTheManagedRecord(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return self.isArrayInstance(id) or self.isVectorInstance(id)
	end 'instanceCreateBuildsTheManagedRecord'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH CONTAINERS' `create` READS THE ENCLOSING LAYOUT DESCRIPTOR when its element is OPAQUE — the
	// family the descriptor-need SEED (`Parser.opaqueContainerConstructionAt`) and the descriptor-reading EMIT
	// (`LowerMaxonToStd.isOpaqueContainerCreateCall`) both read (W134).** It is a WIDER family than the one
	// above, and the two must stay apart: `instanceCreateBuildsTheManagedRecord` names the containers born
	// through `__managed_create` — which is what `containerCreateCall` goes on to EMIT — while a chain is born
	// through `__list_create` and yet reads the very same `destroyFunc@40` word out of the very same descriptor.
	// Fusing them would make one of the two sentences false; asking them separately at each door is what the
	// header above records costing a compiler PANIC.
	export function opaqueInstanceCreateReadsTheDescriptor(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return self.instanceCreateBuildsTheManagedRecord(id) or self.isChainInstance(id)
	end 'opaqueInstanceCreateReadsTheDescriptor'

	// ⭐ **DOES THIS (tag, id) PAIR DENOTE A `Vector with N T`?** — `isArrayInstanceAt`'s twin, and it exists
	// for that function's MEASURED reason and not for symmetry: a `TypeNameId` and a `GenericInstanceId` share
	// one numeric space, so `isVectorInstance` handed a plain `named` id can answer TRUE about an instance the
	// caller never had. Both callers (the receiver dispatch and the `for … in` source test) hold a value's
	// (tag, nameId) pair, which is exactly the shape that hazard bites.
	export function isVectorInstanceAt(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns bool
		return tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance and self.isVectorInstance(nameId)
	end 'isVectorInstanceAt'

	// ⭐ **HOW MANY ELEMENTS INSTANCE `id` HOLDS** — the second coordinate of a sized instance's identity, and
	// `NoFixedSize` for every unsized one. Read by `Vector.create()` (the `__managed_mem_create` count) and by
	// `count()`, which folds to this constant rather than reading `length@8`: a fixed size is a fact about the
	// TYPE, so the value it publishes may not depend on the record.
	export function genericInstanceFixedSize(id GenericInstanceId) returns FixedElementCount
		return self.genericInstances.instanceFixedSize(id)
	end 'genericInstanceFixedSize'

	// The `Vector with <size> <element>` instance — the SIZED twin of `builtinInstanceForElement`, and the one
	// door that mints one. Its two callers are the alias fold (`recordGenericAlias`, off a declaration) and the
	// declaration sweep's `Vector from […]` pre-intern, which must agree on the instance or the parser's own
	// read would be the first write to the shared registry mid-parse (`Parser.atSetFromConstruction`'s rule).
	export function vectorInstanceForElement(element MaxonType, size FixedElementCount) returns GenericInstanceId
		var args = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		args.push(element)
		return self.genericInstances.intern(self.typeNames.intern(VectorBuiltinBaseName), args: args, fixedSize: size)
	end 'vectorInstanceForElement'

	// ⚠ There is deliberately no `mapKeyIsManaged` / `mapValueIsManaged` / `setKeyIsManaged` beside these two
	// selectors. A map carries TWO independently stamped destructors (`keyDrop@40`, `valueDrop@56`) — a
	// `Map with (String, Int)` owns heap keys and trivial values — and a per-column predicate is a second place
	// that has to know which type argument is the key. Every caller already holds the column TYPE (from
	// `setKeyType` / `mapKeyType` / `mapValueType`) and asks `slotTypeIsManaged` of it, so the moved-in value
	// and the stamped destructor read the one answer.

	// Is a SINGLE-ELEMENT container's element itself managed (String / struct / boxed union / nested container)
	// — the slice-1 boundary: a managed-element array needs the element-destroy walk (P1.7 slice 3) and is
	// rejected with a clean E2015 until then. A trivial element (int / Byte / ranged-int / bool) is stored
	// inline and owns nothing.
	//
	// ⚠ **`container`, NOT `array`, AND THE NAME IS LOAD-BEARING.** It reads through `containerElementType`, so
	// a `List` giid answers here exactly as an `Array` one does — and `Parser.dispatchListMethod` and the list
	// move-in/borrow doors ask it that way. Named for `Array` it invited an `isListInstance` arm nobody needs.
	export function containerElementIsManaged(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return self.typeIsManaged(self.containerElementType(id), from: self.typeNames)
	end 'containerElementIsManaged'

	// Is a SINGLE-ELEMENT container's element an OPAQUE TYPE PARAMETER (P1.7 slice 3a)? The instance-keyed
	// spelling of `slotTypeIsOpaque` above, which carries the reason — a `Map`'s VALUE column asks the same
	// question of a slot no `containerElementType` names, so the rule lives on the TYPE and this reads it
	// through the element door.
	export function containerElementIsOpaque(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return self.slotTypeIsOpaque(self.containerElementType(id))
	end 'containerElementIsOpaque'

	// The element type's name AS A DIAGNOSTIC PRINTS IT (`Slot`, `String`) — for the one message that has to
	// say WHICH element type it is refusing (E3106, `Parser.requireArrayResizeHasZeroElement`). It lives here
	// because `typeNames` is this index's own interner and is not the parser's to reach into; the parser holds
	// a per-FILE interner, and an element type id from `containerElementType` belongs to this one.
	export function arrayElementDisplayName(id GenericInstanceId) returns String
		return maxonTypeName(self.containerElementType(id), typeNames: self.typeNames)
	end 'arrayElementDisplayName'

	// ⭐ **THE SOLE PRODUCER OF `element_size@24`** — the number every `__managed_create` is stamped with, every
	// array helper strides by at run time, and every same-shape check compares. A MANAGED element (String /
	// struct / union / instance / nested Array) is a heap box stored as an 8-byte POINTER
	// (`ManagedArrayElementBytes`), so its slot is the pointer, NOT the element's full logical size — the
	// drop walk destroys the pointer, the accessors load/store it. A TRIVIAL element is stored inline, at
	// the width `trivialElementSlot` gives it.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT THE SOURCE `sizeof`, AND NO LONGER ROUTES THROUGH THE LOGICAL-SIZE DOOR.** Both of the
	// storage rules above diverge from `sizeof`: a `String` element's slot is 8 where its `sizeof` is the
	// 48-byte record, and a `Byte` element's slot is 1 where `sizeof(Byte)` is a machine word. Sharing one
	// door forced one answer on two questions — see `typeLogicalByteSize`, which is the other.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **ITS ANSWER IS SIGNED, BECAUSE `element_size@24` CARRIES TWO LADDERS IN ONE FIELD** — a positive
	// byte stride, or a NEGATIVE sub-byte bit width (`elementStrideOf`, which owns the encoding). Every
	// caller of this function wants the stamp and not the unpacked byte width, which is what keeps it one
	// function: the two create sites stamp it, `isBytePackedArrayInstance` asks `== 1` and must answer NO
	// for a packed element, `arrayElementIsMachineWord` asks `== 8`, the append same-shape test compares
	// two of them, and the raw-byte doors have to refuse a slot that is not byte-addressable at all. A
	// second "unpacked byte width" accessor beside this one would be a second producer of a stride, which
	// is the shape this function's own name exists to prevent.
	export function arrayElementSize(id GenericInstanceId) returns ElementStride
		if self.containerElementIsManaged(id) 'managed'
			return ManagedArrayElementBytes as ElementStride
		end 'managed'

		let element = self.containerElementType(id)
		return elementStrideOf(self.arrayElementPackedBits(id), storageBytes: self.trivialElementSlot(element).bytes)
	end 'arrayElementSize'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS TYPE STORED IN A NARROW SIGNED SLOT, AND HOW WIDE? (`enum-narrow-storage`.)** The shared
	// element load is ONE compiled byte loop shared by every array in the program, strided by the record's
	// runtime `element_size@24`, and it ZERO-EXTENDS (`ManagedMemoryRuntime.emitLoadElemAt` ends in a shift and a
	// width mask). There is no signedness in the record for it to consult and there could not be — the stamp
	// is one signed field already carrying two ladders — so a narrow SIGNED value's sign has to reach the read
	// from the COMPILER. This is that fact, asked where the width was decided.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT IS A QUESTION ABOUT THE TYPE AND NOT ABOUT THE CONTAINER, WHICH IS WHAT LETS TWO DOORS SHARE
	// IT — AND THE REASON IS THAT THE EXTENSION IS THE IDENTITY ON A CORRECT VALUE.** The slot was chosen to
	// hold every value the type admits, so every legitimate value already sits inside the signed range the
	// extension maps onto itself; applying it to a value that was never truncated cannot change it. So a
	// reader does not have to establish that its value CAME from a narrow slot — only that it COULD have —
	// and `Parser.emitContainerElementAccessor` (the direct element read) and
	// `Parser.retypeOpaqueReturnValue` (a value handed back through the opaque word by a shared corpus body,
	// which is how `pop`/`last`/`remove` and a cursor's `current()` read one) ask the identical question.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT TAKES A NAME AND NOT A `MaxonType`, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT: IDS DO NOT TRAVEL BETWEEN
	// INTERNERS, NAMES DO.** There are two interners — this index's, and the per-FILE table each parser holds
	// — and they assign ids in their own insertion orders. Every caller of this query holds a type the PARSER
	// re-interned (`containerSlotValueType`, `slotTypeThroughReceiver`), so handing its id to an index-space
	// lookup is right exactly while the two orderings coincide and silently wrong the moment they drift. That
	// is OPEN #56, `slotTypeThroughReceiver` documents three measured faces of it, and this is the third
	// consecutive rung it has appeared in — so the signature is what refuses it here, not a comment.
	//
	// ⚠ **MEASURED IN THIS RUNG'S OWN NEW CODE, before the signature changed:** `[Offset.behind, …].first()`
	// over a three-case enum at `-200 … 200` answered `-200` in a program that also declared
	// `typealias Offsets = Array with Offset` and **65336** in one that did not. Same source, same slot, same
	// ladder — the extra declaration only changed which order the two interners assigned ids in.
	//
	// ⚠ **ONLY A `named` LEAF CAN ANSWER YES, which is why the callers guard on the tag before spelling a
	// name.** Every other trivial tag is unsigned or a full word (`trivialElementSlot`'s own arm says so), and a
	// managed value's slot is a pointer, which is never read as a small negative number. A sub-byte PACKED
	// element answers no through the same route: packing exists only for a ZERO-BASED range
	// (`rangedAliasPackedBits` refuses any other), so its masked read IS its value.
	export function narrowSignedSlotOf(name ByteArray) returns SignExtendSlot
		let slot = self.namedTypeStorageSlot(name)
		return SignExtendSlot.fromBytes(slot.bytes) if slot.signExtends else SignExtendSlot.notASignExtension
	end 'narrowSignedSlotOf'

	// ⭐ **MAY A CONSTANT LITERAL OF THIS INSTANCE'S ELEMENTS BE LAID DOWN AS ONE `.rdata` BLOB?** — the
	// question the constant array literal's blob path turns on (`Parser.parseArrayLiteral`). A blob is the
	// elements' STORED bytes concatenated at `arrayElementSize`'s stride, so an element qualifies exactly
	// when its stored form IS its value, and only two kinds fail that:
	//   • a MANAGED element's slot holds a POINTER to a heap box, and an address the loader chooses is not
	//     a value a compile-time image can contain;
	//   • an OPAQUE type parameter's slot is a machine word whose content depends on the concrete instance
	//     the shared body is called with, so there is no ONE image to lay down.
	// Everything else stores its own value inline at a stride this index already answers — `bool`, a
	// sub-byte-packed ranged alias, a narrow enum tag, `integer`, `ExitCode`, a function pointer.
	//
	// ⚠ **ANSWERING YES IS NOT THE SAME AS REACHING A BLOB, and `float` is the case that shows it.** This
	// predicate admits a float or function element to the COLLECT path; whether that path then lays a blob
	// down is `everyElementIsAKnownConstant`'s question, and that one is the integer domain only. So a
	// `[1.5, 2.5]` literal collects and then builds through `__managed_create` + pushes like any other.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT ASKED "IS THE ELEMENT NARROWER THAN A MACHINE WORD" UNTIL THIS SPEC, AND THAT EXCLUDED THE
	// MACHINE WORD FOR A REASON NO PROGRAM COULD SEE — golden stability.** `specs/codegen-internals.md`
	// states the rule with no width in it (*"Constant array literals (declared with `let`) are stored in
	// the `.rdata` section"*) and pins `[10, 20, 30]` as `i64[] 10, 20, 30`; under the narrow gate that
	// literal reached `__managed_create` + three `__managed_push` and the section held no such bytes AT ALL. The
	// runnable oracle agrees with the spec — the bootstrap lays 24 bytes of `.rdata` for it. Width was
	// never the question; whether the slot owns its own bytes is.
	export function arrayElementMayBeConstantBlob(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return not self.containerElementIsManaged(id) and not self.containerElementIsOpaque(id)
	end 'arrayElementMayBeConstantBlob'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS INSTANCE'S ELEMENT SUB-BYTE PACKED, AND HOW WIDE — THE ONE HOME OF THE PACKING RULE.**
	// `NotSubBytePacked` for every element a byte or wider. The stamp above is derived from it, and so is
	// every runtime decision downstream, so "does this array pack?" is asked here or not at all.
	//
	// The three arms are the whole rule:
	//   • a MANAGED element is an 8-byte pointer to a heap box — there is nothing to narrow, and the drop
	//     walk addresses each slot;
	//   • `bool` is the 1-bit case, and it is an ARM of this classifier rather than a mechanism beside it.
	//     That is what `specs/bool-bit-packing.md` and `specs/ranged-int-bit-packing.md` mean by "the same
	//     machinery extends to any non-negative ranged-integer typealias": there is one packed runtime, and
	//     `bool` reaches it by being `bits == 1`;
	//   • a `named` element is a ranged alias, answered whole-program (`packedBitsInEveryFile`) for the
	//     reason its byte twin is: the instance is interned on the NAME, so it has ONE stride.
	// Every other trivial tag — `integer`, `float`, `ExitCode`, a function pointer — is a byte or wider.
	//
	// ⭐ **shv2 DECIDES THIS AT `__managed_create`, NOT AT FIRST `grow`, AND LEAVES `LayoutDescriptor`'s
	// `bit1=bitPacked` FLAG RESERVED — a deliberate departure from v1.** v1 must stamp the packed width out
	// of the layout descriptor when the buffer is first grown, because its packing decision is made in a
	// LAYOUT pass over generic instances (`BuildLayoutDescriptors.computeLayoutDescriptor`) and the create
	// site cannot see it. shv2's create sites already hold the CONCRETE instance — the parser mints it
	// (`Parser.emitArrayCreateOp`) and the global initializer resolves it off the same registry
	// (`arrayRecordInitializer`) — so the answer is available one tier earlier, at the only two places a
	// record is born. Deciding it there means the record is never briefly unpacked, no descriptor is read at
	// run time, and there is no second stamp site for the two to disagree at.
	export function arrayElementPackedBits(id GenericInstanceId) returns PackedBitWidth
		if self.containerElementIsManaged(id) 'managed'
			return NotSubBytePacked
		end 'managed'

		let element = self.containerElementType(id)
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(element)
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.boolean 'theOneBitCase'
			return BooleanPackedBits
		end 'theOneBitCase'

		if tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'notARangedAlias'
			return NotSubBytePacked
		end 'notARangedAlias'

		return self.typeAliases.packedBitsInEveryFile(self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(element)))
	end 'arrayElementPackedBits'

	// ⭐ **DOES INSTANCE `id`'s ELEMENT STRIDE A FULL MACHINE WORD** — the question a door asks when it reads a
	// buffer ONE WORD PER SLOT and so cannot walk a byte-PACKED element (`Parser.parseListFromConstruction`,
	// whose `__list_from` copies the source array's slots into single-word nodes).
	//
	// It lives HERE, beside the producer, because it is a comparison between two answers this file already
	// owns: `arrayElementSize` above and `ManagedArrayElementBytes`. Written at the call site it would be a
	// THIRD transcription of the machine word — the header on `ManagedMemoryWordElementSize` names the two
	// this compiler admits — and a transcription is what drifts when a width is not what a door assumed.
	export function arrayElementIsMachineWord(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return self.arrayElementSize(id) == ManagedArrayElementBytes
	end 'arrayElementIsMachineWord'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS VALUE — OR THIS DECLARED TYPE — A BYTE-PACKED BUFFER? (R4.7.)** The ONE stride question
	// every byte door in the compiler asks, asked in ONE place. An `Array with T` whose element strides ONE
	// byte IS a raw byte buffer whatever its element is CALLED: the record's `element_size@24` is 1, every
	// accessor moves by one byte, and the bytes it holds are just bytes.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS A STRIDE FACT AND NOT A NAME FACT, BECAUSE shv2 HAS TWO NAMES FOR A BYTE AND THE USER RULING
	// KEEPS THEM DISTINCT.** `Byte` is the user-visible element a `b"…"` literal, a `ByteArray` and
	// `__ManagedMemory` share; `__ManagedByte` is the compiler-owned element EVERY compiler-synthesized byte
	// buffer wears (see `SynthesizedByteElementName` for the two measurements that minted it). They are two
	// instances on purpose — a user may declare `Byte`, and a compiler-minted buffer's stride may not follow —
	// and they meet at a BOUNDARY, which is what this predicate lets a door recognize.
	//
	// The (tag, id) pair is asked through `isArrayInstanceAt`, which is where the tag half's reason lives.
	export function isBytePackedArrayInstance(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns bool
		return self.isArrayInstanceAt(tag, nameId: nameId) and self.arrayElementSize(nameId) == ByteStringElementSize
	end 'isBytePackedArrayInstance'

	// ⭐ **WHAT AN `Array` INSTANCE CALLS ITS ELEMENT, when the element is a `named` LEAF** — `EmptySourceText`
	// for every other (tag, id) pair, which is the answer BOTH SIDES of the door below want: a pair that is not
	// an Array instance at all, and an Array over a String / struct / nested instance, each name no element
	// this file could mistake for a byte, and neither `isByteElementName` nor the `__ManagedByte` comparison
	// answers true for an empty name. One walk, asked once per side, so the two sides cannot come to disagree
	// about where an element's name is read from.
	//
	// ⚠ It reads the element NAME rather than comparing the instance against `internSynthesizedByteInstance()`,
	// because that function WRITES: asking it would mint the view instance into `genericInstances` in every
	// program whose only reason to ask is a diagnostic it is about to raise, and an instance the sweep never
	// interned is one no mangled-name or witness walk expects to find.
	function arrayNamedElementName(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns ByteArray
		if not self.isArrayInstanceAt(tag, nameId: nameId) 'notAnArrayInstance'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'notAnArrayInstance'

		let element = self.containerElementType(nameId)
		if maxonTypeTag(element) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notANamedElement'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'notANamedElement'

		return self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(element))
	end 'arrayNamedElementName'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE BYTE-ELEMENT BOUNDARY DOOR (R4.7)** — the one place a value of one byte element type is
	// admitted where the other is declared, and the whole of what the user ruling *"the two byte element types
	// stay DISTINCT and convert at the BOUNDARY"* means in code. `stdlib/File.maxon:140-146` is what asks:
	// `String.addressableBytes()` yields `Array with __ManagedByte` and `FileWriter.open(path __ManagedMemory)`
	// declares `Array with Byte`, and both are the same 1-byte record.
	//
	// **THE ARRIVING SIDE IS AN IDENTITY TEST OVER THE ROSTER `isByteElementName` HOLDS. THE DECLARED SIDE IS
	// THREE TESTS — NAME, BOUNDS, STRIDE — AND EACH ONE IS THE ONLY REFUSAL ON ITS OWN MEASURED PROGRAM:**
	//
	//   • **The declared side's NAME test.** A program that declares `typealias Small = int(0 to 200)` gets a
	//     byte-PACKED `Array with Small` for free (`rangedAliasStorageBytes`), and a declared side that asked
	//     only the stride admitted a String's byte view into it — `b.get(0)`, an accessor typed
	//     `int(0 to 200)`, then read back **223** with no diagnostic (MEASURED, R4.7 review). A one-byte slot
	//     is not a byte. What may be declared here is one of shv2's TWO byte element names and nothing else.
	//   • **The declared side's BOUNDS test** (`RangedAliasRegistry.holdsEveryByteInEveryFile`). A program
	//     that declares `typealias Byte = int(0 to 200)` puts the SAME range on the ROSTER, and the roster is
	//     a question about the NAME — deliberately, because the base-name strip is what keeps
	//     `stdlib/File.maxon` compiling under a contested `Byte` (`isByteElementName`). So the name test says
	//     yes, the stride test says yes, and R4.7's own reading came back: **exit 223, no diagnostic**, out of
	//     an element declared `int(0 to 200)` (MEASURED on `origin/main`, N2 — `takes(<a cwd buffer>)` with
	//     `typealias Byte = int(0 to 200)`; rename the alias to `Small` and it was correctly refused, so the
	//     defect needed only the most natural name there is). A buffer the COMPILER minted is a run of RAW
	//     bytes; what receives one must admit every value a byte has, and this is where that is asked.
	//   • **The declared side's STRIDE test.** A program that declares `typealias Byte = int(0 to 1000)` makes
	//     every `Array with Byte` — including the one `__ManagedMemory` names — genuinely stride TWO, so the
	//     NAME is on the roster, the BOUNDS hold every byte, and the record is still not a byte buffer.
	//     Adopting a stride-1 view into it would hand every accessor a record it reads at the wrong width: a
	//     silent wrong answer, not a refusal. The stride test refuses exactly there, and it is LIVE —
	//     measured raising `expected 'Array_Byte', got 'Array___ManagedByte'` on that program.
	//
	// ⇒ the three are INDEPENDENT: `int(0 to 200)` clears name and stride but not bounds; `int(0 to 1000)`
	// clears name and bounds but not stride; `Small = int(0 to 255)` clears bounds and stride but not name.
	//
	// ✅ **THE `b"…"` ENTRANCE THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO NAME IS CLOSED (A3r).** A literal's every byte is now
	// range-checked against its element's DECLARED bounds at the one rule function both of the literal's
	// doorways ask (`Parser.requireByteStringBlobFitsItsElement`), reported as E3005. The wide half of that
	// same rule is still the E2015 the paragraph below describes; the two are one function, not two.
	//
	// ✅ **AND THE RAW-BYTE FILLS ARE CLOSED TOO (A4a), AT A RULE OF THEIR OWN RATHER THAN AT THIS DOOR.**
	// `String.toByteArray()` / `.bytes()` (`Parser.parseByteView`), the buffer surface's `setByte` and
	// `__ManagedFile.read` all write bytes that exist only at RUN TIME into a record typed
	// `internArrayByteInstance(<the reading file>)` — the USER's `Byte` — so none of them mints
	// `SynthesizedByteElementName` and this door never sees any of them. All three were silent wrong answers
	// (195, 223, 223) under `typealias Byte = int(0 to 100)`.
	// ⛔ **BUT THE CURE WAS *NOT* TO MINT `__ManagedByte` AT THOSE PRODUCERS, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER
	// THAN PREFERRED.** A byte view is not a buffer of this element's kind: `emitArrayCreateOp` stamps its
	// `element_size@24` from the very instance it is typed as, so a wide `Byte` gives it a genuinely stride-2
	// record that `__str_to_bytes` fills at that stride — correct today, and the STRIDE bullet above is
	// exactly what would refuse a retyped stride-1 view at every declared byte-array position. The element
	// belongs to the program; only its BOUNDS were never asked. ⇒ `Parser.requireRawByteFillFitsItsElement`
	// asks the same `rangeHoldsEveryByte` this door's BOUNDS bullet asks, one quantifier tighter (the reading
	// file's own mint rather than every declaration of the name), and reports E3117.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE ARRIVING SIDE IS AN IDENTITY TEST AND NOT A STRIDE TEST, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A
	// TYPE HOLE MEASURED BEFORE IT SHIPPED.** "Both sides byte-packed" reads as the symmetric rule and is
	// WRONG: `rangedAliasStorageBytes` gives EVERY range that fits `u8.max` a 1-byte slot, so
	// `typealias Small = int(0 to 200)` makes `Array with Small` byte-packed too — and a symmetric door would
	// admit a `Small` buffer wherever a `Byte` one is declared, in ordinary USER code, silently erasing a
	// nominal distinction `namedAggregatesConflict` refuses today. The stride is what the RECORD says; the
	// element's declared RANGE is what it does not, and a 1-byte element is not a byte just because it fits in
	// one. `specs-shv2/bytearray-element-size.md`'s `byte-packed-alias-is-not-interchangeable-with-byte` pins it.
	// ⇒ what may cross is exactly ONE thing: `__ManagedByte`, which is `__`-RESERVED and carries no range of
	// its own for a declared `Byte` to contradict — a compiler-minted byte buffer is bytes
	// (`SynthesizedByteElementName`).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT IS ASKED AT EVERY COERCION SITE, THROUGH `aggregatesConflict`, AND THE ONE RUNG IT WAS NOT
	// REFUSED A LEGAL PROGRAM AT SEVEN OF THEM.** R4.7 wired only the call-argument site
	// (`SemanticCheck.checkOneArgType`) and argued the rest were unreachable: `__ManagedByte` was minted only
	// by `String.addressableBytes()`, which is stdlib-only, so the pair could arise only inside a stdlib
	// module — "a program nobody can write". The command-line rung widened the element to EVERY
	// compiler-synthesized byte buffer, and three of its producers are parsed out of ORDINARY USER SOURCE
	// (`__ManagedDirectory.filename`/`currentPath`, `__Builtins.commandLineArg`), which killed that premise
	// without moving the doors. SIX of the seven were then MEASURED, one probe each, refusing a program the
	// BOOTSTRAP compiles — `return`, reassignment, `otherwise`, a match arm's merge, a struct-literal /
	// union-payload store, and a generic call's type-parameter argument. The seventh is overload SCORING,
	// and ⛔ **the claim that its wrong `incompatible` was UNOBSERVABLE — "a candidate it refuses is
	// refused a second time by every sibling candidate and the call resolves anyway" — WAS FALSE** (N2b
	// review). When no candidate fits, the call does not resolve anyway: it resolves to the FIRST declared
	// overload and reports the refusal against THAT one's parameter. MEASURED by putting the bare rule back
	// into `overloadArgTypeFit` alone: `which(x Ints)` beside `which(m __ManagedMemory)`, called with a cwd
	// buffer, stopped compiling with `expected 'Array_Integer', got 'Array___ManagedByte'` — quoting the
	// parameter of the overload the call was never written against. All EIGHT are load-bearing and all
	// eight are pinned. The shortest of the six is four lines:
	//
	//     function cwd() returns __ManagedMemory
	//         return try __ManagedDirectory.currentPath() otherwise panic("cwd")
	//     end 'cwd'
	//
	// ⇒ the door is folded into `aggregatesConflict`, which every one of those sites already asked and which
	// now takes each side's (tag, nameId) beside its aggregate NAME. That shape is forced: THREE producers
	// spell the name those sites compare (`Parser.aggregateNameOf`, `SemanticCheck.aggregateNameFor`,
	// `typeArgAggregateName`, one per interner), so the name cannot be re-derived here — but the pair
	// CAN be passed, because a `genericInstance`'s id is whole-program in all three. Spelling
	// `namedAggregatesConflict(…) and not byteBufferBoundaryAdmits(…)` at each site instead would be the
	// one-fact-two-answers shape this door exists to end, and is exactly how seven of them came to disagree.
	// `specs-shv2/bytearray-element-size.md`'s `a-synthesized-buffer-crosses-every-declared-byte-buffer-door`
	// pins six of them in one program and
	// `overload-scoring-admits-a-synthesized-buffer-at-a-byte-buffer-candidate` pins the eighth.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND THERE IS A NINTH, WHICH DOES *NOT* COME THROUGH `aggregatesConflict` — `Parser.requireSameArrayInstance`
	// (W5).** `ByteArray.init(managed)` has a rule of its own (the argument's generic instance must EQUAL the
	// receiver alias's, which is element-agnostic and strictly stronger than the byte rule), so it asks raw
	// instance equality rather than an aggregate comparison — and therefore inherited none of the eight above.
	// It refused `stdlib/Console.maxon:68`'s `ByteArray.init(__Builtins.readStdin(n))` with
	// `E3005 expected '__ManagedMemory with Byte', got 'Array___ManagedByte'`, on a module the BOOTSTRAP
	// compiles. It now asks THIS function directly, after its own equality test.
	// ⚠ **SO THE COUNT IS NINE DOORS AND TWO CALL SITES, AND THAT ASYMMETRY IS THE THING TO KEEP IN VIEW WHEN
	// A TENTH ARRIVES**: eight ride `aggregatesConflict` and are covered by construction, one asks here by
	// name. A new whole-container coercion rule spelled as raw instance equality is invisible to the eight.
	// `specs-shv2/array-init.md`'s `a-synthesized-byte-buffer-adopts` and
	// `error.a-synthesized-buffer-is-refused-at-a-narrowed-byte` pin the ninth (admission and the BOUNDS
	// refusal that keeps it honest).
	// ⚠ Not to be confused with `append`'s "NINTH COERCION SITE" one function down: that site is deliberately
	// NOT one of these doors (see its own ⚠ paragraph), so the two nines count different things.
	export function byteBufferBoundaryAdmits(declaredTag ValueTypeTag, declaredNameId TypeNameId, gotTag ValueTypeTag, gotNameId TypeNameId) returns bool
		let declaredElement = self.arrayNamedElementName(declaredTag, nameId: declaredNameId)
		if not isByteElementName(declaredElement) 'declaredElementIsNotNamedForAByte'
			return false
		end 'declaredElementIsNotNamedForAByte'

		if not self.rosterElementHoldsEveryByte(declaredElement) 'declaredElementCannotHoldEveryByte'
			return false
		end 'declaredElementCannotHoldEveryByte'

		if not self.isBytePackedArrayInstance(declaredTag, nameId: declaredNameId) 'declaredRecordDoesNotStrideOneByte'
			return false
		end 'declaredRecordDoesNotStrideOneByte'

		return self.arrayNamedElementName(gotTag, nameId: gotNameId).equals(SynthesizedByteElementName)
	end 'byteBufferBoundaryAdmits'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE INFERRED-INT-COLUMN BOUNDARY — the byte door's twin, and the second place two DIFFERENT
	// container instances denote one record.** `[10, 20, 30]` names no element type: the author wrote three
	// integers and the compiler picked `Array with integer` for them (`internBuiltinIntegerInstance`). A
	// declared `typealias Integer = int(i64.min to i64.max)` + `Array with Integer` — the idiom nearly every
	// spec file in the tree opens with — is a DIFFERENT instance by name, so `IntList.create([10, 20, 30])`
	// was `E3005 expected 'Array_Integer', got 'Array_int'` on a program the runnable oracle compiles and runs
	// (MEASURED both ways; the oracle infers the literal's element from the position it sits in).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ARRIVING SIDE IS THE COMPILER'S OWN INFERENCE, AND THAT ASYMMETRY IS THE WHOLE LICENCE.** An
	// `integer` column is the one column type NO author spelled — every column a user writes arrives
	// `named`/`structRef`/`string` — so admitting it commits nothing the author decided. Two AUTHOR-written
	// spellings stay nominal in both directions, exactly as they do at the byte boundary and for the same
	// reason: shv2 compares declared aggregates by NAME, and this door is about a value that has no name to
	// compare.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE DECLARED SIDE MUST ADD NOTHING TO `int`, AND THAT ONE TEST COVERS BOTH WAYS IT COULD.**
	// `spansEveryIntInEveryFile` demands the full i64 span, which settles the VALUE SET (no value a literal
	// can hold is out of range, so nothing goes unchecked) and, as a consequence rather than a second
	// condition, the SLOT (`rangedAliasStorageBytes` gives the full span the machine word, which is the stride
	// the arriving record was created with). A narrower alias — `Byte`, `int(0 to 100)` — fails it and keeps
	// the refusal it has today, which is the right answer: its record would stride differently and its range
	// would go unchecked.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **A `Map` HAS TWO COLUMNS AND THE DOOR KNEW ONLY ABOUT ONE, WHICH REFUSED `/specs/map.md`'s OWN
	// PROGRAM.** `for-in.nested` writes `typealias IntMap = Map with (Integer, Integer)` and passes it a
	// `[1: 1, 2: 2, 3: 3]` literal — `E3005 expected 'IntMap', got 'Map_int_int'`, the identical sentence the
	// `Array` half of this door was built to stop, one container over. The literal's map is inferred exactly
	// as the literal's array is (`Parser.parseMapLiteralBody` reads both column types off the first pair), so
	// the LICENCE is unchanged and only the ARITY is: every column must independently denote one slot.
	//
	// A column pair that is simply IDENTICAL denotes one slot too. For a two-column `Map` that is what keeps
	// `["a": 1]` reaching a `Map with (String, Integer)`: one column inferred, one written by the author, and
	// refusing the pair because of the half that AGREES would be arbitrary.
	//
	// ⛔ **THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO ADD *"for a single-column `Array` that clause can never fire — identical
	// columns mean identical instances"*, AND THAT PREMISE IS FALSE SINCE `stdlib/Array.maxon` WAS LISTED
	// (ARR2).** A program declaring its own `type Array` leaves TWO `Array` declarations live — its own under
	// the bare name and the corpus's under this compile's mint (`contestStdlibTypeName`) — so
	// `Array with String` and `__Array with String` are DIFFERENT instances with IDENTICAL columns, and the
	// clause fires for a single-column container after all.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS ADMITTING THE RIGHT THING, WHICH IS WHY THE CURE IS THIS CORRECTION AND NOT A NARROWING.**
	// `declarationIsTheManagedRecord` admits an `Array` declaration ONLY in the sole-field-and-that-field-the-buffer
	// shape, so two such declarations over one element denote one record to the byte — same stride, same drop,
	// same clone — which is the very question this door exists to answer. Refusing the pair would leave a
	// program with its own container unable to call `String.from`, `String.split` or `toByteArray` at all,
	// for a nominal distinction with nothing behind it. `specs-shv2/array-declared-record.md` pins both
	// crossings, beside the two refusals that show the MEMBER surfaces staying apart.
	function inferredIntColumnBoundaryAdmits(declaredTag ValueTypeTag, declaredNameId TypeNameId, gotTag ValueTypeTag, gotNameId TypeNameId) returns bool
		if self.isArrayInstanceAt(gotTag, nameId: gotNameId) and self.isArrayInstanceAt(declaredTag, nameId: declaredNameId) 'bothAreArrayInstances'
			return self.columnsDenoteOneSlot(self.containerElementType(declaredNameId), gotColumn: self.containerElementType(gotNameId))
		end 'bothAreArrayInstances'

		// The SHAPE predicate, not the runtime one: this arm is about a container having two columns, which is
		// true of the corpus `Map` exactly as it was of the synthesized one. See `isMapShapedInstanceAt`.
		if self.isMapShapedInstanceAt(gotTag, nameId: gotNameId) and self.isMapShapedInstanceAt(declaredTag, nameId: declaredNameId) 'bothAreMapInstances'
			return self.columnsDenoteOneSlot(self.mapKeyType(declaredNameId), gotColumn: self.mapKeyType(gotNameId)) and self.columnsDenoteOneSlot(self.mapValueType(declaredNameId), gotColumn: self.mapValueType(gotNameId))
		end 'bothAreMapInstances'

		return false
	end 'inferredIntColumnBoundaryAdmits'

	// ⭐ **ONE COLUMN OF THE DOOR ABOVE.** Two columns denote one slot when they are the SAME type — compared
	// on the (tag, id) pair `GenericInstanceRegistry.instanceKey` already treats as a type's whole identity,
	// so this cannot call two types one where the interner calls them two — or when the arriving one is the
	// compiler's own `integer` inference and the declared one is a ranged alias that adds nothing to `int`.
	//
	// A name NO file declares as a ranged alias is a struct / union / interface column, which an integer slot
	// is not — the same `Map.get` channel `rosterElementHoldsEveryByte` reads, with the opposite default,
	// because here a missing declaration means "not an int alias" rather than "the compiler's own".
	function columnsDenoteOneSlot(declaredColumn MaxonType, gotColumn MaxonType) returns bool
		if maxonTypeTag(declaredColumn) == maxonTypeTag(gotColumn) and typeNameIdOf(declaredColumn) == typeNameIdOf(gotColumn) 'oneAndTheSameType'
			return true
		end 'oneAndTheSameType'

		if maxonTypeTag(gotColumn) != ValueTypeTag.integer 'gotColumnWasNamedByTheAuthor'
			return false
		end 'gotColumnWasNamedByTheAuthor'

		if maxonTypeTag(declaredColumn) != ValueTypeTag.named 'declaredColumnIsNotANamedLeaf'
			return false
		end 'declaredColumnIsNotANamedLeaf'

		return try self.typeAliases.spansEveryIntInEveryFile(self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(declaredColumn))) otherwise false
	end 'columnsDenoteOneSlot'

	// The BOUNDS half of the declared side, over a name the roster has already accepted. The registry MISS
	// arm is the same split `undeclaredNamedElementSlot` makes and for the same reason: the two
	// roster names are exactly the ones NO file declares in the general case — `__ManagedByte` is
	// compiler-owned and unspellable, and `Byte` is undeclared in a program whose only `Array with Byte` is
	// the one a `b"…"` literal minted for itself — so what they hold is the COMPILER's to state, and a
	// compiler-minted byte is a whole byte. Every other roster name reaching here IS declared, and its own
	// declarations answer.
	function rosterElementHoldsEveryByte(element ByteArray) returns bool
		return try self.typeAliases.holdsEveryByteInEveryFile(element) otherwise true
	end 'rosterElementHoldsEveryByte'

	// ⭐⭐ **MAY `append` COPY FROM THIS ARRAY INTO A RECEIVER OF `receiverGiid`? — THE NINTH COERCION SITE,
	// ASKED IN ONE PLACE FOR BOTH SURFACES OF THE RECORD (A4b).** `Array.append` and the buffer surface's
	// `append` are one operation with one precondition and two diagnostics (`Parser.requireArrayAppendArg`
	// and `requireManagedMemoryAppendArg`, which differ only in the type each one's SIGNATURE names).
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS NOT THE WHOLE-ARRAY COERCION A DECLARED PARAMETER ASKS, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE SEPARATION.**
	// A parameter HANDS THE CONTAINER OVER — the callee may push into it, and what it pushes is read back
	// through the caller's element — so there the identity of the array is the question, and
	// `aggregatesConflict` refuses `Array_Small` at an `Array_Byte` parameter even though every `Small`
	// value fits a byte. `append` hands nothing over: the argument is BORROWED, its element VALUES are
	// copied into the receiver's own storage, and the receiver stays its own type. So the honest question
	// is the one a `push` of each element would ask — *may this value be stored in that element?* — asked
	// once for the whole run, because a bulk byte copy has nowhere to put the runtime range check a `push`
	// gets (`InsertRangeChecks`). MEASURED: `b.push(<a Small value>)` panics `Range check failed: value
	// outside typealias 'Byte'`, while `b.append(<the Smalls>)` copied the same 150 in silently.
	//
	// ⇒ **TWO QUESTIONS, BOTH ASKED, NEITHER STANDING IN FOR THE OTHER:**
	//
	//   • **REPRESENTATION** (`sameTrivialArrayShape`) — same managedness, same `element_size@24`. This is
	//     what makes `__managed_append`'s byte copy legal at all, and it is the test that was here ALONE. A
	//     width is not a type: it admits EVERY one-byte trivial element in the program, which is exactly
	//     how a `Small` (and a `bool`) walked into a `Bytes` and how two enums walked into each other.
	//   • **VALUE DOMAIN** (`trivialElementAdmitsEveryValueOf`) — every value the argument's element can
	//     hold must be a value the receiver's element admits. Asymmetric on purpose: a `Byte`
	//     (`int(0 to 100)`) source into a `Small` (`int(0 to 200)`) destination cannot produce an
	//     out-of-range read and stays legal, and refusing both directions would be over-refusal.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND THERE IS NO THIRD QUESTION — IN PARTICULAR NOT `byteBufferBoundaryAdmits` (A4b review).** The
	// byte boundary is a NOMINAL whole-container door and is one-way by design: only `__ManagedByte` may be
	// adopted where a byte-holding `Byte` is DECLARED, never the reverse. `append` adopts nothing, so asking
	// it here imported that one-way-ness into a site with no reason for it — MEASURED, a `currentPath()`
	// buffer receiving an `Array with Byte` compiled at `59b56fda1` and stopped compiling once the rule
	// landed, the exact mirror of the acceptance case this rung added. The cure is not a second door in the
	// other direction: it is that the compiler-owned byte element HAS a value set
	// (`elementIntegerValueRange`), which makes the domain test answer both directions on its own. Asking
	// both would be one byte fact with two answers that nothing keeps in agreement — and the one that
	// diverges silently is the nominal door, because it admits rather than refuses.
	//
	// ⛔ **REPLACING THE FIRST WITH INSTANCE IDENTITY WOULD REFUSE A CORRECT PROGRAM.** `[1, 2, 3]` mints
	// `Array with int` (`internBuiltinIntegerInstance`) and `IntArray.create()` an `Array with Integer` —
	// two instances over ONE value set — and `arrays.md`'s `append-empty-to-nonempty` /
	// `append-nonempty-to-empty` join them. The domain test is what admits that pair without admitting the
	// pairs above; the giids genuinely differ and always will, because an instance interns on the element's
	// NAME.
	//
	export function arrayAppendArgAdmits(receiverGiid GenericInstanceId, argTag ValueTypeTag, argNameId TypeNameId, readerFilePath FilePath) returns bool
		if not self.isArrayInstanceAt(argTag, nameId: argNameId) 'notAnArrayAtAll'
			return false
		end 'notAnArrayAtAll'

		// A MANAGED receiver deep-clones each element through the RECEIVER's own `funcAddr(__clone_<E>)`
		// stamp and an OPAQUE one through the receiver's descriptor `copyFunc@32`; both are
		// receiver-specific, so nothing but the receiver's own instance fits. There is no representation to
		// share and no range to widen — the instance IS the question, which is why the two arms are one.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE `==` IS SAFE ONLY BECAUSE THE TAG TEST ABOVE HAS ALREADY RUN, AND THAT ORDER IS AN
		// INVARIANT, NOT AN ACCIDENT.** `TypeNameId` and `GenericInstanceId` share a numeric space
		// (`isArrayInstanceAt`'s own header), so comparing a bare name's number against an instance id can
		// answer TRUE by coincidence — never a compile error, and a non-`Array` argument would be admitted.
		// `isArrayInstanceAt` is what pins `argNameId` to a generic instance; hoisting this arm above it
		// would reintroduce that exact wrong answer.
		// ⚠ `main` grew a SEPARATELY-SPELLED opaque arm in `requireArrayAppendArg` while this rung was in
		// flight (the `list-custom-types` port, citing P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b-β). It states the SAME rule —
		// identical giid — and was subsumed here at the merge rather than kept beside this one, because two
		// spellings of one rule is precisely what this rung exists to remove.
		if self.containerElementIsManaged(receiverGiid) or self.containerElementIsOpaque(receiverGiid) 'receiverCopiesThroughItsOwnElement'
			return argNameId == receiverGiid
		end 'receiverCopiesThroughItsOwnElement'

		if not self.sameTrivialArrayShape(receiverGiid, otherGiid: argNameId) 'noSharedRepresentation'
			return false
		end 'noSharedRepresentation'

		return self.trivialElementAdmitsEveryValueOf(receiverGiid, sourceGiid: argNameId, readerFilePath: readerFilePath)
	end 'arrayAppendArgAdmits'

	// Do two array instances hold TRIVIAL elements of the same storage width — the REPRESENTATION half of
	// the rule above, and the whole of what the byte copy itself requires. Kept as its own function because
	// it is the half that is NOT a statement about types, and the rule's header turns on saying so.
	function sameTrivialArrayShape(giid GenericInstanceId, otherGiid GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return not self.containerElementIsManaged(otherGiid) and self.arrayElementSize(otherGiid) == self.arrayElementSize(giid)
	end 'sameTrivialArrayShape'

	// The VALUE-DOMAIN half. Two spellings of one element are one element, which the INSTANCE already
	// answers for — an `Array` instance interns on its element type, so equal giids are equal elements and
	// no bounds need be read at all. Everything else has to compare the two value sets.
	function trivialElementAdmitsEveryValueOf(destGiid GenericInstanceId, sourceGiid GenericInstanceId, readerFilePath FilePath) returns bool
		if destGiid == sourceGiid 'oneInstanceIsOneElement'
			return true
		end 'oneInstanceIsOneElement'

		return match self.elementIntegerValueRange(destGiid, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'destination'
			notIntegerValued gives false
			inclusive(low, high) gives self.elementValuesFitWithin(sourceGiid, destLow: low, destHigh: high, readerFilePath: readerFilePath)
		end 'destination'
	end 'trivialElementAdmitsEveryValueOf'

	// The SOURCE side of that comparison, split out because one `match` can read only one side and a nested
	// pair would hide which side each arm speaks for — `requireRawByteFillFitsItsElement`'s shape, for its
	// reason.
	function elementValuesFitWithin(sourceGiid GenericInstanceId, destLow ParsedInt, destHigh ParsedInt, readerFilePath FilePath) returns bool
		return match self.elementIntegerValueRange(sourceGiid, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'source'
			notIntegerValued gives false
			inclusive(low, high) gives inclusiveRangeContains(destLow, destHigh: destHigh, sourceLow: low, sourceHigh: high)
		end 'source'
	end 'elementValuesFitWithin'

	// ⭐ **THE INCLUSIVE INTEGER VALUE SET AN ARRAY'S ELEMENT ADMITS, AS `readerFilePath` RESOLVES IT.**
	// Only two element kinds have one: a bare `int` (the machine word's whole signed range) and a ranged
	// INT alias (its declared bounds). A `bool`, an `ExitCode`, an enum, a function type, an opaque `T` and
	// a float alias each have `notIntegerValued` — not because they are exotic, but because there is no
	// value set for a range test to compare and their only honest admission is the instance identity the
	// caller has already tried.
	//
	// ⚠ The reader's own file is the right quantifier, and it is the one A4a chose for the same registry:
	// a range-CONTESTED name is qualified per claimant by the time it is an instance's type argument
	// (`RangedAliasRegistry.settleRangeContests`), so both sides of one `append` resolve the same way and
	// an uncontested name answers identically from every file.
	function elementIntegerValueRange(giid GenericInstanceId, readerFilePath FilePath) returns ElementIntegerRange
		if maxonTypeTag(self.containerElementType(giid)) == ValueTypeTag.integer 'aBareMachineWordInt'
			return ElementIntegerRange.inclusive(BareIntegerElementLow, high: BareIntegerElementHigh)
		end 'aBareMachineWordInt'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE COMPILER-OWNED BYTE ELEMENT HAS A VALUE SET — IT SIMPLY HAS NO DECLARATION TO READ IT
		// OFF, AND ANSWERING `notIntegerValued` MADE THE DOMAIN TEST ONE-WAY (A4b review).** MEASURED: a
		// `currentPath()` buffer receiving an `Array with Byte` whose `Byte` is `int(0 to u8.max)` compiled
		// at `59b56fda1` and stopped compiling here — `expected '__ManagedMemory', got 'Array_Byte'` — the
		// exact MIRROR of `a-byte-that-holds-every-byte-still-appends-a-synthesized-buffer`, which this rung
		// added as an acceptance case. Nothing can be read back out of range in either direction: both
		// elements admit exactly the byte values, so both appends are correct programs.
		//
		// ⚠ The two bounds are `Project.ByteValueSetLow`/`High` — the SAME pair `rangeHoldsEveryByte`
		// measures every declared byte element against, because a second spelling of them here would be a
		// second definition of what a byte is. `arrayAppendArgAdmits`'s header carries the rest of the
		// argument, including why no nominal door belongs beside this one.
		if self.arrayNamedElementName(ValueTypeTag.genericInstance, nameId: giid).equals(SynthesizedByteElementName) 'aCompilerMintedBufferHoldsRawBytes'
			return ElementIntegerRange.inclusive(ByteValueSetLow, high: ByteValueSetHigh)
		end 'aCompilerMintedBufferHoldsRawBytes'

		return match self.arrayElementAliasOf(giid, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'element'
			undeclared gives ElementIntegerRange.notIntegerValued
			declared(alias) gives integerAliasValueRange(alias)
		end 'element'
	end 'elementIntegerValueRange'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS INSTANCE'S ELEMENT ADMIT EVERY BIT PATTERN OF ITS OWN SLOT? (E3118, A4c.)** The
	// question the buffer surface's RAW-BYTE WRITER has to ask, and it is one question at every stride:
	// `setByte` addresses a byte OFFSET, so on a record striding more than a byte it writes a FRAGMENT of an
	// element, and what the ARRAY surface reads back afterwards is an arbitrary UNSIGNED value of the whole
	// slot — the shared element load zero-extends (`ManagedMemoryRuntime.emitLoadElem`), which is what makes the
	// slot's value set unsigned and makes this comparable at all.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT IS NOT `rangeHoldsEveryByte` WIDENED, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A MEASURED WRONG ANSWER IN EACH
	// DIRECTION.** "Admits every BYTE" is this answer at a ONE-byte slot and nothing more general: asked at a
	// wider slot it refuses a correct `Array with Int` (A4a measured that and had to pull it back), and asked
	// only at a one-byte slot it lets `typealias Wide = int(0 to 1000)` read back **57089** through an
	// accessor declared `int(0 to 1000)`. So the one-byte rung ASKS that predicate rather than re-spelling
	// it, and every wider rung asks the slot.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE ADMITTING DIRECTION MUST BE A PROOF.** This door lets RAW BYTES into a declared element, so an
	// element whose value set the compiler cannot compare — a `bool` (two values in a one-byte slot), an
	// `ExitCode` (a PLATFORM-DEPENDENT domain), an enum's ordinal, a float alias whose bounds are IEEE-754
	// bit patterns, an opaque `T`, a function pointer — is REFUSED rather than guessed at. That is
	// `rangeHoldsEveryByte`'s own conservative argument one quantifier out. A MANAGED element never reaches
	// here at all: `Parser.requireBufferBytesAreNotAPointer` (E3110) has refused it a line earlier, because
	// its bytes are an ADDRESS at every offset.
	export function elementAdmitsEverySlotValue(giid GenericInstanceId, readerFilePath FilePath) returns bool
		match self.elementIntegerValueRange(giid, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'valueSet'
			inclusive(low, high) then return rangeHoldsEverySlotValue(low, high: high, slotBytes: self.arrayElementSize(giid))
			notIntegerValued then return self.elementIsAByteNoFileDeclares(giid)
		end 'valueSet'
	end 'elementAdmitsEverySlotValue'

	// The ONE element with no value set that still admits every pattern of its slot: the `Byte` a `b"…"`
	// literal MINTS FOR ITSELF in a program that declares no such alias. `undeclaredNamedElementSlot`
	// gives it a one-byte slot by construction and there is no declared range for a raw byte to fall outside
	// of — exactly the case `Parser.requireRawByteFillFitsItsElement` returns on, asked here of the slot
	// instead of the byte.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE DECLARATION IS ASKED AS WELL AS THE NAME, and dropping either half is a wrong acceptance.**
	// `isByteElementName` is a ROSTER over the two names shv2 gives a byte, so a `typealias Byte = float(…)`
	// is byte-NAMED and declared, and its bounds are IEEE-754 bit patterns no slot test can read; and a name
	// that is NOT on the roster and that no file declares is an ENUM or a boxed union's ordinal, which keeps
	// the machine word and admits only the ordinals its declaration lists. `__ManagedByte` cannot reach here
	// at all — `elementIntegerValueRange` hands it a byte's value set one arm up — so this is the literal's
	// own `Byte` alone.
	//
	// ⚠ The declaration is asked WHOLE-PROGRAM rather than of a reading file, and that is the quantifier the
	// SLOT has: `undeclaredNamedElementSlot` is reached exactly when `storageBytesInEveryFile` misses,
	// and it is the only thing that makes this element's slot one byte. Asking a file instead would accept a
	// name some OTHER file declares wide, whose slot is then that file's width and not a byte.
	function elementIsAByteNoFileDeclares(giid GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		let name = self.arrayNamedElementName(ValueTypeTag.genericInstance, nameId: giid)
		return isByteElementName(name) and not self.typeAliases.anyFileDeclares(name)
	end 'elementIsAByteNoFileDeclares'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE PLACE A RANGED ALIAS'S DECLARED BOUNDS BECOME A STORAGE WIDTH.** A trivial element is
	// stored INLINE, so its slot is as wide as its values need and no wider: a `named` element is a ranged-
	// int alias sized by the width that holds every file's declaration of the name
	// (`RangedAliasRegistry.storageBytesInEveryFile`), and every other trivial tag is its
	// `primitiveTypeByteSize` (`integer`/`float` 8, `boolean` 1, `ExitCode` 4).
	//
	// A trivial element is never a struct or a generic instance — every aggregate is a managed box, caught
	// by the `containerElementIsManaged` arm above — so this has no aggregate arms to carry and needs no
	// interner walk past the element's own name.
	//
	// ⚠ A `named` element that the registry MISSES is not one case but three, and they do not share an answer
	// — `undeclaredNamedElementSlot` is where that split is written down. A payload-free enum takes the slot
	// its tags need; a boxed union keeps the machine word its pointer needs; the byte-string literal's own
	// `Array with Byte` is one byte.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT ANSWERS A SLOT AND NOT A WIDTH, BECAUSE A NARROW SLOT IS TWO FACTS.** `enum-narrow-storage`
	// puts SIGNED values in narrow slots (`i8[] -1, 0, 1` is three bytes), and a reader of such a slot has to
	// sign-extend or it reads `255` for `-1`. Deriving the width here and the signedness somewhere else would
	// be one decision made twice over the same bounds, free to disagree precisely where it matters — so both
	// readers (`arrayElementSize` for the stamp, `narrowSignedSlotOf` for the read) come through this.
	function trivialElementSlot(t MaxonType) returns StorageSlot
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(t)
		if tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'notAName'
			// Every non-`named` trivial tag is an UNSIGNED-or-full-word slot: `bool` (1), `ExitCode` (4, whose
			// domain is `0 to u32.max` at widest), and `integer`/`float`/function at the machine word. None can
			// be negative in a narrow slot, so none extends.
			//
			// ⚠ **AN `ExitCode` ARRAY ELEMENT REACHES THIS ARM ONLY WHEN NOTHING DECLARES THE NAME — and when
			// something does, it is a `named` LEAF and goes below.** E3118's registry doc records the measured
			// consequence of the two answers being read as one: a raw byte at offset 3 of an element compared
			// against an EIGHT-byte slot it does not occupy read back **3741319169**, and `stdlib/Process.maxon`
			// being a listed module is what made the alias registry the single answer. This arm is the tag-only
			// fallback, not the door that sizes a declared `ExitCode`.
			return StorageSlot.create(primitiveTypeByteSize(tag), signExtends: false)
		end 'notAName'

		return self.namedTypeStorageSlot(self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(t)))
	end 'trivialElementSlot'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SLOT A `named` LEAF'S VALUES OCCUPY, KEYED BY NAME — and the NAME is the currency on purpose.**
	// A `MaxonType` carries an interned id, and there are TWO interners: this index's, and the per-FILE table a
	// parser holds. They assign ids in their own insertion orders, so an id crossed between them is right
	// exactly while the two coincide and silently wrong when they drift (OPEN #56, and `slotTypeThroughReceiver`
	// carries the three measured faces of it). A NAME cannot drift, which is what lets a caller holding a
	// FILE-space type ask this the same way `trivialElementSlot` asks it of an INDEX-space one.
	//
	// ⚠ **MEASURED, and it is exactly the hazard that header describes.** `narrowSignedSlotOf` first took a
	// `MaxonType` and was handed the parser's re-interned element type: `[Offset.behind, …].first()` over a
	// three-case enum at `-200 … 200` answered **65336** in a program with no `typealias Offsets` and `-200`
	// in one with it — the same source, the same slot, two interner orderings.
	export function namedTypeStorageSlot(name ByteArray) returns StorageSlot
		let declaredBytes = try self.typeAliases.storageBytesInEveryFile(name) otherwise 'noSuchAlias'
			return self.undeclaredNamedElementSlot(name)
		end 'noSuchAlias'

		// A ranged alias takes the UNSIGNED half of the ladder only — `rangedAliasStorageBytes` carries the two
		// reasons, and both are about that door rather than about the load.
		return StorageSlot.create(declaredBytes, signExtends: false)
	end 'namedTypeStorageSlot'

	// ⭐ **THE STORAGE SLOT OF A `named` ARRAY ELEMENT NO FILE DECLARES AS A RANGED ALIAS** — the MISS arm of
	// `trivialElementSlot`'s registry probe. It is a function and not a bare fallback because a miss means
	// THREE different things, and only one of them is the machine word:
	//
	//   • **`Byte`** — the `Array with Byte` instance a `b"…"` literal MINTS FOR ITSELF
	//     (`internArrayByteInstance`) in a program that declares no such alias. No user type can name that
	//     instance (`Array with Byte` over an undeclared `Byte` does not resolve), so the literal's own record
	//     is the only one there is — and it is stamped byte-PACKED by construction (`ByteStringElementSize`).
	//     Answering the machine word here would leave this door and that record disagreeing about ONE
	//     instance's stride, which is the two-producer shape `arrayElementSize` exists to end.
	//   • **a PAYLOAD-FREE enum or union** — its value is a bare tag, and `enum-narrow-storage` narrows it to
	//     the slot that tag set needs.
	//   • **anything else** — a BOXED union, whose value is a heap POINTER and not a tag at all
	//     (`specs-shv2/array-enum-element-size.md` pins that stride at 8 for exactly such a union), or a name
	//     no declaration binds. `isBoxed` is asked and not assumed: it is the classifier that decides whether
	//     the slot holds a tag or an address, and reading a pointer out of a one-byte slot is not a narrower
	//     answer, it is a wrong one.
	function undeclaredNamedElementSlot(name ByteArray) returns StorageSlot
		if isByteElementName(name) 'bytePacked'
			return StorageSlot.create(ByteStringElementSize as ByteOffset, signExtends: false)
		end 'bytePacked'

		// ⚠ Gated on the NON-ALLOCATING `contains` twin, for `typeNameVisibleHere`'s measured reason: `enumOf`
		// boxes a `DeclaredEnum` even to say `undeclared`, and this door is on `arrayElementSize`'s path —
		// asked for every `named` element in the program, nearly none of which is an enum.
		if not self.containsEnum(name) 'notANominalDeclaration'
			return StorageSlot.machineWord()
		end 'notANominalDeclaration'

		match self.enumOf(name) 'declaration'
			declared(layout) then return StorageSlot.machineWord() if layout.isBoxed() else payloadFreeEnumTagSlot(layout)
			undeclared then panic("undeclaredNamedElementSlot: containsEnum answered yes for '{String.from(name)}' but enumOf answered undeclared — the two read one registry under one key")
		end 'declaration'
	end 'undeclaredNamedElementSlot'

	// The element DESTRUCTOR callee for a managed-element `Array with T` (P1.7 slice 3) — the runtime entry the
	// drop walk calls on each live slot: a String → `__str_decref`, a struct/union that owns managed heap → its
	// `__destruct_<T>` (a scalar-only struct/union box → the trivial `__mm_decref`), a nested Array → `__managed_decref`.
	// Routed through the SHARED `managedFieldDropCallee` over the element type, so the stamped element destructor
	// equals the drop callee any managed FIELD of the same type gets — the moved-in set and the dropped set agree
	// by construction. Precondition: `containerElementIsManaged`.
	export function arrayElementDropCallee(id GenericInstanceId) returns ByteArray
		return managedFieldDropCallee(self, typeNames: self.typeNames, fieldType: self.containerElementType(id))
	end 'arrayElementDropCallee'

	// ⭐ **THE DESTRUCTOR STAMP ONE CONTAINER COLUMN IS BORN WITH, AS A DESCRIPTION** — what a top-level
	// container global's record carries to `ModuleInit`, which has no `ProgramSignatures` to ask (P1.7 slice
	// 2b, widened to every container by spec-port `map-struct-bytearray`). A MANAGED column carries the drop
	// callee its teardown walk invokes through a `callIndirect` on each live slot; a trivial one carries 0
	// and its walk is skipped.
	//
	// It composes the same two accessors `Parser.columnDestroyValue` does, off the same `slotTypeIsManaged`
	// decision, so the destructor a global's record is stamped with equals the one a body's `create()` of the
	// same instance gets. It is asked ONCE PER MANAGED GLOBAL, which is why it may box a union where the
	// parser's per-SITE emitter deliberately does not.
	//
	// ⚠ It is keyed on the column's TYPE and not on a `(giid, which-column)` pair, for the reason its value
	// twin states: a `Map` has TWO of these stamps, and a per-column copy is exactly how the key's and the
	// value's teardown come to disagree about what "managed" means.
	//
	// ⛔ **AN OPAQUE COLUMN HAS NO STAMP HERE AND NEVER HAD ONE — SEE `requireStaticColumnStamp` (W134).**
	function columnDestroyStamp(columnType MaxonType) returns ContainerCreateStamp
		self.requireStaticColumnStamp(columnType, asker: "ProgramSignatures.columnDestroyStamp")
		if self.slotTypeIsManaged(columnType) 'managed'
			return ContainerCreateStamp.dropCallee(self.slotTypeDropCallee(columnType))
		end 'managed'

		return ContainerCreateStamp.inline(TrivialDestructor)
	end 'columnDestroyStamp'

	// ⛔⛔ **THE ONE SENTENCE SAYING AN OPAQUE COLUMN HAS NO STATIC DESTRUCTOR STAMP, AND ITS TWO ASKERS ARE THE
	// TWO PLACES A STAMP IS PRODUCED (W134).** `Parser.columnDestroyValue` (a body's `create()`, as a VALUE) and
	// `columnDestroyStamp` (a top-level global's record, as a DESCRIPTION) each end in a `managed → drop callee`
	// / `otherwise → 0` pair, and **a 0 there is not a refusal, it is a leak that compiles**: the teardown walk
	// reads `element_drop@24` (or `element_destroy@40`) and skips a chain whose elements a managed instantiation
	// really does own. Before W134 both answered 0 for a `typeParameter` column and both were correct only
	// because the parser's element gate refused every opaque chain outright.
	//
	// ⭐ **THAT GATE IS GONE, AND WHAT REPLACES IT IS A BRANCH AT EACH PRODUCER'S CALLER, NOT A DIFFERENT
	// ANSWER HERE.** An opaque `create` reads `destroyFunc@40` off the enclosing instance's threaded layout
	// descriptor at RUN TIME (`Parser.emitOpaqueContainerCreateOp` → `LowerMaxonToStd.appendOpaqueContainerCreate`),
	// so whoever holds an opaque instance takes that road *before* asking for a stamp. This panic is what makes
	// "the caller branched" a checked property of both producers rather than a convention one of them could
	// drift out of — the same discipline `instanceCreateBuildsTheManagedRecord` states for the seed/emit pair.
	//
	// ⚠ The top-level asker cannot reach it TODAY for a structural reason worth writing down rather than
	// relying on: a `.data` global is not inside a generic body, so no type parameter is in scope for its
	// element — and `__module_init` carries no layout-descriptor parameter to read one from even if it were.
	// That is precisely why a silent 0 there would have gone unnoticed.
	export function requireStaticColumnStamp(columnType MaxonType, asker String)
		if self.slotTypeIsOpaque(columnType) 'opaqueColumn'
			panic("{asker}: an OPAQUE type-parameter column has no statically known destructor to stamp — its `create` reads `destroyFunc@40` off the enclosing instance's layout descriptor at run time, so this producer's caller must branch on `containerElementIsOpaque` before asking. Stamping 0 here is not a refusal, it is a leak that compiles")
		end 'opaqueColumn'
	end 'requireStaticColumnStamp'

	// ⭐ **DOES THIS BUILTIN CONTAINER HAVE A `create()` AT ALL?** The one home of that question, so the body
	// door and the top-level door refuse the same instances. Exactly one builtin says no — a
	// `__ManagedListNode` handle, which only an insertion can mint — and it is stated as the exception it is
	// rather than by enumerating the five that do.
	export function instanceHasBuiltinCreate(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return not self.isManagedListNodeInstance(id)
	end 'instanceHasBuiltinCreate'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHOSE CODE BUILDS THIS INSTANCE'S EMPTY RECORD — THE RUNTIME'S, OR THE PROGRAM'S? (W41)** The ONE
	// derivation behind every decision a top-level `<Alias>.create()` turns on, and there are three of them:
	// which callee `__module_init` emits (`containerCreateCall`), whether that callee must be ROOTED and its
	// slot KEPT (`declInitializerCall`), and which gate the top-level door runs the instance through
	// (`Parser.requireGlobalContainerIsCreatable`). Three readers, three different concerns, one fact — and
	// re-spelling `isBuiltinGenericBaseName(genericInstanceBaseName(id))` at each is how two of them would come
	// to disagree about who owns a `create`.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE COMPLEMENT OF THE BUILTIN ROSTER AND NOT A SECOND ROSTER**, which is what makes it
	// self-maintaining: striking `Map` from `isBuiltinGenericBaseName` (W41 listed the module, W105 removed the
	// name) makes this predicate answer true for every `Map with (K, V)` with nothing here edited. A container
	// promoted or demoted moves both answers at once.
	export function instanceCreateIsDeclared(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return not self.isBuiltinGenericBaseName(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id))
	end 'instanceCreateIsDeclared'

	// The `create()` static a DECLARED generic's instance is born through — `Map.create` for every
	// `Map with (K, V)` (W41). It is the SAME name a body's `StrMap.create()` links to
	// (`Parser.qualifiedCallBaseName` mangles the alias to its base, then joins the method), so the record a
	// global's slot holds and the record the identical call in a function body builds come out of one function.
	// Precondition: `instanceCreateIsDeclared`.
	export function declaredInstanceCreateCallee(id GenericInstanceId) returns ByteArray
		return qualifiedMethodName(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id), methodName: CreateMethod)
	end 'declaredInstanceCreateCallee'

	// ⭐⭐ **MAY `readerFilePath` NAME `callee`?** The function half of `Visibility`, answered where a call in a
	// FUNCTION BODY cannot ask it: a top-level initializer's call is decided inside `queryProgramSignatures`,
	// before any file is parsed and long before `FunctionNameIndex` — which is what
	// `SemanticCheck.calleeVisibleFrom` reads — exists. The tier and the declaring file come from the SWEEP's
	// own column, and the rule they are fed to is the one `isVisibleFrom` already owns, so the two askers can
	// disagree about no declaration they both see.
	//
	// A callee no file declares is VISIBLE: "there is no such function" is a different mistake, and the
	// caller's own `globalFactoryReturn` reports it with the right sentence.
	//
	// The refusing TIER rides back with the verdict because the diagnostic's CODE is chosen by it (E3008 vs
	// E3088), and asking for it separately would be a second lookup of the same key that could answer about
	// a different declaration if anything refiled the name in between.
	export function calleeVisibilityFrom(callee ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns CalleeVisibility
		let site = try self.funcDeclSites.get(callee) otherwise return CalleeVisibility.visible
		if self.isVisibleFrom(site.visibility, declFilePath: site.declFilePath, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'nameableHere'
			return CalleeVisibility.visible
		end 'nameableHere'

		return CalleeVisibility.hidden(site.visibility)
	end 'calleeVisibilityFrom'

	// ⭐⭐ **HOW A DIAGNOSTIC SPELLS A CALLEE IT HAS RESOLVED: BY ITS MODULE NAMESPACE (W81).**
	// `stdlib.Expect.equal`, not `Expect.equal`. `specs/testing-assertions.md`'s `error.forgotten-try` is the
	// canonical pin and shv2 answered the bare name, which named a function the reader can find but a MODULE
	// they cannot — the same shortfall E3095's candidate list already refuses to make when two directories
	// contest a name.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE RULE IS THE REFERENCE'S AND IT IS NOT A STDLIB SPECIAL CASE.** The bootstrap qualifies EVERY
	// declaration by its path relative to the compile root (`2-Parser.cs:1184`
	// `NamespaceFromProjectRelativePath`), which is what makes `api.unusedHelper` and `stdlib.helpers.…` one
	// rule rather than two. shv2 keeps its registration names BARE — namespace-qualifying them is a
	// resolution decision it takes only under contest — so the qualification is applied HERE, at the render,
	// which is the one thing the reference's registration name was doing for a human.
	//
	// A callee no file declares — a runtime symbol, a builtin, a name the program got wrong — has no
	// namespace and is returned unchanged; so is a ROOT-level declaration, which is every user function in
	// every fragment the suite compiles. That is what keeps this confined to the callees whose declaring
	// directory the author never opened.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT HAS EXACTLY ONE ASKER TODAY, AND THAT IS A SCOPE DECISION RATHER THAN THE END OF THE RULE.**
	// The asker is E3057's sentence, at the line where `throwsMap.contains(callee)` has just PROVEN the name
	// resolves to a declared throwing function — which is the whole of the line that holds: **qualify a
	// declaration the compiler RESOLVED; never a name it could not.** The reference draws the same line, by
	// accident rather than by rule (its registration name simply IS qualified, so a diagnostic shows it iff
	// it reads the symbol table rather than the source token) — and its unresolved-name messages print the
	// author's spelling for that reason.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SAME LINE PUTS FOUR MORE DIAGNOSTIC FAMILIES ON THIS SIDE OF IT, AND W81 DID NOT MOVE THEM.**
	// E3055 (*"does not throw"*), E3008 / E3088 (not exported / module-scoped — where the namespace is the
	// very fact the sentence is about) and E3036 (arity) all speak about a declaration that resolved, and the
	// oracle qualifies every one of them. MEASURED by building it: doing so moves **~50 committed expectations
	// across 12+ spec files**, three of which (`console-stdin.md:58`, `try-postfix-target.md:452`,
	// `void-call-result.md:489`) carry PROSE recording the bare spelling as a deliberate difference from the
	// reference. That is a tree-wide diagnostic-naming policy with its own corpus churn, not a side effect of
	// listing a stdlib module, so it is reported rather than taken here.
	//
	// ⛔ **AND THE BLANKET VERSION IS UNSOUND, WHICH IS THE OTHER HALF OF WHY THE ASKER IS ONE.** Applied at
	// `SemanticCheck.callDiagnosticNoun` — the single door every call diagnostic already shares — it also
	// qualifies the messages about a name that resolved to NOTHING: `call to undefined function 'helper'`
	// became `… 'alpha.helper'`, naming a symbol absent from the program. That door's header carries the
	// measurement.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN ALREADY-QUALIFIED REGISTRATION NAME IS LEFT ALONE, and the test is the SET that made it
	// qualified rather than a re-scan of its bytes.** A contested free function registers as `alpha.pick`
	// (`freeFunctionRegistrationName`), and joining its namespace again would read `alpha.alpha.pick`.
	// `namespaceQualifiedFunctions` is exactly "this spelling already carries its directory", so the guard
	// and the mint cannot come to disagree about which names do.
	export function diagnosticCalleeSpelling(callee ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		let site = try self.funcDeclSites.get(callee) otherwise return callee
		if self.namespaceQualifiedFunctions.contains(callee) 'theSpellingAlreadyCarriesItsDirectory'
			return callee
		end 'theSpellingAlreadyCarriesItsDirectory'

		let namespace = self.namespaceOf(site.declFilePath)
		if namespace.isEmpty() 'aRootLevelDeclaration'
			return callee
		end 'aRootLevelDeclaration'

		return namespaceQualifiedName(namespace, name: callee)
	end 'diagnosticCalleeSpelling'

	// ⭐ **WHAT A CANDIDATE TOP-LEVEL FACTORY RETURNS, CLASSIFIED FOR THE ONE DECISION THAT READS IT** — may
	// a `.data` slot hold it? A RECORD can (the slot holds a pointer `__module_init` fills); a scalar cannot
	// (its value is written into the image, which happens before any code runs). The scalar arm carries the
	// type's DISPLAY NAME because only this side holds the interner those ids live in.
	export function globalFactoryReturn(callee ByteArray) returns GlobalFactoryReturn
		let declared = try self.returnTypes.get(callee) otherwise return GlobalFactoryReturn.undeclared
		match declared 'returns'
			void then return GlobalFactoryReturn.void
			value(valueType) then return self.globalFactoryValueReturn(valueType)
		end 'returns'
	end 'globalFactoryReturn'

	// ⭐ **THE SAME CLASSIFICATION, REDUCED TO THE ONE BIT A CLAIM TEST NEEDS** (spec-port `lazy-static`) —
	// asked by `Parser.atConstInitializerCall` of a BARE call, which is claimed only when the result can
	// occupy a slot (see there for why that shape asks more than the qualified one does). It routes through
	// `globalFactoryReturn` rather than re-deriving the answer, so the door that CLAIMS a call and the door
	// that REFUSES one cannot come to disagree about what a `.data` slot can hold.
	export function globalFactoryReturnsARecord(callee ByteArray) returns bool
		match self.globalFactoryReturn(callee) 'returns'
			record then return true
			// ⚠ A RANGE, so an arm appended past `undeclared` is EXCLUDED and this site fails to compile until
			// it has been visited — the discipline `TopLevelConstant`'s arms state, applied to the one place
			// that reduces this union to a bit.
			scalar to undeclared then return false
		end 'returns'
	end 'globalFactoryReturnsARecord'

	// The classification of a factory's declared VALUE return type — split out so the `void` arm above stays
	// one line and this one holds the whole managed/scalar question, which is `slotTypeIsManaged`'s.
	function globalFactoryValueReturn(valueType MaxonType) returns GlobalFactoryReturn
		if not self.slotTypeIsManaged(valueType) 'scalar'
			return GlobalFactoryReturn.scalar(maxonTypeName(valueType, typeNames: self.typeNames))
		end 'scalar'

		// ⭐ **THE TAG IS THE WHOLE TEST (S2j).** A record whose type is CONCRETE by now — a `structRef`
		// (`RecordedReturnTypeRule.declaredStruct` has run, over a declared `type` AND a tuple alias), a
		// `string`, a `character`, a `genericInstance` — is one the synthesized init and cleanup can access
		// its slot through. One still spelling a bare `named` is not, and the honest answer is a refusal
		// rather than a width. See `unbridgedRecord`.
		if maxonTypeTag(valueType) == ValueTypeTag.named 'noConcreteSlotAccess'
			return GlobalFactoryReturn.unbridgedRecord(maxonTypeName(valueType, typeNames: self.typeNames))
		end 'noConcreteSlotAccess'

		return GlobalFactoryReturn.record
	end 'globalFactoryValueReturn'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH RUNTIME `create` BUILDS THIS INSTANCE'S RECORD, AND WHAT IT IS STAMPED WITH — the ONE
	// description a top-level container global reaches `ModuleInit` through** (spec-port
	// `map-struct-bytearray`, which widened P1.7 slice 2b's `Array`-only door to every builtin container).
	// The routing is settled HERE, where the instance registry is, because the synthesis holds no index.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS A SECOND COMPOSITION OF THE SAME DECISIONS, NEVER A SECOND DECIDER.** `Parser`'s body
	// emitters (`emitArrayCreateOp` / `emitListCreateOp` / `emitVectorCreateOp`) build the same calls as VALUES and deliberately box no description per site —
	// MEASURED at +1 allocation per creation site in the program (see `Parser.arrayElementDestroyValue`).
	// What must not be written twice is the RULE, and none of it is: which container this is
	// (`instanceCreateIsDeclared`/`isListInstance`/…), which column owns what (`slotTypeIsManaged`), which drop
	// (`slotTypeDropCallee`) and which stride (`arrayElementSize`) are each answered in one place and read
	// from both sides. This is asked ONCE PER MANAGED GLOBAL.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A DECLARED GENERIC IS THE SAME DESCRIPTION WITH NO STAMPS (W41)** — `var g = StrMap.create()` once
	// `stdlib/Map.maxon` is listed and `Map` is an ordinary user generic. A builtin container's `create` is a
	// RUNTIME entry that has to be handed the strides and column destructors it cannot look up; a declared
	// generic's is a real `create()` static the compiler can simply CALL, and it needs none of them — it builds
	// its own fields from its own declaration. So the widening is one arm and nothing downstream moves:
	// `ModuleInit.builtContainerRecord` already emits `create.callee` with `create.stamps`, and an empty stamp
	// list makes that a bare `call Map.create`.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE DICTIONARY COLUMNS ARE NOT THIS DESCRIPTION'S JOB, AND THAT IS WHY THE ARM IS ONE LINE** — the
	// lowering threads them off the record's own type, which `foldedValueType` settles as `genericInstance(id)`
	// for this very outcome. `ModuleInit.builtContainerRecord` states that handoff in full;
	// `Parser.requireDeclaredGenericGlobalCreate` holds the instance to the `Self`-return it rests on.
	function containerCreateCall(id GenericInstanceId) returns ContainerCreateCall
		var stamps = ContainerCreateStampArray.create()

		// A DECLARED generic's `create()` takes no arguments (`Parser.requireDeclaredGenericGlobalCreate`), so it
		// is born EMPTY and carries no stamps; whether anything then FILLS it is `declaredContainerElementAdd`'s
		// question, and it has an answer only for the one declared shape a top-level form hands elements to.
		if self.instanceCreateIsDeclared(id) 'declaredGeneric'
			return ContainerCreateCall.create(self.declaredInstanceCreateCallee(id), stamps: stamps, sizing: ContainerSizing.grown, elementAdd: self.declaredContainerElementAdd(id))
		end 'declaredGeneric'

		// ⚠ **A `Set` ARM AND A `Map` ARM STOOD HERE AND WENT WITH THEIR RUNTIME (W105).** Each named a
		// `__set_create` / `__map_create` and pushed its columns' destructor stamps; both containers are
		// DECLARED generics now, so they return through the `declaredGeneric` arm above with no stamps at all —
		// a declared `create()` builds its own fields from its own declaration.

		// A `List` names none either, and for the `Map`'s reason rather than a shape one: `__list_append` IS the
		// `(record, value)` pair this description carries, but no top-level form hands a list elements — the
		// `List from […]` construction is a function body's (`atListFromConstruction`), and the initializer walk
		// has no list arm — so naming a callee here would be a route nothing takes. See `ContainerElementAdd`.
		if self.isChainInstance(id) 'chain'
			stamps.push(self.columnDestroyStamp(self.containerElementType(id)))
			return ContainerCreateCall.create(ListCreateName, stamps: stamps, sizing: ContainerSizing.grown, elementAdd: ContainerElementAdd.unsupported)
		end 'chain'

		if not self.instanceCreateBuildsTheManagedRecord(id) 'notACreatableContainer'
			panic("ProgramSignatures.containerCreateCall: generic instance {id} is not a builtin container with a `create` — a DECLARED generic returned one arm above, and every builtin reaching here was admitted through `Parser.requireContainerIsCreatable`, which refuses everything this router has no arm for")
		end 'notACreatableContainer'

		// `Array` and `Vector` share `__managed_create` and its two stamps; a `Vector`'s elements are addressable
		// from birth, so its record is then sized. That is the same pair of calls `emitVectorCreateOp` makes.
		stamps.push(ContainerCreateStamp.inline(self.arrayElementSize(id)))
		stamps.push(self.columnDestroyStamp(self.containerElementType(id)))
		return ContainerCreateCall.create(ManagedCreateName, stamps: stamps, sizing: ContainerSizing.fixed(self.genericInstanceFixedSize(id)) if self.isVectorInstance(id) else ContainerSizing.grown, elementAdd: ContainerElementAdd.push(ManagedPushName))
	end 'containerCreateCall'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH DECLARED GENERIC A TOP-LEVEL FORM CAN HAND ELEMENTS TO, AND WHAT THEY JOIN IT THROUGH
	// (W90).** Exactly one does: a `Set`-SHAPED instance, because `Set from […]` is a construction the constant
	// evaluator folds to a member list (`Parser.evalConstSetFrom`) and every other declared generic reaches a
	// global's slot only as a bare `<Alias>.create()`, which produces nothing to add.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE SHAPE QUESTION AND NOT THE RUNTIME ONE — `isSetShapedInstance`, deliberately.** This arm is
	// reached only when the base IS declared — which since W105 is every `Set` there is — and what decides the
	// callee is that the CONSTRUCTION which produced these members is a set's, which is a fact about the base NAME.
	//
	// ⛔ **A DECLARED CONTAINER THAT IS NOT `Set`-SHAPED KEEPS `unsupported`, AND THE PANIC BEHIND IT IS THE
	// POINT** (`ModuleInit.elementAddCallee`): a description arriving with elements and naming no callee means
	// a top-level form was opened without teaching this router what its container adds through, and dropping
	// the elements silently is the wrong answer to that.
	function declaredContainerElementAdd(id GenericInstanceId) returns ContainerElementAdd
		if not self.isSetShapedInstance(id) 'noTopLevelFormProducesElements'
			return ContainerElementAdd.unsupported
		end 'noTopLevelFormProducesElements'

		return ContainerElementAdd.push(qualifiedMethodName(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id), methodName: InsertMethod))
	end 'declaredContainerElementAdd'

	// The element CLONE strategy for a managed-element `Array with T` (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b) — the DUAL of
	// `arrayElementDropCallee`. The front end stamps `funcAddr(<the direct callee>)` into the `_managed`
	// array-copy call, exactly as it stamps `funcAddr(<the drop callee>)` into `element_destroy@40`. Routed
	// through the SAME `managedFieldCloneStrategy` a managed FIELD of the element's type uses, so the element's
	// clone matches its own field-clone by construction. Precondition: `arrayElementSupportsDeepClone`, which
	// guarantees the result is `direct` (an element cloner is invoked through `callIndirect`, a `(box) -> box`
	// shape a `managedArray`/`unsupported` strategy cannot satisfy).
	export function arrayElementCloneStrategy(id GenericInstanceId) returns ManagedCloneStrategy
		return managedFieldCloneStrategy(self, typeNames: self.typeNames, fieldType: self.containerElementType(id))
	end 'arrayElementCloneStrategy'

	// ⭐ **THE `<Base> with integer` INSTANCE FOR AN INFERRED-INT LITERAL CONSTRUCTION.** A bare `[…]` int-array
	// literal (`baseName` = `Array`, P1.7 slice 1) and a `Set from [ints]` construction (`baseName` = `Set`,
	// P1.7b slice B) both infer their `integer` element from the literal, so neither has a typealias to intern
	// its instance from. The SWEEP interns the one well-known instance for its base whenever a file's tokens
	// contain the construct (`Parser.foldDeclaredSignaturesInto`: a `[` for the array, a `Set from` for the set),
	// and the parser reads the same giid back at construction time. Interning is IDEMPOTENT — `TypeNameInterner.intern`
	// and `GenericInstanceRegistry.intern` both return the existing entry without mutating when present — so the
	// sweep's call is the WRITE and every parser call is a pure READ (the instance is always interned before any
	// file is parsed, which keeps a per-file parse from being the first to mutate the shared registry). At this
	// slice the elements are all integer (a non-integer element is a later slice, rejected E2015), so one instance
	// per base suffices. The `integer` element canonicalizes to the `int` conformer (`conformerTypeArgName`), so
	// the witnesses are `__witness_int.*`. It is deliberately NOT folded into the content hash: a file gaining the
	// construct re-parses on its OWN bytes, and no OTHER file's meaning depends on this shared, deterministic instance.
	export function internBuiltinIntegerInstance(baseName ByteArray) returns GenericInstanceId
		return self.genericInstances.intern(self.typeNames.intern(baseName), args: singletonInteger())
	end 'internBuiltinIntegerInstance'

	// ⭐ **THE `Array with Byte` INSTANCE FOR A `b"…"` BYTE-STRING LITERAL (P1.7 slice 2).** A byte-string
	// literal has no typealias to intern from (`b"hi"` names no type), so — like the `[…]` int-array literal
	// above — the SWEEP interns this ONE well-known instance whenever a file's tokens contain a byte-string
	// literal (`Parser.foldDeclaredSignaturesInto`), and the parser reads the giid back at literal time.
	// Interning is IDEMPOTENT, so the sweep is the WRITE and every parser call a pure READ. The element is
	// `named("Byte")` — the SAME reference a user `typealias ByteArray = Array with Byte` interns, so a
	// byte-string and a `ByteArray`-typed value share one gid and are type-identical.
	// ⚠ MEMOIZED — see `arrayByteInstance` for why the answer is stable and why the flag is a flag rather
	// than a sentinel. The first ask is still the WRITE that mints the instance, so the registry contents
	// (and every id derived from them) are exactly what they were before the memo existed.
	// ⚠⚠ **AND IT IS SCOPED TO THE READER (N2), WHICH IS WHAT KEEPS E2015 HONEST.** `Byte` is a name a USER
	// program may declare, so "the `Array with Byte` instance" is not one thing in a program whose
	// declarations of `Byte` disagree about the range: `stdlib/File.maxon`'s byte buffer must stride 1 while
	// the reader that declared `int(0 to 1000)` must still meet the wall its own declaration builds. Pointing
	// this at a stride-1 compiler byte instead would make `Parser.requireByteStringBlobFitsItsElement`'s
	// stride clause — which reads this function's answer through `arrayElementSize`, one call away — see 1 in
	// every program, and `bytearray-element-size.md`'s two wide-`Byte` refusals would go green by losing their
	// premise rather than by being fixed. **Its BOUNDS clause (A3r) rides the same instance and would lose the
	// same premise**, since `arrayElementAliasOf` reads the element off exactly this id.
	export function internArrayByteInstance(readerFilePath FilePath) returns GenericInstanceId
		return self.fileScopedInstance(self.bareArrayByteInstance(), filePath: readerFilePath)
	end 'internArrayByteInstance'

	// The UNSCOPED instance the scoped answers above are all derived from — what the declaration SWEEP mints,
	// and the only form it can mint: the sweep is what DECIDES the contest, so it runs before there is a
	// reader's answer to give. Its two callers are the sweep's own registrations
	// (`Parser.foldDeclaredSignaturesInto`, `registerManagedFileType`/`registerManagedDirectoryType`); every
	// reader goes through `internArrayByteInstance` and gets its own file's.
	export function bareArrayByteInstance() returns GenericInstanceId
		if self.arrayByteInstanceComputed 'alreadyMinted'
			return self.arrayByteInstance
		end 'alreadyMinted'

		var args = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		args.push(MaxonType.named(self.typeNames.intern(ByteElementTypeName)))
		self.arrayByteInstance = self.genericInstances.intern(self.typeNames.intern(self.arrayRecordBaseName()), args: args)
		self.arrayByteInstanceComputed = true
		return self.arrayByteInstance
	end 'bareArrayByteInstance'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES ANY NAME IN THIS PROGRAM HAVE DECLARATIONS THAT DISAGREE — ABOUT A RANGE *OR* ABOUT AN
	// INSTANCE? (N3 review)** The one bool `fileScopedInstance` and `rekeyScopedGenericAliases` both
	// short-circuit on, and it is written ONCE because the two SPELLINGS of it had already cost this rung a
	// wrong answer: `settleGenericAliasContest`'s ⛔ paragraph records `rekeyScopedGenericAliases` gated on
	// the GENERIC half alone, which skipped N2's RANGE re-key and made
	// `bytearray-element-size/a-byte-two-files-disagree-about-is-two-types` stop refusing — invisible to a
	// filtered run, caught only by the full suite. Two spellings of one predicate is this project's signature
	// bug, and here it had already fired once.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE NEXT CONTEST GOES HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE.** A FUNCTION typealias has the identical whole-program
	// defect (`Parser.readTypeAliasDeclaration` names it), so a third disjunct is expected; spelled twice,
	// teaching one copy and not the other is a wrong answer at whichever door was missed, with no compile
	// error at either.
	//
	// ⚠ It is deliberately NOT the gate on `settleRangeScopedInstances`, whose own early return stays
	// range-only: that pass mints RANGE variants and has nothing to do for a generic contest. Its gate
	// answers a different question that happens to mention the same map.
	function anythingContested() returns bool
		return self.typeAliases.anyRangeContest() or self.contestedGenericAliases.count() > 0
	end 'anythingContested'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME INSTANCE, WITH EVERY CONTESTED NAME IN IT SPELLED THE WAY `filePath` MEANS IT (N2 + N3)**
	// — the ONE place a generic instance becomes a particular file's instance, so no door grows a qualifier
	// step of its own. `mangleGenericInstance` and `canonicalInstanceName` are structural over the args, so
	// both inherit the qualified spelling for free and neither learns anything about contests.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **ONE WALK FOR BOTH CONTESTS, AND IT WAS BRIEFLY TWO (N3 review).** N2 scopes a `named` leaf that
	// names a range-contested ELEMENT; N3 scopes one that names an instance-contested GENERIC ALIAS. Written
	// as two triples they shared everything that matters — which tags recurse, which pass through, the
	// re-intern, the "return `id` untouched" contract — and differed only in the leaf. Two copies of a
	// RECURSION 240 lines apart is the shape where, the day a tag other than `genericInstance` starts carrying
	// a nested instance, whoever teaches one walk about it has no reason to look at the other — and the
	// failure is a wrong instance rather than a compile error.
	//
	// ⭐ **A PROGRAM WITH NEITHER CONTEST RETURNS ON THE FIRST LINE — no walk, no `MaxonTypeArray`, no
	// intern.** That is the whole existing corpus and the compiler's own source, which is why not one interned
	// instance, mangled symbol or committed golden moves.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS A PURE READ BY THE TIME A PARSE ASKS IT**, and that is a property `settleRangeScopedInstances`
	// and `settleGenericAliasContest` buy rather than one this function has: `intern` returns an existing id
	// without mutating, so every pair being pre-interned is what keeps a per-file parse from writing the
	// shared registry. The rescope is also IDEMPOTENT — a minted name is never itself contested, having
	// exactly one declaration by construction — so asking it of an already-scoped instance answers with the
	// same id and interns nothing.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE PARAMETER IS `filePath` AND NOT `readerFilePath`**, deliberately: at a parse it is the READER's,
	// at the settle it is the file that WROTE the declaration, and a parameter name true at some call sites
	// and false at others is the contract `declaredSlotType`'s header refuses for the same reason.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT RE-INTERNS AN EXISTING INSTANCE, SO IT MUST CARRY EVERY PART OF THAT INSTANCE'S IDENTITY —
	// AND IT DROPPED THE `fixedSize` (S2q).** `intern`'s key is `(baseId, args, fixedSize)`; this is the ONLY
	// call site that rebuilds one from an instance it already holds rather than from a declaration, so it is
	// the only one that can lose a component by simply not mentioning it — and `fixedSize` DEFAULTS to
	// `NoFixedSize`, which is silent. A `Vector with 8 W` whose `W` two files disagree about therefore came
	// back SIZELESS: `create()` built a zero-length vector and every index was out of bounds.
	// MEASURED, two plain user files and no stdlib at all —
	// `specs-shv2/typealias-file-scope.md`'s `a-sized-vector-keeps-its-element-count-when-its-element-is-contested`
	// panicked `a Vector with 8 has an index 7` where the bootstrap answered `42 8 1779033703`.
	// ⚠ **NO `Array` CASE COULD HAVE CAUGHT IT** — every base but `Vector` is unsized, so on those the
	// omission is exactly a no-op, which is why the whole existing body of contest cases
	// (`bytearray-element-size.md`, all `Array with Byte`) stayed green over it.
	export function fileScopedInstance(id GenericInstanceId, filePath FilePath) returns GenericInstanceId
		if not self.anythingContested() 'nothingInThisProgramIsContested'
			return id
		end 'nothingInThisProgramIsContested'

		return self.genericInstances.intern(self.genericInstances.instanceBaseId(id), args: self.fileScopedArgs(self.genericInstances.instanceArgs(id), filePath: filePath), fixedSize: self.genericInstances.instanceFixedSize(id))
	end 'fileScopedInstance'

	function fileScopedArgs(args MaxonTypeArray, filePath FilePath) returns MaxonTypeArray
		var scoped = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		for arg in args 'eachArg'
			scoped.push(self.fileScopedArg(arg, filePath: filePath))
		end 'eachArg'
		return scoped
	end 'fileScopedArgs'

	// One type argument, scoped. A NESTED instance recurses — `Array with (Array with Byte)`'s inner element
	// belongs to the same file the outer one does — and every other tag passes through: only a `named` leaf
	// can name an alias at all (a struct, a tuple and an opaque type parameter carry no range and no instance,
	// and a primitive carries no name).
	//
	// **THE TWO LEAF RULES ARE DISJOINT, WHICH IS WHY ASKING BOTH IS NOT A PRECEDENCE DECISION.** A name is a
	// RANGED alias or a GENERIC-INSTANCE alias and never both — `readTypeAliasDeclaration` reads one form per
	// declaration, and a name two declarations claim in different forms is a duplicate the type-name registry
	// refuses — so whichever of the two answers, the other would have handed the argument back unchanged.
	function fileScopedArg(arg MaxonType, filePath FilePath) returns MaxonType
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(arg)
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'nested'
			return MaxonType.genericInstance(self.fileScopedInstance(typeNameIdOf(arg), filePath: filePath))
		end 'nested'

		if tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'carriesNoAliasName'
			return arg
		end 'carriesNoAliasName'

		if let contestScoped = try self.contestScopedDeclaredType(arg, slotScopeFilePath: filePath) 'namesAContestedGenericAlias'
			return contestScoped
		end 'namesAContestedGenericAlias'

		let name = self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(arg))
		let scoped = self.typeAliases.rangeScopedName(name, readerFilePath: filePath)
		if scoped.equals(name) 'thisFileNamesTheBareDeclaration'
			return arg
		end 'thisFileNamesTheBareDeclaration'

		return MaxonType.named(self.typeNames.intern(scoped))
	end 'fileScopedArg'

	// ⭐⭐ **SETTLE THE RANGE CONTEST, RE-KEY EVERY DECLARED ALIAS TO ITS OWN FILE'S INSTANCE, AND INTERN
	// EVERY SCOPED VARIANT A PARSE COULD ASK FOR (N2)** — one pass, at the one moment the whole program is
	// known and nothing has read an instance yet.
	//
	// **THE THREE STEPS ARE THREE DIFFERENT PROBLEMS, AND ONLY THE FIRST IS A REGISTRY QUESTION:**
	//
	//   1. **The CONTEST** (`settleRangeContests`) — which names have declarations that disagree, and the
	//      minted declaration each distinct range gets. Everything below reads its answer.
	//   2. **THE ORDERING HAZARD** — `recordGenericAlias` runs DURING the sweep, so `typealias ByteArray =
	//      Array with Byte` was interned with the BARE element before any contest could be known. Repairing
	//      that is `rekeyScopedGenericAliases`', which runs after BOTH contests are settled because it
	//      applies both scopings; see there.
	//   3. **The PARSE MUST NOT WRITE.** Every (instance, reader environment) pair is interned here, so
	//      `fileScopedInstance` is a pure lookup from every parallel per-file parse.
	//
	// ⚠ **ON EVERY PROGRAM IN THE CORPUS THIS IS A NO-OP AFTER STEP 1**, and step 1 is one walk of a map
	// that is EMPTY unless two files declare one name. That is not an optimization — it is the proof that
	// nothing this rung does can move a golden.
	export function settleRangeScopedInstances()
		self.typeAliases.settleRangeContests()
		if not self.typeAliases.anyRangeContest() 'noNameInThisProgramIsContested'
			return
		end 'noNameInThisProgramIsContested'

		// The snapshot is taken BEFORE the loop because the loop interns: the pairs worth minting are those
		// over the instances the sweep produced, and a variant is never itself contested (see
		// `fileScopedInstance`), so a second pass over the grown registry would mint nothing new.
		let swept = self.genericInstances.count()
		for environment in self.typeAliases.rangeContestEnvironments() 'eachEnvironment'
			for id in 0 upto swept 'eachSweptInstance'
				_ = self.fileScopedInstance(id, filePath: environment)
			end 'eachSweptInstance'
		end 'eachEnvironment'
	end 'settleRangeScopedInstances'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH GENERIC-ALIAS NAMES DO TWO FILES DISAGREE ABOUT, AND WHAT DOES EACH FILE MEAN BY ONE? (N3)**
	// — one pass, at the one moment every declaration is in and no file has been parsed.
	//
	// **IT SETTLES THREE THINGS, AND THE THIRD IS THE ONE A SCOPED LOOKUP DOES NOT BUY:**
	//
	//   1. **The CONTEST** — `contestedGenericAliases`, the by-declaring-file index the parser's scoped read
	//      probes (`scopedGenericAliasInstance`), plus `nameableGenericAliases` for its second tier.
	//   2. **THE MINT.** Each distinct instance of a contested name is registered under
	//      `instanceQualifiedAliasName` as an entry of its own, so a name that unambiguously denotes ONE of
	//      the declarations exists at all. It is NOT a second identity: `genericAliases` maps it to exactly
	//      the instance the source name resolves to in its declaring file, and the mangled symbol comes from
	//      the instance, so nothing minted here reaches emitted code.
	//   3. **THE RECORDED DECLARED TYPES** — `resolveRecordedGenericAliasTypes`. This is the half without
	//      which step 1 would be a REGRESSION rather than a fix; see there.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT RUNS AFTER `settleRangeScopedInstances` AND THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING.** The contest is decided
	// on the instance each declaration means IN ITS OWN FILE, and for an element two files declare over two
	// ranges that instance is N2's range-scoped one — so a `Bytes` whose `Byte` differs is contested here
	// precisely because the range settle already split the element. Reversed, every such pair would look like
	// one instance and nothing would be scoped.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT IS A FIXPOINT, AND THE SECOND ROUND IS NOT HYPOTHETICAL — IT WAS A COMPILER PANIC.** An alias's
	// ARGUMENT may itself name a contested alias (`typealias Grid = Array with Slots`), and a nested `named`
	// argument is exactly the spelling the sweep leaves behind. Both files' `Grid` therefore interned to ONE
	// instance — `Array with named("Slots")` — so round one saw `Grid` AGREE while its element denoted two
	// different arrays. MEASURED: `arrayElementDropCallee` took that element straight into
	// `genericAliasInstance`'s refusal. Rescoping the arguments splits `Grid` in two, which makes `Grid`
	// contested, which can split a `Array with Grid` above it — so the walk repeats until a round contests
	// nothing new. It terminates because a name only ever moves from agreeing to contested and there are
	// finitely many of them; the counter below is a bound on that argument, not a heuristic.
	//
	// ⚠ **ON EVERY PROGRAM IN THE CORPUS THE FIXPOINT DOES NOT RUN AT ALL** — no generic-alias name is
	// mentioned by two declarations, so nothing can disagree, nothing is minted, and the contest indexes stay
	// the shared empties. That is not an optimization; it is the proof that nothing here can move an interned
	// instance or a golden, and it is measured: with the gate, the contest costs the ladder NOTHING. (Without
	// it, the round's own first pass cost one map plus an entry per generic-alias NAME — +40 allocations at
	// rung 0 rising to +452 at rung 5, for a question whose answer is "none".)
	// ⛔ **THE GATE WRAPS THE FIXPOINT AND NOT THE WHOLE FUNCTION, AND THE DIFFERENCE WAS A REGRESSION.**
	// `rekeyScopedGenericAliases` below is N2's repair as well as N3's — it is the ONE place a declared alias
	// is re-keyed to its declaring file's instance, for RANGES and for ARGUMENTS both — so an early return in
	// front of it on the GENERIC contest alone skips the RANGE re-key too. MEASURED, by the full suite and
	// invisible to a filtered run: `bytearray-element-size/a-byte-two-files-disagree-about-is-two-types`
	// stopped refusing, because `stdlib/File.maxon`'s `ByteArray` kept its bare element and the reader's own
	// `int(0 to 1000)` then scoped it — the exact wrong answer N2 exists to prevent.
	export function settleGenericAliasContest()
		if self.multiplyDeclaredGenericAliases.count() > 0 'someNameHasASECONDDeclarationToDisagreeWith'
			var rounds = 0 as GenericAliasRoundCount
			while self.settleGenericAliasContestRound() 'aRoundFoundANewContest'
				rounds = rounds + 1
				// ⛔ **THE DECLARATION GRAPH CYCLES — STOP AND RECORD IT, NEVER ABORT.** A round can only deepen
				// an argument that was still bare, so an ACYCLIC graph cannot outlast its own nesting depth and
				// that depth cannot exceed the declaration count. Past it, the walk is chasing a cycle and the
				// program is owed E3091; see `divergedGenericAliasDecl`, which `firstCyclicAliasDecl` answers
				// with. What the indexes hold at this point is the last round's, which is internally consistent
				// — every recorded declared type is still resolved against it below — it is simply not a
				// fixpoint, and no answer read out of it can be observed by a program that compiles.
				if rounds > self.genericAliasDecls.count() 'deeperThanTheDeclarationGraphCanNEST'
					self.divergedGenericAliasDecl = self.firstContestedDeclaration()
					break
				end 'deeperThanTheDeclarationGraphCanNEST'
			end 'aRoundFoundANewContest'
		end 'someNameHasASECONDDeclarationToDisagreeWith'

		self.rekeyScopedGenericAliases()
		self.resolveRecordedGenericAliasTypes()
	end 'settleGenericAliasContest'

	// The first declaration of a name the contest condemned — E3091's anchor when the fixpoint gives up. A
	// contested name is by construction one whose declarations the walk was still re-minting, so any of them
	// names a real participant in the cycle; the FIRST is chosen so the diagnostic is walk-order-free.
	function firstContestedDeclaration() returns AliasCycleResult
		for i in 0 upto self.genericAliasDecls.count() 'eachDeclaration'
			let entry = try self.genericAliasDecls.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.firstContestedDeclaration: genericAliasDecls.get OOB at {i}")
			if self.contestedGenericAliases.contains(entry.aliasName) 'itsNameIsContested'
				return AliasCycleResult.at(i)
			end 'itsNameIsContested'
		end 'eachDeclaration'

		panic("ProgramSignatures.firstContestedDeclaration: the contest fixpoint ran past its bound with no contested name — a round only continues after filing one")
	end 'firstContestedDeclaration'

	// One round of the fixpoint: group every declaration by name over the instance it means in its own file,
	// and (re)file every disagreeing name. Answers whether anything moved, which is the loop's condition.
	//
	// ⚠ **A DISAGREEING NAME IS REFILED EVERY ROUND, NOT ONLY THE ROUND IT FIRST DISAGREED.** A name contested
	// in round 1 can still hold an argument that only becomes contested in round 2 (`Box with (M, Low)` vs
	// `Box with (M, High)` — the pair disagrees on the SECOND argument while `M` is still the ambiguous bare
	// spelling), so a "file it once" rule would leave that name's instances stale and take the element
	// straight into `genericAliasInstance`'s refusal.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND THE MOVEMENT TEST IS THE INSTANCE COUNT, NOT THE CONTEST SET, FOR EXACTLY THAT CASE** — a round
	// that rescopes an argument of an already-contested name adds no NAME while changing what that name
	// means. `intern` is idempotent, so a round that rescopes nothing new interns nothing new and the count
	// stands still: that is the fixpoint.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE VERDICT AND THE INDEX ARE TWO PASSES, AND THAT SPLIT IS MEASURED.** Built as ONE pass — a
	// `GenericAliasDeclarationGroup` per name, accumulating the by-file map as it went — the round cost a
	// group, an inner map and a `FilePath.toString()` **per generic-alias declaration in every program**,
	// which the ladder read as **+326 allocations at rung 0 rising to +6,318 at rung 5** (×1.61 … ×1.95 per
	// doubling: linear, and paid by every compile for a question whose answer is "none"). The first pass
	// answers the verdict with ONE `GenericInstanceId` per NAME and nothing else; the second builds a group
	// only for the names that disagree, which is no names at all in the corpus.
	function settleGenericAliasContestRound() returns bool
		let instancesBefore = self.genericInstances.count()
		var firstInstance = GenericAliasMap.create()
		var disagreeing = sharedEmptyDisagreeingAliasNames
		for entry in self.genericAliasDecls 'eachDeclaration'
			// Only a name a SECOND declaration mentions can disagree with anything, so the walk narrows to
			// those and the map it fills holds one entry per such name rather than one per alias in the
			// program — see `multiplyDeclaredGenericAliases`.
			if not self.multiplyDeclaredGenericAliases.contains(entry.aliasName) 'itIsTheOnlyDeclarationOfThisName'
				continue
			end 'itIsTheOnlyDeclarationOfThisName'

			let scoped = self.fileScopedInstance(entry.internedInstance(), filePath: entry.filePath)
			if let first = try firstInstance.get(entry.aliasName) 'anotherDeclarationCameFirst'
				if first != scoped 'theyNameTwoInstances'
					if disagreeing is sharedEmptyDisagreeingAliasNames 'stillShared'
						disagreeing = DisagreeingAliasNameSet.create()
					end 'stillShared'
					disagreeing.insert(entry.aliasName)
				end 'theyNameTwoInstances'
			end 'anotherDeclarationCameFirst' else 'theFirstDeclarationOfThisName'
				firstInstance.upsert(entry.aliasName, value: scoped)
			end 'theFirstDeclarationOfThisName'
		end 'eachDeclaration'

		if disagreeing.count() == 0 'noNameInThisProgramDisagreesWithItself'
			return self.genericInstances.count() != instancesBefore
		end 'noNameInThisProgramDisagreesWithItself'

		return self.recordGenericAliasContests(disagreeing) or self.genericInstances.count() != instancesBefore
	end 'settleGenericAliasContestRound'

	// The second pass — build the full by-declaring-file group for each DISAGREEING name and file it. Answers
	// whether any of them is newly contested, which is half the fixpoint's condition.
	function recordGenericAliasContests(disagreeing DisagreeingAliasNameSet) returns bool
		var byName = GenericAliasDeclarationGroupMap.create()
		for entry in self.genericAliasDecls 'eachDeclaration'
			if not disagreeing.contains(entry.aliasName) 'itsDeclarationsAgree'
				continue
			end 'itsDeclarationsAgree'

			// Seeded first and then MUTATED IN PLACE, rather than read-modify-upserted: a group read out of
			// the map borrows it, and the write-back would be a mutation through a live borrow (E3070). The
			// fold itself therefore has ONE spelling, whether or not this is the name's first declaration.
			if not byName.contains(entry.aliasName) 'firstDeclarationOfThisName'
				byName.upsert(entry.aliasName, value: GenericAliasDeclarationGroup.create())
			end 'firstDeclarationOfThisName'

			var group = try byName.get(entry.aliasName) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.recordGenericAliasContests: '{String.from(entry.aliasName)}' was just seeded into the group map and is not there")
			group.record(self.fileScopedInstance(entry.internedInstance(), filePath: entry.filePath), declFilePath: entry.filePath, nameable: nameableFromAnywhere(entry.visibility), spellingBits: entry.spellingBits())
		end 'eachDeclaration'

		var foundANewContest = false
		for (aliasName, group) in byName 'eachName'
			foundANewContest = foundANewContest or not self.contestedGenericAliases.contains(aliasName)
			self.recordGenericAliasContest(aliasName, group: group)
		end 'eachName'

		return foundANewContest
	end 'recordGenericAliasContests'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ORDERING HAZARD, REPAIRED ONCE FOR BOTH SCOPINGS (N2 + N3).** `recordGenericAlias` runs DURING
	// the sweep, so `typealias ByteArray = Array with Byte` was interned with the bare element — and
	// `typealias Grid = Array with Slots` with the bare ARGUMENT — before either contest could be known.
	// Every source-declared alias is re-keyed here to the instance its OWN DECLARING FILE means, which is the
	// whole reason `genericAliasDecls` retains every entry.
	//
	// ⭐ The COMPILER-MINTED aliases are deliberately NOT in that array and so keep their unscoped instance:
	// `__ManagedMemory` has no declaring file and means "the READER's byte array", which is exactly what an
	// unscoped entry plus a scoped read gives it.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT WAS TWO PASSES AND IS NOW ONE (N3).** N2 re-keyed for ranges and N3 needed the identical walk for
	// arguments, on the same last-wins rule, over the same array — two copies free to disagree about which
	// declaration an entry names. `fileScopedInstance` is the one composition both wanted.
	//
	// Last-wins by declaration order, which is `genericAliases`' own rule for a name two files declare, so the
	// bare entry and the file it is scoped by name one declaration. The E3017 instantiation SITES are rebuilt
	// on the rule they already had — each carries its own file, so they need no last-wins rule at all.
	function rekeyScopedGenericAliases()
		if not self.anythingContested() 'nothingInThisProgramIsContested'
			return
		end 'nothingInThisProgramIsContested'

		for entry in self.genericAliasDecls 'eachDeclaration'
			self.genericAliases.upsert(entry.aliasName, value: self.fileScopedInstance(entry.internedInstance(), filePath: entry.filePath))
		end 'eachDeclaration'

		var scoped = GenericInstantiationSiteArray.create()
		for site in self.genericAliasSites 'eachSite'
			scoped.push(GenericInstantiationSite.create(self.fileScopedInstance(site.gid, filePath: site.filePath), nameLine: site.nameLine, nameColumn: site.nameColumn, filePath: site.filePath))
		end 'eachSite'
		self.genericAliasSites = scoped
	end 'rekeyScopedGenericAliases'

	// File one contested name into both contest indexes and mint an entry per distinct instance. Copy-on-write
	// off the shared empties happens HERE, at the single writer, so a program with no contest never allocates
	// either index.
	function recordGenericAliasContest(aliasName ByteArray, group GenericAliasDeclarationGroup)
		if self.contestedGenericAliases is sharedEmptyGenericAliasContests 'stillShared'
			self.contestedGenericAliases = ContestedGenericAliasMap.create()
			self.nameableGenericAliases = NameableGenericAliasMap.create()
		end 'stillShared'

		self.contestedGenericAliases.upsert(aliasName, value: group.byDeclaringFile)
		if group.anyNameable 'someDeclarationMayBeWrittenAnywhere'
			self.nameableGenericAliases.upsert(aliasName, value: group.nameableInstance)
		end 'someDeclarationMayBeWrittenAnywhere'

		// ⭐ The MINT — one entry per DISTINCT instance, which falls out of the spelling being DERIVED from the
		// instance rather than from the declaration: two declarations that agree walk to the same name and
		// upsert it twice. It inherits the source declaration's BUFFER-SPELLING bits, which are the two facts a
		// `GenericInstanceId` structurally cannot carry (`Array with __ManagedMemory` and `Array with ByteArray`
		// are one instance, as are `__ManagedMemory with Byte` and `ByteArray`), and losing either at a
		// rewritten field type would change that field's member roster.
		//
		// ⚠ **BOTH SETS, because `retypeField` rewrites the name the READ DOOR derives from (W49).** The
		// element bit was carried here alone while it was the only derived one; its twin now reaches
		// `derivedAliasSurfaceBits` by the same route, so a contested `typealias B = __ManagedMemory with Byte`
		// would otherwise have its field's surface erased by the very pass that scopes it — the defect this
		// loop already existed to prevent, one bit over.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **THE BITS COME FROM *THIS INSTANCE'S* DECLARATIONS, NOT FROM THE CONTESTED NAME (W49), AND THE
		// DIFFERENCE IS A WRONG ANSWER RATHER THAN A REFINEMENT.** The registries are keyed by bare alias NAME
		// and are whole-program, so `contains(aliasName)` answers "did ANY file's declaration of this name
		// write the buffer?" — and a contested name is precisely the case where the files DISAGREE. MEASURED:
		// with `typealias B = __ManagedMemory with Byte` in one file and `typealias B = Array with Int` in
		// another, `b.items.count()` on a field of the SECOND kind was refused with the BUFFER's roster, in
		// both file orders. The group carries each instance's own spelling for exactly this, and the mint is
		// keyed by instance, so the two mints now take the two answers their two declarations wrote.
		for (_, instance) in group.byDeclaringFile 'eachDeclaration'
			let mint = instanceQualifiedAliasName(aliasName, canonicalInstance: self.canonicalInstanceName(instance))
			self.genericAliases.upsert(mint, value: instance)
			let spelling = group.spellingBitsOf(instance)
			if (spelling and SurfaceBitElementIsBuffer) != 0 'elementNamesTheBuffer'
				self.bufferElementAliases.insert(mint)
			end 'elementNamesTheBuffer'

			if (spelling and SurfaceBitIsBuffer) != 0 'baseNamesTheBuffer'
				self.bufferSurfaceAliases.insert(mint)
			end 'baseNamesTheBuffer'
		end 'eachDeclaration'
	end 'recordGenericAliasContest'

	// ⭐⭐ **RESOLVE EVERY RECORDED DECLARED TYPE THAT NAMES A CONTESTED ALIAS, AGAINST THE FILE THAT DECLARED
	// THE SLOT (N3) — the half that keeps the fix from being a wild free.**
	//
	// A struct field and a union payload are recorded by the declaration SWEEP, which runs before any generic
	// alias is interned, so their declared type is a bare `named("Bag")`. The drop and clone cascades read
	// that column with NO reader file (`managedFieldDropCallee`, `managedFieldCloneStrategy`,
	// `denotesArrayInstance`) — while everything was last-wins they simply agreed with the parser, and the
	// program was refused rather than miscompiled. Scope the parser alone and they DISAGREE: `Nums.items`, a
	// buffer of integers, would be routed to `__destruct_Array_String` and its elements freed as pointers.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE SPELLING IS REWRITTEN AND THE TAG IS NOT**, deliberately. Rewriting to `genericInstance(gid)`
	// is what `declaredSlotType` produces and would satisfy the cascades — but it DESTROYS the alias NAME, and
	// `fieldSurfaceOf` reads that name to derive BOTH of the position's buffer-spelling bits
	// (`derivedAliasSurfaceBits`), neither of which a gid can carry. A minted `named` keeps every name-keyed
	// reader on exactly the path it was on and changes only which declaration the name denotes.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHICH IS WHY THE MINT MUST INHERIT BOTH SPELLING SETS** — see `recordGenericAliasContest`, where the
	// two `insert`s sit together for the reason they sit together in `recordGenericAlias`.
	//
	// ⚠ **A SPELLING THIS PASS DOES NOT REACH IS A PANIC, NOT A WRONG ANSWER** — `genericAliasInstance`
	// refuses a contested name outright. That is the whole reason the file-less door has a guard rather than a
	// comment: the set of places a declared type is RECORDED is a thing this rung must get right, and getting
	// it wrong has to be loud.
	function resolveRecordedGenericAliasTypes()
		if self.contestedGenericAliases.count() == 0 'noNameIsContested'
			return
		end 'noNameIsContested'

		for (_, layout) in self.structTypes 'eachStruct'
			for index in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachField'
				let declared = try layout.fieldTypes.get(index) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.resolveRecordedGenericAliasTypes: fieldTypes.get OOB at {index} for `type {String.from(layout.name)}`")
				if let resolved = try self.contestScopedDeclaredType(declared, slotScopeFilePath: layout.declFilePath) 'namesAContestedAlias'
					layout.retypeField(index, declaredType: resolved)
				end 'namesAContestedAlias'
			end 'eachField'
		end 'eachStruct'

		for (_, layout) in self.enumTypes 'eachEnum'
			for caseIndex in 0 upto layout.caseCount() 'eachCase'
				self.resolveRecordedPayloadTypes(layout, caseIndex: caseIndex)
			end 'eachCase'
		end 'eachEnum'

		self.rewriteRecordedReturnTypes(RecordedReturnTypeRule.contestScopedAlias)
	end 'resolveRecordedGenericAliasTypes'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE REWRITE OF `returnTypes`, AND THE THREE RULES THAT DRIVE IT (BATCH28 review).** Three
	// whole-program passes re-decide a recorded return type — N3's contest rescoping, S2j's struct re-tag
	// and P1.7a's interface re-tag — and each had spelled out the same COLLECT-THEN-APPLY walk of the same
	// map, under the same key, reconstructing the same `MaxonReturnType.value`. Nothing made the three
	// agree: the keying rule stated below is a property of THE MAP and not of any one pass, so a fourth
	// pass copying the shape would have had to rediscover it, and a correction to one copy would have
	// reached one caller in three. The walk is one fact and lives here; what differs between the three is a
	// single line, and that line IS the rule (`rewrittenReturnTypeUnder`).
	//
	// ⚠ **COLLECTED, THEN APPLIED**: it rewrites the very map it walks, and rewriting a container mid-walk
	// is the one shape a `for … in` cannot promise anything about. Both columns stay empty for every
	// program whose declarations the rule claims none of — which, for all three rules, is most of the
	// corpus.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS KEYED OFF `returnTypes` AND NOT OFF ANY FILE COLUMN**, so a name whose entry has since been
	// REFILED (a contested free function's namespace-qualified key, a stdlib shadow, a renamed default
	// helper) is rewritten under the key it actually lives at and never resurrected under the one it left.
	// The three refile sites move the file column with the return type for exactly that reason.
	export function rewriteRecordedReturnTypes(rule RecordedReturnTypeRule)
		var rewrittenCallees = ByteArrayArray.create()
		var rewrittenReturns = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		for (callee, declared) in self.returnTypes 'eachReturnType'
			if let rewritten = try self.rewrittenReturnTypeUnder(rule, callee: callee, declared: declared) 'theRuleClaimsIt'
				rewrittenCallees.push(callee)
				rewrittenReturns.push(rewritten)
			end 'theRuleClaimsIt'
		end 'eachReturnType'

		for i in 0 upto rewrittenCallees.count() 'applyEach'
			let callee = try rewrittenCallees.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.rewriteRecordedReturnTypes: rewrittenCallees.get OOB at {i} below count {rewrittenCallees.count()}")
			let rewritten = try rewrittenReturns.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.rewriteRecordedReturnTypes: rewrittenReturns.get OOB at {i} — the two columns are pushed in lockstep")
			self.returnTypes.upsert(callee, value: MaxonReturnType.value(rewritten))
		end 'applyEach'
	end 'rewriteRecordedReturnTypes'

	// The ONE line each rule contributes: the type this entry should now carry, or `MapError.keyNotFound`
	// when the rule does not claim it — the "absence is a throw" convention all three re-tags already
	// shared. A `match` rather than a chain, so a fourth rule cannot be added without being given a line.
	function rewrittenReturnTypeUnder(rule RecordedReturnTypeRule, callee ByteArray, declared MaxonReturnType) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		match rule 'rule'
			contestScopedAlias then return try self.contestScopedReturnTypeOf(callee, declared: declared)
			declaredStruct then return try self.sweptStructReturnOf(declared)
			declaredInterface then return try self.sweptInterfaceReturnOf(declared)
		end 'rule'
	end 'rewrittenReturnTypeUnder'

	// N3's rule: a function's RETURN type resolved against the file that declared the FUNCTION.
	// `funcReturnDeclFiles` carries the measurement of what its absence cost — a `returns Bytes` crossing a
	// file boundary took the CALLER's `Bytes`, and two element widths compared equal. A callee with no file
	// recorded names nothing still to be resolved, which is the rule declining the entry.
	function contestScopedReturnTypeOf(callee ByteArray, declared MaxonReturnType) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		let declFilePath = try self.funcReturnDeclFiles.get(callee)
		return try self.contestScopedReturnType(declared, declFilePath: declFilePath)
	end 'contestScopedReturnTypeOf'

	// The `structRef` a swept `named` denotes; `MapError.keyNotFound` when the name denotes no struct BOX.
	// It follows `sweptInterfaceTypeOf`'s shape — the other recorded-return-type re-tag on this index —
	// including its "absence is a throw" convention, so both read as one kind of pass. A `void` return
	// names no type to re-tag, and a type that is already concrete is refused by the tag guard, which is
	// what makes this idempotent.
	function sweptStructReturnOf(declared MaxonReturnType) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		match declared 'shape'
			void then throw MapError.keyNotFound
			value(inner) then return try self.sweptStructTypeOf(inner)
		end 'shape'
	end 'sweptStructReturnOf'

	// ⚠⚠ **IT RESOLVES THE NAME THROUGH `denotedAggregateName`, AND ASKING `containsStruct` OF THE RAW NAME
	// MADE THE ANSWER A PROPERTY OF THE DISK (BATCH28 review).** A TUPLE TYPEALIAS mints no identity — it
	// names the tuple's own canonical struct and nothing else — so `containsStruct("Pair")` is false while
	// `containsStruct("__Tuple2.Num.Num")` is true, and a rule reading the raw name declined every
	// `returns Pair`. That was invisible while the sweep happened to resolve it: `recordTupleAlias` writes
	// DURING the walk rather than at the end of a file (unlike every other alias registry), so a factory
	// whose file the walk reached AFTER the alias's file got a resolved `structRef` from
	// `Parser.parseTypeReference` and never needed this rule. **MEASURED, one directory, byte-identical
	// source, only the file NAMES changed:** with the alias's file sorting first the program compiled and
	// printed `11 22`; with the factory's file first the identical program was refused. Same program, two
	// answers, decided by a directory walk.
	//
	// `denotedAggregateName` is the one door that already answers "what does this name denote", and it is
	// the SAME door the drop router (`managedFieldDropCallee`) and every managed classifier ask — so what
	// this rule re-tags and what those route are one set by construction. For a declared `type` it is the
	// identity, and re-interning a name the table already holds returns the id it already had, which is why
	// no struct return moves.
	// ⚠⚠ **AND IT CLAIMS AN ALREADY-`structRef` TUPLE TOO, BECAUSE A SWEPT `structRef` IS NOT NECESSARILY
	// THE CANONICAL SPELLING (BATCH28 review).** `parseTypeReference`'s tuple arm is deliberately OUTSIDE
	// the `allFilesFolded` gate its neighbours sit behind, so during the sweep a `returns Tagged` already
	// resolves to `structRef` — through `tupleAliasTarget` → `canonicalNameOfStruct`, which is itself gated
	// on `allFilesFolded` and therefore hands back the RAW spelling at that moment. The recorded type is
	// then concrete and WRONG-NAMED: `__Tuple2.String.Num` where every value of that tuple, and the
	// destructor synthesized for it, carry `__Tuple2.String.int`.
	//
	// **MEASURED:** `typealias Tagged = (String, Num)` with `Num = int(0 to 1000)`, built by a top-level
	// factory, died in the backend — `resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function
	// '__destruct___Tuple2.String.Num'` — while `(String, String)`, whose two spellings coincide, built and
	// ran. So the defect was never "a tuple owns heap"; it was the NAME, at a door that had not
	// canonicalized. This is the third such door and it takes the cure the other two already took —
	// `tuples.md`'s `array-of-managed-element-tuples-drops-each` records the second, in the same words.
	//
	// A tag guard alone would decline exactly the entries that need this most, which is why the shape below
	// is "what should this name be?" rather than "is this tag still bare?". Re-interning a name the table
	// already holds returns the id it already had, and a name already canonical maps to itself — so a
	// declared `type` and an already-canonical tuple both fall out through the `equals` test unmoved, and
	// the rule stays idempotent.
	function sweptStructTypeOf(declared MaxonType) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(declared)
		if not tagMayNameAggregate(tag) 'namesNoStructBox'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'namesNoStructBox'

		let written = self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(declared))
		// `denotedAggregateName` resolves a tuple TYPEALIAS to the tuple's own name; `canonicalNameOfStruct`
		// then settles which SPELLING of that tuple everything downstream uses. Both are the doors the drop
		// router and every managed classifier already ask, so what this re-tags and what those route are one
		// set by construction.
		let denoted = self.canonicalNameOfStruct(self.denotedAggregateName(written))
		if not self.containsStruct(denoted) 'namesNoStructBox'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'namesNoStructBox'

		if tagIsStructRef(tag) and denoted.equals(written) 'alreadyTheSpellingEveryoneUses'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'alreadyTheSpellingEveryoneUses'

		return MaxonType.structRef(self.typeNames.intern(denoted))
	end 'sweptStructTypeOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **RE-TAG EVERY SWEPT `named` THAT DENOTES AN `interface` AS AN `interfaceRef`
	// (P1.7a-existentials slice 1) — the ONE repair that makes the two type registries agree about what an
	// interface annotation MEANS.**
	//
	// The declaration sweep records a struct field's and a function's declared type by running
	// `Parser.parseTypeReference` (`foldDeclaredSignaturesInto`), and the interface registry it would have to
	// consult is filled by `foldInterfaceDeclarations` — which runs at the very END of
	// `queryProgramSignatures`, after every file is swept. So a swept `Backend` is ALWAYS a bare `named`,
	// while the real parse (reading a complete registry) mints `interfaceRef`. That is not a cosmetic
	// difference: `StructLayout.offsetOfField` gives an existential field TWO slots and a `named` one, so the
	// two registries would disagree about a struct's SIZE and about where every field after the first
	// interface field lives — a wrong address rather than a diagnostic.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS A NORMALIZATION, NOT A SECOND RESOLUTION RULE.** It asks the same `lookupInterface` over the
	// same `interfaces` map that `parseTypeReference` and `TypeResolution.denotedNamedType` ask, so there is
	// one statement of "which names are interfaces" and three readers of it. Anything it misses stays a
	// `named` and is caught by the authority as E3011 — never silently sized wrong, because a `named` field
	// is one slot in BOTH registries.
	//
	// ⚠ **A PROGRAM THAT DECLARES NO INTERFACE PAYS ONE `Map.count()`.** The corpus is overwhelmingly of that
	// shape, and the walk below is O(structs × fields + functions) only when it can do something.
	export function normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes()
		if self.interfaces.count() == 0 'noInterfaceIsDeclared'
			return
		end 'noInterfaceIsDeclared'

		for (_, layout) in self.structTypes 'eachStruct'
			for index in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachField'
				let declared = try layout.fieldTypes.get(index) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes: fieldTypes.get OOB at {index} for `type {String.from(layout.name)}`")
				if let asInterface = try self.sweptInterfaceTypeOf(declared) 'namesAnInterface'
					layout.retypeField(index, declaredType: asInterface)
				end 'namesAnInterface'
			end 'eachField'
		end 'eachStruct'

		// ⚠ **THE RETURN-TYPE HALF IS THE SHARED WALK AND THE FIELD HALF CANNOT BE.** A field re-tag writes
		// THROUGH a layout (`retypeField`) and touches no map, so it may run in place above; a return-type
		// re-tag rewrites the very map it walks, which is the collect-then-apply
		// `rewriteRecordedReturnTypes` states once for all three rules.
		self.rewriteRecordedReturnTypes(RecordedReturnTypeRule.declaredInterface)
	end 'normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes'

	// The `interfaceRef` a swept `named` denotes; `MapError.keyNotFound` when the name is not an interface —
	// the single test `normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes` applies to a field type and to a return type, so the two
	// cannot come to disagree about which types it rewrites. A type that is already concrete (a `structRef`,
	// a primitive, an instance) is not a `named` and is refused by the tag guard. It follows
	// `contestScopedDeclaredType`'s shape — the other recorded-type re-tag on this index — including its
	// "absence is a throw" convention, so both read as one kind of pass.
	function sweptInterfaceTypeOf(declared MaxonType) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		if maxonTypeTag(declared) != ValueTypeTag.named 'namesNoInterface'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'namesNoInterface'

		// `isInterfaceName` for `Parser.parseTypeReference`'s reason: this walks every field of every declared
		// struct and every return type, and only needs the answer.
		let id = typeNameIdOf(declared)
		let name = self.nameOfId(id)
		if isInterfaceName(self.interfaces, name: name) 'aBareInterfaceName'
			return MaxonType.interfaceRef(id)
		end 'aBareInterfaceName'

		// ⭐⭐ **AN INSTANCE OF AN INTERFACE IS AN EXISTENTIAL TOO, AND THE FIELD COLUMN IS WHERE MISSING IT
		// CORRUPTS RATHER THAN REFUSES.** `var seq as IntegerSeq` over `typealias IntegerSeq = Seq with Integer`
		// is a swept `named("IntegerSeq")` exactly as a bare `Seq` is a swept `named("Seq")`, and the real parse
		// mints `interfaceRef` for BOTH (`instanceDenotedType`). Left to `declaredSlotType`'s `genericAlias` arm
		// the field would be re-tagged `genericInstance` and budgeted ONE slot, while every reader of the type
		// loaded a witness at `+8` — the next field. So the instance half is repaired HERE, where the re-tag goes
		// through `retypeField` and rebuilds the two-slot prefix sum with it.
		if not self.isGenericAlias(name) 'namesNoInterface'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'namesNoInterface'

		// The alias's own declaring file cannot be known from a type alone, and a CONTESTED alias is the only
		// name for which that matters; the base is what decides this question, and a contest is over the
		// ARGUMENTS of one base name (`settleGenericAliasContest`), never over the base.
		let asInstance = self.instanceDenotedType(self.bareGenericAliasInstance(name), into: self.typeNames)
		if maxonTypeTag(asInstance) != ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'aStructInstance'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'aStructInstance'

		return asInstance
	end 'sweptInterfaceTypeOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **RE-TAG EVERY SWEPT `named` FIELD THAT IS A BARE GENERIC NAME AS THE INSTANCE ITS DECLARING SCOPE
	// BINDS — the field-column twin of the interface normalization above, and for the identical reason.**
	//
	// `Cell` written inside `type Holder uses T` means `Cell with Holder.T`, and that rule
	// (`scopedGenericInstanceOf`) is gated on `allFilesFolded` because the struct registry is still filling
	// while the declaration sweep reads it. So a swept field type is ALWAYS the bare `named("Cell")` — the
	// BASE's declaration view — while the real parse, reading a complete registry, records
	// `genericInstance(Cell with Holder.T)` for the very same field. One declaration, two registries, two
	// answers.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND THE DROP CASCADE READ THE UNREPAIRED ONE, WHICH IS A LEAK WITH NO DIAGNOSTIC.**
	// `substituteInstanceFieldType` reads this column off `baseLayoutOf`, and `substituteThroughInstance`
	// passes a `named` through untouched (it has no name table) — so `Holder with String`'s cascade classified
	// its `cell` field as the BASE `Cell`, whose only field is the opaque `T` and which therefore owns nothing.
	// The drop it emitted was the trivial `__mm_decref`: the cell's BOX was reclaimed and the `String` the type
	// argument put inside it was stranded. **MEASURED before this pass existed** — `Holder with String` built
	// from a `Cell with String`, printed its answer and exited **101**, while the corpus's only case of the
	// shape (`bare-generic-name-as-a-field-type`) is over `Integer`, which has nothing to strand and so agreed.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS NORMALIZED HERE RATHER THAN REPAIRED AT EACH READER, AND THE THIRD READER IS WHY.** The field
	// READ already carried this rule inline (`fieldTypeOf`), the drop cascade did not, and the CLONE gate
	// (`structFieldsSupportDeepClone`) reads the same raw column a fourth time. Repairing the cascade would
	// have made three copies of one rule, and the copies decide ownership: a gate and a strategy that resolve a
	// name differently admit a clone nobody can emit, which is the failure `managedNameCloneStrategy`'s own
	// header records. Recorded once, every reader of the column sees the instance and there is nothing left to
	// remember.
	//
	// ⚠ **STRUCT FIELDS ONLY, AND THAT IS A PROPERTY OF THE LANGUAGE RATHER THAN A GAP.** The rule needs a
	// binding SCOPE — the enclosing declaration's `uses` list — and only a struct layout carries one: a union
	// declared `uses` cannot be instantiated at all (`Maybe with String` is **E2055**, measured on this tree),
	// so a payload has no scope to bind against. A method's recorded RETURN type has the same gap and a
	// different cure already: `Parser.promoteBareGenericResultToCalleeScope` repairs it in the CALLEE's scope,
	// which is not this layout's and so is not this walk's to answer.
	//
	// ⚠ **A PROGRAM THAT DECLARES NO TYPE PARAMETER PAYS ONE `Map.count()`** — `normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes`'
	// gate, over the registry that answers the necessary condition here: no type parameter anywhere means no
	// scope binds anything.
	export function normalizeSweptScopedGenericFields()
		if self.typeParamOwners.count() == 0 'noTypeParameterIsDeclared'
			return
		end 'noTypeParameterIsDeclared'

		for (_, layout) in self.structTypes 'eachStruct'
			// A non-generic declaring type binds nothing, so it cannot make a bare name mean an instance —
			// `fieldTypeOf`'s own measured gate, which exists because `scopedGenericInstanceOf` returns a UNION
			// and Maxon heap-boxes even its payload-free arm.
			if layout.typeParamNames.isEmpty() 'scopeBindsNothing'
				continue
			end 'scopeBindsNothing'

			for index in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachField'
				let declared = try layout.fieldTypes.get(index) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.normalizeSweptScopedGenericFields: fieldTypes.get OOB at {index} for `type {String.from(layout.name)}`")
				if let asInstance = try self.sweptScopedGenericFieldTypeOf(declared, layout: layout) 'namesABareGeneric'
					layout.retypeField(index, declaredType: asInstance)
				end 'namesABareGeneric'
			end 'eachField'
		end 'eachStruct'
	end 'normalizeSweptScopedGenericFields'

	// The instance a swept `named` field of `layout` denotes; `MapError.keyNotFound` when the name is not a
	// bare generic one — `sweptInterfaceTypeOf`'s shape, and its "absence is a throw" convention, so both
	// normalizations of this column read as one kind of pass. The RULE is `scopedGenericInstanceOf`'s, shared
	// with the READ doors that ask it of a different scope; what this adds is only which scope a FIELD is in.
	function sweptScopedGenericFieldTypeOf(declared MaxonType, layout StructLayout) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		if maxonTypeTag(declared) != ValueTypeTag.named 'namesNoBareGeneric'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'namesNoBareGeneric'

		match self.scopedGenericInstanceOf(self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(declared)), declaringTypeName: layout.name, scopeParamNames: layout.typeParamNames) 'scoped'
			notInScope then throw MapError.keyNotFound
			instance(gid) then return MaxonType.genericInstance(gid)
		end 'scoped'
	end 'sweptScopedGenericFieldTypeOf'

	// The return-type wrapper of the test above: a `void` return names no type to re-tag.
	function sweptInterfaceReturnOf(declared MaxonReturnType) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		match declared 'shape'
			void then throw MapError.keyNotFound
			value(inner) then return try self.sweptInterfaceTypeOf(inner)
		end 'shape'
	end 'sweptInterfaceReturnOf'

	// The `contestScopedDeclaredType` wrapper of the same shape — the return-type slot's spelling, resolved
	// against the file that DECLARED the function. See `funcReturnDeclFiles` for the wrong answer this
	// closes.
	function contestScopedReturnType(declared MaxonReturnType, declFilePath FilePath) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		match declared 'shape'
			void then throw MapError.keyNotFound
			value(inner) then return try self.contestScopedDeclaredType(inner, slotScopeFilePath: declFilePath)
		end 'shape'
	end 'contestScopedReturnType'

	// One union case's payload slots, rewritten by the rule above. Split out because a payload array is
	// replaced wholesale (`PayloadField` holds `let` columns), where a struct field column is retyped in place.
	function resolveRecordedPayloadTypes(layout EnumLayout, caseIndex CaseIndex)
		let payloads = layout.payloadsAt(caseIndex)
		var rewritten = PayloadFieldArray.create()
		var changed = false
		for field in payloads 'eachSlot'
			if let resolved = try self.contestScopedDeclaredType(field.declaredType, slotScopeFilePath: layout.declFilePath) 'namesAContestedAlias'
				rewritten.push(PayloadField.create(field.name, declaredType: resolved, declaredSurface: field.declaredSurface))
				changed = true
			end 'namesAContestedAlias' else 'unchanged'
				rewritten.push(field)
			end 'unchanged'
		end 'eachSlot'

		if not changed 'nothingToRewrite'
			return
		end 'nothingToRewrite'

		layout.retypeCasePayloads(caseIndex, payloads: rewritten)
	end 'resolveRecordedPayloadTypes'

	// The minted spelling a recorded `named` should carry when it names a CONTESTED generic alias, resolved
	// against the file that declared the SLOT — or `MapError` when it names no contested alias, which is every
	// declared type in every program in the corpus.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SCOPE FILE IS THE SLOT'S DECLARING FILE AND NEVER A READER'S**, exactly as `declaredSlotType`'s
	// is: a field belongs to the file that wrote it, and a compiler-synthesized layout carries
	// `CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath`, which declares no alias and so resolves like a stranger — the tier-2/3
	// answer, which is the same answer for all of them.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **A TUPLE'S ELEMENT IS *NOT* ONE OF THOSE, AND THIS COMMENT SAID IT WAS (N3 review, overturned by
	// A3v).** It read: *"a tuple is STRUCTURAL — the same spelling in two files is ONE type by construction —
	// so there is no declaring file to prefer, and picking one file's would make two identical spellings two
	// types."* The premise is right and the conclusion does not follow. A tuple's canonical name is derived
	// from its RESOLVED elements, so two files that mean the same thing by `(Bag, Num)` resolve to identical
	// element types and land on ONE name — structural identity is preserved BY per-file resolution, not
	// despite it. Two files that mean different things by `Bag` were never spelling one type. What the
	// stranger's reading actually produced was a THIRD answer agreeing with neither file, and the file that
	// declared both `Bag` and `Pair = (Bag, Num)` could not construct its own field (E3005, MEASURED — see
	// `canonicalTupleElement`). A tuple layout now carries the file that SPELLED it (`tupleStructLayout`), so
	// this pass re-scopes a sweep-minted element list against that file like any other slot.
	function contestScopedDeclaredType(declared MaxonType, slotScopeFilePath FilePath) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		if maxonTypeTag(declared) != ValueTypeTag.named 'namesNoAlias'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'namesNoAlias'

		let name = self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(declared))
		if not self.contestedGenericAliases.contains(name) 'notContested'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'notContested'

		let instance = self.scopedGenericAliasInstance(name, readerFilePath: slotScopeFilePath)
		return MaxonType.named(self.typeNames.intern(instanceQualifiedAliasName(name, canonicalInstance: self.canonicalInstanceName(instance))))
	end 'contestScopedDeclaredType'

	// ⭐ **THE `__ManagedMemory` INSTANCE EVERY COMPILER-SYNTHESIZED BYTE BUFFER WEARS.**
	// `internArrayByteInstance`'s twin, differing only in its element — see `SynthesizedByteElementName` for
	// why a compiler-owned element rather than `Byte`, and for the two measurements that chose it. Its
	// producers are `String.addressableBytes()` (BATCH2 slice 1) and `emitOwnedManagedMemoryResult`'s three
	// OS runtime entries (`__md_filename`, `__md_current_path`, `__cl_arg`). Not memoized like its twin:
	// it is asked once per such call site, never on a hot parser path.
	export function internSynthesizedByteInstance() returns GenericInstanceId
		var args = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		args.push(MaxonType.named(self.typeNames.intern(SynthesizedByteElementName)))
		return self.genericInstances.intern(self.typeNames.intern(self.arrayRecordBaseName()), args: args)
	end 'internSynthesizedByteInstance'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH `Array` INSTANCE A `__ManagedMemory.create(count, elementSize)` YIELDS (R4.4)** — the one
	// place the raw `elementSize` the source writes becomes a STATIC element type, and the reason it has to:
	// `mm.get(0)` and `mm.set(0, value: 300)` are typed off the instance's element, so `300` fits at element
	// size 8 and does not at 1 (`specs/managed-memory-builtin.md:setlength-then-get` writes exactly that).
	// Under R4.2's byte-only binding that program was WRONGLY REJECTED.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SPLIT IS `1 ⇒ Byte, ANYTHING ELSE ⇒ WORD`, WHICH IS v1's RULE VERBATIM** — its own layout comment
	// says so of the field this size lands in: *"element_size i64 (sizeof(Element); 1 for Byte, 8 otherwise)"*
	// (`Compiler/Runtime/runtime.std:4262`). ⚠ It is NOT the whole of what `trivialElementSlot` can answer —
	// since `enum-narrow-storage` that ladder also hands out 2 and 4 for a narrow enum element — it is the
	// whole of what a source `elementSize` may NAME here, and the paragraph below is why.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT DELIBERATELY REFUSES NOTHING, INCLUDING A NON-POSITIVE SIZE.** `managed-memory-methods`'s
	// `managed-memory-error-variants` requires `__ManagedMemory.create(4, 0)` to COMPILE and report
	// `invalidAllocation` at RUN TIME — the buffer's own guard (`__managed_mem_create`) is where a size the
	// allocator cannot use is caught, and hoisting that into a parse-time refusal would turn a case the corpus
	// pins as a caught throw into a compile error. Which instance a doomed `create` was typed as is unobservable.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **PRECONDITION: `managedMemoryElementSizeIsTypeable`.** It has no arm for a width shv2 has no element
	// type of, because there is no honest one — see that predicate for the wrong answer the missing `else`
	// used to give.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE BYTE ARM IS SCOPED TO THE FILE THAT WROTE THE `create` (N2)**, which is what lets
	// `stdlib/File.maxon:71`'s `__ManagedMemory.create(size + extraBytes, 1)` be a genuinely byte-PACKED
	// buffer in a program whose own `Byte` is two bytes wide. Before it, the whole-program width made that
	// buffer stride 2 and `file.read(managed, …)` three lines later refused it — in a program that never
	// mentions `File`.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AND WHERE THE SCOPING CANNOT SAVE IT, THE CALLER NOW REFUSES (A4c).** A file that declares its own
	// wide `Byte` AND writes `create(n, 1)` asks for a byte-PACKED record — `__managed_mem_create` stamps
	// `element_size@24` from that argument — under the `Array with Byte` this arm hands back, which strides
	// what that same file declared. The two disagreed silently: `set(0, value: 300)` read back **44**. This
	// arm still answers, because the refusal is about the DISAGREEMENT and must name the instance it is
	// about; `Parser.requireManagedMemoryElementSize` is where it is raised, beside its two siblings.
	export function managedMemoryInstanceForElementSize(elementSize ParsedInt, readerFilePath FilePath) returns GenericInstanceId
		if elementSize == ByteStringElementSize 'bytePacked'
			return self.internArrayByteInstance(readerFilePath)
		end 'bytePacked'

		return self.internBuiltinIntegerInstance(self.arrayRecordBaseName())
	end 'managedMemoryInstanceForElementSize'

	// ⭐⭐ **CAN shv2 GIVE A BUFFER OF THIS ELEMENT WIDTH A TYPE AT ALL? (R4.4 review.)** The gate the answer
	// above needs in front of it, reading the same two constants so the ACCEPTED set and the MAPPED set cannot
	// come to differ.
	//
	// ⚠ **A WIDTH WITH NO ELEMENT TYPE IS A SILENT WRONG ANSWER, NOT A ROUNDING, AND IT WAS MEASURED.** The
	// front end types `get`/`set` off the INSTANCE while the runtime strides by the record's `element_size@24`,
	// so a `create(4, elementSize: 4)` — typed as the machine-WORD instance, because that was the whole `else`
	// — accepted `mm.set(0, value: 5000000000)` against `int`'s range and stored four bytes of it:
	// `mm.get(0)` read back **705032704**. The two facts about one buffer disagreed, quietly, at run time.
	// R4.2 had no such hole only because it typed every buffer as bytes, so that program was refused by the
	// element's range.
	// ⇒ a source `elementSize` may name exactly the two widths this door can TYPE — a byte or a machine word —
	// because the width it names has to become a `MaxonType` for `get`/`set` to be typed off, and those are the
	// two shv2 has a binding for (`Byte`, `int`). ⚠ **THAT IS A BINDING GAP AND NOT A WIDTH GAP, AND SINCE
	// `enum-narrow-storage` THE DIFFERENCE IS REAL**: the element ladder now produces 2 and 4 for a narrow enum
	// element, so a 2- or 4-byte element TYPE exists — what does not exist is a way for `__ManagedMemory.create`
	// to name one, since a bare `elementSize: 2` says a width and never which type wears it. A diagnostic saying
	// so stays the honest answer until this door takes an element type rather than a number.
	//
	// ⚠ A NON-POSITIVE size is admitted, for the reason above: `create(4, 0)` must compile and throw
	// `invalidAllocation` at run time, and which instance a doomed `create` wore is unobservable.
	export function managedMemoryElementSizeIsTypeable(elementSize ParsedInt) returns bool
		return elementSize <= ByteStringElementSize or elementSize == ManagedMemoryWordElementSize
	end 'managedMemoryElementSizeIsTypeable'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE-TYPE-ARGUMENT CONTAINER INSTANCE FOR AN ELEMENT TYPE NO TYPEALIAS NAMES.** A bare
	// `["a","b"]` / `[p1,p2]`, a `Set from ["…"]` and a `List from [p1, p2]` all build a container whose
	// element type is fixed by the ARRAY LITERAL rather than by a `typealias`, so the SWEEP over-interns the
	// possible element instances (`internLiteralContainerInstances`) and the parser looks up whichever the
	// first element resolves to. Idempotent, so the sweep is the WRITE and the parser a pure READ.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS ONE FUNCTION BECAUSE THE THREE CONTAINERS WERE ALREADY ONE FACT** — it was
	// `arrayInstanceForString` and `setInstanceForString`, two identical three-line bodies differing only in
	// the base name, whose headers each said the other was its "twin". The KEYING is the load-bearing part
	// (a String as `MaxonType.string`, so a literal-built container and a typealias-backed one of the same
	// element share ONE gid), and it is exactly what a third copy would have been free to get subtly wrong.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT TAKES THE ELEMENT AS A RESOLVED `MaxonType`, NOT AS A NAME**, because `List from […]` reads its
	// element straight off the SOURCE ARRAY's instance (`containerElementType`) — already `MaxonType.string`
	// for a String, `named(<id>)` for a struct, a canonicalized `structRef` for a tuple. Going back through a
	// NAME would re-derive the `structRef`-vs-`named` choice `builtinInstanceForNamedElement` carries a
	// measured E3006 about, from an answer that already has it right.
	//
	// ⭐ **`asSpeculative` RIDES THROUGH TO THE REGISTRY'S MARK** (A4q, see `GenericInstanceRegistry.intern`).
	// It defaults to false — what every ordinary caller means, and the safe direction for one that forgets it,
	// since a genuine row is only ever mistaken for a guess by saying so explicitly. The sole `true` is
	// `internAggregateElementInstances`, whose whole output is guesses.
	export function builtinInstanceForElement(baseName ByteArray, element MaxonType, asSpeculative bool = false) returns GenericInstanceId
		var args = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		args.push(element)
		return self.genericInstances.intern(self.typeNames.intern(baseName), args: args, asSpeculative: asSpeculative)
	end 'builtinInstanceForElement'

	// ⭐ **THE TWO-TYPE-ARGUMENT CONTAINER INSTANCE — the `Map` twin of `builtinInstanceForElement`.** A
	// `["a": 1]` literal fixes BOTH of its columns from its first pair, exactly as a `[…]` literal fixes its
	// element from its first element, so the same "the parser looks up whichever the source resolves to"
	// shape needs a door that takes two. The argument ORDER is the instance's identity — `HashTableKeyArgPosition`
	// then `MapValueArgPosition`, which `mapKeyType`/`mapValueType` read back — so the two are named parameters
	// rather than an array a caller could fill in the wrong order.
	export function builtinInstanceForColumns(baseName ByteArray, key MaxonType, value MaxonType) returns GenericInstanceId
		var args = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		args.push(key)
		args.push(value)
		return self.genericInstances.intern(self.typeNames.intern(baseName), args: args)
	end 'builtinInstanceForColumns'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ELEMENT ARG MUST CARRY THE TAG A TYPE REFERENCE TO THAT NAME CARRIES**, because the
	// instance registry keys on `(tag, id)` while `mangleGenericInstance` reads only the NAME. Two tags for
	// one type therefore intern as TWO instances that compile to ONE symbol — which
	// `checkTypeSymbolNamespace` reports as `E3006 duplicate definition of 'Array_<T>'`, naming a
	// `typealias` the author wrote exactly once.
	//
	// A declared struct/union is `named` — that is what `Array with Item`'s alias arg is, which is why a
	// `[<Item>…]` literal and `ItemArray` have always been one type. A synthesized TUPLE is `structRef`:
	// `Parser.parseTypeReference` mints a tuple type that way at every spelling (`(Num, Num)`, and a tuple
	// `typealias` through `tupleAliasTarget`), so `named` here would be a SECOND spelling of one type with
	// nothing to make the two agree. MEASURED: `typealias PairArray = Array with Pair` plus any
	// `[<identifier>…]` literal in the program was rejected with that spurious E3006 (P1.10 review).
	export function builtinInstanceForNamedElement(baseName ByteArray, element ByteArray, asSpeculative bool = false) returns GenericInstanceId
		return self.builtinInstanceForElement(baseName, element: self.namedElementType(element), asSpeculative: asSpeculative)
	end 'builtinInstanceForNamedElement'

	// The rule above, as the `MaxonType` itself — split out because a container ARGUMENT is not the only thing
	// that has to obey it: `Parser.literalColumnTypeFor` names the same type for a bracketed literal's column
	// and would otherwise carry a second copy of the tuple test, which is the exact shape the measured E3006
	// above came from.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A NAME THE PARSER SETTLES SYNTACTICALLY IS ANSWERED WITH THAT BUILTIN'S OWN ARM, NEVER WITH
	// `named`** — the tuple arm's identical defect, two types over, and the only reason it is not live today
	// is that the corpus's own `type String` / `type Character` are not listed yet.
	// `Parser.parseTypeReference` settles both names to `MaxonType.string` / `MaxonType.character` and NEVER
	// to `named`, so every type reference the source writes carries the builtin arm — while
	// `internAggregateElementInstances` over-interns one instance per name in `structTypes`, and the moment
	// those modules are listed those two names are IN it. A `named` answer here therefore minted a SECOND gid
	// for a type the parser already had, and `mangleTypeArg` reads only the NAME
	// (`primitiveTypeTagName(ValueTypeTag.character)` is literally `"Character"`), so the two gids compiled to
	// one symbol.
	//
	// ⇒ MEASURED on this tree with `helpers/string/views.maxon` and `Character.maxon` listed:
	// `typealias CharArray = Array with Character` plus any `[<identifier>…]` literal was rejected with
	// **E3006 duplicate definition of 'Array_Character'**, blaming a `typealias` written once against an
	// instantiation appearing nowhere in the source. `String` is the same collision by the same route and
	// cannot yet be driven to it only because `stdlib/String.maxon` stops parsing earlier (see
	// `StdlibLoader.whitelistedStdlibModules`) — so both arms land together rather than leaving the twin for
	// whoever clears that wall to rediscover.
	//
	// Ahead of the tuple test because neither name can be a tuple's: `isTupleTypeName` keys on the reserved
	// `__Tuple` spelling, which E2051 bars a declaration from taking.
	//
	// ⚠ **NOT ROUTED THROUGH `TypeResolution.builtinTypeNameTag`, WHICH IS THE NEIGHBOURING TABLE ANSWERING A
	// DIFFERENT QUESTION.** That one serves the INTERNER-FREE REBUILD doors (a rendered interface return
	// string, a function typealias's stored `(tag, name)` pair), so it admits `ExitCode` — a name
	// `parseTypeReference` does NOT settle, which reaches here as `named` and must STAY `named`.
	// `denotedNamedType` draws the same line from the other side, admitting that one arm of the table and
	// refusing the rest for exactly this reason. It also carries no `Character` arm, so reusing it would have
	// cured `String` and left the twin.
	export function namedElementType(element ByteArray) returns MaxonType
		if element.equals(StringConformerName) 'theStringBuiltin'
			return MaxonType.string
		end 'theStringBuiltin'

		if element.equals(CharacterConformerName) 'theCharacterBuiltin'
			return MaxonType.character
		end 'theCharacterBuiltin'

		let elementId = self.typeNames.intern(element)
		return MaxonType.structRef(elementId) if isTupleTypeName(element) else MaxonType.named(elementId)
	end 'namedElementType'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE FUNCTION-VALUED TWIN OF `namedElementType`, AND IT EXISTS FOR THAT ONE'S STATED REASON: THE
	// INTERNER.** A container instance's type arguments live in THIS index's interner, and a `function` element
	// carries a `TypeNameId` like every other named type — so a caller holding only the parser's FILE-LOCAL id
	// cannot build the argument itself. It must hand over the NAME and let the registry that owns the ids do
	// the interning, exactly as the `named`/`structRef` arm above does.
	//
	// ⛔ **MEASURED, not defensive.** `Parser.literalColumnTypeFor` first built `MaxonType.function(<the
	// parser's own id>)` directly. The id was a valid id in the WRONG interner, so `[double, triple]` interned
	// an instance whose element named whatever that number meant here — and reading an element back out of it
	// (`ops.get(1)` then calling it) reported the governing name as **'Array'** and PANICKED
	// `functionValueReturnType`. It compiled and parsed cleanly right up to the call; nothing but running it
	// says which interner an id came from.
	export function functionElementType(element ByteArray) returns MaxonType
		return MaxonType.function(self.typeNames.intern(element))
	end 'functionElementType'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE NAME BEHIND A `function` TYPE THAT LIVES IN *THIS INDEX'S* SPACE — the read side of the door
	// above, and the crossing point for every caller holding an index-space type.** A function type is
	// governed by a NAME (see `MaxonType`'s `function` arm), the whole-program shape lookups are keyed by that
	// name, and `ProgramSignatures.typeNames` is a different table from `project.typeNames` with its own
	// insertion order — so an id from here means whatever name happens to sit at that number THERE. The rule
	// this states, once, for both readers: **names travel between interners; ids never do.**
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **BOTH READERS WERE CAUGHT BY IT, ONE LOUDLY AND ONE SILENTLY, WHICH IS WHY IT IS ONE DOOR.**
	//   • The CONTAINER COLUMN read a parser file-local id here and reported the governing name as `'Array'`,
	//     panicking `requireFunctionShapeOf` — a crash, so it was found immediately.
	//   • `SemanticCheck.checkTypeParameterArgsAt`'s SUBSTITUTED type argument read an index-space id through
	//     the PROJECT registry and worked, because the two tables happened to agree at that number. PROVEN by
	//     sabotage rather than by the passing case: `generic-type-parameter-slots.function-value-element`
	//     reaches the line with `id=3`, and id 3 is `UnaryOp` in both tables for that program. A read that is
	//     correct only while two independent insertion orders coincide is a wrong answer waiting for a third
	//     declaration, and no green case can tell the two apart.
	//
	// It is named for the SPACE and not for a caller ("container column" was its first name, and the second
	// caller is not a column) because the space is the whole of what it settles.
	export function indexSpaceTypeName(indexSpaceType MaxonType) returns ByteArray
		return try self.typeNames.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(indexSpaceType)) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.indexSpaceTypeName: type-name id {typeNameIdOf(indexSpaceType)} is not in this index's interner — a container column's type argument and an instance's substituted argument are both interned HERE, so an id that misses came from another table and every name it could answer would be the wrong one")
	end 'indexSpaceTypeName'

	// ⭐ **THE ALIAS AN INDEX-SPACE TYPE ARGUMENT SPELLS, or EMPTY for one that spells nothing** — the third
	// reader of the door above, and the one that made its name honest. A refusal naming a SCALAR type argument
	// owes the author's `typealias` (`Integer`) and not the class word its tag resolves to (`int`): it is the
	// same `declaredName` piece a CONCRETE parameter hands `paramDisplayName` off `sig.paramDeclaredNames`,
	// read for a type ARGUMENT instead. MEASURED against the runnable oracle, which says `expected 'Integer'`
	// where shv2's substituted arm said `expected 'int'`.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT `instanceArgDisplayName`, WHICH IS THE FULL DISPLAY RULE AND IS FAILURE-PATH ONLY** — that
	// one goes through `mangleTypeArg`, a `String` build plus a `toByteArray`, on the strength of *"a path that
	// only a REJECTED program reaches"*. Its caller here is reached by EVERY substituted scalar argument in the
	// program, so it reads the interned leaf directly: one dense read, no allocation. The two cannot disagree
	// about a `named` leaf — a ranged alias's compiled name IS its declared name — and every arg they would
	// disagree about (a nested instance, an opaque parameter) carries a nominal identity and is judged, and
	// spelled, by the arm above this one.
	export function indexScalarAliasName(indexSpaceType MaxonType) returns ByteArray
		if maxonTypeTag(indexSpaceType) != ValueTypeTag.named 'spellsNoAlias'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'spellsNoAlias'

		return self.indexSpaceTypeName(indexSpaceType)
	end 'indexScalarAliasName'

	// Over-intern `<base> with <T>` for the possible element types of an array literal whose elements the
	// sweep's tokens cannot name (P1.7 slice 3): a local identifier's type is unknowable there, so
	// `<base> with String` and one instance per declared struct/union are interned, and the parser looks up
	// whichever the first element resolves to. An UNREFERENCED instance gets no descriptor or destructor
	// synthesized (both gate on usage), so this is bounded registry entries — not codegen — and it leaves
	// int-only-array programs' goldens untouched.
	//
	// ⚠ **TWO BASES ASK IT, and both ask it about the SAME literal.** An `[<identifier>…]` gives its `Array`
	// instance; a `List from [<identifier>…]` gives that array AND the `List` built over it, whose element type
	// is the array's own (`Parser.listInstanceForArrayLiteral` reads it back through `containerElementType`).
	// One walk, told which base — a second copy under a `List` name would have re-derived the whole measured
	// asymptotic below, and its `sawX` gate, for a set of names that is identical by construction.
	// ⚠ **CALLED ONCE, AFTER THE LAST FILE FOLDS — NOT PER FILE, and that is a MEASURED asymptotic and not a
	// tidy-up.** The struct/union tables ACCUMULATE across the sweep, so a walk placed at each
	// `[<identifier>…]`-bearing file re-walks every type folded so far, at every such file: **O(files × types)**.
	// A dedup set used to sit inside the walk, which made the *interning* O(types) — but not the WALK, whose
	// per-entry cost (a `MapIterator` entry tuple, a set probe) is paid whether or not the name is new. The
	// early-out that guarded it was a count-equality that any freshly folded type defeats, so the common shape
	// — every file declaring a type AND using an array literal — defeated it at every file.
	//
	// ⇒ MEASURED, on 64→1024 files each declaring one `type S<k>` and holding one `[<identifier>…]` literal:
	//   `phase signatures` allocations went **×2.16, ×2.38, ×2.68, ×3.03** per DOUBLING (exact column, so this
	//   is not noise), against ×1.99 flat for the same files with no declared type. Hoisted here it reads
	//   ×1.99 flat. The tuple rung (P1.10) did not introduce this — it added a SECOND producer of registered
	//   types, since a tuple layout is registered under a mangled name — but it is what the ladder found it with.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE HOIST IS SAFE, BUT IT IS NOT AN IDENTITY — IT CHANGES TWO THINGS, AND EACH HAS ITS OWN REASON
	// FOR BEING HARMLESS** (audited P1.10 review; the claim here previously read "interns the identical SET",
	// which is the one thing it does NOT do):
	//
	//   • **THE SET GROWS.** Placed inside the sweep, the walk could only see the types folded SO FAR, so a
	//     struct/union declared in a file AFTER the last `[<identifier>…]`-bearing one was never over-interned
	//     at all. One final walk sees the whole program. The extra rows are registry entries and nothing more —
	//     a descriptor and a destructor are both synthesized on USAGE, and an over-interned instance nobody
	//     names has none — so the growth is bounded bookkeeping and reaches no emitted byte.
	//     ⛔ **"REACHES NO EMITTED BYTE" IS NOT "HARMLESS", AND THE DIFFERENCE WAS A WRONG REFUSAL (A4q).** A
	//     row here is also read by `instanceWithTypeArgKind`, whose copy gate REFUSES on it — which is why
	//     each row now carries whether anything NAMED it. See `internAggregateElementInstances`.
	//   • **THE GIDS MOVE.** A gid is intern ORDER, and these interns now all happen after the last file folds
	//     instead of interleaved with it, so every over-interned instance's gid shifts. Nothing DERIVES from a
	//     gid's numeric value — `mangleGenericInstance` is structural (base name + arg manglings) precisely so
	//     that a symbol is stable across cold and warm builds — and the one reader that walks the registry in
	//     gid order (`ParseStaging.checkTypeSymbolNamespace`) uses it only to decide which of a COLLIDING pair
	//     is blamed as the newcomer. VERIFIED rather than argued: the full suite regenerates every
	//     `specs-shv2/fragments/` golden and not one of the 2,372 pre-existing ones moved a byte.
	//
	// What the hoist does rest on is that nothing between the sweep's last fold and this call READS a generic
	// instance — the sweep only records — so no reader can observe the difference between "interned early" and
	// "interned here".
	export function internLiteralContainerInstances()
		if self.sawAggregateArrayLiteral 'aggregateArrayLiteral'
			self.internAggregateElementInstances(self.arrayRecordBaseName())
		end 'aggregateArrayLiteral'

		// A `List from […]` needs the same set one base over: the construction's element type is the SOURCE
		// ARRAY's, which is exactly one of the names walked above. `List with integer` and `List with String`
		// are interned beside it by `internWellKnownContainerInstances`, as the `Set from` arm does — this
		// walk is the aggregate half, and only it needs every declared type.
		if self.sawListFromConstruction 'listFromConstruction'
			self.internAggregateElementInstances(ListBuiltinBaseName)
		end 'listFromConstruction'

		// `<Enum>.allCases` needs the ENUM half of that same set and nothing else — a case value is never a
		// struct and never a String — so it asks for the enum loop rather than for the whole walk.
		if self.sawEnumAllCasesAccess 'enumAllCases'
			self.internEnumElementInstances(self.arrayRecordBaseName())
		end 'enumAllCases'
	end 'internLiteralContainerInstances'

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE INSTANCES THE COMPILER MINTS BECAUSE THE SOURCE NAMED NO TYPE FOR THEM — ONCE PER PROGRAM,
	// AFTER THE STDLIB/USER CONTEST (ARRO).** A `[…]`, a `b"…"`, a `.bytes()`, a `Set`/`List`/`Vector from […]`
	// and the three compiler-owned types all produce a value whose type no declaration in the program spells,
	// so the sweep interns it and the parser reads the giid back — the sweep-WRITES / parser-READS discipline
	// `internBuiltinIntegerInstance` states.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE POSITION IS A CORRECTNESS ONE AND NOT A COST ONE, WHICH IS WHAT SEPARATES IT FROM
	// `internLiteralContainerInstances` ABOVE.** These interns used to run at the END OF EACH FILE'S FOLD.
	// The array-record arms take their base from `arrayRecordBaseName`, whose spelling the contest may MOVE
	// (`contestStdlibTypeName`), and the contest is decided BETWEEN the user and stdlib halves of the sweep —
	// so a user file asked before the answer existed and filed a PROGRAM-WIDE memo (`bareArrayByteInstance`)
	// under the pre-contest bare name. In a program declaring its own `type Array` that name is the PROGRAM's
	// container: `b"Hi!".isEmpty()` was refused as a member the program's array does not have, against a
	// bootstrap oracle that serves it. ⇒ ONE call, after both halves have folded, is the whole fix; nothing
	// had to be derived a second time and the contest did not have to move.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE GIDS MOVE, AND THAT IS THE HOIST ABOVE'S ARGUMENT VERBATIM** — a gid is intern ORDER, nothing
	// derives from its numeric value (`mangleGenericInstance` is structural), and the sweep only RECORDS
	// between its last fold and this call, so no reader can observe "interned per file" from "interned here".
	// It runs BEFORE `settleRangeScopedInstances` for the same reason it used to: the N2 split reads these
	// instances, and the bare byte instance is the one every file-scoped answer is derived from.
	export function internWellKnownContainerInstances()
		if self.sawIntArrayLiteral 'intArrayLiteral'
			_ = self.internBuiltinIntegerInstance(self.arrayRecordBaseName())
		end 'intArrayLiteral'

		if self.sawByteStringLiteral 'byteStringLiteral'
			_ = self.bareArrayByteInstance()
		end 'byteStringLiteral'

		if self.sawStringArrayLiteral 'stringArrayLiteral'
			_ = self.builtinInstanceForElement(self.arrayRecordBaseName(), element: MaxonType.string)
		end 'stringArrayLiteral'

		// A `Set from […]` infers its key type from the source array literal, which the sweep cannot see, so
		// BOTH well-known keys are over-interned and the parser reads back whichever the array resolves to —
		// exactly as `internLiteralContainerInstances` over-interns the array's own possible elements.
		if self.sawSetFromConstruction 'setFromConstruction'
			_ = self.internBuiltinIntegerInstance(SetBuiltinBaseName)
			_ = self.builtinInstanceForElement(SetBuiltinBaseName, element: MaxonType.string)
		end 'setFromConstruction'

		// The `List from […]` twin. Its declared-type half is the aggregate walk one function up, which needs
		// the whole type registry; these two scalars need only the base name.
		if self.sawListFromConstruction 'listFromConstruction'
			_ = self.internBuiltinIntegerInstance(ListBuiltinBaseName)
			_ = self.builtinInstanceForElement(ListBuiltinBaseName, element: MaxonType.string)
		end 'listFromConstruction'

		// PER SIZE, because a vector's size is half of its instance's identity, and over the three element
		// types a literal can give one (`Parser.requireVectorElementType` refuses every managed element, so
		// there is no String or aggregate half to intern).
		for size in self.vectorFromSizes 'eachVectorSize'
			_ = self.vectorInstanceForElement(MaxonType.integer, size: size)
			_ = self.vectorInstanceForElement(MaxonType.float, size: size)
			_ = self.vectorInstanceForElement(MaxonType.boolean, size: size)
		end 'eachVectorSize'

		// The two compiler-owned TYPES ride the same call, and for this function's own reason:
		// `registerManagedFileType` and `registerManagedDirectoryType` intern the byte and word array
		// instances themselves, so leaving them in the per-file fold would have left half the defect in place.
		// (A third, `registerCharacterSetType`, rode it until W129.)
		if self.sawManagedFileProducer 'managedFileProducer'
			self.registerManagedFileType()
		end 'managedFileProducer'

		if self.sawManagedDirectoryProducer 'managedDirectoryProducer'
			self.registerManagedDirectoryType()
		end 'managedDirectoryProducer'
	end 'internWellKnownContainerInstances'

	// ⛔⛔ **EVERY ROW THIS WALK MINTS IS A GUESS, AND ONE READER TOOK THEM FOR FACTS (A4q).** The header above
	// argues the rows are harmless because "a descriptor and a destructor are both synthesized on USAGE, and
	// an over-interned instance nobody names has none — so the growth is bounded bookkeeping and reaches no
	// emitted byte". That is true of EMISSION and beside the point for DIAGNOSIS:
	// `instanceWithTypeArgKind` walks this same registry, and the copy gate it serves
	// (`genericTypeFieldUncopyableInstantiation` ⇒ `Parser.requireOpaqueArrayCopyable`) reads a row as
	// evidence that the program CONTAINS an array of that element — then REFUSES on it.
	//
	// ⇒ MEASURED once `stdlib/Array.maxon`, `File.maxon` and `Directory.maxon` are listed. This walk minted
	// `Array with FileWriter`, `Array with FileReader`, `Array with Directory` and `Array with ArrayIterator`
	// — four uncopyable elements, none of them named by any program (a fifth, `Array with __CharacterSet`, went
	// with that layout's registration at W129) — and any ONE
	// of them refused `Array.clone`'s own `managed.slice` (`stdlib/Array.maxon:145`) for the WHOLE program.
	// `map.md`'s `literal.user-type-key` and `literal.user-type-key-escapes-its-builder` were rejected with a
	// sentence about `Box with String` on programs mentioning no `Box`, no file and no `CharacterSet`; a map
	// literal whose keys are a user struct is what puts the IDENTIFIER after the `[` that triggers the walk.
	//
	// The rows still have to exist — that is what this walk is for, and narrowing WHICH types it guesses at
	// would only move the line. So they are minted as before and MARKED instead: the registry records that
	// nothing named them (`GenericInstanceRegistry.speculative`), a later named intern clears the mark, and
	// the one gate that refuses skips what is still only a candidate.
	function internAggregateElementInstances(baseName ByteArray)
		_ = self.builtinInstanceForElement(baseName, element: MaxonType.string, asSpeculative: true)
		for (name, _) in self.structTypes 'eachStruct'
			_ = self.builtinInstanceForNamedElement(baseName, element: name, asSpeculative: true)
		end 'eachStruct'
		self.internEnumElementInstances(baseName)
	end 'internAggregateElementInstances'

	// The ENUM half of the walk above, split out because `<Enum>.allCases` needs exactly it and nothing else.
	// SPECULATIVE for the walk's stated reason: every row is a guess about which enum was meant, and a guess
	// read as evidence that the program CONTAINS such an array is what the ⛔⛔ block above records. A real
	// `.allCases` read interns its own instance NAMED (`Parser.emitEnumAllCases`), which clears the mark on
	// the one row that was not a guess.
	function internEnumElementInstances(baseName ByteArray)
		for (name, _) in self.enumTypes 'eachEnum'
			_ = self.builtinInstanceForNamedElement(baseName, element: name, asSpeculative: true)
		end 'eachEnum'
	end 'internEnumElementInstances'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT ONE FILE'S TOKENS SAY THE PROGRAM WILL MATERIALIZE — recorded, never acted on (ARRO).** THE
	// one door the sweep files a well-known instance need through, so no arm can be half-moved back into the
	// per-file fold and no need can be noted twice under two spellings. Every parameter is a bit the sweep
	// already holds as a local at the one point it calls this; the whole answer is a union across files, which
	// is why each is OR-ed in rather than assigned.
	//
	// ⚠ **TEN PARAMETERS AND NOT TEN FUNCTIONS**: ten one-line setters is ten chances to add an
	// eleventh need and wire it at nine of the ten call sites. There is exactly one call site, so a need that
	// is not passed does not compile.
	export function noteWellKnownInstanceNeeds(intArrayLiteral bool, byteStringLiteral bool, stringArrayLiteral bool, aggregateArrayLiteral bool, enumAllCasesAccess bool, setFromConstruction bool, listFromConstruction bool, vectorSizes FixedSizeArray, managedFileProducer bool, managedDirectoryProducer bool)
		self.sawIntArrayLiteral = self.sawIntArrayLiteral or intArrayLiteral
		self.sawByteStringLiteral = self.sawByteStringLiteral or byteStringLiteral
		self.sawStringArrayLiteral = self.sawStringArrayLiteral or stringArrayLiteral
		self.sawAggregateArrayLiteral = self.sawAggregateArrayLiteral or aggregateArrayLiteral
		self.sawEnumAllCasesAccess = self.sawEnumAllCasesAccess or enumAllCasesAccess
		self.sawSetFromConstruction = self.sawSetFromConstruction or setFromConstruction
		self.sawListFromConstruction = self.sawListFromConstruction or listFromConstruction
		self.sawManagedFileProducer = self.sawManagedFileProducer or managedFileProducer
		self.sawManagedDirectoryProducer = self.sawManagedDirectoryProducer or managedDirectoryProducer

		for size in vectorSizes 'eachVectorSize'
			self.vectorFromSizes.push(size)
		end 'eachVectorSize'
	end 'noteWellKnownInstanceNeeds'

	// --- Layout-descriptor sizing (P1.6-B1) ---
	//
	// The box byteSize of a concrete generic instance — its BASE struct's own layout (`Box with Integer`
	// has `Box`'s box), or the fixed 48-byte record for an `Array` (which has no base struct). At this slice
	// a `T` field is an opaque 8-byte slot like any other, so no argument is substituted into the layout and
	// the base struct's `sizeBytes` IS the instance's box size. This is the descriptor's `size@0` field, and
	// the interner-owning door lowering computes it through.
	export function genericInstanceBoxSize(id GenericInstanceId) returns ByteOffset
		if self.isArrayInstance(id) 'array'
			return ManagedRecordBytes as ByteOffset
		end 'array'
		// ⚠ A `Set` arm (the fixed 48-byte fused hash-table record) and a `Map` arm (the same record with a
		// value column appended) stood here until W105. Both are declared generics now, so their box size is
		// their own base `StructLayout`'s — the fall-through every declared generic takes.
		// A `List` instance's box is the fixed chain header (`head`/`tail`/`count`/`element_drop`), no base
		// struct — the `Array` twin above. Its NODES are separate allocations the runtime owns.
		if self.isListInstance(id) 'list'
			return ListRecordBytes as ByteOffset
		end 'list'
		// A `Vector` instance's box is an `Array`'s — the SAME fused record, because a fixed-size buffer and a
		// growable one differ in what may be done to them and not in how the bytes sit (see
		// `VectorBuiltinBaseName`). It is a separate arm and not a widened `isArrayInstance` because the two
		// are separate TYPES; only their layouts coincide.
		if self.isVectorInstance(id) 'vector'
			return ManagedRecordBytes as ByteOffset
		end 'vector'
		// A `__ManagedList` instance's box IS a `List`'s — the same chain header, allocated by the same
		// `__list_create` (see `ManagedListBuiltinBaseName`). A separate arm and not a widened
		// `isListInstance` for the `Vector` arm's reason: two TYPES whose layouts coincide.
		if self.isManagedListInstance(id) 'managedList'
			return ListRecordBytes as ByteOffset
		end 'managedList'
		// A node handle IS the node, so its "box" is the node the chain allocated — 40 bytes of `next`, `prev`,
		// `value`, `element_drop` and `owner`. It briefly answered a separate handle record's 16 while a handle
		// was a box holding `list`/`node`; that box is gone with the ruling that required it.
		if self.isManagedListNodeInstance(id) 'managedListNode'
			return ListNodeBytes as ByteOffset
		end 'managedListNode'
		// A cursor's box is its own 16-byte record — `source@0` plus `position@8`, and nothing else, because
		// it reads the source's `buffer`/`length`/`element_size` LIVE (see `ManagedMemoryRuntime.CursorRecordBytes`).
		if self.isManagedMemoryCursorInstance(id) 'managedMemoryCursor'
			return CursorRecordBytes as ByteOffset
		end 'managedMemoryCursor'
		match self.structOf(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id)) 'base'
			declared(layout) then return layout.sizeBytes()
			undeclared then panic("genericInstanceBoxSize: generic instance {id}'s base '{String.from(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id))}' is neither a builtin nor a declared struct, so it has no box — every caller opens with genericInstanceBaseIsKnown")
		end 'base'
	end 'genericInstanceBoxSize'

	// The full source-level `sizeof` of a single-type-parameter instance's ONE type argument — the
	// descriptor's `elementLogicalSize@56`, which `sizeof(T)` reads. `Sizer with bool` ⇒ 1; `Sizer with
	// Pair` (Pair is two Integers) ⇒ 16.
	//
	// ⚠ **SINGLE type argument only** (B1). A multi-parameter generic needs per-argument logical sizes,
	// which a single-slot descriptor cannot carry — `parseSizeof` rejects `sizeof` of a multi-parameter
	// generic's type parameter (a clean E2015), so a call site never asks this of one.
	export function genericInstanceSoleArgLogicalSize(id GenericInstanceId) returns ByteOffset
		let args = self.genericInstances.instanceArgs(id)
		if args.count() != 1 'notSingle'
			panic("genericInstanceSoleArgLogicalSize: instance {id} has {args.count()} type arguments — ask `genericInstanceSoleArgLogicalSizeOrNone` if the caller builds a descriptor for instances of any arity; this door answers only where `sizeof(T)` has one T to mean")
		end 'notSingle'
		return self.typeLogicalByteSize(try args.get(0) otherwise panic("genericInstanceSoleArgLogicalSize: instanceArgs.get(0) failed despite count == 1"))
	end 'genericInstanceSoleArgLogicalSize'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME QUESTION, ASKED WHERE THE INSTANCE MAY HAVE ANY ARITY** — the layout-descriptor builder,
	// which needs a blob for `Map with (Key, Value)` just as much as for `Sizer with bool`.
	//
	// A multi-parameter instance has no single element to size, so it answers `LAYOUT_NO_SOLE_ELEMENT`. That
	// is not a sentinel a reader must remember to test: **`parseSizeof` REFUSES `sizeof` of a
	// multi-parameter generic's type parameter** with a positioned `E2015`, so the one consumer of this field
	// cannot be reached for exactly the instances that write it. The refusal is the guarantee — if it is ever
	// relaxed, this becomes a wrong answer, which is why each names the other.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE PANIC THIS REPLACES WAS NOT PROTECTING ANYTHING — IT WAS DENYING THREE GOOD FIELDS BECAUSE A
	// FOURTH HAD NO ANSWER.** Box size, alignment and slot width are per-instance facts a two-parameter
	// generic needs exactly as much, and the element-destructor relocation hangs off the same blob. It made
	// every two-parameter generic holding a container over one of its own parameters uncompilable —
	// `type Map uses Key, Value` with `var keys as Array with Key`, which is `stdlib/Map.maxon`'s own shape.
	export function genericInstanceSoleArgLogicalSizeOrNone(id GenericInstanceId) returns ByteOffset
		let args = self.genericInstances.instanceArgs(id)
		if args.count() != 1 'noSingleElement'
			return LAYOUT_NO_SOLE_ELEMENT
		end 'noSingleElement'
		return self.genericInstanceSoleArgLogicalSize(id)
	end 'genericInstanceSoleArgLogicalSizeOrNone'

	// The full source-level byte size of a type argument, resolved against THIS index's own interner. A
	// declared struct (stored `named` by the sweep, which does not resolve `structRef`) is its full box; a
	// nested generic instance is its base box; a `named` that is not a struct is a ranged alias (a machine
	// word); every other type is its `primitiveTypeByteSize`.
	//
	// ⚠ **THIS IS THE LOGICAL SIZE — `elementLogicalSize@56`, what `sizeof(T)` reads inside a shared
	// generic body — and it is NOT the array element STRIDE.** A ranged alias is a machine word here even
	// where `arrayElementSize` strides it in one byte, because the two answer different questions and the
	// descriptor header says so (`elementSize@16` vs `elementLogicalSize@56`, "DISTINCT and must stay so").
	// It must keep agreeing with the parser's concrete fold, `Parser.concreteSizeofBytes`, which sizes an
	// already-RESOLVED type and so cannot see an alias's bounds at all: narrowing here and not there made
	// `sizeof(T)` for `Sizer with Byte` answer 1 while `sizeof(Byte)` in the same program answered 8.
	function typeLogicalByteSize(t MaxonType) returns ByteOffset
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(t)
		if tagMayNameAggregate(tag) 'namedOrStruct'
			// ⭐⭐ **RESOLVED THROUGH `denotedAggregateName` (A3e review) — a TYPE ARGUMENT is a RAW stored
			// type, so this door reads a spelling no re-tag has passed over.** An instance's argument is
			// interned once, by whichever pass first met `Sizer with Pair`, and the declaration SWEEP meets it
			// with `parseTypeReference`'s tuple-alias arm answering `isTupleAlias("Pair")` — which is false
			// until the alias's OWN file is walked. So `args[0]` is `named("Pair")` when the two files arrive
			// in one order and `structRef(<the tuple>)` when they arrive in the other, and nothing downstream
			// re-tags it: `genericInstanceSoleArgLogicalSize` hands it straight here.
			//
			// ⚠ **MEASURED, A3e review** — the same two-file program staged in both walk orders: `sizeof(T)`
			// inside `Sizer with Pair` (`typealias Pair = (Integer, Integer)`) returned **16** with the alias
			// file walked first and **8** with it walked last. Both compiled, both ran, and the wrong one is
			// baked into `.rdata` as `elementLogicalSize@56` — a silent wrong ANSWER, not a diagnostic. The
			// unresolved name simply missed `structOf` and fell to `primitiveTypeByteSize(named)`, the
			// machine-word fallback that exists for an enum/union.
			match self.structOf(self.denotedSlotAggregateName(t)) 'lookup'
				declared(layout) then return layout.sizeBytes()
				undeclared then return primitiveTypeByteSize(tag)
			end 'lookup'
		end 'namedOrStruct'
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'nested'
			return self.genericInstanceBoxSize(typeNameIdOf(t))
		end 'nested'
		return primitiveTypeByteSize(tag)
	end 'typeLogicalByteSize'

	// A structural, BUILD-STABLE mangling of a type argument for a descriptor's `.rdata` label —
	// `bool` ⇒ "bool", a declared `Pair` ⇒ "Pair", a nested `Box with Integer` ⇒ "Box_Integer". It is
	// derived from the argument's STRUCTURE (base name + args), never from a gid number, so the label is
	// identical across cold and warm builds (a gid's numeric value is a build-order artifact).
	//
	// ⭐ **THE CLAIM IS TOTAL AGAIN SINCE W14b — IT WAS NARROWED FOR ONE RELEASE AND THE NARROWING IS GONE.**
	// The `namedOrStruct` arm answers a `structRef` NAMING A NESTED TUPLE with the tuple's own NAME, and
	// between W9 and W14b a tuple's name cited a nested element by a MINT-ORDERED token — the exact
	// "build-order artifact" the sentence above bars, arriving through the name rather than through this
	// door. It is now a digest of the nested tuple's own name (`ProgramSignatures.tupleElementTokenFor`),
	// which is a pure function of the program's structure and identical across cold and warm builds and
	// across walk orders, so a tuple argument satisfies the rule for the same reason every other tag does.
	//
	// ⚠ **THAT ARM IS STILL UNPINNED, AND THAT HALF DID NOT CHANGE.** No committed x64 fragment carries such
	// a label today — every tuple name in `specs-shv2/fragments/x64-windows/` is a `__destruct_` symbol,
	// which is a DIFFERENT speller — so nothing here would go red if the arm regressed. Absence of a red is
	// not evidence that the arm cannot be reached.
	export function mangleTypeArg(t MaxonType) returns String
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(t)
		// ⚠ The NAME is read raw and NOT through `denotedSlotAggregateName`: this label must be the argument's
		// SOURCE spelling, and canonicalizing a tuple alias here would rename an emitted symbol. Only the TAG
		// question is shared.
		if tagMayNameAggregate(tag) 'namedOrStruct'
			return String.from(self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(t)))
		end 'namedOrStruct'
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'nested'
			return String.from(self.mangleGenericInstance(typeNameIdOf(t)))
		end 'nested'
		// An OPAQUE type-parameter argument (`Array with T` inside a generic body, P1.7 slice 3b-i) — its
		// instance `Array with <that parameter>` is interned like any other, so its structural mangling names
		// the parameter by its own TOKEN, which is a pure function of `(declaring type, parameter name)` and
		// therefore identical across cold and warm builds and across walk orders — the same property this
		// function's header demands of every other arm.
		//
		// ⛔ **IT SPELLED THE POSITION (`T0`) UNTIL W14, AND THAT WAS THE COLLISION ITSELF WEARING A NAME.**
		// `HoldA`'s `Array with U` and `HoldB`'s `Array with V` both mangled to `Array_T0` — and, worse, both
		// built the same `instanceKey`, so they were not two names for two types but ONE interned type. See
		// `typeParamTokenFor`.
		//
		// Rendered in HEX, the same `0-9a-f` `tupleElementTokenFor` spells its digest in and for its reason:
		// the alphabet holds no `.` and no `#`, so the token cannot disturb the tuple element join or the
		// overload-registration join, and it is injective in the id, which is all a lookup key needs. It is
		// only ever a LOOKUP key (the opaque array never gets a synthesized `__destruct_<mangled>` nor its own
		// descriptor — it drops through `__managed_decref` and reads the enclosing instance's descriptor), and no
		// committed fragment on any target carries such a label today.
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'opaqueParam'
			return "T{typeNameIdOf(t):x}"
		end 'opaqueParam'
		return primitiveTypeTagName(tag)
	end 'mangleTypeArg'

	// ⭐ **THE CANONICAL CONFORMER NAME OF A TYPE ARGUMENT (P1.7a slice 2b).** A ranged int/float alias
	// (`Integer` = `int(0 to u32.max)`) collapses to its underlying primitive name (`int`) — the name its
	// INTRINSIC conformance (`int` implements `Hashable`/`Equatable`) and its witness table (`__witness_int.*`
	// → the synthesized `int.hash`/`int.equals` impls) are keyed by; a struct/String keeps its own name. The
	// ONE reducer shared by the E3017 where-constraint check (`typeConformsTo`) and witness-table
	// materialization (`materializeWitnessArg`), so the conformance lookup, the table label, and the impl
	// symbol cannot disagree on whether a `Box with Integer` conforms via `int`. Distinct from
	// `instanceArgDisplayName`, which keeps the SOURCE spelling (`Integer`) for the E3017 MESSAGE — this
	// canonicalizes only the identity the comparison rests on, exactly as `ConformanceCheck.canonicalTypeName`
	// does for a method-signature type name.
	export function conformerTypeArgName(gid GenericInstanceId, position TypeParamPosition) returns String
		// An instance's stored ARGUMENT was interned by this index, so its ids are this index's own.
		return self.conformerNameOfType(self.substitutedInstanceArg(gid, position: position), idsFrom: self.typeNames)
	end 'conformerTypeArgName'

	// ⭐ **THE CANONICAL CONFORMER NAME A TYPE REDUCES TO — the key every conformance answer, every
	// `__witness_<conformer>.<Iface>` label and every `<conformer>.<method>` impl symbol is filed under.**
	// Split out of `conformerTypeArgName` when the EXISTENTIAL widening doors needed the same reduction of a
	// type they hold DIRECTLY rather than of a generic instance's argument (P1.7a-existentials slice 1): a
	// value passed to an interface-typed parameter has a `MaxonType`, not a `(gid, position)`. Two spellings
	// of "which conformer is this?" would let a widening accept a type whose witness table is filed under a
	// name the lowering then fails to find.
	// ⚠⚠ **IT TAKES THE INTERNER THE TYPE'S IDS LIVE IN, AND THAT IS A CORRECTNESS PARAMETER RATHER THAN A
	// CONVENIENCE.** A `named`/`structRef` carries a PER-INTERNER id, and this index's table and the
	// PROJECT's are two different id spaces (the sweep builds one, `mergeArtifact` folds the artifacts' into
	// the other). Its two callers hold different ones: an instance's stored argument was interned HERE, while
	// an existential widening holds a value type whose ids were remapped project-global. Resolving a project
	// id against this index's table answers with whatever name happens to sit at that number — MEASURED, in
	// exactly the shape a whole-program answer fails in: `interface-param-declared-after-consumer` refused a
	// legal `X64Query implements OpQuery` because the two tables had interned in different orders.
	export function conformerNameOfType(t MaxonType, idsFrom TypeNameInterner) returns String
		// The id-bearing arms are resolved against the CALLER's interner and then re-asked of this index BY
		// NAME — the same "names travel, ids do not" discipline `adoptType` states in full. Every other arm
		// (a primitive, a `GenericInstanceId`, a type-parameter TOKEN — a source-text digest since W14, not
		// the position it replaced) carries no per-interner id, so it goes to the shared mangler untouched.
		if tagMayNameAggregate(maxonTypeTag(t)) 'namesAnAggregate'
			return self.conformerNameOfDeclaredName(try idsFrom.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(t)) otherwise panic("conformerNameOfType: type-name id {typeNameIdOf(t)} is not in the interner it was resolved against — pass the interner this type's ids live in"))
		end 'namesAnAggregate'

		// ⭐⭐ **EVERY CONFORMING `Array with E` REDUCES TO THE ONE CONFORMER `Array` (array-hashable)** — the
		// generic-instance analogue of `Integer` reducing to `int`, and it rests on the same property: the
		// impls it names are ELEMENT-INDEPENDENT. `Array.hash`/`Array.equals` walk the backing buffer's raw
		// bytes (`stdlib/Array.maxon:637-664`), so one pair of `.text` symbols and one pair of
		// `__witness_Array.*` tables answer for every array in the program, whatever it holds — where a
		// per-instance conformer name would mint an identical body per element type.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **AND THE REDUCTION IS CONDITIONAL, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF HOW A CONDITIONAL CONFORMANCE SURVIVES
		// A NAME-KEYED CONFORMANCE MODEL.** An `Array with <non-conforming element>` keeps its MANGLED
		// instance name, which no `implements` and no intrinsic row claims — so `where T is Hashable` refuses
		// it through the ordinary E3017 door, with no second predicate anywhere to keep in step. It is why
		// `isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance`'s `Array` arm may answer unconditionally.
		//
		// ⚠ The id-bearing arms above are settled first: this asks about a `genericInstance`, whose id is a
		// `GenericInstanceId` in THIS index's registry and carries no per-interner name.
		// ⚠ The TAG guard is spelled here rather than through `isArrayInstanceAt` because the conjunction
		// continues into the conformance half: routing it through that door would ask `isArrayInstance` twice
		// over one id. It is the same two-part test and the same reason for it — a `TypeNameId` and a
		// `GenericInstanceId` share a numeric space, so the tag is what makes the id question meaningful.
		if maxonTypeTag(t) == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'anInstance'
			// The UNREDUCED fallback here is the mangled instance name — a name no `implements` and no
			// intrinsic row claims — which is exactly how a non-conforming `Array with E` goes on being
			// refused through the ordinary E3017 door. This door holds no source spelling to answer with.
			match self.instanceConformerReduction(typeNameIdOf(t)) 'reduction'
				arrayBuiltin then return String.from(self.arrayRecordDeclaredName())
				declaredBase(baseName) then return String.from(baseName)
				unreduced then return self.mangleTypeArg(t)
			end 'reduction'
		end 'anInstance'

		return self.mangleTypeArg(t)
	end 'conformerNameOfType'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE CONFORMER NAME OF A GENERIC INSTANCE — the ONE answer, for the two doors that arrive with
	// different things in hand (W41).** `conformerNameOfType` holds a resolved `genericInstance`;
	// `conformerNameOfDeclaredName` holds the NAME of a generic-instance typealias. That split is exactly the
	// one `instanceConformerReduction`'s array arm already exists for, and the reduction below has to live on
	// both sides of it for the same measured reason — see the note there.
	//
	// ⭐ **A DECLARED GENERIC REDUCES TO ITS BASE, because under dictionary-passing its conformance is a
	// property of the DECLARATION and its impls are ONE shared body.** `type MapIterator uses Key, Value
	// implements Iterator with Entry` compiles `MapIterator.current` once, over an opaque layout, and every
	// instantiation dispatches into that one symbol — so one `__witness_MapIterator.Iterator` table answers
	// for all of them, exactly as one `__witness_Array.*` pair answers for every array. A per-instance
	// conformer name would mint an identical table per instantiation AND, worse, name a conformer that no
	// `implements` clause claims: the registry is keyed by the DECLARED struct name, so a mangled instance
	// name misses it and a legal program is refused.
	//
	// ⚠ **MEASURED, on a program with no stdlib in it at all**: `type Wrapper uses T implements Named` +
	// `type Holder uses N where N is Named` + `Holder with (Wrapper with Integer)` is refused at `HEAD` with
	// *"E3017: Type 'IntWrapper' does not satisfy constraint 'Named' required by type parameter 'N' of
	// 'Holder'"* and runs here, printing `wrapped`. It is what `stdlib/Map.maxon`'s `Iterable` conformance
	// dies on too, one instance in — `WithIterIterator with (MapIterator with (Key, Value), …)`.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE `where` CONSTRAINTS OF THE BASE ARE NOT WAIVED BY THIS, AND THAT IS WHY IT MAY BE
	// UNCONDITIONAL WHERE THE `Array` ARM IS NOT.** `MapIterator` carries its own `where Key is Hashable`, and
	// that clause is checked at the `MapIterator with (K, V)` instantiation SITE by `checkOneInstantiation`,
	// which walks every site in the program. The question here is a different one — *"does this declaration
	// implement that interface"* — and the answer to it does not depend on the arguments. `Array`'s arm is
	// conditional because `Array` has no declaration to read a `where` clause off: shv2 synthesizes it, so
	// its conditional conformance has nowhere else to live.
	//
	// A BUILTIN base (`Array`, `Set`, `List`, a `Map` while it is still synthesized) has no `StructLayout` to
	// declare anything, so it keeps the mangled name it always had and the arms above stay authoritative.
	// ⚠⚠ **IT ANSWERS THE REDUCTION, NOT THE NAME, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A DIAGNOSTIC THAT REGRESSED WHEN IT
	// DID ANSWER THE NAME.** The two doors share the RULE and emphatically do not share the FALLBACK: an
	// unreduced instance is its MANGLED name at `conformerNameOfType` (which holds no source spelling) and its
	// ALIAS's own name at `conformerNameOfDeclaredName` (which does, and whose name reaches an author).
	// MEASURED, with a single `String` return folding the two: `array-conditional-conformance-withheld`'s
	// nested case began reporting *"but 'Array_Opaque' does not implement 'Hashable'"* where the corpus pins
	// *"but 'OpaqueArr' does not implement 'Hashable'"* — the compiler's mangling shown to someone who wrote
	// a typealias. So the miss is a CASE the caller must handle, not a default this may pick.
	function instanceConformerReduction(id GenericInstanceId) returns GenericInstanceConformer
		// ⛔⛔ **AN ARRAY IS DECIDED BY ITS `where` CLAUSE AND MAY NEVER FALL THROUGH TO `declaredBase` (A4q).**
		// The paragraph above rests on `Array` having no `StructLayout` — *"a BUILTIN base has no StructLayout
		// to declare anything, so it keeps the mangled name it always had"* — and listing `stdlib/Array.maxon`
		// ENDED that. `structOf("Array")` now answers `declared`, so a NON-conforming `Array with Opaque`
		// stopped falling through to `unreduced` and collapsed to the conformer `Array` instead — which
		// `stdlib/Array.maxon:637`'s `extension Array implements Hashable, Equatable where Element is Hashable
		// and Equatable` registers as a plain declared conformance, because `typeDeclaresInterface` works in
		// NAMES and a name cannot carry a `where` clause. The conditional half was therefore discharged by
		// nothing at all.
		//
		// ⇒ MEASURED, three cases of `array-conditional-conformance-withheld` at once: `Set with (Array with
		// Opaque)` and `Map with (Array with Opaque, Val)` stopped being refused, and
		// `Array with (Array with Opaque)` started SERVING `hash()`. A conformance that had been withheld since
		// the array-hashable rung was granted to every element in the language by a whitelist line.
		//
		// The distinction the `declaredBase` arm draws is between a base whose `where` clause constrains its
		// INSTANTIATION — `MapIterator`'s `where Key is Hashable`, which `checkOneInstantiation` rejects at the
		// site, so every instance that exists satisfies it and the conformance may be read off the declaration
		// — and a base whose conformance is itself CONDITIONAL. `Array with Opaque` is a perfectly legal
		// instantiation that simply is not `Hashable`, so no instantiation check speaks for it and the
		// reduction remains the only thing that can. Until the reduction is per-INTERFACE, `Array` is the one
		// base with such an extension and its own arm stays authoritative for it — including the FALLBACK,
		// which is the half that moved.
		if self.isArrayInstance(id) 'theConditionalConformanceIsAuthoritative'
			if self.arrayElementCarriesConformance(id) 'conformingArray'
				return GenericInstanceConformer.arrayBuiltin
			end 'conformingArray'

			return GenericInstanceConformer.unreduced
		end 'theConditionalConformanceIsAuthoritative'

		let baseName = self.genericInstanceBaseName(id)
		return match self.structOf(baseName) 'base'
			declared gives GenericInstanceConformer.declaredBase(baseName)
			undeclared gives GenericInstanceConformer.unreduced
		end 'base'
	end 'instanceConformerReduction'

	// The conformer a declared NAME reduces to: a ranged int/float alias collapses to its underlying
	// primitive's name (`Integer` -> `int`), which is what its intrinsic conformance and its `__witness_int.*`
	// tables are keyed by; every other name is its own conformer. The name half of `canonicalizeRangedAlias`,
	// split out so the reduction is stated once whether the caller arrived with a type or with a name.
	export function conformerNameOfDeclaredName(name ByteArray) returns String
		if let underlying = try self.aliasUnderlyingInAnyFile(name) 'rangedAlias'
			return primitiveTypeTagName(maxonTypeTag(underlying))
		end 'rangedAlias'

		// ⭐⭐ **A GENERIC-INSTANCE TYPEALIAS OVER A CONFORMING `Array` REDUCES TO THE `Array` CONFORMER
		// (array-hashable) — the ranged alias's rule one alias kind over, and it is NOT optional.** A generic
		// instance stores its type argument as the SOURCE spelling: `Map with (IntArr, Val)` holds
		// `named("IntArr")` exactly as it holds `named("Val")`, and both are reduced HERE rather than at
		// instantiation. Without this arm the key of `specs-shv2/array-hashable.md`'s `int-array-map-key` is
		// the conformer `IntArr` — a name nothing conforms under — so the very witness slot the reduction
		// exists to name is never reachable through an alias, which is the only way the corpus writes one.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE SUPERSEDED GUARD IS `genericAliasInstance`'s PRECONDITION, not caution.** That door refuses
		// a spelling two files contest, and a whole-program reader must ask this first (see there); a
		// superseded spelling falls through to its own name, which conforms to nothing — the same answer a
		// non-conforming array gets, and the safe direction.
		if self.isGenericAlias(name) and not self.genericAliasSpellingIsSuperseded(name) 'genericInstanceAlias'
			return self.conformerNameOfInstanceSpelling(self.genericAliasInstance(name), spelling: name)
		end 'genericInstanceAlias'

		// ⭐⭐ **AN INSTANCE ALSO ARRIVES AS ITS OWN CANONICAL RENDERING, AND THAT SPELLING REDUCES BY THE SAME
		// RULE (W58).** The arm above answers for the spelling an author WROTE (`IntUpCursor`); this one answers
		// for the spelling the COMPILER wrote (`UpCursor_Integer`), which is what `renderDeclaredTypeName`
		// records into a conformance's `withArgs` and therefore what every conformance question asked of a
		// binding is handed. `declaredNameIsManaged` already carries this same arm for the ownership question,
		// with the same sentence — *"a generic instance arrives as its canonical name at some doors, and no
		// registry is keyed by that"* — and the conformance question needed it for the same reason and did not
		// have it. MEASURED at the E3126 door: *"'UpCursor_Integer' does not implement 'Cursor'"* on a program
		// whose `type UpCursor uses Element implements Cursor with Element` declares exactly that, which is
		// `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s `Iterable with (Element, ArrayIter)` to the letter.
		//
		// ⚠ It is asked after the alias door because that one holds the author's spelling and this one the
		// compiler's; `renderedInstanceOfUnclaimedName` is what keeps a mint from outranking a DECLARATION.
		if let rendered = try self.renderedInstanceOfUnclaimedName(name) 'aRenderedInstance'
			return self.conformerNameOfInstanceSpelling(rendered, spelling: name)
		end 'aRenderedInstance'

		return String.from(name)
	end 'conformerNameOfDeclaredName'

	// The reduction the two instance SPELLINGS share — an author's `typealias` and the compiler's own canonical
	// mint — written once so the two doors above cannot come to answer differently about one instance.
	//
	// ⚠ The UNREDUCED fallback is the CALLER'S spelling and never the mangled instance: the alias door holds
	// the name the author wrote, and that name reaches an author through the withheld-extension diagnostic
	// (`array-conditional-conformance-withheld`, which measured the regression when it fell through to the
	// mangler). Either way the answer conforms to nothing, which is the part that decides the program.
	function conformerNameOfInstanceSpelling(id GenericInstanceId, spelling ByteArray) returns String
		match self.instanceConformerReduction(id) 'reduction'
			arrayBuiltin then return String.from(self.arrayRecordDeclaredName())
			declaredBase(baseName) then return String.from(baseName)
			unreduced then return String.from(spelling)
		end 'reduction'
	end 'conformerNameOfInstanceSpelling'

	// ⭐ **HOW A DIAGNOSTIC SPELLS A DECLARED NAME — the name-keyed twin of `instanceDisplayName` (W58).**
	// A conformance's recorded `withArgs` are RENDERED names, so a binding to a generic instance reaches every
	// message that prints one as `Tally_Integer`: the compiler's own canonical mint, which no source line
	// holds and which reads to an author as a type they never wrote. This is the same split
	// `conformerNameOfDeclaredName` is to `conformerNameOfType` — one door for a caller holding a NAME where
	// the other holds a type — and it exists for the reason `InstanceDisplayNameMap` states: a person did not
	// write `Array_String`.
	//
	// ⚠ **NOTHING MAY COMPARE TWO OF THESE**, exactly as `instanceDisplayName`'s header insists: two aliases
	// legitimately spell one type. Every comparison stays on `conformerNameOfDeclaredName`, which is the
	// identity; this answers only what gets printed.
	export function displaySpellingOfDeclaredName(name ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		return self.instanceDisplayName(try self.renderedInstanceOfUnclaimedName(name) otherwise return name)
	end 'displaySpellingOfDeclaredName'

	// A ranged numeric alias resolves to its underlying primitive `MaxonType` (`int(0 to u32.max)` -> `integer`),
	// exactly as `resolveNamedType` does (`alias.underlying`, range dropped); every other type passes through.
	// The one step that lets `conformerTypeArgName` name a primitive-bound argument by its primitive.
	//
	// ⚠ Bare-name for `classifyUnionPayload`'s reason: the file that governs a generic INSTANCE's type
	// argument is the one that wrote the instantiation, and a `GenericInstanceId` does not carry it.
	// Only `underlying` is reachable through that door, never the range.
	function canonicalizeRangedAlias(t MaxonType) returns MaxonType
		if maxonTypeTag(t) == ValueTypeTag.named 'named'
			if let underlying = try self.aliasUnderlyingInAnyFile(self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(t))) 'rangedAlias'
				return underlying
			end 'rangedAlias'
		end 'named'
		return t
	end 'canonicalizeRangedAlias'

	// The structural mangling of a generic instance itself — `Box_Integer` for `Box with Integer` — the
	// base name joined to each argument's mangling. Shared by `mangleTypeArg` (a nested-instance argument)
	// and lowering's descriptor-label builder.
	//
	// ⚠ **DERIVED ONCE PER INSTANCE, AND THAT IS NOT A CONSTANT-FACTOR TIDY-UP** — the derivation is
	// RECURSIVE through `mangleTypeArg`'s nested arm, so re-deriving on every ask was quadratic in
	// nesting depth. `CompiledInstanceNameMap` carries the measurement.
	//
	// Returns the `ByteArray` six of the eight callers want (a symbol name is bytes, and each of those six
	// used to convert on the spot); the two that want a `String` say so. It hands back the memo's own
	// handle, so an ask costs a retain rather than a rebuild — safe because every consumer either READS it
	// (a map key, a `Set.contains` probe) or builds a NEW string around it (`destructorSymbol`,
	// `String.from`), and none writes through it.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND IT IS WHERE A CONTESTED NAME IS MOVED INTO THE RESERVED SPACE — AT THE SOLE PRODUCER, NOT
	// AT A LATER CHECK.** See `reservedIfDeclared`. Deriving the final name HERE is what lets every
	// consumer be a pure read: the PARSER is one of them (`decrefCalleeFor` bakes
	// `__destruct_<mangled>` into an emitted drop call), so a name revised after the parse would leave
	// `main` calling a symbol the destructor synthesis no longer emits.
	// ⭐⭐ **THE STEM BOTH INSTANCE-NAME BUILDERS START FROM — the base name, PLUS a sized container's COUNT.**
	// The two builders (`mangleGenericInstance`, the emitted symbol; `canonicalNameWithin`, the comparison
	// name) differ only in how they render an ARGUMENT, so the head is written once and neither can come to
	// disagree with the other about whether a size is part of a type's name.
	//
	// ⚠ **WITHOUT THE COUNT, TWO LEGAL TYPES SHARE ONE NAME, AND IT IS NOT A COSMETIC CLASH.** `Vector with 3
	// Int` and `Vector with 4 Int` are different types by construction (the size is half of the instance
	// key), and both mangled to `Vector_Int` — MEASURED: a program declaring both is refused outright with
	// **E3006 duplicate definition of 'Vector_Int' — the generic instantiations `Vector with Int` and
	// `Vector with Int` compile to that same name**, a message naming one type twice. `ParseStaging`'s symbol
	// check is what caught it, which is exactly what that check is for.
	function instanceNameStem(id GenericInstanceId) returns String
		var out = String.from(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id))
		let size = self.genericInstanceFixedSize(id)
		if size != NoFixedSize 'sized'
			out.append("_{FixedSizeNameTag}{size}")
		end 'sized'
		return out
	end 'instanceNameStem'

	export function mangleGenericInstance(id GenericInstanceId) returns ByteArray
		if let derived = try self.compiledInstanceNames.get(id) 'alreadyDerived'
			return derived
		end 'alreadyDerived'

		var out = self.instanceNameStem(id)
		for arg in self.genericInstances.instanceArgs(id) 'eachArg'
			out.append("_")
			out.append(self.mangleTypeArg(arg))
		end 'eachArg'

		let name = self.reservedIfDeclared(out.toByteArray())
		self.compiledInstanceNames.upsert(id, value: name)
		return name
	end 'mangleGenericInstance'

	// ⭐⭐ **RESERVED-SPACE DISJOINTNESS: A COMPILED INSTANCE NAME A DECLARATION ALSO CLAIMS IS MINTED
	// BEHIND `__` INSTEAD (P1.7).**
	//
	// An instantiation has no source name, so the compiler builds one, and every per-type symbol the
	// backend emits is derived from that string — `__destruct_<name>`, `__layout_<name>`. A declared
	// `type Box_String` derives ITS symbols from the identical string, so `Box with String` and
	// `type Box_String` minted one `__destruct_Box_String` twice and the later install won: a LEAK when
	// the loser owned a field the winner does not drop, a SILENT SUCCESS when the two layouts happened to
	// agree. That was reported as E3006 — which made a program the language permits (a legal type name,
	// plus an instantiation the author may never have written: a bare `[Foo…]` literal interns
	// `Array with Foo`) fail to compile, naming an instantiation that appears nowhere in the source.
	//
	// `__` is ALREADY reserved — `E2051` refuses to DECLARE a name that starts with it — so a compiled
	// name minted behind it is one no declaration can hold, and the two halves of the namespace are
	// disjoint BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by a check. That is how the bootstrap has always spelled a
	// synthesized instance name (`__Array_Foo`, `maxon-sharp/Compiler/2-Parser.cs`), and why it needs no
	// declaration-vs-instance check at all.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND IT IS WHY NOTHING HERE PROBES THE INSTANCE SET.** A minted name has to be disjoint from
	// every OTHER instance's compiled name too, and that falls out of the same reservation instead of a
	// second probe: `mangleGenericInstance` joins BASE-FIRST, no base may start with `__`, so no BARE
	// compiled name ever does — and two minted names are equal only when the bare names behind them are,
	// which is the pair collision `checkTypeSymbolNamespace` already reports over the FINAL names. See
	// `ReservedTypeNamePrefix` for why a non-reserved prefix would break exactly this, and
	// `nested-contest-mints-onto-a-second-contested-name` for the case that pins it.
	//
	// ⚠ **ON CONTEST ONLY, WHICH IS A COST DECISION.** Prefixing unconditionally would move the mangled
	// symbol in 108 committed goldens across four targets, 45 of them on hosts that cannot be regenerated
	// here. Renaming only what was previously an ERROR moves no name that ever compiled, so every golden
	// stays byte-identical.
	//
	// ⚠ **RE-PROBED, NOT PREFIXED ONCE.** `__Box_String` could itself be declared, and the loop
	// TERMINATES because each pass makes the candidate strictly longer while the declaration set is
	// finite and fixed (the sweep is complete before any name is derived).
	//
	// Two instantiations that compile to ONE name are NOT cured by this and are not meant to be — `_` is a
	// legal name character, so the join is not injective (`Box with A_B` and `Box_A with B` both give
	// `Box_A_B`) and there is no declaration to move out of the way. That stays E3006, in
	// `checkTypeSymbolNamespace`.
	function reservedIfDeclared(bare ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		var name = bare
		while self.claimsCompiledTypeName(name) 'contested'
			name = "{String.from(ReservedTypeNamePrefix)}{String.from(name)}".toByteArray()
		end 'contested'
		return name
	end 'reservedIfDeclared'

	// ⭐ **DOES A DECLARATION ALREADY CLAIM THE COMPILED TYPE NAME `name`?** The declared half of the
	// compiled-name namespace, asked in ONE place: a `type` and an `enum`/`union` each mint
	// `__destruct_<name>` from their bare name (`installStructDestructors` / `installUnionDestructors`),
	// which is the identical string an instance's `__destruct_<mangled>` / `__layout_<mangled>` are built
	// from.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN `interface` AND A `typealias` ARE ABSENT, AND NEITHER IS AN OVERSIGHT.** Neither mints a
	// per-type symbol, so neither can collide with an instance and neither may displace one. An alias
	// mints nothing at all — an instance's methods are emitted under its BASE's name, which is why
	// `typealias Box_Integer = Box with Integer` is legal. An interface's only emitted artifact is
	// `__witness_<conformer>.<interface>`, whose head is the CONFORMER, so an `interface Box_String`
	// beside a `Box with String` shares no symbol with it (measured against the bootstrap oracle: it
	// builds and runs; shv2 used to refuse it with E3006).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE HEAD ARGUMENT ONLY EVER COVERED THE DECLARED HALF, WHICH IS THIS FUNCTION'S HALF, AND IT
	// STILL DOES.** It says nothing about one witness label against ANOTHER — and that half WAS broken,
	// silently: `_`-joined, `(A_B, C)` and `(A, B_C)` spelled one table. The cure is not a check here (a
	// witness table is minted during lowering, long after this sweep, and there is no declaration to move
	// aside) but the SEPARATOR — see `IrInterface.witnessTableLabel`. It also strengthens the claim above
	// by a second, independent route: a `.` occurs in no source identifier, hence in none of the `_`-joined
	// compiled instance names this function guards, so no witness label is spellable from the instance side
	// at all.
	// It reads THIS index's own registries and not `project.typeNameDecls`, because it is asked during
	// the signature sweep — before any file is parsed, which is the whole point (see
	// `mangleGenericInstance`). The two agree by construction for the kinds above: both are folded from
	// the same declarations, and a program whose sweep and parse disagree has a parse diagnostic and
	// fails the build regardless.
	function claimsCompiledTypeName(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.containsStruct(name) or self.containsEnum(name)
	end 'claimsCompiledTypeName'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE CANONICAL TYPE IDENTITY OF A GENERIC INSTANCE (P1.7).** `mangleGenericInstance` names
	// the instance as it was INTERNED, and one type can be interned twice — `Box with N0` and
	// `Box with (Box with S0)` are the same type spelled two ways, and they compile to `Box_N0` and
	// `Box_Box_S0`. That duplicate is pre-existing and is its own rung; what must not be pre-existing is two
	// answers to "are these the same TYPE?", so every comparison site asks THIS, which resolves an
	// alias-named argument through the alias table and therefore gives both spellings ONE string.
	//
	// ⚠ **COMPARISON ONLY — it names nothing that is emitted.** The symbol names (`__destruct_<name>`,
	// `__layout_<name>`) and the E3006 compiled-name space keep asking `mangleGenericInstance`, so no
	// emitted byte moves. That separation is the whole reason this is a second function and a second memo
	// rather than a change to the first.
	// The EMPTY-path entry: the walk below memoizes every instance it names, so this only has to say that a
	// fresh ask starts with nothing open.
	export function canonicalInstanceName(id GenericInstanceId) returns ByteArray
		var path = GenericInstanceIdArray.create()
		return self.canonicalNameWithin(id, path: path)
	end 'canonicalInstanceName'

	// The recursive half, carrying the instances already open on this walk.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE PATH GUARD IS LOAD-BEARING, because this runs in the PARSER** — `aggregateNameOf` asks it, and
	// the parse completes before `checkGenericAliasCycles` (E3091) refuses a cyclic alias. Resolving alias
	// arguments is exactly what makes `typealias A = Box with A` a cycle here, where `mangleTypeArg` never
	// followed one. On revisit it falls back to the INTERNED name, which terminates and is deterministic;
	// the program is refused moments later by E3091 regardless, so the fallback's only job is to get there.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT MEMOIZES AT EVERY LEVEL, AND THAT IS A CUBIC — NOT A TIDY-UP.** The first version consulted the
	// memo only at the outermost ask and recursed raw beneath it, so naming instance `i` of an alias CHAIN
	// re-walked the whole chain and appended a name of length O(i) at each level — O(i²) for one name, O(N³)
	// to name them all. MEASURED, A/B on a chain of N aliases each used to build a value: `semanticCheck` ran
	// 0.4 / 0.7 / 1.3 / 2.9 ms at N = 100/200/400/800 before the canonical namer existed (LINEAR) against
	// 12.3 / 53.5 / 303.8 / 1826.1 ms with it (x4.4, x5.7, x6.0 per doubling — rising), a 630x regression at
	// N=800 that widened with N. Memoizing here restores it: the chain is walked ONCE.
	//
	// A memo hit is the true canonical name whenever it matters. In an ACYCLIC graph the answer does not
	// depend on `path` at all, so a name derived under one walk is correct under every other. In a cyclic one
	// the guard can make a name path-dependent — but that program is refused by E3091 and emits nothing, and
	// `deriveInstanceNames` warms every instance in ascending id order before any check asks, so which walk
	// got there first is a property of the program rather than of the asking site.
	function canonicalNameWithin(id GenericInstanceId, path GenericInstanceIdArray) returns ByteArray
		for open in path 'eachOpen'
			if open == id 'cyclic'
				return self.mangleGenericInstance(id)
			end 'cyclic'
		end 'eachOpen'

		if let derived = try self.canonicalInstanceNames.get(id) 'alreadyDerived'
			return derived
		end 'alreadyDerived'

		path.push(id)
		var out = self.instanceNameStem(id)
		for arg in self.genericInstances.instanceArgs(id) 'eachArg'
			out.append("_")
			out.append(self.canonicalTypeArgName(arg, path: path))
		end 'eachArg'
		_ = try path.pop() otherwise panic("canonicalNameWithin: path.pop on a path this call pushed to")

		let name = out.toByteArray()
		self.canonicalInstanceNames.upsert(id, value: name)
		return name
	end 'canonicalNameWithin'

	// One type argument in canonical form — `mangleTypeArg`'s comparison twin. It differs in exactly one arm:
	// a `named` leaf that names a generic-instance ALIAS becomes THAT instance's canonical name instead of
	// the alias's bare spelling, which is what collapses the two spellings of one type.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE EDGE IS `denotedInstance`'S AND IS NOT SPELLED AGAIN HERE (N2b review).** "Which instance does
	// this type argument denote?" is ONE fact with three readers — this name, the identity + (tag, nameId)
	// pair `checkTypeParameterArgsAt` reads through `canonicalTypeArg`, and the E3091 cycle graph's edge —
	// and it had been written out three times. A reader that learned an arm the others did not would call
	// `Box with Bytes` and `Box with (Array with Byte)` two types where another calls them one, silently,
	// in a name that mangled symbols are built from.
	function canonicalTypeArgName(t MaxonType, path GenericInstanceIdArray) returns String
		if let denoted = try self.denotedInstance(t) 'denotesAnInstance'
			return String.from(self.canonicalNameWithin(denoted, path: path))
		end 'denotesAnInstance'

		// A `named` leaf that denotes no instance is a declared aggregate or a ranged alias: its own name,
		// which is what `mangleTypeArg` would answer for it and is spelled here without the String round trip.
		if maxonTypeTag(t) == ValueTypeTag.named 'namedLeaf'
			return String.from(self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(t)))
		end 'namedLeaf'

		return self.mangleTypeArg(t)
	end 'canonicalTypeArgName'

	// ⭐ **DERIVE EVERY SWEPT INSTANCE'S NAMES NOW, SO THE PARSE ONLY EVER READS THEM (P1.7).**
	//
	// `mangleGenericInstance` memoizes by WRITING into `compiledInstanceNames`, and the real parse became a
	// caller when a generic instance gained its nominal identity (`Parser.aggregateNameOf`). Every other
	// caller — ParseStaging's E3006 sweep, lowering's descriptor labels, the destructor cascade — runs after
	// parsing, single-threaded; the parser does NOT, and a per-file fan-out writing one shared memo is the
	// race the "a file's parse is a pure function of its inputs" invariant exists to prevent. Deriving the
	// whole set here, at the one point the sweep is complete, leaves the index READ-ONLY for the parse.
	//
	// ⚠ **BOTH NAMES, because there are two memos and the parse reads BOTH.** `canonicalInstanceName` arrived
	// with its own map and its own lazy write, and `aggregateNameOf` asks it on the same parse path — so
	// warming only the compiled name left exactly the hole this function exists to close, in silence, one
	// commit after it was closed. A memo the parse can write is the hazard; naming it in the comment above
	// while a second one sat un-warmed beside it is how one fact became two.
	//
	// ⚠ It warms the instances the SWEEP interned, which is exactly the set a parse can name. The builtin
	// instances minted later (`internArrayByteInstance`, `builtinInstanceForElement`, …) still derive lazily on
	// first ask — they are interned during lowering, long after the last parse, so they are never contended.
	export function deriveInstanceNames()
		// ⚠ **FIRST, because it composes `fileScopedInstance`, which INTERNS.** It cannot in fact intern
		// anything new — `settleGenericAliasContest`'s fixpoint runs until exactly this composition over
		// exactly this array stops adding instances — but ordering it ahead of the warming loop means the
		// warming loop covers whatever it produces, so the invariant this whole function exists for ("the
		// parse only ever READS") does not rest on that argument holding.
		self.deriveInstanceDisplayNames()

		for id in 0 upto self.genericInstances.count() 'eachInstance'
			_ = self.mangleGenericInstance(id)
			_ = self.canonicalInstanceName(id)
		end 'eachInstance'
		// The name→instance direction is a THIRD thing the parse can ask and must not have to write (see
		// `indexCanonicalInstanceNames`), so it is warmed here beside the two forward memos rather than being
		// left to whichever reader got there first.
		self.indexCanonicalInstanceNames()
	end 'deriveInstanceNames'

	// ⭐⭐ **INSTANCE ⇒ THE `typealias` AN AUTHOR DECLARED FOR IT — the DISPLAY index (see
	// `InstanceDisplayNameMap`).** Derived from `genericAliasDecls`, which holds every SOURCE declaration and
	// deliberately holds no compiler-minted one (`rekeyScopedGenericAliases` states that split): a name only
	// counts here if a person wrote it, because the whole point of the index is to quote a person's own
	// spelling back at them.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE INSTANCE IS `fileScopedInstance`'d BY THE DECLARING FILE**, exactly as `rekeyScopedGenericAliases`
	// and the contest rounds scope it, so a `typealias Bytes = Array with Byte` in a program where two files
	// declare `Byte` differently is filed against the instance ITS OWN file means and not against whichever
	// bare one the sweep interned.
	//
	// ⚠ **FIRST DECLARATION WINS WHEN TWO NAMES DENOTE ONE INSTANCE** (`Bytes` and `stdlib/File.maxon`'s
	// `ByteArray` over the same `Byte`). Both spellings are TRUE of that one type, so there is nothing to
	// choose between them on correctness; declaration order settles it deterministically and needs no reader
	// file, which the parse-time askers would otherwise all have to supply.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A TYPE-SCOPED ALIAS LOSES TO AN UNSCOPED ONE, AND IT IS THE ONE PLACE IN THE COMPILER THAT RANKS
	// TWO TRUE NAMES** (⚖ USER RULING, W17; narrowed from an outright exclusion by a second ruling at W49b —
	// see `deriveInstanceDisplayNames` for that half, which this paragraph is the FIRST half of and no longer
	// the whole of). `type Character`'s `typealias ByteMemory = __ManagedMemory with Byte` is a true name
	// for `Array with Byte` and a declaration a person wrote — so by the paragraph above it won the display,
	// and `stdlib/Character.maxon` sorting ahead of `stdlib/String.maxon` is the whole of why it beat
	// `String.maxon:23`'s file-scope `ByteArray`. But an inner alias is not a name the author of the refused
	// program could have WRITTEN — `stdlib/String.maxon:25` says exactly that about its own twin, which is why
	// that file declares a file-scope `ByteBuffer` beside it — so quoting `Character.ByteMemory` back at
	// someone who wrote `ByteArray` breaks the 2026-08-04 ruling that a diagnostic names a type THE WAY THE
	// AUTHOR WROTE IT. MEASURED before the exclusion: three cases across `array-init` and
	// `bytearray-element-size` read `got 'Character.ByteMemory'` where `got 'ByteArray'` is correct.
	//
	// ⚠ **EXCLUDED FROM THE DISPLAY AND FROM NOTHING ELSE.** An inner alias goes on resolving exactly as it
	// did — `parseTypeReference`'s inner-alias arm, `scopedGenericAliasInstance`, the contest rounds and the
	// buffer-surface derivation all read `genericAliases`/`genericAliasDecls` and are untouched. What is being
	// said is only that it is not a SPELLING to hand back.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND THE EXCLUSION IS CONDITIONAL, NOT ABSOLUTE — "ADMIT TYPE-SCOPED WHEN IT'S THE ONLY NAME"
	// (⚖ USER RULING, W49b).** It was written as an unconditional skip, and the fallback that left behind is
	// the CANONICAL MINT: an instance whose only declared name is type-scoped was quoted as
	// `Array_Tc458b93abe5c7098`. That serves nobody, and it is the very thing the W17 ruling was reaching for
	// — a diagnostic names a type THE WAY THE AUTHOR WROTE IT — turned against itself, because when
	// `Inner.UArr` is the only name the author CAN write, it is the way the author wrote it. MEASURED:
	// `generic-type-substitution/error.a-name-the-scope-does-not-bind-binds-nothing` read
	// `expected 'Array_Tc458b93abe5c7098', got 'Array_T4032a9ecb1272bfc'` where `expected 'Inner.UArr', got
	// 'Outer.OArr'` is correct.
	//
	// ⭐ **W17's OWN CASE IS PROTECTED BY CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN BY A CARVE-OUT, AND THAT IS WHY THE ROUNDS
	// ARE SPLIT BY KIND.** What W17 fixed was `Character.ByteMemory` beating `stdlib/String.maxon:23`'s
	// file-scope `ByteArray` — and it beat it only because `Character.maxon` sorts ahead of `String.maxon`.
	// BOTH instances in that measurement still hold an unscoped spelling (`Array with Byte` is `ByteArray` in
	// five listed modules; `__ManagedMemory with Byte` is `String.maxon:28`'s `ByteBuffer`, which that file
	// declares for exactly this reason), so round one claims them and round two never sees them. The four
	// cases W17 was protecting — `array-init`'s `error.a-buffer-of-the-wrong-element` and
	// `bytearray-element-size`'s `a-byte-two-files-disagree-about-is-two-types`,
	// `a-buffer-field-refuses-another-files-byte-at-the-construction` and
	// `error.the-two-string-spellings-name-their-own-corpus-types` — go on reading `ByteArray`.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN UNSCOPED SPELLING WINS OVER A TYPE-SCOPED ONE ALWAYS, AND IT IS THE ROUND THAT DECIDES THAT
	// RATHER THAN THE WALK ORDER.** That is a STRENGTHENING of what stood before W17, not a weakening: the
	// contest between the two kinds used to be settled by which file the loader reached first (the
	// `Character.maxon`-before-`String.maxon` accident above), and it is now settled by kind, so it cannot
	// vary with the filesystem. Within ONE kind, first-declaration-wins is unchanged and still walks
	// `genericAliasDecls` in its recorded order.
	function deriveInstanceDisplayNames()
		self.nameInstancesFromDeclarations(DisplayNameRound.unscoped)
		self.nameInstancesFromDeclarations(DisplayNameRound.typeScoped)
	end 'deriveInstanceDisplayNames'

	// ONE ROUND of the walk above, over the declarations of a single scope kind — the two rounds are the same
	// rule applied to two populations, so they are one body and cannot come to disagree about what naming an
	// instance means. Round two is what "only name" is spelled as: an instance round one claimed is already in
	// the map, so `anEarlierDeclarationAlreadyNamedThisType` is exactly the "an unscoped name exists" test, and
	// no second index has to be built to answer it.
	function nameInstancesFromDeclarations(round DisplayNameRound)
		for entry in self.genericAliasDecls 'eachDeclaration'
			let entryRound = DisplayNameRound.typeScoped if entry.typeScoped else DisplayNameRound.unscoped
			if entryRound != round 'aDeclarationForTheOtherRound'
				continue
			end 'aDeclarationForTheOtherRound'

			let instance = self.fileScopedInstance(entry.internedInstance(), filePath: entry.filePath)
			if self.instanceDisplayNames.contains(instance) 'anEarlierDeclarationAlreadyNamedThisType'
				continue
			end 'anEarlierDeclarationAlreadyNamedThisType'

			self.instanceDisplayNames.upsert(instance, value: self.unambiguousAliasSpelling(entry))
		end 'eachDeclaration'
	end 'nameInstancesFromDeclarations'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE AMBIGUITY RULE, AND IT IS THE USER'S (2026-08-04): "if a name is possibly ambiguous it needs
	// to contain its full namespace".** A declared alias is ambiguous EXACTLY when this program's declarations
	// of that name denote DIFFERENT types — which is not a fresh question, it is `contestedGenericAliases`,
	// already settled whole-program by `settleGenericAliasContest`. So the display asks the same index the
	// RESOLUTION asks, and a spelling can never claim to distinguish two types the compiler does not itself
	// hold apart.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE QUALIFIER IS THE NAMESPACE ONLY WHEN THE NAMESPACE ACTUALLY DISTINGUISHES THE CLAIMANTS.** A
	// non-exported `typealias` is FILE-local (`specs/duplicate-typealias.md`) and shv2 scopes a contested one
	// per DECLARING FILE (`GenericAliasByDeclaringFile`) — not per directory — so two files in one directory,
	// or two files in the ROOT (whose namespace is empty), share a namespace and a namespace-qualified
	// spelling would render `expected 'x.Bytes', got 'x.Bytes'`: a refusal with no content, which is the one
	// outcome this whole door exists to prevent. Where the namespaces are distinct the namespace is what an
	// author recognizes and is used; where they are not, the declaring FILE is the qualifier, because the file
	// IS the scope that resolves the name.
	function unambiguousAliasSpelling(entry GenericAliasEntry) returns ByteArray
		if not self.contestedGenericAliases.contains(entry.aliasName) 'onlyOneTypeInThisProgramWearsThisName'
			return entry.aliasName
		end 'onlyOneTypeInThisProgramWearsThisName'

		if self.contestedAliasNamespacesAreDistinct(entry.aliasName) 'theNamespaceTellsThemApart'
			return qualifiedCandidateName(self.moduleDirectoryOf(entry.filePath), bare: entry.aliasName).toByteArray()
		end 'theNamespaceTellsThemApart'

		return joinQualifiedName(entry.filePath.toString().toByteArray(), member: entry.aliasName)
	end 'unambiguousAliasSpelling'

	// Do the files declaring this contested name sit in DIFFERENT modules? Only then does a namespace-qualified
	// spelling separate them. Walked rather than indexed: it runs once per contested alias declaration, and the
	// corpus contests none.
	function contestedAliasNamespacesAreDistinct(aliasName ByteArray) returns bool
		let claimants = try self.contestedGenericAliases.get(aliasName) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.contestedAliasNamespacesAreDistinct: '{String.from(aliasName)}' is contested but carries no declaring-file group — `recordGenericAliasContest` writes both indexes together")
		var seen = ModuleDirectorySet.create()
		for (declFilePath, _) in claimants 'eachClaimant'
			let moduleDirectory = self.moduleDirectoryOfPathText(declFilePath)
			if seen.contains(moduleDirectory) 'twoClaimantsShareAModule'
				return false
			end 'twoClaimantsShareAModule'

			seen.insert(moduleDirectory)
		end 'eachClaimant'

		return true
	end 'contestedAliasNamespacesAreDistinct'

	// ⭐ **HOW A DIAGNOSTIC SPELLS A GENERIC INSTANCE** — the author's `typealias` when the program holds one,
	// else the canonical mint (see `InstanceDisplayNameMap` for why a miss is an answer). It is the DISPLAY
	// sibling of `canonicalInstanceName`, never a substitute for it: nothing may COMPARE two of these, because
	// two aliases legitimately spell one type.
	export function instanceDisplayName(id GenericInstanceId) returns ByteArray
		return try self.instanceDisplayNames.get(id) otherwise self.canonicalInstanceName(id)
	end 'instanceDisplayName'

	// ⭐ **THE NOMINAL IDENTITY OF AN INSTANCE'S SUBSTITUTED TYPE ARGUMENT (P1.7)** — what a parameter
	// declared with the bare type parameter `T` actually demands at a call made through that instance. It is
	// the third source of the aggregate name `namedAggregatesConflict` compares, beside the parser's
	// `aggregateNameOf` and SemanticCheck's `aggregateNameFor`, and it exists for the reason those two are
	// twins rather than one function: the name is resolved against a DIFFERENT interner — an instance's
	// argument types are interned HERE, not in the merged `project.typeNames`. The comparison RULE is still
	// the single shared one; only the extraction is local.
	//
	// `EmptySourceText` for a scalar, a `String`, a ranged alias or an opaque `typeParameter` argument: those
	// carry no nominal identity and meet their argument by TAG (`checkDeclaredType`), so a `Box with Integer`
	// keeps accepting a plain `int` literal.
	// ⚠ **EVERY TAG STATES ITS OWN ANSWER.** This used to end in a bare `return EmptySourceText`, and that
	// was a silent default landing on the ONE rule that reads empty as a positive claim:
	// `namedAggregatesConflict` treats exactly-one-empty as "an aggregate meeting a SCALAR", so an
	// unhandled kind did not abstain — it asserted `scalar` and REJECTED a legal program. A tag appended to
	// `ValueTypeTag` must now fail to compile here rather than inherit a wrong answer.
	// ⚠ **IT TAKES THE ALREADY-SUBSTITUTED, ALREADY-CANONICAL ARGUMENT, NOT `(gid, position)`.** Its one
	// caller (`SemanticCheck.checkTypeParameterArgsAt`) needs the same type THREE ways — this identity, the
	// (tag, nameId) pair the shared `aggregatesConflict` compares with, and the declared tag the scalar half
	// judges against — and `substitutedInstanceArg` returns a heap-boxed `MaxonType`. Asked here by
	// (gid, position) it was substituted once for this answer and again for the `structRef` arm, and a third
	// time at the caller. One substitution, resolved once through `canonicalTypeArg` and read three times, is
	// both fewer boxes and one resolution instead of two.
	export function typeArgAggregateName(arg MaxonType) returns ByteArray
		match maxonTypeTag(arg) 'k'
			// No nominal identity: a scalar, a `String`, an opaque `T` and a function value all coerce by
			// TAG (`checkDeclaredType`), so `Box with Integer` keeps accepting a plain `42`.
			boolean then return EmptySourceText
			integer then return EmptySourceText
			float then return EmptySourceText
			named then return self.namedTypeArgIdentity(arg)
			exitCode then return EmptySourceText
			unresolved then return EmptySourceText
			void then panic("typeArgAggregateName: a type argument is `void` — a type argument is a TYPE, and the parser accepts no void one")
			// A struct argument is named by itself and a nested instance by its compiled name — the same two
			// spellings `mangleTypeArg` produces, which is why it is asked rather than re-derived.
			structRef then return self.mangleTypeArg(arg).toByteArray()
			string then return EmptySourceText
			function then return EmptySourceText
			typeParameter then return EmptySourceText
			// CANONICAL, not `mangleTypeArg`: a nested instance's identity must match what the two
			// value-side extractors produce for the same type, whichever way it was spelled.
			genericInstance then return self.canonicalInstanceName(typeNameIdOf(arg))
			// A Character argument carries no name — the builtin's identity is its tag, `string`'s answer.
			character then return EmptySourceText
			// An interface type is refused as a generic ARGUMENT at the front end (`Parser.checkGenericArgType`
			// rule 4), because an element slot is one machine word and a fat pointer is two. This arm is that
			// refusal's backstop, exactly as `primitiveTypeTagName`'s is.
			interfaceRef then panic("typeArgAggregateName: an interface type reached a generic type ARGUMENT — `Parser.checkGenericArgType` refuses a container of existentials, because an element slot is one machine word and a fat pointer is two")
		end 'k'
	end 'typeArgAggregateName'

	// ⭐⭐ **READ-TIME RESOLUTION OF A TYPE ARGUMENT SPELLED AS A GENERIC-INSTANCE ALIAS (P1.7)** — the type
	// argument as the thing it DENOTES, which for `Box with Bytes` is `Bytes`'s instance and not the name.
	//
	// `typealias N1 = Box with N0` records its argument as a plain `named(N0)` LEAF: only a syntactic
	// `Base with (…)` header becomes `genericInstance` at intern time, and the sweep records a file's
	// aliases at its END, so an alias named before it is interned cannot be seen there at all. Resolving it
	// HERE is what makes the two spellings agree — this runs from `semanticCheck`, long after the whole
	// sweep is folded, so the alias table is COMPLETE and the answer cannot depend on declaration or file
	// order. (`Box with N0` and `Box with (Box with S0)` still intern as two instances with two compiled
	// names; that duplicate is pre-existing and is its own rung. What matters for the comparison is the
	// TYPE ARGUMENT's identity, and both spellings yield `N0`'s instance — the same `Box_S0`.)
	//
	// ⭐ **IT HANDS BACK A TYPE AND NOT A NAME, AND THAT IS WHAT KEEPS THE RESOLUTION SINGLE.**
	// `checkTypeParameterArgsAt` wants the argument BOTH ways — the identity name (`typeArgAggregateName`)
	// and the (tag, nameId) pair the shared `aggregatesConflict` compares with — and a name-returning door
	// could serve only the first. Resolved to a name, `Box with Bytes`'s argument read `Array_Byte` for the
	// identity while its raw tag stayed `named`, so the byte-element boundary was asked about a name id that
	// is not an instance id and answered no: MEASURED, `MemBox.create(<a cwd buffer>)` refused
	// `expected 'Bytes'`.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT IS THE ONLY SPELLING OF THAT EDGE, AND THE EDGE HAD BEEN WRITTEN OUT THREE TIMES (N2b
	// review).** The other two were `canonicalTypeArgName` — the canonical instance NAME, which mangled
	// symbols and every nominal comparison are built from — and `buildInstanceArgGraph`, the E3091 cycle
	// graph. Nothing made the three agree: an arm added to one (a tuple alias, a namespace-qualified alias
	// name, a per-file resolution) would leave the others calling `Box with Bytes` and
	// `Box with (Array with Byte)` two types where this one calls them one, with no compile error at any
	// site. All three now ask here.
	//
	// ⚠ It cannot hang on a cyclic alias: `mangleGenericInstance` recurses only through `genericInstance`
	// args (`mangleTypeArg` returns a `named` arg's bare NAME without resolving it), and those nest
	// syntactically, so the recursion is bounded by source nesting depth. A cycle is refused earlier
	// anyway, by `firstCyclicAliasDecl` (E3091), which runs before the pipeline.
	export function canonicalTypeArg(arg MaxonType) returns MaxonType
		// A `genericInstance` already denotes itself, so it is handed back rather than re-boxed: the box
		// below is minted only where a NAME had to be turned into an instance.
		if maxonTypeTag(arg) != ValueTypeTag.named 'cannotSpellAnAlias'
			return arg
		end 'cannotSpellAnAlias'

		if let denoted = try self.denotedInstance(arg) 'namesAnInstance'
			return MaxonType.genericInstance(denoted)
		end 'namesAnInstance'

		return arg
	end 'canonicalTypeArg'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE EDGE ITSELF: WHICH INSTANCE DOES THIS TYPE ARGUMENT DENOTE?** — its own id when it IS an
	// instance, and the alias table's when it is a `named` leaf that names one. The `MaxonType`-returning
	// door above is this answer BOXED, for the one reader that needs a type; the two readers that need only
	// the id ask here and allocate nothing.
	//
	// ⚠ **THAT SPLIT IS WHY THERE IS A SECOND FUNCTION AND NOT A SECOND SPELLING.** Collapsing the id
	// readers onto `canonicalTypeArg` was MEASURED at +2 allocations / +32 bytes at every rung of the scale
	// ladder — a constant, from boxing an answer both of them immediately unwrapped. One fact, one place,
	// and the box only where a box is wanted.
	//
	// `throws` is `Map.get`'s own channel: "this argument denotes no instance" is the ordinary answer for a
	// scalar, a `String`, a struct or a plain ranged alias, and it is the caller's to interpret — the two
	// id readers skip the edge, and `canonicalTypeArg` hands the argument back unchanged.
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE ONE REMAINING LAST-WINS READER OF A CONTESTED NAME, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE (N3 review).**
	// It bypasses `genericAliasInstance`'s refusal and `scopedGenericAliasInstance`'s tiers alike, and it has
	// to: `buildInstanceArgGraph` walks EVERY interned instance, including the pre-re-key ones and the ones
	// earlier contest rounds superseded, whose arguments are still the bare spellings the sweep left. A door
	// that refused a contested name there would abort the cycle check on the very programs it exists for.
	//
	// What it answers is which instance a type ARGUMENT denotes, for two structural readers — the canonical
	// type-argument name and that cycle graph's edge — and for a contested name it answers the LAST-WINS
	// declaration, which is what every reader of a generic alias answered before this rung. It is written
	// down here rather than left to be rediscovered: this is the one place the rung's own "no plausible
	// answer without a file" rule is not applied, and the reason is that its callers have no file and no
	// program type to be wrong about.
	function denotedInstance(arg MaxonType) returns GenericInstanceId throws MapError
		if maxonTypeTag(arg) == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'itIsAnInstance'
			return typeNameIdOf(arg)
		end 'itIsAnInstance'

		// Only a `named` LEAF can spell an alias — a struct, a tuple and an opaque type parameter carry no
		// alias table entry, and a primitive carries no name at all.
		if maxonTypeTag(arg) != ValueTypeTag.named 'cannotSpellAnAlias'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'cannotSpellAnAlias'

		return try self.genericAliases.get(self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(arg)))
	end 'denotedInstance'

	// The identity of a `named` type argument that `canonicalTypeArg` has already resolved, so the generic
	// alias arm is not restated here.
	//
	// `named` is OVERLOADED — a declared union/enum VALUE (a boxed aggregate) and a ranged numeric alias
	// share it — so only a registered aggregate carries identity, exactly as both twins decide it.
	function namedTypeArgIdentity(arg MaxonType) returns ByteArray
		let name = self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(arg))
		if self.containsStruct(name) or self.containsEnum(name) 'registeredAggregate'
			return name
		end 'registeredAggregate'

		// A RANGED numeric alias (`Integer`), which is a nominal wrapper over a scalar and decays. This is
		// the enumerated last kind a `named` can be, not a fallthrough: the three are alias / aggregate /
		// ranged, the tag match above has already excluded everything that is not `named`, and
		// `canonicalTypeArg` has already taken the alias.
		return EmptySourceText
	end 'namedTypeArgIdentity'

	// ⭐ **THE FIRST GENERIC ALIAS WHOSE TYPE ARGUMENTS LEAD BACK TO IT (P1.7, E3091).** `typealias A = Box
	// with A` and the mutual `A = Box with B` / `B = Box with A` describe INFINITE types — no finite layout,
	// no finite compiled name — and both compiled clean and SEGFAULTED at run time before this check.
	//
	// Asked once per compile over the FINISHED table, so it needs none of the sweep's ordering: an alias
	// reaches itself or it does not, whatever order the files were swept in. The BLAME is read in
	// declaration-record order so the alias reported is a property of the program, not of a map's iteration.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CYCLE SET IS COMPUTED ONCE FOR THE WHOLE GRAPH, NOT ONCE PER ALIAS — AND THAT IS A CUBIC
	// REMOVED, NOT A TIDY-UP.** This first shipped as a per-alias reachability sweep: every alias walked the
	// instances its arguments reach, with a LINEAR scan of a `visited` array at every pop. On a chain of N
	// aliases (`N0 = Box with S0`, `N1 = Box with N0`, …) alias i reaches i instances and scans i entries per
	// pop, so the pass cost N x N x N. MEASURED, A/B in this worktree at dc0e1127c against the same tree with
	// only this check suppressed: the check alone cost 31 / 268 / 1,751 / 12,298 ms at N = 400 / 800 / 1,600 /
	// 3,200 — x8.6, x6.5, x7.0 per doubling, i.e. CUBIC, on programs that COMPILE (the rest of the compile
	// stayed flat at 139…197 ms across the same span). `nodesOnACycle` answers the same question for every
	// instance at once in O(instances + arguments): the same ladder now compiles in 149 / 197 / 307 ms at
	// N = 1,600 / 3,200 / 6,400 against a check-suppressed 143 / 194 / 295 ms, so the check costs 6 / 3 / 12 ms
	// and no longer grows out of the noise floor.
	//
	// A program with no generic alias cannot have a cycle at all — only an alias edge can close one, since a
	// nested `Base with (Base with …)` argument nests syntactically and therefore finitely — so the whole
	// pass, graph included, is skipped rather than merely cheap.
	export function firstCyclicAliasDecl() returns AliasCycleResult
		if self.genericAliasDecls.count() == 0 'noAliasCanCloseACycle'
			return AliasCycleResult.none
		end 'noAliasCanCloseACycle'

		// ⭐ **A CYCLE THE GRAPH BELOW STRUCTURALLY CANNOT SEE (N3 review)** — its edges resolve a `named`
		// argument through the bare LAST-WINS map, so a cycle that exists only once each file's declarations
		// are read as that file's own is invisible here and is found instead by the contest fixpoint failing
		// to converge. Asked FIRST because it is a fact already established, not a walk.
		match self.divergedGenericAliasDecl 'contestDiverged'
			at then return self.divergedGenericAliasDecl
			none then break 'contestDiverged'
		end 'contestDiverged'

		let onCycle = nodesOnACycle(self.buildInstanceArgGraph())
		for i in 0 upto self.genericAliasDecls.count() 'eachDecl'
			let entry = try self.genericAliasDecls.get(i) otherwise panic("firstCyclicAliasDecl: genericAliasDecls.get OOB at {i}")
			// ⚠ **THIS DECLARATION'S OWN INSTANCE, NOT THE BARE MAP'S (N3).** It used to re-probe
			// `genericAliases` by name, which is last-wins across files — so for a name two files declare
			// differently the cycle verdict was read off a stranger's instance, and the E3091 anchor this
			// index feeds pointed at a declaration that may not be the cyclic one.
			let gid = entry.internedInstance()
			if (try onCycle.get(gid) otherwise panic("firstCyclicAliasDecl: alias '{String.from(entry.aliasName)}' names instance {gid}, outside the interner's own instance space")) 'cyclic'
				return AliasCycleResult.at(i)
			end 'cyclic'
		end 'eachDecl'

		return AliasCycleResult.none
	end 'firstCyclicAliasDecl'

	// The "instance ⇒ the instances its type arguments denote" graph, over the dense `GenericInstanceId`
	// space. BOTH edge kinds — a nested `genericInstance` argument, and a `named` leaf that names an alias —
	// are the one question `denotedInstance` answers, so this walk asks it rather than restating the alias
	// lookup: the cycle check and the identity read traverse ONE graph rather than two views of it, and a
	// `named` leaf the alias door starts (or stops) resolving cannot become an edge here and not there.
	//
	// Built through the shared counting sort (`buildCsr`) like every other adjacency in this compiler, and
	// for the reason its header gives: a per-node array would be one refcounted heap object per instance.
	function buildInstanceArgGraph() returns CsrGraph
		var argOf = CsrColumn.create()
		var denotes = CsrColumn.create()

		for gid in 0 upto self.genericInstances.count() 'eachInstance'
			for position in 0 upto self.genericInstanceArgCount(gid) 'eachArg'
				if let denoted = try self.denotedInstance(self.substitutedInstanceArg(gid, position: position)) 'denotesAnInstance'
					argOf.push(gid)
					denotes.push(denoted)
				end 'denotesAnInstance'
			end 'eachArg'
		end 'eachInstance'

		return buildCsr(argOf, to: denotes, nodeCount: self.genericInstances.count())
	end 'buildInstanceArgGraph'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ALIAS NAMES ON THE CYCLE DECLARATION `index` CLOSES, IN WALK ORDER — or EMPTY when the
	// instance graph proves no cycle through it.** `specs/type-checking.md` states the diagnostic as
	// `Circular typealias dependency: A -> B -> A`, so the PATH is part of the answer rather than decoration:
	// it is the only thing that tells the mutual shape from the self one, and the canonical corpus has a case
	// for each. A message naming only the alias it was reported against says identical words for both.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN EMPTY ANSWER IS A REAL ANSWER, AND IT SELECTS THE OTHER DIAGNOSTIC.** `firstCyclicAliasDecl` has
	// a second door — a CROSS-FILE cycle this graph structurally cannot see, because its edges resolve a
	// `named` argument through the last-wins map, caught instead by the contest fixpoint failing to converge
	// (`divergedGenericAliasDecl`). Nothing there proves a cycle or names its members, so there is no path to
	// render and `checkGenericAliasCycles` keeps E3091 and the sentence that says what IS known. The code and
	// the words follow from what the compiler can prove, and this function is what knows which.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT REBUILDS THE GRAPH, AND SCANS THE DECLARATION LIST PER NODE, DELIBERATELY** —
	// `instantiationAnchor`'s discipline and its reason: this is asked only once a cycle report is already
	// being written, never on a program that compiles, so an instance ⇒ alias index would cost every build to
	// serve none.
	export function aliasCyclePath(index FieldIndex) returns ByteArrayArray
		var names = ByteArrayArray.create()
		for gid in shortestCycleThrough(self.buildInstanceArgGraph(), start: self.genericAliasDeclAt(index).internedInstance()) 'eachNodeOnTheCycle'
			let name = self.aliasNamingInstance(gid)
			// A NESTED instance (`Box with (Box with A)`) is interned but no `typealias` spells it, so it has
			// no name to contribute. Skipping it is v1's rendering too (`collectGenericArgNames` collects
			// named arguments only) — the path names the declarations the author can go and edit.
			if not name.isEmpty() 'namedByADeclaration'
				names.push(name)
			end 'namedByADeclaration'
		end 'eachNodeOnTheCycle'

		return names
	end 'aliasCyclePath'

	// The alias that NAMES an interned instance, or EMPTY when none does. Linear over the declaration list
	// for `aliasCyclePath`'s stated reason.
	function aliasNamingInstance(gid GenericInstanceId) returns ByteArray
		for i in 0 upto self.genericAliasDecls.count() 'eachDecl'
			let entry = try self.genericAliasDecls.get(i) otherwise panic("aliasNamingInstance: genericAliasDecls.get OOB at {i}")
			if entry.internedInstance() == gid 'namesIt'
				return entry.aliasName
			end 'namesIt'
		end 'eachDecl'

		return EmptySourceText
	end 'aliasNamingInstance'

	// The declaration record of alias `index`, for the cycle diagnostic's name and anchor.
	export function genericAliasDeclAt(index FieldIndex) returns GenericAliasEntry
		return try self.genericAliasDecls.get(index) otherwise panic("genericAliasDeclAt: genericAliasDecls.get OOB at {index}")
	end 'genericAliasDeclAt'

	// --- Bases without a `StructLayout`: the ONE predicate, and the ONE reason there are two of them ---
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **"IS THIS BASE SYNTHESIZED?" AND "IS THERE A LAYOUT TO WALK?" ARE DIFFERENT QUESTIONS, AND
	// CONFLATING THEM IS WHAT CRASHED THE COMPILER.** A generic instance's base has no `StructLayout` for
	// exactly two reasons, and they are not the same reason:
	//
	//   • **SYNTHESIZED** — `Array` / `Set` / `Map` / `List`. shv2 reads no stdlib and emits their runtime
	//     records itself, so no `type Array uses Element` declaration exists to carry a layout. The type is
	//     LEGAL and the program is fine; it simply has no fields.
	//   • **UNDECLARED** — `Foo with Int`, `Int with Int`. Nothing declares the base at all. The program is
	//     MALFORMED, and `Parser.checkGenericInstance` has already recorded E2055 against the `with` that
	//     wrote it.
	//
	// Every walk over an instance's fields must stop for BOTH, because in both cases there are no fields to
	// read. Four such walks spelled the stop condition as `isArrayInstance(id) or isSetInstance(id)` (the latter
	// deleted at W105) — the
	// SYNTHESIZED half, four times over, each blind to the UNDECLARED half — so an undeclared base fell
	// through into `baseLayoutOf` and the compiler died with a stack trace on a program whose real
	// diagnostic was already written and never printed (measured on `typealias IntMap = Map with (Int, Int)`
	// and on a bare `Foo with Int`: `panic ProgramSignatures.baseLayoutOf`, exit 1, no `error E….`).
	//
	// So the stop condition is `not genericInstanceHasBaseLayout(id)`, written once, and it is exactly
	// `baseLayoutOf`'s precondition. The synthesized half keeps its own name because two callers genuinely
	// need to tell the reasons apart rather than merely stop: `Parser.structLayoutOfType`, whose
	// diagnostic must say "a builtin with no fields YET" for one and "no such type" for the other, and the
	// per-builtin routers below (`genericInstanceBoxSize`, `genericInstanceBoxDropCallee`), which do not stop
	// at all — they answer each of the four builtin containers differently from the others.

	// Is `name` a BUILTIN generic base — one shv2 synthesizes rather than reads a declaration for, so no
	// `StructLayout` exists for it and none is missing. The ONE home of the builtin-container disjunction —
	// every door that means "a container this compiler owns" asks here rather than re-spelling the names. (The
	// count that used to be written into that sentence is gone: it said FOUR against six arms, which is a
	// roster kept in prose with nothing checking it.) It is spelled as the record's arm plus the rest so that
	// the narrower question below is not a second copy of the same list.
	export function isBuiltinGenericBaseName(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.isArrayBaseName(name) or self.isBuiltinGenericBaseNameBesidesTheRecord(name)
	end 'isBuiltinGenericBaseName'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME DISJUNCTION WITHOUT ITS `Array` ARM — the set of container names shv2 SERVES ITSELF AND
	// WHOSE INSTANCES ARE NOT THE MANAGED RECORD (W112).** The split exists for one caller,
	// `noteArrayLiteralConformer`, and it is not a convenience: that function RUNS the discovery
	// `corpusArrayRecordName` is the answer to, so it cannot ask `isArrayBaseName` — the state it would read
	// is the one being built. Every arm here is a bare name comparison against a constant this file exports,
	// so it answers the same at any point in the compile.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **WHAT IT KEEPS OUT, AND IT WAS A MEASURED WRONG ANSWER RATHER THAN A HAZARD.** `isArrayBaseName`'s
	// non-corpus widening admits ANY name a program declares `BuiltinArrayLiteral` on, which is right for a
	// user's own `Bag` and wrong for a name this compiler already serves a container under. **MEASURED on
	// `main` with no compiler edit at all**, from a user file declaring
	// `type Vector uses Element implements BuiltinArrayLiteral` beside an ordinary
	// `typealias Vec3 = Vector with 3 Num`: `Parser.dispatchMethodOnReceiver` asks the ARRAY arm before the
	// vector one, so `Vec3.create()` reported **count 0**, four `push`es took it to **4** and `resize(9)` to
	// **9** — a fixed-size container grown through its own type's name, no diagnostic. The `List` twin is the
	// same program with the name changed: `resize(9)`, a member the list roster does not carry, answered 9.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND THE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME FACT IS A TREE-WIDE REFUSAL, WHICH IS WHY ONE PREDICATE SERVES
	// BOTH.** A SECOND STDLIB conformer takes `corpusArrayRecordName` outright (last-stdlib-wins, see
	// `noteArrayLiteralConformer`), so listing `stdlib/Vector.maxon` made `isArrayBaseName(b"Array")` answer
	// FALSE and refused `stdlib/Array.maxon:114`'s own identity — `function main() returns ExitCode /
	// return 7` failed to compile. Loud where the user route is silent; one cause.
	//
	// ⚠ The `__`-prefixed three cannot be reached from a USER file (`Parser.requireUnreservedName`), and they
	// are here anyway because the set is the CLASS: `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` is the one module allowed those
	// names, so a corpus edit is exactly the route the `Vector` half of the measurement above came in by.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THIS IS NOT A FREE-STANDING ROSTER — `isBuiltinGenericBaseName` DERIVES FROM IT, SO A NAME ADDED
	// HERE JOINS THE SYNTHESIZED SET TOO, AND THAT SET'S CONSUMERS PANIC.** The membership rule for the wide
	// predicate is "shv2 SYNTHESIZES it, so no `StructLayout` exists"; the rule here is "shv2 serves a
	// container under the name and its instances are not the managed record". Today those two coincide on
	// every name but `Array`, which is what makes the derivation legal — they are not the same rule, and the
	// next container to be LISTED is where they come apart. **MEASURED (review, W112)**: one extra arm here
	// for `Set` — a name shv2 still composes `Set.create` / `Set from […]` / `contains` from, but which
	// `stdlib/Set.maxon` has DECLARED since `W105` — turns an ordinary `typealias NumSet = Set with Num` into
	// `panic … builtinGenericTypeParamCount: 'Set' is not a synthesized builtin generic`, out of
	// `Parser.checkGenericInstance`, on the first program compiled. `instanceCreateIsDeclared` and
	// `genericInstanceBoxDropCallee` read the wide predicate the same way.
	//
	// ⇒ **The panic is the reason this is a note and not a defect: the coupling is LOUD and immediate.** A
	// name that belongs in the narrow question but NOT in the synthesized one cannot be added here — it needs
	// the two rosters separated first, with the shared five lifted into a helper both read.
	export function isBuiltinGenericBaseNameBesidesTheRecord(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.isListBaseName(name) or self.isVectorBaseName(name) or self.isManagedListBaseName(name) or self.isManagedListNodeBaseName(name) or self.isManagedMemoryCursorBaseName(name)
	end 'isBuiltinGenericBaseNameBesidesTheRecord'

	// ⭐ **HOW MANY TYPE ARGUMENTS THE SYNTHESIZED GENERIC `name` TAKES** — the arity `Parser.checkGenericInstance`
	// validates a `Base with …` against when there is no declaration to read one off.
	//
	// It sits DIRECTLY BESIDE the predicate above, and takes its precondition from it, because the two are one
	// fact: `checkGenericInstance` used to carry a per-builtin arm that spelled the predicate and the count
	// together, so a third builtin generic meant remembering to widen two lists in two files. Here the miss arm
	// is a `panic` naming the omission, so a name that answers yes above and has no arity here cannot compile a
	// program at all rather than silently skipping its arity check.
	export function builtinGenericTypeParamCount(name ByteArray) returns FieldIndex
		if self.isArrayBaseName(name) 'array'
			return ArrayTypeParamCount
		end 'array'
		if self.isListBaseName(name) 'list'
			return ListTypeParamCount
		end 'list'
		// ⚠ **A `Vector`'s SIZE IS NOT COUNTED HERE, and that is what makes this arm a 1 rather than a 2.**
		// `Vector with 3 Int` supplies one TYPE argument; the 3 is consumed by `readSizedGenericArgs` before
		// the argument list is parsed at all, so the `argCount` this is compared against never saw it.
		if self.isVectorBaseName(name) 'vector'
			return VectorTypeParamCount
		end 'vector'
		if self.isManagedListBaseName(name) 'managedList'
			return ManagedListTypeParamCount
		end 'managedList'
		if self.isManagedListNodeBaseName(name) 'managedListNode'
			return ManagedListNodeTypeParamCount
		end 'managedListNode'
		if self.isManagedMemoryCursorBaseName(name) 'managedMemoryCursor'
			return ManagedMemoryCursorTypeParamCount
		end 'managedMemoryCursor'
		panic("ProgramSignatures.builtinGenericTypeParamCount: `{String.from(name)}` is not a synthesized builtin generic — every caller opens with isBuiltinGenericBaseName, and a name that answers yes there owes an arity here")
	end 'builtinGenericTypeParamCount'

	// Does instance `id`'s base struct have a declared `StructLayout` to read fields out of — the exact
	// precondition of `baseLayoutOf`, and the gate every instance FIELD WALK opens with. False for a
	// synthesized builtin (no declaration to carry one) and for an undeclared base (E2055 already reported).
	export function genericInstanceHasBaseLayout(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		let baseName = self.genericInstanceBaseName(id)
		return not self.isBuiltinGenericBaseName(baseName) and self.containsStruct(baseName)
	end 'genericInstanceHasBaseLayout'

	// Is instance `id`'s base one this compiler KNOWS AT ALL — a builtin it synthesizes, or a `type` some
	// file declares. The UNDECLARED third of the split above, on its own.
	//
	// ⚠ **THIS IS NOT `genericInstanceHasBaseLayout` AND MUST NOT BE COLLAPSED INTO IT: THE TWO DISAGREE ON A
	// BUILTIN, WHICH IS KNOWN AND HAS NO FIELDS.** They are the preconditions of two different queries, and
	// each query needs its own:
	//
	//   • a FIELD WALK needs a layout, so `Array` stops it — `genericInstanceHasBaseLayout`.
	//   • `genericInstanceBoxSize` answers for a builtin (the fixed record) AND for a declared base (its
	//     box), and has nothing to say only for NEITHER — this one.
	//
	// Measured on the door that needed it: `sizeof(IntMap)` for `typealias IntMap = Map with (Int, Int)` died
	// inside `genericInstanceBoxSize` with a stack trace and no `error E….`, because `sizeof` folds at PARSE
	// time and the E2055 `checkGenericInstance` had already recorded is drained whole-program, later.
	export function genericInstanceBaseIsKnown(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		let baseName = self.genericInstanceBaseName(id)
		return self.isBuiltinGenericBaseName(baseName) or self.containsStruct(baseName)
	end 'genericInstanceBaseIsKnown'

	// --- Generic-instance managed drop (P1.6-B2) ---
	//
	// A generic instance is a monomorphized base struct: its box is `Box`'s box, and a managed type
	// argument is MOVED into it (consumed at the concrete call site), so the box OWNS that content and
	// `__destruct_<mangled-instance>` drops it exactly once. These queries substitute the instance's args
	// for the base struct's type parameters and answer the drop cascade's three questions — is a field
	// managed, what drops it, at what offset — over THIS index's own interner (the args and the base
	// layout's field types both live in `self.typeNames`), so a caller never has to choose the interner.

	// The `StructLayout` of instance `id`'s base struct. Precondition: `genericInstanceHasBaseLayout(id)` —
	// an instance is interned for ANY base a `with` names, declared or not, so the caller opens the walk with
	// that gate. See its header for the two ways a base has no layout and why they are one stop condition.
	function baseLayoutOf(id GenericInstanceId) returns StructLayout
		let baseName = self.genericInstanceBaseName(id)
		return try self.structTypes.get(baseName) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.baseLayoutOf: generic instance {id}'s base struct '{String.from(baseName)}' has no declared layout — every field walk opens with genericInstanceHasBaseLayout")
	end 'baseLayoutOf'

	// ⭐ **THE SUBSCRIPT AN OPAQUE `typeParameter` TYPE STANDS FOR — the ONE door from a type-parameter TOKEN to
	// an instance-argument INDEX (W14).** Every instance query below is subscripted by a POSITION in the
	// declaring type's `uses` list, and since W14 a `typeParameter`'s payload is an IDENTITY rather than that
	// position (see `MaxonDialect.TypeParamId` for the collision the position was). This is where the two meet,
	// and it is written once so that no caller re-derives it — `managedOpaqueArrayElementOf` and
	// `SemanticCheck.checkTypeParameterArgsAt` subscript through here.
	//
	// ⚠ **`substituteThroughInstance` DELIBERATELY DOES NOT, AND THAT IS NOT A SECOND DERIVATION.** It reads the
	// whole `typeParamOwner` record because it needs the OWNER as well as the position — a token belonging to
	// another declaration must pass through unchanged rather than be indexed into this instance's argument list.
	// This function cannot answer that, by construction: it projects the record down to the position alone.
	//
	// A miss is an invariant break rather than a case: every token reaching an instance query was minted inside
	// a declaration the sweep folded, and `recordStruct` files a declaration's parameters ahead of every reason
	// it might drop the layout.
	export function opaqueTypeParamPosition(t MaxonType) returns TypeParamPosition
		if maxonTypeTag(t) != ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'notATypeParameter'
			panic("ProgramSignatures.opaqueTypeParamPosition: asked for the `uses`-list position of a `{typeTagName(maxonTypeTag(t))}`, which names no type parameter — every caller tests the tag first")
		end 'notATypeParameter'

		return (try self.typeParamOwnerOf(typeNameIdOf(t)) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.opaqueTypeParamPosition: type-parameter token {typeNameIdOf(t)} has no owner — `recordStruct` and `recordInterfaceDeclaration` file every declared parameter as the sweep folds, so a token with none was minted inside a declaration neither fold saw")).position
	end 'opaqueTypeParamPosition'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE INSTANCE VIEW OF A TYPE THE SHARED GENERIC BODY SPELLS IN ITS DECLARATION VIEW — the ONE
	// substitution, read by every door that meets the two views at once (W25).** shv2 compiles ONE body per
	// generic type (P1.6-A), so a field, a parameter or a return inside `type HoldA uses T` is written in terms
	// of `T`, of `Self`, and of instances OVER those (`Array with T`) — and a concrete `HoldA with Num`
	// receiver fixes every one of them. Three doors need that answer and each used to hold its own, shorter
	// version of it:
	//
	//   • the DROP CASCADE (`substituteInstanceFieldType` below) — what a field holds, so what destroys it;
	//   • the FIELD READ (`Parser.emitFieldLoad`) — the type of the value a `b.items` produces;
	//   • the ARGUMENT CHECK (`SemanticCheck.checkArgTypes`) — the type a parameter accepts at this call.
	//
	// ⛔ **ALL THREE STOPPED AT A BARE `typeParameter`, AND THE PROGRAM THAT FALLS THROUGH THE GAP IS A LEGAL
	// ONE (W25).** A field typed through an inner `typealias Items = Array with T` is a `genericInstance`, not a
	// `typeParameter`, so it was never substituted at all: `b.items` on a `HoldA with Num` kept the shared
	// body's `Array with T`, and handing it to `adopt(other Items)` on another `HoldA with Num` was
	// **`E3005: expected 'HoldANum.Items', got 'HoldA.Items'`** — a program handing a value to a parameter of
	// its own type, refused, while the oracle runs it. The other half of the same gap is worse than a refusal:
	// two DIFFERENT instances of one base (`HoldA with Num` and `HoldA with String`) both read as `HoldA.Items`,
	// so the wrong one was ACCEPTED into a field the box then destroys under the other's element type.
	//
	// ⭐ **v1 RECURSES AND ITS HEADER NAMES THIS EXACT CASE** — `maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/TypeSystem/
	// Substitution.maxon:75-78`, *"Recurses into `genericInstance` argument lists so a
	// `Array with (Pair with Element)` properly becomes `Array with (Pair with Int)` when `Element -> Int`"* —
	// and re-INTERNS the result (`:189`), because the substituted instance may be a type the program never
	// spells. Interning is keyed structurally, so the mint is idempotent and two doors substituting the same
	// pair reach the same `GenericInstanceId`; that identity is what lets the argument check compare them at
	// all.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE `typeParameter` ARM IS GUARDED BY ITS OWNER, WHICH `opaqueTypeParamPosition` ALONE CANNOT BE.**
	// Since W14 a parameter token identifies `(declaring type, parameter name)`, so `HoldA`'s `U` and `HoldB`'s
	// `V` are different tokens — and a token belonging to another declaration must pass through UNCHANGED
	// rather than be indexed into this instance's argument list by position. No caller can reach that today
	// (a body only ever spells its own parameters), which is exactly why the guard belongs to the function and
	// not to its callers.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS INTERNER-FREE, AND THAT IS WHY EVERY CALLER MAY HAND IT ITS OWN FILE'S TYPES.** Both arms key
	// on a WHOLE-PROGRAM payload — a `typeParameter` carries W14's `(declaring type, parameter name)` digest and
	// a `genericInstance` carries a registry id — while every arm that would need a name table (`named`,
	// `structRef`) is one this substitution passes through untouched.
	export function substituteThroughInstance(t MaxonType, id GenericInstanceId) returns MaxonType
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(t)
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'opaqueFormal'
			let owner = try self.typeParamOwnerOf(typeNameIdOf(t)) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.substituteThroughInstance: type-parameter token {typeNameIdOf(t)} has no owner — `recordStruct` and `recordInterfaceDeclaration` file every declared parameter as the sweep folds, so a token with none was minted inside a declaration neither fold saw")
			if not owner.declaringTypeName.equals(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id)) 'anotherDeclarationsParameter'
				return t
			end 'anotherDeclarationsParameter'

			return self.substitutedInstanceArg(id, position: owner.position)
		end 'opaqueFormal'

		if tag != ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'carriesNoDeclarationView'
			return t
		end 'carriesNoDeclarationView'

		return self.substituteInstanceArgsThrough(typeNameIdOf(t), through: id)
	end 'substituteThroughInstance'

	// The `genericInstance` arm of the substitution above: each argument substituted in turn, then the result
	// RE-INTERNED so `Array with T` under `T -> Num` becomes the one `Array with Num` every other door reaches.
	//
	// It returns the ORIGINAL instance when no argument moved, and that early return is what keeps the common
	// case allocation-free in the two senses that matter: a concrete field type (`Array with Item`) neither
	// mints a `MaxonType` box nor builds an intern key, and — because the answer is the same object — the field
	// read's caller can tell "this needed no instance view" from the answer itself rather than from a second
	// predicate over the same shape.
	//
	// ⛔ **AN ARGUMENT THAT IS A TUPLE CROSSES THROUGH THE INTERNED DOOR, NOT THE INTERNER-FREE ONE (W41).** A
	// tuple is the one `structRef` whose identity CITES the declaring type's parameters, and
	// `substituteThroughInstance` passes every `structRef` through untouched because it has no name table. Asked
	// on an ARGUMENT that is a tuple, that silence is a wrong type rather than a missing optimization:
	// `Array with Map.Entry` under `Map -> (String, int)` stayed `Array with Map.Entry`, so the element of the
	// array `Iterable.map` returns never became concrete. MEASURED on `m.map(function(p) gives p)` — the closure
	// was typed with the DECLARATION-view tuple and refused against a correctly substituted parameter,
	// `expected 'fn(__Tuple2.String.int) …', got 'fn(__Tuple2.Tfcd8090be60770aa.T5d84c0fc6312cff6) …'`.
	//
	// The index's OWN interner is the right table here and needs no caller to supply one: `instanceArgs` answers
	// in this index's space, which is the same space `substituteTupleThroughInstance` interns its result into.
	function substituteInstanceArgsThrough(nested GenericInstanceId, through GenericInstanceId) returns MaxonType
		let args = self.genericInstances.instanceArgs(nested)
		var substituted = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		var moved = false
		for arg in args 'eachArg'
			let next = self.substituteThroughInstanceInterned(arg, id: through, interner: self.typeNames)
			if not maxonTypesDenoteSame(next, b: arg) 'argMoved'
				moved = true
			end 'argMoved'
			substituted.push(next)
		end 'eachArg'

		if not moved 'noArgumentMoved'
			return MaxonType.genericInstance(nested)
		end 'noArgumentMoved'

		// The fixed size travels with the re-intern for `GenericInstanceRegistry.intern`'s stated reason: a
		// caller re-interning an instance it already holds must carry every component of its key, and the one
		// silence can lose is this one.
		let substitutedId = self.genericInstances.intern(self.genericInstances.instanceBaseId(nested), args: substituted, fixedSize: self.genericInstances.instanceFixedSize(nested))
		// ⭐ The instance this mints was written by nobody — `through` is the construct that forced it into
		// existence, and the only one an author can be shown. See `SubstitutedInstanceOriginMap`, including
		// why the edge is first-write-wins and why the `moved` early return above is what keeps this off the
		// path a concrete field type takes.
		if not self.substitutedInstanceOrigins.contains(substitutedId) 'firstMintOfThisInstance'
			self.substitutedInstanceOrigins.upsert(substitutedId, value: through)
		end 'firstMintOfThisInstance'

		return MaxonType.genericInstance(substitutedId)
	end 'substituteInstanceArgsThrough'

	// ⭐⭐ **A TUPLE'S INSTANCE VIEW — the third declaration-view shape, and the ONE that cannot ride
	// `substituteThroughInstance`.** `type MyMap uses Key, Value` and `type MyMapIterator uses Key, Value`
	// each write `typealias Entry = (Key, Value)`, and since W14 those are two DIFFERENT tuple types —
	// correctly so, because each cites its own declaring type's parameters (`__Tuple2.T7ec….T240…` against
	// `__Tuple2.Tf5a….T8af…`). They are the SAME type only at an instantiation that fixes both, which is
	// exactly what this reduces: `MyMapIterator.Entry` through a `MyMapIterator with (MyMap.Key, MyMap.Value)`
	// receiver IS `MyMap.Entry`. Measured before it existed, on the renamed `stdlib/Map.maxon`:
	// **`E3005 stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:211:12: argument type mismatch for 'value': expected
	// '__Tuple2.T7ec7c1d76425e7ce.T240e2d961d3637ba', got '__Tuple2.Tf5ab22f57cf9dbd8.T8afb890bfc18c8d0'`** —
	// a sentence naming one type twice, the signature of a missing substitution.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT TAKES A NAME AND ANSWERS A NAME, AND THAT IS WHAT KEEPS `substituteThroughInstance` INTERNER-FREE.**
	// A tuple is a `structRef`, whose payload is an INTERNER-LOCAL id — the one arm that substitution passes
	// through untouched precisely because it would need a name table, and its three callers hold three
	// different ones. A tuple's IDENTITY is its canonical NAME, which is whole-program, so the name is what
	// crosses and the ids stay home. It is `typeArgAggregateName`'s discipline: both steps inside, so the
	// pairing is structural rather than conventional.
	//
	// ⚠ The elements are re-interned through `internTupleType`, which is what makes the answer the SAME name
	// every other door reaches for that element list rather than a second spelling of it. A rejection is an
	// invariant break, not a user error: every element came out of a layout this index already registered and
	// was only substituted, so an element that fails the tuple-element rule now failed it at the declaration.
	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY TUPLE LAYOUT THE WHOLE-PROGRAM INDEX HOLDS — because a SUBSTITUTED one is in no file's
	// artifact and so reaches `project.structTypes` through nothing (W41).**
	//
	// A tuple the source SPELLS is interned while its file is parsed, lands in that file's
	// `artifact.structTypes`, and `ParseStaging.commitStructTypes` copies it into the project registry that
	// `MmRuntime.installStructDestructors` walks. A tuple minted by SUBSTITUTION has neither half: it is
	// created by `substituteTupleThroughInstance` at a CALL SITE, straight into this index, and no artifact
	// ever carries it.
	//
	// ⚠ **MEASURED, and the symptom is a compiler panic rather than a diagnostic**: with the corpus `Map`
	// live, `for (_, value) in m` over a `["a": 10]` walks `MapIterator.current()`, whose declared `Entry =
	// (Key, Value)` substitutes to `__Tuple2.String.int`. The parser emits the drop call for it; nothing
	// synthesizes the body; the backend stops at
	// `resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function '__destruct___Tuple2.String.int'`. A plain
	// `function pair() returns (String, Num)` in the same tree compiles and runs, which is what localizes it
	// to the substituted mint and not to tuples.
	//
	// It answers with the LAYOUTS rather than with the names, because its one caller needs the field list to
	// synthesize a cascade and a name would only send it back here. Walked once per compile, and only by a
	// program that already needs a destructor at all — see the caller's gate.
	export function tupleLayouts() returns StructLayoutArray
		var out = StructLayoutArray.create()
		for (name, layout) in self.structTypes 'eachRegisteredType'
			if isTupleTypeName(name) 'aTuple'
				out.push(layout)
			end 'aTuple'
		end 'eachRegisteredType'
		return out
	end 'tupleLayouts'

	export function substituteTupleThroughInstance(tupleName ByteArray, id GenericInstanceId) returns ByteArray
		let layout = try self.structTypes.get(tupleName) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.substituteTupleThroughInstance: `{String.from(tupleName)}` is not registered — every tuple `structRef` in the compiler was minted by `internTupleType`, which registers the layout before it hands the name back")

		var substituted = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		var moved = false
		for element in layout.fieldTypes 'eachElement'
			let next = self.substituteThroughInstance(element, id: id)
			if not maxonTypesDenoteSame(next, b: element) 'elementMoved'
				moved = true
			end 'elementMoved'
			substituted.push(next)
		end 'eachElement'

		// The ORIGINAL name when nothing moved — `substituteInstanceArgsThrough`'s early return, one shape over,
		// and for its reason: a fully concrete tuple neither re-interns nor builds a name, and the caller can
		// tell "this needed no instance view" from the answer being the same bytes it asked about.
		if not moved 'noElementMoved'
			return tupleName
		end 'noElementMoved'

		return try self.internTupleType(substituted, from: self.typeNames, slotScopeFilePath: layout.declFilePath) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.substituteTupleThroughInstance: the instance view of `{String.from(tupleName)}` was refused as a tuple element list, which its declaration view already passed")
	end 'substituteTupleThroughInstance'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE INSTANCE VIEW OF A DECLARATION-VIEW TYPE, TUPLE INCLUDED — the ONE home of the name/id split
	// the two substitutions above leave open.** `substituteThroughInstance` is deliberately interner-FREE, so it
	// passes a `structRef` through untouched; a TUPLE `structRef` is the one `structRef` whose identity CITES the
	// declaring type's parameters, and it crosses as a NAME rather than as an id. Composing the two therefore
	// needs a name table, and the composition is written HERE — once — rather than at each door that wants it.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT EXISTS BECAUSE THE COMPOSITION HAD ACQUIRED A SECOND SITE, which `Parser.slotTypeThroughReceiver`'s
	// own header had already named as the drift to prevent** (*"a second site holding the name/id split is the
	// drift the function below exists to prevent"*). That door serves the PARSER, in a file's interner; the
	// argument check needs the identical rule in the PROJECT's, and re-spelling it there would have made the
	// warning's own prediction come true.
	//
	// `interner` is read AND written: the declared name is looked up in it and the substituted tuple is interned
	// back into it, so a caller always gets an id its own table resolves. The type is returned UNCHANGED — the
	// same object — when the instance fixes nothing, which is the early-return contract both substitutions above
	// keep and which lets a caller tell "no instance view" from the answer itself.
	export function substituteThroughInstanceInterned(t MaxonType, id GenericInstanceId, interner TypeNameInterner) returns MaxonType
		if not tagIsStructRef(maxonTypeTag(t)) 'notATuple'
			return self.substituteThroughInstance(t, id: id)
		end 'notATuple'

		let declaredName = try interner.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(t)) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.substituteThroughInstanceInterned: structRef id {typeNameIdOf(t)} is not in the interner it was read with — a caller must hand the table its own type was minted from")
		if not isTupleTypeName(declaredName) 'aPlainStruct'
			return t
		end 'aPlainStruct'

		let instanceView = self.substituteTupleThroughInstance(declaredName, id: id)
		return t if instanceView.equals(declaredName) else MaxonType.structRef(interner.intern(instanceView))
	end 'substituteThroughInstanceInterned'

	// The base struct's field `index` type as instance `id` sees it — `substituteThroughInstance` applied to the
	// declared field type. This is what turns `Box`'s opaque `value as T` into `String` for a `Box with String`,
	// and `HoldA`'s `items as Items` into `Array with Num` for a `HoldA with Num`, so the cascade can classify
	// and drop it. Arity was checked at instantiation (E2056), so a type-parameter field's position indexes the
	// args in range.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THE COLUMN IS RESOLVED BEFORE IT IS SUBSTITUTED, AND SKIPPING THAT STEP LEAKED THE `Array` SHAPE
	// ITSELF.** A field declared through an INNER generic alias — `typealias Inner = Cell with T` inside
	// `type Holder uses T`, which is exactly `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s `typealias ElementMemory = __ManagedMemory
	// with Element` — is recorded by the sweep as a bare `named("Holder.Inner")`, because the alias registry is
	// filled after the file is swept. `substituteThroughInstance` passes a `named` through untouched (it has no
	// name table), so the cascade classified the field by its ALIAS NAME: `declaredNameIsManaged` said "a
	// generic alias, so managed" and `managedNameDropCallee` resolved that alias to the UNSUBSTITUTED
	// `Cell with Holder.T`, whose own field is opaque and so owns nothing — the trivial `__mm_decref`.
	// **MEASURED**: a `Holder with String` over a `Cell with String` printed 47 and exited **101**.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS RESOLVED HERE AND THE COLUMN IS LEFT ALONE, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE CHOICE FROM THE BARE
	// GENERIC NAME ONE DOOR OVER** (`normalizeSweptScopedGenericFields`, which rewrites the column). The
	// difference is what the spelling still carries: an alias NAME is what `fieldSurfaceOf` derives a
	// position's buffer-spelling bits from (`derivedAliasSurfaceBits`, and `Character.ByteMemory` is one), and
	// a `genericInstance` cannot hold it — `resolveRecordedGenericAliasTypes`' header states that trap for the
	// registry that already had to respect it. A bare generic NAME carries no such bit, so it may be recorded
	// resolved; an alias may only be resolved at the door that needs the type.
	export function substituteInstanceFieldType(id GenericInstanceId, index FieldIndex) returns MaxonType
		let layout = self.baseLayoutOf(id)
		let fieldType = try layout.fieldTypes.get(index) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.substituteInstanceFieldType: field {index} out of range for instance {id}'s base struct")
		return self.substituteThroughInstance(self.declaredSlotType(fieldType, into: self.typeNames, slotScopeFilePath: layout.declFilePath), id: id)
	end 'substituteInstanceFieldType'

	// Instance `id`'s concrete type argument at type-parameter `position` (P1.6-B2) — what a `T`-typed field
	// read on a concrete receiver retypes to. `adoptType` re-interns it into the caller's interner. Arity was
	// checked at instantiation (E2056), so a type-parameter field's position indexes the args in range.
	// ⭐⭐ **ARE THIS INSTANCE'S TYPE ARGUMENTS A CONTIGUOUS, IN-ORDER RUN OF SOME GENERIC'S OWN PARAMETERS?**
	// `Cell with T` written inside `type Holder uses T` is one — argument 0 is the parameter at `uses`-list
	// position 0 — and so is `Cell with U` inside `Holder uses T, U`, whose run starts at position 1. It is the
	// exact condition under which a descriptor for this instance can be FORWARDED rather than built
	// (`MaxonToStdLowering.instanceLayoutArg`): a descriptor is a run of per-parameter blocks, so one base
	// address serves the callee only while the caller's blocks line up with the callee's from some offset on.
	//
	// A PERMUTATION (`Slot with (V, K)`) and a MIXED list (`Pair with (Integer, T)`) both answer no. Neither
	// has a single base — the first would need the blocks re-ordered and the second has no block at all for
	// its concrete half — and answering yes for either would hand a callee a descriptor whose words describe
	// other types, which is a wrong destructor rather than a diagnostic.
	export function genericInstanceArgsAreACallerParameterRun(gid GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		let count = self.genericInstanceArgCount(gid)
		if count == 0 'noArguments'
			return false
		end 'noArguments'

		let base = self.substitutedInstanceArg(gid, position: 0)
		if maxonTypeTag(base) != ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'firstArgumentIsAType'
			return false
		end 'firstArgumentIsAType'

		let start = self.opaqueTypeParamPosition(base)
		for position in 1 upto count 'eachFurtherArg'
			let arg = self.substitutedInstanceArg(gid, position: position)
			if maxonTypeTag(arg) != ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'concreteArgument'
				return false
			end 'concreteArgument'
			if self.opaqueTypeParamPosition(arg) != start + position 'notTheNextBlock'
				return false
			end 'notTheNextBlock'
		end 'eachFurtherArg'

		return true
	end 'genericInstanceArgsAreACallerParameterRun'

	// ⚠ **IT ANSWERS THE ARGUMENT'S TYPE AND NOT ITS SPELLING, AND THAT IS A LOSS A CALLER MAY HAVE TO REPAIR.**
	// `Array with __ManagedMemory` and `Array with ByteArray` are ONE instance, so the argument comes back
	// identical for both while the member ROSTER a value of it reaches differs. A caller that goes on to ask for
	// a roster wants `surfaceBitsThroughInstanceArg` beside this — see its header for why the fact rides the
	// VALUE and can never ride `id`.
	export function substitutedInstanceArg(id GenericInstanceId, position TypeParamPosition) returns MaxonType
		let args = self.genericInstances.instanceArgs(id)
		return try args.get(position) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.substitutedInstanceArg: type-parameter position {position} out of range for instance {id}'s {args.count()} argument(s) — arity was checked at instantiation (E2056)")
	end 'substitutedInstanceArg'

	// ⭐ **IS INSTANCE `id`'s ARGUMENT AT `position` A MANAGED TYPE?** — asked here rather than by the caller,
	// for the reason T1 exists: `substitutedInstanceArg` returns a type in THIS INDEX's interner, so the only
	// table that can resolve its `named` id is `self.typeNames`, and that table is private. A caller doing the
	// two steps itself must either be handed the interner or adopt the type into its own first — and adopting
	// it is precisely the mistake, because the id then means whatever name happens to sit at that number in
	// the OTHER table. Keeping both steps inside makes the pairing structural instead of conventional, which
	// is the same move `typeArgAggregateName` above already makes.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **ANSWERED OFF `typeOwnershipProtocol`, WHICH IS THE INVARIANT AND NOT A ROUTING CHOICE
	// (W41-trivial).** The layout descriptor's `destroyFunc@40` gate is this, and its `retainFunc@64` is
	// `typeParamRetainCallee` → `typeOwnershipProtocol`; a reference is taken through one word and released
	// through the other, so **the two must be non-zero TOGETHER**, and two predicates cannot promise that.
	// They were two — this said `typeIsManaged` while the drop gate said `typeArgIsOwned` — and a co-owned
	// trivial aggregate fell between them: retained by the rehash, released by nobody. Reading ONE decision is
	// the arrangement the witness pair `destroyFunc@8`/`retainFunc@16` already has off
	// `existentialOwnershipProtocol`, which is why it is spelled the same way here.
	export function instanceArgIsManaged(id GenericInstanceId, position TypeParamPosition) returns bool
		return match self.typeOwnershipProtocol(self.substitutedInstanceArg(id, position: position)) 'protocol'
			none gives false
			byteRecord to aggregate gives true
		end 'protocol'
	end 'instanceArgIsManaged'

	// ⭐ **THE TYPE-CYCLE NODE INSTANCE `id`'s ARGUMENT AT `position` POINTS AT (E4014).** A container OWNS its
	// elements, so each argument is an ownership edge in `TypeCycleCheck`'s graph.
	//
	// ⚠ Here rather than at the caller for `instanceArgIsManaged`'s stated reason: the argument's `named` id is
	// meaningless outside `self.typeNames`, and adopting it into the merged `project.typeNames` is exactly the
	// mistake — the id would then mean whatever name happens to sit at that number in the OTHER table.
	//
	// `denotedInstance` FIRST, because an argument may SPELL a container two ways — a nested
	// `genericInstance`, or a `named` leaf naming a `typealias` — and that door already answers both as one
	// question. It is the same door `buildInstanceArgGraph` asks, so the type-cycle graph and the alias-cycle
	// graph cannot come to disagree about which argument reaches a container.
	export function instanceArgCycleTarget(id GenericInstanceId, position TypeParamPosition) returns TypeCycleTarget
		let arg = self.substitutedInstanceArg(id, position: position)
		if let denoted = try self.denotedInstance(arg) 'spellsAContainer'
			return TypeCycleTarget.container(denoted)
		end 'spellsAContainer'

		return typeCycleTargetOf(arg, typeNames: self.typeNames)
	end 'instanceArgCycleTarget'

	// The number of fields instance `id` carries — its base struct's field count.
	export function genericInstanceFieldCount(id GenericInstanceId) returns FieldIndex
		return self.baseLayoutOf(id).fieldCount()
	end 'genericInstanceFieldCount'

	// The byte offset of instance field `index` in the box — its base struct's own field offset.
	export function genericInstanceFieldOffset(id GenericInstanceId, index FieldIndex) returns ByteOffset
		return self.baseLayoutOf(id).offsetOfField(index)
	end 'genericInstanceFieldOffset'

	// ⭐ **DOES A TYPE ARGUMENT REQUIRE A CALL-SITE CONSUME (a MOVE into the box)?** — the CONSUME boundary,
	// DELIBERATELY NARROWER than both `typeIsManaged` and the box's own field DROP. A TRIVIAL type argument (a
	// scalar, or an all-scalar struct/instance like `Point`/`IntBox`) is NOT consumed: the caller keeps it
	// usable (`PointBox.create(p); … p.getX()`, `generic-trivial-struct-arg`). B2 flips this ONLY for an
	// argument that itself owns managed heap — a `String`, a struct WITH a managed field, a boxed union, or a
	// nested instance that owns managed heap — which the box MOVES in and the caller may not reuse.
	//
	// ⚠ **THIS IS NO LONGER THE DROP BOUNDARY.** Before the retain-escaping rung a trivial `T` argument was a
	// pure BORROW — not consumed AND not dropped — which dangled when the container OUTLIVED the borrowed
	// source (`var b = …; { let pInner = Point.create(…); b = PointBox.create(pInner) }; b.itemDigest()` freed
	// `pInner` at the inner scope while `b` still pointed at it — a use-after-free the witness dispatch first
	// made reachable). The user ruling (2026-07-23) fixes it by RETAIN, generalizing OPEN #40's `__mm_retain`
	// co-ownership: a trivial struct/instance stored into a generic field is `__mm_retain`'d at the FEED
	// (`Parser.coOwnConcreteAggregateFeed`, reached from BOTH of `handleEscapingBorrowFeed`'s sinks — the
	// `Self{item: v}` constructor and the callee-storage `keys.set(…)` alike; the second took no reference at
	// all until W41-trivial, which is what made a trivial-keyed `Map` lose every entry), so the box holds a
	// REAL second reference — CO-OWNED, not
	// borrowed. The DROP boundary therefore moved to `typeIsManaged` (`genericInstanceFieldIsManaged`), which
	// releases that reference at the box's destruction, while THIS consume boundary stays put so the caller's
	// own `p` is still live and dropped once. So the three questions now split three ways on a trivial struct:
	// NOT consumed (borrowed-and-kept), YES dropped (co-owned via retain), and the retain fills the gap between
	// them — freed exactly once on every path. A managed argument is BOTH consumed and dropped (unchanged); a
	// scalar is neither. (The one construct co-ownership cannot yet balance — a SHARED-body reassignment of a
	// co-owned trivial field — is rejected at `emitOpaqueFieldReassign` rather than leaked; the true E3070 NLL
	// borrow-liveness pass, `arr.get(0); arr.push(x)`, is a SEPARATE future rung this did not build.)
	export function typeArgIsOwned(t MaxonType) returns bool
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(t)
		// `tagIsByteRecord`: a Character argument is an owned record exactly as a String one is.
		if tagIsByteRecord(tag) 'byteRecord'
			return true
		end 'byteRecord'
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'instance'
			return self.genericInstanceHasManagedField(typeNameIdOf(t))
		end 'instance'
		if not tagMayNameAggregate(tag) 'notNamedStruct'
			return false
		end 'notNamedStruct'
		// ⭐⭐ **RESOLVED THROUGH `denotedAggregateName`, AND IT MUST BE THE SAME RESOLUTION `typeIsManaged`
		// MAKES (A3e review).** The two are read TOGETHER: `typeArgIsCoOwnedTrivial` is literally
		// `typeIsManaged(t) and not typeArgIsOwned(t)`, so a name the two answer about DIFFERENTLY does not
		// merely give a coarse answer — it manufactures a kind the type does not have. A tuple typealias
		// arrives here as a bare `named` for the reason `denotedAggregateName` states (a RAW instance
		// argument, interned by whichever pass first met `Box with Pair`, and the declaration SWEEP meets it
		// before the alias's own file is walked whenever the filesystem hands that file over second).
		//
		// ⚠ **MEASURED, A3e review** — `typealias TPair = (Integer, String)` (a tuple that OWNS heap) with
		// `typealias PairBox = Box with TPair` beside a `Box with String` whose shared body reassigns its
		// opaque field. Unresolved, `typeIsManaged` said managed (A3e widened it) while this said not-owned,
		// so `genericTypeFieldHasCoOwnedTrivialInstantiation` fired and `emitOpaqueFieldReassign` REFUSED with
		// E2015 — "a trivial-struct instantiation co-owns the field", said of a tuple that owns a String — on
		// the exact program the other walk order compiled and ran to exit 0. Before A3e the same asymmetry ran
		// the other way (both false, so the reassign stored over the old value with no drop: a silent leak).
		// The consume boundary and the drop boundary have to read one name.
		let name = self.denotedSlotAggregateName(t)
		// ⭐⭐ **A GENERIC-INSTANCE ALIAS IS THE SECOND SPELLING THIS DOOR WAS BLIND TO, AND ITS DROP TWIN WAS
		// NOT (A3k).** `typealias N0 = Box with S0` mints no identity of its own, so `structTypes` and
		// `enumTypes` both miss it and the whole function answered `false` — while `typeIsManaged`'s own
		// `named` arm has always ended in `isGenericAlias(name)` and answered `true`. That is the tuple-alias
		// asymmetry A3e's review measured, one alias family over: the pair is read as
		// `typeArgIsCoOwnedTrivial = typeIsManaged and not typeArgIsOwned`, so `Box with N0` classified as a
		// CO-OWNED TRIVIAL box even though `N0` owns a String through `S0`.
		//
		// ⚠ **MEASURED, and order-INDEPENDENTLY** (unlike the tuple half, which needed a walk order): every
		// construction of such a box paid an `__mm_incref` plus a destructor call where a MOVE was owed, and
		// `v1.swap(N0.create(S0.make("y")))` — a shared-body reassign of ANY `Box` field once one such
		// instantiation exists — was refused **E2015** "a trivial-struct instantiation co-owns the field",
		// said of a box that owns a String. The answer is the INSTANCE's, which is exactly what the
		// `genericInstance`-tagged spelling above already returns, so `Box with N0` and
		// `Box with (Box with S0)` now classify alike rather than by which spelling reached the argument.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **THE FORM COMES FROM `declaredFormOf`, THE ONE name→FORM ANSWER — AND THIS IS THE DOOR WITH
		// THE STRONGEST CLAIM ON IT, BECAUSE ITS DIVERGENCE FROM `typeIsManaged` HAS ALREADY COST TWICE
		// (A4n review).** The two paragraphs above are both records of the SAME failure: a declaration form
		// that `typeIsManaged` had an arm for and this cascade did not (a TUPLE alias at A3e, a
		// GENERIC-INSTANCE alias at A3k). That is not a coincidence of two rungs — it is what a second,
		// shorter copy of one classification does, and the pair is read as
		// `typeArgIsCoOwnedTrivial = typeIsManaged(t) and not typeArgIsOwned(t)`, so a form only one of them
		// knows does not merely coarsen an answer, it MANUFACTURES A KIND THE TYPE DOES NOT HAVE — an
		// `__mm_incref` plus a destructor where a MOVE was owed, or that false E2015.
		//
		// A4n gave `typeIsManaged` (through `declaredNameIsManaged`) an exhaustive `match` over
		// `TypeDeclKind`, so a NINTH form is a compile error there. Left as an `if` cascade this door would
		// have gone on answering `false` for it in silence, which is the divergence's third instance waiting
		// to be written. The VERDICTS still differ per form — that is the whole point of this boundary being
		// narrower than the drop one, and every arm below says which way it goes and why; what may not differ
		// is which forms the two doors have HEARD OF.
		//
		// ⚠ The declared TYPES are now asked ahead of the alias registries, where this cascade asked
		// `isGenericAlias` first. That is `declaredFormOf`'s order and it is deliberate: it is the order
		// `typeIsManaged` takes, so an ILL-FORMED program (one name in two registries, E3006/E3061, which are
		// reported by a whole-program check that runs AFTER this classifier has answered) now reaches the two
		// boundaries on ONE path instead of two — which is the agreement this function's header demands.
		let form = try self.declaredFormOf(name) otherwise return false
		match form 'form'
			// NARROWER than the drop boundary on purpose: an all-scalar struct (`Point`) is co-owned by
			// RETAIN and not moved, so only a struct that owns managed heap is consumed.
			structType then return self.structHasManagedField(name)
			// A payload-free enum's value is its i64 tag — nothing to move. `isBoxed` is the same bit the
			// drop side routes on, through the same reader.
			enumType then return self.declaredEnumIsBoxed(name)
			unionType then return self.declaredEnumIsBoxed(name)
			// An interface-typed slot is `E3011 Unknown type` before anything classifies it — existentials
			// are unbuilt. The rung that lands a fat-pointer slot owes this arm its consume answer, which is
			// why it is written out rather than ranged in with a neighbour.
			interfaceType then return false
			// A ranged alias IS its primitive and a function value is a bare code pointer; neither owns a
			// record, so neither is ever moved into a box.
			rangedAlias then return false
			functionAlias then return false
			// A SUPERSEDED spelling consumes nothing — no call site can name it. See
			// `genericAliasSpellingIsSuperseded`, which records the two walks that reach this arm with one.
			genericAlias then return not self.genericAliasSpellingIsSuperseded(name) and self.genericInstanceHasManagedField(self.genericAliasInstance(name))
			// A tuple IS a struct box, and `denotedSlotAggregateName` above has already resolved the alias to
			// the tuple's own canonical name — so this arm is reached only if that resolution is ever
			// narrowed, and it must then answer exactly as the struct arm does. A3e's measured wrong answer
			// is what happens when it does not.
			tupleAlias then return self.structHasManagedField(self.tupleAliasTarget(name))
		end 'form'
	end 'typeArgIsOwned'

	// Does ANY interned instance of the type that DECLARES type parameter `param` classify its argument at
	// that parameter's position as `kind`? The ONE instance-registry read the three
	// type-parameter-instantiation gates (`genericTypeFieldHas{Managed,CoOwnedTrivial,Uncopyable}Instantiation`)
	// delegate to — so the lookup and the `position < args.count()` bound are written once for all three and
	// cannot drift between them. The per-kind predicate is the only thing that varies, in `typeArgHasKind`.
	//
	// ⭐ **THROUGH THE BY-BASE INDEX, NOT A WHOLE-REGISTRY WALK (P1.7 Workstream O).** It used to run
	// `0 upto genericInstances.count()` comparing every instance's base NAME, making the three gates
	// O(call sites × TOTAL instances) — a term that allocates nothing (`nameOf` borrows, `.equals` copies
	// nothing), so no memory instrument could ever show it. `byBase` is filed by the same `intern` that
	// assigns the ids, so this visits only THIS base's instances and the gates become O(instances of the
	// base). A base with no instantiation has no bucket, and that is the answer: nothing instantiates it,
	// so no instantiation classifies anything as `kind`.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT TAKES ONE TOKEN WHERE IT USED TO TAKE A `(baseName, position)` PAIR, AND THAT IS W14's
	// REPRESENTATION ABSORBING A HAND-PAIRING.** Both callers held a bare `typeParameter` field type and had
	// to supply the declaring type ALONGSIDE it from somewhere else — `Parser.requireOpaqueArrayCopyable`
	// from `self.enclosingType`, `Parser.emitOpaqueFieldReassign` from `layout.name` — so "which type's
	// parameter is this?" was answered twice, by two parties, and a caller that paired the wrong two would
	// have scanned another type's instantiations with no complaint from anywhere. The token now carries both
	// halves, so the pairing is read out of ONE fact at ONE door.
	//
	// A token whose owner is not in the ledger, or whose declaring type has no interned instance, answers
	// `false` for the reason a base with no bucket does: nothing instantiates it, so no instantiation
	// classifies anything as `kind`.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **NOT EVERY ROW IN THIS REGISTRY IS A FACT ABOUT THE PROGRAM, AND READING ONE AS EVIDENCE REFUSED
	// VALID PROGRAMS WHOLESALE (A4q).** `internAggregateElementInstances` MINTS a `<base> with T` for every
	// declared struct and union in the program whenever any file holds a `[<identifier>…]` literal — the token
	// sweep cannot name such a literal's element type, so it materializes every candidate. Those rows are a
	// materialization convenience; they are not the source saying that an array of that element exists. That
	// distinction did not matter while the walk's only readers chose an EMISSION (see
	// `internLiteralContainerInstances`, which argues the rows "reach no emitted byte" — true, and beside the
	// point), and it became load-bearing the moment a REFUSAL read the same rows.
	//
	// ⇒ MEASURED once `stdlib/Array.maxon`, `File.maxon` and `Directory.maxon` are listed: `Array.clone`'s own
	// `managed.slice` (`stdlib/Array.maxon:145`) is gated by `genericTypeFieldUncopyableInstantiation`, and
	// the speculative walk minted `Array with FileWriter`, `Array with FileReader`, `Array with Directory`,
	// and `Array with ArrayIterator` — each uncopyable, none named by any program.
	// One of them was enough to refuse **every** `clone`/`slice`/`append` in **every** program holding a
	// `[<identifier>…]`-shaped literal, with a sentence about `Box with String`. A `Map` literal whose keys are
	// a user struct is exactly that shape, which is how `map.md`'s two `literal.user-type-key` cases were
	// rejected on programs mentioning no `Box`, no file and no `CharacterSet`.
	//
	// ⚠ **ONLY THE REFUSAL IGNORES THEM — the two EMISSION gates deliberately still read every row**
	// (`kindIsARefusal`). Over-approximating there costs a descriptor-gated drop on a shape
	// that never occurs, which is a no-op at run time; under-approximating there would drop nothing where a
	// drop is owed. A refusal is the one direction where a phantom's cost is a wrong ANSWER.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND A SECOND CLASS OF ROW IS NOT A FACT EITHER — ONE MINTED BY AN INSTANTIATION E3017 ALREADY OWNS
	// (W90).** A constraint-violating instantiation describes a program that will not exist, so the inner
	// aliases it substitutes are a CONSEQUENCE of the author's mistake rather than the mistake.
	//
	// ⇒ MEASURED, and the cost is not a mis-worded answer but a SILENCED one: `Set with (Array with Opaque)`
	// mints `Array with (Array with Opaque)` for its buckets, `Array.clone`'s `managed.slice` was refused on
	// its behalf, and that refusal is a `ParseError` — so the file stopped, `checkWhereConstraints`
	// short-circuited on `projectHasErrors`, and the two E3017s naming the constraint the author actually
	// broke never spoke at all. The reader was told about `Box with String` and a per-instance cloner, on a
	// program whose fault is that `Opaque` implements neither `Hashable` nor `Equatable`.
	//
	// ⚠ The two filters are applied at DIFFERENT points of the loop below and that is deliberate: the
	// speculative one must run BEFORE the argument is classified (a phantom's argument must not be classified
	// at all), while this one runs AFTER a row has matched, so its chain walk and that walk's allocation stay
	// off every compile that refuses nothing.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT ANSWERS WITH THE INSTANCE, AND THE BOOL IS DERIVED FROM THAT (BLAME).** The walk used to
	// return a bare `true`, which is everything the two EMISSION gates need and one fact short of what a
	// REFUSAL needs: the instance is the thing the USER WROTE, so it is the only handle on a source
	// anchor to blame the refusal at (`Parser.requireOpaqueArrayCopyable` →
	// `ProgramSignatures.instantiationSiteOf`). One walk answers both rather than a second one being
	// written for the identity, which is how the two would come to disagree about which instance matched.
	function instanceWithTypeArgKind(param TypeParamId, kind TypeArgInstantiationKind) returns TypeArgKindMatch
		let owner = try self.typeParamOwnerOf(param) otherwise return TypeArgKindMatch.noInstance
		let position = owner.position
		if let baseId = try self.typeNames.idOf(owner.declaringTypeName) 'namedType'
			if let siblings = try self.genericInstances.instancesOfBase(baseId) 'instantiated'
				for id in siblings 'eachInstance'
					if kindIsARefusal(kind) and self.genericInstances.instanceIsSpeculative(id) 'nothingNamedIt'
						continue
					end 'nothingNamedIt'

					let args = self.genericInstances.instanceArgs(id)
					if position >= args.count() or not self.typeArgHasKind(try args.get(position) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.instanceWithTypeArgKind: instanceArgs.get OOB at {position} despite the range guard"), kind: kind) 'notThisKind'
						continue
					end 'notThisKind'

					if kindIsARefusal(kind) and self.instantiationChainViolatesItsWhereClause(id) 'aRowE3017AlreadyOwns'
						continue
					end 'aRowE3017AlreadyOwns'

					return TypeArgKindMatch.instance(id)
				end 'eachInstance'
			end 'instantiated'
		end 'namedType'

		return TypeArgKindMatch.noInstance
	end 'instanceWithTypeArgKind'

	// The bool the walk above deliberately is not, for the two EMISSION gates that need only membership.
	function anyInstanceTypeArgHasKind(param TypeParamId, kind TypeArgInstantiationKind) returns bool
		return match self.instanceWithTypeArgKind(param, kind: kind) 'match'
			noInstance gives false
			instance gives true
		end 'match'
	end 'anyInstanceTypeArgHasKind'

	// The per-kind argument predicate the instance-scan gates read (see `instanceWithTypeArgKind`). Each arm
	// reads off the SAME `typeArgIsOwned` / `typeArgIsCoOwnedTrivial` / `typeSupportsDeepClone` boundary the
	// corresponding drop, consume or copy site decides on, so a gate and the emission it guards cannot disagree.
	function typeArgHasKind(t MaxonType, kind TypeArgInstantiationKind) returns bool
		match kind 'kind'
			consumeMoved then return self.typeArgIsOwned(t)
			coOwnedTrivial then return self.typeArgIsCoOwnedTrivial(t)
			uncopyableElement then return not self.typeSupportsDeepClone(t, asElement: true, visited: ClonerSet.create())
			managedElement then return self.typeArgIsManagedElement(t)
		end 'kind'
	end 'typeArgHasKind'

	// ⭐ **DOES ANY INTERNED INSTANCE OF THE TYPE THAT DECLARES `param` MAKE THAT PARAMETER MANAGED (P1.7
	// slice 3b-vii)?** A shared generic body reassigns a bare `T` field opaquely; the OLD value's
	// descriptor-gated drop (`__drop_type_param`) is needed ONLY when some instantiation makes that field
	// managed (`Box with String`), and is a sound scalar plain store — no drop — when every instantiation is
	// trivial (`Box with SmallInt`). This is the exact gate that tells a crash-prone managed reassignment (the
	// old value would be leaked and a borrow double-freed by the box's destructor: `0xC0000005` — rejected until
	// the single-value drop runtime is installable off the array floor) from an all-trivial one (left to the
	// unchanged plain store). HOW the instances are found is `instanceWithTypeArgKind`'s business and is
	// described there once; what this gate adds is the KIND — each instance's argument at `position` read
	// through the SAME `typeArgIsOwned` boundary the box's own per-field drop gate
	// (`genericInstanceFieldIsManaged`) uses, so the reject and the destructor agree on what "managed" means.
	export function genericTypeFieldHasManagedInstantiation(param TypeParamId) returns bool
		return self.anyInstanceTypeArgHasKind(param, kind: TypeArgInstantiationKind.consumeMoved)
	end 'genericTypeFieldHasManagedInstantiation'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS GENERIC `typeName` INSTANTIATED ANYWHERE THE DECLARATION SWEEP CAN SEE (W41-opaque)?** The gate
	// on the store-side descriptor-need seed, and it asks the WEAKEST question that separates the two shapes,
	// deliberately:
	//
	// ⛔ **"is some instantiation MANAGED" IS THE QUESTION THIS CANNOT ASK, AND ASKING IT WAS MEASURED AS A
	// REFUSAL ON A LEGAL PROGRAM.** A CONCRETE instantiation is interned by the PARSE that meets it — `for pair
	// in m` is what mints `MapIterator with (String, Integer)` — and this seed is computed before any body is
	// parsed, so at that moment the registry holds only what DECLARATIONS put there. Measured with the managed
	// question in place: `stdlib/Map.maxon`'s `current` reserved nothing and was then refused for want of the
	// descriptor its own store asked for.
	//
	// ⭐ **WHAT A DECLARATION *CAN* SETTLE is whether the type is instantiated at ALL**, because a
	// declaration-view instance (`Map.createIterator() returns MapIterator`, read inside its own type-parameter
	// scope) is interned by the sweep exactly as a `typealias StrBox = Box with String` is. So an all-but-
	// uninstantiated generic — one no `with` in the program ever names — answers `false` and reserves nothing.
	// That matters because such a type's value is held as a bare `structRef(Base)` with no instance behind it
	// (`let b = Box.create(5)`), which no call site can source a descriptor for: reserving one there turns a
	// legal program into `forwardCallerLayout`'s panic, and that panic is reachable today through `sizeof(T)`
	// on the identical shape (it is not this seed's to introduce).
	//
	// ⚠ **AND IT IS SOUND IN THE ONE DIRECTION IT CAN BE WRONG.** A program that instantiates a base ONLY
	// through an expression the sweep cannot see would answer `false` here and then need a reference at the
	// store — where `Parser.requireDescriptorForOpaqueStores` refuses it with a position rather than emitting a
	// raw store. A refusal, never a silent wrong answer.
	export function genericTypeIsInstantiated(typeName ByteArray) returns bool
		let baseId = try self.typeNames.idOf(typeName) otherwise return false
		let siblings = try self.genericInstances.instancesOfBase(baseId) otherwise return false
		return not siblings.isEmpty()
	end 'genericTypeIsInstantiated'

	// ⭐ **IS THE GENERIC THAT DECLARES `param` INSTANTIATED (W41-opaque)?** — the question above, asked of a
	// VALUE's own type-parameter token rather than of a declaration site's name, so the store side and the
	// descriptor-need seed decide off ONE predicate. The token carries both halves of "which type's parameter
	// is this" (W14), which is exactly what lets the store ask it without being handed the enclosing type from
	// somewhere else — the pairing hazard `instanceWithTypeArgKind`'s header records.
	//
	// A token no ledger holds answers `false`, for `anyInstanceTypeArgHasKind`'s reason: nothing declares it,
	// so nothing instantiates it.
	export function typeParamOwnerIsInstantiated(param TypeParamId) returns bool
		let owner = try self.typeParamOwnerOf(param) otherwise return false
		return self.genericTypeIsInstantiated(owner.declaringTypeName)
	end 'typeParamOwnerIsInstantiated'

	// ⭐ **DOES ANY INTERNED INSTANCE OF THE TYPE THAT DECLARES `param` MAKE THAT PARAMETER A CO-OWNED
	// TRIVIAL STRUCT (the retain-escaping rung)?** A trivial struct/instance argument (`Box with
	// Point`) is BORROWED at the call site (`typeArgIsOwned` false — the caller keeps it) yet CO-OWNED by the
	// box (`typeIsManaged` true — `genericInstanceFieldIsManaged` drops it, balanced by the feed's
	// `__mm_retain`). That co-ownership is sound for a field set ONCE at construction, but a SHARED-body
	// REASSIGNMENT of it (`self.saved = next`) has no way to drop the field's OLD co-owned value: the opaque
	// body cannot emit a trivial struct's `__mm_decref` directly, and the two gates the reassign site routes
	// through (`genericTypeFieldHasManagedInstantiation` for the drop, `isManagedOpaqueTypeParamField` for the
	// descriptor it would drop through) both read the CONSUME boundary, so neither fires for this class. So a
	// reassignment would LEAK the old value. This gate lets `emitOpaqueFieldReassign` REJECT that combination up front rather than ship the
	// leak — read off the SAME `typeIsManaged`/`typeArgIsOwned` boundaries the drop and the consume decide on,
	// so the reject fires on exactly the fields the destructor co-owns but the reassign cannot re-balance. A
	// managed instantiation (`Box with String`, both true) reassigns through the descriptor as before; a scalar
	// (both false) is inert; only the borrowed-and-dropped trivial struct — the co-ownership gap — is rejected.
	export function genericTypeFieldHasCoOwnedTrivialInstantiation(param TypeParamId) returns bool
		return self.anyInstanceTypeArgHasKind(param, kind: TypeArgInstantiationKind.coOwnedTrivial)
	end 'genericTypeFieldHasCoOwnedTrivialInstantiation'

	// Is `t` a CO-OWNED TRIVIAL type argument — a heap aggregate the box drops (`typeIsManaged`) but that the
	// call site borrows rather than consumes (`typeArgIsOwned` false)? The one-type predicate the co-ownership
	// gates read: the constructor-feed retain fires for exactly this class of argument (a trivial struct or
	// trivial instance), and the reassign reject refuses it. A String/owning aggregate (`typeArgIsOwned` true)
	// is consumed, not co-owned-by-retain, and a scalar owns no heap — both answer false.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND IT IS THE ONE DOOR WHERE "CLASSIFIES NOTHING" INVERTS, WHICH IS WHY THE SUPERSEDED GUARD HAS TO
	// BE WRITTEN HERE TOO (A3k review).** The other two argument classifiers state their own identity directly
	// — `typeArgIsOwned` answers `false`, `typeSupportsDeepClone` answers `true` so that
	// `uncopyableElement`'s `not` makes it `false` — and every kind is folded by OR in
	// `anyInstanceTypeArgHasKind`, so `false` is what "contributes nothing" means for all three. This one is a
	// COMPOSITION of a GUARDED door and an UNGUARDED one: `typeIsManaged`'s `named` arm ends in
	// `isGenericAlias(name)` and says MANAGED of a superseded spelling, while `typeArgIsOwned` now says NOT
	// OWNED of it — and `managed and not owned` is `true`. A pre-settle orphan would therefore vote CO-OWNED
	// TRIVIAL all by itself, which is precisely the manufactured kind this rung exists to abolish, re-made one
	// spelling over.
	//
	// ⚠ **MEASURED (A3k review), and it is a WRONG ANSWER rather than a coarse one**: two files declaring
	// `typealias N0 = Box with S0` / `= Box with S1` over String-owning structs, plus a
	// `typealias N1 = Box with N0` in each, put the pre-settle `Box with named("N0")` in `Box`'s by-base
	// bucket. Every LIVE instantiation there answers `typeArgIsOwned` TRUE (so co-owned-trivial FALSE), yet
	// `v1.swap(…)` was refused **E2015** "a trivial-struct instantiation co-owns the field" off the orphan's
	// vote alone — while the byte-identical program whose second file spells the same aliases `M0`/`M1`
	// (nothing contested) compiles and runs to exit 0, and so does the INLINE
	// `typealias N1 = Box with (Box with S0)` spelling of the contested one. That is the two spellings
	// disagreeing again, in the direction the rung's own thesis forbids.
	export function typeArgIsCoOwnedTrivial(t MaxonType) returns bool
		if not self.typeIsManaged(t, from: self.typeNames) 'ownsNoDroppableHeap'
			return false
		end 'ownsNoDroppableHeap'

		if self.typeArgSpellingIsSuperseded(t) 'theContestReplacedThisOne'
			return false
		end 'theContestReplacedThisOne'

		return not self.typeArgIsOwned(t)
	end 'typeArgIsCoOwnedTrivial'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS `t` A HEAP AGGREGATE — the boundary a PUBLISHED SLOT is judged by (S2u).** A container that
	// zeroes its slots at birth (`Vector.create()`'s `__managed_resize`) publishes a VALUE for a trivial
	// argument and an ABSENCE for a managed one, so this asks the one question `containerElementIsManaged`
	// asks of a concrete element — `typeIsManaged` — of an instantiation's argument instead. Nothing is
	// composed out of the two neighbouring kinds: `consumeMoved or coOwnedTrivial` would spell the same
	// boundary a second time and be free to drift from `containerElementIsManaged`, which is the door the
	// CONCRETE half of the same refusal reads.
	//
	// ⚠ **A SUPERSEDED SPELLING VOTES NOTHING, exactly as it does for the other REFUSING kind**
	// (`typeSupportsDeepClone`'s `genericAlias` arm, which carries the measurement): these classifiers are
	// driven by a whole-program walk over the registry's PRE-SETTLE memory, and a contested alias name no
	// reader can reach is not an instantiation of anything. Voting off one would refuse a program whose
	// single-file spelling compiles — A3k's measured wrong answer, and the direction a refusal may not err in.
	export function typeArgIsManagedElement(t MaxonType) returns bool
		if self.typeArgSpellingIsSuperseded(t) 'theContestReplacedThisOne'
			return false
		end 'theContestReplacedThisOne'

		return self.typeIsManaged(t, from: self.typeNames)
	end 'typeArgIsManagedElement'

	// ⭐ **DOES ANY INTERNED INSTANCE OF THE TYPE THAT DECLARES `param` MAKE THAT OPAQUE ARRAY ELEMENT
	// UNCOPYABLE (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b-β)?** The FRONT-END gate for an opaque `slice`/`clone`/`append`: the shared
	// body copies each element through the descriptor's single `copyFunc@32`, which can hold a cloner only for a
	// `direct`-clonable element (String / struct / boxed union / trivial-element array — a byte-blit copies a
	// TRIVIAL element correctly with no cloner at all). An instantiation whose argument is a MANAGED type this
	// rung cannot deep-clone as a single-function element — a managed-element array (`Array with (Array with
	// String)`) or a non-Array generic instance (`Box with String`, a later slice) — has no single `copyFunc`,
	// so a copy of it would byte-blit a managed pointer and double-free it; such an instantiation is rejected
	// with a positioned E2015 at the copy site rather than crash at descriptor emit. HOW the instances are found
	// is `instanceWithTypeArgKind`'s business and is described there once; what this gate adds is the KIND —
	// each instance's argument at that parameter's position read through the SAME
	// `typeSupportsDeepClone(asElement: true)` the concrete element-copy gate (`arrayElementSupportsDeepClone`)
	// uses, so the opaque reject and the concrete reject agree on what "deep-cloneable as an element" means. A
	// TRIVIAL argument is copyable (a byte blit), so it never triggers the reject.
	//
	// ⭐ It hands back the INSTANCE and not a bool, alone among the three gates, because it is the only one
	// that REFUSES: the instantiation is what the user wrote, so it is what the refusal is blamed at
	// (`instantiationSiteOf` → `LibraryBlame`). See `instanceWithTypeArgKind`.
	export function genericTypeFieldUncopyableInstantiation(param TypeParamId) returns TypeArgKindMatch
		return self.instanceWithTypeArgKind(param, kind: TypeArgInstantiationKind.uncopyableElement)
	end 'genericTypeFieldUncopyableInstantiation'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES ANY INTERNED INSTANCE OF THE TYPE THAT DECLARES `param` MAKE THAT PUBLISHED SLOT A NULL
	// POINTER (S2u)?** The front-end gate for `Vector with <N> <type parameter>` inside a generic body.
	// A vector's `create()` PUBLISHES all N slots by zeroing them (`Parser.emitVectorCreateOp`'s
	// `__managed_resize`), and every one of the N is then a readable element by the type's own contract —
	// which is true of a trivial argument, whose zero IS a value, and false of a MANAGED one, whose zero is a
	// null pointer. The shared body compiles ONCE and serves every instantiation, so it is admissible only if
	// EVERY instantiation is trivial; one managed instantiation is refused with a positioned E2015 at the
	// `create()`, blamed back at the instantiation the user wrote.
	//
	// ⛔ **AND THE HAZARD IT SHUTS OUT IS MEASURED, ON BOTH ITS HALVES.** With the gate absent,
	// `type Holder uses Element` + `typealias Slot = Vector with 4 Element` at `Holder with String`:
	// `count()` answers **4** while `try slot.get(0)` takes `emitLoadElemAndOkReturn`'s empty-slot branch and
	// THROWS — four elements it does not have, exit 0, no diagnostic — and a `for e in slot` reading `e`
	// SEGFAULTED (139), because `__managed_get_unchecked` dropped that empty-slot arm on the guarantee this
	// refusal is what supplies. It is the CONCRETE managed refusal's exact twin one axis over
	// (`Parser.requireVectorElementType`'s managed arm, whose own header records the same two symptoms for
	// `Vector with 2 IntArray`), and the two must not be relaxed apart.
	//
	// HOW the instances are found is `instanceWithTypeArgKind`'s business and is described there once; what
	// this gate adds is the KIND. It hands back the INSTANCE for `genericTypeFieldUncopyableInstantiation`'s
	// stated reason: the instantiation is what the user WROTE, so it is what the refusal is blamed at.
	export function genericTypeFieldManagedElementInstantiation(param TypeParamId) returns TypeArgKindMatch
		return self.instanceWithTypeArgKind(param, kind: TypeArgInstantiationKind.managedElement)
	end 'genericTypeFieldManagedElementInstantiation'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHERE THE PROGRAM WROTE THIS INSTANTIATION — the source anchor a library-body refusal is blamed
	// at (BLAME).** `genericAliasSites` is the whole-program list of every WRITTEN `Base with Args` carrying a
	// source anchor, built by the token sweep before any file is parsed, so this answer is available to the
	// PURE parser and rides the parse memo's key exactly as every other signature fact does.
	//
	// ⭐ **AN INSTANCE THE PROGRAM DID NOT WRITE IS FOLLOWED BACK TO ONE IT DID.** A generic body's inner
	// `typealias ElementArray = Array with Element` is re-interned per enclosing instance, so
	// `typealias NestedContainer = Container with Bag` mints `Array with Bag` — the instance that gets
	// refused, and one no author ever spelled. `substitutedInstanceOrigins` records the enclosing
	// instantiation each such mint came out of, and the walk chases that chain (an inner alias of an inner
	// alias is two hops) until it reaches something written.
	//
	// ⚠ Some instances still have neither: a compiler-minted alias has no declaring file at all, and an
	// instance whose enclosing instantiation was itself never written bottoms out unwritten. A caller that
	// finds nothing leaves the diagnostic where it was raised — the pre-BLAME behaviour, which loses
	// precision and nothing else.
	//
	// The scan is linear in the written instantiation sites per hop and runs only on the REFUSAL path, so it
	// costs a clean compile nothing; an index would be a map built for every program to serve the failing
	// ones.
	export function instantiationSiteOf(id GenericInstanceId) returns InstantiationSiteLookup
		for gid in self.instantiationOriginChain(id) 'eachHop'
			for site in self.genericAliasSites 'eachSite'
				if site.gid == gid 'written'
					return InstantiationSiteLookup.written(site)
				end 'written'
			end 'eachSite'
		end 'eachHop'

		return InstantiationSiteLookup.unwritten
	end 'instantiationSiteOf'

	// ⭐ **`id`, THEN EVERY ENCLOSING INSTANTIATION THAT MINTED IT** — the one chase up
	// `substitutedInstanceOrigins`, shared by the two questions that ask it: *where did the program WRITE
	// this?* (`instantiationSiteOf`) and *is any hop of it already refused by its own `where` clause?*
	// (`instantiationChainViolatesItsWhereClause`). Written once because the two must agree about which
	// instantiations an unwritten instance stands for, and because a termination argument stated twice is
	// one that comes to be true in only one of the two places.
	//
	// ⚠ The hop count is bounded by the origin map's size, because an origin chain is derived data and a
	// cycle in it must cost a lost answer rather than a hang.
	//
	// It ALLOCATES, so it is reached only from the two REFUSAL paths — one after a diagnostic is already
	// certain, one after a candidate instance has already matched an uncopyable/managed kind.
	export function instantiationOriginChain(id GenericInstanceId) returns GenericInstanceIdArray
		var chain = GenericInstanceIdArray.create()
		var current = id
		var hops = 0 as ElementCount
		while hops <= self.substitutedInstanceOrigins.count() 'followTheChain'
			chain.push(current)
			current = try self.substitutedInstanceOrigins.get(current) otherwise return chain
			hops = hops + 1
		end 'followTheChain'

		return chain
	end 'instantiationOriginChain'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS INSTANCE'S OWN `where` CLAUSE UNMET — i.e. is E3017 already this instantiation's answer
	// (W90)?** The one question a REFUSAL asks before reading a registry row: an instantiation the constraint
	// check owns describes a program that will not exist, so a diagnostic derived from the rows it minted is a
	// CONSEQUENCE reported in place of its CAUSE.
	//
	// The per-constraint verdict is `ConformanceCheck.instantiationMeetsConstraint`, the same one E3017 is
	// reported off, so the suppression and the report cannot come to disagree about which instantiations are
	// unsatisfiable.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT ASKS THAT VERDICT TWICE, AND THE SECOND ASK IS WHAT MAKES THE SUPPRESSION SOUND (review of
	// W90).** A suppression is only allowed to withhold a refusal because E3017 is going to speak instead, so
	// its index must be a SUPERSET of the one E3017 reads — a conformance the report can see and this cannot
	// means the refusal is withheld and nothing replaces it. `sweptConformanceIndex` alone is a strict SUBSET:
	// it is `StructLayout.conformsTo`, which holds the `type` declaration's own clause and never an
	// `extension <T> implements <I>` (`extensionDeclaredConformances` holds those). The header this replaces
	// argued the weaker index was safe because *"a false `unmet` withholds a refusal E3017 raises anyway"* —
	// backwards, because a weaker index yields MORE `unmet` verdicts, not fewer.
	//
	// ⇒ MEASURED at review, on this rung's own tip: `extension Array implements Sizer` for a user `interface
	// Sizer`, a `type Container uses Element where Element is Sizer` holding `Array with Element`, and
	// `typealias NestedContainer = Container with (Array with String)`. The swept index cannot see `Array`'s
	// `Sizer`, so the clause read UNMET and the opaque copy gate was suppressed; the merged index CAN see it,
	// so E3017 read SATISFIED and never spoke. The program compiled with **no diagnostic at all** and
	// **ACCESS-VIOLATED (0xC0000005)** on the byte-blitted managed pointer. With `where Element is Cloneable`
	// — the same shape at an interface `type Array … implements … Cloneable` declares on the TYPE — the same
	// program is refused, which is the control that names the extension clause as the cause.
	//
	// A base with no `where` clause — the whole non-generic-witness corpus — reads the SHARED empty
	// constraint list and iterates zero times.
	function instantiationViolatesItsOwnWhereClause(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		let constraints = self.structWhereConstraints(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id))
		for i in 0 upto constraints.count() 'eachConstraint'
			let constraint = try constraints.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.instantiationViolatesItsOwnWhereClause: whereConstraints.get OOB at {i} below count {constraints.count()}")
			if instantiationMeetsConstraint(self, interfaces: self.interfaces, declared: self.sweptConformanceIndex(), gid: id, constraint: constraint) 'metByADeclarationClause'
				continue
			end 'metByADeclarationClause'

			// The two indexes are asked separately rather than unioned into one map because
			// `typeDeclaresInterface` is a per-declared-name existential (a direct hit, else an `extends`
			// walk), so the OR of the two answers IS the answer over the union — and a union map would be an
			// allocation on a path every clean compile also walks.
			if instantiationMeetsConstraint(self, interfaces: self.interfaces, declared: self.extensionDeclaredConformances, gid: id, constraint: constraint) 'metByAnExtensionClause'
				continue
			end 'metByAnExtensionClause'

			return true
		end 'eachConstraint'

		return false
	end 'instantiationViolatesItsOwnWhereClause'

	// The chain form of the question above: `id` itself, or any instantiation it was substituted out of.
	// A `Set with (Array with Opaque)` mints `Array with (Array with Opaque)` for its buckets, and it is the
	// SET's clause that the program violated — the array the substitution minted violates nothing and has no
	// clause of its own to violate.
	function instantiationChainViolatesItsWhereClause(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		for gid in self.instantiationOriginChain(id) 'eachHop'
			if self.instantiationViolatesItsOwnWhereClause(gid) 'refusedAtThisHop'
				return true
			end 'refusedAtThisHop'
		end 'eachHop'

		return false
	end 'instantiationChainViolatesItsWhereClause'

	// ⭐ **IS `fieldName` OF `structName` A BARE OPAQUE TYPE-PARAMETER field whose element is MANAGED for some
	// instantiation (P1.7 Finding A)** — a `typeParameter`-tagged field (`export var saved as Element` on
	// `type Box uses Element`) that at least one interned instance makes owned (`Box with String`). This is the
	// ONE fact the descriptor-need scan reserves a reassigning method's layout descriptor by: only such a
	// reassignment DROPS the field's old opaque value through the descriptor-gated `__drop_type_param`, so only
	// it needs the descriptor. Read off the SAME raw `fieldTypes` column and the SAME `typeArgIsOwned` managed
	// boundary the emitted drop (`emitOpaqueFieldReassign`) and the box's own destructor branch on, so the
	// reserved slot and the emitted drop decide off ONE fact. False for a non-generic type, an unknown field, a
	// concrete/direct field, or an all-trivial instantiation (`Box with SmallInt` — inert, no drop, no descriptor).
	export function isManagedOpaqueTypeParamField(structName ByteArray, fieldName ByteArray) returns bool
		if not self.structTypes.contains(structName) 'notStruct'
			return false
		end 'notStruct'
		let layout = try self.structTypes.get(structName) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.isManagedOpaqueTypeParamField: structTypes.contains({String.from(structName)}) is true but get failed")
		let index = layout.indexOfField(fieldName)
		if index >= layout.fieldCount() 'noField'
			return false
		end 'noField'
		let fieldType = try layout.fieldTypes.get(index) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.isManagedOpaqueTypeParamField: fieldTypes.get OOB at {index} despite index < fieldCount")
		if maxonTypeTag(fieldType) != ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'notOpaque'
			return false
		end 'notOpaque'
		return self.genericTypeFieldHasManagedInstantiation(typeNameIdOf(fieldType))
	end 'isManagedOpaqueTypeParamField'

	// Is instance field `index` DROPPED by the box once its args are substituted — the per-field drop gate.
	// A type-PARAMETER field is dropped iff its substituted argument is a HEAP AGGREGATE (`typeIsManaged`), the
	// SAME gate a DIRECT field uses. That is DELIBERATELY BROADER than the CONSUME boundary (`typeArgIsOwned`):
	// a trivial `Box with Point` is CO-OWNED, not consumed — its `Point` is `__mm_retain`'d at the FEED
	// (`Parser.coOwnConcreteAggregateFeed`), so the box holds a real second reference the caller keeps
	// usable (`generic-trivial-struct-arg`), and this decref RELEASES that reference at the box's destruction.
	// The retain and this drop are the two ends of one co-ownership (OPEN #40's `__mm_retain` thesis applied to
	// the generic-field escape — the RED `PointBox.create(pInner)` outliving `pInner`'s scope). The two
	// classifiers therefore differ exactly on a trivial struct/instance: consumed = borrowed-and-kept, dropped =
	// co-owned. A managed argument (`String`, an owning struct) is BOTH consumed and dropped, unchanged; a
	// scalar is neither.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE OPAQUE COLUMN'S TWIN OF THIS GATE IS `managedOpaqueArrayElementOf`, and it read the CONSUME
	// boundary until W41-trivial.** A DIRECT field has always been dropped on this boundary; the same field
	// reached through a shared body's opaque `Array with T` was not, so a `Map with (Point, …)` retained keys
	// no column walk released. Both read `typeIsManaged` of the substituted argument now — that header carries
	// the measurement.
	//
	// A shared-body reassignment of a co-owned trivial field would break this balance (the reassign site's own
	// drop gate and descriptor-need seed both read the CONSUME boundary, so neither fires for this class), so
	// it is REJECTED at the reassign site (`emitOpaqueFieldReassign`) — leaving every field this
	// drop fires on either construct-only (co-owned once) or managed/scalar (the pre-existing model).
	//
	// ⭐ **IT ASKS THE INSTANCE VIEW FOR *EVERY* FIELD, NOT ONLY FOR AN OPAQUE ONE.** It used to substitute only
	// a `typeParameter` field and read the RAW declared type otherwise — two derivations that happened to agree,
	// because a `named` struct, a generic alias and a `genericInstance` are all managed in either view. But the
	// drop CALLEE (`genericInstanceFieldDropCallee`) and the drop KIND (`genericInstanceFieldIsExistential`)
	// both read the substituted type, and this rung MEASURED what a third reading of the same column costs: the
	// alias arm resolved `Holder.Inner` to the unsubstituted `Cell with Holder.T` and leaked (see
	// `substituteInstanceFieldType`). One derivation, so the three cannot come to classify three types.
	export function genericInstanceFieldIsManaged(id GenericInstanceId, index FieldIndex) returns bool
		return self.typeIsManaged(self.substituteInstanceFieldType(id, index: index), from: self.typeNames)
	end 'genericInstanceFieldIsManaged'

	// The drop callee for managed instance field `index` — routed through the shared `managedFieldDropCallee`
	// over the SUBSTITUTED concrete type (a String → `__str_decref`, a struct/boxed-union → its
	// `__destruct_<T>`, a nested instance → its instance destructor). Precondition: the field is managed.
	export function genericInstanceFieldDropCallee(id GenericInstanceId, index FieldIndex) returns ByteArray
		return managedFieldDropCallee(self, typeNames: self.typeNames, fieldType: self.substituteInstanceFieldType(id, index: index))
	end 'genericInstanceFieldDropCallee'

	// Is substituted instance field `index` an existential — `structFieldIsExistential`'s instance twin, asked
	// over the SUBSTITUTED type for `genericInstanceFieldDropCallee`'s reason: the drop KIND and the drop
	// CALLEE must classify one type. A type ARGUMENT can never be an existential (`checkGenericArgType`
	// refuses one), so this only ever answers `true` for the base struct's own interface-typed field.
	export function genericInstanceFieldIsExistential(id GenericInstanceId, index FieldIndex) returns bool
		return maxonTypeTag(self.substituteInstanceFieldType(id, index: index)) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef
	end 'genericInstanceFieldIsExistential'

	// Does instance `id` own ANY managed field once substituted — the gate deciding whether it needs a
	// `__destruct_<instance>` cascade at all (else its box drop is the trivial `__mm_decref`).
	export function genericInstanceHasManagedField(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		// A base-layout-less instance owns no field this cascade could drop. An `Array`/`Set` instance is
		// dropped through its own box-drop callee (`__managed_decref` / a `__destruct_<T>` cascade, routed by
		// `genericInstanceBoxDropCallee`), NOT a synthesized per-instance `__destruct_<mangled>`, so `false`
		// is the ANSWER and not a dodge; an UNDECLARED base has no fields at all. Either way the walk below
		// would read a layout that does not exist — see `genericInstanceHasBaseLayout`.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **THE EARLY-OUT IS THIS WALK'S STRUCTURAL PRECONDITION, AND IT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN
		// CAUTIOUS.** Every walk over an instance's substituted fields owes it — the two destructor passes
		// (`MmRuntime.addNestedInstanceDestructors`, `MmRuntime.declareInstanceFieldDropNeeds`) reach one for
		// ANY instance whose `__destruct_<mangled>` is in the needed set, base struct or no base struct.
		// Measured on a compiler that had the guard on only one of a pair of such walks:
		// `panic ProgramSignatures.baseLayoutOf: generic instance 3's base struct 'Array' is not declared` —
		// a stack trace with no diagnostic at all.
		//
		// ⚠ The ROUTE that reached it is closed as of P1.7's reserved-space disjointness, and the guard stays
		// anyway. It used to be a NAME collision: the needed set is keyed by symbol, so a declared
		// `type Array_Foo` owning a String put `__destruct_Array_Foo` there and pulled the base-less
		// `Array with Foo` instance in behind it. That instance is `__Array_Foo` now (`reservedIfDeclared`),
		// so the two names cannot meet — but the guard is the walk's precondition, not a patch on that one
		// route, and `declared-type-owning-a-string-named-like-an-array-instance` is the case that
		// re-measures it.
		if not self.genericInstanceHasBaseLayout(id) 'noFields'
			return false
		end 'noFields'

		for i in 0 upto self.genericInstanceFieldCount(id) 'eachField'
			if self.genericInstanceFieldIsManaged(id, index: i) 'managed'
				return true
			end 'managed'
		end 'eachField'
		return false
	end 'genericInstanceHasManagedField'

	// ⭐ **THE DROP CALLEE FOR AN OWNED INSTANCE BOX** — its own `__destruct_<mangled>` cascade when it owns
	// managed heap, else the trivial `__mm_decref`. The generic-instance twin of `unionBoxDropCallee`, and
	// the door `decrefCalleeFor` (a scope-exit drop) and a nested-instance field drop route through — over
	// this index's own interner, so a caller never misresolves the instance's substituted-field ids.
	export function genericInstanceBoxDropCallee(id GenericInstanceId) returns ByteArray
		// An `Array` drops through `__managed_decref` — which frees its external element buffer (the one thing a
		// generic `__mm_decref` cannot reach) and then the record, mirroring how a String drops through
		// `__str_decref`. Its elements are trivial at this slice, so there is no element walk.
		if self.isArrayInstance(id) 'array'
			return ManagedDecrefName
		end 'array'
		// ⚠ A `Set` arm (`__set_decref`) and a `Map` arm (`__map_decref`) stood here until W105. Both are
		// declared generics now, so each drops through its own `__destruct_<mangled>` cascade — the very
		// A `List` drops through `__list_decref` — the same teardown over a CHAIN rather than a buffer: it walks
		// head→tail destroying each live element through the stamped `element_drop@24`, frees every node, then
		// frees the record. None of those nodes is reachable from a generic `__mm_decref`.
		if self.isListInstance(id) 'list'
			return ListDecrefName
		end 'list'
		// A `Vector` drops through `__managed_decref` — its record IS an `Array`'s (see `genericInstanceBoxSize`'s
		// vector arm), so the buffer it must free and the record it must reclaim are the ones that entry knows.
		if self.isVectorInstance(id) 'vector'
			return ManagedDecrefName
		end 'vector'
		// A `__ManagedList` drops through `__list_decref` — its record IS a `List`'s and the nodes it links
		// are the same nodes, so the teardown that knows them is the one that already exists.
		if self.isManagedListInstance(id) 'managedList'
			return ListDecrefName
		end 'managedList'

		// ⭐ **A CURSOR DROPS THROUGH `__managed_cursor_decref` — the one builtin whose box owns a RECORD rather
		// than a buffer.** It releases the source it retained at creation (through `__managed_decref`, which is
		// what frees that record's own element buffer and walks its managed elements) and then reclaims the
		// 16-byte box. A generic `__mm_decref` here would free the cursor and strand the source's whole
		// allocation — the leak this router's fall-through panic exists to prevent.
		if self.isManagedMemoryCursorInstance(id) 'managedMemoryCursor'
			return ManagedCursorDecrefName
		end 'managedMemoryCursor'

		// ⭐⭐ **A NODE HANDLE DROPS THROUGH `__list_node_decref`, WHICH RELEASES THE NODE ITSELF.** ⚖ USER
		// RULING 2026-08-17: nodes are refcounted and a handle is a second owner (`v1`'s rc=2 invariant,
		// `runtime.std:5330-5337`). At the last owner that entry calls the node's own stamped `element_drop@24`
		// and frees it; every unlink — `remove`, `clear`, the teardown walk — merely drops the CHAIN's reference.
		//
		// ⛔ **THIS ARM WAS A PANIC WHOSE SENTENCE — *"the list owns its nodes and frees them in `__list_clear`,
		// so a node reaching this door was enrolled as an OWNED value and would be freed twice"* — IS NOW FALSE
		// IN BOTH HALVES**, and it is quoted rather than deleted because it was TRUE of the model it guarded and
		// is exactly what the second ruling reversed. `__list_clear` no longer frees a node, and a node reaching
		// this door is enrolled as an owned value on purpose. ⚠ It is NOT `__mm_decref`: that frees the payload
		// and walks nothing, which would leak every managed element a surviving node still holds.
		if self.isManagedListNodeInstance(id) 'managedListNode'
			return ListNodeDecrefName
		end 'managedListNode'

		// ⭐ **THE OMISSION THIS ROUTER USED TO SWALLOW, MADE LOUD** — its two sibling routers
		// (`builtinGenericTypeParamCount`, `genericInstanceBoxSize`) already panic on a builtin they have no arm
		// for, and this one silently did not. A builtin base has NO layout, so `genericInstanceHasManagedField`
		// answers `false` for it by its own early-out and the fallthrough hands back `__mm_decref` — which frees
		// the record and walks NOTHING, leaking every buffer, node and managed element the container links. A
		// leak with no diagnostic, in the one family where a fifth container is the expected next change.
		if self.isBuiltinGenericBaseName(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id)) 'builtinOwesADropCallee'
			panic("ProgramSignatures.genericInstanceBoxDropCallee: `{String.from(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id))}` is a synthesized builtin container with no drop-callee arm — a builtin's record is never reclaimed by __mm_decref, which would free the box and leak everything it links")
		end 'builtinOwesADropCallee'

		if self.genericInstanceHasManagedField(id) 'managed'
			return destructorSymbol(self.mangleGenericInstance(id))
		end 'managed'
		return MmDecrefName
	end 'genericInstanceBoxDropCallee'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES ANY REAL INSTANTIATION OF `declaringTypeName` RELEASE FIELD `index` THROUGH SOMETHING OTHER
	// THAN `sharedCallee`?** — the whole-program half of `Parser.requireSharedBodyReassignHasAStaticDrop`, whose
	// header carries the leak this refuses and why divergence rather than non-concreteness is the test.
	//
	// The shared generic body has exactly ONE callee available for a field write's displaced value, chosen off
	// the DECLARATION view; every instantiation's real drop is chosen off the SUBSTITUTED one. When those agree
	// the write is sound and stays legal — an all-trivial program is the common case and must not be refused.
	//
	// ⚠ **A NOT-MANAGED INSTANTIATION DIVERGES TOO, AND IN THE OTHER DIRECTION**: the body would emit a release
	// for a slot that instantiation owes none, which is an over-release rather than a leak. Both are the same
	// question — "is the one callee right everywhere?" — so both are answered here rather than at the caller.
	//
	// The walk is O(interned instances) and is reached only from a shared body writing to an instance-typed
	// field, i.e. from the one shape that can be wrong; the caller's two tag gates keep every ordinary field
	// write away from it.
	export function instanceFieldDropDivergesAcrossInstantiations(declaringTypeName ByteArray, index FieldIndex, sharedCallee ByteArray) returns bool
		for id in 0 upto self.genericInstanceCount() 'eachInstance'
			if not self.genericInstanceBaseName(id).equals(declaringTypeName) 'anotherBase'
				continue
			end 'anotherBase'
			// A layout-less instance of this base cannot exist (the name is a declared `type`), so the guard is
			// the walk's stated precondition rather than a case — `genericInstanceHasBaseLayout`'s rule, asked
			// where every other instance-field walk asks it.
			if not self.genericInstanceHasBaseLayout(id) 'noFields'
				continue
			end 'noFields'

			if not self.genericInstanceFieldIsManaged(id, index: index) 'thisInstantiationOwesNoDrop'
				return true
			end 'thisInstantiationOwesNoDrop'

			if not self.genericInstanceFieldDropCallee(id, index: index).equals(sharedCallee) 'aDifferentCallee'
				return true
			end 'aDifferentCallee'
		end 'eachInstance'

		return false
	end 'instanceFieldDropDivergesAcrossInstantiations'

	// Does instance `id`'s type argument at `position` OWN managed heap — the CALL-SITE CONSUME gate (P1.6-B2).
	// An owning argument is MOVED into the box (the box drops it, so the caller must not); a trivial argument
	// is BORROWED (the P1.6-A behaviour, unchanged — `PointBox.create(p)` keeps `p` usable). It reads exactly
	// the `typeArgIsOwned` boundary the destructor drops on, so the consumed set equals the dropped set by
	// construction — no double-free, no leak.
	export function genericInstanceArgIsOwned(id GenericInstanceId, position TypeParamPosition) returns bool
		let args = self.genericInstances.instanceArgs(id)
		if position >= args.count() 'oob'
			return false
		end 'oob'
		return self.typeArgIsOwned(try args.get(position) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.genericInstanceArgIsOwned: instanceArgs.get out of range at {position} despite position < count"))
	end 'genericInstanceArgIsOwned'

	// The number of interned generic instances — the iteration bound the B2 destructor-synthesis and
	// needs-closure passes walk (`0 upto genericInstanceCount()` enumerates every gid).
	export function genericInstanceCount() returns GenericInstanceId
		return self.genericInstances.count()
	end 'genericInstanceCount'

	// The enum/union `name` denotes, declared in ANY file — `structOf`'s twin, and the door the
	// PARSER asks every enum question through: is this scrutinee an enum? what tag is a case? which
	// cases does a range arm cover? The layout it hands back carries plain NAMES and INTS (no ids),
	// so every one of those questions is answered with no interner at all.
	export function enumOf(name ByteArray) returns DeclaredEnum
		let layout = try self.enumTypes.get(name) otherwise return DeclaredEnum.undeclared
		return DeclaredEnum.declared(layout)
	end 'enumOf'

	// ⭐ **IS `name` A FLOAT-BACKED ENUM — one whose i64 tag is an f64's IEEE-754 BIT PATTERN (A4o)?** THE
	// question every site that consumes an enum value as a NUMBER asks, so it is stated here once rather
	// than at each of them. A name nothing declares as an enum answers `false`: this is asked of every
	// `named`-tagged value in the program, and "there is no such enum" is not this predicate's to report.
	//
	// ⚠ **ANSWERED OVER `enumTypes` DIRECTLY, NOT THROUGH `enumOf` ABOVE, AND THAT IS `valueIsUnion`'s
	// MEASURED REASON** — `enumOf` boxes a `DeclaredEnum`, a heap object even for `undeclared`, and this
	// runs for BOTH operands of every binary operator and every comparison and for every interpolation hole.
	export function enumIsFloatBacked(name ByteArray) returns bool
		let layout = try self.enumTypes.get(name) otherwise return false
		return layout.backing == EnumBacking.float
	end 'enumIsFloatBacked'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH DECLARATION FORM MINTED `name` — the ONE name→FORM answer, so a new form is a COMPILE ERROR
	// at every door that classifies a name instead of a coverage gap nothing reports (A4n).**
	//
	// Four doors used to carry their own cascade over these registries — `denotedSlotType`, `typeIsManaged`,
	// `managedNameDropCallee` and `managedNameCloneStrategy`. They asked their registries in DIFFERENT ORDERS,
	// and half of that residue was safe by construction while half was not, which is the whole reason this
	// exists:
	//
	//   • **The COVERAGE could disagree, and the failure had a definite shape.** A form taught to one door and
	//     not another reads MANAGED there and `false` here, so the field is moved in and **dropped by
	//     NOBODY** — a leak, exit 101 — while the converse double-frees. That is what this function closes.
	//   • **The ORDERS were held to be safe by construction, and MEASURING IT SHOWED THEY ARE NOT.** The
	//     argument was that an order decides something only for a name held by TWO registries at once, and no
	//     legal program can spell one: a second declaration of a type name is **E3006** and two `typealias`
	//     declarations of one name are **E3061**. Both codes exist and both fire — but they are reported by a
	//     whole-program CHECK that runs *after* these classifiers have already answered during the parse. So
	//     an ILL-FORMED program does put one name in two registries here, and the order picks between them.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THAT IS WHY DECLARED TYPES ARE ASKED BEFORE ALIASES, AND THE ORDER IS NOT A COST CHOICE.**
	// MEASURED on this branch, with the aliases asked first: `type Box … end` + `typealias Box = int(0 to 10)`
	// — `specs-shv2/type-name-collision.md`'s `error.type-then-typealias`, whose whole subject is that E3006
	// catches it — reached `Parser.decrefCalleeFor` for the STRUCT box it had just built, was told `Box` is a
	// ranged typealias, and **PANICKED inside `managedNameDropCallee` before E3006 could be printed**. Every
	// door's previous cascade asked `structTypes` ahead of every alias registry, so type-first is also the
	// order that leaves an ill-formed program on exactly the path it was already on until its diagnostic
	// lands. Within each group the order IS free, and is by expected frequency.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THIS IS THE ONE PLACE A NEW REGISTRY MUST BE TAUGHT, AND `TypeDeclKind` IS THE ONE PLACE A NEW
	// FORM MUST BE.** The guarantee the doors get is over `TypeDeclKind`'s CASES: add a case and every `match`
	// below fails to compile until it is handled. It is NOT over registries — a form recorded in a registry
	// this function never asks would answer `undeclared` at every door, silently. So a rung that adds a
	// registry adds its arm HERE, in the same edit.
	//
	// ⚠ **NO RANGE ARMS OVER `TypeDeclKind` AT ANY DOOR, and that is a rule with teeth rather than taste.**
	// `typeDeclKeyword` and `isTypeAliasDecl` (`Project.maxon`) both use `rangedAlias to tupleAlias`, which
	// silently swallows a case inserted between the endpoints — the invariant `TypeDeclKind`'s own comment
	// records. A door that spelled a range would give up exactly the guarantee this function exists to
	// provide, so every arm below and at every reader is written out, including the ones that agree.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS ANSWERED ALLOCATION-FREE** — a payload-free enum is a scalar and every probe is a
	// `Map.contains` — because `typeIsManaged` reads it and must reach its `false` for a scalar field without
	// boxing (see that function's header, which prices the alternative).
	//
	// It THROWS for a name no registry holds rather than carrying an `undeclared` case, because that is not a
	// declaration FORM and `TypeDeclKind` may not gain one: `denotedSlotType` already answers exactly this way
	// (`MapError.keyNotFound`), and each door's fallthrough — an `adoptType` there, an enum lookup at the
	// payload classifier, `false` here — is the rest of the answer rather than a recovery path.
	export function declaredFormOf(name ByteArray) returns TypeDeclKind throws MapError
		if self.structTypes.contains(name) 'structType'
			return TypeDeclKind.structType
		end 'structType'
		if let enumLayout = try self.enumTypes.get(name) 'enumOrUnion'
			return TypeDeclKind.unionType if enumLayout.isUnion else TypeDeclKind.enumType
		end 'enumOrUnion'
		if self.interfaces.contains(name) 'interfaceType'
			return TypeDeclKind.interfaceType
		end 'interfaceType'
		if self.typeAliases.containsInAnyFile(name) 'rangedAlias'
			return TypeDeclKind.rangedAlias
		end 'rangedAlias'
		if self.genericAliases.contains(name) 'genericAlias'
			return TypeDeclKind.genericAlias
		end 'genericAlias'
		if self.tupleAliases.contains(name) 'tupleAlias'
			return TypeDeclKind.tupleAlias
		end 'tupleAlias'
		if self.functionTypeAliases.contains(name) 'functionAlias'
			return TypeDeclKind.functionAlias
		end 'functionAlias'

		throw MapError.keyNotFound
	end 'declaredFormOf'

	// Is `name` a declared enum/union — the `bool` half of `enumOf`, and a HOT-PATH allocation guard.
	// `enumOf` returns a `DeclaredEnum`, a Maxon union VALUE that allocates a heap box on its
	// `undeclared` case as much as any other, so a caller that only needs "is it an enum?" for the
	// COMMON no answer (`parseDottedPrimary` asks it of every `Point.create(…)` static call) must not
	// pay `enumOf`'s box just to throw it away. A `Map.contains` allocates nothing.
	export function containsEnum(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.enumTypes.contains(name)
	end 'containsEnum'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE RUNTIME ENTRY THAT DROPS AN OWNED BOX OF UNION `unionName`** — `__destruct_<U>` for a
	// managed-payload union (its own tag-conditional cascade), the trivial `__mm_decref` for a scalar-only
	// one. BOTH doors that ask it come here: `decrefCalleeFor`'s scope-exit drop of a boxed union, and
	// `managedNameDropCallee`'s union arm for a boxed-union FIELD.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT TAKES A NAME, NOT A LAYOUT AND AN INTERNER, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.** The payload types
	// are resolved against `self.typeNames` — the interner they were minted against (a signatures layout's
	// payload ids live here, remapped in by `remapEnumLayoutPayloadTypes`) — and the interner is NOT the
	// caller's to choose. It went wrong TWICE, in the two doors, and each was fixed alone:
	//   • `decrefCalleeFor` passed its own ARTIFACT interner, which misread a managed payload whenever a
	//     ranged alias perturbed the interning order — `__mm_decref` for a managed-payload union (a LEAK) or
	//     a wrong `__destruct_<T>` (a wild-free). OPEN #52.
	//   • `managedNameDropCallee`'s union arm passed the caller's `project.typeNames` against a SIGNATURES
	//     layout, which cross-file read a ranged-alias payload as MANAGED and demanded a `__destruct_<U>`
	//     that `installUnionDestructors` — asking over the correctly-paired PROJECT layout — never
	//     synthesized: `bl to unknown function '__destruct_Payloaded'`, a compiler panic on a valid
	//     two-file program. Found in T1's review; see `unionHasManagedPayload`.
	// One fact was spelled twice and only one copy was ever repaired. Taking the NAME leaves nothing for a
	// caller to get wrong, so the second copy cannot come back.
	//
	// The union is a precondition, not a case: both callers reached here through an `enumOf` that answered
	// `declared`.
	export function unionBoxDropCallee(unionName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		return destructorSymbol(unionName) if self.unionHasManagedPayload(unionName) else MmDecrefName
	end 'unionBoxDropCallee'

	// The DECLARED type of field `index` of struct `structName`, ADOPTED into `into` — the calling
	// file's own artifact interner. `returnTypeOf`'s exact shape and exact reason: a `named` type
	// crosses by NAME and is re-interned on arrival, so the id the parser stores is one its own
	// file's merge will resolve.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT TAKES THE LAYOUT AND NOT THE STRUCT NAME — see `fieldSurfaceOf` below for the measurement.**
	// EVERY caller reached this door holding the layout already (it is `emitFieldLoad`'s own parameter, and a
	// `FieldChainStep` carries one), so passing the NAME asked `structTypes` — a NAME-KEYED map — to hash a
	// string per field access to find a record the caller had in hand. For a TUPLE, whose canonical name grows
	// with its arity, that made a W-slot tuple read W times cost O(W²) bytes hashed.
	//
	// The index is a precondition, not a case: `index` is one `indexOfField` handed out.
	//
	// ⭐ **A FIELD TYPED BY A BARE GENERIC NAME IS ALREADY AN INSTANCE BY THE TIME THIS IS ASKED** — `var cell
	// as Cell` inside `type Holder uses T` is a `Cell with Holder.T`, and the column carries exactly that,
	// because `normalizeSweptScopedGenericFields` recorded it once with every file folded. This function used
	// to hold that rule INLINE, which made it the field READ's private answer to a question the drop cascade
	// and the clone gate ask of the same column — and the cascade, reading the raw swept `named`, dropped a
	// `Holder with String` through the base `Cell`'s classification and stranded the string (exit 101). One
	// recorded answer replaced three derivations; see that normalization for the measurement.
	export function fieldTypeOf(layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex, into TypeNameInterner) returns MaxonType
		let declared = try layout.fieldTypes.get(index) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.fieldTypeOf: field {index} is out of range for `type {String.from(layout.name)}`, which declares {layout.fieldCount()} — every index handed out came from `indexOfField`")

		return self.declaredSlotType(declared, into: into, slotScopeFilePath: layout.declFilePath)
	end 'fieldTypeOf'

	// ⭐ **THE UNION-PAYLOAD TWIN OF `fieldTypeOf` (A4k)** — a payload slot's DECLARED type, resolved and
	// ADOPTED into `into`, scoped to the union's own declaring file for the reason `declaredSlotType`'s
	// header gives a struct field.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE THREE PARSER SITES THAT NEEDED IT SPELLED A BARE `adoptType` INSTEAD** — the construct's
	// lowerability check, the match-bind classification, and the payload LOAD's value type — so a payload
	// declared with a generic alias reached each of them still `named`, i.e. an INT. That is A4i's defect
	// one slot over, and it is here rather than in the parser for A4i's cure: the door a payload's type
	// comes out of is a fact about the LAYOUT, so it belongs beside the layout's other one.
	//
	// It takes the LAYOUT and not the union NAME for `fieldTypeOf`'s measured reason: every caller already
	// holds it, and a name-keyed fetch would hash a string per payload to find a record in hand.
	export function payloadTypeOf(field PayloadField, layout EnumLayout, into TypeNameInterner) returns MaxonType
		return self.declaredSlotType(field.declaredType, into: into, slotScopeFilePath: layout.declFilePath)
	end 'payloadTypeOf'

	// ⭐ **THE PRE-ERASURE `__ManagedMemory` SPELLING OF FIELD `index` (A2j / A2m)** — `fieldTypeOf`'s twin
	// for the half a `MaxonType` cannot hold, and the door `Parser.emitFieldLoad` asks at the one site a
	// field read mints its value.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SPLIT IS BY POSITION, NOT BY BIT, AND IT IS FORCED.** Only the TUPLE-SLOT mask is purely stored:
	// a slot must not consult an alias registry at a sweep-fed door at all, because a tuple's element types
	// are canonical by the time anything could repair one, so its answer would be *how far the sweep had got*
	// (`Parser.declaredSurfaceAt`'s `slotElementsFromAliases`). Both ROOT bits are stored AND derived, and
	// their union is the answer: the sweep can settle a root written with the LITERAL spelling
	// (`var buf as __ManagedMemory` is a token fact), and only the read door can settle one written with an
	// ALIAS — `var bufs as BufArray`, `var buf as ByteBuffer` — since at sweep time that alias is not yet
	// registered. So the derived half runs HERE, where every file is folded, off the alias NAME the swept
	// `named` type still carries.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT WAS THE ELEMENT BIT ALONE UNTIL W49, AND THAT WAS A MEASURED DEFECT AND NOT A DESIGN.** This
	// block used to read *"the outright spelling … the SWEEP could answer"*, which is true of the literal
	// spelling and FALSE of an alias for it — the case W43 had just made legal. See
	// `derivedAliasSurfaceBits`.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT TAKES THE LAYOUT AND NOT THE STRUCT NAME, WHICH IS A COST AND NOT A STYLE.** `structTypes` is
	// keyed by NAME, so re-resolving the layout here HASHES that name — and a TUPLE's canonical name grows with
	// its arity, so a W-slot tuple read W times costs O(W²) bytes hashed for a layout `emitFieldLoad` already
	// holds in hand. It was written the name-taking way first and MEASURED: a wide-tuple probe (a W-slot tuple
	// parameter naming no buffer at all, every slot read once), parse CPU ticks minus this compile's ~6.0M
	// floor, interleaved min-of-3 against the merge-base binary —
	//
	//   W        100      200      400      800
	//   base    1.13M    3.25M   10.55M   38.24M     (`fieldTypeOf` alone, hashing the same name)
	//   +this   1.43M    4.28M   15.15M   52.29M     (+0.35M / +1.08M / +4.65M / +14.11M, x3.0 per DOUBLING)
	//   layout  0.89M    2.40M    7.32M   25.01M     (both doors handed the layout: -35% BELOW base)
	//
	// The name was never the caller's fact to give, and `fieldTypeOf`'s identical preamble went with it.
	//
	// The index is a precondition, not a case, exactly as it is for `fieldTypeOf`.
	export function fieldSurfaceOf(layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex) returns DeclaredSurface
		let declared = try layout.fieldTypes.get(index) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.fieldSurfaceOf: field {index} is out of range for `type {String.from(layout.name)}`, which declares {layout.fieldCount()} — every index handed out came from `indexOfField`")

		return self.surfaceWithDerivedAliasBits(layout.fieldDeclaredSurface(index), declared: declared)
	end 'fieldSurfaceOf'

	// ⭐ **THE PRE-ERASURE `__ManagedMemory` SPELLING OF A UNION PAYLOAD (BATCH22)** — `payloadTypeOf`'s twin
	// for the half a `MaxonType` cannot hold, and `fieldSurfaceOf`'s twin at the FIFTH declared position. The
	// door `Parser.emitUnionPayloadLoad` asks at the one site a `match` binding mints its value.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT TAKES THE FIELD ALONE where `fieldSurfaceOf` takes a layout and an index**, because a
	// `PayloadField` is a RECORD and not a set of parallel columns: both halves the answer needs — the stored
	// token facts, and the swept `named` the element bit is derived from — arrive in one value, so there is
	// nothing to look up and no pair that can fall out of lockstep.
	export function payloadSurfaceOf(field PayloadField) returns DeclaredSurface
		return self.surfaceWithDerivedAliasBits(field.declaredSurface, declared: field.declaredType)
	end 'payloadSurfaceOf'

	// ⭐ **WHAT A SLOT'S DECLARED TYPE MEANS ONCE THE ALIAS REGISTRIES ARE KNOWN** — the door a struct
	// FIELD and an `Array` ELEMENT both come out of, ADOPTED into `into` (the calling file's own artifact
	// interner) exactly as `returnTypeOf` does.
	//
	// Every re-tag in `denotedSlotType` — the cascade this reads the name for — exists for the same reason:
	// the DECLARATION SWEEP that recorded
	// the slot ran before any alias was interned, so an alias-typed slot arrives as a bare `named` and the
	// registries are the only thing that can say what it is. Leaving one unresolved is not a cosmetic loss
	// — `named` reads as an INTEGER (`tagIsIntegral`), so a function slot admits an int, a generic-instance
	// slot loses its `count()`, and a float slot is stored as raw i64 bits.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS ONE FUNCTION BECAUSE THE ELEMENT DOOR HAD A PARTIAL COPY OF IT, AND THE PART THE COPY
	// DROPPED WAS REACHABLE.** `Parser.containerSlotValueType` spelled out the generic-alias re-tag and
	// neither of the other two, so an `Array with <function alias>` element stayed `named` — an INT. It
	// went unnoticed while nothing compared an element against its type; the moment the element-argument
	// check (`Parser.requireContainerElementType`) did, that `named` would have read a pushed function VALUE as
	// a function meeting an int slot and refused a program both compilers accept. A rule with two homes
	// drifts, and only the newer home's obligations reveal which half the older one was missing.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **`slotScopeFilePath` IS WHICHEVER FILE *FIXES* THIS SLOT'S TYPE, AND FOR A FIELD THAT IS ITS
	// DECLARING FILE AND NEVER A READER'S (N2) — the difference there is a SILENT WRONG ANSWER.** The
	// generic-alias arm resolves a compiler-minted alias — `__ManagedMemory` — whose element is the
	// reader's `Byte` by design, so a range-contested program has two answers to choose from.
	//
	// **THE KINDS OF ASKER, AND WHY ONE PARAMETER SERVES THEM ALL.** They are not separate policies; they are
	// answers to the one question *"whose reading of a contested alias is this slot's identity?"*:
	//
	//   • **A struct FIELD** (`fieldTypeOf`) passes `StructLayout.declFilePath`. A field's type must be ONE
	//     type program-wide: scoped to the reader, file X could build `Holder{buf: <a stride-1 buffer>}` and
	//     file Y read `h.buf.get(0)` at stride 2 over that very record, with no diagnostic anywhere. Scoped
	//     to the declaration, the two files disagree at the CONSTRUCTION — nominally, loudly (MEASURED, N2
	//     review, both directions: `E3005 … expected 'Array_Byte$0_1000', got 'Array_Byte$0_255'` at the
	//     construction line, and the narrow declarer's own read striding 1 correctly) — and every read of
	//     the slot strides the way the record was stamped.
	//   • **An `Array` ELEMENT** (`Parser.containerSlotValueType`, `Parser.requireContainerElementType`) passes
	//     the PARSING FILE — which reads as "the reader's" and is not that at all. An array INSTANCE is
	//     declared in no file, so it has no declaring one to ask; what it has is the file its `giid` was
	//     already range-scoped FOR, one line earlier in the same parser, and that file is `self.filePath`.
	//     Passing anything else would let an element disagree with the very instance it was read out of.
	//   • **A GENERIC INSTANCE's TYPE ARGUMENT** (`Parser.slotTypeThroughReceiver`, A4i) passes the PARSING
	//     FILE for the `Array` ELEMENT's reason and not the struct FIELD's, and it is the same reason twice
	//     over: an instance is declared in no file, and its `giid` was already range-scoped FOR the reading
	//     file at the receiver's own mint. It is the door a `T`-typed field READ, a `T`-returning METHOD CALL
	//     and a `T`-typed field WRITE all learn what `T` is through, and it reached here as a bare `adoptType`
	//     — see that function for the three faces that cost. ⚠ The WRITE arrived one commit later and by the
	//     SAME lesson: it kept its own copy of the pre-A4i `adoptType(substitutedInstanceArg(…))`, so the fix
	//     reached the read and not its twin, and the twin's consumer (`classifyUnionPayload`) then resolved
	//     that bare `named` through a SECOND cascade with a struct arm and NO generic-alias arm — which
	//     turned a `Holder with IntBox` field write into a SCALAR store that dropped nothing and moved
	//     nothing in. There is only one cascade now (`denotedSlotType`); A4k is what made that true.
	//   • **A UNION PAYLOAD** (`payloadTypeOf`, and the classifier itself) passes `EnumLayout.declFilePath`,
	//     the struct FIELD's answer for the struct FIELD's reason — a payload's type must be ONE type
	//     program-wide, and the alias that governs it is the one visible where the UNION was declared, never
	//     where a `match` reads it. It is the SAME file `resolveRecordedGenericAliasTypes` already settled
	//     that payload's recorded column against (N3), so the column and the classifier cannot come to mean
	//     two different declarations of one contested name.
	//   • **A TUPLE element** (`canonicalTupleElement`, `fieldTypeOf` over a tuple layout) passes the file that
	//     WROTE the element list — the sixth kind of asker, and the one this list did not have (A3v). A tuple
	//     is declared in no file, so it long carried `CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath`; but a SPELLING has a file
	//     even where a declaration does not, and the stranger's reading of `(Bag, Num)` is a third answer
	//     agreeing with neither declaration of a contested `Bag`. MEASURED: the file declaring `Bag` AND
	//     `Pair = (Bag, Num)` could not construct its own field. See `canonicalTupleElement`.
	//   • **A COMPILER-SYNTHESIZED layout** still carries `CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath`, the same probe a file
	//     declaring none of a contested name makes: a type no source spells resolves like a stranger, which is
	//     the one answer that is the same for every reader of it.
	//
	// ⚠ The parameter is NOT named `declFilePath`, deliberately: the two `Array` ELEMENT sites above have no
	// declaration to name, and a parameter whose name is true at some call sites and false at others is the
	// kind of contract a later edit satisfies literally and breaks in fact. (No COUNT is stated here on
	// purpose — this file has already shipped a wrong call-site count four times, and a number in prose is a
	// second derivation of something `grep -n 'declaredSlotType('` derives correctly every time.)
	export function declaredSlotType(declared MaxonType, into TypeNameInterner, slotScopeFilePath FilePath) returns MaxonType
		if maxonTypeTag(declared) == ValueTypeTag.named 'maybeAlias'
			if let denoted = try self.denotedSlotType(self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(declared)), into: into, slotScopeFilePath: slotScopeFilePath) 'theNameDenotesAnotherType'
				return denoted
			end 'theNameDenotesAnotherType'
		end 'maybeAlias'

		return self.adoptType(declared, into: into)
	end 'declaredSlotType'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE CASCADE ITSELF, KEYED BY THE NAME RATHER THAN BY AN ID — SO THE OTHER FAMILY OF ASKER CAN
	// SHARE IT (A4k).** `declaredSlotType` above holds an id minted against THIS index's interner; the
	// whole-program payload/field CLASSIFIERS hold one minted against `project.typeNames` or a file
	// artifact's, and an id is only a number without its table (`typeIsManaged`'s header records both
	// directions in which reading it against the wrong one is a silent wrong answer). A name is the one
	// currency both hold, so the cascade lives here and the id-keyed door is a two-line adapter over it.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT IS ONE FUNCTION BECAUSE THERE WERE TWO, AND THE SECOND ONE'S GAP WAS A READ-AFTER-FREE.**
	// `classifyUnionPayload` resolved a `named` through a cascade of its own with a struct arm and no
	// generic-alias arm, so `Holder with IntBox` fell through to `undeclaredName`, took the SCALAR store,
	// and `h.item = IntBox.create(8)` printed `4557430888798830399` — `0x3F3F3F3F3F3F3F3F`, the free poison
	// (A4i review). Closing that at the caller left the two cascades still disagreeing, and the next caller
	// to hand it a bare `named` — a union PAYLOAD declared with a generic alias — was a clean but wrong
	// `E3011 Unknown type 'PB'` on a program the oracle runs, plus a payload the construct never moved in.
	//
	// **`MapError` IS AN ANSWER, NOT A GAP.** Three names denote nothing this door can re-tag, and each is
	// deliberate: a ranged INT alias (its NAME is what carries the range and the signedness — see
	// `isFloatAlias`), an enum/union name (a `named` IS its type — a payload-free enum's value is its i64
	// tag and a boxed one's is its box), and a name no file declares (E3011's subject). All three are
	// correctly left as the bare `named` they arrived as, which is why the caller's fallthrough — an
	// `adoptType` here, an enum lookup at the payload classifier — is the rest of the answer and not a
	// recovery path.
	function denotedSlotType(name ByteArray, into TypeNameInterner, slotScopeFilePath FilePath) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		let form = try self.declaredFormOf(name)
		match form 'form'
			// ⭐⭐ **A STRUCT-TYPED SLOT (`export var leaf as Leaf`) arrives as a `named` for the reason the
			// alias arms below do** — the SWEEP recorded the declaration before anything could say what the
			// name denoted — and it is the re-tag that was MISSING, with exactly the consequence this
			// cascade's header warns of: `named` reads as an INTEGER. MEASURED before this arm, against the
			// runnable oracle:
			//   • `return self.leaf` from a `returns Leaf` method was `E3005 Cannot return 'int' from
			//     function declared to return 'struct'` — a false reject of a program the oracle runs;
			//   • `fieldStorageType` fell through to its undeclared-name recovery and typed the field's
			//     `loadIndirect` `integer` (the same machine word, so no emitted byte moved — which is why
			//     it went unnoticed);
			//   • `emitFieldLoad` recorded the read as a BOXED-UNION candidate, a slot its own header says a
			//     struct field never touches.
			// It is the arm the payload cascade DID have, which is exactly why the split hid for as long as
			// it did: a struct-typed payload was answered correctly by both, so only the arms the copy
			// lacked — the generic-instance one and the function one — ever showed the disagreement.
			// Re-interned into `into` by NAME exactly as `adoptType`'s `named` arm is.
			structType then return MaxonType.structRef(into.intern(name))
			// An enum/union name denotes nothing this door can re-tag, and that is an ANSWER: a `named` IS
			// its type — a payload-free enum's value is its i64 tag and a boxed one's is its box — so the
			// caller's fallthrough (an `adoptType` at `declaredSlotType`, an enum lookup at the payload
			// classifier) is the rest of the answer rather than a recovery path.
			enumType then throw MapError.keyNotFound
			unionType then throw MapError.keyNotFound
			// ⛔⛔ **THIS ARM MUST STAY A `throw`, AND THE RUNG THAT LANDED FAT-POINTER SLOTS IS WHY**
			// (P1.7a-existentials slice 2). Its predecessor invited the opposite — *"the rung that lands a
			// fat-pointer slot type has to come here and say what one denotes"* — and re-tagging here is
			// exactly the wrong answer, because THIS DOOR IS A READ AND THE SLOT COUNT IS DECIDED IN THE
			// COLUMN. An interface-typed field is re-tagged ONCE, by `normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes`, which
			// goes through `StructLayout.retypeField` and so rebuilds the two-slot prefix sum with it.
			// Answering `MaxonType.interfaceRef` here instead would hand `Parser.emitFieldLoad` an
			// `interfaceRef` for a field the layout budgeted ONE slot for: the witness load at
			// `offset + InterfaceWitnessHalfOffset` would read the NEXT field, and every later field's offset
			// would be one slot short — a wrong answer at run time with no compile error anywhere.
			//
			// Its `functionAlias` sibling below DOES re-tag, and is safe for the one reason this arm is not: it
			// does not change a field's slot count. ⚠ Its `genericAlias` sibling is only CONDITIONALLY safe —
			// an instance over an `interface` base denotes an existential too, and that arm now says so.
			interfaceType then throw MapError.keyNotFound
			// ⭐ A RANGED alias splits, and it is the ONLY arm that does: the FORM is one declaration
			// (`typealias X = <primitive>(range)`, which is why `TypeDeclKind` holds one case for both,
			// exactly as `typeDeclKeyword` gives all three alias forms one keyword), while what the slot
			// DENOTES differs by the underlying primitive. See `rangedAliasSlotType`.
			rangedAlias then return try self.rangedAliasSlotType(name)
			// A FUNCTION-typed slot (`export var op as UnaryOp`) arrives as a `named` — the SWEEP that
			// recorded its declaration ran before the function alias was known — so it is re-tagged
			// `function` HERE (P1.5-A1). One place, so every reader (a field READ, a `Self{op: op}`
			// construction check, the store-width query, a union PAYLOAD's class) sees a function VALUE and
			// not an int.
			functionAlias then return MaxonType.function(into.intern(name))
			// A GENERIC-INSTANCE-typed slot (`export var ops as IntArray`, an `Array` instance) arrives as a
			// `named` for the same reason a function alias does, and is re-tagged `genericInstance` here
			// (P1.7). One place, so every reader (a field READ, a `Self{ops: ops}` construction check, the
			// array method dispatch, the drop cascade) sees the instance and not an `int`. The gid is this
			// index's own, needing no interner remap.
			//
			// ⛔⛔ **UNLESS THE BASE IS AN `interface`, IN WHICH CASE IT IS THE ARM ABOVE.**
			// `instanceDenotedType` makes `Seq with Integer` the EXISTENTIAL `Seq`, so a field declared with such
			// an alias is a two-slot fat pointer that `normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes` re-tags through
			// `retypeField` — the same one place, for the same reason. Re-tagging it `genericInstance` here would
			// be the exact wrong answer the `interfaceType` arm's header describes, arriving through the alias
			// spelling instead of the bare one: one slot budgeted, a witness loaded from the next field.
			genericAlias then return try self.slotTypeOfGenericAlias(name, slotScopeFilePath: slotScopeFilePath)
			// ⭐ A TUPLE-ALIAS-typed slot (`export var p as Pair`, `typealias Pair = (Int, Int)`) is
			// re-tagged to the tuple's own `structRef` — the read-side twin of `parseTypeReference`'s
			// tuple-alias arm. A tuple alias mints NO identity of its own, so the tag carries the CANONICAL
			// tuple name and a `Pair`-typed slot and a `(Int, Int)`-typed one are ONE type, which is the
			// whole rule the alias form has.
			//
			// ⚠ **MEASURED, A3e** — without it, three of this door's readers answered by the filesystem's
			// walk order: `var p as Pair` was `E3005 cannot assign 'struct' to variable 'p' of type 'int'`,
			// a tuple SLOT spelled `(Pair, Int)` canonicalized to `__Tuple2.Pair.int` and read its own
			// element as an int, and `Array with Pair` refused the `push` of a matching literal — each on a
			// program the other walk order compiled and ran. It is also what retired
			// `classifyUnionPayload`'s own `denotedAggregateName` step: a tuple-alias payload is answered by
			// THIS arm now, so the payload door no longer pre-resolves a name the shared cascade resolves.
			tupleAlias then return MaxonType.structRef(into.intern(self.tupleAliasTarget(name)))
		end 'form'
	end 'denotedSlotType'

	// What a slot declared with the GENERIC alias `name` denotes — its instance, or `MapError.keyNotFound`
	// when that instance is an EXISTENTIAL, which this door may not re-tag (see the `interfaceType` arm).
	// It asks `instanceDenotedType`, the one place the `type`/`interface` base split is made, rather than
	// re-testing the base here: a second reading of that split is a field sized one way and read the other.
	function slotTypeOfGenericAlias(name ByteArray, slotScopeFilePath FilePath) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		let denoted = self.instanceDenotedType(self.genericAliasInstanceFrom(name, readerFilePath: slotScopeFilePath), into: self.typeNames)
		if maxonTypeTag(denoted) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'anExistentialSlot'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'anExistentialSlot'

		return denoted
	end 'slotTypeOfGenericAlias'

	// ⭐ What a slot declared with the RANGED alias `name` denotes — the one form whose declaration and whose
	// meaning part company.
	//
	// A ranged FLOAT alias (`export var mass as Weight`) is the one `named` whose VALUE is a genuine f64.
	// Left overloaded it reads as an INTEGER (`tagIsIntegral`), which made `Self{mass: 2.5}` a false E3009 on
	// legal code while `Self{mass: 3}` was admitted unconverted into a slot the store then wrote as an i64.
	// Re-tagged in the ONE door a field's type comes out of, so the construction check, the field READ, the
	// field WRITE and the store-width query all see the float and cannot disagree about it. It returns the
	// bare primitive and interns nothing: a `float` carries no name (`typeNameIdOf` is `UnnamedTypeId`), so
	// there is nothing to adopt. The RANGE is dropped exactly as `TypeResolution.resolveNamedType` drops it —
	// a float alias has no range check today.
	//
	// ⚠ An INT alias is deliberately NOT re-tagged: `named` already reads as an integer everywhere, and its
	// NAME is what carries the range and the signedness that `valueIsUnsigned` and the range checks read off
	// it. So it throws, exactly as the enum/union arms do, and for the same reason — it is an ANSWER.
	function rangedAliasSlotType(name ByteArray) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		if not self.isFloatAlias(name) 'integerAlias'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'integerAlias'

		return MaxonType.float
	end 'rangedAliasSlotType'

	// Does field `index` of struct `structName` own DROPPABLE HEAP — a String, a struct, or a
	// payload-bearing (boxed) union? Drives the struct's `__destruct_<T>` drop CASCADE: every field this
	// answers `true` for is freed (through its own destructor / decref) when the struct box is torn down.
	// P1.4b wave 2c folded the boxed-union field into that cascade — earlier waves refused such a field
	// because the drop did not yet reach it. It is answered ENTIRELY INSIDE this index — over `self.typeNames`, the
	// interner the layout's types were minted against — so it neither re-interns the field type (no
	// `adoptType`, unlike `fieldTypeOf`) nor boxes a `DeclaredEnum`/`DeclaredStruct` to decide struct- or
	// enum-hood: the `contains` guards answer allocation-free, which matters because a scalar field (the
	// overwhelming majority) must reach the `false` return without allocating. A ranged int alias and a
	// payload-free enum are scalars; a `float`/`int`/`bool` is a scalar.
	//
	// The struct and index are preconditions, not cases — every caller reached here through a `structOf`
	// that answered `declared`, and `index` is one `indexOfField` handed out.
	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH OWNERSHIP PROTOCOL CONFORMER `typeName`'s VALUE HALF TAKES — the ONE decision both witness
	// ownership words are derived from** (P1.7a-existentials slice 2b). See `ExistentialOwnershipProtocol` for
	// why it is one decision rather than two agreeing ones.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE BYTE-RECORD BUILTINS ARE ASKED FIRST BECAUSE NO REGISTRY HOLDS THEM.** A `String` or `Character`
	// widened to `Hashable`/`Equatable` is an intrinsic conformance (`isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance`) declared by
	// no file, so `declaredFormOf` answers nothing for it and the managed test below would call the one managed
	// thing in the language unmanaged — a leaked record for every existential-typed String field. `int`, `float`
	// and `bool` fall through to `none`, which is right: their value half is not a pointer at all.
	export function existentialOwnershipProtocol(typeName ByteArray) returns ExistentialOwnershipProtocol
		if typeName.equals(StringConformerName) or typeName.equals(CharacterConformerName) 'byteRecord'
			return ExistentialOwnershipProtocol.byteRecord
		end 'byteRecord'
		if not self.declaredNameIsManaged(typeName) 'ownsNothing'
			return ExistentialOwnershipProtocol.none
		end 'ownsNothing'

		return ExistentialOwnershipProtocol.aggregate
	end 'existentialOwnershipProtocol'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A WITNESS TABLE'S `destroyFunc@8` HOLDS FOR CONFORMER `typeName` — or EMPTY when the
	// conformer owns nothing droppable, which the table records as the 0 `__drop_existential` treats as inert**
	// (P1.7a-existentials slice 2). ONE answer, read by the site that STAMPS the reloc
	// (`LowerMaxonToStd.ensureWitnessTable`) and by the site that ROOTS its target (`MmRuntime.rootConformerDrops`),
	// so the symbol a table names and the symbol the pipeline installs cannot come apart — the arrangement
	// `typeParamDropCallee` already has for the layout descriptor's `destroyFunc@40`.
	//
	// ⚠ **EMPTY IS NOT A SENTINEL RETURN, IT IS THE ANSWER** — "this conformer has no destructor" is a real
	// state of the world with a real encoding in the table (the zero word), and both callers act on it as such.
	export function existentialDestroyCallee(typeName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		match self.existentialOwnershipProtocol(typeName) 'protocol'
			byteRecord then return StrDecrefName
			aggregate then return managedNameDropCallee(self, declaredName: self.denotedAggregateName(typeName))
			none then return EmptySourceText
		end 'protocol'
	end 'existentialDestroyCallee'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A WITNESS TABLE'S `retainFunc@16` HOLDS FOR CONFORMER `typeName`** (P1.7a-existentials slice
	// 2b) — the exact dual of the destroy callee above, off the SAME protocol answer, so the two words are
	// non-zero together by construction.
	export function existentialRetainCallee(typeName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		return retainCalleeForProtocol(self.existentialOwnershipProtocol(typeName))
	end 'existentialRetainCallee'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A LAYOUT DESCRIPTOR'S `retainFunc@64` HOLDS FOR INSTANCE `id`'s TYPE ARGUMENT AT `position`
	// (W41-opaque)** — the witness table's `retainFunc@16` asked of a generic's type ARGUMENT rather than of an
	// existential's conformer. Same three protocols, same callees, ONE speller
	// (`retainCalleeForProtocol`): the two words answer the identical question — *"how does a body that cannot
	// name this type take a reference to it?"* — and a second spelling is exactly how they would come to
	// disagree about a `String`, the one type whose answer is a COPY rather than an incref.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE PROTOCOL IS READ OFF THE TYPE, NOT OFF A CONFORMER NAME, AND THE TWO ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE
	// HERE.** `conformerNameOfType` REDUCES a generic instance to its base (`Array with String` → `Array`,
	// which no `declaredFormOf` holds), because that is right for the CONFORMANCE question; routed through it,
	// a `Holder with (Array with String)` would answer `none` and store an array without a reference. This
	// asks `typeIsManaged`, which is the boundary the concrete record's destructor drops on.
	//
	// EMPTY is the ANSWER for an argument that owns no record at all — a scalar, a ranged alias, a payload-free
	// enum — and not a sentinel: the zero word is what makes `__retain_type_param` inert, exactly as the
	// witness pair's zero makes `__retain_existential` inert.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS *NOT* EMPTY FOR A "TRIVIAL" ARGUMENT, WHICH IS WHAT THIS SAID (W41-trivial).** An all-scalar
	// STRUCT is trivial in the CONSUME sense and is still a refcounted box, so its answer is `__mm_retain`.
	// The claim mattered because `destroyFunc@40` was gated on the consume boundary while this word was gated
	// on ownership: the sentence made the two look like they agreed while the code had them differing on
	// exactly that class. They now read one predicate (`instanceArgIsManaged`).
	export function typeParamRetainCallee(id GenericInstanceId, position TypeParamPosition) returns ByteArray
		return retainCalleeForProtocol(self.typeParamOwnershipProtocol(id, position: position))
	end 'typeParamRetainCallee'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE ANSWER BOTH OF A DESCRIPTOR BLOCK'S OWNERSHIP WORDS ARE A SPELLING OF (found at review)** —
	// `retainFunc@64` through `retainCalleeForProtocol`, `destroyFunc@40` through `typeParamDropCallee`. Written
	// once so the pair is non-zero TOGETHER by construction rather than by two gates agreeing, which is what
	// the witness table's `destroyFunc@8`/`retainFunc@16` have off one `existentialOwnershipProtocol` and what
	// these two twice failed to have.
	//
	// ⛔ **A BUILTIN CONTAINER'S DESCRIPTOR CARRIES NEITHER, BECAUSE NO SHARED BODY READS ONE OUT OF IT.**
	// These two words are consumed only by `__retain_type_param` / `__drop_type_param`, which are handed the
	// block of the ENCLOSING GENERIC INSTANCE the compiled-once body belongs to — and a SYNTHESIZED
	// `Array`/`Set`/`Map` record has no such body: it is served by the runtime off its own stamps. Stamping them
	// on an `Array with String` would bake relocations nothing loads, and the callee they name is a HEAP one,
	// so `MmRuntime.rootManagedOpaqueArrayElementDrops` would then set `usesHeap` for programs that allocate
	// nothing: MEASURED at review, `function main() returns ExitCode return 7 end` grew 1,592 → **1,672** bytes
	// on exactly that.
	//
	// ⭐⭐⭐ **BUT "AN `Array` HAS NO SHARED BODY" STOPPED BEING TRUE, AND THE GAP WAS A DOUBLE FREE (W59).** The
	// gate used to be `genericInstanceHasBaseLayout` alone, on the premise quoted above that only a declared
	// NON-builtin generic is compiled once per instantiation. A record-collapsed `type Array uses Element` is
	// both at once: the record is the compiler's, and the METHODS are a shared body the corpus declares — so
	// `Array.at`'s `return try managed.get(i)` emitted `__retain_type_param(descriptor, element)`, read a ZERO
	// `retainFunc@64`, and handed the caller a BORROW where the hand-off convention promises a `+1`.
	// **MEASURED: `let head = a.at(0)` over an `Array with String` printed the right answer and then took a
	// SEGFAULT at teardown** — `main`'s `__str_decref` and the array's own element walk freeing one record
	// twice. `emitOwnedValueReturn`'s opaque arm was doing its job; the word it dispatches through was 0.
	//
	// ⇒ The gate is "does a shared body read this block?", and a record-collapsed conformer answers yes. The
	// size argument above is untouched: a program that does not DECLARE `type Array` gets `undeclared` from
	// `declarationIsTheManagedRecord` and the same zero words it always had.
	function typeParamOwnershipProtocol(id GenericInstanceId, position TypeParamPosition) returns ExistentialOwnershipProtocol
		if not (self.genericInstanceHasBaseLayout(id) or self.declarationIsTheManagedRecord(self.genericInstanceBaseName(id))) 'noSharedBodyReadsThisBlock'
			return ExistentialOwnershipProtocol.none
		end 'noSharedBodyReadsThisBlock'

		return self.typeOwnershipProtocol(self.substitutedInstanceArg(id, position: position))
	end 'typeParamOwnershipProtocol'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A LAYOUT DESCRIPTOR'S `destroyFunc@40` HOLDS FOR INSTANCE `id`'s TYPE ARGUMENT AT `position`**
	// — the exact dual of the retain word above, off the SAME `typeOwnershipProtocol` answer about the SAME
	// substituted argument, so **the two words are non-zero TOGETHER BY CONSTRUCTION** rather than by two
	// predicates agreeing. That is the invariant `managedOpaqueArrayElementOf` states, and the arrangement the
	// witness table's `destroyFunc@8`/`retainFunc@16` pair has held since P1.7a-existentials.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT WAS GATED ON THE INSTANCE HAVING AN OPAQUE `Array with T` OR BARE `T` FIELD, AND THE RETAIN WAS
	// NOT — SO THE STATED INVARIANT WAS FALSE AND THE GAP LEAKED (found at review).** `retainFunc@64` is a fact
	// about the ARGUMENT; the old drop gate (`genericInstanceHasManagedOpaqueArrayField`) was a fact about the
	// base's FIELD LIST, and a generic that stores a borrowed `T` into a record it builds needs no `T`-typed
	// field at all. MEASURED, against the runnable oracle: `type Pack uses T` with only `var n as Integer`, an
	// instance method doing `let pair = (t, n)` over a borrowed `T`, at `Pack with String` — the oracle prints
	// `7` and exits 0, the merge base prints `7` and exits 0, and this tip printed `7` and exited **101**. The
	// store took `__str_clone` through `retainFunc@64`; `emitOpaqueSlotReleases` called `__drop_type_param`,
	// which loaded a ZERO `destroyFunc@40` and did nothing.
	//
	// ⭐ It also fills a slot that was 0 for an opaque `Array with T` built as a LOCAL rather than held as a
	// field — the shared body's `__managed_create` reads its `element_destroy@40` out of this same word, and a base
	// with no such field left it 0, so that array's elements were never walked.
	//
	// EMPTY is the ANSWER for an argument that owns no record, not a sentinel: `stampOwnershipWord` leaves the
	// zero both `__drop_type_param` and the array walk treat as inert — the identical encoding the retain uses.
	export function typeParamDropCallee(id GenericInstanceId, position TypeParamPosition) returns ByteArray
		return match self.typeParamOwnershipProtocol(id, position: position) 'protocol'
			none gives EmptySourceText
			byteRecord to aggregate gives managedFieldDropCallee(self, typeNames: self.typeNames, fieldType: self.substitutedInstanceArg(id, position: position))
		end 'protocol'
	end 'typeParamDropCallee'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH OWNERSHIP PROTOCOL A TYPE'S VALUE TAKES — `existentialOwnershipProtocol`'s TYPE-KEYED DOOR
	// (W41-opaque).** ONE rule, two entries, the arrangement `conformerNameOfType` /
	// `conformerNameOfDeclaredName` already has and for the same reason: an existential widening holds a
	// CONFORMER NAME, a generic instance holds a resolved `MaxonType` argument, and neither can cheaply become
	// the other. The rule is the same in both spellings — a byte record first (its representation, not a
	// registry, is what decides it), then the one managed classifier, then aggregate.
	//
	// ⚠ The byte-record test is a TAG here where the name door tests two names, and that is the same test:
	// `tagIsByteRecord` is exactly "String or Character", and a name door has no tag to read.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND IT CARRIES THE SUPERSEDED GUARD, WHICH IS THE FOURTH DOOR TO NEED ONE (W41-trivial).** A
	// spelling two files contest names no reader (`genericAliasSpellingIsSuperseded`), so the rule that door
	// states applies here unchanged: **a classifier that meets one contributes NOTHING rather than guessing.**
	// `typeIsManaged`'s `named` arm ends in `isGenericAlias(name)` and says MANAGED of such a spelling, and
	// "managed" is spent HERE on naming a per-type callee — which for a contested alias is exactly the
	// `genericAliasInstance` refusal, reached as a compiler PANIC instead of the diagnostic the program had
	// already earned. `none` is the identity: both descriptor words stay 0 and both runtime entries are inert,
	// which is what a doomed program's descriptor should hold.
	//
	// ⚠ **MEASURED (W41-trivial), on three committed `typealias-file-scope` cases**: widening the descriptor's
	// DROP gate to this boundary without the guard put a contested `Bag`/`N0` through
	// `typeParamDropCallee` → `managedFieldDropCallee` → `managedNameDropCallee`, and
	// `noteDestructorUsage` died at `SignatureIndex.maxon`'s refusal with a stack trace where two `error.`
	// cases expect an E2015 and one expects a clean compile. It is A3k's measurement arriving at a new door,
	// for A3k's reason.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND AN INTERFACE-TYPED ARGUMENT IS THE FIFTH DOOR TO NEED THE SAME GUARD, FOR THE SAME REASON
	// (found at review).** `declaredNameIsManaged` answers `true` of an interface NAME deliberately — that arm
	// serves `existentialDestroyCallee`, whose caller has already routed on a tag — so "managed" reaches here
	// for a `Box with Named`, and "managed" is spent HERE on naming a per-type callee that an existential does
	// not have: it is released through `__drop_existential(witness, value)`, and a descriptor word carries no
	// witness. MEASURED: `typealias NamedBox = Box with Named` compiled at the merge base to a positioned
	// **E2015** naming the fat pointer, and at this tip died in `noteDestructorUsage` with
	// `managedNameDropCallee: 'Named' is an interface` and a stack trace — the diagnostic the program had
	// already earned, replaced by a crash. That is `managedOpaqueArrayElementOf`'s own recorded failure
	// (`genericInstanceHasBaseLayout`, "the build already doomed") arriving at the boundary this rung widened.
	export function typeOwnershipProtocol(t MaxonType) returns ExistentialOwnershipProtocol
		if tagIsByteRecord(maxonTypeTag(t)) 'byteRecord'
			return ExistentialOwnershipProtocol.byteRecord
		end 'byteRecord'
		if self.typeArgSpellingIsSuperseded(t) 'theContestReplacedThisOne'
			return ExistentialOwnershipProtocol.none
		end 'theContestReplacedThisOne'
		if not self.typeIsManaged(t, from: self.typeNames) 'ownsNothing'
			return ExistentialOwnershipProtocol.none
		end 'ownsNothing'
		if self.typeDenotesInterface(t) 'anExistentialHasNoStaticProtocol'
			return ExistentialOwnershipProtocol.none
		end 'anExistentialHasNoStaticProtocol'

		return ExistentialOwnershipProtocol.aggregate
	end 'typeOwnershipProtocol'

	// Is `t` a value held at an INTERFACE type — a two-word fat pointer whose retain and release are its
	// WITNESS's and not any name a descriptor word can hold? BOTH spellings, because a generic type ARGUMENT
	// is a third producer of slot types that `normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes` does not re-tag: the real parse
	// mints `interfaceRef`, and `Box with Named`'s argument arrives as a bare `named` — which is exactly the
	// spelling `managedNameDropCallee`'s panic says cannot exist.
	function typeDenotesInterface(t MaxonType) returns bool
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(t)
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'taggedExistential'
			return true
		end 'taggedExistential'

		if not tagMayNameAggregate(tag) 'namesNoDeclaration'
			return false
		end 'namesNoDeclaration'

		let form = try self.declaredFormOf(self.denotedSlotAggregateName(t)) otherwise return false
		return form == TypeDeclKind.interfaceType
	end 'typeDenotesInterface'

	// Is struct field `index` declared at an INTERFACE type — the two-slot fat pointer whose drop is
	// `__drop_existential` rather than a statically named callee (P1.7a-existentials slice 2)? Asked through
	// the index (not off a caller's own layout) so the destructor cascade, the `usesDropExistential` gate and
	// the struct-clone refusal all read ONE answer.
	export function structFieldIsExistential(structName ByteArray, index FieldIndex) returns bool
		let layout = try self.structTypes.get(structName) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.structFieldIsExistential: no `type {String.from(structName)}` is declared — every caller asks `structOf` first")
		return maxonTypeTag(try layout.fieldTypes.get(index) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.structFieldIsExistential: field {index} is out of range for `type {String.from(structName)}`, which declares {layout.fieldCount()} — every index handed out came from `indexOfField`")) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef
	end 'structFieldIsExistential'

	export function structFieldIsManaged(structName ByteArray, index FieldIndex) returns bool
		let layout = try self.structTypes.get(structName) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.structFieldIsManaged: no `type {String.from(structName)}` is declared — every caller asks `structOf` first")
		let fieldType = try layout.fieldTypes.get(index) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.structFieldIsManaged: field {index} is out of range for `type {String.from(structName)}`, which declares {layout.fieldCount()} — every index handed out came from `indexOfField`")
		return self.typeIsManaged(fieldType, from: self.typeNames)
	end 'structFieldIsManaged'

	// ⭐ **DOES A TYPE DENOTE AN `Array` INSTANCE — DIRECTLY OR THROUGH A GENERIC-ALIAS NAME?** A layout's
	// field type is recorded by the SWEEP, before interning, so an `items as IntArr` field arrives as
	// `named("IntArr")` where an `items as Array with Int` field arrives already tagged `genericInstance`;
	// a generic instance's stored type ARGUMENT carries the same two spellings for the same reason
	// (`conformerNameOfDeclaredName`'s alias arm). One resolution serves both, so a rule written for one
	// spelling cannot miss the other — which is how an array `Map` key spelled through an alias
	// (the deleted `Parser.requireHashTableKeyType`) and an array FIELD stay one question.
	//
	// ⚠ **A SUPERSEDED SPELLING ANSWERS `false` RATHER THAN REACHING `genericAliasInstance` (which PANICS on
	// one).** That door refuses a name two files contest because it can name no reader to choose between
	// them, and a whole-program reader must ask this first — the arrangement `conformerNameOfDeclaredName`
	// states in full. Answering "not an array" is the safe direction and the same one that door takes: it
	// costs a contested field its array treatment, where reaching the panic costs the user their compile.
	export function denotesArrayInstance(t MaxonType) returns bool
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(t)
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'instance'
			return self.isArrayInstance(typeNameIdOf(t))
		end 'instance'
		if tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return false
		end 'notNamed'
		let name = self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(t))
		return self.isGenericAlias(name) and not self.genericAliasSpellingIsSuperseded(name) and self.isArrayInstance(self.genericAliasInstance(name))
	end 'denotesArrayInstance'

	// The `Array` instance the type above denotes. Precondition: `denotesArrayInstance(t)`, so exactly the
	// two spellings it admits reach here.
	export function denotedArrayInstance(t MaxonType) returns GenericInstanceId
		if maxonTypeTag(t) == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'instance'
			return typeNameIdOf(t)
		end 'instance'
		return self.genericAliasInstance(self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(t)))
	end 'denotedArrayInstance'

	// ⭐ **DOES A MANAGED-OPAQUE-ARRAY-HOLDING INSTANCE HAVE A DEEP-CLONEABLE ELEMENT (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b-β)?**
	// The GATE on the descriptor `copyFunc@32` stamp — the CLONE dual of the drop word's gate
	// (`typeParamDropCallee`'s EMPTY answer), and since W17-c the stamp is its only reader (the cloner is
	// DCE-rooted off the stamp itself, by `MmRuntime.noteRelocatedFunctionUsage`). `copyFunc@32` is
	// stamped, and the element's `__clone_<T>` SYNTHESIZED, only when the element is deep-cloneable by this rung's
	// single-`(box) -> newBox` mechanism: a String / boxed-union / struct / trivial-element-array element WHOSE WHOLE
	// reachable type graph is itself clonable. It MUST read the SAME full-graph classifier the front-end reject
	// (`genericTypeFieldUncopyableInstantiation`) and the concrete gate (`arrayElementSupportsDeepClone`) use —
	// `typeSupportsDeepClone(asElement: true)` — NOT merely the element's TOP-LEVEL clone strategy. The reason is the
	// stamp+root synthesizes the cloner UNCONDITIONALLY for every copyable-opaque instance, INCLUDING in a program
	// that never copies (drop-only) and so never reaches the copy-site reject: a struct/union element that is
	// `direct` at the top but owns a non-clonable field (`Array with (Array with String)`, a `Box with String`)
	// would then root a `__clone_<T>` the cloner synthesizer cannot build and PANIC on a valid drop-only program
	// (`noteFieldCloneNeeds`). Reading the full-graph classifier keeps the stamp/root envelope and the reject
	// envelope IDENTICAL, so neither can admit a cloner the other forbids. A non-clonable element's `copyFunc@32`
	// stays 0 — and such an instance is NEVER copied (a base that copies its opaque array with such an
	// instantiation is rejected at the front end off this identical classifier), so the 0 slot is never read. A
	// DROP-only instance of a non-clonable shape (`Container with (Array with String)`, dropped but never copied)
	// thus stamps `destroyFunc@40` but not `copyFunc@32`, exactly right — its element still DROPs through
	// `__managed_decref`, which needs no single-function-pointer form. Reads the SAME `managedOpaqueArrayElementOf`
	// walk as the drop stamp, so the two agree on which element they mean.
	export function genericInstanceOpaqueArrayElementIsCopyable(id GenericInstanceId, position TypeParamPosition) returns bool
		var visited = ClonerSet.create()
		match self.managedOpaqueArrayElementOf(id, position: position) 'element'
			present(element) then return self.typeSupportsDeepClone(element, asElement: true, visited: visited)
			absent then return false
		end 'element'
	end 'genericInstanceOpaqueArrayElementIsCopyable'

	// ⭐ **THE `.text` ELEMENT CLONER to bake into a managed-opaque-array-holding instance's descriptor
	// `copyFunc@32` (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b-β)** — the CLONE analogue of `typeParamDropCallee`. The
	// direct `(box) -> newBox` clone callee of the array's SUBSTITUTED element (a String → `__str_clone`, a
	// managed struct/union → its `__clone_<T>`, a trivial-element nested Array → `__managed_clone`), which the shared
	// opaque-copy wrapper reads back and `callIndirect`s per element. Routed through the SAME
	// `managedFieldCloneStrategy` a concrete `Array with String` element uses (`arrayElementCloneStrategy`), so
	// the opaque stamp and the concrete stamp name the identical cloner. Precondition:
	// `genericInstanceOpaqueArrayElementIsCopyable(id)` — a `managedArray`/`unsupported` element panics as an
	// invariant break, exactly as `Parser.arrayElementCloneValue` does for the concrete top-level element.
	export function managedOpaqueArrayElementCloneCallee(id GenericInstanceId, position TypeParamPosition) returns ByteArray
		match self.managedOpaqueArrayElementOf(id, position: position) 'element'
			present(element) then return directCloneCalleeOf(managedFieldCloneStrategy(self, typeNames: self.typeNames, fieldType: element))
			absent then panic("ProgramSignatures.managedOpaqueArrayElementCloneCallee: instance {id} holds no managed opaque field at type-parameter position {position} — every caller gates on genericInstanceOpaqueArrayElementIsCopyable")
		end 'element'
	end 'managedOpaqueArrayElementCloneCallee'

	// The SUBSTITUTED type of an opaque field at type parameter `position` — the ONE walk the `copyFunc@32`
	// gate (`genericInstanceOpaqueArrayElementIsCopyable`) and its cloner (`managedOpaqueArrayElementCloneCallee`)
	// read, so neither can drift to a different field. It finds a substituted argument in TWO field shapes,
	// both resolved here so ONE stamp serves both: an opaque `Array with T` ELEMENT (P1.7 slice 3b-ii) and a
	// BARE opaque `T` field (P1.7 Finding A). (The public predicates keep their "Array" names for their
	// out-of-tree call sites; the mechanism spans array elements AND bare fields.)
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT NO LONGER GATES `destroyFunc@40`, AND THAT WAS A LEAK (found at review).** The drop word is a
	// fact about the type ARGUMENT and this walk is a fact about the base's FIELD LIST — so a generic that
	// builds a record out of a borrowed `T` without ever declaring a `T`-typed field took a reference through
	// `retainFunc@64` and released it through a zero. `typeParamDropCallee` is the word's gate and its callee
	// now, off the SAME `typeOwnershipProtocol` the retain reads; the paragraphs below are the two earlier
	// wrong answers of that same pair and are kept for what they measured. The CLONE word stays here because
	// a clone genuinely is about the array COLUMN and not about the argument.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT USED TO TAKE THE FIRST MANAGED OPAQUE FIELD OF ANY POSITION, AND THAT WAS A WILD FREE (W43b).**
	// One descriptor carried one `destroyFunc@40`, so a two-parameter generic's two columns shared one
	// destructor: `type Pairs uses Key, Value` holding `Array with Key` and `Array with Value`, instantiated
	// `(String, Integer)`, stamped `__str_decref` on BOTH — the program printed the right answer and exited 139
	// at teardown, freeing its `Integer` column's words as String records. The descriptor is now one block PER
	// PARAMETER (`layoutBlockOffsetFor`) and this walk answers for ONE of them, so a column is stamped with its
	// OWN argument's destructor or with nothing.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND IT GATES ON THE *DROP* BOUNDARY (`instanceArgIsManaged`), NOT ON THE *CONSUME* ONE — IT ASKED
	// `genericInstanceArgIsOwned` FOR THREE RUNGS AND THAT WAS A SILENT WRONG ANSWER (W41-trivial).** The two
	// questions differ on EXACTLY one class, the co-owned trivial aggregate (`typeArgIsCoOwnedTrivial`: managed,
	// not owned) — an all-scalar struct key, a trivial generic instance — and a `destroyFunc@40` read off the
	// narrow one leaves that class with a **RETAIN AND NO RELEASE**. The two words are stamped from one
	// descriptor a few lines apart and were reading two different predicates: `retainFunc@64`
	// (`typeParamRetainCallee` → `typeOwnershipProtocol` → `typeIsManaged`) said `__mm_retain` of a trivial
	// struct while this said "nothing to drop", so `Map.grow()`'s rehash increfed every key through
	// `__retain_type_param` and the new column's element walk released none of them.
	//
	// ⚠ **MEASURED, and it is a WRONG ANSWER with exit 0 and no diagnostic**: `Map with (Point, Val)` over an
	// all-scalar `Point`, filled in a LOOP, printed `total 0` where the oracle prints `total 15` — `count()`
	// right, every key in the column dangling, because the caller BORROWED (the consume boundary is narrow by
	// design and stays so) and nothing on either side ever took a reference. See
	// `specs-shv2/map.md`'s `trivial-key-column-survives-rehash`.
	//
	// ⇒ **THE INVARIANT, recorded here because this is the walk that broke it TWICE: `destroyFunc@40` and
	// `retainFunc@64` are non-zero TOGETHER.** W41-trivial made the two read one ARGUMENT question and left
	// them behind two different FIELD questions, which is the leak the review found; they are now one
	// expression apiece over one `typeOwnershipProtocol` (`typeParamDropCallee` / `typeParamRetainCallee`),
	// which is the arrangement the witness table's `destroyFunc@8`/`retainFunc@16` pair has always held off one
	// `existentialOwnershipProtocol`. A reference taken through one word and released through the other cannot
	// be balanced by two predicates — in EITHER of their two halves.
	function managedOpaqueArrayElementOf(id GenericInstanceId, position TypeParamPosition) returns ManagedOpaqueArrayElement
		// ⚠ **THIS IS THE SITE THE UNDECLARED HALF OF THE GATE WAS MEASURED ON.** The pre-pipeline root that
		// called this over EVERY interned instance is gone (W17-c — see `MmRuntime.noteDestructorUsage`), but
		// `ensureLayoutDescriptor` still asks it of whatever a `with` named: a `Set with Int` (synthesized — no base struct to
		// iterate) and equally a `Map with (Int, Int)` or a misspelled `Foo with Int` (undeclared — E2055
		// already recorded, the build already doomed). Both hold no managed opaque `T` field, and both used
		// to reach `baseLayoutOf` below; the second killed the compiler with a stack trace instead of
		// printing the diagnostic it had already written. See `genericInstanceHasBaseLayout`.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **AN `Array with T` IS ITS OWN COLUMN, AND THE FIELD WALK BELOW CANNOT SEE THAT (ARR3a).**
		// `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s one field is `managed as __ManagedMemory with Element`, which the envelope
		// collapse makes `Array with Element` — the very shape the `arrayField` arm below looks for — but
		// `Array` is a BUILTIN generic base, so `genericInstanceHasBaseLayout` stops the walk before it can
		// look (its header states that split, deliberately: a builtin has no declared layout to iterate). The
		// answer is not "absent", it is the instance's own type ARGUMENT: an `Array with String` holds
		// `String`s and nothing else, whatever a file does or does not declare.
		//
		// ⚠ **MEASURED as a DOUBLE FREE the day `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s own bodies started serving the calls.**
		// `slice`/`append` inside `type Array` are a SHARED generic body over an opaque `Element`, so they
		// emit `__managed_slice_opaque`/`__managed_append_opaque`, each of which branches on `copyFunc@32`. With this walk answering absent, the slot stayed 0 for `Array with String` too, the
		// wrapper took its TRIVIAL branch — a COW view that retains no ELEMENT — and both the view and its
		// parent then cascaded `__str_decref` over the same records: **0xC0000005 at teardown**, measured on
		// `Array with String`, `Array with <struct>`, `Array with <enum>` and `Array with <union>` alike, and
		// on 25 `array-slice-managed-elements` / `array-clone-managed-elements` / `array-sort` cases (that run
		// had `clone` struck as well, which ARR3a put back and ARRH struck for good — since ARRH a corpus
		// `Array.clone` reaches this walk through its own `managed.slice(0, len)`, so the same 25 ride
		// `slice`). The concrete surface never showed any of it, because a concrete receiver picks
		// `__managed_slice_managed` at the call site and never reads a descriptor.
		//
		// ⚠ **POSITION 0 IS NOT AN ASSUMPTION**: an `Array` takes exactly one type parameter, so its element
		// is its only argument, and a `position` past that finds nothing exactly as the field walk would.
		if self.isArrayInstance(id) 'theArrayIsTheColumn'
			if position == 0 and self.instanceArgIsManaged(id, position: position) 'managedElement'
				return ManagedOpaqueArrayElement.present(self.substitutedInstanceArg(id, position: position))
			end 'managedElement'
			return ManagedOpaqueArrayElement.absent
		end 'theArrayIsTheColumn'

		if not self.genericInstanceHasBaseLayout(id) 'noFields'
			return ManagedOpaqueArrayElement.absent
		end 'noFields'
		let layout = self.baseLayoutOf(id)
		for i in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachField'
			let fieldType = try layout.fieldTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.managedOpaqueArrayElementOf: fieldTypes.get OOB at {i}")
			// An opaque `Array with T` field: the descriptor drops each ELEMENT (P1.7 slice 3b-ii).
			if self.denotesArrayInstance(fieldType) 'arrayField'
				let element = self.containerElementType(self.denotedArrayInstance(fieldType))
				// The tag gate is its own `if` rather than the left half of an `and`, because the position is
				// wanted TWICE below and `opaqueTypeParamPosition` may only be asked once the tag says the type
				// names a parameter at all.
				if maxonTypeTag(element) == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'opaqueElement'
					let elementPosition = self.opaqueTypeParamPosition(element)
					if elementPosition == position and self.instanceArgIsManaged(id, position: elementPosition) 'managedArrayElement'
						return ManagedOpaqueArrayElement.present(self.substitutedInstanceArg(id, position: elementPosition))
					end 'managedArrayElement'
				end 'opaqueElement'
				continue
			end 'arrayField'
			// A BARE opaque `T` field (`saved as Element`): a reassignment drops the field ITSELF through the
			// SAME block's `destroyFunc@40` (P1.7 Finding A) — that parameter's argument destructor, exactly as
			// above.
			if maxonTypeTag(fieldType) == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'opaqueBareField'
				let fieldPosition = self.opaqueTypeParamPosition(fieldType)
				if fieldPosition == position and self.instanceArgIsManaged(id, position: fieldPosition) 'managedBareField'
					return ManagedOpaqueArrayElement.present(self.substitutedInstanceArg(id, position: fieldPosition))
				end 'managedBareField'
			end 'opaqueBareField'
		end 'eachField'
		return ManagedOpaqueArrayElement.absent
	end 'managedOpaqueArrayElementOf'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE "does this type own droppable heap?" CLASSIFIER.** A String (`__str_decref`), a struct
	// box (a struct is always a heap box, dropped through its `__destruct_<T>` or the trivial
	// `__mm_decref`), a boxed union, and a GENERIC INSTANCE (a base-struct box, P1.6-B2, dropped through
	// its own instance destructor or `__mm_decref`) are managed; a scalar (int/bool/float/ExitCode, a
	// ranged alias, a payload-free enum), a function code pointer, and an opaque type PARAMETER (a borrow
	// at this rung) are not. `structFieldIsManaged` (a struct field) and the generic-instance field
	// classification delegate here, so "managed" means ONE thing across struct and instance — the dropped
	// set, the moved-in set and the usage gates agree by construction.
	//
	// ⚠ **`classifyUnionPayload` DOES NOT DELEGATE HERE, AND THIS HEADER CLAIMED IT DID UNTIL A4k.** It is a
	// SECOND implementation of the same question and a deliberate one: it must name the KIND (String vs
	// struct vs nested union vs float) and carry the storage type, where this answers a `bool`
	// ALLOCATION-FREE — which is exactly why neither is folded into the other
	// (`Parser.requireContainerElementType`'s header prices the alternative at one `PayloadClass` box per
	// trivial-element `push` site, and this door must reach its `false` for a scalar field without boxing a
	// `DeclaredStruct`). Delegating here to `denotedSlotType` would cost an `into.intern` per read, so it
	// deliberately does not.
	//
	// ⛔ **THEY DID DISAGREE, AND IT WAS A READ-AFTER-FREE.** Until A4k the payload classifier's own name
	// cascade had no generic-alias arm while the cascade below does, so a `Holder with IntBox` field was
	// MANAGED here and `undeclaredName` there. That half is fixed at the source (`denotedSlotType`), and
	// what remains written twice is the name cascade's *projection* — this one to a `bool`, that one through
	// the shared door to a type.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND THE COVERAGE IS NOW BY CONSTRUCTION: BOTH PROJECTIONS READ ONE `declaredFormOf` (A4n).**
	// The name half of this door is `declaredNameIsManaged`, an EXHAUSTIVE `match` over `TypeDeclKind`, and
	// so is `denotedSlotType`'s. Neither has a cascade of its own left to fall out of step, and a ninth
	// declaration form is a COMPILE ERROR at both rather than the failure this paragraph used to describe as
	// a residual: a FORM taught to one and not the other reads MANAGED there and `false` here, so the field
	// is moved in and dropped by NOBODY — a leak, exit 101 — while the converse double-frees.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **WHAT THIS HEADER USED TO CLAIM ABOUT THE *ORDERS* WAS FALSE, AND A4n MEASURED IT.** The
	// inherited argument (A4k review) was that the cascades' differing registry orders were safe by
	// construction, because an order decides something only for a name held by TWO registries at once, which
	// **E3006** and **E3061** refuse. Both codes do fire — but they are reported by a whole-program CHECK
	// that runs *after* these classifiers have already answered during the parse, so an ILL-FORMED program
	// DOES put one name in two registries here. Measured: with the alias registries asked first,
	// `type Box … end` + `typealias Box = int(0 to 10)` reached `Parser.decrefCalleeFor` for the struct box
	// it had just built and PANICKED before E3006 could print. That is why `declaredFormOf` asks declared
	// TYPES before aliases, and why the order is not a cost choice. **Do not re-derive the old argument from
	// the codes' existence: the codes are late.**
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **`from` IS THE INTERNER `fieldType`'s `named` ID WAS MINTED AGAINST, AND IT IS A PARAMETER
	// BECAUSE THE ANSWER IS OTHERWISE DECIDED BY INTERNING ORDER.** A `named` type is only a NUMBER; the
	// name — and with it struct-hood, union-hood and alias-hood — comes out of the table that assigned it.
	// This index's own callers pass `self.typeNames`; a caller holding a type that has been ADOPTED into a
	// file-local artifact interner (`fieldTypeOf(…, into:)`) passes THAT one. It is the same discipline
	// `classifyUnionPayload`, `managedFieldDropCallee` and `foreignNameOf` already state — a type crossing
	// into this index carries the interner named alongside it — and this was the ONE type-taking query
	// that did not, which is exactly how it went wrong:
	//
	// **MEASURED (T1), a cross-file directory project, both directions and both silent.** The parser's
	// field-read guard resolved an artifact-interned id against `self.typeNames`, so the classification
	// followed whatever name held that NUMBER in the program-wide table:
	//   • a scalar `int(0 to 1000)`-alias field (and a payload-free `enum` field) read MANAGED, and a legal
	//     `Gate.twice(21).n` was refused `E2015` — while the byte-identical single-file program ran 42,
	//     because a single file's two tables are folded in the same order and every id coincides;
	//   • a boxed-UNION field read SCALAR, so `requireFieldOutlivesItsRead` ADMITTED the borrow out of a
	//     temporary that D2 exists to refuse — the program compiled and HUNG on the freed box. The verdict
	//     flipped at the FOURTH unrelated `typealias` in the reading file.
	// Both are pinned by `specs-shv2/cross-file-field-classification.md`.
	//
	// It is answered allocation-free for the overwhelming scalar-field majority — a tag test, then
	// `declaredFormOf`'s `Map.contains`/`Map.get` probes over a payload-free enum, which is a scalar — which
	// matters because a scalar field must reach the `false` return without boxing a
	// `DeclaredStruct`/`DeclaredEnum`. That is why the form answer is an enum and not a `DeclaredEnum`-shaped
	// union, and why `declaredEnumIsBoxed` reads `enumTypes` directly rather than through `enumOf`.
	export function typeIsManaged(fieldType MaxonType, from TypeNameInterner) returns bool
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(fieldType)
		// `tagIsByteRecord`: "does this own heap?" is a question about the REPRESENTATION, and a Character
		// owns exactly the record a String does. This is the root every other managed classifier delegates
		// to, so answering "no" here would leak a Character field, element and payload at once.
		if tagIsByteRecord(tag) or tagIsStructRef(tag) 'byteRecordOrStruct'
			return true
		end 'byteRecordOrStruct'
		// ⭐ **EVERY GENERIC INSTANCE IS A BASE-STRUCT BOX — EXCEPT THE ONE THAT IS A GREEN-THREAD HANDLE**
		// (P1.5-B2c). `Promise with T` / `Promise with (T, E)` names a pointer into the scheduler's own slab,
		// reclaimed by `__gt_promise_drop` at the sites the parser tracks and never by a refcount, so a slot
		// holding one owes no drop, no clone and no retain. The whole argument — including why shv2 stores
		// the handle where the bootstrap boxes it — is on `PromiseType.maxon`.
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'instance'
			return not self.instanceIsPromiseHandle(typeNameIdOf(fieldType))
		end 'instance'
		// ⭐ **AN EXISTENTIAL IS MANAGED, AND IT IS THE ONE MANAGED SLOT WHOSE DESTRUCTOR IS A RUN-TIME FACT**
		// (P1.7a-existentials slice 2). Its VALUE half is whatever the conformer's record is — a heap box for
		// every user conformer — so the slot owes exactly one drop, taken through `__drop_existential` off the
		// witness's `destroyFunc@8` (0 for an `int`/`bool`/`float` conformer, which is what makes a
		// scalar-conformer existential inert rather than a wild free). Answering `false` here is what leaked
		// every interface-typed field: `structHasManagedField` skips the type, no cascade is synthesized, and
		// the conformer is never released.
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existential'
			return true
		end 'existential'
		if tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return false
		end 'notNamed'

		// The lookup below is keyed by the NAME, so it is interner-independent once the name is in hand —
		// `classifyUnionPayload`'s arrangement, for its reason.
		// ⭐ A TUPLE-ALIAS field (`export var p as Pair`, A3e) resolves to the tuple's own name here, so the
		// struct arm answers it — a tuple IS a struct box, and left unresolved it read as a scalar, the
		// field was dropped by nobody and the program exited 101 on the leak gate. See `denotedAggregateName`.
		return self.declaredNameIsManaged(self.denotedAggregateName(foreignNameOf(from, id: typeNameIdOf(fieldType))))
	end 'typeIsManaged'

	// Is `name` the CANONICAL rendering of an interned generic instance? The registry is keyed by the
	// instance's structural KEY and its canonical NAME is derived, so the reverse direction needs an index of
	// its own — `canonicalInstanceNameIndex`, whose header carries the measurement of the quadratic the walk
	// this replaces was running. ONE probe now, over an index kept complete by `indexCanonicalInstanceNames`.
	export function isCanonicalInstanceName(name ByteArray) returns bool
		self.indexCanonicalInstanceNames()
		return self.canonicalInstanceNameIndex.contains(name)
	end 'isCanonicalInstanceName'

	// ⭐ **WHICH INSTANCE `name` IS THE CANONICAL RENDERING OF, *IF NO DECLARATION CLAIMS THE SPELLING*** —
	// the half of the same probe that carries the id, for the callers that must go on to ask something of the
	// instance itself (its base declaration, its drop, its display spelling). `MapError.keyNotFound` for a
	// name no instance renders to, which is every ordinary declared name: the miss is the common case here
	// and never an invariant violation.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THE PRECEDENCE IS THE WHOLE OF THIS DOOR, AND WITHOUT IT THE MINT WINS AND THE PROGRAM FAULTS.**
	// A canonical mint is not a reserved spelling: `specs-shv2/type-name-collision.md` writes
	// `type Box_String` beside a `Box with String`, and every one of its cases is about those two claimants
	// staying DIFFERENT objects with different cascades. Let the mint win and a DECLARED struct's drop, clone
	// or conformance is routed through the INSTANCE's — MEASURED, with the index asked ahead of the
	// registries: six of that spec's cases went red at once, two LEAKING (exit 101) and four FAULTING
	// (0xC0000005). `declaredFormOf`'s own header states the identical rule one rank up — declared types are
	// asked before aliases, and *"the order is not a cost choice"* — and this is that rule's last rank: a
	// declaration of ANY form outranks a name the compiler minted.
	export function renderedInstanceOfUnclaimedName(name ByteArray) returns GenericInstanceId throws MapError
		if self.nameIsDeclared(name) 'aDeclarationClaimsTheSpelling'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'aDeclarationClaimsTheSpelling'

		self.indexCanonicalInstanceNames()
		return try self.canonicalInstanceNameIndex.get(name)
	end 'renderedInstanceOfUnclaimedName'

	// Does ANY declaration claim this spelling? The bool half of `declaredFormOf`, for the callers that need
	// the PRECEDENCE and not the form. Every arm answers `true` because the form is not the question; they
	// are written out rather than collapsed to a range for `declaredFormOf`'s own stated reason — a case
	// appended to `TypeDeclKind` must fail to compile here rather than be swallowed.
	export function nameIsDeclared(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return match try self.declaredFormOf(name) otherwise return false 'form'
			structType gives true
			enumType gives true
			unionType gives true
			interfaceType gives true
			rangedAlias gives true
			functionAlias gives true
			genericAlias gives true
			tupleAlias gives true
		end 'form'
	end 'nameIsDeclared'

	// Bring `canonicalInstanceNameIndex` up to date with every instance interned so far — the SOLE writer of
	// that index and of its watermark, so "which ids are in it" is one fact rather than two.
	//
	// ⚠ It normally does NOTHING, and that is what keeps it off the parse's write path. `deriveInstanceNames`
	// calls it at the one point the declaration sweep is complete, so by the time the parallel parse can ask
	// `isCanonicalInstanceName` the watermark already stands at `count()` and the loop body never runs — the
	// same READ-ONLY-during-parse property that function's header describes for the two name memos, extended
	// to this index. The loop is reached again only for instances interned AFTER the last parse (the builtin
	// `Array`/`Set` instances minted during lowering), which is single-threaded.
	function indexCanonicalInstanceNames()
		while self.canonicalNamesIndexedThrough < self.genericInstances.count() 'eachNewInstance'
			let id = self.canonicalNamesIndexedThrough
			self.canonicalInstanceNameIndex.upsert(self.canonicalInstanceName(id), value: id)
			self.canonicalNamesIndexedThrough = id + 1
		end 'eachNewInstance'
	end 'indexCanonicalInstanceNames'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES A VALUE OF THIS DECLARED NAME OCCUPY MORE THAN ONE MACHINE WORD?** The WIDTH question, and
	// it is a different one from `declaredNameIsManaged`'s ownership question — an existential is neither
	// refcounted nor one word. Exactly one declared form answers yes: an INTERFACE type, whose value is the
	// 16-byte fat pointer `(value, witness)`.
	//
	// ⚠ It exists because that fact was being read off the ownership door, which answers `false` for an
	// interface and meant E3120 admitted an associated type bound to an interface NAME. MEASURED: the
	// requirement's impl received one word where the fat pointer's two were passed, and the program
	// SEGFAULTED (exit 139) on x64 and wasm alike.
	export function declaredNameIsFatPointer(name ByteArray) returns bool
		match try self.declaredFormOf(name) otherwise return false 'form'
			interfaceType then return true
			structType to unionType then return false
			// ⚠ **`genericAlias` STAYS `false` EVEN THOUGH `IntegerSeq = Seq with Integer` DENOTES AN
			// EXISTENTIAL, and the reason is that this door never meets that spelling.** Both callers pass a
			// conformance's recorded `withArgs`, and `Parser.readConformanceWithArg` RENDERS what it read — an
			// existential alias renders to its interface name, `Seq` — so the alias reaches the `interfaceType`
			// arm above under the bare name. Answering it here as well would be a second reading of one fact,
			// reachable by nothing; `parameterized-existential.error.associated-type-bound-to-an-existential-alias`
			// is what pins the rendering this rests on.
			rangedAlias to tupleAlias then return false
		end 'form'
	end 'declaredNameIsFatPointer'

	// Does a slot declared with the bare NAME `name` own droppable heap — the name half of `typeIsManaged`,
	// over the ONE name→FORM answer (`declaredFormOf`) so a new form cannot read managed at one door and
	// scalar here.
	//
	// A name no registry holds is not managed: it is E3011's subject, and a slot the front end refuses owns
	// nothing to drop.
	//
	// ⚠ **ALLOCATION-FREE PER ASK, WHICH IS `typeIsManaged`'S STANDING OBLIGATION — but AMORTIZED, not
	// literal, and the difference is worth one sentence.** `isCanonicalInstanceName` below tops up
	// `canonicalInstanceNameIndex` before it probes, and topping up allocates: one `Set` entry, plus
	// whatever `canonicalInstanceName` mints, per instance interned since the last ask. That is bounded by
	// the instance COUNT over the whole compile rather than by the number of asks, and `deriveInstanceNames`
	// has already driven it to completion before the parse — the fan-out this obligation exists for — so
	// every ask on the hot path is genuinely a probe. What it is NOT is the old literal guarantee, and this
	// says so rather than leaving a reader to discover an allocation under a comment promising none. (The
	// walk it replaced honoured the letter no better: it minted a `GenericInstanceIdArray` per instance, on
	// EVERY ask — see `CanonicalInstanceNameSet` for that quadratic's measurement.)
	//
	// ⛔ THIS HEADER SAT ABOVE `isCanonicalInstanceName`, forty lines from the function it describes, and a
	// reader arriving at either found the other one's contract. Moved to its statement (BATCH10 review).
	export function declaredNameIsManaged(name ByteArray) returns bool
		// ⚠ **A GENERIC INSTANCE ARRIVES AS ITS CANONICAL NAME AT SOME DOORS, AND NO REGISTRY IS KEYED BY
		// THAT.** `genericAliases` holds the ALIAS (`IntBox`); `renderDeclaredTypeName` renders an instance as
		// `canonicalInstanceName` (`Box_Integer`), which `declaredFormOf` matches nothing for — so the walk
		// below fell straight through to `return false` and called a heap box unmanaged. MEASURED at the E3120
		// door: `implements Maker with IntBox` and `with IntArr` both compiled and **exited 101**, the leak
		// gate, on x64 and wasm alike. Asked FIRST because it is the one form whose name the registries cannot
		// see, and answered `true` for the same reason the `genericAlias` arm below is: an instance is a heap
		// box — an `Array`'s record and buffer, a `Box`'s box.
		if self.isCanonicalInstanceName(name) 'aRenderedInstance'
			return not self.renderedNameIsPromiseHandle(name)
		end 'aRenderedInstance'

		let form = try self.declaredFormOf(name) otherwise return false
		match form 'form'
			structType then return true
			// A payload-free enum's value IS its i64 tag — a scalar; a boxed one's is a heap box. `isBoxed`
			// is the same bit `unionBoxDropCallee` routes on, so what is called managed here is exactly what
			// gets a drop there.
			enumType then return self.declaredEnumIsBoxed(name)
			unionType then return self.declaredEnumIsBoxed(name)
			// ⭐ An interface-typed slot owns its conformer's record (P1.7a-existentials slice 2) — the same
			// answer `typeIsManaged`'s `interfaceRef` arm gives, which is where the reasoning lives. This arm
			// is reached by NAME rather than by tag: a conformer name handed to `existentialDestroyCallee`,
			// and any `named` annotation `normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes` did not re-tag.
			interfaceType then return true
			// A ranged int or float alias IS its primitive — a scalar either way.
			rangedAlias then return false
			// A function value is a bare code pointer; it owns no record. (A CLOSURE's environment is owned
			// by the closure VALUE, not by the alias that names its signature.)
			functionAlias then return false
			// A generic instance is a heap box — an `Array`'s record + buffer, a `Box`'s box — so it is
			// managed and dropped by the owning struct's cascade. ⚠ EXCEPT a `Promise` instance, whose value
			// is a green-thread handle owning no record (P1.5-B2c) — the same exemption the `genericInstance`
			// TAG arm above makes, and it is needed at BOTH because a `typealias StrPromise = Promise with
			// String` reaches this door by NAME. `bareGenericAliasInstance` is total under this arm's own
			// precondition (`declaredFormOf` answered `genericAlias`, which is `genericAliases.contains`).
			genericAlias then return not self.instanceIsPromiseHandle(self.bareGenericAliasInstance(name))
			// A tuple IS a struct box. `denotedAggregateName` above resolves a tuple alias to the tuple's own
			// canonical name before this is asked, so this arm is reached only if that resolution is ever
			// narrowed — and it must then still answer `true`, for A3e's measured exit-101 reason.
			tupleAlias then return true
		end 'form'
	end 'declaredNameIsManaged'

	// Is the CANONICAL mint `name` (`Promise_String`) the rendering of a promise-handle instance (P1.5-B2c)?
	// The canonical-name arm of `declaredNameIsManaged` needs the id its own probe already found; a name the
	// index cannot resolve is not an instance at all, so it is a box by that arm's own default.
	function renderedNameIsPromiseHandle(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.instanceIsPromiseHandle(try self.renderedInstanceOfUnclaimedName(name) otherwise return false)
	end 'renderedNameIsPromiseHandle'

	// Is the declared enum/union `name` BOXED (some case carries a payload)? The layout lookup split out so
	// the two `declaredFormOf` arms that need it share one spelling, and answered over `enumTypes` directly
	// rather than through `enumOf`, which boxes a `DeclaredEnum` — `typeIsManaged` must not allocate.
	// The declaration is a precondition: only a `declaredFormOf` that answered `enumType`/`unionType` gets here.
	function declaredEnumIsBoxed(name ByteArray) returns bool
		let layout = try self.enumTypes.get(name) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.declaredEnumIsBoxed: `declaredFormOf({String.from(name)})` answered enum/union but `enumTypes.get` failed — the form is derived from that very table")
		return layout.isBoxed()
	end 'declaredEnumIsBoxed'

	// Does struct `structName` own droppable heap in ANY field — the union-side `unionHasManagedField`
	// generalised to a struct, and the gate that decides whether the struct needs a `__destruct_<Struct>`
	// cascade at all (a scalar-only struct's drop is the trivial `__mm_decref`). Answered over the index's
	// own interner, allocation-free per scalar field, exactly as `structFieldIsManaged` is — it is the loop
	// around that per-field predicate, kept ONE function so the destructor synthesis, its usage gate and the
	// caller-side drop routing agree on what "managed" means by construction.
	//
	// The struct is a precondition, not a case: every caller reached here through a `structOf` that answered
	// `declared`.
	export function structHasManagedField(structName ByteArray) returns bool
		let layout = try self.structTypes.get(structName) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.structHasManagedField: no `type {String.from(structName)}` is declared — every caller asks `structOf` first")
		for i in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachField'
			if self.structFieldIsManaged(structName, index: i) 'managed'
				return true
			end 'managed'
		end 'eachField'
		return false
	end 'structHasManagedField'

	// ⭐ **THE UNION TWIN OF `structHasManagedField`, AND BY NAME FOR THE IDENTICAL REASON**: the layout comes
	// out of THIS index's own `enumTypes` and is read over THIS index's own interner, so a caller cannot pair
	// a layout from one registry with an interner from another. The free `unionHasManagedField` still takes
	// both, because its other callers legitimately hold a PROJECT layout and pass `project.typeNames` — a
	// correctly-matched pair. This is the entry for a caller that holds only a NAME.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE PAIRING THIS REPLACES WAS A REACHABLE COMPILER PANIC ON A VALID PROGRAM, MEASURED (T1 review).**
	// `managedNameDropCallee`'s STRUCT arm already asked by name (`structHasManagedField`); its UNION arm
	// fetched `enumOf(name)`'s layout — a SIGNATURES layout — and resolved that layout's payload ids against
	// the CALLER's `project.typeNames`. In ONE file the two tables fold in the same order and every id
	// coincides; ACROSS files they diverge, so a `some(v Num)` payload whose `Num` is a ranged alias
	// misresolved and the union read MANAGED, demanding a `__destruct_<U>` — while `installUnionDestructors`
	// asked the SAME question over the correctly-paired PROJECT layout, read SCALAR, and synthesized nothing.
	// The two answers then linked against each other:
	//   `panic at Arm64Backend: arm64ResolveCallFixups: bl to unknown function '__destruct_Payloaded'`
	// on a two-file program containing no error at all. It is OPEN #56's mismatch — the one
	// `managedStructFieldDropCallee` closed for the struct arm — surviving in the arm nobody closed it for,
	// and it is the same "an id is only a number without its table" defect T1 fixed one door along. Asking by
	// NAME makes the mispairing UNREPRESENTABLE rather than correct-by-convention.
	//
	// The union is a precondition, not a case: the sole caller reached here through an `enumOf` that answered
	// `declared`.
	export function unionHasManagedPayload(unionName ByteArray) returns bool
		let layout = try self.enumTypes.get(unionName) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.unionHasManagedPayload: no `union {String.from(unionName)}` is declared — the caller asks `enumOf` first")
		return unionHasManagedField(self, typeNames: self.typeNames, layout: layout)
	end 'unionHasManagedPayload'

	// ⭐ **CAN A MANAGED-ELEMENT `Array with T` BE DEEP-CLONED by this rung's mechanism (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b)?**
	// The FRONT-END gate for a managed `slice`/`clone`/`append`, and the exact envelope the cloner synthesis
	// handles — the two agree so the synthesizer never meets an `unsupported` field on a program the front end
	// accepted (else it would panic, a compiler crash on valid input). The element is invoked through
	// `callIndirect` (`asElement: true`), so it must be `direct`-cloneable (String / struct / union / trivial-
	// element array), and its whole reachable type graph must be clonable too. Precondition:
	// `containerElementIsManaged` (a trivial-element array never needs this — its copy is a byte blit).
	export function arrayElementSupportsDeepClone(id GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return self.typeSupportsDeepClone(self.containerElementType(id), asElement: true, visited: ClonerSet.create())
	end 'arrayElementSupportsDeepClone'

	// ⭐ **CAN A DECLARED STRUCT BE DEEP-CLONED — the front-end gate for a SOURCE-LEVEL `p.clone()`?**
	// `arrayElementSupportsDeepClone`'s twin, and it exists for that one's exact reason: the gate and
	// `synthesizeStructCloner` must agree, or the synthesizer meets an `unsupported` field on a program the
	// front end accepted and PANICS — a compiler crash on valid-looking input (see `managedNameCloneStrategy`,
	// which records the measured one).
	//
	// It asks by NAME, not by `MaxonType`, because the caller holds a `StructLayout` and the ids in a parser
	// artifact's interner are not this index's — the mispairing this file's `unionHasManagedPayload` header
	// calls unrepresentable when the question is asked by name.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT GOES THROUGH `aggregateNameSupportsDeepClone`, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT THE TWIN DOES ONCE IT
	// HOLDS A NAME — and it did NOT, which was a real divergence.** Calling the field walk directly skipped
	// the compiler-owned arm, so this gate and the element gate disagreed about `CharacterSet` /
	// `__ManagedFile` / `__ManagedDirectory`. The cycle-break seeding comes free with it: that function
	// inserts the name before it walks, so `type Node { var next as Node }` terminates on the same break.
	//
	// ⚠ NOT `asElement`: a struct reached this way is cloned by a DIRECT `__clone_<T>` call the parser emits,
	// never through the element `callIndirect` that restricts a managed-element array.
	export function structSupportsDeepClone(structName ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.aggregateNameSupportsDeepClone(structName, visited: ClonerSet.create())
	end 'structSupportsDeepClone'

	// Recursively: can a value of `type` be deep-cloned? A scalar owns no heap (nothing to clone); a String is a
	// `direct` leaf; a struct/union is clonable iff every managed field/payload is; a trivial-element Array is a
	// `direct` leaf; a managed-element Array is clonable iff its element is itself `direct`-clonable AND clonable
	// (an array-of-managed-arrays is not, this rung); a non-Array generic instance (`Box with String`) is not (a
	// later slice). `asElement` is true when `type` is reached as an ARRAY ELEMENT (invoked through
	// `callIndirect`), which a `managedArray`-strategy type — a managed-element array — cannot satisfy. `visited`
	// (struct/union NAMES) breaks a recursive type (`type Node { var next as Node }`): a name already in flight
	// is assumed clonable, so the walk terminates, and the synthesized cloner recurses at RUN time through the
	// same field, which is correct.
	export function typeSupportsDeepClone(type MaxonType, asElement bool, visited ClonerSet) returns bool
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(type)
		if not self.typeIsManaged(type, from: self.typeNames) 'trivialLeaf'
			return true
		end 'trivialLeaf'
		if tagIsByteRecord(tag) 'byteRecord'
			return true
		end 'byteRecord'
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'instance'
			return self.instanceSupportsDeepClone(typeNameIdOf(type), asElement: asElement, visited: visited)
		end 'instance'
		if not tagMayNameAggregate(tag) 'notManagedRef'
			return false
		end 'notManagedRef'
		// Resolved through `denotedSlotAggregateName`, the same door the clone strategy this gate admits reads
		// (`managedFieldCloneStrategy`) — a name the gate and the strategy disagreed about would admit a clone
		// nobody can emit.
		let name = self.denotedSlotAggregateName(type)
		if self.isGenericAlias(name) 'genericAlias'
			// A SUPERSEDED spelling refuses nothing — no copy site can name it. `typeArgIsOwned`'s twin guard,
			// off the SAME predicate: both are reader-free classifiers that a whole-program walk drives over the
			// registry's pre-settle memory, and this one was ALREADY reachable — a two-file
			// `typealias Bag = Array with <different element>` with `typealias NestedContainer = Container with
			// Bag` took `requireOpaqueArrayCopyable` straight into `genericAliasInstance`'s refusal on a program
			// whose single-file spelling compiles. See `genericAliasSpellingIsSuperseded`.
			if self.genericAliasSpellingIsSuperseded(name) 'theContestReplacedThisOne'
				return true
			end 'theContestReplacedThisOne'

			return self.instanceSupportsDeepClone(self.genericAliasInstance(name), asElement: asElement, visited: visited)
		end 'genericAlias'
		// ⛔ **A COMPILER-OWNED AGGREGATE IS NOT DEEP-CLONEABLE, AND THE GATE HAS TO SAY SO — its layout is
		// REGISTERED, so `containsStruct` below says yes and the walk would happily report its scalar-only
		// field list as clonable (A4n).** MEASURED before this arm: `type Holder { export var f as
		// __ManagedFile }` + `Array with Holder` + `a.clone()` reached the backend and panicked
		// `resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function '__clone___ManagedFile'`, because nothing synthesizes a
		// `__clone_<T>` for a type declared in no file. `managedNameCloneStrategy` answers `unsupported` for
		// exactly these three names, and this is the arm that makes the two agree — which is what this
		// function's own header calls for: a name the gate and the strategy disagreed about would admit a
		// clone nobody can emit. The refusal the front end then reports is `requireArrayElementCopyable`'s
		// positioned E2015.
		//
		// It is a REFUSAL and not a gap: an OS handle cannot be deep-copied at all — duplicating one hands two
		// owners a descriptor whose `__mf_destruct` closes it once.
		return self.aggregateNameSupportsDeepClone(name, visited: visited)
	end 'typeSupportsDeepClone'

	// ⭐ **THE SAME QUESTION ASKED OF A RESOLVED AGGREGATE *NAME*** — the compiler-owned refusal, the cycle
	// break, and the struct/union field walk, which is everything `typeSupportsDeepClone` does once it has a
	// name in hand.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS SPLIT OUT BECAUSE IT HAS A SECOND CALLER THAT SKIPPED THE COMPILER-OWNED ARM.**
	// `structSupportsDeepClone` went straight to the field walk, which meant the SOURCE-LEVEL `p.clone()`
	// gate and the ARRAY-ELEMENT gate disagreed about `CharacterSet` / `__ManagedFile` / `__ManagedDirectory`
	// — the exact three names the arm above records a measured backend panic for
	// (`call to unknown function '__clone___ManagedFile'`). Receiver arms in
	// `dispatchMethodOnReceiver` intercept those types before a clone can reach it today, so the divergence
	// was latent; a divergence held shut by an unrelated dispatch order is the shape this file keeps paying
	// for, so the two callers now ask one function rather than two spellings of it.
	function aggregateNameSupportsDeepClone(name ByteArray, visited ClonerSet) returns bool
		match compilerOwnedAggregateOf(name) 'compilerOwned'
			managedFile to managedDirectory then return false
			userDeclared then break 'compilerOwned'
		end 'compilerOwned'
		if visited.contains(name) 'cycle'
			return true
		end 'cycle'
		visited.insert(name)
		if self.containsStruct(name) 'struct'
			return self.structFieldsSupportDeepClone(name, visited: visited)
		end 'struct'
		match self.enumOf(name) 'union'
			declared(layout) then return self.unionPayloadsSupportDeepClone(layout, visited: visited)
			undeclared then return false
		end 'union'
	end 'aggregateNameSupportsDeepClone'

	// An `Array` instance recurses into its element (a trivial one is a clonable leaf; a managed one must be a
	// `direct`-clonable element via `asElement: true`); any other instance (`Box with String`) is a later slice.
	function instanceSupportsDeepClone(id GenericInstanceId, asElement bool, visited ClonerSet) returns bool
		if not self.isArrayInstance(id) 'notArray'
			return false
		end 'notArray'
		if not self.containerElementIsManaged(id) 'trivialElement'
			return true
		end 'trivialElement'
		// A managed-element array clones its elements through `__managed_clone_managed(field, funcAddr(elementClone))`
		// — a 2-arg call, so it cannot itself be an outer array element (invoked through `callIndirect`).
		if asElement 'nestedManagedArrayElement'
			return false
		end 'nestedManagedArrayElement'
		return self.typeSupportsDeepClone(self.containerElementType(id), asElement: true, visited: visited)
	end 'instanceSupportsDeepClone'

	function structFieldsSupportDeepClone(structName ByteArray, visited ClonerSet) returns bool
		let layout = try self.structTypes.get(structName) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.structFieldsSupportDeepClone: no `type {String.from(structName)}` is declared — the caller reached here through a declared struct name")
		for i in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachField'
			if not self.structFieldIsManaged(structName, index: i) 'scalar'
				continue
			end 'scalar'
			let fieldType = try layout.fieldTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.structFieldsSupportDeepClone: fieldTypes.get OOB at {i}")
			if not self.typeSupportsDeepClone(fieldType, asElement: false, visited: visited) 'unsupportedField'
				return false
			end 'unsupportedField'
		end 'eachField'
		return true
	end 'structFieldsSupportDeepClone'

	function unionPayloadsSupportDeepClone(layout EnumLayout, visited ClonerSet) returns bool
		for ci in 0 upto layout.caseCount() 'eachCase'
			let payloads = layout.payloadsAt(ci)
			for fi in 0 upto payloads.count() 'eachField'
				let field = try payloads.get(fi) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.unionPayloadsSupportDeepClone: payloadsAt count disagrees with get at {fi}")
				if not payloadClassIsManaged(classifyUnionPayload(self, typeNames: self.typeNames, fieldType: field.declaredType, slotScopeFilePath: layout.declFilePath)) 'scalar'
					continue
				end 'scalar'
				if not self.typeSupportsDeepClone(field.declaredType, asElement: false, visited: visited) 'unsupportedPayload'
					return false
				end 'unsupportedPayload'
			end 'eachField'
		end 'eachCase'
		return true
	end 'unionPayloadsSupportDeepClone'

	// The declared return type of `callee`, ADOPTED into `into` — the calling file's own artifact
	// interner. THE one door out of this index (see the header): a `named` type crosses by NAME and
	// is re-interned on arrival, so the id the parser stores is one its own file's merge will
	// resolve.
	export function returnTypeOf(callee ByteArray, into TypeNameInterner) returns CalleeReturn
		let declared = try self.returnTypes.get(callee) otherwise return CalleeReturn.undeclared
		return CalleeReturn.declared(self.adoptReturnType(declared, into: into))
	end 'returnTypeOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES `callee` RETURN THE TYPE THAT DECLARES IT — i.e. is it a `Self`-returning CONSTRUCTOR? THE
	// ONE HOME, because both sides of a hidden ABI block read it (W43b).** A static has no `self`, so its
	// dictionary columns — the layout descriptor (P1.6-B1) and the constraint witnesses (P1.7a slice 2a) —
	// are sourced at the CALL SITE from the call's RESULT, and that is the concrete instance only when the
	// static returns its own type (`retypeGenericAliasConstructorResult`). `Parser.witnessParamCountFor`
	// RESERVES the block on this answer and `LowerMaxonToStd.witnessParamCountForCallee` SUPPLIES it on the
	// same answer, so the two cannot size the block differently — which they must not, because a witness-slot
	// count is ABI and a mismatch is an argument-register disagreement nothing downstream can notice
	// (`witnessConstraintsOfMethod`'s header records what two such disagreements cost).
	//
	// ⚠ **IT READS THE DECLARED RETURN TYPE, NOT `FuncSignature.returnAggregateName`.** That column states
	// *"the identity that does NOT survive resolution"* and is therefore EMPTY for a struct return by design
	// (`Parser.erasedAggregateNameOf`) — measured: reading it here answered `false` for
	// `stdlib/Map.maxon`'s `static function init(…) returns Self`, so the parser reserved two witness slots
	// and the lowering bound none, and the body's first forward panicked.
	//
	// Allocation-free: it compares the interned NAME in this index's own table rather than adopting the type
	// into a caller's interner, because the question is about identity and not about a type crossing out.
	export function calleeReturnsItsDeclaringType(callee ByteArray) returns bool
		match splitQualifiedName(callee) 'qualified'
			notQualified then return false
			parts(typeName, _) then return self.declaredReturnNamesStruct(callee, typeName: typeName)
		end 'qualified'
	end 'calleeReturnsItsDeclaringType'

	// The tail of the question above, split out because a `match` arm may not open one.
	function declaredReturnNamesStruct(callee ByteArray, typeName ByteArray) returns bool
		match try self.returnTypes.get(callee) otherwise return false 'declared'
			void then return false
			value(inner) then return maxonTypeTag(inner) == ValueTypeTag.structRef and self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(inner)).equals(typeName)
		end 'declared'
	end 'declaredReturnNamesStruct'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES `callee` DECLARE A BARE TYPE-PARAMETER RETURN — a `returns T` whose value the shared body
	// compiling it cannot classify (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-a)?** The descriptor-need seed for the RETURN door: such a
	// body hands the caller a `+1` like every other managed return, and when the value it returns is a BORROW
	// it takes that reference through the enclosing instance's `retainFunc@64`, so the method needs the
	// parameter carrying the descriptor.
	//
	// ⚠ **ASKED OF THE RESOLVED DECLARED TYPE RATHER THAN OF THE TOKENS, because both token answers are
	// wrong in a different direction.** An inner alias may NAME the parameter (`typealias Held = Element`), so
	// a token test misses the reservation and the emitted retain then has no descriptor; and a parameter's own
	// function type spells `returns Element` inside the parameter list, so a token test reserves for methods
	// that return nothing of the kind. The index has the resolved type by the time
	// `Queries.foldDescriptorNeeds` runs (after `allFilesFolded`), which is what makes one reading serve both.
	//
	// Allocation-free for `declaredReturnNamesStruct`'s reason: the answer is a TAG, so no type crosses out of
	// this index's own interner.
	export function calleeReturnsBareTypeParameter(callee ByteArray) returns bool
		match try self.returnTypes.get(callee) otherwise return false 'declared'
			void then return false
			value(inner) then return maxonTypeTag(inner) == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter
		end 'declared'
	end 'calleeReturnsBareTypeParameter'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES *SOME* DECLARATION SPELLED `<anything>.name` RETURN A BARE TYPE PARAMETER (W58)?** The
	// descriptor-need seed for the door one hop past the return: a body that CALLS such a method ADOPTS the
	// `+1` it hands back (`Parser.enrolOwnedCallTemp`), and when the receiver's instance binds that position
	// to the CALLER's own type parameter the adopted value is opaque and is released at scope exit through
	// the descriptor-gated `__drop_type_param`. So the caller needs a descriptor too.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS KEYED ON THE SIMPLE NAME BECAUSE THE PRE-SCAN HAS NOTHING ELSE.** `Queries.foldDescriptorNeeds`
	// walks TOKENS before any body is parsed, so at `cell.get(` the receiver's type is unknown — `cell` may be
	// a field, a parameter or a local bound from a call, and only the last is genuinely out of reach of a
	// resolver, which is exactly why a resolver here would have a HOLE. This is the discipline every sibling
	// signal already keeps and states: `hasContainerMoveOut` records ANY `.pop()` whatever its receiver, because
	// **over-reserving costs one ignored parameter and missing one refuses a legal program**
	// (`requireDescriptorForOpaqueStores`) or aborts the compiler.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND IT CARRIES NO `instantiated` GATE, FOR `calleeReturnsBareTypeParameter`'s REASON**: the emission
	// side is `enrolOwnedCallTemp`, which fires off the value's tag while a body is being parsed — strictly
	// later than the instance registry this scan reads. A declaration-level signal must be answerable from the
	// declarations alone.
	export function methodNameEverReturnsBareTypeParameter(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.bareTypeParamReturnerNameSet().contains(name)
	end 'methodNameEverReturnsBareTypeParameter'

	// The set behind the question above, folded ONCE out of `returnTypes` — the only place the program's whole
	// declared-return column lives. A free function is skipped rather than filtered later: `splitQualifiedName`
	// answers `notQualified` for one, and a bare name can never be reached through the `.` this set is asked at.
	// The map's VALUE is not read here: what makes a callee a bare-`T` returner is
	// `calleeReturnsBareTypeParameter`, asked below so the set and the per-callee answer stay one predicate.
	function bareTypeParamReturnerNameSet() returns MethodNameSet
		if self.bareTypeParamReturnerNamesComputed 'alreadyFolded'
			return self.bareTypeParamReturnerNames
		end 'alreadyFolded'

		for (callee, _) in self.returnTypes 'eachDeclaration'
			match splitQualifiedName(callee) 'qualified'
				notQualified then continue
				parts(_, member) then self.recordIfBareTypeParamReturn(callee, member: member)
			end 'qualified'
		end 'eachDeclaration'

		self.bareTypeParamReturnerNamesComputed = true
		return self.bareTypeParamReturnerNames
	end 'bareTypeParamReturnerNameSet'

	// The tail of the fold above, split out because a `match` arm may not open one. The question is asked
	// through `calleeReturnsBareTypeParameter` rather than by re-matching the return type here, so the SET and
	// the PER-CALLEE answer are one predicate — two spellings of "is this a bare `T` return" is precisely how
	// the seed and the emission would come to disagree.
	function recordIfBareTypeParamReturn(callee ByteArray, member ByteArray)
		if not self.calleeReturnsBareTypeParameter(callee) 'notABareTypeParamReturn'
			return
		end 'notABareTypeParamReturn'

		self.bareTypeParamReturnerNames.insert(member)
	end 'recordIfBareTypeParamReturn'

	// Does ANY file declare a function under `callee` — the `bool` half of `returnTypeOf`, and its twin
	// in the sense `containsEnum` is `enumOf`'s: a caller that only needs "does this name resolve to a
	// call at all?" must not pay the answer's cost to throw it away. `returnTypeOf` ADOPTS the return
	// type into the asker's interner, which allocates for every id-bearing arm and needs an interner the
	// asker may not even have in hand; a `Map.contains` allocates nothing and needs none.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS ASKED OF A MANGLED name (`Widget.byteLength`), never a bare one**, because that is the
	// key the index files a method under (`declaredMethodName`) — see `Parser.staticCallClaimsBase` for why a
	// static call's existence has to be decidable before the call is parsed.
	//
	// Its other two askers are the reserved-callee door and the `try` target's throws check
	// (`Parser.requireCalleeIsNotReservedName`, `Parser.requireThrowingNamedTryTarget`), both of which need
	// exactly this — "is there a declaration behind this name?" — on a per-call path, and neither of which
	// has an interner to adopt into or a use for what would be adopted.
	export function declaresCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.returnTypes.contains(callee)
	end 'declaresCallee'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES `typeName` SUPPLY THE CURSOR PROTOCOL A `for … in` OVER A USER TYPE LOWERS TO?**
	// (conditional extensions) — `current()`, which yields the element the trip is positioned on, and a
	// THROWING `advance()`, whose throw is how the walk ends. Both must be declared on the type itself:
	// a DIRECT-CALL source dispatches statically, so the loop emits `<typeName>.current` /
	// `<typeName>.advance` and the declarations ARE what makes the loop expressible.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT ANSWERS FOR THE DIRECT-CALL RECEIVER ONLY; the witness-dispatched one is
	// `Parser.surfaceDeclaresCursorProtocol`'s other arm.** A dispatched receiver's protocol is a REQUIREMENT,
	// which no callee registry holds, so that arm reads the transitive slot list instead. The ONE part of the
	// rule shared between them is the error type, written once as `throwsClauseIsIterationError`.
	//
	// ⛔ **AND THE REST OF THE RULE IS NOT SHARED — THIS SIDE TESTS EXISTENCE AND THE CLAUSE, AND NOTHING
	// ELSE.** The dispatched arm additionally requires the requirement to take a receiver and to declare no
	// parameters (`witnessProtocolTarget`); this one cannot, because `declaresCallee` is a NAME lookup and no
	// per-callee arity is stored to ask. So a `current(at Index)` beside a throwing `advance()` is admitted
	// here and then reported downstream by `emitCall` as **`E3036: 'Weird.current' expects 2 argument(s) but 1
	// were provided`** (MEASURED) — an argument count against a call the author never wrote, where this door
	// exists to give a clean E2015. That is a REAL gap on this side and it predates the dispatched arm; it is
	// recorded here rather than papered over, because the shape of the fix is a per-callee arity the index
	// does not carry.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **STRUCTURAL, NOT A CONFORMANCE — and that is a DEPARTURE FROM v1, taken against the runnable
	// oracle.** v1 requires the source to conform to an interface that itself declares `current` + `advance`
	// and no `createIterator` (`LowerMaxonToStd.receiverImplementsIteratorShapedInterface`). The C# bootstrap
	// does not: MEASURED, a bare `type IntList` with the two methods and NO `implements` clause at all
	// compiles and runs `for item in self` there (exit 60 over `[10, 20, 30]`). An interface requirement here
	// would therefore be a false REJECT of a program the oracle accepts, and it would buy nothing — the two
	// direct calls are all the lowering needs, and a conformance cannot make a missing method appear.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE `advance()` THROW IS PART OF THE SHAPE, AND SO IS THE ERROR TYPE IT NAMES.** The loop's ONLY
	// exit is that throw (`Parser.parseForStatement`'s cursor step carries the error flag as the header's
	// block-arg), so a non-throwing `advance()` would spin forever — and the loop ABSORBS the thrown value
	// without binding it, which is safe for `IterationError` (compiler-owned, payload-FREE, so the throw
	// hands over a scalar tag and there is nothing to release) and would LEAK for an error enum carrying a
	// managed payload. Pinning the clause to `IterationError` is what makes the absorption sound by
	// construction rather than by a rule somewhere else remembering to check the payload — and it is
	// verbatim what `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:145` declares, so it narrows nothing a conforming type writes.
	export function declaresCursorProtocol(typeName ByteArray) returns bool
		if not self.declaresCallee(qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName: CurrentMethodName)) 'noCurrent'
			return false
		end 'noCurrent'

		let advance = qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName: AdvanceMethodName)
		if not self.declaresCallee(advance) 'noAdvance'
			return false
		end 'noAdvance'

		return throwsClauseIsIterationError(self.throwsOf(advance))
	end 'declaresCursorProtocol'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES `typeName` SUPPLY THE `Iterable` HALF OF THE TRAVERSAL PROTOCOL (W3)?** — a
	// `createIterator()` a `for … in` can call once, in the preheader, to obtain the cursor it then walks
	// through `declaresCursorProtocol`'s two names. `declaresCursorProtocol`'s twin, and STRUCTURAL for its
	// exact reason: a direct-call source dispatches statically, so what the loop needs is the declaration, and
	// the runnable oracle accepts a `createIterator` on a type carrying no `implements` clause at all. The
	// existential's half is `Parser.surfaceIterableFactory`, split from this the same way and for the same
	// reason.
	//
	// The EXISTENCE half is this store's; the classification of what the declaration THROWS is
	// `iterableFactoryFromClause`'s, shared with the existential door that reads the same fact off an
	// interface requirement instead of off a declared callee.
	export function iterableFactory(typeName ByteArray) returns IterableFactory
		let create = qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName: CreateIteratorMethodName)
		if not self.declaresCallee(create) 'noFactory'
			return IterableFactory.notDeclared
		end 'noFactory'

		return iterableFactoryFromClause(self.throwsOf(create))
	end 'iterableFactory'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES `written` RESOLVE AS A DIRECTORY-QUALIFIED FREE CALL — THE WHOLE RULE, IN ONE PLACE (N1b)?**
	// It is asked of the WHOLE assembled dotted name (`utils.helper`, `lib.inner.deepHelper`), never of its
	// head alone. Two
	// arms ask it (`Parser.namespaceQualifiedCallDotsAt` for a chain it has just assembled, and
	// `Parser.parseQualifiedCall` for the `Type.method` name it had already built), and they must not be able
	// to disagree about a program that compiles in one position and not the other.
	//
	// **THE TYPE READING WINS WHEREVER THERE IS ONE**, which is the `declaresCallee` half: a `type utils` with
	// a `static helper` keeps `utils.helper()`, so a directory may be named after a type and no existing call
	// moves.
	//
	// ⛔ **ASKING ABOUT THE HEAD ALONE IS THE DESIGN THAT DOES NOT WORK, and it is worth stating because it is
	// the obvious one.** "Is `utils` a known namespace?" invents an ambiguity neither reference has — a
	// directory `feature/` beside a `type feature` would then have two readings of `feature.f()` with nothing
	// to choose between them. The ASSEMBLED name has one reading: it is either a key here or it is not,
	// exactly as `Wrapper.Idx` and `api.Score` are keys in one map and never compete. v1 settles it the same
	// way (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.maxon:14600`, `project.funcReturnTypes.contains(assembled)`).
	//
	// ⛔ **THE `declaresCallee` VETO HAD TO LEARN ABOUT THE CONTEST, AND UNTIL IT DID IT DECLINED THE ONE
	// NAME THAT CALL COULD REACH (N1c review).** N1c registers a contested free function under its
	// directory-qualified spelling, so `declaresCallee("lib.inner.pick")` — which means "a `type lib.inner`
	// wears this key" everywhere else — became TRUE of the free function itself, and the veto fired against
	// it. **MEASURED: `lib.inner.pick()` died as `E2010: Expected ''min' or 'max'' but got 'inner'`**, the
	// chain having fallen through to the numeric-bound arm, while the byte-identical UNCONTESTED program
	// compiled. The one-dot spelling escaped only by accident — `parseQualifiedCall`'s static arm builds the
	// same string and found the same declaration — which is why the two-dot shape is where it showed.
	//
	// The contest set is asked SECOND, so a `Type.method` key still costs one probe and the extra one is
	// paid only by a chain that is already a declared callee.
	export function resolvesAsNamespaceQualifiedFunction(written ByteArray) returns bool
		if self.declaresCallee(written) and not self.contestedRegistrationNames.contains(written) 'aTypeMethodKeyWearsThisName'
			return false
		end 'aTypeMethodKeyWearsThisName'

		return self.namespaceQualifiedFunctions.contains(written)
	end 'resolvesAsNamespaceQualifiedFunction'

	function adoptReturnType(rt MaxonReturnType, into TypeNameInterner) returns MaxonReturnType
		return match rt 'r'
			void gives rt
			value(inner) gives self.adoptValueReturn(rt, inner: inner, into: into)
		end 'r'
	end 'adoptReturnType'

	// Only the ID-BEARING arms are rebuilt. Every other return type is already interner-free, and
	// the STORED union is handed straight back — which is what keeps the per-call path from
	// allocating a fresh `MaxonReturnType` for every `returns bool` in the program (a Maxon union
	// value is a heap object, every case — see MaxonDialect's ValueTypeTag header).
	//
	// ⚠ It cannot simply delegate to `adoptType` and re-wrap unconditionally: that would CONSTRUCT a
	// `MaxonReturnType.value(...)` for every callee in the program, id-bearing or not, which is the
	// allocation the `rt` pass-through exists to avoid. So the id-free arms answer with `rt` here,
	// and `adoptType` answers with `t` there — two functions, one rule, each returning the union it
	// was actually handed.
	//
	// ⚠ The two id-bearing arms are written out, NOT collapsed to a `named to structRef` range:
	// `exitCode` and `unresolved` sit BETWEEN them in `MaxonType`, so that range would silently
	// swallow both and re-wrap them into a freshly allocated union — the band-append invariant,
	// costing an allocation rather than a wrong answer this time.
	function adoptValueReturn(rt MaxonReturnType, inner MaxonType, into TypeNameInterner) returns MaxonReturnType
		return match inner 'r'
			named gives MaxonReturnType.value(self.adoptType(inner, into: into))
			structRef gives MaxonReturnType.value(self.adoptType(inner, into: into))
			boolean to float gives rt
			exitCode to unresolved gives rt
			// A `String` return type carries no interned name (it is a builtin, like a primitive), so
			// crossing files needs no re-interning — it passes through unchanged.
			string gives rt
			// A FUNCTION return type carries its governing name id (P1.5-A1), so it re-interns like `named`.
			function gives MaxonReturnType.value(self.adoptType(inner, into: into))
			// A TYPE-PARAMETER / GENERIC-INSTANCE return type (P1.6-A) — `get() returns T`. Neither carries a
			// per-file interner id (a source-derived DIGEST since W14 / a whole-program giid), so like a
			// primitive it crosses files unchanged. `adoptType` handles both identically; routing through it
			// keeps the one crossing door.
			typeParameter gives MaxonReturnType.value(self.adoptType(inner, into: into))
			genericInstance gives MaxonReturnType.value(self.adoptType(inner, into: into))
			// A `Character` return type carries no interned name either — it crosses files unchanged.
			character gives rt
			// An EXISTENTIAL return type carries the INTERFACE's per-file interner id, so it re-interns like
			// `named`. This is the crossing that makes `let p = f.make()` in another file name the same
			// interface the declaring file did — which is what the result's dispatch resolves its slot against.
			interfaceRef gives MaxonReturnType.value(self.adoptType(inner, into: into))
		end 'r'
	end 'adoptValueReturn'

	// Re-intern the name an id-bearing `MaxonType` carries into `into`, so the type is
	// indistinguishable from one the calling file had parsed itself. THE one place this index's ids
	// cross out of it (see the header's ID PROBLEM); its callers — `fieldTypeOf`, `adoptValueReturn`
	// (for `returnTypeOf`), and the parser's union-payload sites — re-intern a member/return/payload
	// type by NAME, and none re-derives it. It re-interns by NAME, never leaking a raw id, so the
	// returned type's id belongs to `into` and the "an id may not leave this type" invariant holds.
	//
	// Exported for the union-payload sites: unlike a struct field or a return type, a union payload's
	// `declaredType` is already in the caller's hand (it fetched the layout and walked `payloadsAt`),
	// so it needs this bare crossing rather than a name+index fetch door like `fieldTypeOf`. Routing
	// those sites through here is what stopped a signatures-interner payload id being classified /
	// minted against the parser's own interner (OPEN #52).
	export function adoptType(t MaxonType, into TypeNameInterner) returns MaxonType
		return match t 'r'
			named(id) gives MaxonType.named(into.intern(self.nameOfId(id)))
			// ⭐ **A TUPLE `structRef` IS CANONICALIZED ON THE WAY OUT (P1.10), and this door is where it has
			// to be.** A tuple minted by the DECLARATION SWEEP names its elements the way the source spelled
			// them, because the registries that say what those spellings MEAN are still being built when the
			// sweep runs (see `internTupleType`). Every sweep-recorded type reaches a reader through here — a
			// call RESULT via `adoptValueReturn`, a struct FIELD via `declaredSlotType` — so rewriting it once,
			// here, is what makes `let t = makePair(…)` and a locally-built `(10, 32)` the same type. It is the
			// same arrangement `declaredSlotType`'s re-tags live under, one tier down.
			structRef(id) gives MaxonType.structRef(into.intern(self.canonicalNameOfStruct(self.nameOfId(id))))
			boolean to float gives t
			exitCode to unresolved gives t
			// A `String` carries no name id, so like a primitive it passes through unchanged.
			string gives t
			// A FUNCTION value carries its governing name id (P1.5-A1), re-interned by NAME like `named`.
			function(id) gives MaxonType.function(into.intern(self.nameOfId(id)))
			// A type-parameter reference and a generic instance (P1.6-A) carry GLOBAL ids — a
			// `(declaring type, parameter name)` DIGEST and a `GenericInstanceId` keyed on THIS index's own
			// registry — not per-file interner ids, so there is nothing to remap: they pass through
			// unchanged, like a primitive. ⚠ W14 replaced the type parameter's id and the arm still holds,
			// but for a NEW reason that has to be stated: a POSITION was global because every declaration
			// numbers from zero, and a DIGEST is global because it is a pure function of source text. Neither
			// is an interner id, which is the only property this arm needs.
			typeParameter gives t
			genericInstance gives t
			// A `Character` carries no name id, so like a primitive it passes through unchanged.
			character gives t
			// An existential carries the INTERFACE's name id, re-interned by NAME like `named` — which is
			// what lets a field declared at an interface type and a call returning one cross into a reader's
			// own interner and still name the interface the dispatch resolves against.
			interfaceRef(id) gives MaxonType.interfaceRef(into.intern(self.nameOfId(id)))
		end 'r'
	end 'adoptType'

	// Mix one declaration into the running hash: the function's NAME, then its return type's SHAPE,
	// then — for a named type — that TYPE's name. Names, never ids: an id depends on the order the
	// index happened to intern in, so hashing one would let a new declaration in an unrelated file
	// shift every later id and invalidate every parse memo in the project for nothing.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE BUFFER SPELLING MUST RIDE, AND IT IS THE ONE FACT HERE THE RETURN TYPE CANNOT CARRY (A2j).**
	// `returns __ManagedMemory` and `returns ByteArray` resolve to the SAME `genericInstance`, so
	// `mixReturnType` folds them identically — an edit from one to the other would move no hash, and every
	// caller's parse memo would stay valid while what its calls MEAN had changed. Its own discriminator pair,
	// for `DeclShapeAlias`'s reason.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE VISIBILITY TIER MUST RIDE TOO, for `recordAlias`'s reason one declaration kind over.** Adding or
	// removing `export` on a `static function` changes whether ANOTHER file's `var db = Database.create()`
	// compiles at all (`Parser.evalConstFactoryCallInto`), and that decision is baked into that file's memoized
	// parse. The whole ORDINAL rather than an exported bit, exactly as `recordAlias` states: `module` is a
	// third answer a user can write, and a fourth tier would ride automatically.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE RECEIVER BIT MUST RIDE, for the visibility tier's reason exactly.** Adding or removing `static`
	// on a method decides whether a bare `helper()` in ANOTHER file's `extension` body resolves — and, when it
	// does, whether the call carries the enclosing `self` — so the answer is baked into that file's memoized
	// parse and the key that memo is validated against has to move with it.
	function mixDeclaration(state ContentHash, name ByteArray, returnType MaxonReturnType, declaredSurface DeclaredSurface, visibility Visibility, takesReceiver bool, opaqueParams OpaqueParamInfo) returns ContentHash
		let named = self.mixReturnType(mixBytes(state, bytes: name), returnType: returnType)
		let tiered = mix(mixSurface(mix(named, value: ReturnShapeSurface), surface: declaredSurface), value: tierOrdinal(visibility) as ContentHash)
		return mixOpaqueParams(mix(tiered, value: ReceiverShapePresent if takesReceiver else ReceiverShapeAbsent), opaqueParams: opaqueParams)
	end 'mixDeclaration'

	// ⭐ The opaque-parameter half of a declaration's hash (ARRR) — see `DeclShapeOpaqueParams` for what a
	// reader copies out of it. A declaration that takes no bare type parameter mixes NOTHING, so every
	// program without a generic type hashes exactly as it did before this rung: absence is the answer here
	// too, and `recordThrows` skips a non-throwing clause for the same reason.
	function mixOpaqueParams(state ContentHash, opaqueParams OpaqueParamInfo) returns ContentHash
		if not opaqueParams.declaresATypeParameterParam() 'takesNoBareTypeParameter'
			return state
		end 'takesNoBareTypeParameter'

		var mixed = mix(state, value: DeclShapeOpaqueParams)
		for i in 0 upto opaqueParams.paramNames.count() 'eachSlot'
			let paramName = try opaqueParams.paramNames.get(i) otherwise panic("mixOpaqueParams: paramNames.get OOB at {i} despite i < count")
			mixed = mix(mixBytes(mixed, bytes: paramName), value: opaqueParams.positionAt(i))
		end 'eachSlot'
		return mix(mixed, value: opaqueParams.typeParamCount)
	end 'mixOpaqueParams'

	function mixReturnType(state ContentHash, returnType MaxonReturnType) returns ContentHash
		return match returnType 'r'
			void gives mix(state, value: ReturnShapeVoid)
			value(inner) gives self.mixType(mix(state, value: ReturnShapeValue), t: inner)
		end 'r'
	end 'mixReturnType'

	function mixType(state ContentHash, t MaxonType) returns ContentHash
		return match t 'r'
			boolean gives mix(state, value: TypeShapeBoolean)
			integer gives mix(state, value: TypeShapeInteger)
			float gives mix(state, value: TypeShapeFloat)
			named(id) gives mixBytes(mix(state, value: TypeShapeNamed), bytes: self.nameOfId(id))
			exitCode gives mix(state, value: TypeShapeExitCode)
			// `Parser.parseTypeReference` produces only `named` and the three primitive keywords, so
			// a SCANNED signature can never carry `unresolved` — TypeResolution is what mints those,
			// and it runs long after this. The arm exists because the match is exhaustive, and it
			// hashes rather than panicking so that a future producer of `unresolved` signatures is a
			// cache-key question, not a crash.
			unresolved gives mix(state, value: TypeShapeUnresolved)
			// A struct reference, which a scanned signature very much CAN carry: `static function
			// create(…) returns Self` inside `type Point` is recorded as `structRef(Point)` by
			// `Parser.parseTypeReference`, so every declared factory in the program hashes through
			// here. Its own shape (see `TypeShapeStructRef`) and then its NAME — never its id, for
			// `mixDeclaration`'s reason.
			structRef(id) gives mixBytes(mix(state, value: TypeShapeStructRef), bytes: self.nameOfId(id))
			// A `String` (P1.2 wave A) — its own shape, and no name to mix (a builtin carries none). A
			// signature declaring `returns String` hashes distinctly from every other return type.
			string gives mix(state, value: TypeShapeString)
			// A FUNCTION value (P1.5-A1) — its own shape, then its governing NAME (never its id, for
			// `mixDeclaration`'s reason).
			function(id) gives mixBytes(mix(state, value: TypeShapeFunction), bytes: self.nameOfId(id))
			// A TYPE-PARAMETER reference (P1.6-A) — its own shape, then its TOKEN. ⚠ **THE ONE ARM THAT MIXES
			// AN `id` RATHER THAN A NAME, AND SINCE W14 IT DOES SO FOR A BETTER REASON.** `mixDeclaration`'s
			// rule is names-never-ids because an INTERN-ORDER id shifts when an unrelated file declares
			// something; a type-parameter token is not an intern-order id at all, it is a digest of the two
			// NAMES (`typeParamTokenFor`), so mixing it IS mixing those names — it moves when and only when
			// the declaring type or the parameter is renamed, which is exactly when a reader's memo must
			// move. It also finally hashes `uses A, B`'s `returns A` apart from ANOTHER type's `returns X`,
			// where the position hashed them alike.
			//
			// ⚠ W14 therefore invalidates every warm parse memo ONCE. The query cache is in-memory only
			// (`QueryDatabase`), so there is no on-disk artifact to migrate.
			typeParameter(id) gives mix(mix(state, value: TypeShapeTypeParameter), value: id)
			// A GENERIC INSTANCE (P1.6-A) — its own shape, then its base NAME (never its id) and each ARG's
			// shape (recursed), so `Box with Integer` and `Box with Bool` hash apart. `mixGenericInstance`
			// reads the `(baseId, args)` payload out of THIS index's own registry.
			genericInstance(id) gives self.mixGenericInstance(mix(state, value: TypeShapeGenericInstance), id: id)
			// A `Character` (P1.8 slice B) — its own shape, and no name to mix. It hashes DISTINCTLY from
			// `string`: the two share a record but not a type (see `TypeShapeCharacter`).
			character gives mix(state, value: TypeShapeCharacter)
			// An EXISTENTIAL — its own shape, then the INTERFACE's name (never its id, for `mixDeclaration`'s
			// reason). A signature declaring `returns Producer` must hash apart from one declaring
			// `returns Consumer`: the two pick different witness slots at every dispatch of the result.
			interfaceRef(id) gives mixBytes(mix(state, value: TypeShapeInterfaceRef), bytes: self.nameOfId(id))
		end 'r'
	end 'mixType'

	// Fold a generic instance's base name and args into the hash — the recursive half of `mixType`'s
	// `genericInstance` arm, split out because it decodes the registry and folds a list.
	function mixGenericInstance(state ContentHash, id GenericInstanceId) returns ContentHash
		var out = mixBytes(state, bytes: self.nameOfId(self.genericInstances.instanceBaseId(id)))
		for arg in self.genericInstances.instanceArgs(id) 'eachArg'
			out = self.mixType(out, t: arg)
		end 'eachArg'
		return out
	end 'mixGenericInstance'

	// A struct name as a READER outside this index must see it: a TUPLE name is canonicalized (P1.10), every
	// other name is itself. Split out of `adoptType`'s arm so the `__Tuple` prefix test — which every
	// `structRef` crossing pays — is one `bytesStartWith` and states why it is there.
	//
	// The `allFilesFolded` gate is the same one every whole-program answer is behind: before it, a tuple's
	// canonical form is not yet computable and the sweep's own reads must see the spelling the sweep minted.
	function canonicalNameOfStruct(name ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		if not self.allFilesFolded or not isTupleTypeName(name) 'notACanonicalizableTuple'
			return name
		end 'notACanonicalizableTuple'
		return self.canonicalTupleName(name)
	end 'canonicalNameOfStruct'

	function nameOfId(id TypeNameId) returns ByteArray
		return try self.typeNames.nameOf(id) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures: type-name id {id} is not in this index's own interner — every id it stores was minted by foldFile's fold into that table")
	end 'nameOfId'
end 'ProgramSignatures'

// The two field counts the fused record has slots for, named rather than written as a bare `2`/`1` so each
// reads as the thing it counts: `managed` plus the grapheme flag, and `managed` alone.
let StringDeclaredFieldCount = 2 as FieldIndex
let CharacterDeclaredFieldCount = 1 as FieldIndex

// `managed` FIRST and embedded whole — the clause both fused wrappers share, and the one every offset in
// the record rests on: `buffer@0` and `length@8` ARE that field's first two slots, and every runtime
// function that reads a String or a Character reads them there. A free function rather than a third arm
// inside the check, because the two wrappers agree on exactly this much and differ below it.
function requireInlineManagedFieldFirst(layout StructLayout)
	if layout.inlineManagedFieldIndex() != 0 'notFirst'
		panic("ProgramSignatures.checkFusedWrapperRecordLayouts: `type {String.from(layout.name)}` declares its inline `managed` field at index {layout.inlineManagedFieldIndex()} rather than first — the record's `buffer@0`/`length@8` ARE that field's first two slots, so every runtime read of this type would be off by whatever is declared ahead of it")
	end 'notFirst'
	if layout.offsetOfField(0) != ManagedRecordBufferOffset 'notAtZero'
		panic("ProgramSignatures.checkFusedWrapperRecordLayouts: `type {String.from(layout.name)}` puts its first field at {layout.offsetOfField(0)} rather than {ManagedRecordBufferOffset} — the record's own `buffer@0`")
	end 'notAtZero'
end 'requireInlineManagedFieldFirst'
